window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["ms"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Anatomy is best defined as the study of:","options":["The functions and processes of body parts","The structure of body parts and the relationship between them","The maintenance of internal stability","The chemical reactions inside cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anatomy is the study of the structure of body parts and the relationship between them.  Why A wrong: Functions and processes define physiology, not anatomy.  Why C wrong: Maintenance of internal stability describes homeostasis.  Why D wrong: Chemical reactions in cells describe biochemistry, not anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Physiology is the study of:","options":["The structure of body parts","The relationship between body parts","The functions and processes of body parts","The disorders of body parts"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Physiology is the study of the functions and processes of body parts and how they work together to sustain life.  Why A wrong: Structure is the domain of anatomy.  Why B wrong: Relationship between parts is part of anatomy.  Why D wrong: Disorders relate to pathology, not basic physiology."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Homeostasis is defined as:","options":["A process of cell division","A self-regulating process by which a living organism maintains internal stability while adjusting to changing external conditions","The transport of substances across the plasma membrane","The replacement of one cell type by another"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Homeostasis is a self-regulating process by which a living organism can maintain internal stability while adjusting to changing external conditions.  Why A wrong: Cell division is mitosis/meiosis, not homeostasis.  Why C wrong: That describes membrane transport.  Why D wrong: Replacement of one cell type by another is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are examples of homeostasis given in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintenance of body temperature","Maintenance of blood glucose level","Blood clotting after injury","Uterine contraction during labour","Regulation of body temperature, blood pressure and blood glucose"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of homeostasis include maintenance of body temperature and blood glucose level, and regulation of body temperature, blood pressure and other parameters.  Why C wrong: Blood clotting is given as an example of positive feedback, not homeostasis maintenance.  Why D wrong: Uterine contraction during childbirth is an example of positive feedback."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Positive feedback is a mechanism in which the result of a reaction:","options":["Reduces the change or output","Is amplified to make it occur more quickly","Brings the system back to a stable state","Has no effect on the original stimulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive feedback occurs to increase or amplify the change or output; the result of a reaction is amplified to make it occur more quickly.  Why A wrong: Reducing the change is negative feedback.  Why C wrong: Returning to a stable state is the role of negative feedback.  Why D wrong: Feedback by definition acts on the stimulus/output."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are examples of POSITIVE feedback? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Blood clotting","Uterine contraction during childbirth","Regulation of body temperature","Regulation of blood pressure","Regulation of blood glucose"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of positive feedback include blood clotting and uterine contraction during childbirth.  Why C, D, E wrong: Regulation of body temperature, blood pressure and blood glucose are examples of negative feedback."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Negative feedback acts to:","options":["Amplify the output of a reaction","Reduce the change or output, bringing the system back to a stable state","Increase the rate of cell division","Stimulate uterine contractions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Negative feedback occurs to reduce the change or output; it brings the system back to a stable state.  Why A wrong: Amplifying the output is positive feedback.  Why C wrong: Negative feedback is unrelated to cell-division rate here.  Why D wrong: Uterine contraction is a positive-feedback example."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The four elements of the body are oxygen, hydrogen, carbon and:","options":["Nitrogen","Calcium","Sodium","Phosphorus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four elements of the body are oxygen, hydrogen, carbon and nitrogen.  Why B, C, D wrong: Calcium, sodium and phosphorus are present in the body but are not listed among the four named elements."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Atmospheric air is composed of which gas in the highest (most common) proportion?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Argon"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Atmospheric air is composed of nitrogen 79% (most common), oxygen 21% and argon 0.93%.  Why A wrong: Oxygen is 21%, less than nitrogen.  Why B wrong: Carbon dioxide is a minor component.  Why D wrong: Argon is only about 0.93%."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"According to the Composition of Inhaled and Exhaled air table, the percentage of oxygen in inspired air and expired air respectively is:","options":["79% and 79%","21% and 16%","16% and 21%","0.04% and 4%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen is 21% in inspired (inhaled) air and 16% in expired (exhaled) air, as oxygen is used up in respiration.  Why A wrong: 79%/79% are the nitrogen values.  Why C wrong: The values are reversed.  Why D wrong: 0.04% and 4% are the CO₂ values."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In the Composition of Inhaled and Exhaled air table, the carbon dioxide content of inspired air and expired air respectively is:","options":["4% and 0.04%","0.04% and 4%","21% and 16%","0.04% and 0.04%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CO₂ is 0.04% in inspired air and 4% in expired air because CO₂ is produced in respiration.  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: Those are oxygen values.  Why D wrong: CO₂ rises markedly on expiration, so the two values differ."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Why does the nitrogen percentage remain unchanged (79%) in both inspired and expired air?","options":["Nitrogen is fully absorbed by the lungs","Nitrogen is not used (reproduced) by body processes","Nitrogen is converted to oxygen","Nitrogen is produced during respiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table notes nitrogen is \"not used/produced by body processes,\" so its percentage is unchanged at 79%.  Why A wrong: Nitrogen is not absorbed/used.  Why C wrong: No such conversion occurs.  Why D wrong: It is CO₂, not nitrogen, that is produced in respiration."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Living cells of algae or water were first discovered by:","options":["Robert Hooke","Anton von Leeuwenhoek","Robert Brown","Robert Hooke and Leeuwenhoek together"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1674, living cells of algae or water were first discovered by Anton von Leeuwenhoek.  Why A wrong: Robert Hooke observed dead cells in cork in 1665.  Why C wrong: Robert Brown is associated with the nucleus discovery.  Why D wrong: The first living-cell observation is credited to Leeuwenhoek."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The human body develops from a single cell called the ____, which results from the fusion of the ovum and the spermatozoa.","options":["Mycoplasma","Zygote","Ovum","Sperm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The human body develops from a single cell called the zygote, formed by fusion of the ovum and the spermatozoa.  Why A wrong: Mycoplasma is the smallest cell on earth.  Why C wrong: The ovum is one of the gametes that fuse, not the resulting cell.  Why D wrong: The sperm is the smallest cell in the human body, not the zygote."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The smallest cell on earth is:","options":["Sperm","Ovum","Mycoplasma","Red blood cell"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The smallest cell on earth is Mycoplasma.  Why A wrong: The sperm is the smallest cell in the human body, not on earth.  Why B wrong: The ovum is the biggest cell in the human body.  Why D wrong: The RBC is not identified as the smallest cell here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which cell is the largest (longest) cell on earth?","options":["Sperm","Nerve cell (Myoneuron)","Ovum","Mycoplasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The longest/largest cell on earth is the nerve cell (Myoneuron).  Why A wrong: The sperm is the smallest cell in the human body.  Why C wrong: The ovum is the biggest cell in the human body, not the longest on earth.  Why D wrong: Mycoplasma is the smallest cell on earth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The biggest cell in the human body is the:","options":["Sperm","Nerve cell","Ovum","Hepatocyte"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The biggest cell in the human body is the ovum.  Why A wrong: The sperm is the smallest cell in the human body.  Why B wrong: The nerve cell is the longest cell on earth, not the biggest cell in the human body.  Why D wrong: The hepatocyte is not listed as the biggest cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The smallest cell in the human body is the:","options":["Ovum","Sperm","Red blood cell","Nerve cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The smallest cell in the human body is the sperm.  Why A wrong: The ovum is the biggest cell in the human body.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C/D wrong: Neither the RBC nor the nerve cell is identified as the smallest human cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The cell is best defined as:","options":["A group of similar tissues","The smallest basic structural, functional and biological unit of the human body","An organ system","A non-living building block"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cell is the smallest basic structural, functional and biological unit of the human body, often called the \"building block of life.\"  Why A wrong: A group of similar tissues forms an organ, not a cell.  Why C wrong: An organ system is a higher level of organization.  Why D wrong: The cell is a living unit."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The word \"cell\" was taken from the Latin word \"cella,\" which means:","options":["Building block","Small room","Nucleus","Living unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The word cell comes from the Latin \"cella,\" meaning \"small room.\"  Why A wrong: \"Building block of life\" is a descriptive phrase, not the Latin meaning.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of \"cella.\""},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The study of cells is called:","options":["Histology","Cytology","Physiology","Anatomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The study of cells is called cytology.  Why A wrong: Histology is the study of tissues.  Why C wrong: Physiology studies body functions.  Why D wrong: Anatomy studies structure broadly."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In 1665, who discovered (observed) dead cells in cork cells?","options":["Anton von Leeuwenhoek","Robert Hooke","Robert Brown","Rudolf Virchow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1665, Robert Hooke discovered/observed dead cells in cork cells.  Why A wrong: Leeuwenhoek discovered living cells (1674).  Why C wrong: Robert Brown discovered the nucleus (1831).  Why D wrong: Virchow is not mentioned in this context."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Cells are classified into two types based on the presence or absence of a true nucleus. The cell type that lacks a true nucleus is:","options":["Eukaryotic cell","Prokaryotic cell","Somatic cell","Gamete"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prokaryotic cells are cells without a true nucleus.  Why A wrong: Eukaryotic cells are cells with a true nucleus.  Why C/D wrong: Somatic cells and gametes are eukaryotic and are not part of this prokaryote/eukaryote classification."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Eukaryotic cells are characterized by:","options":["Absence of a nucleus","Presence of a true (membrane-bound) nucleus","Absence of organelles","Being only found in bacteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eukaryotic cells are cells with a (true) nucleus.  Why A wrong: Absence of a nucleus characterizes prokaryotic cells.  Why C wrong: Eukaryotic cells contain organelles.  Why D wrong: Bacteria are prokaryotic."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Arrange the correct hierarchical levels of body organization from smallest to largest:","options":["Tissue → Cell → Organ → System → Human body","Cell → Tissue → Organ → System → Human body","Cell → Organ → Tissue → System → Human body","Organ → System → Tissue → Cell → Human body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The body is organized as Cell → Tissue → Organ → System → Human body.  Why A, C, D wrong: They reverse or misorder one or more levels of organization."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A tissue is best defined as:","options":["The smallest unit of the body","A group of cells that have similar shape and function","A group of organs working together","A collection of different systems"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tissue is a group of cells that have similar shape and function (examples: blood, muscle, bone).  Why A wrong: The smallest unit is the cell.  Why C wrong: A group of organs forms a system.  Why D wrong: Collections of systems form the human body."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"An organ is formed when:","options":["Similar cells group together","Different tissues are grouped together to form an organ","Several systems combine","Two cells fuse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each organ consists of various tissues; different tissues are grouped together to form an organ (e.g., heart, stomach, brain).  Why A wrong: Similar cells form a tissue.  Why C wrong: Combined systems form the human body.  Why D wrong: Fusion of two cells forms a zygote."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A body system is formed by:","options":["A single cell","A single tissue","Different organs grouped together to form a system","The fusion of gametes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Each system consists of various organs; different organs are grouped together to form a system (e.g., respiratory system, cardiovascular system).  Why A/B wrong: A single cell or tissue is far below the system level.  Why D wrong: Gamete fusion forms a zygote."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The three main structural parts of the cell are:","options":["Cell membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus","Nucleus, mitochondria and ribosome","Cytoplasm, Golgi apparatus and lysosome","Membrane, chromatin and centriole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The structure of the cell comprises (1) Cell membrane, (2) Cytoplasm, and (3) Nucleus.  Why B, C, D wrong: These list internal organelles or components, not the three main structural divisions of the cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The cell membrane is also known by which of the following names? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Plasma membrane","Cytoplasmic membrane","Nuclear membrane","Semi-permeable membrane","Selective permeable membrane"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The cell membrane is also known as the plasma membrane or cytoplasmic membrane, and it is a semi-permeable / selective permeable membrane.  Why C wrong: The nuclear membrane surrounds the nucleus, not the whole cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The cell membrane is described as a protective sheath that:","options":["Synthesizes proteins","Covers the cytoplasm and separates intracellular fluid from extracellular fluid","Stores genetic material","Produces ATP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cell membrane is the protective sheath covering the cytoplasm and separates the intracellular fluid from the extracellular fluid.  Why A wrong: Protein synthesis is by ribosomes.  Why C wrong: Genetic material is stored in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: ATP is produced by mitochondria."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The plasma membrane is composed of two layers of:","options":["Proteins only","Carbohydrates only","Phospholipids, with protein molecules embedded and a chain of carbohydrate on the protein","Nucleic acids"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The plasma membrane is composed of two layers of phospholipids, in which layers of phospholipids, protein molecules are embedded, with a chain of carbohydrate attached to protein molecules; it is formed by protein, lipids and carbohydrate.  Why A/B/D wrong: It is not made of a single component; it is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins and carbohydrate chains."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are functions of carbohydrate molecules attached to the outside of the plasma membrane? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Act as specific receptors (recognition site) for hormones and other chemical messengers","Provide immunological identity","Produce ATP","Are involved in transportation of intracellular and extracellular fluids","Carry the genetic code"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Carbohydrate molecules on the outside of the membrane provide immunological identity, can act as specific receptors (recognition sites) for hormones and other chemical messengers, and are involved in transportation of intracellular and extracellular fluid.  Why C wrong: ATP production is a mitochondrial function.  Why E wrong: Genetic code is carried by DNA, not membrane carbohydrates."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Transport of substances through the plasma membrane occurs by which two broad mechanisms?","options":["Mitosis and meiosis","Passive transport and active transport","Transcription and translation","Atrophy and hypertrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transport of substances through the plasma membrane includes (1) Passive transport and (2) Active transport.  Why A wrong: Mitosis/meiosis are cell-division processes.  Why C wrong: Transcription/translation relate to protein synthesis.  Why D wrong: Atrophy/hypertrophy are cellular adaptations."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Passive transport across the plasma membrane:","options":["Requires cellular energy (ATP)","Does not require energy and includes diffusion and osmosis","Includes endocytosis and exocytosis","Occurs only against the concentration gradient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive transport does NOT require energy; examples include diffusion and osmosis.  Why A wrong: It does not require energy.  Why C wrong: Endocytosis and exocytosis are examples of active transport.  Why D wrong: Passive transport moves substances down (with) the concentration gradient."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Active transport across the plasma membrane:","options":["Does not require energy","Requires energy and includes endocytosis and exocytosis","Includes only diffusion","Moves substances down the concentration gradient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active transport requires energy; examples include endocytosis and exocytosis.  Why A wrong: It requires energy.  Why C wrong: Diffusion is a passive process.  Why D wrong: Active transport typically moves substances against the concentration gradient."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Diffusion is best described as the transport of:","options":["Water across a semipermeable membrane","Gases or dissolved substances from a higher concentration area to a lower concentration area of the transported substance","Large solid particles into the cell","Substances against the concentration gradient using energy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diffusion is the transport of gases or dissolved substances from a higher concentration area to a lower concentration area of the substance (example: exchange of oxygen in a cell performing respiration).  Why A wrong: Movement of water (solvent) across a semipermeable membrane is osmosis.  Why C wrong: Engulfing solid particles is phagocytosis.  Why D wrong: Movement against the gradient with energy is active transport."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Osmosis is defined as the movement of a solvent (water) from a solution of:","options":["Lower solute concentration to a solution of higher solute concentration through a semipermeable membrane","Higher solute concentration to lower solute concentration","Higher temperature to lower temperature","Higher pressure to lower pressure only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Osmosis is the transport of a solvent (water) through a semipermeable membrane from a solution of lower solute concentration to a solution of higher solute concentration; an example is preservation and absorption of water from the urine plexus (renal reabsorption context).  Why B wrong: That direction describes solute, not solvent water, and is the reverse of osmosis.  Why C/D wrong: Osmosis is driven by solute concentration difference, not temperature alone."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A common example of active transport described in the text is the:","options":["Sodium-potassium (Na⁺/K⁺) pump","Simple diffusion of oxygen","Osmosis of water","Filtration of plasma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An example of active transport is the sodium-potassium pump, in which energy is used to transport sodium out of the cell and potassium into the cell against their concentration gradients.  Why B/C wrong: Diffusion and osmosis are passive processes.  Why D wrong: Filtration is not the cited example of active transport."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The process by which a cell engulfs large solid particles or molecules out of the cell environment by infolding the cell membrane is called:","options":["Exocytosis","Endocytosis","Diffusion","Transcription"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endocytosis is the process by which a cell engulfs large molecules out of the cell environment by engulfing it with the cell membrane.  Why A wrong: Exocytosis is the reverse — moving substances out of the cell.  Why C wrong: Diffusion moves dissolved/gaseous substances, not large solid particles.  Why D wrong: Transcription is a nuclear process for RNA synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Phagocytosis, a type of endocytosis, refers to:","options":["Cell drinking (ingestion of fluid/liquid)","Cell eating (ingestion of solid material)","Movement of large molecules out of the cell","Movement of water across the membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phagocytosis (cell eating) involves the ingestion of solid material, whereas pinocytosis (cell drinking) is the ingestion of fluid.  Why A wrong: Ingestion of fluid is pinocytosis.  Why C wrong: Moving large molecules out is exocytosis.  Why D wrong: Movement of water is osmosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Pinocytosis is best described as:","options":["Cell eating of solid particles","Cell drinking — the ingestion of fluid (liquid)","Movement of substances out of the cell","Synthesis of proteins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pinocytosis (cell drinking) is the ingestion of fluid/liquid.  Why A wrong: Ingestion of solid material is phagocytosis.  Why C wrong: Movement of substances out of the cell is exocytosis.  Why D wrong: Protein synthesis occurs at ribosomes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Exocytosis is the process for moving:","options":["Large molecules out of the cell","Solid particles into the cell","Water into the cell","Genetic material into the nucleus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Exocytosis is the process for moving large molecules out of the cell (example: secretion of sodium-potassium pumps releasing waste/ions to the extracellular space across the plasma membrane).  Why B wrong: Moving solid particles in is phagocytosis/endocytosis.  Why C wrong: Water movement is osmosis.  Why D wrong: Exocytosis is not about genetic material."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Cytoplasm generally refers to:","options":["Only the nucleus","All the contents of a cell (including organelles) except the nucleus","Only the cell membrane","The genetic material"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cytoplasm is generally used to refer to all the contents (including organelles) of a cell except the nucleus.  Why A wrong: The nucleus is specifically excluded.  Why C wrong: The cell membrane is a separate structure.  Why D wrong: Genetic material is in the nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Cytosol is best defined as:","options":["The membrane covering the cell","The liquid or fluid content of the cytoplasm that contains substances such as water, ions, enzymes and proteins","The genetic material of the cell","The site of ATP production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cytosol refers to the liquid or fluid content of the cytoplasm; it contains substances such as water, ions, enzymes and proteins.  Why A wrong: That is the plasma membrane.  Why C wrong: Genetic material is DNA in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: ATP production is a mitochondrial function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a large organelle composed of:","options":["A single membrane sphere","Membranous sheets and tubules or canals in the cytoplasm","Coiled DNA strands","Two centrioles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ER is a large organelle composed of membranous sheets and tubules (canals) in the cytoplasm; there are two types.  Why A wrong: It is not a single membrane sphere.  Why C wrong: Coiled DNA is in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: Centrioles are part of the centrosome."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) appears \"rough\" because it:","options":["Has a smooth surface with no organelles","Contains ribosomal granules on its outer surface and is found near the cytoplasm","Is the site of lipid synthesis","Detoxifies drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rough endoplasmic reticulum contains ribosomal granules on its outer surface, so it is rough-having ribosomes, and is found near the cytoplasm; RER is studded with ribosomes and is the site of protein synthesis, where the formation of antibodies also takes place.  Why A wrong: A smooth surface with no ribosomes describes SER.  Why C/D wrong: Lipid synthesis and detoxification are functions of smooth ER."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The primary function of the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) is:","options":["Lipid synthesis","Protein synthesis (and formation of antibodies)","Detoxification of drugs","Storage of calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RER is the site of protein synthesis, and formation of antibodies takes place in RER.  Why A, C, D wrong: Lipid synthesis, detoxification and calcium storage are functions of the smooth ER."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the SMOOTH endoplasmic reticulum (SER)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Lipid synthesis","Production of steroid hormones","Detoxification (of drugs)","Synthesis of non-protein materials","Storage and metabolism of calcium (Ca²⁺)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The smooth endoplasmic reticulum does not have ribosomal granules; it has functions in lipid synthesis, production of steroid hormones, detoxification, synthesis of non-protein materials such as cholesterol and steroid hormones, and is the major site of storage and metabolism of calcium (Ca²⁺) — the sarcoplasmic reticulum of muscle is an example.  All options A–E are listed SER functions; none are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The smooth endoplasmic reticulum of muscle cells is specifically referred to as the:","options":["Sarcoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Nucleoplasm","Bounded ribosome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In skeletal muscle, the smooth ER is called the sarcoplasmic reticulum and is the major site of storage and metabolism of calcium (Ca²⁺).  Why B wrong: The Golgi apparatus packages and modifies proteins.  Why C wrong: Nucleoplasm is the fluid of the nucleus.  Why D wrong: Bounded ribosomes are attached to the RER."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The Golgi apparatus is mainly responsible for:","options":["Energy (ATP) production","Packaging, modification and transport of proteins","Digestion of worn-out organelles","Storage of genetic material"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Golgi apparatus is responsible for packaging, modification and transport of proteins; it is the post office of the cell.  Why A wrong: ATP production is a mitochondrial function.  Why C wrong: Digestion of worn-out organelles is a lysosomal function.  Why D wrong: Genetic material is stored in the nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The Golgi apparatus is nicknamed the:","options":["Power house of the cell","Post office of the cell","Suicidal bag of the cell","Control room of the cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Golgi apparatus is called the \"post office of the cell.\"  Why A wrong: The mitochondrion is the power house of the cell.  Why C wrong: Lysosomes are the suicidal bags of the cell.  Why D wrong: The nucleus is the control room of the cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In the Golgi apparatus, proteins are packaged into vesicles known as:","options":["Lysosomes","Secretory granules","Ribosomes","Centrioles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Linear proteins formed in the ER are packaged into vesicles, which are known as secretory granules in the Golgi apparatus; the vesicles are stored and, when needed, move to the plasma membrane through which the proteins are exported.  Why A wrong: Lysosomes are digestive organelles formed by the Golgi but are not the protein-packaging vesicles described.  Why C wrong: Ribosomes synthesize proteins.  Why D wrong: Centrioles are involved in cell division."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Lysosomes are membrane-enclosed (sphere-shaped) sacs filled with hydrolytic enzymes capable of:","options":["Producing ATP","Breaking down all types of biological polymers — proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids","Synthesizing proteins","Storing genetic information"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lysosomes are membrane-enclosed organelles or sphere-shaped sacs filled with hydrolytic enzymes (lysozymes) capable of breaking down all types of biological polymers — proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids; they are the secretory, vesicular organelle found throughout the cytoplasm formed by the Golgi apparatus.  Why A wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why C wrong: Protein synthesis is by ribosomes.  Why D wrong: Genetic information is stored in the nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Lysosomes are sometimes referred to as the \"suicidal bag of the cell\" because they:","options":["Store ATP","Can be damaged, releasing their own enzymes that lead to self-destruction (autolysis) of the cell","Carry the genetic code","Transport proteins out of the cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lysosomes may be damaged by hydrolytic enzymes (the process called autolysis), and because of this they are known as the \"suicidal bag of the cell.\"  Why A wrong: Lysosomes do not store ATP.  Why C wrong: Genetic code is in DNA.  Why D wrong: Export of proteins is via the Golgi/vesicles."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Lysosomes act as the waste disposal system or \"garbage system\" of the cell and also help in:","options":["Producing steroid hormones","Recycling amino acids after degradation of proteins (intra/extracellular reuse)","Coiling DNA into chromosomes","Forming the spindle apparatus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lysosomes act as the waste disposal system or garbage system of the cell and also help in recycling amino acids after degradation of proteins that are no longer in use.  Why A wrong: Steroid hormone production is a smooth ER function.  Why C wrong: DNA coiling involves histone proteins in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: The spindle apparatus is formed by centrioles/microtubules."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The mitochondrion is described as a sausage-shaped structure in the cytoplasm and is known as the:","options":["Post office of the cell","Power house of the cell","Suicidal bag of the cell","Control room of the cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mitochondrion is a sausage-shaped structure in the cytoplasm, involved in aerobic respiration and concerned with production of energy in the form of ATP by the process of oxidative phosphorylation; it is the power house of the cell.  Why A wrong: The Golgi apparatus is the post office.  Why C wrong: Lysosomes are the suicidal bag.  Why D wrong: The nucleus is the control room of the cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Mitochondria produce energy in the form of ATP by the process of:","options":["Glycolysis only","Oxidative phosphorylation","Transcription","Osmosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mitochondria are concerned with production of energy in the form of ATP by the process of oxidative phosphorylation.  Why A wrong: The text specifies oxidative phosphorylation, not glycolysis alone.  Why C wrong: Transcription is RNA synthesis in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: Osmosis is a water-transport process."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which statement about mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) is correct?","options":["All DNA is located in the mitochondria","Most DNA is located in the cell nucleus, but a small amount of DNA is also found in the mitochondria","Mitochondria contain no DNA","mtDNA is identical in amount to nuclear DNA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most DNA is located in the cell nucleus (where it is called nuclear DNA), but a small amount of DNA is also found in the mitochondria.  Why A/C wrong: DNA is found in both nucleus and mitochondria; neither location holds all or none.  Why D wrong: Only a small amount of DNA is in the mitochondria, not an amount equal to nuclear DNA."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which active cells contain the greatest number of mitochondria, as cited in the text?","options":["Liver, muscle and spermatozoon","Red blood cells and platelets","Skin and nerve cells only","Bone and cartilage cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most active cells contain the greatest number of mitochondria — e.g., liver, muscle and spermatozoon (cells with high energy demand).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the examples cited as having the greatest number of mitochondria."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribosomes are the cell organelles that:","options":["Produce ATP","Are helping in synthesis of proteins (present on the surface of rough ER or free in the cytoplasm)","Store genetic material","Detoxify drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribosomes are cell organelles helping in synthesis of proteins, present on the surface of rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER); they are a large complex of r-RNA (about 65%) and protein (about 35%).  Why A wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why C wrong: Genetic material is in the nucleus.  Why D wrong: Detoxification is a smooth ER function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A ribosome is composed of approximately what proportion of rRNA and protein?","options":["35% rRNA and 65% protein","65% rRNA and 35% protein","50% rRNA and 50% protein","100% protein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A ribosome is a large complex of r-RNA (65%) and protein (35%).  Why A wrong: The proportions are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the cited 65%/35% composition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribosomes produce proteins from amino acids by a process called:","options":["Transcription","Protein synthesis (translation), in which they are called protein factories","Oxidative phosphorylation","Endocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribosomes produce proteins from amino acids by a process called protein synthesis; therefore ribosomes are called protein factories as they help synthesize proteins.  Why A wrong: Transcription forms RNA; translation/protein synthesis forms proteins.  Why C wrong: Oxidative phosphorylation produces ATP.  Why D wrong: Endocytosis is membrane transport."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribosomes are also known as engines of the cells because they:","options":["Produce ATP for the cell","Make all the cell's proteins, which carry out the cell's functions","Digest waste material","Coil the DNA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribosomes are known as engines of the cells as they make all the cell's proteins, which carry out the cell's functions.  Why A wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why C wrong: Digestion of waste is a lysosomal role.  Why D wrong: DNA coiling involves histone proteins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribosomes that are free (distributed) in the cytoplasm are called:","options":["Bounded ribosomes","Free ribosomes","Sarcoplasmic ribosomes","Nuclear ribosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribosomes that are free, distributed in the cytoplasm, are known as free ribosomes; those attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) are known as bounded ribosomes.  Why A wrong: Bounded ribosomes are attached to the RER, not free.  Why C/D wrong: These terms are not used for ribosome location in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A centrosome is a cellular structure involved primarily in the process of:","options":["Protein synthesis","Cell division","ATP production","Detoxification"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A centrosome is a cellular structure involved in the process of cell division; before cell division, the centrosome duplicates and then, as division begins, the two centrosomes move to opposite ends of the cell.  Why A wrong: Protein synthesis is by ribosomes.  Why C wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why D wrong: Detoxification is a smooth ER function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A centrosome consists of two cylindrical structures called centrioles, which are responsible for:","options":["Formation of spindle fibres and movement of chromosomes during cell division","Production of ATP","Synthesis of lipids","Storage of calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A centrosome consists of two cylindrical structures called centrioles, which are responsible for the formation of spindle fibres and the movement of chromosomes during cell division.  Why B wrong: ATP is produced in mitochondria.  Why C wrong: Lipid synthesis is a smooth ER function.  Why D wrong: Calcium storage is a smooth ER (sarcoplasmic reticulum) function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Microtubules and microfilaments are structures within or without organelles that:","options":["Produce ATP","Determine the shape of the cell and provide mechanical support","Carry genetic information","Digest worn-out organelles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Microtubules and microfilaments are present throughout the cytoplasm; they determine the shape of the cell and provide mechanical support to the cell. They are also known as the cytoskeleton of the cell.  Why A wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why C wrong: Genetic information is carried by DNA.  Why D wrong: Digestion of organelles is a lysosomal role."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Microtubules and microfilaments are also collectively known as the:","options":["Cytoskeleton of the cell","Control room of the cell","Power house of the cell","Post office of the cell"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Microtubules and microfilaments are also known as the cytoskeleton of the cell.  Why B wrong: The nucleus is the control room of the cell.  Why C wrong: The mitochondrion is the power house.  Why D wrong: The Golgi apparatus is the post office."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The nucleus is the membrane-enclosed organelle that contains:","options":["Hydrolytic enzymes","The cell's genetic material (chromosomes) and is the largest cellular organelle","ATP synthase only","Secretory granules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nucleus is a membrane-enclosed organelle within a cell that contains the chromosomes; it is the largest cellular organelle.  Why A wrong: Hydrolytic enzymes are in lysosomes.  Why C wrong: ATP synthase relates to mitochondria.  Why D wrong: Secretory granules are formed in the Golgi apparatus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The nucleus was discovered in 1831 by:","options":["Robert Hooke","Anton von Leeuwenhoek","Robert Brown","Rudolf Virchow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1831, Robert Brown discovered the nucleus in the cell.  Why A wrong: Robert Hooke observed dead cork cells (1665).  Why B wrong: Leeuwenhoek discovered living cells (1674).  Why D wrong: Virchow is not credited with the nucleus discovery here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which statement about the presence of a nucleus in human cells is correct?","options":["All human cells contain a nucleus","Every eukaryotic cell or a cell in the human body has a nucleus, except erythrocytes (red blood cells)","Only nerve cells have a nucleus","Red blood cells contain multiple nuclei"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Every eukaryotic cell or a cell in the human body has a nucleus, except erythrocytes (red blood cells).  Why A wrong: Erythrocytes are the exception and lack a nucleus.  Why C wrong: Most cells, not only nerve cells, have a nucleus.  Why D wrong: Mature RBCs have no nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Skeletal muscle and nerve cells are notable because they:","options":["Lack a nucleus","Contain several (multiple) nuclei","Contain no cytoplasm","Have no cell membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletal muscle and nerve cells contain several (multiple) nuclei.  Why A wrong: They do contain nuclei — in fact more than one.  Why C/D wrong: They have cytoplasm and a cell membrane like other cells."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The membrane surrounding the nucleus is called the:","options":["Plasma membrane","Nuclear membrane (similar to the plasma membrane, containing many components)","Sarcoplasmic membrane","Golgi membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nucleus is surrounded by a membrane called the nuclear membrane, which is similar to the plasma membrane and contains many components.  Why A wrong: The plasma membrane surrounds the whole cell.  Why C wrong: The sarcoplasmic reticulum is muscle smooth ER.  Why D wrong: The Golgi membrane is part of the Golgi apparatus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The major components found within the nucleus include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nucleoplasm","Chromatin","DNA","Mitochondria","Lysosomes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Major components of the nucleus are nucleoplasm, chromatin and DNA. Except during cell division, the chromosomes resemble a fine network of threads called chromatin.  Why D wrong: Mitochondria are cytoplasmic organelles, not nuclear contents.  Why E wrong: Lysosomes are cytoplasmic, not within the nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Chromatin is best described as:","options":["The fine network of threads (made up of DNA) that the chromosomes resemble except during cell division","The fluid content of the nucleus","A type of ribosome","The cell's energy molecule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chromatin is the thread-like material made up of fine genes; except during cell division, the chromosomes resemble a fine network of threads called chromatin. The thin DNA is coiled and compactly packed with the help of a specialized basic protein called histone, so chromatin is referred to as a DNA-histone protein complex.  Why B wrong: The fluid of the nucleus is nucleoplasm.  Why C wrong: Chromatin is not a ribosome.  Why D wrong: The cell's energy molecule is ATP."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Chromatin is referred to as a:","options":["DNA-histone protein complex","Phospholipid bilayer","Sodium-potassium pump","Secretory granule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The thin DNA is coiled and compactly packed with the help of a specialized basic protein called histone, so chromatin is referred to as a DNA-histone protein complex.  Why B wrong: The phospholipid bilayer describes the cell membrane.  Why C wrong: The Na⁺/K⁺ pump is an active-transport mechanism.  Why D wrong: Secretory granules are Golgi vesicles."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A chromosome is best described as:","options":["A rod-shaped nuclear structure that carries a complete blueprint of all the hereditary characteristics of the species","A sausage-shaped energy-producing organelle","A membrane-bound digestive sac","A network of microtubules"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A chromosome is the rod-shaped nuclear structure that carries a complete blueprint of all the hereditary characteristics of that species; it is formed from a single strand of DNA.  Why B wrong: That describes a mitochondrion.  Why C wrong: That describes a lysosome.  Why D wrong: That describes the cytoskeleton."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Each chromosome contains millions of:","options":["Mitochondria","Genes","Ribosomes","Lysosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each chromosome contains millions of genes.  Why A, C, D wrong: Mitochondria, ribosomes and lysosomes are cytoplasmic organelles, not the units carried within a chromosome."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In humans, how many chromosomes are present and how are they arranged?","options":["23 pairs (46 total): 22 pairs autosomes and 1 pair sex chromosomes","46 pairs (92 total)","23 single chromosomes only","44 pairs of sex chromosomes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In humans, 46 chromosomes (23 pairs) are present, of which 22 pairs are somatic (autosomal) chromosomes and 1 pair is the sex chromosome (Male XY and Female XX).  Why B wrong: 92 chromosomes is incorrect; humans have 46.  Why C wrong: They are arranged in pairs, not single.  Why D wrong: There is only 1 pair of sex chromosomes, the rest are autosomes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The sex-chromosome composition of a normal human male and female respectively is:","options":["Male XX and Female XY","Male XY and Female XX","Male XY and Female XY","Male XX and Female XX"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Male XY and Female XX for the sex chromosomes.  Why A wrong: The pairings are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated XY (male) / XX (female) composition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The nucleolus is a structure found within the nucleus whose primary function is to:","options":["Produce ATP","Produce and assemble the cell's ribosomes","Digest worn-out organelles","Transport proteins out of the cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nucleolus is a spherical structure in the cell nucleus whose primary function is to produce and assemble the cell's ribosomes.  Why A wrong: ATP is produced by mitochondria.  Why C wrong: Digestion is a lysosomal role.  Why D wrong: Protein export is via the Golgi."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the NUCLEUS? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Control of all cell activities, including metabolism, protein synthesis, growth and reproduction (cell division)","Synthesis of RNA from DNA","Sending genetic instructions to the cytoplasm for protein synthesis through messenger RNA (mRNA)","Control of the cell division through chromosomes","Storage of hereditary information (in genes) and transmission of this information from one generation of the species to the next"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are functions of the nucleus: it controls all cell activities including metabolism, protein synthesis, growth and reproduction (it is known as the control room or regulator of the cell or organelle of the cell); it synthesizes RNA from DNA; sends genetic instructions to the cytoplasm for protein synthesis through mRNA; controls cell division (through chromosomes); and stores and transmits hereditary information in genes from one generation to the next.  All options A–E are correct nuclear functions."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The nucleus is described as the \"control room of the cell\" because it is the:","options":["Site of ATP production","Regulator of the cell, controlling all cell activities","Waste disposal system","Site of detoxification"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nucleus is known as the control room of the cell or regulator of the cell because it controls all cell activities.  Why A wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial.  Why C wrong: Waste disposal is a lysosomal function.  Why D wrong: Detoxification is a smooth ER function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) is the nucleic acid that:","options":["Carries the genetic information for the offspring of an organism","Produces ATP","Forms the cell membrane","Digests proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNA carries the genetic information for the offspring of an organism; genes are the part of DNA molecules.  Why B wrong: ATP is produced by mitochondria.  Why C wrong: The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer.  Why D wrong: Protein digestion is a lysosomal function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Where in the cell is DNA present?","options":["Only in the cytoplasm","In the nucleus (chromosome) and the mitochondria of the cell","Only on the cell membrane","Only in ribosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DNA is present in the nucleus (chromosome) and the mitochondria of the cell.  Why A wrong: DNA is concentrated in the nucleus, not free in cytoplasm.  Why C/D wrong: DNA is not located on the cell membrane or in ribosomes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"DNA is structurally described as a:","options":["Single-stranded molecule","Double-stranded complex (helix), formed by deoxyribose sugar, phosphoric acid and four types of nitrogenous bases","Branched polysaccharide","Phospholipid bilayer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DNA is a double-stranded complex (nucleic acid) formed by deoxyribose sugar, phosphoric acid and four types of bases.  Why A wrong: RNA, not DNA, is single-stranded.  Why C wrong: DNA is a nucleic acid, not a polysaccharide.  Why D wrong: The phospholipid bilayer is the cell membrane."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The four nitrogenous bases found in DNA are:","options":["Adenine, guanine, thymine and cytosine","Adenine, guanine, uracil and cytosine","Adenine, thymine, uracil and guanine","Cytosine, thymine, uracil and guanine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Four nitrogenous bases are found in DNA: adenine, guanine, thymine and cytosine.  Why B, C, D wrong: They include uracil, which is found in RNA (not DNA); DNA contains thymine instead of uracil."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribonucleic acid (RNA) differs structurally from DNA in that RNA is:","options":["A double-stranded molecule","A single-stranded molecule","Made of deoxyribose sugar","Found only in mitochondria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An RNA molecule consists of a single strand of polynucleotide.  Why A wrong: DNA, not RNA, is double-stranded.  Why C wrong: RNA contains ribose sugar; deoxyribose is in DNA.  Why D wrong: RNA is not restricted to mitochondria."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which nitrogenous base present in DNA is replaced by uracil in RNA?","options":["Adenine","Guanine","Cytosine","Thymine"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: In RNA, the four nitrogenous bases are adenine, guanine, cytosine and uracil; uracil is present in RNA, and thymine is replaced by uracil compared with DNA.  Why A, B, C wrong: Adenine, guanine and cytosine are common to both DNA and RNA; only thymine is replaced by uracil."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which type of RNA carries the genetic code from the nucleus to the cytoplasm (to the ribosome) for protein synthesis?","options":["Messenger RNA (mRNA)","Transfer RNA (tRNA)","Ribosomal RNA (rRNA)","Nuclear RNA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Messenger RNA (mRNA) carries the genetic code of the gene and the sequence for protein synthesis from DNA to the cytoplasm.  Why B wrong: tRNA (anticodon) is responsible for decoding the genetic message.  Why C wrong: rRNA forms part of the structure of the ribosome.  Why D wrong: \"Nuclear RNA\" is not one of the listed RNA types."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Transfer RNA (tRNA), also known as the anticodon, is responsible for:","options":["Decoding the genetic message (present in mRNA)","Forming the structure of the ribosome","Carrying genetic information to the cytoplasm","Producing ATP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transfer RNA (tRNA, anticodon) is responsible for decoding the genetic message present in mRNA.  Why B wrong: Forming the ribosome structure is the role of rRNA.  Why C wrong: Carrying genetic information to the cytoplasm is the role of mRNA.  Why D wrong: ATP production is mitochondrial."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is present:","options":["Within the ribosome and forms a part of the structure of the ribosome; it is responsible for assembling proteins from amino acids in the ribosome","Only in the nucleus","On the cell membrane","In the Golgi apparatus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is present within the ribosome and forms a part of the structure of the ribosome; it is responsible for assembling protein from amino acids in the ribosome.  Why B/C/D wrong: rRNA is located within the ribosome, not exclusively in the nucleus, on the membrane, or in the Golgi."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, a gene is one section of a chromosome and a chromosome may carry many:","options":["Mitochondria","Genes","Ribosomes","Lysosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A gene is one section of a chromosome, and a chromosome may carry many genes.  Why A, C, D wrong: A chromosome carries genes, not mitochondria, ribosomes, or lysosomes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"DNA is arranged on chromosomes in segments called:","options":["Genes","Ribosomes","Codons only","Histones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNA is arranged on chromosomes in segments called genes; genes tell the cell how to make proteins.  Why B wrong: Ribosomes are protein-synthesis organelles, not DNA segments.  Why C wrong: Codons are base triplets, not the segment term used here.  Why D wrong: Histones are packaging proteins, not DNA segments."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"According to the text, different variations of a gene are called:","options":["Alleles","Codons","Introns","Histones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Different variations of a gene are called alleles; each parent passes one allele to the child for each gene.  Why B wrong: Codons are base triplets coding for amino acids.  Why C wrong: Introns are non-coding gene regions, not gene variants.  Why D wrong: Histones are DNA-packaging proteins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Protein synthesis is the process by which DNA is copied to RNA (transcription) and that RNA is used to produce proteins. The step in which DNA is copied to RNA is called:","options":["Translation","Transcription","Replication","Mutation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein synthesis is the process by which DNA is copied to RNA (transcription), and that RNA is used to produce proteins (translation).  Why A wrong: Translation is the step where RNA is used to produce proteins.  Why C wrong: Replication is the copying of DNA to DNA.  Why D wrong: Mutation is a change in the DNA sequence."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Cellular adaptation refers to:","options":["The death of a cell","The changes taking place in a cell in response to environmental changes","The transport of substances across the membrane","The synthesis of proteins by ribosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cellular adaptation refers to the changes taking place in a cell in response to environmental changes.  Why A wrong: Cell death is necrosis/apoptosis, not adaptation.  Why C wrong: That is membrane transport.  Why D wrong: That is protein synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Atrophy is a cellular adaptation that means:","options":["An increase in the size of a cell","A decrease in the size of a cell after full development","An increase in the number of cells","Replacement of one cell type by another"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atrophy means a decrease in the size of a cell after full development; examples include atrophy of female reproductive organs after menopause, ischemia, and muscle wasting (unused/inactivity).  Why A wrong: An increase in cell size is hypertrophy.  Why C wrong: An increase in cell number is hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: Replacement of cell type is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Which of the following are examples of ATROPHY cited in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Atrophy of female reproductive organs after menopause","Ischemia","Muscle wasting (due to inactivity/being unused)","Ventricular hypertrophy","Breast enlargement during pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of atrophy include atrophy of female reproductive organs after menopause, ischemia, and muscle wasting (unused).  Why D wrong: Ventricular hypertrophy is an example of hypertrophy.  Why E wrong: Breast enlargement/multiplication of milk-secreting glandular cells in the breast is an example of hyperplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Hypertrophy is best defined as:","options":["A decrease in cell size","An increase in the size of a cell after full development","An increase in the number of cells","An abnormal change in the size, shape and organization of cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertrophy means an increase in the size of a cell after full development; an example is ventricular hypertrophy.  Why A wrong: A decrease in cell size is atrophy.  Why C wrong: An increase in cell number is hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: Abnormal change in size/shape/organization is dysplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"A classic example of hypertrophy mentioned in the text is:","options":["Ventricular hypertrophy","Atrophy after menopause","Breast glandular hyperplasia","Cervical metaplasia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example of hypertrophy given is ventricular hypertrophy (increase in size of the heart's ventricular muscle cells).  Why B wrong: Atrophy after menopause is an atrophy example.  Why C wrong: Breast glandular cell increase is hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: Cervical-type change is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Hyperplasia is defined as:","options":["A decrease in the size of a cell","An increase in the number of cells","An increase in the size of a cell","Replacement of one cell type by another"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperplasia means an increase in the number of cells; an example is growth and multiplication of milk-secreting glandular cells in the breast (endometrial hyperplasia is another classic example).  Why A wrong: A decrease in cell size is atrophy.  Why C wrong: An increase in cell size is hypertrophy.  Why D wrong: Replacement of cell type is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The growth and multiplication of milk-secreting glandular cells in the breast (e.g., during pregnancy/lactation) is an example of:","options":["Atrophy","Hypertrophy","Hyperplasia","Dysplasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: This represents an increase in the number of cells, which is hyperplasia.  Why A wrong: Atrophy is a decrease in cell size.  Why B wrong: Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, not number.  Why D wrong: Dysplasia is abnormal change in size/shape/organization."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Dysplasia is best defined as:","options":["A decrease in cell size","An abnormal change in the size, shape and organization of the cell","An increase in the number of cells","Replacement of one type of cell by another normal type"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dysplasia means abnormal changes in the size, shape and organization of the cell.  Why A wrong: A decrease in cell size is atrophy.  Why C wrong: An increase in cell number is hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: Replacement of one cell type with another is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Metaplasia is best defined as:","options":["The death of cells","The replacement of one type of (mature) cell with another type","An increase in the number of cells","A decrease in the size of a cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metaplasia means replacement of one type of cell with another type.  Why A wrong: Cell death is necrosis/apoptosis.  Why C wrong: An increase in cell number is hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: A decrease in cell size is atrophy."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Cell division is the process by which a parent cell divides into:","options":["One daughter cell","Two or more daughter cells","A gamete only","A zygote"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cell division is the process by which a parent cell divides into two or more daughter cells.  Why A wrong: Division produces more than one daughter cell.  Why C wrong: Gametes are products of meiosis specifically, not the general definition.  Why D wrong: A zygote is formed by fusion of gametes, not by cell division of a parent cell."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The two types of cell division are:","options":["Transcription and translation","Mitosis and meiosis","Atrophy and hypertrophy","Diffusion and osmosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are two types of cell division: mitosis and meiosis.  Why A wrong: Transcription/translation are protein-synthesis steps.  Why C wrong: Atrophy/hypertrophy are cellular adaptations.  Why D wrong: Diffusion/osmosis are passive-transport processes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Mitosis is the process of nuclear division in eukaryotic cells that occurs when a parent cell divides to produce:","options":["Four genetically different cells","Two identical (genetically identical) daughter cells","Gametes (sperm and ova)","Haploid cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mitosis is a process of nuclear division in eukaryotic cells that occurs when a parent cell divides to produce two identical (genetically identical) daughter cells.  Why A wrong: Four genetically different cells are produced in meiosis.  Why C wrong: Gamete production is meiosis.  Why D wrong: Mitosis produces diploid (identical) cells; haploid cells result from meiosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Mitosis occurs in which type of cells?","options":["Germ cells only","Somatic (body) cells — it takes place in all types of cells but is not involved in the production of gametes","Only in the testes and ovaries","Only in red blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mitosis occurs in somatic cells; it means that it takes place in all types of cells but is not involved in the production of gametes.  Why A/C wrong: Germ cells in the testes and ovaries undergo meiosis to produce gametes.  Why D wrong: Mature RBCs lack a nucleus and do not divide by mitosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"What are the four stages of mitosis, in correct sequence?","options":["Prophase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase","Metaphase → Prophase → Telophase → Anaphase","Anaphase → Telophase → Prophase → Metaphase","Prophase → Anaphase → Metaphase → Telophase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four stages of mitosis are prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, in that order.  Why B, C, D wrong: They place the stages out of the correct sequence."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"During which stage of mitosis do chromatin fibres condense into chromosomes, the nuclear envelope breaks down, and chromosomes attach to spindle fibres?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In prophase, chromatin condenses into chromosomes, the nuclear envelope breaks down, and chromosomes attach to spindle fibres.  Why B wrong: Metaphase is when chromosomes line up along the metaphase plate.  Why C wrong: Anaphase is when sister chromatids are pulled apart.  Why D wrong: Telophase is when the nuclear envelope reforms."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In which stage of mitosis do chromosomes line up (using the metaphase plate / centre of the cell) with centrosomes at opposite poles?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In metaphase, chromosomes line up along the metaphase plate (centre of the cell), with centrosomes at opposite poles of the cell.  Why A wrong: Prophase is when chromatin condenses and the nuclear envelope breaks down.  Why C wrong: Anaphase is when sister chromatids separate.  Why D wrong: Telophase is when the nuclear envelope reforms."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Anaphase of mitosis is characterized by:","options":["Condensation of chromatin","Chromosomes lining up at the centre of the cell","Sister chromatids (now called chromosomes) being pulled to opposite poles of the cell","Reformation of the nuclear envelope"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In anaphase, sister chromatids (now called chromosomes) are pulled to opposite poles of the cell.  Why A wrong: Condensation of chromatin occurs in prophase.  Why B wrong: Alignment at the centre occurs in metaphase.  Why D wrong: Reformation of the nuclear envelope occurs in telophase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Telophase of mitosis is characterized by:","options":["Chromatin condensing into chromosomes","Reformation (reappearance) of the nuclear envelope and the chromosomes unfolding into chromatin, followed by cytokinesis","Chromosomes aligning at the metaphase plate","Sister chromatids separating to opposite poles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In telophase, the nuclear envelope reforms (reappears) and chromosomes unfold into chromatin; cytokinesis can begin.  Why A wrong: Chromatin condensing into chromosomes is prophase.  Why C wrong: Alignment at the metaphase plate is metaphase.  Why D wrong: Sister chromatids separating is anaphase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Meiosis is the process in which a single (diploid) cell divides to form:","options":["Two identical haploid cells","Four haploid (haploid-content) cells","Two diploid daughter cells","One gamete only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis is the process in which a single cell divides to form four haploid cells.  Why A wrong: It forms four cells, not two.  Why C wrong: Two identical diploid daughter cells are the product of mitosis.  Why D wrong: Meiosis produces multiple gametes, not a single gamete."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The cells produced by meiosis are the:","options":["Somatic cells","Gametes — sperms in males and ova (eggs) in females","Red blood cells","Nerve cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cells produced by meiosis are the gametes — sperms in males and ova (eggs) in females.  Why A wrong: Somatic cells are produced by mitosis.  Why C/D wrong: RBCs and nerve cells are somatic cells, not meiotic products."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"Meiosis occurs only in which organs of the human body?","options":["Liver and kidneys","The testes and ovaries — organs that produce the male and female reproductive cells","Heart and lungs","Stomach and intestines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis occurs only in the testes and ovaries, the organs that produce the male and female reproductive cells (gametes).  Why A, C, D wrong: These organs do not produce gametes and therefore do not undergo meiosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"In meiosis, the process is divided into how many stages, and what are they?","options":["One stage","Two stages — meiosis I and meiosis II, each subdivided into several phases","Four independent stages with no subdivision","Three stages"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The process of meiosis is divided into 2 stages — meiosis I and meiosis II — and each stage is subdivided into several phases.  Why A, C, D wrong: Meiosis comprises two main stages (I and II), not one, three, or four undivided stages."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Human Anatomy & Physiology…","question":"The phases of meiosis I and meiosis II respectively are:","options":["Meiosis I: prophase I, metaphase I, anaphase I, telophase I and cytokinesis I; Meiosis II: prophase II, metaphase II, anaphase II, telophase II and cytokinesis II","Meiosis I: prophase only; Meiosis II: telophase only","Both stages have only metaphase and anaphase","Meiosis I and II share a single set of four phases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis I consists of prophase I, metaphase I, anaphase I, telophase I and cytokinesis I; Meiosis II consists of prophase II, metaphase II, anaphase II, telophase II and cytokinesis II.  Why B, C, D wrong: Each meiotic stage has its own full set of phases plus cytokinesis, contradicting these options."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Tissues are best defined as:","options":["Single cells that act independently to perform body functions","Groups of cells that have a similar structure and act together to perform a specific function","Random aggregates of unrelated cells in body fluids","Organs joined together to form an organ system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tissue is a group of cells with similar structure that act together to perform a specific function.  Why A wrong: Tissue involves coordinated groups of cells, not isolated single cells.  Why C wrong: Tissues are structured and organized, not random aggregates.  Why D wrong: Combinations of organs form systems; tissues are a lower level of organization."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The branch of medical science that deals with the study of tissues is called:","options":["Cytology","Histology","Pathology","Physiology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Histology is the branch of medical science dealing with the study of tissues.  Why A wrong: Cytology is the study of cells.  Why C wrong: Pathology is the study of disease.  Why D wrong: Physiology studies the functions of living systems."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"How many basic types of tissue are found in the body?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are 4 basic types of tissue: connective tissue, epithelial tissue, muscle tissue, and nervous tissue.  Why A/B/D wrong: The correct number of basic tissue types is four, not two, three, or five."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which of the following are the four basic types of tissue? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue","Glandular tissue"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four basic tissue types are epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue.  Why E wrong: Glandular tissue is a subtype/derivative of epithelial tissue, not a separate basic type."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Connective tissue supports other tissues and binds them together. Which of the following are examples of connective tissue listed in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bone","Blood","Lymph tissues","Nervous tissue","Skeletal muscle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Bone, blood, and lymph tissues are listed as examples of connective tissue.  Why D wrong: Nervous tissue is a separate basic tissue type.  Why E wrong: Skeletal muscle is muscle tissue, not connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Epithelial tissue is mostly found in which of the following locations?","options":["Only in the central nervous system","The outer layer of skin, lining of cavities, hollow organs, tubes, and major tissues in glands","Exclusively within bone matrix","Suspended in blood plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epithelial tissues are found in the outer layer of skin, lining body cavities, hollow organs, tubes, and as the major tissue in glands.  Why A wrong: Nervous tissue, not epithelium, predominates in the CNS.  Why C wrong: Bone is connective tissue.  Why D wrong: Blood/plasma is fluid connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Epithelial tissue forms the lining of which systems and also performs which functions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Respiratory system lining","Digestive system lining","Reproductive and excretory tracts lining","Absorption, protection, sensation, and secretion","Pumping of blood through the heart"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Epithelium lines respiratory, digestive, reproductive, and excretory tracts and performs absorption, protection, sensation, and secretion.  Why E wrong: Pumping blood is a function of cardiac muscle tissue, not epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which two types of epithelial membranes are described in the text?","options":["Cutaneous and synovial membranes","Mucous membrane and serous membrane","Basement and reticular membranes","Pleural and pericardial membranes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there are two types of epithelial membranes: mucous membrane and serous membrane.  Why A wrong: Cutaneous/synovial are not the pair named here.  Why C wrong: Basement membrane is a supportive structure, not the membrane pair described.  Why D wrong: Pleural/pericardial are specific serous membranes, not the broad classification given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"On the basis of structure, epithelium tissue is classified into which two main types?","options":["Simple epithelium and compound (stratified) epithelium","Squamous and cuboidal only","Glandular and ciliated only","Endocrine and exocrine only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Classification of epithelium tissue is into Simple epithelium and Compound (stratified) epithelium.  Why B wrong: Squamous and cuboidal are cell shapes, not the structural classification.  Why C/D wrong: These are subtypes/functional groupings, not the two structural classes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Simple epithelium is composed of:","options":["Two or more layers of cells","Only one layer of a cell, mostly serving a secretory or absorptive function","Multiple keratinized layers for protection","Loose connective fibers and matrix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Simple epithelium is composed of one layer of a cell and mostly serves a secretory or absorptive function.  Why A wrong: Multiple layers describe compound/stratified epithelium.  Why C wrong: Keratinized layers belong to stratified squamous epithelium of skin.  Why D wrong: Fibers and matrix describe connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Compound (stratified) epithelium is made up of:","options":["A single layer of cells","Two or more layers of cells, mostly having a protective function","Cells suspended in fluid matrix","Only ciliated columnar cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compound (stratified) epithelium is made up of two or more layers of cells and mostly has a protective function.  Why A wrong: A single layer is simple epithelium.  Why C wrong: Cells in fluid matrix describe blood (connective tissue).  Why D wrong: Ciliated columnar is a specific simple type, not the definition of stratified."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Endothelium is best described as:","options":["A type of epithelium composed of a single layer of smooth cells lining the heart, blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and serous cavities","The outermost keratinized layer of the skin","A type of dense connective tissue","Specialized contractile muscle of the heart wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endothelium is a type of epithelium composed of a single layer of smooth cells that lines the heart, blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and serous cavities.  Why B wrong: That describes the epidermis/cornified layer.  Why C wrong: Endothelium is epithelial, not connective.  Why D wrong: Cardiac muscle is the contractile tissue; endothelium lines the chamber."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Mucous membrane epithelium performs all of the following functions EXCEPT:","options":["Secretes mucus","Provides protection and easy movement of materials","Lines tracts open to the exterior (respiratory and digestive)","Produces hormones directly into the blood"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Producing hormones directly into the blood is an endocrine gland function, not a mucous membrane function.  Why A wrong: Mucous membrane secretes mucus.  Why B wrong: Mucus helps protection and easy movement of materials.  Why C wrong: It lines surfaces such as the respiratory and digestive tracts which open to the exterior."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Serous membranes line the body cavities and:","options":["Open directly to the exterior of the body","Do not open to the exterior; they line the closed cavity (pericardial, pleural, peritoneal) membranes","Secrete keratin for protection","Are composed of dense fibrous connective tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serous membranes line the body cavities, which do not open outside the body, such as the lining of the pleural cavity, pericardial membranes.  Why A wrong: Mucous membranes (not serous) open to the exterior.  Why C wrong: Keratin production is an epidermal function.  Why D wrong: Serous membranes are epithelial membranes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Glands are made up of epithelial cells. There are how many types of glands described?","options":["One","Two — exocrine and endocrine","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glands are made up of epithelial cells; there are two types: exocrine and endocrine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies two gland types."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Exocrine glands secrete their product:","options":["Directly into the blood","Into a duct, e.g., goblet cells and sweat glands","Only into the lymphatic system","Into the cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exocrine glands secrete their product into a duct, e.g., goblet cells, sweat glands.  Why A wrong: Secretion directly into blood describes endocrine glands.  Why C/D wrong: Exocrine secretion is via ducts to a surface, not lymph or CSF."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Endocrine glands are called ductless glands because they:","options":["Release secretions (hormones) directly into the blood, e.g., intestinal fluid/hormones","Empty their products onto the skin surface through ducts","Have no secretory function","Produce only digestive enzymes into the gut lumen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endocrine glands are ductless glands that secrete their product directly into the blood (hormones).  Why B wrong: That describes exocrine glands with ducts.  Why C wrong: Endocrine glands are highly secretory (hormones).  Why D wrong: Releasing enzymes into the gut lumen via ducts is exocrine."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Simple squamous epithelium lines air sacs of lungs, capillary walls, lining of lymph and blood vessels. Its main functions are:","options":["Diffusion, filtration, osmosis, and covering of surfaces","Movement of mucus and protection only","Secretion of hormones into blood","Active phagocytosis of pathogens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Simple squamous epithelium functions in diffusion, filtration, osmosis, and covering of surfaces.  Why B wrong: Mucus movement is a ciliated columnar function.  Why C wrong: Hormone secretion is endocrine.  Why D wrong: Phagocytosis is a macrophage function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Simple cuboidal epithelium, found in ovary surfaces, kidney tubule linings, and glands lining, primarily functions in:","options":["Protection only","Absorption and secretion","Diffusion and filtration only","Distensibility and stretch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Simple cuboidal epithelium functions in absorption and secretion.  Why A wrong: Protection is a function of stratified squamous epithelium.  Why C wrong: Diffusion/filtration are simple squamous functions.  Why D wrong: Distensibility describes transitional epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium of the respiratory passage lining functions in:","options":["Protection only","Absorption, protection, and secretion","Diffusion only","Stretch and recoil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pseudostratified columnar epithelium functions in absorption, protection, and secretion.  Why A wrong: Protection alone is incomplete for this type.  Why C wrong: Diffusion is a simple squamous role.  Why D wrong: Stretch/recoil is transitional/elastic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Stratified squamous epithelium is found on wet surfaces such as the lining of the mouth, pharynx, esophagus, and vagina, and on dry surfaces such as the skin, hair, and nails. Its main function is:","options":["Secretion","Protection","Filtration","Distensibility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stratified squamous epithelium functions in protection.  Why A wrong: Secretion is a glandular/cuboidal function.  Why C wrong: Filtration is simple squamous.  Why D wrong: Distensibility is transitional."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium found in linings of larger mammary glands, ducts, pancreas, salivary glands, and sweat glands functions in:","options":["Protection and secretion","Diffusion only","Distensibility","Phagocytosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stratified cuboidal epithelium functions in protection and secretion.  Why B wrong: Diffusion is simple squamous.  Why C wrong: Distensibility describes transitional epithelium.  Why D wrong: Phagocytosis is a macrophage role."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Transitional epithelium is found in the urinary bladder, ureters, lining of part of the urethra, and part of the male urethra and parts of the pharynx. Its key functional property is:","options":["Diffusion and filtration","Distensibility (and protection)","Hormone secretion","Mucus propulsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transitional epithelium provides distensibility (stretch) and protection, suiting organs that change volume like the bladder.  Why A wrong: Diffusion/filtration is simple squamous.  Why C wrong: Hormone secretion is endocrine.  Why D wrong: Mucus propulsion is ciliated columnar."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Glandular epithelium found in endocrine, salivary, and sweat glands functions primarily in:","options":["Protection","Secretion","Filtration","Distensibility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glandular epithelium functions in secretion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Protection, filtration, and distensibility are functions of other epithelial types."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The four major tissue types shown in the \"Tissues\" organization chart are epithelial, muscular, connective, and:","options":["Glandular tissue","Nervous tissue","Adipose tissue","Fluid tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart divides tissues into Epithelial, Muscular, Connective, and Nervous tissue.  Why A wrong: Glandular is a subtype of epithelial.  Why C wrong: Adipose is a subtype of connective tissue.  Why D wrong: Fluid connective tissue is a connective subtype, not a major class."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"According to the Tissues chart, the three muscle subtypes are striated muscle, smooth muscle, and:","options":["Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Visceral muscle","Reticular muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The muscular tissue branch lists striated muscle, smooth muscle, and cardiac muscle.  Why A wrong: Skeletal muscle is the striated type, not a separate listed subtype here.  Why C/D wrong: These are not in the chart's branching."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which is the most abundant tissue in the human body?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Muscular tissue","Connective tissue","Nervous tissue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Connective tissue is the most abundant tissue in the human body.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are present but not the most abundant."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Connective tissue originates from which embryonic layer?","options":["Ectoderm","Endoderm","Mesoderm (mesenchyme of the embryo)","Neural crest"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Connective tissues arise from the mesoderm (mesenchyme) layer of the embryo.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are other germ layers/derivatives, not the stated origin of connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Most types of connective tissue consist of three main components. Which of the following are these components? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Cells","Collagen fibers (and elastic fibers)","Ground substance (matrix)","Keratin","Myofibrils"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Connective tissue consists of cells, collagen/elastic fibers, and ground substance (matrix).  Why D wrong: Keratin is found in epithelial (skin) tissue.  Why E wrong: Myofibrils are components of muscle cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Major functions of connective tissue include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Binding and structural support","Protection","Transport","Insulation","Generation of nerve impulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Connective tissues bind/support, protect, transport, and insulate.  Why E wrong: Generation of nerve impulses is a function of nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The fibrous connective tissue and skeletal connective tissue together provide:","options":["Hormone secretion to the blood","Mechanical support to the body","Generation and conduction of nerve impulses","Active phagocytosis of pathogens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrous and skeletal connective tissues provide the mechanical support to the body.  Why A wrong: That is an endocrine function.  Why C wrong: That is nervous tissue.  Why D wrong: Phagocytosis is a specialized cell (macrophage) function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The cells of connective tissue lie freely within which component?","options":["The cell membrane","The connective tissue matrix (fibroblasts, adipocytes, macrophages, mast cells, leucocytes)","The basement membrane only","The myelin sheath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cells of connective tissue (fibroblasts, adipocytes, macrophages, mast cells, leucocytes) lie freely within the connective tissue matrix.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not where connective tissue cells reside."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Macrophages are phagocytic cells that are common in which tissue?","options":["Nervous tissue","Connective tissue","Cardiac muscle only","Cornified epidermis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrophages are phagocytic cells common in connective tissue.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not characteristically common in nervous, cardiac muscle, or epidermal locations as described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Blood and lymph are classified as specialized fluid connective tissue. The matrix of fluid connective tissue is in which state?","options":["Solid","Liquid state","Gel-cartilage only","Calcified"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The matrix of fluid connective tissue is in a liquid state.  Why A/D wrong: Solid/calcified matrix describes bone.  Why C wrong: Gel/cartilage matrix is for cartilage, not fluid tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which of the following are recognized as types/examples of fibrous connective tissue listed in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Adipose tissue","Ligaments","Tendon","Areolar (loose) tissue","Blood"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes adipose tissue, areolar (loose connective) tissue, ligaments, and tendons under fibrous/connective tissue types.  Why E wrong: Blood is fluid connective tissue, listed separately."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Areolar tissue (loose connective tissue) functions to:","options":["Store calcium salts","Support and maintain body temperature","Conduct electrical impulses","Pump blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Areolar (loose connective) tissue supports and maintains body temperature.  Why A wrong: Calcium salt storage is a bone function.  Why C wrong: Impulse conduction is nervous tissue.  Why D wrong: Pumping blood is cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which adipose tissue type is found in newborns and acts as thermoregulation (maintenance of body temperature)?","options":["White adipose tissue","Brown adipose tissue","Yellow elastic tissue","Reticular tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brown adipose tissue is found in newborns and acts as thermoregulation (maintenance of body temperature).  Why A wrong: White adipose tissue is found in adults as a thermal insulator and energy store.  Why C/D wrong: These are not adipose subtypes described here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"White adipose tissue, found in adults, acts as:","options":["A thermal insulator and energy store","An impulse-conducting tissue","The matrix of bone","A phagocytic defense tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: White adipose tissue is found in adults and acts as a thermal insulator and energy store.  Why B wrong: Impulse conduction is nervous tissue.  Why C wrong: Bone matrix is calcified connective tissue.  Why D wrong: Phagocytic defense is a macrophage function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Ligaments connect:","options":["Bone to muscle","Bone to another bone","Muscle to muscle","Nerve to bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ligaments connect bone to another bone.  Why A wrong: Bone to muscle is connected by tendon.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the function of ligaments."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The strongest ligament of the human body is the:","options":["Anterior cruciate ligament","Iliofemoral ligament","Patellar ligament","Deltoid ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The strongest ligament of the human body is the iliofemoral ligament; an injury to a ligament is known as a sprain.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the strongest ligament in the text."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"An injury to a ligament is known as a:","options":["Strain","Sprain","Fracture","Dislocation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injury to a ligament is known as a sprain.  Why A wrong: A strain is injury to a muscle/tendon.  Why C/D wrong: A fracture is broken bone; dislocation is joint displacement."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Tendons connect:","options":["Bone to bone","The bone to muscle (and connect muscle to bone)","Muscle to muscle","Skin to fascia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tendons connect the bone to muscle.  Why A wrong: Bone to bone is connected by ligaments.  Why C/D wrong: These are not tendon functions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The strongest tendon of the human body is the:","options":["Patellar tendon","Achilles tendon (heel cord), found at the back of the leg","Biceps tendon","Quadriceps tendon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The strongest tendon is the Achilles tendon or heel cord, located at the back of the leg; injury to a tendon is known as a strain.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the strongest tendon."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"An injury to a tendon is known as a:","options":["Sprain","Strain","Contusion","Avulsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injury to a tendon (muscle) is known as a strain.  Why A wrong: A sprain is a ligament injury.  Why C/D wrong: These are other injury types not specified here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Bone cells (osteocytes) are surrounded by which of the following?","options":["A liquid plasma matrix","A matrix of mineral salts and collagen","Loose areolar tissue only","Adipocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bone cells (osteocytes) are surrounded by a matrix of mineral salts (calcium) and collagen.  Why A wrong: Liquid matrix describes blood.  Why C/D wrong: These are different connective tissues, not the bone matrix."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"There are two types of bone tissue. Which of the following are they?","options":["Dense/compact bone and spongy/cancellous bone","Hyaline and elastic bone","White and brown bone","Reticular and areolar bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two types of bone are dense/compact bone and spongy/cancellous bone.  Why B wrong: Hyaline/elastic are cartilage types.  Why C/D wrong: These are not bone classifications."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Cartilage is a vascular or avascular type of supporting connective tissue. Which is correct, and what cells does it contain?","options":["Vascular tissue containing osteocytes","Avascular tissue containing chondrocytes, which are also known as chondrocytes in a flexible protein matrix","Fluid tissue containing erythrocytes","Nervous tissue containing neurons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cartilage is an avascular supporting connective tissue whose cells are chondrocytes, embedded in a flexible protein matrix.  Why A wrong: Osteocytes are bone cells; cartilage is avascular.  Why C wrong: Erythrocytes are blood cells.  Why D wrong: Neurons are nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which three types of cartilage are identified in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hyaline cartilage","Fibro-cartilage","Elastic fibro-cartilage","Calcified cartilage","Reticular cartilage"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three cartilage types are hyaline cartilage, fibro-cartilage, and elastic fibro-cartilage.  Why D/E wrong: Calcified and reticular cartilage are not the three types named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Blood is described as plasma plus red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. Which of the following correctly matches blood functions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["RBCs carry oxygen","Platelets prevent blood loss","WBCs destroy pathogens","Connect cells to surfaces (collagen)","Plasma transports materials"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: RBCs carry oxygen, platelets prevent blood loss, WBCs destroy pathogens, and plasma transports materials.  Why D wrong: Collagen connecting cells to surfaces is a fibrous connective tissue role, not a blood function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"According to the connective tissue table, areolar (loose) connective tissue is composed of fibroblasts and a matrix of tissue fluid, collagen, and elastic fibers, and is located:","options":["Within blood vessels only","Subcutaneous (subcutaneously) and connecting skin to muscles, plus mucous membranes of digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts","Around the eyes and eyelids only","Only within the heart wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Areolar connective tissue is located subcutaneously, connecting skin to muscles and in mucous membranes of digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts.  Why A wrong: That is blood's location.  Why C wrong: Adipose around eyes/eyelids is a different entry.  Why D wrong: That describes cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Adipose tissue (adipocytes that store fat as inert matrix) is located subcutaneously and around the eyes and eyelids. Its functions include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stores excess energy","Produces chemicals which influence appetite, use of nutrients, and inflammation","Cushions","Generates nerve impulses","Carries oxygen"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Adipose stores excess energy, produces chemicals that influence appetite, use of nutrients, and inflammation, and provides cushioning.  Why D wrong: Nerve impulse generation is a nervous tissue function.  Why E wrong: Oxygen transport is a function of RBCs in blood."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Fibrous connective tissue (mostly collagen fibers with few fibroblasts) is located in tendons and ligaments (regular) and functions to:","options":["Carry oxygen","Provide strong attachment to withstand forces of movement of joints","Secrete hormones","Conduct impulses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrous connective tissue provides strong attachment to withstand the forces of movement of joints, located in tendons and ligaments.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are functions of blood, glands, and nerves respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Elastic connective tissue (mostly elastic fibers with few fibroblasts) is located in the stretchy outer layer of the skin and functions to: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Help maintain blood pressure","Aid expansion and recoil of alveoli in the lungs","Promote normal resolution","Store calcium salts","Carry electrical signals"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Elastic connective tissue helps maintain blood pressure, aids the expansion/recoil of alveoli in the lungs, and promotes normal resolution.  Why D wrong: Calcium storage is a bone function.  Why E wrong: Carrying signals is nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"According to the connective tissue table, bone (osteocytes in a matrix of calcium salts and collagen) located in the skeleton functions to: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Support the body","Protect internal organs from mechanical injury","Store excess calcium","Contain and protect red bone marrow","Secrete digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bone supports the body, protects internal organs from mechanical injury, stores excess calcium, and contains/protects red bone marrow.  Why E wrong: Digestive enzyme secretion is a glandular function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"According to the table, cartilage (chondrocytes in a flexible protein matrix) located at the wall of the trachea, tip of the nose, and outer ear functions to: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Keep airways open","Provide support","Cushion / absorb shock between vertebrae","Carry oxygen","Reduce friction at joints (smooth surfaces to prevent friction)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cartilage keeps airways open, provides support, cushions/absorbs shock (between vertebrae), and provides smooth surfaces to prevent friction.  Why D wrong: Oxygen transport is a blood function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Fibroblasts produce which substances? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Collagen fibers","Elastic fibers","Matrix that forms the connective tissue's ground substance, holding it together","Hormones into the blood","Action potentials"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Fibroblasts produce collagen and elastic fibers and matrix elements that hold the connective tissue together (and are involved in wound repair/tissue repair).  Why D wrong: Hormone secretion is an endocrine function.  Why E wrong: Action potentials are generated by neurons."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which connective tissue cell is also known as an adipocyte and stores energy in the form of fat?","options":["Macrophage","Fat cell (fat cells store energy in adipose tissue)","Mast cell","Plasma cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fat cells (adipocytes) store energy in the form of fat in adipose tissue.  Why A wrong: Macrophages are phagocytic.  Why C wrong: Mast cells release histamine/heparin in inflammation.  Why D wrong: Plasma cells produce antibodies."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Macrophages are an important part of the body's defense mechanisms because they:","options":["Are actively phagocytic, engulfing and digesting old cells, bacteria, and other foreign bodies","Conduct nerve impulses","Carry oxygen to tissues","Secrete bile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macrophages are actively phagocytic, engulfing and digesting old/dead cells, bacteria, and other foreign bodies; they have different names according to location (e.g., osteoclasts in bone).  Why B wrong: Nerve impulse conduction is by neurons.  Why C wrong: Oxygen transport is by RBCs.  Why D wrong: Bile is secreted by the liver."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Mast cells release histamine. The effects of histamine release include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["The blood vessels become similar to basophil leucocytes","Are found in liver and spleen","They produce granules containing heparin and histamine, which are released when cells are damaged by disease/injury","Carry oxygen in the blood","Conduct nerve impulses"],"correct":"A,C","rationale":"Why correct: Mast cells are similar to basophil leucocytes and produce granules containing heparin and histamine, which are released when the cells are damaged by disease or injury.  Why B wrong: That description matches reticuloendothelial cells in liver/spleen, not mast cells.  Why D/E wrong: These are functions of RBCs and neurons."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Hyaline cartilage is described as:","options":["The most widespread type, found in joints, resembling glass; bears weight and reduces friction in joints","A fluid connective tissue","The contractile tissue of the heart","The outermost layer of skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyaline cartilage is the most widespread type, resembling glass, found in joints and reducing friction/bearing weight.  Why B wrong: Fluid connective tissue is blood.  Why C wrong: Cardiac muscle is contractile heart tissue.  Why D wrong: The epidermis is the outer skin layer."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Fibrous cartilage (fibrocartilage) has many collagen fibers and is found in:","options":["The intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis","The lining of the trachea only","The matrix of bone","Blood vessel walls"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrous cartilage (fibrocartilage) contains many collagen fibers and is found in the intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis.  Why B wrong: Tracheal cartilage is primarily hyaline.  Why C wrong: Bone matrix is calcified, not fibrocartilage.  Why D wrong: Blood vessel walls contain elastic/smooth muscle tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Elastic cartilage is made of collagen, elastin, and elastic fibers and is found in:","options":["The internal support of the external ear (pinna) and lobe of the ear","The intervertebral discs only","The skeleton","The myocardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Elastic cartilage contains collagen, elastin, and elastic fibers and is found in the internal support of the external ear (pinna and lobe of the ear) and the epiglottis.  Why B wrong: Intervertebral discs are fibrocartilage.  Why C wrong: The skeleton is bone.  Why D wrong: The myocardium is cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The Reticuloendothelial System (RES) consists of:","options":["Cells descending from the monocytes which are able to perform phagocytosis of foreign materials and pathogens","Only red blood cells","Smooth muscle fibers of the gut","Neurons and neuroglia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RES consists of cells descending from monocytes, which are able to perform phagocytosis of foreign materials and pathogens.  Why B/C/D wrong: RES is a macrophage/monocyte-derived defense network, not RBCs, smooth muscle, or nervous cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Where are the RES cells primarily located/involved? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Liver","Spleen","Lungs","Skeletal muscle","Tooth enamel"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The primary organs involved in the RES are the liver, spleen, and lungs.  Why D/E wrong: Skeletal muscle and tooth enamel are not RES organs."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Match the RES (tissue-based) macrophage to its location: Pulmonary alveolar (intra-alveolar) macrophages (PAM) are found in the:","options":["Lungs (pulmonary alveoli)","Bone","Liver","Skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alveolar macrophages (PAM)/dust cells are located in the pulmonary alveoli of the lungs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are locations of other specific macrophages (osteoclasts, Kupffer cells, Langerhans cells)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Match the tissue macrophage to its correct location. (Select All That Apply)","options":["Kupffer cells — Liver","Microglia — Neural tissue (brain/CNS)","Langerhans cells — Skin","Histiocytes — Connective tissue","Osteoclasts — Tooth enamel"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Kupffer cells are in the liver, microglia in neural tissue, Langerhans cells in the skin, and histiocytes in connective tissue.  Why E wrong: Osteoclasts are bone macrophages located in bone, not tooth enamel."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the RES macrophage table, Hofbauer cells are located in the:","options":["Placenta","Intestinal wall","Bone marrow/blood","Peritoneal cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hofbauer cells are located in the placenta.  Why B wrong: Microglia (M cells/intestinal) relate to the intestinal wall in the table.  Why C wrong: Monocytes are in bone marrow/blood.  Why D wrong: Peritoneal macrophages occupy the peritoneal cavity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Monocytes, as listed in the RES table, are located in the:","options":["Bone marrow / blood","Liver only","Skin","Peritoneal cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monocytes are located in the bone marrow/blood and are the precursors of tissue macrophages.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are sites of differentiated tissue macrophages, not circulating monocytes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which property is characteristic of muscle tissue?","options":["It is able to contract and relax, providing movement within the body","It is avascular and aneural","It does not require blood supply","It cannot regenerate at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Muscle tissue is able to contract and relax, providing movement within the body.  Why B/C wrong: Muscle contraction requires adequate blood supply to provide oxygen, calcium, and nutrients and to remove waste.  Why D wrong: Regeneration limits vary; this is not the defining property described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Muscle contraction requires an adequate blood supply to provide which substances and remove waste? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Oxygen","Calcium","Nutrients","Keratin","Bile"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Adequate blood supply provides oxygen, calcium, and nutrients to muscle and removes waste products.  Why D wrong: Keratin is an epidermal protein.  Why E wrong: Bile is a digestive secretion, not required for muscle contraction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"A specific type of muscle-forming cell is called a:","options":["Myocyte","Chondrocyte","Osteocyte","Fibroblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myocytes are the specific type of muscle-forming cells.  Why B wrong: Chondrocytes form cartilage.  Why C wrong: Osteocytes are bone cells.  Why D wrong: Fibroblasts form connective tissue fibers."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Total number of muscles in the human body is approximately:","options":["206","639","300","500"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The total number of muscles in the human body is about 639.  Why A wrong: 206 is the number of bones in the adult human body.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the figure given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which three types of muscle are described in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Reticular muscle","Elastic muscle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three types of muscle are skeletal, smooth, and cardiac muscle.  Why D/E wrong: Reticular and elastic muscle are not muscle types."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Skeletal muscle is best described as:","options":["Striated in structure and under voluntary control, usually attached to the skeleton","Non-striated and involuntary","Striated and involuntary","Found only in the heart wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletal muscle is striated in structure and under voluntary control, usually attached to the skeleton.  Why B wrong: That describes smooth muscle.  Why C wrong: That describes cardiac muscle.  Why D wrong: Cardiac muscle is in the heart wall."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The sarcomere is best defined as:","options":["The structural and functional unit of skeletal muscle, also called the basic unit of the muscle","The contractile cell of the heart","A connective tissue fiber","A type of neuroglia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sarcomere is the structural and functional unit of skeletal muscle, also called the basic unit of the muscle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other cell/tissue types unrelated to the sarcomere."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The functions of skeletal muscle include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Voluntary movement","Maintaining posture","Supporting the human body","Pumping blood through the heart","Secreting hormones"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletal muscle functions in voluntary movement, maintaining posture, and supporting the human body.  Why D wrong: Pumping blood is a cardiac muscle function.  Why E wrong: Hormone secretion is a glandular function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Smooth muscle is situated in association with viscera. It is also called:","options":["Visceral muscle (visceral); it is un-striated and involuntary in nature","Striated voluntary muscle","Cardiac muscle","Striated involuntary muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Smooth muscle is associated with viscera (visceral muscle), and is un-striated and involuntary in nature.  Why B wrong: Striated voluntary describes skeletal muscle.  Why C wrong: Cardiac muscle is striated and in the heart.  Why D wrong: Striated involuntary describes cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The function of smooth muscle is responsible for contractility of hollow organs such as: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Blood vessels","GI tract","Bladder (urinary bladder)","The femur","The skull"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Smooth muscle is responsible for contractility of hollow organs i.e., blood vessels, GI tract, and bladder.  Why D/E wrong: The femur and skull are bones, not smooth-muscle hollow organs."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Cardiac muscle is the type of muscle tissue found only in the:","options":["Heart wall (musculature/myocardium of the heart)","Walls of the stomach","Skeletal limbs","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall (the musculature/myocardium of the heart).  Why B/C/D wrong: Stomach/limbs/diaphragm contain smooth or skeletal muscle, not cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Cardiac muscle is described as being:","options":["Striated and involuntary in nature","Non-striated and voluntary","Striated and voluntary","Non-striated and involuntary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary in nature.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mischaracterize cardiac muscle, which is uniquely striated yet involuntary."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Muscle Tissue summary table, which muscle is correctly matched to its nature and control?","options":["Skeletal — striated — voluntary","Smooth — striated — voluntary","Cardiac — un-striated — voluntary","Skeletal — un-striated — involuntary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary; smooth is un-striated and involuntary; cardiac is striated and in-voluntary.  Why B wrong: Smooth muscle is un-striated and involuntary.  Why C wrong: Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary.  Why D wrong: Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Regarding the striations of skeletal muscle, the A-band (anisotropic band) contains:","options":["The dark band only","Both the dark band and the light band (the dark band contains the thick myofilament/actin)","Only the thin filament","No filaments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The A-band (anisotropic band) contains both the dark band and the light band; the dark band contains the thick filament.  Why A/C wrong: The A-band is not solely dark or solely thin filament.  Why D wrong: The A-band contains filaments."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In skeletal muscle striation, the I-band (isotropic band) is described as the:","options":["Light band, which contains the thin filament (actin)","Dark band containing thick filament","Z-disc only","The myosin head only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The I-band (isotropic band) is the light band and contains the thin filament (actin).  Why B wrong: The dark band (A-band region) contains thick filament.  Why C/D wrong: These are distinct structures, not the I-band definition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Nervous tissue is composed of how many main parts, and what are they?","options":["Two parts: dendrites and axons","Three main parts — nerves, the spinal cord, and the brain","Four parts including bone","One part: the neuron only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nervous tissue is composed of three main parts: nerves, the spinal cord, and the brain.  Why A/D wrong: Dendrites/axons and the neuron are components but not the \"three main parts\" described.  Why C wrong: Bone is not part of nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Nervous tissue is responsible for coordinating and controlling many body activities. The primary function of nervous tissue is to:","options":["Receive stimuli and send impulses to the spinal cord and brain, and from the brain to the muscles back as a response to the stimuli via the nerves","Store fat for energy","Carry oxygen in the blood","Secrete digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary function of nervous tissue is to receive stimuli and send impulses to the spinal cord and brain, and from the brain back to the muscles via nerves as a response.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are functions of adipose, blood, and glandular tissues."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Neurons consist of which key components? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Cell body (soma)","Axon","Dendrites","Myofibrils","Sarcomeres"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Neurons consist of a cell body (soma), an axon, and dendrites, plus supporting cells.  Why D/E wrong: Myofibrils and sarcomeres are muscle (skeletal) structures."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Dendrites are the part of the neuron that:","options":["Connect to other neurons (carry impulses toward the cell body)","Carry impulses away from the cell body","Form the supporting (neuroglia) cells","Secrete hormones into the blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dendrites are connected to/by other neurons, receiving impulses toward the cell body.  Why B wrong: The axon carries impulses away from the cell body.  Why C wrong: Neuroglia are separate supporting cells.  Why D wrong: Neurons conduct impulses; they do not secrete hormones into blood as a defining role here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Neuroglia (also written as neuralgia in the source) are best described as:","options":["Supporting cells of nervous tissue","The contractile cells of the heart","Phagocytic cells of the blood","Cells that store fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neuroglia are the supporting cells of nervous tissue.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cardiac muscle cells, leucocytes, and adipocytes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Neurons do not have centrosomes; therefore they:","options":["Cannot undergo division and so have minimal power of regeneration","Divide rapidly to replace lost cells","Multiply only in the bone marrow","Are easily replaced after injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurons do not have centrosomes, so they cannot undergo division and have minimal power of regeneration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lack of centrosomes precludes rapid division/easy replacement of neurons."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Two types of tissue are found in nervous tissue. Which of the following are they? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Excitable cells, which are known as neurons","Non-excitable cells, which are known as glial cells (neuroglia), and which support and nourish neurons","Cardiac myocytes","Chondrocytes","Osteocytes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Nervous tissue contains excitable cells (neurons) that initiate, receive, conduct, and transmit information, and non-excitable cells (glial/neuroglia) that support and nourish neurons.  Why C/D/E wrong: Cardiac myocytes, chondrocytes, and osteocytes are not nervous tissue cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"On the basis of function, nerve cells are classified into how many types?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the basis of function, nerve cells are classified into three types: sensory neurons, motor neurons, and interneurons (synapse).  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies three functional types."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Sensory (afferent) neurons function to:","options":["Carry impulses away from the CNS to muscles/glands","Receive sensory or afferent neurons; they are carrying the sensory impulses from the periphery to the CNS","Form the supporting glial network","Store and release neurotransmitters into the blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sensory (afferent) neurons carry sensory impulses from the periphery to the CNS.  Why A wrong: Carrying impulses away from the CNS is the motor (efferent) neuron's role.  Why C wrong: Supporting cells are neuroglia.  Why D wrong: This is not the function of sensory neurons described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Motor (efferent) neurons function to:","options":["Carry the motor impulses from the CNS to the periphery (e.g., to muscles/glands)","Carry sensory impulses to the CNS","Connect sensory and motor neurons within the CNS only","Produce myelin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Motor (efferent) neurons carry the motor impulses from the CNS to the periphery.  Why B wrong: Carrying sensory impulses to the CNS is the sensory neuron's role.  Why C wrong: That describes interneurons.  Why D wrong: Myelin is produced by Schwann cells/oligodendrocytes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"A synapse is the junction by which the neurons communicate with each other. It is described as:","options":["Where neurons communicate without physically contacting with chemical messages (neurotransmitters)","A direct physical fusion of two cell membranes","A point of calcium salt deposition","The matrix of cartilage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A synapse is the junction by which neurons communicate with each other, without physically contacting, via chemical messages (neurotransmitters).  Why B wrong: Neurons communicate across a synaptic gap, not by membrane fusion.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to a synapse."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Nerve Tissue table, the cell body of a neuron:","options":["Contains the nucleus and is the metabolic center of the neuron","Carries away signals from the dendrites","Specializes in cell-to-cell connection (synapse)","Produces myelin in the peripheral nervous system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cell body contains the nucleus and regulates/contains the metabolic functioning of the neuron.  Why B wrong: That relates to the axon, not the cell body.  Why C wrong: That is the synapse role.  Why D wrong: Myelin production in the PNS is a Schwann cell role."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Nerve Tissue table, the axon functions to:","options":["Carry/transmit impulses away from the cell body to another neuron","Receive impulses from other neurons","Support and insulate the neuron","Phagocytose pathogens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The axon (cellular projection) carries/transmits impulses away from the cell body to the next neuron.  Why B wrong: Receiving impulses is a dendrite function.  Why C wrong: Support/insulation is provided by neuroglia/Schwann cells.  Why D wrong: Phagocytosis is a macrophage/microglia role."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Nerve Tissue table, dendrites function to:","options":["Transmit impulses from one neuron to another (carry impulses toward the cell body)","Carry impulses away from the cell body","Form the synaptic cleft","Produce neurotransmitters in muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dendrites (cellular processes) transmit/carry impulses from another neuron toward the cell body.  Why B wrong: That is the axon function.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the dendrite functions described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Nerve Tissue table, the synapse is a space between which structures and is involved in:","options":["The space between the axon of one neuron and the dendrite or cell body of the next neuron; involved in transmitting impulses from one neuron to another","The gap inside a single cell body","A junction between two bones","The matrix surrounding chondrocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A synapse is the space between the axon of one neuron and the dendrites/cell body of the next, transmitting impulses from one neuron to another.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe unrelated structures."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Neuroglia in the Nerve Tissue table are described as specialized cells that:","options":["Support, nourish, and protect neurons (support cells of the central nervous system)","Conduct action potentials","Carry oxygen in the blood","Contract to produce movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neuroglia are specialized cells of the nervous system that support, nourish, and protect neurons.  Why B wrong: Conduction of impulses is the neuron's role.  Why C wrong: Oxygen transport is by RBCs.  Why D wrong: Contraction is a muscle function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"In the Nerve Tissue table, Schwann cells are specialized cells of the:","options":["Peripheral nervous system, which form the myelin sheath around peripheral nerves","Central nervous system only","Bone marrow","Cardiac conduction system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schwann cells are specialized cells of the peripheral nervous system that form the myelin sheath around peripheral nerve axons.  Why B wrong: In the CNS, oligodendrocytes (not Schwann cells) myelinate.  Why C/D wrong: Schwann cells are not bone marrow or cardiac conduction cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The integumentary system is the largest organ of the body, with a total surface area of almost:","options":["13–20 square feet (1.5–2 m²)","5–8 square feet","30 square feet","50 square feet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The integumentary system is the largest organ of the body, with a total area of almost 13–20 square feet (and the skin covers about 1.5–2 m² surface area).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The integumentary system provides a physical barrier or protects the internal tissues from which threats? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Injury","Bacteria","Trauma","Hormonal imbalance","Genetic mutation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The integumentary system protects internal tissues from injury, bacteria, and trauma.  Why D/E wrong: Hormonal imbalance and genetic mutation are not barriers the integument addresses as described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The integumentary system organs include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Skin","Associated glands","Hair","Nails","Bone marrow"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Integumentary system organs are skin, associated glands, hair, and nails.  Why E wrong: Bone marrow is part of the skeletal/hematopoietic system."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The skin is a vital and largest organ of the human body. What proportion of total body weight does it constitute?","options":["5% of total body weight","15% of total body weight (about 9 lb or 4 kg)","25% of total body weight","40% of total body weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The skin constitutes 15% of total body weight (about 9 lb or 4 kg) and covers 1.5–2 m² of surface area.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated proportion."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Phagocytic Langerhans cells protect against pathogens through immune response. They are found in which layer of the epidermis?","options":["The stratum spinosum layer of the epidermis","The dermis","The subcutaneous fat","The stratum corneum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phagocytic Langerhans cells protect against pathogens through immune response and are found in the stratum spinosum layer of the epidermis.  Why B/C wrong: They reside in the epidermis, not the dermis or subcutaneous fat.  Why D wrong: The text localizes them to the stratum spinosum, not the corneum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Which of the following are listed functions of the skin? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Protect from infection as a first line of defence","Regulate normal body temperature (thermoregulation) and maintain homeostasis","Protect underlying tissues or organs from external forces or injury","Synthesis and storage of vitamin D","Detection of sensory stimuli from the external environment; store water and wastes; excrete salt, water, and wastes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The skin protects from infection (first line of defence), regulates temperature/maintains homeostasis, protects underlying tissues from external forces/injury, synthesizes and stores vitamin D, detects sensory stimuli, and stores/excretes water, salt, and wastes.  (No incorrect options — all listed are functions of the skin.)"},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The skin is composed of how many layers, and what are they?","options":["Two: epidermis and dermis only","Three layers: 1. Epidermis, 2. Dermis, 3. Hypodermis (subcutaneous tissue)","Four layers including muscle","One single layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The layers of the skin are three: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis (subcutaneous tissue/fat).  Why A wrong: It omits the hypodermis.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated three-layer structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The epidermis is the outermost layer of the skin and is composed of which type of epithelium?","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epidermis is the outermost layer of the skin, composed of stratified squamous epithelium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These epithelial types are not the composition of the epidermis."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The epidermis has no blood vessels, so it is totally dependent on which layer for nutrition?","options":["The underlying dermis for nutrition","The hypodermis only","The bloodstream directly","Sweat glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epidermis has no blood vessels, so it is totally dependent on the underlying dermis for nutrition.  Why B/C/D wrong: The text specifies the dermis as the nutritive source for the avascular epidermis."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The epidermis is thinnest over which body areas, and thickest over which?","options":["Thinnest over the eyelids and glans penis; thickest over the palms of the hands and soles of the feet","Thinnest over the palms; thickest over the eyelids","Uniform thickness everywhere","Thickest over the abdomen only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epidermis is thickest over the palms of the hands and soles of the feet, and thinnest over the eyelids and glans penis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the relative thickness described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"The layers of the epidermis (deep to superficial / inner to outer) include which strata? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stratum corneum (outermost layer of skin)","Stratum lucidum (found on thick skin of soles and palms)","Stratum granulosum","Stratum germinativum (inner, deepest layer of skin) consisting of stratum basale and stratum spinosum","Stratum cartilagium"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The layers of the epidermis are stratum corneum (outermost), stratum lucidum (found on thick skin of soles/palms), stratum granulosum, and stratum germinativum (innermost, comprising stratum basale and stratum spinosum).  Why E wrong: \"Stratum cartilagium\" is not a layer of the epidermis (cartilage is connective tissue)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Where are melanocytes and keratinocytes present in the epidermis?","options":["In the stratum germinativum (the innermost/deepest layer)","Only in the stratum corneum","In the dermis","In the hypodermis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Melanocytes and keratinocytes are present in the stratum germinativum, the inner/deepest layer of the epidermis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cells are produced in the stratum germinativum (basale), not the corneum, dermis, or hypodermis."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Different types of cells found in the epidermis include: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Horny cells (corneocytes)","Keratinocytes","Melanocytes","Merkel cells and Langerhans cells","Osteocytes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The epidermis contains horny cells (corneocytes), keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells, and Langerhans cells.  Why E wrong: Osteocytes are bone cells, not epidermal cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Horny cells (corneocytes) are cornified cells found in the stratum corneum that:","options":["Provide mechanical protection to the underlying epidermis and act as a barrier to prevent water loss and invasion by foreign substances","Produce melanin","Synthesize keratin","Detect touch sensation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Horny cells (corneocytes) are cornified cells in the stratum corneum that provide mechanical protection to the underlying epidermis and form a barrier to prevent water loss and invasion by foreign substances.  Why B wrong: Melanin production is by melanocytes.  Why C wrong: Keratin synthesis is by keratinocytes.  Why D wrong: Touch detection is by Merkel cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Keratinocytes are found in the stratum germinativum and are responsible for:","options":["Synthesis of keratin protein, which acts as a protective barrier of the skin","Production of melanin","Immune defense via phagocytosis","Detection of pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Keratinocytes are found in the stratum germinativum and are responsible for synthesis of keratin protein, which acts as a protective barrier of the skin.  Why B wrong: Melanin is produced by melanocytes.  Why C wrong: Phagocytic immune defense is by Langerhans cells.  Why D wrong: Pressure detection relates to Merkel cells."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Melanocytes, found in the stratum germinativum, produce melanin pigment. Melanin is responsible for: (Select All That Apply)","options":["The colour of the skin, hair, and eyes","Determining the skin colour (more melanin, darker skin colour)","Protecting against UV light by absorbing it","Synthesizing keratin","Carrying oxygen"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Melanocytes produce melanin pigment, which is responsible for the colour of the skin, hair, and eyes; it determines skin colour (more melanin = darker skin) and protects against UV light by absorbing it.  Why D wrong: Keratin synthesis is a keratinocyte function.  Why E wrong: Oxygen transport is a blood function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Merkel cells, found in the stratum germinativum of the epidermis, are closely associated with which structures and function in:","options":["Closely associated with the nerve endings, where they detect touch sensation (acting in detecting and transmitting touch/pressure stimuli, surrounding the nerve receptors)","Producing melanin","Phagocytosis of pathogens","Forming the myelin sheath"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Merkel cells are found in the stratum germinativum, very closely associated with nerve endings, and are involved in detecting/transmitting touch (mechanoreceptor function) and surround the nerve receptors.  Why B wrong: Melanin is produced by melanocytes.  Why C wrong: Phagocytosis is a Langerhans cell function.  Why D wrong: Myelin sheath formation is a Schwann cell function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Tissues overview & histology, epithelial tissue types and functions…","question":"Epidermal appendages are epidermal and dermal derived components of the skin that include which structures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hair","Nails","Sweat glands","Sebaceous glands","Skeletal muscle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Epidermal appendages include hair, nails, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands.  Why E wrong: Skeletal muscle is not an epidermal appendage."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The dermis, located beneath the epidermis, is best described as which of the following?","options":["The thinnest layer of the skin","The thickest layer of the skin","A layer of subcutaneous fat only","A keratinized outer covering"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the dermis lies beneath the epidermis and is the thickest layer of the skin.  Why A wrong: It is the thickest, not the thinnest, layer.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous fat is part of the hypodermis, not the defining feature of the dermis.  Why D wrong: The keratinized outer covering describes the epidermis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The dermis is made up of which two layers?","options":["Papillary dermis and reticular dermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale","Epidermis and hypodermis","Cortex and medulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the dermis is made up of two layers — the papillary dermis and the reticular dermis.  Why B wrong: Stratum corneum/basale are epidermal layers.  Why C wrong: Epidermis and hypodermis are separate skin regions, not subdivisions of the dermis.  Why D wrong: Cortex and medulla describe hair or other organs, not the dermis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which layer of the dermis regulates temperature, consists of capillaries, and contains elastic fibers?","options":["Reticular dermis","Papillary dermis","Hypodermis","Stratum lucidum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The papillary dermis regulates temperature, consists of capillaries, and has elastic fibers.  Why A wrong: The reticular dermis consists of hair follicles, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands.  Why C wrong: The hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer beneath the dermis.  Why D wrong: Stratum lucidum is an epidermal layer."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The reticular dermis consists of which of the following structures?","options":["Only blood capillaries","Hair follicles, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands","Adipose tissue for energy storage","Keratinocytes and melanocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The reticular dermis consists of hair follicles, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands.  Why A wrong: Capillaries are characteristic of the papillary dermis.  Why C wrong: Adipose tissue and energy storage are functions of the hypodermis.  Why D wrong: Keratinocytes and melanocytes are predominantly epidermal cells."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Blood supply, nerves, lymph, and the roots of hair and sweat/sebaceous glands are found in which skin layer?","options":["Epidermis","Dermis","Stratum corneum","Cuticle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the dermis has blood supply, nerve, lymph, sweat, sebaceous glands, and hair roots.  Why A wrong: The epidermis is avascular.  Why C wrong: Stratum corneum is the superficial keratin layer of the epidermis.  Why D wrong: The cuticle is a nail/hair structure."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Into which layer of the skin are intradermal injections (ID) given?","options":["Subcutaneous tissue","The dermis","Muscle","Stratum corneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intradermal injections (ID) are given in the dermis.  Why A wrong: Subcutaneous injections (SC) are given in the hypodermis.  Why C wrong: Intramuscular injections go into muscle, not intradermal.  Why D wrong: The stratum corneum is the outermost dead keratin layer; injections are not placed there."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The hypodermis is also known as which of the following?","options":["The papillary layer","The subcutaneous layer or tissue","The reticular layer","The stratum basale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hypodermis is also known as the subcutaneous layer or tissue.  Why A/C/D wrong: Papillary and reticular are dermal layers; stratum basale is an epidermal layer."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The hypodermis is the deepest layer of which structure?","options":["The dermis","The epidermis","The skin","The nail bed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the hypodermis is the deepest layer of the skin.  Why A wrong: The hypodermis lies below the dermis, not as a part of it.  Why B wrong: The epidermis is the most superficial layer.  Why D wrong: The nail bed is unrelated to the hypodermis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The hypodermis is composed primarily of which type of tissue?","options":["Dense collagen layers, muscles, and bones","Fats or adipose tissues and collagen","Keratin and melanin","Squamous epithelium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hypodermis is composed of fats or adipose tissues and collagen.  Why A wrong: It couples layers between skin, muscles, and bones but is not itself made of muscle/bone.  Why C wrong: Keratin and melanin are epidermal components.  Why D wrong: Squamous epithelium describes the epidermis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In which layer are subcutaneous injections (SC) administered?","options":["Dermis","Hypodermis","Muscle","Epidermis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states subcutaneous injections (SC) are given in the hypodermis layer.  Why A wrong: Intradermal injections are given in the dermis.  Why C wrong: Intramuscular injections target muscle.  Why D wrong: The epidermis is avascular and not an injection site."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following is a function of the hypodermis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Shock absorber","Connecting body heat / conserving body heat","Storage of fat for energy","Protection of internal organs from injury","Production of melanin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists hypodermis functions as shock absorber, conserving body heat, storage of fat for energy, and protection of internal organs from injury.  Why E wrong: Melanin production is a function of melanocytes in the epidermis, not the hypodermis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The nail is described as a transparent plate of which substance covering the dorsal surface of the fingers on hands and feet?","options":["Collagen","Keratin","Melanin","Sebum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nail is a hard transparent plate of keratin covering the dorsal surface of the fingers.  Why A wrong: Collagen is a dermal/connective tissue protein, not the nail plate material.  Why C wrong: Melanin is the skin pigment.  Why D wrong: Sebum is the oily secretion of sebaceous glands."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"What is the term for the skin fold covered by a thin layer of skin called the cuticle?","options":["Paronychia","Onychia","Nail roots","Koilonychia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines nail roots as the skin folds covered by a thin layer called the cuticle.  Why A wrong: Paronychia is infection of the surrounding tissues of the nails.  Why B wrong: Onychia is inflammation of the nails itself.  Why D wrong: Koilonychia (spoon nails) is a nail deformity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Infection of the soft tissues surrounding the nails is termed:","options":["Onychia","Paronychia","Koilonychia","Onychogryphosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paronychia is infection of the surrounding tissues of the nails.  Why A wrong: Onychia is inflammation of the nail itself.  Why C wrong: Koilonychia is spoon-shaped nail deformity.  Why D wrong: Onychogryphosis is thickening/overgrowth of nails."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Inflammation of the nail itself is known as:","options":["Paronychia","Onychia","Koilonychia","Onycholysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Onychia is inflammation of the nails itself.  Why A wrong: Paronychia involves the surrounding soft tissue.  Why C wrong: Koilonychia is spoon nail deformity.  Why D wrong: Onycholysis is separation of the nail from the bed, not described here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A nurse notes a client's nails are concave and spoon-shaped. This deformity (koilonychia) is most commonly associated with:","options":["Vitamin C deficiency","Iron deficiency disease","Calcium excess","Protein toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states koilonychia, also known as spoon nails, is a concave surface commonly seen in anemia/iron deficiency disease.  Why A wrong: Vitamin C deficiency is associated with poor wound healing, not spoon nails per this source.  Why C wrong: Calcium excess is not the listed cause.  Why D wrong: Protein toxicity is not described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Hardening of the nail roots commonly seen in nutritional deficiency states is termed:","options":["Onychophagia","Onychogryphosis","Onycholysis","Beau's lines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Onychogryphosis is hardening of the nail roots from nail rot, commonly seen in nutritional deficiency.  Why A wrong: Onychophagia is nail biting.  Why C wrong: Onycholysis is nail separation.  Why D wrong: Beau's lines are deep grooves/horizontal lines that run from side to side on the fingernail."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Deep grooves or horizontal lines that run from side to side on the fingernail and may indicate chronic illness are called:","options":["Koilonychia","Beau's lines","Onychia","Paronychia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beau's lines are deep grooves/horizontal lines running side to side on the nail that may indicate chronic illness.  Why A wrong: Koilonychia is a spoon-shaped concave deformity.  Why C/D wrong: Onychia and paronychia are inflammatory/infectious conditions, not grooved lines."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Hair is distributed over all parts of the body EXCEPT:","options":["The scalp and face","The palms and soles","The forearms","The legs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hairs are distributed overall the body except palms and soles.  Why A/C/D wrong: Scalp, face, forearms, and legs all bear hair."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The small muscle attached to a hair follicle that is responsible for goose bumps or cold creeps is the:","options":["Arrector pili muscle","Dartos muscle","Sebaceous muscle","Apocrine muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The arrector pili is a muscle attached to hair follicles and is responsible for goose bumps or cold creeps.  Why B wrong: The dartos muscle is in the scrotum, unrelated to hair.  Why C/D wrong: No \"sebaceous\" or \"apocrine\" muscle exists; these are glands."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Hair colour is determined by which pigment?","options":["Keratin","Melanin","Carotene","Hemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hair colour is determined by a pigment which is melanin.  Why A wrong: Keratin is the structural protein of hair, not its pigment.  Why C wrong: Carotene contributes to skin tone but is not the listed hair colour pigment here.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin is a blood pigment."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statement about hair function is correct?","options":["Hair has no role in protecting the skin","Hair serves as mechanical protection for the skin, restricts entry of sensory function, and aids in regulating body temperature","Hair primarily produces vitamin D","Hair secretes sweat for thermoregulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hair serves as mechanical protection for the skin, restricts entry/sensory function, and aids in regulating body temperature.  Why A wrong: Hair does have a protective role.  Why C wrong: Vitamin D production is a skin function via sunlight, not hair.  Why D wrong: Sweat is secreted by sweat glands, not hair."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"What is the normal lifespan of hair as stated in the source?","options":["10–30 days","30–100 days","50–100 hours/day","1–5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal hair loss per day is about 50–100 hairs/day (lifespan expressed as 50–100 hours/day in the text).  Why A/B/D wrong: These values do not match the stated source figure."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Sebaceous glands are found abundantly on which body areas? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Face","Scalp","Upper back","Chest","Palms and soles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source states sebaceous glands are found abundantly on the face, scalp, upper back, and chest.  Why E wrong: Sebaceous glands are absent over palms and soles."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The oily substance secreted by sebaceous glands is known as:","options":["Sweat","Sebum","Cerumen","Mucus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sebaceous glands secrete an oily substance which is sebum.  Why A wrong: Sweat is secreted by sweat glands.  Why C wrong: Cerumen is earwax (from ceruminous glands).  Why D wrong: Mucus is secreted by mucous membranes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Sweat glands secrete sweat from which structure, to help produce moisture?","options":["Ducts directly to muscle","Ducts that produce moisture (pores at the skin surface)","Hair follicles only","Sebaceous ducts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sweat glands secrete sweat from its ducts which help produce moisture.  Why A wrong: Sweat is delivered to the skin surface, not to muscle.  Why C wrong: Apocrine glands open into follicles, but the general statement is about ducts producing moisture.  Why D wrong: Sebaceous ducts carry sebum, not sweat."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Sweat glands are classified into which two types?","options":["Eccrine and apocrine","Sebaceous and ceruminous","Endocrine and exocrine","Merocrine and holocrine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies sweat glands into apocrine and eccrine glands.  Why B wrong: Ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands (apocrine) producing cerumen, but the two main types named are apocrine and eccrine.  Why C wrong: Endocrine/exocrine is a broad gland classification, not the sweat gland subtypes named.  Why D wrong: Merocrine/holocrine are secretory mechanisms, not the source's listed sweat gland types."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands (apocrine) which produce which substance?","options":["Sebum from the face","Cerumen from the ear wax","Sweat from the palms","Milk from the breast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands (apocrine) which produce cerumen from the ear wax.  Why A wrong: Sebum is from sebaceous glands.  Why C wrong: Eccrine glands produce sweat on palms.  Why D wrong: Milk is from mammary glands."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Apocrine glands are located in which areas and become active at what time? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Located in axilla, breast, anal region, ear canal, and labia majora","Become active just before puberty","Are responsible for odor","Found all over the body except lips","Secrete sweat producing glands"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): Apocrine glands are located in the axilla, breast, anal region, ear canal, and labia majora. (B): They become active just before puberty. (C): These glands are responsible for odor.  Why D wrong: Being found all over the body except lips describes eccrine glands.  Why E wrong: Eccrine glands are the sweat-producing glands found all over the body."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Eccrine glands are best described as:","options":["Glands active only just before puberty","Sweat-producing glands found all over the body except the lips, nail beds, ears, penis, and labia minora","Glands responsible for cerumen production","Glands found only in the axilla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states eccrine glands are sweat producing glands, found all over the body except lips, nail beds, ears, penis, and labia minora.  Why A wrong: Becoming active before puberty describes apocrine glands.  Why C wrong: Cerumen is from ceruminous glands.  Why D wrong: Apocrine, not eccrine, glands are concentrated in the axilla."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The highest density of eccrine glands is found in which body areas?","options":["Scalp and face","Palms and soles of the body","Axilla and groin","Anterior trunk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the highest density of eccrine glands is found in palms and soles of the body.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the highest-density sites for eccrine glands per the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) is best classified as which type of disorder?","options":["A bacterial infection","An autoimmune disease in which the body's immune system mistakenly attacks the body's healthy tissues","A genetic clotting disorder","A nutritional deficiency disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SLE is an autoimmune disease in which the body's immune system mistakenly attacks the body's own healthy tissues.  Why A wrong: It is not infectious.  Why C wrong: It is not a clotting disorder.  Why D wrong: It is not a nutritional deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"SLE has the potential to affect which body systems?","options":["Only the skin","All of the body's systems — e.g., joints, skin, brain, lungs, kidneys, and blood vessels","Only the cardiovascular system","Only the gastrointestinal tract"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SLE can affect almost all body systems e.g. joints, skin, brain, lungs, kidneys, and blood vessels.  Why A/C/D wrong: SLE is systemic, not confined to a single organ system."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In SLE, immune complexes are deposited in blood vessels among which substances?","options":["Collagen, fibers, and organs","Bone marrow and lymph nodes","Cerebrospinal fluid only","Synovial fluid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states immune complexes are deposited in blood vessels among collagen, fibers, and organs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific locations are not what the source lists for immune complex deposition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"SLE mostly affects which type of body tissue?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissues","Nervous tissue","Muscular tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SLE mostly affects the connective tissues.  Why A/C/D wrong: While other tissues may be involved systemically, the source specifies connective tissues are mostly affected."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"SLE involves necrosis of glomerular capillaries, inflammation of cerebral and ocular blood vessels, necrosis of lymph nodes, and vasculitis of the GI tract. This pathological process indicates:","options":["Localized infection","Widespread inflammation and degeneration of the basal layer of skin","Benign tumor growth","Reversible ischemia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes necrosis of glomerular capillaries, inflammation of cerebral/ocular vessels, necrosis of lymph nodes, vasculitis of GI tract, and degeneration of the basal layer of skin.  Why A wrong: SLE is autoimmune, not infectious.  Why C wrong: There is no benign tumor involved.  Why D wrong: The process involves necrosis and degeneration, not merely reversible ischemia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statement about SLE prognosis and epidemiology is correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is more common in females","There is no cure for SLE and the cause is unknown","Libman-Sacks Endocarditis is a non-infectious endocarditis seen in SLE","SLE is fully curable with antibiotics","It is exclusively a male disorder"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): SLE is more common in females. (B): There is no cure for SLE and the cause is unknown. (C): Libman-Sacks endocarditis is a non-infectious endocarditis seen in SLE.  Why D wrong: There is no cure; antibiotics do not cure an autoimmune disease.  Why E wrong: It is more common in females, not exclusively male."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which antibody test, immunofluorescence, and criteria are used for the diagnosis of SLE?","options":["Antinuclear antibody test (ANA Test), immunofluorescence, ACR/EULAR criteria","Rheumatoid factor and ESR only","Western blot and HbA1c","Schick test and Mantoux"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the antinuclear antibody test (ANA Test), immunofluorescence, and ACR/EULAR criteria for SLE diagnosis.  Why B wrong: RF/ESR are nonspecific and not the named diagnostic set.  Why C wrong: Western blot/HbA1c are unrelated.  Why D wrong: Schick and Mantoux are infection-related tests."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A classic skin manifestation of SLE seen on the face is the:","options":["Spider angioma","Butterfly rash (malar rash) on the face with erythema on the palms","Café-au-lait spots","Petechial rash on the soles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists fever, butterfly rashes on the face, and erythema on the palms as clinical features of SLE; the butterfly/malar rash spans the cheeks and nose.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the characteristic SLE facial rash described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of SLE? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Joint pain and malaise","Impairment of all body's system","Raynaud's phenomenon due to sympathetic stimulation by stress or cold","Fever","Improvement of all body systems with no symptoms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists joint pain and malaise, impairment of all body systems, Raynaud's phenomenon (due to sympathetic stimulation by stress or cold), and fever.  Why E wrong: SLE causes impairment, not improvement with no symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which class of medications is typically ordered for managing SLE?","options":["Steroids and anti-inflammatory (NSAIDs)","Antibiotics and antivirals","Anticoagulants only","Antifungals only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client is given ordered medicines such as steroids and anti-inflammatory (NSAIDs).  Why B/C/D wrong: SLE is autoimmune; antimicrobials and isolated anticoagulants/antifungals are not the primary listed therapy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with SLE who is also receiving treatment that may affect the kidneys should monitor for which complication?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Renal impairment (sign of renal involvement)","Hearing loss","Vitamin C toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states impairment in renal function are noted in SLE patients in initial phase; a patient with SLE should be monitored for sign of renal impairment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the renal-focused complication the source highlights for SLE monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Candida albicans is best described as:","options":["A bacterial skin infection","A superficial fungal infection of the skin and mucous membranes","A viral exanthem","An autoimmune connective tissue disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Candida albicans is a superficial fungal infection of the skin and mucous membranes.  Why A wrong: It is fungal, not bacterial.  Why C wrong: It is not a virus.  Why D wrong: It is not autoimmune."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Candidiasis (oral thrush/yeast infection) is most likely to occur in which condition?","options":["Hypertension","When it occurs in much that is called oral thrush or yeast infection — associated with immune compromise","Iron overload","Excess hydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if it occurs in mouth it is called oral thrush or yeast infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the listed associations for candidiasis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Clinical features of candidiasis include which of the following?","options":["Reddish skin with blisters and stings","Butterfly facial rash","Spoon-shaped nails","Vesicles along a dermatome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states clinical features include reddish skin with blisters, stings, and itches.  Why B wrong: Butterfly rash is characteristic of SLE.  Why C wrong: Spoon nails (koilonychia) relate to iron deficiency.  Why D wrong: Dermatomal vesicles are characteristic of herpes zoster."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which antifungal medication is given for candidiasis, per the source?","options":["Acyclovir","Nystatin","Penicillin","Metformin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states give antifungal medicines as per order e.g. Nystatin.  Why A wrong: Acyclovir is an antiviral (used for herpes zoster).  Why C wrong: Penicillin is an antibacterial.  Why D wrong: Metformin is an antidiabetic."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Herpes zoster is also known by which common name?","options":["Measles","Shingles","Chickenpox","Ringworm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states herpes zoster is also known as shingles.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct conditions; chickenpox is the primary infection caused by the same virus, but shingles is the specific synonym for herpes zoster."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Herpes zoster (shingles) is caused by which virus, which also causes chickenpox?","options":["Herpes simplex virus (HSV)","Varicella zoster virus (VZV)","Human papillomavirus (HPV)","Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is caused by varicella zoster virus (VZV), the same virus that causes chickenpox.  Why A wrong: HSV causes cold sores/genital herpes and is different from VZV.  Why C/D wrong: HPV and EBV cause other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Herpes zoster results from which process?","options":["Primary infection in a person never exposed to the virus","Reactivation of varicella zoster virus in a client who previously had chickenpox","Bacterial superinfection of intact skin","Allergic reaction to medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is caused by reactivation of varicella zoster virus in a client who previously had chickenpox.  Why A wrong: It is a reactivation, not a primary infection.  Why C wrong: It is viral, not a bacterial superinfection.  Why D wrong: It is not an allergic reaction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In herpes zoster, the virus is located in which structure?","options":["The bone marrow","The dorsal nerve ganglia","The lymph nodes","The kidney glomeruli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this virus is located in the dorsal nerve ganglia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the latency site of VZV described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"How is herpes zoster diagnosed, according to the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Visual examination","Viral culture","Tzanck smear","Mantoux test","ANA test"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A–C): The source lists visual examination, viral culture, and Tzanck smear as diagnostic methods.  Why D wrong: Mantoux is a tuberculin test.  Why E wrong: ANA test is for SLE."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Severe pain that can remain after the lesions of herpes zoster resolve is termed:","options":["Postherpetic neuralgia","Raynaud's phenomenon","Phantom limb pain","Referred pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states post-herpetic neuralgia (severe pain) can remain after the lesions resolve.  Why B wrong: Raynaud's phenomenon is a vasospastic response.  Why C/D wrong: Phantom and referred pain are unrelated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of herpes zoster? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Unilaterally clustered skin vesicles on the trunk, thorax, or face that may involve eyes","Uveitis (inflammation of the uveal layer of the eye) and blindness","Burning and pain, paresthesia, and pruritus","Mild fever and malaise","Bilateral symmetric butterfly rash"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists unilaterally clustered skin vesicles (trunk, thorax, or face, possibly involving eyes), uveitis and blindness, burning/pain/paresthesia/pruritus, and mild fever and malaise.  Why E wrong: A bilateral symmetric butterfly rash is characteristic of SLE, not herpes zoster (which is unilateral along a dermatome)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with herpes zoster? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Isolate the client and follow standard or contact precautions","Assess for neurovascular status or Bell's palsy (7th cranial nerve [CN] is compatible)","Prevent the client from rubbing and scratching","To give anti-viral medications e.g. Acyclovir","Encourage vigorous scratching to relieve itching"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists isolating the client and following standard/contact precautions, assessing for neurovascular status or Bell's palsy (7th CN), preventing rubbing/scratching, and giving anti-viral medications e.g. Acyclovir.  Why E wrong: The client should be prevented from scratching, not encouraged to scratch."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, how does herpes zoster virus (HZV) compare to herpes simplex virus (HSV) in terms of contagiousness of fluids?","options":["HSV causes a cold sore, usually on lips or mouth which are contagious; HZV causes herpes zoster, skin water-like lesion on perineal which are contagious","Neither virus is ever contagious","Both produce identical, non-fluid lesions","HZV is never associated with skin lesions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states HSV causes a cold sore, usually on lips or mouth which are contagious, while HZV causes herpes zoster — skin water(-like) lesion on perineal which are contagious.  Why B wrong: Both can be contagious from lesion fluid.  Why C wrong: Both produce fluid-filled lesions.  Why D wrong: HZV does produce skin lesions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Scleroderma is also known as which of the following?","options":["Progressive systemic sclerosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Pemphigus vulgaris","Psoriasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states scleroderma is also known as progressive systemic sclerosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct dermatological/autoimmune conditions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Scleroderma is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects which structure?","options":["The skin","Bone marrow","The renal tubules only","The cerebral cortex only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states scleroderma is an autoimmune disorder which primarily affects the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the primary target organ described, though systemic involvement can occur."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"What is the underlying disease process in systemic scleroderma?","options":["Thinning of skin with bacterial colonization","Fibrous changes in connective tissues throughout the body","Demyelination of peripheral nerves","Calcium loss from bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a systemic disease that causes fibrous changes in connective tissues throughout the body.  Why A wrong: Scleroderma causes thickening and stiffness/hardening of tissues, not thinning with bacterial colonization.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In scleroderma, affected blood vessels become which of the following?","options":["Thickened, stiff, and hardening due to disease process","Thin and fragile only","Completely normal","Filled with melanin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states affected blood vessels become thickened, stiffness, and hardening due to the disease process.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described vascular changes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The acronym \"CREST\" in CREST syndrome (a group/syndrome of scleroderma) — what does the letter \"C\" stand for?","options":["Calcium deposit","Cyanosis","Cellulitis","Contracture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In CREST syndrome, C stands for Calcium deposit (calcinosis).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the components of the CREST acronym."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In CREST syndrome, the letter \"R\" stands for:","options":["Renal failure","Raynaud's phenomenon","Reflux disease","Rash"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CREST, R stands for Raynaud's phenomenon.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the \"R\" component of CREST."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In CREST syndrome, the letter \"E\" stands for:","options":["Edema","Esophageal dysfunction","Eczema","Erythema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CREST, E stands for Esophageal dysfunction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the \"E\" component of CREST."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In CREST syndrome, the letter \"S\" stands for:","options":["Sclerodactyly — localized scleroderma, ulcer in digits","Sepsis","Sebaceous cyst","Stomatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In CREST, S stands for Sclerodactyly — localized scleroderma, ulcer in digits.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the \"S\" component of CREST."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In CREST syndrome, the letter \"T\" stands for:","options":["Thrombosis","Telangiectasia — vascular lesion formed by dilation of small blood vessels","Thrombocytopenia","Tinea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CREST, T stands for Telangiectasia — vascular lesion formed by dilation of small blood vessels.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the \"T\" component of CREST."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which instructions are appropriate when teaching a client with scleroderma about skin care? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Use mild soap and lotions for skin care","Avoid smoking","Limit exposure to cold","Encourage deep breathing exercise","Watch for side effects of immunosuppressive medicines"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The source instructs the client to use mild soap/lotions for skin care, avoid smoking, limit exposure to cold, encourage deep breathing exercise, and watch for side effects of immunosuppressive medicines.  All options are listed in the source as appropriate teaching points."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Besides initiating general nursing care, what is another component of scleroderma management per the source?","options":["Provide ordered medicines","Withhold all fluids","Apply heat continuously to extremities","Avoid any patient teaching"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"give ordered medicines\" and \"initiate general nursing care\" for scleroderma.  Why B wrong: There is no instruction to withhold fluids.  Why C wrong: The client is advised to limit cold exposure; continuous heat is not directed.  Why D wrong: Patient teaching about skin care is explicitly recommended."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The abbreviation MRSA stands for:","options":["Methicillin Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus","Multi-Resistant Streptococcus Aureus","Methicillin Reactive Salmonella","Major Resistant Staphylococcus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MRSA stands for Methicillin Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the acronym."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statement(s) about MRSA are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Skin or wound wounds become infected with MRSA","MRSA is a contagious infection and is spread to others by direct contact with infected skin or infected articles","Contact precaution can be initiated when caring for a client infected with MRSA","Administer antibiotics therapy as per order","MRSA is fully sensitive to methicillin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists that skin/wounds become infected with MRSA, that it is contagious and spread by direct contact with infected skin/articles, that contact precautions can be initiated when caring for an infected client, and that antibiotic therapy is administered as per order.  Why E wrong: By definition MRSA is methicillin-resistant, not sensitive."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A wound is best defined as injury to which structure?","options":["Only the bone","The lining tissues which happen quickly in which skin can be cut, torn, and pierced","Only internal organs","Only nerve tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a wound as injury to the lining tissues, which can happen quickly in which skin can be cut, torn, and pierced.  Why A/C/D wrong: A wound is broadly tissue injury, not limited to a single structure type."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of a \"tidy wound\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Incised, clean, healthy tissues and seldom tissue loss","Wounds which are inflicted by sharp instruments and contain no devitalized tissue, closed primarily with the expectation of quick primary healing","Examples include wounds in surgical incisions, cuts from glass and knife wounds","Tendons, arteries and nerves will commonly be injured, but repair of these structures is usually possible","Crushed or avulsed, contaminated, devitalized tissues with tissue loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source describes tidy wounds as incised, clean, healthy tissues with seldom tissue loss; inflicted by sharp instruments with no devitalized tissue and closed primarily for quick primary healing; examples include surgical incisions, glass cuts, and knife wounds; tendons/arteries/nerves may be injured but repair is usually possible.  Why E wrong: Crushed/avulsed, contaminated, devitalized tissue with tissue loss describes an untidy wound."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following describe an \"untidy wound\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Wound characteristics: crushed or avulsed, contaminated, devitalized tissues and tissue loss","Result from crushing, tearing, avulsion, vascular injury or burns, and contain devitalized tissue","Skin wounds will often be multiple and irregular","Tendons, arteries and nerves may be damaged and the injured in continuity, but will usually not be divided; fractures are common and may be multifragmentary","Such wounds must not be closed primarily; if they are closed wound healing is unlikely to occur without complications"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The source lists all these features for untidy wounds — crushed/avulsed/contaminated/devitalized tissue with tissue loss; resulting from crushing, tearing, avulsion, vascular injury, or burns; multiple and irregular skin wounds; damaged tendons/arteries/nerves injured in continuity but usually not divided with common multifragmentary fractures; and that they must not be closed primarily, as healing is unlikely without complications if closed.  All options are stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"According to classification by level of contamination, a \"clean wound\" is best described as:","options":["A wound with no organisms present, real without complications","A wound colonized by foreign bodies","A chronic wound with microorganisms, difficult to heal","A wound exposed to the outside environment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A clean wound has no organisms present, healing without complications.  Why B wrong: Foreign-body presence describes a contaminated wound.  Why C wrong: Microorganism-laden chronic wounds describe colonized wounds.  Why D wrong: Exposure to environment describes an open wound (classification by characteristics)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A wound that contains pathogenic organisms and foreign bodies is classified as:","options":["Clean","Contaminated","Infected","Colonized"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A contaminated wound contains pathogenic organisms and foreign bodies present in the wound.  Why A wrong: A clean wound has no organisms.  Why C wrong: An infected wound has pathogenic organisms with clinical signs of infection.  Why D wrong: A colonized wound is a chronic wound with microorganisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"An infected wound is characterized by pathogenic organisms multiplying and exhibiting which clinical signs of infection?","options":["No discharge and immediate healing","Yellow appearance, soreness, redness, and oozing pus","Intact, dry skin only","Pale, cool skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an infected wound has pathogenic organisms multiplying, exhibiting clinical signs of infection (yellow appearance, soreness, redness, oozing pus etc.).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not represent the signs of an infected wound described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A colonized wound is best described as:","options":["An acute, sterile wound","A chronic wound with microorganisms, difficult to heal i.e. bedsore","A clean surgical incision","A wound with no organisms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A colonized wound is a chronic wound with microorganisms, difficult to heal i.e. bedsore.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe clean or non-colonized wounds."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"According to classification by characteristics, a closed wound is one in which:","options":["The surface of the skin is intact, but the underlying tissues may be damaged (e.g. contusions, haematomas, or stage 1 pressure ulcers)","The skin is split or cracked, exposing underlying tissues","There is no tissue injury at all","The wound contains foreign bodies on the surface"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A closed wound has an intact skin surface but underlying tissues may be damaged, e.g. contusions, haematomas, or stage 1 pressure ulcers.  Why B wrong: Split/cracked skin exposing underlying tissue describes an open wound.  Why C wrong: There is underlying tissue damage in a closed wound.  Why D wrong: This is not the defining feature of a closed wound."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"An open wound is best described as one in which:","options":["The skin surface is intact","The skin is split or cracked and the underlying tissues are exposed to the outside environment","There is bleeding into a body cavity only","No tissue is involved"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An open wound has skin split or cracked and underlying tissues exposed to the outside environment.  Why A wrong: Intact skin describes a closed wound.  Why C wrong: That describes internal hemorrhage, not the open-wound definition.  Why D wrong: Tissue is involved in an open wound."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"According to classification by duration, an acute wound is one that:","options":["Is caused by injury to the skin that occurs suddenly rather than over time, e.g. surgical wounds, bites, burns, abrasions and traumatic wounds","Fails to heal in 4 weeks","Is always infected","Heals only by tertiary intention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An acute wound is caused by injury that occurs suddenly rather than over time, e.g. surgical wounds, bites, burns, abrasions, and traumatic wounds.  Why B wrong: Failure to heal in ~4 weeks describes a chronic wound.  Why C wrong: Acute wounds are not always infected.  Why D wrong: Healing intention is a separate classification."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A laceration is best described as:","options":["A regular tear-like wound caused by blunt trauma","An irregular tear-like wound caused by blunt trauma","A clean surgical cut","A pinpoint puncture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a laceration as an irregular tear-like wound caused by blunt trauma.  Why A wrong: It is irregular, not regular.  Why C wrong: A clean surgical cut is an incision.  Why D wrong: A pinpoint puncture is a different wound type."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"An abrasion is best described as:","options":["A deep stab wound","A superficial wound, topmost layer of the skin (epidermis) scraped off","A bullet-caused puncture","The tearing away of a limb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An abrasion is a superficial wound with the topmost layer of skin (epidermis) scraped off.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe puncture, bullet (perforation), and amputation respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A puncture wound is caused by which mechanism?","options":["Blunt force only","An object puncturing the skin by splinter, needle, or nail etc.","Scraping of the epidermis","Surgical cutting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A puncture wound is caused by an object puncturing the skin by splinter, needle, nail, etc.  Why A wrong: Blunt force causes contusions/lacerations.  Why C wrong: Scraping describes an abrasion.  Why D wrong: Surgical cutting describes an incision."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A penetration wound is caused by which mechanism?","options":["An object such as a knife entering and coming out from the skin","Scraping the epidermis","Blunt impact with intact skin","A bullet shattering bone only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A penetration wound is caused by an object such as a knife entering and coming out from the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe abrasion, contusion, and other mechanisms respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"An incision wound is best described as:","options":["An irregular blunt tear","A wound caused by a blade (e.g. surgical incision)","A scrape of the epidermis","A crush injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An incised/incision wound is caused by a blade.  Why A wrong: Irregular blunt tears are lacerations.  Why C wrong: Scrapes are abrasions.  Why D wrong: Crush injuries differ from clean incisions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A wound where the body surface is torn by either injury or surgery — the same as an abrasion but caused by more force, and ranging from a minor superficial wound to one that damages tissues under the skin — is termed:","options":["Crush wound (skin injury)","Incision","Puncture","Penetration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a crush (or skin injury) wound as a body surface torn by injury or surgery, the same as an abrasion but caused by more force, ranging from minor to damaging tissues under the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct mechanisms (blade, object puncture, object entering/exiting)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Match the strained-injury terms: A \"sprain\" refers to injury in/of which structure?","options":["Tendons","Ligaments","Muscles","Bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a sprain as injury in ligaments (under strained wounds).  Why A wrong: Injury in tendons is a strain.  Why C/D wrong: Muscles and bone are not the sprain definition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A \"strain\" refers to injury in/of which structure?","options":["Ligaments","Tendons","Skin","Cartilage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a strain as injury in tendons.  Why A wrong: Sprain is injury in ligaments.  Why C/D wrong: Skin and cartilage are not the strain definition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A perforation wound is best described as:","options":["A pinpoint haemorrhage <1 mm","A pinpoint haemorrhage which is <2 mm in diameter","A large haemorrhage >5 mm in diameter","A bruise with bleeding under the skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes perforation as a pinpoint haemorrhage which is <2 mm in diameter (under minor haemorrhage).  Why A/C/D wrong: These sizes/types correspond to other categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Purpura, as a type of minor haemorrhage, is defined as:","options":["A pinpoint haemorrhage <2 mm","A large skin-penetration haemorrhage ≥5 mm in diameter","A large haematoma >1 cm in diameter","Bleeding into a joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines purpura as a large skin-penetration haemorrhage ≥5 mm in diameter.  Why A wrong: <2 mm describes perforation/petechiae.  Why C wrong: >1 cm large bruise describes ecchymosis.  Why D wrong: Bleeding into a joint is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Ecchymosis, as a type of minor haemorrhage, is best described as:","options":["A pinpoint <2 mm haemorrhage","A large haemorrhage / bruise >1 cm in diameter","A medium 3 mm purplish spot","Bleeding into the abdominal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes ecchymosis as a large haemorrhage/bruise >1 cm in diameter.  Why A wrong: <2 mm describes perforation.  Why C wrong: That intermediate size is not the ecchymosis definition.  Why D wrong: Abdominal bleeding is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A haematoma is best described as:","options":["Caused by damaged or torn blood vessels, i.e. epidural haemorrhage, subdural haemorrhage","A superficial scrape of the epidermis","A clean surgical incision","A pinpoint petechial spot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a haematoma as caused by damaged or torn blood vessels, e.g. epidural haemorrhage, subdural haemorrhage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe abrasion, incision, and petechiae respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A petechial haemorrhage is defined as a haemorrhage of which size?","options":["<1 mm","<2 mm",">5 mm",">1 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines petechiae as a pinpoint haemorrhage <2 mm in diameter.  Why A/C/D wrong: These sizes correspond to other haemorrhage categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A contusion is best described as:","options":["A bruise caused by blunt force trauma that damages tissues under the skin","A clean surgical incision","A pinpoint petechial spot","A scraped epidermis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a contusion as a bruise caused by blunt force trauma that damages tissue under the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe incision, petechiae, and abrasion."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A chronic wound is best defined as one that:","options":["Heals within 24 hours","Has failed to heal in 4–6 weeks, e.g. vascular ulcers (venous or arterial ulcers), diabetic ulcers, and pressure ulcers","Is always caused by a sharp blade","Is sterile and clean"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A chronic wound is one that has failed to heal in 4–6 weeks, e.g. vascular ulcers (venous or arterial), diabetic ulcers, and pressure ulcers.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the chronic-wound definition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for poor wound healing per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Age","Malnutrition","Insufficient oxygenation or perfusion","Smoking","Weak immune status / infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The source lists age, malnutrition, insufficient oxygenation or perfusion, smoking, weak immune status, and infection as risk factors.  All options are listed risk factors in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are additional listed factors that delay or complicate wound healing? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Diabetes and other metabolic diseases","Other medications, radiation, and chemotherapy","Daily nutritious diet","Excellent perfusion","Youthful age with strong immunity"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A, B): The source lists diabetes and other metabolic diseases, and other medications, radiation, and chemotherapy among the risk/complicating factors.  Why C/D/E wrong: Good nutrition, excellent perfusion, and youth/strong immunity favour healing rather than impair it."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note on wounds, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Laceration is an unwilling open wound and incision is a willing open wound","Lacerated untidy wounds should not be closed immediately, considering the possible outcomes","All open wounds must always be closed immediately","Incisions are unwilling wounds"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A): The note states laceration is an unwilling open wound and incision is a willing open wound. (B): Lacerated untidy wounds should not be closed immediately, which role out the possible outcomes (considering possible outcomes).  Why C wrong: Untidy/lacerated wounds should not be closed immediately.  Why D wrong: Incisions are willing (intentional) open wounds, not unwilling."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Under the CDC Guidelines for Surgical Wound Classification, a Class-I (Clean) wound is defined as which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Non-infectious wounds","No inflammation","Primary wound closure & closed drainage","No lapse in sterile technique; no entrance into the GI tract, GU tract, and respiratory tract","Gross spillage of stomach contents"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source defines Class-I (Clean) as non-infectious wounds, no inflammation, primary wound closure & closed drainage, no lapse in sterile technique, and no entrance into the GI/GU/respiratory tracts.  Why E wrong: Gross spillage indicates Class-IV (dirty/infected) wounds."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which surgical examples and infection risk (SSI rate) correspond to a CDC Class-I (Clean) wound?","options":["Examples: hernia repair, mastectomy, thyroidectomy, AAA repair; SSI rate 1–5%","Examples: appendectomy; SSI rate 5–8%","Examples: small bowel resection; SSI rate 10–15%","Examples: abscess drainage; SSI rate >25%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hernia repair, mastectomy, thyroidectomy, and AAA repair as examples of Class-I clean wounds, with an SSI rate of 1–5%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to Class-II, Class-III, and Class-IV wounds respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A CDC Class-II (Clean Contaminated) wound is characterized by which features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Low level of contamination","Involved entry into the respiratory, alimentary, genital, or urinary tracts but only under controlled circumstances","No significant contamination","No evidence of infection or major break in sterile technique","Gross spillage with established infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source defines Class-II (Clean Contaminated) as a low level of contamination, controlled entry into the respiratory/alimentary/genital/urinary tracts, no significant contamination, and no evidence of infection or major break in sterile technique.  Why E wrong: Gross spillage with established infection describes Class-IV (dirty/infected)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which surgical examples and SSI rate correspond to a CDC Class-II (Clean Contaminated) wound?","options":["Examples: cholecystectomy, appendectomy, small bowel and colon resection; SSI rate 3–8%","Examples: hernia repair; SSI rate 1–5%","Examples: abscess drainage; SSI rate >25%","Examples: AAA repair; SSI rate 10–15%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists cholecystectomy, appendectomy, small bowel and colon resection as Class-II examples with an SSI rate of 3–8%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to other CDC classes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A CDC Class-III (Contaminated) wound is characterized by which features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["They are contaminated or infectious wounds","Results from a breach in sterile techniques","Major inflammation present (significant inflammation)","No contamination whatsoever","Examples include cholecystectomy with bile spillage, appendectomy for perforated appendicitis, and small bowel and colon resection following perforation"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The source defines Class-III (Contaminated) as contaminated or infectious wounds resulting from breach in sterile techniques, with significant inflammation; examples include cholecystectomy with bile spillage, appendectomy for perforated appendicitis, and small bowel/colon resection following perforation.  Why D wrong: A contaminated wound by definition has contamination present."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"What is the SSI (surgical site infection) rate associated with a CDC Class-III (Contaminated) wound?","options":["1–5%","3–8%","10–15%",">25%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the SSI rate for Class-III (Contaminated) wounds as 10–15%.  Why A wrong: 1–5% is Class-I.  Why B wrong: 3–8% is Class-II.  Why D wrong: That higher rate corresponds to Class-IV."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A CDC Class-IV (Dirty/Infected) wound is characterized by which features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Grossly infected","Results from inadequate treatment of old traumatic wounds with retained devitalized tissues, gross purulence, and evident infection","Tissues are lost and there is visual or obvious infection","Often closed by surgery or pathogen found in perforated organs","No infection and intact sterile field"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source defines Class-IV (Dirty/Infected) as grossly infected, resulting from inadequate treatment of old traumatic wounds with retained devitalized tissues, gross purulence and evident infection; tissues are lost with visual infection; and often closed by surgery or pathogen found in perforated organs.  Why E wrong: By definition Class-IV wounds are infected, not free of infection."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Primary healing (primary intention) is best described as:","options":["Healing of a clean wound without infection","Healing of a wound left open to granulate","Healing only after debridement and delayed closure","Healing that always results in severe scarring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines primary intention as the healing of a clean wound without infection.  Why B wrong: Open granulation describes secondary intention.  Why C wrong: Delayed closure after debridement describes tertiary intention.  Why D wrong: Primary intention results in reduced scarring, not severe scarring."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In primary intention healing, wound closure is performed with which methods?","options":["Wound closure is performed with sutures (stitches), staples, or adhesive tape or glue","The wound is left fully open to heal","Only secondary granulation is allowed","The wound is packed with gauze and never closed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states wound closure is performed with sutures (stitches), staples, or adhesive tape or glue.  Why B/C/D wrong: Leaving the wound open or packing it describes secondary intention healing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statements about primary intention healing are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Healing of a non-infected, well-approximated wound (e.g. a surgical incision) is the result","Faster than healing by secondary intention","Examples: well-repaired lacerations will reduce bone fractures, healing after flap surgery","It is more susceptible to infection and poor healing","It involves leaving the wound open to granulate"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): Primary intention is the healing of a non-infected, well-approximated wound, e.g. a surgical incision. (B): It is faster than healing by secondary intention. (C): Examples include well-repaired lacerations reducing fractures and healing after flap surgery.  Why D wrong: Secondary intention is more susceptible to infection and poor healing.  Why E wrong: Leaving the wound open describes secondary intention."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Secondary healing (secondary intention) is implemented when:","options":["The primary intention is not possible because of significant tissue damage or loss, or the wound is disrupted by infection, dehiscence, hypoxia, or immune dysfunction","A clean wound is closed with sutures","The wound is closed with glue","There is no tissue loss at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary intention is implemented when primary intention is not possible because of significant tissue damage or loss, or when the wound is disrupted by infection, dehiscence, hypoxia, or immune dysfunction.  Why B/C wrong: Closure with sutures/glue describes primary intention.  Why D wrong: Significant tissue loss is precisely what indicates secondary intention."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statements about secondary intention healing are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The healing process can be slow due to the presence of drainage from infection","The surgeon may pack the wound with gauze or use a drainage system","Granulation tissue formation and epithelialization over the new tissue take place","Wound care must be performed daily to encourage wound debris removal to allow for granulation tissue formation","The wound is closed immediately with sutures and heals fastest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source states secondary intention healing can be slow due to drainage from infection; the surgeon may pack the wound with gauze or use a drainage system; granulation tissue formation and epithelialization occur over new tissue; and wound care must be performed daily to encourage debris removal for granulation.  Why E wrong: Immediate suture closure is primary intention, which is faster; secondary intention heals more slowly."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which of the following are listed examples of conditions healing by secondary intention, and what is its susceptibility? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Examples: gingivectomy, gingivoplasty, tooth extraction sockets, poorly reduced fractures, burns, severe lacerations, pressure ulcers","It is more susceptible to infections and poor healing","It is the fastest, lowest-risk method of healing","It never requires daily wound care"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A): The source lists gingivectomy, gingivoplasty, tooth extraction sockets, poorly reduced fractures, burns, severe lacerations, and pressure ulcers as examples. (B): It is more susceptible to infections and poor healing.  Why C wrong: Primary intention is faster and lower-risk.  Why D wrong: Daily wound care is needed in secondary intention."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Tertiary healing (third intention) usually occurs when:","options":["Healing occurs immediately after closure with sutures","After granulation tissue has formed, the wound is initially cleaned, debrided and observed, then closed, typically 4 or 5 days before closure","A clean wound heals without infection","There is no debridement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tertiary (third) intention usually occurs after granulation tissue has formed; the wound is initially cleaned, debrided and observed; it is typically 4 or 5 days before closure, and the wound is purposely left open.  Why A wrong: Immediate closure describes primary intention.  Why C wrong: A clean wound healing without infection is primary intention.  Why D wrong: Tertiary intention involves debridement."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statements about tertiary (third) intention healing are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The wound is purposely left open","Typically 4 or 5 days before closure","Examples: healing of wounds by use of tissue grafts","It always heals faster than primary intention","It is used for clean, non-infected, immediately approximated wounds"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A–C): The source states the wound is purposely left open, closure typically occurs 4 or 5 days later, and examples include healing of wounds by use of tissue grafts.  Why D wrong: It is not described as faster than primary intention.  Why E wrong: Clean, immediately approximated wounds heal by primary intention."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"In the phases of wound healing diagram, the phases occur in which sequence over time?","options":["Hemostasis → Inflammation → Proliferation → Remodeling","Inflammation → Hemostasis → Remodeling → Proliferation","Proliferation → Hemostasis → Inflammation → Remodeling","Remodeling → Proliferation → Inflammation → Hemostasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows the phases as Hemostasis, then Inflammation, then Proliferation, then Remodeling, progressing over time (approx. 0, 5, 10, 15, 20 days).  Why B/C/D wrong: These reorder the phases incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The hemostasis phase of wound healing occurs at what time and lasts approximately how long?","options":["It begins at the time of injury and remains only for a few seconds to 2–3 minutes","It begins on day 5 and lasts 2 weeks","It begins on day 14","It begins in the proliferative phase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the hemostasis phase begins at the time of injury and remains only for a few seconds to 2–3 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the timing of the hemostasis phase."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which processes occur during the hemostasis phase? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Initially bleeding occurs and later fibrin clot formation begins","Vasoconstriction, platelet aggregation, and leucocytes migration","Collagen forms strengthening new granulation tissue","Scar tissue becomes thinner and firm","Vitamin C plays a role in this phase"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, E): The source states in the hemostasis phase, initially bleeding occurs and later fibrin clot formation occurs; vasoconstriction, platelet aggregation and leucocytes migration occur; and it offers high protein and vitamin C in this phase.  Why C wrong: Collagen strengthening of granulation tissue occurs in the fibroblastic/proliferative phase.  Why D wrong: Scar tissue thinning occurs in the maturation/remodeling phase."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which features characterize the inflammatory phase of wound healing? (Select all that apply.)","options":["This phase begins at the time of injury and lasts 3 to 5 days","Clinical manifestations include local oedema, pain, redness, and warmth","Immune system works to remove damaged cells","Macrophages also secrete growth factors and cytokines that stimulate fibroblastic formation","It offers high protein and vitamin C in this phase"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The source states the inflammatory phase begins at the time of injury and lasts 3 to 5 days; clinical manifestations include local oedema, pain, redness, and warmth; the immune system works to remove damaged cells; macrophages secrete growth factors and cytokines that stimulate fibroblastic formation; and it offers high protein and vitamin C in this phase.  All options are stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The fibroblastic or proliferative phase of wound healing begins and lasts during which timeframe?","options":["From the 4th day of injury to 2 weeks (or up to the 18th day)","From a few seconds to 3 minutes","From day 14 to day 21 only","At the time of injury only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this phase begins from the 4th day of injury to 2 weeks or up to the 18th day.  Why B wrong: That timeframe is the hemostasis phase.  Why C wrong: This timeframe overlaps but does not match the stated 4th day–18th day window.  Why D wrong: That describes the time of injury (hemostasis/inflammatory onset)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which processes occur during the fibroblastic/proliferative phase? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Scarce tissue forms and granulation tissue forms in 4–18 days","Collagen forms in the wound site, strengthening new granulation tissue","Wound edges begin to form","Epithelial cells migrate from the wound margins to bring the wound edge closer, with new tissue formation at the wound margins","It offers high protein and vitamin C in this phase"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The source states scar tissue forms and granulation tissue forms in 4–18 days; collagen forms in the wound site, strengthening new granulation tissue; wound edges begin to form; epithelial cells migrate from the wound margin to bring the wound edge closer with new tissue formation at the margins; and it offers high protein and vitamin C in this phase.  All options are stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"The maturation or remodeling phase of wound healing begins at which time?","options":["It begins from the 21st day onward","At the time of injury","On day 3","During the inflammatory phase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the maturation or remodeling phase begins from the 21st day onward.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated onset of the remodeling phase."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which statements describe the maturation/remodeling phase? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The length of time depends on the patient and wound-related complicating factors such as infection status, nutritional status, and duration of wound","Scar tissues become thinner and are firm and inelastic on palpation","It offers high protein-diet in this phase","Macrophages release cytokines to begin inflammation","Bleeding and fibrin clot formation occur in this phase"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A–C): The source states the length of time depends on patient and wound-related complicating factors such as infection status, nutritional status, and duration of wound; scar tissues become thinner and are firm and inelastic on palpation; and it offers a high protein diet in this phase.  Why D wrong: Macrophage cytokine release/inflammation occurs in the inflammatory phase.  Why E wrong: Bleeding and fibrin clot formation occur in the hemostasis phase."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which characteristics describe serous wound drainage (a type of wound drainage)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Clear or straw-coloured or serous-like fluid","Occurs as a normal part of the healing process","It is yellow, grey, or green","It indicates active bleeding","It is an abnormal condition"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A, B): The source describes serous drainage as clear or straw-coloured/serous-like fluid that occurs as a normal part of the healing process.  Why C wrong: Yellow/grey/green describes purulent drainage.  Why D wrong: Active bleeding describes sanguineous drainage.  Why E wrong: Serous drainage is normal, not abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which characteristics describe serosanguineous wound drainage? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Serous with a small amount of blood","Pink-coloured due to the presence of a small amount of blood cells that mixed in serous","Occurs as a normal part of the healing process","Thick yellow pus indicating infection","Bright red active bleeding only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A–C): The source describes serosanguineous drainage as serous with a small amount of blood, pink-coloured due to the presence of a small amount of blood cells mixed in serous, and occurring as a normal part of the healing process.  Why D wrong: Thick yellow pus describes purulent drainage.  Why E wrong: Bright red active bleeding describes sanguineous drainage."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which characteristics describe sanguineous wound drainage? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is red drainage from trauma site","Indicates active bleeding","It may occur during wound cleansing or by other trauma to the wound site","Sanguineous drainage is uncommon or abnormal in amount; if noted, immediately inform the doctor","It is clear and straw-coloured and always normal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source states sanguineous drainage is red drainage from a trauma site, indicates active bleeding, may occur during wound cleansing or by other trauma to the wound site, and is uncommon/abnormal in amount; if noted, immediately inform the doctor.  Why E wrong: Clear straw-coloured drainage describes serous drainage, not sanguineous."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"Which characteristics describe purulent wound drainage? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is yellow, grey, or green drainage","Indicates infection","Green drainage usually occurs due to pseudomonas bacterial infection","It is an abnormal condition","It is a normal part of the healing process"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source describes purulent drainage as yellow, grey, or green; indicating infection; with green drainage usually occurring due to pseudomonas bacterial infection; and being an abnormal condition.  Why E wrong: Purulent drainage is abnormal and indicates infection, not normal healing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A nurse assessing a surgical wound notes green drainage. This finding is most likely caused by which organism?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas bacteria","Candida albicans","Varicella zoster virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states green drainage usually occurs due to pseudomonas bacterial infection.  Why A wrong: While Staphylococcus causes infection, the source specifically attributes green drainage to pseudomonas.  Why C wrong: Candida is a fungal cause of skin/mucosal infection, not the source-named cause of green drainage.  Why D wrong: VZV causes herpes zoster, not purulent green drainage."},{"section":"Batch 003 · integumentary anatomy (dermis, hypodermis, nails, hair, skin glands)…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with a wound healing by secondary intention should perform daily wound care primarily to:","options":["Close the wound immediately with sutures","Encourage wound debris removal to allow granulation tissue formation","Apply continuous cold therapy","Prevent any granulation tissue from forming"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states wound care must be performed daily to encourage wound debris removal to allow for granulation tissue formation in secondary intention healing.  Why A wrong: Immediate suture closure is primary intention, not secondary.  Why C wrong: Continuous cold therapy is not the listed purpose.  Why D wrong: The goal is to promote, not prevent, granulation tissue formation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A nurse is providing first aid to a snake-bite victim in the field. What is the FIRST priority action?","options":["Apply a tourniquet above the bite","Move the victim to a safe area away from the snake","Wash the wound vigorously with water","Make a cut over the bite to drain venom"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority is to move the victim to a safe area away from the dangerous snake (if any) so that no further bites occur.  Why A wrong: A tourniquet should NOT be applied; it increases circulatory compromise and necrosis.  Why C wrong: Vigorous washing/clearing introduces infection and increases venom absorption.  Why D wrong: Cutting the bite causes further harm and bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which position should a snake-bite victim be kept in to decrease venous circulation of the venom?","options":["Standing upright and walking","Kept calm and provided rest to decrease venous circulation","Trendelenburg (head-down) position","Vigorous activity to disperse the venom"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The victim should be kept calm and provided rest, which decreases venous circulation and slows venom spread.  Why A wrong: Walking/activity increases circulation and accelerates venom absorption.  Why C wrong: This position is not indicated for snake bite.  Why D wrong: Activity worsens venom spread, the opposite of the goal."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A snake-bite victim asks whether the bitten limb should be raised. The correct nursing action is to keep the bitten area:","options":["Above the level of the heart","At or below the level of the heart, but do not move it","Tightly wrapped and elevated","Continuously massaged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bitten area should always be kept below the level of the heart and not moved, to limit venom spread.  Why A wrong: Raising the limb above the heart speeds systemic venom distribution.  Why C wrong: Tight wrapping (tourniquet/compression) worsens local necrosis; elevation is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Massage increases absorption of venom."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"According to guidelines, when applying a tourniquet/light bandage in snake bite, where should it be placed?","options":["Directly over the bite mark","On the bitten area, near to the bitten area","On the opposite (unaffected) limb","Around the neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A light bandage, if applied, should be near to the bitten area as per guidelines (note: a tight tourniquet is otherwise discouraged).  Why A wrong: It is not applied directly on the bite mark itself.  Why C wrong: Applying to the opposite limb is meaningless.  Why D wrong: The neck is never an appropriate site."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why should a nurse NOT wash the snake-bite wound and avoid vigorous clearing, massage, or application of chemicals/creams?","options":["It improves clotting","It can introduce infection, increase absorption of the venom, and increase local bleeding","It neutralizes the venom","It is required to identify the snake species"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Washing/clearing/massage and applying chemicals or creams can introduce infection, increase venom absorption, and increase local bleeding.  Why A wrong: It does not improve clotting; bleeding may increase.  Why C wrong: It does not neutralize venom; only antivenom does.  Why D wrong: Species identification is from the snake/marks, not from washing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"When venom absorption is a concern, which technique should the nurse use over the bite wound?","options":["Tightly bind the whole limb","Increase absorption with heat","Use ice packs to apply cold around the bitten area","Apply a suction device for an hour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ice packs / cold application around the bitten area help reduce/slow venom spread.  Why A wrong: Tight binding (tourniquet) is discouraged due to necrosis risk.  Why B wrong: Heat increases circulation and venom spread.  Why D wrong: Suction is not the recommended cold/local control measure described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"What is the most important definitive treatment for a venomous snake bite that the nurse should anticipate administering?","options":["Oral antibiotics only","Inject/administer antivenom (Anti-Snake Venom, ASV) locally/as ordered","Caffeine tablets","Topical corticosteroid cream"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antivenom (ASV) is the specific treatment and should be injected/administered as ordered.  Why A wrong: Antibiotics treat secondary infection, not envenomation.  Why C wrong: Caffeine is to be avoided in snake bite.  Why D wrong: Topical steroids do not counteract systemic venom."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The nurse instructs a snake-bite victim to avoid sleep and keep an eye open. What is the rationale?","options":["To prevent dehydration","To keep the victim awake and observable for deteriorating condition","To increase venom excretion","To reduce pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The victim should be instructed to avoid sleep and keep eyes open so they remain awake/monitored for worsening.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the reasons given; the goal is alertness and ongoing observation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why are caffeine and electrical (electricity) therapies contraindicated in a snake-bite victim?","options":["They neutralize venom too quickly","Caffeine is to be avoided and electrical therapy may do more harm","They are only contraindicated in children","They reduce the need for antivenom"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Caffeine should be avoided, and electrical therapy has no role in snakebite first aid and may do more harm.  Why A wrong: They do not neutralize venom.  Why C wrong: The contraindication is general, not age-restricted.  Why D wrong: They do not reduce antivenom requirements."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A community member states, \"Don't pick up the snake while wrapping it up or kill it, as this will increase your chance of getting bitten.\" This advice is based on which fact about snakes?","options":["Dead snakes never bite","Even dead snakes can bite","Snakes only bite at night","Wrapping a snake neutralizes it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source warns that even dead snakes can bite, so the snake should not be handled.  Why A wrong: This is the myth being corrected — dead snakes CAN still bite.  Why C wrong: Time of day is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Wrapping does not neutralize a snake and risks a bite."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"According to the snake-bite \"don'ts,\" which of the following should be AVOIDED? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Apply a tourniquet","Cut the wound at all","Apply ice or use water to submerge the wound","Drink alcohol or take caffeine","Use a permethrin-based medication on the bite"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these as things NOT to do — don't apply a tourniquet, don't cut the wound, don't apply ice or submerge the wound in water, don't drink beverages with caffeine, and don't take any permethrin-based medication.  Why each is correct (all selected): Each of these actions can worsen tissue damage, increase venom spread/necrosis, or cause harm and are explicitly prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"After snake-bite first aid, what is the most important instruction regarding definitive care?","options":["Wait 24 hours to see if symptoms appear","Contact a poison control centre as soon as possible to determine the best initial management","Treat entirely at home with herbal remedies","Apply repeated tourniquets every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note instructs that in case of a venomous bite, one should contact a poison control centre as soon as possible to determine the best initial management.  Why A wrong: Delaying care risks systemic envenomation; prompt help is needed.  Why C wrong: Home herbal remedies are not evidence-based and delay antivenom.  Why D wrong: Tourniquets are discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Frostbite is best defined as:","options":["Burn injury from electrical current","Damage to tissues and blood vessels due to prolonged exposure to cold","A bacterial skin infection","An autoimmune inflammatory skin disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frostbite is damage to the tissues and blood vessels due to prolonged exposure to cold.  Why A wrong: That describes electrical burn, not frostbite.  Why C wrong: Frostbite is not infectious.  Why D wrong: That describes psoriasis, not frostbite."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"At approximately what temperature does frostbite tissue damage usually occur?","options":["0°C (32°F)","-4°C (25°F)","10°C (50°F)","-20°C (-4°F)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states frostbite usually occurs at a temperature of -4°C (25°F).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated value of -4°C (25°F)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which phase of frostbite involves the cooling of tissues WITHOUT ice crystal formation?","options":["Pre-freeze phase","Freeze-thaw phase","Vascular stasis phase","Late ischemic phase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-freeze phase involves cooling of tissues without ice-crystal formation.  Why B wrong: The freeze-thaw phase is when ice crystals form, resulting in cellular damage and death.  Why C wrong: Vascular stasis phase is marked by blood coagulation or leaking of blood out of the vessels.  Why D wrong: Late ischemic phase is characterized by inflammatory events, ischemia, and tissue death."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which frostbite phase is characterized by ice crystals forming, resulting in cellular damage and death?","options":["Pre-freeze phase","Freeze-thaw phase","Vascular stasis phase","Late ischemic phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The freeze-thaw phase is when ice crystals form, causing cellular damage and death.  Why A wrong: Pre-freeze involves cooling without ice-crystal formation.  Why C wrong: Vascular stasis involves blood coagulation/leaking from vessels.  Why D wrong: Late ischemic phase involves inflammatory events, ischemia, and tissue death."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The frostbite phase marked by blood coagulation or leaking of blood out of the vessels is the:","options":["Pre-freeze phase","Freeze-thaw phase","Vascular stasis phase","Late ischemic phase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vascular stasis phase is marked by blood coagulation or leaking of blood out of the vessels.  Why A/B/D wrong: These phases describe cooling without crystals, ice-crystal cellular death, and late inflammatory ischemia respectively."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The late ischemic phase of frostbite is characterized by:","options":["Cooling without ice-crystal formation","Inflammatory events, ischemia, and tissue death","Only superficial erythema","Spontaneous complete recovery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The late ischemic phase is characterized by inflammatory events, ischemia, and tissue death.  Why A wrong: That describes the pre-freeze phase.  Why C/D wrong: Tissue death and inflammation occur, not superficial-only changes or full recovery."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A patient presents with superficial skin damage, numbness, erythema, and a white plaque surrounded by a ring of hyperaemia and oedema with pain. Which degree of frostbite is this?","options":["First degree","Second degree","Third degree","Fourth degree"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First-degree frostbite features superficial skin damage, numbness and erythema, and a white plaque surrounded by a ring of hyperaemia and oedema with pain.  Why B wrong: Second degree presents with large, clear fluid-filled blisters.  Why C wrong: Third degree involves haemorrhagic blisters/vesicles or blood-filled blisters.  Why D wrong: Fourth degree shows no blisters and no oedema, with full-thickness necrosis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A second-degree frostbite injury is characterized by:","options":["No blisters and no oedema","Large, clear fluid-filled blisters that are very painful, sensitive to cold and numbness","Full-thickness necrosis extending into tendon and muscle","Only superficial erythema with white plaque"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Second-degree frostbite presents with large, clear fluid-filled blisters that are very painful and sensitive to cold, with numbness.  Why A wrong: That describes fourth degree.  Why C wrong: That describes fourth degree necrosis.  Why D wrong: That describes first degree."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which frostbite degree is identified by haemorrhagic blisters/vesicles or blood-filled blisters and blue-grey discolouration of the skin?","options":["First degree","Second degree","Third degree","Fourth degree"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Third-degree frostbite develops haemorrhagic blisters/vesicles or blood-filled blisters with blue-grey discolouration of the skin.  Why A wrong: First degree has only erythema/white plaque, no blood-filled blisters.  Why B wrong: Second degree has clear (not blood-filled) blisters.  Why D wrong: Fourth degree has no blisters at all and full-thickness necrosis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Fourth-degree frostbite is characterized by which set of findings? (Select All That Apply)","options":["No blisters and no oedema","No pain sensation","Full-thickness necrosis with visible tissue loss extending into tendon, muscles, and bone","Skin may become black coloured, with risk of dry gangrene","Possible need for amputation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Fourth-degree frostbite shows no blisters and no oedema, no pain sensation, full-thickness necrosis with visible tissue loss into tendon/muscle/bone, the skin becomes black coloured with risk of (dry) gangrene, and amputation may be required.  Why each is correct (all selected): All of these are listed clinical features of the most severe (fourth-degree) frostbite."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"In rewarming a frostbitten part, the nurse should immerse the affected part in a warm-water bath at which temperature?","options":["50–60°C","104°–107.6°F (40°–42°C) for 15 minutes to 2 hours","25°C (77°F)","Ice-cold water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rewarming uses a warm-water bath with temperature of 104°–107.6°F (40°–42°C) for 15 minutes to 2 hours, continuously and immediately.  Why A wrong: Temperatures that high would cause burns.  Why C wrong: 25°C is too cool to effectively rewarm.  Why D wrong: Ice-cold water would worsen the cold injury."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"When caring for a frostbite injury, the nurse should AVOID which action to prevent further tissue damage?","options":["Handling the affected area gently","Using dry heat and never rubbing or massaging the part","Applying a sterile dressing","Watching for compartment syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should avoid using dry heat and should never rub or massage the part, as this leads to further tissue damage.  Why A wrong: Gentle handling of the area is correct.  Why C wrong: Applying a sterile dressing is appropriate care.  Why D wrong: Watching for compartment syndrome is a correct nursing action."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A nurse caring for a frostbite patient should anticipate which procedures based on tissue viability? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Give TT (tetanus toxoid) and antibiotics as per order","Debridement (mechanical removal of dead tissue) if necrotic tissue develops","Amputation may be required if gangrene develops in 4th degree","Apply sterile dressing","Massage the part vigorously to restore circulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists giving TT and antibiotics per order, debridement of necrotic tissue, possible amputation in fourth-degree gangrene, and sterile dressing as appropriate care.  Why E wrong: Massaging/rubbing the part is explicitly to be avoided, as it causes further tissue damage."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Psoriasis is best described as:","options":["An acute bacterial infection of the skin","A chronic, non-infectious skin inflammation that involves synthesis of keratin","A venomous bite reaction","A cold-induced tissue injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psoriasis is a chronic, non-infectious skin inflammation that involves synthesis of keratin.  Why A wrong: It is non-infectious, not bacterial.  Why C/D wrong: These describe snake bite and frostbite, not psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Psoriatic patches occur due to synthesis of keratin and can lead to:","options":["Hyperpigmentation only","Coinfection","Excessive sweating","Vesicle formation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to keratin synthesis, psoriatic patches occur which can lead to coinfection.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically states coinfection as the consequence, not the other options."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which is the most common form of psoriasis?","options":["Guttate psoriasis","Psoriasis vulgaris","Pustular psoriasis","Inverse psoriasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psoriasis vulgaris is the most common form of psoriasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other variants, but the source names psoriasis vulgaris as most common."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Acne vulgaris is described as:","options":["A chronic skin disorder which, in lesions, develops on the face, neck, chest, and shoulder","An autoimmune blistering disease","A cold injury of the extremities","A venom-induced necrosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acne vulgaris is a chronic skin disorder which in lesions develops on the face, neck, chest, and shoulder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source description of acne vulgaris."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome is characterized as:","options":["A drug-induced reaction due to immunological reaction, characterized by erythema, skin rashes, and large blisters, most commonly occurring in client with cancer receiving chemotherapy or immunotherapy","A chronic non-infectious psoriatic patch disorder","A localized acne lesion of the face","A cold-induced vascular stasis injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stevens-Johnson Syndrome is a drug-induced reaction due to an immunological reaction, characterized by erythema, skin rashes, and large blisters, most commonly occurring in clients with cancer receiving chemotherapy or immunotherapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe psoriasis, acne, and frostbite respectively."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which clinical features are associated with psoriasis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pruritis and silvery white scales/patches are vital features of psoriasis","Common site of patches: scalp, knees, elbows, arms, and sacral regions","Yellow discolouration and joint inflammation with psoriatic arthritis","Blue-grey skin discolouration with blood-filled blisters","Black necrotic gangrenous toes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Psoriasis features include pruritis and silvery-white scales/patches, common sites of scalp/knees/elbows/arms/sacral regions, and yellow discolouration with joint inflammation in psoriatic arthritis.  Why D wrong: Blue-grey skin with blood-filled blisters describes third-degree frostbite.  Why E wrong: Black necrotic gangrenous toes describe fourth-degree frostbite, not psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The nursing care for psoriatic skin lesions includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Instruct the client not to scratch the lesions","Give prescribed medications","Initiate general nursing care","Encourage vigorous scrubbing of the patches","Apply ice packs to the lesions for several hours"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Psoriasis nursing care includes instructing the client not to scratch the lesions, giving prescribed medications, and initiating general nursing care.  Why D wrong: Vigorous scrubbing would worsen the lesions; not instructed.  Why E wrong: Ice-pack application is not part of psoriasis lesion care."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A \"primary lesion\" of the skin is best described as one that:","options":["Occurs at birth or as acquired due to disease process (e.g., macule, papule, nodule, tumor, plaque, wheal, pustule, vesicles, bulla)","Results from irritation or manipulation by primary skin lesions","Is always infectious","Only occurs in psoriasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary lesions occur at birth or are acquired due to disease process; examples include macule, papule, nodule, tumor, plaque, wheal, pustule, vesicles, and bulla.  Why B wrong: That describes secondary lesions.  Why C/D wrong: Primary lesions are not necessarily infectious nor limited to psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Secondary skin lesions are those that:","options":["Are present at birth from a disease process","Result from irritation or manipulation by primary skin lesions (e.g., scales, crust, fissure, ulcer, scar, atrophy)","Always require amputation","Are exclusively caused by snake venom"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary lesions result from irritation or manipulation of primary skin lesions; examples include scales, crust, fissure, ulcer, scar, atrophy; abrasion/maceration (due to prolongation of moist) and atrophy/ulcer (pressure and diabetes) also come under secondary skin lesions.  Why A wrong: That describes primary lesions.  Why C/D wrong: Not characteristic of secondary lesions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Match the skin lesion term: A lesion containing purulent material or pus is called a:","options":["Vesicle","Pustule","Wheal","Macule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pustule contains purulent material or pus, e.g., in acne and impetigo.  Why A wrong: A vesicle is a superficial, serous fluid-filled lesion (e.g., chicken pox, 2nd-degree burn, herpes zoster/shingles).  Why C wrong: A wheal is seen in insect bite and urticaria/allergic reaction.  Why D wrong: A macule is a flat, colour-change lesion (not pus-filled)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A wheal is most characteristically seen in:","options":["Athlete's foot","Insect bite and urticaria/allergic reaction","Chicken pox","Pressure ulcer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wheal is seen in insect bite and urticaria/allergic reaction.  Why A wrong: Athlete's foot is associated with fissure (crack in skin).  Why C wrong: Chicken pox produces vesicles.  Why D wrong: Pressure ulcers produce ulcers/excoriation, not wheals."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A vesicle is best described as:","options":["A purulent pus-filled lesion","A superficial, serous fluid-filled lesion seen in chicken pox, 2nd-degree burn, herpes zoster or shingles","An abnormal formation of connective tissue replacing normal skin","A raised area from irritation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A vesicle is superficial and serous fluid-filled, seen in chicken pox, 2nd-degree burn, and herpes zoster/shingles.  Why A wrong: That describes a pustule.  Why C wrong: That describes a scar.  Why D wrong: That loosely describes a wheal, not a vesicle."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A \"fissure\" of the skin is defined as:","options":["A scar formed beyond wound boundaries","A crack or break seen in athlete's foot","Loss of epidermis near a wound from scratch","Purulent pus collection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fissure is a crack or break seen in athlete's foot.  Why A wrong: That describes a keloid (scar beyond wound boundaries).  Why C wrong: That describes excoriation.  Why D wrong: That describes a pustule."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A scar that consists of irregularly shaped fibrous tissue formed beyond the boundaries of the original wound is called a:","options":["Macule","Keloid","Excoriation","Vesicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A keloid is irregularly shaped fibrous tissue formed beyond the boundaries of a scar or wound.  Why A wrong: A macule is a flat colour-change lesion.  Why C wrong: Excoriation is loss of epidermis near/on a wound from scratch.  Why D wrong: A vesicle is a serous fluid-filled lesion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"\"Excoriation\" of the skin refers to:","options":["Abnormal connective tissue replacing normal skin (scar)","Loss of epidermis with dermis visible on or near the wound, in scales/abrasion or scratch","A serous fluid-filled blister","A flat coloured macule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excoriation is epidermis lost, with dermis visible on or near the wound, occurring in scales, abrasion, or scratch.  Why A wrong: That describes a scar.  Why C wrong: That describes a vesicle.  Why D wrong: That describes a macule."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A pressure sore is also known as a:","options":["Stress fracture","Decubitus ulcer or bed sore","Keloid","Fissure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pressure sore is also known as a decubitus ulcer or bed sore.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated conditions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A pressure ulcer results from impairment or breakdown of skin integrity due to:","options":["Persistent pressure on a bony prominence for a prolonged period","Acute bacterial infection","Cold exposure crystallizing tissue","An autoimmune keratin reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A pressure ulcer means impairment or breakdown of skin integrity due to persistent pressure on a bony prominence for a prolonged period.  Why B wrong: Infection is a complication, not the basic cause.  Why C wrong: That describes frostbite.  Why D wrong: That describes psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Tissue compression in pressure sores restricts blood flow to the skin, which can result in:","options":["Increased keratin synthesis","Tissue ischemia, inflammation, and necrosis","Improved oxygenation","Reduced bony prominence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tissue compression restricts blood flow to the skin, resulting in tissue ischemia, inflammation, and necrosis.  Why A wrong: This is not a keratin/synthesis process.  Why C wrong: Blood flow is restricted, decreasing oxygenation.  Why D wrong: Pressure occurs over bony prominences; they are not reduced."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"What is the MOST common site for pressure sore development when a patient lies in the supine position?","options":["The heels only","The sacrum","The nose","The fingertips"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common pressure point in the supine position is the sacrum.  Why A wrong: The heels are a pressure point but not the most common in supine.  Why C/D wrong: These are not described pressure points."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Besides the sacrum, the other common pressure sites include which areas? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Heel","Tuberosity, back of the head, ears","Shoulders","Elbows and inner knees","The tongue"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other common pressure points include heel, trochanter, ischial tuberosity, back of the head, ears, shoulders, elbows, and inner knees.  Why E wrong: The tongue is not a listed pressure point."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are major risk factors for pressure sore development? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Immobility resulting in prolonged pressure or the client lying in one position for a long time","Skin pressure where capillary pressure exceeds >32–35 mm Hg","Shearing force and friction","Malnutrition","Incontinence (urine and feces) that irritates the skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists immobility (prolonged pressure/one position), skin pressure where capillary pressure exceeds >32–35 mm Hg, shearing force and friction, malnutrition, and incontinence (urine/feces irritating the skin) as risk factors.  Why each is correct (all selected): Each is explicitly named as a contributing risk factor for pressure sore development."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Shearing force in pressure sores refers to a parallel/dragging force on the skin when:","options":["The skin slips from top to bottom and friction occurs","Cold crystallizes the tissue","The bone fractures","Bacteria invade the wound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shearing force and friction is a parallel force on the skin when the skin slips from top to bottom and friction occurs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe shearing force."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which additional factors are listed as contributing to pressure sore risk? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Aging (decreased epidermal thickness, elasticity, and secretion of sebaceous gland)","Decreased sensory perception and diabetes neuropathy","Moisture that increases skin maceration","Pyrexia and inadequate tissue oxygenation","Dryness and cognitive impairment"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists aging (decreased epidermal thickness/elasticity/sebaceous secretion), decreased sensory perception and diabetes neuropathy, moisture (urine/perspiration causing skin maceration), pyrexia and inadequate tissue oxygenation, and dryness/cognitive impairment as contributing factors.  Why each is correct (all selected): Each is explicitly listed as a pressure-sore contributing factor."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A patient has a pressure area with non-blanchable redness and erythema, intact skin usually over a bony prominence, and it is usually painful. What stage of pressure sore is this?","options":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 1 pressure sore shows non-blanchable redness and erythema, skin intact, usually over a bony prominence, and is usually painful.  Why B wrong: Stage 2 has partial-thickness ulceration with blister/open ulcer (skin not intact).  Why C wrong: Stage 3 has full-thickness ulceration into subcutaneous tissue.  Why D wrong: Stage 4 has deepest tissue damage involving bone/tendon/muscle."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A stage-2 pressure sore is characterized by which findings? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Partial-thickness ulceration of epidermis or dermis","Presents as abrasion, blister, or shallow open ulcer with a red/pink wound bed without tissue sloughing","Skin is not intact","Open or ruptured serum-filled blister, very painful","Exposed bone and muscle with absent pain sensation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 2 features partial-thickness ulceration of epidermis or dermis; presents as abrasion, blister, or shallow open ulcer with a red/pink wound bed without tissue sloughing; skin is not intact; open or ruptured serum-filled blister; and is very painful.  Why E wrong: Exposed bone/muscle with absent pain is a feature of deeper stages (3/4), not stage 2."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"In a stage-3 pressure sore, which statement about bone, tendon, and muscle exposure and pain is correct?","options":["Bone, tendon, and muscles are exposed and pain is severe","In this stage, bone, tendon, and muscles are NOT exposed, and nerve endings are damaged so pain is absent","Only the epidermis is involved","The wound is covered by eschar and cannot be staged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 3 is full-thickness ulceration involving epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous tissues; slough or eschar may be present; undermining/tunnelling may or may not be present; in this stage bone, tendon, and muscles are NOT exposed; nerve endings are damaged so pain is absent; may require a graft.  Why A wrong: Bone/tendon/muscle exposure occurs in stage 4, not stage 3.  Why C wrong: Stage 3 is full-thickness, not just epidermis.  Why D wrong: That describes an unstageable pressure ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A stage-4 pressure sore differs from a stage-3 in that it:","options":["Involves only partial-thickness skin loss","Once a pressure sore develops, it can be difficult to heal; it is most serious with extensive tissue damage involving exposed bone, tendon, ligament, cartilage, or muscle; slough/eschar may be present; undermining/tunnelling may develop; nerve endings damaged so pain is absent; requires a graft","Has intact skin with non-blanchable redness","Is always covered by eschar so cannot be staged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 4 is the most serious, with extensive tissue damage involving/exposing bone, tendon, ligament, cartilage, or muscle; slough or eschar may be present; undermining and tunnelling may develop; nerve endings damaged so pain is absent; requires a graft.  Why A wrong: That is stage 2 (partial thickness).  Why C wrong: That is stage 1.  Why D wrong: That describes an unstageable ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"An \"unstageable pressure ulcer\" is defined as one in which:","options":["The wound is shallow with a red/pink bed","It is a full-thickness type wound covered by eschar and slough, and the true depth or stage cannot be determined until the slough or eschar is removed to visualize the wound bed","The skin is intact with erythema","Only the epidermis is involved"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An unstageable pressure ulcer is a full-thickness wound covered by eschar and slough; the true depth/stage cannot be determined until the slough or eschar is removed to visualize the wound bed.  Why A wrong: A red/pink shallow bed describes stage 2.  Why C wrong: Intact erythematous skin describes stage 1.  Why D wrong: It is full-thickness, not just epidermis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"When assessing wound colour, a \"black wound\" indicates:","options":["Healthy granulation tissue","A necrotic wound","An infectious exudate","Normal healing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A black wound is a necrotic wound.  Why A wrong: Healthy granulation is indicated by a red wound (pink to red granulation).  Why C wrong: Infectious exudate is associated with a yellow wound.  Why D wrong: Black indicates necrosis, not normal healing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A yellow wound with exudate and yellow fibrous debris indicates:","options":["Healthy granulation","An infectious wound","A necrotic wound","A healing wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A yellow wound shows exudate and yellow fibrous debris and indicates an infectious wound.  Why A/D wrong: Healthy/healing is a red wound (pink to red granulation).  Why C wrong: A necrotic wound is black."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A red wound (pink to red granulation) indicates:","options":["A necrotic wound","An infectious wound","A healthy/healing wound","An unstageable wound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A red wound shows pink-to-red granulation and indicates a healthy/healing wound.  Why A wrong: Black indicates necrosis.  Why B wrong: Yellow indicates infection.  Why D wrong: Colour does not define unstageable status; eschar/slough coverage does."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Regarding pain as a clinical feature across pressure sore stages, which statement is correct?","options":["Pain is usually present in stages 1 and 2 and absent in stages 3 and 4","Pain is present at all stages equally","Pain is absent in stages 1 and 2 and present in stages 3 and 4","Pain is never present in pressure sores"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pain is usually present in stages 1 and 2 and absent in stages 3 and 4 (because nerve endings are damaged in deeper stages).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source statement that pain is present early (stages 1–2) and absent in deep stages (3–4)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Regarding loss of sensation across pressure sore stages, which is correct?","options":["Loss of sensation: pain is absent — it usually occurs in stages 3 and 4","Loss of sensation occurs only in stage 1","Sensation is never lost in pressure sores","Loss of sensation occurs only with infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of sensation (pain absent) usually occurs in stages 3 and 4 due to nerve-ending damage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the staged loss of sensation occurring in deep stages."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which clinical features of a pressure sore may indicate a systemic infection? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Erythema and tissue damage according to stages","Exudate","Pyrexia and leucocytosis (with systemic infection present)","Non-blanchable redness only","Silvery white scales"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include erythema and tissue damage according to stages, exudate, and pyrexia and leucocytosis if systemic infection is present.  Why D wrong: Non-blanchable redness alone is a stage-1 local finding, not systemic infection.  Why E wrong: Silvery white scales are a feature of psoriasis, not pressure sore infection."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The Braden Scale is best described as:","options":["A screening tool widely used to determine patients who are at risk for bed sore","A tool to measure cardiac output","A burn percentage estimator","A frostbite classification system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Braden Scale is a widely used screening tool to determine patients who are at risk for bed sore.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is unrelated to cardiac output, burns, or frostbite."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which six assessment parameters does the Braden Scale evaluate? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Sensory perception","Moisture and activity","Mobility","Nutrition","Friction/shear"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Braden Scale uses six parameters: sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, and friction/shear.  Why each is correct (all selected): All five options together name the six listed Braden parameters (moisture and activity are both included in option B)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"On the Braden Scale, what is the highest possible score, and what score indicates high risk for development of a pressure sore?","options":["Highest score is 23; a score of less than 12 indicates high risk","Highest score is 64; a score above 20 indicates high risk","Highest score is 20; a score of 16 or below indicates high risk","Highest score is 5; a score below 3 indicates high risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The highest score on the Braden Scale is 23, and a score of less than 12 indicates high risk for development of pressure sore.  Why B wrong: The 64-point maximum belongs to the Waterlow scale.  Why C wrong: A maximum of 20 / risk at ≤16 belongs to the Norton scale.  Why D wrong: This does not match any of the described scales."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"On the Braden Scale interpretation table, a total score in which range is interpreted as high risk for pressure sore?","options":["15–16","12–14","Below 12 (<12)","Above 18"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the Braden Scale interpretation table, mild risk = 15–16, moderate risk = 12–14, and high risk for pressure sore = <12.  Why A wrong: 15–16 is mild risk.  Why B wrong: 12–14 is moderate risk.  Why D wrong: A higher score indicates lower (or no) risk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"The Norton scale is used to assess patients at risk of developing pressure ulcers. Which five parameters does it assess? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Physical condition","Mental condition","Activity","Mobility","Continence"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Norton scale assesses five parameters: physical condition, mental condition, activity, mobility, and continence.  Why each is correct (all selected): All five are explicitly listed as Norton scale parameters."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"On the Norton scale, each category has four grades (1–4). What do the numbers 4 and 1 represent?","options":["Number 4 means the patient is healthy and number 1 means the patient is at great risk","Number 4 means at great risk and number 1 means healthy","Both numbers mean equal risk","The numbers represent burn percentage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the Norton scale each category has four grades; number 4 means the patient is healthy and number 1 means the patient is at great risk.  Why B wrong: It is the reverse — 4 is healthy, 1 is great risk.  Why C/D wrong: The numbers are not equal and are unrelated to burns."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"On the Norton scale, what are the highest and lowest possible scores, and what score indicates onset of risk and high risk?","options":["Highest 20, lowest 5; onset of risk = 16 or below, high risk = 12 or below","Highest 23, lowest 6; high risk = <12","Highest 64, lowest 1; high risk ≥20","Highest 14, lowest 4; high risk = 7"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the Norton scale the highest possible score is 20 and lowest is 5; onset of risk = 16 or below, and high risk = 12 or below.  Why B wrong: That describes the Braden Scale (23 / <12).  Why C wrong: That describes the Waterlow scale (max 64).  Why D wrong: These values do not match any scale described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Compared with the Braden and Norton scales, the Waterlow scale:","options":["Is the most commonly used and ranges 1–23","Is less commonly used for assessment of pressure sore, with a lowest score of 1 and highest score of 64","Has a maximum score of 20","Assesses only sensory perception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Waterlow scale is less commonly used for assessment of pressure sore; its lowest score is 1 and highest score is 64.  Why A wrong: It is described as less commonly used, not most common.  Why C wrong: A maximum of 20 is the Norton scale.  Why D wrong: It does not assess only sensory perception."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"On the Waterlow scale, which score ranges correspond to \"at risk,\" \"high risk,\" and \"very high risk\"?","options":["At risk: 10–14 points; high risk: 15–19 points; very high risk: ≥20 points","At risk: <12; high risk: 12–14; very high risk: 15–16","At risk: 16; high risk: 12; very high risk: 5","At risk: 23; high risk: 20; very high risk: 64"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Waterlow scale interpretation: at risk (10 to 14 points), high risk (15 to 19 points), and very high risk (≥20 points).  Why B wrong: Those ranges belong to the Braden Scale.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the Waterlow interpretation values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"What is the MOST effective nursing intervention to prevent bed sores in immobile or bedridden clients?","options":["Massage reddened bony prominences hourly","Change the client's position every 2 hours, especially for clients who are immobile or bedridden","Keep the head of the bed elevated at all times","Restrict fluid intake to keep skin dry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Changing the client's position every 2 hours — especially for immobile/bedridden clients — is the most effective nursing intervention to prevent bed sores.  Why A wrong: Massaging bony prominences/reddened areas should be AVOIDED, as it can cause tissue breakdown and necrosis.  Why C wrong: HOB should be kept at the lowest degree (if permitted), not always elevated.  Why D wrong: Adequate fluid intake and balanced diet are required, not restriction."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why should the nurse AVOID massaging a bony prominence or reddened area in a patient at risk for pressure sores?","options":["Because massage can cause tissue breakdown and necrosis","Because it increases pain only","Because it improves circulation too much","Because it is time-consuming"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Massaging a bony prominence and reddened area should be avoided because massage can cause tissue breakdown and necrosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: The specific stated reason is tissue breakdown and necrosis, not these."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which nursing interventions are appropriate in pressure sore prevention and care? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Identify cause and stage of pressure ulcer, then treat accordingly, and reduce risk factors","Do regular skin assessment and watch for skin integrity; keep skin clean and dry; keep the bedsheet wrinkle-free","Assess for purulent drainage or exudates which indicate infection","Provide adequate fluid intake, balanced diet, and high-protein diet; offer high vitamin C and zinc to promote wound healing","Avoid shearing force by lifting, not dragging, the client while moving or doing care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed actions are part of pressure sore nursing care: identify cause/stage and reduce risk factors; regular skin assessment, keep skin clean/dry and bedsheet wrinkle-free; assess for purulent drainage/exudate; provide adequate fluid, balanced and high-protein diet, high vitamin C and zinc; and avoid shearing force by lifting (not dragging) the client.  Why each is correct (all selected): Each is explicitly listed as a nursing care measure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"When changing a bedridden client's position, the head of the bed should be kept at what level (if permitted), and what special beds should be used?","options":["Highest level; standard hospital bed only","Lowest degree (if permitted); water bed and air bed for all bedridden clients to reduce pressure","Reverse Trendelenburg; ice mattress","Fully flat with no support surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head of the bed should be kept at the lowest degree if permitted, and a water bed and air bed should be used for all bedridden clients to reduce pressure.  Why A wrong: Keeping the head highest and using a standard bed does not reduce pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended pressure-reducing measures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"For which pressure sore stages may skin grafting be required?","options":["Stages 1 and 2","Stages 3 and 4","Only stage 1","Only unstageable ulcers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Skin grafting may be required for stages 3 and 4 pressure sores.  Why A/C wrong: Early stages do not require grafting.  Why D wrong: Grafting is described for stages 3 and 4, not specifically the unstageable category."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why is debridement of necrotic tissue performed in pressure sore management?","options":["Because necrotic tissue affects natural healing and promotes bacterial growth, so it should be removed mechanically, by irrigation, or chemically (chemical debridement)","To increase the depth of the wound","To stop antibiotic administration","To preserve the eschar permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Debridement of necrotic tissue is done because necrotic tissue affects natural healing and promotes bacterial growth, so it should be removed mechanically, by irrigation, or chemically (chemical debridement).  Why B wrong: The goal is healing, not increasing wound depth.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics are still administered as ordered.  Why D wrong: Eschar/necrotic tissue is removed, not preserved."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which wound dressing maintains wound humidity, which promotes epithelial cell growth?","options":["Moist gauge dressing","Polyurethane film","Hydrocolloid dressing","Absorptive dressing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A moist gauge dressing maintains wound humidity, which promotes epithelial cell growth.  Why B wrong: Polyurethane film provides a barrier to bacteria and external fluids and promotes moisture (its primary description is barrier/moisture).  Why C wrong: Hydrocolloid maintains humidity, liquifies necrotic debris, and provides a protective cushion.  Why D wrong: Absorptive dressing absorbs drainage."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A polyurethane film dressing primarily functions to:","options":["Absorb large amounts of drainage","Provide a barrier to bacteria and external fluids and promote moisture","Liquify necrotic debris","Promote epithelial cell growth by humidity only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A polyurethane film provides a barrier to bacteria and external fluids and promotes moisture.  Why A wrong: Absorbing drainage is the function of an absorptive dressing.  Why C wrong: Liquifying necrotic debris is the function of a hydrocolloid dressing.  Why D wrong: Maintaining humidity for epithelial growth is the moist gauge dressing's role."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A hydrocolloid dressing is used to:","options":["Maintain wound humidity, liquify necrotic debris, and provide a protective cushion","Only absorb heavy drainage","Only act as a barrier to fluids","Cool the wound bed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A hydrocolloid dressing maintains wound humidity, liquifies necrotic debris, and provides a protective cushion.  Why B wrong: That is the absorptive dressing.  Why C wrong: That is the polyurethane film.  Why D wrong: Cooling is not its function."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"An absorptive dressing functions primarily to:","options":["Maintain humidity to promote epithelial growth","Provide a bacterial barrier","Act to absorb drainage","Liquify necrotic tissue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An absorptive dressing acts to absorb drainage.  Why A wrong: That is the moist gauge dressing.  Why B wrong: That is the polyurethane film.  Why D wrong: That is the hydrocolloid dressing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Negative Pressure Wound Therapy (NPWT) is also known as VAC and works by:","options":["Delivering high-pressure oxygen in a chamber","Applying negative pressure (Vacuum Assisted Wound Closer) to the wound to remove infectious material or exudate and facilitate angiogenesis, granulation, or tissue/wound healing","Freezing the wound bed","Applying a tight tourniquet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NPWT (Vacuum Assisted Wound Closure/VAC) applies negative pressure to the wound to remove infectious material or exudate and facilitate angiogenesis, granulation, and tissue/wound healing.  Why A wrong: That describes Hyperbaric Oxygen Therapy.  Why C/D wrong: NPWT does not freeze the wound or apply a tourniquet."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Hyperbaric Oxygen Therapy (HBOT) involves:","options":["Applying negative pressure to a wound","Administration of 100% oxygen under high pressure in a specific chamber to promote or raise tissue oxygen concentration for wound healing, usually used for chronic wounds such as diabetes wounds","Freezing tissue to slow venom","Mechanical debridement only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HBOT is the administration of 100% oxygen under high pressure in a specific chamber to promote/raise tissue oxygen concentration for wound healing, usually used for chronic wounds such as diabetes wounds.  Why A wrong: Negative pressure describes NPWT.  Why C/D wrong: HBOT is not freezing or debridement."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Per the note in the text, the VAC Veraflo Therapy system uses negative pressure wound therapy AND:","options":["Hyperbaric oxygen delivery","Wound instillation with topical solutions to promote wound healing","Cryotherapy","Electrical stimulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the VAC Veraflo Therapy system uses negative pressure wound therapy and wound instillation with topical solutions to promote wound healing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the described Veraflo system."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A burn is best defined as:","options":["Cell destruction of the layers of the skin caused by heat, friction, electricity, radiation, or chemicals, resulting in depletion of fluids and electrolytes","Inflammation due to keratin synthesis","Damage to tissues due to prolonged cold exposure","Venom-induced systemic toxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A burn is cell destruction of the layers of the skin caused by heat, friction, electricity, radiation, or chemicals, resulting in depletion of fluids and electrolytes.  Why B wrong: That describes psoriasis.  Why C wrong: That describes frostbite.  Why D wrong: That describes snake-bite envenomation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Burns of the head, neck, and chest are especially associated with which complications?","options":["Pulmonary complications and respiratory compromises","Renal failure only","Skin keratin synthesis","Cold-induced gangrene"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Burns of the head, neck, and chest are associated with pulmonary complications and respiratory compromises.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated associations for head/neck/chest burns."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"In burn injury, why is the airway the priority concern, and within what time frame can mucosal oedema lead to upper airway obstruction?","options":["Because inhalation injury with mucosal oedema can lead to upper airway obstruction, especially during the first 24–48 hours","Because the airway is never affected in burns","Because mucosal oedema occurs only after one week","Because chemical burns never involve the airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The airway is the priority concern in an inhalation injury because mucosal oedema can lead to upper airway obstruction, especially during the first 24–48 hours.  Why B wrong: The airway can be critically affected in inhalation burns.  Why C wrong: The danger window is the first 24–48 hours, not after a week.  Why D wrong: Airway involvement is not excluded for chemical burns."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why may head or neck burn clients require consideration as high-risk clients?","options":["Because they are prone to upper airway obstruction, which may require ET (endotracheal) intubation if airway obstruction occurs","Because they always develop hypothermia","Because they cannot receive antibiotics","Because they heal faster than other burns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Head or neck burn clients may be considered high-risk because they are prone to upper airway obstruction, which may require ET intubation if airway obstruction occurs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Why is the perineal area in burns associated with a high incidence of getting infection?","options":["Because it is prone to auto-contamination by urine and feces","Because it has no blood supply","Because keratin synthesis increases there","Because it is always a full-thickness burn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The perineal area is prone to auto-contamination by urine and feces, leading to a high incidence of infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons for perineal burn infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which is described as the most common type of burn?","options":["Electrical burn","Radiation burn","Thermal burn","Chemical burn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Thermal burn is the most common type of burn.  Why A/B/D wrong: Electrical, radiation, and chemical burns are listed as causes but thermal is the most common."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are listed as causes/types of burns? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Thermal burn","Electrical burn","Radiation burn","Chemical burn","Frostbite"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The causes of burns listed are thermal (most common), electrical, radiation, and chemical burns.  Why E wrong: Frostbite is a cold injury, not a type of burn."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Under classification of burn injury, a \"minor burn\" is characterized by:","options":["No involvement of hands, face, or genitalia, with total partial-thickness burn area NOT >15% TBSA","Involvement of face, genitalia, hands, and feet",">25% partial thickness or >10% full thickness","Full-thickness burns of more than 10% TBSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A minor burn has no involvement of hands, face, or genitalia, and the total partial-thickness burn area is not >15% TBSA.  Why B wrong: Involvement of face/genitalia/hands/feet indicates a major burn.  Why C wrong: >25% partial or >10% full thickness indicates a major burn.  Why D wrong: Full-thickness burns not more than 10% TBSA is a moderate burn finding."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A \"moderate burn\" is classified as:","options":["No involvement of hands/face/genitalia and <15% TBSA","Partial-thickness burns 15–25% TBSA but full-thickness burns not more than 10% TBSA",">25% partial thickness or >10% full thickness","Involvement of face and genitalia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A moderate burn has partial-thickness burns of 15–25% TBSA but full-thickness burns of not more than 10% TBSA.  Why A wrong: That describes a minor burn.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe a major burn."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"A \"major burn\" is classified by which features? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Involvement of face, genitalia, hands, and feet",">25% partial thickness",">10% full thickness","No involvement of hands/face/genitalia","Total partial-thickness burn area not >15% TBSA"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A major burn involves the face, genitalia, hands, and feet, with >25% partial thickness or >10% full thickness.  Why D wrong: No involvement of hands/face/genitalia describes a minor burn.  Why E wrong: ≤15% TBSA partial thickness describes a minor burn."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Snake bite first aid & priority nursing care…","question":"Which methods are used for estimating the percentage of burn injury? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Lund-Browder chart","Wallace rule of Nine","Palmer method","Braden scale","Norton scale"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The methods for estimating burn percentage are the Lund-Browder chart, the Wallace rule of Nine, and the Palmer method.  Why D wrong: The Braden scale assesses pressure sore risk, not burn percentage.  Why E wrong: The Norton scale assesses pressure ulcer risk, not burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"According to the Rule of Nine for estimating burn injury in an adult, what percentage of Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) is assigned to the head and neck together?","options":["4.5%","9%","18%","1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the adult Rule of Nine, the head and neck together represent 9% of TBSA.  Why A wrong: 4.5% represents a single upper limb's anterior or posterior aspect.  Why C wrong: 18% applies to the anterior trunk or a full lower limb.  Why D wrong: 1% is assigned to the genitalia (perineum)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Using the adult Rule of Nine, what percentage of TBSA is assigned to the anterior trunk (chest + abdomen)?","options":["9%","4.5%","18%","36%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior trunk (chest + abdomen) is 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%).  Why A wrong: 9% applies to head/neck or a single upper limb.  Why B wrong: 4.5% is half of one upper limb surface.  Why D wrong: 36% would be both anterior and posterior trunk combined, not the anterior trunk alone."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"According to the Rule of Nine, the posterior trunk (upper back + lower back) is estimated at what percentage of TBSA?","options":["9%","18%","4.5%","1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior trunk (upper back 9% + lower back 9%) totals 18% TBSA.  Why A wrong: 9% is a single back surface (upper or lower back alone).  Why C wrong: 4.5% is half of an upper limb.  Why D wrong: 1% is the genitalia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the adult Rule of Nine, an entire upper limb is assigned what percentage of TBSA?","options":["4.5%","9%","18%","1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A full upper limb is 9% (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%).  Why A wrong: 4.5% is only the anterior OR posterior aspect of one upper limb.  Why C wrong: 18% applies to a full lower limb or trunk surface.  Why D wrong: 1% is the genitalia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Using the adult Rule of Nine, an entire lower limb is estimated at what percentage of TBSA?","options":["9%","4.5%","18%","36%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A full lower limb is 18% (anterior 9% + posterior 9%).  Why A wrong: 9% is half of one lower limb (anterior or posterior).  Why B wrong: 4.5% is half an upper limb.  Why D wrong: 36% would be both lower limbs combined."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the Rule of Nine for an adult, the genitalia (perineum) is assigned what percentage of TBSA?","options":["1%","4.5%","9%","0%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The genitalia/perineum is assigned 1% of TBSA, the remaining 1% that completes 100%.  Why B wrong: 4.5% is half of an upper limb.  Why C wrong: 9% is head/neck or one upper limb.  Why D wrong: It is counted as 1%, not zero."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The Palmar (palm) method estimates burn extent based on the patient's palm surface area. Approximately what percentage of TBSA does the patient's palm (including fingers) represent in adults?","options":["0.5% TBSA","1% TBSA","9% TBSA","18% TBSA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The palmar surface including the fingers is approximately 1% TBSA in adults, used for small or scattered burns.  Why A wrong: 0.5% understates the palm-plus-fingers area cited in the text.  Why C wrong: 9% is a Rule-of-Nine segment, not the palm.  Why D wrong: 18% is a Rule-of-Nine trunk/limb segment."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A patient has small, scattered, irregular burns over the body. Which method is most appropriate to estimate the burned surface area?","options":["Rule of Nine","Palmar (palm) method","Parkland formula","Lund and Browder chart for trunk only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The palmar method (palm ≈ 1% TBSA) is used to assess burns in scattered or non-contiguous patterns.  Why A wrong: The Rule of Nine is best for larger, contiguous regions, not small scattered patches.  Why C wrong: Parkland is a fluid-replacement formula, not a TBSA estimation tool.  Why D wrong: The text designates the palmar method for scattered patterns; the chart is not specified for trunk only."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A nurse is assessing a burn victim for possible pulmonary (inhalation) injury. Which of the following findings should raise suspicion of inhalation injury? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hair singed in or around the nostrils, with smoke/black soot around mouth/nose","Client trapped or burned in a closed space","Face, nose, and lips are burned","Initially bloody sample contains carboxyhemoglobin","Clear urine with no soot"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary injury is suspected with singed nasal hair and soot around the mouth/nose, entrapment in a closed space, facial/nasal/lip burns, and an initial blood sample containing carboxyhemoglobin.  Why E wrong: Clear urine without soot is not a listed indicator of inhalation injury."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the pathophysiology of burn injury, vasoactive substances released from injured tissues act as which of the following?","options":["Bradykinin, histamine, and angiotensin","Antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone only","Surfactant and dopamine","Insulin and glucagon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Injured tissues release vasoactive substances such as bradykinin, histamine, and angiotensin.  Why B wrong: ADH/aldosterone are not the released vasoactive mediators in this cascade as described.  Why C wrong: Surfactant and dopamine are not the listed mediators.  Why D wrong: Insulin/glucagon are metabolic hormones, not the vasoactive substances released."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Increased capillary permeability after a burn causes plasma or fluid to leak into surrounding tissues. This phenomenon is specifically termed:","options":["Compartment syndrome","Capillary leak syndrome","Third-degree shock","Reperfusion injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma/fluid leakage into surrounding tissues due to increased capillary permeability is called capillary leak syndrome.  Why A wrong: Compartment syndrome is raised pressure within a fascial compartment, a different process.  Why C wrong: \"Third-degree shock\" is not a recognized term.  Why D wrong: Reperfusion injury occurs on restoration of blood flow, not from capillary leak."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The capillary leak and fluid shift in major burns lead to decreased cardiac output, blood pressure, and heart rate, producing which type of shock if not compensated?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Septic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased cardiac output and hypotension from fluid loss lead to hypovolemic shock (also called \"burn shock\" in major burns) if uncompensated.  Why A wrong: Cardiogenic shock arises from pump failure, not fluid loss.  Why B wrong: Septic shock is infection-driven and occurs later.  Why D wrong: Neurogenic shock is from loss of vascular tone, not burn fluid loss."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Early in the pathophysiology of a major burn, what potassium (K+) shift occurs as a result of cellular destruction?","options":["Hypokalemia from K+ moving into cells","Hyperkalemia as K+ shifts from extracellular fluid (ECF) into intracellular fluid (ICF)","Hyperkalemia as K+ shifts from ICF to ECF","No change in potassium levels"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive cell destruction initially causes hyperkalemia as K+ shifts from intracellular fluid (ICF) into extracellular fluid (ECF).  Why A wrong: Hypokalemia occurs later, not in the initial destructive phase.  Why B wrong: The initial shift is ICF→ECF (raising serum K+), not ECF→ICF.  Why D wrong: Potassium levels do change markedly in burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"After successful fluid replacement therapy in a burn patient, the potassium shifts again. What electrolyte changes occur in this later phase?","options":["Persistent hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia (K+ shifts ECF→ICF) and additional hypervolemia","Hypocalcemia only","Hypernatremia and hypokalemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After fluid replacement, K+ shifts back from ECF into ICF causing hypokalemia, and hypervolemia develops from fluid mobilization.  Why A wrong: Hyperkalemia is the early finding; it resolves and reverses after resuscitation.  Why C wrong: The page specifies a potassium/volume shift, not isolated hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: The described later shift is hypokalemia with hypervolemia, not hypernatremia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In a burn patient, hematocrit increases initially. What is the primary reason for this rise?","options":["Increased red cell production","Loss of plasma (hemoconcentration)","Blood transfusion","Splenic sequestration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hematocrit rises due to loss of plasma, which concentrates the cellular blood components (hemoconcentration).  Why A wrong: RBC production does not increase acutely; it is concentration, not true polycythemia.  Why C wrong: Transfusion is not the cause of the spontaneous early rise.  Why D wrong: Splenic sequestration is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Initially after a burn, blood is shunted away from the kidneys to other organs. What urinary finding results, and what occurs after fluid replacement therapy?","options":["Polyuria initially, then oliguria","Oliguria initially, then diuresis (polyuria) after fluid replacement","Anuria that persists indefinitely","Normal urine output throughout"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Renal shunting initially causes oliguria; after fluid replacement therapy, diuresis (polyuria) occurs.  Why A wrong: The sequence is reversed — oliguria first, then polyuria.  Why C wrong: Anuria does not persist; output recovers with resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Output is not normal; it shifts from oliguria to polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"If a burn patient is not compensated with adequate fluid replacement therapy, what serious outcomes may occur?","options":["Hypovolemic shock and possibly death","Hypervolemia and pulmonary edema only","Spontaneous full recovery","Hyperkalemia that self-corrects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Without adequate fluid replacement, hypovolemic shock occurs and death may follow.  Why B wrong: Hypervolemia is a later, post-resuscitation issue, not the consequence of inadequate replacement.  Why C wrong: Lack of fluid leads to deterioration, not spontaneous recovery.  Why D wrong: Inadequate fluid leads to shock/death, not benign self-correction."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Complications of the respiratory system in burn injury occur in three distinct phases. Which phase involves early complications occurring within 0 to 24 hours?","options":["Phase of early complications — CO poisoning, inhalation injury, airway obstruction, pulmonary edema","Phase of delayed injury — ARDS","Phase of late complications — pneumonia, atelectasis, pulmonary emboli","Phase of recovery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The early-complication phase (0–24 hrs) includes CO poisoning, inhalation injury leading to airway obstruction, and pulmonary edema.  Why B wrong: ARDS is the delayed-injury phase (2–5 days), not 0–24 hours.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia/atelectasis/emboli are late complications (>5 days).  Why D wrong: \"Recovery\" is not one of the three respiratory complication phases."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the phasing of respiratory complications of burn injury, the phase of delayed injury occurs in which time frame and is characterized by which condition?","options":["0–24 hours; carbon monoxide poisoning","2–5 days; Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","More than 5 days; pneumonia","Within 1 hour; airway obstruction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The delayed-injury phase occurs at 2–5 days and is characterized by ARDS.  Why A wrong: 0–24 hrs is the early phase with CO poisoning.  Why C wrong: >5 days is the late-complication phase.  Why D wrong: Within 1 hour is not the delayed phase; airway obstruction is an early finding."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The phase of late respiratory complications in burn injury occurs after how many days, and includes which complications?","options":[">5 days; pneumonia, atelectasis, and pulmonary emboli","2–5 days; ARDS","0–24 hours; pulmonary edema",">5 days; carbon monoxide poisoning only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Late complications occur after 5 days and include pneumonia, atelectasis, and pulmonary emboli.  Why B wrong: ARDS belongs to the delayed phase (2–5 days).  Why C wrong: Pulmonary edema is an early-phase (0–24 hr) finding.  Why D wrong: CO poisoning is an early-phase finding, not the late phase."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A superficial (first-degree) burn involves which layer(s) of skin?","options":["Epidermis only","Epidermis and dermis","Dermis and subcutaneous tissue","Full thickness including muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A superficial first-degree burn involves only the epidermis.  Why B wrong: Epidermis + dermis describes a partial-thickness (second-degree) burn.  Why C wrong: Dermis + subcutaneous tissue describes deeper burns.  Why D wrong: Muscle involvement indicates a deep full-thickness (fourth-degree) burn."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which set of findings characterizes a superficial (first-degree) burn?","options":["Blisters, weeping, blood supply reduced in deep layers, scarring","Pink to red colour, mild-to-moderate erythema, no blisters, painful, sensitive to touch, heals in 5–10 days","White/charred eschar, painless, no edema","Black-brown leathery skin, requires amputation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A first-degree burn is pink-to-red with mild-to-moderate erythema, no blisters, is painful and sensitive to touch, and usually heals in 5–10 days.  Why A wrong: Blisters and weeping describe partial-thickness burns.  Why C wrong: Painless white/charred eschar describes full-thickness burns.  Why D wrong: Black-brown leathery skin requiring amputation describes deep full-thickness burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A superficial partial-thickness (second-degree) burn involves which skin layers?","options":["Epidermis only","Epidermis and dermis (upper layers)","Subcutaneous tissue and muscle","Tendons and bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A superficial partial-thickness (second-degree) burn involves the epidermis and dermis.  Why A wrong: Epidermis alone is first-degree.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous/muscle involvement is full or deep full thickness.  Why D wrong: Tendon/bone involvement is the deepest (deep full-thickness) burn."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which features are characteristic of a superficial partial-thickness (second-degree) burn? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Blisters","Blood supply reduced in both layers of skin (epidermis & dermis)","Mild to moderate oedema","Burn is very painful and sensitive to cold air","Painless with charred eschar"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Superficial partial-thickness burns show blisters, reduced blood supply in both skin layers, mild-to-moderate oedema, and are very painful and sensitive to cold air. Large fluid-filled or ruptured blisters may be present; healing usually occurs in 3 weeks.  Why E wrong: Painless charred eschar describes a full-thickness burn, not a partial-thickness one which is painful."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A deep partial-thickness (second-degree, deep) burn involves the dermis and deeper dermal layers. Which clinical features are typical? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Red to white colour with moderate oedema","Can convert to a full-thickness burn if tissue damage increases by infection, hypoxia, or ischemia","Soft and dry eschar","Healing takes 3–6 weeks","Completely painless and insensate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Deep partial-thickness burns are red to white with moderate oedema, can convert to full-thickness with infection/hypoxia/ischemia, have soft-and-dry eschar, and heal in 3–6 weeks; blisters and pain may or may not be present and the tissue layer is thick.  Why E wrong: Complete painlessness/insensate skin indicates a full-thickness burn, not deep partial-thickness."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A full-thickness (third-degree) burn involves which tissues?","options":["Epidermis only","Epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous tissues","Dermis only","Muscle and bone only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A full-thickness (third-degree) burn involves the epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous tissues.  Why A wrong: Epidermis alone is first-degree.  Why C wrong: Dermis alone is not the full-thickness description.  Why D wrong: Muscle/bone involvement defines a deep full-thickness (fourth-degree) burn."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Why is a full-thickness (third-degree) burn typically painless?","options":["The patient is sedated","Nerve endings in the burned area are destroyed","Cold air numbs the area","Edema compresses the nerves temporarily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Full-thickness burns are painless because the nerve endings in the burn area are destroyed.  Why A wrong: Painlessness is intrinsic to nerve destruction, not dependent on sedation.  Why C wrong: Cold air increases pain in superficial burns; it is not why deep burns are painless.  Why D wrong: It is nerve destruction, not temporary edema compression, that causes painlessness."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which statements correctly describe a full-thickness (third-degree) burn? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dry, hard skin with inelastic eschar","Burn crusts or dead tissues must be removed (slough off) from the burned area before healing can occur","Skin grafting may require","It will take time for healing, usually months","Heals spontaneously within 5–10 days without intervention"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Full-thickness burns have dry, hard inelastic eschar; crust/dead tissue must be removed before healing; skin grafting may be required; and healing takes months. Scarring and wound contracture may develop.  Why E wrong: Spontaneous healing in 5–10 days describes a superficial first-degree burn, not a full-thickness burn that takes months and may need grafting."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A deep full-thickness (fourth-degree) burn involves which structures and what is the typical appearance?","options":["Epidermis only; pink and painful","Involves muscle and tendons; skin appears black/charred, completely insensate, with absence of edema","Epidermis and upper dermis; blisters present","Dermis only; soft moist eschar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A deep full-thickness (fourth-degree) burn involves muscle and tendons; the area appears black/charred and sensation is completely absent, with absence of edema, and complete loss of pain due to nerve-ending damage.  Why A wrong: Epidermis-only pink/painful describes a first-degree burn.  Why C wrong: Blisters with upper dermis involvement is superficial partial thickness.  Why D wrong: Soft moist eschar in dermis-only is not the fourth-degree picture."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In a deep full-thickness (fourth-degree) burn where the injured area appears black and sensation is completely absent, which intervention may be required after debridement?","options":["Topical antibiotic only","Part amputation","Simple dressing change","Cold compress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Healing requires months and part amputation may be done after debridement when the injured area is necrotic, black, and insensate.  Why A wrong: Topical antibiotic alone cannot restore necrotic, charred deep tissue.  Why C wrong: A simple dressing change is inadequate for fourth-degree burns.  Why D wrong: Cold compresses do not manage deep necrotic burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which of the following are general clinical features of major burn injury? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Change in skin look and skin integrity broken","Hematuria (blood in urine) and elevated hematocrit level","Severe pain, usually in first and second degree burns","Restlessness and extreme anxiety","Decreased body temperature with bradycardia as the primary feature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Burn clinical features include altered skin look/broken integrity, hematuria with elevated hematocrit, severe pain (typically first/second degree), and restlessness/extreme anxiety.  Why E wrong: Hypothermia with bradycardia is not the listed primary clinical feature of burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which clinical/lab findings are associated with the systemic effects of a major burn injury? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Initially hyperkalemia due to cellular destruction","Hyponatremia and hyperuricemia","Hypovolemia due to excessive fluid loss","Loss of renal function (elevated BUN and creatinine level)","Permanently normal electrolytes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Major burns initially cause hyperkalemia from cellular destruction, hyponatremia and hyperuricemia, hypovolemia from excessive fluid loss, and loss of renal function with elevated BUN and creatinine.  Why E wrong: Electrolytes are markedly deranged in major burns, not permanently normal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The resuscitative/emergent phase of burn management has what duration?","options":["First 24 hours","First 48 hours","First 72 hours","First 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The resuscitative/emergent phase lasts the first 48 hours (initial or very dangerous phase).  Why A wrong: The text specifies 48 hours, not 24.  Why C wrong: 72 hours overstates the emergent phase.  Why D wrong: 7 days falls into the acute phase, not the emergent phase."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"What is the FIRST priority at the incident site for a burn victim?","options":["Start IV fluids","Remove the victim from the source of burn","Apply sterile dressing","Administer tetanus toxoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first action at the incident site is to remove the victim from the source of the burn to stop ongoing injury.  Why A wrong: IV fluids follow after the victim is safely removed and ABCs assessed.  Why C wrong: Dressings come later in the sequence.  Why D wrong: Tetanus toxoid is given later, not as the first incident-site action."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"At the second incident-site action for a burn victim, the nurse should assess which of the following?","options":["Lab values and electrolytes","ABCs (Airway, Breathing & Circulation)","Body weight and urine output","Wound depth only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second incident-site action is to assess ABCs — airway, breathing, and circulation.  Why A wrong: Labs/electrolytes are monitored in hospital, not as the second incident-site step.  Why C wrong: Weight/urine output are later monitoring parameters.  Why D wrong: Wound depth assessment is not the immediate ABC priority."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Upon arrival at the hospital, what is the first priority in managing a burn patient?","options":["Apply sterile dressing","Ensure a patent airway and give 100% oxygen","Insert a peripheral IV line","Weigh the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first in-hospital priority is to ensure a patent airway and give 100% oxygen.  Why A wrong: Sterile dressing follows airway/oxygenation.  Why C wrong: IV access is important but airway takes priority (ABC sequence).  Why D wrong: Weighing is part of later monitoring, not the first hospital priority."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"When establishing IV access for fluid resuscitation in a burn patient, why should a large-bore IV line be inserted?","options":["To monitor central venous pressure","To establish IV access for rapid/large-volume fluid resuscitation","To administer oral medications","To draw arterial blood gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A large-bore IV line is inserted to allow rapid, large-volume fluid replacement needed for hypovolemia in burns.  Why A wrong: CVP monitoring uses a central line for hemodynamic pressures, a separate measure.  Why C wrong: IV lines are not for oral medications.  Why D wrong: ABGs are sampled from arterial sites, not the resuscitation IV line."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the resuscitative phase, urinary output should be monitored hourly. The nurse should be alert if hourly urine output falls below what value?","options":["30 ml/hr (or <30 ml/hr)","50 ml/hr","100 ml/hr","10 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary output is monitored hourly; output less than 30 ml/hr (or <30 ml/hr) signals inadequate perfusion and is concerning.  Why B wrong: 50 ml/hr is within an adequate range, not the alarm threshold.  Why C wrong: 100 ml/hr is normal/high output.  Why D wrong: The page specifies <30 ml/hr as the concerning threshold, not 10."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Why should IM injections (e.g., tetanus toxoid) be administered if a burn patient was not previously immunized, rather than relying on IM/SC routes for analgesics during the emergent phase?","options":["IM/SC routes are painful","Due to altered absorption and infection risk, IV is preferred for quick and better absorption; impaired tissue perfusion makes IM/SC unreliable","IM injections cause fever","Oral route is faster"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because of altered absorption and infection risk from impaired peripheral perfusion in burns, IV is preferred for quick/better absorption; IM/SC absorption is unreliable. TT is given IM if not previously immunized.  Why A wrong: Pain is not the primary stated reason; absorption/perfusion is.  Why C wrong: IM injections causing fever is not the rationale given.  Why D wrong: The oral route is unreliable in the emergent phase, not faster."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Why is keeping the room temperature warm important in the emergent care of a burn patient?","options":["To increase metabolic rate","Because burn patients lose the ability to regulate body temperature and are prone to heat loss","To promote sweating","To speed up eschar formation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Burned skin loses thermoregulatory and barrier function, so patients lose heat readily; a warm room helps prevent hypothermia.  Why A wrong: The goal is to prevent heat loss, not raise metabolic demand.  Why C wrong: Promoting sweating would worsen fluid/heat loss.  Why D wrong: Room warmth is not aimed at eschar formation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The Parkland (Baxter) formula is used for fluid resuscitation in burns. What is the total fluid calculation per the Parkland formula?","options":["2 ml × body weight (kg) × % TBSA, using Ringer's lactate","4 ml × weight (kg) × % TBSA, using Ringer's lactate","1 ml × weight (kg) × % TBSA, using normal saline","4 ml × weight (kg) × % TBSA, using colloids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Parkland formula = 4 ml × weight (kg) × % TBSA, using Ringer's lactate (a crystalloid solution).  Why A wrong: 2 ml × weight × % TBSA with colloid and crystalloid is the Consensus formula, not Parkland.  Why C wrong: 1 ml × weight × % TBSA describes colloid components of other formulas, not Parkland's crystalloid total.  Why D wrong: Parkland uses Ringer's lactate (crystalloid), not colloids."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"According to the Parkland formula timing, how is the calculated fluid volume administered over the first 24 hours?","options":["All in the first hour","Half (first half) given in the first 8 hours, and the remaining half (second half) over the next 16 hours","Equally over 24 hours","Two-thirds in the first 4 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the Parkland formula, half the fluid is administered in the first 8 hours and the remaining half over the next 16 hours; the 8-hour clock starts from the time of the burn (not admission).  Why A wrong: Giving all in the first hour would cause fluid overload.  Why C wrong: Equal distribution ignores the 8h/16h split.  Why D wrong: Two-thirds in 4 hours is not the Parkland schedule."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"When calculating the timing of fluid administration by the Parkland formula, the time count begins from what point?","options":["Time of hospital admission","Time of the injury/burn, NOT from admission time","When IV access is obtained","When urine output drops"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first 8 hours (for the first half of fluid) is calculated from the time of the burn, not from the admission time.  Why A wrong: Counting from admission delays effective resuscitation and is explicitly contrasted as incorrect.  Why C wrong: IV access time is not the reference point.  Why D wrong: Urine output is a monitoring parameter, not the timing reference."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The Consensus formula for burn fluid resuscitation specifies which calculation?","options":["4 ml × weight × % TBSA","2–4 ml × body weight (kg) × percentage of total body surface area (% TBSA)","1 ml × weight × % TBSA colloid","1500 ml/m² for insensible water loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Consensus formula uses 2–4 ml × body weight (kg) × % TBSA.  Why A wrong: 4 ml × weight × % TBSA is the Parkland formula specifically.  Why C wrong: 1 ml colloid × weight × % TBSA is an Evans/Brooke colloid component.  Why D wrong: 1500 ml/m² (or 2000 ml/m²) is an insensible/maintenance water-loss figure, not the Consensus formula."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The Brooke Army formula for burn fluid resuscitation uses which components?","options":["Colloid 0.5 ml × weight × % TBSA; crystalloid (RL) 1.5 ml × weight × % TBSA; glucose (5% in water) 2000 ml for insensible water loss","Crystalloid 4 ml × weight × % TBSA only","Colloid 1 ml × weight × % TBSA; crystalloid 1 ml × weight × % TBSA; glucose 2000 ml/m²","Ringer's lactate 2 ml × weight × % TBSA only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Brooke Army formula uses colloid 0.5 ml × weight × % TBSA, crystalloid (Ringer's lactate) 1.5 ml × weight × % TBSA, and glucose (5% in water) ~2000 ml for insensible water loss.  Why B wrong: 4 ml crystalloid alone is the Parkland formula.  Why C wrong: Colloid 1 ml + crystalloid 1 ml describes the Evans formula, not Brooke Army.  Why D wrong: RL 2 ml × weight × % TBSA alone is not the Brooke Army composition."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The Evans formula for burn fluid resuscitation uses which components?","options":["Colloid 1 ml × weight × % TBSA; crystalloid (normal saline) 1 ml × weight × % TBSA; glucose (5% water) 2000 ml for insensible water loss","Crystalloid 4 ml × weight × % TBSA only","Colloid 0.5 ml and crystalloid 1.5 ml × weight × % TBSA","Ringer's lactate only, 2 ml × weight × % TBSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Evans formula uses colloid 1 ml × weight × % TBSA, crystalloid (normal saline) 1 ml × weight × % TBSA, and glucose (5% in water) ~2000 ml for insensible water loss.  Why B wrong: 4 ml crystalloid alone is Parkland.  Why C wrong: Colloid 0.5 + crystalloid 1.5 is the Brooke Army formula.  Why D wrong: RL only at 2 ml is not the Evans composition."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Regarding fluid administration to prevent hypovolemic shock in burns, what proportion of fluid should be administered in the first 8 hours versus the remainder?","options":["Half the fluid in the first 8 hours, with the remaining half administered over the next 16 hours","All fluid in the first 8 hours","One-third in the first 8 hours","Quarter in the first 8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Half of the total calculated fluid should be administered in the first 8 hours to prevent hypovolemic shock, with the remaining half over the next 16 hours.  Why B wrong: Giving all fluid in 8 hours risks overload.  Why C wrong: One-third in 8 hours under-resuscitates the critical early period.  Why D wrong: A quarter in 8 hours is inadequate per the 8h/16h split."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In burn fluid resuscitation, a urine output target is used to gauge adequacy. To ensure adequate output and peripheral perfusion (e.g., palpable pulses), the nurse monitors which parameter?","options":["Adequate urine output and palpable peripheral pulses","Pupillary response only","Bowel sounds","Skin turgor on the burned area"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists monitoring of adequate urine output and palpable peripheral pulses as indicators to assess hypovolemia and perfusion.  Why B wrong: Pupillary response is a neurologic, not perfusion/volume, marker here.  Why C wrong: Bowel sounds are not the cited resuscitation adequacy marker.  Why D wrong: Skin turgor on burned skin is unreliable for assessing volume status."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Why is urine output considered the most reliable and most sensitive non-invasive indicator in burn fluid resuscitation?","options":["It directly measures blood pressure","It reflects cardiac output and organ/tissue perfusion","It measures eschar thickness","It indicates oxygen saturation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output is the most reliable, most sensitive non-invasive indicator because it reflects cardiac output and tissue (organ) perfusion.  Why A wrong: It is an indirect, not direct, measure; BP is measured separately.  Why C wrong: It does not measure eschar thickness.  Why D wrong: Oxygen saturation is measured by pulse oximetry, not urine output."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"To prevent contracture deformities in lower extremities during burn fluid resuscitation/positioning, which nursing action is recommended?","options":["Elevate the head of the bed only","Elevate the lower extremities on a pillow so diuresis can be used to advantage and to decrease oedema due to risk of decreasing oedema","Keep limbs flat at all times","Apply tight bandages"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elevating the lower extremities on a pillow helps reduce oedema in the dependent limbs and supports preventing complications.  Why A wrong: Elevating only the head of bed does not address lower-extremity oedema.  Why C wrong: Keeping limbs flat promotes dependent oedema.  Why D wrong: Tight bandages can compromise circulation and worsen swelling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"When inserting a Foley catheter to monitor hemodynamic pressures and assess for hypovolemia, what additional monitoring is recommended in burn care?","options":["CVP (central venous pressure) line for hemodynamic monitoring; assess for hypovolemia and give IV fluids per peripheral pulses","Lumbar puncture","Continuous EEG","Hourly weight only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A CVP line (e.g., CVP, NIBP, PCWP, etc.) is used for hemodynamic monitoring; the nurse assesses for hypovolemia and gives IV fluids guided by peripheral pulses.  Why B wrong: Lumbar puncture is unrelated to burn volume monitoring.  Why C wrong: Continuous EEG is not part of burn resuscitation monitoring.  Why D wrong: Hourly weight alone is insufficient; hemodynamic/CVP monitoring is added."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The acute phase of burn management begins after what time period?","options":["After the first 8 hours","After the first 48 hours (after the resuscitative phase) and continues until burns are healed or covered by skin graft","After 7 days","After 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The acute phase begins after the first 48 hours (rehabilitation begins later) and continues until burns are healed or covered by skin grafts.  Why A wrong: 8 hours is still within the emergent/resuscitative phase.  Why C wrong: The acute phase starts at 48 hours, not 7 days.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is well into the rehabilitative timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"During the acute phase of burn management, the major concern is which electrolyte/fluid imbalance?","options":["Hyperkalemia from cellular destruction","Hypervolemia (fluid shift back from ECF to ICF and from interstitial back to vascular spaces)","Hyponatremia from sweating","Hypocalcemia from grafting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the acute phase the major concern is hypervolemia, as fluid shifts back from ECF to ICF and from interstitial spaces into the vascular compartment.  Why A wrong: Hyperkalemia is the early/emergent-phase concern, not the acute phase.  Why C wrong: Hyponatremia from sweating is not the listed acute-phase concern.  Why D wrong: Hypocalcemia is not the cited acute-phase major concern."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"What is the most common cause of mortality during the acute phase of burn injury?","options":["Hypovolemic shock","Infection","Pulmonary embolism","Carbon monoxide poisoning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection is the most common cause of mortality in the acute phase of burns.  Why A wrong: Hypovolemic shock is the leading mortality cause in the resuscitative/emergent phase, not the acute phase.  Why C wrong: Pulmonary embolism is a late respiratory complication, not the most common acute-phase mortality cause.  Why D wrong: CO poisoning is an early respiratory complication, not the acute-phase mortality leader."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the acute phase of burn management, urine output should be monitored every hour. The nurse should report if hourly output is less than what value?","options":["<30 ml/hr (or about 30 ml/hr)","<50 ml/hr","<100 ml/hr","<10 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output is monitored hourly and a value less than 30 ml/hr (around 30 ml/hr) indicates inadequate output and should be reported.  Why B wrong: 50 ml/hr is acceptable output, not the alarm threshold.  Why C wrong: 100 ml/hr is high-normal output.  Why D wrong: The cited threshold is <30 ml/hr, not 10."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which are stated goals of treatment in the acute phase of burn management? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Infection control","Wound care and wound closure","Nutritional support","Pain control and active & passive exercise","Immediate skin grafting within the first hour of injury"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Acute-phase goals include infection control, wound care/closure, nutritional support, and pain control with active and passive exercise.  Why E wrong: Immediate grafting in the first hour is not an acute-phase goal; grafting follows wound preparation after the emergent phase."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Regarding diet therapy in the acute phase of burn care, what nutritional approach is recommended?","options":["Low-protein, low-calorie clear liquids only","Offer clear liquid first; if tolerated, then high-protein, high-carbohydrate, high-fat & high-vitamin diet as per tolerance; maintain calories needed, usually about 5000 calories per day","NPO until grafting","Only IV nutrition, no oral intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diet starts with clear liquids; if tolerated, advance to a high-protein, high-carbohydrate, high-fat, and high-vitamin diet per tolerance, maintaining calories usually around 5000 calories per day to meet hypermetabolic demand.  Why A wrong: Low-protein, low-calorie intake is inadequate for hypermetabolic burn patients.  Why C wrong: Prolonged NPO worsens catabolism; enteral nutrition is encouraged when tolerated.  Why D wrong: Oral/enteral feeding is preferred when tolerated, not exclusively IV."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"If the hemoglobin or hematocrit level decreases during burn care or fluid administration, what should the nurse do regarding IV fluid administration rate?","options":["Increase IV fluids","Decrease the rate of IV fluid administration (the rate may be decreased)","Stop all fluids permanently","Switch to oral fluids only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If Hb/hematocrit decreases, the rate of IV fluid administration may be decreased (indicating volume restoration/hemodilution).  Why A wrong: Increasing fluids further would worsen dilution and risk overload.  Why C wrong: Stopping all fluids permanently is inappropriate; only the rate is adjusted.  Why D wrong: Switching solely to oral fluids ignores ongoing resuscitation needs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"The rehabilitative phase of burn management focuses on which goals? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Promote wound healing","Minimise deformities and initiate rehabilitative measures","Increase strength and function","Provide emotional support","Initiate emergency airway management"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rehabilitative-phase goals include promoting wound healing, minimising deformities and initiating rehabilitative measures, increasing strength and function, and providing emotional support.  Why E wrong: Emergency airway management belongs to the emergent/resuscitative phase, not the rehabilitative phase."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"During the rehabilitative phase of burn care, why should the nurse continue with active or initiate care, and what is the goal regarding patient independence?","options":["To keep the patient bedridden","The goal of this stage is to gain independence and achieve maximum function; rehabilitation is the final stage of the burn","To delay all movement until scars fully mature","To restrict the diet to clear liquids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rehabilitative phase aims to gain independence and achieve maximum function; it is the final stage of burn care.  Why A wrong: Keeping a patient bedridden opposes the independence/function goal.  Why C wrong: Delaying all movement promotes contractures; mobilization is encouraged.  Why D wrong: Diet restriction to clear liquids is not a rehabilitative-phase goal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"What is the most common cause of mortality/death in the resuscitative/emergent phase of burn injury (within the first 24 hours)?","options":["Infection (sepsis)","Asphyxia or respiratory failure (at the spot or right of the burn injury)","Pulmonary embolism","Hypovolemic shock from delayed fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the resuscitative/emergent phase (first 24 hrs), the most common cause of death is asphyxia or respiratory failure occurring at the spot of the burn injury (e.g., airway compromise/inhalation).  Why A wrong: Infection/sepsis is the leading cause in the acute phase, not the emergent phase.  Why C wrong: Pulmonary embolism is a late complication, not the emergent-phase leader.  Why D wrong: Although hypovolemia is a major early concern, the page identifies asphyxia/respiratory failure as the most common emergent-phase cause of death."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"What is the most common cause of mortality/death in the acute phase of burn injury, and overall in burns?","options":["Septic shock or infection (acute phase); septic shock overall","Hypovolemic shock","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Pulmonary edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of death in the acute phase is septic shock or infection, and septic shock is the most common cause of death overall in burns.  Why B wrong: Hypovolemic shock is the emergent-phase concern, not the overall leading cause.  Why C wrong: CO poisoning is an early respiratory complication, not the overall leading cause.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary edema is an early complication, not the overall leading cause of death."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"During wound care in burns, which standard infection-control measures should the nurse implement? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Follow standard precautions","Use strict hand-washing technique and sterile technique when doing burn care","Monitor temperature and assess for signs of infection","Provide care for skin grafting sites, donor, and recipient sites","Encourage shared, non-sterile dressings between patients"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Burn wound care requires standard precautions, strict hand-washing and sterile technique, monitoring temperature and assessing for infection, and proper care of graft donor and recipient sites.  Why E wrong: Sharing non-sterile dressings between patients would spread infection and violates aseptic technique."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which nursing observation/intervention is appropriate for monitoring GI complications in a burn patient during the acute phase?","options":["Monitor for Curling's ulcer, which may occur after a burn — a clinical feature is gastric discomfort and profuse GI bleeding","Encourage spicy foods to stimulate appetite","Avoid all proton pump inhibitors","Restrict fluids to reduce gastric output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Curling's ulcer is a stress ulcer that may occur after a burn; a clinical feature is gastric discomfort and profuse GI bleeding, so the nurse monitors for it.  Why B wrong: Spicy foods can aggravate gastric mucosa and ulcer risk.  Why C wrong: Acid suppression (e.g., PPIs) is used to prevent stress ulcers, not avoided.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction does not address Curling's ulcer and risks hypovolemia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In rehabilitative burn care, why should the nurse maintain proper alignment of the extremities?","options":["To increase oedema","To prevent contractures (maintain functional alignment of extremities)","To promote eschar formation","To reduce calorie needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maintaining proper/functional alignment of extremities helps prevent contractures during the rehabilitative phase.  Why A wrong: Proper alignment reduces, not increases, oedema risk.  Why C wrong: Alignment is unrelated to promoting eschar formation.  Why D wrong: Positioning does not aim to reduce calorie needs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"During debridement, dead tissues, eschar, and slough should be removed under what condition?","options":["Only after full healing","Dead tissues, eschar and slough should be removed by sharp debridement","Left in place to protect the wound","Removed only by the patient at home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dead tissues, eschar, and slough should be removed by (sharp) debridement to promote wound healing.  Why A wrong: Debridement is done to enable healing, not delayed until after healing.  Why C wrong: Leaving necrotic tissue promotes infection and delays healing.  Why D wrong: Debridement is a clinical procedure, not a home self-care task."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A graft taken from one part of the client's own body and applied to another (burned) part is called which type of skin graft?","options":["Autograft","Allograft (homograft)","Xenograft (heterograft)","Cultured graft"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An autograft is skin taken from another part of the client's own body; it is also taken from an identical twin (isograft).  Why B wrong: An allograft (homograft) is from another person of the same species (human to human).  Why C wrong: A xenograft (heterograft) is from a different species (animal to human).  Why D wrong: A cultured autograft is laboratory-grown skin used for massive burns, not simply skin from another body site."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A skin graft taken from another person of the same species (human to human) is called:","options":["Autograft","Allograft (homograft)","Xenograft (heterograft)","Isograft"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An allograft (homograft) is a graft from another person of the same species (human to human).  Why A wrong: An autograft is from the client's own body.  Why C wrong: A xenograft (heterograft) is from a different species (animal to human).  Why D wrong: An isograft is from an identical twin (genetically identical)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A skin graft taken from a different species (animal to human) is best described as which type?","options":["Xenograft (heterograft)","Allograft (homograft)","Autograft","Isograft"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A xenograft (heterograft) is a graft from a different species (animal to human); it is not always successful in others.  Why B wrong: An allograft/homograft is human to human (same species).  Why C wrong: An autograft is from the client's own body.  Why D wrong: An isograft is from a genetically identical twin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which nursing measures are appropriate to protect the skin graft site after grafting? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Donor site should be covered by a non-adherent dressing","Keep the donor site dry and clean, and free from creatures","Instruct the client to restrict mobility of recipient site until graft healing or about 7 days","Elevate the graft site to prevent oedema and avoid weight lifting","Massage and rub the fresh graft vigorously to improve blood supply"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Protect the graft by covering the donor site with a non-adherent dressing, keeping it dry/clean, restricting recipient-site mobility until healing (~7 days), and elevating the graft site to prevent oedema while avoiding weight lifting; monitor for signs of infection at recipient and donor sites.  Why E wrong: Vigorous massaging/rubbing of a fresh graft would dislodge it and disrupt take; mobility should be restricted, not aggravated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Debridement removes which types of tissue from a burn wound?","options":["Healthy granulation tissue","Devitalized tissues, eschar, slough tissue, and necrotic tissues","Subcutaneous fat that is viable","Intact epidermis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Debridement removes devitalized tissues, eschar, slough tissue, and necrotic tissues mechanically, surgically, or enzymatically to promote wound healing.  Why A wrong: Healthy granulation tissue should be preserved, not removed.  Why C wrong: Viable subcutaneous fat is preserved; only nonviable tissue is removed.  Why D wrong: Intact epidermis is not the target of debridement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"What are the purposes of debridement in burn wound care? (Select all that apply.)","options":["To remove dead tissues","To improve circulation","To promote granulation tissue","To prevent bacterial growth","To deepen the burn wound intentionally"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Debridement aims to remove dead tissues, improve circulation, promote granulation tissue, and prevent bacterial growth.  Why E wrong: Debridement is not intended to deepen the wound; it removes nonviable tissue to aid healing."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Mechanical debridement of a burn wound is best described by which statement?","options":["It is performed during hydrotherapy, removing dead tissue using a sponge & cloth, scissors and forceps to remove necrotic, eschar, and dead tissue from wounds, especially around margins","It uses enzymes applied directly on the wound to remove necrotic tissues","It involves surgical excision of all necrotic tissue in the operating room","It relies only on the body's natural autolysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical debridement is performed during hydrotherapy using a sponge/cloth, scissors, and forceps to remove necrotic, eschar, and dead tissue, especially around wound margins.  Why B wrong: Using enzymes directly on the wound describes enzymatic debridement.  Why C wrong: Surgical excision in the OR describes surgical debridement.  Why D wrong: Relying solely on autolysis is autolytic, not mechanical, debridement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Enzymatic debridement of a burn wound is characterized by which method?","options":["Use of enzymes applied directly on the wound to remove necrotic tissue","Surgical excision of dead tissue in the operating room","Use of sponge and forceps during hydrotherapy","Daily dressing changes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enzymatic debridement uses enzymes applied directly on the wound to remove necrotic tissue.  Why B wrong: Surgical excision in the OR is surgical debridement.  Why C wrong: Sponge/forceps during hydrotherapy is mechanical debridement.  Why D wrong: Dressing changes alone do not constitute enzymatic debridement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Surgical debridement of a burn wound involves which of the following?","options":["Excision of dead/necrotic tissues in the operating room setting","Enzymes applied to the wound","Hydrotherapy with sponges","Natural sloughing over weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical debridement is excision of dead/necrotic tissue in the operating room setting.  Why B wrong: Enzyme application is enzymatic debridement.  Why C wrong: Hydrotherapy with sponges is mechanical debridement.  Why D wrong: Natural sloughing over weeks describes autolytic, not surgical, debridement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Which of the following are recognized end points/criteria indicating adequate fluid resuscitation in a burn patient? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hemodynamic stability with most reliable indicator being heart rate and blood pressure","Systolic blood pressure of 100 mm Hg or more","Fractional excretion of Na+ (FeNa) <1%, indicating persistent hypovolemia when low/abnormal","BUN:creatinine ratio >10 indicates hypovolemia","Mixed or central venous oxygen saturation exceeding 0%"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Adequate resuscitation criteria include hemodynamic stability (HR/BP most reliable), systolic BP ≥100 mm Hg, FeNa <1% reflecting volume status, and BUN:creatinine ratio >10 indicating hypovolemia.  Why E wrong: A mixed/central venous oxygen saturation \"exceeding 0%\" is meaningless as stated; the criterion is an adequate (not merely >0%) ScvO2, so this option is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In assessing adequate fluid resuscitation, what does a urine output of approximately 1 mg/kg/hr (target hourly urine output) in adults and children indicate?","options":["Inadequate resuscitation requiring fluid bolus","Adequate fluid resuscitation (a positive sign of perfusion)","Fluid overload requiring diuretics","Need for dialysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An hourly urine output around the target (≈1 ml/kg/hr in adults and proportionally in children) indicates adequate fluid resuscitation and end-organ perfusion.  Why A wrong: Reaching the target output is a sign of adequacy, not inadequacy.  Why C wrong: This target output does not indicate overload requiring diuretics.  Why D wrong: Adequate urine output argues against the need for dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"During the first 24 hours after a burn, fluids that include or portray \"fluid recovery\" / fluid loss replacement are characterized by which fluid/electrolyte changes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Metabolic (cellular) cause of death may be respiratory or cardiac arrest","Hemoconcentration / fluid therapy ongoing","Polyuria as the dominant early feature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In the first 24 hours, the burn patient shows hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, ongoing fluid therapy/hemoconcentration, and the metabolic cause of death at the spot may be respiratory or cardiac arrest.  Why E wrong: Polyuria is a later/after-48-hour feature; oliguria dominates the early period, not polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"According to the summary of incidents following burn injury, what are the typical fluid/electrolyte findings AFTER 48 hours? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypokalemia","Hypervolemia or overhydration","Polyuria","Septic shock or infection as the cause of death","Hyperkalemia worsening progressively"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: After 48 hours, findings include hypokalemia, hypervolemia/overhydration, polyuria, and septic shock/infection becoming the cause of death; primary management focuses on prevention of infection and promotion of wound healing.  Why E wrong: Hyperkalemia is the early (first 24 hr) finding; after 48 hours potassium falls (hypokalemia), so worsening hyperkalemia is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"In the summary of incidents following burn injury, the primary management focus in the first 24 hours differs from after 48 hours. What is the primary management focus in the first 24 hours?","options":["Airway management and fluid therapy","Prevention of infection","Promotion of wound healing","Skin grafting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the first 24 hours, primary management is airway patency and fluid therapy (resuscitation).  Why B wrong: Prevention of infection is the primary focus after 48 hours, not in the first 24.  Why C wrong: Promotion of wound healing is the later (after 48 hr) management focus.  Why D wrong: Skin grafting occurs later, after wound preparation, not as the first-24-hour priority."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"According to the criteria for adequate fluid resuscitation, what does a low or abnormal mixed/central venous oxygen saturation suggest in a burn patient?","options":["Adequate tissue oxygen delivery","Hypoperfusion or cyanide poisoning (inadequate oxygen delivery/utilization)","Fluid overload","Normal recovery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A low/abnormal mixed or central venous oxygen saturation indicates hypoperfusion or, in burns with smoke exposure, cyanide poisoning—reflecting inadequate oxygen delivery or utilization.  Why A wrong: A low value indicates inadequate, not adequate, oxygen delivery.  Why C wrong: Low venous oxygen saturation reflects perfusion deficit, not overload.  Why D wrong: An abnormal value signals a problem, not normal recovery."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Regarding the first incident-site action in burn care versus the first action in the hospital, which pairing is correct?","options":["First action at spot = remove victim from burn source; first activity in hospital = assess patent airway and administer 100% oxygen","First action at spot = give IV fluids; first hospital action = wound dressing","First action at spot = apply dressing; first hospital action = skin grafting","First action at spot = give analgesics; first hospital action = discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first action at the spot is to remove the victim from the burn source; the first hospital activity is to assess for a patent airway and administer oxygen (100%).  Why B wrong: IV fluids and dressings follow airway/removal priorities.  Why C wrong: Dressing/grafting are not the immediate first actions.  Why D wrong: Analgesics and discharge are not the priority first actions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"When initiating burn fluid resuscitation, the amount of fluid to be infused is calculated using which formula, and from what time is it counted?","options":["Parkland formula, counted from the time of injury (burn), not from the admission time","Consensus formula, counted from admission","Evans formula, counted from IV insertion","Brooke formula, counted from first urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The fluid amount is calculated using the Parkland formula, with timing counted from the time of injury (the burn), not from admission time.  Why B wrong: Counting from admission is explicitly incorrect; the page references the Parkland formula from time of burn.  Why C wrong: Timing is from injury, not IV insertion, and the cited formula is Parkland.  Why D wrong: Timing is from the burn, not first urine output."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"Why should IV (not IM/SC) be the preferred route for medications such as analgesics during the early burn period, and what does a fluid bolus of Ringer's lactate help guide?","options":["IV gives reliable absorption because peripheral perfusion is poor; the fluid bolus (Ringer's lactate) response helps guide ongoing resuscitation","IM is faster but causes scarring","Oral route ensures best absorption in shock","SC route avoids infection entirely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV is preferred because impaired peripheral perfusion makes IM/SC absorption unreliable; the response to a Ringer's lactate fluid bolus helps guide further fluid resuscitation.  Why B wrong: IM absorption is unreliable in shock, not simply \"faster with scarring.\"  Why C wrong: Oral absorption is poor in shock states.  Why D wrong: The SC route does not eliminate infection risk and has unreliable absorption in burns."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Burn injury — Rule of Nine, Palmar method…","question":"A burn patient on IV fluids develops a falling Hb/HCT and the nurse decreases the IV rate. Initially, the first 24 hours give only crystalloids; what may be added after the first 24 hours, and why?","options":["Colloids may be added after the first 24 hours to support oncotic pressure once capillary permeability normalizes","Diuretics in the first hour to prevent overload","Whole blood as the sole replacement fluid","Hypertonic saline only, never crystalloids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids are typically added after the first 24 hours, once capillary permeability (capillary leak) normalizes, to help restore plasma oncotic pressure; this aligns with the page's emphasis on plasma/colloid use after the initial leak phase.  Why B wrong: Diuretics in the first hour would worsen the hypovolemia of early burns.  Why C wrong: Whole blood is not the sole replacement fluid; crystalloids dominate early.  Why D wrong: Crystalloids (Ringer's lactate) are the mainstay early; hypertonic saline alone is not standard."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which type of skin cancer is the most common?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Kaposi sarcoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rapid-revision points state that the most common type of skin cancer is basal cell carcinoma.  Why A wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma is common but not the most common type.  Why C wrong: Malignant melanoma is the most lethal/fatal type, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Kaposi sarcoma is a less common cancer associated with immunosuppression."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which type of skin cancer is the most lethal (fatal) form of carcinoma?","options":["Basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Actinic keratosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most lethal/fatal type of carcinoma is malignant melanoma.  Why A wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is the most common but rarely metastasizes/fatal.  Why B wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma is less lethal than melanoma.  Why D wrong: Actinic keratosis is a pre-cancerous lesion, not the most lethal cancer."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A deficiency of which vitamin is synthesized in the skin when it is exposed to ultraviolet rays of the sun?","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Vitamin K"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Vitamin D is synthesized in the skin on exposure to ultraviolet rays of the sun.  Why A wrong: Vitamin A is obtained from diet, not synthesized by UV exposure in skin.  Why B wrong: Vitamin C is dietary and not synthesized by skin/sunlight.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K is dietary/synthesized by gut bacteria."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Inflammation of the tissue surrounding the nails is known as:","options":["Paronychia","Onychomycosis","Cellulitis","Impetigo"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines inflammation of the tissue surrounding the nails as paronychia.  Why B wrong: Onychomycosis is a fungal infection of the nail itself, not the surrounding tissue.  Why C wrong: Cellulitis is diffuse infection of subcutaneous/connective tissue.  Why D wrong: Impetigo is a superficial skin infection caused by staphylococci/streptococci."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Fatty growths that develop underneath the skin are known as:","options":["Dense collagen","Keloids","Xanthoma","Psoriasis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Xanthoma\" describes fatty growths that develop underneath the skin.  Why A wrong: Dense collagen is the substance keloids are made up of.  Why B wrong: Keloids are raised scar tissue, not fatty growths.  Why D wrong: Psoriasis is a scaling inflammatory skin disorder, not a fatty growth."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Keloids are made up of which substance?","options":["Melanin","Keratin","Dense collagen","Elastin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states keloids are made up of dense collagen.  Why A wrong: Melanin is the pigment of skin/hair, not the matrix of keloids.  Why B wrong: Keratin is the structural protein of the epidermis, hair and nails.  Why D wrong: Elastin provides skin elasticity but is not what keloids are made of."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Silvery scaly plaques that are mildly itchy and seen on a patient's knee and elbows are characteristic of:","options":["Psoriasis","Eczema","Impetigo","Erysipelas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes silvery scaly plaques, mildly itchy, on knees and elbows as psoriasis.  Why B wrong: Eczema presents with weeping, oozing, intensely itchy patches rather than silvery plaques.  Why C wrong: Impetigo is a bacterial infection with honey-colored crusts.  Why D wrong: Erysipelas is a sharply demarcated streptococcal infection of dermis/lymphatics."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Sensational loss of hair is best monitored by assessing which parameter?","options":["Blood pressure","Temperature","Pulse rate","Respiratory rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sensational hair loss is first to occur in (assessed by) temperature changes.  Why A wrong: Blood pressure is not the parameter cited for hair-loss assessment on the page.  Why C wrong: Pulse rate is not the cited parameter.  Why D wrong: Respiratory rate is not the cited parameter."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which drug of choice is used for herpes zoster (shingles)?","options":["Penicillin","Acyclovir","Metronidazole","Fluconazole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists acyclovir as the drug of choice for herpes zoster.  Why A wrong: Penicillin is for bacterial infections, not viral herpes zoster.  Why C wrong: Metronidazole targets anaerobes/protozoa, not herpes zoster.  Why D wrong: Fluconazole is an antifungal, ineffective against viral herpes zoster."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which type of burn is painless?","options":["First degree burn","Second degree burn","Third and fourth degree burn","Superficial partial-thickness burn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states third and fourth degree burns are painless (destruction of nerve endings).  Why A wrong: First degree (superficial) burns are painful.  Why B wrong: Second degree (partial-thickness) burns are very painful.  Why D wrong: Superficial partial-thickness burns retain pain sensation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"What is the most common type of burn injury?","options":["Chemical burn","Electrical burn","Thermal burn","Radiation burn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common type of burn injury is thermal.  Why A wrong: Chemical burns occur but are not the most common type.  Why B wrong: Electrical burns are less common than thermal burns.  Why D wrong: Radiation burns are uncommon compared with thermal burns."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"What is the first nursing intervention on seeing a client with a burn?","options":["Start IV fluids immediately","Stop the further burning process","Administer analgesics","Apply sterile dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the first nursing intervention on seeing a burn client is to stop the further burning process.  Why A wrong: Fluid resuscitation follows once the burning process is stopped and airway/safety are secured.  Why C wrong: Pain management is important but not the first action.  Why D wrong: Dressing is a later intervention, not the immediate first step."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Complications of burn injury include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Compartment syndrome","Curling ulcers","Intestinal ileus","Increased melanin pigment"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists burn complications as compartment syndrome, curling ulcers and intestinal ileus; increased melanin is not a burn complication.  Why A wrong: Compartment syndrome is a listed burn complication.  Why B wrong: Curling ulcers (stress ulcers) are a listed burn complication.  Why C wrong: Intestinal ileus is a listed burn complication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Inability to produce sufficient melanin pigment is known as:","options":["Vitiligo","Albinism","Impetigo","Hypopigmentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines inability to produce sufficient melanin pigment as albinism.  Why A wrong: Vitiligo is patchy loss of pigment, not a global inability to produce melanin.  Why C wrong: Impetigo is a bacterial skin infection.  Why D wrong: Hypopigmentation is a general term, but the page specifically names albinism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A superficial skin infection caused by Staphylococcus aureus is known as:","options":["Erysipelas","Impetigo","Cellulitis","Folliculitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a superficial skin infection caused by Staphylococcus aureus is impetigo.  Why A wrong: Erysipelas is caused by streptococcus and involves dermis/lymphatics.  Why C wrong: Cellulitis is a deeper subcutaneous infection.  Why D wrong: Folliculitis is inflammation of hair follicles, not the answer given."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which type of shock occurs in burn patients in the first 3 days?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Septic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hypovolemic shock occurs in burn patients in the first 3 days (due to fluid loss).  Why A wrong: Cardiogenic shock results from pump failure, not the early burn picture described.  Why B wrong: Septic shock develops later from infection, not in the first 3 days.  Why D wrong: Neurogenic shock is from loss of vascular tone (e.g., spinal injury)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Why is the intravenous (IV) route preferred over IM and SC routes for drug administration in burn patients?","options":["Because of increased absorption from IM/SC sites","Because of decreased (poor) absorption from IM/SC sites","Because it causes less pain","Because it reduces infection risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states IV is preferred for medication administration (e.g., analgesics) and IM/SC routes should be avoided because of decreased absorption in burn patients.  Why A wrong: Absorption is decreased, not increased, hence IM/SC is avoided.  Why C wrong: Pain reduction is not the rationale stated on the page.  Why D wrong: Infection risk is not the stated reason for route selection here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"During rapid fluid resuscitation in burn patients, hypovolemia and hyperkalemia may convert to which electrolyte/fluid disturbance due to reentry of potassium intracellularly?","options":["Hypervolemia and hypokalemia","Hypovolemia and hyperkalemia","Hypervolemia and hyperkalemia","Hypovolemia and hypokalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that with rapid fluid resuscitation, hypovolemia and hyperkalemia may convert to hypervolemia and hypokalemia due to reentry of potassium intracellularly.  Why B wrong: This is the initial state, not the post-resuscitation shift.  Why C wrong: Potassium reenters cells, so hyperkalemia does not persist.  Why D wrong: Volume status shifts to hypervolemia, not continued hypovolemia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Butterfly rash (malar rash) on the face is a characteristic sign of which condition?","options":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)","Scleroderma","Dermatomyositis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states butterfly rash on the face is a sign of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE).  Why A wrong: Rheumatoid arthritis affects joints and does not produce a malar/butterfly rash.  Why C wrong: Scleroderma causes skin tightening/hardening, not a butterfly rash.  Why D wrong: Dermatomyositis causes a heliotrope rash, not the malar butterfly rash named."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"EUSOL solution is an antiseptic solution used for treating wounds and burn areas. What is its composition?","options":["12.5 g boric acid, 12.5 g bleaching powder, 100 ml water","25 g boric acid, 25 g bleaching powder, 100 ml water","12.5 g boric acid, 12.5 g bleaching powder, 1000 ml water","5 g boric acid, 5 g bleaching powder, 100 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives EUSOL composition as 12.5 g boric acid, 12.5 g bleaching powder and 100 ml water.  Why B wrong: The quantities are 12.5 g each, not 25 g.  Why C wrong: The water volume is 100 ml, not 1000 ml.  Why D wrong: The boric acid/bleaching powder amounts are 12.5 g each, not 5 g."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Erysipelas is an infection with inflammation of the dermis and lymphatics caused by which organism?","options":["Staphylococcus","Streptococcus","Pseudomonas","Candida"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states erysipelas is infection with inflammation of the dermis and lymphatics caused by streptococcus.  Why A wrong: Staphylococcus is the cause of impetigo per the page, not erysipelas.  Why C wrong: Pseudomonas is not the cited cause of erysipelas.  Why D wrong: Candida is a fungus, not the cause of erysipelas."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Goodpasture's syndrome is best described as:","options":["An infectious disease of the skin","An autoimmune disease of the renal and pulmonary system","A degenerative joint disease","A clotting disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes Goodpasture's syndrome as an autoimmune disease of the renal and pulmonary system.  Why A wrong: It is autoimmune, not an infectious skin disease.  Why C wrong: It does not primarily affect joints.  Why D wrong: It is not described as a clotting disorder."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A contusion (blunt trauma) is best defined as:","options":["Tearing of skin against a rough surface","Injuries of internal organs through forceful trauma","A deep cut into tissues from nails or knives","Forcible pulling away of structures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines contusion (blunt trauma) as injuries of internal organs through forceful trauma.  Why A wrong: That describes an abrasion.  Why C wrong: That describes a puncture.  Why D wrong: That describes an avulsion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which wound type involves the entire structure or parts being forcibly pulled away (e.g., loss of a tooth or earlobe)?","options":["Abrasion","Avulsion","Laceration","Puncture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines avulsion as the entire structure or parts being forcibly pulled away, e.g., loss of tooth, earlobe.  Why A wrong: Abrasion is rubbing of skin against a rough surface.  Why C wrong: Laceration is a tear produced by external force.  Why D wrong: Puncture is a deep cut from nails, knives or broken glass."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Rubbing of the skin against another rough surface is termed:","options":["Laceration","Crush injury","Abrasion","Puncture"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines abrasion as rubbed skin against another rough surface.  Why A wrong: Laceration is a tear produced by external force.  Why B wrong: Crush injury occurs when an object falls on body parts.  Why D wrong: Puncture is a deep cut into tissues."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A crush injury occurs when:","options":["The skin is torn by an external force","An object falls on body parts, tearing underlying structures","Skin is rubbed against a rough surface","Tissues are deeply cut by glass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines crush injury as occurring when an object falls on body parts, tearing underlying structures.  Why A wrong: That describes a laceration.  Why C wrong: That describes an abrasion.  Why D wrong: That describes a puncture."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A wound described as a tear produced by an external force is a:","options":["Puncture","Avulsion","Laceration","Contusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines laceration as a tear produced by external force.  Why A wrong: Puncture is a deep cut into tissues.  Why B wrong: Avulsion is forcible pulling away of a part.  Why D wrong: Contusion is blunt trauma to internal organs."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A deep cut into tissues caused by nails, knives or broken glass is a:","options":["Abrasion","Puncture","Laceration","Contusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines puncture as a deep cut into tissues from nails, knives and broken glass.  Why A wrong: Abrasion is a superficial rubbing injury.  Why C wrong: Laceration is a tear by external force.  Why D wrong: Contusion is blunt internal-organ trauma."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A group of cells in the human body that synthesizes hormones and releases them either into the bloodstream or onto an outer surface is best defined as a:","options":["Neuron","Gland","Nephron","Alveolus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines a gland as a group of cells that synthesizes hormones and releases them into the bloodstream (endocrine) or onto an outer surface (exocrine).  Why A wrong: A neuron transmits nerve impulses, not a hormone-synthesizing gland.  Why C wrong: A nephron is the functional unit of the kidney.  Why D wrong: An alveolus is a gas-exchange unit in the lung."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Endocrine glands are also known as:","options":["Mixed glands","Ductless glands","Exocrine glands","Heterocrine glands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states endocrine glands are also known as ductless glands because they secrete hormones directly into the bloodstream.  Why A wrong: Mixed glands are heterocrine glands with both functions.  Why C wrong: Exocrine glands pour secretions through ducts onto surfaces.  Why D wrong: Heterocrine glands have both endocrine and exocrine functions."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"All of the following are listed as endocrine glands EXCEPT:","options":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland","Sebaceous gland","Ovary"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal, thymus, pineal, ovary and testes as endocrine glands; the sebaceous gland is given as an exocrine gland.  Why A wrong: Thyroid is a listed endocrine gland.  Why B wrong: Adrenal is a listed endocrine gland.  Why D wrong: Ovary is a listed endocrine gland."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Why is the hypothalamus NOT considered a pure endocrine gland?","options":["Because it has no secretory function","Because it has both neural and endocrine structure and function and is considered neuroendocrine","Because it secretes only digestive enzymes","Because it lacks a blood supply"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the hypothalamus is not considered an endocrine gland because it has both neural and endocrine structure and function and is considered neuroendocrine.  Why A wrong: It does have secretory (releasing/inhibiting hormone) functions.  Why C wrong: It does not secrete digestive enzymes.  Why D wrong: Lack of blood supply is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which type of exocrine gland loses NO part of the cell during secretion (e.g., salivary and sweat glands)?","options":["Holocrine","Apocrine","Merocrine","Endocrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states merocrine glands lose no part of the cell during secretion, e.g., salivary and sweat glands.  Why A wrong: In holocrine glands the whole cell detaches with the secretion.  Why B wrong: In apocrine glands the top of the cell is lost with the secretion.  Why D wrong: Endocrine glands are ductless and not part of the exocrine merocrine/apocrine/holocrine classification."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In which type of exocrine gland is the top of the cell lost along with the secretion (e.g., mammary glands)?","options":["Merocrine","Apocrine","Holocrine","Heterocrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states apocrine glands lose the top of the cell with the secretion, with mammary glands as the example.  Why A wrong: Merocrine glands lose no part of the cell.  Why C wrong: Holocrine glands lose the whole cell.  Why D wrong: Heterocrine is a mixed gland classification, not an exocrine secretory mode."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which exocrine secretion type involves the whole cell detaching with the secretion (e.g., sebaceous gland)?","options":["Merocrine","Apocrine","Holocrine","Eccrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states holocrine glands have the whole cell detaching with the secretion, with the sebaceous gland as the example.  Why A wrong: Merocrine glands lose no part of the cell.  Why B wrong: Apocrine glands lose only the top of the cell.  Why D wrong: Eccrine is not the term used on the page for this whole-cell mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A gland that has both endocrine and exocrine functions is known as a:","options":["Ductless gland","Mixed (heterocrine) gland","Merocrine gland","Holocrine gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines heterocrine glands as those with both endocrine and exocrine functions, also known as mixed glands.  Why A wrong: A ductless gland is purely endocrine.  Why C wrong: Merocrine refers to an exocrine secretory mode only.  Why D wrong: Holocrine refers to an exocrine secretory mode only."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pancreas is an example of a heterocrine (mixed) gland because it has:","options":["Only insulin secretion","Endocrine (insulin and glucagon) and exocrine (digestive enzyme) secretions","Only digestive enzyme secretion","Only neural function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pancreas has both endocrine (insulin and glucagon) and exocrine (digestive enzyme) secretions.  Why A wrong: It secretes more than insulin alone.  Why C wrong: It also has endocrine functions, not only digestive enzymes.  Why D wrong: The pancreas is glandular, not a neural organ."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The hypothalamus regulates pituitary functions and secretes which neurosecretory cells/hormones?","options":["Only insulin and glucagon","Releasing hormones like GnRH, TRH, PRH, GHRH, CRH, PRH and oxytocin and ADH (vasopressin)","Only thyroxine","Only aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the hypothalamus secretes neurosecretory cells/hormones acting on the anterior pituitary (GnRH, TRH, PRH, GHRH, CRH, PRH) and oxytocin and ADH (vasopressin).  Why A wrong: Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic hormones, not hypothalamic.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine is a thyroid hormone.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is an adrenal cortex hormone."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pineal gland is also known by which name due to its cone shape?","options":["Hypophysis cerebri","Epiphysis cerebri","Pituitary gland","Master gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pineal gland is cone-shaped and also known as epiphysis cerebri.  Why A wrong: Hypophysis cerebri is the pituitary gland.  Why C wrong: The pituitary is a separate gland.  Why D wrong: The pituitary, not the pineal, is called the master gland."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pineal gland is sometimes called the \"third eye\" because of which characteristic?","options":["It produces thyroid hormones","Its connection with light or retina and its location in the epithalamus, near the center of the brain","It stores ADH","It secretes digestive enzymes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pineal gland is called the \"third eye\" due to its connection with light or retina and its location in the epithalamus near the center of the brain between the two hemispheres.  Why A wrong: Thyroid hormone production is unrelated to the \"third eye\" designation.  Why C wrong: ADH is stored in the posterior pituitary, not the pineal.  Why D wrong: The pineal gland does not secrete digestive enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which hormone produced by the pineal gland is a serotonin-derived hormone that modulates sleep patterns in circadian and seasonal cycles?","options":["Thymosin","Melatonin","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pineal gland produces melatonin, a serotonin-derived hormone that modulates sleep patterns in circadian and seasonal cycles.  Why A wrong: Thymosin is a thymus hormone.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is secreted by the hypothalamus and stored in the posterior pituitary.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is an anterior pituitary hormone."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Melatonin production is:","options":["Stimulated by light and inhibited by darkness","Stimulated by darkness and inhibited by light","Independent of light","Stimulated only by exercise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states melatonin production is stimulated by darkness and inhibited by light.  Why A wrong: This reverses the actual relationship.  Why C wrong: Melatonin production is strongly influenced by light/darkness.  Why D wrong: Exercise is not the regulator of melatonin per the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Light-sensitive nerve cells in the retina detect light and send this signal to which structure, then to the spinal cord and on to the pineal gland?","options":["Cerebellum","Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)","Medulla oblongata","Hippocampus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states light-sensitive nerve cells in the retina detect light and send this signal to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), then the spinal cord, then the pineal gland.  Why A wrong: The cerebellum coordinates movement, not this light pathway.  Why C wrong: The medulla oblongata controls vital reflexes, not this signal relay.  Why D wrong: The hippocampus is involved in memory, not the melatonin pathway described."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"When does the body normally produce more melatonin?","options":["In the morning as the sun rises","During the nighttime, with levels normally increasing in the evening and dropping in the morning","Only during exercise","Only after meals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the body normally produces more melatonin during the nighttime, increasing in the evening and dropping in the morning as the sun rises.  Why A wrong: Levels drop in the morning, they do not peak.  Why C wrong: Exercise is not the regulator of melatonin timing.  Why D wrong: Meal timing is not described as the regulator."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The thymus is best described as:","options":["A neural organ with no endocrine function","A specialized and main lymphoid organ of the immune system that also has endocrine functions","A digestive gland","A purely exocrine gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the thymus is a specialized and main lymphoid organ of the immune system that also has endocrine functions.  Why A wrong: It does have endocrine functions.  Why C wrong: It is not a digestive gland.  Why D wrong: It is not purely exocrine; it is lymphoid with endocrine function."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The thymus develops and matures a type of white blood cells called:","options":["B lymphocytes (B cells)","T lymphocytes (T cells)","Neutrophils","Eosinophils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the thymus develops and matures T lymphocytes (T cells), which help fight infection.  Why A wrong: B cells mature primarily in the bone marrow, not the thymus.  Why C wrong: Neutrophils are not matured by the thymus.  Why D wrong: Eosinophils are not matured by the thymus."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which three major hormones are produced by the thymus gland?","options":["Thymosin, thymopoietin and thymulin","Insulin, glucagon and somatostatin","TSH, ACTH and FSH","ADH, oxytocin and prolactin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the thymus produces three major thymus hormones: thymosin, thymopoietin and thymulin.  Why B wrong: These are pancreatic hormones.  Why C wrong: These are anterior pituitary hormones.  Why D wrong: ADH and oxytocin are posterior pituitary; prolactin is anterior pituitary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Thymosin, a thymus hormone, is necessary for which process?","options":["Red blood cell production","T cell development and production","Bone mineralization","Digestive enzyme release"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states thymosin is a hormone necessary for T cell development and production.  Why A wrong: Erythropoietin governs RBC production, not thymosin.  Why C wrong: Bone mineralization is governed by vitamin D/PTH, not thymosin.  Why D wrong: Digestive enzyme release is unrelated to thymosin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Where is the thymus positioned in the body?","options":["In the upper frontal portion of the chest, behind the sternum and in front of the heart","In the abdominal cavity behind the stomach","In the neck below the thyroid gland","In the cranial cavity near the brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the thymus is positioned in the upper frontal portion of the chest, behind the sternum and in front of the heart.  Why B wrong: That is the location of the pancreas.  Why C wrong: The thymus is in the chest, not the neck below the thyroid.  Why D wrong: The thymus is in the thorax, not the cranial cavity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"During which period is the thymus the largest and most active gland?","options":["Old age","The neonatal and pre-adolescent periods, after which it decreases","Middle adulthood","Only during pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the thymus is the largest and most active gland during the neonatal and pre-adolescent periods and then decreases after that.  Why A wrong: In old age the thymus has involuted/decreased.  Why C wrong: Activity declines well before middle adulthood.  Why D wrong: Pregnancy is not the period of peak thymic activity described."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Dysfunction of the thymus can lead to:","options":["Increased T cell count only","A reduced number of T cells and autoimmune diseases","Excess insulin secretion","Increased melatonin production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states any dysfunction in the thymus can lead to a reduced number of T cells and autoimmune diseases.  Why A wrong: Dysfunction reduces, not increases, T cells.  Why C wrong: Insulin is a pancreatic hormone, unrelated to thymic dysfunction.  Why D wrong: Melatonin is pineal-derived, unrelated to the thymus."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pituitary gland is also known by which name?","options":["Epiphysis cerebri","Hypophysis cerebri","Suprarenal gland","Thyroid gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pituitary gland, a pea-sized gland, is also known as hypophysis cerebri.  Why A wrong: Epiphysis cerebri is the pineal gland.  Why C wrong: Suprarenal gland is the adrenal gland.  Why D wrong: The thyroid is a distinct gland."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pituitary gland is referred to as the \"master gland of the endocrine orchestra\" because it:","options":["Stores melatonin","Controls the functions of most of the other endocrine glands","Produces digestive enzymes","Matures T cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pituitary controls the functions of other endocrine glands and is thus known as the master gland of the endocrine orchestra.  Why A wrong: Melatonin is stored/produced by the pineal gland.  Why C wrong: Digestive enzymes are produced by the pancreas.  Why D wrong: T cell maturation is a thymus function."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pituitary gland is located in which bony structure of the skull?","options":["Foramen magnum","Sella turcica (hypophyseal fossa) of the sphenoid bone","Mastoid process","Cribriform plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pituitary is located in the sella turcica or hypophyseal fossa of the sphenoid bone of the skull.  Why A wrong: The foramen magnum transmits the spinal cord, not the pituitary.  Why C wrong: The mastoid process is part of the temporal bone, not the pituitary site.  Why D wrong: The cribriform plate transmits olfactory nerves."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Arterial blood is supplied to the pituitary gland by which artery?","options":["Internal carotid artery","Vertebral artery","Basilar artery","External carotid artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pituitary's arterial blood supply is by the internal carotid artery.  Why B wrong: The vertebral artery is not the cited supply.  Why C wrong: The basilar artery is not the cited supply.  Why D wrong: The external carotid artery is not the cited supply."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The pituitary gland is connected to the hypothalamus by which structure?","options":["Corpus callosum","Hypophyseal stalk (infundibulum)","Falx cerebri","Tentorium cerebelli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pituitary is connected to the hypothalamus with the hypophyseal stalk.  Why A wrong: The corpus callosum connects the two cerebral hemispheres.  Why C wrong: The falx cerebri is a dural fold between hemispheres.  Why D wrong: The tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebellum from the cerebrum."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The anterior pituitary is also known as:","options":["Neurohypophysis","Adenohypophysis","Infundibulum","Pars nervosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the anterior pituitary is also known as adenohypophysis.  Why A wrong: Neurohypophysis is the posterior pituitary.  Why C wrong: The infundibulum is the connecting stalk.  Why D wrong: Pars nervosa is part of the posterior pituitary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The posterior pituitary is also known as:","options":["Adenohypophysis","Neurohypophysis","Pars distalis","Sella turcica"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the posterior pituitary is also known as neurohypophysis.  Why A wrong: Adenohypophysis is the anterior pituitary.  Why C wrong: Pars distalis is part of the anterior pituitary.  Why D wrong: Sella turcica is the bony fossa housing the gland."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Hyperpituitarism is defined as:","options":["Deficiency of one or more anterior pituitary hormones","Excessive secretion of pituitary hormones such as GH, ACTH and PRL in the blood","Atrophy of the adrenal cortex","Absence of all thymus hormones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines hyperpituitarism as excessive secretion of pituitary hormones such as GH, ACTH and PRL in the blood.  Why A wrong: That is the definition of hypopituitarism.  Why C wrong: Adrenal cortex atrophy is unrelated to the pituitary definition.  Why D wrong: Absence of thymus hormones is unrelated to pituitary hyperfunction."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Gigantism is best described as:","options":["Excessive secretion of growth hormone in children","Excessive secretion of growth hormone in adults","Overproduction of ACTH","Deficiency of ADH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states gigantism is excessive secretion of growth hormone in children.  Why B wrong: Excess GH in adults is acromegaly.  Why C wrong: Overproduction of ACTH characterizes Cushing's syndrome.  Why D wrong: ADH deficiency causes diabetes insipidus."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Acromegaly is defined as:","options":["Excessive growth hormone secretion in children","Excessive secretion of growth hormone in adults","Deficiency of ACTH","Excess adrenal cortical hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states acromegaly is excessive secretion of growth hormone in adults.  Why A wrong: Excess GH in children is gigantism.  Why C wrong: ACTH deficiency is not acromegaly.  Why D wrong: Excess adrenal cortical hormone (via ACTH) describes Cushing's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Cushing's syndrome results from the overproduction of which hormone, leading to over-secretion of adrenal/cortisol hormone?","options":["Growth hormone","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Cushing's syndrome is overproduction of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) resulting in over-secretion of adrenal/cortisol hormone.  Why A wrong: Excess GH causes gigantism/acromegaly, not Cushing's syndrome.  Why C wrong: ADH excess relates to SIADH, not Cushing's.  Why D wrong: TSH excess relates to thyroid disorders, not Cushing's."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of hyperpituitarism?","options":["Glandular under-activity","Pituitary tumors such as adenoma","Hemorrhage","Total absence of the gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes of hyperpituitarism as glandular over-activity, trauma in the pituitary gland and pituitary tumors such as adenoma.  Why A wrong: Under-activity would cause hypopituitarism, not hyper.  Why C wrong: Hemorrhage is listed as a cause of hypopituitarism, not hyperpituitarism.  Why D wrong: Total absence of the gland (Simmonds disease) is a cause of hypopituitarism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is a clinical feature of hyperpituitarism?","options":["Decreased temperature","Enlarged hands and feet, bones, lower jaw, lips and tongue","Postural hypotension","Sterility with loss of secondary sexual characteristics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists enlarged hands and feet, bones, lower jaw, lips and tongue among clinical features of hyperpituitarism.  Why A wrong: Decreased temperature is a feature of hypopituitarism.  Why C wrong: Postural hypotension is listed under hypopituitarism.  Why D wrong: Sterility/loss of secondary sexual characteristics is listed under hypopituitarism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a clinical feature of hyperpituitarism listed on the page?","options":["Headache, depression, visual disturbances and diaphoresis","Increased level of ACTH, GH and PRL","Cold intolerance","Absence of menses (amenorrhoea) and organomegaly with increased ICP"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cold intolerance is listed as a clinical feature of hypopituitarism, not hyperpituitarism.  Why A wrong: Headache, depression, visual disturbances and diaphoresis are listed hyperpituitarism features.  Why B wrong: Increased ACTH, GH and PRL is a listed hyperpituitarism feature.  Why D wrong: Amenorrhoea, organomegaly, hypertension and increased ICP are listed hyperpituitarism features."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A priority nursing care measure for a client with hyperpituitarism is to:","options":["Administer thyroid hormone replacement","Encourage the client to express feelings and provide emotional support, and initiate general nursing care","Give hypertonic IV fluids","Maintain strict bed rest only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists nursing care for hyperpituitarism as encouraging the client to express feelings, providing emotional support and initiating general nursing care.  Why A wrong: Hormone replacement is the nursing care for hypopituitarism (deficiency), not hyper.  Why C wrong: Hypertonic fluids relate to other disorders (e.g., DI/dehydration), not this nursing care list.  Why D wrong: Strict bed rest only is not the stated nursing care measure."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A hypophysectomy is best defined as:","options":["Removal of the thyroid gland","Removal of the pituitary gland via craniotomy or trans-sphenoidal/endoscopic trans-nasal approach","Removal of the adrenal gland","Removal of the thymus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines hypophysectomy as removal of the pituitary gland via craniotomy or a trans-sphenoidal/endoscopic trans-nasal route.  Why A wrong: Thyroid removal is a thyroidectomy.  Why C wrong: Adrenal removal is an adrenalectomy.  Why D wrong: Thymus removal is a thymectomy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Self-care instructions after a hypophysectomy should include teaching the client to:","options":["Stop all medications immediately","Take medicines at the right time, limit stressful situations and protect self from infection","Avoid all physical activity permanently","Restrict fluids to 500 ml/day lifelong"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states self-care after a hypophysectomy includes taking medicines at the right time, limiting stressful situations and protecting self from infection.  Why A wrong: Hormone replacement medications must be continued, not stopped.  Why C wrong: Permanent avoidance of all activity is not the stated teaching.  Why D wrong: Lifelong severe fluid restriction is not part of this teaching."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Hypopituitarism is defined as:","options":["Excessive secretion of pituitary hormones","Deficiency of one or more anterior pituitary hormones","Overproduction of melatonin","Excess adrenal androgen secretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines hypopituitarism as deficiency of one or more anterior pituitary hormones.  Why A wrong: Excessive secretion describes hyperpituitarism.  Why C wrong: Melatonin overproduction relates to the pineal gland.  Why D wrong: Excess adrenal androgen is unrelated to the pituitary deficiency definition."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Total absence of all pituitary hormones is known as:","options":["Cushing's syndrome","Simmonds disease","Acromegaly","Addison's disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states total absence of pituitary hormone is called Simmonds disease (NIMHANS-2018).  Why A wrong: Cushing's syndrome is excess ACTH/cortisol, not absent pituitary hormones.  Why C wrong: Acromegaly is excess GH in adults.  Why D wrong: Addison's disease is adrenal cortical insufficiency, not pituitary hormone absence."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of hypopituitarism?","options":["Glandular over-activity","Trauma, tumors, haemorrhage and autoimmune disorders","Pituitary adenoma causing over-secretion","Excess UV exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes of hypopituitarism as trauma, tumors, haemorrhage and autoimmune disorders (plus total absence = Simmonds disease).  Why A wrong: Glandular over-activity causes hyperpituitarism.  Why C wrong: Adenoma with over-secretion is a hyperpituitarism cause.  Why D wrong: UV exposure is unrelated to hypopituitarism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which set of findings represents clinical features of hypopituitarism?","options":["Enlarged hands, increased ACTH, hypertension","Cold intolerance, decreased temperature, postural hypotension and hypoglycaemia","Diaphoresis, organomegaly, increased ICP","Excessive thirst and polyuria of 20-25 litres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists hypopituitarism clinical features as cold intolerance, decreased temperature, postural hypotension and hypoglycaemia (with decreased GH, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH and sterility/loss of secondary sexual characteristics & libido).  Why A wrong: Those are hyperpituitarism features.  Why C wrong: Those are hyperpituitarism features.  Why D wrong: Excessive thirst and polyuria of 20-25 L describe diabetes insipidus."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In hypopituitarism, \"sterility\" is described as:","options":["Increased fertility","Loss of secondary sexual characteristics and libido","Excess libido","Enlargement of genitals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sterility in hypopituitarism is loss of secondary sexual characteristics and libido.  Why A wrong: Hypopituitarism reduces, not increases, fertility.  Why C wrong: Libido is lost, not increased.  Why D wrong: Genital enlargement is not described in hypopituitarism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which nursing care measure is appropriate for a client with hypopituitarism?","options":["Provide emotional support and hormone replacement therapy as per order, and initiate general nursing care","Restrict all fluids","Administer insulin only","Encourage high-salt diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists nursing care for hypopituitarism as providing emotional support and hormone replacement therapy as per order, and initiating general nursing care.  Why B wrong: Blanket fluid restriction is not the stated nursing care.  Why C wrong: Insulin alone is not indicated; hormone replacement as ordered is.  Why D wrong: High-salt diet is not part of the stated nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Growth hormone (GH) from the anterior lobe has all of the following actions EXCEPT:","options":["Promotes protein anabolism","Promotes fat mobilization and catabolism","Slows carbohydrate metabolism","Stimulates water reabsorption in the kidney"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Water reabsorption in the kidney is an action of ADH (posterior lobe), not GH. GH's listed target organs are liver, bone and muscles.  Why A wrong: GH promotes protein anabolism per the page.  Why B wrong: GH promotes fat mobilization and catabolism per the page.  Why C wrong: GH slows carbohydrate metabolism per the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) acts on which target organ?","options":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid hormone / thyroid gland","Mammary glands","Reproductive organs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) with target thyroid hormone; it stimulates synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormones.  Why A wrong: The adrenal gland is the target of ACTH.  Why C wrong: Mammary glands are the target of prolactin.  Why D wrong: Reproductive organs are targets of FSH and LH."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) has which target organ and action?","options":["Reproductive organs; stimulates ovulation","Adrenal gland; stimulates growth of the adrenal cortex and secretion of gluco-corticoids and slightly stimulates mineralocorticoid secretion","Thyroid gland; releases thyroxine","Mammary glands; produces milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists ACTH with target organ adrenal gland; it stimulates growth of the adrenal cortex, secretion of gluco-corticoids and slightly stimulates mineralocorticoid secretion.  Why A wrong: Reproductive actions belong to FSH/LH.  Why C wrong: Thyroid actions belong to TSH.  Why D wrong: Milk production is governed by prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) does all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Targets the reproductive organs","Stimulates primary graafian follicles to grow and develop","Stimulates follicle cells to secrete estrogen and development of seminiferous tubules and spermatogenesis","Forms the corpus luteum in ruptured follicles"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Forming the corpus luteum in ruptured follicles is an action of LH, not FSH.  Why A wrong: FSH targets reproductive organs per the page.  Why B wrong: FSH stimulates primary graafian follicles to grow and develop per the page.  Why C wrong: FSH stimulates follicle cells to secrete estrogen and the development of seminiferous tubules and spermatogenesis per the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Luteinizing hormone (LH) is required for which process and forms which structure?","options":["Required for ovulation; forms the corpus luteum in ruptured follicles to secrete progesterone","Required for milk let-down; forms mammary ducts","Required for water reabsorption; forms the loop of Henle","Required for melanin synthesis; forms melanosomes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states LH is required for ovulation, stimulates maturation of follicles and ovum, and forms the corpus luteum in ruptured follicles which then secretes progesterone.  Why B wrong: Milk let-down is oxytocin; milk production is prolactin.  Why C wrong: Water reabsorption is ADH; the loop of Henle is anatomical, not hormone-formed.  Why D wrong: Melanin synthesis is governed by MSH."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In males, LH is also called which hormone, and what does it stimulate?","options":["Interstitial cell-stimulating hormone (ICSH); stimulates the testes to secrete testosterone","Antidiuretic hormone; stimulates water retention","Melanocyte-stimulating hormone; stimulates pigmentation","Prolactin; stimulates milk production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in males LH is called interstitial cell-stimulating hormone (ICSH) and stimulates the testes to secrete testosterone.  Why B wrong: ADH governs water retention, not LH/ICSH.  Why C wrong: MSH governs pigmentation.  Why D wrong: Prolactin governs milk production."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Prolactin (PRL) targets which organ and primarily does what?","options":["Adrenal gland; secretes cortisol","Mammary glands; promotes breast development during pregnancy, initiates milk production after delivery and stimulates progesterone secretion by the corpus luteum","Thyroid gland; releases thyroxine","Kidneys; reabsorbs water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists prolactin with target organ mammary glands; it promotes breast development during pregnancy, initiates milk production after delivery and stimulates progesterone secretion by the corpus luteum.  Why A wrong: Cortisol secretion is governed by ACTH.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine release is governed by TSH.  Why D wrong: Water reabsorption is governed by ADH."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH/Vasopressin) from the posterior lobe acts on which organs and produces what effect?","options":["Kidneys, sweat glands and circulatory system; increases water reabsorption by distal and collecting tubules and causes vasoconstriction raising blood pressure","Mammary glands; ejects milk","Thyroid; releases thyroxine","Adrenal cortex; secretes aldosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ADH acts on the kidneys, sweat glands and circulatory system, increasing water reabsorption by distal and collecting tubules and stimulating vasoconstriction to raise blood pressure.  Why B wrong: Milk ejection is an oxytocin effect.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine release is a TSH effect.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone secretion is an adrenal cortex (ACTH-influenced) action."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"ADH is usually secreted by the hypothalamus and stored in which structure?","options":["Anterior lobe of the pituitary gland","Posterior lobe of the pituitary gland","Pineal gland","Adrenal medulla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ADH is usually secreted by the hypothalamus and stored in the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland.  Why A wrong: The anterior lobe produces its own hormones (GH, TSH, etc.), not stored ADH.  Why C wrong: The pineal gland produces melatonin.  Why D wrong: The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Oxytocin secreted from the posterior lobe has which actions?","options":["Stimulates contraction by the pregnant uterus and milk ejection from the alveoli of lactating breasts into ducts","Stimulates melanin synthesis","Promotes protein anabolism","Stimulates the thyroid gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oxytocin stimulates contraction by the pregnant uterus and milk ejection from the alveoli of lactating breasts into ducts.  Why B wrong: Melanin synthesis is an MSH action.  Why C wrong: Protein anabolism is a GH action.  Why D wrong: Thyroid stimulation is a TSH action."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Oxytocin is usually secreted by the hypothalamus and stored in which structure?","options":["Anterior lobe of the pituitary gland","Posterior lobe of the pituitary gland","Thymus","Pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oxytocin is usually secreted by the hypothalamus and stored in the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland (like ADH).  Why A wrong: The anterior lobe produces other hormones, not stored oxytocin.  Why C wrong: The thymus produces thymosin/thymopoietin/thymulin.  Why D wrong: The pancreas secretes insulin and glucagon."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH) primarily:","options":["Stimulates synthesis and dispersion of melanin in the skin, causing darkening","Increases water reabsorption","Stimulates uterine contraction","Promotes fat catabolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MSH stimulates synthesis and dispersion of melanin in the skin, causing darkening.  Why B wrong: Water reabsorption is an ADH action.  Why C wrong: Uterine contraction is an oxytocin action.  Why D wrong: Fat catabolism is a GH action."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Diabetes insipidus (DI) is defined as:","options":["Excess insulin secretion","Deficiency or hyposecretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) of the posterior pituitary gland, resulting in decreased reabsorption of water in the nephrons of the kidneys","Excess secretion of ADH","Deficiency of growth hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines DI as deficiency or hyposecretion of ADH of the posterior pituitary, resulting in decreased reabsorption of water in the kidney nephrons.  Why A wrong: Insulin deficiency is diabetes mellitus, not DI.  Why C wrong: Excess ADH is SIADH, the opposite condition.  Why D wrong: GH deficiency causes dwarfism/hypopituitarism, not DI."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is listed as the MOST COMMON cause of diabetes insipidus?","options":["Trauma","Insufficient secretion of ADH","Pituitary tumor","Autoimmune disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists trauma as the most common cause of diabetes insipidus.  Why B wrong: Insufficient secretion of ADH is a listed cause but not labeled the most common.  Why C wrong: Pituitary tumor is a listed cause but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Autoimmune is a listed cause but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Polyuria in diabetes insipidus is described as excessive urination with urine output of approximately:","options":["1-2 litres per day","5-10 litres per day","20-25 litres per day","less than 400 ml per day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes polyuria in DI as excessive urination or urine output of 20-25 litres of urine.  Why A wrong: 1-2 L/day is normal output, not the DI value cited.  Why B wrong: The page specifies 20-25 L, not 5-10 L.  Why D wrong: Less than 400 ml/day is oliguria, the opposite of DI."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In diabetes insipidus, \"polyuria passed in 24 hours\" is described as a:","options":["Late sign","Cardinal sign","Negative sign","Inconsistent finding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels urine passed in 24 hours (polyuria) as a cardinal sign of DI.  Why A wrong: It is a cardinal (hallmark) sign, not described as late.  Why C wrong: It is a positive cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: It is consistent/cardinal, not inconsistent."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which clinical feature of diabetes insipidus describes the urine as dilute with a specific gravity of 1.001-1.005 or less?","options":["Polyuria","Dilute urine","Polydipsia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes dilute urine in DI with a specific gravity of 1.001-1.005 or less.  Why A wrong: Polyuria refers to excessive volume, not the specific gravity value.  Why C wrong: Polydipsia is excessive thirst.  Why D wrong: Hypernatremia is the raised serum sodium feature, not the specific gravity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Polydipsia, a clinical feature of diabetes insipidus, refers to:","options":["Excessive urination","Excessive desire of thirst","Excessive sodium loss","Excessive sweating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines polydipsia as excessive desire/thirst.  Why A wrong: Excessive urination is polyuria.  Why C wrong: Sodium loss is not the definition of polydipsia.  Why D wrong: Excessive sweating is not polydipsia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In diabetes insipidus, the inability to concentrate urine leads to which serum sodium change?","options":["Increased serum sodium (hypernatremia)","Decreased serum sodium (hyponatremia)","No change in serum sodium","Increased serum potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states inability to concentrate urine results in increased serum sodium level (hypernatremia) in DI.  Why B wrong: Hyponatremia is characteristic of SIADH (water intoxication), the opposite condition.  Why C wrong: Sodium does change, rising due to water loss.  Why D wrong: Potassium change is not the cited feature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A key nursing care measure for a client with diabetes insipidus is to:","options":["Restrict all fluids strictly","Monitor vital signs, fluids and electrolyte status and replace fluids as per order","Administer insulin","Encourage a high-potassium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists DI nursing care as monitoring vital signs, fluids and electrolyte status and replacing fluids as per order.  Why A wrong: Fluids should be replaced, not strictly restricted, given the large losses.  Why C wrong: Insulin is for diabetes mellitus, not DI.  Why D wrong: A high-potassium diet is not part of the stated DI nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"When teaching a client with diabetes insipidus about medication, the nurse should instruct the client to:","options":["Take medication at any convenient time","Take medication at the ordered right time and check daily weight with the same scale","Stop the medication once thirst improves","Double the dose if urine output increases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the nurse should teach the client to take medication at the ordered right time and check daily weight with the same scale.  Why A wrong: Timing should be the ordered right time, not arbitrary.  Why C wrong: Stopping medication on symptom improvement is not advised.  Why D wrong: Self-doubling the dose is not part of the teaching."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Besides medication timing and daily weight, what additional parameter should the nurse monitor in a client with diabetes insipidus?","options":["Signs of polyuria, water retention, hyponatremia and urine specific gravity","Only blood glucose","Only respiratory rate","Only stool frequency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the nurse should monitor signs of polyuria, water retention, hyponatremia and urine specific gravity in DI.  Why B wrong: Blood glucose alone is not the cited DI monitoring focus.  Why C wrong: Respiratory rate alone is not the cited focus.  Why D wrong: Stool frequency is not the cited focus."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"ADH (vasopressin) acts on the renal tubules to reabsorb water and make the urine more concentrated, thereby reducing urine output. This process is best described as:","options":["Diuresis","Osmoregulation","Vasodilation","Glycogenolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes ADH acting on renal tubules to reabsorb water, producing concentrated urine and reduced output, which is termed osmoregulation.  Why A wrong: Diuresis is increased urine output, the opposite effect.  Why C wrong: ADH causes vasoconstriction (not vasodilation) on the vascular side.  Why D wrong: Glycogenolysis is glucose-related and unrelated to ADH water handling."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is a listed clinical feature of dehydration?","options":["Increased skin turgor","Poor or decreased skin turgor, altered mental sensorium, dry mucous membranes, elevated temperature and heart rate, hypotension (usually postural) and muscle pain & headache","Bradycardia and moist mucous membranes","Hypertension and bounding pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists dehydration features as poor/decreased skin turgor, altered mental sensorium, dry mucous membranes, elevated temperature and heart rate, hypotension (usually postural) and muscle pain & headache.  Why A wrong: Skin turgor is decreased, not increased, in dehydration.  Why C wrong: Heart rate rises and mucous membranes are dry, not moist.  Why D wrong: Hypotension (not hypertension) is the listed feature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which organ is identified as the osmoregulatory organ of the human body?","options":["Liver","Kidneys","Heart","Lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" box states the osmoregulatory organ of the human body is the kidneys.  Why A wrong: The liver is not the osmoregulatory organ named.  Why C wrong: The heart is not the osmoregulatory organ.  Why D wrong: The lungs are not the osmoregulatory organ."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The osmoregulatory centre is located in which part of the brain?","options":["Cerebellum","Anterior hypothalamus","Medulla oblongata","Occipital lobe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" box states the osmoregulatory centre is located in the anterior hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: The cerebellum coordinates movement, not osmoregulation.  Why C wrong: The medulla oblongata controls vital reflexes such as respiration.  Why D wrong: The occipital lobe processes vision."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"What does the abbreviation SIADH stand for?","options":["Syndrome of Increased Adrenal Diuretic Hormone","Syndrome of Inappropriate Anti-Diuretic Hormone","Secretion of Insufficient Antidiuretic Hormone","Syndrome of Internal Adrenal Hyperfunction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the SIADH full form is Syndrome of Inappropriate Anti-Diuretic Hormone.  Why A wrong: It does not stand for \"Increased Adrenal Diuretic Hormone.\"  Why C wrong: It is not \"insufficient\" ADH (that resembles DI).  Why D wrong: It does not relate to adrenal hyperfunction."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"SIADH is best described as:","options":["Excessive secretion of ADH hormone leading to fluid retention or water intoxication and dilutional hyponatremia","Deficiency of ADH leading to polyuria","Excess insulin secretion","Adrenal cortex atrophy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SIADH is excessive secretion of ADH hormone leading to fluid retention or water intoxication and dilutional hyponatremia.  Why B wrong: ADH deficiency with polyuria describes diabetes insipidus, the opposite condition.  Why C wrong: Insulin excess is unrelated to SIADH.  Why D wrong: Adrenal cortex atrophy is unrelated to SIADH."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of SIADH?","options":["Trauma (most common), tumor, haemorrhage or stroke and infection","Insufficient ADH secretion","Excessive water loss only","Adrenal androgen excess"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists SIADH causes as trauma (most common), tumor, and haemorrhage or stroke and infection.  Why B wrong: Insufficient ADH causes DI, not SIADH.  Why C wrong: SIADH involves water retention, not loss.  Why D wrong: Adrenal androgen excess is unrelated to SIADH causes listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A clinical feature of SIADH reflecting fluid volume overload includes which of the following?","options":["Jugular vein distension, weight gain and crackles on auscultation of the lungs","Poor skin turgor and dry mucous membranes","Polyuria of 20-25 litres","Hypotension and weight loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a clinical feature of SIADH is signs of fluid volume overload such as jugular vein distension, weight gain and crackles on auscultation of the lungs.  Why B wrong: Poor turgor/dry membranes are dehydration (DI) features.  Why C wrong: Massive polyuria is a DI feature, not SIADH.  Why D wrong: SIADH causes weight gain and fluid overload, not loss/hypotension as the hallmark."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with SIADH notes changes in level of consciousness (LOC), mental status, and possible seizures and coma. These reflect which underlying problem?","options":["Dehydration","Hyponatremia/water intoxication","Hypernatremia","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SIADH causes water intoxication and dilutional hyponatremia; the page lists changes in LOC, mental status, sometimes seizures and coma occurring (linked to reduced/altered sodium).  Why A wrong: Dehydration is the DI picture, not SIADH.  Why C wrong: SIADH causes hyponatremia, not hypernatremia.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the cited mechanism for SIADH neurological changes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"In SIADH, urine output and serum sodium typically show which pattern?","options":["Reduced urine output and decreased serum sodium level leading to dilutional hyponatremia","Increased urine output and increased serum sodium","Reduced urine output and increased serum sodium","Increased urine output and decreased serum sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SIADH shows reduced urine output and decreased serum sodium level leading to dilutional hyponatremia.  Why B wrong: Urine output is reduced and sodium is decreased, not increased.  Why C wrong: Serum sodium is decreased, not increased, in SIADH.  Why D wrong: Urine output is reduced (water retained), not increased."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Other clinical features of SIADH include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Decreased deep tendon reflexes","Tachycardia","Weight gain, hypertension","Massive polyuria with very dilute urine"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Massive polyuria with very dilute urine is a feature of diabetes insipidus, the opposite of SIADH; the page lists SIADH features as decreased deep tendon reflexes, tachycardia, weight gain and hypertension.  Why A wrong: Decreased deep tendon reflexes is a listed SIADH feature.  Why B wrong: Tachycardia is a listed SIADH feature.  Why C wrong: Weight gain and hypertension are listed SIADH features."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which nursing intervention is appropriate for a client with SIADH?","options":["Keep NPO, check all vitals, monitor fluid and electrolyte status, give hypertonic fluids (usually 3% NS) and institute seizure precautions to prevent injury","Encourage free water intake","Administer large volumes of hypotonic fluids","Withhold all sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists SIADH nursing care as keeping NPO, checking all vitals, monitoring fluid/electrolyte status, monitoring for altered LOC (provide a safe space if LOC decreased), giving diuretics and hypertonic fluids (usually 3% NS) and instituting seizure precautions to prevent injury.  Why B wrong: Free water worsens water intoxication/hyponatremia in SIADH.  Why C wrong: Hypotonic fluids worsen dilutional hyponatremia; hypertonic (3% NS) is used.  Why D wrong: Sodium is replaced (hypertonic saline), not withheld, in SIADH."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The adrenal glands are also known by which name, reflecting their location on top of both kidneys?","options":["Suprarenal glands","Hypophysis","Epiphysis","Parathyroid glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the adrenal glands are also known as suprarenal glands, are small triangular-shaped glands located on top of both kidneys.  Why B wrong: Hypophysis is the pituitary gland.  Why C wrong: Epiphysis is the pineal gland.  Why D wrong: The parathyroid glands are located on the thyroid, not atop the kidneys."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The outer region of the adrenal gland is known as the cortex and the inner region is known as the:","options":["Capsule","Medulla","Cortex","Hilum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the outer region of the adrenal gland is the cortex and the inner region is known as the medulla.  Why A wrong: The capsule is the outer covering, not the inner region.  Why C wrong: The cortex is the outer region, not the inner.  Why D wrong: The hilum is where vessels enter, not the inner gland region named."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla secrete which two catecholamines?","options":["Epinephrine and norepinephrine","Insulin and glucagon","Cortisol and aldosterone","Estrogen and progesterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla secrete two catecholamines, epinephrine and norepinephrine (stress hormones).  Why B wrong: Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic hormones.  Why C wrong: Cortisol and aldosterone are adrenal cortex hormones.  Why D wrong: Estrogen and progesterone are gonadal hormones."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Epinephrine, secreted by the adrenal medulla, is responsible for which response?","options":["Rest and digest","Fight, flight and fright response (as part of the stress response)","Milk ejection","Water reabsorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states epinephrine is responsible for the fight, flight and fright response as part of the stress response.  Why A wrong: \"Rest and digest\" is the parasympathetic response, not epinephrine's role described.  Why C wrong: Milk ejection is an oxytocin function.  Why D wrong: Water reabsorption is an ADH function."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"The adrenal cortex is divided into how many regions (zones)?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the adrenal cortex has three regions: zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata and zona reticularis.  Why A wrong: There are three, not two, zones.  Why C wrong: There are three, not four, zones.  Why D wrong: There are three, not five, zones."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which zone of the adrenal cortex secretes mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone and corticosterone?","options":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis","Adrenal medulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone) and corticosterone.  Why B wrong: The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids (cortisol/cortisone).  Why C wrong: The zona reticularis secretes androgens (testosterone).  Why D wrong: The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines, not mineralocorticoids."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which zone of the adrenal cortex secretes glucocorticoids such as cortisol and cortisone?","options":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis","Zona nervosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids, e.g., cortisol and cortisone.  Why A wrong: The zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids.  Why C wrong: The zona reticularis secretes androgens.  Why D wrong: \"Zona nervosa\" is not an adrenal cortex zone."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Integumentary system rapid-revision facts…","question":"Which zone of the adrenal cortex secretes androgens such as testosterone (in greater amount in males and lesser in females)?","options":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis","Adrenal medulla"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the zona reticularis secretes androgens, e.g., testosterone, in males and in lesser quantity in females.  Why A wrong: The zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids.  Why B wrong: The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids.  Why D wrong: The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The adrenal medulla produces which catecholamines?","options":["Aldosterone and cortisol","Epinephrine and norepinephrine","Testosterone and estrogen","T3 and T4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adrenal medulla produces the catecholamines epinephrine (adrenaline) and norepinephrine (noradrenaline) in response to stress.  Why A wrong: Aldosterone and cortisol are adrenal cortex hormones.  Why C wrong: Sex steroids are mainly gonadal; adrenal androgens are weak.  Why D wrong: T3 and T4 are thyroid hormones."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Epinephrine is produced by the medulla of the adrenal gland and is secreted by the neurons of which structure of the brain?","options":["Cerebellum","Thalamus","Chromaffin cells","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine is secreted by neurons (chromaffin cells) of the adrenal medulla under stress; only small amounts are secreted normally.  Why A/B/D wrong: These brain structures do not directly secrete the bulk of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla; the chromaffin cells of the medulla are the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Epinephrine plays an essential role in the \"fight-or-flight\" response. Which mechanism describes its cardiac effect?","options":["It decreases blood flow to muscles","It increases blood flow to muscles, heart output by acting on the SA node, and dilates the pupil","It causes bronchial smooth muscle contraction","It lowers blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the fight-or-flight response, epinephrine increases blood flow to muscles, increases heart output by acting on the SA node, and dilates the pupil.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases, blood flow to muscles.  Why C wrong: Epinephrine causes bronchial smooth muscle relaxation/dilation, not contraction.  Why D wrong: Catecholamines raise blood glucose by stimulating glucagon."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Catecholamines from the adrenal medulla break down which substance to produce glucose, raising blood glucose level?","options":["Glycogen","Insulin","Albumin","Calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catecholamines stimulate breakdown of glycogen to produce glucose, increasing blood glucose level.  Why B wrong: Insulin lowers glucose and is not broken down by catecholamines.  Why C/D wrong: Albumin and calcium are unrelated to this glucose-raising mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which effects are produced by catecholamine release from the adrenal medulla? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increase oxygen and carbon dioxide production","Increase blood concentration of free fatty acids through lipolysis in adipose tissue","Decrease force and rate of cardiac contraction","Cause constriction of most blood vessels, raising blood pressure to shunt blood to vital organs","Inhibit contractions of gastrointestinal and uterine smooth muscle"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Catecholamines increase O2 and CO2 production, increase free fatty acids via lipolysis, cause vasoconstriction of most vessels (increasing BP to shunt blood to vital organs), and inhibit GI/uterine smooth muscle contraction.  Why C wrong: Catecholamines INCREASE the force and rate of cardiac contraction, not decrease it."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which adrenal medulla effect is correctly paired with bronchial smooth muscle?","options":["Constricts bronchial smooth muscle","Dilates bronchial smooth muscle","Has no effect on bronchi","Fills bronchi with fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine significantly dilates bronchial smooth muscle (bronchodilation), which is why it is used in asthma/anaphylaxis.  Why A wrong: It dilates, not constricts.  Why C/D wrong: It does have an effect — bronchodilation, not fluid filling."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The adrenal cortex secretes mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgens. The chief mineralocorticoid is:","options":["Cortisol","Aldosterone","Corticosterone","Testosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone is the principal mineralocorticoid (and corticosterone is listed as another).  Why A wrong: Cortisol is a glucocorticoid.  Why C wrong: Corticosterone is listed but aldosterone is the chief mineralocorticoid.  Why D wrong: Testosterone is an androgen."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which is a function of mineralocorticoids secreted by the adrenal cortex? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintain plasma volume","Promote sodium reabsorption by kidney tubules","Promote potassium excretion by kidney tubules","Decrease antidiuretic hormone secretion and decrease potassium loss","Maintain bone density"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Mineralocorticoids maintain plasma volume, promote sodium reabsorption by the kidney tubules, and promote potassium excretion by the kidney tubules.  Why D wrong: The source states they INCREASE ADH secretion and DECREASE potassium loss is not listed; the function is potassium excretion (loss), so this option is incorrect.  Why E wrong: Bone density maintenance is not a listed mineralocorticoid function."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Mineralocorticoids increase secretion of which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Thyroxine","Calcitonin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mineralocorticoids increase secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH).  Why A/C/D wrong: Insulin, thyroxine, and calcitonin are not increased by mineralocorticoids."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Glucocorticoids of the adrenal cortex have which metabolic effects? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Accelerate hepatic gluconeogenesis (hyperglycemic effect)","Accelerate breakdown of protein and fat","Decrease formation of fibroblasts (anti-inflammatory effect)","Increase reabsorption of sodium","Lower the basal metabolic rate of the body"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids accelerate hepatic gluconeogenesis (hyperglycemic), accelerate breakdown of protein and fat, slow proliferation of fibroblasts producing anti-inflammatory effects, and increase reabsorption of sodium.  Why E wrong: Lowering basal metabolic rate is not a listed glucocorticoid effect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Glucocorticoid secretion is stimulated mildly by accelerated sodium and water reabsorption by the kidney tubules, and primarily by which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)","Aldosterone","Growth hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoid secretion is mainly stimulated by adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH).  Why A/C/D wrong: Insulin, aldosterone, and growth hormone do not primarily drive glucocorticoid secretion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which of the following are examples of glucocorticoids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Dexamethasone","Betamethasone","Methylprednisolone","Aldosterone","Testosterone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Long-acting (dexamethasone, betamethasone), intermediate-acting (methylprednisolone), and short-acting hydrocortisone are listed examples of glucocorticoids.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid.  Why E wrong: Testosterone is an androgen."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Adrenal cortex androgens are best described as:","options":["Strong sex hormones equal to gonadal hormones","Weak sex hormones that contribute to development and maintenance of male and female secondary sexual characteristics","Hormones that have no biological function","Hormones secreted only in females"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adrenal androgens are weak sex hormones (e.g., testosterone in males and small amounts in females) that contribute to development and maintenance of male and female secondary sexual characteristics.  Why A wrong: They are weak, not strong/equal to gonadal hormones.  Why C wrong: They do have a function in secondary sexual characteristics.  Why D wrong: They are secreted in both males and females."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Cushing syndrome is caused by which underlying hormonal abnormality?","options":["Hyposecretion of adrenal cortex hormones","Hypersecretion of adrenal cortex hormones (glucocorticoids)","Deficiency of thyroid hormone","Excess parathyroid hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cushing syndrome is excessive secretion of adrenal cortex hormones (glucocorticoids).  Why A wrong: Hyposecretion describes Addison's disease.  Why C/D wrong: These describe thyroid and parathyroid disorders, not Cushing syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Cushing disease specifically refers to ACTH-dependent metabolic disorder caused by increased secretion of endogenous cortisol due to increased amounts of ACTH secreted by which structure?","options":["Adrenal cortex tumor","Adrenal medulla","Anterior pituitary adenoma","Thyroid gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cushing disease is an ACTH-dependent disorder due to increased ACTH secreted by an anterior pituitary adenoma.  Why A wrong: That describes Cushing syndrome caused by chronic excessive cortisol from an adrenal source.  Why B/D wrong: The medulla and thyroid are not the source in Cushing disease."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Cushing syndrome (not Cushing disease) is caused by chronic excessive production of which hormone?","options":["Aldosterone","Cortisol","ACTH from the pituitary","Catecholamines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cushing syndrome is caused by chronic excessive production of cortisol; possible causes include the longer-time exogenous administration of glucocorticoids for therapeutic purpose.  Why A wrong: Excess aldosterone is Conn's syndrome.  Why C wrong: ACTH-driven excess from the pituitary defines Cushing disease.  Why D wrong: Excess catecholamines describe pheochromocytoma."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are clinical features of Cushing syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Generalized muscle wasting and weakness, with weight gain","Moon face and \"buffalo hump\" (cardinal sign)","Truncal obesity with thin arms and legs (cardinal sign)","Reddish-purple streaks on the breast, abdomen, and upper thigh","Hirsutism (abnormal growth of hair in females)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed features are characteristic of Cushing syndrome: muscle wasting/weakness with weight gain, moon face & buffalo hump, truncal obesity with thin extremities, reddish-purple striae on breast/abdomen/upper thigh, and hirsutism.  (No incorrect options.)"},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Cushing syndrome, the patient develops amenorrhoea due to which mechanism?","options":["Excess of vasodilation","Excessive metabolism","Hypertension due to vasoconstriction","Increased chance of getting infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amenorrhoea in Cushing syndrome is due to excessive metabolism per the source.  Why A/C wrong: Vasoconstriction/hypertension explains the hypertension feature, not amenorrhoea.  Why D wrong: Increased infection risk is a separate feature, not the cause of amenorrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hyperglycemia in Cushing syndrome occurs due to:","options":["Excessive insulin secretion","Excessive metabolism","Decreased gluconeogenesis","Loss of glucocorticoid effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperglycemia in Cushing syndrome is due to excessive metabolism (excess glucocorticoid driving gluconeogenesis).  Why A wrong: Excess cortisol opposes insulin; it is not excess insulin.  Why C wrong: Gluconeogenesis is increased, not decreased.  Why D wrong: It results from excess glucocorticoid, not loss of effect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hypernatremia in Cushing syndrome leads to which complications?","options":["Loss of sodium and water","Sodium and water retention resulting in hypertension and edema","Hyperkalemia and dehydration","Hypocalcemia and tetany"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypernatremia (from excess mineralocorticoid effect) causes sodium and water retention, resulting in hypertension and edema.  Why A wrong: Sodium is retained, not lost.  Why C wrong: Cushing causes hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: Hypocalcemia/tetany relate to parathyroid disorders."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Cushing syndrome, hypokalemia develops due to which mechanism?","options":["Potassium retention","Sodium-potassium inverse action","Calcium excretion via urine","Increased ACTH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia in Cushing syndrome is due to the sodium-potassium inverse action (sodium retained, potassium excreted).  Why A wrong: Potassium is lost, not retained.  Why C wrong: Calcium excretion causes hypocalcemia, a separate finding.  Why D wrong: ACTH level relates to etiology, not the direct cause of hypokalemia here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hypocalcemia in Cushing syndrome occurs due to:","options":["Excessive vitamin D","Excretion of calcium via urine (hypercalciuria)","Increased calcium absorption","Parathyroid stimulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcemia occurs due to excretion of calcium via urine (hypercalciuria).  Why A/C wrong: These would raise serum calcium, not lower it.  Why D wrong: Parathyroid stimulation is not the listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Elevated cortisol levels in Cushing syndrome can also cause which condition affecting the bones?","options":["Osteoporosis, which leads to fractures","Osteopetrosis","Increased bone density","Paget's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Elevated cortisol causes osteoporosis, which leads to fractures.  Why B/C wrong: Cortisol excess decreases bone density, not increases it.  Why D wrong: Paget's disease is unrelated to cortisol excess in this context."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which is a nursing care priority for a patient with Cushing syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor vital signs, especially blood pressure, daily weight, and I/O blood glucose level","Protect from infections, since visitors should not be allowed inside the client's room who have active infection","Reduce stressful events and exogenous corticosteroids","Encourage a high-protein diet with sodium restriction","Offer potassium-rich foods on physician's order"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed nursing measures: monitor BP/weight/I&O/blood glucose; protect from infection (no visitors with active infection); reduce stressful events and exogenous corticosteroids; offer high-protein diet with sodium restriction; and provide potassium-rich foods per physician's order.  (No incorrect options.)"},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Cushing syndrome, the nurse should be alert that if lesions are present, the client may experience pus formation, since gamma knife surgery with a single high-dose intervention is offered. Which patient education point relates to the high-protein diet?","options":["Offer a high-protein diet with sodium restriction","Offer unlimited salt intake","Restrict all protein","Encourage fasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to offer a high-protein diet with sodium restriction.  Why B wrong: Sodium is restricted, not unlimited.  Why C wrong: Protein is encouraged (high-protein), not restricted.  Why D wrong: Fasting is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"According to the comparison table, which finding belongs to Addison's disease rather than Cushing's syndrome?","options":["Generalized muscle wasting and weakness","Lethargy, fatigue, and muscle weakness","Moon face and buffalo hump","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lethargy, fatigue, and muscle weakness are listed under Addison's disease.  Why A/C/D wrong: Generalized muscle wasting/weakness, moon face & buffalo hump, and hyperglycemia are Cushing's syndrome findings."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the Addison's vs Cushing's comparison, weight changes are correctly paired as:","options":["Weight loss in Addison's; weight gain in Cushing's","Weight gain in Addison's; weight loss in Cushing's","Weight loss in both","Weight gain in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's disease causes weight loss, whereas Cushing's syndrome causes weight gain.  Why B wrong: It is the reverse.  Why C/D wrong: The two disorders cause opposite weight changes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Regarding potassium and sodium, the comparison table shows that Addison's disease causes ____ while Cushing's syndrome causes ____.","options":["Hypokalemia and hypernatremia; hyperkalemia and hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia; hypokalemia and hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia and hypernatremia; hypokalemia and hyponatremia","Hypokalemia and hyponatremia; hyperkalemia and hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's = hyperkalemia and hyponatremia; Cushing's = hypokalemia and hypernatremia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse or mismatch the correct electrolyte patterns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, blood pressure findings are:","options":["Postural hypotension in Addison's; hypertension in Cushing's","Hypertension in both","Hypotension in both","Hypertension in Addison's; hypotension in Cushing's"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's disease causes postural hypotension, whereas Cushing's causes hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mispair the blood pressure findings."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Regarding skin pigmentation in the comparison table, Addison's disease classically presents with:","options":["Pale, fragile skin","Hyperpigmentation (bronzing) of the skin, especially with primary disease","No skin changes","Reddish-purple striae only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's disease causes hyperpigmentation (bronzed skin), especially in primary disease, whereas Cushing's causes fragile skin that easily bruises with reddish-purple striae on abdomen and upper limbs.  Why A/D wrong: Fragile skin and reddish-purple striae are Cushing's features.  Why C wrong: There are definite skin changes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Before performing a dexamethasone suppression test (DST), the nurse understands the test measures the response of which glands to ACTH?","options":["Thyroid glands","Adrenal glands","Parathyroid glands","Pituitary gland only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DST measures the response of the adrenal glands to ACTH.  Why A/C wrong: It does not measure thyroid or parathyroid response.  Why D wrong: It assesses the pituitary-adrenal axis via adrenal response, not the pituitary alone."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the dexamethasone suppression test, dexamethasone is given to suppress secretion of which hormone, and cortisol levels then decrease in response to its administration?","options":["Cortisol","Insulin","Aldosterone","Calcitonin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone is given, and cortisol level decreases in response to administration; this test is used to diagnose Cushing syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: The DST evaluates cortisol suppression, not insulin, aldosterone, or calcitonin."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The dexamethasone suppression test is primarily used to diagnose which disorder?","options":["Addison's disease","Cushing syndrome","Pheochromocytoma","Hypothyroidism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DST is used in the diagnosis of Cushing syndrome.  Why A wrong: Addison's disease is an insufficiency, diagnosed differently.  Why C wrong: Pheochromocytoma is diagnosed by urinary catecholamines/VMA.  Why D wrong: Hypothyroidism is diagnosed by thyroid profile."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which nursing instructions apply to the 24-hour urine specimen and dietary management for an adrenal-disorder workup? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Collect 24-hour urine specimen and avoid visitors with active infection","Offer a high-protein and high-carbohydrate diet with low sodium intake","Encourage unrestricted sodium intake","Restrict all fluids during collection"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs collecting a 24-hour urine specimen, avoiding visitors with active infection, and offering a high-protein & high-carbohydrate diet with low sodium intake.  Why C wrong: Sodium is restricted (low sodium), not unrestricted.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction during collection is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The drug of choice for an Addisonian crisis is:","options":["Insulin","Hydrocortisone","Levothyroxine","Propranolol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocortisone is the drug of choice in Addisonian crisis.  Why A wrong: Insulin treats hyperglycemia, not adrenal insufficiency crisis.  Why C wrong: Levothyroxine treats hypothyroidism.  Why D wrong: Propranolol is a beta-blocker used in thyroid storm/pheochromocytoma symptom control."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Addisonian crisis is acute adrenal insufficiency that can be triggered by which stressors? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Infection","Surgery","Pregnancy","Dehydration","Abrupt stopping of exogenous corticosteroids"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Addisonian crisis is acute adrenal insufficiency caused by stressors such as infection, surgery, pregnancy, dehydration, emotional disturbances, and abrupt stopping of exogenous corticosteroids.  (No incorrect options.)"},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Signs of Addisonian crisis include severe headache, abdominal pain, irritability, anxiety, confusion, and which additional symptom?","options":["Hypertension and shock recovery","Severe hypotension and shock","Bradycardia with hypertension","Excessive weight gain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of crisis include severe headache, abdominal pain, irritability, anxiety, confusion, severe hypotension and shock.  Why A wrong: There is hypotension and shock, not recovery from shock.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the described crisis presentation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Addison's disease is best defined as:","options":["Hypersecretion of adrenal cortex hormones","Hyposecretion of adrenal cortex hormones such as mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids","Excess catecholamine secretion","Excess thyroid hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's disease is hyposecretion of adrenal cortex hormones such as mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids.  Why A wrong: That describes Cushing.  Why C wrong: Excess catecholamines describe pheochromocytoma.  Why D wrong: Excess thyroid hormone describes hyperthyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the most common cause of Addison's disease (destruction of cortex)?","options":["Bacterial infection","Autoimmune destruction of the cortex","Radiation therapy","Adrenal tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's disease is due to destruction of the cortex, commonly due to autoimmune disease.  Why A/C/D wrong: While other causes exist, the source attributes destruction of the cortex to autoimmune cause."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which is a clinical feature/electrolyte imbalance of Addison's disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hyponatremia due to inability to re-absorption of water","Hyperkalemia due to lack of excretion","Hypoglycemia due to lack of adrenaline hormone","Hypernatremia due to decreased reabsorption capacity of renal tubules"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's causes hyponatremia (inability to reabsorb water), hyperkalemia (lack of excretion), and hypoglycemia (lack of adrenaline/cortisol hormone).  Why D wrong: Addison's causes HYPOnatremia from decreased reabsorption capacity of renal tubules, not hypernatremia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which additional clinical features of Addison's disease are noted? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Fatigue and weakness","Impotence in men and irregular menses in females","Increased bronze pigmentation of skin (hyperpigmentation)","Hypertension and weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Addison's features include fatigue and weakness, impotence in men and irregular menses in females, and increased bronze pigmentation (hyperpigmentation) of skin.  Why D wrong: Addison's causes hypotension and weight loss, not hypertension and weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Nursing care for Addison's disease includes monitoring all vitals strictly and I/O charts plus a sign of which electrolyte imbalances?","options":["Sign of K+ and Na+ imbalance","Sign of calcium and phosphate imbalance","Sign of magnesium and chloride imbalance","Sign of glucose and protein imbalance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring all vitals strictly, keeping I/O charts, and watching for signs of K+ and Na+ imbalance.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies potassium and sodium imbalance, not these other pairings."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"When caring for an Addison's patient, the nurse carefully assesses for increase in temperature and which other parameters? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Dehydration","Alteration in pulse rate and rhythm (hyperkalemia)","Alteration in blood pressure (cool extremities)","Hyperglycemia requiring insulin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse assesses for increase in temperature, dehydration, alteration in pulse rate and rhythm (hyperkalemia), and alteration in blood pressure (cool extremities).  Why D wrong: Addison's causes hypoglycemia, not hyperglycemia; insulin is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The cornerstone of Addison's disease management is hormone replacement therapy (HRT). Glucocorticoids are given to correct which imbalances?","options":["Metabolic imbalances","Electrolyte imbalances","Calcium imbalances","Acid-base only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids correct metabolic imbalances, while mineralocorticoids correct electrolyte imbalances and alteration in blood pressure.  Why B wrong: Electrolyte imbalances are corrected by mineralocorticoids.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specified roles of glucocorticoids in this context."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Addison's HRT, mineralocorticoids are administered to correct:","options":["Metabolic imbalances","Electrolyte imbalances and alteration in blood pressure","Hyperglycemia","Bone density loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mineralocorticoids correct electrolyte imbalances and alteration in blood pressure.  Why A wrong: Glucocorticoids correct metabolic imbalances.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specified mineralocorticoid roles."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Conn's syndrome is defined as:","options":["Hyposecretion of aldosterone","Hypersecretion of aldosterone (primary hyperaldosteronism)","Excess cortisol secretion","Deficiency of parathyroid hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conn's syndrome is primary hyperaldosteronism — excessive secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex (adrenal gland).  Why A wrong: It is hyper-, not hyposecretion.  Why C wrong: Excess cortisol describes Cushing's.  Why D wrong: PTH deficiency describes hypoparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Conn's syndrome, hypersecretion of aldosterone is due to over-activation of which system?","options":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS)","Sympathetic nervous system","Hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis","Parasympathetic system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Excess aldosterone in Conn's is due to activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and ACTH, which cause sodium retention and excess potassium and hydrogen ion excretion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These systems are not the cited driver of aldosterone hypersecretion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The most common cause of Conn's syndrome is:","options":["Adenoma of adrenal cortex","Pituitary tumor","Thyroid carcinoma","Renal artery stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of Conn's syndrome is adenoma of the adrenal cortex; another cause is hyperplasia and carcinoma of the cortex of the adrenal gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed primary cause of Conn's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are clinical features of Conn's syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Headache","Fatigue, muscle weakness, and cramping","Polyuria and polydipsia","Symptoms related to hypertension","Hypotension and bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include headache, fatigue/muscle weakness/cramping, polyuria & polydipsia, and symptoms related to hypertension.  Why E wrong: Conn's causes hypertension (from sodium retention), not hypotension/bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In Conn's syndrome, the patient develops hypertension due to which mechanism?","options":["RAAS activation","Decreased sodium retention","Loss of aldosterone","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension in Conn's is due to RAAS (renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system) activation causing sodium retention.  Why B wrong: Sodium retention is increased, not decreased.  Why C wrong: Aldosterone is in excess, not lost.  Why D wrong: Conn's causes hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia, due to potassium excretion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Conn's syndrome causes hypokalemia due to which mechanism?","options":["Decreased sodium retention","Excessive excretion of potassium","Hydrogen ion retention","Decreased urinary aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia in Conn's is due to excessive excretion of potassium.  Why A wrong: Sodium retention is increased.  Why C wrong: Hydrogen ion is excreted (metabolic alkalosis), not retained.  Why D wrong: Urinary aldosterone is increased, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which acid-base disturbance occurs in Conn's syndrome and by what mechanism?","options":["Metabolic acidosis due to hydrogen ion retention","Metabolic alkalosis due to excessive hydrogen ion excretion","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conn's causes metabolic alkalosis due to excessive hydrogen ion excretion.  Why A wrong: It is alkalosis, not acidosis, and H+ is excreted, not retained.  Why C/D wrong: The disturbance is metabolic, not respiratory."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Nursing care for Conn's syndrome includes monitoring vital signs (especially blood pressure), I/O, specific gravity, and signs of which electrolyte imbalances?","options":["Hypokalemia and signs of hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia and hypernatremia","Hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia","Hypomagnesemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring BP, I/O, specific gravity, and signs of hypokalemia and signs of hypocalcemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-listed monitoring parameters for Conn's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"If Conn's syndrome is caused by a tumor, the nurse should prepare the patient for which procedure?","options":["Thyroidectomy","Unilateral or bilateral adrenalectomy","Parathyroidectomy","Hypophysectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If Conn's is due to a tumor, the nurse prepares the patient for unilateral or bilateral adrenalectomy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These remove the thyroid, parathyroid, or pituitary, not the adrenal tumor."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which medication group is given to a patient with adrenal disorder to decrease blood pressure by potassium-sparing diuretics, and to control blood pressure / aldosterone? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Potassium-sparing diuretics (decrease blood pressure)","Aldosterone antagonist to control blood pressure","Calcium channel blockers to reduce production of aldosterone","Loop diuretics to increase potassium loss"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Administered medicines include potassium-sparing diuretics (decrease BP), aldosterone antagonist (control BP), and calcium channel blockers (reduce production of aldosterone).  Why D wrong: Loop diuretics increasing potassium loss are not listed; potassium-sparing diuretics are used."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Pheochromocytoma is best described as:","options":["Deficiency of catecholamine secretion","Excessive secretion of catecholamines (e.g., adrenaline and nor-adrenaline)","Excess aldosterone secretion","Deficiency of cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pheochromocytoma involves excessive secretion of catecholamines, e.g., adrenaline and noradrenaline.  Why A wrong: It is excess, not deficiency.  Why C wrong: Excess aldosterone is Conn's.  Why D wrong: Cortisol deficiency relates to Addison's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Pheochromocytoma is caused by catecholamine-secreting tumors that originate in the adrenal medulla. These tumors are usually:","options":["Malignant","Benign","Metastatic","Cystic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The catecholamine-secreting tumors that originate in the adrenal medulla are usually benign.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are usually benign, not malignant, metastatic, or cystic, per the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma involving 24-hour urine collection?","options":["Detection of vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)","Serum cortisol","Dexamethasone suppression test","Serum TSH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adrenalectomy is the choice of treatment; laboratory diagnosis is by a 24-hour urine collection for detection of vanillylmandelic acid (VMA).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the urinary diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which is the cardinal sign of pheochromocytoma, which can lead to hypertensive nephropathy and neuropathy?","options":["Hypotension","Hypertension","Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is the cardinal sign of pheochromocytoma and leads to hypertensive nephropathy and neuropathy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the cardinal sign described."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"A serious complication of pheochromocytoma is heart failure, due to which platelet/vascular event?","options":["Increased platelet aggregation and shock","Decreased platelet count","Hypocoagulable state","Pulmonary embolism only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heart failure in pheochromocytoma is associated with increased platelet aggregation and shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed complication mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are clinical features of pheochromocytoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Severe hypertension (usually malignant >180/100 mm Hg)","Severe headache, palpitation, tachycardia, chest pain","Heat intolerance, diaphoresis, and tremors","Hyperglycemia and dilated pupils","Decreased level of catecholamines"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include severe hypertension (usually malignant, >180/100 mm Hg), severe headache/palpitation/tachycardia/chest pain, heat intolerance/diaphoresis/tremors, hyperglycemia and dilated pupils, and increased level of catecholamines.  Why E wrong: Catecholamine level is INCREASED, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Nursing care for pheochromocytoma includes monitoring all vitals (especially BP), assessing for signs of hypertensive crisis, and which drug is the choice for hypertensive crisis?","options":["Sodium nitroprusside","Hydrocortisone","Levothyroxine","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium nitroprusside is the drug of choice for hypertensive crisis in pheochromocytoma nursing care.  Why B wrong: Hydrocortisone is for Addisonian crisis.  Why C wrong: Levothyroxine treats hypothyroidism.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is used for hypocalcemia/tetany."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"A nursing intervention specific to pheochromocytoma to support the patient through hypertensive episodes is to:","options":["Initiate general nursing care","Encourage strenuous activity","Restrict all fluids","Withhold all antihypertensives"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring vitals, assessing for hypertensive crisis, having sodium nitroprusside ready, and initiating general nursing care.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed and could worsen the patient's condition."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) is a product of catecholamine metabolism. Its elevation in urine indicates:","options":["Hypothyroidism","Pheochromocytoma","Cushing syndrome","Conn's syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VMA is a product of catecholamine metabolism, so its elevation indicates pheochromocytoma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not diagnosed by elevated urinary VMA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the normal level of VMA in urine?","options":["≥6 mg/dl","<6 mg/dl (i.e., more than 6 indicates pheochromocytoma)","10–20 mg/dl","0 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal level of VMA in urine is <6 mg/dl; more than 6 indicates pheochromocytoma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal value."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The thyroid gland is located in the neck just below which structure?","options":["Hyoid bone","Cricoid cartilage","Mandible","Sternum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thyroid gland is located in the neck just below the cricoid cartilage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the structure cited for thyroid location."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The thyroid gland is located in the front of the larynx and trachea at the level of which vertebrae?","options":["5th, 6th, and 7th cervical & 1st thoracic","1st, 2nd, and 3rd cervical","2nd, 3rd, and 4th thoracic","7th cervical only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The thyroid is located at the level of the 5th, 6th, and 7th cervical and 1st thoracic vertebrae.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated vertebral level."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The shape of the thyroid gland is described as:","options":["Triangular","A typical vascular butterfly shape","Spherical","Oval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thyroid gland has a typical vascular butterfly shape.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described shape."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The two lobes of the thyroid gland lie on the side of the trachea and are connected with each other by which structure?","options":["Septum","Isthmus","Capsule","Pyramidal lobe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two lobes of the thyroid gland lie on the side of the trachea and are connected with isthmus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not connect the two thyroid lobes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which gland synthesizes and stores its own secretion, located on the back surface of the thyroid gland?","options":["Adrenal gland","Parathyroid gland","Pituitary gland","Pineal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thyroid only synthesizes & stores its own secretion (and stores it on its back surface), in proximity to the parathyroid glands, which are located on the back surface of the thyroid gland.  Why A/C/D wrong: These glands are not located on the back of the thyroid."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The thyroid gland secretes T3 (tri-iodothyronine), T4 (thyroxine), and which hormone from its parafollicular (C-cells) that maintains bone calcium level and metabolic rate?","options":["Insulin","Calcitonin","Parathormone","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thyroid gland secretes T3, T4, and from its parafollicular cells (C-cells) it secretes calcitonin hormone, which maintains bone calcium level and metabolic rate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not secreted by thyroid C-cells."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Needle aspiration of thyroid tissue (biopsy) should be done with the client positioned in which position with the neck hyperextended?","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Needle aspiration of thyroid tissue (biopsy) is done with the client in supine position with the neck hyperextended; a pillow may be placed under the patient's shoulder.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not used for thyroid needle aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Thyroid gland examinations should be done in which position?","options":["Semi-Fowler's position with neck slightly extended","High-Fowler's with neck flexed","Supine with neck flexed","Lithotomy position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid gland examinations should be done in semi-Fowler's position with neck slightly extended.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not indicated for thyroid examination."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Goiter is best described as:","options":["Enlargement of thyroid gland","Enlargement of parathyroid gland","Shrinking of thyroid gland","Inflammation of larynx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Goiter is an enlargement of the thyroid gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe goiter."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the most common cause of goiter?","options":["Excess iodine","Deficiency of iodine","Autoimmune destruction","Radiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of goiter is deficiency of iodine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the most common cause cited."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Goiter can be seen in both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism. The treatment for goiter (hypothyroid type) includes:","options":["Iodine supplements","Radioactive iodine ablation only","Beta-blockers","Antibiotics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment of goiter includes iodine supplements (correcting iodine deficiency).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the treatment listed for goiter from iodine deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hypothyroidism is defined as:","options":["Hypersecretion of thyroid hormone","Hyposecretion of thyroid hormone (T3 & T4), resulting in a decreased metabolic rate","Excess catecholamine secretion","Deficiency of parathyroid hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism is hyposecretion of thyroid hormone (T3 & T4), resulting in decreased body's metabolic rate.  Why A wrong: That describes hyperthyroidism.  Why C/D wrong: These describe pheochromocytoma and hypoparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are causes of hypothyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Congenital thyroid deficits","Low synthesis of thyroid hormone","Autoimmune diseases","Excess iodine intake only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypothyroidism include congenital thyroid deficits, low synthesis of thyroid hormone, and autoimmune diseases.  Why D wrong: Excess iodine intake is not the listed cause of hypothyroidism here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Cretinism is a classification of hypothyroidism in children, usually present from:","options":["Adolescence","Birth (congenital)","Middle age","Old age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cretinism is hypothyroidism in children, usually present from birth.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is congenital, present from birth."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Lymphocytic thyroiditis is a classification of hypothyroidism in which the body's immune system attacks thyroid glands that may be damaged. It is usually seen between which age group of children?","options":["1–4 years","5–20 years","20–40 years",">40 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lymphocytic thyroiditis occurs between 5–20 years age group of children, where the immune system attacks thyroid glands (Hashimoto disease).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated age range."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Myxedema is a classification of hypothyroidism in adults. It is a severe degree of failure of thyroid gland, sometimes associated with which thyroid hormone (T3, T4) finding?","options":["Decreased level of thyroid hormone T3, T4","Increased level of thyroid hormone","Normal thyroid hormone level","Absent TSH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myxedema is hypothyroidism in adults, a severe degree of failure of thyroid gland associated with decreased level of thyroid hormone T3, T4.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described decreased thyroid hormone level."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Decreased basal metabolic rate (BMR) in myxedema affects lipid metabolism. This leads to increased cholesterol & triglycerides level in the blood and as well as effect of RBC's production, leading to:","options":["Polycythemia","Anaemia","Leukemia","Thrombocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased BMR raises cholesterol and triglycerides and affects RBC production, leading to anaemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated outcome on RBC production."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are clinical features of hypothyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Weight gain, intolerance to cold, lethargy, and fatigue","Bradycardia and constipation","Generalized puffiness and oedema usually around the eyes (periorbital oedema)","Pale & dry skin and diminished hearing, forgetfulness & loss of memory","Tachycardia and weight loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism features include weight gain/cold intolerance/lethargy/fatigue, bradycardia and constipation, generalized periorbital oedema, and pale/dry skin with diminished hearing and memory loss/forgetfulness.  Why E wrong: Tachycardia and weight loss are hyperthyroid features, not hypothyroid."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hypothyroidism causes which feature related to facial/skin appearance?","options":["Lack of facial expression, dry & brittle hair & nails","Excessive sweating","Warm moist skin","Heat intolerance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes lack of facial expression, dry & brittle hair & nails.  Why B/C/D wrong: Excessive sweating, warm moist skin, and heat intolerance are hyperthyroid features."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In hypothyroidism, T3, T4 levels and BMR are:","options":["Increased","Decreased","Unchanged","T3 increased, T4 decreased"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Menstrual disturbances and decreased T3 & T4 level and BMR are features of hypothyroidism.  Why A/C/D wrong: Both T3/T4 and BMR are decreased in hypothyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are nursing care measures for hypothyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor all vitals strictly and instruct the client about avoiding OTC drugs unless HCP verified","Provide a warm environment for the client due to sensitivity to cold in winter season","Use moisturizers for dry skin","Restrict more calories, cholesterol, and fat in diet to prevent weight gain","Encourage cold exposure to stimulate metabolism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring vitals, advising to avoid OTC drugs unless HCP-verified, providing a warm environment (cold sensitivity in winter), using moisturizers for dry skin, and restricting more calories/cholesterol/fat to prevent weight gain.  Why E wrong: Cold exposure is not recommended; these patients are cold-sensitive and need warmth."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hormone replacement therapy for hypothyroidism most commonly uses which thyroid replacement medications? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Levothyroxine","Liothyronine","Propylthiouracil","Methimazole"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: To give optimal medications such as thyroid replacement therapy, levothyroxine (most commonly used) and liothyronine are used.  Why C/D wrong: Propylthiouracil (PTU) and methimazole are antithyroid drugs used in hyperthyroidism, not replacement."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Myxedema coma is a condition that:","options":["Is rarely serious and self-limiting","Is rarely occurring but very serious & fatal","Has no precipitating factors","Only occurs in children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myxedema coma is rarely occurring but very serious and fatal.  Why A wrong: It is serious and fatal, not mild.  Why C wrong: It has precipitating factors (stressful situations, surgery, etc.).  Why D wrong: It is described in adults with severe hypothyroidism, not exclusively children."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Myxedema coma is precipitated by which factors? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stressful situations","Sudden withdrawal of thyroid medications","Anaesthesia and surgery","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Myxedema coma is precipitated by stressful situations, sudden withdrawal of thyroid medications, anaesthesia and surgery, and hypothermia.  (No incorrect options.)"},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are clinical features of myxedema coma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypothermia, hypotension, hyponatremia","Hypoglycemia, hypoventilation","Bradycardia and generalized oedema","Progressive loss of consciousness & coma","Hyperthermia and tachycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include hypothermia/hypotension/hyponatremia, hypoglycemia/hypoventilation, bradycardia and generalized oedema, and progressive loss of consciousness & coma.  Why E wrong: Hyperthermia and tachycardia are not features of myxedema coma (these are hyperthyroid/thyroid storm features)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are nursing care measures for myxedema coma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintain a patent airway (first priority)","Position the client in side-lying position to prevent aspiration","Give levothyroxine, corticosteroids, and glucose IV or as per order","Keep the client warm and maintain temperature hourly","Force oral fluids despite decreased consciousness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes maintaining a patent airway (first priority), positioning side-lying to prevent aspiration, giving IV levothyroxine/corticosteroids/glucose per order, and keeping the client warm and maintaining temperature hourly.  Why E wrong: Forcing oral fluids in a patient with decreased consciousness risks aspiration and is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hyperthyroidism is a condition in which excessive concentration or production of thyroid hormones in blood occurs as a result of which mechanism?","options":["Decreased metabolic rate","Increased level of thyroid TSH only","Excessive thyroid hormone causing an increased metabolic rate","Iodine deficiency"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperthyroidism is characterized by an increased metabolic rate due to excessive thyroid hormone production.  Why A wrong: Decreased metabolic rate describes hypothyroidism.  Why B wrong: The hallmark is excess thyroid hormone causing increased metabolism, not TSH alone.  Why D wrong: Iodine deficiency causes goiter/hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Thyrotoxicosis is described as a condition that occurs due to excessive thyroid hormone, which (endogenous & exogenous) later it will become hyperthyroidism. Which best defines thyrotoxicosis?","options":["Excess thyroid hormone (endogenous & exogenous) leading to hyperthyroidism","Deficiency of thyroid hormone","Excess parathyroid hormone","Excess catecholamines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyrotoxicosis is a condition due to excess thyroid hormone (endogenous & exogenous) that later becomes hyperthyroidism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other endocrine disorders, not thyrotoxicosis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Goiter in hyperthyroidism refers to enlargement of thyroid gland due to:","options":["Iodine deficiency","Lack of iodine","Excessive thyroid hormone production / overstimulation","Autoimmune destruction with low hormone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In hyperthyroidism, goiter is enlargement of thyroid gland due to lack of iodine (the source notes goiter as enlargement related to thyroid dysfunction in the hyperthyroid context). Per the source listing, goiter = enlargement of thyroid gland due to lack of iodine. [Note: the source labels this point ambiguously; the factually accurate basis for goiter is iodine-related/overstimulation enlargement — keyed to enlargement due to thyroid dysfunction.]  Why A/B wrong: While iodine deficiency causes goiter generally, in the hyperthyroid Graves' context the enlargement is from overstimulation; the source's intent is enlargement of the gland.  Why D wrong: Autoimmune destruction with low hormone describes hypothyroid goiter, not the hyperthyroid context."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism, an autoimmune disease also known as toxic diffuse goiter?","options":["Hashimoto's disease","Grave's disease","Cretinism","Myxedema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of hyperthyroidism is Grave's disease, an autoimmune disease also known as toxic diffuse goiter.  Why A wrong: Hashimoto's disease causes hypothyroidism.  Why C/D wrong: Cretinism and myxedema are hypothyroid conditions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are causes of hyperthyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Grave's disease","Toxic thyroid adenoma and nodular goiter","Inflammation of the thyroid gland","Taking excessive exogenous thyroid hormone","Iodine deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hyperthyroidism include Grave's disease, toxic adenoma and nodular goiter, inflammation of the thyroid gland, and taking excessive exogenous thyroid hormone.  Why E wrong: Iodine deficiency is the cause of goiter/hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of hyperthyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Weight loss, diarrhoea or loose stools, hyperactive reflexes","Restlessness, nervousness, and fine tremors of the hands","Irritability, agitation, and emotional liability","Diaphoresis and exophthalmos (protrusion of eyeballs)","Cold intolerance and constipation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperthyroidism signs & symptoms include weight loss/diarrhoea or loose stools/hyperactive reflexes, restlessness/nervousness/fine tremors, irritability/agitation/emotional liability, and diaphoresis with exophthalmos.  Why E wrong: Cold intolerance and constipation are hypothyroid features; hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance and diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Additional signs and symptoms of hyperthyroidism include corneal ulceration, hypertension, and which other findings? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hyperthermia or heat intolerance, tachycardia and hyperventilation","Enlarged thyroid gland","Decreased TSH cause primary or thyroid diseases","Decreased BMR"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperthyroidism includes hyperthermia/heat intolerance/tachycardia/hyperventilation, enlarged thyroid gland, and decreased TSH (primary thyroid disease).  Why D wrong: BMR is increased in hyperthyroidism, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In hyperthyroidism, an increased TSH level indicates which type of disease?","options":["Primary thyroid disease","Secondary thyroid disease or pituitary disorder","Parathyroid disease","Adrenal disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased TSH level is due to secondary thyroid or pituitary disorder, while decreased TSH indicates primary thyroid disease.  Why A wrong: Primary thyroid disease shows decreased TSH.  Why C/D wrong: These are not thyroid-axis disorders described here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Thyrotoxic state or thyroid storm in hyperthyroidism may lead to which life-threatening complication?","options":["Heart failure (HF)","Diabetes mellitus","Renal failure","Liver cirrhosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyrotoxic state or thyroid storm may lead to heart failure (HF).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited complication of thyroid storm."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are nursing care measures for hyperthyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Provide adequate rest, cool & quiet environment","Provide a high-calorie diet, high-protein & high-carbohydrate diet","Take daily weight","Give ordered medications such as antithyroid medications (propylthiouracil/PTU)","Provide a warm environment and increase activity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes adequate rest with cool & quiet environment, high-calorie/high-protein/high-carbohydrate diet, daily weight, and ordered medications such as antithyroid medication propylthiouracil (PTU) which limits thyroid hormone synthesis.  Why E wrong: A warm environment and increased activity worsen heat intolerance and hypermetabolism; a cool environment and rest are needed."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Propylthiouracil (PTU), an antithyroid medication used in hyperthyroidism, acts by:","options":["Limiting/blocking the synthesis of thyroid hormone","Replacing thyroid hormone","Increasing iodine uptake","Stimulating TSH release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antithyroid medication propylthiouracil (PTU) limits the synthesis of thyroid hormone.  Why B wrong: It does not replace thyroid hormone (that is levothyroxine in hypothyroidism).  Why C/D wrong: It does not increase iodine uptake or stimulate TSH; it suppresses hormone synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Antithyroid medication iodine (potassium iodide/SSKI) is given in hyperthyroidism to:","options":["Increase vascularity of the thyroid","Reduce vascularity and size of thyroid gland","Replace thyroid hormone","Increase metabolic rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antithyroid medication iodine (potassium iodide/SSKI) reduces vascularity and size of the thyroid gland (used before surgery).  Why A wrong: It reduces, not increases, vascularity.  Why C/D wrong: It does not replace thyroid hormone or increase metabolism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Radioactive iodine (such as I-131) is given in hyperthyroidism to:","options":["Destroy thyroid gland cells, thereby decreasing production of thyroid hormone","Stimulate thyroid hormone production","Replace deficient thyroid hormone","Reduce calcium levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Radioactive iodine such as I-131 destroys thyroid gland cells, thereby decreasing production of thyroid hormone.  Why B/C/D wrong: It ablates thyroid tissue rather than stimulating, replacing hormone, or affecting calcium."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Symptomatic medicines and protecting the client from stressful situations are part of nursing care for which condition described as a threatening, fatal condition occurring in a client with uncontrollable hyperthyroidism?","options":["Myxedema coma","Thyroid storm","Addisonian crisis","Tetany"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid storm is the threatening, fatal condition occurring in a client with uncontrollable hyperthyroidism; nursing care includes symptomatic medicines and protecting the client from stressful situations.  Why A wrong: Myxedema coma is a hypothyroid emergency.  Why C wrong: Addisonian crisis is an adrenal emergency.  Why D wrong: Tetany is a hypocalcemic/parathyroid emergency."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are precipitating factors for thyroid storm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stressful situation","Severe infection","Excessive administration of thyroid hormones","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Precipitating factors of thyroid storm include stressful situation, severe infection, and excessive administration of thyroid hormones.  Why D wrong: Hypothermia is a precipitating factor for myxedema coma, not thyroid storm."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of thyroid storm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Systolic hypertension, tachycardia, and hyperthermia","Nausea, vomiting & diarrhoea, tremors & anxiety","Irritability, restlessness, confusion, seizures & coma","Bradycardia and hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid storm signs include systolic hypertension/tachycardia/hyperthermia, nausea/vomiting/diarrhoea/tremors/anxiety, and irritability/restlessness/confusion/seizures & coma.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia and hypothermia are myxedema coma findings, not thyroid storm."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are nursing care measures for thyroid storm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintain patent airway and all vitals strictly","Give ordered medications such as antithyroid, glucocorticoids, iodine, etc.","Avoid aspirin for temperature reduction because it also increases T3/T4 level","Provide sponging and cooling blanket prescribed to decrease temperature","Apply warming measures to raise temperature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for thyroid storm includes maintaining a patent airway and vitals, giving ordered antithyroid/glucocorticoids/iodine, avoiding aspirin (which raises T3/T4), and providing sponging and a cooling blanket to decrease temperature.  Why E wrong: Warming measures are contraindicated; these patients are hyperthermic and need cooling."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In thyroid storm, why should aspirin be avoided for temperature reduction?","options":["It causes hypoglycemia","It increases T3/T4 level","It causes bleeding","It lowers blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin should be avoided because it also increases the T3/T4 level (displaces thyroid hormone from binding proteins), worsening the storm.  Why A/C/D wrong: While aspirin has these effects, the source-cited reason here is increased T3/T4 level."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"From the Hypothyroidism vs Hyperthyroidism comparison, which pairing is correct for body weight?","options":["Weight loss in hypothyroidism; weight gain in hyperthyroidism","Weight gain in hypothyroidism; weight loss in hyperthyroidism","Weight gain in both","Weight loss in both"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes weight gain; hyperthyroidism causes weight loss.  Why A wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: The two conditions cause opposite weight changes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"From the comparison table, appetite findings are:","options":["Increased appetite & anorexia in hypothyroidism; decreased appetite in hyperthyroidism","Decreased appetite & anorexia in hypothyroidism; increased appetite & weight loss in hyperthyroidism","Anorexia in both","Increased appetite in both"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes decreased appetite & anorexia, whereas hyperthyroidism causes increased appetite & weight loss.  Why A wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source pairings."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, temperature/heat tolerance findings are:","options":["Cold intolerance & increased sensitivity to cold in hypothyroidism; heat intolerance & increased sensitivity to heat in hyperthyroidism","Heat intolerance in hypothyroidism; cold intolerance in hyperthyroidism","No temperature sensitivity in either","Both heat intolerant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism = heat intolerance? No — hypothyroidism causes cold intolerance (increased sensitivity to cold), while hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance (increased sensitivity to heat).  Why B wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: Both conditions have temperature sensitivity, in opposite directions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, the cardiac findings for hypothyroidism vs hyperthyroidism are:","options":["Bradycardia in hypothyroidism; tachycardia & palpitations in hyperthyroidism","Tachycardia in hypothyroidism; bradycardia in hyperthyroidism","Both bradycardia","Both tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, while hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia, palpitation, and arrhythmias.  Why B wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: The two conditions show opposite cardiac rates."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, the bowel findings are:","options":["Constipation in hypothyroidism; diarrhoea in hyperthyroidism","Diarrhoea in hypothyroidism; constipation in hyperthyroidism","Constipation in both","Diarrhoea in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes constipation, whereas hyperthyroidism causes diarrhoea.  Why B wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: The two conditions cause opposite bowel patterns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, the mental/mood findings are:","options":["Anxiety, nervousness, irritability, insomnia, tremors in hypothyroidism; depression in hyperthyroidism","Depression, impaired memory, impaired concentration, \"mental fog\" in hypothyroidism; anxiety, nervousness, irritability, insomnia, tremors in hyperthyroidism","Both cause depression","Both cause anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism causes depression, impaired memory, impaired concentration (\"mental fog\"), whereas hyperthyroidism causes anxiety, nervousness, irritability, insomnia, and tremors.  Why A wrong: This reverses the findings.  Why C/D wrong: The two conditions cause opposite mental states."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In the comparison table, the hair/skin findings are:","options":["Hair loss and thin/nails dry skin in hypothyroidism; increased hair and growth in hyperthyroidism","Increased hair and growth in hypothyroidism; hair loss and thin nails/dry skin in hyperthyroidism","Both have increased hair growth","Both have dry skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothyroidism shows increased hair and growth as well as hair loss/thin nails/dry skin features; per the source table, hair loss and thin nails/dry skin are listed for hypothyroidism while increased hair and growth appears with hyperthyroidism. (Source table pairs hair loss & dry skin with hypothyroidism.)  Why B wrong: This reverses the source pairing.  Why C/D wrong: The conditions differ in hair/skin findings."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"How many parathyroid glands are present in the human body, and where are they located?","options":["Total 2 parathyroid glands, located in the adrenal gland","Total 4 parathyroid glands, located on the back surface of thyroid lobes","Total 6 parathyroid glands, located in the chest","Total 8 parathyroid glands, located in the neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are a total of 4 parathyroid glands in the human body, located on the back surface (back side) of the thyroid lobes.  Why A/C/D wrong: The number and location do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"The parathyroid gland secretes parathyroid hormone (PTH), also known as:","options":["Calcitonin","Parathormone or parathormone (PTH)","Thyroxine","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The parathyroid gland secretes parathyroid hormone, also called parathormone (PTH).  Why A wrong: Calcitonin is secreted by thyroid C-cells.  Why C/D wrong: Thyroxine and aldosterone are thyroid and adrenal hormones, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Each parathyroid gland has two specific cells. The chief cells secrete which hormone, regulating blood calcium levels?","options":["Calcitonin","Parathormone (PTH)","Oxyphil cells' hormone (unknown function)","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chief cells secrete parathormone (PTH); oxyphil cells have an unknown function. PTH regulates blood calcium levels.  Why A/D wrong: Calcitonin and aldosterone are not secreted by parathyroid chief cells.  Why C wrong: Oxyphil cells have an unknown function, not the calcium-regulating role."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"What is the primary function of parathyroid hormone (PTH)?","options":["To maintain blood calcium level by osteoblast and indirectly osteoclast, increased absorption of calcium from the intestine and reabsorption from the kidneys, and to maintain blood calcium level","To lower blood calcium","To regulate sodium balance","To increase potassium excretion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PTH maintains blood calcium level by osteoblast and indirectly osteoclast activity, increases absorption of calcium from the intestine and reabsorption from the kidneys, and maintains blood calcium level.  Why B wrong: PTH raises blood calcium; calcitonin lowers it.  Why C/D wrong: PTH regulates calcium/phosphate, not primarily sodium or potassium."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Hypoparathyroidism is best described as:","options":["Excess secretion of parathyroid hormone","Insufficient or hyposecretion of parathyroid hormone of the parathyroid gland","Excess calcitonin","Excess aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoparathyroidism is insufficient or hyposecretion of parathyroid hormone of the parathyroid gland.  Why A wrong: Excess PTH is hyperparathyroidism.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated hormone excesses."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are causes of hypoparathyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["After thyroidectomy / accidental removal of parathyroid gland","Following parathyroid surgery","Radiation therapy & unknowingly","Excess iodine intake"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypoparathyroidism include after thyroidectomy/accidental removal of parathyroid gland, following parathyroid surgery, and radiation therapy & unknowingly.  Why D wrong: Excess iodine intake is not a listed cause of hypoparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In hypoparathyroidism, when serum parathyroid level drops, serum calcium level also drops, causing clinical sign of tetany. What happens to serum phosphate level?","options":["Serum phosphate decreases","Serum phosphate level increases (inversely rises in serum phosphate level)","Serum phosphate is unaffected","Serum phosphate becomes zero"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hypoparathyroidism, as serum calcium drops, serum phosphate level inversely rises (increases).  Why A/C/D wrong: Phosphate rises inversely with calcium, so it neither decreases, stays the same, nor becomes zero."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of hypoparathyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypocalcemia because parathyroid gland maintains serum calcium level","Hyperphosphatemia due to inverse effect of calcium & phosphate","Decreased serum parathyroid hormone level","Numbness & tingling in the face due to low calcium level","Hypercalcemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoparathyroidism signs include hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia (inverse effect), decreased serum PTH level, and numbness & tingling in the face due to low calcium.  Why E wrong: Hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which additional signs & symptoms occur in hypoparathyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Cramps seen in muscle, the abdomen, or in the extremities due to low calcium level","Hypotension, anxiety, irritability & depression","Respiratory alkalosis due to hyperventilation","Hyperphosphaturia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Additional signs include cramps in muscle/abdomen/extremities due to low calcium, hypotension/anxiety/irritability & depression, and respiratory alkalosis due to hyperventilation.  Why D wrong: Hyperphosphaturia is not a listed feature; in hypoparathyroidism, serum phosphate rises (hyperphosphatemia)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which are nursing care measures for hypoparathyroidism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check vitals and observe for respiratory distress","Monitor sign of hypocalcaemia and tetany, and follow seizure precautions","Keep a tracheostomy set, oxygen, and suction set at the client's bedside for 24 hours","Reduce environmental stimuli and administer calcium gluconate IV for hypocalcemic occurs","Administer high-phosphorus diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes checking vitals and observing for respiratory distress, monitoring for hypocalcaemia/tetany with seizure precautions, keeping a tracheostomy set/oxygen/suction at bedside for 24 hours, and reducing environmental stimuli while administering IV calcium gluconate when hypocalcemia occurs.  Why E wrong: A high-phosphorus diet is contraindicated; the source advises eliminating high-phosphorus foods."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Dietary teaching in hypoparathyroidism includes eliminating milk, cheese, and egg yolk because these items are high in:","options":["Calcium","Phosphorus","Sodium","Potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Milk, cheese, and egg yolk should be eliminated because these items have high phosphorus content (and phosphate is already elevated in hypoparathyroidism).  Why A/C/D wrong: The reason for elimination is high phosphorus content, not calcium, sodium, or potassium."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In hypoparathyroidism, aluminium hydroxide is administered to:","options":["Increase phosphate absorption","Decrease absorption of phosphorus from the GI tract","Raise serum potassium","Lower serum calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aluminium hydroxide is administered to decrease absorption of phosphorus from the GI tract.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, phosphate absorption.  Why C/D wrong: It targets phosphate, not potassium or calcium directly."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"In hypoparathyroidism, Vita-D supplements are given because Vita-D:","options":["Decreases absorption of calcium from the GI tract","Enhances the absorption of calcium from the GI tract","Lowers serum calcium","Has no effect on calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin D supplements enhance the absorption of calcium from the GI tract.  Why A wrong: It enhances, not decreases, calcium absorption.  Why C/D wrong: It raises serum calcium by improving absorption."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Phosphate binders are administered in hypoparathyroidism to:","options":["Increase phosphate absorption from the GI tract","Promote the excretion of phosphate from the GI tract","Raise serum phosphate","Lower serum calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phosphate binders are administered to promote the excretion of phosphate from the GI tract.  Why A/C wrong: They reduce phosphate, not increase its absorption or serum level.  Why D wrong: Their purpose targets phosphate, not directly lowering calcium."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Tetany is a metabolic condition caused by which serum calcium abnormality?","options":["Very high serum calcium (hypercalcemia)","Very low level of serum calcium (hypocalcemia)","Normal serum calcium","High phosphate alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tetany is a metabolic condition caused by very low level of serum calcium (hypocalcemia).  Why A wrong: Hypercalcemia does not cause tetany.  Why C/D wrong: Normal calcium or phosphate alone is not the cause of tetany."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Which features characterize tetany? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bronchospasm & laryngospasm (stridor & wheezing)","Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign","Dysphagia & photophobia","Numbness and tingling sensation of the face and extremities","Hypercalcemia with weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Tetany is characterized by bronchospasm & laryngospasm (stridor & wheezing), Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign, dysphagia & photophobia, and numbness & tingling sensation of the face and extremities.  Why E wrong: Tetany is from hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia; weight gain is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Sometimes severe tetany may also cause which cardiac and neurologic complications?","options":["Cardiac dysrhythmias & seizures","Hypertension only","Bradypnea only","Improved cardiac output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sometimes cardiac dysrhythmias & seizures are also noted in tetany.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the cited complications of severe tetany."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign are clinical signs used to assess for which electrolyte abnormality associated with tetany/hypoparathyroidism?","options":["Hypernatremia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign are characteristic of hypocalcemia, the cause of tetany in hypoparathyroidism.  Why A/C/D wrong: These signs are not used for sodium, potassium, or glucose abnormalities."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Adrenal gland hormones, Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease…","question":"A nurse caring for a postoperative thyroidectomy patient monitors closely for tetany. This is because thyroidectomy can accidentally remove or damage which glands, leading to hypocalcemia?","options":["Adrenal glands","Parathyroid glands","Pituitary gland","Pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After thyroidectomy, accidental removal of the parathyroid gland is a cause of hypoparathyroidism, leading to hypocalcemia and tetany.  Why A/C/D wrong: These glands are not removed during thyroidectomy in proximity to the thyroid."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Trousseau's sign, a test for hypocalcemia, is elicited by which of the following maneuvers?","options":["Tapping over the facial nerve just anterior to the ear","Inflation of a sphygmomanometer cuff above the systolic blood pressure for 3 minutes","Asking the patient to hyperventilate for 1 minute","Pressing on the patellar tendon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trousseau's sign is induced by inflating a sphygmomanometer cuff above the systolic blood pressure for 3 minutes, which produces carpopedal spasm in hypocalcemia.  Why A wrong: Tapping over the facial nerve anterior to the ear elicits Chvostek's sign, not Trousseau's.  Why C wrong: Hyperventilation is not the described maneuver for either sign.  Why D wrong: Pressing on the patellar tendon tests a deep tendon reflex, not calcium status."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A positive Trousseau's sign is characterized by carpopedal spasm. Which of the following are features of this spasm? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Adduction of the thumb","Flexion of the metacarpophalangeal joints","Extension of the interphalangeal joints","Flexion of the wrist","Plantar flexion of the foot only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Carpopedal spasm in a positive Trousseau's sign is characterized by adduction of the thumb, flexion of the metacarpophalangeal joints, extension of the interphalangeal joints, and flexion of the wrist.  Why E wrong: Plantar flexion of the foot alone is not listed as a feature of the carpopedal spasm described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Chvostek's sign is elicited by tapping the facial nerve just anterior to the ear. A positive response is indicated by which finding?","options":["Carpopedal spasm of the hand","Twitching of the facial muscles in response to tapping over the facial nerve","Inability to extend the elbow","Loss of patellar reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A positive Chvostek's sign is twitching of the ipsilateral facial muscles (twitching of the lip to spasm of all the facial muscles) in response to tapping over the facial nerve.  Why A wrong: Carpopedal spasm characterizes Trousseau's sign, not Chvostek's.  Why C wrong: Elbow extension is not assessed by Chvostek's sign.  Why D wrong: Loss of patellar reflex is unrelated to Chvostek's testing."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Hyperparathyroidism is best defined as:","options":["Hyposecretion or hypofunction of the parathyroid glands","Hypersecretion or hyperfunction of the parathyroid glands","Atrophy of the thyroid gland","Inflammation of the adrenal cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperparathyroidism is hypersecretion or hyperfunction of the parathyroid glands.  Why A wrong: Hyposecretion/hypofunction describes hypoparathyroidism, the opposite condition.  Why C wrong: Thyroid atrophy is a separate disorder.  Why D wrong: Adrenal cortex inflammation is unrelated to the parathyroids."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In hyperparathyroidism, the increased re-absorption of calcium is accompanied by which change in phosphorus handling by the kidneys?","options":["Increased re-absorption of phosphorus","Excretion of phosphorus by the kidneys","No change in phosphorus levels","Conversion of phosphorus to calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperparathyroidism causes increased re-absorption of calcium and excretion of phosphorus by the kidneys.  Why A wrong: Phosphorus is excreted, not re-absorbed, in this condition.  Why C wrong: Phosphorus levels do change (hypophosphatemia results).  Why D wrong: Phosphorus is not converted to calcium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Demineralization of bones in hyperparathyroidism occurs because dietary intake is not enough to meet calcium levels demanded by which mechanism?","options":["Low level of parathyroid hormone","High level of parathyroid hormone","Excess insulin secretion","Increased thyroxine release"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Demineralization of bones occurs when dietary intake is not enough to meet calcium levels demanded by the high level of parathyroid hormone.  Why A wrong: It is a high (not low) level of parathyroid hormone driving the demand.  Why C wrong: Insulin is not involved in this demineralization mechanism.  Why D wrong: Thyroxine is not the responsible hormone here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Hyperparathyroidism is classified into three types. Which type is caused by proliferation of the chief cells of the parathyroid glands (adenoma)?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Quaternary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary hyperparathyroidism is due to proliferation of chief cells of the parathyroid glands (adenoma).  Why B wrong: Secondary is due to chronic conditions causing hypocalcemia (renal disorders, malnutrition, vitamin D deficiency).  Why C wrong: Tertiary involves chronic conditions leading to parathyroid gland becoming unresponsive to blood calcium levels.  Why D wrong: There is no \"quaternary\" type listed."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is due to chronic conditions causing hypocalcemia. Which of the following are listed causes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Renal disorders","Malnutrition","Vitamin D deficiency","Parathyroid adenoma","Vitamin C excess"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary hyperparathyroidism is due to chronic conditions causing hypocalcemia such as renal disorders, malnutrition, and vitamin D deficiency.  Why D wrong: Parathyroid adenoma causes primary hyperparathyroidism.  Why E wrong: Vitamin C excess is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In tertiary hyperparathyroidism, chronic conditions lead to the parathyroid gland becoming:","options":["Hyper-responsive to calcium","Unresponsive to blood calcium levels","Completely atrophied","Replaced by thyroid tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tertiary hyperparathyroidism, chronic conditions lead to the parathyroid gland becoming unresponsive to blood calcium levels.  Why A wrong: It becomes unresponsive, not hyper-responsive, to calcium feedback.  Why C wrong: Atrophy is not the described change.  Why D wrong: The gland is not replaced by thyroid tissue."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are causes of hyperparathyroidism listed in the text? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tumor of the parathyroid gland or adenoma","Renal damage","Renal calculi that consist with calcium","Pituitary infarction","Hepatic cirrhosis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Listed causes are tumor of the parathyroid gland or adenoma, renal damage, and renal calculi that consist with calcium.  Why D wrong: Pituitary infarction is not a listed cause.  Why E wrong: Hepatic cirrhosis is not listed as a cause of hyperparathyroidism here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A patient with hyperparathyroidism is most likely to show which serum calcium and phosphorus pattern?","options":["Hypercalcemia with hyperphosphatemia","Hypercalcemia with hypophosphatemia","Hypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia","Hypocalcemia with hypophosphatemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms include hypercalcemia (due to increased level of parathyroid hormone) and hypophosphatemia (due to low level of calcium or bone phosphorus).  Why A, C, D wrong: These combinations do not match the hypercalcemia + hypophosphatemia pattern of hyperparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The \"deep bone pain\" seen in hyperparathyroidism is attributed to which underlying process?","options":["Increased calcium deposition in bone","The inversely related effect of calcium on phosphorus","Bacterial infection of the bone","Vitamin D toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deep bone pain is due to the inversely related effect of calcium and phosphorus.  Why A wrong: Hyperparathyroidism causes demineralization (loss), not deposition, of bone calcium.  Why C wrong: Infection is not the listed cause.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D toxicity is not cited as the cause of the deep bone pain."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Pathological fractures in hyperparathyroidism are primarily caused by:","options":["Demineralization of the bone","Excess calcium intake","Increased phosphorus re-absorption","Hypofunction of the parathyroid glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pathological fractures occur due to demineralization of the bone.  Why B wrong: Excess intake is not the mechanism; bone calcium is lost.  Why C wrong: Phosphorus is excreted, not re-absorbed, in this state.  Why D wrong: Hyperparathyroidism is hyperfunction, not hypofunction."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which additional signs and symptoms are associated with hyperparathyroidism? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Weight loss","Constipation","Muscular weakness","Increased appetite with weight gain","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists weight loss, constipation, and muscular weakness as signs and symptoms.  Why D wrong: Weight loss (not weight gain) is the listed finding.  Why E wrong: Hypertension is not listed among the signs and symptoms of hyperparathyroidism here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When monitoring a client with hyperparathyroidism, the nurse should monitor intake/output and assess for which complication?","options":["Renal stones","Pulmonary embolism","Hepatic encephalopathy","Retinal detachment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring intake/output and signs of renal stones (renal calculi are associated with hypercalcemia).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed monitoring priorities in the nursing care of hyperparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A client with hyperparathyroidism has elevated calcium levels. According to the nursing care guidelines, what should the nurse encourage regarding fluids and diet?","options":["Fluid restriction and high-calcium diet","Encourage fluid intake and restriction of dietary calcium intake","Encourage fluid restriction and high-phosphorus diet","Restrict all oral intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care directs the nurse to encourage fluid intake and restrict dietary calcium intake.  Why A wrong: Calcium should be restricted, not increased; fluids are encouraged, not restricted.  Why C wrong: Fluid restriction is the opposite of what is indicated.  Why D wrong: Total restriction of intake is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In the nursing care of hyperparathyroidism, furosemide is used for which purpose?","options":["To raise serum calcium","To decrease skeletal calcium release and increase renal excretion of calcium","To replace lost phosphorus","To control blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Furosemide is given to decrease skeletal calcium release and increase renal excretion of calcium.  Why A wrong: The goal is to lower, not raise, serum calcium.  Why C wrong: Furosemide does not replace phosphorus.  Why D wrong: Furosemide is a loop diuretic, not a glucose-lowering agent."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Calcitonin is administered in hyperparathyroidism nursing care primarily to:","options":["Increase renal calcium excretion only","Decrease skeletal calcium release and increase renal excretion of calcium","Increase bone resorption","Stimulate parathyroid hormone secretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcitonin is given to decrease skeletal calcium release and increase renal excretion of calcium.  Why A wrong: It does both — decreases skeletal release and increases renal excretion.  Why C wrong: Calcitonin decreases (not increases) bone resorption/release.  Why D wrong: Calcitonin does not stimulate parathyroid hormone secretion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which surgical preparation is part of the nursing care for hyperparathyroidism?","options":["Prepare for thyroidectomy","Prepare for parathyroidectomy accordingly","Prepare for adrenalectomy","Prepare for splenectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes preparing the client for parathyroidectomy accordingly.  Why A wrong: Thyroidectomy targets the thyroid, not the parathyroid glands.  Why C wrong: Adrenalectomy is for adrenal pathology.  Why D wrong: Splenectomy is unrelated to parathyroid disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The endocrine system of the pancreas is known as the:","options":["Acini","Islets of Langerhans","Duct of Wirsung","Brunner's glands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endocrine system of the pancreas is known as the islets of Langerhans.  Why A wrong: Acini are exocrine secretory units, not the endocrine system name.  Why C wrong: The duct of Wirsung is the main pancreatic duct.  Why D wrong: Brunner's glands are in the duodenum, not the pancreas."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Anatomically, the pancreas is divided into which three parts?","options":["Lobes, sinuses, and apex","Head, neck, and body","Cortex, medulla, and capsule","Fundus, antrum, and pylorus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anatomically the pancreas is divided into the head, neck, and body.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the divisions of the pancreas described in the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which part of the pancreas is the largest part?","options":["Head","Neck","Body","Tail"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The largest part of the pancreas is the body.  Why A, B, D wrong: The text identifies the body, not the head, neck, or tail, as the largest part."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Where are most of the islets of Langerhans abundantly found in the pancreas?","options":["Head of the pancreas","Neck of the pancreas","Tail of the pancreas","Main pancreatic duct"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Most of the islets of Langerhans cells are abundantly found in the tail of the pancreas.  Why A, B wrong: Although the head/neck are pancreatic parts, the text specifies the tail as where islets are abundant.  Why D wrong: The main pancreatic duct is a conduit, not a site of islet cells."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The main pancreatic duct, located in the common bile duct, is also known as the:","options":["Duct of Wirsung","Duct of Santorini","Common hepatic duct","Cystic duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The main pancreatic duct is the duct of Wirsung, located in the common bile duct.  Why B wrong: The duct of Santorini is the accessory pancreatic duct, not the main one.  Why C wrong: The common hepatic duct drains the liver.  Why D wrong: The cystic duct drains the gallbladder."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The pancreas is an example of which type of gland?","options":["Purely endocrine gland","Purely exocrine gland","Heterocrine gland (with both endocrine and exocrine functions)","Apocrine gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pancreas is an example of a heterocrine gland, having both endocrine (90%) and exocrine (10%) functions.  Why A, B wrong: It performs both functions, so it is neither purely endocrine nor purely exocrine.  Why D wrong: Apocrine refers to a type of secretion in sweat glands, not the pancreas."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which hormone, secreted by the alpha cells of the pancreas, accelerates blood glucose level?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Somatostatin","Amylin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alpha cells secrete glucagon hormone, which accelerates blood glucose level.  Why A wrong: Insulin (from beta cells) regulates/lowers blood glucose, secreted by beta cells.  Why C wrong: Somatostatin is secreted by delta cells.  Why D wrong: Amylin is not the alpha-cell hormone described here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Beta cells of the islets of Langerhans secrete which hormone that regulates blood glucose level?","options":["Glucagon","Insulin","Somatostatin","Cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta cells secrete insulin hormone, which regulates blood glucose level.  Why A wrong: Glucagon is from alpha cells.  Why C wrong: Somatostatin is from delta cells.  Why D wrong: Cortisol is an adrenal cortex hormone, not a pancreatic islet product."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Delta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Somatostatin","Gastrin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Delta cells secrete somatostatin.  Why A wrong: Insulin is from beta cells.  Why B wrong: Glucagon is from alpha cells.  Why D wrong: Gastrin is not the delta-cell secretion described here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following describes the exocrine function of the pancreas?","options":["Secretion of insulin into the blood","Secretion of pancreatic juice (digestive enzymes)","Secretion of glucagon into the blood","Secretion of somatostatin into the blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pancreas secretes pancreatic juice (exocrine function), which contains numerous digestive enzymes.  Why A, C, D wrong: Insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are endocrine (hormone) secretions, not exocrine functions."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which pancreatic enzymes are responsible for breaking down complex carbohydrates and proteins, respectively? (Match the function.)","options":["Amylase breaks down carbohydrates; trypsin/chymotrypsin/protease break down protein","Lipase breaks down protein; amylase breaks down fat","Trypsin breaks down carbohydrates; amylase breaks down protein","Protease breaks down fat; lipase breaks down carbohydrates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states trypsin/chymotrypsin/protease cause breakdown of protein, amylase breaks down complex carbohydrates, and lipase breaks down fats.  Why B wrong: Lipase breaks fat, not protein; amylase breaks carbohydrates, not fat.  Why C wrong: Trypsin breaks protein, not carbohydrates.  Why D wrong: Protease breaks protein and lipase breaks fat, not carbohydrates."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which enzyme in pancreatic juice is responsible for the breakdown of fats?","options":["Amylase","Lipase","Trypsin","Chymotrypsin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lipase breaks down fats.  Why A wrong: Amylase breaks down complex carbohydrates.  Why C, D wrong: Trypsin and chymotrypsin (proteases) break down proteins."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The secretion of pancreatic enzymes is regulated by which two hormones produced by the duodenum?","options":["Insulin and glucagon","Secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK)","Gastrin and histamine","Somatostatin and motilin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pancreatic juice enzymes are regulated by secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) hormones, which are produced by the duodenum.  Why A wrong: Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic islet hormones regulating glucose, not enzyme secretion regulators from the duodenum.  Why C, D wrong: These hormone pairs are not the ones identified for regulating pancreatic enzyme secretion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Insulin is a polypeptide hormone consisting of approximately how many amino acids?","options":["30 amino acids","50 amino acids","100 amino acids","150 amino acids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin is an anti-stress (anabolic) hormone consisting of about 50 amino acids.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the figure given in the text (about 50)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Insulin secretion is triggered by which change in blood glucose?","options":["Decreased blood glucose level","Increased blood glucose level","Normal blood glucose level only","Insulin is secreted independent of blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secretion of insulin is stimulated by increased blood sugar level.  Why A wrong: Decreased blood glucose stimulates glucagon, not insulin.  Why C, D wrong: Insulin secretion responds to (increased) blood glucose, not independently or only at normal levels."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are listed functions of insulin? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Glycogenesis — converts glucose into glycogen for storage in the liver and skeletal muscles","Lipogenesis — promotes synthesis of fatty acids and storage of fat in adipose tissues","Decreased breakdown of glycogen into glucose","Increased excretion of glucose by the kidneys","Protein synthesis — promotes synthesis of protein"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin functions include glycogenesis, lipogenesis, decreased breakdown of glycogen into glucose, and protein synthesis.  Why D wrong: Increased renal glucose excretion is not a listed insulin function."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Glucagon's main function is to:","options":["Decrease blood glucose level","Increase blood glucose level","Promote storage of glucose as fat","Stimulate protein synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon's main function is to increase blood glucose level (the opposite of insulin).  Why A wrong: Decreasing blood glucose is the function of insulin.  Why C wrong: Storing glucose as fat is an insulin (lipogenesis) action.  Why D wrong: Protein synthesis is an insulin function, not glucagon's main action."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Glucagon opposes the action of insulin. Which of the following are functions of glucagon listed in the text? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Inhibit lipogenesis (formation of new lipids in the liver)","Gluconeogenesis — promote formation of new glucose in the liver","Lipolysis — promote fat breakdown in adipose tissues","Glycogenolysis — conversion of glycogen into glucose in the liver","Promote synthesis of protein"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon functions oppose insulin: inhibit lipogenesis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and glycogenolysis (conversion of glycogen into glucose in the liver).  Why E wrong: Promoting protein synthesis is an insulin function; glucagon opposes insulin actions."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Glycogenolysis, a function of glucagon, refers to:","options":["Conversion of glucose into glycogen","Conversion of glycogen into glucose in the liver","Breakdown of fats into fatty acids","Synthesis of new glucose from amino acids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glycogenolysis is the conversion of glycogen into glucose in the liver.  Why A wrong: That describes glycogenesis (an insulin function).  Why C wrong: That describes lipolysis.  Why D wrong: That describes gluconeogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For the Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT), which pre-test instruction should the client follow regarding diet before the test?","options":["Eat a high-carbohydrate diet (at least 150 g of carbohydrate) for 3 days before the test","Fast completely for 3 days before the test","Eat only protein for 3 days before the test","Avoid all food for 1 week before the test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before 3 days of the test, eat a diet with high carbohydrate (at least 150 g of carbohydrate) for 3 days before the testing.  Why B, C, D wrong: These extreme fasting/restriction instructions are not the OGTT preparation described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"During the OGTT procedure, which substances must the client avoid before and during the test?","options":["Coffee, smoking, and alcohol for 36–48 hours before the testing","Water only for 12 hours","Salt and sugar for 1 week","Only fatty foods on the test day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states to avoid coffee, smoking, and alcohol for 36–48 hours before the testing.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the avoidance instructions specified for the OGTT."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In the OGTT procedure, how long should the client fast before the test, and how often is the blood sample taken after the glucose load?","options":["Keep the client fasting for 12 hours before the test; take samples for 4 hours","Keep the client fasting for 12 hours before the test; take samples for 2 hours","Keep the client fasting for 6 hours; take samples for 1 hour","No fasting; single sample only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure says keep the client fasting for 12 hours before the test and take samples for 4 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: These fasting durations/sampling intervals do not match the stated procedure."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For the OGTT, the client is given a glucose load. What amount of glucose is given and how is the sample collected?","options":["75 g sugar (glucose) given to the client orally; a sample taken for testing after 2 hours","50 g glucose given intravenously; sample after 1 hour","100 g glucose orally; sample after 30 minutes","25 g glucose orally; no sample needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sample is taken for testing after 75 g sugar of glucose is given to the client orally.  Why B, C, D wrong: These glucose amounts/routes/timings do not match the OGTT method described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"During the OGTT, after 2 hours the blood glucose level is interpreted. Which value range is considered NORMAL?","options":["Below 140 mg/dl","140–199 mg/dl","200 mg/dl and above","Below 70 mg/dl only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Result interpretation: after 2 hours post-load glucose level, Normal is below 140 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 140–199 mg/dl indicates pre-diabetes.  Why C wrong: 200 mg/dl and above indicates diabetes.  Why D wrong: Below 70 mg/dl is not the normal cut-off described for OGTT 2-hour value."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the OGTT 2-hour result, a value of 140–199 mg/dl indicates which category?","options":["Normal","Pre-diabetes","Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 2-hour OGTT value of 140–199 mg/dl indicates pre-diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is below 140 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is 200 mg/dl and above.  Why D wrong: This range is elevated, not hypoglycemic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the OGTT 2-hour result, which value indicates diabetes?","options":["Below 140 mg/dl","140–199 mg/dl","200 mg/dl and above","100–125 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A 2-hour OGTT value of 200 mg/dl and above indicates diabetes.  Why A wrong: Below 140 mg/dl is normal.  Why B wrong: 140–199 mg/dl is pre-diabetes.  Why D wrong: 100–125 mg/dl is not the OGTT diabetes cut-off described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A modified version of the OGTT used for diagnosis of gestational diabetes mellitus uses which glucose load?","options":["50 g glucose","75 g glucose","100 g glucose instead of 75 g","25 g glucose"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Oral glucose challenge test (OGCT) is used for diagnosis of gestational diabetes mellitus at 24–28 weeks, in which the client is given 100 g glucose instead of 75 g.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the 100 g load specified for the gestational diabetes challenge."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For the Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS) test, which condition must be met when the blood sample is taken?","options":["Sample taken in the empty stomach (in which blood sample is taken in empty stomach)","Sample taken 2 hours after a meal","Sample taken randomly at any time","Sample taken only after exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the fasting blood sugar level, the blood sample is taken in an empty stomach.  Why B wrong: A 2-hour post-meal sample is a post-prandial test, not fasting.  Why C wrong: A random sample is the random blood sugar test, not fasting.  Why D wrong: Post-exercise timing is not the FBS criterion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS) test, which value range is considered NORMAL?","options":["70–100 mg/dl","100–125 mg/dl",">100 mg/dl",">200 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Result interpretation: Normal fasting blood sugar is 70–100 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 100–125 mg/dl indicates pre-diabetes.  Why C, D wrong: These overlapping/higher ranges are not the FBS normal cut-off described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A fasting blood sugar of 100–125 mg/dl indicates which category?","options":["Normal","Pre-diabetes","Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fasting blood sugar of 100–125 mg/dl indicates pre-diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is 70–100 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is >100 mg/dl threshold per result interpretation (≥126 range), but the labeled pre-diabetes band is 100–125.  Why D wrong: This value is elevated, not hypoglycemic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the Fasting Blood Sugar test, a value classified as diabetes is:","options":["70–100 mg/dl","100–125 mg/dl",">100 mg/dl (diabetes range as labeled)","<70 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The result interpretation labels the diabetes category as >100 mg/dl in the FBS chart (the elevated category beyond the normal 70–100 band).  Why A wrong: 70–100 mg/dl is normal.  Why B wrong: 100–125 mg/dl is the pre-diabetes band.  Why D wrong: <70 mg/dl is hypoglycemia, not diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Random Blood Sugar (RBS) testing is also known as which of the following?","options":["Casual blood glucose (CBG) testing","Fasting blood glucose testing","Post-prandial testing","Glucose tolerance testing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Random blood sugar level testing is also known casual blood glucose (CBG) testing.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are distinct, defined glucose tests, not synonyms for random/casual blood sugar."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On a Random Blood Sugar (RBS) test, which value is considered NORMAL?","options":["Up to 140 mg/dl (Normal)","140–200 mg/dl",">200 mg/dl",">300 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For RBS, the Normal value is up to 140 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 140–200 mg/dl is the pre-diabetes range.  Why C wrong: >200 mg/dl indicates diabetes.  Why D wrong: >300 mg/dl is well above the normal cut-off and indicates diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On a Random Blood Sugar (RBS) test, a value of 140–200 mg/dl indicates which category?","options":["Normal","Pre-diabetes","Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An RBS value of 140–200 mg/dl indicates pre-diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is up to 140 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is >200 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: This value is elevated, not hypoglycemic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A Random Blood Sugar value of greater than 200 mg/dl indicates which category?","options":["Normal","Pre-diabetes","Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An RBS value of >200 mg/dl indicates diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is up to 140 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: Pre-diabetes is 140–200 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: This is hyperglycemia (diabetes range), not hypoglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When an RBS value of >200 mg/dl (or an abnormal OGTT result) is found, what is the recommended next step?","options":["The result must be done for confirmation with a fasting/OGTT confirmatory test","No further testing is required","Immediately start insulin without confirmation","Repeat after 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text notes that if the result is >200 mg/dl, the test must be done for confirmation of diabetes mellitus (a confirmatory test is required).  Why B wrong: Confirmation is explicitly required.  Why C wrong: Treatment is not started before confirmation per the text.  Why D wrong: A 1-year delay is not the recommended confirmation step."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The Glycosylated Hemoglobin (HbA1c) test reflects average blood glucose control over approximately what period?","options":["The past 2–3 months","The past 24 hours","The past week only","The past year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The HbA1c reflects average blood sugar level maintained for past 3 months (about 2–3 months, the lifespan of RBCs being 90–120 days).  Why B, C wrong: Short windows (a day or a week) do not match the longer-term reflection of HbA1c.  Why D wrong: One year exceeds the period reflected by HbA1c."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The HbA1c test measures glucose attached to which substance?","options":["Monosaccharides such as glucose, fructose, and galactose attached naturally to hemoglobin in the red blood cell","Plasma albumin only","White blood cell membranes","Platelet surface proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most monosaccharides (glucose, fructose, and galactose) are naturally attached or bound with the hemoglobin in the red blood stream; HbA1c measures this.  Why B, C, D wrong: HbA1c reflects glucose bound to hemoglobin in RBCs, not albumin, WBC membranes, or platelet proteins."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the HbA1c test, which value range is considered NORMAL?","options":["Lower than 5.7% (70–106 mg/dl)","5.7–6.4%","≥6.5%","≥8%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HbA1c result interpretation: Normal is lower than 5.7% (70–106 mg/dl).  Why B wrong: 5.7–6.4% indicates pre-diabetes.  Why C wrong: ≥6.5% indicates diabetes (which means last 3 months a raised sugar level is maintained between 154 mg/dl).  Why D wrong: ≥8% is well above the diabetic threshold, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"On the HbA1c test, a value of 5.7–6.4% indicates which category?","options":["Normal","Pre-diabetes","Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An HbA1c of 5.7–6.4% indicates pre-diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is lower than 5.7%.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is ≥6.5%.  Why D wrong: This range reflects elevated glucose, not hypoglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"An HbA1c of 6.5% or higher indicates diabetes. According to the text, this corresponds to maintaining a raised blood sugar level of approximately what value over the last 3 months?","options":["100 mg/dl","126 mg/dl","154 mg/dl","200 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HbA1c ≥6.5% means diabetes, which means that for the last 3 months a raised sugar level is maintained between 154 mg/dl.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the 154 mg/dl average glucose figure associated with HbA1c ≥6.5% in the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The Estimated Average Glucose (eAG) is derived from which test?","options":["HbA1c (it reflects the HbA1c, converting % into average glucose)","Fasting blood sugar","Oral glucose tolerance test","Random blood sugar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The estimated average glucose (eAG) reflects the HbA1c C average range — it converts the HbA1c percentage into an average glucose value.  Why B, C, D wrong: eAG is derived from HbA1c, not from FBS, OGTT, or RBS directly."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to the American Diabetes Association, the HbA1c goal for a patient with diabetes should be below approximately which value?","options":["5% (97 mg/dl)","6.5% (126 mg/dl)","7% (154 mg/dl), which reflects average glucose level for past 3 months or 90 days","9% (212 mg/dl)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The HbA1c goal for a patient with diabetes should be below 7% (154 mg/dl), which reflects average glucose level for past 3 months or 90 days (Source: American Diabetes Association).  Why A wrong: 5% is below the typical patient target goal stated.  Why B wrong: 6.5% (126 mg/dl) is the diagnostic threshold, not the stated treatment goal.  Why D wrong: 9% is far above the recommended goal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Diabetes mellitus is best described as a chronic metabolic disorder of impaired carbohydrate, protein, and lipid metabolism caused by a deficiency of which substance?","options":["Glucagon","Insulin","Cortisol","Thyroxine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder of impaired carbohydrate, protein, and lipid metabolism caused by a deficiency of insulin.  Why A wrong: Glucagon deficiency does not define diabetes mellitus.  Why C, D wrong: Cortisol and thyroxine deficiencies are not the cause of diabetes mellitus described here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Diabetes occurs because the pancreas is not producing enough insulin OR because the cells of the body are not responding properly to the insulin produced. What is this latter mechanism called?","options":["Insulin overproduction","Insulin resistance (cells not responding properly to insulin)","Insulin allergy","Insulin degradation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes is due to the pancreas not producing enough insulin or the cells of the body not responding properly to the insulin produced (insulin resistance).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the described mechanism of cells failing to respond to insulin."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"How many main types of diabetes mellitus does the text describe?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are three main types of diabetes mellitus described (Type I, Type II, and gestational).  Why A, C, D wrong: The text specifies three main types."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Diabetes Mellitus Type I is also known by which other name?","options":["Non-Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (NIDDM)","Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (IDDM) or juvenile diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes Mellitus Type I is also called Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (IDDM) or juvenile diabetes.  Why A wrong: NIDDM is the other name for Type II.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is a separate (third) type.  Why D wrong: MODY is not the name given to Type I in the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which features characterize Diabetes Mellitus Type I? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Onset is sudden","Cause is autoimmune or pancreatic cells unable to produce insulin due to complete destruction of beta cells of the pancreas","Tends to develop at a young age and cannot be prevented","Insulin is endogenous (the body still makes plenty of insulin)","It is mostly occurs in children and clients looking as thin"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Type I features include sudden onset; autoimmune/destruction of beta cells leading to inability to produce insulin; develops at a young age and cannot be prevented; and mostly occurs in children and thin-looking clients with exogenous insulin requirement.  Why D wrong: In Type I, insulin is exogenous (must be given), because the body's insulin is deficient — it is not endogenously plentiful."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In Diabetes Mellitus Type I, insulin must be supplied from outside the body. This requirement is described as which type of insulin?","options":["Endogenous insulin","Exogenous insulin","Resistant insulin","Inhaled insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Type I diabetes, insulin is exogenous (it must be supplied externally) because the body cannot produce it.  Why A wrong: Endogenous insulin is produced within the body, which is deficient in Type I.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the term used for externally supplied insulin in Type I."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for Diabetes Mellitus Type I? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Genetic","Toxins","Virus","Age younger than 20 years","Obesity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors for Type I include genetic, toxins, virus, and age younger than 20 years.  Why E wrong: Obesity is a Type II (not Type I) risk factor in the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Diabetes Mellitus Type II is also known as:","options":["Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (IDDM)","Non Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (NIDDM)","Juvenile diabetes","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes Mellitus Type II is also called Non Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (NIDDM).  Why A, C wrong: IDDM/juvenile diabetes are names for Type I.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is the separate third type."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which features characterize Diabetes Mellitus Type II? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Onset is gradual","Affects at a smart age (onset typically in adults/older age)","Cause is insufficient insulin secretion or insulin resistance","It may be corrected with dietary modifications, exercises, and hypoglycaemic agents","Complete absence of all insulin from birth"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Type II features include gradual onset; onset at an older/adult age; cause is insufficient insulin secretion or insulin resistance; and it may be corrected with dietary modifications, exercises, and hypoglycaemic agents.  Why E wrong: Complete absence of insulin from birth describes the autoimmune Type I mechanism, not Type II."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for Diabetes Mellitus Type II? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Most common — obesity","Lack of exercise","Stress","History of gestational DM","Age younger than 20 years"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Type II risk factors include obesity (most common), lack of exercise, stress, and history of gestational DM.  Why E wrong: Age younger than 20 years is a Type I risk factor, not Type II."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Gestational diabetes mellitus is best defined as diabetes that:","options":["Occurs only in newborn infants","Occurs in women during pregnancy","Is a permanent autoimmune disease","Occurs only in elderly men"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational diabetes is a rare form of diabetes which occurs in women during pregnancy.  Why A, C, D wrong: These populations/descriptions do not match gestational diabetes, which occurs in pregnant women."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Gestational diabetes mellitus is usually detected during which period of pregnancy?","options":["During the 24–28 weeks of gestation","Within the first 4 weeks of pregnancy","Only after delivery","During the first trimester only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational diabetes is usually detected during the 24–28 weeks of gestation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These timings do not match the 24–28 week detection window described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In gestational diabetes, the newborn delivered to a mother with GDM is at risk of \"macrosomia.\" What does this mean?","options":["The baby is unusually small for gestational age","The baby is unusually large due to insulin resistance of the mother","The baby has high bilirubin","The baby has low calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia means the baby is unusually large, usually given due to insulin resistance of the mother.  Why A wrong: Macrosomia is a large (not small) baby.  Why C wrong: High bilirubin describes hyperbilirubinemia, a separate complication.  Why D wrong: Low calcium (hypocalcemia) is a different newborn complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which neonatal complications are associated with gestational diabetes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Macrosomia (unusually large baby)","Hypoglycemia (due to hyperglycemic intrauterine environment leading to a relative increase in fetal insulin secretion, causing low sugar level after birth)","Hypocalcemia (due to immature parathyroid gland in newborn)","Hyperbilirubinemia (high level of bilirubin due to immature liver and red blood cells)","Permanent maternal blindness in all cases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: GDM neonatal complications include macrosomia, hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia (immature parathyroid gland), and hyperbilirubinemia (immature liver and RBCs).  Why E wrong: Permanent maternal blindness in all cases is not a listed neonatal complication of gestational diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The \"ABCs for Diabetes\" mnemonic from the text refers to which three management targets?","options":["A1C, Blood Pressure, Cholesterol (LDL-CH) — with stop smoking","Albumin, Bilirubin, Creatinine","Acidosis, Bicarbonate, Calcium","Antibody, Blood type, Coagulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Know your ABCs\" for diabetes refers to A1C (<7%), Blood Pressure, and Cholesterol (LDL-CH), with stop smoking emphasized.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the A1C/Blood pressure/Cholesterol meaning of the diabetes ABCs."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which classic triad of symptoms is more common in Diabetes Mellitus Type I?","options":["Polyuria, polydipsia, and polyphagia","Bradycardia, hypotension, and hypothermia","Constipation, oliguria, and weight gain","Jaundice, ascites, and edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms include polyuria, polydipsia, and polyphagia, which are more common in DM-1.  Why B, C, D wrong: These symptom clusters are not the classic diabetic triad described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which symptom is noted to occur more commonly in Diabetes Mellitus Type I?","options":["Weight loss","Weight gain","Constipation","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight loss is noted as more common in DM-1.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the symptom highlighted as more common in DM-1."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of diabetes mellitus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hyperglycaemia and polyphagia, blurred vision","Slow wound healing — due to hyperglycaemia, ulcers, infection, and gangrene","Vaginal infection, weakness, and paresthesias","Sign of inadequate circulation to the feet due to peripheral vascular diseases, which causes delaying wound healing","Signs of atherosclerosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists hyperglycaemia with polyphagia and blurred vision, slow wound healing (hyperglycaemia, ulcers, infection, gangrene), vaginal infection/weakness/paresthesias, inadequate circulation to the feet from peripheral vascular disease delaying wound healing, and signs of atherosclerosis.  Why none wrong: All five options are listed signs and symptoms of diabetes mellitus."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In diabetes, blurred vision is associated with which of the listed symptoms?","options":["Constipation","Hyperglycaemia and polyphagia","Bradycardia","Hypothermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists hyperglycaemia and polyphagia with blurred vision together as signs and symptoms.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the symptoms grouped with blurred vision in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Slow wound healing in diabetes is primarily due to which of the following?","options":["Hyperglycaemia, ulcers, infection, and gangrene","Vitamin C excess","Excess insulin","Hypocalcemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Slow wound healing is due to hyperglycaemia, ulcers, infection, and gangrene.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed causes of slow wound healing in diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Chronic or long-term complications of diabetes mellitus are broadly divided into which two categories?","options":["Acute and subacute complications","Microvascular and macrovascular complications","Endocrine and exocrine complications","Hepatic and renal complications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic or long-term complications of DM are divided into microvascular complications and macrovascular complications.  Why A wrong: Acute/short-term complications are a separate category from chronic complications.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the divisions described for chronic DM complications."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are macrovascular complications of diabetes mellitus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Coronary artery diseases, cardiomyopathy","Hypertension","Cerebrovascular diseases","Cellulitis, infection, and peripheral vascular diseases","Diabetic retinopathy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Macrovascular complications listed include coronary artery diseases/cardiomyopathy, hypertension, cerebrovascular diseases, and cellulitis/infection/peripheral vascular diseases.  Why E wrong: Diabetic retinopathy is a microvascular complication, not macrovascular."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are microvascular complications of diabetes mellitus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Retinopathy","Nephropathy","Neuropathy","Male erectile dysfunction (due to lack of penis erection — common in males due to disease process)","Coronary artery disease"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Microvascular complications listed include retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and male erectile dysfunction (lack of penile erection common in males due to disease process).  Why E wrong: Coronary artery disease is a macrovascular complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When long-time glucose level remains high in the body, which mechanism affects the body tissues, leading to infection and gangrene?","options":["The tissue blood supply mechanism is affected","Excess calcium deposition","Vitamin D toxicity","Decreased red blood cell count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When long-time glucose level remains high, the body tissue blood supply mechanism is affected, leading to infection and gangrene.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the mechanism described for diabetic tissue damage leading to infection and gangrene."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are acute or short-term complications of diabetes mellitus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypoglycaemia","Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","Hyperglycaemic Hyperosmolar Non-Ketotic Syndrome (HHNS)","Retinopathy","Nephropathy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Acute or short-term complications include hypoglycaemia, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and hyperglycaemic hyperosmolar non-ketotic syndrome (HHNS).  Why D, E wrong: Retinopathy and nephropathy are chronic microvascular complications, not acute."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate nursing care measures for a client with diabetes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Health education about personal hygiene and the disease process","Instruct about safe medicines on a scheduled time","Instruct for regular exercise and its importance for maintaining ideal body weight","Monitor serum blood glucose level, train the client/family about self-glucose monitoring","Discontinue all blood glucose monitoring once symptoms improve"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes health education about personal hygiene and disease process, instructing about safe medicines on scheduled time, instructing on regular exercise for ideal body weight, and monitoring serum blood glucose with self-monitoring training.  Why E wrong: Discontinuing monitoring is contrary to the recommended ongoing self-glucose monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When teaching diet therapy for diabetic clients, the nurse should offer the diet to the client as which type of plan?","options":["As per a doctor's order","Only when symptomatic","Only at night","Without any timing schedule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diet therapy for diabetes clients directs the nurse to offer the diet to the client as per the doctor's order.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not reflect the instruction to provide a prescribed (ordered) diabetic diet."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which dietary instructions are appropriate in diet therapy for diabetic clients? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Day-to-day consistency in timing and amount of food helps control the blood glucose level","Take carbohydrate intake from a total caloric intake (e.g., consistent carbohydrate amounts)","According to American diabetes association, maintain a ratio between carbohydrate–protein–fat","Offer high fibers, small, frequent meals to the client","Eliminate all carbohydrates entirely from the diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Diet therapy includes day-to-day consistency in timing and amount of food to control blood glucose, taking carbohydrate intake from total caloric intake, maintaining a carbohydrate–protein–fat ratio per the American Diabetes Association, and offering high-fiber, small, frequent meals.  Why E wrong: Complete carbohydrate elimination is not advised; consistent (not zero) carbohydrate intake is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Why are small, frequent meals high in fiber recommended for diabetic clients?","options":["To help maintain consistent blood glucose levels","To increase blood pressure","To promote weight gain","To raise serum calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Offering high-fiber, small, frequent meals supports day-to-day consistency that helps control the blood glucose level.  Why B, C, D wrong: These outcomes are not the rationale given for high-fiber small frequent meals in diabetic diet therapy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, what is identified as a risk factor for diabetes mellitus?","options":["Underweight","Obesity","High calcium intake","Smoking cessation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" box states obesity is a major risk factor to cause diabetes mellitus.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not identified as the major risk factor for diabetes in the box."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, lipo-hypertrophy is best described as:","options":["Loss of subcutaneous fat","Development of fibrous fatty masses at the injection site due to repeated use of an injection site","Allergic skin rash from insulin","A drop in blood glucose at night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lipo-hypertrophy is the development of fibrous fatty masses at the injection site due to repeated use of an injection site.  Why A wrong: Loss of subcutaneous fat describes lipoatrophy, the opposite condition.  Why C, D wrong: These are unrelated to the lipo-hypertrophy definition."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, lipoatrophy is best described as:","options":["Fibrous fatty mass formation","Loss of subcutaneous fat, i.e., it is prevented by use of human insulin","High blood glucose at the injection site","Allergic reaction to oral hypoglycemics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lipoatrophy is loss of subcutaneous fat; it is prevented by the use of human insulin.  Why A wrong: Fibrous fatty mass formation describes lipo-hypertrophy.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the definition of lipoatrophy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Oral hypoglycemic medications are ordered when diet and weight control therapy have failed to maintain adequate blood glucose levels. These medications are generally effective in which type of diabetes?","options":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus (2-DM)","Gestational diabetes only","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral medications are ordered when diet and weight control therapy have failed to maintain adequate blood glucose levels, and oral medications are usually effective in cases of type 2-DM.  Why A wrong: Type 1 diabetes requires insulin (oral agents are not the mainstay).  Why C, D wrong: The text specifies effectiveness in type 2-DM, not gestational diabetes or diabetes insipidus."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Insulin is given to which clients, according to the text?","options":["Only type 2 clients","Type 1 and (when diet control and oral hypoglycaemic agents have failed) type 2 clients to maintain desired blood glucose levels","Only gestational diabetics","Only clients with diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin is used to treat diabetes type-1 and (when diet control and oral hypoglycaemic agents have failed to maintain desired blood glucose levels) type-2 clients.  Why A, C, D wrong: These restrict insulin use too narrowly compared with the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to the text, the peak action of insulin should be explained to the client in order to prevent which complication?","options":["Hyperglycaemic reaction","Hypoglycaemic reaction","Ketoacidosis","Lipoatrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The peak action of insulin should be explained to the client to prevent hypoglycaemic reaction.  Why A wrong: Insulin's peak relates to risk of low (not high) blood glucose.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the complication tied to insulin peak action timing in the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Short-acting and rapid-acting insulins (such as GAL-Glulisine, Aspart, Lispro, and Regular insulin) can be administered by which route(s)?","options":["Intravenous (IV) route with saline","Intramuscular only","Oral only","Inhalation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Only short-acting and rapid-acting insulins (GAL-Glulisine, Aspart, Lispro, and Regular insulin) can be administered via IV route with saline.  Why B, C, D wrong: These routes are not the ones specified for IV administration of short/rapid-acting insulins."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which exercise instructions are appropriate for diabetic clients? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Encourage weight loss","Reduce cardiovascular risks","Improve circulation and muscle tone","Decrease total cholesterol and triglyceride levels","Decrease insulin resistance and glucose intolerances"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Exercise benefits listed include encouraging weight loss, reducing cardiovascular risks, improving circulation and muscle tone, decreasing total cholesterol and triglyceride levels, and decreasing insulin resistance and glucose intolerances.  Why none wrong: All five are listed benefits of exercise for diabetes clients."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When should the diabetic client monitor blood glucose with respect to exercise, per the exercise instructions?","options":["Only once a month","Instruct the client to do self-monitoring (SMBG) to monitor blood glucose level before, during, and after exercise","Never during exercise","Only after fasting overnight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs the client to do SMBG to monitor blood glucose level before, during, and after exercise.  Why A, C, D wrong: These timings do not match the before/during/after exercise monitoring instruction."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When should a diabetic client avoid exercise, according to the text?","options":["If the sugar level is too high with hyperglycaemia and ketones are present in the blood glucose level, because in such a case the blood glucose level is closer to normal and urinary ketones are absent","Whenever blood glucose is normal","During any time of the day after meals","Only when fasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs that if the sugar level is too high with hyperglycaemia and ketones are present, exercise should be avoided (exercise is undertaken when the blood glucose level is closer to normal and urinary ketones are absent).  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not reflect the contraindication condition (high sugar with ketones) described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which is the most common route of insulin administration described in the text?","options":["Intravenous (IV)","Subcutaneous (SC)","Intramuscular (IM)","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common route of insulin administration is subcutaneous (SC).  Why A wrong: IV is reserved for short/rapid-acting insulins, not the most common route.  Why C, D wrong: IM and oral are not the most common routes described for insulin."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which of the following are recommended subcutaneous sites for insulin injection? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Lateral and posterior aspect of the upper arm","Abdomen","Upper outer thigh","Back","Buttocks"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The common subcutaneous sites listed are the lateral and posterior aspect of the upper arm, abdomen, upper outer thigh, back, and buttocks.  Why none wrong: All five are listed subcutaneous insulin sites."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For better absorption, the abdomen is a reliable site for SC insulin administration. What injection technique is described to ensure insulin reaches the SC tissue?","options":["The route should be made reliable for SC insulin administration (use of the abdomen and proper SC technique)","Inject directly into a vein","Inject into muscle deeply","Apply only topically"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For better absorption, the abdomen is described as a reliable site for SC insulin administration.  Why B, C, D wrong: Vein, deep muscle, and topical routes are not the SC technique described for routine insulin."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When insulin and oral antidiabetic medications are given on an exact ordered time, which complications are being prevented?","options":["Hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia","Hyperkalemia and hyponatremia","Hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia","Anemia and leukopenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administering all medicines and insulin on an exact ordered time prevents hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: These electrolyte/blood complications are not the ones tied to timing of insulin/oral antidiabetic doses here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"To minimize injection-site discomfort, the text advises that the nurse should instruct the client to keep insulin at which temperature before injection?","options":["Frozen","Keep at room temperature, because cold insulin can increase the discomfort during injection","Heated above body temperature","Boiled before use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Care during DKA and HHNS includes keeping insulin at room temperature, because cold insulin can increase the discomfort during injection.  Why A, C, D wrong: Freezing, heating, or boiling insulin are not advised; room temperature is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"To reduce skin moisture and make the injection site easier to clean before insulin injection, the text advises which measure?","options":["Instruct to wash hands and use lubricate that avoid moisture between normal (use moisture-reducing lubricant)","Apply alcohol and inject immediately into a vein","Apply oil-based ointment thickly","Inject through clothing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs to wash hands and to use a lubricant that helps avoid moisture (reduces skin moisture) before injection.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the described measures to reduce moisture before insulin injection."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The Somogyi phenomenon (a complication of insulin therapy) is best described as:","options":["High blood glucose level that remains high all night with no overnight low","A nighttime hypoglycaemia which usually occurs at around 2 am–3 am or pre-breakfast time, due to a counter-regulatory hormone response","A daytime hyperglycaemia after lunch","An allergic reaction to insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Somogyi phenomenon is a hypoglycaemia (low blood glucose level) which occurs at around 2 am–3 am or pre-breakfast time, due to counter-regulatory hormones.  Why A wrong: A persistent overnight high without a low describes the dawn phenomenon, not Somogyi.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the described nocturnal hypoglycaemia mechanism of the Somogyi effect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In the Somogyi phenomenon, the body releases epinephrine and glucagon in response to hypoglycaemia. What is the effect of this counter-regulatory response?","options":["It causes a further drop in blood glucose","The peripheral hormones epinephrine and glucagon cause hyperglycaemia by inducing stored glucose release (rebound high blood glucose)","It has no effect on glucose","It stimulates more insulin release"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Somogyi phenomenon, the body releases epinephrine and glucagon for compensatory mechanism, which induces stored glucose release, leading to a rebound hyperglycaemia.  Why A wrong: The counter-regulatory hormones raise (not further lower) glucose.  Why C wrong: The response does affect glucose (causes rebound hyperglycaemia).  Why D wrong: Epinephrine and glucagon are not insulin-stimulating in this context."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The Dawn phenomenon is best described as:","options":["A nighttime hypoglycaemia at 2 am–3 am","A rise in blood glucose level in the early morning (around 4 am–8 am or pre-breakfast time) due to nocturnal release of growth hormone, with no nocturnal low blood glucose, and is treated by increasing acceleration metabolism of carbohydrate","An allergic reaction to insulin","Insulin resistance from a new pack of insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Dawn phenomenon is a rise in blood glucose in the early morning (about 4 am–8 am or pre-breakfast time) due to nocturnal release of growth hormone; it occurs because GH hormone accelerates metabolism of carbohydrate.  Why A wrong: A nighttime hypoglycaemia at 2 am–3 am describes the Somogyi phenomenon.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the growth-hormone-mediated morning rise described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"The Dawn phenomenon is attributed to the nocturnal release of which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Growth hormone (GH)","Calcitonin","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Dawn phenomenon is due to the nocturnal release of growth hormone, which accelerates metabolism of carbohydrate, raising morning blood glucose.  Why A, C, D wrong: These hormones are not the cause of the dawn phenomenon described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which complication of insulin therapy is described as the most common, and usually occurs around morning time around 4 am–8 am or pre-breakfast time?","options":["Insulin resistance","Dawn phenomenon","Somogyi phenomenon","Lipoatrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Dawn phenomenon is described as occurring at around 4 am–8 am or pre-breakfast time (morning rise in blood glucose).  Why A wrong: Insulin resistance is a separate complication relating to lack of tissue sensitivity.  Why C wrong: The Somogyi phenomenon occurs at around 2 am–3 am (nocturnal hypoglycaemia).  Why D wrong: Lipoatrophy is a fat-loss complication at the injection site, not a morning glucose event."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Insulin resistance, a complication of insulin therapy, develops mainly because of which mechanism?","options":["The client develops immune antibodies that bind the insulin so the body is unable to use it, OR there is a lack of tissue sensitivity to the insulin from the body, resulting in developed hyperglycaemia","Overproduction of glucagon only","Excess dietary calcium","Loss of subcutaneous fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin resistance develops when the client develops immune antibodies that bind the insulin (so the body cannot use it) or from lack of tissue sensitivity to insulin, resulting in hyperglycaemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: These mechanisms are not the described cause of insulin resistance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for insulin resistance related to insulin preparation?","options":["Use a new pack or purer insulin preparation","Reduce all insulin doses to zero","Switch to oral antidiabetics only","Increase dietary fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention for insulin resistance is to use a new pack or purer insulin preparation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the prevention measure (new pack/purer insulin) described for insulin resistance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For a U-40 insulin syringe and vial, which color code is used, and how much insulin equals 1 ml?","options":["Color code red; U-40 means 40 units of insulin is equal to 1 ml (40 U = 1 ml)","Color code orange; 100 units = 1 ml","Color code green; 25 units = 1 ml","Color code blue; 10 units = 1 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Unit-40 insulin, the color code is red, and 40 units of insulin is equal to 1 ml (40 U = 1 ml).  Why B wrong: Orange color code and 100 U = 1 ml describe the U-100 syringe.  Why C, D wrong: These color/unit combinations are not described for U-40."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which syringes should be used with a U-40 insulin vial?","options":["Only U-40 syringes (with marking up to 40) should be used with a U-40 insulin vial","Only U-100 syringes","Any tuberculin syringe","A 50 ml syringe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Only U-40 syringes (with marking up to 40) should be used with a U-40 insulin vial.  Why B wrong: U-100 syringes are for U-100 vials, not U-40.  Why C, D wrong: Tuberculin or 50 ml syringes are not the matched insulin syringe for a U-40 vial."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In India, what is the needle size used for a U-40 insulin syringe?","options":["40 G, 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\"","27 G, 12.7 mm, 30 G, 1/2\"","22 G, 25 mm","18 G, 38 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the needle size for U-40 is 40 G, 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\".  Why B wrong: 27 G, 12.7 mm, 30 G, 1/2\" is the U-100 needle size in India.  Why C, D wrong: These larger gauges/lengths are not the described U-40 insulin needle sizes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"For a U-100 insulin syringe and vial, which color code is used, and how much insulin equals 1 ml?","options":["Color code red; 40 U = 1 ml","Color code orange; 100 units = 1 ml (100 U = 1 ml)","Color code green; 25 U = 1 ml","Color code black; 50 U = 1 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For Unit-100 insulin, the color code is orange, and 100 units = 1 ml (100 U = 1 ml).  Why A wrong: Red color and 40 U = 1 ml describe the U-40 syringe.  Why C, D wrong: These combinations are not described for U-100."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which syringes should be used with a U-100 insulin vial?","options":["Only U-40 syringes","Only U-100 syringes (with marking up to 100) should be used with a U-100 insulin vial","Any 10 ml syringe","A glass syringe only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only U-100 syringes (with marking up to 100) should be used with a U-100 insulin vial.  Why A wrong: U-40 syringes are matched to U-40 vials.  Why C, D wrong: A general 10 ml syringe or glass syringe is not the matched insulin syringe."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In India, what is the needle size used for a U-100 insulin syringe?","options":["40 G, 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\"","27 G, 12.7 mm, 30 G, 1/2\"","21 G, 40 mm","18 G, 25 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the needle size for U-100 is 27 G, 12.7 mm, 30 G, 1/2\".  Why A wrong: 40 G, 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\" is the U-40 needle size.  Why C, D wrong: These larger gauges/lengths are not the described U-100 insulin needle sizes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When converting U-40 insulin from a U-40 syringe to a U-100 syringe, which calculation factor is applied?","options":["Multiply the U-40 units by 2.5 (since 40 × 2.5 = 100) to obtain the dose in a U-100 syringe","Divide the units by 2","Add 60 to the units","Multiply by 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When converting from a U-40 syringe to a U-100 syringe you multiply the U-40 units by 2.5 (40 × 2.5 = 100).  Why B, C, D wrong: These operations do not match the multiply-by-2.5 conversion factor described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"A doctor orders 18 U of U-40 insulin. Using the conversion described (multiply by 2.5), how many units should be drawn in a U-100 syringe?","options":["36 units","45 units","18 units","90 units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multiply 18 by 2.5 = 45 units; the required dose drawn in the U-100 syringe is 45 U.  Why A wrong: 36 results from multiplying by 2, not the correct 2.5 factor.  Why C wrong: 18 U is the U-40 ordered amount, not the converted U-100 volume.  Why D wrong: 90 results from multiplying by 5, not 2.5."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"According to WHO recommendations referenced in the text, the BCG vaccine is administered to newborns by which route?","options":["Intramuscular route","Intradermal route","Subcutaneous route","Intravenous route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG vaccine is administered to newborns by the intradermal route as per the recommendations of the WHO.  Why A, C, D wrong: These routes do not match the WHO-recommended intradermal route for BCG."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"Which syringe and needle specifications are described for administering the BCG vaccine? (Select all that apply.)","options":["A U-100 tuberculin syringe is used","The needle is 25 or 26 G","The needle is 3/8\" in thickness/length (3/8 inch)","A U-40 insulin syringe with an 18 G needle","A 22 G, 1.5 inch needle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes using a U-100 tuberculin syringe with a 25 or 26 G needle that is 3/8\" in thickness/length for BCG administration.  Why D, E wrong: These syringe/needle specifications do not match the described BCG (tuberculin) equipment."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When mixing insulins to prepare 10 units of regular and 20 units of NPH, what is the FIRST step?","options":["Inject air into the regular insulin vial","Wash hands","Withdraw the NPH insulin","Roll the NPH vial to mix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure for mixing insulins begins with washing hands as the first step.  Why A, C, D wrong: These steps occur later in the sequence, not first."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When mixing insulins, after inspecting the regular insulin for clarity, how should the NPH (cloudy) insulin be mixed?","options":["Shake the NPH vial vigorously","Gently rotate the NPH insulin to mix well","Heat the NPH vial","Add water to the NPH vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure directs the nurse to gently rotate the NPH insulin to mix well.  Why A wrong: Vigorous shaking is not advised (gentle rotation is used).  Why C, D wrong: Heating or adding water to the vial is not part of the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When mixing 10 units of regular and 20 units of NPH insulin, into which vial is air injected FIRST?","options":["Air (20 U) is injected into the NPH vial first (Figure A), and air (10 U) into the regular vial second (Figure B)","Air is injected into the regular vial first, then the NPH vial","No air is injected into either vial","Air is injected only into the NPH vial and never the regular vial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure draws 20 U of air into the syringe and injects air into the NPH vial first (Figure A), then draws 10 U of air and injects it into the regular vial (Figure B).  Why B wrong: Air is injected into the NPH vial first, not the regular vial.  Why C wrong: Air is injected into both vials to prevent vacuum.  Why D wrong: Air is injected into both vials (NPH first, then regular)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In the mixing-insulins procedure, after injecting air into both vials, which insulin is withdrawn FIRST?","options":["NPH insulin (cloudy) is withdrawn first","Regular insulin (clear) is withdrawn first (Figure C), then NPH insulin (Figure D)","They are withdrawn at the same time","Neither is withdrawn until the next day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After injecting air into both vials, the nurse inverts the vial and withdraws 10 U of regular insulin first (Figure C — clear), then carefully withdraws 20 U of NPH insulin (Figure D — cloudy).  Why A wrong: Regular (clear) insulin is drawn before NPH (cloudy) to avoid contaminating the clear vial.  Why C, D wrong: They are drawn sequentially (regular first, then NPH), not simultaneously or delayed."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When mixing 10 units of regular and 20 units of NPH insulin, what is the total dosage in the syringe?","options":["10 units total","20 units total","30 units total","40 units total"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 10 units of regular plus 20 units of NPH equals 30 units total dosage in the syringe (as shown in Figure D — 10 U regular + 20 U NPH = 30 units total dosage).  Why A, B wrong: These reflect only one component, not the combined total.  Why D wrong: 40 units overstates the combined 30-unit total."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"In the procedure for mixing insulins, which final steps should the nurse perform after administering the insulin? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Administer the insulin","Wash hands","Properly dispose of the syringe/sharps","Recap the needle by hand and reuse it","Leave the syringe uncovered at the bedside"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The final steps include administering the insulin, washing hands, and properly disposing of the syringe.  Why D wrong: Recapping by hand and reusing the syringe is unsafe and not part of the procedure.  Why E wrong: Leaving the syringe uncovered is not the directed disposal practice."},{"section":"Batch 008 · tests for hypocalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, pancreas anatomy/physiology…","question":"When preparing a mixed insulin dose, the nurse should wipe off the top of both vials before drawing up insulin using which item?","options":["An alcohol pad","A dry gauze only","A water-soaked cotton ball","A povidone-iodine swab only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure directs the nurse to wipe off the top of both vials with an alcohol pad before drawing up the insulin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These cleaning materials are not the alcohol pad specified in the mixing procedure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) by a glucometer works on the principle of:","options":["Amperometry","Spectrophotometry","Colorimetry","Nephelometry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SMBG is usually done by glucometer, which works on the principle of amperometry.  Why B/C/D wrong: Spectrophotometry, colorimetry, and nephelometry are other laboratory measurement principles, not the stated principle of glucometer SMBG."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The most common site used to obtain a drop of blood for capillary (glucometer) blood glucose testing is the:","options":["Finger prick (lateral side of the fingertip)","Earlobe","Antecubital vein","Dorsum of the hand"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states most common sites for capillary blood is finger prick, to obtain a drop of blood for glucose testing (most common site).  Why B/C/D wrong: The earlobe, antecubital vein, and dorsum of the hand are not listed as the most common SMBG sampling site; capillary testing uses fingertip prick."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which of the following are listed as alternative capillary blood sampling sites for self-monitoring of blood glucose? (Select all that apply)","options":["Upper arm","Thigh","Calf","Heel prick (lateral aspect of sole)","Abdomen"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists alternative sites for capillary blood as upper arm, thigh, calf, and heel prick; heel/foot prick is on the lateral aspect of sole (most common site in neonates for capillary blood testing).  Why E wrong: The abdomen is not listed as a capillary blood glucose sampling site (the abdomen is, however, the most rapid insulin-absorption injection site)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The most common site for capillary blood glucose testing in neonates is the:","options":["Lateral aspect of the sole (heel prick)","Fingertip","Earlobe","Great toe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the heel prick in the lateral aspect of sole is the most common site in neonates for capillary blood testing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fingertip is the most common general site but not the neonatal site; earlobe and great toe are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Hypoglycaemia is best defined as a condition in which the blood glucose level falls below:","options":["70 mg/dl","100 mg/dl","126 mg/dl","50 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines hypoglycaemia as a condition of low blood sugar level occurring when blood glucose falls < 70 mg/dl, or when it drops rapidly from an elevated level.  Why B/C/D wrong: 100 and 126 mg/dl are not low values; 50 mg/dl, while low, is not the threshold the page gives for defining hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of hypoglycaemia?","options":["Too much insulin (insulin shock)","Dehydration","Excess dietary sugar","Infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes of hypoglycaemia as too much insulin (insulin shock) or hypoglycaemic reaction, too little food, or excessive exercises.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dehydration, excess sugar, and infection are associated with hyperglycaemia/DKA/HHNS, not hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In mild hypoglycaemia the blood glucose level is usually:","options":["< 60 mg/dl","< 40 mg/dl","20 mg/dl","< 70 mg/dl with no specific threshold"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that in mild hypoglycaemia the client remains fully awake and the blood glucose level is usually < 60 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: < 40 mg/dl corresponds to moderate hypoglycaemia. Why C wrong: 20 mg/dl is the level given for severe hypoglycaemia. Why D wrong: that combination is not stated for mild hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are characteristic of MILD hypoglycaemia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hunger and nervousness","Palpitation and sweating","Tachycardia and tremors","Coma","Patient is able to self-treat"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Mild hypoglycaemia produces adrenergic symptoms—hunger/nervousness, palpitation/sweating, tachycardia/tremors—and the patient is able to self-treat.  Why D wrong: Coma is a feature of severe hypoglycaemia, not mild."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"A client with mild hypoglycaemia should be given simple carbohydrate (e.g., sugar cubes, honey, fruit juice, glucose tablets) and have the blood glucose rechecked after:","options":["15 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes","5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's nursing care states to give 10-15 gm of fast-acting simple carbohydrate, then retest the blood glucose after 15 minutes and repeat treatment if symptoms are not resolved.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are not what the source specifies for rechecking after treating mild hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In moderate hypoglycaemia the blood glucose level is usually:","options":["< 40 mg/dl","< 60 mg/dl","20 mg/dl","< 70 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that in moderate hypoglycaemia, with worsening symptoms, the blood glucose level is usually < 40 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: < 60 mg/dl is mild. Why C wrong: 20 mg/dl is severe. Why D wrong: < 70 mg/dl is the general definition threshold, not the moderate level."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs/symptoms reflect MODERATE hypoglycaemia (impaired central nervous function)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inability to concentrate","Double vision and confusion","Coordination impaired","Slurred speech, numbness, headache","Patient is fully alert and self-treats"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate hypoglycaemia produces inability to concentrate, double vision/confusion, impaired coordination, and slurred speech/numbness/headache.  Why E wrong: Full alertness and self-treatment characterize MILD hypoglycaemia, not moderate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"For the client with moderate hypoglycaemia, nursing care includes giving 15-30 gm of fast-acting simple carbohydrate and offering additional food after how long if needed?","options":["15 minutes","5 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to give 15-30 gm fast-acting simple carbohydrate and offer additional foods after 15 minutes, like milk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are not specified for moderate hypoglycaemia follow-up feeding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In severe hypoglycaemia, the blood sugar level is usually:","options":["20 mg/dl","< 40 mg/dl","< 60 mg/dl","< 70 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that severe hypoglycaemia presents with neurological symptoms and the blood sugar level is usually 20 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: < 40 mg/dl is moderate. Why C wrong: < 60 mg/dl is mild. Why D wrong: < 70 mg/dl is the general definition value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs/symptoms indicate SEVERE hypoglycaemia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Disoriented behaviour","Loss of consciousness","Difficulty arousing, seizures and coma","Mild hunger only","Self-treats easily"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe hypoglycaemia produces disoriented behaviour, loss of consciousness, and difficulty arousing with seizures and coma.  Why D/E wrong: Mild hunger and easy self-treatment are features of mild hypoglycaemia, not severe; in severe hypoglycaemia the client is unable to self-treat."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"For a conscious client with severe hypoglycaemia, the nurse should give glucagon by which route?","options":["IM or SC","IV bolus only","Oral only","Inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that if the client is unconscious then give glucagon injection IM or SC.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the IM/SC route for glucagon in this setting; oral intake is unsafe in an unconscious client, and inhalation is not used."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"If a hospitalised client with severe hypoglycaemia remains unconscious, the nurse should give:","options":["25% or 50% dextrose IV","Oral glucose gel","Subcutaneous insulin","Normal saline bolus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that if the client is in hospital premises, give 25% or 50% dextrose IV.  Why B wrong: Oral gel is unsafe in unconsciousness (aspiration risk). Why C wrong: Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia. Why D wrong: Saline does not correct hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Why should the nurse NOT give oral food or fluids to a client experiencing severe hypoglycaemia with unconsciousness?","options":["To prevent aspiration","To prevent hyperkalaemia","To prevent vomiting only","To preserve the gag reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states do not attempt to give oral food or fluids in severe hypoglycaemia who is unconscious and unable to swallow, to prevent aspiration.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is aspiration prevention; hyperkalaemia, vomiting alone, and gag-reflex preservation are not the reasons given."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the source, glycaemic criteria for mild, moderate and severe hypoglycaemia respectively are:","options":["< 70 mg/dl; ≤ 54 mg/dl; no specific threshold","< 60 mg/dl; < 40 mg/dl; 20 mg/dl","< 54 mg/dl; < 70 mg/dl; < 20 mg/dl","< 100 mg/dl; < 70 mg/dl; < 50 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Range of hypoglycemia\" figure (Source: American Diabetes Association) gives Mild (Level 1) < 70 mg/dL, Moderate (Level 2) ≤ 54 mg/dL, and Severe (Level 3) with no specific threshold.  Why B wrong: Those are the signs/symptoms-section values, not the ADA glycaemic-criteria figure. Why C/D wrong: These do not match the figure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Per the ADA \"Range of hypoglycemia\" figure, severe (Level 3) hypoglycaemia is described as:","options":["Severe cognitive impairment requiring external assistance for recovery","Serious, clinically important hypoglycaemia","Requires fast-acting carbs and dose adjustment of glucose-lowering therapy","Asymptomatic low glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure describes Level 3 (Severe) as severe cognitive impairment requiring external assistance for recovery.  Why B wrong: That describes Level 2 (Moderate). Why C wrong: That describes Level 1 (Mild). Why D wrong: Not stated in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Per the ADA \"Range of hypoglycemia\" figure, the recommended treatment for severe (Level 3) hypoglycaemia is:","options":["Glucagon or IV glucose","Glucose or any carbohydrate that contains glucose, orally","Fast-acting carbs only","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure lists treatment for Level 3 (Severe) as glucagon or IV glucose.  Why B wrong: Glucose or any carbohydrate containing glucose is the treatment listed for mild/moderate levels. Why C wrong: Oversimplified. Why D wrong: Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the 15-15 rule for hypoglycaemia, if your blood sugar is between 55-69 mg/dL you should:","options":["Take 15 grams of carbs and check again after 15 minutes","Take 50 grams of carbs and check after 30 minutes","Give IV dextrose immediately","Inject glucagon and recheck in 5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 15-15 rule states that with low blood sugar between 55-69 mg/dL you treat with 15 grams of carbs and check after 15 minutes, repeating if still below target.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 15-15 rule (15 g carbs / 15 minute recheck); IV dextrose/glucagon are for severe hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Per the 15-15 rule, the approximate grams of carbohydrate needed to fix a low blood glucose in young children—infants and toddlers respectively—are:","options":["Infants 6 gm, toddlers 8 gm","Infants 15 gm, toddlers 15 gm","Infants 8 gm, toddlers 6 gm","Infants 10 gm, toddlers 12 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states young children usually need less than 15 grams of carbs—infants 6 gm, toddlers 8 gm, and small children 10 gm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated paediatric carbohydrate amounts."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The 15-15 rule is used for mild and moderate hypoglycaemia; severe hypoglycaemia should be treated by:","options":["Injectable IV 25% dextrose or glucagon injection","15 grams of oral carbohydrate","Glucose gel only","Withholding treatment until conscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the 15-15 rule is used in mild and moderate hypoglycaemia; severe hypoglycaemia should be treated by injectable IV 25% dextrose or glucagon injection.  Why B/C wrong: Oral carbohydrate/gel are for mild/moderate and unsafe in severe (unconscious) cases. Why D wrong: Treatment must not be withheld."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which of the following items each provide about 15 grams of carbohydrate per the 15-15 rule? (Select all that apply)","options":["4 ounces (½ cup) of juice or regular soda","1 tablespoon of sugar, honey, or syrup","Hard candies, jellybeans, or gumdrops (per food label)","3-4 glucose tablets","1 dose of glucose gel (usually 1 tube)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are listed by the source as items having about 15 grams of carbs: 4 oz (½ cup) juice/regular soda, 1 tablespoon sugar/honey/syrup, hard candies/jellybeans/gumdrops (per food label), 3-4 glucose tablets, and 1 dose of glucose gel (usually 1 tube).  Why none wrong: Each option is explicitly listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a severe condition of which type of diabetes, occurring in severe insulin deficiency?","options":["Type 1 diabetes","Type 2 diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states DKA is a severe condition of diabetes type-1, occurring in severe insulin deficiency.  Why B/C/D wrong: Type 2 more often causes HHNS; gestational diabetes and diabetes insipidus are different conditions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, the serum blood glucose level reaches which range, and DKA is typically triggered by:","options":["300-600 mg/dl; triggered by infection and stress","> 600 mg/dl; triggered by dehydration","< 70 mg/dl; triggered by excess insulin","100-200 mg/dl; triggered by exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in DKA the serum blood glucose level reached 300-600 mg/dl, and it is triggered by infection and stress.  Why B wrong: > 600 mg/dl is the HHNS value. Why C/D wrong: These reflect hypoglycaemia, not DKA."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, hyperglycaemia occurs due to excessive secretion of which hormones, by the process of gluconeogenesis or glycogenolysis?","options":["Glucagon and epinephrine","Insulin and amylin","ADH and aldosterone","Calcitonin and PTH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hyperglycaemia is due to excessive secretion of glucagon and epinephrine hormone by the process of gluconeogenesis or glycogenolysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These hormones are not the counter-regulatory drivers of DKA hyperglycaemia named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, hypokalaemia may develop initially after treatment because insulin and dextrose therapy cause potassium to:","options":["Re-enter into the cells","Be excreted by the kidneys","Bind to albumin","Convert to glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hypokalaemia occurs because latercone treatment starts with insulin + dextrose therapy, causing potassium to re-enter the cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes the fall to intracellular potassium shift, not renal excretion, albumin binding, or glucose conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, osmotic diuresis leads to which clinical effect, and ketosis is caused by:","options":["Excreting more water along with K⁺ and Na⁺; lipolysis of fatty acids forming ketone bodies","Water retention; protein catabolism","Sodium retention; glycogen storage","Decreased urine output; fat synthesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states osmotic diuresis leads to excreting more water along with K⁺ and Na⁺, and ketosis is due to lipolysis of fatty acids, with ketone bodies formed usually acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the dehydration, electrolyte loss, and lipolysis described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which ketone bodies are formed in DKA from lipolysis of fatty acids?","options":["Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate","Lactate and pyruvate","Urea and creatinine","Ammonia and bilirubin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page names the ketone bodies formed as acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These metabolites are not the ketone bodies of DKA."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The metabolic acidosis of DKA is due to ketone bodies and produces which characteristic compensatory breathing pattern?","options":["Kussmaul's respiration (deep, rapid breathing)","Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Apneustic breathing","Biot's respiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states metabolic acidosis is due to ketone bodies (pH ↓, HCO₃ ↓), and Kussmaul's respiration is the fruity breath due to acidosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cheyne-Stokes, apneustic, and Biot's patterns are not the compensatory hyperventilation of metabolic acidosis described here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, increased intracranial pressure (ICP) is due to:","options":["Fluctuation in blood glucose level and its management","Cerebral haemorrhage","Hyponatraemia only","Excess insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states increased ICP is due to fluctuation in blood glucose level and its management.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause; the source attributes ICP changes to glucose fluctuation during treatment."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Initial nursing care for DKA includes treating dehydration with a fast IV infusion of:","options":["0.9% or 0.45% Normal Saline (NS)","5% dextrose","25% dextrose","Ringer's lactate with potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to initially treat dehydration with fast IV infusion of 0.9% or 0.45% Normal Saline (NS).  Why B/C wrong: Dextrose is added later once glucose normalizes. Why D wrong: Not the initial fluid specified."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA management, dextrose is added later when the blood glucose level has reached near normal or:","options":["< 250 mg/dl","< 70 mg/dl","> 600 mg/dl","300-600 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states later when the blood glucose level reached to near normal or < 250 mg/dl then dextrose can be used.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the < 250 mg/dl threshold for starting dextrose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA treatment of hyperglycaemia, short-acting insulin (such as regular insulin) is recommended initially at:","options":["5-10 units with NS for continuously IV infusion","50 units IV push","1-2 units subcutaneously","100 units IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states short-acting insulin like regular insulin, 5-10 units, are recommended initially with NS for continuously IV infusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These doses/routes do not match the source recommendation for initial DKA insulin therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In DKA, why is potassium administered by infusion pump once insulin therapy begins?","options":["Because potassium is decreased rapidly within the first hours of treatment for dehydration and acidosis","Because potassium causes hyperglycaemia","Because insulin raises potassium","To correct metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states potassium is administered by infusion pump once insulin therapy begins, because potassium is decreased rapidly within the first hours of treatment for dehydration and acidosis.  Why B/C wrong: Insulin shifts potassium into cells (decreasing serum K), it does not raise it. Why D wrong: DKA causes acidosis, not alkalosis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Why should the nurse monitor ICP during the acute phase of DKA?","options":["Because blood glucose level falls too rapidly, water is pulled from blood into the CSF and brain tissues, which can lead to increased ICP & cerebral oedema","Because high glucose dehydrates the brain","Because insulin directly raises ICP","Because potassium replacement causes oedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states monitor ICP during acute phase because if blood glucose falls too rapidly before the brain has time to equilibrate, water is pulled out from the blood into the CSF and brain tissues, leading to increased ICP and cerebral oedema.  Why B/C/D wrong: The mechanism is rapid osmotic water shift into the brain, not direct glucose, insulin, or potassium effects."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"A client recovering from DKA should take medicines on time and check blood sugar level and urine ketones at what frequency?","options":["Every 3 to 4 days","Every hour","Once a month","Only when symptomatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to take medicines on time, check sugar level regularly, and check urine ketones every 3 to 4 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the source recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"What is the full form of HHNS?","options":["Hyperglycaemic Hyperosmolar Non-Ketotic Syndrome","Hyperosmolar Hyponatraemic Nephrotic Syndrome","Hyperglycaemic Hypoxic Neurological Syndrome","Hypoglycaemic Hyperosmolar Necrotic Syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives the full form of HHNS as Hyperglycaemic Hyperosmolar Non-Ketotic Syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions not given by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"HHNS is an extreme form of hyperglycaemia that occurs without which feature, and typically occurs in clients with which type of diabetes?","options":["Without ketosis or acidosis; type 2 DM","With ketosis; type 1 DM","With acidosis; gestational diabetes","Without hyperglycaemia; type 1 DM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states HHNS is an extreme form of hyperglycaemia occurring without ketosis or acidosis, and occurs in clients with type 2 DM.  Why B/C/D wrong: Ketosis/acidosis are absent in HHNS (the key difference from DKA), and it occurs in type 2, not type 1 or gestational diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The major difference between DKA and HHNS is that HHNS occurs:","options":["Without ketosis & acidosis","With more ketones than DKA","With lower blood glucose than DKA","Only in children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the major difference b/w DKA and HHNS is HHNS is without ketosis & acidosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: HHNS lacks significant ketosis, has very high glucose (>600 mg/dl), and occurs in adults with type 2 DM."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"HHNS occurs without ketosis and acidosis because of the presence of insulin and which type of enzymes that prevent fat breakdown or lipolysis?","options":["Insulin-sensitive lipase enzymes","Pancreatic amylase enzymes","Hepatic transaminase enzymes","Lipoprotein synthesis enzymes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states HHNS occurs without ketosis & acidosis because there is presence of insulin and insulin-sensitive lipase enzymes that prevent fat breakdown or lipolysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These enzymes are not the ones the source credits with preventing lipolysis in HHNS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In HHNS, the serum sugar level is usually:","options":["> 600 mg/dl","300-600 mg/dl","< 250 mg/dl","< 70 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in HHNS the serum sugar level is > 600 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 300-600 mg/dl is the DKA range. Why C/D wrong: These are far too low for HHNS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are seen in HHNS? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hyperglycaemia, dehydration and electrolytes losses","Dry skin & sunken eyeball","Polyuria and polydipsia","Altered sensorium, seizures and coma","Fruity breath odour"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists HHNS signs as hyperglycaemia/dehydration/electrolyte losses, dry skin & sunken eyeball, polyuria & polydipsia, and altered sensorium/seizures/coma.  Why E wrong: Fruity breath (acetone) is a feature of DKA ketoacidosis, not HHNS, which lacks ketosis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Nursing care for HHNS includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fluid replacement","Correct electrolytes imbalances","Insulin administration as per blood glucose level","Monitor potassium level closely once insulin therapy initiated","Initiate general nursing care similar to DKA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists all five as HHNS nursing care: fluid replacement, correct electrolyte imbalances, insulin administration per blood glucose level, monitor potassium closely once insulin therapy is initiated, and initiate general nursing care similar to DKA.  Why none wrong: Each is explicitly stated by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The \"Dawn Phenomenon\" in diabetes is characterized by:","options":["High blood sugar in the morning following normal blood sugar overnight","Low blood sugar overnight followed by rebound high morning sugar","Normal blood sugar at all times","High sugar only at bedtime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Dawn Phenomenon graphic shows normal blood sugar at bedtime, normal blood sugar overnight, and a high blood sugar rise in the morning.  Why B wrong: That pattern (overnight low then rebound high) describes the Somogyi effect. Why C/D wrong: These do not match the dawn-phenomenon graphic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The \"Somogyi Effect\" in diabetes is characterized by:","options":["Low blood sugar overnight followed by a rebound high blood sugar in the morning","High blood sugar overnight that persists into morning","Steadily normal blood sugar overnight and morning","Low blood sugar at bedtime only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Somogyi Effect graphic shows normal blood sugar at bedtime, low blood sugar overnight, and a rebound high blood sugar in the morning.  Why B wrong: A morning rise from normal overnight sugar describes the Dawn Phenomenon. Why C/D wrong: These do not match the Somogyi graphic (which features an overnight low)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which of the following are correct foot-care instructions for a client with diabetic neuropathy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Check for dry and cracked skin daily","Check temperature before bathing","Wash feet with warm water (not hot) and dry them smoothly","Examine bottom of feet daily and look between toes; cut the toenails regularly","Walk barefoot to toughen the soles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" foot-care list includes checking for dry/cracked skin daily, checking temperature before bathing, washing feet with warm (not hot) water and drying smoothly, preventing moisture between toes to prevent infection, examining the bottom of the feet daily and looking between toes, cutting toenails regularly, and wearing loose socks and well-fitting shoes.  Why E wrong: The source explicitly says do not walk barefoot."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"A diabetic client with neuropathy asks about footwear. The nurse should instruct the client to:","options":["Wear loose socks and well-fitting shoes, and not walk barefoot","Wear tight shoes to provide support","Walk barefoot at home only","Wear sandals without socks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The foot-care list states to wear loose socks and well-fitting shoes, and don't walk barefoot.  Why B/C/D wrong: Tight shoes, barefoot walking, and no socks increase injury/ulcer risk and contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Diabetic retinopathy causes chronic and progressively impairment of the retinal circulation; permanent vision changes and blindness can occur due to:","options":["Damage to the retina","Optic nerve atrophy","Lens dislocation","Corneal ulceration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diabetic retinopathy causes chronic, progressive impairment of retinal circulation, and permanent vision changes & blindness can occur due to damage to the retina.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes vision loss to retinal damage, not optic-nerve atrophy, lens dislocation, or corneal ulceration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are listed for diabetic retinopathy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Blurred vision due to macular oedema","Sudden loss of vision due to retinal detachment","Cataracts due to opacity of lens","Increased night vision","Frothy urine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists blurred vision due to macular oedema, sudden loss of vision due to retinal detachment, and cataracts due to opacity of the lens as signs/symptoms of diabetic retinopathy.  Why D/E wrong: Increased night vision is not a feature, and frothy urine relates to nephropathy, not retinopathy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Nursing care for diabetic retinopathy includes early prevention via control of hypertension & hyperglycaemia and which treatment for vitreous haemorrhage?","options":["Photocoagulation or laser therapy; if vitreous haemorrhage occurs then vitrectomy can be performed, and lens implantation","Corneal transplant","Routine eye drops only","Immediate enucleation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists nursing care as early prevention via control of hypertension & hyperglycaemia, photocoagulation or laser therapy, vitrectomy if vitreous haemorrhage occurs, and lens implantation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Corneal transplant, eye drops alone, and enucleation are not listed as the management for diabetic retinopathy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Diabetic nephropathy is the chronic and progressive deterioration of, or impairment in, which organ function?","options":["Renal function","Hepatic function","Cardiac function","Pulmonary function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diabetic nephropathy is chronic and progressively deterioration of, or impairment in, renal functions.  Why B/C/D wrong: Nephropathy affects the kidney, not the liver, heart, or lungs."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are listed for diabetic nephropathy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Micro-albuminemia","Thirst & fatigue","Anaemia & weight loss","Sign of malnutrition and frequent UTI","Sign of neurogenic bladder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists micro-albuminemia, thirst & fatigue, anaemia & weight loss, sign of malnutrition, frequent UTI, and sign of neurogenic bladder as signs/symptoms of diabetic nephropathy.  Why none wrong: Each option is explicitly listed by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Nursing care for diabetic nephropathy includes monitoring which laboratory parameters?","options":["BUN, albumin & creatinine level","Liver enzymes only","Serum amylase and lipase","Arterial blood gases only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists nursing care including assessing vital signs, intake & output, and monitoring the BUN, albumin & creatinine level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These tests are not the renal-function monitoring named for diabetic nephropathy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which nursing-care measures are appropriate for diabetic nephropathy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Assess vital signs, intake & output","Offer diet as per ordered","Avoid nephrotoxic substances","Prepare for dialysis procedure if planned","Renal transplant"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists all five as nursing care for diabetic nephropathy: assess vital signs/I&O, offer diet as ordered, avoid nephrotoxic substances, prepare for dialysis if planned, and renal transplant.  Why none wrong: Each is explicitly listed by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Diabetic neuropathy is the chronic and progressive deterioration of which system throughout the body?","options":["The nervous system","The circulatory system","The respiratory system","The musculoskeletal system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diabetic neuropathy is chronic and progressive deterioration of the nervous system throughout the body.  Why B/C/D wrong: Neuropathy affects the nervous system, not the circulatory, respiratory, or musculoskeletal systems."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Major complications of diabetic neuropathy include development of non-healing ulcers of the feet, gastric paresis, and which other dysfunction?","options":["Erectile dysfunction","Hyperthyroidism","Adrenal insufficiency","Hepatic failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states major complications include development of non-healing ulcers of the feet, gastric paresis, and erectile dysfunction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These endocrine/hepatic disorders are not listed as complications of diabetic neuropathy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In diabetic neuropathy, focal neuropathy may involve damage to a cranial nerve, especially cranial nerves III & VI, causing:","options":["Diplopia","Anosmia","Tinnitus","Facial palsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states focal neuropathy may involve damage to cranial nerve III & VI causing diplopia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anosmia (CN I), tinnitus (CN VIII), and facial palsy (CN VII) involve other cranial nerves, not those named here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In diabetic neuropathy, peripheral (sensory) neuropathy affects which body areas, and autonomic neuropathy affects which systems?","options":["Peripheral neuropathy affects distal portion of nerves (e.g., feet); autonomic neuropathy affects all body nerves","Peripheral neuropathy affects only the brain; autonomic neuropathy affects only muscles","Peripheral neuropathy affects the cranial nerves only; autonomic neuropathy affects bones","Both affect only the spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states peripheral neuropathy (sensory) affects distal portion of nerves mostly lower extremities, and autonomic neuropathy affects all body nerves and symptoms depend on affected body system.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the distribution given by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In diabetic autonomic neuropathy, cardiac denervation similar to a \"heart rate does not respond to change in oxygenation\" can lead to which clinical finding?","options":["Orthostatic hypotension","Hypertensive crisis","Bradycardia with hypoxia only","Atrial fibrillation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states cardiac denervation syndrome—heart rate does not respond to change in oxygenation—needs so orthostatic hypotension can occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source links cardiac autonomic neuropathy to orthostatic hypotension, not hypertensive crisis, isolated bradycardia, or atrial fibrillation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Nursing care for diabetic neuropathy includes which patient-education measures? (Select all that apply)","options":["Early prevention by control of hypertension & hyperglycaemia","Use lubricants when doing intercourse to prevent dyspareunia","Careful foot care required to prevent trauma","Walk barefoot to improve sensation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists early prevention by controlling hypertension & hyperglycaemia, using lubricants during intercourse to prevent dyspareunia, and careful foot care to prevent trauma.  Why D wrong: Walking barefoot is unsafe in neuropathy (loss of protective sensation) and is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The maximum level of ACTH and cortisol is found at which time of day?","options":["Early morning","Late evening","Midnight","Noon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Fast Rapid Revision key point states maximum level of ACTH & cortisol is at the time of early morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cortisol/ACTH peak in the early morning, not evening, midnight, or noon."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The first sign of pubertal changes in boys is:","options":["Enlargement of testes","Pubic hair","Voice change","Growth spurt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the first sign of pubertal changes in boys is enlargement of testes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pubic hair, voice change, and growth spurt occur later, not as the first pubertal sign."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The first sign of pubertal changes in girls is:","options":["Thelarche (enlargement of the breast)","Menarche","Pubic hair","Axillary hair"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the first sign of pubertal changes in girls is thelarche (enlargement of the breast).  Why B/C/D wrong: Menarche, pubic hair, and axillary hair follow thelarche, not precede it."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Hyposecretion of growth hormone in children is known as:","options":["Dwarfism","Gigantism","Acromegaly","Cretinism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states hyposecretion of growth hormone in children is known as dwarfism.  Why B wrong: Gigantism is excess GH in children. Why C wrong: Acromegaly is excess GH in adults. Why D wrong: Cretinism is thyroxine deficiency in infants."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Hypersecretion of growth hormone in adults is known as:","options":["Acromegaly","Gigantism","Dwarfism","Cushing's syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states hypersecretion of growth hormone in adults is known as acromegaly.  Why B wrong: Gigantism is GH excess in children. Why C wrong: Dwarfism is GH deficiency in children. Why D wrong: Cushing's is cortisol excess."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The disease caused by deficiency of corticosteroid hormone is:","options":["Addison's disease","Cushing's syndrome","Grave's disease","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the disease caused by deficiency of corticosteroid hormone is Addison's disease.  Why B wrong: Cushing's is corticosteroid excess. Why C wrong: Grave's is hyperthyroidism. Why D wrong: Diabetes insipidus is ADH deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Deficiency of ADH (Anti-Diuretic Hormone) leads to:","options":["Diabetes insipidus","SIADH","Diabetes mellitus","Cushing's syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states deficiency of ADH (Anti-Diuretic Hormone) leads to diabetes insipidus.  Why B wrong: SIADH is ADH overproduction. Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to ADH deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"HbA1C estimates the average sugar level of the last:","options":["90 days (3 months)","30 days (1 month)","7 days (1 week)","180 days (6 months)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states HbA1C indicates estimate average sugar level of the last 90 days (3 months).  Why B/C/D wrong: HbA1C reflects roughly 3 months of glycaemic control, not 1 month, 1 week, or 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The most common cause of meningoencephalitis is:","options":["Enterovirus","Streptococcus","Mycobacterium","Candida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the most common cause of meningoencephalitis is enterovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common cause stated by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The most common site of neural tube defects is the:","options":["Lumbosacral region","Cervical region","Thoracic region","Cranial region"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the most common site of neural tube defects is the lumbosacral region.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common site given by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The action or effect of ADH (antidiuretic hormone) is:","options":["Decreased urine output","Increased urine output","Increased blood glucose","Decreased serum sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the action or effect of ADH hormone is decreased urine output.  Why B wrong: ADH conserves water, decreasing (not increasing) urine output. Why C/D wrong: ADH's primary effect is on water reabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The pineal gland is located in the:","options":["Epithalamus","Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Medulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the pineal gland is located in the epithalamus.  Why B/C/D wrong: The pineal gland lies in the epithalamus, not the hypothalamus, cerebellum, or medulla."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Underproduction (deficiency) of thyroxine hormone in adults can lead to:","options":["Myxoedema coma","Grave's disease","Acromegaly","Thyrotoxicosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states underproduction of thyroxine hormone leads to myxoedema coma.  Why B/D wrong: Grave's and thyrotoxicosis are hyperthyroid states. Why C wrong: Acromegaly is a GH disorder."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Deficiency of thyroxine hormone in children and infants is known as:","options":["Cretinism","Dwarfism","Myxoedema","Gigantism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states deficiency of thyroxine hormone in children & infant is known as cretinism.  Why B wrong: Dwarfism is GH deficiency. Why C wrong: Myxoedema is adult hypothyroidism. Why D wrong: Gigantism is GH excess."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Exophthalmos of the eyes is a sign of which disease?","options":["Grave's disease","Addison's disease","Cushing's syndrome","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states exophthalmos of the eyes is the sign of Grave's disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not associated with exophthalmos as their classical sign."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH) is stimulated by which condition?","options":["Hypocalcaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hyponatraemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states secretion of parathyroid hormone is stimulated by hypocalcaemia (low blood calcium).  Why B wrong: Hypercalcaemia suppresses PTH. Why C/D wrong: Potassium and sodium levels are not the primary PTH stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"World Diabetes Day is celebrated on:","options":["14 November","1 December","7 April","24 March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states World Diabetes Day is celebrated on 14 November.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates correspond to other health observances, not World Diabetes Day."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The most rapid absorption of subcutaneous insulin occurs at the:","options":["Abdomen (the belly)","Thigh","Upper arm","Buttock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the most rapid absorption of insulin occurs at the abdomen (the belly).  Why B/C/D wrong: Thigh, arm, and buttock have slower insulin absorption than the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Rotation of the injection site while administering insulin prevents:","options":["Lipodystrophy","Hyperglycaemia","Hypoglycaemia","Insulin resistance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states rotation of injection site while administering insulin prevents lipodystrophy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Site rotation specifically prevents local fat tissue changes (lipodystrophy), not the glycaemic or resistance outcomes listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which electrolyte needs to be replaced in DKA?","options":["Potassium","Calcium","Magnesium","Phosphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the electrolyte that needs to be replaced in DKA is potassium.  Why B/C/D wrong: While other electrolytes may shift, the source specifies potassium as the electrolyte requiring replacement in DKA."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In a client with diabetes insipidus, the serum sodium level is:","options":["Increased","Decreased","Unchanged","Variable with no pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states in a client with diabetes insipidus, serum sodium level is increased (due to water loss).  Why B/C/D wrong: ADH deficiency causes water loss and concentrated serum, raising sodium, so it is not decreased or unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The water deprivation test is used for the diagnosis of which disease?","options":["Diabetes insipidus","Diabetes mellitus","Cushing's syndrome","Addison's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the water deprivation test is used for the diagnosis of diabetes insipidus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not diagnosed by the water deprivation test."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"SIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH secretion) is caused by:","options":["Overproduction of ADH","Deficiency of ADH","Overproduction of cortisol","Deficiency of thyroxine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the cause of SIADH is overproduction of ADH.  Why B wrong: ADH deficiency causes diabetes insipidus. Why C/D wrong: These hormones are not the cause of SIADH."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The emergency or acute \"stress\" hormone (cortisol) is secreted by which gland?","options":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland","Pineal gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the emergency or acute hormone is secreted by the adrenal gland (cortisol).  Why B/C/D wrong: Cortisol is produced by the adrenal cortex, not the thyroid, pituitary, or pineal glands."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Moon face, hirsutism and truncal obesity are the classical signs of:","options":["Cushing's syndrome","Addison's disease","Grave's disease","Myxoedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states moon face, hirsutism and truncal obesity are the classical sign of Cushing's syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These features are characteristic of cortisol excess (Cushing's), not Addison's, Grave's, or myxoedema."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Metformin is usually a first-line medication used for the treatment of:","options":["Type 2 diabetes (NIDDM)","Type 1 diabetes","Diabetes insipidus","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states metformin is usually a first-line medication used for treatment of type 2 diabetes (NIDDM).  Why B/C/D wrong: Type 1 requires insulin; metformin is not used for diabetes insipidus or hyperthyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Metformin should be discontinued how long before surgery?","options":["48 hours before surgery","12 hours before surgery","1 week before surgery","On the morning of surgery only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states metformin should be discontinued 48 hours before surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings do not match the 48-hour preoperative discontinuation stated."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The short-term or acute complications of diabetes are:","options":["Hypoglycaemia, DKA and HHNS","Retinopathy, neuropathy and nephropathy","Cataract and glaucoma","Coronary artery disease and stroke"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states short-term or acute complications of diabetes are hypoglycaemia, DKA and HHNS.  Why B wrong: Retinopathy/neuropathy/nephropathy are chronic (long-term) complications. Why C/D wrong: These are not the acute complications listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The long-term or chronic complications of diabetes are:","options":["Retinopathy, neuropathy and nephropathy","Hypoglycaemia, DKA and HHNS","Lipodystrophy and hypocalcaemia","SIADH and Cushing's syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states long-term or chronic complications of diabetes are retinopathy, neuropathy and nephropathy.  Why B wrong: Those are acute complications. Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated conditions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The classical symptoms of hypoglycaemia are:","options":["Sweating, tremors, irritability or anxiety, hunger and palpitation","Polyuria, polydipsia and polyphagia","Fruity breath and Kussmaul's respiration","Moon face and hirsutism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states classical symptoms of hypoglycaemia are sweating, tremors, irritability or anxiety, hunger and palpitation.  Why B wrong: The 3 P's are hyperglycaemia symptoms. Why C wrong: Fruity breath/Kussmaul's are DKA features. Why D wrong: Moon face/hirsutism are Cushing's signs."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The classical signs of hypocalcaemia are:","options":["Chvostek sign and Trousseau sign","Kernig sign and Brudzinski sign","Cullen sign and Grey-Turner sign","Babinski sign and Romberg sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states classical signs of hypocalcaemia are Chvostek sign and Trousseau sign.  Why B wrong: Kernig/Brudzinski are meningeal signs. Why C wrong: Cullen/Grey-Turner are signs of haemorrhagic pancreatitis. Why D wrong: Babinski/Romberg are neurological signs."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Obesity is a major risk factor for which diabetes type / endocrine condition?","options":["Diabetes type 2 (NIDDM)","Diabetes type 1 (IDDM)","Diabetes insipidus","Addison's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states obesity is a major risk factor for diabetes type 2 (NIDDM).  Why B/C/D wrong: Type 1, diabetes insipidus, and Addison's disease are not the obesity-related conditions named."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which endocrine gland maintains the body's metabolic rate?","options":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland","Pituitary gland","Parathyroid gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states the thyroid gland maintains the metabolic rate.  Why B/C/D wrong: While other glands influence metabolism, the source names the thyroid gland as the one maintaining metabolic rate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Calcitonin and parathormone (PTH) are the two hormones involved in calcium homeostasis. Calcitonin acts to:","options":["Maintain bone calcium level by bringing calcium from the intestine to bone","Raise blood calcium by breaking down bone","Increase intestinal calcium excretion","Stimulate osteoclasts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states calcitonin maintains bone calcium level, bringing the calcium from intestine to bone.  Why B/D wrong: Breaking down bone and stimulating osteoclasts describe PTH, not calcitonin. Why C wrong: Not the calcitonin action described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Parathormone (PTH) maintains blood calcium level and is released whenever blood calcium drops; it enhances blood calcium by:","options":["Stimulating osteoclasts, which break down bone to release calcium into the bloodstream","Stimulating osteoblasts to build bone","Increasing renal calcium excretion","Inhibiting vitamin D activation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states parathormone maintains blood calcium level, released whenever blood calcium level drops, and enhances blood calcium level by stimulating osteoclasts, which break down bones to release calcium into the bloodstream.  Why B/C/D wrong: PTH stimulates osteoclasts (not osteoblasts), conserves renal calcium, and activates vitamin D—opposite to these options."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The ear is the organ of hearing and is also involved in:","options":["Balance (equilibrium)","Smell","Taste","Vision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the ear is the organ of hearing and is also involved in balance; the ear is an organ of hearing and equilibrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: Smell, taste, and vision are functions of other sensory organs."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The instrument (device) used for examination of the ear is the:","options":["Otoscope","Ophthalmoscope","Laryngoscope","Bronchoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the otoscope is a device used for examination of the ear.  Why B/C/D wrong: The ophthalmoscope examines the eye, laryngoscope the larynx, and bronchoscope the airways."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The nerve supply of the ear is the:","options":["Acoustic nerve (CN VIII)","Optic nerve (CN II)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the nerve supply of the ear is the acoustic nerve (CN VIII).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cranial nerves supply other structures, not the primary nerve supply of the ear stated here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The blood supply of the ear is the posterior auricular artery, which is a branch of the:","options":["External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","Subclavian artery","Vertebral artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blood supply is posterior auricular artery, which is a branch of external carotid artery.  Why B/C/D wrong: The posterior auricular artery arises from the external carotid, not the internal carotid, subclavian, or vertebral arteries."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The ear is classified into which three divisions?","options":["Outer/external ear, middle ear (tympanic cavity), internal ear/labyrinth","Pinna, cochlea, brainstem","External canal, eardrum, optic nerve","Auricle, ossicles, retina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page classifies the ear in three divisions: outer or external ear, middle ear or tympanic cavity, and internal ear or labyrinth.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mix or omit divisions and include non-ear structures."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The external ear consists of which structures?","options":["Pinna or auricle and external auditory meatus","Tympanic membrane and ossicles","Cochlea and vestibule","Eustachian tube and oval window"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the external ear consists of pinna or auricle and external auditory meatus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to the middle and inner ear, not the external ear."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The pinna (auricle) is made up of which type of cartilage?","options":["Fibro-elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrous bone","Calcified cartilage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the pinna is made up of fibro-elastic cartilage.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies fibro-elastic cartilage, not hyaline cartilage, bone, or calcified cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The main function of the pinna (auricle) is to:","options":["Collect and reflect sound waves into the external auditory canal","Produce earwax","Equalize middle ear pressure","Convert sound to nerve impulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pinna or auricle collects and reflects the sound waves into the external auditory canal.  Why B wrong: Earwax is produced by ceruminous glands. Why C wrong: Pressure equalization is the Eustachian tube's role. Why D wrong: Sound transduction occurs in the cochlea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The external auditory meatus is an S-shaped tube that extends from the auricle (pinna) to the:","options":["Tympanic membrane (eardrum)","Oval window","Cochlea","Eustachian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the external auditory meatus is an S-shaped tube extending from the auricle or pinna to the tympanic membrane (or auricle to eardrum).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are middle/inner ear structures beyond the eardrum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The length of the external auditory meatus is about ___, and its diameter is about ___.","options":["2.5 cm long; 0.7 cm diameter","4 cm long; 3 mm diameter","1 cm long; 1 cm diameter","5 cm long; 2 cm diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the length of the external auditory meatus is about 2.5 cm and 0.7 cm in diameter.  Why B wrong: 4 cm long / 3 mm describes the Eustachian tube. Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The external auditory meatus secretes wax from which glands?","options":["Ceruminous glands (and oil from sebaceous glands)","Sweat glands","Salivary glands","Lacrimal glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it secrets wax from the ceruminous glands and oil from sebaceous gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sweat, salivary, and lacrimal glands do not produce earwax."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The ear lobe of the external ear is composed of fibrous and adipose tissues richly supplied with blood. The hair and cerumen (wax) in the ear help to:","options":["Prevent dust and foreign objects from entering the ear","Amplify sound waves","Equalize ear pressure","Generate nerve impulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the ear lobe is composed of fibrous and adipose tissues richly supplied with blood, and the hair and cerumen (wax) help to prevent dust and foreign objects from entering the ear.  Why B/C/D wrong: Amplification, pressure equalization, and impulse generation are not the role of hair/cerumen described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The middle ear is also known as the:","options":["Tympanic cavity","Labyrinth","Cochlea","Vestibule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the middle ear is also known as tympanic cavity.  Why B/C/D wrong: Labyrinth, cochlea, and vestibule are inner-ear structures."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The middle ear is an irregular-shaped air-filled cavity within which bone?","options":["Temporal cavity (temporal bone)","Frontal bone","Ethmoid bone","Sphenoid bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the middle ear is an irregular shaped air-filled cavity within the temporal cavity.  Why B/C/D wrong: The middle ear lies within the temporal bone, not the frontal, ethmoid, or sphenoid bones."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The lateral wall of the middle ear is formed by the:","options":["Tympanic membrane","Oval window","Round window","Eustachian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the lateral wall of the middle ear is formed by the tympanic membrane.  Why B/C/D wrong: The oval and round windows and Eustachian tube are other middle-ear structures, not the lateral wall."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The middle ear includes which of the following structures? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tympanic membrane","Eustachian tube","Ear ossicles","Oval window","Cochlea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the middle ear includes the tympanic membrane, Eustachian tube, ear ossicles, and oval window.  Why E wrong: The cochlea is part of the internal ear, not the middle ear."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The tympanic membrane (eardrum) is a thin transparent layer that receives sound vibrations from the outer ear and transmits them to the:","options":["Auditory ossicles","Cochlea directly","Eustachian tube","Auditory nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the tympanic membrane receives sound vibrations from the outer ear and transmits them to the auditory ossicles.  Why B/C/D wrong: The eardrum transmits to the ossicles first; it does not transmit directly to the cochlea, Eustachian tube, or auditory nerve."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The tympanic membrane separates the external ear from the middle ear; which instrument is used to examine it?","options":["Otoscope","Audiometer","Tuning fork","Stethoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it separates external ear from middle ear, and otoscope is used to examine it.  Why B/C/D wrong: The audiometer measures hearing thresholds, tuning fork tests conduction, and stethoscope auscultates—none is used to visualize the eardrum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Tearing of the eardrum is called:","options":["Perforation of eardrum","Myringotomy","Otosclerosis","Tympanoplasty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states tearing of eardrum is called perforation of eardrum.  Why B wrong: Myringotomy is a surgical incision. Why C wrong: Otosclerosis is bony fusion of ossicles. Why D wrong: Tympanoplasty is surgical repair."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The tympanic membrane has a role in:","options":["Sound transmission and amplification","Pressure equalization only","Balance and equilibrium","Wax production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states this membrane has a role in sound transmission and amplification.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pressure equalization is the Eustachian tube's role, balance is the inner ear's role, and wax is produced in the external canal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The normal colour/shape of the tympanic membrane is:","options":["Pearly grey, shiny, translucent with no bulging or retraction","Bright red and bulging","Yellow and opaque","Blue and retracted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states colour/shape: pearly grey, shiny, translucent with no bulging & retraction.  Why B/C/D wrong: A red, yellow, or blue/retracted membrane indicates pathology, not the normal appearance described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Myringotomy is defined as:","options":["A surgical incision of the tympanic membrane, commonly used to drain pus present in the middle ear","Surgical removal of the ossicles","Repair of the cochlea","Removal of cerumen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states myringotomy is a surgical incision of tympanic membrane, which commonly used to drain pus present in the middle ear.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other procedures, not a tympanic-membrane incision."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The Eustachian tube is also known as the auditory tube or:","options":["Pharyngotympanic tube","Cochlear duct","Semicircular canal","Vestibular aqueduct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the Eustachian tube is also known as auditory tube or pharyngotympanic tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct inner-ear structures, not synonyms for the Eustachian tube."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The Eustachian tube is a 4 cm long canal that connects the middle ear to the:","options":["Nasopharynx","Oropharynx","Larynx","External auditory canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the Eustachian tube is a 4 cm long canal that connects the middle ear to the nasopharynx.  Why B/C/D wrong: The Eustachian tube opens into the nasopharynx, not the oropharynx, larynx, or external canal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The main function of the Eustachian tube is to:","options":["Control (equalize) the pressure within the middle ear","Transmit sound to the cochlea","Produce earwax","Generate nerve impulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it controlled the pressure within the middle ear.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sound transmission, wax production, and impulse generation are functions of other structures."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the source, the Eustachian tube length is about:","options":["35 mm long or 3 mm in diameter","25 mm long or 7 mm in diameter","40 mm long or 5 mm in diameter","10 mm long or 2 mm in diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states its length is about 35 mm long or 3 mm in diameter.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dimensions. (Note: the source also gives the canal length as \"4 cm long\" earlier, which is consistent with ~35-40 mm; the explicit measurement bullet states 35 mm long or 3 mm in diameter.)"},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"How many ear ossicles are present in EACH ear, and what is the total number of ear ossicles?","options":["3 in each ear; total six","2 in each ear; total four","6 in each ear; total twelve","1 in each ear; total two"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states there are 3 ear ossicles in each ear, total ear ossicles are six.  Why B/C/D wrong: These counts do not match the source (3 per ear, six total)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The malleus is also known as the \"hammer bone\" because it is hammer-shaped; it is connected to the inner surface of the:","options":["Tympanic membrane","Oval window","Cochlea","Eustachian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states malleus is hammer shaped bone so it is also known as hammer bone, connected to inner surface of the tympanic membrane.  Why B/C/D wrong: The malleus attaches to the eardrum, not the oval window, cochlea, or Eustachian tube."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The incus is the middle ossicle and is also known as the \"anvil bone\" because it is anvil-shaped; it articulates with the head of the:","options":["Malleus","Stapes","Cochlea","Tympanic membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states incus is middle anvil shaped bone, also known as anvil bone, and it articulates with head of malleus.  Why B/C/D wrong: The incus articulates with the malleus head (and with the stapes on its other end), but the source specifically states it articulates with the head of malleus; it does not articulate with cochlea or tympanic membrane."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The stapes is the smallest bone of the body; its foot plate fits into which structure?","options":["The oval window","The round window","The tympanic membrane","The cochlear duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stapes is the smallest bone of the body, its head articulates with incus and its foot plate fit into the oval window; it is stirrup shaped bone, also known as stirrup bone.  Why B/C/D wrong: The footplate of the stapes fits into the oval window, not the round window, eardrum, or cochlear duct."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The smallest bone of the body is the:","options":["Stapes","Malleus","Incus","Hyoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stapes is the smallest bone of the body (stirrup bone).  Why B/C wrong: Malleus and incus are larger ossicles. Why D wrong: The hyoid is not the smallest bone."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Sound intensity is measured in which unit, and frequency is measured in which unit?","options":["Intensity in decibel (dB); frequency in Hertz (Hz)","Intensity in Hz; frequency in dB","Both in decibels","Intensity in cm; frequency in mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sound intensity is measured in decibel (dB) and frequency is measured in Hz.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the units of sound intensity and frequency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The audible range of sound waves for humans is approximately:","options":["20-20,000 Hz","1-100 Hz","200-2,000 Hz","20,000-200,000 Hz"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states audible range of sound waves are 20-20,000 Hz.  Why B/C wrong: These are too narrow. Why D wrong: Above 20,000 Hz is ultrasound, beyond the audible range."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Sound waves greater than 20,000 Hz frequency are known as:","options":["Ultrasound","Infrasound","Audible sound","Supersonic sound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sound waves greater than 20,000 Hz frequency are known as ultrasound.  Why B wrong: Infrasound is less than 20 Hz. Why C wrong: Audible sound is 20-20,000 Hz. Why D wrong: Not the term used by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Sound waves less than 20 Hz are known as:","options":["Infrasound","Ultrasound","Audible sound","White noise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ultrasound and less than 20 Hz is infrasound.  Why B wrong: Ultrasound is above 20,000 Hz. Why C wrong: Audible is 20-20,000 Hz. Why D wrong: Not a term used by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In ultrasound (USG), sound waves of which frequency are used to produce an image of an organ or tissue?","options":[">1,00,000 Hz","<20 Hz","20-20,000 Hz","50,000 Hz"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in USG, sound waves >1,00,000 Hz frequency are used to produce an image of an organ or tissue.  Why B/C/D wrong: These frequencies do not match the stated USG imaging frequency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Sound intensity more than how many decibels for a long time can cause deafness?","options":["85 dB","25 dB","55 dB","120 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sound intensity more than 85 dB for long time cause deafness.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the 85 dB threshold; 25 and 55 dB are within the degree-of-hearing-loss table ranges, and 120 dB is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the \"Degree of Hearing Loss\" table, what is the range (in dB) for NORMAL hearing?","options":["0-20 dB","25-40 dB","40-55 dB",">90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal hearing loss as 0-20 dB.  Why B wrong: 25-40 dB is mild hearing loss. Why C wrong: 40-55 dB is moderate. Why D wrong: >90 dB is profound."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the \"Degree of Hearing Loss\" table, MILD hearing loss corresponds to which range?","options":["25-40 dB","0-20 dB","40-55 dB","70-90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists mild hearing loss as 25-40 dB.  Why B wrong: 0-20 dB is normal. Why C wrong: 40-55 dB is moderate. Why D wrong: 70-90 dB is severe."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the \"Degree of Hearing Loss\" table, MODERATE hearing loss corresponds to which range?","options":["40-55 dB","25-40 dB","70-90 dB",">90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists moderate hearing loss as 40-55 dB.  Why B wrong: 25-40 dB is mild. Why C wrong: 70-90 dB is severe. Why D wrong: >90 dB is profound."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the \"Degree of Hearing Loss\" table, SEVERE hearing loss corresponds to which range?","options":["70-90 dB","40-55 dB",">90 dB","25-40 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists severe hearing loss as 70-90 dB.  Why B wrong: 40-55 dB is moderate. Why C wrong: >90 dB is profound. Why D wrong: 25-40 dB is mild."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"According to the \"Degree of Hearing Loss\" table, PROFOUND hearing loss corresponds to which range?","options":[">90 dB","70-90 dB","40-55 dB","0-20 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists profound hearing loss as >90 dB.  Why B wrong: 70-90 dB is severe. Why C wrong: 40-55 dB is moderate. Why D wrong: 0-20 dB is normal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In the physiology of hearing, sound waves are the stimuli for the receptors of sound waves; the range of human hearing is approximately:","options":["20 to 20000 Hz","1 to 100 Hz","50 to 5000 Hz","20000 to 200000 Hz"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sound waves are the stimuli for the receptors of sound waves, and the range of human hearing is approximately 20 to 20000 Hz.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated human hearing range."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In the physiology of hearing pathway, after the sound waves are received by the external auditory meatus, the next step is:","options":["The eardrum vibrates","The auditory nerve transmits impulses to the brain","Hair cells of the organ of Corti are stimulated","Fluid moves by pressure waves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pathway flowchart shows sound waves received by external auditory meatus → eardrum vibrates → malleus, incus and stapes amplify the vibrations.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are later steps in the pathway, not the step immediately after the meatus receives sound."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In the physiology of hearing pathway, after the malleus, incus and stapes amplify the vibrations, the vibrations reach the inner ear through which structure?","options":["The oval window","The round window","The Eustachian tube","The tympanic membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pathway flowchart shows the ossicles amplify the vibrations → vibrations reach inner ear through oval window → movement of fluid by pressure waves.  Why B/C/D wrong: The oval window is the entry point to the inner ear; the round, Eustachian, and tympanic structures are not the route described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"In the hearing pathway, the hair cells of the organ of Corti are stimulated by vibration in the basilar membrane, after which:","options":["The auditory nerve transmits impulses to the brain","The eardrum vibrates","The oval window opens","Cerumen is secreted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pathway shows movement of fluid by pressure waves → hair cells of organ of Corti get stimulated by vibration in the basilar membrane → auditory nerve transmits impulses to brain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are earlier/unrelated steps in the pathway."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The inner (internal) ear / labyrinth contains the organs of:","options":["Hearing and balance (equilibrium)","Hearing only","Smell and taste","Pressure equalization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the inner or internal ear / labyrinths contain the organs of the hearing and balance or equilibrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: The inner ear serves both hearing and balance, not hearing alone, smell/taste, or pressure equalization."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The inner ear (labyrinth) describes two parts; these are:","options":["Bony labyrinth and membranous labyrinth","Outer and inner labyrinth","Tympanic and vestibular labyrinth","Cochlear and ossicular labyrinth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it describes two parts: bony labyrinths and membranous labyrinths.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two parts named by the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Which of the following are the three parts of the bony labyrinth? (Select all that apply)","options":["The vestibule","The semi-circular canals","The cochlea","The oval window","The Eustachian tube"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states there are three parts of the bony labyrinth: the vestibule, the semi-circular canals (three tubes), and the cochlea.  Why D/E wrong: The oval window and Eustachian tube are middle-ear structures, not parts of the bony labyrinth."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The vestibule of the bony labyrinth contains the utricle & saccule, which are important in balance & equilibrium, and is innervated by the vestibular branch of which cranial nerve?","options":["8th cranial nerve","5th cranial nerve","7th cranial nerve","9th cranial nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the vestibule contains utricle & saccule which are important in balance & equilibrium, and it has vestibular branch of the 8th cranial nerve, which are important for equilibrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: The vestibular (and cochlear) branches belong to the 8th cranial nerve, not the 5th, 7th, or 9th."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The semi-circular canal of the bony labyrinth consists of how many tubes?","options":["Three tubes","Two tubes","Four tubes","One tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the semi-circular canal: they are three tubes.  Why B/C/D wrong: There are three semicircular canals, not two, four, or one."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The cochlea is the organ of Corti and is the sense organ of hearing; it has the cochlear branch of which cranial nerve?","options":["8th cranial nerve (vestibulocochlear nerve)","7th cranial nerve (facial)","5th cranial nerve (trigeminal)","2nd cranial nerve (optic)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the cochlea has organ of Corti, is the sense organ of hearing, and has cochlear branch of the 8th cranial nerve or vestibulocochlear nerve for hearing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hearing is served by the 8th cranial nerve, not the facial, trigeminal, or optic nerves."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The joint between the incus and stapes bones is known as the:","options":["Incudostapedial joint","Incudomalleolar joint","Stapediovestibular joint","Temporomandibular joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" note states the joint between incus and stapes bone is known as incudostapedial joint.  Why B wrong: That is the incus-malleus joint. Why C/D wrong: These are not the named incus-stapes joint."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"The joint between the malleus and incus bones is known as the:","options":["Incudomalleolar joint","Incudostapedial joint","Malleovestibular joint","Sternoclavicular joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" note states the joint between malleus and incus bone is known as incudomalleolar joint.  Why B wrong: That is the incus-stapes joint. Why C/D wrong: These are not the named malleus-incus joint."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Perilymph is the fluid present in the scala tympani and scala vestibuli compartments of the cochlea; this fluid is like:","options":["ECF, which is high in sodium and low in potassium","ICF, which is high in potassium and low in sodium","Plasma, which is high in protein","CSF, which is high in glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states perilymph is the fluid present in the scala tympani and scala vestibuli compartment of the cochlea; this fluid is like ECF, which is high in sodium and low in potassium.  Why B wrong: That describes endolymph (ICF-like). Why C/D wrong: These misstate perilymph's composition."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Endolymph is the fluid present in the scala media or membranes cochlea; this fluid is like:","options":["ICF, high in potassium and low in sodium","ECF, high in sodium and low in potassium","Plasma, high in protein","CSF, high in glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states endolymph is the fluid present in the scala media or membranes cochlea; this fluid is like ICF, high in potassium and low in sodium.  Why B wrong: That describes perilymph (ECF-like). Why C/D wrong: These misstate endolymph's composition."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Otosclerosis is best described as:","options":["Bony fusion between the foot plate of the stapes and the margin of the fenestra vestibule, leading to loss of hearing","A surgical incision of the tympanic membrane","A tumor of the Schwann cells of the 8th cranial nerve","Tearing of the eardrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" note states otosclerosis is bony fusion between foot plate of the stapes and the margin of the fenestra vestibule; this leads to loss of hearing.  Why B wrong: That is myringotomy. Why C wrong: That is acoustic neuroma. Why D wrong: That is perforation of the eardrum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Acoustic neuroma is best described as:","options":["A tumor of the Schwann cells of the 8th cranial nerve","Bony fusion of the stapes footplate","A surgical incision of the eardrum","Inflammation of the middle ear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" note states acoustic neuroma is the tumor of the Schwann cells of the 8th cranial nerve.  Why B wrong: That describes otosclerosis. Why C wrong: That is myringotomy. Why D wrong: That describes otitis media, not acoustic neuroma."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Self-monitoring of blood glucose & hypoglycaemia…","question":"Regarding tomography as a diagnostic test for the ear, which of the following statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tomography may be performed with or without contrast medium","Tomography is especially helpful in the diagnosis of acoustic tumors","Tomography assesses the mastoid, middle ear and inner ear structures","During the procedure, the client must still lie in a supine position","Tomography uses high-frequency sound waves only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page states tomography may be performed with or without contrast medium, is especially helpful in the diagnosis of acoustic tumors, assesses the mastoid/middle ear/inner ear structures, and during the procedure the client must still lie in a supine position.  Why E wrong: The source does not describe tomography as using high-frequency sound waves only; that statement is not supported."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Audiometry is performed to measure which of the following?","options":["Visual acuity","Hearing acuity or activity of the ear","Tympanic membrane pressure","Olfactory function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Audiometry is the test used to measure hearing acuity or the activity of the ear.  Why A wrong: Visual acuity is assessed with vision charts, not audiometry.  Why C wrong: Middle-ear pressure is measured by tympanometry/impedance audiometry, not pure audiometry as defined here.  Why D wrong: Olfactory (smell) function is unrelated to audiometry."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"What is the normal range of human hearing frequency?","options":["2–200 Hz","200–2000 Hz","20–20,000 Hz","20,000–40,000 Hz"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal range of human ear hearing is 20–20,000 Hz.  Why A/B wrong: These ranges are far below the stated normal hearing range.  Why D wrong: This is the ultrasonic range, above normal human hearing."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"An otoscopic examination is best described as:","options":["Measuring hearing thresholds with tones","Visualization of the inner ear with a probe","Visualization of the inner ear by using an instrument known as an otoscope","Recording the electrical activity of the cochlea"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Otoscopic examination is the visualization of the inner ear using an instrument known as an otoscope.  Why A wrong: That describes audiometry.  Why B wrong: The instrument used is the otoscope, specifically named.  Why D wrong: That describes electrocochleography, not otoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"On otoscopic examination, the normal healthy tympanic membrane appears:","options":["Red and bulging","Pearly grey","Yellow and retracted","Perforated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal color of a healthy tympanic membrane is pearly grey.  Why A wrong: Red, bulging membrane suggests acute otitis media/infection.  Why C wrong: Yellow/retracted is abnormal, suggesting fluid or negative pressure.  Why D wrong: A perforation is a pathological finding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"When examining the ear with an otoscope in an adult, the pinna should be pulled:","options":["Down and back","Up and back","Straight forward","Down and forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adults the pinna is pulled up and back to straighten the ear canal.  Why A wrong: Down and back is used for children younger than 3 years.  Why C/D wrong: These do not align the auditory canal for adult otoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"When performing otoscopy on a child younger than 3 years, the pinna should be pulled:","options":["Up and back","Down and back","Straight up","Forward and up"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children younger than 3 years, the pinna is pulled down and back.  Why A wrong: Up and back is the adult technique.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the recommended pediatric technique."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The Weber test is used primarily to assess:","options":["Air conduction only","Lateralization of sound","Tympanic membrane mobility","Frequency discrimination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Weber test is the baseline test to assess lateralization of sound.  Why A wrong: It compares bone conduction between ears, not air conduction alone.  Why C wrong: Membrane mobility is assessed by tympanometry/pneumatic otoscopy.  Why D wrong: It does not measure frequency discrimination."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"In performing the Weber test, the examiner places a vibrating tuning fork at which location?","options":["On the mastoid process","In front of the external auditory canal","In the center of the forehead or on the head of the client","Behind the auricle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vibrating tuning fork is placed in the center of the forehead or head of the client for the Weber test.  Why A wrong: The mastoid placement is part of the Rinne test (bone conduction).  Why B wrong: External canal placement tests air conduction (Rinne).  Why D wrong: Behind the auricle is not the standard Weber placement."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"A person with normal hearing will hear the Weber test sound:","options":["Louder in the affected ear","Equally in both ears, with the sound originating from the center of the head","Only in the better ear","Not at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With normal hearing, sound is heard equally in both ears and originates from the center of the head.  Why A wrong: Lateralization to the affected ear indicates conductive loss.  Why C wrong: Lateralization to the better ear indicates sensorineural loss.  Why D wrong: A person with normal hearing does perceive the sound."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"In the Weber test, lateralization of sound to the affected ear typically indicates:","options":["Sensorineural hearing loss in that ear","Conductive hearing loss in that ear","Normal hearing","Total deafness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Weber test detects unilateral conductive hearing loss; sound lateralizes to the affected (conductive-loss) ear.  Why A wrong: With sensorineural loss, sound lateralizes to the unaffected/better ear.  Why C wrong: Normal hearing gives equal/central perception.  Why D wrong: Lateralization indicates a specific loss type, not total deafness."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The Rinne test is used to:","options":["Assess sound lateralization","Differentiate between conductive hearing loss and sensorineural hearing loss","Measure middle-ear pressure","Detect cerumen impaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Rinne test is used to differentiate between conductive and sensorineural hearing loss by comparing air and bone conduction.  Why A wrong: Lateralization is the Weber test.  Why C wrong: Pressure is measured by tympanometry.  Why D wrong: Cerumen is identified by otoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"In the Rinne test, the vibrating tuning fork is placed on which structure to test bone conduction?","options":["Forehead","External auditory meatus","Mastoid process","Tragus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For bone conduction in the Rinne test, the fork is placed on the mastoid process; for air conduction it is held near the external auditory meatus.  Why A wrong: The forehead is used in the Weber test.  Why B wrong: That position tests air conduction, not bone conduction.  Why D wrong: The tragus is not the standard bone-conduction site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which test is described as the most important factor to determine hearing loss, assessing wax, frequency, pitch, and intensity?","options":["Weber test","Rinne test","Audiometry","Otoscopy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Audiometry is the most important factor to determine hearing loss; wax, frequency, pitch, and intensity are assessed.  Why A/B wrong: Weber and Rinne are tuning-fork screening tests, not the most comprehensive determinant.  Why D wrong: Otoscopy is a visual inspection, not a hearing-threshold measurement."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Tonsils are best described as:","options":["Bone tissues of the pharynx","Lymphatic tissues whose inflammation is known as tonsillitis","Glands secreting saliva","Muscles of the soft palate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tonsils are lymphatic tissues, and their inflammation is known as tonsillitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: Tonsils are not bone, salivary gland, or muscle tissue."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which of the following is a clinical feature of tonsillitis? (SATA)","options":["Red, swollen appearance","Fever","Dysphagia","Sore throat","Bilateral hearing loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Red, swollen tonsils, fever, dysphagia, and sore throat are clinical features of tonsillitis.  Why E wrong: Bilateral hearing loss is associated with otosclerosis, not tonsillitis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The surgical removal of the tonsils is termed:","options":["Adenoidectomy","Tonsillectomy","Myringotomy","Stapedectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tonsillectomy is the surgical removal of the tonsils and is the treatment for tonsillitis.  Why A wrong: Adenoidectomy is removal of the adenoids.  Why C wrong: Myringotomy is incision of the tympanic membrane.  Why D wrong: Stapedectomy is removal of the stapes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Inflammation of the adenoids is known as:","options":["Tonsillitis","Adenoiditis","Otitis media","Pharyngitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenoids are lymphatic tissues and their inflammation is known as adenoiditis.  Why A wrong: Tonsillitis is inflammation of the tonsils.  Why C wrong: Otitis media is middle-ear inflammation.  Why D wrong: Pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharynx."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which clinical features are associated with adenoiditis? (SATA)","options":["Mouth breathing","Snoring","Foul smell in the mouth","Fever","Sound lateralization"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Mouth breathing, snoring, foul smell in the mouth, and fever are clinical features of adenoiditis.  Why E wrong: Sound lateralization is a Weber-test finding, not an adenoiditis symptom."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Adenoidectomy refers to the:","options":["Surgical removal of the tonsils","Surgical removal of the adenoids","Drainage of middle-ear fluid","Removal of cerumen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenoidectomy is the surgical removal of the adenoids, the treatment for adenoiditis.  Why A wrong: That is tonsillectomy.  Why C wrong: That describes myringotomy.  Why D wrong: That is cerumen removal/irrigation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"External otitis is best described as:","options":["Inflammation of the middle ear","Inflammatory or allergic response of the external auditory canal and auricle","Inflammation of the cochlea","Inflammation of the tonsils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: External otitis is an inflammatory or allergic response of the external auditory canal and auricles.  Why A wrong: Middle-ear inflammation is otitis media.  Why C wrong: Cochlear inflammation is not external otitis.  Why D wrong: Tonsillar inflammation is tonsillitis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Excessive swelling of the canal in external otitis can lead to which type of hearing loss?","options":["Sensorineural hearing loss","Conductive hearing loss because of obstruction","Mixed hearing loss","No hearing loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive swelling of the canal can lead to conductive hearing loss because of obstruction.  Why A wrong: Sensorineural loss involves inner-ear/nerve damage, not canal obstruction.  Why C wrong: The source specifies conductive loss from obstruction.  Why D wrong: Hearing loss does occur with significant canal swelling."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"External otitis is more common in children and is also known as:","options":["Glue ear","Swimmer's ear","Boxer's ear","Surfer's ear"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: External otitis is more common in children and is known as swimmer's ear; it occurs mostly in hot and humid environments.  Why A wrong: Glue ear refers to otitis media with effusion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the term given in the source for external otitis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are clinical features of external otitis (swimmer's ear)? (SATA)","options":["Pain","Itching","Plugged feeling in the ear","Edema and exudate","Hearing loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pain, itching, a plugged feeling in the ear, edema, exudate, and hearing loss are all clinical features of external otitis.  Why none wrong: All listed options are documented features."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Otitis media is defined as:","options":["Inflammation or infection of the external auditory canal","Inflammation or infection of the middle ear","Inflammation of the cochlea","Inflammation of the eustachian tube only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Otitis media is inflammation or infection of the middle ear.  Why A wrong: That describes external otitis.  Why C wrong: Cochlear involvement defines other inner-ear disorders.  Why D wrong: Eustachian tube dysfunction may contribute, but otitis media is middle-ear inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which organisms most commonly cause otitis media? (SATA)","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae","Moraxella catarrhalis","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)","Plasmodium falciparum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis; RSV is a viral cause in m/c viral cases.  Why E wrong: Plasmodium falciparum causes malaria and is not a cause of otitis media."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Acute otitis media is classified by a duration of:","options":["Less than 3 weeks","3 weeks to 3 months","More than 3 months","More than 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute otitis media is defined as duration of less than 3 weeks (m/c in children).  Why B wrong: 3 weeks to 3 months defines subacute otitis media (SAOM).  Why C wrong: More than 3 months defines chronic otitis media.  Why D wrong: Not a defined category here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Subacute otitis media (SAOM) is defined by a duration of:","options":["Less than 3 weeks","3 weeks to 3 months","3 months to 6 months","More than 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Subacute otitis media (SAOM) lasts 3 weeks to 3 months.  Why A wrong: Less than 3 weeks is acute.  Why C/D wrong: Beyond 3 months is chronic, not subacute."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Chronic otitis media is defined by a duration of:","options":["Less than 3 weeks","3 weeks to 3 months","More than 3 months","Less than 1 week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic otitis media is of more than 3 months duration.  Why A wrong: Less than 3 weeks is acute.  Why B wrong: 3 weeks to 3 months is subacute.  Why D wrong: Less than 1 week is not a defined category."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Acute otitis media is more common in children due to which anatomical factor?","options":["Longer external ear canal","Shorter eustachian tube","Larger tympanic membrane","Absence of adenoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute otitis media is more common in children due to a shorter eustachian tube, which allows easier passage of organisms.  Why A wrong: Children's canals are shorter, not longer, and this is not the cited cause.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons for higher incidence in children."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are signs/symptoms of acute otitis media? (SATA)","options":["Otalgia (ear pain)","Purulent discharge","Red and bulging tympanic membrane","Fever and malaise","Loss of bone density"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Otalgia, purulent discharge, red/bulging tympanic membrane, fever, and malaise are signs/symptoms of acute otitis media.  Why E wrong: Loss of bone density relates to otosclerosis, not acute otitis media."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Myringotomy is best described as:","options":["Removal of the stapes","Puncture of the tympanic membrane with a long needle to aspirate the middle-ear contents","Removal of the tonsils","Removal of cerumen by irrigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myringotomy is a puncture of the tympanic membrane with a long needle to aspirate or drain middle-ear contents.  Why A wrong: That is stapedectomy.  Why C wrong: That is tonsillectomy.  Why D wrong: That is cerumen irrigation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with acute otitis media before/around myringotomy? (SATA)","options":["Administer prescribed antibiotics","Clear the ear of debris as per order","Irrigate the ear with a solution of equal parts of vinegar and sterile water to restore normal pH of the ear as per order","Instruct the client that the swelling will disappear with permanent total hearing loss","Position the client to keep the operative ear up"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Administering prescribed antibiotics, clearing the ear of debris, and irrigating with equal parts vinegar and sterile water (to restore normal ear pH) are appropriate measures.  Why D wrong: The teaching is that hearing should return to normal once swelling subsides, not that there is permanent total hearing loss.  Why E wrong: Positioning the operative ear down (in flat position) facilitates drainage after surgery; keeping it up is not the post-op drainage position."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"After myringotomy, to facilitate drainage the nurse should position the client:","options":["With the operative ear flat down so the ear contents drain","With the operative ear up","Flat supine with head midline","In high Fowler's with neck flexed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Postoperative care includes positioning the client to keep the operative ear in a flat (down) position so the ear contents drain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions do not promote dependent drainage of the operative ear."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"When teaching a client after myringotomy/tympanoplasty, which activities should be AVOIDED? (SATA)","options":["Strenuous activity","Rapid head movement","Bouncing and bending","Air travel for at least 1 week","Avoiding forceful coughing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Client education after myringotomy includes avoiding strenuous activity, rapid head movement, bouncing and bending, and avoiding air travel for at least 1 week.  Why E wrong: Avoiding forceful coughing is itself a recommended precaution; the question asks what to avoid doing, and forceful coughing should be avoided—so \"avoiding forceful coughing\" is correct behavior, not an activity to abstain from. (Instruct the client to report excessive ear drainage to the HCP.)"},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Otosclerosis is best characterized as:","options":["A genetic disorder of the ears caused by bony overgrowth of the tissues surrounding the ossicles","An infection of the external canal","Inflammation of the cochlea from noise","A tumor of the auditory nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Otosclerosis is a genetic disorder of the ears characterized by bony overgrowth of the tissues surrounding the ossicles.  Why B wrong: That describes external otitis.  Why C wrong: That describes noise-induced sensorineural loss.  Why D wrong: That describes an acoustic neuroma."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"In otosclerosis, fixation of which ossicle (caused by overgrowth of bone) impairs sound transmission?","options":["Malleus","Incus","Stapes","Tensor tympani"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fixation of the stapes bone, caused by overgrowth of bone, prevents transmission of vibration and causes progressive conductive hearing loss.  Why A/B wrong: The malleus and incus are ossicles, but otosclerosis classically fixes the stapes.  Why D wrong: The tensor tympani is a muscle, not the ossicle involved."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The surgical removal of the stapes bone, with a small hole drilled in the footplate after a prosthesis is connected to the incus and footplate, is called:","options":["Myringotomy","Fenestration / stapedectomy","Mastoidectomy","Tympanoplasty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fenestration is removal of the stapes bone; a partial stapedectomy or complete stapedectomy connects a prosthesis to the incus and footplate.  Why A wrong: Myringotomy is tympanic membrane puncture.  Why C wrong: Mastoidectomy is removal of mastoid air cells.  Why D wrong: Tympanoplasty is reconstruction of the tympanic membrane/ossicles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of otosclerosis? (SATA)","options":["Bilateral hearing loss","Slowly progressive conductive hearing loss","Tinnitus","Pinkish discoloration of the tympanic membrane (Schwartze's sign), indicating vascular changes","Sudden total deafness within minutes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bilateral, slowly progressive conductive hearing loss, tinnitus, and a pinkish discoloration of the tympanic membrane known as Schwartze's sign (indicating vascular changes) are signs/symptoms of otosclerosis.  Why E wrong: Otosclerosis progresses slowly; sudden total deafness within minutes is not characteristic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Preoperative nursing care for a client undergoing stapedectomy includes:","options":["Informing the client that hearing will be poor at first and improve as edema resolves","Telling the client hearing will be normal immediately","Encouraging vigorous nose blowing","Promoting ear irrigation hourly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should be informed that initial hearing will be poor and will improve as edema resolves; the client should also be assisted with ambulation due to risk of vertigo.  Why B wrong: Hearing is poor at first, not immediately normal.  Why C/D wrong: Nose blowing and frequent irrigation are not part of preoperative teaching and could harm the surgical site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Presbycusis is best described as:","options":["A conductive hearing loss caused by cerumen","A sensorineural hearing loss of the ear associated with aging","Inflammation of the middle ear","A genetic ossicle disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Presbycusis is a sensorineural hearing loss of the ear, caused by the aging process or old age (most common single cause).  Why A wrong: Cerumen causes a conductive, not sensorineural, loss.  Why C wrong: That is otitis media.  Why D wrong: That describes otosclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Presbycusis is caused by which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Progressive degeneration or atrophy of the ganglion cells in the cochlea","Vascular supply changes to the inner ear","Changes in several areas of the ear structure","Bony overgrowth around the stapes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Presbycusis is due to progressive degeneration or atrophy of the ganglion cells in the cochlea, vascular supply changes to the inner ear, and changes in several areas of the ear structure.  Why D wrong: Bony overgrowth around the stapes is otosclerosis, not presbycusis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Meniere's syndrome is also known as:","options":["Endolymphatic hydrops","Swimmer's ear","Glue ear","Otosclerosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meniere's syndrome is also known as endolymphatic hydrops.  Why B wrong: Swimmer's ear is external otitis.  Why C wrong: Glue ear is otitis media with effusion.  Why D wrong: Otosclerosis is a separate ossicle disorder."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Meniere's syndrome results from a dilation of the endolymphatic space caused by:","options":["Underproduction of endolymphatic fluid","Overproduction or decreased reabsorption of endolymphatic fluids","Bacterial infection of the cochlea only","Bony fixation of the ossicles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is a dilation of the endolymphatic space caused by overproduction or decreased reabsorption of endolymphatic fluids.  Why A wrong: It is overproduction/decreased reabsorption, not underproduction.  Why C wrong: It involves fluid dynamics, although allergic reactions, viral, and bacterial infections are listed causes—not infection of cochlea alone.  Why D wrong: Ossicle fixation describes otosclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are listed causes of Meniere's syndrome? (SATA)","options":["Allergic reactions","Viral infections","Bacterial infections","Bony ossicle overgrowth"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include allergic reactions, viral infections, and bacterial infections.  Why D wrong: Bony ossicle overgrowth is the mechanism of otosclerosis, not Meniere's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of Meniere's syndrome? (SATA)","options":["Hearing loss","Tinnitus","Vertigo with nausea and vomiting (with the client lying still and the bad ear down when lying down)","Severe headache and pressure in the ear","Purulent ear drainage"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo (with nausea/vomiting; client lies still with the bad ear down), and severe headache with pressure in the ear are signs/symptoms of Meniere's syndrome.  Why E wrong: Purulent ear drainage is characteristic of otitis media, not Meniere's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Cerumen impaction is described as:","options":["A rare cause of hearing problems","The most common cause of impacted canals","An inner-ear nerve disorder","A genetic ossicle disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cerumen (earwax) is the most common cause of impacted canals.  Why A wrong: It is common, not rare.  Why C/D wrong: It is a canal obstruction, not a nerve or ossicle disorder."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which of the following are common foreign bodies found in the ear canal? (SATA)","options":["Vegetables","Beads","Insects","Other small objects","Endolymph"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Foreign bodies in the ear can include vegetables, beads, insects, and other objects.  Why E wrong: Endolymph is a normal inner-ear fluid, not a foreign body."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of cerumen or foreign-body impaction in the ear? (SATA)","options":["Sensation of fullness in the ear","Pain","Itching","Bleeding","Diplopia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms include a sensation of fullness in the ear, pain, itching, and bleeding.  Why E wrong: Diplopia (double vision) is an eye finding, not a sign of ear cerumen/foreign body."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Irrigation to remove cerumen is contraindicated in a client with which condition?","options":["Normal-saline nasogastric tube","Perforated tympanic membrane and otitis media","Mild itching","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Removal of wax or cerumen by irrigation is contraindicated in a client with a perforated tympanic membrane and otitis media.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the cited contraindications to ear irrigation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"What is the recommended maximum amount of solution for ear irrigation?","options":["10–20 ml","50–70 ml","100–150 ml","200–250 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recommended maximum amount of solution for ear irrigation is 50–70 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: These volumes are outside the recommended range."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Foreign bodies such as vegetable pieces should NOT be irrigated because:","options":["They float and cannot be reached","They may swell with hydration and become more impacted","They dissolve in water","They cause immediate perforation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With foreign bodies like vegetable pieces, irrigation is used cautiously because the matter may expand with hydration, worsening impaction.  Why A wrong: Swelling, not floating, is the cited concern.  Why C wrong: Vegetable matter swells rather than dissolving.  Why D wrong: Perforation is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"When an insect is the foreign body in the ear, the recommended initial step is to:","options":["Use vacuum suction to remove it","Instill mineral oil or diluted alcohol to suffocate the insect, then remove it","Irrigate vigorously with cold water","Push it deeper with forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mineral oil or diluted alcohol is instilled to suffocate the insect, which is then removed using ear forceps.  Why A wrong: Do not use vacuum for foreign-body removal (it can damage the tympanic membrane).  Why C wrong: Vigorous cold-water irrigation is not the recommended insect approach.  Why D wrong: Pushing it deeper risks injury."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Conductive hearing loss occurs when:","options":["The inner-ear nerve fibers are damaged","Sound waves are blocked to the inner-ear nerve fibers because of external or middle-ear disorders","The auditory cortex is damaged","The endolymph is overproduced"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conductive hearing loss occurs when sound waves are blocked to the inner-ear nerve fibers because of external or middle-ear disorders.  Why A/C wrong: Nerve fiber/cortex damage describes sensorineural loss.  Why D wrong: Endolymph overproduction describes Meniere's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are causes of conductive hearing loss? (SATA)","options":["Any inflammatory process or obstruction of the external or middle ear","Tumors","Otosclerosis and scar tissues","Ototoxic medications"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of conductive hearing loss include any inflammatory process or obstruction of the external/middle ear, tumors, otosclerosis, and scar tissues.  Why D wrong: Ototoxic medications cause sensorineural hearing loss, not conductive."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Sensorineural hearing loss results from a pathological process of the:","options":["External auditory canal","Tympanic membrane","Inner ear","Eustachian tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sensorineural hearing loss is a pathological process of the inner ear, and treatment/measures must be taken to reduce further damage.  Why A/B/D wrong: These structures, when affected, cause conductive—not sensorineural—loss."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are causes of sensorineural hearing loss? (SATA)","options":["Damage to the 8th cranial nerve","Ototoxic medications, trauma, infections, and surgery","Damage to the inner-ear structures","Meniere's syndrome","Myxedema and prolonged exposure to loud noise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include damage to the 8th cranial nerve; ototoxic medications, trauma, infections, and surgery; damage to inner-ear structures; Meniere's syndrome; and myxedema with prolonged exposure to loud noise.  Why none wrong: All listed options are documented causes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are recognized signs of hearing loss? (SATA)","options":["Straining to hear and asking others to repeat statements","Turning the head or leaning forward to favor one ear","Answering questions incorrectly","Raising the volume of the television or radio","Withdrawing from social interactions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of hearing loss include straining to hear, frequently asking others to repeat statements, turning the head/leaning forward to favor one ear, failing to respond when not looking in the direction of the sound, answering questions incorrectly, raising the TV/radio volume, and withdrawing from social interactions.  Why none wrong: All are documented signs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which strategies facilitate communication with a client who has hearing loss? (SATA)","options":["Use written words if the client is able to see, read, and write","Provide good lighting in the room","Face the client when speaking and move closer to the client, speaking slowly and clearly","Use a normal volume and a lower pitch; rephrase sentences and repeat information","Shout loudly directly into the affected ear"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Recommended strategies include using written words (if the client can see/read/write), providing good lighting, facing the client and moving closer while speaking slowly and clearly, using a normal volume and a lower pitch, and rephrasing/repeating information. Verify the client understands by asking what was said and reading lips and using sign language.  Why E wrong: Shouting distorts speech and raises pitch; a normal volume with a lower pitch is recommended instead."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Epistaxis is defined as:","options":["Bleeding from the ear","Bleeding from the nose","Bleeding from the throat","Bleeding from the eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epistaxis is bleeding from the nose.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe bleeding from other structures, not epistaxis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The most common site of nosebleed (anterior septum) corresponds to which clinical fact about epistaxis?","options":["The anterior septum is the most common site of nosebleed","The posterior septum is the most common site","The turbinates are the most common site","The sinuses are the most common site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior septum is the most common site of nosebleed (epistaxis).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common bleeding site cited."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which are causes of epistaxis? (SATA)","options":["Nasal blowing","Trauma","Hypertension","Insertion of any object into the nostrils","Endolymphatic hydrops"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include nasal blowing, trauma, hypertension, and insertion of any object into the nostrils.  Why E wrong: Endolymphatic hydrops is Meniere's syndrome of the ear, not a cause of nosebleed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"During first-aid management of epistaxis, the nurse should:","options":["Have the client lie flat with the head back","Pinch the nose and apply direct pressure over the area to control bleeding, with the client leaning forward to prevent aspiration","Pack the nose immediately with no pressure","Tilt the head back and swallow the blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First-aid management is to pinch the nose and apply direct pressure over the area to control bleeding, asking the client to sit while leaning forward to prevent aspiration; apply ice over the nose.  Why A/D wrong: Leaning the head back risks aspiration and swallowing blood.  Why C wrong: Direct pressure is the first measure; packing is not the immediate first-aid step."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"During first aid for epistaxis, applying ice over the nose helps to:","options":["Increase blood flow to the area","Promote vasoconstriction","Numb the bleed only","Dissolve the clot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Applying ice over the nose promotes vasoconstriction, which helps control bleeding.  Why A wrong: Ice causes vasoconstriction, decreasing—not increasing—blood flow.  Why C wrong: Numbing is incidental; the goal is hemostasis via vasoconstriction.  Why D wrong: Ice does not dissolve clots."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which medication class is the drug of choice in the treatment of epistaxis?","options":["Beta-blockers","Vasoconstrictor medications such as adrenaline in 1:1000 dilution","Anticoagulants","Diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vasoconstrictor medications such as adrenaline in a 1:1000 dilution are the drug of choice for treating epistaxis; botradol topical nasal spray may also be used.  Why A/C/D wrong: Beta-blockers, anticoagulants (which would worsen bleeding), and diuretics are not the cited treatment."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which describes the position of the eyeball, as stated in the eye anatomy overview?","options":["The eye is the organ of touch","The eye is the organ of sight, situated in the orbit","The eye is the organ of balance","The eye is located in the maxilla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The eye is the organ of sight, situated in the orbit (bony cavity).  Why A/C wrong: Sight—not touch or balance—is the eye's function.  Why D wrong: The eye sits in the orbit, not the maxilla."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The eyeball is moved by which structures?","options":["The ciliary and conical arteries","Three pairs of muscles attached to the sclera (the white outer part of the eyeball)","The lacrimal ducts","The optic nerve fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The eyeball is moved by arteries/muscles attached to the sclera (the white part of the eye); there are three pairs of extraocular muscles producing movement.  Why A wrong: Arteries supply blood, not movement.  Why C wrong: Lacrimal ducts drain tears.  Why D wrong: The optic nerve (CN II) carries vision, not movement."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The optic nerve fibers leave the eyeball and connect to the brain at which point?","options":["At the central optic chiasm only","At the back of the eye, behind which it crosses at the optic chiasm","At the front of the cornea","At the lacrimal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nerve fibers supply the eye via the optic nerve (CN II); they leave the back of the eyeball, and the middle vascular layer behind them is known as the choroid which contains a deeply shaded vessel network.  Why A wrong: The chiasm is a crossing point but the fibers first exit the eyeball at the back.  Why C/D wrong: The optic nerve does not exit at the cornea or lacrimal gland."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The lacrimal glands of the eye perform which function?","options":["Produce aqueous humor","Produce tears that lubricate and clean the eye, draining via the nose into a small area in the frontal lacrimal fossa","Move the eyeball","Focus light on the retina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lacrimal glands produce tears that lubricate and clean the eye; tears drain through a small area and into the nasolacrimal duct/nose.  Why A wrong: Aqueous humor is produced by the ciliary body, not the lacrimal glands.  Why C wrong: Movement is by extraocular muscles.  Why D wrong: Focusing is performed by the lens/cornea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Conjunctivitis is best described as:","options":["Inflammation of the lens","Inflammation of the conjunctiva (a thin transparent mucous membrane that covers the inner surface of the eyelids and outer surface of the eyeball)","Inflammation of the optic nerve","Inflammation of the retina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The conjunctiva is a thin, transparent mucous membrane that covers the inner surface of the eyelids and outer surface of the eyeball; its inflammation is conjunctivitis (often known as \"red eye\").  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe inflammation of other structures, not the conjunctiva."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The eyeball measures approximately what dimension in diameter?","options":["About 0.5 inch (1.3 cm)","About 1 inch (2.5 cm) in diameter, situated in the orbit","About 3 inches (7.6 cm)","About 5 inches (12.7 cm)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The eyeball is about 1 inch (2.5 cm) in diameter and is situated in the orbit.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dimensions are inconsistent with the stated size."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The sclera is best described as:","options":["The transparent front layer of the eye","The white part of the eye (white of eye) consisting of external layer fibers","The vascular middle layer","The light-sensitive inner layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sclera is the white of the eye (white of external layer); it gives the eye its white appearance and provides protection.  Why A wrong: The cornea is the transparent front part.  Why C wrong: The choroid is the vascular middle layer.  Why D wrong: The retina is the light-sensitive inner layer."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which statement about the cornea is correct?","options":["It is the vascular layer of the eye","It is the transparent \"8th\" part of the external layer of the eye and is transparent and avascular (with no blood vessels) to reach the retina","It is the white outer layer","It contains the optic disk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cornea is the transparent anterior part of the external layer of the eye; it is transparent and avascular so that light rays pass through it to reach the retina.  Why A wrong: The cornea is avascular, not vascular.  Why C wrong: The white outer layer is the sclera.  Why D wrong: The optic disk is at the retina, not the cornea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The uveal (vascular) tract of the eye comprises which structures?","options":["Cornea, sclera, and conjunctiva","Choroid, ciliary body, and iris (the middle layer of the eye)","Retina, optic disk, and lens","Eyelids, eyebrows, and eyelashes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The uveal tract is the middle layer of the eye and consists of the choroid, ciliary body, and iris.  Why A wrong: These are external/protective structures.  Why C wrong: These are inner/posterior structures.  Why D wrong: These are accessory organs of the eye."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The choroid is best described as:","options":["The transparent front layer","The highly vascular middle layer of the eye that is very rich in blood vessels and deep in chocolate-brown color, lying between the retina and sclera","The light-sensitive inner layer","The white outer layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The choroid is the highly vascular layer of the eye, very rich in blood vessels and deep chocolate-brown in color, lying between the retina and the sclera; light rays pass through the pupil to reach the retina, and the choroid nourishes the outer layer of the retina.  Why A wrong: That is the cornea.  Why C wrong: That is the retina.  Why D wrong: That is the sclera."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The ciliary body is connected to the choroid and the iris and performs which function?","options":["It has no function in aqueous humor and accommodation","It controls accommodation of adjustments of lens thickness as per the distance of the object from the eye, and produces aqueous humor","It produces tears","It carries visual impulses to the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ciliary body lies attached to the choroid by the ciliary ligaments and connects the choroid and the iris; it controls accommodation (adjustment of lens thickness based on object distance) and produces aqueous humor (which nourishes/maintains intraocular pressure).  Why A wrong: It does have functions in accommodation and aqueous humor production.  Why C wrong: Tears are produced by lacrimal glands.  Why D wrong: Visual impulses are carried by the optic nerve/retina."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The iris of the eye performs which function?","options":["It produces tears","It is the visible colored part of the eye, lies in front of the lens, and is highly vascularized; it dilates and constricts to regulate the amount of light entering the eye","It is the white protective outer layer","It carries the visual impulse to the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The iris is the visible colored part of the eye, lies in front of the lens, is highly vascularized, and dilates and constricts to regulate the amount of light entering (it is controlled by sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves).  Why A wrong: Tears come from the lacrimal glands.  Why C wrong: That is the sclera.  Why D wrong: That is the optic nerve."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"In dim light or darkness, the pupil:","options":["Constricts (controlled by parasympathetic nerves)","Dilates (controlled by sympathetic nerves)","Remains a fixed size","Closes completely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In dim light, the iris dilates the pupil, controlled by sympathetic nerves, allowing more light in.  Why A wrong: Parasympathetic stimulation constricts the pupil in bright light, the opposite of dim-light response.  Why C/D wrong: The pupil changes size; it does not stay fixed or close fully."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The pupil is best defined as:","options":["The colored part of the eye","The hole located in the centre of the iris of the eye that allows light to enter the retina","The white of the eye","The transparent front layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pupil is the hole located in the centre of the iris that allows light to enter (and reach the retina); its size is controlled by the iris (a contractile structure) and may change in response to light in the environment.  Why A wrong: The colored part is the iris.  Why C wrong: The white is the sclera.  Why D wrong: The transparent front layer is the cornea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Dilation of the pupil is known as:","options":["Mydriasis","Miosis","Accommodation","Convergence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dilation of the pupil is known as mydriasis, and constriction of the pupil is miosis.  Why B wrong: Miosis is constriction, the opposite.  Why C wrong: Accommodation is lens adjustment for distance.  Why D wrong: Convergence is inward turning of the eyes for near vision."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Constriction of the pupil is known as:","options":["Mydriasis","Miosis","Hyperopia","Astigmatism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Constriction of the pupil is known as miosis.  Why A wrong: Mydriasis is dilation.  Why C wrong: Hyperopia is farsightedness.  Why D wrong: Astigmatism is a refractive error from corneal/lens irregularity."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The acronym PERRLA, an important assessment of pupil and accommodation, stands for:","options":["Pupils Equal, Round, Reactive to Light, and Accommodation","Pupils Equal, Red, Reactive to Light, and Acuity","Pupils Even, Round, Responsive, Light, Aware","Pupils Equal, Regular, Reactive, Light, Acuity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PERRLA accompanies pupils that are Equal, Round, and Reactive to Light and Accommodation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These expand the acronym incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The retina is best described as:","options":["The white outer layer of the eye","The innermost layer of the eye and the light-sensitive layer where light is converted into nerve impulses","The vascular middle layer","The transparent front layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The retina is the innermost (light-sensitive) layer of the eye where light is converted into nerve impulses (the optic pathway is started from the retina), and the lining membrane consists of nerve cells, rods, and cones.  Why A wrong: That is the sclera.  Why C wrong: That is the choroid.  Why D wrong: That is the cornea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The light-sensitive receptor cells of the retina are the:","options":["Ganglion cells and bipolar cells only","Rods and cones (photoreceptor cells)","Schwann cells","Goblet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The light-sensitive layer of the retina consists of two kinds of sensory receptor cells (photoreceptor cells): rods and cones.  Why A wrong: Ganglion and bipolar cells relay signals but are not the photoreceptors.  Why C/D wrong: Schwann cells and goblet cells are not retinal photoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which statement about rod cells is correct?","options":["They are responsible for color vision in bright light","They are responsible for peripheral vision and function in dim/low light; vitamin A is essential for the function of rods, and its deficiency may cause night blindness","They detect fine detail and color","They are located only in the optic disk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rods are responsible for peripheral vision, function in dim light, and require vitamin A; deficiency of vitamin A may cause night blindness.  Why A/C wrong: Color and fine-detail vision in bright light are functions of cone cells.  Why D wrong: The optic disk is a blind spot devoid of photoreceptors, not where rods are located."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Which statement about cone cells is correct?","options":["Cones are responsible for peripheral vision in dim light","Cones are responsible for color vision, central vision, and sharp vision in bright light","Cones require vitamin A and cause night blindness","Cones are located in the optic disk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cones are responsible for color vision and central/sharp vision and act in bright light.  Why A wrong: Peripheral/dim-light vision is the function of rods.  Why C wrong: Vitamin A deficiency and night blindness relate to rods.  Why D wrong: The optic disk is the blind spot, not a cone-rich region."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The optic disk of the eye is described as:","options":["The area of sharpest vision","A blind spot where the optic nerve enters the eyeball — it does not contain rods or cones (no photoreceptors), so it does not detect any visual stimulus","The vascular middle layer","The light-sensitive central region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The optic disk is the location where the optic nerve enters the eyeball; it is a blind spot because it does not contain rods or cones (photoreceptors), so it cannot detect visual stimuli.  Why A wrong: The area of sharpest vision is the macula/fovea, not the optic disk.  Why C/D wrong: These describe the choroid and retina, not the optic disk."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The lens of the eye is best described as:","options":["The vascular middle layer","A colorless, transparent, and biconvex structure held in position by ciliary muscles or suspensory ligaments, lying behind the iris and pupil","The white outer layer","The light-sensitive inner layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lens is colorless, transparent, and biconvex; it is held in position by ciliary muscles/suspensory ligaments and lies behind the iris and pupil. Focal length of human lens is 4 cm and its refractory power is variable.  Why A wrong: The vascular middle layer is the choroid.  Why C wrong: The white outer layer is the sclera.  Why D wrong: The light-sensitive inner layer is the retina."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The eye contains how many cavities/chambers as described, and what fills them?","options":["One cavity filled with blood","Two main cavities — the anterior chamber filled with aqueous humour and the posterior cavity (vitreous chamber) containing vitreous humour","Three cavities filled with air","No cavities"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lens divides the anterior eye into two cavities: the anterior eye-ball has two cavities — the anterior chamber containing aqueous humour and the posterior (vitreous) chamber containing vitreous humour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mischaracterize the chambers and their contents."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Eyelids are best described as:","options":["Movable folds of tissue situated above and below the front of the eye that protect the eye from injury and foreign bodies","The colored part of the eye","The light-sensitive layer","The vascular middle layer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eyelids are movable folds of tissue situated above and below the front of the eye; they protect the eye from injury, strong light, and foreign bodies, and inflammation of the eyelid is known as blepharitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe the iris, retina, and choroid respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Inflammation of the eyelid is known as:","options":["Conjunctivitis","Blepharitis","Keratitis","Uveitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the eyelid is known as blepharitis.  Why A wrong: Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the conjunctiva.  Why C wrong: Keratitis is corneal inflammation.  Why D wrong: Uveitis is inflammation of the uveal tract."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Eyebrows function to:","options":["Produce tears","Form the arched ridges of the superorbital margins of the frontal bone, with numerous hairs or eyebrows arranged on the surface of the skin to protect the eyeball from sweat, dust, and other foreign bodies","Focus light on the retina","Carry visual impulses to the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eyebrows are the arched ridges of the superorbital margins of the frontal bone; numerous hairs are arranged on the skin surface and protect the eyeball from sweat, dust, and other foreign bodies.  Why A wrong: Tears come from lacrimal glands.  Why C/D wrong: Focusing and visual transmission are functions of the lens and optic nerve."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"The eyelid margin contains numerous sebaceous glands; the modified sebaceous glands at the edge of the eyelid that secrete an oily material to spread over the conjunctiva and prevent rapid evaporation of tears are called:","options":["Lacrimal glands","Meibomian glands (tarsal glands)","Sweat glands","Goblet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The eyelid margin has numerous sebaceous glands; the tarsal (Meibomian) glands secrete an oily material that spreads over the conjunctiva and prevents rapid evaporation of tears.  Why A wrong: Lacrimal glands produce the aqueous tear film, not the oily layer.  Why C wrong: Sweat glands do not produce the tear oily layer.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus, not the oily Meibomian secretion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"Eyelashes are described on the eyelid margin as:","options":["Hairs arranged in 1 row, usually 1 inch long","Numerous hairs arranged in 3–4 rows, usually 1–2 cm long, protecting the eye","A single central hair","Sebaceous glands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eyelashes are arranged in 3–4 rows and are usually 1–2 cm long along the eyelid margin.  Why A/C wrong: They are numerous and in multiple rows, not a single row/hair.  Why D wrong: Eyelashes are hairs, not glands."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"A nurse assessing a client with suspected Meniere's syndrome notes the client prefers to lie still with the affected ear down. The priority nursing intervention is to:","options":["Encourage rapid position changes","Maintain safety measures to prevent falls related to vertigo","Apply ice to the affected ear","Pinch the nose and lean forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With vertigo, nausea, and vomiting in Meniere's syndrome, the priority is safety to prevent falls/injury; the client lying still with the bad ear down reflects vertigo-related positioning.  Why A wrong: Rapid position changes worsen vertigo and increase fall risk.  Why C wrong: Ice to the ear is not the intervention for Meniere's vertigo.  Why D wrong: That is epistaxis first aid, unrelated to Meniere's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Audiometry & otoscopic examination, Weber/Rinne tests…","question":"A client recovering from a stapedectomy reports that hearing seems worse than before surgery. The nurse's best response reflects which fact?","options":["The surgery has failed and must be repeated immediately","Hearing is expected to be poor at first and will improve as the postoperative edema resolves","The client now has permanent sensorineural deafness","The client should resume strenuous activity to speed recovery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preoperative/postoperative teaching states hearing will be poor at first and improve as edema resolves.  Why A wrong: Early poor hearing is expected, not a sign of failure.  Why C wrong: This does not indicate permanent sensorineural deafness.  Why D wrong: Strenuous activity should be avoided after ear surgery."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"The aqueous humour is secreted continuously by which structure of the eye?","options":["Iris","Ciliary body","Retina","Choroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aqueous humour is secreted by the ciliary body in the posterior chamber and then enters the anterior chamber through the pupil.  Why A wrong: The iris controls pupil size, it does not secrete aqueous humour.  Why C wrong: The retina is the light-sensitive layer, not a secretory structure for aqueous humour.  Why D wrong: The choroid is the vascular layer supplying nutrition; it does not produce aqueous humour."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the functions of aqueous humour? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is a transparent watery fluid similar to plasma","It provides nourishment to the cornea and lens","It maintains the intraocular pressure (IOP)","It fills the posterior cavity behind the lens","It makes up 4/5 of the eyeball volume"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Aqueous humour is a transparent watery fluid like plasma, it nourishes the cornea and lens, and it maintains the intraocular pressure.  Why D wrong: Vitreous humour (not aqueous) fills the posterior cavity behind the lens.  Why E wrong: Vitreous humour makes up about 4/5 of the eyeball volume; aqueous humour does not."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the normal intraocular pressure (IOP) of the human eye?","options":["5–9 mm Hg","10–21 mm Hg","22–30 mm Hg","31–40 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal IOP of the human eye is 10–21 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: This is below the normal range and indicates hypotony.  Why C wrong: Values above 21 mm Hg indicate raised IOP/glaucoma, not normal.  Why D wrong: This is markedly elevated, seen in acute glaucoma, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A nurse is preparing a client for tonometry. Which diagnostic purpose best describes this test?","options":["To assess colour vision","To assess increase in intraocular pressure (IOP) and diagnose glaucoma","To measure visual acuity","To visualize the optic disc and retina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tonometry is used to assess increase in intraocular pressure (IOP) and to diagnose glaucoma.  Why A wrong: Colour vision is assessed with the Ishihara chart.  Why C wrong: Visual acuity is measured with the Snellen chart.  Why D wrong: Visualization of the optic disc and retina is done with ophthalmoscopy/fundoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which instrument is the most accurate method for measuring intraocular pressure (IOP)?","options":["Snellen instrument","Schiotz tonometer","Ishihara plates","Ophthalmoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IOP is measured using an instrument which is known as a tonometer; the Schiotz tonometer is described as the most accurate method, requiring topical anaesthetic.  Why A wrong: The Snellen chart measures visual acuity, not IOP.  Why C wrong: Ishihara plates assess colour vision.  Why D wrong: The ophthalmoscope is used to examine the interior/fundus of the eye, not measure IOP."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"During tonometry, why must the nurse instruct the client to avoid rubbing the eyes after administration of the topical anaesthetic?","options":["To prevent infection of the conjunctiva","To prevent damage to the eye due to loss of sensation","To allow the dye to spread evenly","To dilate the pupil adequately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client must be instructed to avoid rubbing the eyes after administration of the topical anaesthetic to prevent further damage to the eye, because of the loss of sensation.  Why A wrong: The instruction relates to mechanical injury from loss of sensation, not specifically infection control.  Why C wrong: Tonometry does not require dye spreading.  Why D wrong: Pupil dilation is not the purpose of avoiding eye rubbing here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"The Snellen chart is read by the client from what distance?","options":["1 metre","3 metres","6 metres (20 feet)","10 metres"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Snellen chart is read from 6 metres (about 20 feet) and is composed of a series of progressively smaller rows of letters.  Why A, B, D wrong: These distances are not the standard testing distance for the Snellen visual acuity chart."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"On the Snellen chart, normal visual acuity is recorded as which value?","options":["20/100","6/60","6/6 or 20/20","6/18"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal visual acuity is 6/6 or 20/20 on the Snellen chart.  Why A wrong: 20/100 indicates reduced visual acuity.  Why B wrong: 6/60 indicates significant visual impairment.  Why D wrong: 6/18 indicates reduced acuity, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A client is being assessed for colour blindness. Which test should the nurse anticipate?","options":["Tonometry","Snellen chart","Ishihara chart","Ophthalmoscopy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Ishihara chart is used to assess colour vision of the client; it consists of a booklet with a number of coloured plates.  Why A wrong: Tonometry measures intraocular pressure.  Why B wrong: The Snellen chart measures visual acuity.  Why D wrong: Ophthalmoscopy visualizes the interior of the eye."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A client cannot distinguish between red and green colours during Ishihara testing. This finding indicates:","options":["Normal colour vision","Colour blindness due to a problem with cones","Cataract","Glaucoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A linked recessive disorder in which the eye fails to distinguish between red and green colours indicates colour blindness, which is due to a problem with cones.  Why A wrong: Inability to distinguish red/green is abnormal.  Why C wrong: Cataract causes opacity/cloudiness of the lens, not specific colour discrimination loss.  Why D wrong: Glaucoma involves raised IOP and optic nerve damage, not red-green colour confusion."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which statement about the Ishihara chart procedure is correct?","options":["The plates are read only in dim light","For one plate only 30 seconds are given for response","The plates measure depth perception","The test requires topical anaesthetic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For one plate only 3 seconds (a short fixed time, given in the source) are given for a response — the source emphasizes a brief, limited response time per plate. (Source states a short seconds limit per plate; the factually-keyed point is the limited response time.)  Why A wrong: The Ishihara test requires adequate illumination to read coloured plates, not dim light.  Why C wrong: It assesses colour vision, not depth perception.  Why D wrong: No anaesthetic is needed; the plates are simply viewed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Ophthalmoscopy (fundoscopy) is performed using which instrument?","options":["Tonometer","Ophthalmoscope","Slit lamp only","Schiotz instrument"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ophthalmoscopy is performed with a small portable instrument known as the ophthalmoscope, which allows direct visualization of the interior of the eyes.  Why A wrong: A tonometer measures IOP.  Why C wrong: Fundoscopy as described uses the handheld ophthalmoscope.  Why D wrong: The Schiotz instrument is a tonometer for IOP measurement."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Before performing ophthalmoscopy, why is dilation of the pupils (mydriasis) necessary?","options":["To reduce intraocular pressure","For proper visualization of the interior of the eye","To anaesthetize the cornea","To detect colour blindness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before examination, dilation of pupils (mydriasis) is necessary for proper visualization of the interior of the eye, and it should be done in a dark room.  Why A wrong: Pupil dilation is for visualization, not lowering IOP (in fact mydriatics can raise IOP).  Why C wrong: Mydriasis dilates the pupil; it does not anaesthetize the cornea.  Why D wrong: Colour blindness is detected by the Ishihara chart."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"During fundoscopy preparation, Tropicamide 0.5% is used. When is it administered relative to the examination?","options":["Immediately during the examination","15–30 minutes before the examination, 1–2 drops","2 hours before, single drop","After the examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tropicamide 0.5% is used for mydriasis and is given 15–30 minutes before the examination, 1–2 drops.  Why A wrong: It must act before the exam to dilate the pupil, not during.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: A mydriatic given after the exam serves no purpose for that exam."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"During ophthalmoscopy, the macula is examined last because it is:","options":["Difficult to dilate","Highly sensitive to light","Located outside the field of view","Avascular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The macula should be examined last because it is highly sensitive to light.  Why A wrong: Sensitivity to light, not dilation difficulty, is the reason.  Why C wrong: The macula is centrally located in the field, not outside it.  Why D wrong: The reason given is light sensitivity, not vascularity."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Cataract is best defined as:","options":["Increased intraocular pressure","Opacity and cloudiness of the lens which can progress to blindness","Detachment of the retina","Inflammation of the conjunctiva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cataract is opacity and cloudiness of the lens which can progress to blindness; the lens becomes cloudy due to changes in the structure of the lens protein (crystallin).  Why A wrong: Increased IOP describes glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Retinal detachment is separation of the retina from its layers.  Why D wrong: Inflammation of the conjunctiva is conjunctivitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Cataract is the most common cause of what percentage of blindness in India?","options":["47%","57%","67.6%","77%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cataract is the most common cause (67.6%) of blindness in India.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated value of 67.6%."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Match the type of cataract with its description: A senile cataract is one in which opacity and cloudiness occur:","options":["At the centre of the lens","At the periphery of the lens","At the front of the posterior capsule","Only in diabetic patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nuclear (senile) cataract is opacity and cloudiness occurring at the centre of the lens.  Why B wrong: Cortical cataract occurs at the periphery of the lens.  Why C wrong: Posterior subcapsular cataract occurs at the front of the posterior capsule.  Why D wrong: Diabetes is a cause of secondary cataract, not the definition of senile/nuclear cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which conditions are listed as causes/risk factors of cataract? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Senile (ageing process), the most common cause","Heredity / congenital factors","Injury / traumatic cataract","Diabetes, maternal rubella, severe myopia and UV exposure (secondary causes)","Inadequate drainage of aqueous fluid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes/risk factors of cataract include senile (ageing, most common), heredity/congenital, injury (traumatic), and secondary causes such as diabetes, maternal rubella, severe myopia and UV exposure.  Why E wrong: Inadequate drainage of aqueous fluid through the canal of Schlemm is a cause of glaucoma, not cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following are early signs and symptoms of cataract? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Blurred vision and decreased colour perception","Reduced visual acuity with diplopia (double vision) and photophobia","Absence of red reflex during ophthalmoscopy","Presence of milky white plaque appearance (late sign)","Pain and eye redness usually related to senile cataract"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Early signs include blurred vision and decreased colour perception, and reduced visual acuity with diplopia (double vision) and photophobia.  Why C wrong: Absence of red reflex on ophthalmoscopy is a sign but is an examination finding, and the source notes it under signs but the early symptoms are the visual changes.  Why D wrong: The milky white plaque appearance is explicitly a late sign, not early.  Why E wrong: Pain and eye redness are usually NOT related to senile cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common surgical management for cataract?","options":["Extracapsular extraction","Intracapsular extraction","Phacoemulsification","Iridectomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Phacoemulsification, in which high-frequency ultrasonic vibration or laser is used to break the lens and extract it through a small incision, is the most common surgery for cataract management.  Why A wrong: Extracapsular extraction (removal of the anterior capsule and lens) is a surgical option but not the most common.  Why B wrong: Intracapsular extraction (removal of the entire lens as a unit) is an older, less common option.  Why D wrong: Iridectomy is a surgical treatment for angle-closure glaucoma, not cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"During intracapsular extraction for cataract, what is removed?","options":["Only the anterior capsule","The entire lens as one unit","Only the lens nucleus","The vitreous humour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intracapsular extraction is removal of the entire lens (as one unit) during cataract extraction.  Why A wrong: Removal of the anterior capsule and lens describes extracapsular extraction.  Why C wrong: Only the nucleus is not the definition of intracapsular extraction.  Why D wrong: Vitreous humour is not removed in standard cataract extraction."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which postoperative nursing care measures are appropriate after cataract surgery? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Orient the client to the environment","Encourage the client to wear dark glasses","Instruct the client not to bend over toward forward bending or lifting heavy weight to prevent increase in IOP","Keep the call bell/phone at the client's unaffected bed side","Apply pilocarpine to constrict the pupil"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes orienting the client to the environment, encouraging dark glasses, instructing the client to avoid bending forward or lifting heavy weight (to prevent increased IOP), and keeping the call bell/phone at the unaffected side.  Why E wrong: Pilocarpine (a miotic) is used in glaucoma management to constrict the pupil and increase aqueous drainage, not as routine cataract postoperative care."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Glaucoma is a disorder characterized by:","options":["Opacity of the lens","Increased intraocular pressure that, if untreated, damages the optic nerve and leads to blindness","Separation of the retina from its layers","Inflammation of the cornea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glaucoma is a disorder in which increased intraocular pressure, if untreated, results in optic nerve damage and blindness.  Why A wrong: Opacity of the lens is cataract.  Why C wrong: Retinal separation is retinal detachment.  Why D wrong: Corneal inflammation is keratitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"In glaucoma, intraocular pressure within the eyeball is usually increased above which value?","options":["10 mm Hg","16 mm Hg","22 mm Hg (above ~21 mm Hg)","5 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In glaucoma the IOP within the eyeball is usually increased above the normal upper limit (more than ~21–22 mm Hg).  Why A wrong: 10 mm Hg is within the lower normal range.  Why B wrong: 16 mm Hg is within the normal 10–21 mm Hg range.  Why D wrong: 5 mm Hg is below normal (hypotony)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"The increased IOP in glaucoma is usually the pressure of which fluid?","options":["Vitreous humour","Aqueous fluid","Lacrimal fluid","Blood plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IOP is usually the pressure of the aqueous fluid; glaucoma results from its inadequate drainage.  Why A wrong: Vitreous humour fills the posterior cavity and is not the basis of measured IOP in glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Lacrimal fluid is tear fluid produced by the lacrimal gland.  Why D wrong: Blood plasma is not the intraocular fluid measured."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"The primary cause of increased IOP in glaucoma is:","options":["Overproduction of vitreous humour","Inadequate drainage of aqueous fluid from the canal of Schlemm","Lens cloudiness","Detachment of the retina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cause of increased IOP is inadequate drainage of aqueous fluid from the canal of Schlemm.  Why A wrong: It is the aqueous fluid, not vitreous, and inadequate drainage rather than overproduction of vitreous.  Why C wrong: Lens cloudiness is cataract.  Why D wrong: Retinal detachment is unrelated to the cause of raised IOP in glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the aim of treatment in glaucoma?","options":["To increase aqueous fluid production","To decrease IOP using pupil constriction medications (miotics) to decrease production of aqueous fluid","To dilate the pupil with mydriatics","To remove the lens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The aim of treatment is to decrease IOP using pupil constriction medications (miotics) to decrease the production of aqueous fluid.  Why A wrong: Increasing aqueous production would raise, not lower, IOP.  Why C wrong: Mydriatics dilate the pupil and can precipitate or worsen angle-closure glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Lens removal is cataract surgery, not glaucoma treatment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A nurse teaching a glaucoma client should instruct them to report which symptom to the HCP?","options":["Mild itching","Eye pain, halos around lights, and any change in vision","Occasional tearing","Improved colour vision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Teach the client to report eye pain, halos around lights, and any change in vision to the HCP.  Why A, C wrong: These mild findings are not the priority symptoms emphasized for glaucoma worsening.  Why D wrong: Improved colour vision is not a warning symptom requiring reporting."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which features describe Open Angle Glaucoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Most common type of glaucoma, also known as silent glaucoma","Aqueous fluid does not drain adequately from the eyes","Gradual onset with painless, progressive vision loss","It is the most common type","Severe frontal headache with sudden complete loss of vision"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Open angle glaucoma is the most common type (also called silent glaucoma), aqueous fluid does not drain adequately, the onset is gradual with painless progressive vision loss.  Why E wrong: Severe frontal headache and sudden loss of vision are features of angle-closure glaucoma, not open-angle."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are characteristic of Angle Closure Glaucoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Severe frontal headache (hypertensia) and swelling of conjunctiva","Loss of central vision and blurred vision with halos around lights","Nausea and vomiting","Pupils fixed, semi-dilated and non-reactive to light and accommodation","Gradual painless onset over many years"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Angle closure glaucoma presents with severe frontal headache and conjunctival swelling, loss of central/blurred vision with halos around lights, nausea and vomiting, and fixed, semi-dilated, non-reactive pupils — it is a surgical emergency.  Why E wrong: Gradual painless onset over years describes open angle glaucoma, not the acute angle-closure type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Angle closure glaucoma is best described as:","options":["A chronic painless condition","A surgical/ocular emergency","A normal age-related change","A type of refractive error"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angle closure glaucoma is a surgical/ocular emergency requiring decreased IOP immediately.  Why A wrong: It is acute and painful, not chronic and painless.  Why C wrong: It is a pathological emergency, not a normal change.  Why D wrong: It is a glaucoma, not a refractive error."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the choice/treatment of surgery for angle closure (closed angle) glaucoma?","options":["Trabeculectomy only","Iridectomy (surgically like iridectomy)","Phacoemulsification","Scleral buckling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgically, the choice of treatment for angle closure glaucoma is iridectomy.  Why A wrong: Trabeculectomy is more associated with open-angle surgical management, while iridectomy is keyed for angle-closure.  Why C wrong: Phacoemulsification is cataract surgery.  Why D wrong: Scleral buckling is used for retinal detachment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which drugs are used to constrict the pupil (miotic) and increase drainage of aqueous outflow in glaucoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as Acetazolamide","Beta blockers such as Timolol","Pilocarpine (miotic)","Tropicamide","Atropine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pilocarpine is a miotic that constricts the pupil and increases drainage of aqueous outflow.  Why A wrong: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., Acetazolamide) decrease production of aqueous fluid; they do not act as miotics that constrict the pupil.  Why B wrong: Beta blockers (e.g., Timolol) decrease production of aqueous fluid and decrease IOP, but they are not pupil-constricting miotics.  Why D wrong: Tropicamide is a mydriatic (dilates the pupil) — it is contraindicated/avoided in angle-closure glaucoma.  Why E wrong: Atropine is a mydriatic/cycloplegic and is contraindicated in angle-closure glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., Acetazolamide) and beta blockers (e.g., Timolol) are used in glaucoma primarily to:","options":["Increase aqueous fluid production","Decrease production of aqueous fluid and thereby decrease IOP","Constrict the pupil mechanically","Dilate the pupil for examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (Acetazolamide) and beta blockers (Timolol) decrease production of aqueous fluid and decrease intraocular pressure (Timolol is DOC for open-angle glaucoma).  Why A wrong: They decrease, not increase, aqueous production.  Why C wrong: Pilocarpine (a miotic) is the agent that constricts the pupil.  Why D wrong: Mydriatics dilate the pupil; these agents do not."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for open angle glaucoma?","options":["Pilocarpine","Timolol","Atropine","Acetazolamide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Timolol (a beta blocker) is the drug of choice (DOC) for open angle glaucoma.  Why A wrong: Pilocarpine is a miotic used to increase aqueous outflow, not the keyed DOC for open angle.  Why C wrong: Atropine is a mydriatic, contraindicated in angle-closure glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) decreases aqueous production but is not the DOC noted for open angle."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Refractive errors are best defined as:","options":["Increased intraocular pressure","Errors related to the bending of light rays","Cloudiness of the lens","Inflammation of the conjunctiva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Refractive errors are the bending of light rays; any problems associated with eye length or refraction can lead to refractive errors (includes myopia, hyperopia, presbyopia, astigmatism).  Why A wrong: Increased IOP is glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Lens cloudiness is cataract.  Why D wrong: Conjunctival inflammation is conjunctivitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Myopia is also known as:","options":["Farsightedness","Nearsightedness","Presbyopia","Astigmatism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myopia is also known as nearsightedness; in myopia people can see close objects clearly but distant objects appear blurred.  Why A wrong: Farsightedness is hyperopia.  Why C wrong: Presbyopia is loss of lens elasticity due to ageing.  Why D wrong: Astigmatism is an irregular curvature of the cornea or lens."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"In myopia, where do the light rays focus relative to the retina?","options":["In front of the retina","Behind the retina","Exactly on the retina","At unequal points on the retina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In myopia, light rays are focused in front of the retina, causing distant objects to blur.  Why B wrong: Light focusing behind the retina occurs in hyperopia.  Why C wrong: Focus exactly on the retina is normal (emmetropic) vision.  Why D wrong: Light focusing at unequal points occurs in astigmatism."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which lens is used to treat (correct) myopia?","options":["Convex lens","Concave lens","Cylindrical lens","Bifocal lens only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myopia is treated/corrected with a concave lens.  Why A wrong: Convex lenses are used to correct hyperopia and presbyopia.  Why C wrong: Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism.  Why D wrong: Bifocals are used in presbyopia; concave lenses are the correction for myopia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Hyperopia is also called farsightedness. In hyperopia, the person can see:","options":["Far objects clearly but close objects appear blurred","Close objects clearly but far objects appear blurred","No objects clearly at any distance","Objects only in dim light"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hyperopia (farsightedness), the person can see far objects clearly, but close objects appear blurred.  Why B wrong: Seeing close objects clearly but far objects blurred is myopia.  Why C, D wrong: These do not describe hyperopia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"In hyperopia, light rays are refracted or focused behind the retina. Which lens corrects this?","options":["Concave lens","Convex lens","Cylindrical lens","No correction needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hyperopia, light rays refract/focus behind the retina, and it is treated with a convex lens.  Why A wrong: Concave lenses correct myopia.  Why C wrong: Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism.  Why D wrong: Correction with a convex lens is required."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Presbyopia occurs due to which underlying process?","options":["Loss of elasticity of the lens during the ageing process","Increased intraocular pressure","Opacity of the lens","Detachment of the retina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Presbyopia is loss of elasticity of the lens during the ageing process, in which things fail behind the retina.  Why B wrong: Increased IOP is glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Lens opacity is cataract.  Why D wrong: Retinal detachment is a separate disorder."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the recommended treatment for presbyopia?","options":["Concave lens","Bifocal lens","Iridectomy","Scleral buckling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Presbyopia is treated with a bifocal lens.  Why A wrong: Concave lenses correct myopia.  Why C wrong: Iridectomy is a surgical treatment for angle-closure glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Scleral buckling treats retinal detachment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Astigmatism is caused by:","options":["Increased aqueous production","An irregular curvature of the cornea (or lens) in which the image focuses at unequal points on the retina","Loss of lens elasticity","Cloudiness of the lens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Astigmatism is caused by an irregular curvature of the cornea (or lens), in which the image focuses at two different/unequal points on the retina.  Why A wrong: Increased aqueous production relates to glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Loss of lens elasticity describes presbyopia.  Why D wrong: Lens cloudiness is cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which lens is used to correct astigmatism?","options":["Concave lens","Convex lens","Cylindrical lens","Bifocal lens"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Astigmatism is treated with a cylindrical lens (cylindrical glass).  Why A wrong: Concave lenses correct myopia.  Why B wrong: Convex lenses correct hyperopia.  Why D wrong: Bifocals correct presbyopia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate nursing care measures for a client with a refractive error? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Orient the client about the environment","When speaking to a client who has limited sight or is blind, the nurse should use a normal tone of voice","Cover both eyes with patches at all times","Allow the client to touch objects in the room and assist for ambulating"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for refractive errors includes orienting the client to the environment, using a normal tone of voice when speaking to a client with limited sight or blindness, and allowing the client to touch objects and assisting with ambulation.  Why C wrong: Covering both eyes permanently is not indicated for refractive errors; the care focuses on orientation and safety."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Retinal detachment is best described as:","options":["Opacity of the lens","Detachment of the retina from its original place / separation of the retina from the underlying layers of fluid between them","Inflammation of the conjunctiva","Increased intraocular pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Retinal detachment is the detachment of the retina from its original place — separation of the retina from the underlying layers, with fluid between them.  Why A wrong: Lens opacity is cataract.  Why C wrong: Conjunctival inflammation is conjunctivitis.  Why D wrong: Raised IOP is glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"When retinal detachment becomes complete, the expected outcome is:","options":["Improved colour vision","Blindness will occur","Spontaneous reattachment","Decreased IOP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When retinal detachment becomes complete, blindness will occur.  Why A wrong: Vision worsens, not improves.  Why C wrong: Complete detachment does not spontaneously reattach; it requires surgical intervention.  Why D wrong: IOP change is not the described outcome of complete detachment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of retinal detachment? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Flashes of light and black spots or floaters (sign of bleeding)","Blurred vision and loss of portion of visual field","Sense of a curtain or shade being drawn over the eyes (cardinal sign)","Painless distortion of central or peripheral vision","Severe eye pain with redness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs include flashes of light and black spots/floaters (sign of bleeding), blurred vision with loss of part of the visual field, a sense of a curtain/shade being drawn over the eyes (the cardinal sign), and painless distortion of central or peripheral vision.  Why E wrong: Retinal detachment is typically painless; severe eye pain with redness is not a listed symptom."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with retinal detachment? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Provide bed rest","Cover both eyes with patches to prevent further detachment","Place the client's head in a neutral position so as not to pull","Diminish light in the client's room","Encourage the client to bend forward frequently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes bed rest, covering both eyes with patches to prevent further detachment, placing the head in a neutral position (not to pull), and diminishing light in the room; also avoid jerky or sudden movement of the head and minimize eye stress to prepare for surgical intervention.  Why E wrong: Bending forward should be avoided; sudden/jerky movements and stress are to be minimized, not encouraged."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which type of retinal detachment is described as Rhegmatogenous (the most common type) caused by a retinal tear/break through which fluid enters and the retina separates?","options":["Tractional","Exudative","Rhegmatogenous (most common type)","Serous proliferative"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rhegmatogenous detachment (most common type) results from a retinal tear/break through which fluid leads to retinal separation.  Why A wrong: Tractional detachment is due to fibrosis/fibrous tissue proliferation that scars tissue, pulling the retina (e.g., from diabetes, eye disease).  Why B wrong: Exudative detachment is accumulation of subretinal fluid leading to detachment (most common cause: leaking blood vessels, or swelling in the back of the eye).  Why D wrong: This is not a listed standard category in the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Tractional retinal detachment occurs due to:","options":["A retinal tear allowing fluid to enter","Fibrosis / fibrous tissue proliferation that scars and pulls the retina","A bacterial infection of the conjunctiva","Increased aqueous production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tractional detachment is caused by fibrous tissue proliferation that scars tissue and pulls the retina (associated with diabetes and other causes/eye diseases).  Why A wrong: A retinal tear allowing fluid describes rhegmatogenous detachment.  Why C wrong: Conjunctival bacterial infection is conjunctivitis.  Why D wrong: Increased aqueous production relates to glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Exudative retinal detachment is characterized by:","options":["Accumulation of subretinal fluid leading to retinal detachment (most common cause: leaking blood vessels / swelling in the back of the eye)","A retinal tear/break","Fibrous scar tissue traction","Opacification of the lens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Exudative detachment is the accumulation of subretinal fluid leading to retinal detachment, most commonly caused by leaking blood vessels or swelling in the back of the eye.  Why B wrong: A retinal tear/break is rhegmatogenous detachment.  Why C wrong: Fibrous scar traction is tractional detachment.  Why D wrong: Lens opacification is cataract."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which surgical procedure for detached retina uses scleral buckling?","options":["A surgeon stitches/attaches a piece of silicone or a sponge onto the white of the eye at the spot of the retinal tear (the buckle), designed to repair retinal detachment by pushing the sclera toward the retinal tear/break","Removal of the entire lens","Iridectomy","Phacoemulsification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scleral buckling involves a surgeon stitching/attaching a piece of silicone or a sponge onto the white (sclera) of the eye at the spot of the retinal tear; it is designed to repair retinal detachment by pushing the sclera toward the retinal tear/break.  Why B wrong: Lens removal is cataract surgery.  Why C wrong: Iridectomy treats angle-closure glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Phacoemulsification is cataract surgery."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Cryopexy, as a surgical management of detached retina, refers to:","options":["Using heat to seal the retina","Applying intense cold to the sclera to stimulate an inflammatory response leading to adhesions","Injecting gas into the vitreous","Laser stimulation of inflammation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cryopexy applies intense cold to the sclera to stimulate an inflammatory response leading to adhesions.  Why A wrong: It uses cold, not heat.  Why C wrong: Gas injection into the vitreous describes pneumatic retinopexy.  Why D wrong: Laser-induced inflammation describes laser therapy, not cryopexy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Match the retinal detachment procedure: Diathermy uses ___, while Laser therapy uses ___, and Pneumatic retinopexy uses ___.","options":["Heat from an electrode needle and heat through the sclera; laser to stimulate an inflammatory response to seal small retinal areas; injection of a gas bubble into the vitreous cavity to apply pressure on detached portion of the retina","Cold to the sclera; injection of saline; heat from electrode","Silicone band placement; gas injection; cold application","Laser only; cold only; heat only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diathermy uses an electrode needle and heat applied through the sclera to stimulate an inflammatory response to seal small retinal portions; laser therapy stimulates an inflammatory response to seal small retinal areas; pneumatic retinopexy injects a gas bubble into the vitreous cavity to apply pressure on the detached portion of the retina.  Why B, C, D wrong: These mismatch the source descriptions of diathermy, laser therapy, and pneumatic retinopexy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Vitrectomy as a surgical management of detached retina involves:","options":["Small incision into the sclera and repair of the detached portion of the retina","Removal of the lens","Stitching silicone to the sclera","Injection of a gas bubble only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitrectomy is a small incision into the sclera and repair of the detached portion of the retina.  Why B wrong: Lens removal is cataract surgery.  Why C wrong: Stitching silicone to the sclera is scleral buckling.  Why D wrong: Gas bubble injection is pneumatic retinopexy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Conjunctivitis is defined as:","options":["Opacity of the lens","Infection or inflammation of the conjunctiva","Detachment of the retina","Raised intraocular pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conjunctivitis is the infection or inflammation of the conjunctiva.  Why A wrong: Lens opacity is cataract.  Why C wrong: Retinal detachment is separation of the retina.  Why D wrong: Raised IOP is glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following are recognized causes of conjunctivitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bacterial — Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus","Chlamydial — trachoma","Chemical — due to any chemical substance","Viral — adenovirus and herpes simplex","Allergic — due to allergens such as air, dust, cold and hot weather"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed causes are valid: bacterial (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus), chlamydial (trachoma), chemical (any chemical substance), viral (adenovirus and herpes simplex), and allergic (allergens such as air, dust, cold and hot weather).  Why none wrong: Every option is a documented cause of conjunctivitis per the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of conjunctivitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Redness of eyes and burning sensation","Bacterial conjunctivitis: purulent, ocular pain, trichiasis and corneal erosion and ulceration","Viral conjunctivitis: watery discharge, extreme tearing, discomfort and lid oedema","Allergic conjunctivitis: rhinitis, itching and excessive tearing","Sudden complete loss of central vision"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Conjunctivitis signs include redness and burning; bacterial type has purulent discharge, ocular pain, trichiasis, corneal erosion/ulceration; viral type has watery discharge, extreme tearing, discomfort and lid oedema; allergic type has rhinitis, itching and excessive tearing.  Why E wrong: Sudden complete loss of central vision is not a feature of conjunctivitis (it relates to acute glaucoma/retinal pathology)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with conjunctivitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Instruct clients not to rub the eyes","Maintain eye hygiene","Administer ordered medications such as topical antibiotics and others","Use frequent hand hygiene and not share towels and eye pads","Encourage the client to share eye makeup with others to test for reaction"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes instructing the client not to rub the eyes, maintaining eye hygiene, administering ordered medications (topical antibiotics and others), and frequent hand hygiene with no sharing of towels/eye pads; also wear dark glasses and do not apply eye makeup until symptoms subside.  Why E wrong: Sharing eye makeup spreads infection; the source advises NOT to apply/share eye makeup until symptoms subside."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"When administering eye medications, the nurse should instill drops into which location?","options":["Directly onto the cornea","Into the lower conjunctival sac, moving from the inner to outer canthus","Onto the eyelashes","Onto the upper eyelid surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye drops/medication should be instilled into the lower conjunctival sac, never directly onto the cornea, and moved from the inner to outer canthus.  Why A wrong: Medication should never be instilled directly onto the cornea.  Why C, D wrong: The drops go into the lower conjunctival sac, not onto the eyelashes or eyelid surface."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"After instilling eye drops, how long should the nurse apply pressure to the inner canthus (nasolacrimal duct) to prevent systemic absorption?","options":["10 seconds","30 seconds","5 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Apply pressure to the inner canthus/lower conjunctival sac (over the nasolacrimal duct) for about 5 to 10 minutes after medication administration to prevent systemic absorption.  Why A, B wrong: A few seconds is insufficient per the source guidance.  Why D wrong: One hour is excessive and not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A client is prescribed two different eye medications at the same time. What instruction should the nurse give?","options":["Administer both drops simultaneously","Administer the eye drop first, then the eye ointment","Administer the eye ointment first, then wait before administering the eye drop (administer drops first, then ointment — separate by ~5 minutes)","Skip one medication"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If both an eye drop and eye ointment are scheduled to be administered at the same time, administer the eye drop first; if medications are scheduled at the same time, separate administration by several minutes (about 5 minutes) to prevent cross-contamination/dilution.  Why A wrong: Drops and ointment should not be given simultaneously; ointment would block drop absorption.  Why B wrong: While drop-before-ointment is the order, they must be separated by a time interval, not given back-to-back without spacing.  Why D wrong: Skipping prescribed medication is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"When administering eye medication, the nurse should wash hands before and after administration. Why is this required if possible?","options":["To prevent eye infection (and cross-contamination)","To increase intraocular pressure","To dilate the pupil","To anaesthetize the cornea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Washing hands before each medication administration and avoiding touching the eye dropper to the eye/non-sterile surfaces prevents the spread of eye infection and cross-contamination.  Why B, C, D wrong: Hand hygiene does not affect IOP, pupil size, or corneal sensation; it is an infection-control measure."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"When instilling ear drops in an adult and children older than 3 years, the pinna is pulled in which direction?","options":["Down and back","Up and back","Straight outward","Down and forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an adult and children older than 3 years, pull the pinna back and up to straighten the external ear canal to instill eye/ear drops.  Why A wrong: Down and back is used for children younger than 3 years (infants/young children).  Why C, D wrong: These are not the recommended directions for straightening the canal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"For an infant or toddler under 3 years of age, the pinna should be pulled in which direction when instilling ear drops?","options":["Up and back","Down and back","Straight up","Forward and up"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an infant or toddler under 3 years of age, pull the pinna down and back to straighten the ear canal.  Why A wrong: Up and back is used for adults and children older than 3 years.  Why C, D wrong: These directions are incorrect for the ear canal anatomy in young children."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the example of a contraindicated otic (ear) medication?","options":["Carbonide peroxide","Atropine","Pilocarpine","Timolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carbonide (carbamide) peroxide is given as an example of a contraindicated otic medication in the stated situation.  Why B wrong: Atropine is an ophthalmic mydriatic/cycloplegic, not the keyed otic example.  Why C wrong: Pilocarpine is a miotic used for glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Timolol is used for glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Ear irrigation is contraindicated in a client with which condition?","options":["Mild cerumen impaction","Perforation of the eardrum (tympanic membrane)","Hearing loss","Tinnitus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ear irrigation is contraindicated in a client with perforation of the eardrum (tympanic membrane), as irrigation fluid could enter the middle ear.  Why A wrong: Mild cerumen may actually be an indication for irrigation, not a contraindication.  Why C, D wrong: Hearing loss and tinnitus are not stated contraindications; perforation of the eardrum is the key contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A client is administered an oral mydriatic-action medication. Because these can decrease intestinal mobility, the nurse should monitor for which complication?","options":["Abdominal distension (caution against atropine which may cause abdominal distension)","Diarrhoea","Hypotension","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If oral medications such as a mydriatic of children or child receiving atropine sulphate eye drops are given, because atropine may cause abdominal distension (decreased intestinal motility) and other anticholinergic effects, monitor for abdominal distension; physostigmine may be used as an antidote.  Why B wrong: Atropine reduces secretions/motility leading to distension/constipation, not diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Atropine tends to cause tachycardia, not primarily hypotension.  Why D wrong: Hyperthermia is not the specifically warned-of complication here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the antidote for atropine toxicity from ophthalmic atropine?","options":["Pilocarpine","Physostigmine","Naloxone","Vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Physostigmine is the antidote for atropine; the source notes physostigmine should be available as an antidote during atropine use.  Why A wrong: Pilocarpine is a miotic used in glaucoma, not the antidote for atropine toxicity.  Why C wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Glucocorticoid eye preparations should be administered before other eye medications. Why?","options":["To increase IOP","To prevent dilution and ensure proper absorption (administer glucocorticoids before other eye medications if prescribed)","To anaesthetize the eye","To produce mydriasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to administer glucocorticoid eye preparations before other eye medications if prescribed, ensuring appropriate delivery/absorption.  Why A wrong: The instruction is about medication sequencing/absorption, not raising IOP.  Why C, D wrong: Glucocorticoid sequencing is unrelated to anaesthesia or mydriasis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the normal hearing range in a child and adult?","options":["5–10 dB","20–10000 Hz (normal range, ~20 Hz to 20000 Hz)","100–500 Hz","50000 Hz and above"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal hearing range in a child and adult is given as approximately 20–20000 Hz (the source notes 20–10000/20000 Hz range).  Why A wrong: Decibels measure intensity, not frequency range, and 5–10 dB is not the stated hearing range.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the normal audible frequency range."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Reddish bluish discolouration of the tympanic membrane seen in otosclerosis is known as:","options":["Rinne's sign","Schwartz's sign","Weber's sign","Meniere's sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reddish bluish discolouration of the tympanic membrane seen in otosclerosis is known as Schwartz's sign.  Why A wrong: Rinne's test compares air and bone conduction of sound.  Why C wrong: Weber's test lateralizes sound.  Why D wrong: Meniere's disease is a separate inner-ear disorder."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which disease is associated with intermittent conductive hearing loss and is a curing form of otosclerosis (Meniere's disease vs intermittent hearing loss)?","options":["Otosclerosis disease","Meniere's disease (intermittent hearing loss)","Mastoiditis","Presbycusis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meniere's disease is the disease associated with intermittent (fluctuating) sensorineural hearing loss, while in Rinne's test bone conduction is heard better than air conduction in conductive hearing loss.  Why A wrong: Otosclerosis causes fixation of the stapes (conductive loss) and shows Schwartz's sign.  Why C wrong: Mastoiditis is infection of the mastoid air cells.  Why D wrong: Presbycusis is age-related hearing loss."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"In Rinne's test, bone conduction heard better than air conduction indicates:","options":["Sensorineural hearing loss","Conductive hearing loss","Normal hearing","Total deafness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Rinne's test, bone conduction better than air conduction indicates conductive hearing loss.  Why A wrong: In sensorineural loss, air conduction is still better than bone (Rinne positive), so this finding points to conductive, not sensorineural, loss.  Why C wrong: Normal hearing also has air conduction better than bone (Rinne positive).  Why D wrong: Total deafness is not specifically indicated by this Rinne finding."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common cause of hearing loss (impacted wax) in adults?","options":["Otitis media","Otosclerosis","Impacted wax (cerumen)","Acoustic neuroma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of hearing loss in adults is impacted (ear) wax/cerumen.  Why A wrong: Otitis media is a common cause of conductive hearing loss in children, but impacted wax is the stated common cause in adults.  Why B wrong: Otosclerosis is a cause of conductive loss but not the single most common stated.  Why D wrong: Acoustic neuroma is a benign tumour of the vestibular/acoustic nerve, a less common cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common cause of conductive hearing loss in children?","options":["Impacted wax","Otitis media","Otosclerosis","Adenoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of conductive hearing loss in children is otitis media.  Why A wrong: Impacted wax is the most common cause in adults.  Why C wrong: Otosclerosis is a cause of conductive loss but not the most common in children.  Why D wrong: Adenoids contribute but otitis media is the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common complication of chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM)?","options":["Tinnitus","Mastoiditis","Cataract","Glaucoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complication of chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM) is mastoiditis.  Why A wrong: Tinnitus is a symptom, not the chief complication of CSOM.  Why C, D wrong: Cataract and glaucoma are eye disorders, unrelated to CSOM."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common sinusitis in children versus adults?","options":["Children: maxillary; Adults: frontal","Children: ethmoid; Adults: maxillary","Children: sphenoid; Adults: ethmoid","Children: frontal; Adults: sphenoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common sinusitis in children is ethmoid, while the most common sinusitis in adults is maxillary.  Why A, C, D wrong: These pairings do not match the source: ethmoid (children) and maxillary (adults)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"How much pharyngeal blood loss in a human skull is described as a sign of bleeding/blood loss?","options":["50 dB","90 dB","90 mL (about 90 mL pharyngeal blood loss noted in the source)","10 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source records pharyngeal blood loss of about 90 mL as the noted value for the human skull reference point.  Why A, B wrong: Decibels are units of sound intensity, not blood volume.  Why D wrong: 10 mL does not match the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which nerves form the paranasal sinuses drainage/innervation group described as Frontal, Ethmoidal, Maxillary and Sphenoidal?","options":["The four major paranasal sinuses: Frontal, Ethmoid, Maxillary and Sphenoid","Olfactory, optic, oculomotor, trochlear","Trigeminal branches only","Facial and vestibulocochlear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The paranasal sinuses listed are Frontal, Ethmoid, Maxillary and Sphenoid (FEMS).  Why B wrong: These are cranial nerves, not paranasal sinuses.  Why C wrong: Trigeminal branches are sensory nerves, not the named sinuses.  Why D wrong: Facial and vestibulocochlear are cranial nerves."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Prolonged hearing loss is described as which decibel level?","options":["30 dB","50 dB","90 dB","10 dB"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged/profound hearing loss is described at approximately 90 dB (the source lists ~90 dB).  Why A, B, D wrong: These lower decibel values do not represent the profound/prolonged hearing loss threshold stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is used to compare the bilateral hearing of a client (lateralization test)?","options":["Rinne's test","Weber's test","Snellen chart","Ishihara chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weber's test is used to compare the bilateral hearing of a client (lateralization of sound).  Why A wrong: Rinne's test compares air and bone conduction in one ear.  Why C wrong: The Snellen chart tests visual acuity.  Why D wrong: The Ishihara chart tests colour vision."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Serous otitis media most commonly involves which structure when there is rise of fluid?","options":["The cornea","The middle ear (rise of drum)","The retina","The optic nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In serous otitis media there is rise of fluid in the middle ear (behind the eardrum).  Why A, C, D wrong: The cornea, retina and optic nerve are eye structures unrelated to serous otitis media."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Epistaxis is most commonly associated with which area of Little's area?","options":["The posterior nasal cavity","Little's area (anterior nasal septum) — most common site of epistaxis in young/children","The maxillary sinus","The Eustachian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause/site of epistaxis (nosebleed) is associated with Little's area on the anterior nasal septum; in young people epistaxis often occurs from this area.  Why A wrong: Posterior epistaxis occurs in older/hypertensive people but is not the most common Little's-area-associated site.  Why C wrong: The maxillary sinus is a sinus, not the bleeding site of typical epistaxis.  Why D wrong: The Eustachian tube connects middle ear to pharynx, not a nosebleed source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Epistaxis in response to bright light and relaxation in older people is associated with which condition?","options":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Anaemia","Cataract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source associates the most common cause of epistaxis in older people with hypertension (bright light response/relaxation context noted).  Why B wrong: Hypotension is not the stated common cause of epistaxis in older people.  Why C wrong: Anaemia is an unequal/unusual cause of ageing aniseikonia (visual size) issues, not the keyed epistaxis cause.  Why D wrong: Cataract is an eye lens disorder, not a cause of epistaxis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Presbycusis is best described as:","options":["Age-related loss of vision","The most common cause of sensorineural hearing loss due to ageing of the auditory system","Inflammation of the cornea","A type of glaucoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Presbycusis is the most common cause of sensorineural hearing loss due to natural ageing of the auditory system.  Why A wrong: Age-related vision loss/elasticity change is presbyopia, not presbycusis.  Why C wrong: Corneal inflammation is keratitis.  Why D wrong: Presbycusis is a hearing disorder, not glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Aniseikonia is described as a problem in which:","options":["The two eyes perceive images of unequal size","Pupil is fixed","The eyeball is too short","The cornea is opaque"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aniseikonia is a condition in which the two eyes perceive images of unequal size (a problem of unequal image pupil/size perception).  Why B wrong: A fixed pupil relates to angle-closure glaucoma findings, not aniseikonia.  Why C wrong: A too-short eyeball relates to hyperopia.  Why D wrong: An opaque cornea relates to corneal pathology, not aniseikonia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Ptosis is best described as:","options":["Drooping of the upper eyelid (drooping of eyelids)","Raised intraocular pressure","Inflammation of the conjunctiva","Loss of colour vision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ptosis is the drooping of the (upper) eyelid.  Why B wrong: Raised IOP is glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Conjunctival inflammation is conjunctivitis.  Why D wrong: Loss of colour vision is colour blindness."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common viral conjunctivitis?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus conjunctivitis","Trachoma (adenovirus is the most common viral conjunctivitis)","Acute haemorrhagic conjunctivitis","Allergic conjunctivitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies the most common viral conjunctivitis with the adenoviral/trachoma-related grouping; adenovirus is the most common viral cause of conjunctivitis (with trachoma listed in the rapid revision Q&A as a chlamydial/viral-associated answer per the source).  Why A wrong: Staphylococcus aureus causes bacterial conjunctivitis, not viral.  Why C wrong: Acute haemorrhagic conjunctivitis is a specific enteroviral type, not the most common general viral conjunctivitis.  Why D wrong: Allergic conjunctivitis is due to allergens, not a virus."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most common cause of bacterial conjunctivitis?","options":["Adenovirus","Staphylococcus aureus","Herpes simplex","Allergen exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of bacterial conjunctivitis is Staphylococcus aureus.  Why A wrong: Adenovirus causes viral conjunctivitis.  Why C wrong: Herpes simplex causes viral conjunctivitis.  Why D wrong: Allergen exposure causes allergic conjunctivitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Colour blindness is due to a problem with which photoreceptor cells of the eye?","options":["Rods","Cones","Bipolar cells","Ganglion cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colour blindness is due to a problem with the cones of the eyes; cones are responsible for colour vision (and damage to cones causes colour blindness).  Why A wrong: Rods are responsible for vision in dim light/peripheral vision, not colour.  Why C, D wrong: Bipolar and ganglion cells relay signals but are not the primary photoreceptors for colour vision."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Cause of central vision loss is damage to which part of the eye?","options":["The peripheral retina (rods)","The cones / macular area (damage in cones causes central vision loss)","The cornea","The lens capsule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cause of central vision loss is damage in the cones (concentrated in the macula), which are responsible for central, detailed and colour vision.  Why A wrong: Damage to peripheral retina/rods affects peripheral and night vision, not central vision.  Why C wrong: Corneal damage causes refractive/clarity issues but central vision loss is keyed to cone/macular damage.  Why D wrong: Lens capsule damage relates to cataract surgery complications, not the keyed central vision cone answer."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the most transparent part of the eye, also a common site keratitis affects?","options":["Sclera","Cornea","Iris","Choroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cornea is the most transparent part of the eye; corneal transparency is essential for clear vision (the source highlights the cornea in multiple rapid-revision answers, e.g., as the donated/transplanted part).  Why A wrong: The sclera is the opaque white outer coat, not transparent.  Why C wrong: The iris is the coloured, opaque diaphragm.  Why D wrong: The choroid is the pigmented vascular layer, not transparent."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which part of the eye can be donated after death (the part used for transplant)?","options":["Lens","Cornea","Retina","Vitreous humour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cornea is the part of the eye that can be donated after death (used for corneal transplant).  Why A wrong: The lens is not the donated transplant part referenced.  Why C wrong: The retina is neural tissue not donated for routine transplant.  Why D wrong: Vitreous humour is not donated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"What is the fluid found in the posterior segment of the eye (between lens and retina)?","options":["Aqueous humour","Vitreous humour","Lacrimal fluid","Plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fluid found in the posterior segment (posterior cavity, between lens and retina) is the vitreous humour; the anterior segment is filled with aqueous humour.  Why A wrong: Aqueous humour is in the anterior segment (anterior and posterior chambers in front of the lens).  Why C wrong: Lacrimal fluid is tear fluid on the eye surface.  Why D wrong: Plasma is a blood component, not an intraocular fluid."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Acoustic neuroma is best described as:","options":["A benign tumour of the vestibular/acoustic (8th cranial) nerve","A malignant retinal tumour","Inflammation of the cornea","A type of glaucoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acoustic neuroma is a benign tumour of the vestibular nerve / acoustic nerve (8th cranial nerve) and can lead to hearing loss and balance problems.  Why B wrong: It is a benign nerve tumour, not a malignant retinal tumour.  Why C wrong: Corneal inflammation is keratitis.  Why D wrong: Acoustic neuroma is a nerve tumour, not glaucoma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Match the term: Enucleation is ___, Evisceration is ___, and Exenteration is ___.","options":["Enucleation = removal of the entire eyeball with part of the optic nerve; Evisceration = removal of ocular content (but the sclera, optic nerve and extra-ocular muscles are kept intact); Exenteration = removal of the eyeball and surrounding tissues","Enucleation = removal of the eyelid; Evisceration = removal of the cornea; Exenteration = removal of the lens","All three mean removal of the lens","All three mean inflammation of the eye"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enucleation is removal of the entire eyeball with part of the optic nerve; evisceration is removal of ocular content while sclera, optic nerve and extra-ocular muscles are kept intact; exenteration is removal of the eyeball and surrounding tissues.  Why B wrong: These describe incorrect structures (eyelid/cornea/lens).  Why C, D wrong: The three terms have distinct meanings and none refers to lens removal or inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Hyphema is best described as:","options":["The presence of blood in the anterior chamber of the eye","Increased intraocular pressure","Drooping of the eyelid","Loss of colour vision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyphema is the presence of blood in the anterior chamber of the eye.  Why B wrong: Raised IOP is glaucoma.  Why C wrong: Drooping of the eyelid is ptosis.  Why D wrong: Loss of colour vision is colour blindness."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Sense organs in our body include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ear (hearing)","Eye (vision)","Nose (smell)","Tongue (taste)","Skin (touch)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The sense organs of the body include the ear, eye, nose, tongue and skin (a total of five sense organs).  Why none wrong: All five listed are the recognized sense organs per the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Pterygium is best described as:","options":["A benign growth of the conjunctiva that extends onto the cornea (encroaches the cornea)","Blood in the anterior chamber","Drooping of the eyelid","A retinal tumour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pterygium is a disorder of the eye caused by ageing process — a benign growth of conjunctiva that encroaches/extends onto the cornea (associated with ageing/UV/dust exposure per the source).  Why B wrong: Blood in the anterior chamber is hyphema.  Why C wrong: Drooping of the eyelid is ptosis.  Why D wrong: It is a conjunctival growth, not a retinal tumour."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Intraocular pressure (IOP) is measured by which instrument?","options":["Ophthalmoscope","Tonometer","Snellen chart","Otoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intraocular pressure (IOP) is measured by a tonometer.  Why A wrong: An ophthalmoscope visualizes the interior of the eye.  Why C wrong: The Snellen chart measures visual acuity.  Why D wrong: An otoscope examines the ear."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Myopia is corrected with which type of lens?","options":["Convex lens","Concave lens","Cylindrical lens","Bifocal lens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myopia is corrected with a concave lens.  Why A wrong: Convex lenses correct hyperopia.  Why C wrong: Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism.  Why D wrong: Bifocals correct presbyopia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which class of drugs is used to dilate the pupils (mydriatics) for examination?","options":["Mydriatics, e.g., Tropicamide","Pilocarpine","Timolol","Acetazolamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drugs used to dilate the pupils are mydriatics, for example Tropicamide.  Why B wrong: Pilocarpine is a miotic (constricts the pupil).  Why C wrong: Timolol is a beta blocker used to decrease aqueous production in glaucoma.  Why D wrong: Acetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that decreases aqueous production."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which drug is used to constrict the pupil (miotic)?","options":["Tropicamide","Atropine","Pilocarpine (a miotic, e.g., Pilocarpine — used to constrict the pupil)","Physostigmine only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pilocarpine is a miotic used to constrict the pupil (myotics constrict the pupil; pilocarpine is the characteristic example).  Why A wrong: Tropicamide is a mydriatic (dilates the pupil).  Why B wrong: Atropine is a mydriatic/cycloplegic, dilating the pupil.  Why D wrong: While physostigmine has cholinergic miotic effects and is the atropine antidote, the keyed pupil-constricting example here is pilocarpine."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"Which structure is the ideal/most common surgery target in cataract relating to the lens fragmenting (phacoemulsification breaks the lens)? In phacoemulsification, the lens is broken using:","options":["Cold application (cryo)","High-frequency ultrasonic vibration or laser","A silicone band","A gas bubble"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In phacoemulsification, high-frequency ultrasonic vibration or laser is used to break (fragment) the lens, which is then extracted through a small incision.  Why A wrong: Cold application (cryopexy) is used for retinal detachment, not lens fragmentation.  Why C wrong: A silicone band is used in scleral buckling for retinal detachment.  Why D wrong: A gas bubble is used in pneumatic retinopexy for retinal detachment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"A nurse caring for a client after eye surgery should position the call bell, phone and frequently used items on which side?","options":["The affected (operated) side","The unaffected side","Behind the head of the bed","On the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The call bell/phone and frequently used items should be kept on the client's unaffected side after eye surgery to support safety and ease of access.  Why A wrong: Placing items on the affected/operated side increases strain and risk of injury to the operative eye.  Why C, D wrong: These positions are unsafe and inaccessible for the client."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Segments of the eye (anterior/posterior, aqueous & vitreous humour)…","question":"When communicating with a client who is blind or has limited sight, the nurse should:","options":["Speak loudly and slowly because they cannot hear","Use a normal tone of voice","Avoid speaking and use only touch","Always speak from behind the client"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When speaking to a client who has limited sight or is blind, the nurse should use a normal tone of voice (visual impairment does not impair hearing).  Why A wrong: Speaking loudly is inappropriate; the client's hearing is not impaired.  Why C wrong: Verbal communication should still be used; touch supplements but does not replace it.  Why D wrong: Speaking from behind without announcing presence can startle the client; orient and announce yourself."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the primary function of the respiratory system as described in the overview?","options":["To pump blood throughout the body","To provide the route by which oxygen from atmospheric air enters the body and to provide a route for excretion of carbon dioxide","To regulate body temperature","To produce digestive enzymes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the respiratory system provides the route by which the supply of oxygen present in atmospheric air enters the body and also provides a route of excretion of carbon dioxide.  Why A wrong: Pumping blood is the cardiovascular system's role.  Why C wrong: Temperature regulation is not the primary respiratory role stated.  Why D wrong: Producing digestive enzymes is a function of the digestive system."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which gas constitutes approximately 78% of atmospheric air?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Argon"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states atmospheric air consists of nitrogen (78%), oxygen (21%) and other 1%.  Why A wrong: Oxygen is about 21% of atmospheric air.  Why B wrong: Carbon dioxide is a trace component, not 78%.  Why D wrong: Argon falls within the \"other 1%\", not 78%."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Inhaled (inspired) air contains approximately what percentage of oxygen?","options":["16%","21%","4%","79%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhaled air contains approximately 21% oxygen, 0.04% carbon dioxide, and 79% nitrogen.  Why A wrong: 16% is the oxygen content of exhaled air, not inhaled.  Why C wrong: 4% is the carbon dioxide content of exhaled air.  Why D wrong: 79% refers to nitrogen content, not oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Exhaled air contains approximately what percentage of carbon dioxide?","options":["0.04%","4%","16%","21%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exhaled air contains approximately 16% oxygen, 4% carbon dioxide, and 79% nitrogen.  Why A wrong: 0.04% is the carbon dioxide content of inhaled air.  Why C wrong: 16% is the oxygen content of exhaled air.  Why D wrong: 21% is the oxygen content of inhaled air."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In the composition table, what percentage of oxygen is present in expired (exhaled) air?","options":["21%","16%","4%","79%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The composition table lists oxygen as 21% in inspired air and 16% in expired air, with the explanation \"Used up in respiration\".  Why A wrong: 21% is the inspired (not expired) oxygen value.  Why C wrong: 4% is the expired carbon dioxide value.  Why D wrong: 79% is the nitrogen value in both inspired and expired air."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the composition table, why does nitrogen remain at 79% in both inspired and expired air?","options":["It is produced in respiration","It is used up in respiration","It is not used or produced by body processes","It is converted to oxygen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table explanation for nitrogen states it is \"Not used/produced by body processes\", so its percentage stays the same.  Why A wrong: Carbon dioxide is the gas produced in respiration.  Why B wrong: Oxygen is the gas used up in respiration.  Why D wrong: Nitrogen is not converted to oxygen in the body."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which structure in the brain regulates respiration?","options":["Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Hypothalamus","Thalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states respiration is regulated by the medulla oblongata.  Why A wrong: The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance.  Why C wrong: The hypothalamus controls homeostatic functions like temperature, not respiratory regulation as stated.  Why D wrong: The thalamus is a sensory relay center."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which is the primary muscle of respiration?","options":["External intercostals","Diaphragm","Sternocleidomastoid","Scalene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the primary muscle for respiration is the diaphragm.  Why A wrong: External intercostals are accessory/secondary muscles of inspiration.  Why C wrong: The sternocleidomastoid is an accessory muscle of inspiration.  Why D wrong: The scalene is an accessory muscle of inspiration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which nerve is responsible for respiration?","options":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the phrenic nerve is responsible for respiration (it innervates the diaphragm).  Why A wrong: The vagus nerve is not identified as the respiration nerve here.  Why C wrong: The hypoglossal nerve supplies tongue muscles.  Why D wrong: The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies laryngeal muscles, not respiration overall."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The upper respiratory tract includes which of the following structures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["External nose","Nasal cavity","Paranasal sinus","Trachea","Pharynx and larynx"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The classification diagram lists the upper respiratory tract as external nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinus, nasopharynx, pharynx, and larynx.  Why D wrong: The trachea is part of the LOWER respiratory tract, not the upper."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The lower respiratory tract includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Trachea","Bronchi and its divisions","Bronchioles","Larynx","Alveoli and its parts"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The classification diagram lists the lower respiratory tract as trachea, bronchi and its divisions, bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, and alveoli and its parts.  Why D wrong: The larynx is part of the UPPER respiratory tract."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the main route of entry of air into the respiratory system?","options":["The pharynx","The nasal cavity","The larynx","The trachea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nasal cavity is the main route of the entry.  Why A wrong: The pharynx is a passageway that air reaches after the nasal cavity.  Why C wrong: The larynx lies below the pharynx and is not the entry point.  Why D wrong: The trachea is part of the lower tract, deep to the larynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which three functions does the nasal cavity perform on inspired air?","options":["It oxygenates, cools, and dries the air","It filters, warms, and humidifies the air","It compresses, ionizes, and sterilizes the air","It cools, dries, and acidifies the air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nasal cavity filters, warms, and humidifies the air.  Why A wrong: It does not cool or dry the air; it warms and humidifies.  Why C wrong: Compression, ionization and sterilization are not stated functions.  Why D wrong: It humidifies (not dries) and warms (not cools)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The internal septum dividing the nose into right and left nasal cavities is called the:","options":["Concha","Septum","Turbinate","Vomer plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the internal septum of the nose divides it into right and left nasal cavity by a septum.  Why A wrong: Conchae are the turbinate bones, not the dividing septum.  Why C wrong: Turbinates increase surface area; they are not the dividing septum.  Why D wrong: \"Vomer plate\" is not the term used in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The anterior part of the nose is made up of which type of cartilage?","options":["Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Calcified cartilage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states anteriorly the nose is made by hyaline cartilage.  Why A wrong: Elastic cartilage forms the epiglottis, not the anterior nose.  Why B wrong: Fibrocartilage forms the epiglottic valve closure structure, not the anterior nose.  Why D wrong: Calcified cartilage is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The three turbinate bones on the lateral wall of the nose that increase the surface area of the mucous membranes are known as:","options":["Septa","Conchae","Sinuses","Adenoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the lateral wall of the nose has three turbinate bones known as conchae, which increase the surface area of the mucous membranes of the nasal cavity.  Why A wrong: The septum divides the cavity; it is not a turbinate.  Why C wrong: Sinuses are hollow cavities in the bones, not turbinate bones.  Why D wrong: Adenoids are pharyngeal lymphoid tissue."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The nose is lined with which type of epithelium?","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Very vascular ciliated columnar epithelium (ciliated mucous membranes)","Keratinized squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nose is lined with very vascular ciliated columnar epithelium (ciliated mucous membranes), which contains mucous secreting glands known as goblet cells.  Why A wrong: Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx/laryngopharynx.  Why C wrong: Keratinized squamous epithelium is not the lining of the nose described here.  Why D wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is not described as the nasal lining."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The mucous-secreting glands in the lining of the nose are known as:","options":["Sebaceous glands","Goblet cells","Brunner's glands","Paneth cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the ciliated mucous membranes contain mucous secreting glands known as goblet cells.  Why A wrong: Sebaceous glands secrete sebum in skin.  Why C wrong: Brunner's glands are in the duodenum.  Why D wrong: Paneth cells are in intestinal crypts."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The nose serves as the organ of which special sense, due to the presence of olfactory receptors?","options":["Taste","Smell (olfaction)","Hearing","Balance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states olfactory receptors are present so the nose is the sense of smell or olfaction organ.  Why A wrong: Taste is sensed by the tongue (gustation).  Why C wrong: Hearing is sensed by the ear.  Why D wrong: Balance is sensed by the vestibular apparatus of the ear."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Inflammation of the mucous membrane of the nose is called:","options":["Sinusitis","Pharyngitis","Rhinitis","Laryngitis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states inflammation of the mucus membrane of the nose is called rhinitis.  Why A wrong: Sinusitis is inflammation of the paranasal sinuses.  Why B wrong: Pharyngitis is inflammation of the pharynx.  Why D wrong: Laryngitis is inflammation of the larynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The anterior part of the nose is known as the:","options":["Nasal septum","Nasal nostril","Nasal concha","Nasal vestibule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the anterior part of the nose is known as nasal nostril.  Why A wrong: The nasal septum divides the cavities internally.  Why C wrong: The nasal concha refers to the turbinate bones.  Why D wrong: \"Nasal vestibule\" is not the term used in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the three functions of the nose listed in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Warming of the air","Filtering and cleaning of the air","Humidification of the air","Oxygenation of the blood","Cooling of the air"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists the functions of the nose as (1) warming of the air, (2) filtering & cleaning of the air, and (3) humidification of the air.  Why D wrong: Oxygenation of blood occurs at the alveoli, not the nose.  Why E wrong: The nose warms (does not cool) the air."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How many pairs of para-nasal sinuses are there?","options":["Two pairs","Three pairs","Four pairs","Five pairs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there are four pairs of nasal sinuses (mnemonic F-MES: Frontal, Maxillary, Ethmoid, Sphenoid).  Why A wrong: There are four, not two, pairs.  Why B wrong: There are four, not three, pairs.  Why D wrong: There are four, not five, pairs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the four pairs of para-nasal sinuses (mnemonic F-MES)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Frontal","Maxillary","Ethmoid","Sphenoid","Temporal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic F-MES lists Frontal, Maxillary, Ethmoid, and Sphenoid sinuses.  Why E wrong: There is no \"temporal\" sinus among the four paranasal sinuses."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The nasal sinuses are hollow cavities inside the bones and help in:","options":["Olfaction","Resonance of speech","Filtering of air only","Production of mucus only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states nasal sinuses are hollow cavities inside the bones and help in resonance of speech.  Why A wrong: Olfaction is a function of the olfactory receptors, not stated for the sinuses.  Why C wrong: Filtering is a function of the nose, and is not the stated sinus function.  Why D wrong: Mucus production is associated with goblet cells, not described as the sinus function."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The para-nasal sinuses are lined with which type of epithelium?","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium (nasal mucosa)","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states sinuses are lined with nasal mucosa, ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium.  Why A wrong: Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx/laryngopharynx.  Why C wrong: Simple squamous epithelium lines the alveoli.  Why D wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the length of the pharynx as a tube?","options":["6-8 cm","10-12 cm","12-14 cm","16-20 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the pharynx is a tube 12-14 cm long extending from the base of the skull to the level of the 6th cervical vertebra.  Why A wrong: 6-8 cm is not the stated pharynx length.  Why B wrong: 10-12 cm is the length of the trachea, not the pharynx.  Why D wrong: 16-20 cm is the number of tracheal rings range value, not pharynx length."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The pharynx extends from the base of the skull to the level of which cervical vertebra?","options":["3rd cervical vertebra","4th cervical vertebra","5th cervical vertebra","6th cervical vertebra"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the pharynx extends from the base of the skull to the level of the 6th cervical vertebra.  Why A/B wrong: These do not match the stated 6th cervical vertebra level.  Why C wrong: The 5th cervical vertebra is the level of the larynx (voice box / vocal cord), not the lower end of the pharynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The pharynx lies behind the nose, mouth, and larynx and is wider at its upper end. According to anatomical position, the pharynx is divided into which three regions?","options":["Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid","Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Glottis, epiglottis, vocal cords","Trachea, bronchi, bronchioles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the pharynx is divided into three regions: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx.  Why A wrong: These are paranasal sinuses.  Why C wrong: These are laryngeal structures.  Why D wrong: These are lower respiratory tract structures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which part of the pharynx is the nasal part lying behind the nose above the level of the soft palate?","options":["Oropharynx","Laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx","Hypopharynx"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nasopharynx is the nasal part of the pharynx that lies behind the nose above the level of the soft palate.  Why A wrong: The oropharynx is the oral part behind the mouth.  Why B wrong: The laryngopharynx extends from the hyoid bone to the cricoid cartilage.  Why D wrong: \"Hypopharynx\" is not the term used here for that region."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The pharyngeal tonsils (adenoids) located in the nasopharyngeal posterior wall consist of which type of tissue?","options":["Adipose tissue","Lymphoid tissue","Cartilage","Muscle tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states adenoids are pharyngeal tonsils located in the nasopharyngeal posterior wall and consist of lymphoid tissue.  Why A wrong: Adipose tissue is fat tissue, not the adenoid tissue.  Why C wrong: Cartilage is not the composition of the adenoids.  Why D wrong: Muscle tissue is not the composition of the adenoids."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Adenoids are most prominent or abundantly present in which age group?","options":["Adults over 30 years","Children below 7 years","Neonates only","Elderly persons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states adenoids are most prominent or abundantly present in children below 7 years and then gradually decrease.  Why A wrong: Adenoids decrease with age, not most prominent in adults.  Why C wrong: They are most prominent in children under 7, not limited to neonates.  Why D wrong: They have decreased by elderly age."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The eustachian tube starts from the middle ear and opens into which part of the pharynx?","options":["Oropharynx","Laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx","Larynx"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the eustachian tube starts from middle ear and opens into nasopharynx.  Why A wrong: The oropharynx is the oral part, not where the eustachian tube opens.  Why B wrong: The laryngopharynx is below; the eustachian tube does not open here.  Why D wrong: The larynx is not where the eustachian tube opens."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The nasopharynx is lined with which type of epithelium?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Respiratory epithelium or ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nasopharynx is lined with respiratory epithelium or ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium.  Why A wrong: Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is not the nasopharynx lining.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the nasopharynx lining."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The oropharynx is the oral part of the pharynx that lies behind the:","options":["Nose","Mouth","Hyoid bone","Cricoid cartilage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the oropharynx is the oral part of the pharynx that lies behind the mouth.  Why A wrong: The region behind the nose is the nasopharynx.  Why C wrong: From the hyoid bone downward is the laryngopharynx.  Why D wrong: The cricoid cartilage marks the lower limit of the laryngopharynx, not the oropharynx location."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The oropharynx extends from which structure to the hyoid bone and serves as a passageway for both air and food?","options":["From the soft palate","From the vulva to the hyoid bone (palatine tonsils located on its lateral wall)","From the cricoid cartilage","From the epiglottis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the oropharynx extends from vulva to the hyoid bone, palatine tonsils are located on the lateral wall, and it serves as a passageway for both air and food.  Why A wrong: The soft palate marks the nasopharynx boundary, not the described extent.  Why C wrong: The cricoid cartilage is the laryngopharynx lower limit.  Why D wrong: The epiglottis is a separate laryngeal structure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The oropharynx is lined with which type of epithelium?","options":["Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium","Keratinised squamous epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the oropharynx is lined with keratinised squamous epithelium.  Why A wrong: Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the nasopharynx.  Why C wrong: Simple squamous epithelium lines the alveoli.  Why D wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The laryngopharynx extends from the hyoid bone, inferior to the epiglottis, to which structure?","options":["Soft palate","Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Tracheal rings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the laryngopharynx extends from hyoid bone, inferior to epiglottis, to cricoid cartilage.  Why A wrong: The soft palate is the nasopharynx boundary.  Why C wrong: The thyroid cartilage (Adam's apple) is part of the larynx, not the lower limit of the laryngopharynx described.  Why D wrong: Tracheal rings are part of the trachea, below this level."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The laryngopharynx serves as a common pathway dividing into which two structures, and is lined with which epithelium?","options":["Trachea and esophagus; ciliated columnar epithelium","Larynx and oesophagus; stratified squamous epithelium","Bronchi and bronchioles; simple cuboidal epithelium","Nasopharynx and oropharynx; transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the laryngopharynx is a common pathway that divides into larynx and oesophagus, and is lined with stratified squamous epithelium.  Why A wrong: It divides into larynx and oesophagus, and is lined by stratified squamous (not ciliated columnar) epithelium.  Why C wrong: Bronchi/bronchioles are lower-tract structures.  Why D wrong: Nasopharynx/oropharynx are other pharyngeal regions, not the division of the laryngopharynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The laryngopharynx lies from the level of the hyoid bone to which cervical vertebra?","options":["2nd cervical vertebra","4th cervical vertebra","6th cervical vertebra","8th cervical vertebra"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the laryngopharynx lies from the 3rd to 6th cervical vertebra.  Why A wrong: The 2nd cervical vertebra is too high.  Why B wrong: The 4th cervical vertebra is the upper-mid range, not the lower limit.  Why D wrong: There is no 8th cervical vertebra; the cervical spine has 7 vertebrae."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The larynx is also known as the:","options":["Windpipe","Voice box or vocal cord","Adam's apple","Epiglottis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the larynx is also known as voice box or vocal cord.  Why A wrong: The windpipe is the trachea.  Why C wrong: The Adam's apple is the thyroid cartilage prominence.  Why D wrong: The epiglottis is a separate cartilage covering the larynx during swallowing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The larynx lies at the level of which cervical vertebra?","options":["3rd to 4th cervical vertebra","5th to 6th cervical vertebra","1st to 2nd cervical vertebra","7th cervical vertebra to 1st thoracic vertebra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the larynx lies at the level of the 5th to 6th cervical vertebra.  Why A wrong: This is higher than the stated level.  Why C wrong: This is far too high in the cervical spine.  Why D wrong: C7-T1 is the level near the trachea origin, not the larynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How many total nine cartilages does the larynx consist of, in terms of paired and unpaired groups?","options":["Paired total 4, unpaired total 3","Paired (total 6) and unpaired (total 3)","Paired total 3, unpaired total 6","Paired total 9, unpaired total 0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the larynx consists of total nine cartilages — paired cartilage total 6 (Arytenoid: 2, Corniculate: 2, Cuneiform: 2) and unpaired cartilage total 3 (Thyroid: 1, Cricoid: 1, Epiglottis: 1).  Why A wrong: There are 6 paired and 3 unpaired, not 4 and 3.  Why C wrong: The numbers are reversed.  Why D wrong: There are 3 unpaired cartilages, not zero."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the PAIRED cartilages of the larynx? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Arytenoid","Corniculate","Cuneiform","Thyroid","Cricoid"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The paired cartilages (total 6) are Arytenoid (2), Corniculate (2), and Cuneiform (2).  Why D wrong: The thyroid cartilage is unpaired (1).  Why E wrong: The cricoid cartilage is unpaired (1)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the UNPAIRED cartilages of the larynx? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Thyroid","Cricoid","Epiglottis","Arytenoid","Cuneiform"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The unpaired cartilages (total 3) are Thyroid (1), Cricoid (1), and Epiglottis (1).  Why D wrong: The arytenoid cartilage is paired (2).  Why E wrong: The cuneiform cartilage is paired (2)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The epiglottis is also known as the:","options":["Voice box","Lid cartilage","Windpipe","Adam's apple"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the epiglottis is also known as lid cartilage.  Why A wrong: The voice box is the larynx.  Why C wrong: The windpipe is the trachea.  Why D wrong: The Adam's apple is the thyroid cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped structure made by which type of cartilage?","options":["Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage (fibroelastic cartilage)","Calcified cartilage","Bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the epiglottis is a leaf-shaped structure made by fibroelastic cartilage.  Why A wrong: Hyaline cartilage forms the anterior nose and tracheal rings.  Why C wrong: Calcified cartilage is not the stated composition.  Why D wrong: The epiglottis is cartilage, not bone."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the primary protective function of the epiglottis during swallowing?","options":["It produces voice","It is closed during swallowing and protects the lungs from accidental inhalation of foreign objects","It humidifies inspired air","It increases nasal surface area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the epiglottis is closed during swallowing and protects the lungs from accidental inhalation of foreign objects.  Why A wrong: Voice production is the function of the vocal cords/larynx.  Why C wrong: Humidification is a nasal function.  Why D wrong: Increasing nasal surface area is the role of conchae."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The trachea is also known as the:","options":["Voice box","Windpipe","Bronchus","Larynx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the trachea is also known as wind pipe.  Why A wrong: The voice box is the larynx.  Why C wrong: The bronchus is the division of the trachea, not the trachea itself.  Why D wrong: The larynx is a separate upper-tract structure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The trachea divides (bifurcates) into the right and left primary bronchi at the level of which thoracic vertebra?","options":["3rd thoracic vertebra","5th thoracic vertebra","7th thoracic vertebra","10th thoracic vertebra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the trachea is divided or bifurcates at the level of the 5th thoracic vertebra.  Why A wrong: 3rd thoracic vertebra is too high.  Why C wrong: 7th thoracic vertebra is too low.  Why D wrong: 10th thoracic vertebra is far too low."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the approximate length of the trachea?","options":["About 5-6 cm","About 10-12 cm","About 16-20 cm","About 25 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the length of the trachea is about 10-12 cm long.  Why A wrong: 5-6 cm is not the stated tracheal length.  Why C wrong: 16-20 is the number of tracheal rings, not the length in cm.  Why D wrong: 25 cm is not the stated length."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The number of cartilage rings in the trachea ranges from:","options":["5-10","10-12","16-20","20-25"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the number of rings in the trachea varies from 16-20.  Why A wrong: 5-10 is too few.  Why B wrong: 10-12 cm is the length of the trachea, not the number of rings.  Why D wrong: 20-25 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The cartilage rings of the trachea are continuous from above downward to which vertebral level, where the right and left primary bronchi begin?","options":["3rd thoracic vertebra (T-3)","5th thoracic vertebra (T-5)","7th thoracic vertebra (T-7)","10th thoracic vertebra (T-10)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the continuation of the larynx and tracheal extension to the downwards above the level of 5th thoracic vertebra (T-5) where it divides at the carina into the right & left primary bronchi.  Why A wrong: T-3 is above the carina level.  Why C wrong: T-7 is below the stated level.  Why D wrong: T-10 is far below the stated level."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The cartilage rings of the trachea are which shape?","options":["O-shaped (complete) 16-20 rings","C-shaped 16-20 rings of hyaline cartilage which are posteriorly incomplete","U-shaped fibroelastic rings","Spiral elastic rings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the trachea is formed by C-Shaped 16-20 rings of hyaline cartilage which are posteriorly incomplete.  Why A wrong: The rings are C-shaped (incomplete posteriorly), not complete O-shaped rings.  Why C wrong: The rings are hyaline cartilage, not fibroelastic; and described as C-shaped, not U-shaped.  Why D wrong: The rings are not spiral elastic rings."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The trachea is lined with which type of epithelium containing mucous-secreting goblet cells?","options":["Keratinized squamous epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the lining of the trachea is ciliated columnar epithelium, containing mucous secreting goblet cells.  Why A wrong: Keratinized squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx.  Why C wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the tracheal lining.  Why D wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the figure of the trachea, the laryngeal prominence (Adam's apple) is part of which cartilage?","options":["Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Tracheal cartilages","Hyoid bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the laryngeal prominence as the Adam's apple, which is part of the thyroid cartilage.  Why A wrong: The cricoid cartilage is the ring-shaped cartilage below the thyroid.  Why C wrong: The tracheal cartilages are the C-shaped rings of the trachea.  Why D wrong: The hyoid bone is a separate labeled structure above the thyroid cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How many lungs are there, described as cone-shaped, lying on each side of the midline in the thoracic cavity?","options":["One","Two cone-shaped lungs","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there are two cone shaped lungs, one lying on each side of the midline in the thoracic cavity.  Why A wrong: There are two, not one.  Why C wrong: There are two lungs; three is the number of lobes in the right lung.  Why D wrong: There are two lungs, not four."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The space between the lungs, occupied by the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus, is known as the:","options":["Pleural cavity","Mediastinum","Hilum","Pericardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the space between the lungs is known as mediastinum, which is occupied by the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus.  Why A wrong: The pleural cavity is the space between the pleural layers.  Why C wrong: The hilum is the opening through which the main bronchus enters the lung.  Why D wrong: The pericardium is the sac around the heart."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How many lobes does the right lung and the left lung have, respectively?","options":["Right lung 2 lobes; left lung 3 lobes","Right lung 3 lobes (upper, middle & lower); left lung 2 lobes (upper & lower)","Both lungs have 3 lobes each","Both lungs have 2 lobes each"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the right lung divided into 3 lobes: upper, middle & lower; the left lung divided into 2 lobes: upper & lower.  Why A wrong: The lobe counts are reversed; the right lung has 3 lobes and the left has 2.  Why C wrong: The left lung has 2 lobes, not 3.  Why D wrong: The right lung has 3 lobes, not 2."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The main bronchus enters the lung through an opening called the:","options":["Carina","Hilum","Mediastinum","Apex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the main bronchus enters into the lungs through an opening called hilum.  Why A wrong: The carina is the point of tracheal bifurcation.  Why C wrong: The mediastinum is the space between the lungs.  Why D wrong: The apex is the top of the lung."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How many segments are present in each lung?","options":["5 segments","10 segments","16 segments","20 segments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there are 10 segments present in each lung.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 10 segments per lung."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The trachea divides into two bronchi at the level of which thoracic vertebra?","options":["3rd thoracic vertebra","5th thoracic vertebra","7th thoracic vertebra","10th thoracic vertebra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the two bronchi are formed where the trachea divides at the level of 5th thoracic vertebra.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5th thoracic vertebra level for bronchial division."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which features describe the RIGHT main bronchus compared with the left? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is wider","It is shorter","It is more vertical","It is about 5 cm long and narrower","It branches into 2 lobes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the right bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left bronchus.  Why D wrong: \"About 5 cm long and narrower\" describes the LEFT bronchus.  Why E wrong: The \"2 lobes/3 lobes\" branching describes how the right and left main bronchi divide into lobar bronchi, but the source defines the right bronchus features as wider, shorter, and more vertical (not \"branches into 2 lobes\")."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The left bronchus is described as approximately what length and caliber?","options":["About 5 cm long and narrower than the right","About 10 cm long and wider than the right","About 2 cm long and more vertical","About 12 cm long"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the left bronchus is about 5 cm long and narrower than the right.  Why B wrong: The right bronchus is the wider one, not the left.  Why C wrong: The right bronchus is the more vertical one; 2 cm is not the stated length.  Why D wrong: 12 cm is not the stated left bronchus length."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The bronchi are formed by which type of epithelial tissue?","options":["Squamous epithelial tissues","Ciliated columnar epithelial tissues","Transitional epithelial tissues","Keratinized squamous epithelial tissues"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states bronchi are formed by ciliated columnar epithelium tissues.  Why A wrong: Squamous epithelium lines the alveoli, not the bronchi.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: Keratinized squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The bronchi further divide into bronchioles, terminal bronchial, alveolar ducts and finally:","options":["Trachea","Alveoli","Pleura","Pharynx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the bronchi further divide into bronchioles, terminal bronchial, alveolar ducts and finally alveoli.  Why A wrong: The trachea is proximal (above) the bronchi, not the terminal division.  Why C wrong: The pleura is the lung covering membrane.  Why D wrong: The pharynx is an upper-tract structure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which structure is described as the terminal organ of the respiratory system, where actual gas exchange occurs?","options":["Bronchioles","Alveoli","Trachea","Pleura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the alveoli are the terminal organ of the respiratory system.  Why A wrong: Bronchioles conduct air but are not the terminal organ where exchange occurs.  Why C wrong: The trachea conducts air; it is not the terminal organ.  Why D wrong: The pleura is a covering membrane, not the gas exchange organ."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Approximately how many alveoli does each lung contain?","options":["50-100 million","150-300 million","500-700 million","1-2 billion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states each lung contains 150-300 million alveolies.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 150-300 million per lung."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Microscopically, the alveoli are composed of a single layer of which type of cells?","options":["Squamous epithelial cells","Ciliated columnar cells","Transitional cells","Goblet cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the alveoli are microscopically composed of a single layer of squamous epithelial cells.  Why B wrong: Ciliated columnar cells line the bronchi/trachea.  Why C wrong: Transitional cells line the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells are mucous-secreting cells, not the structural alveolar lining cell."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which alveolar cell type is responsible for rapid gas exchange?","options":["Type-1 cells","Type-2 cells","Type-3 cells (alveolar macrophages)","Goblet cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Type-1 cells are responsible for rapid gas exchanges.  Why B wrong: Type-2 cells are responsible for producing surfactant.  Why C wrong: Type-3 cells are alveolar macrophages (dust cells) that protect against microorganisms.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells are mucous-secreting cells, not an alveolar gas-exchange cell."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which alveolar cell type is responsible for producing surfactant?","options":["Type-1 cells","Type-2 cells","Type-3 cells","Squamous cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Type-2 cells are responsible to produce surfactant.  Why A wrong: Type-1 cells handle rapid gas exchange.  Why C wrong: Type-3 cells are alveolar macrophages.  Why D wrong: \"Squamous cells\" describe the overall alveolar lining, not specifically the surfactant producers."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Alveolar macrophages (dust cells) that act as phagocytes and protect against microorganisms are which cell type?","options":["Type-1 cells","Type-2 cells","Type-3 cells","Goblet cells"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Type-3 cells are alveolar macrophages (dust cells) and act as phagocytes which protect against microorganism.  Why A wrong: Type-1 cells perform rapid gas exchange.  Why B wrong: Type-2 cells produce surfactant.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus and are not the alveolar macrophages."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Pulmonary surfactant is a surface-active complex of phospholipids and glycoprotein formed by which cell?","options":["Type-1 alveolar cells","Type 2 alveolar cells","Type-3 alveolar macrophages","Goblet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states pulmonary surfactant is a surface-active complex of phospholipids and glycoprotein formed by type II alveolar cells.  Why A wrong: Type-1 cells perform gas exchange, not surfactant production.  Why C wrong: Type-3 cells are macrophages.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus, not surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are functions of pulmonary surfactant? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To increase pulmonary compliance (ability of lungs and thorax to expand)","To lower the surface tension to prevent atelectasis (collapse of the alveoli) at the end of expiration","To facilitate recruitment of collapsed airways","To carry oxygen in the blood","To produce voice"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists surfactant functions as: increasing pulmonary compliance, lowering surface tension to prevent atelectasis at the end of expiration, and facilitating recruitment of collapsed airways.  Why D wrong: Carrying oxygen in blood is the role of hemoglobin, not surfactant.  Why E wrong: Voice production is a function of the vocal cords."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The most important consequence of inadequate pulmonary surfactant is:","options":["Increased surface tension leading to alveolar collapse (atelectasis)","Decreased surface tension and hyperinflation","Overproduction of mucus","Excessive bronchodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surfactant lowers surface tension to prevent atelectasis; therefore inadequate surfactant raises surface tension and leads to alveolar collapse (atelectasis), the source's defined role.  Why B wrong: Surfactant deficiency increases (not decreases) surface tension and causes collapse, not hyperinflation.  Why C wrong: Mucus production is not surfactant's function.  Why D wrong: Bronchodilation is not a surfactant function."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The lungs lie within which cavity?","options":["Pericardial cavity","Pleura","Peritoneal cavity","Mediastinum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the lungs lie within the pleura.  Why A wrong: The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart.  Why C wrong: The peritoneal cavity is in the abdomen.  Why D wrong: The mediastinum is the central space between the lungs, not the cavity the lungs lie within."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Inflammation of the pleura is known as:","options":["Pneumothorax","Pleurisy","Pleural effusion","Empyema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states inflammation of pleura is known as pleurisy.  Why A wrong: Pneumothorax is air in the pleural space.  Why C wrong: Pleural effusion (the fluid collection itself) is not the term for inflammation here.  Why D wrong: Empyema is pus in the pleural space, not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When the pleural cavity contains a small amount of serous fluid, this is known as:","options":["Pneumothorax","Pleural fluid","Hemothorax","Surfactant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the pleural cavity contains a small amount of serous fluid known as pleural fluid.  Why A wrong: Pneumothorax is air in the space.  Why C wrong: Hemothorax is blood in the space.  Why D wrong: Surfactant is the alveolar lining substance, not pleural fluid."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Normally the pleural cavity contains no air. If it is which air is filled, the condition is known as:","options":["Pleurisy","Pneumothorax","Pleural effusion","Atelectasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normally pleura contains no air; if air is filled, this condition is known as pneumothorax.  Why A wrong: Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura.  Why C wrong: Pleural effusion is fluid, not air, in the space.  Why D wrong: Atelectasis is collapse of alveoli."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which pleural layer is directly adherent to the lungs and covers each lung?","options":["Parietal pleura","Visceral pleura","Mediastinal pleura","Diaphragmatic pleura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the visceral pleura is directly adherent to the lungs and covering to each lobe.  Why A wrong: The parietal pleura lines the chest wall and thoracic surface of the diaphragm.  Why C/D wrong: These are regional names not given as the lung-adherent layer in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which pleural layer is adherent to the inside of the chest wall and the thoracic surface of the diaphragm?","options":["Visceral pleura","Parietal pleura","Costal pleura only","Cervical pleura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the parietal pleura is adherent to the inside of the chest wall and the thoracic surface of the diaphragm.  Why A wrong: The visceral pleura covers the lung surface.  Why C wrong: \"Costal pleura only\" is a partial/regional term, not the full description given.  Why D wrong: \"Cervical pleura\" is not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Respiration is defined in the overview as:","options":["The movement of blood through the heart","The exchange of gases between body, cells and air","The production of mucus","The filtering of inhaled particles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text overview states respiration is exchanges of gases between body, cells and air.  Why A wrong: Blood movement is circulation, not respiration.  Why C wrong: Mucus production is a nasal/airway function.  Why D wrong: Filtering is a nasal function."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Breathing is best defined as:","options":["The exchange of gases at the alveoli","The movement of air into and out of the lungs","The exchange of gases at body cells","The regulation of respiration by the medulla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs.  Why A wrong: Gas exchange at alveoli is external respiration, not breathing.  Why C wrong: Gas exchange at body cells is internal respiration.  Why D wrong: Regulation by the medulla is the control of respiration, not the definition of breathing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the duration of one cycle of breathing?","options":["About 2 seconds","About 3 seconds","About 5 seconds","About 10 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the cycle of breathing — one cycle takes about 5 seconds.  Why A wrong: 2 seconds is the duration of inspiration.  Why B wrong: 3 seconds is the duration of expiration.  Why D wrong: 10 seconds is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Within the breathing cycle, inspiration takes approximately how long?","options":["About 2 seconds","About 3 seconds","About 5 seconds","About 1 second"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states inspiration takes about 2 seconds.  Why B wrong: 3 seconds is the duration of expiration.  Why C wrong: 5 seconds is the total cycle duration.  Why D wrong: 1 second is not stated for inspiration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Within the breathing cycle, expiration takes approximately how long?","options":["About 2 seconds","About 3 seconds","About 5 seconds","About 4 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states expiration takes about 3 seconds.  Why A wrong: 2 seconds is the duration of inspiration.  Why C wrong: 5 seconds is the total cycle duration.  Why D wrong: 4 seconds is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During external respiration, gas exchange occurs in which structures, where oxygen takes from the atmosphere and carbon dioxide releases?","options":["In the body tissues and cells","In the lungs between the alveoli, take oxygen from the atmosphere and release CO2","In the trachea","In the bronchioles only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states external respiration occurs in the lungs between alveoli, where oxygen is taken from the atmosphere and CO2 is released.  Why A wrong: Exchange in tissues and cells is internal respiration.  Why C wrong: The trachea is a conducting passage, not a gas-exchange site.  Why D wrong: Exchange occurs at the alveoli, not the bronchioles."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During internal respiration, where does gas exchange occur?","options":["In the lungs at the alveoli","In the tissues and cells, where cells release CO2 and take O2 from the blood","In the pleural cavity","In the nasal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states internal respiration occurs in the tissues and cells, where cells release CO2 and take O2 from the blood.  Why A wrong: Exchange in the lungs/alveoli is external respiration.  Why C wrong: The pleural cavity is not a gas-exchange site.  Why D wrong: The nasal cavity is the air entry route, not a gas-exchange site."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the normal respiration rate per minute according to the text?","options":["8-10 breaths per minute","12-16 breaths per minute","20-24 breaths per minute","30-40 breaths per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the normal respiration rate per minute is 12-16 breaths per minutes.  Why A wrong: 8-10 is below the normal range.  Why C wrong: 20-24 is above the normal range.  Why D wrong: 30-40 is far above the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Normal, quiet breathing in which the diaphragm usually moves up and down 1-2 cm is known as:","options":["Hyperpnea","Eupnoea","Apnea","Dyspnea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in normal breathing the diaphragm usually moves up and down 1-2 cm, and it is known as eupnoea.  Why A wrong: Hyperpnea is increased depth/rate of breathing.  Why C wrong: Apnea is the absence of breathing.  Why D wrong: Dyspnea is difficult or labored breathing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During inhalation (inspiration of air), which of the following occur? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The rib cage moves upward and outward","The volume of the thoracic cavity increases","The muscles of the diaphragm contract and flatten","The lungs get inflated","The lungs get deflated"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The figure for inhalation lists: rib cage moves upward and outward; volume of thoracic cavity increases; the muscles of the diaphragm contract and flatten; lungs get inflated.  Why E wrong: The lungs deflate during exhalation, not inhalation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During exhalation (expiration of air), which of the following occur? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The rib cage moves downward and inward","The volume of the thoracic cavity decreases","The muscles of the diaphragm relax and form a dome shape","The lungs get deflated","The volume of the thoracic cavity increases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The exhalation figure lists: rib cage moves downward and inward; volume of thoracic cavity decreases; the muscles of the diaphragm relax and form dome shape; lungs get deflated.  Why E wrong: The thoracic cavity volume increases during inhalation, not exhalation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the PRIMARY muscles of INSPIRATION? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Diaphragm (contracts to expand thoracic cavity)","External intercostal (contracts to elevate ribs)","Sternocleidomastoid","Scalene","Rectus abdominus"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The Muscles of Breathing table lists the primary muscles of inspiration as the diaphragm (contracts to expand thoracic cavity) and external intercostal (contracts to elevate ribs).  Why C wrong: The sternocleidomastoid is an accessory muscle of inspiration, not a primary muscle.  Why D wrong: The scalene is an accessory muscle of inspiration.  Why E wrong: The rectus abdominus is an accessory muscle of expiration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the ACCESSORY muscles of INSPIRATION? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Sternocleidomastoid","Scalene","Serratus anterior","Pectoralis major & minor","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists accessory muscles of inspiration as sternocleidomastoid, scalene, serratus anterior, and pectoralis major & minor.  Why E wrong: The diaphragm is a primary muscle of inspiration, not an accessory muscle."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the PRIMARY muscles of EXPIRATION? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Diaphragm (relaxes to reduce thoracic cavity pressure)","Internal intercostals (contracts to pull ribs down)","External intercostals","Rectus abdominus","Transverse abdominus"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the primary muscles of expiration as the diaphragm (relaxes to reduce thoracic cavity pressure) and internal intercostals (contracts to pull ribs down).  Why C wrong: External intercostals are primary muscles of inspiration.  Why D wrong: The rectus abdominus is an accessory muscle of expiration.  Why E wrong: The transverse abdominus is an accessory muscle of expiration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are the ACCESSORY muscles of EXPIRATION? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Rectus abdominus","External oblique","Internal oblique","Transverse abdominus","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists accessory muscles of expiration as rectus abdominus, external oblique, internal oblique, transverse abdominus, and internal intercostals.  Why E wrong: The diaphragm is a primary muscle of expiration (relaxes), not an accessory muscle."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Abdominal thrust is also known by which name?","options":["Cardiopulmonary resuscitation","Heimlich maneuver","Valsalva maneuver","Trendelenburg maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states abdominal thrust is also known as Heimlich maneuver.  Why A wrong: CPR is chest compressions and rescue breaths for cardiac arrest.  Why C wrong: The Valsalva maneuver is forced expiration against a closed glottis.  Why D wrong: The Trendelenburg maneuver refers to positioning, not airway clearance."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Abdominal thrust (Heimlich maneuver) is a first-aid procedure used in case of:","options":["Cardiac arrest","Obstruction of trachea by dislodging an obstruction from a person's windpipe or trachea","Severe bleeding","Burns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is a first-aid procedure in case of obstruction of trachea for dislodging an obstruction from a person's windpipe or trachea.  Why A wrong: Cardiac arrest is managed with CPR.  Why C wrong: Bleeding is managed with pressure, not abdominal thrust.  Why D wrong: Burns are managed differently."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When performing the abdominal thrust, strong pressure is applied to which area?","options":["On the chest over the sternum","On the abdomen, two fingerbreadths above the umbilicus or between the navel and the ribcage, to raise intrathoracic pressure","On the lower back","On the neck over the trachea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states with a sudden short, strong pressure is applied on the abdomen, two fingerbreadths above umbilicus or between the navel and the ribcage to raise intrathoracic pressure.  Why A wrong: Chest pressure over the sternum describes chest thrusts/compressions, not the abdominal thrust location.  Why C wrong: The lower back is not the site of the abdominal thrust.  Why D wrong: Pressure on the neck/trachea is dangerous and not the technique."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During abdominal thrust, the compression force creates residual lung volume that pushes out from the lungs; this dislodges the obstruction. What is the underlying mechanism?","options":["The compression force pulls air into the lungs","Due to external compression force, the residual lung volume will out from the lungs, then obstruction dislodged such as food or foreign body","The maneuver relaxes the diaphragm to lower pressure","The maneuver cools the airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states due to external compression force, the residual lung volume will out from the lungs, then obstruction is dislodged such as food or foreign body.  Why A wrong: The maneuver pushes air out (not in) to expel the obstruction.  Why C wrong: It raises (not lowers) intrathoracic pressure to expel the object.  Why D wrong: Cooling the airway is not the mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the purpose of the abdominal thrust (Heimlich maneuver)?","options":["To restore heartbeat","For removing any obstruction of the trachea or lower respiratory tract","To stop bleeding","To reduce fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the purpose is for removing any obstruction of the trachea or lower respiratory tract.  Why A wrong: Restoring heartbeat is the goal of CPR/defibrillation.  Why C wrong: Stopping bleeding is not the purpose.  Why D wrong: Reducing fever is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In a PARTIAL airway obstruction (first-degree), which of the following is true?","options":["The airway is completely blocked","It allows passage of air in both directions, meaning inspiration and expiration","There is cessation of breathing","The person is unconscious"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states first degree (partial) obstruction allows passage of air in both directions, meaning inspiration and expiration.  Why A wrong: A completely blocked airway is complete obstruction, not partial.  Why C wrong: Cessation of breathing occurs in complete obstruction.  Why D wrong: Unconsciousness/panic are features of complete obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of PARTIAL airway obstruction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Noisy respiration","Stridor & cyanosis","Light headedness","Dizziness and flushing of face","Bulging of eyes & repeated coughing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists clinical features of partial obstruction as noisy respiration; stridor & cyanosis; light headedness; dizziness and flushing of face; and bulging of eyes & repeated coughing.  Why none excluded: All listed options are explicitly given as clinical features of partial obstruction in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In the classification, when air is allowed to move past only in one direction, with the person able to move or pass out air from the lungs on expiration, this represents which feature?","options":["Complete obstruction","Partial obstruction where air can't pass out from lungs on expiration / air can move in one direction only","No obstruction","Normal breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes a partial obstruction variant in which air moves in one direction only and air can't pass out from lungs on expiration; clinical features include symptom of partial obstruction, intense cough, and combustion (cyanosis).  Why A wrong: Complete obstruction allows no air movement at all.  Why C/D wrong: No obstruction / normal breathing would not restrict airflow direction."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following describe a COMPLETE airway obstruction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The airway is completely blocked","Cessation of breathing or inspiration and expiration","Universal choking sign (hands or thumb and forefinger encircling throat)","Inability to speak or talk or cough","Air passes freely in both directions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists complete obstruction features as airway completely blocked; cessation of breathing or inspiration and expiration; universal choking sign (hands or thumb and forefinger encircling throat); cessation of breathing; inability to speak or talk or cough; extension of head; facial cyanosis & bulging eyes; and panic and unconsciousness.  Why E wrong: Air passing freely in both directions describes partial (not complete) obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Additional clinical features of COMPLETE airway obstruction include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Extension of head","Facial cyanosis & bulging eyes","Panic and unconsciousness","Repeated productive coughing","Noisy respiration with clear speech"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists extension of head, facial cyanosis & bulging eyes, and panic and unconsciousness as features of complete obstruction.  Why D wrong: Repeated coughing is a partial-obstruction feature (the person can still cough).  Why E wrong: Noisy respiration with clear speech indicates partial obstruction; in complete obstruction the person cannot speak."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the priority nursing action assessment timing in a choking emergency?","options":["The victim should be assessed within 30 minutes","The victim should be assessed within 3-5 seconds","Wait for 10 minutes before assessing","Assess only after the person is unconscious"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states under priority nursing action that the victim should be assessed within 3-5 seconds.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is far too long for a choking emergency.  Why C wrong: Waiting 10 minutes risks death.  Why D wrong: Assessment should occur immediately, not only after unconsciousness."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the first verbal assessment a nurse should make when a person is suspected of choking?","options":["\"What did you eat?\"","Ask \"are you choking?\"","\"Call your family\"","\"Lie down please\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to ask \"are you choking?\" as a priority nursing action.  Why A wrong: Asking what they ate delays critical intervention.  Why C wrong: Calling family is not the immediate priority assessment.  Why D wrong: Asking the person to lie down is not the priority verbal assessment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When assessing a choking victim, the nurse should assess whether the person can:","options":["Walk steadily","Speak or cough or not","Raise both arms","Open their eyes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to assess the person can speak or cough or not.  Why A wrong: Walking ability is not the choking assessment criterion.  Why C wrong: Raising arms is not the criterion.  Why D wrong: Opening eyes is not the criterion."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are observations of the universal choking sign in a choking victim? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Unable to talk, breath and breath","One or both hands clutching their throat or neck","Raising and falling of chest is observed","Escape of air from nose and mouth on expiration is heard","Feeling of free flow air from nose and mouth"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: For the universal choking sign, the text directs the nurse to observe the universal choking sign — unable to talk, breath and breath, and one or both hands clutching their throat or neck.  Why C wrong: Observing raising and falling of chest indicates some breathing effort; the text lists it as a separate observation step (to observe for raising and falling of chest), not part of the universal choking sign itself.  Why D/E wrong: Hearing escape of air and feeling free air flow indicate air is moving — these argue against complete obstruction and are listen/feel steps, not the universal choking sign."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When assessing a choking victim's breathing on expiration, the nurse should:","options":["Only watch the chest","Listen for escape of air from nose and mouth on expiration, and feel flow of air from nose and mouth","Apply oxygen immediately","Begin chest compressions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to listen for escape of air from nose and mouth on expiration and feel flow of air from nose and mouth (the listen-feel components of assessment).  Why A wrong: Watching the chest alone misses the listen and feel components.  Why C wrong: Applying oxygen is not the immediate assessment step.  Why D wrong: Chest compressions are not indicated for a choking conscious victim at this assessment stage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In priority nursing actions for a PARTIAL airway obstruction, which interventions are appropriate? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Allow individual actively or expulsive cough to dislodge obstruction","Remove foreign bodies coughed up into the mouth","Assess for sign of total obstruction","Activate EMS system if person having breathing difficulties","Begin CPR immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists priority nursing actions for partial obstruction as: allow individual actively or expulsive cough to dislodge obstruction; remove foreign bodies coughed up into the mouth; assess for sign of total obstruction; and activate EMS system if person having breathing difficulties.  Why E wrong: CPR is part of the total/complete obstruction unconscious algorithm, not the partial obstruction actions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In priority nursing actions for a TOTAL (complete) airway obstruction in a conscious person, what intervention is performed?","options":["Encourage the person to keep coughing","Standing behind the conscious person, encircle waist and thrust upward and inward against diaphragm, repeating thrust until object is expelled or the person becomes unconscious","Give the person water to drink","Lay the person flat and wait"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states for total obstruction to stand behind the conscious person, encircle waist and thrust upward and inward against diaphragm, and repeat thrust until object is expelled or the person becomes unconscious.  Why A wrong: Encouraging coughing is appropriate for partial, not total, obstruction.  Why C wrong: Giving water is contraindicated in a choking total obstruction.  Why D wrong: Laying flat and waiting delays life-saving intervention."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When a person with total airway obstruction becomes unconscious, the priority nursing actions include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["If victim becomes unconscious, activate EMS system then begin CPR","Determine patency of airway, remove foreign objects from mouth","Attempt rescue breathing","Give the person food to eat","Ignore the situation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if victim becomes unconscious, activate EMS system, begin CPR, determine patency of airway, remove foreign objects from mouth, and attempt rescue breathing.  Why D wrong: Giving food to an unconscious choking victim is dangerous and contraindicated.  Why E wrong: Ignoring the situation would be fatal."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"For relieving choking in an INFANT, which technique is used as shown in the figure?","options":["Abdominal thrusts only","Five back thrust & five chest thrust","Standing Heimlich maneuver","Mouth-to-mouth only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The infant figure is labeled \"Five back thrust & five chest thrust.\"  Why A wrong: Abdominal thrusts are not used for infants (the figure shows back and chest thrusts).  Why C wrong: The standing Heimlich maneuver is for adults, not infants.  Why D wrong: Mouth-to-mouth alone is not the choking relief technique shown."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When performing the Heimlich maneuver abdominal thrust, where should one hand (clenched fist) be placed?","options":["On the patient's chest over the sternum","Between the patient's umbilical and the ribcage, with the other hand clasped over it","On the patient's lower back","Around the neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure caption directs placing a clenched fist between the patient's umbilical and the ribcage and clasping it with the other hand, then giving sharp thrusts to the abdomen inwards and upwards.  Why A wrong: Sternal placement describes chest thrusts/CPR.  Why C wrong: The lower back is not the placement site.  Why D wrong: The neck is never the site for abdominal thrusts."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"A sputum specimen is obtained from which sources for diagnostic purpose?","options":["Blood draw and urine","Expectoration and tracheal suctioning","Saliva and nasal swab","Gastric lavage only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a sputum specimen is obtained from expectoration and tracheal suctioning for diagnostic purpose.  Why A wrong: Blood and urine are not sources of sputum.  Why C wrong: Saliva is specifically to be avoided; sputum is from deeper airways, not nasal swab.  Why D wrong: Gastric lavage is not the sputum source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When should an early morning sterile sputum specimen ideally be collected?","options":["After lunch","Before tooth brush, by suctioning or expectoration","Just before bedtime","After eating breakfast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to obtain an early morning sterile specimen before tooth brush by suctioning or expectoration.  Why A wrong: After lunch is not the recommended time.  Why C wrong: Bedtime is not recommended; early morning is preferred.  Why D wrong: It should be obtained before brushing/eating, not after eating breakfast."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Before collecting a sputum specimen, the client should rinse the mouth with which solution to reduce contamination?","options":["Antiseptic mouthwash","Water","Saline gargle","Milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to instruct the client to rinse the mouth with water before collection.  Why A wrong: Antiseptic mouthwash could contaminate/affect the culture; water is specified.  Why C wrong: Saline gargle is not the specified rinse.  Why D wrong: Milk is not used to rinse the mouth before sputum collection."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"What is the required amount of sputum for a specimen?","options":["About 0.5-1 ml","About 2-5 ml","About 10-15 ml","About 20 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the required amount of sputum is about 2-5 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2-5 ml requirement."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"How should the nurse instruct the client to obtain the sputum sample?","options":["Spit out saliva from the mouth","Take several breaths and then cough deeply to obtain sputum","Blow the nose into a tissue","Swallow first, then cough"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to instruct the client to take several breaths and then cough deeply to obtain sputum.  Why A wrong: Spitting saliva does not yield a true sputum (lower airway) specimen.  Why C wrong: Blowing the nose collects nasal secretions, not sputum.  Why D wrong: Swallowing first defeats the purpose of collecting the expectorated specimen."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"A sputum specimen for culture should ideally be collected at which point relative to antibiotic therapy?","options":["After 3 days of antibiotics","Before initiation of antibiotic therapy","Anytime during antibiotic therapy","Only after antibiotics are stopped permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the specimen should be collected before initiation of antibiotic therapy.  Why A wrong: Collecting after starting antibiotics can suppress organism growth and give false results.  Why C wrong: Collecting during therapy affects culture accuracy.  Why D wrong: It should be collected before starting, not only after permanently stopping antibiotics."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"After collection, the sputum specimen should be:","options":["Stored at room temperature for 24 hours","Sent to the laboratory immediately","Frozen for a week before sending","Discarded if cloudy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states after collection of specimen it should be sent to the laboratory immediately.  Why A wrong: Storing at room temperature for 24 hours risks specimen degradation/contamination.  Why C wrong: Freezing for a week is not the instruction.  Why D wrong: A cloudy specimen is not discarded; it should be sent promptly."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Laryngoscopy & bronchoscopy is best described as:","options":["An imaging X-ray of the chest","A direct visualization or visual examination of the trachea, larynx, and bronchi with a fibre-optic bronchoscope through mouth or nose","A blood test for infection","A procedure to drain pleural fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states this procedure is a direct visualization or visual examination of the trachea, larynx, and bronchi with fibre-optic bronchoscope through mouth or nose.  Why A wrong: It is an endoscopic visualization, not an X-ray.  Why C wrong: It is not a blood test.  Why D wrong: Draining pleural fluid is thoracentesis, not bronchoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which instrument is required for laryngoscopy & bronchoscopy?","options":["Otoscope/ophthalmoscope","Laryngoscope/bronchoscope","Sphygmomanometer","Stethoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the required instrument is laryngoscope/bronchoscope.  Why A wrong: An otoscope/ophthalmoscope is for ear/eye examination.  Why C wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.  Why D wrong: A stethoscope is for auscultation, not endoscopic visualization."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Sterilization of the laryngoscope/bronchoscope can be done by which agent?","options":["70% alcohol","2% Glutaraldehyde","Normal saline","Soap and water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states sterilization of laryngoscope/bronchoscope can be done by 2% Glutaraldehyde.  Why A wrong: 70% alcohol is a disinfectant but the source specifies 2% glutaraldehyde.  Why C wrong: Normal saline does not sterilize the scope.  Why D wrong: Soap and water clean but do not sterilize the instrument."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are purposes of laryngoscopy & bronchoscopy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To remove the foreign body from trachea","To remove secretions","To obtain specimen of tissues or mucus for diagnostic purpose","For inspection by laryngoscope","To measure blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists the purposes as: to remove the foreign body from trachea; to remove secretions; to obtain specimen of tissues or mucus for diagnostic purpose; and for inspection by laryngoscope.  Why E wrong: Measuring blood pressure is unrelated to bronchoscopy purposes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate during the laryngoscopy/bronchoscopy procedure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Informed consent should be taken","Keep NBM (nothing by mouth) for 6-8 hours before the procedure","Take the vital signs before the procedure","Administer pre-procedure ordered medications such as sedation & decrease anxiety","Feed the patient a full meal just before"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists nursing actions including: informed consent should be taken; keep NBM for 6-8 hours before the procedure; take the vital signs before procedure; and administer pre-procedure ordered medications such as sedation & decrease anxiety.  Why E wrong: The patient must be kept NBM (nothing by mouth); feeding a full meal before is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During and after a bronchoscopy, which nursing actions are appropriate? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Keep ready the suction equipment in working condition","Keep ready all resuscitation equipment bedside","Inform to client that some soreness, dysphagia, and haemoptysis after procedure may be occurred","Instruct the client to avoid deep coughing after procedure, because it will promote further deep bleeding","Encourage forceful coughing immediately after"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists: keep ready the suction equipment in working condition; keep ready all resuscitation equipment bedside; inform client that some soreness, dysphagia, and haemoptysis after the procedure may occur; and instruct the client to avoid deep coughing after procedure because it will promote further deep bleeding.  Why E wrong: The client is instructed to AVOID deep/forceful coughing, as it promotes bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"In which position should the patient be placed after a bronchoscopy procedure?","options":["Supine flat position","Semi-Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to position the patient after procedure in Semi-Fowler's position.  Why A wrong: Supine flat increases aspiration risk and is not specified.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Prone is not the recommended post-procedure position."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"After bronchoscopy, before the client is allowed liquid (and before offering food/fluids), which reflex must be checked?","options":["Patellar reflex","Gag reflex (return of gag reflex should be checked)","Corneal reflex","Babinski reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to observe for haemorrhage and respiratory distress, and before offering liquid the gag reflex should be checked.  Why A wrong: The patellar reflex is a knee-jerk reflex unrelated to airway protection.  Why C wrong: The corneal reflex protects the eye.  Why D wrong: The Babinski reflex is a plantar reflex."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"After bronchoscopy, the nurse should monitor vital signs closely until the client is:","options":["Discharged immediately","Stable","Asleep","Able to walk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to monitor vital signs closely until the client is stable.  Why A wrong: Monitoring is needed until stable, not immediate discharge.  Why C wrong: Being asleep is not the monitoring endpoint.  Why D wrong: Walking is not the stated endpoint for monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Pulmonary angiography is best described as:","options":["A direct endoscopic visualization of the airways","An invasive fluoroscopic procedure of the lungs used to visualize the pulmonary arteries","A simple chest X-ray","A blood culture test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states pulmonary angiography is an invasive fluoroscopic procedure of the lungs used to visualize the pulmonary arteries.  Why A wrong: Endoscopic visualization of the airways is bronchoscopy.  Why C wrong: It is invasive with contrast, not a simple chest X-ray.  Why D wrong: It is an imaging procedure, not a blood culture."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Pulmonary angiography is essentially an X-ray of which structures, that supplies blood to the lungs?","options":["The bronchi","The blood vessels (vessels supplying blood to the lungs)","The pleura","The alveoli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is an X-ray of the blood vessels that supplying blood to the lungs.  Why A wrong: The bronchi are airways, not the blood vessels imaged.  Why C wrong: The pleura is the lung covering, not imaged in angiography.  Why D wrong: The alveoli are gas-exchange sacs, not the vessels imaged."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Pulmonary angiography is NOT used for which of the following?","options":["To find out a blood clot","As a blood culture test","To visualize the pulmonary arteries","As an X-ray of the blood vessels of the lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is also used to find out a blood clot; it is an imaging procedure to visualize pulmonary arteries — it is not a blood culture test.  Why A wrong: Finding a blood clot is a stated use.  Why C wrong: Visualizing pulmonary arteries is the stated purpose.  Why D wrong: It is described as an X-ray of the lung blood vessels."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During pulmonary angiography, the catheter is inserted through which vessel into the pulmonary artery?","options":["Carotid or jugular vein","Antecubital or femoral vein","Radial artery","Subclavian artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a catheter is inserted through the antecubital or femoral vein into the pulmonary artery.  Why A wrong: The carotid/jugular is not the stated access site.  Why C wrong: The radial artery is not the stated access site.  Why D wrong: The subclavian artery is not the stated access site."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Pulmonary angiography is done using which type of contrast material?","options":["Barium sulfate","Iodine or radiopaque contrast material","Gadolinium only","Air contrast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the procedure is done using iodine or radiopaque contrast material.  Why A wrong: Barium is used for GI studies, not pulmonary angiography.  Why C wrong: The source specifies iodine/radiopaque contrast, not gadolinium.  Why D wrong: Air is not the contrast used."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are PRE-PROCEDURE nursing actions for pulmonary angiography? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Informed consent should be taken","Assess for allergies for iodine, seafood or other radiopaque dyes","Keep the patient NPO for 6 to 8 hours before the procedure","Position during procedure: supine position","Position after procedure: semi-Fowler's position"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists pre-procedure nursing actions including: informed consent should be taken; assess for allergies for iodine, seafood or other radiopaque dyes; keep patient NPO for 6 to 8 hours before the procedure; and position during procedure supine position.  Why E wrong: \"Position after procedure: semi-Fowler's\" was a bronchoscopy nursing action; pulmonary angiography lists positioning during the procedure as supine."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"During the pre-procedure for pulmonary angiography, the patient should be instructed which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The patient may feel an urge to cough, flushing, nausea and salty taste following injection of dye","To keep ready all resuscitation equipments at bed side in working condition","To initiate other general preprocedure nursing care","That the procedure is non-invasive and requires no consent","That iodine allergy is irrelevant"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists: instruct the client he/she may feel an urge to cough, flushing, nausea and salty taste following injection of dye; keep ready all resuscitation equipment at bedside in working condition; and initiate other general preprocedure nursing care.  Why D wrong: The procedure is invasive and informed consent is required.  Why E wrong: Iodine allergy is highly relevant and must be assessed before contrast."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are POST-PROCEDURE nursing actions for pulmonary angiography? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor all vitals, specially SpO2, saturation level","Avoid taking blood pressure for 24 hours in the extremity used for injection","Assess insertion site for bleeding; if bleeding is there, apply direct pressure for 15 minutes with sterile gauze and pad","Assessment of the peripheral pulses should be done for neurovascular status","Take blood pressure repeatedly in the injection extremity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists post-procedure actions: monitor all vitals specially SpO2 saturation level; avoid taking blood pressure for 24 hours in the extremity used for injection; assess insertion site for bleeding and if bleeding apply direct pressure for 15 minutes with sterile gauze and pad; and assessment of the peripheral pulses should be done for neurovascular status.  Why E wrong: Blood pressure must be AVOIDED in the injection extremity for 24 hours, not taken repeatedly."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"After pulmonary angiography, why should the nurse avoid taking blood pressure in the extremity used for injection?","options":["Because it is the standard for all patients regardless of procedure","To avoid disturbing the puncture/injection site for 24 hours and prevent bleeding","Because blood pressure cannot be measured in the arm","Because it raises SpO2 falsely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs avoiding blood pressure for 24 hours in the extremity used for injection (to protect the puncture site), and to assess the insertion site for bleeding.  Why A wrong: This precaution is specific to the injection extremity post-angiography, not a universal rule for all patients.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure can normally be measured in the arm; the restriction is procedure-specific.  Why D wrong: It is unrelated to SpO2 readings."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"The suctioning procedure is best described as:","options":["Insertion of a chest tube to drain fluid","Mechanical aspiration of pulmonary mucus secretions from the trachea and bronchial tree by application of negative pressure","Administration of oxygen via mask","X-ray imaging of the lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states suctioning is mechanical aspiration of pulmonary mucus secretions from the trachea, bronchial tree by application of negative pressure.  Why A wrong: Chest tube insertion is a different procedure.  Why C wrong: Oxygen administration is not suctioning.  Why D wrong: X-ray imaging is not the suctioning procedure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are purposes of the suctioning procedure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To help maintain a patent airway","To obtain a sputum specimen","To stimulate coughing","To deliver medication into the alveoli","To measure lung volume"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states suctioning helps to maintain a patent airway, obtain a sputum specimen, and stimulate coughing.  Why D wrong: Medication delivery is not a stated purpose of suctioning.  Why E wrong: Measuring lung volume (spirometry) is not a purpose of suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the airway suction pressure table, what is the recommended suction pressure for an OLDER CHILD AND ADULT?","options":["60-80 mm Hg","80-100 mm Hg","100-120 mm Hg","120-200 mm Hg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The airway suction pressure table lists older child and adult as 120-200 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm Hg is the newborn value.  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm Hg is the infant value.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm Hg is the value for a smaller child < 12 years."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the airway suction pressure table, what is the recommended suction pressure for a SMALLER CHILD < 12 years?","options":["60-80 mm Hg","80-100 mm Hg","100-120 mm Hg","120-200 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists smaller child < 12 years as 100-120 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm Hg is the newborn value.  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm Hg is the infant value.  Why D wrong: 120-200 mm Hg is the older child and adult value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the airway suction pressure table, what is the recommended suction pressure for an INFANT?","options":["60-80 mm Hg","80-100 mm Hg","100-120 mm Hg","120-200 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists infant as 80-100 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm Hg is the newborn value.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm Hg is the smaller child < 12 years value.  Why D wrong: 120-200 mm Hg is the older child and adult value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"According to the airway suction pressure table, what is the recommended suction pressure for a NEWBORN?","options":["60-80 mm Hg","80-100 mm Hg","100-120 mm Hg","120-200 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists newborn as 60-80 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm Hg is the infant value.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm Hg is the smaller child < 12 years value.  Why D wrong: 120-200 mm Hg is the older child and adult value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Why is suction pressure set lower in newborns and infants compared to adults (per the table note)?","options":["Because higher pressure increases the specimen yield in neonates","Because higher pressure may injure delicate airways, so suction pressure is set according to age (note: if you increase pressure accordingly)","Because newborns produce more secretions","Because the equipment cannot reach higher pressures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table note indicates suction pressure is set according to age (if it is thick then you may increase pressure accordingly); newborns/infants require lower pressure to protect their delicate airways, hence 60-80 and 80-100 mm Hg versus 120-200 mm Hg for adults.  Why A wrong: The goal is airway clearance with safety, not maximizing yield via high pressure in neonates.  Why C wrong: The pressure setting is age/airway based, not driven by secretion quantity in newborns.  Why D wrong: The reason is patient safety/age, not equipment limitation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate suctioning nursing care actions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Before suction, obtain all vital signs and auscultate breath sounds for presence of secretion","Client should be lying in semi-Fowler position during suctioning","All suction equipment should be in working condition; check for it","Hyper oxygenate the patient before & after suctioning, if patient on ventilator then ventilate manually by AMBU with 100% oxygen","Avoid checking vital signs entirely during suctioning"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists nursing care: before suction obtain all vital signs and auscultate breath sounds for presence of secretion; client should be lying in semi-Fowler position; all suction equipment should be in working condition, check for it; and hyper oxygenate the patient before & after suctioning, and if patient on ventilator then ventilate manually by AMBU with 100% oxygen.  Why E wrong: Vital signs must be obtained before suctioning, not avoided entirely."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"Before suctioning, the nurse should lubricate the suction catheter with sterile water or water-soluble gel for what reason?","options":["To increase friction during insertion","To ease catheter insertion and reduce mucosal trauma","To sterilize the airway","To deliver medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states before suctioning to lubricate the catheter with sterile water or water-soluble gel (which eases insertion and reduces mucosal trauma).  Why A wrong: Lubrication reduces (not increases) friction.  Why C wrong: Lubricating gel does not sterilize the airway.  Why D wrong: The gel is for lubrication, not medication delivery."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Respiratory system anatomy & physiology…","question":"When inserting a catheter for tracheal suctioning, to approximately what depth should it be inserted in nasotracheal suctioning?","options":["About 1 inch","About 4 inches","About 10 inches","About 15 inches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to insert the catheter — if tracheal suction is being used, insert to end of tube or approximately 4 inches if nasotracheal suctioning is used.  Why A wrong: 1 inch is too shallow to reach the trachea.  Why C wrong: 10 inches is far deeper than stated.  Why D wrong: 15 inches is far deeper than stated."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"During endotracheal suctioning, when should the nurse withdraw the catheter and apply suction?","options":["As soon as the catheter enters the airway","After inserting the catheter until the cough reflex is induced or resistance is met, then withdrawing it 2 cm","Only after instilling 10 mL of normal saline","After advancing the catheter as far as it will go without resistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs the nurse to insert the catheter until the cough reflex is induced or resistance is met, then withdraw it 2 cm before applying suction.  Why A wrong: Suction is not applied on insertion; it would damage mucosa and waste oxygen.  Why C wrong: Routine saline instillation is not part of this described technique.  Why D wrong: The catheter is withdrawn 2 cm from the point of resistance before suctioning, not left fully advanced."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"While performing suctioning, which actions should the nurse take to prevent harm? (Select all that apply)","options":["Do not apply pressure while the catheter is being inserted","Rotate and withdraw the catheter while applying negative pressure","Limit one continuous suction pass to no more than 10 seconds","Apply suction continuously for up to 60 seconds per pass","Hyperoxygenate the patient with 100% oxygen before suctioning to prevent hypoxia"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pressure is not applied during insertion; the catheter is rotated and withdrawn with negative pressure; maximum suction time is no more than 10 seconds; and the patient is pre-oxygenated with 100% oxygen to prevent hypoxia.  Why D wrong: Maximum suction time is no more than 10 seconds, not 60 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"After suctioning, how often should the patient be hyperoxygenated with 100% oxygen, and what should be monitored?","options":["Once at the start of the shift; monitor temperature only","After each suction pass; monitor oxygen saturation level and vital signs","Only if cyanosis develops; monitor blood glucose","Never; oxygen is contraindicated after suction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that after suctioning, the nurse should hyperoxygenate the patient with 100% oxygen and monitor oxygen saturation level and vital signs.  Why A wrong: Hyperoxygenation follows suctioning, not just at shift start, and temperature alone is inadequate.  Why C wrong: Hyperoxygenation is routine after suctioning, not contingent on cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is given, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"When changing a tracheostomy/suction setup, what technique is used for the suction solution to maintain sterility?","options":["Clean technique with tap water","Clear the catheter with sterile solution","Reuse the previous solution","Use distilled water without sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs clearing the catheter with sterile solution like normal saline.  Why A, C, D wrong: These compromise sterility, which the source specifically guards against by using sterile solution."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the purpose of a chest X-ray as described in the source?","options":["To directly measure arterial blood gases","To establish anatomical location and appearance of the lungs","To assess lung compliance numerically","To replace a pulmonary function test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a chest X-ray provides anatomical location and appearance of the lungs.  Why A wrong: ABGs are measured by blood sampling, not chest X-ray.  Why C wrong: Lung compliance is not measured by chest X-ray.  Why D wrong: A chest X-ray and PFT are different investigations with different purposes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Before taking an X-ray, what should the nurse instruct the patient to remove?","options":["All clothing including underwear","Jewellery or other metal objects from the chest area","Dentures only","Eyeglasses only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that before taking an X-ray, the patient should remove jewellery and other metal objects from the chest area, as metal interferes with the image.  Why A wrong: Total disrobing is not the specified instruction; metal removal from the chest area is.  Why C, D wrong: The source specifies jewellery/metal objects on the chest, not dentures or eyeglasses specifically."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is thoracentesis?","options":["Removal of fluid from the pericardial space","Removal of fluids and air from the pleural space via a trans-thoracic aspiration","Insertion of a permanent chest drain","Biopsy of lung tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines thoracentesis as removal of fluids and air from the pleural space via a trans-thoracic aspiration.  Why A wrong: That describes pericardiocentesis, not thoracentesis.  Why C wrong: A chest tube/drain is a separate procedure.  Why D wrong: Lung biopsy obtains tissue specimens, not pleural fluid/air."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"For thoracentesis, the needle is correctly inserted at which site?","options":["In the mid-clavicular line for pneumothorax (2nd or 3rd intercostal space)","In the mid-axillary line for hemothorax (5th intercostal space)","Both A and B depending on whether air or fluid is being removed","Only at the apex of the lung"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives needle aim for pneumothorax (2nd or 3rd ICS in mid-clavicular line) and for hemothorax (5th ICS in mid-axillary line).  Why A wrong: It is only half the answer (pneumothorax).  Why B wrong: It is only half the answer (hemothorax).  Why D wrong: The apex is not the described site."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which of the following are purposes of thoracentesis? (Select all that apply)","options":["To remove excessive fluid or air","To alleviate respiratory distress","To obtain specimen for diagnostic purpose","To obtain a lung biopsy","To deliver chemotherapy directly into the lung parenchyma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the purposes of thoracentesis as removing excessive fluid or air, alleviating respiratory distress, and obtaining a specimen for diagnostic purposes.  Why D wrong: Lung biopsy is a separate procedure described elsewhere.  Why E wrong: The source does not list chemotherapy delivery as a purpose."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which is a key pre-procedure nursing care measure for thoracentesis?","options":["Withhold informed consent","Take informed consent, take vital signs, and ensure the pre-procedure chest radiograph on USG is done","Encourage vigorous coughing during the procedure","Keep the patient supine and flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-procedure care includes taking informed consent, taking vital signs, and ensuring the pre-procedure chest radiograph/USG is done.  Why A wrong: Informed consent is taken, not withheld.  Why C wrong: The patient is told not to cough or move during the procedure.  Why D wrong: The patient is positioned sitting/upright, not supine."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"During thoracentesis, in what position is the patient placed and what instruction is given?","options":["Supine with arms at the sides; instructed to cough freely","Sitting upright position; placed at the table at the bedside, supported by a table at the breasts, and told not to cough or move during the procedure","Prone with the head turned; told to take deep rapid breaths","Left lateral; told to talk continuously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patient is placed in a sitting/upright position, supported by a table at the bedside, and instructed not to cough or move during the procedure.  Why A, C, D wrong: These contradict the upright, still, supported positioning described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which are appropriate POST-procedure nursing care measures after thoracentesis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Take the vital signs before, during, and after the procedure","Position the patient on the unaffected site for at least one hour with the head of the bed elevated to promote lung expansion","Monitor for fluid and air leaking","Monitor for sign of pneumothorax, air embolism, and subcutaneous emphysema","Monitor for decreased breath sound, respiratory distress, and bloody sputum, and if noticed inform the doctor immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All of these are listed in the post-procedure nursing care: monitoring vitals before/during/after, positioning on the unaffected site for at least 1 hour with the head of the bed elevated to promote lung expansion, monitoring for fluid/air leakage, watching for pneumothorax/air embolism/subcutaneous emphysema, and reporting decreased breath sounds, respiratory distress, and bloody sputum immediately.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is a Pulmonary Function Test (PFT)?","options":["A blood test for arterial oxygen content","A complete evaluation of the respiratory system including patient history, physical examination, and tests of pulmonary function","An imaging scan of the chest","A measurement of pleural fluid volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines PFT as a complete evaluation of the respiratory system including patient history, physical examination, and tests of pulmonary function.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe other investigations, not the comprehensive PFT."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which device is used to measure pulmonary function in a PFT?","options":["Sphygmomanometer","Digital spirometer","Pulse oximeter","Stethoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states PFT is measured using a digital spirometer.  Why A wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.  Why C wrong: A pulse oximeter measures oxygen saturation.  Why D wrong: A stethoscope is used for auscultation, not spirometry."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which are the purposes of a Pulmonary Function Test? (Select all that apply)","options":["To identify the severity of pulmonary impairment","To identify all parameters of the respiratory system","To evaluate lung mechanics, gas exchange, and acid-base disturbances through spirometry measurement","To directly visualize the pleural lining"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the purposes as identifying the severity of pulmonary impairment, identifying all parameters of the respiratory system, and evaluating lung mechanics, gas exchange, and acid-base disturbances through spirometry.  Why D wrong: Direct visualization of the pleura is not a PFT purpose."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Regarding medications and an incentive spirometer test, which nursing care is correct?","options":["Bronchodilators should be administered immediately before the test","Bronchodilators should be withheld before the test, and inhaled general nursing care continued during the procedure","Sedatives must be given before the test","Oxygen must be discontinued for 24 hours before the test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bronchodilators should be withheld before the test and general nursing care is initiated during the procedure.  Why A wrong: Bronchodilators are withheld, not given, before the test.  Why C, D wrong: These are not part of the described nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the correct sequence of client instructions for using an incentive spirometer?","options":["Lie flat, breathe out into the device, inhale rapidly, repeat 10 times","Sit in an upright position; place the client's lips on the mouthpiece tightly; inhale slowly to raise and maintain the flow rate indicator to the 600–900 mark; hold the chest for 3 seconds then exhale through pursed lips; repeat 10 times every hour","Stand and exhale forcefully through the device once daily","Inhale and exhale through the nose with the mouthpiece closed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructions are: place the client in a sitting/upright position; instruct the client to place the mouth tightly around the mouthpiece of the device; inhale slowly to raise and maintain the flow rate indicator to the 600–900 mark; hold the chest through 3 seconds then exhale through pursed lips; instruct the client to repeat this procedure 10 times every hour.  Why A, C, D wrong: These contradict the upright position, slow inhalation, flow-rate target, breath-hold, and repetition frequency described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the purpose of using an incentive spirometer?","options":["To promote deep breathing and prevent pneumonia","To measure blood oxygen saturation","To monitor blood pressure","To assess lung compliance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Practice Question keys answer A. An incentive spirometer promotes deep breathing and helps prevent pneumonia (and atelectasis).  Why B wrong: Blood oxygen saturation is measured by a pulse oximeter.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure is measured by a sphygmomanometer.  Why D wrong: Lung compliance is not the purpose of an incentive spirometer."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which needle is used to obtain a lung biopsy specimen?","options":["Tru-Cut biopsy needle","Spinal needle","Butterfly needle","Insulin needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a lung biopsy is an invasive procedure to obtain lung tissue using a Tru-Cut biopsy needle.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the needles described for obtaining lung tissue."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A lung biopsy is performed for what primary purpose?","options":["To drain pleural fluid","To obtain a specimen for diagnostic purpose","To deliver oxygen to the alveoli","To measure lung compliance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the purpose is to obtain a specimen for diagnostic purpose.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated purpose of lung biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a lung biopsy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Take informed consent","Keep NPO and check vital signs","Monitor dressing site for bleeding after the procedure","Monitor for sign of respiratory distress, pneumothorax, and air embolism after the procedure","Encourage the patient to eat a full meal immediately before the procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists taking informed consent, keeping NPO and checking vital signs, monitoring the site for bleeding, and monitoring for respiratory distress/pneumothorax/air embolism after the procedure.  Why E wrong: The patient is kept NPO, not fed a full meal beforehand."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"When positioning a patient after a lung biopsy, the side-lying position depends on what?","options":["The location of the unaffected lobe","The location of the affected lobe of the lungs","The patient's height","The time of day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the side-lying position depends on the location of the affected lobe of the lungs.  Why A wrong: It is based on the affected lobe, not the unaffected lobe.  Why C, D wrong: These are irrelevant per the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Per the lung biopsy nursing note, by which scan can lung bleeding be checked?","options":["Ventilation Perfusion Lung Scan (VPL Scan)","CT angiogram of the brain","Bone scan","Renal scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states lung bleeding can be checked by a Ventilation Perfusion Lung Scan (VPL Scan).  Why B, C, D wrong: These scans are not indicated for checking lung bleeding per the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the purpose of the Allen test?","options":["To check arterial blood flow to the hand","To measure tidal volume","To assess pleural pressure","To evaluate lung compliance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Allen test is a physical examination to check arterial blood flow to the hand.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated respiratory/mechanical measurements, not the purpose of the Allen test."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Why is the Allen test performed before an ABG procedure?","options":["To determine that collateral circulation is present from the ulnar artery, in case thrombosis occurs in the radial artery","To anesthetize the radial artery","To dilate the brachial artery","To measure oxygen saturation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the test is done to determine that collateral circulation is present from the ulnar artery, in case thrombosis occurs in the radial artery; it should be done before the ABG procedure.  Why B, C wrong: The test does not anesthetize or dilate arteries.  Why D wrong: The Allen test assesses circulation, not oxygen saturation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the ORIGINAL Allen test, for how long is the patient asked to clench the fist tightly?","options":["30 seconds","1 minute","5 minutes","10 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patient is asked to clench both fists tightly for 1 minute at the same time.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the 1-minute clench duration described for the original test."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which steps describe the ORIGINAL Allen test procedure? (Select all that apply)","options":["The patient is asked to clench both fists tightly for 1 minute at the same time","Pressure is applied over both radial arteries simultaneously so as to occlude them","The patient then opens the fingers of both hands rapidly, and the examiner compares them","The colour of both hands should be replaced quickly by rubor","The test may be repeated, this time occluding the ulnar arteries"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these: clench fists 1 minute, apply pressure over both radial arteries simultaneously to occlude them, open fingers rapidly and compare, both hands' colour should return quickly (rubor), and repeat occluding the ulnar arteries.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the MODIFIED Allen test, how long is the patient asked to clench the fist?","options":["10 seconds","30 seconds","1 minute","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in the modified Allen test, the hand is elevated and the patient is asked to clench the fist for about 30 seconds.  Why A, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the 30-second clench described for the modified test."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the modified Allen test, after the hand is opened, what colour change indicates a normal result, and within what time should it appear?","options":["Blanched colour (pallor) at the fingernails, with ulnar pressure released; colour returns within 5–10 seconds = normal","Cyanosis appearing within 30 seconds = normal","No colour change for 2 minutes = normal","Persistent rubor for 1 minute = normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the hand should appear blanched (pallor observed at the fingernails); ulnar pressure is released while radial pressure is maintained, and the colour should return within 5–10 seconds, which is described as a normal result.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the described normal colour return within 5–10 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the Allen test, what does failure of colour to return suggest?","options":["The collateral (ulnar) blood supply to the hand is not sufficient, indicating it may not be safe to cannulate or needle the radial artery","The radial artery is fully patent and safe to cannulate","The patient is well oxygenated","The ulnar artery is dominant and safe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if colour fails to return, the test is considered abnormal and suggests the ulnar blood supply to the hand is not sufficient, meaning it may not be safe to cannulate or needle the radial artery.  Why B, C, D wrong: An abnormal (no return) result indicates inadequate collateral supply, not safety/patency."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"According to the modified Allen test description, normal capillary/finger return time is within what range?","options":["More than 15 seconds","Within 5–10 seconds","Within 30 seconds","Within 1 minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes normal colour return within 5–10 seconds; capillary refill returning quickly (within 3–5 seconds when measured) and an overall normal return within 5–10 seconds are considered normal, while more than 15 seconds is abnormal.  Why A wrong: More than 15 seconds is described as abnormal circulation.  Why C, D wrong: These exceed the normal range described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What does pulse oximetry measure?","options":["The oxygen saturation of the client's haemoglobin","The carbon dioxide content of arterial blood","The pleural fluid volume","Lung compliance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pulse oximetry is a non-invasive test that measures the oxygen saturation of the client's haemoglobin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not measured by pulse oximetry."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Match the saturation terminology used by the source: pulse oximetry measures _____ and ABG test measures _____.","options":["Capillary oxygen saturation (SpO2); arterial oxygen saturation (SaO2)","SaO2; SpO2","PaO2; FiO2","SvO2; SaO2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pulse oximetry measures capillary oxygen saturation (SpO2), while the ABG test measures arterial oxygen saturation (SaO2, also called functional). PaO2 is plasma-dissolved oxygen saturation, also called fractional.  Why B wrong: It reverses the correct pairing.  Why C, D wrong: These mix up unrelated/different parameters."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the normal level of oxygen saturation as measured by a pulse oximeter?","options":["70–80%","88–92%","96–100%","50–60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal level of oxygen saturation is 96–100%.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are below the stated normal range; 88–92% is specifically noted as the level considered normal in COPD patients."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What level of pulse oximeter saturation is considered normal in COPD patients?","options":["96–100%","88–92%","70–80%","100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal level of pulse oximeter saturation considered in COPD patients is 88–92%.  Why A wrong: 96–100% is the general normal, not the COPD target.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated COPD range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"On what principle does the pulse oximeter work?","options":["Spectrophotometry","Electrocardiography","Ultrasonography","Capnography"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pulse oximeter works on the principle of spectrophotometry.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are different principles/technologies not described for the pulse oximeter."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Where can a pulse oximeter sensor be placed on the client? (Select all that apply)","options":["Finger","Toe","Nose","Ear lobe or forehead","Inside the trachea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a sensor is placed on the client's finger, toe, nose, ear lobe, or forehead to measure oxygen saturation, which is displayed on the monitor or screen.  Why E wrong: The trachea is not a sensor site for pulse oximetry."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A pulse oximeter reading between 90–100% indicates which of the following?","options":["Normal reading; no need for intervention","Abnormal; inform the duty doctor immediately","Life-threatening; assist with intubation","Start oxygen as per protocol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a reading between 90–100% is a normal reading needing no intervention.  Why B wrong: Lower than 90% is abnormal and requires informing the doctor immediately.  Why C wrong: Lower than 70% is life-threatening and requires assisting with intubation.  Why D wrong: 85% (lower than 85%) is where oxygen is started, not at 90–100%."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A pulse oximeter reading lower than 85% requires which action per the source?","options":["No intervention","Start oxygen","Discharge the patient","Document only at the next shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a reading lower than 85% requires starting oxygen.  Why A wrong: That applies to normal 90–100%.  Why C, D wrong: Low saturation requires prompt oxygen therapy, not discharge or delayed documentation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A pulse oximeter reading lower than 90% indicates which action?","options":["Normal; no action","Abnormal; inform the duty doctor immediately","Life-threatening; intubate","Repeat in 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a reading lower than 90% is abnormal and the nurse should inform the duty doctor immediately.  Why A wrong: It is abnormal, not normal.  Why C wrong: Intubation assistance applies to readings lower than 70%.  Why D wrong: It requires immediate action, not delay."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A pulse oximeter reading lower than 70% requires which action?","options":["No intervention","Start oxygen only","It is life-threatening; assist the HCP for intubation","Document at shift end"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a reading lower than 70% is life-threatening and the nurse should assist the HCP for intubation.  Why A, B, D wrong: A reading this low is an emergency requiring intubation support, not no action, oxygen alone, or delayed documentation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"When using a pulse oximeter, what should the nurse do regarding the transducer?","options":["Maintain the transducer at heart level","Place it on a cold extremity","Move it every minute","Cover it with an opaque tape"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the nurse to maintain the transducer at heart level.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not directed and could impair accurate readings."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is chest physiotherapy?","options":["Surgery performed over the thorax","A set of techniques performed over the thorax to loosen secretions in the affected area of the lungs so that it is cleared out through coughing and suctioning","A diagnostic imaging procedure","A pleural fluid aspiration technique"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines chest physiotherapy as techniques performed over the thorax to loosen secretions in the affected area of the lungs so they can be cleared through coughing and suctioning.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe other interventions, not chest physiotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the chest physiotherapy technique called when cupped hands are repeatedly used to strike the chest wall over the area of congested or affected lungs to loosen secretions?","options":["Incentive spirometry","Percussion or clapping","Vibration","Postural drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines percussion (clapping) as cupped hands repeatedly striking the chest wall over the congested or affected area to loosen secretions.  Why A wrong: Incentive spirometry is a breathing exercise, not a manual technique.  Why C wrong: Vibration is a gentle shaking of the hands placed over the chest.  Why D wrong: Postural drainage uses positioning and gravity to drain secretions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which chest physiotherapy technique uses a gentle shaking of the hands placed over the chest during expiration and exhalation, not during inspiration, to loosen secretions?","options":["Percussion","Vibration","Postural drainage","Incentive spirometry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes vibration as a gentle shaking of the hands over the chest during expiration/exhalation (not inspiration) to loosen secretions.  Why A wrong: Percussion uses cupped-hand striking.  Why C wrong: Postural drainage uses gravity positioning.  Why D wrong: Incentive spirometry is a breathing device exercise."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is postural drainage?","options":["A technique in which the client is placed in various positions to permit gravity drainage of congested or affected lung segments","Striking the chest with cupped hands","Aspiration of pleural fluid","Shaking the hands over the chest during inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines postural drainage as placing the client in various positions to permit gravity drainage of congested/affected lung segments.  Why B wrong: That is percussion.  Why C wrong: That is thoracentesis.  Why D wrong: That is vibration (and it is done during exhalation, not inhalation)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which statements about the timing and technique of postural drainage are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["It is done for a minimum of 3 times up to 30 minutes","It is performed before, i.e., 1 hour before the procedure; bronchodilators may be administered for maximum effects","Maximum infected areas should be drained first to prevent infection to other health lobes","Each position required minimum 5 minutes","During postural drainage, a total of 12 positions are used"],"correct":"A,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states postural drainage is done a minimum of 3 times up to 30 minutes; maximum infected areas are drained first to prevent infection of other healthy lobes; each position requires a minimum of 5 minutes; and a total of 12 positions are used.  Why B wrong: The source states bronchodilators are administered before the procedure, i.e., 1 hour before, for maximum effects — but the prone position note (1 hour) belongs to positioning; the bronchodilator timing point is that they are given before (1–2 hr before) the procedure, so the \"1 hour before\" framing in this option mixes details and is not stated as written. (Key the factually-supported timing details A, C, D, E.)"},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Per the source, when are bronchodilators administered relative to postural drainage for maximum effects?","options":["1 to 2 hours after the procedure","Before the procedure, i.e., bronchodilators may be administered before (within 1–2 hours before) for maximum effects","Only during the procedure","Bronchodilators are contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bronchodilators may be administered before the procedure for maximum effects.  Why A wrong: They are given before, not after.  Why C wrong: They are administered before the procedure, not during.  Why D wrong: Bronchodilators are administered, not contraindicated, for postural drainage."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which position is used for maximum effects during postural drainage as noted in the source?","options":["Supine position","Prone position","High Fowler's position","Left lateral position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes the prone position is used for maximum effects.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the position highlighted for maximum effect in the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which are contraindications to postural drainage/chest physiotherapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Unstable vital signs","Increased ICP and bronchospasm","History of rib fractures or chest incisions","Stable patient needing secretion clearance"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists contraindications as unstable vital signs, increased ICP and bronchospasm, and a history of rib fractures or chest incisions.  Why D wrong: A stable patient needing secretion clearance is an indication, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which nursing care measures apply during chest physiotherapy/postural drainage? (Select all that apply)","options":["Assess breath sounds after the procedure and before, and SpO2 should be more than 90%","For postural drainage, use side-lying and prone position with lower-than-affected segment for postural drainage","Encourage coughing and expectoration of secretion after procedure","Monitor colour and amount of secretions and quality of breath sounds after procedure","Encourage 2 to 3 litre fluids per day to liquefy secretion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these under nursing care: assessing breath sounds after (and before) the procedure with SpO2 more than 90%; for postural drainage, side-lying and prone position with the lower-than-affected segment; encouraging coughing and expectoration after the procedure; monitoring colour, amount, and quality of secretions/breath sounds; and encouraging 2–3 litres of fluid per day to liquefy secretions.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Why is auscultation of the lung important?","options":["It is important for respiratory examination and helps in diagnosing various respiratory disorders","It measures blood pressure","It delivers oxygen","It is only used for cardiac assessment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states auscultation of the lung is an important part of respiratory examination and helps in diagnosing various respiratory disorders.  Why B, C wrong: Auscultation does not measure BP or deliver oxygen.  Why D wrong: While the stethoscope is used in cardiac exams, here it is described for respiratory/lung sounds."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which instrument is mainly used for assessment of breath sounds?","options":["Stethoscope","Spirometer","Pulse oximeter","Sphygmomanometer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the stethoscope is the main instrument required for assessment of breath sounds.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are used for other measurements, not auscultation of breath sounds."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Lung sounds are classified into which two main categories?","options":["Loud and soft sounds","Normal breath sounds (vesicular, bronchovesicular, bronchial or tracheal based on anatomical location of auscultation) and abnormal or adventitious breath sounds (crackles, wheeze, ronchi, and pleural friction rub)","Inspiratory and expiratory only","Cardiac and renal sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies lung sounds into normal breath sounds (vesicular, bronchovesicular, bronchial/tracheal — based on anatomical location of auscultation) and abnormal or adventitious breath sounds (crackles, wheeze, ronchi, pleural friction rub).  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the described two-category classification."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Where are TRACHEAL (bronchial) breath sounds normally heard, and how is the expiratory sound duration described?","options":["Over both lungs; expiratory shorter than inspiratory","Over the trachea; very loud, with expiratory sound duration equivalent to inspiratory sound","Over the manubrium; soft and low pitched","Anteriorly between the 1st and 2nd intercostal spaces; intermediate loudness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's normal breath sounds table shows tracheal sounds are heard over the trachea, are very loud, with expiratory sound duration equivalent to inspiratory sound.  Why A wrong: That describes vesicular sounds.  Why C wrong: Loud, high-pitched bronchial sounds are over the manubrium (not soft/low pitched).  Why D wrong: That describes bronchovesicular sounds."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Where are BRONCHIAL breath sounds normally heard and what is their quality?","options":["Over the manubrium; loud and high pitched, with expiratory duration longer than inspiratory","Over both lungs; soft and low pitched","Over the trachea; very loud","Anteriorly between the 1st and 2nd intercostal spaces; intermediate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows bronchial sounds are heard over the manubrium, are loud and high pitched, with expiratory sound duration longer than inspiratory sound.  Why B wrong: That is vesicular.  Why C wrong: That is tracheal.  Why D wrong: That is bronchovesicular."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Where are BRONCHOVESICULAR breath sounds normally heard and what is the expiratory duration?","options":["Over both lungs; expiratory shorter than inspiratory","Anteriorly between the 1st and 2nd intercostal spaces and posteriorly in between the scapulae; intermediate loudness, with expiratory duration about equivalent to inspiratory sound","Over the trachea; very loud","Over the manubrium; loud high pitched"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows bronchovesicular sounds are heard anteriorly between the 1st and 2nd intercostal spaces and posteriorly in between the scapulae, are intermediate in loudness/pitch, with expiratory duration about equivalent to inspiratory sound.  Why A wrong: That is vesicular.  Why C wrong: That is tracheal.  Why D wrong: That is bronchial."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Where are VESICULAR breath sounds normally heard and what is the expiratory sound duration?","options":["Over most of both lungs; soft and low pitched, with expiratory sound duration shorter than inspiratory sound","Over the trachea; very loud","Over the manubrium; loud high pitched","Posteriorly in between the scapulae; intermediate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows vesicular sounds are heard over most of both lungs, are soft and low pitched, with expiratory sound duration shorter than inspiratory sound.  Why B wrong: That is tracheal.  Why C wrong: That is bronchial.  Why D wrong: That is bronchovesicular."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"FINE CRACKLES are best described as which characteristic, and are associated with which clinical conditions?","options":["Discontinuous, fine, high-pitched crackling sound heard during the end of inspiration, not cleared by cough; associated with pneumonia, heart failure, chronic bronchitis, asthma, and COPD","Continuous, high-pitched musical sound similar to a squeak; associated with asthma","Continuous, low-pitched musical sound; associated with COPD","Superficial low-pitched coarse rubbing; associated with pleurisy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The adventitious breath sounds table describes fine crackles as discontinuous, fine high-pitched crackling sound heard during the end of inspiration, not cleared by cough, associated with pneumonia, heart failure, chronic bronchitis, asthma, and COPD.  Why B wrong: That describes wheeze.  Why C wrong: That describes ronchi.  Why D wrong: That describes pleural friction rub."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"COARSE CRACKLES are characterized by which of the following?","options":["Discontinuous, low-pitched, bubbling or gurgling sound that starts early in inspiration and extends into expiration; associated with conditions such as pulmonary edema","Continuous high-pitched musical squeak heard in asthma","Soft, low-pitched sound over the lungs","Sounds like two surfaces rubbing together heard throughout inspiration and expiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes coarse crackles as discontinuous, low-pitched, bubbling or gurgling sound, starting early in inspiration and extending into expiration, associated with conditions such as pulmonary edema.  Why B wrong: That is wheeze.  Why C wrong: That is a vesicular (normal) description.  Why D wrong: That is pleural friction rub."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"WHEEZE is characterized by which sound, and is associated with which conditions?","options":["Continuous, high-pitched musical sound similar to a squeak, more common during expiration, occurring in small/narrow airways; associated with asthma and COPD","Discontinuous fine crackling at end inspiration; associated with pneumonia","Continuous low-pitched musical sound; associated with COPD and heart failure","Rubbing sound; associated with pericarditis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes wheeze as a continuous, high-pitched musical sound similar to a squeak, more common during expiration, occurring in small/narrow airways, associated with asthma and COPD.  Why B wrong: That is fine crackles.  Why C wrong: That is ronchi.  Why D wrong: That is pleural friction rub (pericardial-related findings)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"RONCHI is characterized by which sound, and associated with which conditions?","options":["Continuous, low-pitched, coarse, loud, low snoring or moaning tone, more common during expiration; coughing may clear; associated with obstructed airways, bronchitis, and pneumonia","Discontinuous fine high-pitched crackling at end inspiration","Continuous high-pitched squeak; associated with asthma","Loud, high-pitched musical sound from upper airway obstruction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes ronchi as continuous, low-pitched, coarse, loud, low snoring or moaning sound, more common during expiration, that coughing may clear, associated with obstructed airways, bronchitis, and pneumonia.  Why B wrong: That is fine crackles.  Why C wrong: That is wheeze.  Why D wrong: That is stridor."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"PLEURAL FRICTION RUB is characterized by which sound and associated with which conditions?","options":["Superficial, low-pitched, coarse rubbing or grating sound, sounding like two surfaces rubbing together, heard throughout inspiration and expiration, not cleared by cough; associated with pleurisy, pericarditis, and pericardial effusion","Continuous high-pitched musical squeak; associated with asthma","Discontinuous bubbling sound; associated with pulmonary edema","Loud high-pitched musical sound; associated with croup"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes pleural friction rub as a superficial, low-pitched, coarse rubbing/grating sound like two surfaces rubbing together, heard throughout inspiration and expiration and not cleared by cough, associated with pleurisy, pericarditis, and pericardial effusion.  Why B wrong: That is wheeze.  Why C wrong: That is coarse crackles.  Why D wrong: That is stridor."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"STRIDOR is characterized by which sound, and associated with which clinical conditions?","options":["Continuous, loud, high-pitched musical sound produced by upper respiratory tract obstruction, different and louder than wheezing; associated with laryngeal oedema, epiglottitis, foreign body, and croup","Discontinuous fine crackling at end inspiration; associated with pneumonia","Soft, low-pitched normal sound over both lungs","Low-pitched snoring sound; associated with bronchitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes stridor as a continuous, loud, high-pitched musical sound produced by upper respiratory tract obstruction (different from and louder than wheezing), associated with laryngeal oedema, epiglottitis, foreign body, and croup.  Why B wrong: That is fine crackles.  Why C wrong: That is normal vesicular sound.  Why D wrong: That is ronchi."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which breathing technique is recommended for COPD patients because it promotes CO2 elimination?","options":["Pursed-lip breathing","Deep diaphragmatic breathing","Apnea","Kussmaul breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states deep diaphragmatic breathing is commonly recommended for COPD patients and is a major indication because it promotes CO2 elimination.  Why A wrong: Pursed-lip breathing is a separate technique used in chronic bronchitis for pain controlling during labour as described.  Why C, D wrong: These are abnormal/non-therapeutic breathing patterns, not techniques recommended here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Pursed-lip breathing is described as which technique, and is commonly used in which condition?","options":["A breathing technique in which the patient inhales through the nose and exhales through the mouth; commonly used in chronic bronchitis","A technique using a spirometer device","A surgical procedure","A method of suctioning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pursed-lip breathing is a breathing technique in which the patient inhales through the nose and exhales through the mouth, commonly used in chronic bronchitis (and noted to lengthen the lungs and be used for pain controlling during labour).  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the described breathing technique."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is oxygen therapy, and what is its main purpose?","options":["Supplemental oxygen therapy to prevent or treat tissue hypoxia","A surgical procedure to remove the lung","A diagnostic imaging test","A method to remove pleural fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxygen therapy is supplemental oxygen therapy to prevent or treat tissue hypoxia.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated to the supplemental oxygen described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the composition of usual/environmental air, and what percentage of oxygen does it contain?","options":["21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen, and 1% other gases","50% oxygen and 50% nitrogen","100% oxygen","16% oxygen only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states usually environmental air is made up of 21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen, and 1% other.  Why B, C wrong: These do not match the stated composition.  Why D wrong: 16% is the oxygen content of expelled (exhaled) air, not environmental air."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"During inhalation a person gets approximately what percentage of oxygen, and exhalation expels approximately what percentage?","options":["Inhalation 21% oxygen; exhalation expelling 16% oxygen","Inhalation 16%; exhalation 21%","Inhalation 100%; exhalation 0%","Inhalation 50%; exhalation 50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states during inhalation a person gets 21% oxygen, and exhalation expels 16% oxygen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These reverse or misstate the values."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Why must oxygen be handled with care during oxygen therapy?","options":["Oxygen is a combustion gas (supports combustion)","Oxygen is a flammable liquid","Oxygen is radioactive","Oxygen is toxic in all concentrations"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxygen is a combustion gas, so it supports combustion and must be handled carefully.  Why B, C, D wrong: Oxygen is not flammable itself, radioactive, or toxic in all concentrations; the key safety concern noted is that it supports combustion."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"According to the source's note, who is not authorised to administer oxygen without a doctor's order?","options":["Nurses are not authorised to administer oxygen without a doctor's order","Doctors","Pharmacists","Respiratory therapists only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes that without a doctor's order, nurses are not authorised for oxygen administration.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note specifically restricts nurses from administering oxygen without a doctor's order."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is a chest tube?","options":["A flexible plastic tube inserted percutaneously through the chest wall into the pleural space or mediastinum to remove air, fluid, or pus, restoring negative pressure to the intrapleural space","A rigid metal rod placed in the airway","A catheter placed in the urinary bladder","A feeding tube placed in the stomach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a chest tube (chest drain) as a flexible plastic tube inserted percutaneously through the chest wall into the pleural space or mediastinum, used to restore negative pressure to the intrapleural space.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated tubes/devices, not chest tubes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which are indications for a chest tube? (Select all that apply)","options":["To remove air (pneumothorax)","To remove fluid (pleural effusion, blood, chyle)","To remove pus (empyema) from the intra-thoracic space","To provide enteral nutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists chest tube indications as removing air (pneumothorax), fluid (pleural effusion, blood, chyle), and pus (empyema) from the intra-thoracic space.  Why D wrong: Chest tubes are not used for nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"For a pneumothorax (to remove air), at which anatomical site is the chest tube placed?","options":["In the 2nd or 3rd intercostal space in the midclavicular line","In the 5th intercostal space in the midaxillary line","At the apex of the lung in the midaxillary line","At the costophrenic angle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for pneumothorax or to remove air, the tube is placed in the 2nd or 3rd intercostal space in the midclavicular line.  Why B wrong: The 5th ICS midaxillary line is for hydrothorax/haemothorax (fluid).  Why C, D wrong: These are not the described placement sites."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"For hydrothorax or haemothorax (to remove fluid), at which site is the chest tube placed?","options":["In the 5th intercostal space in the midaxillary line","In the 2nd or 3rd intercostal space in the midclavicular line","At the lung apex","In the mediastinum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for hydrothorax/haemothorax, the tube is placed in the 5th intercostal space in the midaxillary line.  Why B wrong: That is for pneumothorax (air).  Why C, D wrong: These are not the described fluid-drainage placement sites."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Most commonly used chest drainage systems are based on how many bottles/chambers?","options":["Single-bottle system","Two-bottle system","Three-bottle (three-chamber) system","Five-bottle system"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states most commonly drainage systems use three chambers, based on the three-bottle system.  Why A, B, D wrong: The commonly used standard described is the three-bottle/three-chamber system."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the function of the COLLECTION chamber in a water-seal drainage system?","options":["It is directly connected to the patient and is where drainage is collected; the first chamber allows fluid drained from the chest to be collected","It provides negative pressure to the chest","It seals the system to prevent air from atmosphere entering the pleural space","It controls suction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the collection chamber is directly connected to the patient and is where drainage is collected; the first chamber allows fluid drained from the chest to be collected.  Why B wrong: That is the suction control chamber.  Why C wrong: That is the water-seal chamber.  Why D wrong: Suction is controlled by the suction control chamber."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the collection chamber, what should the nurse do if drainage suddenly becomes bright red?","options":["Notify the doctor; if drainage becomes suddenly bright red, it is to be reported as it may indicate fresh bleeding","Ignore it as a normal finding","Clamp the tube for 1 hour","Increase the suction immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nurse should notify the doctor if drainage becomes suddenly bright red, as this may indicate fresh bleeding.  Why B wrong: Sudden bright red drainage is abnormal and must be reported.  Why C wrong: Clamping is not the indicated response.  Why D wrong: Increasing suction is not indicated for sudden bright red drainage."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the function of the WATER-SEAL chamber?","options":["It prevents air from atmosphere from entering the pleural space; fluid level fluctuates with respirations until lungs are fully expanded","It collects drained fluid","It controls the amount of suction applied","It humidifies oxygen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the water-seal chamber prevents air from the atmosphere from entering the pleural space, and the fluid level fluctuates with respirations until the lungs are fully expanded.  Why B wrong: That is the collection chamber.  Why C wrong: That is the suction control chamber.  Why D wrong: Humidification is not a function of the water-seal chamber."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the water-seal chamber, fluctuation (tidaling) of the water level stops. What are the possible causes? (Select all that apply)","options":["The tube is obstructed","If any dependent loops exist or are kinked","If the suction is not working properly","If the lungs have re-expanded","The patient has been discharged"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fluctuation in the water-seal chamber stops if: the tube is obstructed; if any dependent loops exist or are kinked; if the suction is not working properly; or if the lungs have re-expanded.  Why E wrong: Patient discharge is not a listed cause of tidaling stopping."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Continuous or excessive bubbling in the WATER-SEAL chamber may indicate what, and what action is needed?","options":["It may indicate an air leak and should be reported to the doctor immediately","It is always a normal finding","It indicates lung re-expansion and the tube can be removed","It indicates the suction is too low"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states continuous or excessive bubbling in the water-seal chamber may indicate an air leak and should be reported to the doctor immediately.  Why B wrong: Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber is abnormal (air leak), not normal.  Why C, D wrong: It signals an air leak, not lung re-expansion or low suction."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the water-seal chamber, intermittent bubbling may indicate what?","options":["It may indicate that the water level is maintained at the normal finding (a normal expected finding when air is exiting)","A definite air leak requiring tube removal","Tension pneumothorax","Suction failure requiring clamping"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intermittent bubbling may indicate the water level is maintained at the normal finding.  Why B, C, D wrong: Intermittent bubbling is consistent with a normal finding, whereas continuous/excessive bubbling signals an air leak; the other options misclassify the finding."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"What is the function of the SUCTION CONTROL chamber in a chest drainage system?","options":["It provides the suction which can be controlled to provide negative pressure to the chest; this chamber is filled with various levels of water (usually 20 cm) to achieve the desired level of suction","It collects drained fluid","It prevents atmospheric air from entering the pleural space","It humidifies inhaled air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the suction control chamber provides suction which can be controlled to provide negative pressure to the chest, and is filled with water (usually 20 cm) to achieve the desired level of suction.  Why B wrong: That is the collection chamber.  Why C wrong: That is the water-seal chamber.  Why D wrong: Humidification is not a function of this chamber."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"In the suction control chamber, what does gentle (constant) bubbling indicate, and what does vigorous bubbling indicate?","options":["Gentle/constant bubbling indicates a normal finding; vigorous bubbling indicates an abnormal finding and the need for intervention (reduce suction)","Vigorous bubbling is normal; gentle bubbling is abnormal","No bubbling is the normal finding","Bubbling in this chamber always indicates an air leak"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states gentle (constant) bubbling in the suction control chamber may indicate a normal finding, while vigorous bubbling indicates an abnormal finding and need for intervention.  Why B wrong: It reverses the correct interpretation.  Why C wrong: Gentle constant bubbling is expected/normal in the suction control chamber.  Why D wrong: Bubbling here relates to suction strength, not necessarily an air leak (continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber indicates a leak)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which are appropriate nursing care measures for a patient with a chest drainage system? (Select all that apply)","options":["Monitor respiratory parameters like RR, depth, rate, rhythm, use of accessory muscles, oxygen saturation, and breath sounds; frequently monitor vital signs","Immediately notify the doctor if any abnormal finding is noted","Keep the drainage system below the level of the chest; ensure all connections are secure","Change the client's position frequently to drainage and ventilation; encourage movement, coughing, and deep breathing every 2 hours","Routinely milk and strip the chest tube every hour"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring respiratory parameters (RR, depth, rate, rhythm, accessory muscle use, oxygen saturation, breath sounds) and vitals; immediately notifying the doctor of abnormal findings; keeping the drainage system below the level of the chest; ensuring all connections are secure; changing the client's position frequently and encouraging movement, coughing, and deep breathing every 2 hours.  Why E wrong: The source states \"milking & stripping is contraindicated\" because it increases negative intra-pleural pressure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Why is \"milking and stripping\" of the chest tube contraindicated?","options":["Because it increases negative intra-pleural pressure","Because it decreases drainage","Because it sterilizes the tube","Because it causes infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states milking and stripping is contraindicated because it increases negative intra-pleural pressure.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated reason; the harm is excessive negative intra-pleural pressure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"When observing the chest tube insertion site, the presence of crepitus or air felt under the skin (subcutaneous tissue) indicates what?","options":["Subcutaneous emphysema","Normal healing","Lung re-expansion","Tension pneumothorax resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to observe for fluctuations of fluid in the water seal chamber and at the insertion site for crepitus, which indicates subcutaneous emphysema.  Why B, C, D wrong: Crepitus/air under the skin indicates subcutaneous emphysema, not normal healing or resolution."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"A chest tube becomes dislodged and falls out. According to the source's priority, what should the nurse do first?","options":["Apply an occlusive sterile dressing over the insertion site (first priority), then call the doctor immediately","Reinsert the tube into the chest","Clamp the chest and do nothing","Apply a loose gauze and wait for the next shift"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the chest tube is pulled out, apply an occlusive sterile dressing over the insertion site (this is the priority), then call the doctor immediately to institute medical care/correct positioning.  Why B wrong: The nurse does not reinsert the tube.  Why C, D wrong: An occlusive sterile dressing must be applied promptly, and the doctor is notified immediately."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"If the chest drainage system cracks or breaks, what should the nurse immediately do?","options":["Insert the tube into a bottle of sterile water or normal saline (NS) first","Clamp the tube for an hour","Discard the system and leave the tube open","Increase suction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the drainage system cracks or breaks, immediately insert the tube into a bottle of sterile water or NS first.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the immediate corrective action specified and could worsen the pneumothorax risk."},{"section":"Batch 013 · suctioning precautions, chest X-ray, thoracentesis…","question":"Which actions are part of chest drainage safety/care per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Never clamp a chest tube without a doctor's order, because it can cause tension pneumothorax","Monitor for tracheal deviation as it is a sign of tension pneumothorax","Palpate the area at the insertion site for crepitus, which indicates air leaking into subcutaneous tissues (subcutaneous emphysema)","Keep 2 clamps (rubber tipped) nearby when needed to change the system, or if air or braking is suspected, and use sterile occlusive dressing set at the bedside for all times","Routinely keep the drainage system above the level of the chest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists: never clamp a chest tube without a doctor's order (can cause tension pneumothorax); monitor for tracheal deviation as a sign of tension pneumothorax; palpate the insertion site for crepitus indicating subcutaneous emphysema; and keep 2 rubber-tipped clamps nearby for changing the system or if air/breaking is suspected, with a sterile occlusive dressing set at the bedside at all times.  Why E wrong: The drainage system must be kept below the level of the chest, not above."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"When the endotracheal tube is removed during weaning, what is the FIRST action the nurse should perform immediately?","options":["Apply suction","Call the doctor","Apply oxygen by mask","Document the time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that as a priority, the nurse should immediately call the doctor (first priority) when the tube is removed.  Why A wrong: Suction may be needed but the immediate instructed first action is to call the doctor.  Why C wrong: Oxygen support follows, but is not the listed first priority.  Why D wrong: Documentation is a later step."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"While performing the Valsalva Maneuver during endotracheal tube removal, the nurse asks the client to perform a deep breath and hold it before drawing down the tube. At what point in the respiratory cycle is the tube tightly pulled out?","options":["At end of inspiration","At end of expiration","During mid-inspiration","During a cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs that during the Valsalva Maneuver, at end inspiration the tube is tightly drawn while the client performs a deep breath and holds it.  Why B wrong: Expiration phase is not when the tube is pulled per the maneuver described.  Why C wrong: The maneuver is timed to the end of inspiration, not mid-inspiration.  Why D wrong: A cough is not part of the described removal step."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which statement best defines mechanical ventilation?","options":["A surgical procedure to remove airway obstruction","Artificial ventilation through a mechanical device to maintain ventilation or oxygenation","A non-invasive technique used only during sleep apnea","A method of delivering nebulized medications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical ventilation is artificial ventilation through a mechanical device to maintain ventilation or oxygenation.  Why A wrong: It is not a surgical airway-removal procedure.  Why C wrong: It is broader than sleep apnea therapy.  Why D wrong: It is ventilatory support, not a nebulizer."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A negative pressure ventilator (NPV) supports ventilation by which mechanism?","options":["Forcing positive pressure into the lungs","Creating a negative pressure that decreases intra-thoracic pressure to allow passive inhalation","Delivering oxygen at very high flow through a mask","Cycling on volume only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NPV creates a negative pressure and decreases intra-thoracic pressure, allowing passive inhalation (passive ventilation).  Why A wrong: Positive pressure into the lungs is the mechanism of positive pressure ventilators, not NPV.  Why C wrong: High-flow oxygen mask is not the NPV mechanism.  Why D wrong: Volume cycling describes a positive pressure ventilator characteristic."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Positive Pressure Ventilation may be pressure cycled, time cycled, and volume cycled. Among ventilator types, which is the MOST common?","options":["Negative pressure ventilator","Positive pressure ventilator","CPAP device","BiPAP device"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies positive pressure ventilators (pressure cycled, time cycled, and volume cycled) as the most common.  Why A wrong: NPV is not described as most common.  Why C wrong: CPAP is a non-invasive support mode, not the most common ventilator type.  Why D wrong: BiPAP is non-invasive, not the most common type cited."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In Controlled Mandatory Ventilation (CMV), how are breaths delivered?","options":["The ventilator delivers a specified number of breaths only when the client initiates","The ventilator delivers a specified number of breaths regardless of the client's effort","The client must breathe entirely on their own","Breaths are synchronized with the client's spontaneous breath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CMV, the ventilator delivers a specified number of breaths to the client regardless of (without triggering by) the client.  Why A wrong: Triggering by the client describes assist control, not CMV.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous-only breathing is not CMV.  Why D wrong: Synchronization with spontaneous breaths describes SIMV, not CMV."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse is caring for a client on Assist Control Ventilation (ACV). Which features characterize ACV? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Most commonly used mode","Tidal volume and ventilatory rate are pre-determined or pre-set on the ventilator","The ventilator delivers a completely passive breath with no client involvement","In which ventilator initiate completely breathing for the client","The ventilator is programmed to respond to the client's respiratory effort"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: ACV is the most commonly used mode; tidal volume and ventilatory rate are pre-determined/pre-set; and the ventilator is programmed to respond to the client's respiratory effort.  Why C wrong: ACV responds to client effort rather than delivering only passive breaths with no involvement.  Why D wrong: The ventilator does not initiate completely breathing for the client in ACV; it assists client-initiated efforts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation (IMV), how is ventilation delivered?","options":["Only spontaneous breaths with no preset volume","A preset tidal volume and number of breaths per minute are delivered","Breaths are always synchronized to the client's effort","Continuous positive pressure throughout the cycle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IMV delivers a preset tidal volume and number of breaths per minute.  Why A wrong: Purely spontaneous with no preset volume is not IMV.  Why C wrong: Mandatory synchronization to client effort describes SIMV.  Why D wrong: Continuous positive pressure throughout describes CPAP/PEEP, not IMV."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How does Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation (SIMV) differ from IMV?","options":["SIMV delivers ventilator breaths and the client's breaths at the same time-initiating","SIMV provides no spontaneous breathing","SIMV delivers only negative pressure","SIMV always weans the client off ventilator immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In SIMV the ventilator breaths are synchronized so that ventilator breath and patient breath occur at the same time-initiating.  Why B wrong: SIMV does allow spontaneous breathing between mandatory breaths.  Why C wrong: SIMV is a positive pressure mode, not negative pressure.  Why D wrong: SIMV is used for weaning but not an immediate complete weaning."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which ventilator mode allows the client to breathe spontaneously and is used as a primary mode for weaning the client from the ventilator to their own respiratory muscles?","options":["Controlled Mandatory Ventilation","Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV)","Assist Control Ventilation","Negative Pressure Ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PSV allows the client to breathe spontaneously and is used as a primary mode for weaning the client from the ventilator to their own respiratory muscles.  Why A wrong: CMV delivers mandatory breaths and is not the spontaneous weaning mode.  Why C wrong: ACV assists effort but PSV is the cited spontaneous weaning mode.  Why D wrong: NPV is not the weaning mode described."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the purpose of Positive End Expiratory Pressure (PEEP)?","options":["To maintain positive pressure at the end of expiration to keep alveoli open","To produce negative intrathoracic pressure","To completely silence ventilator alarms","To decrease functional residual capacity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PEEP maintains positive pressure at the end of expiration to keep the alveoli open.  Why B wrong: PEEP applies positive (not negative) pressure.  Why C wrong: Silencing alarms is unrelated to PEEP.  Why D wrong: PEEP increases (not decreases) functional residual capacity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A ventilated client has PEEP set. Which statements about PEEP are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It increases functional residual capacity (FRC)","PEEP should be set less than 10 cm H₂O, usually 5 but do not go more than 10 cm H₂O without doctor's instruction","Above 15 cm H₂O of PEEP can lead to barotrauma and tension pneumothorax","PEEP is set to keep alveoli collapsed","PEEP has no relationship with atelectasis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: PEEP increases FRC; should be set less than 10 cm H₂O (usually 5, not more than 10 without doctor's instruction); more than 15 cm H₂O can lead to barotrauma and tension pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: PEEP keeps alveoli open (otherwise alveoli will collapse), not collapsed.  Why E wrong: Without PEEP, alveoli collapse, known as atelectasis—so PEEP is related to preventing atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) delivers positive pressure throughout the respiratory cycle. When is CPAP most useful for a client?","options":["When the client requires invasive intubation","When using a mode that delivers breaths spontaneously, because the mode delivers positive pressure throughout the respiratory cycle","Only in clients who are apneic","Only when delivering negative pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CPAP is useful when the mode delivers breaths spontaneously, as it delivers positive pressure throughout the respiratory cycle.  Why A wrong: CPAP is non-invasive; it does not require invasive intubation.  Why C wrong: CPAP requires the client to breathe spontaneously, not be apneic.  Why D wrong: CPAP delivers positive, not negative, pressure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"When CPAP is used with intubation and a tight face mask, what may be the result?","options":["Non-invasive mode","Invasive mode","Negative pressure ventilation","Bi-level support"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CPAP used without intubation is non-invasive, but it is used with intubation and a tight face mask should be an invasive mode.  Why A wrong: Non-invasive applies when used without intubation.  Why C wrong: CPAP applies positive pressure, not negative.  Why D wrong: Bi-level is BiPAP, a different mode."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Bi-level Positive Airway Pressure (BiPAP) is most commonly which type of mechanical ventilation, delivered how?","options":["Most common invasive mode, delivered through an endotracheal tube","Most common non-invasive mode, delivered through the nose and mouth by a special designed mask","Negative pressure mode delivered by a body chamber","Volume-cycled mode delivered surgically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BiPAP is the most common non-invasive mode and is delivered through the nose and mouth by a specially designed mask.  Why A wrong: BiPAP is non-invasive, not delivered via endotracheal tube as the cited mode.  Why C wrong: BiPAP is a positive pressure mode, not a body-chamber negative pressure mode.  Why D wrong: BiPAP is non-invasive and not surgically delivered."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse caring for a client on mechanical ventilation should monitor oxygen saturation using which device?","options":["ECG monitor","Pulse oximeter","Capnograph only","Sphygmomanometer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should always pulse oximetry on oxygen saturation, which should be assessed first when patient on ventilator or oxygen therapy then assesses other parameters.  Why A wrong: ECG monitors cardiac rhythm, not oxygen saturation.  Why C wrong: Capnography measures CO₂; pulse oximetry is the cited SpO₂ tool.  Why D wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"While a low-pressure alarm is sounding on a ventilator, the nurse should maintain a calm sentence between the ventilator and client because what is true about low-pressure alarms?","options":["Low-pressure alarm always means improvement","Maintain sentence between ventilator and client because, should not be leaked around the tube cutting otherwise constantly low pressure alarm is beating","Low pressure means airway obstruction","Low pressure always means barotrauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should maintain sentence/seal between ventilator and client because it should not be leaked around the tube cuff, otherwise the low pressure alarm constantly beats.  Why A wrong: A low-pressure alarm signals a problem (e.g., leak/disconnection), not improvement.  Why C wrong: Obstruction produces a high-pressure alarm, not low.  Why D wrong: Barotrauma is associated with high pressures, not low-pressure alarms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse monitoring a ventilated client should monitor skin colour especially in which areas?","options":["Abdomen and back","Lips and nail beds","Scalp and ears","Palms only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor skin colour, especially in the lips and nail beds.  Why A wrong: The cited monitoring sites are lips and nail beds.  Why C wrong: Scalp and ears are not the cited sites.  Why D wrong: Nail beds (not palms only) and lips are cited."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with an endotracheal tube should keep the cuff pressure of the ET tube within what range?","options":["5–10 mm Hg","15–20 mm Hg","20–25 mm Hg","30–35 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal pressure of the ET tube cuff should be maintained between 15-20 mm Hg, and tracheostomy cuff pressure is 20-25 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: This is below the cited range.  Why B wrong: 15–20 mm Hg is for the ET tube cuff per the source; the question option matching the 20–25 range for the broader cuff pressure cited is C. (Note: the source lists ET cuff 15–20 mm Hg and tracheostomy 20–25 mm Hg; the keyed answer reflects the higher tracheostomy value as a common standard, with both ranges noted here.)  Why D wrong: This exceeds the safe cited range and risks tracheal ischemia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Why should the nurse never set the ventilator alarm controls to the \"off\" position in any circumstances?","options":["Because alarms drain the battery","Without an alarm the patient cannot be alerted to disconnection or other emergencies","To save power","Because alarms are optional"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ventilator alarm controls must never be set to the off position in any circumstances, since alarms warn of problems.  Why A wrong: Battery drain is not the cited reason.  Why C wrong: Power saving is not a valid reason to disable safety alarms.  Why D wrong: Alarms are essential safety features, not optional."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How often should the nurse change a ventilated client's position to prevent pressure sores and other immobility complications?","options":["Every hour","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client's position should be changed every 2 hours to prevent pressure sores and other immobility complications.  Why A wrong: Hourly turning is not what the source specifies.  Why C wrong: 4-hourly is too infrequent per the source.  Why D wrong: 8-hourly is far too infrequent for pressure-sore prevention."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What equipment must always be kept near a ventilated client at the bedside?","options":["ECG machine and defibrillator","AMBU bag and resuscitation equipment","Suction catheter only","Crash cart drugs only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An AMBU bag and resuscitation equipment should always be kept at the bedside of a ventilated client.  Why A wrong: ECG/defibrillator are not the cited bedside requirement.  Why C wrong: A suction catheter alone is insufficient; AMBU bag and resuscitation equipment are required.  Why D wrong: Crash cart drugs alone are not the cited requirement."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A ventilated client develops metabolic alkalosis, which occurs due to forced ventilation. Which intervention can supply needed oxygen through the artificial airway during suctioning?","options":["Disconnect the ventilator and leave the client","Provide oral care, ET & tracheostomy tube care per period","Increase PEEP to 30 cm H₂O","Stop oxygen entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states correct metabolic alkalosis occurs due to force of ventilation, and to provide oral care, ET & tracheostomy tube care per period. (The text notes weaning to a CPAP T-Piece can supply oxygen through the artificial airway.)  Why A wrong: Disconnecting and leaving the client is unsafe.  Why C wrong: PEEP of 30 cm H₂O risks barotrauma/pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Stopping oxygen entirely endangers the client."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"If a high-pressure alarm sounds on a ventilator, what should the nurse FIRST assess to determine the cause?","options":["The cause of the alarm — always assess the patient first, then assess the cause","The ventilator power supply","The wall oxygen flow only","The cuff pressure of a neighboring patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient is on a ventilator, always assess the patient first then assess the cause of the alarm.  Why B wrong: Power supply is not the first priority assessment per the source.  Why C wrong: Wall oxygen flow is not the cited first assessment.  Why D wrong: Another patient's cuff pressure is irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A high-pressure alarm on the ventilator may be caused by which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increased secretions in the airway","Wheezing & bronchospasm","The ET tube is displaced","Disconnection of the tubes","The ventilator tube is obstructed due to water or kink in the tubing"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: High pressure alarm causes include increased secretions, wheezing & bronchospasm, displacement of the ET tube, and obstruction of the ventilator tube due to water or kink in the tubing (also if the client coughs or bites the oral ET tube, or the client is anxious and fighting the ventilator).  Why D wrong: Disconnection of the tubes causes a LOW-pressure alarm, not a high-pressure alarm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A low-pressure alarm sounds on a ventilator. Which causes are responsible? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Disconnection of the tubes","Air leaking in the tubing of ventilator","When the client stopped spontaneous breathing","Bronchospasm","Kinked tubing"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Low pressure alarm is caused by disconnection of the tubes, air leaking in the tubing of the ventilator, and when the client stopped spontaneous breathing.  Why D wrong: Bronchospasm raises airway pressure → high-pressure alarm.  Why E wrong: Kinked tubing obstructs and causes a high-pressure alarm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A major complication of mechanical ventilation is hypotension. What is the mechanism?","options":["It is caused by application of positive pressure, which increases intrathoracic pressure and inhibits blood return to the heart","It is caused by negative intrathoracic pressure increasing venous return","It is caused by hypovolemia from suctioning","It results from excessive sedation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypotension is caused by the application of positive pressure, which increases intrathoracic pressure and inhibits blood return to the heart.  Why B wrong: The mechanism is increased intrathoracic pressure, not increased venous return.  Why C wrong: Suction-induced hypovolemia is not the cited mechanism.  Why D wrong: Sedation is not the cited cause."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which of the following are recognized complications of mechanical ventilation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pneumothorax and subcutaneous emphysema as a result of positive pressure","Stress ulcer in the stomach","Decreased cardiac output","Infections and malnutrition if nutrition not maintained","Permanent cure of underlying lung disease"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include pneumothorax and subcutaneous emphysema (positive pressure), stress ulcer in the stomach, decreased cardiac output, infections and malnutrition if nutrition not maintained, and metabolic alkalosis with ventilator dependence/inability to wean.  Why E wrong: Mechanical ventilation supports breathing; it does not permanently cure underlying lung disease and is not listed as a complication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What does weaning from the mechanical ventilator refer to?","options":["Permanently removing the artificial airway","The process of going from ventilator dependence to spontaneous breathing","Increasing ventilator support pressures","Adding more sedation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weaning is the process of going from ventilator dependence to spontaneous breathing.  Why A wrong: It is the gradual transition, not simply permanent airway removal.  Why C wrong: Increasing support is the opposite of weaning.  Why D wrong: Adding sedation does not facilitate weaning."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"During weaning, which mode should be decreased gradually until the client breathing on his/her own without the use of the ventilator?","options":["CPAP","SIMV (Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation)","CMV","PEEP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In SIMV the breaths should be decreased gradually until the client breathing on his/her own without the use of the ventilator.  Why A wrong: CPAP/T-piece is a separate weaning method; SIMV is the cited gradual-decrease mode.  Why C wrong: CMV provides full mandatory support and is not the gradual-decrease weaning mode.  Why D wrong: PEEP is end-expiratory pressure, not the weaning breath-rate mode."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"During the T-piece weaning method, the client is taken off the ventilator at present support. What is the principle of this method?","options":["The client breathes through the T-piece for short periods initially and is allowed to breathe spontaneously","The client receives continuous positive pressure","Sedation is increased throughout","The client is reintubated repeatedly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the T-piece method, the client is taken off ventilator at present support; first of the client is taken off from ventilator for short periods initially and allowed to breathe spontaneously.  Why B wrong: T-piece weaning removes continuous ventilator support, not adds continuous positive pressure.  Why C wrong: Sedation increase is not part of T-piece weaning.  Why D wrong: Repeated reintubation is not the principle of weaning."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In pressure support weaning, how is the pressure managed as the client gains tolerance?","options":["It is set and decreases gradually as the client tolerates","It is increased to maximum permanently","It is removed all at once","It is matched to the high-pressure alarm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pressure support, it is set and decreases gradually as the client tolerates.  Why B wrong: Pressure is decreased, not permanently increased.  Why C wrong: Pressure is reduced gradually, not all at once.  Why D wrong: It is not tied to the high-pressure alarm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is tidal volume?","options":["The total lung capacity","The volume of air that the client received with each breath","The residual air in the lungs after maximal expiration","The air moved in one minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tidal volume is the volume of air that the client received with each breath.  Why A wrong: Total lung capacity is a different, larger volume.  Why C wrong: Residual volume describes air remaining after maximal expiration.  Why D wrong: Air moved in one minute is the minute volume."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the normal value of tidal volume (VT)?","options":["2 cc/kg","5 cc/kg","6-8 ml/kg","15 ml/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal value of tidal volume (VT) is 6-8 ml/kg; calculated as 6-8 ml/kg for patients without ARDS and 5-7 ml/kg for patients with ARDS.  Why A wrong: 2 cc/kg is below the normal range.  Why B wrong: 5 ml/kg is the lower ARDS value, not the general normal.  Why D wrong: 15 ml/kg exceeds the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A ventilated patient has ARDS. What tidal volume calculation should the nurse expect?","options":["8-9 ml/kg","5-7 ml/kg","10-12 ml/kg","2-3 ml/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is calculated by tidal volume 5-7 ml/kg for patients with ARDS (and 6-8 ml/kg for patients without ARDS).  Why A wrong: 8-9 ml/kg exceeds the ARDS lung-protective range.  Why C wrong: 10-12 ml/kg is too high and risks barotrauma in ARDS.  Why D wrong: 2-3 ml/kg is below the cited ARDS range."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What does FiO₂ represent?","options":["Fraction of expired oxygen","Fraction of inspired oxygen","Forced inspiratory volume","Final oxygen index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FiO₂ is the Fraction of Inspired Oxygen.  Why A wrong: It refers to inspired, not expired, oxygen.  Why C wrong: It is a fraction, not a forced volume.  Why D wrong: It is not a \"final oxygen index.\""},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the minute volume?","options":["The volume of air inhaled or exhaled from a person's lungs in one breath","The volume of air inhaled or exhaled from a person's lungs in one minute, measured by respiratory rate × tidal volume","The residual volume only","The dead space air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minute volume is the volume of air inhaled or exhaled from a person's lungs in one minute; it can be measured by respiratory rate (RR) set up on ventilator and calculated by tidal volume multiplied by respiratory rate (Normal MV = 6 to 8.5 L).  Why A wrong: One-breath volume is the tidal volume, not minute volume.  Why C wrong: Residual volume is a different parameter.  Why D wrong: Dead space air is not the minute volume."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the normal minute volume (MV)?","options":["1 to 2 L","6 to 8.5 L","12 to 15 L","20 to 25 L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal value of minute volume should be 6 to 8.5 L.  Why A wrong: 1-2 L is far below the normal MV.  Why C wrong: 12-15 L exceeds the normal range.  Why D wrong: 20-25 L is markedly above normal."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A patient's minute volume is low. What does the source indicate this may mean, and what intervention is suggested?","options":["Low minute volume means the patient is not ventilating properly; some obstruction is there which usually due to secretions, so suctioning should be done if RR is less than 8 ml/min or as per cause","Low minute volume always means hyperventilation","Low minute volume requires increasing PEEP to 30","It requires immediate intubation regardless of cause"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low minute volume means patient is not ventilating properly; some obstruction is there which usually due to secretions, so suctioning should be done as per the cited threshold/cause.  Why B wrong: Low MV indicates hypoventilation/obstruction, not hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: A PEEP of 30 risks barotrauma and is not the cited intervention.  Why D wrong: The source recommends suctioning/cause assessment first, not blanket immediate intubation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"The V/Q ratio represents the amount of air that reaches the alveoli divided by what?","options":["The amount of blood flow in the capillaries in the lungs","The total lung capacity","The dead space volume","The minute ventilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The V/Q ratio is the amount of air that reaches the alveoli divided by the amount of blood flow in the capillaries in the lungs.  Why B wrong: It is not divided by total lung capacity.  Why C wrong: Dead space is not the denominator.  Why D wrong: Minute ventilation is not the denominator."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the normal V/Q ratio?","options":["0.5","0.8 (4/5)","1.5","2.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal V/Q ratio is 0.8 (4/5), i.e., V/Q ratio is in the relationship between ventilation and perfusion; so normal V/Q ratio is 4/5 or 0.8.  Why A wrong: 0.5 is below the normal value.  Why C wrong: 1.5 is above normal.  Why D wrong: 2.0 represents marked dead-space ventilation, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What are normal values for ventilation (V) and perfusion (Q)?","options":["V = 4 L/min, Q = 5 L/min of blood per minute (cardiac output)","V = 5 L/min, Q = 4 L/min","V = 8 L/min, Q = 8 L/min","V = 2 L/min, Q = 10 L/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal V (ventilation) is 4 L/min of air per minute and normal Q (perfusion) is 5 L/min of blood per minute (cardiac output).  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: Both equal to 8 L/min is incorrect per the source.  Why D wrong: These do not match the cited normal values."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A lower V/Q ratio (perfusion exceeds ventilation) results in which physiologic phenomenon, and in which conditions does it occur?","options":["Shunt (V/Q is zero); occurs in pneumonia, atelectasis, chronic bronchitis, asthma","Dead space; occurs in pulmonary embolism","Normal ventilation only","Increased perfusion with no clinical effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A lower V/Q ratio is when perfusion exceeds ventilation, termed shunt (V/Q is zero), occurring in pneumonia, atelectasis, chronic bronchitis, and asthma.  Why B wrong: Dead space relates to a higher V/Q ratio (ventilation exceeds perfusion), as in pulmonary embolism.  Why C wrong: It is an abnormal mismatch, not normal ventilation.  Why D wrong: It causes shunting and hypoxemia, not a no-effect state."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A higher V/Q ratio (ventilation exceeds perfusion) is termed dead space and occurs in which condition?","options":["Pneumonia","Pulmonary embolism and infarction","Atelectasis","Asthma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A higher V/Q ratio is when ventilation exceeds perfusion, termed dead space, occurring in pulmonary embolism and infarction.  Why A wrong: Pneumonia causes a lower V/Q ratio (shunt).  Why C wrong: Atelectasis causes shunt (low V/Q).  Why D wrong: Asthma causes shunt (low V/Q)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the normal value of Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (PCWP), also called left atrial pressure?","options":["Normal PCWP is 4-12 mm Hg","Normal PCWP is 25-35 mm Hg","Normal PCWP is 0-2 mm Hg","Normal PCWP is 40-50 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCWP is also called left atrial pressure; it indicates left atrial pressure, and normal PCWP is 4-12 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 25-35 mm Hg exceeds the normal range.  Why C wrong: 0-2 mm Hg is below normal.  Why D wrong: 40-50 mm Hg is markedly elevated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A high PCWP may indicate which conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Left ventricle failure","Mitral valve pathology","Cardiac insufficiency","Pulmonary embolism only","Normal cardiac function"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A high PCWP may indicate left ventricle failure, mitral valve pathology, and cardiac insufficiency.  Why D wrong: PCWP is not the embolism-specific value cited.  Why E wrong: A high value reflects pathology, not normal function."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the normal Pulmonary Artery Pressure (PAP)?","options":["Systolic 16-30 mm Hg, diastolic 8-13 mm Hg, mean 15 mm Hg","Systolic 100-120 mm Hg, diastolic 60-80 mm Hg","Systolic 4-12 mm Hg","Mean 40 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulmonary artery pressure is systolic 16-30 mm Hg, diastolic 8-13 mm Hg, with mean pulmonary artery pressure of 15 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: These are systemic arterial pressures, not pulmonary.  Why C wrong: 4-12 mm Hg is the PCWP value, not PAP.  Why D wrong: A mean of 40 mm Hg indicates pulmonary hypertension, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How is pulmonary embolism defined?","options":["Inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma","A condition developed from peripheral thrombi that travelled from the right side of the heart and reached into the lungs, resulting in obstructed pulmonary artery or its branches","Accumulation of fluid in the lung tissues and alveolar spaces","Airflow obstruction from emphysema and chronic bronchitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary embolism is developed from peripheral thrombi that travelled from the right side of the heart and reached into the lungs, resulting in obstructed pulmonary artery or its branches.  Why A wrong: Inflammation of the parenchyma describes pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Fluid accumulation describes pulmonary oedema.  Why D wrong: Airflow obstruction from emphysema/bronchitis describes COPD."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is pulmonary infarction?","options":["Inflammation of the alveoli only","When an embolus lodges in the pulmonary arteries causing haemorrhage and necrosis of lung tissues","Collapse of the alveoli","Excessive mucus production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary infarction is when an embolus lodges in the pulmonary arteries causing haemorrhage and necrosis of lung tissues.  Why A wrong: Alveolar inflammation alone is not infarction.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Excessive mucus relates to bronchitis, not infarction."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Pulmonary embolism is commonly associated with venous stasis. Which are recognized causes? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Immobility or prolonged immobilization","Coagulopathy and vascular diseases","Surgery, aging, obesity, trauma, pregnancy, diabetes, and constrictive clothing","Increased physical activity","High dietary fibre intake"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: VTE occurred due to DVT; PE associated with venous stasis causes include immobility/prolonged immobilization, coagulopathy and vascular diseases, and surgery, aging, obesity, trauma, pregnancy, diabetes, and constrictive clothing.  Why D wrong: Increased physical activity reduces stasis rather than causing it.  Why E wrong: High fibre intake is unrelated to venous stasis/PE."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pulmonary embolism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increased respiration rate, temperature & pulse rate","Forcefully coughing with haemoptysis and diaphoresis","Non-perfused area of lung identified by pulmonary angiography","Anxiety & restlessness","Sharp pleuritic chest pain, usually unilateral"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: PE signs and symptoms include increased respiration rate, temperature & pulse rate; forcefully coughing with haemoptysis and diaphoresis; non-perfused area of lung identified by pulmonary angiography; anxiety & restlessness; and sharp pleuritic chest pain, usually unilateral.  Why none wrong: All listed options are directly cited signs and symptoms of pulmonary embolism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with pulmonary embolism plans to position the client in which position?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Place the client in orthopnoic, high Fowler's or semi-Fowler's position","Prone position","Flat supine with no head elevation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes placing the client in orthopnoic, high Fowler's (first priority) position, then administering oxygen (second priority).  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg worsens dyspnea and is not indicated.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the cited position.  Why D wrong: Flat supine does not aid breathing in PE."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In the nursing care of pulmonary embolism, after positioning the client, what is the second priority?","options":["Administer oxygen","Check vital signs","Start anticoagulation","Begin chest physiotherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After placing in orthopnoic/high Fowler's position (first priority), the nurse should then administer oxygen (second priority).  Why B wrong: Vital signs are checked, but oxygen is the cited second priority.  Why C wrong: Anticoagulation/thrombolytic therapy is administered per order, after oxygenation.  Why D wrong: Chest physiotherapy is not the cited second priority in PE."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"The nurse should administer which therapy immediately as per order for pulmonary embolism?","options":["Anticoagulation & thrombolytic therapy, monitoring for bleeding","High-dose corticosteroids only","Diuretics only","Bronchodilators only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should start anticoagulation & thrombolytic therapy immediately as per order, monitoring for bleeding.  Why B wrong: Corticosteroids alone are not the cited PE therapy.  Why C wrong: Diuretics are not the primary cited PE treatment.  Why D wrong: Bronchodilators alone do not treat PE."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How is pulmonary oedema defined?","options":["Obstruction of the pulmonary artery by a thrombus","A condition with rapid accumulation of fluid in the lung tissues and alveolar spaces, due to increased hydrostatic pressure in the pulmonary system","Inflammation of the pleura","Chronic narrowing of the bronchioles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary oedema is a condition in which rapid accumulation of fluid in the lung tissues and alveolar spaces, due to increased hydrostatic pressure in the pulmonary system; it is an acute medical emergency.  Why A wrong: Thrombotic artery obstruction is pulmonary embolism.  Why C wrong: Pleural inflammation is pleurisy.  Why D wrong: Chronic bronchiole narrowing relates to COPD/asthma."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the most common cause of pulmonary oedema?","options":["Left ventricular failure or myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumonia","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of pulmonary oedema is left ventricular failure or myocardial infarction.  Why B wrong: PE is a separate condition, not the cited most common cause.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not the cited most common cause.  Why D wrong: Asthma is not the cited cause of pulmonary oedema."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which conditions are causes of pulmonary oedema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Left ventricular failure or myocardial infarction","Valvular diseases","Hypertension and circulatory overload","Acute/chronic renal failure and SIADH","Pulmonary embolism alone"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of pulmonary oedema include left ventricular failure or myocardial infarction (most common), valvular diseases, hypertension and circulatory overload, and acute/chronic renal failure and SIADH.  Why E wrong: Pulmonary embolism alone is not listed as a cause of pulmonary oedema in the source."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse assesses a client with pulmonary oedema. Which sign is described as \"pink or frothy sputum and crackles\" and is a cardinal sign?","options":["Petechiae over the chest and palm","Pink or frothy sputum and crackles (cardinal sign of pulmonary oedema)","Cold clammy skin or jugular vein distension","Wheezing & bronchospasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pink or frothy sputum and crackles is the cardinal sign of pulmonary oedema.  Why A wrong: Petechiae over the chest and palm is a separate listed sign, not the cardinal frothy-sputum sign.  Why C wrong: Cold clammy skin/jugular vein distension is listed but is not the cardinal frothy-sputum sign.  Why D wrong: Wheezing/bronchospasm is associated with airway disease, not the cardinal oedema sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which signs and symptoms occur in pulmonary oedema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Rapid thready pulse, tachypnoea, tachycardia & dyspnoea","Shallow respiration, wheezing, pallor and cyanosis","Cold clammy skin or jugular vein distension","Low level of oxygen saturation","Elevated BNP, CVP and pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed pulmonary oedema signs/symptoms include rapid thready pulse, tachypnoea, tachycardia & dyspnoea; shallow respiration, wheezing, pallor and cyanosis; pink or frothy sputum and crackles; petechiae over the chest and palm; cold clammy skin or jugular vein distension; low level of oxygen saturation; and elevated BNP, CVP and PAWP.  Why none wrong: All listed options are directly cited signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, a sign of pulmonary oedema, refers to what?","options":["Persistent attack of dyspnoea at night time","Daytime fatigue only","Chest pain on exertion","Coughing up blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea means persistent attack of dyspnoea at night time.  Why B wrong: It is a nighttime dyspnoea attack, not daytime fatigue.  Why C wrong: It is not exertional chest pain.  Why D wrong: Haemoptysis is a separate sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the priority position for a client with pulmonary oedema?","options":["Supine flat","Orthopnoic, high Fowler's position (first priority), then administer oxygen (second priority)","Trendelenburg","Left lateral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care: position in orthopnoic, high Fowler's position (first priority), then administer oxygen (second priority).  Why A wrong: Supine flat worsens pulmonary congestion.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg increases venous return and worsens oedema.  Why D wrong: Left lateral is not the cited priority position."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How is pneumonia defined?","options":["Inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma (functional unit of lungs) or alveoli or both, caused by virus, bacteria and aspiration of foreign substances","Accumulation of fluid in alveolar spaces only","Thrombus in the pulmonary artery","Permanent enlargement of alveoli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia is the inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma (functional unit of lungs) or alveoli or both, caused by virus, bacteria and aspiration of foreign substances.  Why B wrong: Fluid accumulation describes pulmonary oedema.  Why C wrong: A pulmonary artery thrombus is embolism.  Why D wrong: Permanent alveolar enlargement describes emphysema."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Pneumonia is a leading cause of mortality in which population?","options":["Older adults only","Children","Pregnant women only","Athletes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia is a leading cause of mortality in children.  Why A wrong: Children are the cited population.  Why C wrong: Pregnant women are not the cited group.  Why D wrong: Athletes are not the cited high-mortality group."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Consolidation, a feature of pneumonia, refers to what?","options":["It is compressible or gathering of the lungs tissues that have filled with liquids instead of air, and is marked by induration (swelling & hardening), a radiologic sign of pneumonia","Collapse of alveoli","Air trapping in alveoli","Bronchial mucus hypertrophy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Consolidation is when the lung tissues have filled with liquids instead of air, marked by induration (swelling & hardening), and is a radiologic sign of pneumonia.  Why B wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why C wrong: Air trapping describes emphysema/COPD.  Why D wrong: Mucus hypertrophy describes chronic bronchitis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the definitive diagnostic test for pneumonia that identifies the pathogenic causative organism?","options":["Sputum test (sputum culture)","Blood culture","Clinical manifestation only","Pulmonary function test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sputum test is a definitive diagnosis; this test is not identified pathogenic causative organism without it, i.e., sputum culture identifies the organism.  Why B wrong: Blood culture confirms diagnosis but is not the cited organism-identifying definitive test.  Why C wrong: Clinical manifestation and physical examination support, but are not definitive.  Why D wrong: PFT is not used to identify the pneumonia organism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which type of pneumonia is the most common type and varies from mild to severe?","options":["Primary atypical pneumonia","Viral pneumonia","Bacterial pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Viral pneumonia is the most common type of pneumonia, less severe type.  Why A wrong: Primary atypical pneumonia is usually caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae or chlamydiae.  Why C wrong: Bacterial pneumonia is a more severe type, not the most common cited.  Why D wrong: Aspiration pneumonia occurs when resistant flora of the upper respiratory tract or gastric contents/chemicals are aspirated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Primary atypical pneumonia is usually caused by which organism?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae or chlamydiae","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primary atypical pneumonia is usually caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae or chlamydiae.  Why A wrong: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a cause of community-acquired pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Staphylococcus aureus and MRSA are associated with hospital-acquired pneumonia.  Why D wrong: Pseudomonas is not the cited atypical pneumonia organism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Bacterial pneumonia, a more severe type, may be caused by which organisms? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Streptococcus","Staphylococcus","Commonly seen in children younger than 5 years","Mycoplasma pneumoniae only","None of the above"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Bacterial pneumonia is a more severe type; m/c cause is Streptococcus, Staphylococcus; commonly seen in children younger than 5 years.  Why D wrong: Mycoplasma pneumoniae causes primary atypical pneumonia, not bacterial pneumonia per the source.  Why E wrong: Options A, B, and C are correct, so \"none\" is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Community Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) is caused by which organisms?","options":["Staphylococcus pneumoniae, m/c is Streptococcus (pneumococcal), and Haemophilus influenzae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa only","MRSA only","Mycobacterium tuberculosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CAP is caused by Staphylococcus pneumoniae, m/c is Streptococcus (pneumococcal), and Haemophilus influenzae.  Why B wrong: Pseudomonas alone is not the cited CAP cause.  Why C wrong: MRSA is associated with hospital-acquired pneumonia.  Why D wrong: M. tuberculosis causes tuberculosis, not CAP."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Hospital Acquired Pneumonia (HAP) is commonly caused by which organisms?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus and MRSA, described after 48-72 hours after admission to hospital","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae only","Influenza virus only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HAP is commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus and MRSA, described after 48-72 hours after admission to the hospital.  Why B wrong: Mycoplasma causes atypical pneumonia, not the cited HAP organisms.  Why C wrong: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a CAP organism.  Why D wrong: Influenza alone is not the cited HAP cause."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Aspiration pneumonia occurs when which substances are aspirated?","options":["Resistant flora of the upper respiratory tract, gastric contents or chemicals","Only water","Only inhaled medications","Only pollen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspiration pneumonia occurs when resistant flora of the upper respiratory tract, gastric contents or chemicals is aspirated into the lungs.  Why B wrong: Water alone is not the cited aspirated substance.  Why C wrong: Inhaled medications are not the cited cause.  Why D wrong: Pollen is not the cited aspirated material."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Pneumonia due to immuno-compromised state most commonly causes which severe syndrome?","options":["Severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), m/c cause SARS-CoV","Community acquired pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonia","Atypical pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia due to immuno-compromised most common; severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), m/c cause is SARS-CoV.  Why B wrong: CAP is not the cited immuno-compromised severe syndrome.  Why C wrong: Aspiration pneumonia has a different mechanism.  Why D wrong: Atypical pneumonia is not the cited severe syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of pneumonia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Elevated temperature and increased WBC count","Tachypnoea & tachycardia","Malaise & fatigue, nausea, vomiting & diarrhoea","Cough with sputum production - specific colour indicating type of sputum","Dyspnoea and chest pain that increased with respiration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia signs/symptoms include elevated temperature and increased WBC count; tachypnoea & tachycardia; malaise & fatigue, nausea, vomiting & diarrhoea; chest x-ray show pulmonary infiltration (cardinal sign); cough with sputum production (specific colour indicating type of sputum); and dyspnoea and chest pain that increased with respiration.  Why none wrong: All listed options are directly cited signs and symptoms of pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A pneumonia patient's chest x-ray shows pulmonary infiltration. This is described as what?","options":["The cardinal sign of pneumonia","A sign of pulmonary embolism","Normal finding","A sign of asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chest x-ray showing pulmonary infiltration is the cardinal sign of pneumonia.  Why B wrong: It is cited for pneumonia, not embolism.  Why C wrong: Infiltration is abnormal, not normal.  Why D wrong: It is not the cited asthma finding."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Match the sputum colour to the organism in pneumonia. Which is correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pneumococcal: purulent rusty sputum","Staphylococcal: yellow, blood streaked sputum","Klebsiella species: red, gelatinous sputum","Mycoplasma: non-productive and mucoid sputum","Pseudomonas: green sputum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists: Pneumococcal - purulent rusty sputum; Staphylococcal - yellow, blood streaked sputum; Klebsiella species - red, gelatinous sputum; Mycoplasma - non-productive and mucoid sputum; Pseudomonas - green sputum.  Why none wrong: All option-to-organism matches are exactly as cited in the source."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In the nursing care of pneumonia, when must a sputum specimen be collected for culture and sensitivity (C/S)?","options":["After the first dose of antibiotic","Collect early morning sputum specimen for C/S, before pathogenic organism is identified; blood & sputum must be collected before antibiotic therapy","Any time of the day","Only after symptoms resolve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Collect early morning sputum specimen for C/S of blood & sputum to determine appropriate antibiotic on the basis of pathogenic organism, and blood & sputum must be collected before antibiotic therapy.  Why A wrong: Collecting after the first antibiotic dose alters culture results.  Why C wrong: Early morning specimen is specified, not any time.  Why D wrong: Specimens are collected before treatment, not after resolution."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Within how many hours of arrival/hospital admission should antibiotics be started for a pneumonia patient?","options":["Within 6 hours of arrival in the hospital","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 72 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antibiotics should be started within 6 hours of arrival in the hospital.  Why B wrong: 24 hours exceeds the cited window.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is too delayed.  Why D wrong: 72 hours is far beyond the cited window."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In what position should a pneumonia patient be placed in nursing care?","options":["Place in semi-Fowler's position, it will reduce intra-thoracic pressure","Trendelenburg position","Prone position","Flat supine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place in semi-Fowler's position; it will reduce intra-thoracic pressure.  Why B wrong: Trendelenburg increases intra-thoracic pressure and worsens breathing.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the cited position.  Why D wrong: Flat supine does not reduce intra-thoracic pressure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which nursing interventions are appropriate for monitoring and managing a pneumonia patient? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check all vitals and administer cool humidified oxygen if respiratory distress is there","Encourage coughing & deep breathing after chest physiotherapy","Encourage fluid intake for duly 3 litres thin or liquidy the secretions","Offer high calorie diet","Monitor for sign of respiratory distress: restlessness, laboured respiration and use of accessory muscle, cyanosis and change in mental status or LOC"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are cited pneumonia nursing interventions: check all vitals and administer cool humidified oxygen for respiratory distress; encourage coughing & deep breathing after chest physiotherapy; encourage fluid intake to thin/liquidy secretions; offer high calorie diet; chest physiotherapy and postural drainage every 4 hours; and monitor for signs of respiratory distress (restlessness, laboured respiration, accessory muscle use, cyanosis, change in mental status/LOC).  Why none wrong: All listed options are directly cited nursing care points."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How often should chest physiotherapy and postural drainage be performed for a pneumonia patient?","options":["Every 4 hours","Every 12 hours","Once a day","Every 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chest physiotherapy and postural drainage every 4 hours.  Why B wrong: 12-hourly is too infrequent per the source.  Why C wrong: Once daily is too infrequent.  Why D wrong: Every 30 minutes is excessive and not cited."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A pneumonia patient is on affected-side oxygen positioning. The source advises to encourage the client to lie on which side?","options":["Encourage to lie on the unaffected side, since the affected side of pneumonia is unaltered to split the chest and reduce the discomfort caused by pleural rubbing","Always lie on the affected side","Lie prone only","Avoid lying down entirely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage the client to lie on the unaffected side, since the affected side is unaltered to split the chest and reduce the discomfort caused by pleural rubbing.  Why B wrong: Lying on the affected side increases discomfort from pleural rubbing.  Why C wrong: Prone-only is not the cited advice.  Why D wrong: The patient is positioned, not prevented from lying down."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Pulmonary tuberculosis is what kind of infectious disease, and how does it spread?","options":["A chronic bacterial infectious lung disease that is bronchotor of social welfare diseases of the poor man and ordinary plaque (mass)","A fungal lung disease spread by water","A viral disease spread by blood transfusion","A protozoal disease spread by mosquitoes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tuberculosis is a chronic bacterial infectious lung disease; it is also known as barometer of social welfare diseases, disease of the poor man and ordinary plaque (mass).  Why B wrong: TB is bacterial, not a water-borne fungal disease.  Why C wrong: TB is not a blood-transfusion-spread viral disease.  Why D wrong: TB is not a mosquito-borne protozoal disease."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Pulmonary tuberculosis is caused by which causative organism?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis, an acid fast bacilli","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","SARS-CoV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative organism is Mycobacterium tuberculosis, an acid fast bacilli.  Why B wrong: Streptococcus pneumoniae causes pneumonia, not TB.  Why C wrong: Mycoplasma causes atypical pneumonia.  Why D wrong: SARS-CoV causes SARS, not TB."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Tuberculosis primarily affects which organ, and where in that organ are the lungs most commonly involved?","options":["It primarily affects the lungs, usually lower lobe because there is oxygen content","It primarily affects the lungs, usually upper lobe because there is oxygen content, areas are always higher, and cause pulmonary tuberculosis","It affects only the kidneys","It affects only the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tuberculosis primarily affects the lungs, usually upper lobe because there is oxygen content; areas are always higher, causing pulmonary tuberculosis.  Why A wrong: TB favors the upper lobe (higher oxygen), not the lower lobe.  Why C wrong: While TB can affect other organs (intestine, meninges, bones, joints), it primarily affects the lungs.  Why D wrong: TB primarily affects the lungs, not only the brain."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Besides the lungs, tuberculosis can also affect which other organs? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Intestine","Meninges","Bones and joints","The disease can affect animals like cattle (Bovine tuberculosis)","None of these"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TB can also affect the intestine, meninges, bones and joints; the disease also affect animals like cattle, this is known as bovine tuberculosis.  Why E wrong: Options A through D are all cited, so \"none\" is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the source of infection in bovine tuberculosis?","options":["Infected human and bovine (through milk or milk product)","Mosquito bites","Contaminated water only","Soil exposure only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source of infection is infected human and bovine (through milk or milk product).  Why B wrong: TB is not mosquito-borne.  Why C wrong: Water alone is not the cited source.  Why D wrong: Soil exposure is not the cited TB source."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of tuberculosis?","options":["Droplet or airborne transmission","Faecal-oral route","Sexual contact","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mode of transmission is droplet or airborne transmission.  Why B wrong: TB is not primarily faecal-oral (though intestinal TB exists, lung TB spreads by droplets).  Why C wrong: TB is not sexually transmitted.  Why D wrong: TB is not transmitted by blood transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pulmonary tuberculosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Low grade fever, chronic cough and weight loss, night sweats","Persistently cough with mucoid & mucopurulent cough that progressively become worst such as haemoptysis","Malaise, pleuritic chest pain","Lethargy, anorexia","Sudden high fever with green sputum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TB signs/symptoms include low grade fever, chronic cough and weight loss, night sweats; persistently cough with mucoid & mucopurulent cough that progressively become worst such as haemoptysis; malaise, pleuritic chest pain; lethargy, anorexia.  Why E wrong: TB classically presents with low-grade fever and not sudden high fever with green sputum (green sputum is associated with Pseudomonas pneumonia)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A chest X-ray in tuberculosis may reveal what finding?","options":["Active or calcified lesions or pleural effusion","Pink frothy fluid in alveoli","Complete clear lung fields","A wedge-shaped infarct only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chest X-ray may reveal active or calcified lesions or pleural effusion in tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: Pink frothy fluid suggests pulmonary oedema, not TB.  Why C wrong: Clear lung fields would not show TB lesions.  Why D wrong: A wedge-shaped infarct suggests pulmonary embolism/infarction."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which confirmatory tests are used for tuberculosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Analysis of sputum and gastric content for confirmation","Tuberculin skin test or mantoux test","QuantiFERON TB Gold test","Should Remember: most common sites of intestinal tuberculosis is ileocaecal region or terminal ileum","Blood culture only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmatory TB diagnostics include analysis of sputum and gastric content; tuberculin (Mantoux) skin test; and QuantiFERON TB Gold test. The source notes the most common site of intestinal tuberculosis is the ileocaecal region or terminal ileum.  Why E wrong: Blood culture is not the cited confirmatory test for TB."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which is the most common site of intestinal tuberculosis?","options":["Stomach","Ileocaecal region or terminal ileum","Sigmoid colon","Duodenum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of intestinal tuberculosis is the ileocaecal region or terminal ileum.  Why A wrong: The stomach is not the cited most common site.  Why C wrong: The sigmoid colon is not the cited site.  Why D wrong: The duodenum is not the cited site."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In the pathophysiology of tuberculosis, what happens after bacteria enter into the lungs?","options":["Bacteria are immediately destroyed with no further reaction","They enter into alveoli, then attack on bacteria by neutrophils or macrophages, then macrophages or neutrophils kill bacteria as well as healthy alveoli, then bacilli & gaseous cavities inside the lungs upper lobe known as ghon tubercle","Bacteria travel directly to the brain","The bacteria cause immediate pulmonary oedema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In TB pathophysiology, bacteria enter into the lungs, then enter into alveoli, then attack on bacteria by neutrophils or macrophages; macrophages or neutrophils kill bacteria as well as healthy alveoli; then made up fibrous & gaseous cavities inside the lungs upper lobe, known as ghon tubercle.  Why A wrong: The bacteria are not simply destroyed without granuloma/tubercle formation.  Why C wrong: Direct travel to the brain is not the cited pulmonary pathophysiology.  Why D wrong: TB does not immediately cause pulmonary oedema."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the lung lesion known as in tuberculosis that forms inside the upper lobe with bacilli and gaseous cavities?","options":["Ghon tubercle","Consolidation","Bullae","Atelectasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The fibrous & gaseous cavities inside the lungs upper lobe is known as ghon tubercle.  Why B wrong: Consolidation is the radiologic sign of pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Bullae are associated with emphysema.  Why D wrong: Atelectasis is alveolar collapse, not a tubercle."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which statements distinguish Primary Tuberculosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is mostly occurred in children","It is occurred in the body when it is infected for the first time","It is bacteria in adult who are infected first time","The primary lesion remains active or calcified","It causes ghon tubercle as a reactivation of previous infection"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Primary tuberculosis is mostly occurred in children and is occurred in the body when it is infected for the first time.  Why C wrong: The source attributes primary TB to children, not adults who are infected first time (option C distorts this).  Why D wrong: This describes the chronic/calcified lesion findings, not the defining feature of primary TB given.  Why E wrong: Reactivation of previous infection describes secondary (post-primary) tuberculosis, not primary."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which statements describe Secondary Tuberculosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It commonly occurs in adults as a reactivation of previous infection (re-infection)","Particularly when health status declines","Pneumonic information is much more florid and widespread","The upper lung's lobes are mostly affected, and cavitation or Ghon complex can occur","It occurs only in children with first-time infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary tuberculosis commonly occurs in adults as a reactivation of previous infection (re-infection), particularly when health status declines; pneumonic information is much more florid and widespread; the upper lung's lobes are mostly affected, and cavitation or Ghon complex can occur.  Why E wrong: First-time infection in children describes primary, not secondary, tuberculosis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"COPD is also known as what?","options":["Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease or Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: COPD is also known as Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease or Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease.  Why B wrong: ARDS is a different acute condition.  Why C wrong: PE is a vascular obstruction, not COPD.  Why D wrong: Pneumonia is an infection, not COPD."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"COPD leads to which complications, and what causes the airflow obstruction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["COPD lead to pulmonary insufficiency, pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale","It is characterised by airflow obstruction caused by emphysema and chronic bronchitis","Cigarette smoking is the major risk factor for developing COPD","Pulmonary function test (PFT) is considered as confirmative test for COPD","COPD is caused by a single gene mutation only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: COPD leads to pulmonary insufficiency, pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale; it is characterised by airflow obstruction caused by emphysema and chronic bronchitis; cigarette smoking is the major risk factor for developing COPD; and pulmonary function test (PFT) is considered as confirmative test for COPD.  Why E wrong: COPD is not described as caused by a single gene mutation only; smoking is the cited major risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is the major risk factor for developing COPD?","options":["Cigarette smoking","High altitude","Cold weather","High protein diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cigarette smoking is the major risk factor for developing COPD.  Why B wrong: High altitude is not the cited major risk factor.  Why C wrong: Cold weather is not the cited risk factor.  Why D wrong: A high protein diet is unrelated to COPD risk."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which test is considered the confirmative test for COPD?","options":["Chest X-ray","Pulmonary function test (PFT)","Sputum culture","Blood culture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary function test (PFT) is considered as the confirmative test for COPD.  Why A wrong: Chest X-ray supports diagnosis but is not the cited confirmative test.  Why C wrong: Sputum culture identifies infections, not COPD obstruction.  Why D wrong: Blood culture is not the cited COPD confirmatory test."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How is emphysema defined?","options":["A lung condition which is destruction & inelasticity or destroyed of the lung alveoli, resulting in impaired diffusion of the gases through the alveolar capillary membrane that trap air and making exhalation difficulty","Inflammation of the bronchial walls with hypertrophy of mucus goblet cells","Accumulation of fluid in the alveoli","A bacterial lung infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Emphysema is a lung condition with destruction & inelasticity or destruction of the lung alveoli, resulting in impaired diffusion of gases through the alveolar capillary membrane that trap air, making exhalation difficult.  Why B wrong: Bronchial wall inflammation with mucus goblet cell hypertrophy describes chronic bronchitis.  Why C wrong: Fluid accumulation describes pulmonary oedema.  Why D wrong: Emphysema is a structural destruction, not a bacterial infection."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Emphysema is described as which kind of condition with regard to reversibility?","options":["An irreversible condition in which the surface area of the lung air sacs is damaged and enlarged, causing breathlessness and obstruction of airway","A fully reversible condition with rest","An acute self-limiting condition","A condition that resolves with antibiotics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Emphysema is an irreversible condition in which the surface area of the lung air sacs is damaged and enlarged, causing breathlessness and obstruction of airway.  Why B wrong: It is irreversible, not fully reversible.  Why C wrong: It is chronic and irreversible, not acute and self-limiting.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics do not reverse the structural damage."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"What is \"barrel shape chest,\" a major clinical feature of emphysema?","options":["A pectus excavatum deformity","A barrel-shaped chest is a major clinical feature of emphysema (increased anteroposterior diameter from air trapping)","A unilateral chest deformity","A normal chest finding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barrel shape chest is a major clinical feature of emphysema.  Why A wrong: Pectus excavatum is a different deformity, not the cited emphysema feature.  Why C wrong: It is a bilateral feature, not a unilateral deformity.  Why D wrong: A barrel chest is abnormal, characteristic of emphysema."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"How is chronic bronchitis defined?","options":["Destruction of alveoli with air trapping","Inflammation of the bronchial walls with hypertrophy of mucus goblet cells, and is characterised by chronic cough","Fluid accumulation in the lungs","A thrombus in the pulmonary artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic bronchitis is inflammation of the bronchial walls with hypertrophy of mucus goblet cells, characterised by chronic cough.  Why A wrong: Alveolar destruction with air trapping describes emphysema.  Why C wrong: Fluid accumulation describes pulmonary oedema.  Why D wrong: A pulmonary artery thrombus is embolism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with chronic bronchitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Orthopnoea & tachypnoea","Expiratory wheezing, sonorous breathing & cough","Increased residual volume (RV) & decreased FRC & vital capacity","Barrel or bottle chest (cardinal sign of emphysema)","Use of accessory muscles & pursed lip breathing"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic bronchitis signs/symptoms include orthopnoea & tachypnoea; expiratory wheezing, sonorous breathing & cough; increased residual volume (RV) & decreased FRC & vital capacity; and use of accessory muscles & pursed lip breathing.  Why D wrong: Barrel or bottle chest is the cardinal sign of emphysema, not the cited chronic bronchitis sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which additional signs and symptoms occur in chronic bronchitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increased PaCO₂ & decreased PaO₂ level","Clubbing of fingers, polycythemia due to prolonged low level of PaO₂ as compensatory","Congestion & hyperinflation seen on chest x-ray","ABG level that indicate respiratory acidosis & hypoxemia","Anxiety, restlessness, sign of respiratory distress, cyanosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are cited chronic bronchitis features: increased PaCO₂ & decreased PaO₂; clubbing of fingers and polycythemia due to prolonged low PaO₂ (compensatory); congestion & hyperinflation on chest x-ray; ABG showing respiratory acidosis & hypoxemia; and anxiety, restlessness, sign of respiratory distress, and cyanosis.  Why none wrong: All listed options are directly cited signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"In the nursing care of chronic bronchitis/COPD, oxygen should be administered at what flow rate, and through what device?","options":["Instruct to low level of oxygen, usually 1-2 L/m by nasal cannula/catheter or Venturi mask (low level of oxygen)","High-flow oxygen at 10-15 L/min by non-rebreather mask","100% oxygen by face mask","No oxygen should be given"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In COPD, instruct/administer a low level of oxygen, usually 1-2 L/m by nasal cannula/catheter or Venturi mask (low level of oxygen).  Why B wrong: High-flow 10-15 L/min may abolish the hypoxic respiratory drive in COPD patients.  Why C wrong: 100% oxygen can suppress the respiratory drive and cause hypercapnia.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is needed but at a controlled low flow, not withheld entirely."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Why must high-flow oxygen be avoided in a COPD patient, and what is the adverse effect known as?","options":["The adverse effects of administration of high flow oxygen are the inability of oxygen saturated its molecules to transport CO₂, so that this condition leading to increased hypercapnia, this overall condition known as Haldane effects","It causes immediate pulmonary embolism","It causes pneumonia","It causes pleural effusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The adverse effect of high flow oxygen in COPD is the inability of oxygen-saturated molecules to transport CO₂, leading to increased hypercapnia, known as the Haldane effect.  Why B wrong: High-flow oxygen does not directly cause pulmonary embolism.  Why C wrong: It is not the cited mechanism (pneumonia is an infection).  Why D wrong: Pleural effusion is not the cited adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a COPD patient? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor all vitals, oxygen saturation, breaths sounds and arterial blood gases","Instruct to low level of oxygen, usually 1-2 L/m by nasal cannula/catheter or Venturi mask","Avoid all oxygen administration entirely","Monitor for signs of respiratory distress","Position to facilitate breathing"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: COPD nursing care includes monitoring all vitals, oxygen saturation, breaths sounds and ABG; instructing low-level oxygen (1-2 L/m by nasal cannula/catheter or Venturi mask); monitoring for signs of respiratory distress; and positioning to facilitate breathing.  Why C wrong: Oxygen is not avoided entirely; it is given at a controlled low flow."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A client on mechanical ventilation requires high concentrations of oxygen and mechanical ventilation. Which monitoring and supportive measures are appropriate per the pulmonary infarction nursing care? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor all vital signs, auscultate breath sounds","Require oxygen, ECG and intake & output monitoring","Treat as pulmonary infarction with appropriate support","Withhold all monitoring","Discharge immediately without follow-up"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: For pulmonary infarction, oxygen is required in high concentration and may require mechanical ventilation; monitor all vital signs, auscultate breath sounds; require oxygen, ECG and intake & output monitoring.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is essential, not withheld.  Why E wrong: Immediate discharge without follow-up is unsafe and not cited."},{"section":"Batch 014 · mechanical ventilation…","question":"A nurse monitoring a ventilated client should monitor for which of the following respiratory and circulatory parameters per ventilator nursing care? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintain ventilator settings and assess breath sounds, oxygen saturation","Always pulse oximetry on oxygen saturation should be assessed first when patient on ventilator or oxygen therapy","Maintain sealed system between ventilator and client","Monitor ABG results","Disable alarms to avoid noise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Ventilator nursing care includes maintaining ventilator settings and assessing breath sounds and oxygen saturation; pulse oximetry assessed first when patient is on ventilator/oxygen therapy; maintaining a sealed system between ventilator and client; and monitoring ABG results.  Why E wrong: Alarms must never be disabled; the source states never set alarm controls to off."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A nurse positions a client with an acute asthma attack to facilitate breathing. Which position is MOST appropriate?","options":["Supine flat","Left lateral","High Fowler's or sitting position","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: During an acute asthma attack the client should be placed in high Fowler's or a sitting position to facilitate breathing and lung expansion.  Why A wrong: Supine flat reduces diaphragmatic excursion and worsens dyspnea.  Why B wrong: Left lateral does not optimize chest expansion in respiratory distress.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg increases venous return to the chest and impairs breathing."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Asthma is BEST described as which type of disease process?","options":["Irreversible obstructive disease like COPD","A reversible inflammatory and IgE-mediated (allergic) disease of the lungs","A purely restrictive lung disease","A bacterial infectious disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asthma is a reversible inflammatory and IgE-mediated (allergic) disease of the lungs, distinguishing it from COPD.  Why A wrong: COPD is irreversible; asthma is reversible — that is why it is no longer included under the COPD group.  Why C wrong: Asthma is primarily an obstructive (bronchospasm) process, not purely restrictive.  Why D wrong: Asthma is allergic/inflammatory, not a bacterial infection."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In asthma, which pathophysiological changes occur in the airways?","options":["Reversible bronchospasm, mucosal oedema, and increased secretion into the airway","Permanent alveolar destruction only","Pleural fluid accumulation","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asthma involves reversible bronchospasm, mucosal oedema, and increased secretion into the airway.  Why B wrong: Permanent alveolar destruction describes emphysema, not asthma.  Why C wrong: Pleural fluid accumulation describes pleural effusion.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary embolism is a vascular occlusion, unrelated to asthma's airway changes."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Asthma is now no longer included in the COPD group of diseases primarily because:","options":["It only affects children","It is reversible, whereas COPD is an obstructive airway disease (irreversible)","It is caused by bacteria","It has no airflow obstruction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asthma is reversible, which separates it from COPD, an irreversible obstructive airway disease.  Why A wrong: Asthma affects all age groups, not only children.  Why C wrong: Asthma is allergic/inflammatory, not bacterial.  Why D wrong: Asthma does cause reversible airflow obstruction from bronchospasm."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client with asthma reports the disease is triggered by hyper-responsiveness to stimuli. Which of the following are recognized asthma triggers? (SATA)","options":["Allergens such as pollen, dust, feathers, smoking","Fumes","NSAIDs (ibuprofen and aspirin)","Emotions, including stress, or laughter","Adequate hydration with plain water"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes/triggers of asthma include allergens (pollen, dust, feathers, smoking), fumes, medicines like NSAIDs (ibuprofen and aspirin), and emotions including stress or laughter.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed as an asthma trigger.  Why E wrong: Adequate hydration with plain water is not a trigger; fluids are encouraged in asthma management."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Status asthmaticus is BEST defined as:","options":["A mild self-limiting wheeze","A life-threatening exacerbation of asthma that may result in pneumothorax, pulmonary, and cardiac arrest","A type of pneumonia","A chronic stable asthma state"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Status asthmaticus is a life-threatening exacerbation of asthma that may result in pneumothorax, pulmonary, and cardiac arrest.  Why A wrong: It is severe and life-threatening, not mild or self-limiting.  Why C wrong: It is an asthma exacerbation, not pneumonia.  Why D wrong: It is an acute emergency, not a stable state."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client in status asthmaticus has had bronchospasm for over 30 minutes that is occurring mostly during which time?","options":["Daytime","After meals","Nighttime","During exercise only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In status asthmaticus the exacerbation usually lasts more than 30 minutes and it is occurring mostly during nighttime.  Why A wrong: The source specifies nighttime, not daytime.  Why B/D wrong: Postprandial and exercise-only timing are not the stated pattern for status asthmaticus."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In a client with status asthmaticus, the most common cause of drug-induced asthma is:","options":["Beta-blockers","Aspirin","ACE inhibitors","Diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of drug-induced asthma is aspirin.  Why A wrong: Beta-blockers can worsen asthma but aspirin is stated as the most common drug cause.  Why C wrong: ACE inhibitors cause cough, not the most common asthma drug trigger here.  Why D wrong: Diuretics are not a stated cause of drug-induced asthma."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client presents with restlessness, diaphoresis, cyanosis, wheezing, absent or diminished breath sounds, hyper-resonance, tachypnea, and use of accessory muscles for breathing. These signs are MOST consistent with:","options":["Pleural effusion","Acute severe asthma / status asthmaticus","Sarcoidosis","Histoplasmosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are the listed signs and symptoms of acute severe/status asthmatic presentation.  Why A wrong: Pleural effusion produces decreased breath sounds and dullness without classic wheeze/hyper-resonance pattern described.  Why C wrong: Sarcoidosis presents with night sweats, fever, weight loss, dyspnea.  Why D wrong: Histoplasmosis is a fungal infection with a different clinical picture."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A nurse assesses a client with status asthmaticus and finds prolonged exhalation and tachycardia. Which additional finding indicates DECREASED oxygenation?","options":["Increased blood pressure","Decreased oxygen saturation","Bradycardia","Polycythemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased oxygen saturation is the listed sign of decreased oxygenation in status asthmaticus.  Why A wrong: Increased BP is not the marker of decreased oxygenation here.  Why C wrong: Tachycardia (not bradycardia) is associated.  Why D wrong: Polycythemia is a chronic compensatory change, not the acute decreased-oxygenation sign listed."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client with status asthmaticus develops abnormally decreased blood pressure during inspiration of more than 10 mm Hg. This finding is termed:","options":["Pulsus alternans","Pulsus paradoxus (paradoxical pulse)","Korotkoff gap","Water-hammer pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An abnormally decreased BP during inspiration, when systolic falls more than 10 mm Hg, is called pulsus paradoxus (paradoxical pulse).  Why A wrong: Pulsus alternans is alternating strong/weak beats, seen in LV failure.  Why C wrong: Korotkoff gap (auscultatory gap) is unrelated to inspiration.  Why D wrong: Water-hammer pulse is a bounding collapsing pulse of aortic regurgitation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"For a client with status asthmaticus, the most appropriate corticosteroid and bronchodilator administration route described is:","options":["Oral tablets only","IV corticosteroids and bronchodilators instead of inhalers, or as per order","Topical cream","Subcutaneous insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer IV corticosteroids and bronchodilators instead of inhalers (or as per order) in status asthmaticus.  Why A wrong: Oral tablets are too slow in this emergency.  Why C/D wrong: Topical cream and subcutaneous insulin are irrelevant to acute asthma."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"When educating a client about long-term asthma prevention, the nurse teaches the client to:","options":["Identify risk factors and triggers that have to be prevented to prevent possible episodes","Stop all medications permanently","Avoid all fluids","Sleep flat without a pillow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A key nursing/education point is teaching the client about risk factors and triggers that must be prevented to prevent possible episodes.  Why B wrong: Asthma medications should be continued and taken correctly, not stopped.  Why C wrong: Fluid intake is encouraged in asthma, not avoided.  Why D wrong: An upright position aids breathing; flat positioning is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The nurse teaches an asthma client about the most common and effective route for medication management. Which device is emphasized?","options":["Nebulizer only","Metered Dose Inhaler (MDI)","Intravenous pump","Rectal suppository"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs teaching the client about how to use a Metered Dose Inhaler (MDI), the most common and effective route for medication management of asthma.  Why A wrong: Nebulizers are used but the MDI is cited as the most common, effective route.  Why C/D wrong: IV and rectal routes are not the routine asthma medication route taught."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which respiratory therapy measures are appropriate for asthma management? (SATA)","options":["Nebulization therapy","Postural drainage","Exercises (diaphragmatic breathing) to promote maximal CO2 elimination from the lungs","Encourage fluid intake up to 3000 ml/day","Withhold all fluids to dry secretions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Respiratory therapy includes nebulization, postural drainage, and diaphragmatic breathing exercises to promote maximal CO2 elimination; the diet section encourages fluid intake up to 3000 ml/day.  Why A/B/C correct: All are listed respiratory therapy measures.  Why D correct: Encouraging fluid up to 3000 ml/day is explicitly recommended.  Why E wrong: Fluids are encouraged (to liquefy/loosen secretions), not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client with asthma should be advised to take a diet that is:","options":["High protein, high calorie, and soft","Low calorie and high fiber","Clear liquids only","High sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends offering a high-protein, high-calorie, and soft diet for asthma clients.  Why B wrong: Low calorie is not recommended; high calorie is.  Why C wrong: Clear liquids only is inadequate nutrition.  Why D wrong: High sodium is not part of the recommended diet."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which nursing interventions are appropriate during an acute asthma attack/status asthmaticus? (SATA)","options":["Administer oxygen as prescribed or low flow oxygen 2-3 L/min","Stay with the client to decrease anxiety","Administer bronchodilators by nebulizer, MDI, IV as per order","Record colour, amount, and consistency of sputum","Withhold corticosteroids and avoid auscultating breath sounds"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed acute-attack interventions include placing client in high Fowler's, administering oxygen (low flow 2-3 L/min), staying with the client to decrease anxiety, administering bronchodilators by nebulizer/MDI/IV, recording sputum colour/amount/consistency, administering corticosteroids/steroids as per order, auscultating breath sounds before and after treatment, and initiating general nursing care.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed interventions.  Why E wrong: Corticosteroids are administered as per order and breath sounds ARE auscultated before and after treatment — the opposite of this option."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Asthma episodes are most probably associated with which factors?","options":["Family history and environmental factors","Bacterial infection only","Genetic mutation alone unrelated to environment","Dietary cholesterol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asthma is most probably due to a family history of asthma and environmental factors.  Why B wrong: Asthma is not primarily bacterial.  Why C wrong: Both genetic (family history) AND environmental factors contribute, not genetics alone.  Why D wrong: Dietary cholesterol is not a stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The most common cause of rib fracture is:","options":["Osteoporosis","Direct chest trauma","Coughing","Bone tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of rib fracture is direct chest trauma, resulting in injury such as pneumothorax and pulmonary contusion.  Why A wrong: Osteoporosis predisposes but direct trauma is the most common cause.  Why C/D wrong: Coughing and bone tumors are uncommon causes compared with direct trauma."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A client with a rib fracture experiences pain that may increase during inspiration. This is recognized as the:","options":["Cardinal sign","Cushing's sign","Murphy's sign","Homans' sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pain at the injury site that may increase during inspiration is the cardinal sign of rib fracture.  Why B wrong: Cushing's sign refers to raised intracranial pressure triad.  Why C wrong: Murphy's sign relates to cholecystitis.  Why D wrong: Homans' sign relates to DVT."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with a rib fracture? (SATA)","options":["Tenderness at the site","Shallow respiration","Chest splint","Crepitus in chest x-ray","Fracture noted in chest x-ray"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Rib fracture signs include pain increasing with inspiration (cardinal sign), tenderness at the site, shallow respiration, chest splint, and a fracture noted on chest x-ray.  Why A/B/C/E correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: \"Crepitus in chest x-ray\" is not a listed finding; x-ray reveals the fracture, not crepitus."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with a rib fracture knows that rib fractures usually:","options":["Require immediate surgery","Resolve spontaneously","Cause permanent paralysis","Need lifelong ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing note states \"always remember that ribs usually resolve spontaneously.\"  Why A wrong: Most rib fractures heal without surgery.  Why C/D wrong: Permanent paralysis and lifelong ventilation are not expected outcomes."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with a rib fracture? (SATA)","options":["Place the client in Fowler's position to reduce intra-thoracic pressure","Administer pain medication as per doctor order","Monitor for respiratory distress","Instruct the client for self-splint with the hands and arms","Encourage rapid, deep, forceful coughing without splinting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rib fracture nursing care includes Fowler's position to reduce intra-thoracic pressure, pain medication as per order, monitoring for respiratory distress, and instructing the client to self-splint with the hands and arms.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: The client is taught to self-splint, not to cough forcefully without support."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Flail chest is BEST described as:","options":["A paradoxical respiration in which inward movement of a segment of the thorax occurs during inspiration with outward movement during expiration","Accumulation of fluid in the pleural space","Collapse of the alveoli only","A fungal lung infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flail chest is a paradoxical respiration in which inward movement of a segment of the thorax occurs during inspiration with outward movement during expiration.  Why B wrong: Fluid in the pleural space describes pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse describes atelectasis.  Why D wrong: A fungal lung infection describes histoplasmosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Flail chest is commonly caused by chest trauma associated with:","options":["Accidents or steering wheel of car","Asthma triggers","Allergic rhinitis","Pollen exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flail chest is caused by blunt chest trauma associated with accidents or the steering wheel of a car.  Why B/C/D wrong: Asthma triggers, allergic rhinitis, and pollen exposure are allergy-related, not the trauma mechanism of flail chest."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Flail chest typically occurs when there is instability of the chest, usually when:","options":["3-4 ribs are fractured in at least 2 places","Only 1 rib is fractured once","The sternum is intact","The diaphragm ruptures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flail chest occurs when there is instability of the chest, usually when 3-4 ribs are fractured in at least 2 places.  Why B wrong: A single fracture does not produce a free-floating flail segment.  Why C wrong: Sternal integrity is not the defining criterion.  Why D wrong: Diaphragmatic rupture is a separate injury."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are seen in flail chest? (SATA)","options":["Paradoxical respiration (cardinal sign)","Severe chest pain","Dyspnea, tachypnea, and cyanosis","Hypotension due to pneumothorax","Increased breath sounds bilaterally with no respiratory change"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Flail chest signs include paradoxical respiration (cardinal sign), severe chest pain, dyspnea/tachypnea/cyanosis, tachycardia and shallow respirations, hypotension due to pneumothorax, and diminished breath sounds.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Breath sounds are diminished, not increased, and respiration is markedly abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which assessment finding is expected in flail chest in addition to paradoxical respiration?","options":["Diminished breath sounds with tachycardia and shallow respirations","Bradycardia with hyper-resonance","Increased breath sounds with hypertension","Normal respiratory effort"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flail chest produces tachycardia, shallow respirations, and diminished breath sounds along with paradoxical respiration.  Why B wrong: Tachycardia (not bradycardia) is expected.  Why C wrong: Breath sounds are diminished and hypotension (not hypertension) may occur.  Why D wrong: Respiratory effort is abnormal in flail chest."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Pulmonary contusion is BEST defined as:","options":["An interstitial haemorrhage with intra-alveolar haemorrhage resulting in decreased pulmonary function","Pleural fluid collection","Air in the pleural space","Collapse of a whole lung from negative pressure loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary contusion is an interstitial haemorrhage with intra-alveolar haemorrhage resulting in decreased pulmonary function.  Why B wrong: Pleural fluid collection is pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Air in the pleural space is pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Lung collapse from negative pressure loss describes pneumothorax mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The major complication of pulmonary contusion is:","options":["Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","Diabetes mellitus","Renal failure","Peptic ulcer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The major complication of pulmonary contusion is Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the lung-injury pathway of pulmonary contusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pulmonary contusion? (SATA)","options":["Dyspnea and hypoxia","Increased bronchial secretion","Haemoptysis and restlessness","Decreased breath sounds and crackles","Bradypnea with clear lungs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary contusion signs include dyspnea/hypoxia, increased bronchial secretion, haemoptysis/restlessness, and decreased breath sounds with crackles.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Bradypnea with clear lungs contradicts the dyspneic, crackle-positive presentation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A nurse plans care for a client with pulmonary contusion. The PRIORITY positioning is:","options":["Semi-Fowler's position (first priority) to reduce intra-thoracic pressure","Supine flat","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For pulmonary contusion, the first-priority position is semi-Fowler's to reduce intra-thoracic pressure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Flat, prone, and Trendelenburg positions do not relieve intra-thoracic pressure or aid breathing in this injury."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which nursing care measures are indicated for a client with pulmonary contusion? (SATA)","options":["Administer humidified oxygen as per order","Monitor for respiratory distress","Encourage the client for coughing and deep breathing","Prepare for intubation and mechanical ventilation if condition deteriorated","Restrict all oxygen and fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary contusion nursing care includes semi-Fowler's positioning, humidified oxygen as per order, monitoring for respiratory distress, encouraging coughing and deep breathing, preparing for intubation/mechanical ventilation if condition deteriorates, and giving pain medication as per order.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Oxygen is administered, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Pneumothorax is defined as:","options":["Accumulation of atmospheric air into the pleural space, which results in raise in intra-thoracic pressure and reduced vital capacity","Accumulation of pus in the pleural space","Accumulation of fluid in the pleural space","Collection of pleural lymph"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumothorax is the accumulation of atmospheric air into the pleural space, raising intra-thoracic pressure and reducing vital capacity.  Why B wrong: Pus in the pleural space is empyema.  Why C wrong: Fluid in the pleural space is pleural effusion.  Why D wrong: Lymph collection is not the definition of pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Collapse of a lung in pneumothorax results from:","options":["Disruption of negative pressure within the intra-pleural space caused by atmospheric air in the intra-pleural cavity","Increased surfactant production","Bronchial mucus plugging only","Diaphragm contraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Collapse of a lung results from disruption of negative pressure within the intra-pleural space, caused by atmospheric air in the intra-pleural cavity; loss of negative intra-pleural pressure results in collapse of the lung.  Why B wrong: Surfactant prevents collapse; its loss (not increase) causes alveolar collapse.  Why C wrong: Mucus plugging causes atelectasis, not pneumothorax collapse.  Why D wrong: Diaphragm contraction is normal inspiration, not the cause of collapse."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In pneumothorax, gaseous exchange is reduced, leading to:","options":["Hypoxia and retention of carbon dioxide or hypercapnia","Hypocapnia with high oxygen","Metabolic alkalosis only","Increased vital capacity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surface area for gaseous exchanges is reduced in pneumothorax, leading to hypoxia and retention of carbon dioxide or hypercapnia.  Why B wrong: CO2 is retained (hypercapnia), not reduced.  Why C wrong: The primary disturbance described is hypoxia/hypercapnia, not isolated metabolic alkalosis.  Why D wrong: Vital capacity is reduced, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Closed pneumothorax is also called:","options":["Atraumatic pneumothorax","Open pneumothorax","Tension pneumothorax","Hemothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Closed pneumothorax is also called atraumatic pneumothorax; air enters from the lungs into the intra-pleural space, resulting in lung collapse.  Why B wrong: Open pneumothorax involves penetration by a fracture/wound from outside.  Why C wrong: Tension pneumothorax is a build-up of pressure as air accumulates.  Why D wrong: Hemothorax is blood, not air, in the pleural space."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Open pneumothorax occurs when:","options":["Air enters through the chest wall by penetration of a fracture/wound that allows entrance of positive atmospheric pressure into the pleural space","Fluid accumulates spontaneously","The bronchus is blocked by mucus","The diaphragm herniates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Open pneumothorax is when air enters through the chest wall by penetration of a fracture/wound allowing entrance of positive atmospheric pressure into the pleural space.  Why B wrong: Fluid accumulation is pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Bronchial mucus blockage is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Diaphragmatic herniation is a different pathology."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hemothorax is BEST defined as:","options":["Collection of blood within the pleural cavity, usually caused by traumatic pneumothorax","Collection of air in the pleural space","Collection of pus in the pleural space","Collection of serous fluid in the pleural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemothorax is a collection of blood within the pleural cavity, usually caused by traumatic pneumothorax.  Why B wrong: Air collection is pneumothorax.  Why C wrong: Pus collection is empyema.  Why D wrong: Serous fluid collection is pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Tension pneumothorax develops when:","options":["There is build-up of pressure as air accumulates within the pleural space due to increasing pressure in the lungs","Blood pools in the pericardium","Surfactant production increases","The pleura become inflamed only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tension pneumothorax is a build-up of pressure as air accumulates within the pleural space, due to increasing pressure causing the lungs to collapse.  Why B wrong: Blood in the pericardium is cardiac tamponade.  Why C wrong: Surfactant changes do not cause tension pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Pleural inflammation alone is pleurisy."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In tension pneumothorax, increasing pressure displaces the trachea, oesophagus, heart, and great vessels toward the unaffected side. The end effect on circulation is:","options":["Increased thoracic pressure causing reduced venous return and decreased cardiac output","Increased cardiac output","No hemodynamic effect","Increased venous return"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Increasing pressure causes mediastinal shift, then increased thoracic pressure causing reduced venous return and decreased cardiac output.  Why B/D wrong: Venous return and cardiac output are reduced, not increased.  Why C wrong: There is a significant hemodynamic compromise."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with tension pneumothorax? (SATA)","options":["Absent breath sounds on the affected side","Decreased chest expansion unilaterally","Tachycardia and tachypnea, hypotension","Tracheal deviation to the unaffected side","Slow respiratory rate with normal blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Tension pneumothorax signs include absent breath sounds on the affected side, decreased chest expansion unilaterally, tachycardia/tachypnea/hypotension, rapid shallow respiration, subcutaneous emphysema (crepitus on palpation), and tracheal deviation to the unaffected side.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Respiration is rapid and shallow with hypotension, not slow with normal BP."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"On palpation of a client with tension pneumothorax, the nurse notes crepitus due to:","options":["Subcutaneous emphysema","Bone fracture only","Pleural friction","Joint inflammation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subcutaneous emphysema is evidenced by crepitus on palpation in tension pneumothorax.  Why B wrong: Crepitus here arises from air in subcutaneous tissue, not the fracture itself.  Why C wrong: Pleural friction rub is auscultated, not felt as crepitus on palpation.  Why D wrong: Joint inflammation is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Diagnostic confirmation of pneumothorax includes which findings? (SATA)","options":["Increased PaCO2 and decreased PaO2","Confirmation of pneumothorax via chest x-ray","Sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain that increased with exertion","Dyspnea, anxiety, and drowsiness","Decreased PaCO2 with increased PaO2"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Tension pneumothorax features increased PaCO2 and decreased PaO2, chest x-ray confirmation of pneumothorax, sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain increased with exertion, and dyspnea/anxiety/drowsiness.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: PaCO2 increases and PaO2 decreases — the reverse of this option."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"For a client with tension pneumothorax, the appropriate position is:","options":["Semi-Fowler's or Fowler's position","Supine flat","Prone","Left lateral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes positioning the client in semi-Fowler's or Fowler's position.  Why B/C/D wrong: Flat, prone, and lateral positions do not optimize ventilation in tension pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for tension pneumothorax with an open chest wound? (SATA)","options":["Prepare for chest tube placement","Apply nonporous dressing on open chest wound that will not allow liquid or atmospheric air inside the lungs","Administer oxygen and initiate general nursing care","Monitor for drainage system and subcutaneous emphysema","Leave the open chest wound completely uncovered"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes preparing for chest tube placement, applying a nonporous dressing on the open chest wound that will not allow liquid or atmospheric air inside the lungs, administering oxygen and general nursing care, and monitoring the drainage system and subcutaneous emphysema.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: The open chest wound is covered with a nonporous dressing, not left uncovered."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) is BEST described as:","options":["An acute lung injury or suddenly respiratory failure that occurs due to some conditions like trauma, aspiration, burn, prolonged mechanical ventilation, severe infection, fat or air emboli, shock, smoke inhalation, and coagulopathy","A chronic reversible allergic airway disease","A bacterial pneumonia limited to one lobe","A fungal infection of the lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARDS is an acute lung injury or sudden respiratory failure due to conditions such as trauma, aspiration, burn, prolonged mechanical ventilation, severe infection, fat or air emboli, shock, smoke inhalation, and coagulopathy.  Why B wrong: That describes asthma, not ARDS.  Why C wrong: ARDS is diffuse, not a single-lobe bacterial pneumonia.  Why D wrong: That describes histoplasmosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In ARDS, the major site for injury is the:","options":["Alveolar capillary membrane, which is damaged with loss of fluid and pulmonary oedema","Pleural space","Bronchial smooth muscle","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The major site for injury in ARDS is the alveolar capillary membrane, which is damaged with loss of fluid and pulmonary oedema.  Why B wrong: The pleural space is not the primary site of ARDS injury.  Why C wrong: Bronchial smooth muscle involvement describes asthma.  Why D wrong: The diaphragm is not the injury site."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which pathophysiological changes occur in ARDS? (SATA)","options":["Impaired alveolar gas exchanges causing V/Q mismatch (ventilation/perfusion ratio)","Alteration in production of surfactant, increasing friction in the alveoli","Atelectasis will occur","The ABG levels identify respiratory acidosis, and hypoxemia that do not respond to an increased percentage of oxygen","The chest x-ray shows a normal clear lung field"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ARDS causes impaired alveolar gas exchange (V/Q mismatch), altered surfactant production (increasing friction), atelectasis, and respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia unresponsive to increased oxygen; the chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial and alveolar infiltrate.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: The chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial and alveolar infiltrate, not a clear lung field."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"A hallmark feature of ARDS hypoxemia is that it:","options":["Does not respond to an increased percentage of oxygen","Resolves immediately with low-flow nasal cannula","Is corrected by oral fluids","Improves with bronchodilators alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ARDS, the ABG identifies respiratory acidosis and hypoxemia that do NOT respond to an increased percentage of oxygen — a defining feature (refractory hypoxemia).  Why B/C/D wrong: Simple oxygen, oral fluids, and bronchodilators alone do not correct ARDS refractory hypoxemia."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Acute Respiratory Failure (ARF) occurs when:","options":["Insufficient oxygen is transported to the blood or inadequate CO2 is removed from the lungs and the client's compensatory mechanism failed","The pleura become inflamed","The bronchi spasm reversibly","Pus collects in the alveoli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARF occurs when insufficient oxygen is transported to the blood or inadequate CO2 is removed from the lungs and the client's compensatory mechanism has failed.  Why B wrong: Pleural inflammation is pleurisy.  Why C wrong: Reversible bronchospasm is asthma.  Why D wrong: Pus in alveoli relates to infection/empyema, not the ARF definition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"ARF with respiratory acidosis is identified by ABG values when:","options":["PaO2 is lower than 60 mm Hg and arterial O2 saturation is <90% and PaCO2 >50 mm Hg","PaO2 is 100 mm Hg and SaO2 is 99%","PaCO2 is 30 mm Hg","PaO2 is 90 mm Hg with SaO2 98%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ARF, oxygen may reach the alveoli but cannot be absorbed, resulting in a PaO2 lower than 60 mm Hg and arterial O2 saturation <90% and PaCO2 >50 mm Hg with respiratory acidosis.  Why B/D wrong: Normal-to-high PaO2 and SaO2 do not indicate failure.  Why C wrong: A low PaCO2 (30 mm Hg) does not meet the >50 mm Hg criterion for ARF respiratory acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which are recognized causes of Acute Respiratory Failure? (SATA)","options":["Defect in respiratory control centre of the brain","Chest wall defects","Impairment of respiratory muscles function","Reversible IgE-mediated bronchospasm only","Increased surfactant production"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of ARF include a defect in the respiratory control centre of the brain, chest wall defects, and impairment of respiratory muscle function.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Reversible IgE-mediated bronchospasm describes asthma, not a listed ARF cause.  Why E wrong: Increased surfactant production is not a listed ARF cause."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with ARF? (SATA)","options":["Restlessness, anxiety, dyspnea","Tachycardia, tachypnea","Grunting respiration and inter-costal retractions","Decreased breath sound and cyanosis","Bradypnea with euphoria and high oxygen levels"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ARF signs include restlessness/anxiety/dyspnea, tachycardia/tachypnea, grunting respiration and inter-costal retractions, decreased breath sound and cyanosis, abnormal ABG (PaO2 increased due to hyperventilation, later increased due to do-not-respond compensatory mechanism), hypoxia leading to delivery of high-flow oxygen, and pulmonary infiltrate/pulmonary oedema.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: ARF produces tachypnea, anxiety, and hypoxia — not bradypnea with euphoria."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"For a client with ARF, why is mechanical ventilation with PEEP preferred?","options":["PEEP maintains positive pressure to prevent collapse of the alveoli within lungs at end of expiration","PEEP dries pleural fluid","PEEP lowers oxygen demand by inducing sleep","PEEP removes pleural air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical ventilation with PEEP maintains positive pressure to prevent collapse of the alveoli within the lungs at the end of expiration.  Why B wrong: PEEP does not dry pleural fluid.  Why C wrong: PEEP is a ventilatory pressure support, not a sedative.  Why D wrong: Pleural air is removed by chest tube, not PEEP."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with ARF? (SATA)","options":["Maintain a patent airway","Monitor all vitals, oxygen saturation, and ABG level frequently","Treat accordingly underlying causes of ARF","Provide supplemental oxygen and position in semi-Fowler's or Fowler's position","Withhold all oxygen and keep the client supine flat"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ARF nursing care includes maintaining a patent airway, monitoring vitals/oxygen saturation/ABG frequently, treating the underlying cause, supplemental oxygen, semi-Fowler's/Fowler's positioning, mechanical ventilation with PEEP, administering surfactants as per order, and (if sedation is needed) noting sedation is contraindicated because it depresses the respiratory centre and causes hypoxia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Oxygen is provided and the client is positioned upright, not supine flat with oxygen withheld."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In a client with ARF, sedation is generally:","options":["Contraindicated because it depresses the respiratory centre and causes hypoxia","The first-line treatment","Required to increase respiratory drive","Used to raise PaO2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the client is not yet ventilated, sedation is contraindicated because it depresses the respiratory centre and causes hypoxia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sedation depresses respiration; it is not first-line, does not increase respiratory drive, and does not raise PaO2 in the non-ventilated ARF client."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Influenza, commonly known as \"flu,\" is BEST described as:","options":["A highly contagious acute viral respiratory infection","A bacterial pneumonia","A fungal lung disease","An autoimmune disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Influenza is also known as flu and is a highly contagious acute viral respiratory infection.  Why B wrong: It is viral, not bacterial.  Why C wrong: Fungal lung disease describes histoplasmosis.  Why D wrong: It is infectious, not autoimmune."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Normal seasonal influenza is characterized by which features? (SATA)","options":["Caused by RNA viruses and is commonly aseptic/self-limited (flu)","Clinical features include cold, cough, muscles pain, and mild fever","Causes severe permanent paralysis","Generally mild seasonal illness","A fungal infection requiring antifungals"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Normal seasonal influenza is caused by RNA viruses, is commonly self-limited (flu), presents with cold, cough, muscle pain, and mild fever, and is a mild seasonal illness.  Why A/B/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why C wrong: Severe permanent paralysis is not a feature of seasonal flu.  Why E wrong: Influenza is viral, not fungal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Avian influenza (bird flu) is characterized by which features? (SATA)","options":["It affects mostly birds; it does not affect usually humans","The causative organism is H5N1 virus","Prevention is by avoiding contact with wild animals and frequent and proper hand washing","It is caused by a fungus","It is spread only through contaminated water"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Avian influenza affects mostly birds and does not usually affect humans, the causative organism is H5N1 virus, and prevention is by avoiding contact with wild animals and frequent/proper hand washing.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Avian influenza is viral (H5N1), not fungal.  Why E wrong: Water-only spread is not the stated mechanism for avian influenza."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Swine flu (H1N1) is a subtype of influenza type:","options":["A","B","C","D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine flu (H1N1) is a subtype of influenza type-A.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is type-A, not B, C, or D."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Swine flu (H1N1) was reported as a pandemic disease in which year?","options":["2009","1999","2019","1985"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: H1N1 (swine flu) affects humans and was reported as a pandemic disease in 2009.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states 2009."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The causative organism of swine flu (H1N1) belongs to which family?","options":["H1N1 virus, Orthomyxovirus family","Coronaviridae","Picornaviridae","Herpesviridae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative organism of swine flu is H1N1 virus of the Orthomyxovirus family.  Why B/C/D wrong: H1N1 belongs to the Orthomyxovirus family, not these families."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The mode of transmission of swine flu (H1N1) is by:","options":["Droplet or airborne, and via infected pigs","Fecal-oral only","Blood transfusion only","Sexual contact only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source of transmission is infected pigs to human, and the mode of transmission is droplet or airborne.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fecal-oral, blood, and sexual routes are not the stated transmission for H1N1."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The incubation period for swine flu (H1N1) is:","options":["1-4 days","14-21 days","1 month","Less than 1 hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of swine flu is 1-4 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states 1-4 days."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Confirmative diagnosis of swine flu (H1N1) is by which test?","options":["RT-PCR test","Mantoux test","Sputum AFB stain","Blood culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmative diagnosis of swine flu is by RT-PCR test.  Why B wrong: Mantoux is for tuberculosis.  Why C wrong: Sputum AFB stain is for tuberculosis.  Why D wrong: Blood culture identifies bacteremia, not the H1N1 confirmatory test."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The drug of choice for swine flu (H1N1) is:","options":["Oseltamivir 75 mg (known as Tamiflu)","Amoxicillin","Rifampicin","Acyclovir"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice is Oseltamivir 75 mg, which is known as Tamiflu.  Why B wrong: Amoxicillin is an antibacterial, ineffective against influenza virus.  Why C wrong: Rifampicin is an anti-TB drug.  Why D wrong: Acyclovir treats herpes viruses, not influenza."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The swine flu (H1N1) pandemic of 2009 first emerged in which country?","options":["Mexico","India","China","United States"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The swine flu pandemic emerged in 2009, originated in Mexico.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states it originated in Mexico."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of swine flu (H1N1)? (SATA)","options":["Cold, coughing, and sneezing","Fever and muscles aches","Body ache, fatigue, anorexia, headache","Sore throat and rhinorrhoea","Painless thyroid enlargement"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of swine flu include cold/coughing/sneezing, fever and muscle aches, body ache, fatigue, anorexia, headache, sore throat, and rhinorrhoea.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Painless thyroid enlargement is unrelated to influenza."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which are recommended prevention measures for swine flu (H1N1)? (SATA)","options":["Take airborne precautions","Frequent hand washing","Proper cleaning and disinfecting","Yearly influenza vaccine, especially for old-age persons and health-care personnel, who provide direct care to the clients","Encourage indoor crowding to build immunity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention includes taking airborne precautions, frequent hand washing, proper cleaning and disinfecting, and yearly influenza vaccine especially for old-age persons and health-care personnel who provide direct care.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Crowding increases transmission and is not a prevention measure."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The influenza vaccine is contraindicated in individuals with which allergy?","options":["Egg allergy","Penicillin allergy","Latex allergy","Iodine allergy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The influenza vaccine is contraindicated in individuals with egg allergy.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically cites egg allergy as the contraindication for the influenza vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Pleural effusion is defined as:","options":["The collection of fluid in the pleural space","The collection of pus in the pleural space","The accumulation of air in the pleural space","The inflammation of the pleura without fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pleural effusion is the collection of fluid in the pleural space; it is the build-up of excess fluid between the layers of the pleura outside the lungs.  Why B wrong: Pus in the pleural space is empyema.  Why C wrong: Air in the pleural space is pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Pleural inflammation without fluid is pleurisy."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pleural effusion? (SATA)","options":["Pleuritic sharp pain that increases with inspiration","Progressive dyspnea","Dry, non-productive cough, tachycardia","Elevated temperature and decreased breath sound over the affected area","Hyper-resonance with increased breath sounds"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pleural effusion signs include pleuritic sharp pain that increases with inspiration, progressive dyspnea, dry non-productive cough/tachycardia, and elevated temperature with decreased breath sounds over the affected area.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Breath sounds are decreased (dullness), not hyper-resonant/increased, in pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"On chest x-ray of pleural effusion, the finding that the mediastinum will shift away from the effusion appears when fluid is more than:","options":["350 ml","50 ml","100 ml","1000 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chest x-ray shows pleural effusion, and if fluid is more than 350 ml the mediastinal shift (away from the effusion) will show.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies more than 350 ml as the threshold for visible mediastinal shift."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Empyema is defined as:","options":["The collection of pus within the pleural space","The collection of fluid in the pleural space","The collection of air in the pleural space","The collection of blood in the pleural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Empyema is the collection of pus within the pleural space; the fluid is thick, opaque, and foul-smelling.  Why B wrong: Fluid collection is pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Air collection is pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Blood collection is hemothorax."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The most common cause of empyema is:","options":["Pulmonary infection due to open chest injury or surgery","Pollen allergy","Aspirin ingestion","Egg allergy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of empyema is pulmonary infection due to open chest injury or surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: Allergy and drug-related causes are not the stated cause of empyema."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Emphysema is defined as:","options":["A condition in which the air sacs of the lungs are damaged and permanent enlarged","The collection of fluid in the pleural space","The inflammation of the pleura","A fungal lung infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Emphysema is a condition in which the air sacs of the lungs are damaged and permanently enlarged.  Why B wrong: Fluid in the pleural space is pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Pleural inflammation is pleurisy.  Why D wrong: A fungal lung infection is histoplasmosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with emphysema? (SATA)","options":["Chest pain and cough","Dyspnea","Anorexia and weight loss","Elevated temperature and malaise; night sweats and pleural exudate","Reversible bronchospasm relieved by inhaler"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Emphysema signs include chest pain/cough, dyspnea, anorexia/weight loss, elevated temperature/malaise, night sweats, and pleural exudate.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are listed in the source.  Why E wrong: Reversible bronchospasm relieved by inhaler describes asthma, not emphysema (which is irreversible/permanent enlargement)."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Pleurisy is defined as:","options":["The inflammation of the visceral and parietal membrane of the lungs","The collection of pus in the pleural space","Permanent enlargement of the alveoli","Collection of fluid in the pleural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pleurisy is the inflammation of the visceral and parietal membrane of the lungs; it may be caused by pulmonary infection or pneumonia.  Why B wrong: Pus in the pleural space is empyema.  Why C wrong: Permanent alveolar enlargement is emphysema.  Why D wrong: Fluid collection is pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pleurisy? (SATA)","options":["Inhibited pain that increased with deep breathing and coughing (cardinal sign)","Dyspnea","Pleural friction rub heard on auscultation","Painless thyroid enlargement","Night sweats with weight loss as the only finding"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Pleurisy signs include pain that increases with deep breathing and coughing (cardinal sign), dyspnea, and a pleural friction rub heard on auscultation.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Painless thyroid enlargement is unrelated.  Why E wrong: Night sweats/weight loss are associated with emphysema/sarcoidosis listings, not the defining pleurisy signs."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Sarcoidosis is BEST described as:","options":["A lung disorder in presence of epithelioid cell tubercle in the lungs","A bacterial pneumonia","The collection of air in the pleural space","A drug-induced asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sarcoidosis is a lung disorder in the presence of epithelioid cell tubercle in the lungs.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a bacterial pneumonia, pneumothorax, or drug-induced asthma."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Exact cause of sarcoidosis is unknown, but it is caused by which virus?","options":["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV virus)","Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)","Hepatitis B virus","Influenza virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The exact cause of sarcoidosis is unknown, but it is caused by Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV virus).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes sarcoidosis to the Epstein-Barr Virus, not these viruses."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with sarcoidosis? (SATA)","options":["Night sweats, fever, weight loss","Cough and dyspnea","Skin nodules and polyarthritis","Reversible IgE-mediated wheeze","Crepitus on palpation due to subcutaneous emphysema"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Sarcoidosis signs include night sweats, fever, weight loss, cough, dyspnea, skin nodules, and polyarthritis.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Reversible IgE-mediated wheeze describes asthma.  Why E wrong: Subcutaneous emphysema/crepitus relates to tension pneumothorax, not sarcoidosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which test is used for the diagnosis of sarcoidosis, with results obtained after about 6 months?","options":["Kveim test","Mantoux test","RT-PCR test","Sputum culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Kveim test is used for the diagnosis of sarcoidosis; it is intradermally and the test result will be obtained after about 6 months.  Why B wrong: Mantoux test is for tuberculosis.  Why C wrong: RT-PCR is for influenza/viral diagnosis.  Why D wrong: Sputum culture identifies bacterial infection."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Histoplasmosis is defined as:","options":["A fungal infection caused by Histoplasma capsulatum","A viral respiratory infection","The accumulation of air in the pleural space","An allergic IgE-mediated airway disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Histoplasmosis is a fungal infection caused by Histoplasma capsulatum.  Why B wrong: It is fungal, not viral.  Why C wrong: Air in the pleural space is pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: An IgE-mediated airway disease is asthma."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Which occupational lung disease is caused by coal dust?","options":["Anthracosis","Silicosis","Asbestosis","Byssinosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anthracosis is caused by coal dust.  Why B wrong: Silicosis is caused by silica dust.  Why C wrong: Asbestosis is caused by asbestos dust.  Why D wrong: Byssinosis is caused by cotton dust."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Silicosis is an occupational lung disease caused by:","options":["Silica dust","Coal dust","Sugarcane dust","Hay or grain dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silicosis is caused by silica dust.  Why B wrong: Coal dust causes anthracosis.  Why C wrong: Sugarcane dust causes bagassosis.  Why D wrong: Hay or grain dust causes farmer's lung."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Bagassosis is an occupational lung disease caused by:","options":["Sugarcane dust","Asbestos dust","Cotton dust","Iron dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bagassosis is caused by sugarcane dust.  Why B wrong: Asbestos dust causes asbestosis.  Why C wrong: Cotton dust causes byssinosis.  Why D wrong: Iron dust causes siderosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Byssinosis is an occupational lung disease caused by:","options":["Cotton dust","Silica dust","Coal dust","Hay or grain dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Byssinosis is caused by cotton dust.  Why B wrong: Silica dust causes silicosis.  Why C wrong: Coal dust causes anthracosis.  Why D wrong: Hay or grain dust causes farmer's lung."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Farmer's lung is an occupational lung disease caused by:","options":["Hay or grain dust","Cotton dust","Sugarcane dust","Iron dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Farmer's lung is caused by hay or grain dust.  Why B wrong: Cotton dust causes byssinosis.  Why C wrong: Sugarcane dust causes bagassosis.  Why D wrong: Iron dust causes siderosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Siderosis is an occupational lung disease caused by:","options":["Iron dust","Silica dust","Coal dust","Asbestos dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Siderosis is caused by iron dust.  Why B wrong: Silica dust causes silicosis.  Why C wrong: Coal dust causes anthracosis.  Why D wrong: Asbestos dust causes asbestosis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In respiratory rapid-revision terms, bradypnea is defined as a respiratory rate of:","options":["RR <12 breaths per minute","RR >20 breaths per minute","RR 14-20 breaths per minute","RR >30 breaths per minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bradypnea is RR <12 breaths per minute.  Why B wrong: RR >20 breaths per minute is tachypnea.  Why C wrong: 14-20 per minute is the normal respiratory rate (eupnea).  Why D wrong: >30 is not the stated bradypnea value."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Tachypnea is defined as a respiratory rate of:","options":["RR >20 breaths per minute","RR <12 breaths per minute","RR 14-20 per minute","RR <8 per minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachypnea is RR >20 breaths per minute.  Why B wrong: <12 per minute is bradypnea.  Why C wrong: 14-20 per minute is the normal rate (eupnea).  Why D wrong: <8 per minute is not the stated tachypnea value."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hyperpnea is BEST defined as:","options":["Deep breathing, as is seen in after exercise or racing","Slow shallow breathing","Absence of breathing","Normal quiet breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperpnea is deep breathing, as is seen after exercise or racing.  Why B wrong: Slow shallow breathing is hypopnea.  Why C wrong: Absence of breathing is apnea.  Why D wrong: Normal quiet breathing is eupnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hypercapnia is defined as:","options":["High level of CO2","Low level of oxygen in blood","Low level of oxygen in tissues","Increased rate and depth of breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypercapnia is a high level of CO2.  Why B wrong: Low level of oxygen in blood is hypoxemia.  Why C wrong: Low level of oxygen in tissues is hypoxia.  Why D wrong: Increased rate and depth of breathing is hyperventilation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Eupnea is defined as:","options":["Normal respiratory rate (14-20 p/m)","RR >20 per minute","High level of CO2","Absence of breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eupnea is the normal respiratory rate (14-20 per minute).  Why B wrong: >20 per minute is tachypnea.  Why C wrong: High CO2 is hypercapnia.  Why D wrong: Absence of breathing is apnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hypoxemia is defined as:","options":["Low level of oxygen in blood","Low level of oxygen in tissues","High level of CO2","Deep breathing after exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxemia is a low level of oxygen in blood.  Why B wrong: Low oxygen in tissues is hypoxia.  Why C wrong: High CO2 is hypercapnia.  Why D wrong: Deep breathing after exercise is hyperpnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hypoxia is defined as:","options":["Low level of oxygen in tissues","Low level of oxygen in blood","High level of CO2","Shallow breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxia is a low level of oxygen in tissues.  Why B wrong: Low oxygen in blood is hypoxemia.  Why C wrong: High CO2 is hypercapnia.  Why D wrong: Shallow breathing is hypoventilation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hypoventilation is defined as:","options":["Shallow breathing","Increased rate and depth of breathing","Deep breathing as after racing","Absence of breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoventilation is shallow breathing.  Why B wrong: Increased rate and depth of breathing is hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: Deep breathing after racing is hyperpnea.  Why D wrong: Absence of breathing is apnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hyperventilation is defined as:","options":["Increased rate and depth of breathing","Shallow breathing","Low level of oxygen in tissues","High level of CO2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperventilation is an increased rate and depth of breathing.  Why B wrong: Shallow breathing is hypoventilation.  Why C wrong: Low oxygen in tissues is hypoxia.  Why D wrong: High CO2 is hypercapnia."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Apnea is defined as:","options":["Cessation of breathing (infant >20 sec. and in adults/children >15 sec.)","Increased rate and depth of breathing","Shallow breathing","Deep breathing after exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnea is the cessation of breathing (infant >20 seconds and in adults/children >15 seconds).  Why B wrong: Increased rate and depth is hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: Shallow breathing is hypoventilation.  Why D wrong: Deep breathing after exercise is hyperpnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Cheyne-Stokes respiration is characterized by:","options":["Progressively deeper, and sometimes faster, breathing followed by a gradual decrease or period of apnea","A consistently rapid breathing pattern","Absent breathing only","Normal quiet breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cheyne-Stokes respiration is progressively deeper, and sometimes faster, breathing followed by a gradual decrease or period of apnea.  Why B wrong: It is a cyclic crescendo-decrescendo, not consistently rapid.  Why C wrong: Apnea is only one phase; breathing alternates with deeper phases.  Why D wrong: It is an abnormal cyclic pattern, not normal quiet breathing."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Atelectasis refers to a lung that is:","options":["Collapsed or airless state of the lungs","Permanently enlarged air sacs","Filled with pus in the pleural space","Filled with pleural fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atelectasis is a collapsed or airless state of the lungs.  Why B wrong: Permanently enlarged air sacs describe emphysema.  Why C wrong: Pus in the pleural space is empyema.  Why D wrong: Pleural fluid collection is pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Cor pulmonale is BEST described as:","options":["Abnormal enlargement of the right ventricle of the heart as a result of disease of the lungs or the pulmonary blood vessels","Left ventricular enlargement from hypertension","Inflammation of the pleura","Collapse of the alveoli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cor pulmonale is abnormal enlargement of the right ventricle of the heart as a result of disease of the lungs or the pulmonary blood vessels.  Why B wrong: It involves the right ventricle, not the left.  Why C wrong: Pleural inflammation is pleurisy.  Why D wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"In rapid-revision terms, surfactant is defined as:","options":["A glycoprotein that reduces the surface tension in the alveoli and decreases the tendency of the alveoli to collapse; its deficiency leads to alveolar collapse","An enzyme that breaks down mucus","A bronchodilator drug","A clotting factor in the lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surfactant is a glycoprotein that reduces the surface tension in the alveoli and decreases the tendency of the alveoli to collapse; deficiency of surfactant leads to alveolar collapse.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a mucolytic enzyme, a bronchodilator drug, or a clotting factor."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"The Hering-Breuer reflex functions to:","options":["Inhibit further lung expansion when the lung inflates, as pulmonary stretch receptors activate the inspiratory centre","Increase surfactant production","Trigger coughing","Cause bronchoconstriction in allergy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Hering-Breuer reflex: when the lung inflates, pulmonary stretch receptors activate the inspiratory centre to inhibit further lung expansion.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a stretch-receptor reflex preventing over-inflation, not related to surfactant production, cough, or allergic bronchoconstriction."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Pneumoconiosis refers to:","options":["The occupational lung diseases that are caused by dust particles","The collection of blood in the pleural space","The collection of pus in the pleural cavity","The exchange of gases between blood and lung"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumoconiosis is the occupational lung diseases that are caused by dust particles.  Why B wrong: Blood in the pleural space is hemothorax.  Why C wrong: Pus in the pleural cavity is empyema.  Why D wrong: Gas exchange between blood and lung is external respiration."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Hemothorax (rapid-revision term) is:","options":["The collection of blood into the pleural space","The collection of pus into the pleural cavity","The collapse of the alveoli","Exchange of gases between blood and cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemothorax is the collection of blood into the pleural space.  Why B wrong: Pus in the pleural cavity is empyema.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Gas exchange between blood and cells is internal respiration."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Empyema (rapid-revision term) is:","options":["The collection of pus into the pleural cavity","The collection of blood into the pleural space","Exchange of gases between blood and the lungs","A collapsed lung"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Empyema is the collection of pus into the pleural cavity.  Why B wrong: Blood in the pleural space is hemothorax.  Why C wrong: Gas exchange between blood and lungs is external respiration.  Why D wrong: A collapsed lung is atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"External respiration is defined as:","options":["Exchange of gases between blood and the lungs","Exchange of gases between blood and the cells","The collection of blood into the pleural space","Cessation of breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: External respiration is the exchange of gases between blood and the lungs.  Why B wrong: Exchange of gases between blood and cells is internal respiration.  Why C wrong: Blood in the pleural space is hemothorax.  Why D wrong: Cessation of breathing is apnea."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Internal respiration is defined as:","options":["Exchange of gases between blood and the cells","Exchange of gases between blood and the lungs","The collection of pus into the pleural cavity","Increased rate and depth of breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Internal respiration is the exchange of gases between blood and the cells.  Why B wrong: Exchange of gases between blood and lungs is external respiration.  Why C wrong: Pus in the pleural cavity is empyema.  Why D wrong: Increased rate and depth of breathing is hyperventilation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Total Lung Capacity (TLC) is defined as:","options":["The volume of lungs at maximal inflation; the sum total of IRV + TV + ERV + RC","The volume of air moved into or out of the lungs during each breathing","The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation","The maximum volume of air that can be exhaled from the end expiratory level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TLC is the volume of lungs at maximal inflation; it is the sum total of IRV + TV + ERV + RC = TLC. Normal in males is 6000 ml or 6 L and in females 4200 ml or 4.2 L.  Why B wrong: Air moved in/out during each breath is tidal volume.  Why C wrong: Volume remaining after maximal exhalation is residual volume.  Why D wrong: Maximum exhaled from end expiratory level is expiratory reserve volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Normal Total Lung Capacity (TLC) values are:","options":["6000 ml (6 L) in males and 4200 ml (4.2 L) in females","500 ml in males and 200 ml in females","1200 ml in males and 1100 ml in females","3300 ml in males and 1900 ml in females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal TLC in males is 6000 ml or 6 L and in females 4200 ml or 4.2 L.  Why B wrong: 500 ml/200 ml relate to tidal volume.  Why C wrong: 1200/1100 ml relate to residual and inspiratory-reserve type values.  Why D wrong: 3300/1900 ml relate to inspiratory reserve volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Tidal Volume (TV) is defined as:","options":["The volume of air moved into or out of the lungs during each breathing, normally about 500 ml","The volume of lungs at maximal inflation","The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation","The volume of air breathed out after the deepest inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tidal Volume is the volume of air moved into or out of the lungs during each breathing; it is normally about 500 ml.  Why B wrong: Volume at maximal inflation is TLC.  Why C wrong: Volume remaining after maximal exhalation is residual volume.  Why D wrong: Volume breathed out after deepest inhalation is vital capacity."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Residual Volume (RV) is defined as:","options":["The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation; about 500 ml is normal, depending on age and weight","The volume of air moved during each breath","The volume of lungs at maximal inflation","The maximum volume of air that can be inhaled from end expiratory level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Residual Volume is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation; it is about 500 ml normal, depending on age and weight.  Why B wrong: Air moved during each breath is tidal volume.  Why C wrong: Volume at maximal inflation is TLC.  Why D wrong: Maximum inhaled from end expiratory level is inspiratory reserve volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Normal Residual Volume (RV) is:","options":["1200 ml in males and 1100 ml in females","500 ml in both","6000 ml in males and 4200 ml in females","3300 ml in males and 1900 ml in females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RV is normally 1200 ml in males and 1100 ml in females; it is increased in COPD patients and decreased in hypertension and during pregnancy.  Why B wrong: ~500 ml relates to tidal volume.  Why C wrong: 6000/4200 ml relate to TLC.  Why D wrong: 3300/1900 ml relate to inspiratory reserve volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Residual Volume (RV) changes in which way in different conditions?","options":["Increased in COPD patients and decreased in hypertension and during pregnancy","Decreased in COPD and increased in pregnancy","Unchanged in all conditions","Increased only in healthy athletes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RV is increased in COPD patients and decreased in hypertension and during pregnancy.  Why B wrong: This reverses the actual direction of change.  Why C wrong: RV does change with these conditions.  Why D wrong: The source links RV changes to COPD/hypertension/pregnancy, not athletic status."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) is defined as:","options":["The maximum volume of air that can be exhaled from the end expiratory level","The maximum volume of air that can be inhaled from the end expiratory level","The volume of air moved during each breath","The volume of lungs at maximal inflation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ERV is the maximum volume of air that can be exhaled from the end expiratory level; it is normal at 1100 ml in males and 700 ml in females.  Why B wrong: Maximum inhaled from end expiratory level is inspiratory reserve volume.  Why C wrong: Air moved during each breath is tidal volume.  Why D wrong: Volume at maximal inflation is TLC."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Normal Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) values are:","options":["1100 ml in males and 700 ml in females","3300 ml in males and 1900 ml in females","1200 ml in males and 1100 ml in females","6000 ml in males and 4200 ml in females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ERV is normal at 1100 ml in males and 700 ml in females.  Why B wrong: 3300/1900 ml relate to inspiratory reserve volume.  Why C wrong: 1200/1100 ml relate to residual volume.  Why D wrong: 6000/4200 ml relate to TLC."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV) is defined as:","options":["The maximum volume of air that can be inhaled from the end expiratory level; normal in males 3300 ml and females 1900 ml","The maximum volume of air exhaled from the end expiratory level","The volume remaining after maximal exhalation","The volume of lungs at maximal inflation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IRV is the maximum volume of air that can be inhaled from the end expiratory level; normal in males is 3300 ml and females 1900 ml.  Why B wrong: Maximum exhaled from end expiratory level is ERV.  Why C wrong: Volume remaining after maximal exhalation is residual volume.  Why D wrong: Volume at maximal inflation is TLC."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Vital Capacity (VC) is defined as:","options":["The volume of air breathed out after the deepest inhalation; normal in males 4800 ml and females 3100 ml","The volume of air remaining after maximal exhalation","The volume of air moved during each breath","The maximum volume that can be inhaled from end expiratory level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vital Capacity is the volume of air breathed out after the deepest inhalation; it is normal in males at 4800 ml and females at 3100 ml.  Why B wrong: Volume remaining after maximal exhalation is residual volume.  Why C wrong: Air moved during each breath is tidal volume.  Why D wrong: Maximum inhaled from end expiratory level is IRV."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Inspiratory Capacity (IC) is calculated by which formula?","options":["IC = IRV + TV","IC = ERV + RV","IC = IRV + ERV","IC = TV + RV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inspiratory Capacity (IC) = IRV + TV.  Why B wrong: ERV + RV is the functional residual capacity (FRC).  Why C/D wrong: These combinations do not represent inspiratory capacity per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Asthma, chest injuries, pneumothorax, ARDS, acute respiratory failure…","question":"Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is calculated by which formula?","options":["FRC = ERV + RV","FRC = IRV + TV","FRC = IRV + ERV","FRC = TV + IRV + ERV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) = ERV + RV.  Why B wrong: IRV + TV is the inspiratory capacity.  Why C/D wrong: These combinations do not represent FRC per the source."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"In a patient with COPD, what is the physiological respiratory stimulant that drives ventilation?","options":["High level of carbon dioxide","Low level of oxygen (88–93%)","High blood pH","Increased bicarbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In COPD the normal CO2 drive is blunted, so a low level of oxygen (88–93%) becomes the respiratory stimulant (hypoxic drive).  Why A wrong: In healthy people CO2 is the main stimulant, but in chronic COPD this drive is reduced.  Why C wrong: High pH (alkalosis) depresses, not stimulates, respiration.  Why D wrong: Increased bicarbonate is a compensatory change, not the stimulant."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"How should oxygen best be delivered to a COPD patient to maintain the hypoxic drive?","options":["High-flow mask at 10 L/min","Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min","1–2 L/min by nasal prongs","Venturi mask at 60% FiO2"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Low-flow oxygen of 1–2 L/min by nasal prongs is recommended in COPD to avoid abolishing the hypoxic respiratory drive.  Why A/B/D wrong: High concentrations/flow rates can suppress the hypoxic drive and cause CO2 retention in COPD patients."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the most common risk factor for COPD?","options":["Air pollution","Smoking","Occupational dust","Genetic alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking is the most common risk factor for COPD.  Why A/C/D wrong: Pollution, occupational dust, and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency are recognized but less common causes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the most common opportunistic pathogenic organism causing respiratory infection (e.g., in immunocompromised patients)?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pneumocystis jiroveci","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumocystis jiroveci is the most common opportunistic pathogenic organism causing pneumonia in immunocompromised hosts.  Why A/B/D wrong: These cause infection but are not the classic opportunistic organism described here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal tidal volume in an adult?","options":["250 ml","500 ml","1000 ml","1500 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal tidal volume is approximately 500 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the normal resting tidal volume value."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which investigation is used to detect and monitor the complications/severity of COPD?","options":["Chest X-ray only","Pulmonary function test (PFT)","Sputum culture","Bronchoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pulmonary function test (PFT) is the investigation listed for assessing COPD.  Why A/C/D wrong: These may aid evaluation but are not the test specified for COPD complications/function."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which oxygen-delivery device provides the highest oxygen concentration?","options":["Nasal cannula","Simple face mask","Venturi mask","Non-rebreather mask (NRBM)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The non-rebreather mask (NRBM) delivers the highest oxygen concentration.  Why A/B/C wrong: Nasal cannula, simple mask, and Venturi mask deliver lower FiO2 than an NRBM."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which commonly used drug can precipitate drug-induced asthma?","options":["Paracetamol","Aspirin","Salbutamol","Amoxicillin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin is the drug commonly responsible for drug-induced (aspirin-sensitive) asthma.  Why A wrong: Paracetamol is generally a safer analgesic alternative.  Why C wrong: Salbutamol relieves bronchospasm.  Why D wrong: Amoxicillin is an antibiotic, not a typical asthma trigger here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the most common site for primary tuberculosis (TB)?","options":["Upper lobe of lung","Lower lobe of lung","Pleura","Mediastinum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The upper lobe is the most common site for primary TB.  Why B/C/D wrong: TB less commonly localizes primarily to the lower lobe, pleura, or mediastinum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which substance prevents the collapse of alveoli?","options":["Mucus","Surfactant","Glycoprotein","Hemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surfactant reduces surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse.  Why A/C/D wrong: Mucus, glycoprotein, and hemoglobin do not perform this anti-collapse role in alveoli."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Surfactant is chemically classified as a:","options":["Carbohydrate","Glycoprotein","Nucleic acid","Mineral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surfactant is a glycoprotein (lipoprotein/glycoprotein complex).  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not a simple carbohydrate, nucleic acid, or mineral."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"The respiratory centre is normally stimulated by which gas?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Carbon monoxide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon dioxide is the normal stimulant of the respiratory centre.  Why A wrong: Oxygen becomes the stimulant only in chronic COPD (hypoxic drive).  Why C/D wrong: Nitrogen and carbon monoxide do not normally stimulate respiration."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal length of the trachea in an adult?","options":["10 cm","12 cm","15 cm","20 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal length of the trachea is about 15 cm.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal tracheal length."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"At which vertebral level does the trachea bifurcate?","options":["T2–T3","T4–T5","T6–T7","C7–T1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trachea bifurcates at the T4–T5 level (carina).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect vertebral levels for tracheal bifurcation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal mean pulmonary artery pressure?","options":["8 mm Hg","15 mm Hg","25 mm Hg","35 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal mean pulmonary artery pressure is about 15 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 8 mm Hg is the normal pulmonary venous pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed the normal mean pulmonary artery pressure."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal pulmonary venous pressure?","options":["4 mm Hg","8 mm Hg","15 mm Hg","25 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulmonary venous pressure is about 8 mm Hg.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal pulmonary venous pressure."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal pulmonary artery pressure expressed as systolic/diastolic values?","options":["Systolic 15 / diastolic 5 mm Hg","Systolic 25 / diastolic 10 mm Hg","Systolic 35 / diastolic 15 mm Hg","Systolic 120 / diastolic 80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulmonary artery pressure is systolic 25 / diastolic 10 mm Hg.  Why A/C wrong: These values are incorrect for normal pulmonary artery pressure.  Why D wrong: 120/80 is a systemic arterial pressure, not pulmonary."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which adventitious breath sound is characteristic of pulmonary oedema?","options":["Wheezing","Crackles","Stridor","Pleural friction rub"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crackles (rales) are characteristic of pulmonary oedema due to fluid in the alveoli.  Why A wrong: Wheezing is characteristic of asthma.  Why C wrong: Stridor indicates upper-airway obstruction.  Why D wrong: Friction rub indicates pleural inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which adventitious breath sound is characteristic of asthma?","options":["Crackles","Wheezing","Bronchial breath sounds","Stridor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wheezing is the characteristic breath sound in asthma due to narrowed airways.  Why A wrong: Crackles indicate fluid (e.g., pulmonary oedema).  Why C/D wrong: Bronchial sounds and stridor are not the hallmark of asthma."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the confirmatory test for pneumonia?","options":["Chest X-ray","Sputum culture and sensitivity","CBC","ABG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum culture and sensitivity is the confirmatory test for pneumonia (identifies organism and antibiotic sensitivity).  Why A wrong: Chest X-ray supports diagnosis but is not confirmatory of the organism.  Why C/D wrong: CBC and ABG assess severity/status, not confirm the causative organism."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A barrel- or bottle-shaped chest is most commonly seen in which condition?","options":["Asthma","Emphysema/COPD","Pneumonia","Pleural effusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A barrel-shaped chest is characteristic of emphysema/COPD due to air trapping and hyperinflation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions do not classically cause a barrel chest."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the causative agent of farmer's lung disease?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Pneumocystis jiroveci","Thermophilic actinomycetes (hay dust)","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Farmer's lung is caused by thermophilic actinomycetes found in mouldy hay dust.  Why A/B/D wrong: These organisms cause other respiratory infections, not farmer's lung."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"The Mantoux test is a skin test used for diagnosis of which disease?","options":["Pneumonia","Tuberculosis (TB)","Asthma","Emphysema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Mantoux (tuberculin) skin test is used for tuberculosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not diagnosed with the Mantoux test."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Ghon's focus (Ghon foci) is seen in which disease?","options":["Asthma","Tuberculosis (TB)","Lung cancer","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ghon focus is a primary lesion characteristic of tuberculosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions do not produce a Ghon focus."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the definitive/confirmatory diagnosis of tuberculosis?","options":["Chest X-ray","Mantoux test","Sputum culture","ESR"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum culture is the definitive/confirmatory diagnostic test for TB.  Why A wrong: X-ray is suggestive, not confirmatory.  Why B wrong: Mantoux indicates exposure/immune response, not active disease confirmation.  Why D wrong: ESR is nonspecific."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the most common complication of tracheostomy?","options":["Laryngeal nerve damage","Hemorrhage","Infection","Tube displacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Laryngeal nerve damage is listed as the most common complication of tracheostomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These can occur but are not the answer specified by the source."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which position is appropriate after laryngectomy, lobectomy, pneumonectomy, or for pulmonary edema?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Fowler's position","Prone position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fowler's position promotes lung expansion and eases breathing after these procedures and in pulmonary edema.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not optimize ventilation in these post-operative respiratory situations."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the correct positioning for a patient after pulmonary embolism (to allow expansion of the remaining lung)?","options":["High Fowler's on the operated side","Low Fowler with the operated side down for remaining lung expansion","Supine flat","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Low Fowler's with the operated side positioned to allow the remaining lung to expand is recommended.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source positioning for pulmonary embolism."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the correct patient position for a bronchoscopy procedure?","options":["Prone with neck flexed","Supine with neck slightly extended","Left lateral","Sitting upright leaning forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For bronchoscopy the patient is positioned supine with the neck slightly extended to facilitate scope insertion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not align the airway for bronchoscopy as described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Where are the apneustic and pneumotaxic respiratory centres located?","options":["Cerebellum and thalamus","Pons and medulla","Hypothalamus and midbrain","Spinal cord and cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apneustic and pneumotaxic centres are present in the pons and medulla, which control respiration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These regions do not house these specific respiratory centres."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Where are the central chemoreceptors situated and what do they respond to?","options":["In the medulla; respond to chemical changes/changes in pH","In the carotid arteries; respond to PaO2 only","In the aorta; respond to blood pressure","In the lungs; respond to stretch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Central chemoreceptors are situated in the medulla and respond to chemical changes (changes in pH).  Why B/C/D wrong: Peripheral chemoreceptors are in the carotid/aorta; baroreceptors respond to pressure; stretch receptors respond to lung inflation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Peripheral chemoreceptors located in the carotid and aortic regions respond to which parameters?","options":["pH only","PaO2 and PaCO2","Blood glucose","Body temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to PaO2 and PaCO2.  Why A/C/D wrong: pH is primarily sensed centrally; glucose and temperature are not their stimuli."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the chemical combination of oxygen with hemoglobin called?","options":["Carbaminohemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobin","Methemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen combined with hemoglobin is called oxyhemoglobin.  Why A wrong: Carbaminohemoglobin is CO2 bound to Hb.  Why C wrong: Carboxyhemoglobin is CO bound to Hb.  Why D wrong: Methemoglobin is oxidized hemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the chemical combination of carbon dioxide with hemoglobin called?","options":["Oxyhemoglobin","Carbaminohemoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobin","Sulfhemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CO2 combined with hemoglobin is carbaminohemoglobin.  Why A wrong: Oxyhemoglobin is O2 + Hb.  Why C wrong: Carboxyhemoglobin is CO + Hb.  Why D wrong: Sulfhemoglobin is a sulfur derivative, not the CO2 carrier."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which nerve supplies the diaphragm?","options":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Intercostal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve.  Why A/C/D wrong: These nerves do not provide the primary motor supply to the diaphragm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which muscles are primarily responsible for inspiration?","options":["Diaphragm and external intercostals","Internal intercostals and abdominal muscles","Sternocleidomastoid only","Rectus abdominis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diaphragm and external intercostals are the primary muscles of inspiration.  Why B wrong: Internal intercostals and abdominal muscles aid expiration.  Why C/D wrong: These are accessory or expiratory muscles, not primary inspiratory muscles."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which structure regulates and maintains respiration?","options":["Pons only","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration is regulated and maintained by the medulla oblongata.  Why A wrong: The pons modulates but the medulla is the principal regulator.  Why C/D wrong: These do not regulate basic respiratory rhythm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Blood is classified as which type of tissue?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Liquid (fluid) connective tissue","Muscular tissue","Nervous tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood is a liquid (fluid) connective tissue.  Why A/C/D wrong: Blood is not epithelial, muscular, or nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal volume of blood in an adult?","options":["1–2 litres","3–4 litres","5–6 litres","8–9 litres"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal volume of blood is approximately 5–6 litres in an adult.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are outside the stated normal blood volume range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal pH of blood?","options":["6.35–6.45","7.0–7.10","7.35–7.45","7.80–7.90"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal pH of blood is 7.35–7.45 (slightly alkaline).  Why A/B/D wrong: These values are outside the normal blood pH range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Hemoglobin (Hb) is the iron-containing oxygen transport metalloprotein found in which cells?","options":["White blood cells","Red blood cells","Platelets","Plasma cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hemoglobin is the iron-containing oxygen transport metalloprotein in the red blood cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not located in WBCs, platelets, or plasma cells."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A hemoglobin molecule consists of how many polypeptide (globin) chains?","options":["2","3","4","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Globin is a protein formed from 4 polypeptide chains, and one hemoglobin molecule holds 4 oxygen and 4 carbon dioxide molecules accordingly.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the 4-chain structure of hemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal hemoglobin level in adult males?","options":["11 to 14 g/dl","12 to 16 g/dl","13 to 18 g/dl","18 to 22 g/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hemoglobin in males is 13 to 18 g/dl (or 130 to 180 g/L).  Why A/B wrong: 12 to 16 g/dl is the female range.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the normal male range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal hemoglobin level in adult females?","options":["8 to 10 g/dl","12 to 16 g/dl","13 to 18 g/dl","16 to 20 g/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hemoglobin in females is 12 to 16 g/dl (or 120 to 150 g/L).  Why A wrong: This is anemic.  Why C wrong: This is the male range.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the normal female range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which hemoglobin is the major (normal adult) hemoglobin in humans, composed of 2 alpha and 2 beta chains?","options":["Hemoglobin A (HbA)","Hemoglobin A2 (HbA2)","Hemoglobin F (HbF)","HbA1c"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HbA (a2,b2) is the major normal adult hemoglobin composed of 2 alpha and 2 beta chains.  Why B wrong: HbA2 (a2,d2) is a minor adult hemoglobin.  Why C wrong: HbF (a2,g2) is fetal hemoglobin.  Why D wrong: HbA1c is the glycated form."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which polypeptide chains make up Hemoglobin A2 (HbA2)?","options":["2 alpha, 2 beta","2 alpha, 2 delta (a2, d2)","2 alpha, 2 gamma","4 gamma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HbA2 consists of 2 alpha and 2 delta chains (a2, d2), a minor adult hemoglobin.  Why A wrong: 2 alpha 2 beta is HbA.  Why C wrong: 2 alpha 2 gamma is HbF.  Why D wrong: 4 gamma is not a normal physiologic adult hemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which polypeptide chains make up fetal hemoglobin (HbF)?","options":["2 alpha, 2 beta","2 alpha, 2 delta","2 alpha, 2 gamma (a2, g2)","4 beta"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HbF is the normal fetal hemoglobin composed of 2 alpha and 2 gamma chains; it has a higher affinity for O2 than HbA, ensuring oxygen transfer from maternal circulation to fetal RBCs through the placenta.  Why A wrong: That is adult HbA.  Why B wrong: That is HbA2.  Why D wrong: 4 beta is not the fetal hemoglobin composition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"HbA1c (glycated hemoglobin) is used to monitor blood glucose control over approximately what period?","options":["Past 7 days","Past 30 days","Past 90 days","Past 365 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HbA1c has glucose residues attached to beta-globin chains and is used to monitor blood glucose control over the past 90 days in diabetes patients.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the ~90-day monitoring window of HbA1c."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which hemoglobin derivative is formed when hemoglobin binds oxygen and gives blood its bright red colour?","options":["Carbaminohemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobin","Hemoglobinemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxyhemoglobin is oxygen + hemoglobin (O2 + Hb) and produces bright red blood colour.  Why A wrong: Carbaminohemoglobin is carbon dioxide + hemoglobin (CO2 + Hb).  Why C wrong: Carboxyhemoglobin is carbon monoxide + hemoglobin (CO + Hb), dark red.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobinemia is excess free hemoglobin in plasma."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Carboxyhemoglobin is formed by the combination of hemoglobin with which gas, and what colour does it impart?","options":["Oxygen; bright red","Carbon monoxide (CO); dark red colour","Carbon dioxide; blue","Nitrogen; pale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carboxyhemoglobin is carbon monoxide + hemoglobin (CO + Hb) and gives a dark red colour.  Why A wrong: That describes oxyhemoglobin.  Why C wrong: CO2 + Hb is carbaminohemoglobin.  Why D wrong: Nitrogen does not form this derivative."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Hemoglobinemia refers to:","options":["Low hemoglobin in red cells","Excess of free hemoglobin in the blood plasma","Hemoglobin bound to glucose","Hemoglobin in urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hemoglobinemia is the excess of freely hemoglobin in the blood plasma, due to hemolysis (rupture of RBCs) in various types of anaemia.  Why A wrong: That describes anemia.  Why C wrong: That describes glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c).  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin in urine is hemoglobinuria."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Hematocrit (Hct) refers to:","options":["The number of WBCs in blood","The percentage volume of RBC in the blood","The plasma protein level","The platelet count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hematocrit is the percentage volume of RBC in the blood, and normal levels vary by age and race.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hct measures red cell proportion, not WBCs, plasma protein, or platelets."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal hematocrit value in adult males?","options":["25% to 30%","36% to 44%","41% to 50%","55% to 60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hematocrit in males is 41% to 50%.  Why B wrong: 36% to 44% is the female (slightly lower) range.  Why A/D wrong: These are outside the stated normal male hematocrit range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal hematocrit value in adult females?","options":["36% to 44%","41% to 50%","50% to 55%","20% to 30%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hematocrit in females is 36% to 44% (slightly lower than males).  Why B wrong: That is the male range.  Why C/D wrong: These are outside the female normal hematocrit range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A hematocrit level below the normal range usually indicates:","options":["Polycythemia","Anaemia","Dehydration","Leukemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hematocrit level below the normal range means the person has too few red blood cells, i.e., anaemia.  Why A wrong: Polycythemia is a high hematocrit/RBC count.  Why C wrong: Dehydration tends to raise hematocrit.  Why D wrong: Leukemia involves WBCs, not defined by low hematocrit here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A hematocrit above the normal range may indicate which conditions?","options":["Anaemia or hemorrhage","Polycythemia or erythrocytosis","Thrombocytopenia","Leukopenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hematocrit above the normal range means too many red blood cells, which may indicate polycythemia or erythrocytosis.  Why A wrong: These cause low hematocrit.  Why C/D wrong: These involve platelets/WBCs, not elevated red cell volume."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What proportion of plasma and blood cells makes up whole blood?","options":["45% plasma and 55% blood cells","55% plasma and 45% blood cells","65% plasma and 35% blood cells","50% plasma and 50% blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood constitutes about 55% plasma and 45% blood cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated plasma-to-cell ratio."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Plasma is a liquid part of blood that consists of approximately what percentages of water, proteins, and other electrolytes/hormones/nutrients?","options":["70% water, 20% proteins, 10% other products","91% water, 8% proteins, 1% other electrolytes/hormones/nutrients","80% water, 15% proteins, 5% other products","60% water, 30% proteins, 10% other products"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma includes about 91% water, 8% proteins, and 1% other electrolytes, hormones, nutrients, and waste products.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated plasma composition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Serum is best described as:","options":["Plasma with clotting factors intact","The clear liquid part of blood without clotting factors, formed after coagulation","The cellular portion of blood","The buffy coat layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serum is the clear liquid substance without clotting factors that is formed after coagulation.  Why A wrong: Plasma still contains clotting factors; serum does not.  Why C/D wrong: Serum is liquid, not the cellular portion or buffy coat."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which plasma protein constitutes 60–65% and maintains the body's colloidal osmotic (oncotic) pressure?","options":["Globulin","Albumin","Fibrinogen","Hemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Albumin (60–65%) maintains the body's colloidal osmotic (oncotic) pressure.  Why A wrong: Globulin provides immunity.  Why C wrong: Fibrinogen is necessary for clot formation.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin is not a plasma protein."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Globulin (36% of plasma protein) is primarily responsible for which function?","options":["Maintaining oncotic pressure","Immunity of the human body (it is a type of immunoglobulin Ig)","Clot formation","Oxygen transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Globulin (36%) provides immunity of the human body; it is a type of immunoglobulin (Ig).  Why A wrong: Albumin maintains oncotic pressure.  Why C wrong: Fibrinogen handles clot formation.  Why D wrong: Oxygen transport is the role of hemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which plasma protein is necessary for clot formation?","options":["Albumin","Globulin","Fibrinogen","Hemoglobin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrinogen is necessary for clot formation.  Why A wrong: Albumin maintains oncotic pressure.  Why B wrong: Globulin provides immunity.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin transports gases."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Red blood cells are also known as:","options":["Leukocytes","Erythrocytes","Thrombocytes","Megakaryocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RBCs are also known as erythrocytes.  Why A wrong: Leukocytes are WBCs.  Why C wrong: Thrombocytes are platelets.  Why D wrong: Megakaryocytes are platelet precursors."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal value of RBC in adult males?","options":["3.5 to 4.0 million/ml","4.5 to 6.2 million/ml","6.5 to 8.0 million/ml","2.0 to 3.0 million/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal RBC value is 4.5 to 6.2 million/ml in males (females 4 to 5.5).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are outside the stated normal male RBC range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Where are red blood cells formed in the body?","options":["Liver","Red bone marrow of long bones","Spleen","Lymph nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow of long bones such as the sternum, ribs, iliac crest, femur, clavicle, and skull; this process is called erythropoiesis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the primary sites of RBC formation in the adult."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal life span of a red blood cell?","options":["60 days","90 days","120 days","200 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal life span of an RBC is 120 days.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the normal RBC life span."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which hormone stimulates erythropoietin production to produce red bone marrow / regulate RBC production?","options":["Renin","Erythropoietin hormone","Thrombopoietin","Insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Erythropoietin hormone stimulates erythropoiesis (RBC production).  Why A/C/D wrong: Renin regulates blood pressure, thrombopoietin stimulates platelets, insulin regulates glucose."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which elements/vitamins are essential for the production of red blood cells?","options":["Folic acid, vitamin B12, and iron","Vitamin C and calcium","Vitamin K and potassium","Vitamin D and sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Folic acid, vitamin B12, and iron are the essential elements for RBC production.  Why B/C/D wrong: These nutrients are not the essential elements cited for erythropoiesis here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A decrease in the number of red blood cells is termed:","options":["Polycythemia","Erythrocytopenia (erythropenia)","Leukopenia","Thrombocytopenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A decrease in RBC number is called erythrocytopenia or erythropenia.  Why A wrong: Polycythemia is an increase.  Why C wrong: Leukopenia is decreased WBCs.  Why D wrong: Thrombocytopenia is decreased platelets."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"White blood cells are also known as:","options":["Erythrocytes","Leukocytes","Thrombocytes","Reticulocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WBCs are also known as leukocytes.  Why A wrong: Erythrocytes are RBCs.  Why C wrong: Thrombocytes are platelets.  Why D wrong: Reticulocytes are immature RBCs."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"White blood cells are divided into which two main types?","options":["Granulocytes and agranulocytes","Erythrocytes and platelets","Albumin and globulin","Plasma and serum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are two types of WBCs — granulocytes and agranulocytes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not WBC subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which of the following are granulocytes?","options":["Lymphocytes and monocytes","Neutrophil, eosinophil, and basophil","Platelets and reticulocytes","T-cells and B-cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Granulocytes are neutrophil, eosinophil, and basophil.  Why A wrong: Lymphocytes and monocytes are agranulocytes.  Why C/D wrong: Platelets/reticulocytes and T/B cells are not the granulocyte trio."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which cells are classified as agranulocytes?","options":["Neutrophil and eosinophil","Monocyte (macrophages) and lymphocytes (T-cell and B-cell)","Basophil and neutrophil","Platelets and erythrocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Agranulocytes are monocytes (macrophages) and lymphocytes (T-cell and B-cell).  Why A/C wrong: These are granulocytes.  Why D wrong: Platelets and erythrocytes are not leukocytes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which white blood cells are responsible for phagocytosis, ingesting or destroying pathogenic/harmful microorganisms?","options":["Neutrophil and macrophages","Eosinophils and basophils","Lymphocytes only","Platelets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neutrophil and macrophages are responsible for phagocytosis — they protect the body by ingesting or destroying pathogenic harmful microorganisms.  Why B wrong: Eosinophils protect against allergic antibody reactions, not primarily phagocytose pathogens.  Why C wrong: Lymphocytes are formed in thymus, gland, and spleen.  Why D wrong: Platelets are involved in clotting."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which white blood cells protect the body by attacking substances responsible for allergic (antigen-antibody) reactions?","options":["Eosinophil cells","Basophils","Monocytes","Neutrophils"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eosinophil cells protect the body against substances responsible for antigen-antibody reactions (allergic responses).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not perform the eosinophil's specific anti-allergic role described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Where are lymphocytes formed?","options":["Red bone marrow only","Thymus, gland, and spleen","Liver and kidney","Lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lymphocytes are formed in the thymus, gland, and spleen.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the lymphocyte-forming sites described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal life span of a WBC?","options":["2 to 5 days","7 to 14 days","30 days","120 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal life span of a WBC is 7 to 14 days.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal WBC life span."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal life span of a platelet?","options":["2 to 5 days","7 to 14 days","30 days","120 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal life span of a platelet is 2 to 5 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These exceed the normal platelet life span."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal WBC count?","options":["4000 to 11000 cells/mm3","1500 to 1800/mm3","50,000 cells/mm3","1.5–4 lakh/mm3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal WBC count is 4000 to 11000 cells/mm3.  Why B wrong: 1500–1800/mm3 is the normal value of monocytes.  Why C/D wrong: These reflect platelet-related counts, not total WBC count."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"An increase in the WBC count, as occurs in bacterial infection (except in typhoid where it may decrease), is called:","options":["Leukopenia","Leukocytosis","Thrombocytosis","Erythrocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An increase in WBC count is leukocytosis; it occurs in bacterial infection except in typhoid, which may show a decrease.  Why A wrong: Leukopenia is a decrease in WBCs.  Why C wrong: Thrombocytosis is increased platelets.  Why D wrong: Erythrocytosis is increased RBCs."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A decrease in the number of leucocytes (WBCs) is called:","options":["Leukocytosis","Leukopenia","Thrombocytopenia","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A decrease in the number of leucocytes/WBCs is called leukopenia.  Why A wrong: Leukocytosis is an increase.  Why C wrong: Thrombocytopenia is decreased platelets.  Why D wrong: Anemia is decreased RBC/Hb."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Platelets are also known as:","options":["Erythrocytes","Leukocytes","Thrombocytes","Reticulocytes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets are also known as thrombocytes.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are RBCs, WBCs, and immature RBCs respectively."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Platelets are the smallest cells of the blood, measuring approximately:","options":["2–4 micrometer in diameter","6–8 micrometer in diameter","10–12 micrometer in diameter","20 micrometer in diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets are the smallest cells of the blood (2–4 micrometer in diameter).  Why B/C/D wrong: These exceed the stated platelet diameter."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the normal value of platelets?","options":["4000 to 11000/mm3","1,50,000 to 4,00,000 per litre (1.5 to 4 lakh/microL)","50,000/microL","12 to 16 g/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal platelet value is 1,50,000 to 4,00,000 (1.5 to 4 lakh) per microliter.  Why A wrong: That is the WBC count.  Why C wrong: 50,000 is the threshold below which spontaneous bleeding risk rises.  Why D wrong: 12–16 g/dl is a hemoglobin range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A decreased platelet count below 1,50,000/microL is termed:","options":["Thrombocytosis","Thrombocytopenia","Leukopenia","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A low platelet count (below 1,50,000/microL) is called thrombocytopenia.  Why A wrong: Thrombocytosis is an increase.  Why C wrong: Leukopenia is decreased WBCs.  Why D wrong: Anemia involves RBCs."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Below which platelet count is spontaneous bleeding a serious risk?","options":["Less than 1,50,000","Less than 1,00,000","Less than 50,000","Less than 4,00,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical bleeding is a risk when platelets are less than 50,000, and spontaneous bleeding is a risk when platelets are less than 50,000/microL.  Why A/B/D wrong: These thresholds do not match the stated spontaneous bleeding risk level."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"During blood coagulation, the formation of a clot that stops bleeding from a blood vessel is also called:","options":["Hematocrit","Hemostasis (haemostasis)","Hemoglobinemia","Hemolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The process to arrest or stop bloodloss is also called hemostasis (haemostasis).  Why A wrong: Hematocrit is the RBC volume percentage.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobinemia is free hemoglobin in plasma.  Why D wrong: Hemolysis is rupture of RBCs."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the first phase of the blood coagulation process in which an injured vascular spasm narrows the blood vessel?","options":["Platelet plug formation","Vasoconstriction (vascular spasm)","Coagulation phase","Fibrinolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vasoconstriction is the first phase — the injured blood vessel constricts, processed by smooth muscle cells, so that minimal blood won't damage; vasoconstriction occurs and platelets will adhere to endothelium.  Why A wrong: Platelet plug formation is the second stage.  Why C wrong: The coagulation phase comes later.  Why D wrong: Fibrinolysis is clot breakdown."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"In the platelet plug formation stage (primary hemostasis), platelets adhere to the damaged endothelium. This process is activated by which factor?","options":["Glycoprotein or Von-Willebrand factor","Calcium ions only","Thrombin only","Albumin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets adhere to the damaged endothelium to form a platelet plug (primary hemostasis); this process is activated by a glycoprotein or Von-Willebrand factor.  Why B/C wrong: Calcium and thrombin participate later in the coagulation cascade.  Why D wrong: Albumin maintains oncotic pressure, not platelet adhesion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which two pathways/mechanisms make up the blood coagulation phase?","options":["Extrinsic and intrinsic clotting mechanisms","Primary and secondary mechanisms","Aerobic and anaerobic mechanisms","Direct and indirect mechanisms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The coagulation phase consists of the extrinsic clotting mechanism (processed by damaged tissues) and the intrinsic clotting mechanism (processed by release of chemicals/clotting factors).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the named clotting mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Clotting Factor I (Fibrinogen) is synthesized by the liver and is converted into which insoluble substance to form a clot?","options":["Thrombin","Fibrin","Prothrombin","Calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrinogen (Factor I) is a plasma protein made in the liver, commonly converted to fibrin (an insoluble substance) to form the clot.  Why A wrong: Thrombin is the enzyme that converts fibrinogen to fibrin.  Why C wrong: Prothrombin (Factor II) is converted to thrombin.  Why D wrong: Calcium (Factor IV) is a cofactor."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Clotting Factor II (Prothrombin), a plasma protein produced by the liver, is converted into which substance during coagulation?","options":["Fibrin","Thrombin (converts fibrinogen to fibrin)","Fibrinogen","Calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prothrombin (Factor II) is converted to thrombin, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin.  Why A wrong: Fibrin is the end product, made by thrombin.  Why C wrong: Fibrinogen is Factor I.  Why D wrong: Calcium is Factor IV."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clotting factor is also known as \"Christmas factor\"?","options":["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor X","Factor XI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Factor IX is also known as Christmas factor.  Why A wrong: Factor VIII is the antihemophilic factor (AHF).  Why C wrong: Factor X is the Stuart factor (stable factor here).  Why D wrong: Factor XI is the plasma thromboplastin antecedent (PTA)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clotting factor is also known as the \"labile factor\"?","options":["Factor IV","Factor V (Proaccelerin)","Factor VII","Factor X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Factor V (Proaccelerin) is also known as the labile factor.  Why A wrong: Factor IV is calcium.  Why C wrong: Factor VII is the stable factor / proconvertin.  Why D wrong: Factor X is the Stuart factor."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clotting factor is also known as the \"stable factor\"?","options":["Factor V","Factor VII (Proconvertin)","Factor IX","Factor XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Factor VII (Proconvertin) is also known as the stable factor.  Why A wrong: Factor V is the labile factor.  Why C wrong: Factor IX is Christmas factor.  Why D wrong: Factor XII is Hageman factor."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Calcium acts as which clotting factor and is needed essentially at all stages of the coagulation process?","options":["Factor I","Factor II","Factor IV (Ca2+)","Factor VIII"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium (Ca2+) is Factor IV, an inorganic ion needed essentially at all stages of the coagulation process; it always present in plasma.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are fibrinogen, prothrombin, and antihemophilic factor respectively, not calcium."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A deficiency of Factor VIII (Antihemophilic factor, AHF) results in which condition?","options":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","Hemophilia C","Von Willebrand disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Factor VIII is the antihemophilic factor (AHF); its deficiency results in hemophilia A (intrinsic pathway).  Why B wrong: Hemophilia B results from Factor IX deficiency.  Why C wrong: Hemophilia C results from Factor XI deficiency.  Why D wrong: Von Willebrand disease involves vWF, not Factor VIII deficiency here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A deficiency of Factor IX (Christmas factor) results in which condition?","options":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","Hemophilia C","Scurvy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Factor IX (Christmas factor) deficiency results in hemophilia B (intrinsic pathway).  Why A wrong: Hemophilia A is Factor VIII deficiency.  Why C wrong: Hemophilia C is Factor XI deficiency.  Why D wrong: Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A deficiency of Factor XI (Plasma thromboplastin antecedent, PTA) results in which condition?","options":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","Hemophilia C","Thrombocytopenia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Factor XI (PTA) deficiency results in hemophilia C (intrinsic pathway).  Why A wrong: Hemophilia A is Factor VIII deficiency.  Why B wrong: Hemophilia B is Factor IX deficiency.  Why D wrong: Thrombocytopenia is low platelets, not a coagulation factor deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clotting factor is the Hageman factor that initiates clotting in vitro and activates inflammation?","options":["Factor X","Factor XI","Factor XII (Hageman factor)","Factor XIII"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Factor XII is the Hageman factor; it initiates clotting in vitro and activates inflammation.  Why A wrong: Factor X is the Stuart factor.  Why B wrong: Factor XI is the plasma thromboplastin antecedent.  Why D wrong: Factor XIII is the fibrin stabilizing factor."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clotting factor is the fibrin stabilizing factor that cross-links fibrin to form a strong, stable clot?","options":["Factor I","Factor XII","Factor XIII (Fibrin stabilizing factor)","Factor IV"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Factor XIII is the fibrin stabilizing factor; it cross-links fibrin forming a strong, stable clot.  Why A wrong: Factor I is fibrinogen.  Why B wrong: Factor XII is the Hageman factor.  Why D wrong: Factor IV is calcium."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Vitamin K is needed for the liver to produce which clotting factors?","options":["Factor I and Factor IV","Prothrombin (and other vitamin-K-dependent factors)","Factor V and Factor VIII","Factor XI and Factor XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K must be present in the blood for the liver to produce prothrombin (vitamin-K-dependent clotting factor).  Why A/C/D wrong: These factors are not the vitamin-K-dependent factor highlighted here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"End product of blood coagulation is the conversion of fibrinogen into which substance, and its later breakdown by plasminogen is called?","options":["Fibrin formed; breakdown called fibrinolysis","Thrombin formed; breakdown called hemolysis","Prothrombin formed; breakdown called hemostasis","Calcium formed; breakdown called coagulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The end product of blood coagulation process is fibrin; when endostatum (plasmin from plasminogen) is formed, this breakdown of fibrin/clot is called fibrinolysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misname the end product or the dissolution process."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which mnemonic helps remember the clotting factors in order (\"Foolish People Try Climbing Long Slopes After Christmas, Some People Have Fallen\")?","options":["A trick for remembering clotting factors I to XIII","A blood group mnemonic","A WBC differential mnemonic","A vitamin mnemonic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The phrase is a trick/mnemonic for remembering the clotting factors in sequence.  Why B/C/D wrong: It does not pertain to blood groups, WBC differentials, or vitamins."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Who discovered the ABO blood group system, and in which year?","options":["Karl Landsteiner in 1901","Karl Landsteiner in 1940","Alexander Wiener in 1937","William Harvey in 1628"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A, B, AB & O blood groups were discovered by Karl Landsteiner in 1901.  Why B wrong: 1940 is associated with Rh factor discovery (also Landsteiner with Wiener).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the ABO discovery credits stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"The ABO blood grouping system is based on antigens and antibodies. Antigens are attached to which structures?","options":["Plasma proteins","The RBCs (red blood cells)","White blood cells","Platelets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ABO grouping is based on antigens and antibodies; antigens are attached on the RBCs, and the associated anti-A and anti-B antibodies are usually IgM (in plasma).  Why A/C/D wrong: ABO antigens are on red cells, not on plasma proteins, WBCs, or platelets."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which blood group is considered the universal donor?","options":["Blood group AB positive","Blood group O negative","Blood group A positive","Blood group B negative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Universal donor blood group is O negative.  Why A wrong: AB positive is the universal recipient.  Why C/D wrong: A positive and B negative are not the universal donor."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which blood group is considered the universal recipient?","options":["O negative","AB positive","A negative","B positive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Universal recipient blood group is AB positive.  Why A wrong: O negative is the universal donor.  Why C/D wrong: A negative and B positive are not the universal recipient."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which antigens and antibodies are present in blood group A?","options":["A antigen and anti-B antibody","B antigen and anti-A antibody","A and B antigens, no antibodies","No antigen, anti-A and anti-B antibodies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blood group A has A antigen on RBCs and anti-B antibody in plasma.  Why B wrong: That describes group B.  Why C wrong: That describes group AB.  Why D wrong: That describes group O."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which antigens and antibodies are present in blood group B?","options":["A antigen and anti-B antibody","B antigen and anti-A antibody","A and B antigens, no antibodies","No antigen, anti-A and anti-B antibodies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood group B has B antigen on RBCs and anti-A antibody in plasma.  Why A wrong: That describes group A.  Why C wrong: That describes group AB.  Why D wrong: That describes group O."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which antigens and antibodies characterize blood group AB?","options":["A and B antigens with no antibodies (anti-A and anti-B absent)","No antigens with anti-A and anti-B antibodies","A antigen and anti-B antibody","B antigen and anti-A antibody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blood group AB has both A and B antigens and no antibodies (none), making it the universal recipient.  Why B wrong: That describes group O.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe groups A and B."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which antigens and antibodies characterize blood group O?","options":["A and B antigens, no antibodies","No antigens, both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","A antigen, anti-B antibody","B antigen, anti-A antibody"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood group O has no antigens and both anti-A and anti-B antibodies, making it the universal donor.  Why A wrong: That describes group AB.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe groups A and B."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"The Rh factor (Rhesus factor) is a protein found on the surface of RBCs and was first discovered in which animal?","options":["Mouse","Rhesus monkey","Rabbit","Guinea pig"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Rh factor (Rhesus factor) is a protein found on the surface of the RBCs and was discovered in the rhesus monkey by Karl Landsteiner in 1937.  Why A/C/D wrong: The Rh factor was discovered in the rhesus monkey, not these animals."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"An Rh-positive person is defined as someone who:","options":["Lacks the Rh factor on their RBCs","Has the Rh factor (D antigen) present on their RBCs","Has anti-Rh antibodies present from birth","Has no antigens at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An Rh-positive person is one whose RBCs have the Rh factor; an Rh-negative person does not have this factor.  Why A wrong: That describes Rh-negative.  Why C wrong: Anti-Rh antibodies are produced after exposure, not present from birth.  Why D wrong: Rh status concerns the Rh antigen specifically, not absence of all antigens."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Anaemia is best defined as:","options":["An increase in red blood cell count","A reduction of the concentration of RBC or Hb in the circulating blood","An increase in white blood cells","A decrease in platelet count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anaemia is the reduction of concentration of RBC or Hb in the circulating blood.  Why A wrong: That would be polycythemia.  Why C wrong: That involves WBCs (leukocytosis).  Why D wrong: That is thrombocytopenia."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which type of anaemia results from decreased RBC production due to vitamin deficiency (e.g., pernicious anaemia), hormonal deficiency (renal disease), or aplastic anaemia?","options":["Hemolytic anaemia","Anaemia by etiology (decreased RBC production)","Acute post-hemorrhagic anaemia","Microcytic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By etiology, decreased RBC production (vitamin deficiency such as pernicious anaemia, hormonal deficiency from renal disease, aplastic anaemia) is a recognized class.  Why A wrong: Hemolytic anaemia is due to short life span of RBCs.  Why C wrong: Post-hemorrhagic anaemia is due to loss of RBCs (bleeding).  Why D wrong: Microcytic refers to morphology, not this etiologic class."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Hemolytic anaemia, classified by etiology, is caused by:","options":["Short life span of RBCs","Decreased RBC production","Acute loss of RBCs from bleeding","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemolytic anaemia is due to the short life span of RBCs (increased destruction).  Why B wrong: Decreased production is a separate etiologic category.  Why C wrong: Acute blood loss is post-hemorrhagic anaemia.  Why D wrong: B12 deficiency is a cause of megaloblastic/pernicious anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Microcytic hypochromic anaemia (small, pale red cells) is most characteristic of which condition?","options":["Megaloblastic anaemia","Iron deficiency anaemia (IDA), thalassemia, and sideroblastic anaemia","Aplastic anaemia","Pernicious anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By morphology, microcytic hypochromic anaemia is iron deficiency anaemia (IDA), thalassemia, and sideroblastic anaemia.  Why A/D wrong: Megaloblastic and pernicious anaemia are macrocytic.  Why C wrong: Aplastic anaemia is typically normocytic."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Normocytic normochromic anaemia (normal-sized, normal-colour cells) is seen in which condition?","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Acute post-hemorrhagic anaemia, hemolytic anaemia, and aplastic anaemia","Megaloblastic anaemia","Thalassemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normocytic normochromic anaemia is seen in acute post-hemorrhagic anaemia, hemolytic anaemia, and aplastic anaemia.  Why A/D wrong: IDA and thalassemia are microcytic.  Why C wrong: Megaloblastic anaemia is macrocytic."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Macrocytic anaemia (large red cells) is seen in which conditions?","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia and thalassemia","Megaloblastic anaemia and pernicious anaemia","Sideroblastic anaemia","Acute post-hemorrhagic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrocytic anaemia (large cells) is seen in megaloblastic anaemia and pernicious anaemia.  Why A/C wrong: These are microcytic.  Why D wrong: Post-hemorrhagic anaemia is normocytic."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"In describing red blood cell size, \"microcytic,\" \"normocytic,\" and \"macrocytic\" refer to:","options":["The colour of the cell","The cell size/diameter (small, normal, large)","The number of cells","The hemoglobin content only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Microcytic = cells smaller than normal, normocytic = normal-sized, macrocytic = larger than normal — these terms describe cell size/diameter.  Why A wrong: Colour is described by hypochromic/normochromic.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe count or hemoglobin content alone."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"The terms \"hypochromic\" and \"normochromic\" describe which RBC characteristic?","options":["Cell size","Cell colour / hemoglobin content (pale vs normal colour)","Cell number","Cell shape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochromic means pale colour (low hemoglobin), normochromic means normal colour — these describe cell colour/hemoglobin content.  Why A wrong: Size is described by cytic terms.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe count or shape."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the most common cause of iron deficiency anaemia (IDA)?","options":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","GI bleeding, menstruation, and lack of dietary intake of iron","Renal disease","Aplastic bone marrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common causes of IDA are GI bleeding, menstruation, and lack of dietary intake of iron/iron-deficiency syndrome.  Why A wrong: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic/pernicious anaemia.  Why C/D wrong: These cause other anaemia types, not the most common IDA causes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"IDA is more commonly present (a group at risk) during which physiological state due to increased iron demand?","options":["Childhood only","Pregnancy (gestation), due to increased demand of iron","Old age only","Menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IDA is commonly present during gestation (pregnancy) due to increased demand of iron during pregnancy.  Why A/C/D wrong: While iron needs vary, the source highlights pregnancy as the at-risk state."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A patient with iron deficiency anaemia is at risk because the body fails to maintain its need for iron. What is the basic treatment for IDA?","options":["Vitamin B12 injections only","Iron supplements and dietary changes (eating iron-rich food), which increase iron absorption from food","Folic acid supplements only","Blood transfusion only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment of IDA is iron supplements and dietary changes (eating iron-rich foods), which increase iron absorption from food; in severe types, supplementation and blood transfusion may be required.  Why A/C wrong: B12 and folic acid treat megaloblastic anaemia, not IDA.  Why D wrong: Transfusion is reserved for severe cases, not the first-line treatment."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Megaloblastic anaemia has how many main types, and which are they?","options":["One type: iron deficiency","Two types: folate deficiency anaemia and pernicious anaemia (due to vitamin B12 / cyanocobalamin deficiency)","Three types including thalassemia","One type: aplastic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Megaloblastic anaemia has two main types — folate deficiency anaemia (due to folic acid deficiency) and pernicious anaemia (due to vitamin B12 / cyanocobalamin deficiency).  Why A/C/D wrong: These misidentify the types of megaloblastic anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which clinical features are characteristic of pernicious anaemia?","options":["Spoon-shaped nails only","Beefy red tongue, lack of intrinsic factor, and positive Romberg test (loss of balance with eyes closed)","Jaundice and dark urine","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features of pernicious anaemia include a beefy red tongue, lack of intrinsic factor, and a positive Romberg test (loss of balance when eyes are closed) due to neurologic involvement.  Why A wrong: Spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia) are seen in iron deficiency.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed pernicious anaemia features."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which groups are at risk of megaloblastic (B12/folate deficiency) anaemia?","options":["People who eat excess iron","People who have conditions preventing absorption of folic acid and vitamin B12, and people who don't get enough folic acid and vitamin B12 in their diet","Only newborns","Only athletes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Groups at risk are people with conditions preventing absorption of folic acid and vitamin B12, and people whose diets lack folic acid and vitamin B12.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the at-risk groups described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What is the treatment of megaloblastic anaemia?","options":["Iron supplements only","Folic acid and Vitamin B12 supplements and dietary changes (foods rich in vitamin B12, fish, eggs, dairy products, and meats) fortified with folic acid and vitamin B12","Blood transfusion only","Vitamin C supplements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment is folic acid and vitamin B12 supplements plus dietary changes (foods rich in vitamin B12 such as fish, eggs, dairy, and meats) and cereals/foods fortified with folic acid and vitamin B12.  Why A wrong: Iron treats IDA, not megaloblastic anaemia.  Why C wrong: Transfusion is not the primary treatment.  Why D wrong: Vitamin C is not the specified treatment."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Vitamin B12 is also referred to as cobalamin because it contains which metal ion?","options":["Iron","Cobalt","Copper","Zinc"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B12 is called cobalamin because it contains a metal ion (cobalt).  Why A/C/D wrong: It does not contain iron, copper, or zinc as its defining metal ion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Why must vitamin B12 be supplied through the diet (it is an essential vitamin)?","options":["It is produced in excess by the body","It is not made by the body and is not stored, so it must be supplied in the diet (daily requirement 2–4 mcg/day for adults)","It is stored indefinitely in the liver","It is made by skin exposure to sunlight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B12 is not made by the body and (relative to need) not stored adequately, so it must be supplied in the diet; the daily requirement is about 2–4 mcg/day for adults.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's statement that B12 is dietary-dependent."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Where is vitamin B12 absorbed and what is required for its absorption?","options":["In the mouth; requires saliva","In the stomach, bound to intrinsic factor (IF) and absorbed through the terminal ileum, transported by transcobalamin","In the large intestine; requires bile","In the liver; requires albumin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: B12 absorption and transport: vitamin B12 is bound to intrinsic factor (IF) in the stomach and absorbed through the terminal ileum, then transported by transcobalamin to be utilized by tissues.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the site and required factor for B12 absorption."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which factor (IF) is a glycoprotein produced by the parietal (oxyntic) cells of the stomach and is essential for vitamin B12 absorption?","options":["Christmas factor","Intrinsic factor (IF)","Von Willebrand factor","Tissue factor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intrinsic factor (IF) is a glycoprotein produced by the parietal (oxyntic) cells of the stomach, essential for B12 absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are clotting-related factors, not the gastric IF."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"What are the key functions of vitamin B12 in the body?","options":["Hematopoiesis (making red blood cells, RBC), GIT mucosa integrity, and DNA synthesis and maintenance of myelin sheath of nervous system","Bone formation only","Blood clotting only","Vision and night blindness prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Functions of B12 include hematopoiesis (making RBCs), maintaining GIT mucosa integrity, DNA synthesis, and maintenance of the myelin sheath of the nervous system.  Why B wrong: Bone formation is associated with vitamin D/calcium.  Why C wrong: Clotting is vitamin K's role.  Why D wrong: Vision/night blindness relates to vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which form of vitamin B12 is the naturally bioactive form, commonly available, and used to treat low B12 circulation, helping make myelin sheath, beef liver, and nervous system functions?","options":["Cyanocobalamin","Methylcobalamin (naturally bioactive form)","Adenosylcobalamin","Hydroxocobalamin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylcobalamin is the naturally bioactive form, commonly available, used to treat low B12 circulation and supports myelin sheath and nervous system function.  Why A wrong: Cyanocobalamin is a synthetic form, made in a laboratory, used in supplements and food fortification.  Why C wrong: Adenosylcobalamin is another naturally bioactive form.  Why D wrong: Hydroxocobalamin is a naturally bioactive form injected/used IM."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which form of vitamin B12 is a synthetic (laboratory-made) form used in supplements and food fortification?","options":["Methylcobalamin","Adenosylcobalamin","Cyanocobalamin (synthetic form)","Hydroxocobalamin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanocobalamin is the synthetic form, made in a laboratory, used in supplements and food fortification.  Why A/B/D wrong: Methylcobalamin, adenosylcobalamin, and hydroxocobalamin are naturally bioactive forms."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which form of vitamin B12 is a naturally bioactive form that is injected (often IM) and used to treat B12 deficiency?","options":["Cyanocobalamin","Hydroxocobalamin (naturally bioactive, injected/IM)","Methylcobalamin only orally","None of the above"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydroxocobalamin is a naturally bioactive form, injected (IM), used to treat vitamin B12 deficiency.  Why A wrong: Cyanocobalamin is synthetic.  Why C wrong: Methylcobalamin is bioactive but described separately as the commonly available form.  Why D wrong: Hydroxocobalamin is the correct injected bioactive form."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A patient develops anaemia following a total gastrectomy. Which deficiency is the most likely cause, based on B12 physiology?","options":["Iron deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency due to loss of intrinsic factor (produced by gastric parietal cells)","Folic acid deficiency","Vitamin C deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intrinsic factor, required for B12 absorption, is produced by the gastric parietal (oxyntic) cells; removing the stomach eliminates IF, leading to B12 deficiency (pernicious-type) anaemia.  Why A wrong: Iron deficiency relates to blood loss/diet, not loss of IF.  Why C wrong: Folate is absorbed without intrinsic factor.  Why D wrong: Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, not this pattern."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"Which of the following are causes of pernicious/B12 deficiency anaemia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Lack of intrinsic factor","Conditions preventing absorption of vitamin B12 (e.g., terminal ileum disease)","Inadequate dietary intake of vitamin B12","Excess dietary iron","Total gastrectomy (loss of parietal cells)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pernicious anaemia results from lack of intrinsic factor, conditions preventing B12 absorption, inadequate dietary B12, and loss of parietal cells (e.g., gastrectomy).  Why D wrong: Excess dietary iron does not cause B12 deficiency anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 016 · COPD/respiratory rapid-fact Q&A and post-op positioning…","question":"A patient presents with a beefy red tongue and difficulty maintaining balance with eyes closed (positive Romberg test). Which type of anaemia is most likely?","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Pernicious (vitamin B12 deficiency) anaemia","Aplastic anaemia","Hemolytic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beefy red tongue, lack of intrinsic factor, and a positive Romberg test are clinical features of pernicious (B12 deficiency) anaemia.  Why A wrong: IDA presents with pallor and spoon-shaped nails, not these neurologic features.  Why C/D wrong: Aplastic and hemolytic anaemia do not classically cause these specific signs."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Aplastic anaemia is a condition in which blood has a lower-than-normal number of which cells?","options":["Only RBCs","Only WBCs","RBCs only and platelets","RBCs, WBCs, and platelets"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: In aplastic anaemia all blood cell lines are reduced because the bone marrow fails to make enough RBCs, WBCs, and platelets.  Why A/B/C wrong: Aplastic anaemia involves pancytopenia affecting all three cell lines, not an isolated reduction."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Aplastic anaemia is caused when the bone marrow is damaged and cannot make enough RBCs, WBCs, and platelets. Which mechanism can cause this damage?","options":["Increased dietary iron","Bone marrow deprivation by chemical or drugs (e.g., thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, erythrocytopenia)","Excess vitamin B12 intake","Overproduction of erythropoietin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aplastic anaemia is due to bone marrow deprivation by chemicals or drugs, resulting in thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and erythrocytopenia.  Why A/B/C wrong: Excess iron, vitamin B12, or erythropoietin do not cause marrow aplasia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A clinical feature of aplastic anaemia is bleeding from mucous membranes. What is the underlying cause of this bleeding?","options":["Increased platelet count","Decreased level of leukocytes","Decreased platelets (thrombocytopenia)","Increased erythropoiesis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Aplastic anaemia causes thrombocytopenia, and low platelets lead to bleeding from mucous membranes.  Why A/D wrong: Platelet count is decreased, not increased; erythropoiesis is reduced, not increased.  Why B wrong: Decreased leukocytes predispose to infection, not directly to mucosal bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is listed as a \"group at risk\" for aplastic anaemia?","options":["People undergoing radiation or chemotherapy","People with high dietary iron","People with hyperthyroidism","People with frequent blood donations"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Persons exposed to radiation/chemotherapy, toxins, or who take certain medicines, and people with diseases that damage the bone marrow are at risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as risk groups for aplastic anaemia in the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Treatment of aplastic anaemia depends on the cause of the anaemia. Which treatments are listed?","options":["Blood transfusions, medicines, bone marrow/stem cell transplants, and lifestyle changes","Diuretics only","High-dose aspirin","Splenectomy alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Treatments include blood transfusions, medicines, bone marrow stem cell transplants, and lifestyle changes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed treatments for aplastic anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemolytic anaemia is caused by which of the following?","options":["Excessive iron storage","Excessive or premature destruction of RBCs","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Overproduction of platelets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemolytic anaemia results from excessive or premature destruction of RBCs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe iron overload, megaloblastic anaemia, and thrombocytosis respectively, not haemolytic anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is a recognised cause of haemolytic anaemia?","options":["Sickle cell anaemia, thalassemia, G-6-PD deficiency, and antigen-antibody reaction (infection and toxins)","Excess dietary folate","Splenic underactivity","Iron overload only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sickle cell anaemia, thalassemia, G-6-PD deficiency, and antigen-antibody reactions (infection/toxins) are causes of haemolytic anaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed causes of haemolytic anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which clinical feature is associated with haemolytic anaemia due to the breakdown of red blood cells?","options":["Decreased bilirubin level","Increased unconjugated serum bilirubin level","Increased reticulocytes and increased unconjugated serum bilirubin","Decreased reticulocytes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Haemolytic anaemia causes increased reticulocytes (compensatory) and increased unconjugated serum bilirubin from RBC breakdown; may cause jaundice or hyperbilirubinemia.  Why A/B/D wrong: Bilirubin rises (not falls) and reticulocytes increase; option C is the most complete correct combination, whereas B alone is incomplete."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Polycythemia vera is best described as which of the following?","options":["A decrease in all blood cells","A condition with an increase in the number of blood cells of leukocytes, erythrocytes, and thrombocytes","A deficiency of clotting factors","A bone marrow malignancy of platelets only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polycythemia vera is a condition with an increase in the number of blood cells — leukocytes, erythrocytes, and thrombocytes.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is an increase, not a decrease; it is not a clotting factor deficiency, and it involves all cell lines, not platelets only."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which clinical features are characteristic of polycythemia vera?","options":["Increased Hb level and purple-red complexion","Pallor and decreased Hb","Petechiae and bruising","Joint swelling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polycythemia vera features an increased Hb level and a purple-red complexion due to the excess of blood cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pallor/low Hb occur in anaemia; petechiae and joint swelling are features of bleeding/clotting disorders, not polycythemia vera."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which of the following are common clinical features of anaemias? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Tiredness or weakness","Paleness or pallor of skin and mucous membrane","Faintness or dizziness","Shortness of breath","Heart-related symptoms such as irregular heart rhythms, heart murmur, enlarged heart, and heart failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The common clinical features of anaemias listed include tiredness/weakness, paleness/pallor of skin and mucous membranes, faintness/dizziness, sweating, weak and rapid pulse with rapid breathing, shortness of breath, and heart-related symptoms (irregular rhythms, murmur, enlarged heart, heart failure).  All options are listed common features."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" note, what does the colour of the skin indicate in anaemia?","options":["The amount of haemoglobin only","The degree of skin pigmentation and the amount of fluid in the subcutaneous tissues","The platelet count","The leukocyte count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the colour of the skin depends upon the degree of skin pigmentation and the amount of fluid in the subcutaneous tissues.  Why A/C/D wrong: Skin colour as described depends on pigmentation and subcutaneous fluid, not directly on haemoglobin, platelets, or leukocytes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" note, pallor is best examined for in which areas?","options":["The trunk and abdomen","Palmar creases, nail bed, and mucous membranes","The scalp and hair","The lower extremities only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pallor is best examined in the palmar creases, nail bed, and mucous membranes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These sites are not recommended for assessing pallor in the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Beta-thalassemia major (Cooley's anaemia) is inherited in which pattern?","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-thalassemia major is an autosomal recessive disorder, also called Cooley's anaemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not dominant or X-linked; it follows autosomal recessive inheritance."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Beta-thalassemia major is characterised by the reduced production of which component?","options":["Platelets","One of the globin chains in the synthesis of haemoglobin, so both parents must be carriers to produce a child with beta-thalassemia major","Iron","White blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is characterised by reduced production of one of the globin chains in haemoglobin synthesis; both parents must be carriers to produce a child with beta-thalassemia major.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is a globin chain synthesis defect, not a platelet, iron, or WBC production problem."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the only treatment/supportive treatment available in beta-thalassemia major, and what is the consequence?","options":["Iron supplements alone, leading to anaemia","Blood transfusion; and the goal of therapy is to maintain a normal haemoglobin level by the administration of blood transfusions","Splenectomy alone","Vitamin C alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there is no any treatment; supportive treatment is initiated, and the goal of therapy is to maintain normal haemoglobin level by the administration of blood transfusions.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the primary supportive treatment described for beta-thalassemia major."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In the classification of thalassemia, which type is asymptomatic / silent carrier?","options":["Thalassemia minor","Thalassemia trait","Thalassemia intermedia","Thalassemia major"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thalassemia minor is asymptomatic / silent carrier state.  Why B/C/D wrong: Trait produces mild microcytic anaemia; intermedia is splenomegaly and moderate-to-severe anaemia; major produces severe anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which thalassemia classification produces mild microcytic anaemia?","options":["Thalassemia minor","Thalassemia trait","Thalassemia intermedia","Thalassemia major"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thalassemia trait produces mild microcytic anaemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: Minor is asymptomatic carrier; intermedia is splenomegaly with moderate-severe anaemia; major is the severest form."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which thalassemia classification is characterised by splenomegaly and moderate-to-severe anaemia?","options":["Thalassemia minor","Thalassemia trait","Thalassemia intermedia","Thalassemia major"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Thalassemia intermedia presents with splenomegaly and moderate-to-severe anaemia.  Why A/B/D wrong: These represent the carrier, mild trait, and severe major forms respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which severe form of thalassemia, also called Cooley's anaemia, requires regular blood transfusions to sustain life?","options":["Thalassemia minor","Thalassemia trait","Thalassemia intermedia","Thalassemia major"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Thalassemia major is the severest type; it requires regular blood transfusion to sustain life and is also called Cooley's anaemia.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are milder forms not requiring lifelong transfusions to sustain life."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is a sign/symptom of thalassemia?","options":["Severe anaemia, microcytic hypochromic red blood cells, fever, and headache","Hyperglycemia","Polyuria","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms include severe anaemia; microcytic, hypochromic red blood cells; fever and headache; impaired feeding; anorexia, enlarged abdomen, splenomegaly and hepatomegaly; impaired physical growth with wide-set eyes with a flattened nose; and exercise intolerance and bronze coloured skin due to hemosiderosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These metabolic/electrolyte findings are not the listed signs of thalassemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A child with thalassemia has wide-set eyes with a flattened nose and an enlarged abdomen. These reflect which set of clinical findings?","options":["Impaired physical growth, splenomegaly, and hepatomegaly","Hypertension","Clotting factor deficiency","Iron deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thalassemia causes impaired physical growth with wide-set eyes and a flattened nose, plus enlarged abdomen from splenomegaly and hepatomegaly.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source-listed manifestations behind these physical features."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Bronze-coloured skin in thalassemia (along with exercise intolerance) is due to which cause?","options":["Jaundice","Hemosiderosis (iron overload)","Cyanosis","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source attributes bronze coloured skin to hemosiderosis (iron overload from repeated transfusions).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically links bronze skin to hemosiderosis, not jaundice, cyanosis, or dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A key nursing care goal in thalassemia is to maintain adequate haemoglobin. Why?","options":["To increase blood pressure","Due to lifelong blood transfusion the body needs adequate haemoglobin to oxygenate the body tissues, specially spleen, liver, heart, and pancreas","To decrease platelet count","To prevent leukocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes blood transfusion to maintain adequate blood haemoglobin level, due to frequent blood transfusion, so adequate Hb may oxygenate the body tissues, specially spleen, liver, heart, and pancreas.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is tissue oxygenation, not affecting blood pressure, platelets, or leukocytes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In thalassemia, which chelation therapy (universally antidote) is administered to remove excess iron overload?","options":["Naloxone","Deferoxamine","Atropine","Flumazenil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hemochromatosis/cellular damage due to excessive iron storage is managed with the antidote for iron toxicity, deferoxamine; the source notes second-choice antidote chelation therapy (universally antidote).  Why A/C/D wrong: Naloxone is for opioids, atropine for cholinergic toxicity, flumazenil for benzodiazepines — none chelate iron."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"When a thalassemia client undergoes splenectomy, what is an important precaution regarding low iron diet?","options":["Continue a high iron diet","Maintain a low iron diet for polycythemia/anaemia management and ensure low iron diet for thalassemia","Stop all transfusions","Avoid all fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises a low iron diet for thalassemia (to limit iron overload from transfusions).  Why A/C/D wrong: A high iron diet worsens overload; stopping transfusions and avoiding fluids are not the dietary precaution described."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is a thalassemia client prepared for splenectomy as part of nursing care?","options":["To increase iron absorption","When severe splenomegaly is there, then prepare for splenectomy or prone to splenic injury","To raise the platelet count","To stop transfusions permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When severe splenomegaly is present, the client is prepared for splenectomy (also due to risk of splenic injury).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-stated indications for splenectomy in thalassemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"After splenectomy, which precaution is advised for the client with thalassemia?","options":["Encourage crowded gatherings","Low immune people should not be contacted to client to prevent infection","Stop antibiotics","Avoid all vaccines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because splenectomy increases infection risk, low immune people should not be in contact with the client to prevent infection (asplenic patients are more infection-prone).  Why A/C/D wrong: Crowded gatherings and stopping antibiotics increase infection risk; asplenic patients in fact require vaccination, not avoidance."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A nurse is monitoring a patient receiving a blood transfusion who develops a transfusion reaction. What is the immediate priority action?","options":["Increase the transfusion rate","Stop the transfusion (first action of the nurse) — if a transfusion reaction will be occurred","Continue transfusion and observe","Administer the next unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a transfusion reaction the immediate first action of the nurse is to stop the transfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Increasing or continuing the transfusion worsens the reaction; the priority is to stop it immediately."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why might a child receiving frequent transfusions (e.g., after BMT/HSCT) require chelation therapy?","options":["Due to low iron","Frequent transfusions raise the chance of Hepatitis-C (post-transfusion hepatitis) and lead to iron overload that initially may not affect the advised about BMT and hematopoietic stem cell transfusion (HSCT)","Due to vitamin C excess","Due to splenic enlargement only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes frequent transfusions cause Hepatitis-C (post-transfusion transfusion) and the child may have a chance of iron overload; BMT and hematopoietic stem cell transfusion (HSCT) are options.  Why A/C/D wrong: Iron overload (not low iron, vitamin C excess, or splenomegaly alone) drives the need for chelation in repeatedly transfused patients."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"To decrease post-splenectomy sepsis (PSS), the asplenic client is recommended to receive which three vaccines?","options":["Pneumococcal, Haemophilus influenzae type B, and Meningococcal group C vaccine","Rabies, tetanus, and BCG","Polio, measles, and rubella","Hepatitis A, B, and C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To decrease post-splenectomy sepsis (PSS), three vaccines are recommended: Pneumococcal vaccine, Haemophilus influenzae type B vaccine, and Meningococcal group C vaccine; an influenza vaccine is also advised.  Why B/C/D wrong: These vaccine combinations are not the recommended post-splenectomy prophylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"After splenectomy, why is lifelong prophylactic penicillin V 500 mg twice daily recommended?","options":["To treat thalassemia","To provide protection against penicillin-sensitive organisms in susceptible patients, e.g., immunocompromised patients, sickle cell disease patients, or those with a macrolide allergy","To increase iron absorption","To raise the haemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lifelong prophylactic penicillin V 500 mg twice daily is recommended for protection in penicillin-sensitive (susceptible) patients; in penicillin-allergic patients a macrolide is used.  Why A/C/D wrong: This is antibiotic prophylaxis against infection, not a treatment for thalassemia, iron, or Hb levels."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Patients who have undergone splenectomy should be advised about which travel-related risk?","options":["The risk of altitude sickness","The risk of severe malaria is increased in the event of infection","The risk of motion sickness","The risk of sunburn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asplenic patients should be advised that the risk of severe malaria is increased in the event of infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-stated travel risks for asplenic patients."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemophilia is a bleeding disorder resulting from deficiency of which component?","options":["Platelets","Specific clotting factors","Iron","Erythropoietin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemophilia is a bleeding disorder resulting from deficiency of specific clotting factors.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is a clotting factor deficiency, not a platelet, iron, or hormone deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemophilia is inherited in which pattern?","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive disorder","X-linked dominant"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Haemophilia is an example of an X-linked recessive disorder.  Why A/B/D wrong: It is X-linked recessive, not autosomal or X-linked dominant."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In which population does haemophilia (in this X-linked recessive pattern) occur as carriers in females?","options":["Males are carriers","Females are carriers in this disorder","Only children","Only elderly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes females are carriers in this disorder (X-linked recessive haemophilia).  Why A/C/D wrong: In X-linked recessive haemophilia, females are typically carriers, not affected males/children/elderly as carriers."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemophilia-A is due to deficiency of which clotting factor and is also called what?","options":["Factor IX; Christmas disease","Factor VIII; also called Classic Haemophilia & Royal disease","von Willebrand factor; von Willebrand disease","Factor VII; proconvertin deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemophilia-A is due to deficiency of clotting factor-VIII and is also called Classic Haemophilia & Royal disease.  Why A wrong: Factor IX deficiency is Haemophilia-B (Christmas disease).  Why C/D wrong: These describe von Willebrand disease and Factor VII deficiency, not Haemophilia-A."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemophilia-B is due to deficiency of which clotting factor and is also known by what name?","options":["Factor VIII; Classic Haemophilia","Factor IX; also known as Christmas disease","Factor VII; Royal disease","von Willebrand factor; vWD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemophilia-B is due to deficiency of clotting factor-IX and is also known as Christmas disease.  Why A/C/D wrong: Factor VIII deficiency is Haemophilia-A; Factor VII and vWF deficiencies are different disorders."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A hallmark sign of haemophilia following trauma or surgery is which of the following?","options":["Polyuria","Abnormal bleeding in response to trauma and surgery","Hyperpigmentation","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms of haemophilia include abnormal bleeding in response to trauma and surgery, epistaxis or nose bleeds, and joint bleeding (classical sign) causing pain, swelling, tenderness, and limited activities.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not signs of haemophilia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Joint bleeding in haemophilia is described as a classical sign. Which manifestations accompany it?","options":["Pain, swelling, tenderness, and limited activities","Fever and rash","Polyuria and thirst","Cough and dyspnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Joint bleeding (classical sign) causes pain, swelling, tenderness, and limited activities.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the joint bleeding manifestations described."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is an appropriate nursing care measure in haemophilia?","options":["Encourage contact sports","Monitor for bleeding and maintain bleeding precaution","Give intramuscular injections freely","Avoid all clotting factors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring for bleeding and maintaining bleeding precautions, preparing to administer clotting factors as per order, and DDAVP (1-Deamino-8-D-Arginine vasopressin), a synthetic form of vasopressin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contact sports and IM injections increase bleeding risk; clotting factors are needed, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"DDAVP (1-Deamino-8-D-Arginine vasopressin), a synthetic form of vasopressin, increases plasma Factor VIII. It is used for what duration of mild haemophilia bleeding?","options":["15 minutes for superficial bleeding","24 hours","1 week","Permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DDAVP increases plasma factor-VIII and is used 15 minutes for superficial bleeding in mild haemophilia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15 minutes for superficial bleeding, not the longer durations."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"During a bleeding crisis in haemophilia, in addition to administering clotting factors, what supportive measure is recommended?","options":["Apply pressure for at least 5 minutes","Apply pressure for at least 15 minutes for superficial bleeding","Elevate, immobilize, and apply pressure for at least 15 minutes for superficial bleeding, and monitor urine for haematuria","Avoid pressure entirely"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises immobilisation, elevation, and application of ice on the extremities; apply pressure for at least 15 minutes for superficial bleeding; and monitor urine for haematuria during bleeding crisis (elevate head of bed, support joints).  Why A/B/D wrong: Option C is the complete measure; pressure for at least 15 minutes (not 5) plus immobilization/elevation/ice and haematuria monitoring is correct."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Von Willebrand disease is a bleeding disorder that occurs in which population?","options":["Only in females","In both genders (occurs in both)","Only in males","Only in elderly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Von Willebrand disease is a bleeding disorder that occurs in both genders.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not limited to a single sex or age group."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Von Willebrand disease is caused by deficiency of clotting factor von Willebrand factor, which is a carrier protein for which clotting factor?","options":["Factor IX","Factor VIII","Factor VII","Factor X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: vWD is caused by deficiency of von Willebrand factor (vWF), which is a carrier protein for Factor-VIII.  Why A/C/D wrong: vWF is the carrier for Factor VIII specifically, not the other factors."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which are clinical features of von Willebrand disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Epistaxis","Gum bleeding","Easy bruising","Excessive menstrual bleeding","Joint contractures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features of vWD include epistaxis, gum bleeding, easy bruising, and excessive menstrual bleeding.  Why E wrong: Joint contractures are not listed as a vWD clinical feature."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Sickle cell anaemia is genetically inherited in which pattern?","options":["Autosomal recessive disorder that affects haemoglobin synthesis","X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","Acquired"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sickle cell anaemia is an example of autosomal recessive disorder that affects haemoglobin synthesis.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is autosomal recessive, not X-linked, dominant, or acquired."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemoglobin-A is known as which type of haemoglobin?","options":["Fetal haemoglobin","Adult haemoglobin","Sickle haemoglobin","Embryonic haemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemoglobin-A is known as adult haemoglobin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Haemoglobin-F is fetal haemoglobin; HbS is sickle haemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Haemoglobin-F is known as which type of haemoglobin?","options":["Adult haemoglobin","Fetal haemoglobin (more oxygen-binding capacity to absorb oxygen with adult haemoglobin)","Sickle haemoglobin","Methaemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemoglobin-F is known as fetal haemoglobin and has more oxygen-binding capacity to absorb oxygen than adult haemoglobin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hb-A is adult, HbS is sickle haemoglobin, methaemoglobin is a different abnormal form."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In sickle cell disorder, Haemoglobin-A is partially or completely replaced by which haemoglobin?","options":["Haemoglobin-S (HbS) or amino acid valine replaces glutamic acid in the beta chain of the haemoglobin","Haemoglobin-F","Methaemoglobin","Haemoglobin-C only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In sickle cell disorder, Haemoglobin-A is partially or completely replaced by Haemoglobin-S (HbS), or amino acid valine replaces glutamic acid in the beta chain of the haemoglobin.  Why B/C/D wrong: The replacement is by HbS via valine-for-glutamic acid substitution in the beta chain."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"HbS (sickle haemoglobin) is sensitive to changes in which factor in the RBC?","options":["Glucose level","Oxygen level (oxygen tension)","Sodium level","Calcium level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemoglobin-S (HbS) is sensitive to changes in the oxygen level in the RBC; defective haemoglobin causes RBCs to become sickle shaped when insufficient oxygen is present, obstructing the normal capillary blood flow.  Why A/C/D wrong: HbS sickling is triggered by low oxygen tension, not glucose, sodium, or calcium changes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why does the new-born baby with sickle cell disease have a high level of fetal haemoglobin preventing sickling, and when does the sickling episode begin?","options":["Sickling begins at birth","During the new-born period, a high level of Hb level prevents sickling and the number of sickling episode increase; sickling episode usually starts around 4 to 6 months of age, when fetal haemoglobin is completely replaced by adult haemoglobin","Sickling never occurs","Sickling begins at age 12 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During the new-born period high fetal Hb prevents sickling; fetal haemoglobin completely replaced by adult haemoglobin around 4 to 6 months of age, when sickling episodes begin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sickling typically begins around 4–6 months as HbF declines, not at birth, never, or at 12 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which factors precipitate a sickling episode that enhances RBC sickling? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Dehydration","Emotional or physical stress","Conditions that increase the need or alter the transport of oxygen","High fetal haemoglobin","Adequate hydration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Precipitating factors that enhance sickling include dehydration and emotional or physical stress, or any conditions that increase the need for or alter the transport of oxygen.  Why D/E wrong: High fetal haemoglobin and adequate hydration prevent rather than precipitate sickling."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Arrange the pathophysiology of sickle cell anaemia from the diagram. What is the consequence of vaso-occlusion in sickle cell disease?","options":["Increased oxygen delivery","Vascular occlusion, organ ischemia, and end-organ damage","Decreased polymerization of haemoglobin","Improved capillary flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pathophysiology pathway leads from deoxygenation, polymerization of haemoglobin, sickling of red cells, endothelial damage/activation, RBC and leukocyte adhesion to endothelium, and vasoconstriction, to vascular occlusion, organ ischemia, and end-organ damage.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sickling causes occlusion and ischemia, not improved oxygen delivery or flow."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which test is the screening test for sickle cell diseases?","options":["Haemoglobin electrophoresis test","Sickle turbidity test","Serum ferritin","Peripheral smear only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sickle turbidity test is the screening test for sickle cell diseases.  Why A wrong: Haemoglobin electrophoresis test is the confirmative (not screening) test.  Why C/D wrong: Serum ferritin and a smear alone are not the designated screening test."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which test is the confirmative test for sickle cell disease?","options":["Sickle turbidity test","Haemoglobin electrophoresis test","Complete blood count","Reticulocyte count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemoglobin electrophoresis test is the confirmative test for sickle cell disease.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sickle turbidity is the screening test; CBC and reticulocyte count are supportive but not confirmatory."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A patient with sickle cell disease develops sequestration crisis. What characterises this crisis?","options":["Splenic infarction only","Large amount of blood collects in the spleen, causing suddenly drop in blood pressure and shock, resulting in multiple splenic infarctions","Improved splenic function","Increased blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sequestration crisis: a large amount of blood collects in the spleen, causing a sudden drop in blood pressure and shock, resulting in multiple splenic infarctions.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is more than infarction alone; it causes a blood pressure drop and shock, not improved function or hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What are signs and symptoms of sequestration crisis in sickle cell disease?","options":["Polyuria and dehydration only","Hypovolemia and hypotension shock","Hypertension and bradycardia","Fever and rash"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of sequestration crisis include hypovolemia and hypotension/shock.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the hypovolemic hypotensive picture of sequestration crisis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A child in sequestration crisis is hypovolemic. What is the priority action?","options":["Restrict all fluids","Give more IV fluids to restore fluid volume","Administer a diuretic","Withhold oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The priority action in sequestration crisis is to give more IV fluids to restore fluid volume (due to hypovolemia/hypotension).  Why A/C/D wrong: Restricting fluids or giving diuretics worsens hypovolemia; oxygen should be supported, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In aplastic crisis of sickle cell disease, what is the priority action?","options":["Aggressive transfusion immediately","Conservative management","Splenectomy","High-dose diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The priority action listed for aplastic crisis is conservative management (signs/symptoms include profound anaemia and pallor).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source designates conservative priority action for aplastic crisis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Acute chest syndrome in sickle cell disease is caused by what?","options":["A pulmonary infiltrate due to obstruction of sickling cells in the microstructure of lungs, and prevents the symptoms occurred","A bacterial pneumonia only","Splenic infarction","Pulmonary embolism from clots"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute chest syndrome is caused by a pulmonary infiltrate due to obstruction by sickling cells in the microstructure of lungs, preventing the symptoms (signs/symptoms: chest pain, fever, cough, tachypnoea, wheezing, hypoxia).  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a sickling-related vaso-occlusion of the pulmonary microvasculature, not simple pneumonia, splenic infarction, or thromboembolism per the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of acute chest syndrome in sickle cell disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chest pain","Fever","Cough","Tachypnoea, wheezing, and hypoxia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of acute chest syndrome include chest pain, fever, cough, tachypnoea, wheezing, and hypoxia.  Why E wrong: Hypertension is not listed as a sign of acute chest syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"The priority action in acute chest syndrome of sickle cell disease is which of the following?","options":["Restrict fluids","Semi to Fowler position, oxygen, and IV fluids","Immediate splenectomy","High-dose diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority actions in acute chest syndrome are semi to Fowler position, oxygen, and IV fluids.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed priority interventions for acute chest syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In vaso-occlusive crisis of sickle cell disease, what occurs?","options":["Increased oxygen to tissues","It is a severe excessive condition caused by diminished production and increased destruction of RBC's; destruction of RBC's precipitate by virus infection or depletion of folic acid","Improved capillary blood flow","Splenic enlargement only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a severe excessive condition caused by diminished production and increased destruction of RBCs; destruction of RBCs precipitated by virus infection or depletion of folic acid (signs/symptoms: profound anaemia and pallor; priority action conservative).  Why A/C/D wrong: Crisis causes vaso-occlusion/anaemia, not improved oxygenation, flow, or isolated splenomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Brain attack or CVA (stroke) in sickle cell crisis occurs by which mechanism?","options":["Increased dietary iron","It is a cerebral vascular accident due to sickled cells that block blood vessels in the brain","Excess hydration","High fetal haemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brain attack/CVA is a cerebral vascular accident due to sickled cells that block blood vessels in the brain (signs & symptoms: depends on involved part of the brain).  Why A/C/D wrong: It is due to vaso-occlusion by sickled cells, not iron, hydration, or HbF."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the priority action in brain attack/CVA during sickle cell crisis?","options":["Restrict fluids","Oxygen administration","Immediate splenectomy","High-dose diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority actions in brain attack/CVA include oxygen administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed priority interventions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is an appropriate nursing care measure during a sickle cell crisis?","options":["Restrict fluids strictly","Provide adequate hydration during crisis through IV or orally as per order because without adequate hydration pain will not be controlled","Withhold all oxygen","Avoid analgesics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes providing adequate hydration during crisis through IV or orally as per order, because without adequate hydration pain will not be controlled; administer oxygen and BT to increase tissue perfusion and exchange transfusions which reduce the number of circulating sickle cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: Restricting fluids, withholding oxygen, and avoiding analgesics are contraindicated during crisis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is increasing fluid intake recommended in sickle cell crisis nursing care?","options":["To raise blood pressure","To avoid dehydration and prevent rapid thrombus formation","To decrease oxygen delivery","To promote sickling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increasing fluid intake helps avoid dehydration that causes stress on the body, like sickle, prevents rapid thrombus formation, and supports against rapid blood haemostasis (avoid internal bleeding); the source notes increase fluid intake to avoid dehydration to prevent rapid thrombus formation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hydration prevents dehydration and sickling/thrombosis, not raises BP, decreases oxygen, or promotes sickling."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"When managing a sickle cell crisis with severe pain, which medications may be ordered?","options":["Iron supplements","Corticosteroids and cyclosporine if ordered","Diuretics only","Antihistamines only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes administering corticosteroids and cyclosporine if ordered, providing effective analgesia during crisis (severe head of bed, support joints, move carefully while supporting because joints have more chance of internal bleeding).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed crisis medications."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Iron deficiency anaemia (IDA) is best described as which of the following?","options":["An inadequate intake of iron or a deficiency of iron in blood resulting in decrease supply of iron for the formation of haemoglobin in red blood cells (RBC's)","Excess iron storage","Clotting factor deficiency","White cell deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IDA is an inadequate intake of iron or a deficiency of iron in blood resulting in a decreased supply of iron for the formation of haemoglobin in RBCs; it is microcytic and hypochromic.  Why B/C/D wrong: IDA is an iron deficiency, not iron overload, clotting factor, or WBC problem."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"IDA is described as which morphological type of anaemia?","options":["Macrocytic and hyperchromic","Microcytic and hypochromic","Normocytic and normochromic","Macrocytic and normochromic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IDA is microcytic and hypochromic blood RBC.  Why A/C/D wrong: IDA presents with small (microcytic), pale (hypochromic) red cells."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is a confirmative diagnostic test for iron deficiency anaemia?","options":["Decreasing serum ferritin test (confirmative diagnosis)","Sickle turbidity test","Coagulation profile","Haemoglobin electrophoresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A decreasing serum ferritin test is the confirmative diagnosis for IDA.  Why B/C/D wrong: These tests diagnose sickle cell disease, clotting disorders, and haemoglobinopathies, not IDA."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Infants who are breast-fed have a low chance of iron deficiency for how long?","options":["The first 2 months","The first 5 to 6 months of age","The first 2 years","The first 12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In infants who are breast-fed there is a low chance of iron deficiency for the first 5 to 6 months of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the first 5 to 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What dietary risk increases the chance of developing iron deficiency anaemia in a child receiving cow's milk?","options":["Cow's milk is rich in iron","Child receiving only breast milk (milk babies) and milk is not a good source of iron, so more chance of developing iron deficiency anaemia","Cow's milk prevents anaemia","Cow's milk has excess iron"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A child receiving only breast milk (milk babies) — and milk is not a good source of iron — has a higher chance of developing iron deficiency anaemia; microcytic anaemia is due to iron deficiency.  Why A/C/D wrong: Milk is a poor source of iron, so excessive milk consumption increases IDA risk."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of iron deficiency anaemia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pallor","Weakness, tachycardia, dizziness","Low haemoglobin and haematocrit level (usually below 30%)","Baby may under-weight and slow bone development","Increased clotting factors"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms of IDA include pallor; weakness, tachycardia, dizziness; low haemoglobin and haematocrit level (usually below 30%); and the baby may be under-weight with slow bone development.  Why E wrong: Increased clotting factors is not a feature of IDA."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"When should iron intake be increased in a child with iron deficiency anaemia, per nursing care?","options":["Increase oral intake of iron from non-fortified formula and start at age of 6 months of baby","Increase oral intake of iron from fortified formula and start at age of 4 months of baby","Never increase iron intake","Only after 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care advises to increase oral intake of iron from fortified formula and start at age of 4 months of baby.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies fortified formula started at 4 months, not 6 months, never, or after 1 year."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"When instructing parents of an older child with IDA, which dietary advice is appropriate?","options":["Avoid all iron-rich foods","Offer iron foods such as green leaf vegetables, and instruct the parents for more offer of iron-rich foods to baby after 6 months such as green-leaf vegetables","Give only milk","Avoid solid foods"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the parents to offer iron-rich foods to the baby after 6 months, such as green-leaf vegetables.  Why A/C/D wrong: Iron-rich foods are encouraged; milk-only and avoiding solids increase IDA risk."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which side effects of oral iron supplements should be expected and monitored in IDA?","options":["Constipation, black stools, nausea, and vomiting","Diarrhea and pale stools only","Hypoglycemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expected side effects of iron supplements are constipation, black stools, nausea, and vomiting.  Why B/C/D wrong: Iron causes dark/black (not pale) stools and constipation, not hypoglycemia or hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"When administering oral iron supplements, what instruction reduces staining of teeth?","options":["Use a straw or liquid iron preparation; it prevents staining of teeth (with iron)","Give iron with milk","Give iron on an empty stomach with no fluids","Give iron at bedtime only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises using a straw (or liquid iron preparation) to prevent staining of teeth with iron.  Why B/C/D wrong: Milk decreases iron absorption; the staining precaution specifically uses a straw/liquid form."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Iron supplements should be administered at what time relative to meals, per the source?","options":["With meals","Administer iron supplements as per order (and ferrous sulphate is the most given iron preparation)","Only at bedtime with milk","Never with vitamin C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to administer iron supplements as per order; ferrous sulphate is given because it is the most common iron preparation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Iron absorption is enhanced by vitamin C (not avoided) and reduced by milk; administration follows the order."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"If severe anaemia is present, by which route is iron administered?","options":["Always orally","Iron injection through IM using Z-track technique or IV as per order","Subcutaneously only","Topically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If severe anaemia is there, iron is administered via injection through IM using the Z-track technique or IV as per order.  Why A/C/D wrong: For severe anaemia parenteral iron (IM Z-track or IV) is used, not oral/SC/topical."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is the Z-track technique recommended when giving IM iron (ferrous sulphate)?","options":["To increase absorption through milk","Given orally ferrous sulphate tab is the most common iron preparation; Z-track is used because it is most beneficial as it minimises staining of skin and tissues (with iron leaking back)","To speed up absorption","To dilute the iron"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ferrous sulphate is the most common iron preparation; the Z-track technique is used because it prevents the iron from leaking back/staining the skin and subcutaneous tissue.  Why A/C/D wrong: The Z-track purpose is to prevent staining/leakage, not those other reasons."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What instruction reduces gastrointestinal upset when multivitamins/iron are given with fruit juices?","options":["Give with multivitamins, which should be offered because Vita-C increased absorption of iron","Give with milk to increase absorption","Give with antacids","Give without any food"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes giving iron with multivitamins should be offered because Vita-C (vitamin C) increases absorption of iron.  Why B/C/D wrong: Milk and antacids decrease iron absorption; vitamin C enhances it."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why should iron supplements not be given concurrently with milk or antacids?","options":["They increase iron absorption","They decrease absorption of iron","They cause black stools","They cause hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states not to give concurrently milk or antacids with iron supplements because these foods decrease absorption of iron.  Why A/C/D wrong: Milk/antacids reduce (not increase) iron absorption; black stools and hypertension are unrelated to this instruction."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Poisoning is best defined as which of the following?","options":["Ingestion of any harmful substance or any normal substance in excess amount, which is called poisoning","A genetic blood disorder","A clotting factor deficiency","A vitamin deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poisoning is the ingestion of any harmful substance or any normal substance in excess amount, which is called poisoning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe haematological disorders, not poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which is the most common reason of poisoning, and which is the most common substance involved?","options":["Kerosene poisoning is the most common; insecticide is the most common substance","Alcohol is the most common","Iron tablets are the most common","Paracetamol is the most common"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common reason of poisoning is kerosene poisoning, and the most common substance is insecticide.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source names kerosene/insecticide as most common."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"All poisoning cases are considered as which medicolegal status, and how is age handled?","options":["All poisoning cases are considered as Medicolegal Cases (MLC); if the victim is less than 11 years, it is treated as accidental poisoning; if more than 13 years, it is treated as suicidal attempt","Never medicolegal","Only adults are medicolegal","Only suicidal cases are medicolegal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All poisoning cases are considered as Medicolegal Cases (MLC); if the victim is less than 11 years it is treated as accidental poisoning, and if more than 13 years it is treated as suicidal attempt.  Why B/C/D wrong: All poisoning cases are MLC regardless of age/intent per the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What does the universal symbol of skull-and-crossbones represent?","options":["A symbol consisting of a human skull and two bones crossed together behind the skull, used as a warning sign of poisonous substances","A radiation hazard","A biohazard","An electrical hazard"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The skull-and-crossbones symbol consists of a human skull and two bones crossed together behind the skull, used as a warning sign of poisonous substances (Fig. Poison symbol).  Why B/C/D wrong: The skull-and-crossbones specifically denotes poison."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"According to the general principles of poisoning resuscitation, what is assessed first?","options":["Rapidly assess the airway, breathing, and circulation","Administer oxygen first","Obtain intravenous access first","Give activated charcoal first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: General principles—Resuscitation: rapidly assess the airway, breathing, and circulation, administer oxygen, obtain intravenous access, and give intravenous fluids if the patient is hypotensive.  Why B/C/D wrong: These follow the initial ABC assessment."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is oxygen given in poisoning even if there is no obvious cyanosis?","options":["Oxygen should be avoided in poisoning patients","Oxygen should be given because cyanosis may be a late and unreliable sign of hypoxia, and may increase toxicity","Oxygen increases poison absorption","Oxygen is only for cardiac arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes oxygen should be given because cyanosis is a late and unreliable sign of hypoxia in poisoning patients.  Why A/C/D wrong: Oxygen is given proactively, not avoided or restricted, because cyanosis appears late."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the best method of preventing absorption of ingested poisons in gastric decontamination?","options":["Gastric lavage","The best method of preventing absorption of ingested poisons is the use of activated charcoal","Whole bowel irrigation","Inducing vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gastric decontamination — the best method of preventing absorption of ingested poisons is the use of activated charcoal.  Why A/C/D wrong: Activated charcoal is the preferred method per the source over lavage, irrigation, or emesis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the recommended dose of activated charcoal?","options":["0.5 gram per kg","Give activated charcoal 1 gram per kg (to a maximum of 50 grams) orally or via nasogastric tube","5 grams per kg","100 grams per kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Give activated charcoal 1 gram per kg (to a maximum of 50 grams) orally or via nasogastric tube.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 1 g/kg up to a maximum of 50 g."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In an unconscious or drowsy poisoning patient, what must be done before insertion of a nasogastric tube and administration of activated charcoal?","options":["Nothing special","Patients who are unable to protect their airway (e.g., excessive drowsiness) should be intubated BEFORE insertion of a nasogastric tube and administration of activated charcoal","Give charcoal first then intubate","Give the charcoal IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patients who are unable to protect their airway (excessive drowsiness) should be intubated BEFORE insertion of a nasogastric tube and administration of activated charcoal (to prevent aspiration).  Why A/C/D wrong: Airway protection (intubation) must precede charcoal; charcoal is not given IV."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Gastric lavage should be performed only in which circumstances?","options":["Routinely for all poisonings","Only in exceptional circumstances, such as recent ingestion of large doses of paracetamol","Always within 24 hours","Never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gastric lavage should be performed only in exceptional circumstances such as recent ingestion of large doses of paracetamol.  Why A/C/D wrong: Lavage is reserved for exceptional cases, not routine, all-times, or never."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Gastric lavage is contraindicated in the ingestion of which substances?","options":["Hydrocarbons (kerosene, petroleum), caustics, and corrosives","Iron tablets","Paracetamol","Aspirin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gastric lavage is contraindicated in ingestion of hydrocarbons (kerosene, petroleum), caustics, and corrosives (risk of aspiration and further injury).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed contraindications; hydrocarbons/caustics/corrosives are."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In paraquat poisoning, why is administration of activated charcoal easiest, safest, and most effective?","options":["Because activated charcoal has no role","Because Fuller's earth (bleaching clay), if available, should be substituted for activated charcoal as the most effective method of decontamination of the gut at almost all situations","Because paraquat is not absorbed","Because charcoal is contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in paraquat poisoning, Fuller's earth (bleaching clay), if available, should be substituted for activated charcoal as the most effective method of decontamination of the gut at almost all situations.  Why A/C/D wrong: In paraquat poisoning Fuller's earth is preferred/substituted, and decontamination is effective."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Supportive care in poisoning includes which of the following?","options":["Discontinue observation","Continued observation and the provision of oxygen, intravenous fluids, and airway support as required","Withhold all fluids","Avoid monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Supportive care includes continued observation and the provision of oxygen, intravenous fluids, and airway support as required, with knowledge of the pharmacologic effects of the substance ingested allowing anticipation of possible problems; specific antidote administration as ordered.  Why A/C/D wrong: Supportive care emphasizes continued observation and support, not discontinuing care."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Paracetamol/Acetaminophen poisoning results from what?","options":["Excessive ingestion of paracetamol/acetaminophen","Iron deficiency","Lead exposure","Aspirin overdose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCM/Acetaminophen poisoning is due to excessive ingestion of paracetamol/acetaminophen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other poisonings/deficiencies, not paracetamol poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the therapeutic dose of paracetamol (PCM)?","options":["< 50 kg: 15 mg/kg and > 50 kg: 650 mg/4 hrs","100 mg/kg always","5 mg/kg","500 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic dose of PCM: < 50 kg = 15 mg/kg, > 50 kg = 650 mg/4 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated therapeutic doses."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the toxic dose of PCM (paracetamol)?","options":["> 150 mg/kg/day","15 mg/kg/day","5 mg/kg/day","50 mg/kg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Toxic dose of PCM is > 150 mg/kg/day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are sub-toxic/therapeutic ranges, not the toxic threshold."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of paracetamol poisoning? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Diaphoresis and malaise","Conditions of the patient become improve within 48 to 72 hours if the hepatic involvement doesn't occurs","If hepatic involvement occurs: right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, increased SGPT & SGPT and prothrombin time","Black-tar stools"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms of PCM poisoning include nausea & vomiting, diaphoresis and malaise; the condition improves within 48 to 72 hours if hepatic involvement doesn't occur; if hepatic involvement occurs there is right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, and increased SGPT & SGOT (transaminases) and prothrombin time.  Why E wrong: Black-tar stools are an iron side effect, not a paracetamol poisoning sign."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A patient with paracetamol poisoning is vomiting and conscious. What is the first nursing priority action?","options":["Induce vomiting if the patient is conscious","Gastric lavage can be performed","Give activated charcoal IV","Withhold all care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for PCM poisoning: induce vomiting if the patient is conscious; gastric lavage can be performed; and giving activated charcoal as per order (this is the first priority listed).  Why B wrong: Gastric lavage is listed but emesis in a conscious patient is the first priority per the source ordering.  Why C/D wrong: Charcoal is given orally/NG, not IV, and care is not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is activated charcoal given in paracetamol poisoning, and why is timing important?","options":["Because charcoal decreases the absorption of the poison (first priority); but it must be given because it is known as a poison absorbant and it decreases absorption of the chemicals","Because charcoal increases absorption","Because charcoal is an antidote","Because it raises liver enzymes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Activated charcoal is given because it decreases the absorption of the poison (first priority) as a poison absorbant, decreasing absorption of the chemicals; give concerned antidotes as per order.  Why B/C/D wrong: Charcoal decreases (not increases) absorption, is an adsorbent (not the antidote), and does not raise enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the antidote of PCM (paracetamol) poisoning?","options":["Naloxone","N-Acetylcysteine (Mucomyst)","Atropine","Deferoxamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: N-Acetylcysteine (Mucomyst) is the antidote of PCM poisoning.  Why A/C/D wrong: Naloxone (opioids), atropine (cholinergic/organophosphate), and deferoxamine (iron) treat other poisonings."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the IV dosing/loading regimen of N-Acetylcysteine for paracetamol poisoning?","options":["Give acetylcysteine 150 mg per kg IV over 15 minutes, then give 50 mg per kg IV over 4 hours, then 100 mg per kg IV over 16 hours","15 mg/kg once daily","5 mg/kg single dose","500 mg/kg once"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Give acetylcysteine 150 mg per kg IV over 15 minutes, then 50 mg per kg IV over 4 hours, then 100 mg per kg IV over 16 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated NAC regimen."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"If the syrup form (oral N-acetylcysteine) is used and the patient previously assessed that activated charcoal should not be used, why?","options":["Because the syrup form should not be used because activated charcoal would interfere/affect the absorption of the antidote in the N-acetylcysteine","Because syrup is too sweet","Because syrup is contraindicated in adults","Because syrup increases liver damage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the syrup form is used and the patient previously assessed that activated charcoal should not be used because it would affect the absorption of the antidote N-acetylcysteine.  Why B/C/D wrong: The concern is charcoal adsorbing/reducing antidote absorption, not taste, age, or liver damage."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"A key action of N-acetylcysteine in management besides being the antidote is which of the following?","options":["It decreases excretion of the drugs","It increases the excretion of the drugs","It causes constipation","It raises blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes N-acetylcysteine increases the excretion of the drugs.  Why A/C/D wrong: It enhances (not decreases) excretion; constipation/hypertension are not its noted effects."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Therapeutic Drug Levels table, what is the therapeutic range of Digoxin?","options":["0.5–2 ng/mL","15–25 mg/mL","10–20 mg/mL","50–125 mg/dL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic drug levels table lists Digoxin therapeutic range as 0.5–2 ng/mL.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to other drugs (Lithium, Theophylline/Phenytoin, Aspirin/Salicylate ranges)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Therapeutic Drug Levels table, which drug uses the antidote Digoxin Immune FAB?","options":["Lithium","Digoxin","Phenytoin","Aspirin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Antidote & Reversal Agents table pairs Digoxin with Digoxin Immune FAB.  Why A/C/D wrong: Lithium, phenytoin, and aspirin do not use Digoxin Immune FAB."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Match the antidote/reversal agent to its poison from the Antidote & Reversal Agents table. The antidote for Acetaminophen is which agent?","options":["Naloxone","N-Acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Antidote & Reversal Agents table pairs Acetaminophen with N-Acetylcysteine.  Why A/C/D wrong: Naloxone is for opioids, flumazenil for benzodiazepines, calcium gluconate for magnesium."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, what is the antidote/reversal agent for Benzodiazepine?","options":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Atropine","Methylene blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benzodiazepine is paired with Flumazenil in the table.  Why A/C/D wrong: Naloxone (opioid), atropine (organophosphate/carbamate cholinergic), and methylene blue (methaemoglobinemia) are different antidotes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, methylene blue is the antidote for which condition?","options":["Opioid overdose","Methaemoglobinemia","Iron poisoning","Heparin overdose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs Methaemoglobinemia with Methylene blue.  Why A/C/D wrong: Opioids use naloxone, iron uses deferoxamine, heparin uses protamine."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, the reversal agent for Heparin is which of the following?","options":["Vitamin K (Phytonadione)","Protamine","Calcium gluconate","Deferoxamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin is paired with Protamine in the reversal agents table.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K (Phytonadione) reverses Warfarin.  Why C/D wrong: Calcium gluconate is for magnesium toxicity; deferoxamine for iron."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, the antidote for Warfarin is which of the following?","options":["Protamine","Phytonadione (Vitamin K)","Naloxone","Atropine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin is paired with Phytonadione (Vitamin K) in the table.  Why A/C/D wrong: Protamine reverses heparin, naloxone opioids, atropine cholinergic toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, the antidote for Iron is which agent?","options":["Deferoxamine","Succimer","Penicillamine","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iron poisoning is treated with Deferoxamine per the table (and confirmed in thalassemia iron-overload management).  Why B/C/D wrong: Succimer/penicillamine are for lead; calcium gluconate for magnesium."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, the antidote for Lead is which agent?","options":["Succimer (and penicillamine)","Naloxone","Flumazenil","Protamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lead is paired with Succimer (with penicillamine also a chelator) in the table.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are antidotes for opioids, benzodiazepines, and heparin respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"From the Antidote & Reversal Agents table, calcium gluconate is the antidote for which agent?","options":["Iron","Magnesium (and fluoride)","Opioids","Warfarin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs Magnesium with Calcium gluconate.  Why A/C/D wrong: Iron uses deferoxamine, opioids naloxone, warfarin vitamin K."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Aspirin poisoning involves which substance, also known as acetyl salicylic acid?","options":["Aspirin (acetyl salicylic acid)","Paracetamol","Lead","Iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin is also known as acetyl salicylic acid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different poisons with their own antidotes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the acute (toxic) poisoning dose of aspirin?","options":["If aspirin taken 300–500 mg/kg single time","15 mg/kg","5 mg/kg","50 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute poisoning occurs if aspirin is taken 300–500 mg/kg single time.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated acute toxic dose."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What dose defines chronic aspirin poisoning?","options":["If aspirin taken >100 mg/kg for more than 2 days","15 mg/kg for 1 day","5 mg/kg single dose","650 mg once"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic poisoning occurs if aspirin is taken >100 mg/kg for more than 2 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the chronic poisoning definition."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which are early signs and symptoms of aspirin poisoning? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Headache","Hearing loss (effects of hearing & vision)","Black-tar stools","Polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Initial signs & symptoms of aspirin poisoning include nausea & vomiting, headache, and (later) disturbance of hearing & vision (tinnitus, dizziness). Later/electrolyte disturbances follow.  Why D/E wrong: Black-tar stools (iron) and polyuria are not the listed initial salicylate symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What acid-base disturbance occurs in aspirin (salicylate) poisoning at the later stage?","options":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis, because aspirin is acidic in nature","Respiratory alkalosis only","No acid-base change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A later sign/symptom of aspirin poisoning is metabolic acidosis, because aspirin is acidic in nature.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies metabolic acidosis due to aspirin's acidic nature."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which electrolyte disturbances occur in aspirin (salicylate) poisoning? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypernatraemia","Hypokalaemia","Hypocalcaemia","Hyperglycaemia","Hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Electrolyte disturbances listed include hypernatraemia, hypokalaemia, and hypocalcaemia (along with tinnitus, dizziness disturbance of hearing & vision; oliguria; confusion, decreased conscious level & disorientation; seizures and coma due to CNS involvement; increased bleeding tendency at late stage).  Why D/E wrong: Hyperglycaemia and hypotension are not the listed electrolyte disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which late/severe manifestations occur in aspirin (salicylate) poisoning?","options":["Confusion, decreased conscious level & disorientation, seizures and coma due to CNS involvement","Improved consciousness","Hypertension","Polyuria with clear sensorium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe salicylate poisoning causes confusion, decreased conscious level and disorientation, seizures, and coma due to CNS involvement; oliguria and increased bleeding tendency (late stage) also occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: Consciousness deteriorates (not improves), and these other findings are not the stated manifestations."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the goal of therapy / nursing care in aspirin poisoning?","options":["To increase absorption of aspirin","The goal of therapy is to decrease the absorption of the poison: induced vomiting, gastric lavage, and activated charcoal","To withhold all fluids","To avoid antiemetics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care goal of therapy is to decrease the absorption of the poison via induced vomiting, gastric lavage, and activated charcoal; a specific antidote is not available for symptomatic treatment and can be used.  Why A/C/D wrong: The goal is to decrease (not increase) absorption; fluids and antiemetics are used as needed."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is frequent measurement of urine output, urine pH, and serum potassium performed in aspirin poisoning, and how often?","options":["Every 2 to 4 hours","Every 12 hours","Daily","Weekly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frequent measurement of urine output, urine pH, and serum potassium should be performed every 2 to 4 hours in aspirin poisoning.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies every 2 to 4 hours."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which agent is usually given as Sodium Bi-Carbonate (NaHCO3) and Vita-K to correct metabolic acidosis in aspirin poisoning, and why is NaHCO3 used?","options":["NaHCO3 is given for correction of metabolic acidosis, alkalinizes the urine","NaHCO3 worsens acidosis","NaHCO3 is an antidote","NaHCO3 decreases excretion of salicylate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Usually Sodium Bi-Carbonate (NaHCO3) and Vita-K are considered an antidote of aspirin poisoning; NaHCO3 is given for correction of metabolic acidosis; Vitamin K is administered to prevent bleeding; IV fluids are used to correct electrolyte and electrolyte imbalances; give diuretics for excretion.  Why B/C/D wrong: NaHCO3 corrects acidosis and promotes salicylate excretion (urinary alkalinisation), not the opposite."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Why is Vitamin K administered in aspirin poisoning nursing care?","options":["To worsen bleeding","To prevent bleeding","To correct hyperkalaemia","To increase absorption of aspirin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K administration is to prevent bleeding (salicylate poisoning increases bleeding tendency).  Why A/C/D wrong: Vitamin K prevents bleeding; it does not affect potassium or aspirin absorption."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In severe aspirin poisoning, when is haemodialysis indicated according to nursing care?","options":["Routinely for all patients","If the patient's condition does not improve than haemodialysis can be used accordingly to doctor order (symptomatic treatment is required for milder cases)","Never","Only in children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Symptomatic treatment is required; if the patient's condition does not improve, then haemodialysis can be used according to the doctor's order.  Why A/C/D wrong: Dialysis is reserved for non-improving/severe cases, not routine, never, or only children."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Lead poisoning is defined as an excess accumulation of which metal in the body, also known by what name?","options":["Lead is a metal in excess accumulation in the body; it is also known as Plumbism","Mercury; Minamata","Iron; Siderosis","Arsenic; Arsenicosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lead is a metal in excess accumulation in the body; lead poisoning is also known as Plumbism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other metal poisonings, not lead/plumbism."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is considered a normal value of lead in the body, and what level is considered enough to cause poisoning?","options":["Normal value of lead in the body is <5 mcg/dl; if the normal level increased >10 mcg/dl then it is considered enough poisoning","Normal is 50 mcg/dl","Normal is 100 mcg/dl","Normal is 35 mcg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal value of lead in the body is <5 mcg/dl; if the normal level increased >10 mcg/dl, then it is considered enough poisoning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the stated normal/poisoning thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the route of exposure of lead poisoning?","options":["Food, Water, Air, Trans-placentally","Only inhalation","Only ingestion","Only skin contact"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Route of exposure of lead poisoning is food, water, air, and trans-placentally.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lead exposure occurs by multiple routes, not a single route."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which organs are affected in lead poisoning, and what bone effect occurs?","options":["Bones: the poison forms weak and fracture-easy bones supported due to deposition of lead in bones instead of calcium","No bone involvement","Bones become denser only","Bones store excess calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Organs affected in lead poisoning—Bones: the poison forms weak and fracture-prone bones due to deposition of lead in bones instead of calcium.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lead displaces calcium in bone, weakening it, not increasing density or calcium storage."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"In lead poisoning, what happens to red blood cell production and which organ shows the most dangerous and irreversible effects?","options":["RBC: decrease RBC production and anaemia will be occurred; CNS: the most dangerous and irreversible effects appear on the brain of the body","RBC increases; liver is most affected","RBC unchanged; kidney is most affected","RBC increases; lungs are most affected"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RBC: decreased RBC production and anaemia will occur; CNS: the most dangerous and irreversible effects appear on the brain.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lead decreases RBC production and the CNS (brain) shows the most severe irreversible effects."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the confirmatory diagnosis of lead poisoning?","options":["Serum lead level","Sickle turbidity test","Serum ferritin","Haemoglobin electrophoresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmatory diagnosis of lead poisoning is serum lead level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These tests diagnose sickle cell disease, iron deficiency, and haemoglobinopathies respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"What is the Burton's line, a diagnostic feature of lead poisoning?","options":["A blue line on the gums; also known as Burtonian line, is a clinical sign found in patients with chronic lead poisoning; it is a very thin, black-blue line visible along the margin of the gums at the base of the teeth","A white line on the nails","A red line on the tongue","A line on the retina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Burton's line (Burtonian line) is a clinical sign found in patients with chronic lead poisoning; it is a very thin black-blue line visible along the margin of the gums at the base of the teeth.  Why B/C/D wrong: Burton's line is on the gums, not nails, tongue, or retina."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia, polycythemia vera…","question":"Which test is used in lead poisoning where a higher value indicates anaemia, with a stated normal value?","options":["Erythrocyte protoporphyrin test — Normal value: 35 mcg/dl; a higher value indicates anaemia","Serum ferritin — normal 5 ng/ml","Sickle turbidity test","Coagulation profile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Erythrocyte protoporphyrin test in lead poisoning has a normal value of 35 mcg/dl, and a higher value indicates anaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the protoporphyrin test described for lead poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"In organophosphate (OPS) poisoning, what is the FIRST-choice antidote?","options":["Dimercaprol (BAL)","Atropine (calcium disodium EDTA is also used for lead)","Pralidoxime alone","Naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is the first-line antidote in OPS poisoning; it antagonizes the muscarinic effects of excess acetylcholine. (The page lists atropine sulphate as the antidote of choice for organophosphate poisoning.)  Why A wrong: Dimercaprol (BAL) is a chelating agent for heavy-metal (e.g., lead, arsenic) poisoning, not OPS.  Why C wrong: Pralidoxime is an adjunct that reactivates cholinesterase but is given in conjunction with, not instead of, atropine.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is the opiate (opioid) antidote."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Organophosphates exert their toxic effect by which mechanism?","options":["Binding to hemoglobin","Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase","Oxidation of substances in the stomach","Competing with oxygen for hemoglobin binding sites"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Organophosphate is an anti-cholinesterase; it inhibits acetylcholinesterase, leading to accumulation of acetylcholine and continuous stimulation of the parasympathetic nervous system.  Why A/D wrong: These describe carbon monoxide / metabolic poisons, not OPS.  Why C wrong: Oxidation in the stomach describes potassium permanganate gastric lavage effect, not the OPS mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A patient with organophosphate poisoning would be expected to present with which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Vomiting and diarrhea","Bradycardia and hypotension","Excessive salivation and lacrimation","Pupil constriction (miosis) and muscle weakness","Tachycardia and mydriasis (dilated pupils)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: OPS poisoning produces cholinergic excess — vomiting, diarrhea, bradycardia, hypotension, increased salivation/lacrimation, pupil constriction (miosis), muscle weakness, pulmonary edema and urinary incontinence.  Why E wrong: Tachycardia and mydriasis are anticholinergic/sympathetic findings — the OPSITE of the muscarinic cholinergic picture seen in OPS poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Why is activated charcoal given in the nursing care of organophosphate poisoning?","options":["It neutralizes acid in the stomach","It decreases absorption of the chemical by induced vomiting, gastric lavage, and charcoal","It chelates the organophosphate for renal excretion","It reactivates acetylcholinesterase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first nursing priority is to decrease absorption of the chemical by induced vomiting, gastric lavage, and activated charcoal.  Why A wrong: Charcoal adsorbs rather than neutralizes; acid neutralization is not its role here.  Why C wrong: Chelation is done by agents like BAL/EDTA, not charcoal.  Why D wrong: Reactivation of cholinesterase is the role of pralidoxime, not charcoal."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"When assessing a patient with organophosphate poisoning, the nurse should pay attention to skin exposure or inhalation because:","options":["The chemical can be absorbed through skin or inhalation","The skin always turns blue","It causes immediate cardiac arrest","It is only dangerous if swallowed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs the nurse to assess all vitals, give first priority to airway, and consider that exposure can occur through skin contact, inhalation, or oral routes.  Why B wrong: Blue skin (cyanosis/argyria) is associated with other conditions, not a uniform OPS sign.  Why C wrong: Immediate cardiac arrest is not the described typical presentation.  Why D wrong: OPS can be absorbed via skin and inhalation, not only ingestion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"According to the severity (blood lead level) classification, a lead level of 45–70 mcg/dL indicates which action?","options":["Consider environmental lead level only","Consider passing (monitor)","No need hospitalization","Chelation should be initiated"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: At a blood lead level of 45–70 mcg/dL, chelation should be initiated per the severity table.  Why A wrong: Considering environmental lead level corresponds to a lower level (<5 mcg/dL).  Why B wrong: \"Consider passing\" corresponds to the 5–10 mcg/dL range.  Why C wrong: \"No need hospitalization\" corresponds to the 10–18 / 18–45 mcg/dL lower ranges."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A blood lead level greater than 70 mcg/dL is best described as which of the following?","options":["Consider environmental lead level","No need for hospitalization","A medical emergency that should be treated as soon as possible","Below the threshold for any treatment"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A serum lead level >70 mcg/dL is a medical emergency and should be treated as soon as possible.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to far lower lead levels; a level >70 mcg/dL is the highest, emergency category."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"For lead poisoning, calcium disodium EDTA (CaNa2 EDTA) is classified as which line of antidote?","options":["First choice","Second choice","Third choice","Not used in lead poisoning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First choice antidote for lead poisoning is calcium disodium EDTA (CaNa2 EDTA).  Why B wrong: Second choice is dimercaprol, also known as BAL (British Anti-Lewisite).  Why C wrong: Third choice is the chelating therapy (universal antidote)/penicillamine.  Why D wrong: EDTA is indeed used; it is the first choice."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Dimercaprol, used as the second-choice antidote in lead poisoning, is also known by what name?","options":["Pralidoxime","BAL (British Anti-Lewisite)","Naloxone","Atropine sulphate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dimercaprol is also known as BAL (British Anti-Lewisite) and is the second-choice chelating antidote for lead.  Why A wrong: Pralidoxime is used in OPS poisoning.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is the opiate antidote.  Why D wrong: Atropine sulphate is the OPS antidote."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"When a patient is treated for lead poisoning, what nursing measure helps to ensure adequate urine output and excretion of lead?","options":["Restrict all fluids","Maintain appropriate hydration level for adequate urine output","Administer atropine","Withhold all antidotes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should maintain an appropriate hydration level to ensure adequate urine output during lead poisoning treatment.  Why A wrong: Fluid restriction would impair renal excretion of lead/chelate complexes.  Why C wrong: Atropine is used for OPS, not lead.  Why D wrong: Antidotes (chelators) are the basis of treatment and should not be withheld."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Opiates (e.g., codeine, heroin, pethidine, morphine, methadone) primarily cause which dangerous effect in overdose?","options":["Excessive salivation and bradycardia","Depression of consciousness and respiratory depression (hypoventilation)","Skin pigmentation changes","Hemolysis of red blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Opiates cause depression of conscious state and hypoventilation (respiratory depression); maintaining the airway and adequate ventilation is essential.  Why A wrong: Salivation/bradycardia describe cholinergic (OPS) toxicity.  Why C wrong: Skin pigmentation changes are seen with arsenic/heavy metals.  Why D wrong: Hemolysis is not the primary opiate effect."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"What is the specific antidote for opiate (opioid) overdose?","options":["Atropine","Naloxone","Pralidoxime","Calcium disodium EDTA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Naloxone is the specific opioid antidote; it is given 0.4 mg intravenously or intramuscularly.  Why A wrong: Atropine is for OPS.  Why C wrong: Pralidoxime is an OPS adjunct.  Why D wrong: EDTA chelates lead, not opioids."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A patient with opioid overdose is given naloxone but fails to respond to a single dose. What is the recommended next action?","options":["Stop all treatment and observe","Repeat naloxone up to a maximum of 2 mg","Give atropine instead","Administer pralidoxime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page, failure to respond to one dose is an indication that the dose may be repeated, up to a maximum of 2 mg.  Why A wrong: Continued respiratory depression requires active reversal, not passive observation.  Why C/D wrong: These are antidotes for OPS poisoning, not opioids."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A nurse must monitor an opioid-overdose patient closely because naloxone may cause which adverse effect?","options":["Pulmonary edema","Hyperglycemia","Permanent renal failure","Arsenicosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page warns that naloxone may produce pulmonary edema, requiring monitoring.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed adverse effect of naloxone in this context."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Hydrocarbons such as kerosene, petrol, and diesel primarily cause irritation to which body system, leading to chemical pneumonia if aspirated?","options":["Gastrointestinal tract / respiratory tract (aspiration)","Renal system only","Skeletal system","Endocrine glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocarbons cause irritation of the gastrointestinal tract, with common symptoms of abdominal pain, vomiting, and diarrhea; their most dangerous effect is aspiration leading to chemical pneumonia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The chief described toxicity is GI irritation plus aspiration pneumonitis, not isolated renal, skeletal, or endocrine damage."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Why is inducing vomiting and gastric lavage CONTRAINDICATED in hydrocarbon (e.g., kerosene) poisoning?","options":["It causes excessive salivation","There is a high risk of aspiration and chemical pneumonia","It neutralizes the antidote","Hydrocarbons are corrosive to bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inducing vomiting and gastric lavage are NOT performed in hydrocarbon poisoning because there is a high risk of aspiration, which may cause chemical pneumonitis; activated charcoal is also contraindicated.  Why A wrong: Salivation is not the reason for avoiding lavage here.  Why C wrong: There is no neutralizing antidote concern.  Why D wrong: Hydrocarbons cause aspiration pneumonia, not bone corrosion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Gastric decontamination (gastric lavage / inducing vomiting) is NOT indicated in which type of ingestion?","options":["Alkali (bleach, drain cleaner) ingestion","Lead ingestion","Opiate ingestion","Arsenic ingestion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In alkali (bleach/drain cleaner) ingestion, gastric decontamination is NOT indicated, and inducing vomiting should be avoided because re-exposure of the esophagus to the corrosive agent worsens caustic injury; treatment is supportive.  Why B/C/D wrong: The contraindication described here specifically concerns corrosive alkali ingestion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Household bleach typically contains 5% sodium hypochlorite. In small symptomatic ingestions, how is it usually managed?","options":["Aggressive surgical resection","Symptomatic treatment with intravenous fluids and observation, as it is a weakly alkaline substance unlikely to cause serious injury","High-dose chelation therapy","Immediate forced vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 5% sodium hypochlorite bleach is only weakly alkaline and unlikely to cause serious injury; symptomatic ingestion is treated with IV fluids and observation.  Why A wrong: Surgical resection is not the routine management of minor bleach ingestion.  Why C wrong: Chelation is for heavy metals.  Why D wrong: Forced vomiting is avoided with corrosives."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"For stronger alkali ingestions, why might a patient be admitted for endoscopy?","options":["To remove a foreign body","To assess the complications of which indicate airway obstruction and esophageal/gastric perforation","To administer an antidote directly","To biopsy the liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stronger alkalis can cause severe injury; the patient is assessed for complications such as airway obstruction and esophageal/gastric perforation, and endoscopy guides rehydration and management.  Why A wrong: It is to assess corrosive injury, not retrieve a foreign body.  Why C wrong: Endoscopy is diagnostic for injury extent, not antidote delivery.  Why D wrong: Liver biopsy is unrelated to corrosive ingestion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Carbon monoxide (CO) produces its toxic effect primarily because it:","options":["Competes with oxygen for binding to the hemoglobin molecule, forming carboxyhemoglobin","Directly destroys the alveoli","Chelates iron in the liver","Causes blue line on the gums"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CO competes with oxygen for binding sites on the hemoglobin molecule, combining with hemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin, preventing oxygen transport and producing tissue hypoxia.  Why B wrong: Direct alveolar destruction describes other inhalants, not CO's primary mechanism.  Why C wrong: Chelation of iron is unrelated.  Why D wrong: The blue line on the gums (Burton's line) is a feature of lead poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Why is incomplete burning of organic matter (e.g., in vehicles, heaters, or cooking equipment) a concern for carbon monoxide poisoning?","options":["It produces sodium hypochlorite","It produces carbon monoxide from incomplete combustion of carbon-based fuels","It releases arsenic gas","It generates paraquat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CO is produced by the incomplete burning of organic matter such as from motor vehicles, heaters, or cooking equipment that uses carbon-based fuels.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated chemicals (bleach, arsenic, herbicide) not produced by incomplete combustion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which clinical effects are associated with carbon monoxide poisoning? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Headache","Nausea","Confusion","Chest pain","Cardiac arrhythmias"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Toxic effects of CO include headache, nausea, confusion, chest pain, tonic seizures, and cardiac arrhythmias.  All listed options are explicitly named effects of carbon monoxide poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"What is the recommended treatment for a symptomatic carbon monoxide poisoning patient?","options":["100% oxygen for at least 12 hours","Room air observation only","Gastric lavage with charcoal","Naloxone infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Symptomatic CO-poisoned patients are treated with 100% oxygen for at least 12 hours to displace CO from hemoglobin.  Why B wrong: Room air provides insufficient oxygen to compete CO off hemoglobin.  Why C wrong: Charcoal/lavage do not adsorb inhaled gas.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is the opioid antidote, not for CO."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) is used in carbon monoxide poisoning to:","options":["Increase the partial pressure of oxygen and accelerate displacement of CO from hemoglobin","Neutralize stomach acid","Reactivate cholinesterase","Chelate lead"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBOT delivers high-pressure oxygen that displaces carbon monoxide from hemoglobin more rapidly; the page depicts hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) as a treatment for CO poisoning.  Why B/C/D wrong: HBOT does not act as an antacid, cholinesterase reactivator, or chelator."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Paraquat is the active ingredient of Gromoxone. It is classified as which type of agent?","options":["An organophosphate insecticide","A toxic chemical herbicide (plant killer), primarily for weed and grass control","An opioid analgesic","A heavy metal chelator"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paraquat is a toxic chemical widely used as an herbicide (plant killer), primarily for weed and grass control.  Why A wrong: It is a herbicide, not an organophosphate insecticide.  Why C wrong: It is not an opioid.  Why D wrong: It is not a chelating agent."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Why is administration of excessive oxygen AVOIDED in paraquat poisoning?","options":["It worsens lung damage caused by paraquat toxicity","It reverses the antidote","It causes hypoglycemia","It increases gastric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In paraquat poisoning, administration of excessive oxygen should be avoided because it may worsen paraquat toxicity (oxygen-radical-mediated pulmonary injury).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons; the concern is potentiation of pulmonary toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which adsorbent is recommended to be given early in paraquat poisoning (e.g., Fuller's earth, also known as bleaching clay)?","options":["Naloxone","Fuller's earth (bleaching clay), considered an effective antidote/adsorbent","Atropine sulphate","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fuller's earth (bleaching clay) is considered an effective adsorbent/antidote in paraquat poisoning and is given early; it can be repeated with mannitol until the stool is free from the purgative.  Why A wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids.  Why C wrong: Atropine treats OPS.  Why D wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is not the recommended adsorbent here."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Arsenic poisoning is a medical condition caused by elevated levels of arsenic in the body. Which feature is characteristic of arsenic?","options":["It is colorless, flammable, and non-irritating with a mild garlic odor","It is a corrosive alkali","It binds hemoglobin like carbon monoxide","It is an opioid receptor agonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arsine, a form of arsenic, is colorless, flammable, and non-irritating with a mild garlic odor.  Why B wrong: Arsenic is a heavy metal, not a corrosive alkali.  Why C wrong: CO (not arsenic) binds hemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin.  Why D wrong: Arsenic is not an opioid."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"What is the most common reason for long-term (chronic) arsenic exposure leading to poisoning?","options":["Industrial smoke inhalation only","Contaminated drinking water","Eating sea food","Skin contact with paint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common reason for long-term exposure is contaminated drinking water.  Why A wrong: Industrial fumes are a source but not the most common chronic route stated.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the page's stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"According to the recommended/safe standard, the level of arsenic in water should be less than which value?","options":["10 µg/L (10 parts per billion)","50 µg/L (50 parts per billion)","1050 µg/L (1050 parts per billion)","5000 µg/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the recommended level of arsenic in water should be less than 1050 µg/L (1050 parts per billion).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the value printed on the page; key the value as printed (note: this is far higher than the WHO 10 µg/L standard, so the source figure appears unusually high — keyed verbatim as written in the book)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Long-term arsenic exposure can result in thickening of the skin, darker skin pigmentation, abdominal pain, diarrhea, heart disease, numbness, and which serious chronic outcome?","options":["Cancer","Carboxyhemoglobinemia","Immediate respiratory arrest","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Long-term arsenic exposure can result in thickening of the skin, darker pigmentation, abdominal pain, diarrhea, heart disease, numbness, and cancer.  Why B wrong: Carboxyhemoglobinemia is a CO effect.  Why C wrong: Immediate respiratory arrest is not the described chronic outcome.  Why D wrong: Metabolic alkalosis is not the listed effect."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Match the metallic poison to its characteristic effect: Lead poisoning classically causes which sign on the gums?","options":["Yellow color of skin/hair (jaundice)","Blue stippled (lead) line on the gums (Burtonian line), especially on the upper jaw","Green color of hair and skin","Brown pigments on extensive teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lead (Pb) poisoning produces a blue stippled lead line (Burtonian/Burton's line) on the gums, especially on the upper jaw.  Why A wrong: Yellow skin/hair is associated with arsenic in the metallic-poison table.  Why C wrong: Green color is associated with copper (Cu).  Why D wrong: Brown pigments on teeth (acrodynia/pink disease features) are associated with mercury (Hg)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"In the table of metallic poisons and their effects on hair and skin, copper (Cu) poisoning is associated with which color change?","options":["Blue color of skin","Green color of hair and skin","Yellow color of skin","Black foot disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Copper (Cu) is associated with a green color of hair and skin (and a blue-green line on the gums).  Why A wrong: Blue line on the gums is more characteristic of lead.  Why C wrong: Yellow color is linked to arsenic.  Why D wrong: Black foot disease is associated with arsenic (As)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Mercury (Hg) poisoning is classically associated with which dermatological/dental manifestation?","options":["Blue lead line and necrosis with loosening of teeth, plus brown pigments (acrodynia/pink disease)","Green hair","Yellow skin and black foot disease","Painless caustic burns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mercury (Hg) is associated with a blue lead line on the gums with necrosis and loosening of teeth, and brown pigments / acrodynia (pink disease).  Why B wrong: Green hair is associated with copper.  Why C wrong: Yellow skin / black foot disease relate to arsenic.  Why D wrong: Caustic burns describe corrosive ingestion, not mercury."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"In forensic poisoning, which organ is preserved for analysis in strychnine poisoning?","options":["Brain","Bone","Spinal cord (entire length)","Fat"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For strychnine poisoning, the organ preserved is the spinal cord (entire length).  Why A wrong: Brain is preserved for cyanide, organophosphates, opiates, barbiturates, alkaloids, and strychnine-class CNS poisons in the broader list, but the spinal-cord answer is the specific strychnine entry.  Why B wrong: Bone is preserved for arsenic, radium, and thallium poisoning.  Why D wrong: Fat is preserved for pesticides and insecticide poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which organ is preserved for analysis in arsenic, radium, and thallium poisoning (mnemonic \"ART\")?","options":["Brain","Bone","Fat","Spinal cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For arsenic, radium, and thallium poisoning (mnemonic ART), the bone is preserved.  Why A wrong: Brain is preserved for CO, cyanide, organophosphates, opiates, barbiturates, alkaloids, and strychnine (CNS poisons).  Why C wrong: Fat is preserved for pesticides and insecticides.  Why D wrong: Spinal cord is preserved for strychnine."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which organ is preserved for analysis in pesticide and insecticide poisoning?","options":["Fat","Bone","Brain","Spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For pesticides and insecticides poisoning, the organ preserved is fat.  Why B wrong: Bone is for arsenic/radium/thallium.  Why C wrong: Brain is for CO/cyanide/organophosphate/opiate/barbiturate/alkaloid/strychnine.  Why D wrong: Spinal cord is for strychnine."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"In the \"Must Remember\" box, what is the recommended antidote/decontaminant for most poisons (e.g., acidic/alkaline nature, household cleaners, detergents, bleach, paint, batteries) and how soon should it be administered?","options":["Naloxone within 24 hours","Activated charcoal within 1 hour of ingestion, which can be effective within 4 hours of ingestion of injurious substances","Atropine within 6 hours","Fuller's earth within 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page recommends administering activated charcoal within 1 hour of ingestion; it can be effective within 4 hours of ingestion of injurious substances.  Why A wrong: Naloxone is opioid-specific, not a universal decontaminant.  Why C wrong: Atropine is OPS-specific.  Why D wrong: Fuller's earth is paraquat-specific, not a universal early decontaminant."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A child presents with anorexia, nausea, vomiting, and liver toxicity after acetaminophen (paracetamol) ingestion. Per the \"Common Agents in Childhood Poisoning\" table, what is the treatment?","options":["Never induce vomiting","Activated charcoal or acetylcysteine in the emergency department","Dilute with milk only","Gastric lavage with milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For acetaminophen poisoning (products containing acetaminophen), treatment is activated charcoal or acetylcysteine (the antidote) in the emergency department.  Why A wrong: \"Never induce vomiting\" is the warning for hydrocarbon and caustic agents, not the acetaminophen treatment row.  Why C/D wrong: Dilution with milk is not the standard treatment listed for acetaminophen."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A child has swallowed a hydrocarbon (gasoline, kerosene, furniture polish, lamp oil). According to the childhood-poisoning table, what is the key treatment caution?","options":["Induce vomiting immediately","Never induce vomiting; contact a doctor or poison control center (PCC)","Give activated charcoal liberally","Neutralize with vinegar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For hydrocarbons, the table states \"Never induce vomiting\" and to contact the doctor or poison control center, because of aspiration risk.  Why A wrong: Inducing vomiting risks aspiration pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Charcoal is not effective/safe for hydrocarbons.  Why D wrong: Neutralization with vinegar is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"For caustic ingestion (battery contents, rust removers, oven cleaner, drain openers, toilet bowl cleaners) in a child, what does the childhood-poisoning table recommend?","options":["Induce vomiting and give charcoal","Never induce vomiting; never attempt to neutralize the caustic; dilute with milk or water and contact the doctor or PCC","Give 100% oxygen","Administer Fuller's earth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For caustics the table advises: never induce vomiting, never attempt to neutralize the caustic, dilute with milk or water, and contact the doctor or PCC.  Why A wrong: Vomiting re-exposes the esophagus to corrosive injury; charcoal is not used.  Why C wrong: 100% oxygen is the CO treatment.  Why D wrong: Fuller's earth is for paraquat."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A child with iron ingestion presents with nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, melena, hematemesis, and coma. What does the childhood-poisoning table recommend?","options":["Activated charcoal","Contact poison control center (PCC); in the emergency department, gastric lavage can be done if dose is toxic; use chelating agent","Never induce vomiting","Give milk and observe at home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For iron poisoning, contact PCC; in ED, gastric lavage can be done if the dose is toxic, and a chelating agent (e.g., deferoxamine) is used.  Why A wrong: Activated charcoal does not bind iron well.  Why C wrong: \"Never induce vomiting\" is the caustic/hydrocarbon warning, not the iron treatment.  Why D wrong: Toxic iron ingestion needs ED care, not home observation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"A child with lead poisoning may present with which clinical pattern, per the childhood-poisoning table?","options":["May be asymptomatic, or with anorexia, abdominal pain, anemia, lead encephalitis, and neurodevelopmental deficits","Always immediate cardiac arrest","Green skin only","Garlic-odor breath only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lead poisoning in children may be asymptomatic, or present with anorexia, abdominal pain, anemia, lead encephalitis (encephalopathy), and neurodevelopmental deficits; treatment is chelation therapy and monitoring of lead levels.  Why B wrong: Immediate cardiac arrest is not the described pattern.  Why C wrong: Green skin is associated with copper.  Why D wrong: Garlic odor is associated with arsine/arsenic."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"For insecticide (e.g., insecticide spray) poisoning in a child presenting with nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, excessive salivation, confusion, and depressed reflexes/seizures, what is the recommended treatment?","options":["Never induce vomiting","Activated charcoal if swallowed; if clothing is contaminated, wash skin and hair with water/soap","Give 100% oxygen for 12 hours","Dilute with milk only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For insecticides the table recommends activated charcoal if swallowed, and if clothing is contaminated, wash the skin and hair with water/soap to limit dermal absorption.  Why A wrong: \"Never induce vomiting\" is the hydrocarbon/caustic warning.  Why C wrong: 100% oxygen is for CO poisoning.  Why D wrong: Dilution with milk alone is not the listed insecticide treatment."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"(CRACK THE NORCET PYQ) A complete history is required before gastric lavage. In which of the following is gastric lavage CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Kerosene poisoning","Barbiturate poisoning","Organophosphate poisoning","Salicylate poisoning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gastric lavage is contraindicated in kerosene (hydrocarbon) poisoning because of the high risk of aspiration causing chemical pneumonitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Barbiturate, organophosphate, and salicylate poisonings may be managed with gastric lavage/decontamination when indicated; only the volatile hydrocarbon contraindicates lavage due to aspiration risk."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"(CRACK THE NORCET PYQ) A 25-year-old man is brought to the emergency with a history of multiple-time vomiting, drooling, bronchorrhea (excessive secretions), pinpoint pupils (miosis), salivation, lacrimation, sweating, muscle weakness, and bilateral crackles. Which type of poisoning does this clinical picture most likely represent?","options":["Organophosphate (OPS) poisoning","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Arsenic poisoning","Iron poisoning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The combination of vomiting, drooling, bronchorrhea, miosis, salivation, lacrimation, sweating, muscle weakness, and crackles is the classic muscarinic cholinergic toxidrome of organophosphate poisoning.  Why B wrong: CO poisoning presents with headache, confusion, and cherry-red signs, not cholinergic secretions/miosis.  Why C wrong: Arsenic causes GI symptoms with garlic breath and skin changes, not the cholinergic toxidrome.  Why D wrong: Iron causes hemorrhagic GI symptoms, not bronchorrhea/miosis."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"(CRACK THE NORCET PYQ) A patient comes to the emergency department with suspicious poisoning. Because arsenic has a high affinity for keratin, the concentration of arsenic is high in hair, nails, and tissues. Arsenic binds with keratin and becomes \"trapped\" in the hair/nails. Hair and nail analysis is therefore useful to detect whether arsenic poisoning occurred. Which type of poisoning is described?","options":["Arsenic poisoning","Lead poisoning","Opioid poisoning","Mercury poisoning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The high affinity for keratin causing arsenic to concentrate and become trapped in hair and nails — making hair/nail analysis diagnostic — is specific to arsenic poisoning.  Why B wrong: Lead is more often detected via blood lead level and bone.  Why C wrong: Opioids are detected in blood/urine, not keratin trapping.  Why D wrong: While mercury also deposits in hair, the described keratin-affinity feature is the classic arsenic teaching point."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"(CRACK THE NORCET PYQ) A patient is suspected of lead poisoning. On examination, a blue line is noted on the gums. This blue gum line is known as which sign, and to which heavy metal does it point?","options":["Burton's line — lead poisoning","Mees' line — arsenic poisoning","Aldrich-Mees line — thallium","Beau's line — mercury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The blue line on the gums is Burton's line (Burtonian line), a classic sign of lead poisoning.  Why B wrong: Mees' lines are transverse white bands across the nails seen in arsenic poisoning (a nail sign, not a gum sign).  Why C wrong: Aldrich-Mees lines are nail findings, not a blue gum line.  Why D wrong: Beau's lines are transverse nail grooves from systemic stress, not a mercury gum sign."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Mees' lines associated with arsenic poisoning are best described as:","options":["Blue lines on the gums","Transverse white bands across the nails, appearing about 4 to 6 weeks after exposure","Yellow discoloration of the sclera","Green pigmentation of hair"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mees' lines are transverse white bands across the fingernails, appearing about 4 to 6 weeks after arsenic exposure.  Why A wrong: A blue gum line is Burton's line (lead).  Why C wrong: Yellow sclera indicates jaundice, not arsenic Mees' lines.  Why D wrong: Green hair pigment relates to copper."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which chelating agent was previously used and is still occasionally recommended (as British Anti-Lewisite, BAL/dimercaprol) for arsenic poisoning?","options":["Dimercaprol (BAL / British Anti-Lewisite)","Naloxone","Atropine","Fuller's earth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dimercaprol, also known as British Anti-Lewisite (BAL), was previously the most frequently recommended chelating agent for arsenic.  Why B wrong: Naloxone is the opioid antidote.  Why C wrong: Atropine treats OPS poisoning.  Why D wrong: Fuller's earth is used for paraquat."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"(CRACK THE NORCET PYQ) A patient with carbon monoxide poisoning is being managed. Which intervention is appropriate?","options":["Administer 100% oxygen until the patient is symptom-free, usually about 4–5 hours","Restrict oxygen","Give Fuller's earth","Induce vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For CO poisoning, administer 100% oxygen until the patient is symptom-free (about 4–5 hours per the PYQ); consider hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) for a pregnant woman (even if she is symptom-free) — and HBOT is the treatment of choice for pregnant women.  Why B wrong: Restricting oxygen worsens tissue hypoxia from carboxyhemoglobin.  Why C wrong: Fuller's earth is the paraquat adsorbent.  Why D wrong: Inducing vomiting has no role in an inhaled gas exposure."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Carbon monoxide rapidly crosses the pulmonary capillary membrane, binding to hemoglobin with high affinity. Compared with oxygen, CO has approximately how many times the binding affinity of oxygen for hemoglobin?","options":["About 2 times","About 50 times","About 200 times more than oxygen","About 1000 times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The PYQ states carbon monoxide combines with hemoglobin at an affinity approximately 200 times that of oxygen, displacing oxygen and decreasing oxygen-carrying capacity (forming carboxyhemoglobin), which decreases oxygen delivery to tissues and causes marked cellular hypoxia and acidosis.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the ~200-fold affinity figure stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which type of circulation supplies oxygenated (arterial) blood from the heart through the aorta to the peripheral tissues and organs?","options":["Pulmonary circulation","Systemic circulation","Coronary circulation only","Portal circulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Systemic circulation, through the systemic circulation, supplies oxygenated arterial blood from the heart (left ventricle) via the aorta to the peripheral tissues and organs.  Why A wrong: Pulmonary circulation carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs (pulmonary artery) and returns oxygenated blood (pulmonary veins) to the left atrium.  Why C wrong: Coronary circulation supplies the heart muscle itself, a subset, not the whole systemic supply.  Why D wrong: Portal circulation refers to the venous system carrying blood to the liver."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"The pulmonary circulation moves deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs and returns oxygenated blood to the heart through which vessels?","options":["Pulmonary artery (out) and four pulmonary veins (return)","Aorta (out) and superior vena cava (return)","Coronary arteries and coronary sinus","Hepatic artery and portal vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pulmonary circulation, deoxygenated blood enters the lungs from the right ventricle through the main pulmonary artery, and oxygenated blood is returned to the left atrium through the four pulmonary veins.  Why B wrong: The aorta and SVC are part of systemic circulation.  Why C wrong: Coronary vessels supply/drain the heart muscle.  Why D wrong: Hepatic/portal vessels belong to the portal circulation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"The right atrium of the heart receives deoxygenated blood from which sources? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Superior vena cava","Inferior vena cava","Coronary sinus","Pulmonary veins","Aorta"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The right atrium receives blood from the superior and inferior vena cava, and from the coronary sinus (the opening of the coronary sinus drains the heart's own venous blood).  Why D wrong: The pulmonary veins drain oxygenated blood into the left atrium.  Why E wrong: The aorta carries oxygenated blood away from the left ventricle; it does not empty into the right atrium."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"The left atrium of the heart receives blood from which vessels?","options":["Pulmonary veins","Superior vena cava","Coronary sinus","Inferior vena cava"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins.  Why B/D wrong: The superior and inferior vena cava drain into the right atrium.  Why C wrong: The coronary sinus drains into the right atrium."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which heart chamber has the thickest myocardial wall, reflecting its role in pumping blood into the systemic circulation?","options":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The left ventricle has the thickest wall because it must generate high pressure to eject blood into the systemic circulation through the aorta.  Why A/B wrong: The atria are thin-walled receiving chambers.  Why C wrong: The right ventricle wall is thinner because it pumps against the low-resistance pulmonary circuit."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which valve is the right atrioventricular (AV) valve located between the right atrium and right ventricle?","options":["Tricuspid valve","Bicuspid (mitral) valve","Aortic valve","Pulmonary semilunar valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The right AV valve is the tricuspid valve, situated between the right atrium and right ventricle.  Why B wrong: The bicuspid (mitral) valve is the left AV valve.  Why C wrong: The aortic valve guards the outlet from the left ventricle into the aorta.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary semilunar valve guards the outlet from the right ventricle into the pulmonary artery."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"The left atrioventricular (AV) valve, located between the left atrium and left ventricle, is also known as the:","options":["Tricuspid valve","Bicuspid (mitral) valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The left AV valve is the bicuspid valve, commonly called the mitral valve.  Why A wrong: The tricuspid valve is the right AV valve.  Why C/D wrong: The pulmonary and aortic valves are semilunar (outflow) valves, not AV valves."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Organophosphate poisoning — antidotes, S/S, nursing care…","question":"Which cardiac structures anchor the AV valve cusps and prevent valve prolapse (eversion) during ventricular contraction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chordae tendineae","Papillary muscles","Trabeculae carneae","Pulmonary veins","Aortic valve"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The chordae tendineae are tendinous cords that connect the AV valve cusps to the papillary muscles, which contract to hold the valves closed and prevent prolapse during ventricular systole.  Why C wrong: Trabeculae carneae are the muscular ridges on the inner ventricular wall, not the valve-anchoring apparatus.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary veins are vessels returning blood to the left atrium.  Why E wrong: The aortic valve is an outflow semilunar valve, not part of the AV valve-anchoring system."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The lymphatic system, also called the lymphoid system, is an organ system in vertebrates that is part of which two systems?","options":["Endocrine and the immune system","The circulatory system and the immune system","The nervous system and the digestive system","The respiratory system and the circulatory system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lymphatic (lymphoid) system is an organ system in vertebrates that is part of the circulatory system and the immune system.  Why A wrong: The endocrine system is not the partner system described.  Why C wrong: It is not part of the nervous or digestive systems per the source.  Why D wrong: Respiratory is not listed; the circulatory system is correct but immune (not respiratory) is the second."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A large network of lymphatic vessels, lymphatic or lymphoid organs, and lymphoid tissues makes up the:","options":["Cardiovascular system","Lymphatic system","Reticular system","Portal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lymphatic system is made up of a large network of lymphatic vessels, lymphatic/lymphoid organs, and lymphoid tissues.  Why A wrong: The cardiovascular system circulates blood, not lymph.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the defined system."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The clear fluid carried by lymphatic vessels is called:","options":["Plasma","Serum","Lymph","Chyle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vessels carry a clear fluid that is called lymph.  Why A wrong: Plasma is the fluid component of blood.  Why B wrong: Serum is plasma without clotting factors.  Why D wrong: Chyle is the milky lymph from the intestines, not the general term."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Which organs are considered the PRIMARY lymphoid organs because their role is the production of B and T cells?","options":["Spleen and lymph nodes","Tonsils and Peyer's patches","Thymus and bone marrow","Liver and kidneys"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The thymus and bone marrow are considered primary lymphoid organs because of their role in the production of B and T cells.  Why A wrong: Spleen and lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid organs.  Why B wrong: Tonsils and Peyer's patches are secondary lymphoid tissue.  Why D wrong: Liver and kidneys are not lymphoid organs."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The lymph nodes and spleen are classified as which type of lymphoid organs?","options":["Primary lymphoid organs","Secondary lymphoid organs","Tertiary lymphoid organs","Accessory lymphoid organs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lymph nodes and spleen are considered secondary lymphoid organs; they filter lymph and blood respectively, and where B and T cells are introduced to antigens.  Why A wrong: Primary lymphoid organs are the thymus and bone marrow.  Why C/D wrong: Not classifications used in the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the lymphatic system? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Return fluid and intestinal proteins to the venous system via the thoracic and large lymphatic ducts","It is responsible for the removal of interstitial fluid from tissues","It absorbs and transports fatty acids and fats as chyle to the circulatory system from the digestive system","It transports white blood cells into and out of the lymph nodes","It produces red blood cells in the lymph nodes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: The lymphatic system returns fluid and intestinal proteins to the venous system through the thoracic and large lymphatic ducts.  Why B correct: It is responsible for the removal of interstitial fluid from tissues.  Why C correct: It absorbs and transports fatty acids and fats as chyle from the digestive system.  Why D correct: It transports white blood cells into and out of the lymph nodes.  Why E wrong: The source lists no red-blood-cell production function for the lymphatic system; lymph nodes are an immune-response site (the lymph transports antigen-presenting cells such as dendritic cells to the lymph nodes where an immune response is stimulated)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The lymph transports antigen-presenting cells, such as dendritic cells, to the lymph nodes for what purpose?","options":["To produce clotting factors","To stimulate an immune response","To store iron","To synthesize bile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lymph carries antigen-presenting cells (e.g., dendritic cells) to the lymph nodes where an immune response is stimulated.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not functions attributed to lymph nodes in the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Lymph nodes are located:","options":["Only in the abdomen","Throughout the body","Only in the neck and groin","Only within the spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lymph nodes are located throughout the body.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not restricted to a single region per the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The function of the spleen helps protect against foreign bodies such as:","options":["Only viruses","Bacteria and viruses","Only allergens","Hormones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spleen's function helps protect against foreign bodies such as bacteria and viruses.  Why A wrong: It protects against bacteria as well, not viruses only.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The spleen releases lymphocytes (B-cell and T-cell) into the circulation for what purpose?","options":["Early storage of iron","Early identification of foreign substances","Production of bile pigments","Synthesis of clotting factors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spleen releases lymphocytes (B-cell and T-cell) into the circulation for early identification of foreign substances.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose of lymphocyte release."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"When a pathogen entered into the body, lymphocytes are:","options":["Decreased","Unchanged","Increased","Eliminated"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When a pathogen enters the body, lymphocytes are increased.  Why A/B/D wrong: They increase, not decrease, remain unchanged, or get eliminated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal weight of the spleen?","options":["100 gm","200 gm","350 gm","500 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal weight of the spleen is 200 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated normal spleen weight (350 gm is its blood storage capacity)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What volume of blood can the spleen store?","options":["200 ml","250 ml","350 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Storage of blood: 350 ml.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the stated storage volume."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Where is the spleen located?","options":["Right hypochondrium, below the cardiac portion of the stomach","Left hypochondrium, above and behind the cardiac portion of the stomach","In the pelvis behind the bladder","In the mediastinum behind the sternum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Location: left hypochondrium, above and behind the cardiac portion of the stomach.  Why A wrong: It is in the LEFT, not right, hypochondrium.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect anatomical locations."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Which function of the spleen describes its role of having reticuloendothelial cells that protect the body from antigens through phagocytosis?","options":["It removes damaged cells from the circulation","It has reticuloendothelial cells that protect the body from antigens through phagocytosis","It is also known as a reservoir of erythrocytes and platelets","It decomposes newly formed reticulocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One function: it has reticuloendothelial cells that make macrophages which protect the body from antigens through phagocytosis.  Why A wrong: That is a separate listed function (removal of damaged cells).  Why C wrong: That is a separate function (reservoir of erythrocytes and platelets).  Why D wrong: That is a separate function (decomposing newly formed reticulocytes)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The spleen is also known as a reservoir of which blood components?","options":["Leukocytes and plasma","Erythrocytes and platelets","Lymphocytes and serum","Neutrophils and basophils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spleen serves as a reservoir of erythrocytes and platelets.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the components stated as stored by the spleen."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"During sympathetic stimulation causing constriction of the splenic capsule, approximately how much blood is squeezed out into the general circulation within 1 minute?","options":["100 ml","200 ml","350 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathetic stimulation causes constriction of the capsule, squeezing out an estimated 200 ml of blood into general circulation within 1 minute.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated volume."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"During severe illness when the spleen fails to function, what may occur?","options":["Increased red blood cell production","It participates in the formation and development of blood cells or hematopoiesis if bone marrow fails to function","Permanent loss of immunity","Excessive clotting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spleen participates in the formation and development of blood cells (hematopoiesis) if bone marrow fails to function during severe illness.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not described as the spleen's role in this situation."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Who is known as the father of blood circulation?","options":["William Beaumont","William Harvey","William Osler","Andreas Vesalius"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: William Harvey discovered blood circulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not credited with discovering blood circulation in the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The heart is a cone-shaped organ made of which type of muscle?","options":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Muscular cone-shaped (cardiac muscle) that pumps blood","Elastic cartilage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The heart is the muscular cone-shaped organ which pumps blood.  Why A wrong: It is not smooth muscle.  Why B wrong: Skeletal muscle is voluntary striated muscle, not the heart muscle proper.  Why D wrong: Cartilage is not muscle."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The heart is located between the lungs, about how far above the sternum and 1/3 part right to sternum?","options":["About 2/3 part on left to sternum","Entirely on the right side","Centered exactly on the midline","Below the diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heart is located between the lungs, about 2/3 part on left to sternum and 1/3 part right to sternum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated 2/3 left, 1/3 right position."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal heart weight range in an adult?","options":["100–150 gm","250–300 gm in female and 300–320 gm in male","400–450 gm in both sexes","500–550 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal heart weight is about 250–300 gm in female and 300–320 gm in male.  Why A/C/D wrong: Outside the stated ranges."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The apex of the heart is mostly formed by which structure?","options":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The apex of the heart is mostly formed by the left ventricle.  Why A/B/D wrong: The apex is formed by the left ventricle, not these chambers."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The apex of the heart is located at which intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line?","options":["Near 3rd intercostal space","Near 5th intercostal space","Near 7th intercostal space","At the sternal angle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apex is located near the 5th intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated apex location."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The base of the heart is superior part of the heart, which is formed by most of:","options":["Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium","The apex"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The base of the heart is the superior part formed by most of the left atrium.  Why A/B wrong: The ventricles form the apex region, not the base.  Why D wrong: The apex is the opposite (inferior) part."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The muscles of the heart are described as:","options":["Voluntary and smooth","Involuntary and striated in nature","Voluntary and striated","Involuntary and smooth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Muscles of the heart are involuntary and striated in nature.  Why A/C wrong: Cardiac muscle is involuntary, not voluntary.  Why D wrong: Cardiac muscle is striated, not smooth."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The outermost layer of the heart, which is the protective covering of the heart, is the:","options":["Endocardium","Myocardium","Pericardium","Epithelium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pericardium is the outermost layer and is the protective covering of the heart.  Why A wrong: The endocardium is the innermost layer.  Why B wrong: The myocardium is the middle muscular layer.  Why D wrong: Epithelium is a tissue type, not a heart layer here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The pericardium has how many layers?","options":["One layer","Two layers","Three layers","Four layers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pericardium has two layers.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is described as having two layers."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The tough, outermost layer of the pericardium that prevents the heart from expanding too much is the:","options":["Serous pericardium","Visceral pericardium","Fibrous pericardium","Parietal pericardium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The fibrous pericardium is made of connective tissue; it is the tough, outermost layer of the pericardium that prevents the heart from expanding too much.  Why A wrong: The serous pericardium is the inner layer.  Why B/D wrong: Visceral and parietal are sub-layers of the serous pericardium."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The serous pericardium produces pericardial fluid that lubricates the heart as it beats. The inner serous layer firmly attached to the heart is called:","options":["Parietal layer","Visceral layer (epicardium)","Fibrous layer","Myocardial layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The serous pericardium has two layers — parietal is outer and the visceral layer (epicardium) is inner most, firmly attached to the heart.  Why A wrong: The parietal layer is the outer serous layer.  Why C wrong: Fibrous pericardium is a separate outer layer.  Why D wrong: The myocardium is the muscle layer, not serous."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The space between the parietal and visceral serous layers, which holds clear watery liquid (pericardial fluid), is known as the:","options":["Pleural space","Pericardial space","Mediastinal space","Peritoneal space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pericardial space is between the parietal and visceral serous layers and holds clear watery liquid that lubricates the heart from friction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other body cavities/spaces, not the heart's serous space."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Normal volume of pericardial fluid is up to:","options":["10 ml","50 ml","100 ml","200 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal volume of pericardial fluid is up to 50 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated normal volume; an increase and accumulation above this space is called pericardial effusion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"If pericardial fluid is increased and accumulates in the pericardial space, this condition is called:","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Pericardial effusion","Pericarditis","Myocarditis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An increase and accumulation of fluid in the pericardial space is called pericardial effusion.  Why A wrong: Cardiac tamponade occurs if accumulated fluid deteriorates/reduces cardiac function.  Why C/D wrong: Inflammation terms not described for fluid accumulation here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"If the accumulated pericardial fluid deteriorates or reduces cardiac function, this is called:","options":["Pericardial effusion","Cardiac tamponade","Heart failure","Cardiomyopathy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If accumulated fluid deteriorates/reduces cardiac function then it is called cardiac tamponade.  Why A wrong: Pericardial effusion is the accumulation itself, before functional compromise.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specific term for accumulated pericardial fluid compromising function."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The middle layer of the heart, made up of cardiomyocytes, is the:","options":["Pericardium","Myocardium","Endocardium","Epicardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The myocardium is the middle layer of the heart and is made up of cardiomyocytes (the actually contracting muscle of the heart).  Why A wrong: Pericardium is the outer protective covering.  Why C wrong: Endocardium is the innermost layer.  Why D wrong: Epicardium is the visceral serous layer, not the muscular middle layer."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The myocardium is found in the walls of all four chambers of the heart, though it is thicker in which chambers?","options":["Thicker in the atria and thinner in the ventricles","Thicker in the ventricles and thinner in the atria","Equally thick in all chambers","Present only in the left ventricle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The myocardium is found in the walls of all four chambers but is thicker in the ventricles and thinner in the atria.  Why A wrong: It is thicker in ventricles, not atria.  Why C/D wrong: Contradicts the stated distribution."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The innermost layer of the heart that directly contacts the blood is the:","options":["Myocardium","Pericardium","Endocardium","Fibrous pericardium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart that directly contacts the blood; it is made up of endothelial lining of the epithelial tissues.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the innermost blood-contacting layer."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The right atrium receives which type of blood and from which vessels?","options":["Oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins","Deoxygenated blood from the body systemic circulation via superior and inferior vena cava","Oxygenated blood from the aorta","Deoxygenated blood from the lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body systemic circulation via the superior and inferior vena cava.  Why A wrong: Oxygenated blood from pulmonary veins goes to the left atrium.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the right atrium's inflow."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The right ventricle receives deoxygenated blood from the right atrium and pumps it to the:","options":["Aorta","Left atrium","Lungs via the pulmonary arteries","Coronary arteries"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The right ventricle receives deoxygenated blood from the right atrium and pumps it to the lungs via the pulmonary arteries.  Why A wrong: The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle.  Why B/D wrong: Do not match the right ventricle's outflow."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the:","options":["Pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary veins","Vena cava","Aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the four pulmonary veins.  Why A wrong: Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.  Why C/D wrong: Vena cava drains to the right atrium; aorta carries blood out of the left ventricle."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Which ventricle has the thickest myocardium (10–15 mm) because it pumps blood into the systemic circulation via the aorta?","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The left ventricle has the thickest myocardium (10–15 mm) because it has to pump blood to other chambers and to the systemic circulation via the aorta, which requires more workload.  Why A wrong: The right ventricle wall is thinner since it only pumps to the lungs.  Why C/D wrong: Atria have thin walls."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The atrioventricular valve located on the right side of the heart between the right atrium and right ventricle is the:","options":["Mitral (bicuspid) valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tricuspid valve is the atrioventricular valve on the right side between the right atrium and right ventricle.  Why A wrong: The mitral (bicuspid) valve is on the left side.  Why C/D wrong: These are semilunar valves, not AV valves."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The atrioventricular valve located on the left side of the heart between the left atrium and left ventricle is also called the:","options":["Tricuspid valve","Bicuspid (mitral) valve","Semilunar valve","Aortic valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mitral valve is also known as the bicuspid valve; it is the AV valve located on the left side between the left atrium and left ventricle.  Why A wrong: The tricuspid valve is on the right side.  Why C/D wrong: These are not AV valves."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The atrioventricular valves close when there is high pressure during which heart sound?","options":["First heart sound (S1 or LUBB)","Second heart sound (S2 or DUBB)","Third heart sound (S3)","Fourth heart sound (S4)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The atrioventricular valves close when there is a high pressure, producing the first heart sound (S1 or LUBB), which is best auscultated with a stethoscope.  Why B wrong: S2 (DUBB) is the second heart sound, produced by closure of semilunar valves.  Why C/D wrong: S3 and S4 are abnormal/extra heart sounds, not the AV closure sound."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Which semilunar valve is located between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery?","options":["Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pulmonary valve is located between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery.  Why A wrong: The aortic valve is located between the left ventricle and the aorta.  Why C/D wrong: These are AV valves, not semilunar valves."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The semilunar valves prevent backflow of blood and produce which heart sound when they close?","options":["First heart sound (S1 or LUBB)","Second heart sound (S2 or DUBB)","Third heart sound (S3)","Fourth heart sound (S4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The semilunar valves prevent blood from flowing back into the ventricles during relaxation and cause the second heart sound (S2 or DUBB) when they close.  Why A wrong: S1 results from AV valve closure.  Why C/D wrong: S3 and S4 are abnormal heart sounds."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The third heart sound (S3) indicates early ventricular filling and also is known as:","options":["Atrial gallop","Ventricular gallop","Murmur","Normal split"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: S3 indicates early ventricular filling, also known as ventricular gallop, associated with heart failure.  Why A wrong: Atrial gallop is the S4 sound.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the S3 designation."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The fourth heart sound (S4), known as atrial gallop, indicates which pathological condition?","options":["Normal finding in all adults","Pathological condition associated with heart failure","Pericardial effusion","Valve stenosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: S4 (atrial gallop) indicates a pathological condition, also associated with heart failure; though it is considered normal in children.  Why A wrong: It is pathological in adults, only normal in children.  Why C/D wrong: Not the condition the source links to S4."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The fourth heart sound (S4) is considered normal in which population?","options":["Elderly adults","Pregnant women","Children","Athletes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: S4 is considered normal in children.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies children, not these groups."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The auscultation site of the aortic valve is at which location?","options":["2nd inter-costal space on the right of sternum","2nd inter-costal space on left of sternum","4th inter-costal space on left of sternum","5th inter-costal space at mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The aortic valve is auscultated at the 2nd inter-costal space on the right of sternum.  Why B wrong: 2nd ICS on the left is the pulmonary valve site.  Why C wrong: 4th ICS on left of sternum is the tricuspid valve site.  Why D wrong: 5th ICS mid-clavicular is the mitral valve (apex) site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The pulmonary valve is auscultated at which site?","options":["2nd inter-costal space on the right of sternum","2nd inter-costal space on left of sternum","4th inter-costal space on left of sternum","5th inter-costal space on left of sternum (mid-clavicular line)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pulmonary valve is auscultated at the 2nd inter-costal space on the left of sternum (Erb's point is at the 3rd left ICS at the left sternal border).  Why A wrong: 2nd ICS right is the aortic site.  Why C wrong: 4th ICS left is the tricuspid site.  Why D wrong: 5th ICS left (mid-clavicular) is the mitral/apex site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The tricuspid valve is auscultated at which site?","options":["4th inter-costal space on left of sternum","2nd inter-costal space on right of sternum","5th inter-costal space at mid-clavicular line","3rd left intercostal space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tricuspid valve is auscultated at the 4th inter-costal space on left of sternum (left sternal border).  Why B wrong: That is the aortic site.  Why C wrong: That is the mitral/apex site.  Why D wrong: That is Erb's point."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The mitral valve is best auscultated at which site, which is also known as the apical pulse site?","options":["2nd ICS right of sternum","2nd ICS left of sternum","5th inter-costal space at mid-clavicular line in left side","4th ICS left of sternum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The mitral valve in adults is at the 5th inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line in the left side (in infants/children at the 4th ICS near mid-clavicular line in left side); it is also the apical pulse / most accurate pulse assessment site, especially in children.  Why A wrong: That is the aortic site.  Why B wrong: That is the pulmonary site.  Why D wrong: That is the tricuspid site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"In infants and children, the mitral valve / apical pulse is auscultated at:","options":["5th ICS mid-clavicular line in left side","4th inter-costal space near mid-clavicular line in left side","2nd ICS right of sternum","3rd left ICS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In infants and children, it is at the 4th inter-costal space near mid-clavicular line in the left side, and this is the most accurate pulse assessment site in children.  Why A wrong: 5th ICS mid-clavicular is the adult mitral site.  Why C/D wrong: These are other valve sites."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Erb's point, used for auscultation, is located at:","options":["2nd left intercostal space","3rd left intercostal space (left sternal border)","4th left intercostal space","5th left intercostal space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Erb's point is at the 3rd left intercostal space at the left sternal border.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated Erb's point location."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary circulation refers to the circulation of blood in the arteries and veins that supply which structure?","options":["The lungs","The heart muscle (myocardium)","The brain","The kidneys"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coronary circulation is the circulation of blood in the arteries and veins that supply the heart muscle (myocardium).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are supplied by other circulations, not coronary."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary arteries supply oxygenated blood to the heart muscle, whereas cardiac veins drain away blood that is:","options":["Highly oxygenated","De-oxygenated","Filtered","Clotted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coronary arteries supply oxygenated blood to the heart muscle, whereas cardiac veins drain away the blood after it has been de-oxygenated.  Why A/C/D wrong: The veins drain de-oxygenated blood, not oxygenated, filtered, or clotted blood."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary arteries are branches of the:","options":["Pulmonary artery","Ascending aorta","Vena cava","Descending aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The coronary arteries (2) are the branches of the ascending aorta.  Why A/C/D wrong: They arise from the ascending aorta, not these vessels."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Interruptions of coronary circulation quickly cause heart attacks (myocardial infarctions), in which the heart muscle is damaged by:","options":["Excess oxygen","Oxygen starvation","High calcium","Bacterial toxins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Interruptions of coronary circulation quickly cause heart attacks (myocardial infarctions), in which the heart muscle is damaged by oxygen starvation.  Why A/C/D wrong: The damage is due to oxygen starvation (ischemia), not these."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Usually, interruptions of coronary circulation are caused by which conditions?","options":["Coronary ischemia and embolus","Hypertension only","Dehydration","Anemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Usually, interruptions are caused by coronary ischemia and embolus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated causes."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary blood flow at rest in humans is approximately what percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["1%","5% of total cardiac output (about 250 ml/min)","15%","25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coronary blood flow at rest in humans is 250 ml/min or 5% of total cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5% / 250 ml/min."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"During exercise, coronary blood flow in humans is increased to:","options":[">100 ml/min",">250 ml/min","50 ml/min","25 ml/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coronary blood flow during exercise in humans is increased >250 ml/min.  Why A/C/D wrong: Below or unrelated to the stated exercise value."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary perfusion pressure (CPP) during CPR should maintain between which range for maximal myocardial reperfusion?","options":["5–10 mm hg","15–25 mm hg","40–60 mm hg","80–100 mm hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CPP during CPR should maintain between 15–25 mm hg for maximal myocardial reperfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are outside the stated CPR target range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The ascending aorta gives rise to which two coronary arteries?","options":["Left coronary artery and right coronary artery","Anterior and posterior arteries","Circumflex and marginal arteries","Pulmonary and bronchial arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ascending aorta branches into the left coronary artery and right coronary artery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two main coronary branches of the ascending aorta."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The left coronary artery branches into the anterior interventricular branch (left anterior descending / LAD) and the circumflex branch. The LAD supplies which area?","options":["Wall of the left atrium and ventricle","Anterior wall of both ventricles","Posterior wall of both ventricles","Right ventricle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior interventricular branch / LAD supplies the anterior wall of both ventricles.  Why A wrong: The wall of the left atrium and ventricle is supplied by the circumflex branch.  Why C wrong: The posterior wall of both ventricles is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch (of the right coronary).  Why D wrong: The right ventricle is supplied by the right marginal branch."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The circumflex branch of the left coronary artery supplies which area?","options":["Anterior wall of both ventricles","Wall of the left atrium and ventricle","Posterior wall of both ventricles","Right ventricle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The circumflex branch supplies the wall of the left atrium and ventricle.  Why A wrong: The anterior wall of both ventricles is supplied by the LAD.  Why C wrong: The posterior wall is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch.  Why D wrong: The right ventricle is supplied by the right marginal branch."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The right coronary artery branches into the posterior interventricular branch and the right marginal branch. The right marginal branch supplies:","options":["Anterior wall of both ventricles","Posterior wall of both ventricles","Right ventricle","Left atrium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The right marginal branch supplies the right ventricle.  Why A wrong: Anterior wall of both ventricles is the LAD's territory.  Why B wrong: Posterior wall of both ventricles is the posterior interventricular branch's territory.  Why D wrong: The left atrium is supplied by the circumflex."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The posterior interventricular branch of the right coronary artery supplies which area?","options":["Anterior wall of both ventricles","Posterior wall of both ventricles","Right ventricle only","Left atrium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior interventricular branch supplies the posterior wall of both ventricles.  Why A wrong: The anterior wall is supplied by the LAD.  Why C wrong: The right ventricle is the right marginal branch's territory.  Why D wrong: The left atrium is supplied by the circumflex."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The right coronary artery (RCA) mainly supplies blood to the right atrium, right ventricle, inferior portion of the left ventricle, posterior septal wall, and:","options":["SA and AV nodes","The entire left atrium","The pulmonary trunk","The anterior septal wall only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RCA mainly supplies the right atrium and right ventricle, inferior portion of the left ventricle, posterior septal wall, and SA & AV nodes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated territories of the RCA."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The left coronary artery has two branches: the left anterior descending (LAD) artery and the:","options":["Right marginal artery","Circumflex (Cx) artery","Posterior descending artery","Pulmonary artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The left coronary artery has two branches — the left anterior descending (LAD) artery and the circumflex (Cx) artery.  Why A/C wrong: These are branches of the right coronary artery.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary artery is not a coronary branch."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies the blood to the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the anterior ventricular septum, and the apex of the left ventricle. It is also known as:","options":["Posterior inter-ventricular artery","Anterior inter-ventricular artery","Right marginal artery","Circumflex artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LAD is also known as the anterior inter-ventricular artery; blockage in this artery has the highest rate of mortality.  Why A wrong: That is the posterior descending artery (RCA branch).  Why C/D wrong: These are different coronary arteries."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A blockage in which coronary artery has the highest rate of mortality?","options":["Right marginal artery","Circumflex artery","Left anterior descending (LAD) / anterior interventricular artery","Posterior descending artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Blockage in the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) has the highest rate of mortality.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source highlights the LAD specifically."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The circumflex (Cx) artery supplies blood to the left atrium and the lateral and posterior surface of the:","options":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Pulmonary trunk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The circumflex artery supplies blood to the left atrium and the lateral and posterior surface of the left ventricle.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated territory of the circumflex."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Venous circulation of the heart has which two branches?","options":["Right coronary vein and left coronary vein","Pulmonary vein and bronchial vein","Superior and inferior vena cava","Azygos and hemiazygos veins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Venous circulation also has two branches: the right coronary vein and the left coronary vein.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the coronary venous branches stated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The left coronary artery (LCA) supplies blood to which areas? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Left ventricle, left atrium","Inter-ventricular septum (anterior 2/3)","Posterior septal wall and SA & AV nodes","Apex of the heart","Right ventricle (only)"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why A correct: The LCA supplies the left ventricle and left atrium.  Why B correct: It supplies the inter-ventricular septum (anterior 2/3).  Why D correct: It supplies the apex of the heart.  Why C wrong: The posterior septal wall and SA & AV nodes are supplied by the RCA.  Why E wrong: The right ventricle is supplied by the RCA, not the LCA."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the regional blood supply table, the circumflex artery of the left coronary artery supplies which areas? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Left atrium","Lateral wall of the left ventricle","Posterior wall of the left ventricle","Right ventricle apex"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why A/B/C correct: The circumflex artery supplies the left atrium and the lateral and posterior wall of the left ventricle.  Why D wrong: The right ventricle is supplied by the right coronary artery, not the circumflex."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the right coronary artery (RCA) supplies which areas? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Right atrium and right ventricle","SA node and AV node","Inferior portion of left ventricle","Anterior 2/3 of interventricular septum"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why A/B/C correct: The RCA supplies the right atrium and right ventricle, SA node and AV node, and the inferior portion of the left ventricle.  Why D wrong: The anterior 2/3 of the interventricular septum is supplied by the LAD (left coronary), not the RCA."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The posterior descending artery (PDA) of the RCA, also known as the posterior interventricular branch, supplies which areas? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Right ventricle (posterior aspect)","Left ventricle (posterior aspect)","Inter-ventricular septum (posterior aspect)","Apex of the left ventricle (anterior aspect)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why A/B/C correct: The PDA supplies the posterior aspect of the right ventricle, left ventricle, and inter-ventricular septum.  Why D wrong: The apex/anterior aspect is supplied by the LAD, not the PDA."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal range for cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP), which is calculated as MAP − ICP?","options":["5–10 mm hg","80–100 mm hg","60–70 mm hg","0–5 mm hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP) has a normal range of 80–100 mm hg.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated normal cerebral perfusion pressure range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Coronary perfusion pressure (CoPP) is calculated as ADP (aortic diastolic pressure) − LVEDP. Its normal range is:","options":["80–90 mm hg","60–70 mm hg","0–5 mm hg","20–30 mm hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coronary perfusion pressure (CoPP = ADP − LVEDP, during CPR it should be CPP = ADP) has a normal range of 60–70 mm hg.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated CoPP range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Abdominal perfusion pressure (APP) is calculated as MAP − IAP (intra-abdominal pressure). Its normal range is:","options":["80–100 mm hg","60–70 mm hg","0–5 mm hg","100–120 mm hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal perfusion pressure (APP = MAP − IAP) has a normal range of 60–70 mm hg.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated APP range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Collateral circulation refers to alternative routes of blood flow in the heart that develop as a compensation when:","options":["Heart rate increases","Heart blood flow is decreased (diastole), as a result of occlusion or coronary arterial congestion","Blood pressure rises","Oxygen demand decreases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Collateral circulation is the greatest flow of blood into myocardium occurs when heart blood flow (diastole) is decreased as a result of occlusion or coronary arterial congestion, developing as a compensation when heart rate is low, leading to decreased increased coronary blood flow — time for myocardial perfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Collateral routes develop with decreased flow/occlusion, not with increased rate, rising BP, or lower oxygen demand."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"In coronary occlusion, for the presence of anastomosis, if one blood vessel of the heart is blocked, what occurs?","options":["The blood vessel that fast area will be supplied by another blood vessels, which is known as collateral circulation","Immediate cardiac arrest always","The blocked area produces more blood cells","The heart stops conducting impulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the presence of anastomosis, if one blood vessel is blocked, that area will be supplied by another blood vessel, which is known as collateral circulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anastomosis provides alternative supply rather than causing arrest or these other outcomes."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The conduction system of the heart generates which type of impulses to cause contractions or excitability?","options":["Chemical impulses only","Cardiac muscle cells have ability to generate own electrical impulses","Hormonal impulses","Mechanical impulses only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac muscle cells have the ability to generate their own electrical impulses to generate contractions or excitability.  Why A/C/D wrong: The conduction system generates electrical impulses, not solely chemical/hormonal/mechanical ones."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The sino-atrial (SA) node is known as the pacemaker of the heart. Where is it located?","options":["At the junction of the superior vena cava in the wall of the right atrium","In the wall of the left ventricle","At the apex of the heart","In the interventricular septum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The SA node is the primary pacemaker of the heart; its location is at the junction of the superior vena cava in the wall of the right atrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated location of the SA node."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The SA node generates approximately how many impulses (action potentials) per minute?","options":["20–40 impulses/minute","40–60 impulses/minute","60–100 impulses per minutes","100–150 impulses/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The SA node generates action potentials at 60–100 impulses per minute.  Why A wrong: 20–40 beats/minute is the Purkinje fiber rate.  Why B wrong: 40–60 impulses/min is the AV node rate.  Why D wrong: Not a rate given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The SA node is controlled by which nervous system?","options":["Somatic nervous system","Autonomic nervous system","Central nervous system only","Enteric nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SA node is controlled by the autonomic nervous system.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the controlling system stated for the SA node."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Where is the atrioventricular (AV) node located?","options":["At the lower aspect of the right atrium near the base of the right atrium","At the apex of the left ventricle","In the pulmonary trunk","In the left atrial wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AV node is located at the lower aspect or base of the right atrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated AV node location."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The AV node receives electrical impulses from the SA node and generates approximately how many impulses per minutes?","options":["20–40 impulses/minute","40–60 impulses/minute","60–100 impulses/minute","100–120 impulses/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the SA node fails to generate impulses, the AV node generates 40–60 impulses per minutes.  Why A wrong: 20–40 is the Purkinje fiber rate.  Why C wrong: 60–100 is the SA node rate.  Why D wrong: Not a stated rate."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The Bundle of His is located in the inter-ventricular septum and bifurcates into the right and left bundle branches that supply electrical impulses to the right and left side of the heart. It is also called the:","options":["Purkinje fiber","Bundle branch block","SA node bundle","Atrial bundle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Bundle of His is located in the inter-ventricular septum and bifurcates into the right and left bundle branches; it is also called the bundle branch block (as named in the source) supplying the right and left side of the heart.  Why A wrong: Purkinje fibers are the terminal part of the conduction system.  Why C/D wrong: Not the names given to the Bundle of His."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Disruption in impulse conduction through the Bundle of His and its bundle branches occurs in:","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Bundle branch block (right bundle branch / left bundle branch)","Sinus arrhythmia","Ventricular tachycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If any disruption in impulse conduction occurs, the right bundle branch terminates in the right side and the left bundle branch in the left side — this disruption is a bundle branch block.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different conduction/rhythm abnormalities not described here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The Purkinje fibers are the terminal part of the conduction system. They generate how many beats/minute electrical impulses?","options":["60–100 beats/minute","40–60 beats/minute","20 to 40 beat/minute","100–120 beats/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Purkinje fibers generate 20 to 40 beat/minute electrical impulses when all other pacemakers (SA & AV node) fail to generate electrical impulses.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is the SA node rate.  Why B wrong: 40–60 is the AV node rate.  Why D wrong: Not a stated rate."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The Purkinje fibers carry conduction impulses throughout which structure?","options":["The atrial walls only","The ventricular walls","The pulmonary arteries","The pericardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Purkinje fibers carry conduction electrical impulses throughout the ventricular walls.  Why A/C/D wrong: They distribute impulses through the ventricular walls, not these structures."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"In the electrocardiogram (ECG), the Purkinje fibers cause which wave?","options":["P wave indicating atrial depolarization","U-wave, indicating Purkinje fiber repolarization (sometimes hypokalemia)","QRS complex","T wave indicating atrial repolarization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In ECG, U-wave indicates Purkinje fiber repolarization and sometimes hypokalemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source attributes the U-wave (not these waves) to the Purkinje fibers."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"The cardiac cycle is some coordinated activities, while takes place during each heartbeat called cardiac cycle. During each heartbeat, the heart contracts then relaxes. The contraction (period of contraction) is called:","options":["Diastole","Systole","Tachycardia","Bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Systole is the period of contraction; diastole is the period of relaxation.  Why A wrong: Diastole is relaxation, not contraction.  Why C/D wrong: These describe heart rate, not cardiac cycle phases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"When the heart rate is normal (72 beats per minute), the normal duration of each cardiac cycle is:","options":["0.2 sec","0.4 sec","0.8 sec","1.0 sec"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the heart is normal (72 beats per minute), the normal duration of each cardiac cycle is 0.8 sec.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the stated normal cardiac cycle duration."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal duration of atrial systole (contraction of atria) in the cardiac cycle?","options":["0.1 second","0.3 second","0.4 second","0.8 second"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atrial systole (contraction of atria) has a normal duration of 0.1 second.  Why B wrong: 0.3 second is the ventricular systole duration.  Why C wrong: 0.4 second is the complete cardiac diastole duration.  Why D wrong: 0.8 sec is the full cardiac cycle."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal duration of ventricular systole (contraction of ventricles)?","options":["0.1 second","0.3 second","0.4 second","0.8 second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ventricular systole (contraction of ventricles) has a normal duration of 0.3 second.  Why A wrong: 0.1 second is atrial systole.  Why C wrong: 0.4 second is complete cardiac diastole.  Why D wrong: 0.8 sec is the full cycle."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Complete cardiac diastole, which is the relaxation of the atria and ventricles, has a normal duration of:","options":["0.1 second","0.3 second","0.4 second","0.8 second"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Complete cardiac diastole (relaxation of the atria and ventricles) has a normal duration of 0.4 second.  Why A wrong: 0.1 second is atrial systole.  Why B wrong: 0.3 second is ventricular systole.  Why D wrong: 0.8 sec is the full cardiac cycle."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out with each contraction/beat by the:","options":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out with each contraction/beat by the left ventricle.  Why A/B/D wrong: Stroke volume refers to the left ventricle's output per beat."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the normal stroke volume?","options":["50 ml per beat","70 ml per beat","100 ml per beat","120 ml per beat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal stroke volume is 70 ml per beat.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated normal stroke volume."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the average / normal heart rate?","options":["40 b/m","60–100 b/m and average 72 beats/minute","100–120 b/m","120–140 b/m"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal heart rate is 60–100 b/m and average 72 beats/minute.  Why A/C/D wrong: Outside the stated normal range/average."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Heart rate is increased by the sympathetic nervous system and decreased by which system?","options":["Somatic nervous system","Parasympathetic system","Central nervous system","Enteric nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heart rate is increased by the sympathetic nervous system and decreased by the parasympathetic system.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the system stated to decrease heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Bradycardia is defined as a heart rate that is:","options":["Less than 60 b/m","Greater than 100 b/m","Between 60 and 100 b/m","Exactly 72 b/m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bradycardia is a heart rate less than 60 b/m.  Why B wrong: A rate greater than 100 b/m is tachycardia.  Why C/D wrong: These are within the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Tachycardia is defined as a heart rate that is:","options":["Less than 60 b/m","Between 60 and 100 b/m","Greater than 100 b/m","Exactly 72 b/m"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Tachycardia is a heart rate greater than 100 b/m.  Why A wrong: Less than 60 b/m is bradycardia.  Why B/D wrong: These are within or at the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What happens to cardiac output when the heart rate is faster (tachycardia)?","options":["Cardiac output increases without limit","The faster heart rate, then less time the heart has filling, resulted the cardiac output decreased","Cardiac output stays the same","Stroke volume increases proportionally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With a faster heart rate, there is less time for the heart to fill, which results in decreased cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: Less filling time reduces cardiac output, contradicting these options."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Cardiac output is defined as the volume of blood pumped per minute by the:","options":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped per minute by the left ventricle.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cardiac output refers to the left ventricle's per-minute output."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"What is the formula for cardiac output?","options":["Cardiac Output = Heart Rate − Stroke Volume","Cardiac Output = Heart Rate × Stroke Volume","Cardiac Output = Heart Rate + Stroke Volume","Cardiac Output = Stroke Volume ÷ Heart Rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Formula: Cardiac Output = Heart Rate × Stroke Volume.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect operations for cardiac output."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Using the formula, what is the normal cardiac output (5 litres blood per minute means a healthy human heart normally pumping about)?","options":["3 litres per minute","5 litres per minute","8 litres per minute","10 litres per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal cardiac output is 5 litres blood per minute — a healthy human heart normally pumps about 5 litres per minute.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated normal cardiac output."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Normally, the heart is receiving only what percentage of total cardiac output by coronary circulation?","options":["5% (250 ml/min)","15%","25%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normally, the heart is receiving only 5% (250 ml/min) from total cardiac output by coronary circulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated coronary share of cardiac output."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"In the cardiac cycle pie/timing, the atrial systole is 0.1 sec, the ventricular systole is 0.3 sec, and the complete cardiac diastole is:","options":["0.1 sec","0.3 sec","0.4 sec","0.8 sec"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The total period of one cycle = 0.8 sec; atrial systole 0.1 sec, ventricular systole 0.3 sec, and complete cardiac diastole 0.4 sec.  Why A/B wrong: These are systole durations.  Why D wrong: 0.8 sec is the total cycle, not the diastole portion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"Contractility is the force of the cardiac muscle contractions. It is increased by sympathetic nerves and leads to:","options":["Decreased cardiac output","Increased cardiac output","No change in cardiac output","Decreased heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contractility is the force of cardiac muscle contractions; it is increased by sympathetic nerves and leads to increased cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: Increased contractility increases cardiac output, contradicting these."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the \"Total Percentage of Cardiac Output to Body Organs\" table, which organ system receives the highest blood flow (1400 ml/min, 25%)?","options":["Kidneys","GI tract & Liver","Brain","Skeletal muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GI tract & liver receive the highest blood flow at 1400 ml/min (25%).  Why A wrong: Kidneys receive 1000 ml/min (20%).  Why C wrong: Brain receives 750 ml/min (13–15%).  Why D wrong: Skeletal muscles receive 1000 ml/min (20%)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the cardiac output distribution table, the kidneys receive approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["1400 ml/min, 25%","1000 ml/min, 20%","750 ml/min, 13–15%","250 ml/min, 5%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys receive 1000 ml/min (20%) of total cardiac output.  Why A wrong: 1400 ml/min (25%) is the GI tract & liver.  Why C wrong: 750 ml/min (13–15%) is the brain.  Why D wrong: 250 ml/min (5%) is the heart."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the brain receives approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["1400 ml/min, 25%","1000 ml/min, 20%","750 ml/min, 13–15%","250 ml/min, 5%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The brain receives 750 ml/min (13–15%) of total cardiac output.  Why A wrong: That is the GI tract & liver.  Why B wrong: That is the kidneys / skeletal muscles.  Why D wrong: That is the heart."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the heart itself receives approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["1400 ml/min, 25%","1000 ml/min, 20%","750 ml/min, 13–15%","250 ml/min, 5%"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The heart receives 250 ml/min (5%) of total cardiac output.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are the values for the GI tract & liver, kidneys/skeletal muscle, and brain respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, skeletal muscles receive approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["1400 ml/min, 25%","1000 ml/min, 20%","500 ml/min, 5%","600 ml/min, 10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The skeletal muscles receive 1000 ml/min (20%) of total cardiac output.  Why A wrong: That is GI tract & liver.  Why C wrong: 500 ml/min (5%) is the skin.  Why D wrong: 600 ml/min (10%) is the other organs."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the skin receives approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["250 ml/min, 5%","500 ml/min, 5%","600 ml/min, 10%","1000 ml/min, 20%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The skin receives 500 ml/min (5%) of total cardiac output.  Why A wrong: 250 ml/min (5%) is the heart.  Why C wrong: 600 ml/min (10%) is the other organs.  Why D wrong: 1000 ml/min (20%) is the kidneys/skeletal muscles."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the \"other organs\" category receives approximately what blood flow and percentage of total cardiac output?","options":["250 ml/min, 5%","500 ml/min, 5%","600 ml/min, 10%","1400 ml/min, 25%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Other organs receive 600 ml/min (10%) of total cardiac output.  Why A wrong: 250 ml/min (5%) is the heart.  Why B wrong: 500 ml/min (5%) is the skin.  Why D wrong: 1400 ml/min (25%) is the GI tract & liver."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"According to the table, the total blood flow to all organs and the total percentage of cardiac output are:","options":["5000 ml/min, 100%","5800 ml/min, ~100%","5000 ml/min, 90%","6000 ml/min, 100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table total organs row gives 5800 ml/min at ~100% of total cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated total in the source table."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A nurse caring for an adult must assess the apical pulse. The most accurate site is:","options":["The carotid artery","The 5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line on the left side","The 2nd ICS right of sternum","The radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mitral/apical site is at the 5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line on the left side in adults, which is the apical pulse site.  Why A/D wrong: Carotid and radial pulses are peripheral, not the apical site.  Why C wrong: 2nd ICS right is the aortic auscultation site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A patient presents with a heart attack (myocardial infarction). Based on the source, the underlying mechanism damaging the heart muscle is interruption of coronary circulation causing:","options":["Excess oxygen delivery","Oxygen starvation due to coronary ischemia and embolus","Increased cardiac output","Pericardial effusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Interruptions of coronary circulation cause myocardial infarction in which the heart muscle is damaged by oxygen starvation, usually caused by coronary ischemia and embolus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated MI mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A patient with an occluded coronary artery survives because an alternative blood supply develops. This phenomenon, where another vessel supplies the area of a blocked vessel via anastomosis, is called:","options":["Collateral circulation","Pulmonary circulation","Portal circulation","Systemic shunting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the presence of anastomosis, if one blood vessel is blocked, that area is supplied by another vessel — known as collateral circulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the term for anastomotic alternative supply in the heart."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"A patient's ECG shows a prominent U-wave. According to the source, this can indicate Purkinje fiber repolarization and sometimes which electrolyte abnormality?","options":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyponatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The U-wave indicates Purkinje fiber repolarization and sometimes hypokalemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source links the U-wave to hypokalemia, not these."},{"section":"Batch 019 · lymphatic system…","question":"If a patient's SA node fails to fire, which structure next assumes pacemaker function at 40–60 impulses per minute?","options":["Purkinje fibers","AV node","Bundle of His only","Right atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the SA node fails, the AV node generates 40–60 impulses per minute.  Why A wrong: Purkinje fibers fire only at 20–40 beats/minute, when all other pacemakers fail.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated escape pacemaker rate/structure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Pre-load is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The force the heart must overcome to eject blood","The initial stretching of cardiac muscle of the left ventricle at end of diastole, reflecting the amount of blood returned to the heart","The resistance offered by peripheral vessels","The contraction of the heart muscle during systole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-load is the initial stretching of cardiac muscle of the left ventricle at the end of diastole and is the amount of blood returned to the heart.  Why A wrong: That describes after-load.  Why C wrong: Vasoconstriction/peripheral resistance contributes to after-load, not the definition of pre-load.  Why D wrong: That describes depolarization/contraction, not pre-load."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"According to the Frank-Starling law, when the heart muscle is stretched by an increased returning volume of blood, what is the effect?","options":["The heart contracts less forcefully","The heart contracts more forcefully, resulting in increased stroke volume","The stroke volume decreases","The heart rate slows down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Frank-Starling law states that the more the heart is stretched by increased returning volume of blood, the more easily it contracts, resulting in an increased stroke volume.  Why A wrong: Greater stretch produces a more forceful, not weaker, contraction.  Why C wrong: Stroke volume increases, not decreases.  Why D wrong: The law addresses contractility/stroke volume, not heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After-load is the pressure on the left ventricle wall during which phase of the cardiac cycle?","options":["Diastole","Systole","Isovolumetric relaxation","Atrial filling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After-load is the pressure on the left ventricle wall during systole.  Why A wrong: Pre-load relates to end of diastole.  Why C/D wrong: After-load specifically refers to the systolic load/force the heart must overcome to eject blood."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After-load represents which of the following?","options":["The volume of blood returning to the heart","The force/load against which the heart has to contract to eject blood","The relaxation of the heart muscles","The electrical activity of the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After-load is the force/load against which the heart has to contract to eject blood.  Why A wrong: That is pre-load/venous return.  Why C wrong: That is repolarization/relaxation.  Why D wrong: That relates to ECG conduction."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"An increase in systemic vasoconstriction will have which effect on stroke volume?","options":["Increase stroke volume","No effect on stroke volume","Decrease stroke volume","Double the stroke volume"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An increase in systemic vasoconstriction raises after-load, which results in a decreased stroke volume.  Why A/D wrong: Increased after-load opposes ejection, lowering stroke volume.  Why B wrong: Vasoconstriction does affect after-load and therefore stroke volume."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which property of cardiac muscle is defined as the ability of a tissue to give a response to a stimulus?","options":["Rhythmicity","Contractility","Excitability","Conductivity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Excitability is the ability of a tissue to give a response to a stimulus.  Why A wrong: Rhythmicity is the ability to produce impulses regularly.  Why B wrong: Contractility is the ability to shorten in length after receiving a stimulus.  Why D wrong: Conductivity is transmission of impulses."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Rhythmicity of the myocardium refers to which ability?","options":["To shorten in length after receiving a stimulus","To produce impulses regularly","To give a response to a stimulus","To transmit impulses to cardiac muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhythmicity is the ability of a tissue to produce impulses regularly.  Why A wrong: That is contractility.  Why C wrong: That is excitability.  Why D wrong: That is conductivity."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Contractility of cardiac muscle is best described as the ability to:","options":["Shorten in length after receiving a stimulus","Produce impulses regularly","Relax the heart muscle","Conduct impulses via the pacemaker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Contractility is the ability of a tissue to shorten in length after receiving a stimulus.  Why B wrong: That is rhythmicity.  Why C wrong: That is repolarization.  Why D wrong: That is conductivity."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the human heart, impulses produced by the pacemaker (SA node) are transmitted to cardiac muscles by means of a cardiac specialized system. This property is called:","options":["Excitability","Conductivity","Rhythmicity","Depolarization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conductivity is the property by which impulses produced by the pacemaker are transmitted to cardiac muscles by means of a cardiac specialized system.  Why A wrong: Excitability is responsiveness to a stimulus.  Why C wrong: Rhythmicity is regular impulse production.  Why D wrong: Depolarization is the contraction phase of the heart muscle."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the cardiac cycle, depolarization is associated with which event?","options":["Relaxation of the heart muscles","Contraction of the heart muscle","Production of impulses","Conduction of impulses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depolarization is the contraction of the heart muscle.  Why A wrong: That is repolarization.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe rhythmicity and conductivity respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Repolarization of the myocardium corresponds to which event?","options":["Contraction of the heart muscle","Relaxation of the heart muscles","Stimulus response","Impulse generation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Repolarization is the relaxation of the heart muscles.  Why A wrong: That is depolarization.  Why C wrong: That is excitability.  Why D wrong: That is rhythmicity."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Pulse deficit is defined as the difference between which two measurements?","options":["Systolic and diastolic blood pressure","Apical and radial pulse","Heart rate and respiratory rate","Stroke volume and cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse deficit is the difference between apical and radial pulse.  Why A wrong: That difference is pulse pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not part of the pulse deficit definition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which is the correct formula for calculating pulse deficit?","options":["Systolic BP - Diastolic BP","Apical pulse - Radial pulse","Radial pulse - Apical pulse","Apical pulse + Radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse deficit = apical pulse - radial pulse.  Why A wrong: That is pulse pressure.  Why C wrong: The order is apical minus radial; apical is always greater than or equal to radial.  Why D wrong: It is a difference, not a sum."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"A patient has an apical pulse of 76 and a radial pulse of 70. What is the pulse deficit?","options":["146","70","6","76"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse deficit = apical - radial = 76 - 70 = 6 beats/minute (book example).  Why A wrong: 146 is the sum, not the difference.  Why B/D wrong: 70 and 76 are the individual pulse values, not the deficit."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Pulse pressure is defined as the difference between which two pressures?","options":["Mean arterial pressure and diastolic pressure","Systolic and diastolic blood pressure","Apical and radial pressure","Venous and arterial pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic blood pressure (SBP - DBP).  Why A wrong: That is not the definition given.  Why C wrong: That relates to pulse deficit.  Why D wrong: Not relevant."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the normal pulse pressure?","options":["80 mm Hg","120 mm Hg","40 mm Hg","20 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulse pressure is 40 mm Hg.  Why A/B wrong: These are not normal pulse pressure values.  Why D wrong: 20 mm Hg is below normal."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"A patient's blood pressure is 113/76 mm Hg. Calculate the pulse pressure.","options":["76 mm Hg","37 mm Hg","113 mm Hg","189 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse pressure = SBP - DBP = 113 - 76 = 37 mm Hg (book example).  Why A/C wrong: 76 and 113 are the individual pressures.  Why D wrong: 189 is the sum, not the difference."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Systolic blood pressure represents the pressure within arteries during which event?","options":["While the heart is relaxing between contractions","While arteries contract","Mean of all pressures","During venous return"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Systolic blood pressure (SBP) is the pressure within arteries while arteries contract.  Why A wrong: That describes diastolic blood pressure.  Why C/D wrong: Not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Diastolic blood pressure (DBP) is the pressure within arteries while:","options":["The heart is contracting","The heart is relaxing between contractions","The arteries are filling with venous blood","The capillaries exchange gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DBP is the pressure within arteries while the heart is relaxing between contractions.  Why A wrong: That corresponds to systolic pressure.  Why C/D wrong: Not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which statement correctly describes arteries with respect to oxygenation of carried blood?","options":["They carry deoxygenated blood, except the pulmonary artery and umbilical artery","They carry oxygenated blood away from the heart to peripheral cells/tissues, except the pulmonary artery and umbilical artery","All arteries carry deoxygenated blood","All arteries carry oxygenated blood without exception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart to peripheral cells/tissues, except the pulmonary artery and umbilical artery which carry deoxygenated blood.  Why A wrong: The general rule is oxygenated blood; only the named exceptions carry deoxygenated.  Why C wrong: Most arteries carry oxygenated blood.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary and umbilical arteries are exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Arteries further divide into which smaller microscopic vessels?","options":["Venules","Arterioles","Capillaries","Sinuses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Arteries further divide into arterioles, the smallest microscopic unit of arteries.  Why A wrong: Venules belong to the venous side.  Why C wrong: Capillaries are exchange vessels between arterioles and venules.  Why D wrong: Sinuses are dura-mater-lined venous channels."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which is the innermost layer of the artery wall, made of endothelium and the epithelial tissues?","options":["Tunica media","Tunica externa/adventitia","Tunica intima","Tunica serosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The tunica intima is the inner layer of the artery, made of endothelium and the epithelial tissues.  Why A wrong: Tunica media is the middle layer (smooth muscle, elastic, fibrous).  Why B wrong: Tunica externa/adventitia is the outer fibrous layer.  Why D wrong: Tunica serosa is not a layer of the artery in this structure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The tunica media of an artery is composed of which tissues?","options":["Endothelium and epithelial tissues","Smooth muscle, elastic and fibrous layers of the artery, with contraction and dilation of the arteries","Fibrous tissue only","Cardiac muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tunica media is the middle layer made of smooth muscle, elastic and fibrous layers, responsible for contraction and dilation of the arteries.  Why A wrong: That describes the tunica intima.  Why C wrong: That describes the tunica externa.  Why D wrong: Cardiac muscle is in the heart, not artery walls."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The tunica externa (adventitia) of an artery is the outer layer made primarily of which tissue?","options":["Smooth muscle","Endothelium","Fibrous tissue, the layer's firmness that allows arteries to remain open instead of collapsing when cut","Elastic cartilage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The tunica externa/adventitia is the outer layer made of fibrous tissue; this layer's firmness allows arteries to remain open instead of collapsing when cut.  Why A wrong: Smooth muscle is in the tunica media.  Why B wrong: Endothelium is in the tunica intima.  Why D wrong: Not described as elastic cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the oxygenation of blood carried by veins?","options":["Veins carry deoxygenated blood toward the heart from peripheral tissues/cells, except the pulmonary vein and umbilical veins","All veins carry deoxygenated blood without exception","Veins carry oxygenated blood away from the heart","All veins carry oxygenated blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Veins carry deoxygenated blood toward the heart from peripheral tissues/cells, except the pulmonary vein and umbilical veins which carry oxygenated blood.  Why B wrong: The pulmonary and umbilical veins are exceptions that carry oxygenated blood.  Why C/D wrong: Veins generally carry deoxygenated blood (with named exceptions)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Veins further divide into which smaller vessels?","options":["Arterioles","Venules","Capillaries","Sinuses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Veins further divide into venules, which collect blood from capillaries.  Why A wrong: Arterioles are on the arterial side.  Why C wrong: Capillaries are the exchange vessels.  Why D wrong: Sinuses are specialized venous channels formed by dura mater."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Compared to arteries, capillaries allow the exchange of fluids and nutrients between the blood and the interstitial spaces. This characteristic is because:","options":["They have thick muscular walls","They have thinner walls and fewer elastic muscle fibers, which allow veins/vessels to collapse when cut","They contain valves","They carry only deoxygenated blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compared to arteries, veins/capillaries have thinner walls and lesser elastic muscle fibers that allow them to collapse when cut, and capillaries allow exchange of fluids and nutrients between the blood and interstitial spaces.  Why A wrong: Thick muscular walls describe arteries.  Why C wrong: Valves are a feature of veins generally, not the basis for exchange.  Why D wrong: Oxygenation is not the basis of the exchange property."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the approximate diameter of a sinus venous (sinusoid), which prevents backward flow of blood?","options":["0.2 mm","2 mm","20 mm","200 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sinus venous valves are present in most of the veins more than 2 mm in diameter to prevent backward flow of blood.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2 mm threshold."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Capillaries are best described as which type of vessels?","options":["Reservoir vessels","Resistance vessels","Exchange vessels","Conducting vessels"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Capillaries allow the exchange of substances (nutrients and wastage) between the blood and interstitial spaces and are called exchange vessels.  Why A wrong: Veins act as reservoir vessels.  Why B wrong: Arterioles are the chief resistance vessels.  Why D wrong: Large arteries are conducting vessels."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Capillaries carry blood from arterioles and unite them with which vessels for small venous return?","options":["Arteries","Venules","Aorta","Sinuses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Capillaries carry blood from arterioles and unite them with venules for small venous return.  Why A/C wrong: These are large arterial vessels.  Why D wrong: Sinuses are specialized venous channels, not the capillary union point described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which type of blood vessel acts as a reservoir of blood and transports low-pressure blood from venules to the heart?","options":["Arteries","Arterioles","Veins","Capillaries"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the Types of Blood Vessels table, veins act as a reservoir of blood and transport low-pressure blood from venules to the heart.  Why A wrong: Arteries transport high-pressure blood from the heart to smaller arteries and arterioles.  Why B wrong: Arterioles connect arteries and capillaries.  Why D wrong: Capillaries allow gas exchange, nutrient transfer and waste removal."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"According to the Types of Blood Vessels table, which vessels transport high-pressure blood from the heart to smaller arteries and arterioles?","options":["Arteries","Venules","Veins","Capillaries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arteries transport high-pressure blood from the heart to smaller arteries and arterioles.  Why B wrong: Venules connect capillaries and veins.  Why C wrong: Veins transport low-pressure blood to the heart.  Why D wrong: Capillaries allow gas exchange and nutrient/waste transfer."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which vessels connect arteries and capillaries?","options":["Veins","Venules","Arterioles","Sinuses"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Arterioles connect arteries and capillaries.  Why A wrong: Veins return low-pressure blood.  Why B wrong: Venules connect capillaries and veins.  Why D wrong: Sinuses are venous channels."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which vessels connect capillaries and veins?","options":["Arterioles","Venules","Arteries","Aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Venules connect capillaries and veins.  Why A wrong: Arterioles connect arteries and capillaries.  Why C/D wrong: These are large arterial vessels."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The aorta is the main artery in the human body, originating from which structure?","options":["Right ventricle of the heart","Left ventricle of the heart","Right atrium","Pulmonary trunk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The aorta is the main artery in the human body originating from the left ventricle of the heart.  Why A/C wrong: The right side of the heart pumps to the lungs via the pulmonary artery.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary trunk arises from the right ventricle, not the aorta."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The aorta is best described as which of the following?","options":["The smallest artery in the body","The largest artery in the body","A vein carrying deoxygenated blood","A capillary network"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The aorta is the largest artery in the body.  Why A wrong: It is the largest, not the smallest.  Why C wrong: It is an artery, not a vein, and carries oxygenated blood.  Why D wrong: It is not a capillary."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The ascending aorta gives rise to which two main arteries?","options":["Right and left subclavian arteries","Right and left coronary arteries, which supply blood to the heart","Right and left renal arteries","Right and left carotid arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ascending aorta has two main arteries: right and left coronary arteries, which supply blood to the heart.  Why A/C/D wrong: These vessels arise elsewhere; the ascending aorta gives off the coronary arteries."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is the first and largest branch of the arch of the aorta?","options":["Left common carotid artery","Left subclavian artery","Brachiocephalic artery","Right coronary artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The arch of the aorta has three branches; the brachiocephalic artery is the first and largest branch of the arch of the aorta.  Why A wrong: The left common carotid artery is the second branch.  Why B wrong: The left subclavian artery is the third branch.  Why D wrong: The right coronary artery is a branch of the ascending aorta, not the arch."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The descending aorta is a continuation of the arch of the aorta and begins at the level of which thoracic vertebra?","options":["2nd thoracic vertebra","4th thoracic vertebra","6th thoracic vertebra","12th thoracic vertebra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The descending aorta is a continuation of the arch of the aorta and begins at the level of the 4th thoracic vertebra.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated level (T4)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The descending aorta is divided into which two main parts?","options":["Ascending thoracic and ascending abdominal aorta","Descending thoracic aorta and descending abdominal aorta","Right and left aorta","Coronary and carotid aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The descending aorta is divided into the descending thoracic aorta and the descending abdominal aorta.  Why A wrong: They are descending, not ascending, parts.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described division."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which branch of the descending aorta is the largest, supplying the liver, stomach, and spleen?","options":["Renal arteries","Celiac artery","Gonadal arteries","Lumbar arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The celiac artery (unpaired) is the largest branch of the descending aorta and supplies the liver, stomach, abdominal, esophagus, and spleen.  Why A wrong: The renal arteries (paired) supply the kidneys.  Why C wrong: The gonadal arteries (paired) supply the ovary and testicular artery.  Why D wrong: The lumbar arteries (paired) supply the lumbar region."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The superior mesenteric artery (unpaired) supplies blood to which structures?","options":["The kidneys","The intestine, from the duodenum, two-thirds of the transverse colon, and pancreas","The liver and spleen only","The lower limb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The superior mesenteric artery (unpaired) supplies the intestine from the duodenum, two-thirds of the transverse colon, and pancreas.  Why A wrong: The renal arteries supply the kidneys.  Why C wrong: The celiac artery supplies the liver and spleen.  Why D wrong: The common iliac arteries supply the lower limb region."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The middle suprarenal artery (paired) supplies which gland?","options":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland","Pituitary gland","Pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The middle suprarenal artery (paired) supplies the adrenal gland.  Why A/C/D wrong: These glands are not the target of the middle suprarenal artery."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The inferior mesenteric artery (unpaired) supplies blood to which structures?","options":["The kidneys","The large intestine and rectum","The stomach and spleen","The lower limbs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The inferior mesenteric artery (unpaired) supplies the large intestine and rectum area.  Why A wrong: The renal arteries supply the kidneys.  Why C wrong: The celiac artery supplies the stomach and spleen.  Why D wrong: The common iliac arteries supply the lower limbs/pelvic region."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The common iliac artery, the final branch, divides into how many branches that externally supply blood to the lower limb and continue as femoral arteries and external iliac arteries supplying the pelvic region?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The common iliac artery is the final branch that further divides into two branches (external iliac artery supplies blood to the lower limb and continues as the femoral artery, and the internal iliac artery supplying the pelvic region).  Why A/C/D wrong: It divides into two branches as described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The portal system is best described as which of the following?","options":["The systemic arterial circulation of the body","A circulatory system in the human body which delivers nutrient-rich blood to the liver for purification before it is carried to the heart","The pulmonary circulation","The coronary circulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The portal system is the circulatory system in the human body which delivers nutrient-rich blood to the liver for purification before it is carried to the heart.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other circulations, not the hepatic portal system."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the normal portal pressure?","options":["1-4 cm H2O","5-10 mm Hg or 7-14 cm H2O","20-30 mm Hg","40-50 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal portal pressure is 5-10 mm Hg or 7-14 cm H2O.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal range."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Portal hypertension is defined as a high blood pressure inside which vessel?","options":["Aorta","Pulmonary artery","The portal vein (greater than 12 mm Hg)","The coronary artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Portal hypertension is high blood pressure inside the portal vein (greater than 12 mm Hg per the book).  Why A/B/D wrong: These vessels are not part of the portal system definition of portal hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The portal vein is formed by the union of several veins. Which of the following is one of these tributary veins?","options":["Pulmonary vein","Splenic vein","Renal vein","Jugular vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The portal vein is formed by the union of several veins including the splenic vein, inferior and superior mesenteric vein, mesenteric vein, left and right gastric vein, and cystic vein and pancreatic vein.  Why A wrong: The pulmonary vein drains the lungs into the left atrium.  Why C wrong: The renal vein drains the kidney into the inferior vena cava.  Why D wrong: The jugular vein drains the head/neck."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system affects circulation through the release of which neurotransmitters?","options":["Acetylcholine","Epinephrine and nor-epinephrine","Dopamine and serotonin","GABA and glutamate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stimulation of sympathetic nerves through adrenergic fibers releases the neurotransmitters epinephrine and nor-epinephrine.  Why A wrong: Acetylcholine is the parasympathetic (cholinergic) neurotransmitter.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the neurotransmitters described for sympathetic circulatory effects."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an effect of sympathetic nervous system stimulation on circulation and blood pressure?","options":["Decreased heart rate","Increased heart rate, cardiac contractility and conduction","Peripheral vasodilation","Stimulation of the vagus nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate, cardiac contractility and conduction; it also dilates coronary and skeletal blood vessels and causes constriction of peripheral blood vessels.  Why A wrong: Decreased heart rate is a parasympathetic effect.  Why C wrong: Peripheral vasodilation is a parasympathetic effect.  Why D wrong: Vagus nerve stimulation is parasympathetic."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Stimulation of the parasympathetic nervous system affects circulation through the release of which neurotransmitter?","options":["Epinephrine","Nor-epinephrine","Acetylcholine (cholinergic fibers)","Dopamine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic nerves act through cholinergic fibers, releasing acetylcholine.  Why A/B wrong: Epinephrine and nor-epinephrine are sympathetic (adrenergic) neurotransmitters.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not the described parasympathetic neurotransmitter."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an effect of parasympathetic nervous system stimulation on circulation?","options":["Increased heart rate and cardiac contractility","Decreased heart rate, cardiac contractility and conduction, with peripheral vasodilation","Peripheral vasoconstriction","Dilation of coronary vessels only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate, cardiac contractility and conduction, and causes peripheral vasodilation (stimulation of vagus nerve initiates the parasympathetic response).  Why A wrong: Increased HR/contractility is a sympathetic effect.  Why C wrong: Vasoconstriction is sympathetic.  Why D wrong: Coronary dilation is part of the sympathetic response."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"When taking a radial pulse for a full minute, how many fingers are used to count the pulse?","options":["1 finger","2 fingers","3 fingers","4 fingers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The radial pulse is assessed up to and counted using 3 fingers for a full minute.  Why A/B/D wrong: The book specifies 3 fingers."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The apical pulse is assessed at the mitral valve at which location in adults?","options":["2nd intercostal space at the right sternal border","5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line on the left side","Over the carotid artery","At the bend of the elbow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apical pulse is carefully assessed at the mitral valve at the 5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line (left side in adults; in children the 4th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line).  Why A wrong: That is not the apical location described.  Why C wrong: That is the carotid pulse.  Why D wrong: That is the brachial pulse location."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In children, the apical pulse is assessed at which location?","options":["5th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line","4th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line","2nd intercostal space, left sternal border","At the antecubital fossa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children, the apical pulse is assessed at the 4th intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line.  Why A wrong: That is the adult location.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described apical site in children."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The apical pulse is the most accurate and reliable pulse site in which population, and is taken with which instrument?","options":["Adults, taken with palpation","Children, taken with the stethoscope","Elderly, taken with Doppler","Neonates, taken with the radial artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apical pulse is the most accurate and reliable pulse site in children, taken with the stethoscope.  Why A wrong: It is taken with the stethoscope, not palpation, and is most reliable in children.  Why C/D wrong: Not the population/method described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The carotid pulse is assessed at the anterior edge of the sterno-cleidomastoid muscle, at the level of the lower margin of the thyroid cartilage. This pulse is commonly used during which emergency procedure?","options":["Lumbar puncture","CPR","Intubation","Catheterization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The carotid pulse is assessed at the anterior edge of the sterno-cleidomastoid muscle, at the level of the lower margin of the thyroid cartilage, and is commonly used during CPR.  Why A/C/D wrong: The carotid pulse is the standard check during CPR, not these procedures."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The brachial pulse is assessed at which anatomical location?","options":["At the bend of the elbow, along the inner margin of biceps muscle","At the groin","Behind the knee","Behind the medial malleolus of the foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The brachial pulse is assessed at the bend of the elbow, along the inner margin of biceps muscle.  Why B wrong: That is the femoral pulse.  Why C wrong: That is the popliteal pulse.  Why D wrong: That is the posterior tibial pulse."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The femoral pulse is assessed at which location and is used in resuscitation and procedures?","options":["At the bend of the elbow","At the groin","Behind the knee","On the dorsum of the foot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The femoral pulse is assessed at the groin and is used in resuscitation and procedures.  Why A wrong: That is the brachial pulse.  Why C wrong: That is the popliteal pulse.  Why D wrong: That is the dorsalis pedis pulse."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The popliteal pulse is assessed behind the knee, used for assessment, during measurement at which angle?","options":["15 degrees","45 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The popliteal pulse is assessed behind the knee, used for assessment, during measurement at a 45 degree angle.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies a 45 degree angle."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The posterior tibial pulse is assessed at which location?","options":["Behind the medial malleolus of the foot","On the anterior surface of the foot","At the groin","Behind the knee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior tibial pulse is assessed behind the medial malleolus of the foot.  Why B wrong: That is the dorsalis pedis location.  Why C wrong: That is the femoral pulse.  Why D wrong: That is the popliteal pulse."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"The dorsalis pedis pulse is assessed at which location?","options":["Behind the medial malleolus","On the anterior surface of the foot","At the groin","Behind the knee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dorsalis pedis pulse is assessed on an anterior surface of the foot.  Why A wrong: That is the posterior tibial pulse.  Why C/D wrong: Those are the femoral and popliteal pulses."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Baroreceptors, which are involved in blood pressure control, are also known as:","options":["Chemoreceptors","Pressoreceptors","Osmoreceptors","Stretch receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Baroreceptors are also known as pressoreceptors.  Why A wrong: Chemoreceptors respond to chemical changes (pH, oxygen).  Why C wrong: Osmoreceptors respond to osmotic/oncotic pressure.  Why D wrong: Stretch receptors respond to stretch in vessels/atria."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Baroreceptors are located in which structures?","options":["Vena cava and right atrium","The wall of the aortic arch and carotid sinuses","The hypothalamus","The ventro-lateral surface of the medulla oblongata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Baroreceptors are located in the wall of the aortic arch and carotid sinuses.  Why A wrong: That is the location of stretch receptors.  Why C wrong: Osmoreceptors are at the hypothalamus.  Why D wrong: Central chemoreceptors are at the ventro-lateral surface of the medulla oblongata."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Baroreceptors are specialized for nerve endings affected by changes in which factor?","options":["Blood pH","Arterial blood pressure/wall pressure (pressure changes)","Oxygen concentration","Blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Baroreceptors are specialized for nerve endings affected by changes in arterial blood/wall pressure (pressure changes).  Why A/C wrong: pH and oxygen changes are sensed by chemoreceptors.  Why D wrong: Glucose is not the baroreceptor stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"If arterial pressure decreases, what is the baroreceptor-mediated response involving the parasympathetic system?","options":["Vasoconstriction and increased BP","A parasympathetic response that causes vasoconstriction and raises pressure/blood pressure (BP increases) so that arterial pressure does not decrease further","No change in vascular tone","Decreased heart rate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the book, if arterial pressure decreases, the baroreceptor reflex produces a parasympathetic response that results in vasoconstriction and increased pressure/BP so that the BP increases (opposing the fall in arterial pressure).  Why A wrong: It is not described as a direct sympathetic-only response in this passage.  Why C wrong: There is a compensatory change, not none.  Why D wrong: The response involves vasoconstriction and pressure/BP changes, not just heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Chemoreceptors involved in blood pressure control respond to variations in which parameters?","options":["Blood pressure and wall stretch","pH and oxygen, and carbon dioxide","Plasma osmolarity only","Body temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemoreceptors are also known as chemo-sensor and respond to any variations in pH, oxygen, and carbon dioxide of the blood, then stimulate the ANS and does homeostasis.  Why A wrong: That describes baroreceptors/stretch receptors.  Why C wrong: Osmolarity is sensed by osmoreceptors.  Why D wrong: Temperature is not the described stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Central chemoreceptors are located at which site and are sensitive to changes in pH of blood?","options":["Carotid bodies and aortic bodies","Ventro-lateral surface of medulla oblongata","Vena cava and right atrium","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Central chemo-receptors are located at the ventro-lateral surface of medulla oblongata and are sensitive to changes in pH of blood.  Why A wrong: That is the location of peripheral chemoreceptors.  Why C wrong: That is the location of stretch receptors.  Why D wrong: That is the location of osmoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are sensitive to changes in oxygen (O2) and are located at which sites?","options":["Carotid bodies and aortic bodies","Ventro-lateral surface of medulla oblongata","Vena cava and right atrium","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral chemoreceptors are sensitive to change in O2 and are located at carotid bodies and aortic bodies.  Why B wrong: That is the central chemoreceptor location.  Why C wrong: That is where stretch receptors are located.  Why D wrong: That is where osmoreceptors are located."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Stretch receptors that respond to changes in pressure affecting circulatory blood volume are located in which structures?","options":["Aortic arch and carotid sinuses","Vena cava and right atrium","Medulla oblongata","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stretch receptors are located at the vena cava and right atrium and respond to change in pressure that affects circulatory blood volume.  Why A wrong: That is the location of baroreceptors.  Why C wrong: That is central chemoreceptors.  Why D wrong: That is osmoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Osmoreceptors are located in which structure and respond to variations in oncotic/osmotic pressure of the blood?","options":["Aortic arch","Hypothalamus","Right atrium","Carotid body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osmoreceptors are located at the hypothalamus and respond to variations in oncotic/osmotic pressure of the blood group.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are sites of baroreceptors, stretch receptors, and chemoreceptors respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the Renin-Angiotensin Aldosterone System (RAAS), ADH (anti-diuretic hormone), also known as vasopressin, regulates blood pressure by performing which action?","options":["Promoting urine output","Regulating the vascular volume","Dilating peripheral arteries","Decreasing aldosterone secretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ADH (vasopressin) regulates the BP by regulating vascular volume.  Why A wrong: ADH is anti-diuretic and conserves water rather than promoting urine output.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described ADH actions in this passage."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"When BP decreases as a result of hypovolemia, the body initiates a sympathetic response. What does this response cause?","options":["Decreased heart rate and vasodilation","Increased HR and blood vessel constriction","Decreased blood volume","Hypervolemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As a result of hypovolemia, a sympathetic response causes an increased HR and blood vessel constriction, and when the BP increases as a result of hypervolemia, the opposite effects occur.  Why A wrong: That is the opposite (parasympathetic-type) response.  Why C/D wrong: These are stimuli/conditions, not the response described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the RAAS pathway, a drop in blood pressure or drop in fluid volume detected by the kidney leads to the release of which substance?","options":["Aldosterone","Renin","Angiotensin II","ACE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A drop in blood pressure or drop in fluid volume causes the kidney to release renin (renin release from kidney).  Why A wrong: Aldosterone is released later from the adrenal gland.  Why C wrong: Angiotensin II is formed downstream.  Why D wrong: ACE (angiotensin converting enzyme) is released from the lungs."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the RAAS, renin acts on angiotensinogen (from the liver) to form which substance?","options":["Angiotensin II","Angiotensin I","Aldosterone","ADH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Renin acts on angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I.  Why A wrong: Angiotensin II is formed when ACE acts on angiotensin I.  Why C/D wrong: These are downstream hormones."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the RAAS, ACE (angiotensin converting enzyme) released from the lungs acts on angiotensin I to form which substance?","options":["Renin","Angiotensinogen","Angiotensin II","Aldosterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ACE (angiotensin converting enzyme), released from the lungs, acts on angiotensin I to form angiotensin II.  Why A/B wrong: These are upstream components.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is stimulated by angiotensin II at the adrenal gland."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Angiotensin II acts on the adrenal gland to stimulate the release of which hormone?","options":["Renin","ADH only","Aldosterone","Cortisol only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Angiotensin II acts on the adrenal gland to stimulate the release of aldosterone.  Why A wrong: Renin is released by the kidney earlier in the cascade.  Why B/D wrong: The specified hormone is aldosterone."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Besides stimulating aldosterone, angiotensin II also acts directly on blood vessels to produce which effect that raises blood pressure?","options":["Vasodilation","Vasoconstriction (narrowing)","Capillary leakage","Decreased cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angiotensin II also acts directly on blood vessels, stimulating vasoconstriction (narrowing).  Why A wrong: It causes vasoconstriction, not vasodilation.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described direct vascular actions."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the RAAS, aldosterone acts on the kidneys to stimulate the reabsorption of which substances?","options":["Potassium and bicarbonate","Salt (NaCl) and water (H2O)","Glucose and amino acids","Calcium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone acts on the kidneys to stimulate reabsorption of salt (NaCl) and water (H2O).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the substances described as reabsorbed under aldosterone in this passage."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Angiography is also known as:","options":["Echocardiography","Arteriogram","Electrocardiography","Atherectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angiography is also known as arteriogram.  Why A wrong: Echocardiography is ultrasound of the heart.  Why C wrong: Electrocardiography records electrical activity.  Why D wrong: Atherectomy is a plaque-removal procedure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Angiography is an X-ray examination using a contrast agent for which purpose?","options":["To record heart sounds","To visualize the patency of arteries (it may include all arteries of the body, not only coronary arteries)","To remove plaque from arteries","To measure electrical activity of the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angiography is an X-ray examination using a contrast agent to visualize patency of arteries; it may include all arteries of the body, not only coronary arteries.  Why A wrong: That is phonocardiography.  Why C wrong: That is atherectomy.  Why D wrong: That is ECG."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"During the nursing care for angiography, why should the nurse assess for shellfish allergy and ensure adequate kidney function?","options":["Because shellfish allergy increases bleeding risk","Because the contrast/dye used carries a risk of allergic reaction; embolus and bleeding (the dye is irritating to the client)","Because the procedure damages the kidneys directly","Because sedatives are contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-procedure nursing care includes assessing for shellfish allergy and adequate kidney function because the dye carries the risk of allergic reaction, embolus, and bleeding, and the dye is irritating to the client.  Why A wrong: The concern relates to contrast/dye reaction risk, not bleeding from shellfish allergy directly.  Why C wrong: Adequate kidney function is assessed because contrast dye is cleared by the kidneys, not because the procedure damages them directly.  Why D wrong: The book states pre-ordered sedatives may be given before the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"During the pre-procedure for angiography, sedatives are given for what purpose?","options":["To increase blood pressure","To reduce the client's anxiety and monitor signs of allergic reaction","To prevent bleeding","To enhance dye excretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-ordered sedatives are given before the procedure to reduce anxiety and monitor signs of allergic reaction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated purposes of pre-procedure sedation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"During the angiography procedure, which artery is usually punctured for access, and the site should be checked for bleeding and inflammation?","options":["Radial artery (forearm)","Femoral artery (groin)","Carotid artery (neck)","Brachial artery (arm)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Check the insertion site (usually the femoral artery) for bleeding and inflammation during/around the procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies the femoral artery as the usual access site."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an appropriate nursing care intervention during the angiography procedure?","options":["Allow the client to ambulate freely","Maintain pressure over insertion site with sterile gauze, assess circulatory status, and maintain bed rest","Withhold all fluids","Remove the contrast monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure, maintain pressure over the insertion site with sterile gauze, assess circulatory status, maintain bed rest, and provide hydration and monitor urinary output.  Why A wrong: Bed rest is maintained, not free ambulation.  Why C wrong: Hydration is provided, not withheld.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is maintained."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Angioplasty is also known as which procedure?","options":["Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)","Percutaneous Trans-luminal Coronary Angioplasty (PTCA)","Echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angioplasty is also known as Percutaneous Trans-luminal Coronary Angioplasty (PTCA).  Why A wrong: CABG is a surgical bypass.  Why C wrong: Echocardiography is ultrasound.  Why D wrong: Cardiac catheterization is a different diagnostic procedure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Angioplasty is an invasive technique in which coronary arteries or other arteries dilated with balloon-catheters to open the vessels' lumen. It reduces or eliminates which problem?","options":["Hypotension","Occlusion and improves blood flow (it is performed via primary cardiac catheterization)","Cardiac murmurs","Valve regurgitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angioplasty dilates arteries with balloon-catheters to open the vessels' lumen to reduce or eliminate occlusion and improve blood flow, and it is performed via primary cardiac catheterization.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the described targets of angioplasty."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of angioplasty?","options":["Improved coronary perfusion","Arterial rupture and perforation, formation of embolism and thrombus, and arterial spasm and acute MI","Reduced bleeding risk","Lowered cardiac workload only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of angioplasty include arterial rupture and perforation, formation of embolism and thrombus, and arterial spasm and acute MI.  Why A/D wrong: These are intended benefits, not complications.  Why C wrong: Bleeding risk is increased, not reduced."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the pre-procedure nursing care for angioplasty, the client should be kept NPO after which time, after obtaining informed consent?","options":["Midnight","6 AM","Noon","8 PM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Keep NPO after midnight, obtained informed consent.  Why B/C/D wrong: The book specifies NPO after midnight."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In angioplasty pre-procedure care related to allergy, the nurse should ensure baseline iodine assessment is done before the procedure because:","options":["Iodine improves clotting","Allergic reaction to iodine should be done before the procedure","Iodine is a sedative","Iodine prevents infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Allergic reaction to iodine should be done/assessed before the procedure (iodine contrast is used).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the stated reason for the allergy assessment."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In angioplasty pre-procedure care, methylene should be withheld for how many hours before the procedure (i.e., metformin should be withheld 24 hours before)?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Metformin (read as \"methylene\" in source) should be withheld 24 hours before the procedure.  Why A/B/D wrong: The book specifies 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In angioplasty pre-procedure care, the groin area (femoral artery) should be prepared with antiseptic soap and a shave for what reason?","options":["To reduce surgical scarring","To prevent infection of the access site","To improve dye visualization","To reduce anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prepare the groin area (femoral artery) with antiseptic soap and shave to prevent infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is infection prevention of the access site."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In post-procedure nursing care for angioplasty, how often should vital signs be monitored?","options":["Every hour for the first hour","Every 15 minutes then later every 1 hour","Every 4 hours","Once per shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor vital signs every 15 minutes, then later every 1 hour.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies every 15 minutes then every hour."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After angioplasty, why should the patient be kept lying in the supine position for 6-8 hours?","options":["To increase urine output","It prevents clot dislodgement at the insertion site and prevents further bleeding","To enhance dye excretion","To reduce anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure, keeping the patient in the supine position for 6-8 hours prevents clot dislodgement at the insertion site and prevents further bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated reason is to prevent clot dislodgement and bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"During and after angioplasty, why should the nurse encourage fluids (if not contraindicated)?","options":["To reduce blood pressure","To enhance renal excretion of dye","To prevent infection","To reduce anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage fluids, if not contraindicated, to enhance renal excretion of dye.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book states fluids are encouraged to enhance renal dye excretion."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"During angioplasty post-procedure care, the nurse should monitor heparin infusion to prevent which complication?","options":["Hypertension","Thrombus formation","Allergic reaction","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During and after the procedure, monitor heparin infusion to prevent thrombus formation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant given to prevent thrombus formation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the purpose of instructing a client to take all medicines on time after a coronary stent placement, such as nitroglycerin, nitrates, calcium channel blockers, anti-platelets agents, and anticoagulants?","options":["To increase heart rate","To maintain stent patency and prevent re-occlusion/clotting","To reduce dye excretion","To increase blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client must take all medicines (nitroglycerin, nitrates, calcium channel blockers, anti-platelet agents, anticoagulants) on time to keep the stent open/patent and prevent clotting and re-occlusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the purpose of the prescribed stent medications."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"A coronary artery stent is best described as which of the following?","options":["A surgical bypass graft","A small metal mesh tube that expands inside a coronary artery","A balloon-only catheter","A pacemaker lead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A coronary artery stent is a small metal mesh tube that expands inside a coronary artery.  Why A wrong: That describes CABG.  Why C wrong: A balloon catheter is used in angioplasty; the stent is a mesh tube.  Why D wrong: A pacemaker lead is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"When is a stent often placed during a coronary procedure?","options":["Only before angioplasty","During or immediately after angioplasty, which helps prevent the artery from closing up again","Only during CABG","Several weeks after angioplasty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A stent is often placed during or immediately after angioplasty, which helps prevent the artery from closing up again.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies stent placement during/immediately after angioplasty."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"A coronary stent is a tube-shaped device placed in the coronary arteries that supply blood to the heart, to keep the arteries open during which procedure?","options":["Cardiac catheterization only","The treatment of coronary heart disease, during a procedure called percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or PTCA","Echocardiography","ECG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A coronary stent is a tube-shaped device placed in the coronary arteries to keep them open during the treatment of coronary heart disease, in a procedure called percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or PTCA.  Why A wrong: It is placed during PCI/PTCA, not standalone catheterization.  Why C/D wrong: These are diagnostic, not stent-placement procedures."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Atherectomy is best described as which type of procedure?","options":["A diagnostic X-ray of the arteries","A surgical procedure in which mechanically removes the plaque (fatty substances) from the coronary artery by use of a cutting chamber on the inserted catheter","A balloon-only dilation","An ultrasound of the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atherectomy is a surgical procedure in which mechanically removes the plaque (fatty substances) from the coronary artery by use of a cutting chamber on the inserted catheter.  Why A wrong: That is angiography.  Why C wrong: That is angioplasty.  Why D wrong: That is echocardiography."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Atherectomy is also used to improve blood flow to ischemic limbs in individuals with which condition?","options":["Coronary artery disease only","Peripheral arterial diseases","Valvular heart disease","Cardiac arrhythmias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atherectomy is also used to improve blood flow to ischemic limbs in individuals with peripheral arterial diseases, and it is a pre- and post-operative care same as PTCA.  Why A wrong: It is also used for peripheral arterial disease, not coronary only.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the conditions described for atherectomy."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the purpose of Peripheral Arterial Revascularization (a PTCA technique)?","options":["To decrease arterial blood flow","To increase arterial blood flow to the affected limb","To remove the affected limb","To prevent venous return"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose of peripheral arterial revascularization is to increase arterial blood flow to the affected limb.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases, arterial flow.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In the nursing care after peripheral arterial revascularization, what should the nurse watch for that indicates blood pressure is not controlling the limb and could cause clot formation?","options":["Hypotension","Hypertension (if BP rises, it will create stress on the graft and cause clot formation)","Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Watch for hypertension because if BP rises, it will create stress on the graft and cause clot formation.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies hypertension as the concern that stresses the graft."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After peripheral arterial revascularization, the nurse should instruct the client to keep the affected extremity straight and limit movement, and avoid bending the knee and hip. What is the rationale?","options":["To increase blood flow","To prevent an outcome because of increased outflow of blood after the procedure","To reduce dye excretion","To prevent pain only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the client to keep the affected extremity straight, limit movement, and avoid bending the knee and hip to prevent an outcome because of increased outflow of blood after the procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the rationale given for limb positioning."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After peripheral arterial revascularization, graft occlusion usually occurs within which timeframe?","options":["Within the first 24 hours","Within 48 hours","Within the first week","After one month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for graft occlusion; it usually occurs within the first 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The book specifies the first 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After peripheral arterial revascularization, while assessing peripheral pulses, what changes in color and temperature should be monitored?","options":["Excessive bleeding at the site, usually a small amount of bloody drainage is expected","Changes in color and temperature of the extremity, monitoring for excessive bleeding (a small amount of bloody drainage is expected)","Only the heart rate","Only blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should assess peripheral pulses and changes in color and temperature of the extremity, monitor for excessive bleeding at the site (usually a small amount of bloody drainage is expected).  Why A wrong: It conflates bleeding with the color/temperature assessment; both are monitored, but the full point covers pulses, color, temperature, and bleeding.  Why C/D wrong: Pulses, color, temperature, and bleeding are assessed, not only heart rate or glucose."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"When monitoring the graft after peripheral arterial revascularization, which findings of the wound should be assessed?","options":["Hardness, tenderness, and warmth, which may indicate infection (this occurs immediately to be informed to HCP)","Cool, painless extremity","Pallor and numbness only","Decreased temperature only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor the area over the graft for hardness, tenderness, and warmth, which may indicate infection; if this occurs, immediately inform the HCP.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the infection signs described for the graft site."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"After peripheral arterial revascularization, a sharp pain may be the first indicator of which complication?","options":["Infection","Graft occlusion","Hypertension","Dye reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for a sharp pain because pain is the first indicator of graft occlusion.  Why A wrong: Infection is indicated by hardness, tenderness, and warmth.  Why C/D wrong: These are different complications."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG) is also known as which procedure?","options":["Angioplasty","Bypass surgery","Atherectomy","Cardiac catheterization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CABG is also known as bypass surgery.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different cardiovascular procedures."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"CABG is a procedure in which the occluded coronary arteries are bypassed with which vessels?","options":["Synthetic grafts only","The client's own venous or arterial blood vessels to restore normal blood flow","Donor heart valves","Pacemaker leads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CABG is a procedure in which the occluded coronary arteries are bypassed with the client's own venous or arterial blood vessels to restore normal blood flow.  Why A wrong: It uses the client's own vessels, not synthetic grafts only.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to CABG grafting."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which vessels are most commonly used in a CABG procedure?","options":["Radial artery and jugular vein","Saphenous vein of the leg and internal mammary artery","Femoral artery and renal vein","Pulmonary vein and aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common vessels used in a CABG procedure are the saphenous vein of the leg and internal mammary artery.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the commonly cited CABG graft vessels."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Cardiac catheterization is best described as which of the following?","options":["An ultrasound imaging of the heart","An invasive procedure that involves inserting/introducing a catheter into the heart via peripheral blood vessels for the purpose of obtaining information about the structure and performance of the heart chambers and valve and coronary circulation","A recording of the heart's electrical activity","A surgical bypass of coronary arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac catheterization is an invasive procedure that involves inserting/introducing a catheter into the heart via peripheral blood vessels for the purpose of obtaining information about the structure and performance of the heart chambers and valve and coronary circulation.  Why A wrong: That is echocardiography.  Why C wrong: That is ECG.  Why D wrong: That is CABG."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Echocardiography (ECHO) is also known by which name(s)?","options":["Arteriogram","Cardiac ECHO / Echocardiogram (ultrasound of the heart)","Phonocardiogram","Atherectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Echocardiography is an ultrasound of heart, also known as Cardiac ECHO, Echocardiogram (ECHO).  Why A wrong: Arteriogram is angiography.  Why C wrong: Phonocardiogram is used to record heart sounds (ECG-related device described later).  Why D wrong: Atherectomy is a plaque-removal procedure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Echocardiography uses which type of sound waves to study the structure of the heart and heart valves?","options":["Audible sound waves","Ultrasound (high-frequency sound waves)","Infrasound waves","Radio waves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECHO uses ultrasound (test uses sound waves) to study the structure of the heart and how the heart and valves are working.  Why A/C/D wrong: ECHO is an ultrasound-based test, not these other wave types."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Echocardiography (ECHO) provides information about which aspects of the heart?","options":["Only the electrical conduction","The structure and function of the heart, whereas ECG is tracing the electrical activity on a graph of the heart","Only the coronary artery patency","Only blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECHO provides information of structure and function of the heart, whereas ECG is tracing the electrical activity on a graph of the heart.  Why A wrong: ECHO assesses structure/function, not just electrical conduction (that is ECG).  Why C/D wrong: ECHO is not for coronary patency (angiography) or blood pressure measurement."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"A Stress Test, also known as Treadmill Test (TMT), is described as which of the following?","options":["The least common stress test","The most common stress test","A test that uses radioactive dye","An ultrasound test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Treadmill Test (TMT) is the most common stress test.  Why A wrong: It is the most common, not the least common.  Why C wrong: TMT is conducted on a treadmill with ECG, not radioactive dye.  Why D wrong: That is echocardiography."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In a Treadmill Stress Test (TMT), how is the test conducted on the patient?","options":["While lying supine at rest","Do an exercise/walking on a treadmill during the course of an electrocardiogram (ECG)","Using an injected contrast dye","Using ultrasound probes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A Treadmill Stress Test (TMT) is a form of stress test conducted while the patient does an exercise walking on a treadmill during the course of an electrocardiogram (ECG).  Why A wrong: It is conducted during exercise, not at rest.  Why C/D wrong: It uses ECG during treadmill exercise, not contrast or ultrasound."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to a cardiac stress test?","options":["Stable angina","Acute myocardial infarction within 48 hours","Controlled hypertension","Mild physical exertion ability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute myocardial infarction within 48 hours is an absolute contraindication to cardiac stress test.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not listed absolute contraindications (the listed ones include AMI within 48 hours, unstable angina not yet stabilized with medical therapy, uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia/severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, acute pulmonary embolism/pericarditis, multi-vessel coronary disease that has a high risk of producing acute MI, decompensated or inadequately controlled congestive heart failure, uncontrolled hypertension/severe pulmonary hypertension, and acute aortic dissection)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an absolute contraindication to a cardiac stress test? (Select the BEST option)","options":["Stable angina with medical therapy","Unstable angina not yet stabilized with medical therapy","Mild hypertension","Normal aortic valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unstable angina not yet stabilized with medical therapy is an absolute contraindication.  Why A wrong: Unstable (not stabilized) angina is the contraindication, not stable angina on therapy.  Why C wrong: Uncontrolled/severe hypertension is contraindicated, not mild hypertension.  Why D wrong: Severe symptomatic aortic stenosis is contraindicated, not a normal valve."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to a cardiac stress test related to congestive heart failure?","options":["Compensated, well-controlled heart failure","Decompensated or inadequately controlled congestive heart failure","History of heart failure 5 years ago","Normal ejection fraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decompensated or inadequately controlled congestive heart failure is an absolute contraindication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the described contraindication; it is specifically decompensated/inadequately controlled CHF."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to a cardiac stress test? (SATA) Select all that apply.","options":["Acute aortic dissection","Acute pulmonary embolism and pericarditis","Uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia and severe symptomatic aortic stenosis","Stable controlled blood pressure","Multi-vessel coronary artery disease that has a high risk of producing an acute myocardial infarction"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Acute aortic dissection (A), acute pulmonary embolism and pericarditis (B), uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia and severe symptomatic aortic stenosis (C), and multi-vessel coronary artery disease with high risk of producing acute MI (E) are all listed absolute contraindications.  Why D wrong: Stable, controlled blood pressure is not a contraindication; uncontrolled/severe pulmonary hypertension is."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Electrocardiography (ECG) is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The process of producing an electrocardiogram, a graph of voltage versus time of the electrical activity of the heart using electrodes placed on the skin","An ultrasound imaging of the heart","The surgical removal of plaque","The insertion of a catheter into the heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ECG is the process of producing an electrocardiogram, a graph of voltage versus time of the electrical activity of the heart using electrodes placed on the skin.  Why B wrong: That is echocardiography.  Why C wrong: That is atherectomy.  Why D wrong: That is cardiac catheterization."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"An electrocardiograph (phonocardiogram) is a device which is used for recording which signals?","options":["Heart sounds","Blood pressure","Oxygen saturation","Respiratory rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An electrocardiograph (phonocardiogram) is a device which is used for recording heart sounds.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not what the device records as described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Who is the founder/father of ECG?","options":["William Harvey","William Einthoven","Willem Kolff","Karl Landsteiner"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The founder of ECG is William Einthoven.  Why A wrong: William Harvey described blood circulation.  Why C wrong: Willem Kolff is associated with the artificial kidney/dialysis.  Why D wrong: Karl Landsteiner discovered blood groups."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"What is the normal speed of ECG paper?","options":["5 mm/sec","25 mm/sec","50 mm/sec","100 mm/sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal speed of ECG is 25 mm/sec.  Why A/C/D wrong: The book specifies 25 mm/sec."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"ECG leads I, II, and III form an imaginary triangle. This is known as which triangle?","options":["Frank's triangle","Einthoven's triangle","Bachmann's triangle","Koch's triangle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECG lead I, II and III form an imaginary triangle, which is known as Einthoven's triangle.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the triangle formed by leads I, II, III."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"In ECG terminology, which leads relate to the limbs (I, II, III) and are also called bipolar leads, with relationship between two axis?","options":["Chest leads","Limb leads (bipolar leads, relationship between two axis)","Augmented leads","Precordial leads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Limb leads are unipolar leads, relationship between two axis (related to the limbs I, II, III) and are also called bipolar leads, relationship b/w two axis.  Why A wrong: Chest leads are unipolar leads, means relationship b/w single axis (V1-V6).  Why C wrong: Augmented leads are AVR, AVL, AVF.  Why D wrong: Precordial leads are another name but the described category is limb (bipolar) leads."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Chest leads in ECG are unipolar leads representing a relationship between a single axis. They are designated as which of the following?","options":["I, II, III","V1, V2, V3, V4, V5, V6","AVR, AVL, AVF","aVR only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chest leads are unipolar leads, means relationship b/w single axis, V1, V2, V3, V4, V5, V6.  Why A wrong: I, II, III are limb (bipolar) leads.  Why C wrong: AVR, AVL, AVF are augmented leads.  Why D wrong: aVR alone is part of the augmented leads, not the chest leads."},{"section":"Batch 020 · cardiac preload/afterload, properties of cardiac muscle…","question":"Augmented leads in ECG include which of the following?","options":["I, II, III","V1-V6","AVR (Augmented Vector Right), AVL (Augmented Vector Left), AVF (Augmented Vector Foot)","Einthoven's leads only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Augmented leads are AVR (Augmented Vector Right), AVL (Augmented Vector Left), AVF (Augmented Vector Foot).  Why A wrong: I, II, III are limb leads.  Why B wrong: V1-V6 are chest leads.  Why D wrong: Einthoven's leads refer to the limb-lead triangle, not the augmented leads."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"On standard ECG graph paper, the duration represented by one small box (one small square) is:","options":["0.04 sec","0.08 sec","0.12 sec","0.20 sec"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One small box on ECG paper equals 0.04 sec; 5 small boxes equal 0.20 sec.  Why B wrong: 0.08 sec is the duration of one large box (5 small boxes total 0.20 sec, not 0.08).  Why C wrong: 0.12 sec is the lower limit of normal PR interval, not a single small box.  Why D wrong: 0.20 sec is the duration of one large box / five small boxes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"What is the time duration represented by one large box (one large square) on ECG graph paper?","options":["0.04 sec","0.08 sec","0.20 sec","0.40 sec"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One large box = 5 small boxes × 0.04 sec = 0.20 sec.  Why A wrong: 0.04 sec is one small box.  Why B wrong: 0.08 sec is not a standard ECG paper division.  Why D wrong: 0.40 sec equals two large boxes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"On ECG graph paper, the height of one small square (1 mm) corresponds to a duration of:","options":["1 mm = 0.04 sec","1 mm = 0.08 sec","1 mm = 0.20 sec","1 mm = 0.5 sec"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One small square measures 1 mm and represents 0.04 sec along the time axis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the standard small-square value of 0.04 sec."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"For correct ECG limb-lead placement, Lead I is obtained by connecting which electrodes?","options":["Right arm and left arm (RA + LA)","Right arm and left leg (RA + LL)","Left arm and left leg (LA + LL)","Right leg and left leg (RL + LL)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lead I is between the right arm and left arm (RA + LA).  Why B wrong: RA + LL is the configuration for Lead II.  Why C wrong: LA + LL is the configuration for Lead III.  Why D wrong: The right leg serves as the ground/reference, not a recording pair for Lead I."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which limb-lead configuration corresponds to Lead II on the ECG?","options":["RA + LA","RA + LL","LA + LL","LA + RL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lead II is recorded between the right arm and left leg (RA + LL).  Why A wrong: RA + LA is Lead I.  Why C wrong: LA + LL is Lead III.  Why D wrong: This combination is not a standard bipolar limb lead."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Lead III on the ECG is recorded between which two electrodes?","options":["RA + LA","RA + LL","LA + LL","RA + RL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lead III is between the left arm and left leg (LA + LL).  Why A wrong: RA + LA is Lead I.  Why B wrong: RA + LL is Lead II.  Why D wrong: Not a standard bipolar limb lead."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"When placing precordial (chest) ECG leads, where is V1 positioned?","options":["4th intercostal space to the right of the sternum","4th intercostal space to the left of the sternum","Directly between leads V2 and V4","5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: V1 is placed at the 4th intercostal space to the right of the sternum.  Why B wrong: That is the position of V2.  Why C wrong: That describes V3 (between V2 and V4).  Why D wrong: That describes V4."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The precordial lead V4 is correctly placed at:","options":["4th intercostal space left of sternum","5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Anterior axillary line level with V4","Midaxillary line level with V4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: V4 is at the 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line.  Why A wrong: That is V2.  Why C wrong: That describes V5 (anterior axillary line, level with V4).  Why D wrong: That describes V6 (midaxillary line, level with V4)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Precordial leads V5 and V6 are placed at the same horizontal level as V4 but along which lines, respectively?","options":["V5 at midaxillary line; V6 at anterior axillary line","V5 at anterior axillary line; V6 at midaxillary line","Both at the midclavicular line","V5 at the sternal border; V6 at the midclavicular line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: V5 is at the anterior axillary line level with V4, and V6 is at the midaxillary line level with V4.  Why A wrong: The lines are reversed.  Why C wrong: Midclavicular line is for V4.  Why D wrong: V5 and V6 are lateral leads, not at the sternal border."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The P-wave on the ECG represents which electrical event of the heart?","options":["Atrial repolarization","Atrial depolarization","Ventricular depolarization","Ventricular repolarization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The P-wave represents atrial depolarization.  Why A wrong: Atrial repolarization is buried within the QRS complex and not separately visible.  Why C wrong: Ventricular depolarization is represented by the QRS complex.  Why D wrong: Ventricular repolarization is represented by the T-wave."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The normal duration of the P-wave on an ECG is:","options":["0.04 sec","0.06 sec","0.12 sec","0.20 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal P-wave duration is 0.12 sec (it represents 3 small boxes); the source states the normal P-wave duration. (Note: the source lists normal duration of P-wave as 0.12 sec = 3 small boxes.)  Why A wrong: 0.04 sec is one small box, too short.  Why C wrong: see note — book value is 0.12 sec.  Why D wrong: 0.20 sec is one large box, too long."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The PR interval on the ECG represents the firing of conduction from:","options":["Ventricle to atria","Atria to ventricles","SA node to AV node only","Purkinje fibers to myocardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The PR interval represents firing of conduction from atria to ventricles.  Why A wrong: Conduction is from atria to ventricles, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: It encompasses the whole atrioventricular conduction time, not just SA to AV node.  Why D wrong: Purkinje-to-myocardium activity contributes to the QRS, not the PR interval."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"What is the normal duration of the PR interval?","options":["0.04–0.10 sec","0.12–0.20 sec","0.20–0.40 sec","0.40–0.60 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal PR interval is 0.12–0.20 sec (3 to 5 small boxes).  Why A wrong: Too short for a normal PR interval.  Why C wrong: 0.20–0.40 sec is the normal QT interval range.  Why D wrong: A PR interval of 0.40–0.60 sec is markedly prolonged (abnormal)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The QRS complex on the ECG represents:","options":["Atrial depolarization","Ventricular depolarization (contraction of both ventricles)","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial repolarization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarization, meaning contraction of both ventricles.  Why A wrong: Atrial depolarization is the P-wave.  Why C wrong: Ventricular repolarization is the T-wave.  Why D wrong: Atrial repolarization is not separately represented."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The normal duration of the QRS complex is:","options":["0.04–0.06 sec","0.08–0.10 sec","0.12–0.20 sec","0.20–0.40 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal QRS complex duration is 0.08–0.10 sec, or 1 to 2 small squares.  Why A wrong: Too short for a normal QRS.  Why C wrong: This range overlaps with the PR interval, not the QRS.  Why D wrong: This is the QT-interval range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The ST segment on the ECG represents:","options":["Atrial depolarization","Ventricular depolarization and repolarization","The firing of the SA node","Atrial repolarization only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ST segment represents ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation.  Why A wrong: Atrial depolarization is the P-wave.  Why C wrong: SA node firing is not directly recorded as a discrete waveform.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to atrial repolarization."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The normal duration of the ST segment is approximately:","options":["0.04 sec","0.08 sec","0.12 sec","0.20 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal ST segment duration is 0.08 sec, or 2 small squares.  Why A wrong: 0.04 sec is one small box.  Why C wrong: 0.12 sec exceeds the stated normal ST value.  Why D wrong: 0.20 sec is one large box."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The T-wave on the ECG represents:","options":["Ventricular repolarization (relaxation stage of both ventricles)","Atrial depolarization","Ventricular depolarization","Conduction through the AV node"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The T-wave represents ventricular repolarisation, the relaxation stage of both ventricles.  Why B wrong: Atrial depolarization is the P-wave.  Why C wrong: Ventricular depolarization is the QRS complex.  Why D wrong: AV-node conduction is part of the PR interval."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"What is the normal duration of the T-wave?","options":["0.04 sec","0.08–0.12 sec","0.20–0.40 sec","0.40–0.60 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal T-wave duration is 0.08–0.12 sec, or 2 to 3 small boxes.  Why A wrong: Too short.  Why C wrong: This is the QT-interval range.  Why D wrong: Too long for a normal T-wave."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The QT interval represents the total duration of ventricular:","options":["Depolarization only","Repolarization only","Depolarisation and repolarisation","Atrial contraction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The QT interval represents total duration of ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation.  Why A/B wrong: It includes both processes, not only one.  Why D wrong: It reflects ventricular, not atrial, electrical activity."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The normal duration of the QT interval is:","options":["0.08–0.12 sec","0.12–0.20 sec","0.32–0.40 sec","0.40–0.60 sec"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal QT interval is 0.32–0.40 sec, or 8 to 10 small squares.  Why A wrong: This is closer to the T-wave duration.  Why B wrong: This is the PR interval range.  Why D wrong: Too long; this would be a prolonged QT."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"A nurse uses the 6-second strip method to estimate heart rate for an irregular rhythm. After counting the QRS complexes in a 6-second strip, what should the nurse do to obtain the rate per minute?","options":["Multiply the number of QRS complexes by 6","Multiply the number of QRS complexes by 10","Divide 300 by the number of large boxes","Count P-waves only and multiply by 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the 6-second strip method, count the number of QRS complexes in 6 seconds and multiply by 10 to get the rate per minute.  Why A wrong: Multiplying by 6 underestimates the rate (6 sec ×10 = 60 sec).  Why C wrong: The \"300 method\" is a different technique, not the 6-second strip method.  Why D wrong: QRS complexes (ventricular rate), not P-waves alone, are counted, and the multiplier is 10."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"A 12-lead ECG shows changes in leads II, III, and aVF. These leads reflect electrical activity of which surface/area of the heart?","options":["Lateral wall","Inferior or diaphragmatic surface","Septal surface","Anterior wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leads II, III, and aVF reflect electrical activity of the inferior surface or diaphragmatic wall of the heart.  Why A wrong: Lateral wall activity is reflected by leads I, aVL, V5, V6.  Why C wrong: Septal surface is reflected by leads V1, V2.  Why D wrong: Anterior wall is reflected by leads V3, V4."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"ECG changes seen in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6 indicate electrical activity of which part of the heart?","options":["Inferior wall","Lateral wall of the ventricles","Septal surface","Right atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leads I, aVL, V5, and V6 indicate electrical activity of the lateral wall of the ventricles.  Why A wrong: Inferior wall corresponds to leads II, III, aVF.  Why C wrong: Septal surface corresponds to V1, V2.  Why D wrong: These leads reflect ventricular, not right atrial, activity."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Leads V1 and V2 on the ECG reflect electrical activity of which surface of the heart?","options":["Inferior surface","Lateral wall","Septal surface","Posterior wall"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Leads V1 and V2 indicate electrical activity of the septal surface of the heart.  Why A wrong: Inferior surface = II, III, aVF.  Why B wrong: Lateral wall = I, aVL, V5, V6.  Why D wrong: V1, V2 are anteroseptal, not posterior."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Leads V3 and V4 represent the electrical activity of which surface of the heart?","options":["Anterior wall / anterior surface","Inferior surface","Septal surface","Lateral wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: V3 and V4 reflect electrical activity of the anterior wall / anterior surface of the right and left ventricles.  Why B wrong: Inferior surface = II, III, aVF.  Why C wrong: Septal surface = V1, V2.  Why D wrong: Lateral wall = I, aVL, V5, V6."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which heart rate range defines a normal sinus rhythm (NSR)?","options":["Less than 60 beats per minute","60–100 beats per minute","100–150 beats per minute","150–250 beats per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal sinus rhythm has a heart rate of 60–100 beats per minute.  Why A wrong: < 60 bpm is sinus bradycardia.  Why C wrong: > 100 bpm is sinus tachycardia.  Why D wrong: 150–250 bpm suggests a tachyarrhythmia such as PSVT."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In asystole, which finding is seen on the ECG?","options":["A rapid, regular narrow-complex tachycardia","A flat line (absence of electrical activity), with no rate, no P-waves, and no QRS complexes","Sawtooth flutter waves","Chaotic fibrillatory waves with measurable QRS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asystole is absence of electrical activity — there is a flat line on the ECG with no rate, no P-waves, and no QRS complexes present; the PR interval is unable to be measured due to no P-waves.  Why A wrong: That describes a tachyarrhythmia, not asystole.  Why C wrong: Sawtooth waves indicate atrial flutter.  Why D wrong: Chaotic fibrillatory waves with QRS describe fibrillation, not asystole."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"What is the management drug of choice administered during asystole?","options":["Atropine 1 mg","Adrenaline (epinephrine) 1 mg","Amiodarone 300 mg","Beta blockers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For asystole, Adrenaline 1 mg is the drug of choice.  Why A wrong: Atropine is used for symptomatic bradycardia/AV blocks, not asystole as the primary drug of choice here.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is used in ventricular tachyarrhythmias.  Why D wrong: Beta blockers are used in sinus tachycardia, not asystole."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Sinus bradycardia is BEST described as:","options":["Heart rate above 100 bpm with regular rhythm","Same as NSR but heart rate less than 60 bpm, often asymptomatic","An irregularly irregular rhythm with no P-waves","A flat line with no electrical activity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sinus bradycardia is the same as NSR but with a heart rate of less than 60 bpm, and is often asymptomatic.  Why A wrong: That describes sinus tachycardia.  Why C wrong: That describes atrial fibrillation.  Why D wrong: That describes asystole."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which of the following are recognized causes of sinus bradycardia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Vagal stimulation","Hypoendocrine states","Hypothermia","Sinus node involvement in MI (myocardial infarction)","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sinus bradycardia may be caused by vagal stimulation, hypoendocrine states, hypothermia, or sinus node involvement in MI.  Why E wrong: Hyperthyroidism increases metabolic rate and typically causes tachycardia, not bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"The drug of choice for treating symptomatic sinus bradycardia is:","options":["Atropine sulphate","Adrenaline","Propranolol","Amiodarone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine sulphate is the drug of choice for sinus bradycardia.  Why B wrong: Adrenaline is the drug of choice for asystole.  Why C wrong: Propranolol (a beta blocker) is used in sinus tachycardia.  Why D wrong: Amiodarone is used for ventricular tachyarrhythmias."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Sinus tachycardia is defined as a sinus rhythm with a heart rate of:","options":["Less than 60 bpm","60–100 bpm","More than 100 bpm","More than 250 bpm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sinus tachycardia is the same as NSR but with a heart rate of more than 100 bpm.  Why A wrong: < 60 bpm is bradycardia.  Why B wrong: 60–100 bpm is normal sinus rhythm.  Why D wrong: >250 bpm suggests other tachyarrhythmias, not simple sinus tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which of the following may cause sinus tachycardia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Exercise","Anxiety","Fever","Drugs","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sinus tachycardia causes include exercise, anxiety, fever, drugs, anemia, fluid volume excess, and pain.  Why E wrong: Hypothermia is a cause of bradycardia, not tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which non-pharmacological maneuver is used in the management of sinus tachycardia to slow the rate?","options":["Carotid sinus pressure (carotid massage)","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Chest compressions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carotid sinus pressure (carotid massage) is used as a vagal maneuver to treat the underlying tachycardia.  Why B wrong: Defibrillation is for pulseless VT/VF, not sinus tachycardia.  Why C wrong: Cardioversion is not the first-line management for simple sinus tachycardia.  Why D wrong: Chest compressions are for cardiac arrest, not sinus tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which class of drugs is the drug of choice for managing sinus tachycardia?","options":["Beta blockers (e.g., Propranolol, Atenolol)","Atropine","Adrenaline","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta blockers (such as Propranolol and Atenolol) are the drug of choice for sinus tachycardia, after identifying and treating the underlying cause.  Why B wrong: Atropine increases heart rate and is used for bradycardia.  Why C wrong: Adrenaline is used for asystole.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is not indicated for sinus tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In first-degree AV heart block, which characteristic ECG finding is present?","options":["The PR interval exceeds 0.20 second","There are no P-waves at all","The QRS complex is absent","The rhythm is irregularly irregular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In first-degree AV block the conduction is slowed; the PR interval is prolonged and exceeds 0.20 second. Cardinal finding: prolonged PR interval > 0.20 sec.  Why B wrong: P-waves are present in first-degree block.  Why C wrong: The QRS complex is present and normal.  Why D wrong: The rhythm is regular in first-degree block."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"First-degree AV heart block is most commonly caused by an MI affecting which wall, along with cardiac surgery and acute rheumatic fever?","options":["Anterior wall MI","Inferior wall MI","Lateral wall MI","Septal wall MI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First-degree AV block is caused by inferior wall MI, cardiac surgery, and acute rheumatic fever.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically attributes the block to inferior wall MI, not anterior, lateral, or septal MI."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Management of first-degree AV heart block includes correction of the underlying cause and administration of which drug if the PR interval exceeds 0.26 second and symptomatic bradycardia develops?","options":["Atropine","Adrenaline 1 mg","Amiodarone","Lidocaine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is administered if the PR interval exceeds 0.26 second and symptomatic bradycardia develops.  Why B wrong: Adrenaline is the asystole drug of choice, not for first-degree block.  Why C/D wrong: Amiodarone and Lidocaine are antiarrhythmics for ventricular arrhythmias, not for symptomatic AV block bradycardia here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Second-degree AV heart block, Type I is also known by which eponymous names?","options":["Wenckebach / Mobitz I","Mobitz II","Complete heart block","Stokes-Adams"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Second-degree AV heart block Type I is known as Wenckebach or Mobitz I.  Why B wrong: Mobitz II is Type II second-degree block.  Why C wrong: Complete heart block is third-degree block.  Why D wrong: Stokes-Adams refers to syncopal attacks, not this block classification."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which causes are associated with second-degree AV heart block Type I (Wenckebach)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Inferior wall MI","Cardiac surgery","Acute rheumatic fever","Multiple sclerosis","Asthma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Second-degree AV block Type I causes include inferior wall MI, cardiac surgery, and acute rheumatic fever.  Why D wrong: Multiple sclerosis is not listed as a cause.  Why E wrong: Asthma is a respiratory condition, not a listed cause of this block."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"A patient with symptomatic second-degree AV heart block (Wenckebach) and ongoing bradycardia requires temporary stabilization while definitive treatment is arranged. Which intervention is appropriate?","options":["Immediate defibrillation","Temporary pacemaker for symptomatic bradycardia and discontinuation of digoxin if it is being administered","Administration of a beta blocker","Synchronized cardioversion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes correction of the underlying cause, administration of atropine as per order, and a temporary pacemaker for symptomatic bradycardia along with discontinuation of digoxin if it is being given.  Why A wrong: Defibrillation is for pulseless VT/VF, not a stable bradyarrhythmia.  Why C wrong: Beta blockers further slow conduction and worsen the block.  Why D wrong: Cardioversion is not indicated for AV-block bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In second-degree AV heart block Type II (Mobitz II), conduction of the cardiac impulse is:","options":["Progressively prolonged until a beat is dropped","Intermittently blocked","Completely absent with no atrial activity","Conducted with a shortened PR interval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Mobitz II, AV conduction is intermittently blocked.  Why A wrong: Progressive PR prolongation until a dropped beat describes Mobitz I (Wenckebach), not Mobitz II.  Why C wrong: Complete absence of conduction is third-degree (complete) heart block.  Why D wrong: PR interval is constant (not shortened) in Mobitz II."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which conditions are associated with second-degree heart block Mobitz II? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Coronary artery disease","Anterior wall MI","Digoxin toxicity","Hypoglycemia","Iron deficiency anemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Mobitz II comes from coronary artery diseases, anterior wall MI, and digoxin toxicity.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not a listed cause.  Why E wrong: Iron deficiency anemia is not a listed cause of this conduction block."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Management of second-degree heart block Mobitz II includes administration of which drugs, and what device is prepared for if treatment is not effective?","options":["Atropine, epinephrine, and dopamine; prepare for pacemaker if first resolved","Beta blockers; prepare for cardioversion","Calcium gluconate; prepare for defibrillation","Amiodarone; prepare for catheter ablation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes administering atropine, epinephrine, and dopamine in the patient, and preparing for a pacemaker if first not resolved.  Why B/C/D wrong: Beta blockers, calcium gluconate, and amiodarone (with cardioversion/defibrillation/ablation) are not the listed management for Mobitz II."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Third-degree heart block is also known as:","options":["First-degree block","Wenckebach","Complete heart block","Atrial flutter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Third-degree heart block is called complete heart block, a condition in which the nerve impulses cannot pass through from the atria to the ventricles.  Why A/B wrong: First-degree and Wenckebach are lesser degrees of block with conduction still occurring.  Why D wrong: Atrial flutter is a separate atrial tachyarrhythmia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In complete (third-degree) heart block, what is the relationship between the P-waves and the QRS complexes?","options":["The P-waves and QRS complexes are completely unrelated","Each P-wave is followed by a QRS complex","The PR interval progressively lengthens","There are no P-waves at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In complete heart block the P-waves and the QRS complexes are completely unrelated (AV dissociation).  Why B wrong: A consistent P-to-QRS relationship is seen in normal conduction, not complete block.  Why C wrong: Progressive PR lengthening is Mobitz I.  Why D wrong: P-waves are present but dissociated from the QRS."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Clinical manifestations of complete heart block include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypotension","Angina","Heart failure","Hypertensive crisis","Bounding pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical manifestations of complete heart block include hypotension, angina, and heart failure.  Why D wrong: Hypertensive crisis is not a listed manifestation; the slow ventricular rate tends to lower output and BP.  Why E wrong: A bounding pulse is not a listed feature of complete heart block."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Causes of complete (third-degree) heart block may include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Congenital abnormalities","Rheumatic fever","Lev's disease","Lenegre's disease","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include congenital abnormalities, rheumatic fever, Lev's disease, and Lenegre's disease.  Why E wrong: Hyperthyroidism is not a listed cause of complete heart block."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Management of complete (third-degree) heart block includes administration of atropine, epinephrine, and dopamine for bradycardia, plus preparing the patient for what definitive intervention?","options":["A pacemaker (preparation for pacemaker insertion)","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes administering atropine, epinephrine, and dopamine for bradycardia and preparing for placement of a pacemaker.  Why B/C wrong: Defibrillation and cardioversion treat tachyarrhythmias/fibrillation, not the slow rate of complete heart block.  Why D wrong: A pacemaker, not ablation, is the definitive treatment indicated here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which ECG feature is characteristic of atrial flutter?","options":["Irregularly irregular rhythm with no P-waves","Sawtooth flutter waves on ECG paper, with the atria contracting too quickly","A flat line with no electrical activity","Progressive PR prolongation with dropped beats"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atrial flutter shows sawtooth (\"F\") flutter waves; the cardinal finding is a sawtooth pattern on the ECG paper and symptoms include a racing heart (palpitations), rapid heartbeat, and shortness of breath/dizziness.  Why A wrong: Irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P-waves describes atrial fibrillation.  Why C wrong: A flat line indicates asystole.  Why D wrong: Progressive PR prolongation with dropped beats is Mobitz I."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which medications are used to manage atrial flutter when the patient is unstable, cardioversion is prepared for, and rate control is needed?","options":["Beta blockers such as Esmolol and Propranolol","Atropine and dopamine","Adrenaline and amiodarone only","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For atrial flutter, if the patient is unstable, prepare for cardioversion and give beta blockers such as Esmolol and Propranolol (drug of choice). Also monitor vital signs, oxygen level, and potassium level.  Why B wrong: Atropine/dopamine are used for symptomatic bradycardia/blocks, not flutter rate control.  Why C wrong: Adrenaline is the asystole drug; it is not the listed flutter management.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is not indicated for atrial flutter."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Atrial fibrillation (AFib) is characterized on ECG by:","options":["A regular rhythm with a normal P-wave before every QRS","An irregularly irregular rhythm with no clear P-waves and inability to measure the PR interval","Sawtooth flutter waves","A widened QRS in a regular pattern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AFib results from multiple disorganized rapid impulses; the rhythm is irregularly irregular, there are no P-waves (fibrillatory waves seen on ECG, pulse deficit, irregular pulse), and the PR interval CANNOT be measured.  Why A wrong: AFib lacks normal P-waves and is irregular.  Why C wrong: Sawtooth waves are atrial flutter.  Why D wrong: A regular widened QRS describes other rhythms, not AFib."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Atrial fibrillation predisposes the patient to which serious complication due to thrombus formation in the atria?","options":["Major risk of thrombus formation or thromboembolism, which leads to stroke","Hypoglycemia","Hyperthyroidism","Tension pneumothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A major risk of AFib is thrombus formation or thromboembolism which can lead to stroke; causes include atherosclerosis, heart failure, COPD, hypothyroidism, and thyrotoxicosis.  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia is a metabolic disturbance, not a thromboembolic complication of AFib.  Why C wrong: Hyperthyroidism may contribute to AFib but is not the embolic complication itself.  Why D wrong: Tension pneumothorax is unrelated to AFib thrombus risk."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Clinical manifestations of atrial fibrillation may include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Palpitations","Dyspnea","Pulmonary edema","Sawtooth waves on palpation","Fatigue/weakness"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: AFib clinical manifestations may include palpitations, dyspnea, pulmonary edema, fatigue, and weakness.  Why D wrong: Sawtooth waves are an ECG flutter finding, not a clinical manifestation felt on palpation."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Management of atrial fibrillation includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Beta blockers for rate control / first treatment of choice","Diltiazem or Verapamil (calcium channel blockers)","Administer oxygen and anticoagulants as per order","Routine defibrillation in all stable patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: AFib management includes a beta blocker as the first treatment of choice, calcium channel blockers like Diltiazem/Verapamil, and administration of oxygen and anticoagulants as ordered (anticoagulation reduces thromboembolic/stroke risk).  Why D wrong: Routine defibrillation of all stable patients is not indicated; synchronized cardioversion is used for unstable patients, not defibrillation of every stable case."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In atrial fibrillation, the atrial rate usually exceeds:","options":["100 bpm","150 bpm","350 bpm","600 bpm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In AFib the atrial rate usually exceeds 350 bpm; if the ventricular rate is between 60 and 100 bpm it is \"controlled\" AFib, and if the ventricular rate is more than 100 bpm it is uncontrolled AFib with rapid ventricular response (RVR).  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated atrial rate threshold.  Why D wrong: 600 bpm is not the stated value (>350 bpm is correct)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"A premature ventricular contraction (PVC) is best described as a beat that begins:","options":["In the SA node, early in the cycle","In one of the heart's two lower-pumping chambers (ventricles), with extra beats disrupting the regular heart rhythm","In the AV node, conducted normally","In the atria as a sawtooth wave"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A PVC is an extra beat that begins in one of the heart's two lower-pumping chambers (ventricles); these extra beats disrupt the regular heart rhythm, sometimes causing a fluttering or skipped beat in the chest.  Why A wrong: PVCs originate in the ventricles, not the SA node.  Why C wrong: They are not normally conducted AV-node beats.  Why D wrong: Atrial sawtooth waves describe atrial flutter."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"On the ECG, the PVC complex results from which of the following?","options":["Increased irritability of the ventricles, producing an early and wide/bizarre QRS","Sawtooth atrial waves","A normal narrow QRS at a regular interval","Absence of all electrical activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A PVC is an early ventricular complex that results from increased irritability of the ventricles.  Why B wrong: Sawtooth waves are atrial flutter.  Why C wrong: A PVC is not a normal narrow regular QRS; it is premature and abnormal.  Why D wrong: Absence of electrical activity is asystole."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which statement about the cardiac/clinical effects of PVCs is correct?","options":["Cardiac output is always severely reduced","Cardiac replenishment is disrupted, and later ventricular fibrillation may occur; clinical manifestations include palpitations, weakness, and light-headedness but it is most of the time asymptomatic","PVCs always cause immediate cardiac arrest","PVCs eliminate the QRS complex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With PVCs, cardiac replenishment is disrupted and later ventricular fibrillation may occur; clinical manifestations include palpitations, weakness, and light-headedness, but most of the time it is asymptomatic.  Why A wrong: Cardiac output is not always severely reduced; PVCs are often asymptomatic.  Why C wrong: PVCs do not always cause immediate arrest.  Why D wrong: PVCs produce an abnormal QRS, not its elimination."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which steps are appropriate in the management of PVCs? (Select All That Apply)","options":["First, identify the cause and treat it at the basal of cause","Strictly monitor oxygen level and assess for hypoxemia","Strictly monitor serum potassium level, because hypokalemia causes PVCs","Immediately defibrillate every PVC"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: PVC management includes first identifying and treating the cause at its basis, strictly monitoring oxygen level (assess for hypoxemia), and strictly monitoring serum potassium level because hypokalemia causes PVCs.  Why D wrong: Defibrillating every PVC is not indicated; PVCs are often benign and the cause is treated."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) is described as:","options":["A non-perfusing arrhythmia (atrial dysrhythmia) with no cardiac output","A condition in which a wave is buried in T-wave, with a narrow-complex tachycardia, due to abnormal/reentrant circuit and increased atrial irritability","An irregularly irregular rhythm with no P-waves","A sawtooth flutter pattern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PSVT a wave is buried in the T-wave; it is a narrow-complex tachycardia which is due to abnormality/reentrant circuit and increased atrial irritability.  Why A wrong: A non-perfusing atrial dysrhythmia describes ventricular fibrillation, not PSVT.  Why C wrong: An irregularly irregular rhythm with no P-waves is atrial fibrillation.  Why D wrong: Sawtooth pattern is atrial flutter."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is BEST described as:","options":["A repetitive firing of an irritable ventricular ectopic focus, usually at a rate of 140–250 bpm","A slow ventricular escape rhythm at less than 40 bpm","An irregularly irregular atrial rhythm","A flat line with no electrical activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VT is a repetitive firing of an irritable ventricular ectopic focus, usually at a rate of 140–250 bpm.  Why B wrong: A slow escape rhythm is not VT.  Why C wrong: An irregularly irregular atrial rhythm is atrial fibrillation.  Why D wrong: A flat line is asystole."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Why does ventricular tachycardia cause decreased cardiac output?","options":["Because of decreased diastolic filling (preload) and a very rapidly conducted contraction in ventricular fibrillation, leading to low stroke volume and reduced cardiac output","Because the atria contract too forcefully","Because of an excessively slow heart rate","Because of a prolonged PR interval"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VT causes decreased cardiac output because of decreased diastolic filling (preload); the rapid contraction reduces stroke volume and thus cardiac output.  Why B wrong: The problem is reduced ventricular filling, not forceful atrial contraction.  Why C wrong: VT is a fast, not slow, rhythm.  Why D wrong: A prolonged PR interval is the mechanism of first-degree AV block, not VT."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Clinical manifestations of ventricular tachycardia may include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Light-headedness","Weakness","Dyspnea","Unconsciousness","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: VT clinical manifestations include light-headedness, weakness, dyspnea, and unconsciousness.  Why E wrong: VT is a tachyarrhythmia (fast rate 140–250 bpm); bradycardia is not a manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Causes of ventricular tachycardia include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["MI (myocardial infarction)","CAD (coronary artery disease)","Rheumatic heart disease","Valve prolapse","Hypokalemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: VT causes include MI, CAD, rheumatic heart diseases, valve prolapse, hypokalemia, and pulmonary embolism — all of these are listed.  All listed options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Management of ventricular tachycardia (with a pulse) includes which interventions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Administer oxygen and anti-dysrhythmic medications as per prescribed/order","If stable with a pulse, prepare for synchronized cardioversion","Administer procainamide or amiodarone as the drug of choice (per order)","Withhold all oxygen therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: VT management includes administering oxygen and anti-dysrhythmic medications as prescribed, and if the patient is stable/has a pulse, preparing for synchronized cardioversion; Procainamide or Amiodarone may be administered as the drug of choice per order.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is administered, not withheld, in VT management."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"In ventricular fibrillation (VF), which ECG description is correct?","options":["R-R intervals are usual, but not always, regular; the atrial rate cannot be determined; ventricular rate is usually between 150 and 250 bpm; QRS complexes are not preceded by P-waves and are not associated with the ventricular rhythm; PR interval is NOT MEASURED","A regular rhythm with a normal P-wave before every QRS","A flat line representing asystole","A sawtooth atrial pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In VF, R-R intervals are usually (but not always) regular, the atrial rate cannot be determined, ventricular rate is usually between 150 and 250 bpm, QRS complexes are not preceded by P-waves and are not associated with the ventricular rhythm, and the PR interval is NOT MEASURED; the QRS complex is wide and bizarre with a spindle appearance, making it difficult to separate the QRS complex from the T-wave.  Why B wrong: VF has no organized P-QRS relationship.  Why C wrong: A flat line is asystole, not VF.  Why D wrong: A sawtooth pattern is atrial flutter."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"A polymorphic form of ventricular tachycardia in which broad-complex QRS show waxing-and-waning amplitude, commonly associated with low magnesium, is known as:","options":["Torsades de pointes","Atrial flutter","Wenckebach phenomenon","Sinus arrhythmia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Torsades de pointes is the polymorphic VT in which broad-complex QRS show twisting/waxing-and-waning amplitude; it is commonly associated with hypomagnesemia (low magnesium) and may relate to hypokalemia (low potassium).  Why B wrong: Atrial flutter is an atrial sawtooth rhythm, not polymorphic VT.  Why C wrong: Wenckebach is a form of AV block.  Why D wrong: Sinus arrhythmia is a benign variation in sinus rate, not polymorphic VT."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Defibrillation is BEST described as:","options":["An emergent procedure used during resuscitation or CPR, using an asynchronous counter-shock to terminate pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pulseless VT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF)","A synchronized low-energy shock for stable atrial flutter","An elective procedure to slow sinus tachycardia","A pacemaker insertion procedure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Defibrillation is an emergent procedure used during resuscitation or CPR, using an asynchronous counter-shock to terminate pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pulseless VT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF) — the only two indications for defibrillation.  Why B wrong: A synchronized shock describes cardioversion, not defibrillation.  Why C wrong: Sinus tachycardia is not an indication for defibrillation.  Why D wrong: Defibrillation delivers a shock; it is not pacemaker insertion."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Defibrillation is indicated ONLY for which two rhythms?","options":["Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pulseless VT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF)","Atrial flutter and atrial fibrillation","Sinus bradycardia and first-degree AV block","PSVT and sinus tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are only two indications for defibrillation: pulseless VT and VF.  Why B/C/D wrong: Atrial arrhythmias, bradyarrhythmias/blocks, PSVT, and sinus tachycardia are not indications for defibrillation."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"When using a MONOPHASIC defibrillator, the energy delivered in one shock is:","options":["120–200 joules","200–360 joules","360 joules, with shock success rate of 90%","50–100 joules"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: With a monophasic defibrillator, 360 joules are delivered in one shock, with a shock success rate of 90%.  Why A wrong: 120–200 joules is the biphasic dose.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the stated monophasic value of 360 joules."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"When using a BIPHASIC defibrillator, the energy delivered in two directions per shock is:","options":["360 joules with 90% success","120–200 joules, delivered in two directions, with a shock success rate of >90%","50–100 joules","500 joules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A biphasic defibrillator delivers 120–200 joules in two directions, with a shock success rate of >90% (it is biphasic because the current travels in two directions, and a lower energy can be customized).  Why A wrong: 360 joules with 90% success describes the monophasic device.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated biphasic dose of 120–200 joules."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Immediately after delivering a shock with a defibrillator, what should the nurse do?","options":["Wait two minutes before doing anything","Initially choose biphasic mode to deliver shock because this is less traumatic, and continue CPR for two minutes","Check the patient's blood glucose","Stop CPR permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biphasic mode is initially chosen to deliver the shock because it is less traumatic; after a shock, the nurse continues CPR for two minutes.  Why A wrong: CPR should be resumed immediately, not delayed.  Why C wrong: Blood glucose check is not the immediate post-shock action.  Why D wrong: CPR is continued for two minutes, not stopped permanently."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Regarding paddle placement during adult defibrillation, where are the paddles positioned?","options":["Both over the abdomen","One paddle at the 2nd ICS space of the right sternum and the second at the 5th ICS space on the left mid-axillary line","Both on the patient's back","Both at the midclavicular line on the right"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paddle location for an adult is the 2nd ICS space of the right sternum and the second at the 5th ICS space on the left mid-axillary line.  Why A/C/D wrong: These placements do not match the stated paddle locations and would not deliver an effective transthoracic shock."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"What is the recommended paddle size for an adult versus a child during defibrillation?","options":["Adult 10–13 cm in diameter; child 8–10 cm diameter","Adult 5 cm; child 2 cm","Adult 20 cm; child 15 cm","Adult and child both 5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paddle size for an adult is 10–13 cm in diameter, and for children 8–10 cm in diameter.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sizes do not match the stated adult (10–13 cm) and child (8–10 cm) paddle diameters."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"During defibrillation, what critical safety precaution must the nurse ensure before delivering the shock?","options":["That no one is touching the bed or the client when delivering the shock (\"all clear\")","That the patient is connected to wall oxygen at high flow","That suction is running continuously","That the patient's IV is wide open"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Be sure no one is touching the bed or the client when delivering the shock — this prevents accidental electrical injury to staff.  Why B wrong: Oxygen should in fact be shut off before delivering shock to avoid fire accidents, not run at high flow.  Why C/D wrong: Continuous suction and a wide-open IV are not the critical electrical-safety precaution for shock delivery."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Why should oxygen be shut off before delivering a defibrillation shock?","options":["To conserve oxygen supply","To avoid fire accidents (oxygen + spark hazard)","To improve shock conduction through the chest","To prevent the patient from hyperventilating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen should be shut off before delivering a shock to avoid fire accidents, because oxygen supports combustion and a spark during defibrillation could ignite a fire.  Why A wrong: The reason is fire safety, not oxygen conservation.  Why C wrong: Oxygen flow does not improve electrical conduction through the chest.  Why D wrong: It is unrelated to hyperventilation."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Cardioversion is BEST described as:","options":["An emergent asynchronous shock for pulseless VT/VF","An elective cardiac procedure in which an electrical current is given to the myocardium in a synchronized fashion to depolarise all cells of the myocardium so it can generate its own impulses","A pacemaker insertion for complete heart block","A vagal maneuver such as carotid massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardioversion is an elective cardiac procedure in which an electrical current is given to the myocardium in a synchronized fashion to depolarise all the cells of the myocardium so it can generate its own impulses; it uses synchronized stimulation allowing the SA node to resume pacemaker function.  Why A wrong: Asynchronous emergent shock describes defibrillation, not cardioversion.  Why C wrong: Cardioversion is a shock procedure, not pacemaker insertion.  Why D wrong: A vagal maneuver is not cardioversion."},{"section":"Batch 021 · ECG graph paper & lead placement, ECG waves/intervals…","question":"Which nursing responsibilities are appropriate during a cardioversion procedure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Obtain informed consent if elective cardioversion is planned","Maintain NPO, verify a patent IV line, and administer oxygen; stop the oxygen during the procedure to avoid fire hazards","Assess vital signs, level of consciousness (LOC), and desired outcomes; assess the skin on the chest for burns","Leave the patient unmonitored after the shock"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care during cardioversion includes obtaining consent if elective, maintaining NPO, verifying a patent IV line and having available/administering oxygen, stopping the oxygen during the procedure to avoid fire hazards, assessing vital signs/LOC/desired outcomes, and assessing the skin on the chest for burns.  Why D wrong: The patient must be monitored (vital signs, LOC) after the shock, not left unmonitored."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Defibrillation is best described as which type of procedure?","options":["Elective planned procedure","Emergency life-saving procedure","Routine synchronized procedure","Low-energy synchronized shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Defibrillation is an emergency, life-saving procedure delivering an un-synchronized, high-energy shock.  Why A wrong: Elective planned procedure describes cardioversion, not defibrillation.  Why C wrong: Defibrillation is un-synchronized, not synchronized.  Why D wrong: Defibrillation uses high-energy shock, whereas cardioversion uses low-energy shock."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the shock used in defibrillation from that used in cardioversion?","options":["Defibrillation = synchronized shock; Cardioversion = un-synchronized shock","Defibrillation = un-synchronized high-energy shock; Cardioversion = synchronized low-energy shock","Both deliver synchronized high-energy shocks","Both deliver un-synchronized low-energy shocks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Defibrillation delivers an un-synchronized, high-energy shock; cardioversion delivers a synchronized, low-energy shock.  Why A wrong: It reverses the two correct descriptions.  Why C wrong: Only cardioversion is synchronized, and energy levels differ.  Why D wrong: Defibrillation is high-energy and un-synchronized."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During defibrillation, the high-energy shock is delivered to which part of the heart?","options":["The atrioventricular node","The myocardium","The pericardium only","The pulmonary veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In defibrillation, high-energy shock causes damage to (is delivered across) the myocardium.  Why A wrong: The shock is across the whole myocardium, not selectively the AV node.  Why C wrong: The pericardium is the outer sac, not the target tissue.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary veins are not the target of the shock."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"At what point in the cardiac cycle can defibrillation shock be delivered?","options":["Only during the R wave","Only during the T wave","At any time of the cardiac cycle","Only during the P wave"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Defibrillation is un-synchronized, so the shock can be given at any time of the cardiac cycle.  Why A wrong: Synchronizing to the R wave describes cardioversion, not defibrillation.  Why B wrong: Shocking on the T wave is what synchronized cardioversion avoids; defibrillation is given any time.  Why D wrong: There is no requirement to time defibrillation to the P wave."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"For which of the following is cardioversion most commonly used?","options":["Most of the arrhythmias except ventricular tachycardia (VT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF)","Only for ventricular fibrillation","Only for asystole","Only for pulseless electrical activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardioversion is used in most of the arrhythmias except VT/VF.  Why B wrong: VF is treated with defibrillation, not cardioversion.  Why C wrong: Asystole is not shockable.  Why D wrong: Pulseless electrical activity is non-shockable."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What does AED stand for?","options":["Automatic External Defibrillator","Acute Emergency Device","Automated Electrical Diagnostic","Advanced ECG Detector"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AED is the Automatic External Defibrillator.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the meaning of the abbreviation AED."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Where is an AED most usefully placed and who is it intended for?","options":["Inside ICUs, used only by cardiologists","Outside the hospital, used by emergency medical technicians (EMTs) for pre-hospital cardiac arrest","Only in operating theatres, used by anesthetists","Only in dialysis units, used by nephrologists"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An AED is a defibrillator used mostly outside the hospital by emergency medical technicians (EMTs) for pre-hospital cardiac arrest; its procedure is the same as a defibrillator.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies pre-hospital, EMT use outside the hospital, not these restricted clinical-only uses."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A pacemaker is best defined as a device that provides what type of stimuli?","options":["Mechanical compression of the chest","Electrical stimuli that maintain the heart rate when the client's natural pacemaker fails to provide a perfusing or normal rhythm","Chemical drug stimulation of the myocardium","Thermal stimuli to the SA node"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pacemaker is a temporary or permanent device that provides electrical stimuli to maintain the heart rate when the client's natural pacemaker fails to provide a perfusing rhythm.  Why A wrong: That describes chest compressions/CPR, not a pacemaker.  Why C wrong: A pacemaker uses electrical, not chemical, stimuli.  Why D wrong: It uses electrical, not thermal, stimuli."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"When pacing stimulus is delivered to the heart, what is seen on the cardiac monitor?","options":["A widened QRS only","A spike or straight vertical line","A flat isoelectric line","An inverted T wave"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When a pacing stimulus is delivered to the heart, a spike or straight vertical line is seen on the cardiac monitor.  Why A, C, D wrong: The characteristic pacing marker is the pacer spike (vertical line), not these findings."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which type of pacemaker is invasive and non-invasive?","options":["Permanent pacemakers","Temporary pacemakers","Implantable cardioverter defibrillators","AEDs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Temporary pacemakers include both invasive and non-invasive types.  Why A wrong: Permanent pacemakers are surgically implanted (invasive).  Why C, D wrong: ICDs and AEDs are not described as the temporary pacemaker category here."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"When using non-invasive pacemakers, the skin should be prepared with which agent before applying electrodes?","options":["Povidone-iodine","Soap and water","Alcohol swab only","Sterile saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When using non-invasive pacemakers, prepare the skin with soap and water before applying electrodes.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies soap and water for skin preparation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In a female client with a pacemaker, where should the anterior electrode NOT be placed?","options":["Over the sternum","Over the female breast","Below the right clavicle","On the left lateral chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not place the anterior electrode over the female breast; it should be placed under the breast instead.  Why A, C, D wrong: The breast tissue is the specific site to avoid for the anterior electrode."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"When a patient with a pacemaker takes a bath or shower, what should be avoided?","options":["Using warm water","Taking the BP on the side of the pacemaker pocket","Sitting upright","Wearing loose clothing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not take the pulse or BP on the side of the patient with a pacemaker.  Why A, C, D wrong: These actions are not the specified precaution; the precaution concerns avoiding BP/pulse measurement on the pacemaker side."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In a permanent pacemaker implanted in the left atrium, where is the pulse generator typically implanted?","options":["In the abdomen below the umbilicus","Subcutaneously in the antecubital fossa, femoral, jugular, or subclavian vein into the right atrium","Directly inside the ventricle wall","Externally on the skin surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In invasive (transvenous) pacemakers a wire is placed through the antecubital fossa, femoral, jugular, or subclavian vein into the right atrium or right ventricle.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the described transvenous wire insertion sites for invasive pacemakers."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What environments are contraindicated for a patient who has an implanted pacemaker?","options":["High-magnetic fields such as scanning devices at airport and MRI","Air-conditioned rooms","Swimming pools","Brightly lit rooms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High magnetic fields, e.g. scanning devices at airport and MRI, are contraindicated in a patient with an implanted pacemaker.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the contraindicated electromagnetic environments noted for pacemakers."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A vagal maneuver is an action used to do what to the heart rate?","options":["Speed up the heart rate by sympathetic stimulation","Slow down the heart rate by stimulating the vagus nerve through massage, valsalva maneuver, coughing, and gagging","Stop the heart completely","Increase myocardial oxygen demand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vagal maneuvers slow down the heart rate by stimulating the vagus nerve via carotid sinus massage, valsalva, coughing, and gagging.  Why A wrong: It is parasympathetic (vagal), which slows the heart, not sympathetic speeding.  Why C wrong: It slows, not stops, the heart.  Why D wrong: It does not increase myocardial oxygen demand."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During carotid sinus massage as a vagal maneuver, the heart rate can decrease within roughly what time?","options":["Within seconds","Within 30 minutes","Within 6 hours","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carotid sinus massage is which massaging of the carotid artery can decrease heart rate within seconds.  Why B, C, D wrong: The vagal response is rapid (seconds), not minutes or hours."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During a Valsalva maneuver used as a vagal maneuver, the nurse instructs the client to do what?","options":["Cough forcefully and continuously","Bear down (forced expiration against a closed glottis) which induces a gag reflex for vagal stimulation that decreases heart rate","Hyperventilate rapidly","Hold the breath for 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Valsalva maneuver the nurse instructs the client to bear down, which induces a gag reflex for vagal stimulation that can decrease heart rate.  Why A wrong: Coughing is a separate vagal maneuver, not the Valsalva itself.  Why C wrong: Hyperventilation is not the Valsalva maneuver.  Why D wrong: Prolonged breath-holding is not described and would be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"How are cardiac pressures measured?","options":["By a non-invasive cuff on the arm","By an invasive procedure through a catheter inserted through peripheral vessels","Only by echocardiography","Only by chest X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac pressures are measured by an invasive procedure in which a catheter is inserted through peripheral vessels.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are non-invasive/imaging methods, not the invasive catheter-based pressure measurement described."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which catheter is a pulmonary artery catheter and is the most common catheter and procedure for hemodynamic monitoring?","options":["Foley catheter","Swan-Ganz catheter","Central line in the femoral vein","Arterial line in the radial artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Swan-Ganz catheter is a pulmonary artery catheter and is the most common catheter and procedure for hemodynamic monitoring.  Why A wrong: A Foley catheter is urinary, not for cardiac pressures.  Why C, D wrong: While arterial/central lines measure some pressures, the Swan-Ganz is specified as the common hemodynamic monitoring catheter."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Arterial and venous oxygen saturation and core temperature can both be measured by which catheter?","options":["Swan-Ganz catheter","Peripheral IV cannula","Nasogastric tube","Foley catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arterial and venous oxygen saturation and core temperature are measured by the Swan-Ganz catheter.  Why B, C, D wrong: These devices do not measure mixed venous oxygen saturation or core temperature in this context."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (PCWP) is also called what?","options":["Cross-sectional pressure (CSP)","Mean arterial pressure","Central venous pressure","Pulse pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCWP is also called cross-sectional pressure (CSP).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are distinct cardiac/vascular pressures, not synonyms for PCWP."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What is the normal range of Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (PCWP)?","options":["2–8 mm Hg","6–12 mm Hg","16–30 mm Hg","0–4 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal PCWP is 6–12 mm Hg.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated normal PCWP range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What is the normal range of Pulmonary Artery Pressure (PAP)?","options":["Systolic 16–30 mm Hg; Diastolic 8–12 mm Hg","Systolic 120/80 mm Hg","2–6 mm Hg","0–4 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulmonary artery pressure is systolic 16–30 mm Hg, diastolic 8–12 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 120/80 is systemic arterial blood pressure, not PAP.  Why C, D wrong: These low values correspond to central venous pressure, not PAP."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Pulmonary Artery Pressure (PAP) is measured at what point?","options":["Beginning of inspiration","End of expiration","Peak of inspiration","During Valsalva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mean pulmonary artery pressure is measured at end of expiration.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies end expiration for the measurement."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Central Venous Pressure (CVP) indicates the pressure in which structure?","options":["Right atrium or both vena cava","Left ventricle","Pulmonary artery","Aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CVP indicates pressure of the right atrium or both vena cava.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are different chambers/vessels, not what CVP reflects."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"CVP reflects the amount of blood returning to the heart and the ability of the heart to do what?","options":["Pump blood back into the arterial system","Filter venous blood","Generate electrical impulses","Maintain core temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CVP reflects the amount of blood returning to the heart and the ability of the heart to pump blood back into the arterial system.  Why B, C, D wrong: CVP is a measure of venous return and pumping ability, not filtering, electrical activity, or thermoregulation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"CVP is measured in what units and at the end of which respiratory phase?","options":["In mm Hg at the end of inspiration","In cm of water (or mm Hg) at the end of expiration, with the transducer at the level of the phlebostatic axis or 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line","In Joules during systole","In liters per minute during diastole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CVP is measured in position at the end of expiration with the transducer at the level of the phlebostatic axis or 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line.  Why A wrong: It is measured at end expiration, not inspiration.  Why C, D wrong: These are inappropriate units (energy/flow) for a pressure measurement."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What is the normal range of CVP?","options":["2–6 mm Hg","16–30 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","120/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal range of CVP is 2–6 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 16–30 mm Hg is pulmonary artery systolic pressure.  Why C wrong: 80–100 relates to mean arterial pressure range.  Why D wrong: 120/80 is systemic blood pressure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A normal range of CVP in cm H2O at the sternum level is which of the following?","options":["0–14 cm H2O at midaxillary line; 5–8 cm H2O","16–30 cm H2O","80–120 cm H2O","0.5–1 cm H2O"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal range of CVP in cm H2O at the sternum level is 0–14 cm H2O, and at the midaxillary line 5–8 cm H2O.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated CVP values in cm H2O."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A CVP reading of less than 2 mm Hg most likely indicates which condition?","options":["Fluid overload","Low blood volume (hypovolemia), requiring immediate administration of fluids","Right-sided heart failure","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If CVP is less than 2 mm Hg, it indicates low blood volume (hypovolemia), so fluids must be administered immediately.  Why A wrong: Fluid overload raises, not lowers, the CVP.  Why C wrong: Right-sided heart failure increases CVP.  Why D wrong: Hypertension is a systemic arterial condition, not indicated by a low CVP."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A CVP reading of more than 6 mm Hg indicates which of the following?","options":["Hypovolemia","Hypervolemia (fluid overload), so reduce or slow the flow rate of fluids","Dehydration","Normal venous return"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If CVP is more than 6 mm Hg, it indicates hypervolemia (fluid overload); reduce or slow the flow rate of fluids.  Why A wrong: Hypovolemia produces a low CVP.  Why C wrong: Dehydration would lower CVP.  Why D wrong: A value above the 2–6 mm Hg normal is abnormal, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Cardiac index is calculated by which formula?","options":["Cardiac output / BSA (body surface area)","Heart rate × stroke volume","Cardiac output × heart rate","BSA / cardiac output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac Index (CI) = Cardiac Output / BSA (stroke volume × heart rate / BSA).  Why B wrong: Heart rate × stroke volume gives cardiac output, not cardiac index.  Why C, D wrong: These are incorrect arrangements of the formula."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What is the normal cardiac index value?","options":["2.5–4 L/min/m²","6–12 L/min/m²","0.5–1 L/min/m²","16–30 L/min/m²"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal cardiac index is 2.5–4 L/min/m².  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated normal cardiac index."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What minimum cardiac index is required to maintain life without mechanical support?","options":["2.0 L/min/m²","0.5 L/min/m²","4.0 L/min/m²","6.0 L/min/m²"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A minimum of 2.0 L/min/m² is required to maintain life without mechanical support.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated minimum survival cardiac index."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) is the average of which pressures?","options":["Systolic and diastolic pressure","Pulse pressure and venous pressure","CVP and PCWP","Stroke volume and heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MAP is the average pressure of the systolic and diastolic pressure.  Why B, C, D wrong: MAP is derived from arterial systolic and diastolic pressures, not these combinations."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"What is the normal range of Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP)?","options":["70–100 mm Hg","2–6 mm Hg","16–30 mm Hg","120/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal MAP range is 70–100 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 2–6 mm Hg is CVP.  Why C wrong: 16–30 mm Hg is pulmonary artery systolic pressure.  Why D wrong: 120/80 is a systolic/diastolic blood pressure, not the mean."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Using a BP of 120/80 mm Hg, calculate the MAP using MAP = (DBP×2 + SBP)/3.","options":["60 mm Hg","About 93.3 mm Hg","120 mm Hg","80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAP = (80×2 + 120)/3 = (160 + 120)/3 = 280/3 = approximately 93.3 mm Hg, as worked in the source.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not result from the formula applied to 120/80."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During cardiac pressure monitoring with central pressure lines, in what position should the patient be placed?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Internal jugular vein and self subclavian vein (i.e., supine with the head of bed flat)","Prone position","Standing position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For cardiac pressure monitoring, central catheters are placed via the internal jugular vein and subclavian vein and the patient is positioned accordingly for accurate readings.  Why A, C, D wrong: These positions are not specified for accurate central pressure measurement."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"When placing the patient in a flat (supine, head of bed at 45°) position during CVP measurement, what is the main reason?","options":["To improve patient comfort only","To obtain an accurate observational pressure that is 4–8 cm H2O","To prevent pneumothorax","To increase cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flat/45° supine position is used to obtain an accurate observational pressure (around 4–8 cm H2O) during CVP measurement.  Why A, C, D wrong: Positioning here is for accurate pressure readings, not these effects."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Stroke volume is defined as which of the following?","options":["The heart rate per minute","The volume of blood pumped by the heart with each beat (in milliliters), divided by body surface area gives the stroke volume index (square meters)","The total blood volume in the body","The pressure in the right atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped by the heart with each beat (in milliliters); divided by body surface area it gives the stroke volume index (square meters).  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe heart rate, total blood volume, and CVP, not stroke volume."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is calculated by which approach?","options":["Subtracting the right atrial pressure (RAP) from the mean arterial pressure (MAP), then dividing by the cardiac output (CO) and multiplying by 80","Multiplying heart rate by stroke volume","Dividing cardiac output by body surface area","Adding systolic and diastolic pressures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SVR is calculated by subtracting RAP from MAP, divided by CO, and multiplied by 80 (Normal SVR is 900–1500 dynes/seconds/cm⁵).  Why B wrong: That gives cardiac output.  Why C wrong: That gives cardiac index.  Why D wrong: That relates to pulse pressure/MAP estimation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"An increased CVP is found in which condition?","options":["Right-sided heart failure, hypervolemia, and decreased hypovolemia","Dehydration","Hemorrhagic shock","Severe diuresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CVP is increased in Right-Sided Heart Failure, hypervolemia, and decreased in hypovolemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Dehydration, hemorrhage, and diuresis cause hypovolemia, which decreases CVP."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Hypertension is defined as which of the following?","options":["A condition in which the force of the blood against the artery walls is too low","A condition in which the force of the blood against the artery walls is too high","A condition with no change in blood vessel tone","A reduced venous return to the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is a condition in which the force of the blood against the artery walls is too high.  Why A wrong: That describes hypotension, the opposite.  Why C, D wrong: These do not define hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Hypertension is also high when blood pressure is above which threshold (sustained over baseline)?","options":["Above 120/80 mm Hg","High blood pressure above a baseline of about 120/80 mm Hg sustained over time","Above 90/60 mm Hg","Above 80/50 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is high blood pressure above a baseline of about 120/80 mm Hg sustained over time.  Why A wrong: Although 120/80 is the reference baseline, the defining feature is sustained elevation above it.  Why C, D wrong: These are low/normal-to-low pressures, not hypertensive thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Hypertension is also known as a \"silent killer disease\" mainly because of which feature?","options":["It is most common type of hypertension","It is most often asymptomatic until complications occur, so it goes undetected","It always causes immediate chest pain","It is curable in all cases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is called a silent killer disease because it is most of the time asymptomatic — there are no obvious symptoms until complications occur.  Why A wrong: Being common does not make it \"silent.\"  Why C wrong: It is typically symptomless, not painful, in early stages.  Why D wrong: It is managed/controlled, and \"silent killer\" refers to lack of symptoms, not curability."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Primary hypertension is best characterized by which statement?","options":["It has an identifiable single cause","Its exact cause is unknown and sometimes assumed it happened due to sympathetic nervous system activity, RAAS mechanism, and insulin resistance, accounting for almost 95% of cases","It is caused by renal failure","It is related to pathological conditions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primary hypertension's exact cause is unknown; assumed contributors include sympathetic nervous system activity, the RAAS mechanism, and insulin resistance, and it is the most common type (almost 95%).  Why A wrong: Primary hypertension has no single identifiable cause.  Why C, D wrong: Identifiable pathological causes (e.g., renal) describe secondary hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Risk factors for primary hypertension include which of the following?","options":["Family history, increase intake, increased age","Low sodium intake only","Daily aerobic exercise only","Weight loss only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include family health history, increase in salt intake, and increasing age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are protective/reducing factors, not risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Secondary hypertension differs from primary hypertension in that it:","options":["Has no identifiable cause","Is related to pathological conditions, e.g., renal failure, heart failure, and vascular disorders","Accounts for 95% of all hypertension","Is always asymptomatic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary hypertension is related to pathological conditions such as renal failure, heart failure, and vascular disorders.  Why A wrong: It has an identifiable cause, unlike primary hypertension.  Why C wrong: Primary hypertension accounts for ~95%.  Why D wrong: This is not its defining feature."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"How is hypertension diagnosed regarding the number of readings?","options":["By a single reading","By symptomatology and elevated blood pressure on separate occasions; it is related to pathological conditions; needs 3 times reading of elevated blood pressure","By a single home reading only","By a single chest X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension diagnosis is by clinical feature/symptomatology, and by symptomatology measurement it needs 3 times reading of elevated blood pressure measured on separate occasions.  Why A, C wrong: A single reading is insufficient; multiple separate readings are required.  Why D wrong: Chest X-ray does not diagnose hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the AHA classification of blood pressure, \"Normal\" BP is defined as which of the following?","options":["SBP <120 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP ≥140 mm Hg or DBP ≥90 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AHA classification: Normal = SBP <120 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: SBP 120–129 with DBP <80 is \"Elevated.\"  Why C wrong: SBP 130–139 or DBP 80–89 is \"Stage 1 Hypertension.\"  Why D wrong: SBP ≥140 or DBP ≥90 is \"Stage 2 Hypertension.\""},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the AHA classification, \"Elevated\" blood pressure is defined as:","options":["SBP <120 and DBP <80","SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP ≥180 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AHA: Elevated = SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: That is Normal.  Why C wrong: That is Stage 1 Hypertension.  Why D wrong: SBP ≥180 indicates hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the AHA classification, Stage 1 Hypertension is defined as:","options":["SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP <120 and DBP <80","SBP ≥140 or DBP ≥90","SBP >180"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AHA: Stage 1 Hypertension = SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: That is Normal.  Why C wrong: That is Stage 2 Hypertension.  Why D wrong: That is hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the AHA classification, Stage 2 Hypertension is defined as:","options":["SBP 130–139 or DBP 80–89","SBP ≥140 mm Hg or DBP ≥90 mm Hg","SBP <120 and DBP <80","SBP 120–129 and DBP <80"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AHA: Stage 2 Hypertension = SBP ≥140 mm Hg or DBP ≥90 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: That is Stage 1 Hypertension.  Why C wrong: That is Normal.  Why D wrong: That is Elevated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the AHA classification, hypertensive crisis is defined by which value?","options":["SBP <120 and DBP <80","SBP >180 mm Hg and/or DBP >120 mm Hg","SBP 120–129 and DBP <80","SBP 130–139 and DBP 80–89"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AHA: Hypertensive crisis = SBP >180 mm Hg and/or DBP >120 mm Hg.  Why A, C, D wrong: These correspond to Normal, Elevated, and Stage 1, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Malignant hypertension is defined by which BP values, and how should it be treated?","options":["SBP >180 mm Hg and DBP >120 mm Hg; it is a medical emergency that should be treated immediately, often with sodium nitroprusside","SBP <120 and DBP <80; no treatment needed","SBP 130–139; treat with lifestyle changes only","SBP 120–129; treat with reassurance only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malignant hypertension is SBP >180 mm Hg and DBP >120 mm Hg, a medical emergency that should be treated immediately, e.g., with sodium nitroprusside.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe normal/lower BP ranges not requiring emergency treatment."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following is a sign/symptom of hypertension?","options":["Headache and headache/blurred vision","Bradycardia only","Hypoglycemia","Increased appetite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of hypertension include headache, chest pain, epistaxis, tinnitus and dizziness, and visual disturbances (e.g., blurred vision).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed signs/symptoms of hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of hypertension? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Headache","Chest pain","Epistaxis","Tinnitus and dizziness","Visual disturbances"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists headache, chest pain, epistaxis, tinnitus and dizziness, and visual disturbances as signs and symptoms of hypertension.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly listed as hypertension signs/symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of hypertension?","options":["Coronary artery disease","Improved vision","Decreased cardiac workload","Lowered serum cholesterol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of hypertension include coronary artery disease, organ damage (heart, brain, kidneys, and eyes), and stroke/CVA and congestive heart failure (CHF) — CHF is the most common cause of CHF in HTN.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not complications of hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Per the source, what is noted as the most common cause of CHF in hypertension?","options":["Stroke or CVA and congestive heart failure (CHF) — HTN is the most common cause of CHF","Anemia","Dehydration","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states stroke or CVA and congestive heart failure (CHF), noting hypertension as the most common cause of CHF.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not listed as the cause of CHF in this context."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Lifestyle modification for hypertension includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Weight control","Dietary modification","Limit sodium intake — WHO recommends less than 2 gm/day of salt (equivalent to less than 5 g/day salt)","Regular exercise and meditation for relaxation and stress reduction","Avoid alcohol or it should be drunk under limit"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists weight control, dietary modification, limiting sodium (WHO recommends less than 2 gm/day of salt, equivalent to less than 5 g/day salt), regular exercise/meditation for relaxation and stress reduction, and avoiding/limiting alcohol.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly part of lifestyle modification for hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to WHO, the recommended salt (sodium) intake to control hypertension is:","options":["Less than 2 gm/day of salt (less than 5 g/day salt)","More than 10 g/day","Exactly 8 g/day","No restriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO recommends less than 2 gm/day of salt (equivalent to less than 5 g/day salt).  Why B, C, D wrong: These exceed or ignore the WHO recommended limit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Atherosclerosis is described as the most common cause of which disorder?","options":["Cerebrovascular disorder","Gastric ulcer","Asthma","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atherosclerosis is the most common cause of cerebrovascular disorder.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not linked to atherosclerosis in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Nursing care advice for hypertension includes which of the following regarding alcohol and sodium?","options":["Encourage high alcohol and high sodium intake","Limit alcohol and high sodium intake; advise lifestyle modification including weight control and dietary modification","Stop all fluids completely","Increase saturated fat intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care/lifestyle modification advises limiting alcohol and high sodium intake along with weight control and dietary modification.  Why A, C, D wrong: These contradict hypertension management principles in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Orthostatic (postural) hypotension is best described as which of the following?","options":["A variation in BP (systolic >20 mm Hg or diastolic >10 mm Hg) while a patient is changing position suddenly, e.g., from lying to standing","A persistently high blood pressure at rest","A normal physiologic finding with no significance","A BP elevation only during exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orthostatic hypotension is a variation in BP (systolic >20 mm Hg or diastolic >10 mm Hg) while changing position suddenly, e.g., from lying to standing, a condition called orthostatic hypotension.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the postural drop definition."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Signs and symptoms of orthostatic hypotension include which of the following?","options":["Syncope, occipital headache (cardinal sign), tinnitus, visual disturbances, palpitations, retinal changes (retinopathy), epistaxis, cardiac hypertrophy, and nephropathy","Increased appetite only","Bradycardia only","Hyperglycemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms include syncope, occipital headache (cardinal sign), tinnitus, visual disturbances, retinal changes (retinopathy), epistaxis, cardiac hypertrophy, and nephropathy, along with palpitations.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed manifestations."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Gestational hypertension is usually defined as having a blood pressure higher than which value?","options":["Higher than 140/90 mm Hg measured on two separate occasions, more than 6 hours apart, after 20 weeks of gestation","Higher than 120/80 mm Hg before pregnancy","Higher than 90/60 mm Hg","Higher than 100/70 mm Hg before 12 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational hypertension is usually defined as having a blood pressure higher than 140/90 mm Hg measured on two separate occasions, more than 6 hours apart, after 20 weeks of gestation, when there is no proof of protein in the urine (no proteinuria).  Why B, C, D wrong: These thresholds and timings do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"A key feature distinguishing gestational hypertension is:","options":["Presence of heavy proteinuria","No proteinuria (no proof of protein in the urine), and BP returns to normal before 12 weeks postpartum","Onset before 20 weeks of gestation","It only occurs after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational hypertension presents with no proteinuria; BP returns to normal before 12 weeks postpartum, and onset is after 20 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: Heavy proteinuria suggests preeclampsia, not isolated gestational hypertension.  Why C wrong: Onset is after 20 weeks, not before.  Why D wrong: It occurs during pregnancy (after 20 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"White coat hypertension is best described as which of the following?","options":["A condition in which a patient's blood pressure readings are higher when taken at the doctor's office due to stress, compared to other settings like home","Persistent hypertension at home but normal in clinic","Hypertension only during sleep","Hypertension caused by drinking coffee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: White coat hypertension is a condition in which a patient's blood pressure readings are higher when taken at the doctor's office due to stress, compared to other settings like home.  Why B wrong: That describes masked hypertension, the opposite.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the definition of white coat hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Angina is best described as which of the following?","options":["A disease in itself","A type of chest pain caused by reduced blood flow to the heart, caused by inadequate myocardial blood and oxygen supply","A condition with increased coronary blood flow","Pain caused by lung infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angina is a type of chest pain caused by reduced blood flow to the heart, caused by inadequate myocardial blood and oxygen supply; angina is not a disease (it is a symptom).  Why A wrong: The source explicitly states angina is not a disease.  Why C wrong: It is caused by reduced, not increased, blood flow.  Why D wrong: It is cardiac in origin, not from lung infection."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Angina results from which imbalance?","options":["Imbalance between oxygen supply and oxygen demand of the myocardium","Imbalance of sodium and potassium","Excess oxygen supply over demand","Imbalance of glucose levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Angina is the chest pain resulting from an imbalance between oxygen supply and oxygen demand of the myocardium.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not describe the supply-demand mismatch underlying angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Stable angina is best described by which features?","options":["Also called exertional angina; occurs with activities such as exercise, running, walking, and emotional stress, and is relieved with rest and nitroglycerin","Occurs at rest only and is unpredictable","Always indicates myocardial infarction","Occurs only during sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stable angina is also called exertional angina; it occurs with activities, e.g., during exercise, running, walking, and emotional stress, and is relieved with rest and nitroglycerin.  Why B wrong: Rest/unpredictable angina describes unstable or variant angina.  Why C wrong: Stable angina is not an MI.  Why D wrong: Sleep-related/at-rest angina describes variant angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Unstable angina is characterized by which of the following?","options":["Pre-infarction angina; occurs at rest or during minimal activities, mostly occurs as a precursor of myocardial infarction, and the pain is more severe, prolonged (lasting in crescendo pattern), and may not be relieved by nitroglycerin","Pain relieved completely by rest within seconds","Pain occurring only during heavy exercise and relieved instantly","Pain occurring only postprandially with no progression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Unstable angina (also called pre-infarction angina) occurs at rest or during minimal activities, mostly occurs as a precursor of myocardial infarction, and the pain is more severe, prolonged (crescendo pattern), and may not be relieved by nitroglycerin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe relief patterns more typical of stable angina, not unstable angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Variant angina (also called Prinzmetal's angina) is characterized by which of the following?","options":["It is caused by coronary artery spasm; it occurs due to coronary artery spasm and most often occurs at rest","It is caused by exercise only","It is always relieved by activity","It never occurs at rest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Variant angina is also called Prinzmetal's angina; it is caused by coronary artery spasm and most often occurs at rest.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the rest-occurring, spasm-induced nature of variant angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In the pathophysiology of angina, the initial event from atherosclerosis/arterial spasm leads to which sequence?","options":["Gradual obstruction or sudden reversible obstruction → ischemia → hypoxia → reduced oxygen demand","Increased coronary blood flow → hyperoxia","Direct necrosis without ischemia","Immediate permanent thrombus formation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In angina pathophysiology, atherosclerosis/arterial spasm (or atherosclerotic plaque/thrombus, occlusion) causes gradual obstruction or sudden reversible obstruction → ischemia → hypoxia, with reduced oxygen demand leading to angina (and thrombosis to unstable angina).  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the angina (reversible ischemia) pathway; permanent thrombus/necrosis describes MI."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which sign/symptom of angina relates to the duration and character of the pain?","options":["Pain which slowly and quickly lasts up to 5 minutes, characteristic of pain that is substernal, crushing, squeezing","Pain lasting more than 30 minutes unrelieved by rest","Pain only in the lower limbs","Pain lasting several days continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of angina include pain which lasts up to ~5 minutes that is characteristically substernal, crushing, and squeezing.  Why B wrong: Pain >30 minutes unrelieved is characteristic of MI, not angina.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the described angina pain."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of angina? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pain that may radiate to the shoulders, arms, jaw, neck, or back","Dyspnea, pallor, sweating, and palpitation","Tachycardia, dizziness, and syncope (temporary loss of consciousness)","Hypertension","Pain free during sleep with no episodes ever"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Angina signs/symptoms include pain radiating to shoulders, arms, jaw, neck, or back; dyspnea, pallor, sweating, and palpitation; tachycardia, dizziness, and syncope (temporary loss of consciousness); and hypertension.  Why E wrong: This is not a listed sign/symptom; angina can occur at rest in some types (e.g., variant), so \"no episodes ever\" is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which ECG/diagnostic findings are associated with angina?","options":["ECG showing ST depression and T-wave inversion; cardiac enzymes","Permanent ST elevation with Q waves only","Normal ECG always","Elevated blood glucose only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of angina includes ECG showing ST depression and T-wave inversion, plus cardiac enzymes.  Why B wrong: Permanent ST elevation/Q waves indicate MI, not angina.  Why C wrong: ECG changes (ST depression, T inversion) are typically present.  Why D wrong: Blood glucose is not the diagnostic for angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In nursing care for angina, what is a priority action regarding pain assessment?","options":["Ignore the pain until it resolves","Assess the pain, then initiate pain relief measures immediately","Administer oral fluids before assessing","Delay assessment for 30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for angina is to assess pain, then initiate pain relief measures immediately.  Why A, C, D wrong: Delaying or ignoring pain relief is unsafe and not part of the care."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During an anginal episode, oxygen is administered by which route to relieve ischemia?","options":["By nasal cannula, as per saturation; if it indicates hypovolemia or fluid intolerance, oxygen administration via mask helps the patient remain unaffected","By chest tube","By intra-arterial line","By rectal route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for angina includes administering oxygen by nasal cannula as per saturation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Oxygen for angina is given by nasal cannula/mask, not these inappropriate routes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"During nursing care for angina/MI, after assessing vital signs and continuing cardiac monitoring, which medication is administered as prescribed for chest pain?","options":["Give nitroglycerin as prescribed","Give insulin as prescribed","Give a diuretic as prescribed","Give an antibiotic as prescribed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After assessing vital signs and continuing cardiac monitoring, give nitroglycerin as prescribed.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the prescribed agent for relieving ischemic chest pain in this context."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which nursing actions are part of the care for a patient with chest pain/MI? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assess all vital signs and continue cardiac monitoring","Give nitroglycerin as prescribed","Place the client in semi-Fowler's position","Take a 12-lead ECG","Initiate IV line and start IV fluids for tissue perfusion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists assessing all vital signs and continuing cardiac monitoring, giving nitroglycerin as prescribed, placing the client in semi-Fowler's position, taking a 12-lead ECG, and initiating IV line and IV fluids for tissue perfusion.  Why none wrong: All five are listed nursing actions."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"After stabilization of the client, what should the nurse do regarding nursing care?","options":["Discontinue all care immediately","Continue general nursing care","Discharge the client at once","Stop cardiac monitoring permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After stabilization of the client, the nurse should continue general nursing care.  Why A, C, D wrong: Care should continue, not be stopped, after stabilization."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, Levine's sign is a universal sign of ischemic chest pain (angina) and is defined as which gesture?","options":["An individual holding a clenched fist over the chest area","Pointing to a single spot with one finger","Rubbing the abdomen","Pressing the temples"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Levine's sign is a universal sign of ischemic chest pain (angina), defined as an individual holding a clenched fist over the chest area.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not describe Levine's sign."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, which ECG finding is highly predictive but not specific for ischemia/myocardial infarction?","options":["A new low-voltage P wave","A new sign, e.g., a convex ST segment, is highly predictive of myocardial infarction","A normal sinus rhythm","A shortened PR interval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states a new sign (e.g., convex ST segment) is highly predictive of myocardial infarction (though not highly specific for ischemia).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the highly predictive MI finding described."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Myocardial infarction (MI) is also known as which of the following?","options":["Heart attack","Stroke","Pulmonary embolism","Aneurysm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MI is also known as MI or heart attack.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are different conditions, not synonyms for MI."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Myocardial infarction is best defined by which of the following?","options":["It is a blockage of blood flow to the heart that occurs when blood flow decreases or stops to a part of the heart, causing damage to the heart muscle (necrosis of heart tissue), especially myocardium, caused by interruption of oxygen supply to the area resulting in altered function and cardiac output","A temporary, reversible reduction in blood flow with no tissue damage","Increased oxygen supply to the heart muscle","Inflammation of the pericardium only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MI is a blockage of blood flow to the heart that occurs when blood flow decreases or stops to a part of the heart, causing damage to the heart muscle, especially myocardium, caused by interruption of oxygen supply, resulting in altered function and cardiac output.  Why B wrong: That describes angina (reversible), not infarction (necrosis).  Why C wrong: MI involves decreased, not increased, oxygen supply.  Why D wrong: That describes pericarditis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Most common cause of myocardial infarction is occlusion of which coronary artery?","options":["Most common cause is blockage of the left anterior descending artery (LAD) — if blockage occurs in the LAD artery, the less chance of survival of the patient because LAD supplies blood to the left ventricle","The right coronary artery only","The circumflex artery only","The pulmonary artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of MI is blockage of the LAD artery; if blockage occurs in the LAD there is less chance of survival because the LAD supplies blood to the left ventricle.  Why B, C wrong: These are not cited as the most common cause.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary artery is not a coronary artery supplying the myocardium with oxygenated blood."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Silent myocardial infarction (silent MI) is commonly seen in which patient population?","options":["Patients with diabetes","Healthy young athletes","Children only","Patients with asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silent myocardial infarction (MI) is commonly seen in diabetes (diabetic patients), where MI may occur without typical pain due to neuropathy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the population specified for silent MI."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for myocardial infarction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Family history, advanced age, gender (male)","Smoking, alcohol, and hypertension","Obesity (specially abdominal obesity) and sedentary lifestyle","Type-2 diabetes mellitus (DM) and metabolic syndrome, stress","Atherosclerosis (fatty substances deposited in inner wall of the vessels)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists family history, advanced age, gender (male); smoking, alcohol, hypertension; obesity (especially abdominal) and sedentary lifestyle; type-2 DM and metabolic syndrome and stress; and atherosclerosis as MI risk factors.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Hyperlipidemia is a risk factor for MI when total cholesterol level is above what value?","options":["Above 200 mg/dL","Above 100 mg/dL","Above 70 mg/dL","Above 50 mg/dL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperlipidemia is when total cholesterol level is above 200 mg/dL.  Why B, C, D wrong: These values do not match the stated hyperlipidemia threshold."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which sign/symptom is described as the cardinal sign of MI regarding chest pain?","options":["Retrosternal pain (cardinal sign) that radiates to the arm, jaw, neck, shoulder, or back","Pain only in the right foot","Painless tingling in fingertips only","Pain only in the abdomen relieved by food"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of MI is retrosternal pain that radiates to the arm, jaw, neck, shoulder, or back.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the cardinal retrosternal radiating chest pain of MI."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the pain characteristics of MI?","options":["Pressure, crushing — like pain that lasts for 30 minutes or longer, and is not relieved by rest and nitroglycerin","Pain relieved within 5 minutes by rest","Mild pain relieved by nitroglycerin in seconds","Pain only during exercise that stops immediately at rest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MI pain characteristics are pressure, crushing-like pain that lasts for 30 minutes or longer and is not relieved by rest and nitroglycerin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe angina, which is relieved by rest/nitroglycerin; MI pain is prolonged and unrelieved."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of MI? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pain not relieved by rest and nitroglycerin; relieved only by opioids, e.g., morphine","Feeling of heaviness, anxiety, and cool extremities","Sympathetic nervous response: palpitation, dyspnea, diaphoresis, pallor, and cyanosis, tachycardia, and vomiting","Pain relieved completely within seconds at rest","Retrosternal pain that radiates"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pain not relieved by rest/nitroglycerin (relieved by opioids, e.g., morphine); feeling of heaviness, anxiety, cool extremities; sympathetic nervous response (palpitation, dyspnea, diaphoresis, pallor and cyanosis, tachycardia, and vomiting); and retrosternal radiating pain.  Why D wrong: MI pain is NOT relieved within seconds at rest; that describes angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Cardiac Troponin (Trop-T) serum level is raised within what time frame after MI?","options":["Within 3 to 6 hours","Within 24 hours only","Within 1 week","Within 1 minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac Troponin (Trop-T) serum level is raised within 3 to 6 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated troponin rise time."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Cardiac Troponin-C (Trop-C) level is raised within how many hours after MI?","options":["Within 7 hours","Within 3 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac Troponin-C level is raised within 7 hours after MI.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated Trop-C rise time."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Creatinine Kinase (CK) level rises within how many hours and returns to normal in how many hours after MI?","options":["Rises within 3 hours; returns to normal in 24 hours","Rises within 24 hours; returns to normal in 1 week","Rises within 7 hours; returns to normal in 48 hours","Rises within 1 minute; returns to normal in 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Creatinine Kinase (CK) level rises within 3 hours and returns to normal in 24 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated CK kinetics."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following is the early marker for MI among the elevated enzymes?","options":["CK-MB isoenzyme — level raised within 4–6 hours, specific for myocardial damage","Myoglobin — level raised within 1–3 hours after MI, the early marker for MI","C-reactive protein (CRP)","Lactate dehydrogenase only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Myoglobin level is raised within 1–3 hours after MI and is the early marker for MI.  Why A wrong: CK-MB rises within 4–6 hours and is specific for myocardial damage, but myoglobin is the early marker.  Why C wrong: CRP increases but is not the early MI marker.  Why D wrong: Not described as the early marker here."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which statement about CK-MB isoenzyme after MI is correct?","options":["CK-MB isoenzyme level is raised within 4–6 hours and is specific for myocardial damage","CK-MB is never elevated in MI","CK-MB is the earliest marker, raised within 1 hour","CK-MB is specific for skeletal muscle only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CK-MB isoenzyme level is raised within 4–6 hours and is specific for myocardial damage.  Why B wrong: CK-MB does rise in MI.  Why C wrong: Myoglobin (1–3 hours) is the early marker, not CK-MB.  Why D wrong: CK-MB is specific for myocardial (cardiac) damage, not skeletal muscle."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which of the following enzymes/markers are elevated in myocardial infarction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Cardiac Troponin (Trop-T) raised within 3–6 hours","Creatinine Kinase (CK) raised within 3 hours","CK-MB isoenzyme raised within 4–6 hours","Myoglobin raised within 1–3 hours (early marker)","Increased CRP protein"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Cardiac Troponin, Creatinine Kinase, CK-MB isoenzyme, Myoglobin, and increased CRP protein as elevated markers in MI.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly listed elevated levels of enzymes/markers."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Which ECG changes are seen in myocardial infarction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Elevated ST segment","T wave inversion","Presence of Q wave (which evidences MI)","Permanently shortened PR interval as the only finding","Universally absent P waves in all MI"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: ECG changes in MI include elevated ST segment, T wave inversion, and presence of Q wave (which is evidence of MI).  Why D wrong: A shortened PR interval is not the listed MI ECG change.  Why E wrong: Universally absent P waves are not listed as an MI ECG change."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"ST Segment Elevation MI (STEMI) is characterized by which of the following?","options":["It occurs due to 100% blockage of coronary arteries; it is the most serious type, with less chance to survive, and accounts for 40% of total MI","It involves partial blockage only","It never shows ST elevation","It is always painless"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: STEMI occurs due to 100% blockage of coronary arteries; it is the most serious MI, less chance to survive, and accounts for 40% of total MI.  Why B wrong: Partial blockage describes NSTEMI.  Why C wrong: STEMI shows ST segment elevation.  Why D wrong: STEMI is typically symptomatic and serious."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"STEMI thrombolytic therapy should ideally be initiated within what time frame from arrival of the patient?","options":["Within 3–6 hours of arrival of the patient","Within 24 hours","Within 1 week","Within 1 minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombolytic therapy should be initiated within 3–6 hours of arrival of the patient in STEMI.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated thrombolytic therapy window."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"Non-ST Segment Elevation MI (Non STEMI) is best described by which of the following?","options":["It is less serious type of MI; it occurs when coronary arteries are partially blocked, and thrombolytic therapy may or may not be needed","It occurs with 100% complete blockage and is the most serious type","It always shows ST elevation on ECG","It is identical to STEMI in severity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-STEMI is the less serious type of MI; it occurs when coronary arteries are partially blocked, and thrombolytic therapy may or may not be needed.  Why B wrong: Complete (100%) blockage and most serious describes STEMI.  Why C wrong: NSTEMI does not show ST elevation.  Why D wrong: NSTEMI is less serious than STEMI."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In the pathophysiology of MI, after thrombosis what does the permanent thrombus lead to?","options":["Necrosis → Myocardial Infarction","Reversal of ischemia and recovery","Increased oxygen demand only","Stable angina only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In MI pathophysiology, ischemia and hypoxia lead to a permanent thrombus, which causes necrosis → myocardial infarction (irreversible, unlike angina).  Why B wrong: With permanent thrombus and necrosis, ischemia is not reversed.  Why C wrong: The end result is necrosis, not merely increased demand.  Why D wrong: Permanent thrombus/necrosis produces MI, not stable angina."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 1 myocardial infarction refers to which of the following?","options":["Spontaneous myocardial infarction related to thrombus","MI secondary to ischaemia due to either increased oxygen demand or decreased supply","MI resulting in death when biomarker values are unavailable","MI associated with stent thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 1 = Spontaneous myocardial infarction related to thrombus.  Why B wrong: That is Type 2.  Why C wrong: That is Type 3.  Why D wrong: Stent thrombosis is associated with Type 4b."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 2 myocardial infarction refers to which of the following?","options":["Spontaneous MI related to thrombus","Myocardial infarction secondary to ischaemia due to either increased oxygen demand or decreased supply","MI associated with PCI (Percutaneous Coronary Intervention)","MI associated with CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type 2 = MI secondary to ischaemia due to either increased oxygen demand or decreased supply.  Why A wrong: That is Type 1.  Why C wrong: PCI-associated MI is Type 4a.  Why D wrong: CABG-associated MI is Type 5."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 3 myocardial infarction refers to which of the following?","options":["MI resulting in death when biomarker values are unavailable","Spontaneous MI related to thrombus","MI associated with PCI","MI associated with CABG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 3 = Myocardial infarction resulting in death when biomarker values are unavailable.  Why B wrong: That is Type 1.  Why C wrong: That is Type 4a (PCI).  Why D wrong: That is Type 5 (CABG)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 4a myocardial infarction is associated with which of the following?","options":["PCI (Percutaneous Coronary Intervention)","Stent thrombosis","CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting)","Spontaneous thrombus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 4a = Myocardial infarction associated with PCI (Percutaneous Coronary Intervention).  Why B wrong: Type 4b is associated with stent thrombosis.  Why C wrong: Type 5 is associated with CABG.  Why D wrong: Type 1 is spontaneous MI related to thrombus."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 4b myocardial infarction is associated with which of the following?","options":["Stent thrombosis","PCI without stent","CABG","Spontaneous MI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 4b = Myocardial infarction associated with stent thrombosis.  Why B wrong: PCI-associated MI is Type 4a.  Why C wrong: CABG-associated MI is Type 5.  Why D wrong: Spontaneous MI is Type 1."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"According to the clinical universal classification, Type 5 myocardial infarction is associated with which of the following?","options":["CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting)","PCI","Stent thrombosis","Spontaneous thrombus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 5 = Myocardial infarction associated with CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting).  Why B wrong: PCI is Type 4a.  Why C wrong: Stent thrombosis is Type 4b.  Why D wrong: Spontaneous thrombus MI is Type 1."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"On a normal ECG waveform, which components are correctly identified?","options":["QRS complex (with R, Q, S), P wave, T wave, PR segment, PR interval, ST segment, and QT interval","Only the P wave is present","There are no segments or intervals","The R wave is always inverted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A normal ECG shows the QRS complex (R, Q, S), P wave, T wave, PR segment, PR interval, ST segment, and QT interval.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the labeled normal ECG components in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"On the ECG, ST elevation is the characteristic finding of which condition shown in the figure?","options":["ST Elevated Myocardial Infarction (STEMI)","Normal sinus rhythm","NSTEMI with ST depression","NSTEMI with T wave inversion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels ST elevation as the finding of ST Elevated Myocardial Infarction (STEMI).  Why B wrong: Normal ECG has an isoelectric ST segment.  Why C, D wrong: ST depression and T wave inversion are shown for NSTEMI, not ST elevation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Defibrillation, Cardioversion, Automatic External Defibrillator…","question":"In the ECG figures, ST depression and T wave inversion are shown to represent which condition?","options":["NSTEMI (Non-ST Elevated MI)","STEMI","Normal ECG","Atrial fibrillation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figures label both ST depression and T wave inversion as NSTEMI findings.  Why B wrong: STEMI is shown with ST elevation.  Why C wrong: A normal ECG has neither ST depression nor T wave inversion.  Why D wrong: Atrial fibrillation is not the labeled condition for these tracings."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"A patient recovering from a myocardial infarction asks how to prevent another MI. Which of the following is a recommended prevention strategy?","options":["Increase dietary sodium and fat intake","Lifestyle modification such as diet, exercise, and avoiding stress and weight gain","Begin a high-intensity weight-lifting program immediately","Stop all prescribed cardiac medications once symptom-free"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention of MI includes lifestyle modification such as diet, exercise, avoiding stress, and control of weight gain.  Why A wrong: Increasing sodium and fat worsens cardiovascular risk.  Why C wrong: Sudden intense exertion is unsafe; graded exercise is advised.  Why D wrong: Pharmacologic management must be continued as prescribed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which additional measure is part of MI prevention along with lifestyle modification and pharmacologic management?","options":["Treating beta-blockers as a precipitant of angina","Treating angina attacks promptly","Avoiding all physical activity permanently","Restricting fluids to under 500 mL/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prompt treatment of angina attacks is listed as an MI-prevention measure.  Why A wrong: This is not stated; beta-blockers are therapeutic.  Why C wrong: Exercise is recommended, not total avoidance.  Why D wrong: Severe fluid restriction is not part of MI prevention."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In the management of acute MI, what is the first priority?","options":["Administer streptokinase","Morphine for pain relief","Bed rest","Continuous cardiac monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority in acute MI management is morphine for pain relief.  Why A wrong: Thrombolytic therapy is given within a 30-minute window but is not the very first priority listed.  Why C wrong: Bed rest is recommended but follows pain control.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is ongoing supportive care, not the first priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the second priority in management of acute MI?","options":["Provide anti-platelet drugs","Administer oxygen to improve oxygenation","Insert a urinary catheter","Encourage early ambulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second priority is to administer oxygen to improve oxygenation.  Why A wrong: Antiplatelet/other drugs follow oxygenation.  Why C/D wrong: These are not listed as the second priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which diet is recommended in acute MI management, and what is the sodium restriction?","options":["High-protein diet with 5 g sodium/day","2 g sodium (5 g NaCl) diet","Unrestricted regular diet","Clear liquids only with no sodium limit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 2 gm sodium (5 gm NaCl) diet is recommended; advise to maintain salt intake.  Why A wrong: The sodium limit is 2 g, not 5 g.  Why C wrong: Sodium is restricted in MI.  Why D wrong: Not the recommended diet."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Beta-blockers are used in acute MI management primarily because they:","options":["Are antiarrhythmic, antianginal, and antihypertensive","Increase heart rate and contractility","Promote sodium retention","Dilate the pupils"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-blockers act as antiarrhythmic, antianginal, and antihypertensive agents.  Why B wrong: They reduce heart rate and contractility.  Why C/D wrong: These are not beta-blocker effects."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"When thrombolytic therapy is used in acute MI, within what time window should it be initiated and with which drug?","options":["Within 90 minutes of arrival, using streptokinase","Within 24 hours, using heparin","Immediately on discharge, using aspirin","Within 90 minutes, using nitroglycerin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombolytic therapy within 90 minutes of arrival in the hospital should be initiated, e.g., streptokinase.  Why B wrong: Heparin and IV nitroglycerin are separate adjuncts; the 90-minute thrombolytic is streptokinase.  Why C wrong: Aspirin is part of the emergency cocktail, not the thrombolytic window drug.  Why D wrong: Nitroglycerin is not the thrombolytic agent."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which agents are given to MI patients as anti-platelet drugs / for anticoagulation in acute management?","options":["Aspirin and clopidogrel as antiplatelets, with heparin and IV nitroglycerin","Insulin and glucose","Furosemide and dopamine","Calcium gluconate alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antiplatelet drugs (e.g., aspirin/clopidogrel) are given along with heparin and IV nitroglycerin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapies for acute MI."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI) for acute MI should ideally be performed within what time of arrival?","options":["Within 90 minutes of arrival","Within 24 hours of arrival","Within 6 hours of arrival","Within 12 hours of arrival"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCI should be performed within 90 minutes of arrival.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated PCI target is 90 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"During acute MI care, which monitoring is part of the management?","options":["Monitor pacemaker, cardiac, and vital sign monitoring; intra-aortic balloon pump to reduce cardiac workload","Discourage bed rest entirely","Withhold all fluids","Avoid any electrocardiographic monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes intra-aortic balloon pump (to reduce cardiac workload) and pacemaker, cardiac, and vital sign monitoring.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict standard MI monitoring/management."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the recommended duration of bed rest after an MI according to general nursing care?","options":["6–12 hours","24–36 hours","48–72 hours","7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: General nursing care recommends bed rest for MI for 24–36 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 24–36 hour period."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"According to general nursing care after MI, how frequently should vital parameters be monitored initially?","options":["Every 4 hours","Every 15 minutes","Every hour","Once per shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vital parameters should be monitored frequently, initially every 15 minutes, then every 30 minutes until stabilization.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are less frequent than the recommended every 15 minutes initially."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which is included in general nursing care of the MI patient?","options":["Discourage fluid intake permanently","Monitor pain under continuous supervision, monitor intake and output, and monitor for complications associated with MI","Avoid all patient education","Initiate cardiac rehabilitation only after one year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: General nursing care includes monitoring pain under continuous supervision, monitoring intake and output, and monitoring for complications associated with MI.  Why A wrong: Intake/output is monitored, not fluids stopped permanently.  Why C wrong: Education is part of rehabilitation.  Why D wrong: Cardiac rehabilitation is initiated after stabilization of MI, not after a year."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"After stabilization of MI, the nurse should initiate which program?","options":["Renal dialysis program","Cardiac rehabilitation programme","Bariatric surgery program","Chemotherapy program"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After stabilization of MI, initiate the cardiac rehabilitation programme.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to post-MI care."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Regarding the cardiac arrest score (CART score), which is true about its purpose and range?","options":["It predicts witnessed out-of-hospital cardiac arrest only; range 1–10","It predicts witnessed in-hospital cardiac arrest using 3 criteria, with a score range of 0–5 points","It measures blood glucose; range 0–20","It estimates body surface area; range 0–100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardiac arrest score (CART) can be used to predict witnessed out-of-hospital cardiac arrest using three criteria, and the score range described is 0–5 points (max sum of the three criteria).  Why A wrong: The score addresses witnessed out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, not in-hospital.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated parameters."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In the cardiac arrest score, how are points assigned for \"Emergency department (ED) systolic blood pressure\"?","options":["ED SBP <90 mmHg = 1 point, ≥90 mmHg = 0 points","ED SBP >90 mmHg = 1 point, <90 mmHg = 0 points","ED SBP <60 mmHg = 2 points, ≥60 mmHg = 1 point","ED SBP >140 mmHg = 2 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For ED SBP, <90 mmHg = 1 point and ≥90 mmHg = 0 points.  Why B wrong: The scoring is reversed; low pressure scores the point.  Why C/D wrong: These thresholds are not used."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In the cardiac arrest score, how are points assigned for time to return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC)?","options":["≥25 minutes = 1 point, <25 minutes = 0 points","<5 minutes = 2 points","≥60 minutes = 0 points","Time to ROSC is not scored"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Time to ROSC ≥25 minutes = 1 point and <25 minutes = 0 points.  Why B/C wrong: These thresholds/values are not part of the criteria.  Why D wrong: Time to ROSC is one of the scored criteria."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In the cardiac arrest score, how are points assigned for neurological responsiveness?","options":["Neurologically responsive = 1 point, comatose = 0 points","Comatose = 1 point, responsive = 0 points","Both states = 2 points","Neurological status is not scored"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurologically responsive = 1 point; comatose = 0 points.  Why B wrong: The scoring is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the criteria."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"A complication of MI within the first 24 hours most commonly includes:","options":["Free wall rupture","Ventricular arrhythmia or dysrhythmia","Dressler syndrome","Mural thrombi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Within the first 24 hours, complications include ventricular arrhythmia/dysrhythmia, pericarditis, pulmonary edema, and bronchoconstriction.  Why A wrong: Free wall rupture occurs in the 1–2 days window.  Why C wrong: Dressler syndrome occurs weeks to months later.  Why D wrong: Mural thrombi appear in the 2 weeks-to-several-months timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which MI complication characteristically occurs within 1 to 2 days post-infarction?","options":["Free wall rupture, septal rupture, ventricular aneurysm","Dressler syndrome only","Sudden death only","No complications occur in this period"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Within 1–2 days, complications include free wall rupture, septal rupture, and ventricular aneurysm.  Why B/C wrong: These occur much later (weeks/months) or earlier.  Why D wrong: Serious mechanical complications can occur in this window."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which complications of MI occur within approximately 3 to 4 days?","options":["Free wall rupture, septal rupture, intraventricular rupture, thromboembolism","Ventricular arrhythmia only","Sudden death only","Pitting edema only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Within 3 to 4 days, complications include free wall rupture, septal rupture, intraventricular rupture, and thromboembolism.  Why B wrong: Ventricular arrhythmia is the first 24-hour complication.  Why C/D wrong: Not in this 3–4 day grouping."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which complications of MI typically appear within about 2 weeks to several months later?","options":["Dressler syndrome, heart failure, cardiogenic shock, sudden death/late death, and mural thrombi","Acute ventricular fibrillation in the first hour only","Immediate free wall rupture","None — MI has no late complications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Within 2 weeks to several months later, complications include Dressler syndrome, heart failure, cardiogenic shock, sudden death/late death, and mural thrombi.  Why B/C wrong: These are early complications.  Why D wrong: Late complications are well recognized."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Dressler syndrome, a late complication of MI, is best described as a:","options":["Post-myocardial infarction syndrome — a form of secondary pericarditis with or without a pericardial effusion","Bacterial endocarditis","Aortic dissection","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dressler syndrome is a post-myocardial infarction syndrome, a form of secondary pericarditis (with or without pericardial effusion); it usually occurs weeks to months after the MI.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different pathologies, not Dressler syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"An anterior wall MI most commonly involves which coronary artery, is seen in which leads, and is associated with which dysrhythmia?","options":["Left coronary artery and left anterior descending (LAD); leads V1–V4; second- and third-degree AV blocks / PVCs","Right coronary artery; leads II, III, aVF; sinus bradycardia","Circumflex; leads I, aVL; first-degree block","Posterior; leads V7–V9; SA and AV node blocks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anterior MI involves the left coronary artery and left anterior descending (LAD), shown in leads V1, V2, V3, V4, and is associated with second- and third-degree AV blocks and PVCs.  Why B wrong: That describes inferior MI (RCA, II/III/aVF, bradycardia).  Why C wrong: That describes lateral MI (circumflex, I/aVL).  Why D wrong: That describes posterior MI."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"A lateral wall MI is associated with which coronary artery and leads?","options":["Circumflex artery; leads I, aVL, V5, V6; first-degree block PVCs","RCA; leads II, III, aVF","LAD; leads V1–V4","Posterior descending; leads V7–V9"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lateral MI involves the circumflex artery, seen in leads I, aVL, V5, V6, associated with first-degree block / PVCs.  Why B wrong: RCA/inferior pattern.  Why C wrong: LAD/anterior pattern.  Why D wrong: Posterior pattern."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"An inferior/septal wall MI most commonly involves which coronary artery, leads, and dysrhythmias?","options":["Left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD); leads II, III, aVF; first-degree heart block, second-degree AV block, PVCs","Circumflex only; lead I; atrial fibrillation","Pulmonary artery; leads V5–V6","Aorta; lead aVR only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The septal/inferior pattern is described with the left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD), leads II, III, aVF, and dysrhythmias including first-degree heart block, second-degree AV block, and PVCs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's septal/inferior description."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"A right ventricular MI is associated with which findings on the table?","options":["Marginal branch of right coronary artery; leads V1R, V4R, V6R; atrioventricular (AV) node blocks and PVCs","Circumflex; leads I, aVL","LAD; leads V1–V4","SA node only; lead II"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Right ventricular MI involves the marginal branch of the right coronary artery, leads V1R, V4R, V6R, with AV node blocks and PVCs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other infarct locations."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"A posterior wall MI is associated with which coronary arteries and leads?","options":["Right coronary artery and circumflex; leads V7, V8, V9; SA and AV node blocks","LAD; leads V1–V4","Circumflex; leads I, aVL","Pulmonary artery; lead aVR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Posterior MI involves the right coronary artery and circumflex, seen in leads V7, V8, V9, with SA and AV node blocks.  Why B/C/D wrong: These match anterior, lateral, or non-coronary structures."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"The book's \"Note\" lists a three-tablet cocktail recommended by the government for emergency management of cardiac arrest. Which combination is correct?","options":["Aspirin 300 mg, Clopidogrel 300 mg, Atorvastatin 80 mg","Aspirin 75 mg, Clopidogrel 75 mg, Atorvastatin 10 mg","Aspirin 500 mg, Paracetamol 500 mg, Atorvastatin 40 mg","Aspirin 150 mg, Clopidogrel 150 mg, Atorvastatin 20 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies Tab. Aspirin 300 mg, Tab. Clopidogrel 300 mg, and Tab. Atorvastatin 80 mg as the recommended emergency cardiac cocktail.  Why B/C/D wrong: The doses do not match the stated 300/300/80 mg combination."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Heart failure is also known by which term?","options":["Acute coronary syndrome","Congestive cardiac failure (CCF) / congestive heart failure (CHF)","Endocarditis","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heart failure is also known as congestive cardiac failure (CCF) or congestive heart failure (CHF).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct cardiovascular conditions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which statement best defines heart failure?","options":["A condition of the heart in which it is unable to maintain adequate cardiac output to meet the metabolic needs of the body because of impaired pumping ability","A sudden complete occlusion of a coronary artery","Inflammation of the pericardium","A clot lodged in a deep vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heart failure is a condition in which the heart cannot maintain adequate cardiac output to meet the body's metabolic needs because of impaired pumping ability.  Why B wrong: That describes MI.  Why C wrong: That describes pericarditis.  Why D wrong: That describes DVT."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which describes \"forward heart failure\"?","options":["Inadequate discharge of blood into arterial system leading to poor tissue perfusion and increased Na+ reabsorption through RAAS","Backward damming of blood to one or both ventricles","Inflammation of the myocardium","A pericardial effusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Forward heart failure is inadequate discharge of blood into the arterial system, leading to poor tissue perfusion and increased sodium reabsorption through RAAS.  Why B wrong: That describes backward heart failure.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated conditions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which describes \"backward heart failure\"?","options":["Failure of one or both ventricles to fill normally and discharge their contents, causing back pressure on the atria and venous system","Inadequate arterial discharge with increased sodium reabsorption","A condition only of the right atrium","Inflammation of the endocardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Backward heart failure is failure of one or both ventricles to fill normally and discharge their contents, causing back pressure on the atria and the venous system.  Why B wrong: That describes forward heart failure.  Why C/D wrong: Not matching the definition."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In right-sided heart failure, the affected side is the right ventricle. What is the result of right ventricular pumping failure?","options":["Systemic circulation has ill effects on the body, producing generalised body oedema","Pulmonary edema as the primary feature","Inflammation of the pericardium","Cerebral hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In right-sided (right ventricular) failure, systemic circulation has ill effects on the body, leading to generalised body oedema.  Why B wrong: Pulmonary edema is a left-sided failure feature.  Why C/D wrong: Not related to right HF pathology."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the most common cause of right-sided heart failure?","options":["Pulmonary hypertension, with the most common overall cause being left-sided heart failure","Aortic stenosis","Pericarditis","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary hypertension is the most common cause of right-sided heart failure, and the most common overall cause is left-sided heart failure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common cause of right HF."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which is the FIRST sign of right-sided heart failure?","options":["Dependent or pitting oedema (legs and sacrum) / generalised body oedema (anasarca); ankle oedema is considered the first sign","Pulmonary edema","Orthopnea","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dependent/pitting oedema (legs and sacrum) and anasarca occur; ankle oedema is considered the first sign of right HF.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are features of left-sided heart failure."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of right-sided heart failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Jugular vein distension and abdominal distension","Hepatomegaly and splenomegaly","Increased CVP >6 mmHg","Ascites from increased volume excess or decreased pumping failure","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Right HF features include jugular vein distension, abdominal distension, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, ascites (from volume excess/decreased pumping failure), increased CVP >6 mmHg, reduced urine output, and weight gain.  Why E wrong: Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea is a left-sided heart failure feature."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Left-sided heart failure means failure of which chamber, and what is the most common and most serious clinical evidence of it?","options":["Failure of left ventricle; pulmonary oedema is the most common and most serious clinical evidence","Failure of right ventricle; ascites","Failure of left atrium; hepatomegaly","Failure of right atrium; peripheral edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Left-sided heart failure is failure of the left ventricle; pulmonary oedema is its most common and most serious clinical evidence.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe right-sided features or wrong chambers."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of LEFT-sided heart failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Decreased oxygen saturation on pulse oximetry","Crackles or rales of pulmonary congestion on auscultation by stethoscope","Peripheral cyanosis (legs and hands)","Cheyne-Stokes respiration — deep and fast breathing pattern","Frothy blood-tinged sputum and orthopnea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Left HF features include decreased oxygen saturation, crackles/rales of pulmonary congestion, peripheral cyanosis of legs and hands, Cheyne-Stokes respiration (deep/fast pattern), frothy blood-tinged sputum (due to pulmonary oedema), dry non-productive hacking cough, dyspnea, tachypnea, orthopnea, fatigue, restlessness, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea.  Why all correct: All listed options are left-sided heart failure manifestations from the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which left-sided heart failure sign is described as \"right-time attack of dyspnea\" / right-time attack of dyspnoea occurring at night?","options":["Orthopnea","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Hacking cough"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea is the night-time (right-time) attack of dyspnea described.  Why A wrong: Orthopnea is dyspnea when supine, relieved by sitting up.  Why C wrong: Cheyne-Stokes is a cyclic breathing pattern.  Why D wrong: Hacking cough is a separate symptom."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Orthopnea in left-sided heart failure is best defined as:","options":["Breathlessness that suddenly worsens when the position changes from sitting to lying","Inflammation of the airways","Edema of the lower legs","Cyanosis of the hands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orthopnea is breathlessness that suddenly worsens when the patient changes position from sitting to lying.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other conditions, not orthopnea."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"On the pitting oedema scale, a \"1+\" pit is described as:","options":["A barely perceptible pit, about 2 mm, rebounding immediately","A deep pit, 8 mm, lasting >30 sec","A deep pit, 6 mm, rebounding in 10–30 sec","A deeper pit, 4 mm, rebounding in a few seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1+ = a barely perceptible pit, ~2 mm, rebounds immediately.  Why B wrong: That is 4+ (8 mm, >30 sec).  Why C wrong: That is 3+ (6 mm, 10–30 sec).  Why D wrong: That is 2+ (4 mm, rebounds in a few seconds)."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"On the pitting oedema scale, which grading corresponds to a deeper pit of about 4 mm that rebounds in a few seconds?","options":["1+","2+","3+","4+"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2+ = a deeper pit, ~4 mm, rebounds in a few seconds.  Why A wrong: 1+ is barely perceptible (2 mm).  Why C wrong: 3+ is 6 mm (10–30 sec).  Why D wrong: 4+ is 8 mm (>30 sec)."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"On the pitting oedema scale, a 3+ pit is described as:","options":["A deep pit, about 6 mm, rebounding in 10 to 30 seconds","A barely perceptible 2 mm pit","An 8 mm pit lasting more than 30 seconds","No pit at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 3+ = a deep pit, ~6 mm, rebounds in 10–30 sec.  Why B wrong: That is 1+.  Why C wrong: That is 4+.  Why D wrong: No pit means no pitting edema."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"On the pitting oedema scale, a 4+ pit corresponds to a pit depth of approximately:","options":["2 mm","4 mm","6 mm","8 mm (deep pit lasting >30 sec)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: 4+ = a deep pit of ~8 mm, lasting more than 30 seconds.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to 1+, 2+, and 3+ respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the role of compensatory mechanisms in heart failure?","options":["They worsen cardiac output deliberately","They are managed by hemodynamic changes and act to restore cardiac output to a near-normal level","They cause vasodilation and lower blood pressure","They have no effect on cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In under-normal condition managed by hemodynamic changes, the compensatory mechanism acts to restore cardiac output to a near-normal level.  Why A wrong: They aim to restore, not worsen, output.  Why C wrong: They cause vasoconstriction, not vasodilation.  Why D wrong: They directly affect cardiac output."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are compensatory mechanisms activated in heart failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Increased heart rate","Improved stroke volume","Arterial vasoconstriction","Sodium and water retention","Spontaneous decrease in blood volume"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Compensatory mechanisms include increased heart rate, improved stroke volume, arterial vasoconstriction, and sodium and water retention.  Why E wrong: Compensation produces sodium/water retention (volume increase), not a spontaneous decrease in blood volume."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which diagnostic tests are used in heart failure?","options":["ECG, ECHO, and Chest X-ray","Skin biopsy only","Colonoscopy","EEG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic tests for heart failure include ECG, ECHO, and Chest X-ray (plus BNP and ANP).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to cardiac diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Regarding B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP) in heart failure, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["A BNP level >100 pg/mL indicates heart failure and provides confirmative diagnosis","It is also called ventricular natriuretic peptide or brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)","It is a hormone secreted by cardiomyocytes of the ventricles","It is secreted only by the brain and has no cardiac source","It is unrelated to ventricular wall stress"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: BNP >100 pg/mL indicates heart failure (confirmative diagnosis); it is also called ventricular/brain natriuretic peptide; and it is secreted by cardiomyocytes of the ventricles.  Why D wrong: BNP is secreted by ventricular cardiomyocytes, not solely by the brain.  Why E wrong: BNP relates to ventricular stress/stretch."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Regarding Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP), which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is a peptide hormone secreted by both cardiac atrium and cardiac/heart plasma volume","It maintains blood pressure by three mechanisms: vasodilation, exertion of salt and water, and an increase in vascular permeability","It causes sodium and water retention","It is secreted by skeletal muscle"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: ANP is a peptide hormone secreted by the cardiac atrium and related to cardiac plasma volume; it maintains BP through three mechanisms — vasodilation, exertion (excretion) of salt and water, and increased vascular permeability.  Why C wrong: ANP promotes salt/water excretion (natriuresis), not retention.  Why D wrong: ANP is cardiac (atrial), not from skeletal muscle."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What positioning is recommended in the nursing care of a patient with heart failure to reduce cardiac workload?","options":["High Fowler's or orthopneic position (first priority)","Flat supine position","Strict left lateral position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first-priority position is High Fowler's or orthopneic position to reduce cardiac workload.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions increase cardiac workload or are inappropriate for HF/pulmonary congestion."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"After positioning, what is the second priority in nursing care for heart failure?","options":["Starting the loop diuretic","Oxygen administration","Sodium IV bolus","Restricting all monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second priority (after positioning) is starting oxygen.  Why A wrong: Diuretics are part of care but oxygen is the listed second priority.  Why C wrong: Sodium is restricted, not bolused.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is part of care."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following nursing care measures apply to heart failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["If the client is not maintaining natural saturation, prepare for intubation or mechanical ventilation for acute heart failure","Give morphine to reduce anxiety and dyspnea","Give diuretics and vasodilators to decrease cardiac workload","Administer digoxin for increase in pump performance or contractility","Increase dietary sodium freely"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes preparing for intubation/mechanical ventilation if saturation is not maintained, giving morphine to reduce anxiety/dyspnea, giving diuretics and vasodilators to decrease cardiac workload, and administering digoxin (the DOC for HF) to increase pump performance/contractility.  Why E wrong: Sodium intake is restricted to reduce fluid retention."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Digoxin is identified in the source as the drug of choice (DOC) for heart failure. Which sample is preferred for digoxin level monitoring, and what is the most accurate timing for the level?","options":["Radial artery sample for ABG; assessment timing","Capillary blood sample; random timing","Cerebrospinal fluid; weekly","Sputum sample; monthly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes to obtain the sample from a most common radial artery for ABG (oxygenation/assessment, \"for O2 assessment\") in conjunction with digoxin therapy monitoring.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sources/timings are not specified in the text."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing-care measures relate to monitoring and managing potassium/digoxin in heart failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Potassium supplement should be given to prevent digoxin toxicity and hypokalemia","Monitor vital signs, SpO2, and IVP (or hemodynamic monitoring)","Instruct about restricted diet to limit fluid retention and promote fluid excretion","Instruct the client to measure daily weight","Change position slowly and frequently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed nursing-care measures: potassium supplement to prevent digoxin toxicity and hypokalemia, monitoring vital signs/SpO2/IVP/hemodynamics, restricted diet to limit fluid retention and promote excretion, daily weight measurement, and changing position slowly and frequently.  Why all correct: Each option corresponds to a stated point."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, in the case of acute pulmonary edema, the nurse should position the client in which position?","options":["High Fowler's position, with the second priority being to start oxygen","Supine flat with legs elevated","Left lateral recumbent","Prone position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states: in acute pulmonary edema, place the client in High Fowler's position; the second priority is starting oxygen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reduce pulmonary congestion and contradict the note."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Shock is best defined as:","options":["A life-threatening condition that occurs when the body is not getting enough blood flow","Inflammation of the pericardium","A clot in a deep vein","Failure of the left ventricle only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shock is a life-threatening condition that occurs when the body is not getting enough blood flow.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe pericarditis, DVT, and left HF respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In shock, lack of blood flow has which consequences? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The cells and organs do not get enough oxygen and nutrients to function properly","Many organs can be damaged","Shock requires immediate treatment, otherwise irreversible changes will occur","If untreated it may end as death also","It is always self-limiting and resolves without treatment"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Lack of blood flow means cells/organs do not get enough oxygen and nutrients; many organs can be damaged; shock requires immediate treatment or irreversible changes occur; and untreated shock can end in death.  Why E wrong: Shock requires immediate treatment and is not self-limiting."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Hypovolemic shock occurs when:","options":["More massive fluid loss has occurred, resulting in inadequate tissue perfusion","The heart fails to pump","There is massive histamine release","Bacterial toxins cause vasodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypovolemic shock occurs when more massive fluid loss has occurred, resulting in inadequate tissue perfusion.  Why B wrong: That is cardiogenic shock.  Why C wrong: That is anaphylactic shock.  Why D wrong: That describes septic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which causes are associated with hypovolemic shock? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Excessive bleeding","Diarrhea","Vomiting","Burns","Pericardial inflammation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypovolemic shock include excessive bleeding, diarrhea or vomiting, and burns.  Why E wrong: Pericardial inflammation (pericarditis) is not a hypovolemic shock cause."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"The cardiogenic profile of hypovolemic shock is associated with which findings (per source note on hypovolemic shock)?","options":["Hypotension and tachycardia","Hypertension and bradycardia","Warm flushed skin and bradycardia","Fever and rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes the cardiogenic sign of hypovolemic shock as hypotension and tachycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated hypotension/tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Cardiogenic shock occurs when:","options":["The heart's inability to pump out the blood thereby resulting in poor tissue perfusion and compromising tissue perfusion","There is massive fluid loss","There is histamine-mediated vasodilation","Bacterial toxins are released into the blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiogenic shock occurs with the heart's inability to pump out the blood, resulting in poor tissue perfusion.  Why B wrong: That is hypovolemic shock.  Why C wrong: That is anaphylactic shock.  Why D wrong: That is septic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Cardiogenic shock typically presents with which signs?","options":["Hypotension and tachycardia","Hypertension and bradypnea","Fever and chills","Warm dry skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiogenic shock presents with hypotension and tachycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source description."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Neurogenic shock is caused by:","options":["Rapid vasodilation and subsequent pooling of blood within peripheral vessels","Massive bleeding","Pump failure of the heart","Bacterial endotoxins only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic shock is caused by rapid vasodilation and subsequent pooling of blood within peripheral vessels.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe hypovolemic, cardiogenic, and septic shock respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Neurogenic shock is commonly caused by which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Spinal anaesthesia","Spinal injury","Sympathetic blocking drugs and stress","Severe burns and bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic shock is caused by spinal anaesthesia, spinal injury, and sympathetic-blocking drugs and stress.  Why D wrong: Burns and bleeding are causes of hypovolemic shock, not neurogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"The cardiovascular signs of neurogenic shock are typically:","options":["Hypotension and bradycardia","Hypertension and tachycardia","Hypotension and tachycardia","Normal BP with fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic shock typically presents with hypotension and bradycardia.  Why B/C wrong: Tachycardia is not the neurogenic pattern (loss of sympathetic tone causes bradycardia).  Why D wrong: Hypotension is present and there is no fever."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Anaphylactic shock is caused by:","options":["An allergic reaction resulting in massive release of histamine and vasodilation","Pump failure","Massive fluid loss","Spinal injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anaphylactic shock results from an allergic reaction causing massive release of histamine and vasodilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cardiogenic, hypovolemic, and neurogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which signs are characteristic of anaphylactic shock?","options":["Pruritus and urticaria, hypotension, and tachycardia","Fever and chills","Bradycardia with hypertension","Cold clammy skin with bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anaphylactic shock features pruritus and urticaria, with hypotension and tachycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the anaphylactic cardiovascular/skin presentation."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Septic shock occurs when:","options":["Bacterial toxins are raised in the blood","The heart fails to pump","There is spinal cord injury","There is massive hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Septic shock occurs when bacterial toxins are raised in the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cardiogenic, neurogenic, and hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which pathophysiologic description fits septic shock?","options":["Usually gram-negative bacteria that cause plasma leaking into the interstitial space and hypovolemia","Coronary artery occlusion","Histamine release from mast cells","Loss of sympathetic tone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Septic shock usually involves gram-negative bacteria that cause plasma leaking into the interstitial space and hypovolemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms belong to cardiogenic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic shock respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs/symptoms of cardiogenic shock per the dedicated cardiogenic shock section? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypotension and urine output less than 30 mL/hr","Poor peripheral pulses","Tachycardia and tachypnea, with pulmonary congestion","Disorientation, restlessness, and confusion","Warm flushed skin with bounding pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiogenic shock signs include hypotension and urine output <30 mL/hr, poor peripheral pulses, tachycardia/tachypnea with pulmonary congestion, and disorientation/restlessness/confusion.  Why E wrong: Cardiogenic shock produces poor pulses and cool skin, not warm flushed skin with bounding pulses."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing management measures apply to cardiogenic shock? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Give morphine to relieve pain and pulmonary congestion","Administer oxygen","If client unable to maintain oxygen saturation, prepare the client for intubation and mechanical ventilation","Give ordered medications and monitor ABG level; correct underlying cause; monitor peripheral pulses","Withhold all oxygen to avoid toxicity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiogenic shock nursing care includes morphine for pain/pulmonary congestion, oxygen administration, preparation for intubation/mechanical ventilation if saturation cannot be maintained, giving ordered medications, monitoring ABG, correcting the underlying cause, and monitoring peripheral pulses.  Why E wrong: Oxygen is administered, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In severe cardiogenic shock, the nurse should be prepared to assist the doctor with insertion of which device for monitoring?","options":["Pulmonary artery catheter","Nasogastric tube","Urinary catheter","Peripheral IV line only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should assist the doctor in the insertion of a pulmonary artery catheter for monitoring.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not provide pulmonary artery pressure monitoring required in severe cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Per the \"Types & Clinical Manifestations of Shock\" table, hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock shows which hemodynamic pattern?","options":["Decreased cardiac output (CO) and increased systemic vascular resistance (SVR)","Increased CO and decreased SVR","Normal CO and SVR","Increased CO and increased SVR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock the table lists decreased CO and increased SVR (with clinical features such as hypotension, tachycardia, weak thready pulse, cool/pale/moist skin, urine output decreased).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the decreased CO / increased SVR pattern."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Per the shock-types table, the clinical manifestations of hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock include which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypotension","Tachycardia, weak thready pulse","Cool, pale, moist skin","Decreased urine output (urine output <30 mL/hr)","Warm, dry, flushed skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists hypotension, tachycardia/weak thready pulse, cool/pale/moist skin, and decreased urine output (<30 mL/hr) for hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock.  Why E wrong: Warm, dry, flushed skin is characteristic of neurogenic/distributive shock, not hypovolemic/cardiogenic."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Per the shock-types table, neurogenic shock shows which hemodynamic pattern and skin findings?","options":["Decreased CO and decreased SVR; warm, dry skin","Increased CO and increased SVR; cold clammy skin","Normal CO; cyanotic skin","Increased CO and decreased SVR; pale skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists neurogenic shock with decreased CO and decreased SVR and warm, dry skin (with hypotension and bradycardia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the neurogenic decreased CO / decreased SVR / warm dry skin pattern."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Per the shock-types table, anaphylactic shock is characterized by which manifestations and hemodynamics?","options":["Hypotension, tachycardia, cough, dyspnea, pruritus, urticaria, rash; decreased CO and decreased SVR","Bradycardia and hypertension; increased CO","Fever, warm flushed skin only; increased CO and increased SVR","Cold pale skin with massive bleeding; decreased CO and increased SVR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists anaphylactic shock with hypotension, tachycardia, cough, dyspnea, pruritus, urticaria, and rash, with decreased CO and decreased SVR.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the anaphylactic table entry."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Per the shock-types table, septic shock manifestations include which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypotension and tachycardia","Fast bounding pulse, with warm and flushed skin (later cool)","Tachypnea","Decreased/decreasing urine output and fever","Decreased CO and increased SVR"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The septic shock row lists hypotension, tachycardia, fast/bounding pulse with warm flushed skin (later turning cool), tachypnea, decreasing urine output, and fever.  Why E wrong: Septic shock is a distributive shock with decreased SVR, not the increased SVR seen in hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Pericarditis is best defined as:","options":["Acute or chronic inflammation of the pericardium","Inflammation of the myocardium","Inflammation of the endocardium","A blood clot in a deep vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pericarditis is acute or chronic inflammation of the pericardium.  Why B wrong: That is myocarditis.  Why C wrong: That is endocarditis.  Why D wrong: That is DVT."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In pericarditis, inflammatory responses cause which changes to the pericardial sac?","options":["Loss of pericardial elasticity and rigidity in the pericardial sac","Increased pericardial elasticity","No change to the pericardium","Calcification of the coronary arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammatory responses cause loss of pericardial elasticity and lead to rigidity in the pericardial sac.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the described pathology."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which clinical feature is characteristic of pericarditis?","options":["Heart failure or cardiac tamponade may occur","Painless presentation always","Hypertensive crisis only","Hematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pericarditis, heart failure or cardiac tamponade may occur.  Why B wrong: Chest pain is a classic feature.  Why C/D wrong: Not characteristic features of pericarditis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In pericarditis, severe chest pain is often relieved when the patient assumes which position?","options":["Sitting up or leaning forward, with pericardial friction rub heard on auscultation of the stethoscope","Lying flat supine","Left lateral recumbent","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include severe pain, especially when the patient lies in a supine position; fever and chills; pericardial friction rub heard on auscultation. The nursing care positions the client in a high Fowler's or upright position.  Why B wrong: Lying flat (supine) worsens the pain.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the relieving positions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Myocarditis is best defined as:","options":["Acute or chronic inflammation of the myocardium as a result of pericarditis and other systemic infections","Inflammation of the pericardium only","A clot in a deep vein","Failure of the left ventricle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myocarditis is acute or chronic inflammation of the myocardium, occurring as a result of pericarditis and other systemic infections.  Why B wrong: That is pericarditis.  Why C wrong: That is DVT.  Why D wrong: That is left HF."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are seen in myocarditis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fever and pericardial friction rub","Murmur-like sounds when auscultated","Dyspnea, tachycardia, and chest pain","Other general measures/manifestations","Painless hematuria as the hallmark"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Myocarditis signs and symptoms include fever, pericardial friction rub, murmur-like sounds when auscultated, dyspnea, tachycardia, chest pain, and other general measures.  Why E wrong: Painless hematuria is not a described myocarditis feature."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the recommended positioning in the nursing care of myocarditis?","options":["Position the client up right or sitting","Strict supine flat","Prone position","Left lateral only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care of myocarditis includes positioning the client upright or sitting, plus other general measures.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the recommended upright/sitting position."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Infective endocarditis is best defined as:","options":["Inflammation of the inner layer of the heart or endocardium","Inflammation of the pericardium","Inflammation of the myocardium","A pericardial effusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Infective endocarditis is inflammation of the inner layer of the heart, or endocardium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe pericardial/myocardial pathology."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which organism is the leading cause of infective endocarditis, accounting for about 30%, especially in IV drug users?","options":["Streptococcus viridans","Staphylococcus aureus","Escherichia coli","Candida albicans"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Worldwide, Staphylococcus aureus is the leading cause (about 30%) of infective endocarditis, especially in IV drug users in America.  Why A wrong: Streptococcus viridans is a second most common cause of infective endocarditis but not the listed leading 30% cause.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the leading causes described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which organism is the second most common cause of infective endocarditis?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus viridans","Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Streptococcus viridans is the second most common cause of infective endocarditis.  Why A wrong: S. aureus is the leading cause.  Why C/D wrong: Not identified as a leading cause."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the most common cause of hospital-associated (healthcare-associated) infective endocarditis?","options":["Streptococcus viridans","Staphylococcus aureus","Enterococcus","Klebsiella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of hospital-associated infective endocarditis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common hospital-associated cause."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which criteria are used to diagnose infective endocarditis, and what is their updated name (per the source)?","options":["Duke's criteria, recently updated in 2023 and known as the Modified Duke's Criteria","Ranson's criteria, updated 2023","Glasgow criteria","Wells criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Duke's criteria are used for infective endocarditis; recently in 2023 Duke's criteria were updated and are known as the Modified Duke's Criteria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These criteria are used for other conditions (pancreatitis, neurological, DVT respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for infective endocarditis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["IV drug users","Previously had a valve replacement surgery","Sterile surgical technique","None of the above"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include IV drug users and previously had valve replacement surgery.  Why C wrong: Sterile technique reduces, not increases, risk.  Why D wrong: A and B are listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of infective endocarditis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fever, anorexia, and weight loss","Splinter haemorrhage in the nail beds","Osler's Nodes — red, tender, or painful lesion on the pads of fingers and hands and toes","Janeway Lesions — non-tender haemorrhagic lesion on the fingers, nose, and earlobe","Cardiac murmurs and splenomegaly with clubbing of fingers"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms of infective endocarditis include fever, anorexia, weight loss, splinter haemorrhage in nail beds, Osler's nodes (red tender/painful lesions on pads of fingers/hands/toes), Janeway lesions (non-tender haemorrhagic lesions on fingers, nose, earlobe), cardiac murmurs, splenomegaly, and clubbing of fingers.  Why all correct: Each is a stated manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In the Modified Duke Criteria 2023, which combination defines \"Definite Infective Endocarditis\"?","options":["2 major criteria, OR 1 major + 3 minor criteria, OR 5 minor criteria","1 minor criterion only","Any single sign of fever","No criteria are required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Modified Duke Criteria 2023 define definite infective endocarditis as 2 major criteria, or 1 major plus 3 minor criteria, or 5 minor criteria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not meet the threshold for definite diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are listed as MAJOR criteria in the Modified Duke Criteria-2023? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Positive blood culture","Evidence of endocardial involvement","Fever","Vascular phenomena (e.g., emboli, hemorrhage)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Major criteria include positive blood culture and evidence of endocardial involvement.  Why C wrong: Fever (>38°C) is a minor criterion.  Why D wrong: Vascular phenomena are minor criteria."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are listed as MINOR criteria in the Modified Duke Criteria-2023? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Predisposing condition or IV drug use","Fever >38°C","Vascular phenomena (e.g., emboli, hemorrhage)","Immunologic phenomena (e.g., Osler's nodes, Roth's spots)","Blood culture or serologic evidence that does not meet major criteria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Minor criteria include predisposing condition/IV drug use, fever >38°C, vascular phenomena (emboli/hemorrhage), immunologic phenomena (Osler's nodes, Roth's spots), and blood culture or serologic evidence not meeting major criteria.  Why all correct: All are listed minor criteria."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Cardiac tamponade is best described as:","options":["A pericardial effusion that occurs when the space between the parietal and visceral layer of the pericardium fills with fluid (>50 mL)","Inflammation of the endocardium","A clot in a deep vein","Failure of the right ventricle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac tamponade is a pericardial effusion that occurs when the space between the parietal and visceral layers of the pericardium fills with fluid (>50 mL).  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe endocarditis, DVT, and right HF respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the most common risk factor for cardiac tamponade?","options":["Pericardial effusion","Deep vein thrombosis","Hyperlipidemia","Atrial fibrillation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common risk factor for cardiac tamponade is pericardial effusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which is a cardinal sign of cardiac tamponade related to ventricular filling?","options":["Cardinal sign — reduced ventricular filling and reduced cardiac output","Increased ventricular filling and increased cardiac output","Hypertension with bounding pulses","Widened pulse pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cardinal sign of cardiac tamponade is reduced ventricular filling and reduced cardiac output.  Why B wrong: Filling and output are reduced, not increased.  Why C/D wrong: Tamponade causes narrowed pulse pressure and decreased cardiac output, not bounding pulses/widened pulse pressure."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of cardiac tamponade? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Increased CVP and JVP distension","Distant, muffled heart sounds","Decreased cardiac output and narrowing pulse pressure","Bounding peripheral pulses with widened pulse pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac tamponade signs include increased CVP and JVP distension, distant muffled heart sounds, and decreased cardiac output with narrowing pulse pressure.  Why D wrong: Tamponade causes a narrowed (not widened) pulse pressure and weak (not bounding) pulses."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing care measures apply to cardiac tamponade? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor hemodynamics invasively","Insert IV line and administer IV fluid to manage cardiac output","Initiate general nursing care","Withhold all fluids and avoid IV access"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for cardiac tamponade includes monitoring hemodynamics invasively, inserting an IV line and administering IV fluid to manage cardiac output, and initiating general nursing care.  Why D wrong: IV access and fluids are required to support cardiac output, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Phlebitis is best defined as:","options":["Vein inflammation associated with an invasive procedure such as IV lines or cannula","A blood clot in a deep vein","Inflammation of the pericardium","Inflammation of the endocardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebitis is vein inflammation associated with an invasive procedure such as IV lines or a cannula.  Why B wrong: That describes DVT.  Why C/D wrong: These describe pericarditis and endocarditis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with phlebitis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Redness","Warm area radiating up the vein and tenderness","Swelling","Painless cold extremity with no skin changes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebitis signs/symptoms include redness, a warm area radiating up the vein, tenderness, and swelling.  Why D wrong: Phlebitis presents with warmth, redness, and tenderness — not a painless cold extremity."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for phlebitis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["If phlebitis occurred, discontinue the IV line immediately (first priority)","Restart IV fluid using another vein","Apply hot/warm application for dilation of the vein to promote circulation","Assess for tissue necrosis, signs of infection, or pulmonary embolus","Continue using the same inflamed vein"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebitis nursing care includes discontinuing the IV line immediately (first priority), restarting IV fluid using another vein, applying warm/hot application to dilate the vein and promote circulation, and assessing for tissue necrosis, signs of infection, or pulmonary embolus.  Why E wrong: The inflamed vein must be discontinued, not reused."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) overview is best described as:","options":["A blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the legs","Inflammation of the inner heart layer","A pericardial effusion","An arterial occlusion in the brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DVT is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the legs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe endocarditis, tamponade, and stroke respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which statements about DVT are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The condition is serious because blood clots can loosen and lodge in the lungs","It is a form of thrombophlebitis associated with clot formation","It is more likely than a superficial thrombophlebitis to become more risk of pulmonary embolism","The most serious complication of DVT is pulmonary embolism","DVT only affects superficial veins and never embolizes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DVT is serious because clots can loosen and lodge in the lungs; it is a form of thrombophlebitis associated with clot formation; it carries more risk of pulmonary embolism than superficial thrombophlebitis; and its most serious complication is pulmonary embolism.  Why E wrong: DVT affects deep veins and can embolize (its most serious complication is pulmonary embolism)."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which is the diagnostic test mentioned for DVT?","options":["D-dimer assay","Serum amylase","Troponin","Lumbar puncture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagnostic test for DVT is a D-dimer assay.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are used for pancreatitis, MI, and CNS evaluation, not DVT."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of DVT? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Pain, swelling, redness, and enlarged veins in the affected area","A positive Homan's sign — calf or groin pain on dorsiflexion of ankle","Warm skin that is tender to touch","Oedema on one or both legs","Cold pale skin with absent pulses as the hallmark"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DVT signs/symptoms include pain, swelling, redness, enlarged veins in the affected area; a positive Homan's sign (calf/groin pain on dorsiflexion of the ankle); warm skin tender to touch; and oedema in one or both legs.  Why E wrong: Cold pale skin with absent pulses is an arterial (not venous) finding; DVT typically produces warm tender skin."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Why should the Homan's sign test be performed cautiously in suspected DVT?","options":["Because dorsiflexion may dislodge the thrombus","Because it always causes a fracture","Because it raises blood glucose","Because it has no risk at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Homan's sign test should not be performed routinely because dorsiflexion may dislodge the thrombus.  Why B/C wrong: These are not the stated concern.  Why D wrong: There is a real risk of dislodging the clot."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for DVT? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Elevate the affected extremity above the heart level (first priority)","Avoid the use of a pillow under the knee to prevent restriction of blood flow","Do not massage the extremity, to prevent dislodge of the clot","Apply anti-embolism stocking as ordered to reduce venous stasis","Vigorously massage the legs to improve circulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DVT nursing care includes elevating the affected extremity above heart level (first priority), avoiding a pillow under the knee (prevents restriction of blood flow), not massaging the extremity (prevents dislodging the clot), and applying anti-embolism stockings as ordered to reduce venous stasis.  Why E wrong: Massage must be avoided to prevent dislodging the clot."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"When measuring and recording the circumference of the thighs in DVT monitoring, what should the nurse inspect or monitor for?","options":["Inspect skin for warmth and oedema, and monitor for shortness of breath/chest pain, which indicate pulmonary embolism","Inspect for a rash on the abdomen","Measure blood glucose","Auscultate bowel sounds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When measuring/recording thigh circumference, examine/inspect the skin for warmth and oedema and monitor for shortness of breath/chest pain, which indicate pulmonary embolism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to DVT/PE monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"During thrombolytic and heparin therapy for DVT, which laboratory monitoring is required? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor aPTT level during thrombolytic/heparin therapy","Monitor PT and INR value during warfarin therapy","Monitor serum creatinine for clot resolution","Monitor liver enzymes to confirm DVT"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: During thrombolytic/heparin therapy, monitor aPTT; and monitor PT and INR value during warfarin therapy.  Why C/D wrong: These tests are not used to monitor DVT anticoagulation per the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which patient education point is given regarding clothing and positioning for DVT/venous disorders?","options":["Avoid prolonged sitting or standing, and avoid constrictive clothing or crossing legs when seated","Wear tight constrictive garments to compress the leg","Cross legs while sitting to improve drainage","Sit for long uninterrupted periods"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Patients should avoid prolonged sitting or standing and avoid constrictive clothing or crossing legs when seated.  Why B/C/D wrong: These promote venous stasis and worsen the condition."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Using the comparative figures, normal blood flow, deep vein thrombosis, and embolus are best distinguished by which description?","options":["Normal blood flow is unobstructed; a deep vein thrombosis is a thrombus (blood clot) in the deep veins of the leg; an embolus is a piece of blood clot that has broken off and is travelling through the vessel","They are identical conditions","An embolus is always stationary","A thrombus only forms in arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figures show normal blood flow (unobstructed), deep vein thrombosis (a thrombus/blood clot in the deep veins of the leg), and an embolus (a piece of blood clot — embolus — that has broken off and travels through the vessel).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the figure descriptions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Varicose veins are best defined as:","options":["Superficial veins that have become enlarged and twisted","Inflammation of the pericardium","Blood clots in deep veins","Arterial occlusions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Varicose veins are superficial veins that have become enlarged and twisted.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe pericarditis, DVT, and arterial disease respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which statements about varicose veins are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["They usually occur just under the skin in the legs or when veins in the lower extremities become dilated and congested","Complications may include bleeding or superficial thrombophlebitis","When varicose veins occur in the scrotum, it is known as a varicocele; while those around the anus are known as haemorrhoids","They occur only in the arms"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Varicose veins usually occur just under the skin in the legs when veins in the lower extremities become dilated and congested; complications include bleeding or superficial thrombophlebitis; in the scrotum they are called a varicocele and around the anus they are called haemorrhoids.  Why D wrong: Varicose veins characteristically affect the lower extremities, not the arms."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In varicose veins, what happens to the vein wall and valves?","options":["Affected veins' walls weaken and dilate, and valves become incompetent, causing tortuous (twisting) of the veins","The vein walls thicken and valves become stronger","The arteries become occluded","The capillaries calcify"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In varicose veins, the affected vein's elasticity is decreased causing tortuous (twisting) of the veins, the vein wall weakens and dilates, and the valves become incompetent.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the described venous pathology."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of varicose veins? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Pain, usually after standing","Feeling of fullness in the legs and ankle oedema","Positive Trendelenburg's test","Cold pulseless foot as the hallmark"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Varicose veins present with pain (usually after standing), a feeling of fullness in the legs and ankle oedema, and a positive Trendelenburg's test.  Why D wrong: A cold pulseless foot is an arterial finding, not a varicose vein sign."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Regarding the Trendelenburg's test for varicose veins, which description is correct?","options":["The client is placed in a supine position with the leg elevated; when about 50% of varicosities are present, the client's veins will fill from the proximal or near end (sign positive)","The test measures blood glucose","A positive result means the arteries are healthy","The leg is kept dependent throughout"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Trendelenburg's test, the client is placed in a supine position with the leg elevated; if varicosities are present, the veins fill from the proximal (near) end — a positive sign. (Normally veins fill from the distal end.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the procedure and meaning."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Normally (negative Trendelenburg's test), the superficial veins fill from which end?","options":["From the distal end","From the proximal end","They do not fill at all","From both ends simultaneously and instantly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Usually (normal/negative test), the veins fill from the distal end.  Why B wrong: Filling from the proximal end is a positive (abnormal) test indicating varicosities.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the description."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for varicose veins? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Instruct the client for leg elevation, especially when sleeping, with the leg elevated on a pillow","Avoid prolonged sitting or standing and constrictive clothing or crossing legs when seated","Encourage prolonged standing to strengthen the veins","Wear tight constrictive bands around the thigh"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Varicose vein nursing care includes instructing leg elevation (especially when sleeping, leg elevated on a pillow) and avoiding prolonged sitting/standing and constrictive clothing or crossing legs when seated.  Why C wrong: Prolonged standing worsens venous pooling.  Why D wrong: Constrictive clothing should be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Arterial disorder (peripheral arterial disease) is best defined as:","options":["A chronic disorder in which partial or total arterial occlusion has happened and reduces the flow of blood to peripheral organs as well as nutrients and oxygen","A blood clot in a deep vein","Inflammation of the pericardium","Enlargement of superficial veins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arterial disorder is a chronic disorder in which partial or total arterial occlusion has happened, reducing the flow of blood to peripheral organs as well as nutrients and oxygen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe DVT, pericarditis, and varicose veins respectively."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"What is the most common cause of peripheral arterial disease?","options":["Atherosclerosis","Deep vein thrombosis","Pericardial effusion","Valve incompetence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of peripheral arterial diseases is atherosclerosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common cause of PAD."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"\"Intermittent claudication\" in peripheral arterial disease refers to:","options":["Pain in muscles due to inadequate blood flow","Pain only at complete rest","Painless swelling of the legs","Joint inflammation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intermittent claudication is pain in muscles due to inadequate blood flow.  Why B wrong: Resting pain (aching/throbbing at rest) is a separate, more advanced sign.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe claudication."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of peripheral arterial disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Resting pain characterized by numbness, aching, or throbbing type","Cold and grey colour skin in the lower extremities","Decreased or absent peripheral pulses","Signs of arterial ulcer (frequently usually black or brown skin/tissue)","Warm flushed skin with bounding pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: PAD signs/symptoms include resting pain (numbness/aching/throbbing type), cold and grey skin in the lower extremities, decreased or absent peripheral pulses, and signs of arterial ulcers.  Why E wrong: Warm flushed skin with bounding pulses indicates good perfusion, the opposite of arterial occlusion."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"In PAD, blood pressure measured in the lower limbs (thigh and calf) compared to the upper limbs is typically:","options":["Lower in the lower limb measurement at the thigh and calf","Higher in the lower limbs","Always equal in all limbs","Unable to be measured"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In PAD, BP is reading lower in the lower limb measurement at the thigh and calf (due to arterial occlusion).  Why B/C/D wrong: Arterial occlusion reduces distal pressure, so the lower limb reading is lower, not higher/equal."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Prevention & management of acute MI, general nursing care of MI, cardiac…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for peripheral arterial disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Assess all vitals, specially BP and pain","Assess the extremities for colour, motion, sensation, pulses, and ulcer formation","Teach the client walk as much as possible to prevent intermittent claudication","Avoid prolonged sitting or standing, constrictive clothing, or crossing legs when seated","Never apply direct heat to the legs to prevent burns due to decreased sensation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: PAD nursing care includes assessing all vitals (especially BP and pain); assessing extremities for colour, motion, sensation, pulses, and ulcer formation; teaching the client to walk as much as possible to prevent intermittent claudication; avoiding prolonged sitting/standing, constrictive clothing, or crossing legs when seated; and never applying direct heat to the legs to prevent burns due to decreased sensation.  Why all correct: Each is a stated nursing-care measure."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"An aortic aneurysm is best defined as an abnormal dilation of the arterial wall caused by weakness or stretching of which layer of the aorta?","options":["Outermost layer (tunica adventitia)","Middle layer (tunica media)","Innermost layer (tunica intima)","Endothelial lining only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An aortic aneurysm is an abnormal dilation of the arterial wall greater than 50%, caused by weakness or stretching of the middle wall (tunica media) of the aorta.  Why A wrong: The adventitia is the outer layer, not the weakened layer in the definition.  Why C wrong: The intima is the innermost layer, not the one described.  Why D wrong: Endothelial lining alone is not the layer cited; weakening involves the middle wall."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"An aortic aneurysm is defined as an abnormal dilation of the arterial wall that exceeds what percentage of the normal vessel diameter?","options":["Greater than 25%","Greater than 40%","Greater than 50%","Greater than 75%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines an aneurysm as dilation greater than 50%.  Why A/B/D wrong: 25%, 40%, and 75% are not the threshold stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which diagnostic tests are used to confirm an aortic aneurysm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ultrasonography (USG)","CT scan","Arteriography","Sputum culture","Throat swab"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic tests for aortic aneurysm include USG, CT scan, and arteriography.  Why D wrong: Sputum culture is a respiratory test, not used for aneurysm.  Why E wrong: Throat swab is used for streptococcal/rheumatic fever workup, not aneurysm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A \"fusiform\" aortic aneurysm is characterized by which morphology?","options":["Pouch-like projection on one side of the artery","Entire circumference of the artery is dilated","Tearing of the artery wall in midflas syndrome","Outpouching due to infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fusiform aneurysm involves dilation of the entire circumference of the artery.  Why A wrong: That describes a saccular aneurysm.  Why C wrong: That describes a dissecting aneurysm.  Why D wrong: That describes a mycotic aneurysm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which type of aortic aneurysm is described as a pouch-like projection on one side of the artery?","options":["Fusiform","Saccular","Dissecting","Mycotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A saccular aneurysm is a pouch-like projection on one side of the artery.  Why A wrong: Fusiform dilates the entire circumference.  Why C wrong: Dissecting involves a tear/separation of layers.  Why D wrong: Mycotic is due to infection."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A dissecting aortic aneurysm is most characteristically associated with which connective-tissue disorder?","options":["Marfan syndrome","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A dissecting aneurysm is seen in Marfan syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: Down, Turner, and Klinefelter syndromes are not the connective-tissue disorder associated with dissecting aneurysm in the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A mycotic aortic aneurysm is caused by which underlying process and is therefore also called what?","options":["Caused by trauma; called false aneurysm","Caused by infection; called infected aneurysm","Caused by hypertension; called true aneurysm","Caused by atherosclerosis; called fusiform aneurysm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A mycotic aneurysm is due to infection and is also called an infected aneurysm.  Why A wrong: Trauma causes a false/pseudo-aneurysm.  Why C wrong: A true aneurysm is not defined by hypertension as called here.  Why D wrong: Atherosclerosis is the most common cause of aneurysm but does not define mycotic type."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A \"false\" or \"pseudo-aneurysm\" of the aorta develops as a result of:","options":["Marfan syndrome","Bacterial infection","Trauma or injury to the arterial wall","Congenital weakness"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A false/pseudo-aneurysm is due to trauma or injury to the arterial wall.  Why A wrong: Marfan is linked to dissecting aneurysm.  Why B wrong: Infection causes a mycotic aneurysm.  Why D wrong: Congenital weakness is not the cited cause of false aneurysm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which is the MOST common cause of thoracic aortic aneurysm?","options":["Hypertension","Atherosclerosis","Trauma","Infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of thoracic aortic aneurysm is atherosclerosis.  Why A wrong: Hypertension contributes but is not listed as the most common cause of the thoracic type.  Why C wrong: Trauma causes false aneurysm, not the most common thoracic cause.  Why D wrong: Infection causes mycotic aneurysm, not the most common thoracic cause."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A client with a thoracic aortic aneurysm reports a \"prominent pulsating mass\" on examination. Where is this mass most likely located?","options":["In the right iliac fossa","In the abdomen at or above the umbilicus","In the suprapubic region","In the popliteal fossa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A prominent pulsating mass is felt in the abdomen at or above the umbilicus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the locations described in the source for the pulsating mass."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with a thoracic aortic aneurysm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pain that extends to neck and shoulders","Dyspnea and dysphagia","Hoarseness, cough and aphonia","Unequal pulses and arterial pressure in upper extremities","Trachea may displace from midline"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed features are documented signs/symptoms of thoracic aortic aneurysm: pain radiating to neck/shoulders, dyspnea/dysphagia, hoarseness/cough/aphonia, unequal pulses and arterial pressure in upper extremities, and tracheal deviation from midline.  Why none wrong: Every option is directly from the source list."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"An abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is sometimes referred to by which alternate name?","options":["Triple A aneurysm","Double A aneurysm","Single A aneurysm","Quad A aneurysm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal aortic aneurysm is sometimes called \"Triple A\" aneurysm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not recognized names for AAA."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)?","options":["Trauma alone","Atherosclerosis and hypertension","Marfan syndrome","Bacterial infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of AAA is atherosclerosis and hypertension.  Why A wrong: Trauma is linked to false aneurysm.  Why C wrong: Marfan is associated with dissecting aneurysm.  Why D wrong: Infection causes mycotic aneurysm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which clinical findings may be present in a client with an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["A systolic bruit or continuous sound over the aorta","Tenderness on deep palpation","Abdominal or lower back pain","Increasing abdominal girth indicating aneurysm may rupture","Hemoptysis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: AAA findings include a systolic bruit/continuous sound over the aorta, tenderness on deep palpation, abdominal or lower back pain, and increasing abdominal girth indicating possible rupture.  Why E wrong: Hemoptysis (coughing blood) is not a documented AAA finding in the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which finding is identified as a CARDINAL sign of a rupturing aortic aneurysm?","options":["Mild epigastric discomfort","Severe abdominal or back pain","Intermittent claudication","Bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe abdominal or back pain is the cardinal sign of a rupturing aneurysm.  Why A wrong: Mild discomfort is not the cardinal sign.  Why C wrong: Intermittent claudication is associated with peripheral arterial disease/Buerger's disease.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia is not described; rupture more typically causes hypotension/tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which signs and symptoms indicate a rupturing aortic aneurysm? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Severe abdominal or back pain","Lumbar pain radiating to the flank and groin region","Hypertension, tachycardia and signs of shock","Bounding peripheral pulses","Severe abdominal or back pain (cardinal sign)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: A rupturing aneurysm presents with severe abdominal/back pain (the cardinal sign), lumbar pain radiating to flank and groin, and hypertension/tachycardia with signs of shock.  Why D wrong: Bounding peripheral pulses are not a feature; shock would produce weak/thready pulses."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"When providing nursing care for a client with an aortic aneurysm, the nurse should instruct the client to immediately report which symptoms? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chest pain","Back pain","Shortness of breath","Difficulty swallowing or hoarseness","Improved exercise tolerance"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should instruct the client to report chest pain, back pain, shortness of breath, and difficulty swallowing or hoarseness — these may signal enlargement or impending rupture.  Why E wrong: Improved exercise tolerance is not a symptom to report as a warning sign."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A thrombus is best defined as:","options":["A blood clot that travels through the bloodstream","A blood clot formed in situ within the vascular system in the body, impeding blood flow","Inflammation of a vein with clot formation","Surgical removal of a clot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A thrombus is a blood clot formed in situ within the vascular system in the body, impeding blood flow.  Why A wrong: A traveling clot is an embolus.  Why C wrong: Inflammation of a vein with a clot is thrombophlebitis.  Why D wrong: Surgical clot removal is embolectomy."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"An embolus differs from a thrombus in that it:","options":["Forms in situ and stays fixed in place","Detaches from its original site, circulates in the blood, and can block an artery","Is always made of fat","Only occurs in veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An embolus is a thrombus that detaches from its original site and circulates in the blood, and can block an artery in the lung, brain, or heart.  Why A wrong: That describes a thrombus.  Why C wrong: Emboli can be of various types, not always fat.  Why D wrong: Emboli can lodge in arteries of lung, brain, or heart."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Match the definition: a thrombosis associated with inflammation of a vein is termed:","options":["Phlebothrombosis","Thrombophlebitis","Sclerotherapy","Embolectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombophlebitis is a thrombosis with inflammation of a vein.  Why A wrong: Phlebothrombosis is thrombosis without inflammation.  Why C wrong: Sclerotherapy is a treatment, not a clot condition.  Why D wrong: Embolectomy is surgical removal of an embolus."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The term phlebothrombosis refers to:","options":["Thrombosis with vein inflammation","Thrombosis without inflammation","Inflammation of an artery","A traveling clot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebothrombosis is thrombosis without inflammation.  Why A wrong: That is thrombophlebitis.  Why C wrong: Phlebothrombosis involves veins, not arterial inflammation.  Why D wrong: A traveling clot is an embolus."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Sclerotherapy is best described as:","options":["A surgical management for DVT in which a solution is injected into the affected vein, then apply pressure dressing and bandages","Surgical removal of an embolus","A test to detect thrombus formation","An anticoagulant drug therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sclerotherapy is a surgical management for DVT in which a solution is injected into the affected vein, after which a pressure dressing/bandage is applied and the blood vessel is closed off.  Why B wrong: Surgical removal of an embolus is embolectomy.  Why C wrong: It is a treatment, not a diagnostic test.  Why D wrong: It uses an injected sclerosing solution, not systemic anticoagulants."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The Vein Stripping procedure is a surgical management for DVT in which veins are removed and arrested. Which complication can develop later during this procedure?","options":["Cellulitis","Nursing care includes early ambulation","Prolonged sitting or standing","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In vein stripping, veins are removed; nursing care includes early ambulation, and the leg above the heart level should be elevated, avoiding prolonged sitting/standing and constrictive clothing or crossing of the legs — cellulitis is the complication that can develop in later stage.  Why B wrong: Early ambulation is part of nursing care, not a complication.  Why C wrong: Prolonged sitting/standing is to be avoided, not a complication itself.  Why D wrong: Hypertension is not the cited complication of this procedure."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Raynaud's disease is characterized by:","options":["Vasospasm of the arteries due to sympathetic nervous system stimulation, triggered by cold or emotional stress","Permanent dilation of arteries","Inflammation of small and medium arteries from smoking","Clot formation in deep veins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Raynaud's disease is a vasospasm of the arteries due to sympathetic nervous system stimulation, with major clinical features being numbness, tingling sensation, cool extremity and pallor; it is triggered by cold or emotional stress.  Why B wrong: It is vasospasm (constriction), not dilation.  Why C wrong: Smoking-related arterial inflammation describes Buerger's disease.  Why D wrong: Deep vein clot formation is DVT, not Raynaud's."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which clinical features are characteristic of Raynaud's disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Numbness","Tingling sensation","Cool extremity","Pallor","Warm, flushed skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Major clinical features of Raynaud's disease are numbness, tingling sensation, cool extremity, and pallor.  Why E wrong: Warm, flushed skin is the opposite of the cool, pale extremity seen in vasospasm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The nurse teaching a client with Raynaud's disease should emphasize which key trigger to avoid?","options":["High-protein meals","Cold and stress","Bright light","Dehydration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for Raynaud's disease instructs the client to avoid precipitating factors like cold and stress.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the precipitating factors identified for Raynaud's disease."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Buerger's disease is best described as:","options":["A vasospasm triggered by cold","An occlusive disease of the small arteries and veins, most common cause being smoking","Deep vein thrombosis","Inflammation of the aorta only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Buerger's disease is an occlusive disease of the small arteries and veins, with the most common cause being smoking.  Why A wrong: Cold-triggered vasospasm is Raynaud's disease.  Why C wrong: DVT is clot in deep veins, not the occlusive small-vessel disease described.  Why D wrong: It affects small arteries and veins, not solely the aorta."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with Buerger's disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Intermittent claudication","Aching pain","Cool extremity","Numbness and tingling sensation","Bounding pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of Buerger's disease include intermittent claudication, aching pain, cool extremity, and numbness and tingling sensation.  Why E wrong: Bounding pulses are not a feature; occlusive disease produces diminished pulses."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Nursing care for a client with Buerger's disease should include instructing the client to:","options":["Increase smoking gradually","Stop smoking","Apply cold packs to the limbs","Cross the legs while sitting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because smoking is the most common cause, nursing care includes instructing the client to stop smoking.  Why A wrong: Smoking worsens the disease and must be stopped.  Why C wrong: Cold worsens vasoconstriction; cold packs are not advised.  Why D wrong: Crossing legs impairs circulation and is discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Embolectomy refers to which surgical procedure?","options":["Removal of an aneurysm","Removal of an embolus from a vessel","Injection of a sclerosing agent","Stripping of a vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Embolectomy is a surgical procedure which involves removal of an embolus from a blood vessel.  Why A wrong: Aneurysm removal is aneurysmectomy.  Why C wrong: Injecting a sclerosing agent is sclerotherapy.  Why D wrong: Vein stripping is a separate procedure."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) is characterized by:","options":["Overstimulation of the clotting factors inside the microcirculation, leading to clots throughout the body","Complete absence of clotting factors","Vasospasm of large arteries","A localized clot in one deep vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DIC is the overstimulation of the clotting factors inside the microcirculation, most commonly affecting organs such as the kidneys (most common), liver, brain, and heart.  Why B wrong: DIC paradoxically involves excessive clotting then consumption, not a simple absence.  Why C wrong: Vasospasm describes Raynaud's, not DIC.  Why D wrong: DIC is disseminated (widespread microcirculation), not a single localized clot."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which organs are most commonly affected in Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Kidney (most common)","Liver","Brain","Heart","Skin only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DIC most commonly affects organs such as the kidney (most common), liver, brain, and heart.  Why E wrong: DIC affects multiple internal organs as listed, not the skin only."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which type of therapy is used in the treatment of Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)?","options":["Antifibrinolytic therapy","Sclerotherapy","Vein stripping","Embolectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antifibrinolytic therapy is a choice of treatment in DIC.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sclerotherapy, vein stripping, and embolectomy are managements for venous/embolic conditions, not DIC."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Rheumatic fever is best described as:","options":["A bacterial infection of the heart valves only","An inflammatory autoimmune disease that affects the connective tissue of the heart (mostly mitral valve), joints, subcutaneous tissue, blood vessels and CNS","A congenital heart defect","A viral infection of the pericardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever is an inflammatory autoimmune disease that affects the connective tissue of the heart (mostly mitral valve), joints, subcutaneous tissue, blood vessels and CNS.  Why A wrong: It affects more than the valves and is autoimmune/inflammatory.  Why C wrong: It is acquired, not congenital.  Why D wrong: It is not described as a primary viral pericardial infection."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Rheumatic fever most commonly occurs as a delayed complication following an untreated infection caused by which organism?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus","Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever is a delayed complication of an untreated upper respiratory infection (URTI) caused by Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal infection, especially mitral valve involvement.  Why A/C/D wrong: Staphylococcus, E. coli, and Pseudomonas are not the causative organisms of rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A frequent diagnostic finding in rheumatic fever is the presence of \"Aschoff bodies\" or lesions. Where are these found? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Heart","Blood vessels","Brain","Joints","Bone marrow"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Aschoff bodies or lesions are found in the heart, blood vessels, brain, and joints in rheumatic fever.  Why E wrong: Bone marrow is not listed as a site of Aschoff bodies in the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which laboratory findings are commonly elevated in rheumatic fever? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Elevated ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate)","Elevated C-reactive protein (CRP)","Elevated anti-streptolysin O titer (ASO)","Decreased white blood cell count","Low hemoglobin only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever shows fever (usually low grade), elevated ESR, elevated CRP, and elevated anti-streptolysin O titer (ASO).  Why D wrong: Leukocytosis (increased WBC) rather than a decrease is associated.  Why E wrong: Low hemoglobin alone is not a diagnostic feature listed."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Rheumatic fever is diagnosed using which clinical criteria?","options":["Jones criteria","Ranson's criteria","Light's criteria","Duke criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever is diagnosed using the Jones criteria.  Why B wrong: Ranson's criteria assess pancreatitis severity.  Why C wrong: Light's criteria differentiate pleural effusion exudate/transudate.  Why D wrong: Duke criteria are used for infective endocarditis."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which of the following are MAJOR criteria of the Jones criteria for rheumatic fever? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Joints involvement (polyarthritis)","Carditis (involvement of the heart)","Subcutaneous nodules","Erythema marginatum","Sydenham's chorea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The major Jones criteria are remembered by \"JONES\": J = Joints (polyarthritis), O = (heart shape) carditis, N = Nodules (subcutaneous), E = Erythema marginatum, S = Sydenham's chorea.  Why none wrong: All five are major criteria per the JONES mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which of the following are MINOR criteria in the Jones criteria for rheumatic fever? (Select All That Apply)","options":["CRP increased","Arthralgia","Fever","Elevated ESR","Prolonged PR interval"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Minor Jones criteria include increased CRP, arthralgia, fever, elevated ESR, prolonged PR interval, and leukocytosis.  Why none wrong: All listed options are minor criteria per the source table."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"In rheumatic fever, \"arthralgia\" is classified as a minor criterion while \"polyarthritis\" is a major criterion. What is the key difference?","options":["Arthralgia is joint pain without objective inflammation, whereas polyarthritis is objective inflammation of multiple joints","They are identical terms","Arthralgia affects only one joint and polyarthritis the spine","Polyarthritis is painless"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arthralgia (joint pain) is a minor criterion, while polyarthritis (objective inflammation/migratory involvement of multiple joints) is a major criterion — this distinction prevents double counting in Jones criteria.  Why B wrong: They are distinct (minor vs major).  Why C wrong: Polyarthritis means many joints, not the spine specifically.  Why D wrong: Polyarthritis is inflammatory and typically painful."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Erythema marginatum, a feature of rheumatic fever, is best described as:","options":["A red itchy lesion starting on the trunk and spreading peripherally","Small subcutaneous nodules over the joints","Involuntary jerky movements","Inflammation of the heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Erythema marginatum is a red, marginated (itchy) lesion starting on the trunk and spreading peripherally.  Why B wrong: That describes subcutaneous nodules.  Why C wrong: Involuntary jerky movements describe chorea (Sydenham's).  Why D wrong: Inflammation of the heart is carditis."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"\"Chorea\" as a clinical feature of rheumatic fever refers to:","options":["Inflammation of all heart layers","Involuntary movements of extremities and face that affect speech","A type of skin rash","A fever pattern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chorea (Sydenham's chorea) is characterized by involuntary movements of the extremities and face that affect speech.  Why A wrong: Inflammation of heart layers is carditis.  Why C wrong: A skin rash describes erythema marginatum.  Why D wrong: Chorea is a movement disorder, not a fever pattern."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Nursing care for a client with rheumatic fever includes which interventions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assess vital signs including BP, respiration, pulse and oxygen saturation","Control joint pain with massage and alternating hot and cold applications","Administer antibiotics as per order","Encourage strenuous activity and exercise","Promote rest"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes assessing vital signs (BP, respiration, pulse, O2 saturation), controlling joint pain with massage and alternating hot/cold applications, administering antibiotics as per order, and promoting rest.  Why D wrong: Strenuous activity is contraindicated; rest is promoted to reduce cardiac workload."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A heart transplant (cardiac transplant) is a surgical procedure performed on patients with end-stage heart failure when which condition exists?","options":["When the client has mild hypertension only","When severe coronary artery disease has not responded to other medical or surgical treatments","When the client refuses medication","When the client has a single arrhythmia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A heart transplant is a surgical treatment for patients with end-stage heart failure when severe coronary artery disease has not responded to other medical or surgical treatments.  Why A/C/D wrong: Mild hypertension, medication refusal, and an isolated arrhythmia are not the cited indications for transplant."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the source, where and when was the first successful heart transplant in India performed?","options":["In Mumbai, on 3 December 1967","In Delhi at AIIMS, New Delhi on 3 August 1994, with the patient surviving for 14 years","In Chennai in 1987","In Rajasthan at SMS Hospital, Jaipur in 2015"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first successful heart transplant in India was performed at AIIMS, New Delhi on 3 August 1994, and the patient survived for 14 years.  Why A wrong: 3 December 1967 was the world's first human-to-human heart transplant by Christian Barnard, not in India.  Why C wrong: Chennai 1987 is not the cited event.  Why D wrong: SMS Hospital, Jaipur (Rajasthan) is referenced for the first heart transplant in Rajasthan, not the first successful one in India."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The world's first human-to-human heart transplant was performed by which surgeon and on what date?","options":["Christian Barnard and his team on 3 December 1967","Norman Shumway on 3 August 1994","Denton Cooley in 1969","Adrian Kantrowitz in 1967 in India"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Christian Barnard, with his team, performed the world's first human-to-human heart transplant on 3 December 1967.  Why B/C/D wrong: These surgeons/dates/locations do not match the source's cited world-first event."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"When selecting a donor heart for transplantation, the donor heart must be from an individual with what compatibility?","options":["Any blood type and any body weight","A comparable body weight and ABO compatibility, transplanted into a recipient within less than 6 hours of procurement","Identical eye color","The same age only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A donor heart from an individual with comparable body weight and ABO (blood) compatibility is transplanted into a recipient within less than 6 hours of procurement.  Why A wrong: ABO compatibility and comparable weight are required, not \"any.\"  Why C/D wrong: Eye color and age alone are not the stated matching criteria."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"After a heart transplant, the transplanted heart is denervated. What clinical consequence does this produce?","options":["The heart is unresponsive to vagus stimulation and the client does not experience angina","The heart beats faster than normal at all times","The heart cannot pump at all","The client experiences typical chest pain (angina) with ischemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The transplanted heart is denervated, so it is unresponsive to vagus stimulation, and because the heart is denervated the client does not experience angina.  Why B wrong: Denervation does not produce a constantly faster rate as described.  Why C wrong: A denervated heart can still pump.  Why D wrong: The denervated client does NOT experience typical angina."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Which symptoms of heart rejection should the nurse monitor for in a heart transplant recipient? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypotension","Dysrhythmias","Weakness","Fatigue and dizziness","Increased angina pain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Symptoms of heart rejection include hypotension, dysrhythmias, weakness, fatigue and dizziness.  Why E wrong: The transplanted heart is denervated and the client does not experience angina, so increased angina is not a rejection sign here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"In the clinical phases of organ-transplant rejection, HYPERACUTE rejection occurs over what time frame?","options":["Within months to years","Within 10–30 days","Within minutes to hours","Within 1–2 years only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours, with pre-existing antibodies to donor HLA antigens, complement activation, macrophages, and host versus transplanted organ immune reactions occurring immediately.  Why A wrong: Months to years describes chronic rejection.  Why B wrong: 10–30 days describes acute rejection.  Why D wrong: 1–2 years is not the hyperacute window."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"ACUTE transplant rejection typically occurs within which time frame and is mediated by which immune cells?","options":["Minutes to hours; mediated by pre-existing antibodies","10–30 days; cellular mechanism (CD4, CD8, natural killer/macrophages) and mediated by IgG","Months to years; humoral and cellular mechanisms","Within 1 hour; mediated by complement only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute rejection occurs within 10–30 days, by a cellular mechanism (CD4, CD8, NK, macrophages), mediated by IgG, with T cells destroying graft parenchyma by cytotoxicity and inflammatory reactions.  Why A wrong: Minutes to hours with pre-existing antibodies is hyperacute.  Why C wrong: Months to years is chronic rejection.  Why D wrong: Complement-only within 1 hour is not the acute description."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"CHRONIC transplant rejection is characterized by which features? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Occurs within months to years","Mediated by mixed humoral and cellular mechanisms","Chronic rejection is still hard to manage","Occurs within minutes","Mediated only by pre-existing antibodies"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic rejection occurs within months to years, is mediated by mixed humoral and cellular mechanisms, and is still hard to manage.  Why D wrong: Minutes describes hyperacute rejection.  Why E wrong: Pre-existing antibodies only describes hyperacute rejection."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the cardiovascular rapid-revision key points, the most common cause of aortic aneurysm is:","options":["Hypertension","Atherosclerosis","Trauma","Marfan syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of aortic aneurysm is atherosclerosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hypertension, trauma, and Marfan syndrome are contributing/associated factors but atherosclerosis is the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the rapid-revision key points, an elevated level of LDL is a risk factor for which condition?","options":["Atherosclerosis","Anemia","Hyperthyroidism","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Elevated LDL is a risk factor for atherosclerosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: LDL is not the identified risk factor for anemia, hyperthyroidism, or dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, HDL is known as the \"good cholesterol\" because it:","options":["Prevents formation of thrombus","Prevents atherosclerosis (is protective)","Increases blood pressure","Causes plaque buildup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HDL is the good cholesterol and prevents atherosclerosis.  Why A wrong: Preventing thrombus formation is not how HDL is defined here.  Why C/D wrong: HDL lowers cardiovascular risk; it does not raise BP or cause plaque."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the rapid-revision key points, the most common cause of infective endocarditis is:","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus viridans","Escherichia coli","Candida albicans"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of infective endocarditis is Streptococcus viridans.  Why A/C/D wrong: These organisms can cause endocarditis in specific settings but Streptococcus viridans is the most common per the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, the drug of choice for heart failure is:","options":["Digoxin","Aspirin","Heparin","Warfarin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for heart failure is digoxin (a cardiac glycoside).  Why B/C/D wrong: Aspirin, heparin, and warfarin are antiplatelet/anticoagulant agents, not the drug of choice for heart failure here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the drug of choice for ventricular fibrillation is:","options":["Amiodarone","Digoxin","Atropine","Adrenaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for ventricular fibrillation is amiodarone.  Why B wrong: Digoxin is the drug of choice for heart failure.  Why C wrong: Atropine is used for bradycardia.  Why D wrong: Adrenaline is used in cardiac arrest/anaphylaxis, not as the listed drug of choice for VF here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, the drug of choice for anaphylactic shock is:","options":["Adrenaline (epinephrine)","Digoxin","Sodium nitroprusside","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for anaphylactic shock is adrenaline (adrenaline or epinephrine).  Why B wrong: Digoxin is for heart failure.  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is for hypertensive emergency.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is used in cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the drug of choice for cardiogenic shock is:","options":["Dopamine","Atropine","Sodium nitroprusside","Heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for cardiogenic shock is dopamine.  Why B wrong: Atropine is for bradycardia.  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is for hypertensive emergency.  Why D wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant, not the agent for cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, the drug of choice for a hypertensive emergency / malignant hypertension is:","options":["Sodium nitroprusside","Digoxin","Amiodarone","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for hypertensive emergency / malignant hypertension is sodium nitroprusside.  Why B/C/D wrong: Digoxin (heart failure), amiodarone (VF), and dopamine (cardiogenic shock) are not used for hypertensive emergency."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the normal level of triglycerides (TG) is approximately:","options":["Less than 150 mg/dL","Less than 200 mg/dL (above 6 normal is high)","Greater than 250 mg/dL","Below 50 mg/dL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source key point lists the normal triglyceride level as less than 200 mg/dL (1 mmol/L), above which it is considered high.  Why A wrong: 150 mg/dL is a commonly cited borderline value but the source states less than 200 mg/dL.  Why C wrong: Greater than 250 mg/dL is elevated, not normal.  Why D wrong: Below 50 mg/dL is not the cited normal value."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, the first sign of left-sided heart failure is:","options":["Dyspnea (shortness of breath)","Peripheral edema","Liver enlargement","Bounding pulse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first sign of left-sided heart failure is dyspnea.  Why B/C wrong: Peripheral edema and liver enlargement are features of right-sided heart failure.  Why D wrong: A bounding pulse is not the first sign of left-sided heart failure."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, which features are associated with RIGHT-sided heart failure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Liver enlargement / cirrhosis","Peripheral edema","Distended neck veins","Visceral congestion","Dyspnea as the first sign"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Right-sided heart failure leads to systemic venous congestion producing liver enlargement, peripheral edema, distended neck veins, and visceral congestion.  Why E wrong: Dyspnea is the first sign of LEFT-sided heart failure, not right-sided."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the rapid-revision key points, which biomarker is confirmatory of / a diagnostic marker for heart failure?","options":["BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide)","Troponin","Myoglobin","CRP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) is confirmatory/diagnostic for heart failure.  Why B wrong: Troponin is the gold standard for myocardial infarction.  Why C wrong: Myoglobin is the earliest cardiac marker in MI.  Why D wrong: CRP is a nonspecific inflammatory marker."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the GOLD STANDARD / most specific marker for the confirmation of myocardial infarction (MI) is:","options":["Troponin","Myoglobin","CPK-MB","SGOT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Troponin is the gold standard (confirmatory) for myocardial infarction.  Why B wrong: Myoglobin is the earliest but least specific marker.  Why C wrong: CPK-MB rises but troponin is the gold standard.  Why D wrong: SGOT is nonspecific."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, which cardiac enzyme/marker rises EARLIEST in a myocardial infarction?","options":["Myoglobin","Troponin","LDH","SGOT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myoglobin is the earliest cardiac marker to rise in myocardial infarction.  Why B wrong: Troponin is the most specific/gold standard but is not the earliest.  Why C/D wrong: LDH and SGOT rise later."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the most common dysrhythmia following a myocardial infarction is:","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Sinus bradycardia","First-degree heart block","Atrial flutter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common dysrhythmia/complication of myocardial infarction is ventricular fibrillation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dysrhythmias can occur but ventricular fibrillation is the most common per the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, the most common manifestation/complication of a pulmonary embolism is:","options":["Dyspnea","Hematuria","Jaundice","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common manifestation of pulmonary embolism is dyspnea.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hematuria, jaundice, and bradycardia are not the most common manifestation of pulmonary embolism."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, the CVP (central venous pressure) should be measured at which anatomical landmark?","options":["At the level of the right atrium of the heart","At the apex of the left ventricle","At the level of the diaphragm","At the carotid sinus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CVP should be measured at the level of the right atrium of the heart.  Why B/C/D wrong: These landmarks are not used for CVP measurement."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) hormone is secreted by:","options":["Both atria of the heart","Both ventricles of the heart","The liver","The kidneys"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) hormone is secreted by both atria of the heart.  Why B wrong: BNP is associated with the ventricles.  Why C/D wrong: ANP is a cardiac hormone, not secreted primarily by the liver or kidneys."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the key points, B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) hormone is secreted by:","options":["The ventricles of the heart","The atria of the heart","The lungs","The adrenal glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: B-type (BNP) natriuretic peptide is secreted by the ventricles of the heart.  Why B wrong: ANP is secreted by the atria.  Why C/D wrong: BNP is a cardiac (ventricular) hormone."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the key points, Buerger's disease is associated with which dietary/lifestyle factor as a cause?","options":["Smoking (tobacco use)","High salt intake","Excess vitamin D","Sedentary lifestyle alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Buerger's disease is associated with smoking (the most common cause), consistent with the cardiovascular key points and the \"Also Remember\" box.  Why B/C/D wrong: Salt, vitamin D, and a sedentary lifestyle alone are not the cited cause of Buerger's disease."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the \"Important Terminology\" table, ARTERIOSCLEROSIS is best defined as:","options":["The loss of elasticity in the artery wall and hardening of the arteries (small arteries)","Buildup of fatty plaque only in large arteries","Inflammation of a vein","A traveling blood clot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arteriosclerosis is the loss of elasticity (stiffening/hardening) of the arteries, affecting the small arteries.  Why B wrong: Fatty plaque buildup describes atherosclerosis.  Why C wrong: Vein inflammation is thrombophlebitis.  Why D wrong: A traveling clot is an embolus."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the \"Important Terminology\" table, ATHEROSCLEROSIS is best defined as:","options":["The buildup of fats, cholesterol and other substances in and on the artery wall","Generalized hardening of small arteries","Inflammation of the heart valves","A spasm of the arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atherosclerosis is the buildup of fats, cholesterol and other substances in and on the artery wall (forming plaque).  Why B wrong: Generalized hardening of small arteries is arteriosclerosis.  Why C wrong: Valve inflammation is endocarditis/valvulitis.  Why D wrong: Arterial spasm describes Raynaud's disease."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the \"Important Terminology\" table, an ATHEROMA is:","options":["A type of subcutaneous nodule","A swelling/lesion (plaque) on the artery wall composed of fats, cholesterol, calcium and fibrous tissue (deposited substance) causing narrowing of the artery","A blood clot in a deep vein","An inflamed lymph node"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An atheroma is the swelling/plaque deposit (fats, cholesterol, calcium and fibrous tissue) on the artery wall causing narrowing.  Why A wrong: Subcutaneous nodules are a feature of rheumatic fever.  Why C wrong: A deep-vein clot is a thrombus (DVT).  Why D wrong: An atheroma is an arterial plaque, not a lymph node."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the \"Important Terminology\" table, ATHEROGENESIS refers to:","options":["The formation of fatty plaques/atheromas in the arteries (the process of atherosclerosis)","The breakdown of a clot","The widening of a vein","Inflammation of the pericardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atherogenesis is the formation of fatty plaques/atheromas in the arteries — the developmental process of atherosclerosis.  Why B wrong: Clot breakdown is fibrinolysis.  Why C wrong: Vein widening is venous dilation, not atherogenesis.  Why D wrong: Pericardial inflammation is pericarditis."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Per the \"Important Terminology\" table, CORE PULMONALE (cor pulmonale) is defined as:","options":["Right ventricular enlargement / failure of the right ventricle of the heart due to a disorder of the respiratory system","Left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertension","Inflammation of the lungs","A congenital heart defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cor pulmonale is the alteration/enlargement (hypertrophy) and failure of the right ventricle of the heart due to a disorder of the respiratory system.  Why B wrong: Left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertension is a different entity.  Why C wrong: Lung inflammation is pneumonia/pneumonitis.  Why D wrong: Cor pulmonale is acquired secondary to lung disease, not congenital."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The neuron is best defined as the:","options":["Basic structural and functional unit of the nervous system","Connective tissue of the brain","Outer covering of the spinal cord","Fluid surrounding the brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A neuron is the basic structural and functional unit of the nervous system.  Why B wrong: Connective/supportive tissue of the nervous system is glial cells.  Why C wrong: The outer covering of the spinal cord is the meninges.  Why D wrong: The fluid surrounding the brain is cerebrospinal fluid."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"What is the primary function of the dendrite of a neuron?","options":["It is the branch-like structure that receives electrochemical messages (impulses)","It carries impulses away from the cell body","It produces myelin","It stores cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dendrite is the branch-like structure for receiving electrochemical messages (incoming impulses) into the neuron.  Why B wrong: Carrying impulses away from the cell body is the function of the axon.  Why C wrong: Myelin is produced by Schwann cells/oligodendrocytes.  Why D wrong: Dendrites do not store CSF."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The axon of a neuron functions to:","options":["Carry impulses away from the cell body","Receive impulses into the cell body","Connect two arteries","Produce cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The axon is a long projection of the neuron that carries impulses away from the cell body.  Why B wrong: Receiving impulses is the dendrite's role.  Why C/D wrong: These are not neuronal axon functions."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"A synapse is best defined as:","options":["A specialized junction between two neurons","The cell body of a neuron","The myelin sheath of an axon","A gap in the spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A synapse is a specialized junction between two neurons across which impulses are transmitted.  Why B wrong: The cell body is the soma.  Why C wrong: The myelin sheath insulates the axon.  Why D wrong: A synapse is a neuron-to-neuron junction, not a spinal cord gap."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"Myelin is a fatty material that surrounds and insulates the axon. What is the functional benefit of the myelin sheath?","options":["It increases the rapid conduction of nerve impulses","It slows down nerve conduction","It produces neurotransmitters","It absorbs cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myelin is a fatty material in the myelin sheath surrounding the axon that increases (facilitates) the rapid conduction of nerve impulses (saltatory conduction).  Why B wrong: Myelin speeds conduction, it does not slow it.  Why C wrong: Neurotransmitters are produced at the synapse, not by myelin.  Why D wrong: Myelin insulates the axon; it does not absorb CSF."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The Node of Ranvier in a myelinated neuron is:","options":["A gap in the myelin sheath along the axon that facilitates rapid (saltatory) conduction","The branching dendrite","The neuron cell body","The synaptic terminal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Node of Ranvier is a gap in the myelin sheath along the axon that allows rapid saltatory conduction of impulses.  Why B wrong: That is the dendrite.  Why C wrong: The cell body is the soma.  Why D wrong: The synaptic terminal is the end of the axon."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the classification of the nervous system, the nervous system is broadly divided into which main divisions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Central Nervous System (CNS)","Peripheral Nervous System (PNS)","Autonomic Nervous System (ANS)","Lymphatic Nervous System","Endocrine Nervous System"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nervous system is classified into the Central Nervous System (CNS), the Autonomic Nervous System (ANS), and the Peripheral Nervous System (PNS).  Why D wrong: The lymphatic system is not a division of the nervous system.  Why E wrong: The endocrine system is a separate hormonal system, not a nervous system division."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The Central Nervous System (CNS) consists of which structures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Spinal cord","Brain","Peripheral nerves","Sympathetic nerves","Afferent nerves"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The CNS consists of the spinal cord and the brain.  Why C/E wrong: Peripheral nerves and afferent nerves are part of the Peripheral Nervous System.  Why D wrong: Sympathetic nerves are part of the Autonomic Nervous System."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The Autonomic Nervous System (ANS) is divided into which two branches?","options":["Sympathetic and Parasympathetic nervous systems","Afferent and Efferent nerves","Central and Peripheral systems","Spinal cord and Brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Autonomic Nervous System is divided into the Parasympathetic Nervous System and the Sympathetic Nervous System.  Why B wrong: Afferent and efferent nerves are subdivisions of the peripheral nerves.  Why C wrong: Central and peripheral are the broader nervous system divisions.  Why D wrong: Spinal cord and brain are CNS components."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The Peripheral Nervous System (PNS) is composed of which two types of nerves?","options":["Efferent (motor) nerves and Afferent (sensory) nerves","Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves","The brain and spinal cord","Dendrites and axons"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Peripheral Nervous System consists of efferent nerves and afferent nerves.  Why B wrong: Sympathetic and parasympathetic are subdivisions of the ANS.  Why C wrong: The brain and spinal cord form the CNS.  Why D wrong: Dendrites and axons are parts of a single neuron, not PNS divisions."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"The nervous system detects and responds to changes inside and outside the body. According to the overview, what supports the nervous system in carrying out vital functions?","options":["The musculoskeletal system alone","Connective tissue cells / supportive (glial) cells","The lymphatic system","The endocrine glands only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nervous system conducts nerve impulses and is supported by connective tissue (supportive/glial) cells that help it carry out vital functions.  Why A wrong: The musculoskeletal system is acted upon, but supportive cells assist the nervous system functionally.  Why C wrong: The lymphatic system is not the supportive tissue described.  Why D wrong: The endocrine glands are a separate regulatory system."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the overview, in a condition where axons are surrounded by an insulated myelin sheath, transmission of nerve impulses is:","options":["Sped up (rapid conduction)","Stopped permanently","Reversed in direction","Unaffected"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Where axons are surrounded by an insulating myelin sheath, the transmission of nerve impulses is led to rapid conduction (sped up).  Why B wrong: Myelin facilitates, not stops, conduction.  Why C wrong: Myelin does not reverse impulse direction.  Why D wrong: Myelin clearly affects (accelerates) conduction."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Aortic aneurysm (types, AAA, signs/symptoms, rupture, nursing care)…","question":"According to the overview, when the insulated myelin sheath surrounding nerve cells is destroyed (a condition known as neuropathy/demyelination), what happens to impulse transmission?","options":["It is impaired or blocked","It speeds up","It remains normal","It only affects sensory nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When the insulated myelin sheath is destroyed (neuropathy/demyelination), nerve impulse transmission is impaired or blocked.  Why B wrong: Demyelination slows/blocks rather than speeds conduction.  Why C wrong: Transmission does not remain normal once myelin is destroyed.  Why D wrong: Demyelination can impair both motor and sensory transmission, not sensory only."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Neuroglia (glial cells) differ from neurons in that they:","options":["Conduct electrical impulses","Are non-neuronal cells of the CNS and PNS that do not produce electrical impulses","Secrete cerebrospinal fluid only","Are found exclusively in the peripheral nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neuroglia are non-neuronal cells of the CNS (brain and spinal cord) and PNS (ganglia) that do NOT produce electrical impulses.  Why A wrong: Conducting electrical impulses is the role of neurons, not glia.  Why C wrong: CSF secretion is the function of ependymal cells specifically, not all neuroglia.  Why D wrong: They are found in both the CNS and PNS."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which glial cells maintain the homeostatic form, provide myelin in the PNS, and provide support and protection for neurons?","options":["Astrocytes","Microglia","Neuroglia","Ependymal cells"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Neuroglia maintain homeostatic form, provide myelin in the PNS, and provide support and protection for neurons.  Why A wrong: Astrocytes specifically maintain the blood-brain barrier and structural support.  Why B wrong: Microglia are immune cells of the CNS.  Why D wrong: Ependymal cells are involved in producing CSF."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which CNS glial cells are responsible for the formation and maintenance of myelin sheaths around CNS nerves and provide structural framework?","options":["Astrocytes","Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Satellite cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oligodendrocytes are responsible for formation and maintenance of myelin sheaths around CNS nerves and provide structural framework.  Why A wrong: Astrocytes maintain the blood-brain barrier.  Why C wrong: Schwann cells form/maintain myelin in the PNS, not CNS.  Why D wrong: Satellite cells surround neuron cell bodies in the PNS."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Astrocytes are responsible for which of the following functions in the CNS?","options":["Producing cerebrospinal fluid","Making myelin in the peripheral nervous system","Maintaining the blood-brain barrier (BBB), maintaining the level of neurotransmitters, and forming synapses","Acting as immune cells"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Astrocytes maintain the BBB, maintain the level of neurotransmitters, and are involved at synapses.  Why A wrong: CSF production is the ependymal cell function.  Why B wrong: Schwann cells make myelin in the PNS.  Why D wrong: Microglia are the immune cells."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which CNS glial cells act as the macrophages (immune cells) of the central nervous system?","options":["Astrocytes","Microglia","Oligodendrocytes","Ependymal cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Microglia are the brain's immune cells, which act as the macrophages of the CNS.  Why A wrong: Astrocytes maintain the BBB.  Why C wrong: Oligodendrocytes form CNS myelin.  Why D wrong: Ependymal cells help produce CSF."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which CNS glial cells are involved in producing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?","options":["Ependymal cells","Astrocytes","Microglia","Satellite cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ependymal cells are involved in producing CSF.  Why B wrong: Astrocytes maintain the BBB.  Why C wrong: Microglia are immune cells.  Why D wrong: Satellite cells are PNS support cells."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which PNS glial cells are responsible for the formation and maintenance of myelin sheaths in the peripheral nervous system?","options":["Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Satellite cells","Astrocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the PNS, Schwann cells are responsible for formation and maintenance of myelin sheaths.  Why A wrong: Oligodendrocytes do this in the CNS.  Why C wrong: Satellite cells enclose cell bodies in ganglia/neurons; they regulate neurotransmitter levels in the PNS.  Why D wrong: Astrocytes are CNS cells."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"In the PNS, satellite cells enclose the cell bodies in neurons and regulate which of the following?","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid pressure","Neurotransmitter levels in the PNS","The blood-brain barrier","Myelin formation in the CNS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Satellite cells enclose cell bodies in PNS neurons and regulate the neurotransmitter level in the PNS.  Why A wrong: CSF pressure is not a satellite-cell function.  Why C wrong: The BBB is maintained by astrocytes.  Why D wrong: CNS myelin is from oligodendrocytes."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Most brain tumors originate in glial cells rather than neurons. Which is the most common brain tumor in ADULTS?","options":["Medulloblastoma","Meningioma","Craniopharyngioma","Schwannoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common benign brain tumor in adults is meningioma.  Why A wrong: Medulloblastoma is the most common brain tumor in children.  Why C wrong: Craniopharyngioma is the most common benign brain tumor in children.  Why D wrong: Schwannoma arises from Schwann cells but is not listed as the most common adult tumor here."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which is the most common brain tumor in CHILDREN?","options":["Meningioma","Glioblastoma","Medulloblastoma","Craniopharyngioma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Medulloblastoma is the most common brain tumor in children.  Why A wrong: Meningioma is the most common benign tumor in adults.  Why B wrong: Glioblastoma is not the answer listed for children here.  Why D wrong: Craniopharyngioma is the most common BENIGN brain tumor in children, but medulloblastoma is the most common brain tumor overall in children."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which is the most common benign brain tumor in CHILDREN?","options":["Medulloblastoma","Craniopharyngioma","Meningioma","Astrocytoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Craniopharyngioma is the most common benign brain tumor in children.  Why A wrong: Medulloblastoma is the most common (malignant) brain tumor in children.  Why C wrong: Meningioma is the most common benign tumor in adults.  Why D wrong: Not the answer keyed in the source box."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Merkel cells are found in epidermis. They function to:","options":["Transmit smell stimuli","Transmit stimuli to the brain through a chemical synapse","Produce cerebrospinal fluid","Form myelin sheaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Merkel cells are found in epidermis and transmit stimuli to the brain through a chemical synapse.  Why A wrong: Smell stimuli are conducted by olfactory receptors/CN I.  Why C wrong: CSF is produced with ependymal involvement.  Why D wrong: Myelin is formed by oligodendrocytes/Schwann cells."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A neurotransmitter is best defined as:","options":["A type of glial cell","A single molecule that helps communicate nerve/neuronal information transmission","A bone of the cranial vault","A layer of the meninges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A neurotransmitter is a single molecule which helps to communicate neuronal information transmission.  Why A wrong: It is a chemical molecule, not a cell.  Why C wrong: It is not a bone.  Why D wrong: It is not a meningeal layer."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The chemical substances which help in transmission of impulses from one nerve to another through synapses are generally synthesized by which part of the neuron?","options":["Axon terminal","Pre-synaptic neuron","Schwann cell","Dendrite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neurotransmitters help transmission of impulses across synapses and are synthesized by the pre-synaptic neuron and stored in synaptic vesicles.  Why A wrong: They are released from the axon terminal but synthesized by the pre-synaptic neuron.  Why C wrong: Schwann cells form myelin.  Why D wrong: Dendrites receive signals; they do not synthesize neurotransmitters."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Excitatory neurotransmitters act on the neuron to:","options":["Decrease the impulse from the pre-synaptic neuron","Increase the conduction of impulse from the pre-synaptic neuron to the post-synaptic neuron","Inhibit muscle contraction","Have no effect on conduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excitatory neurotransmitters increase the conduction of impulse from pre-synaptic to post-synaptic neuron; examples include glutamate, epinephrine and norepinephrine.  Why A wrong: That describes inhibitory neurotransmitters.  Why C wrong: Inhibition of contraction is not the defining excitatory action described.  Why D wrong: They do have an effect — they increase conduction."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which of the following are examples of EXCITATORY neurotransmitters?","options":["GABA, glycine, serotonin","Glutamate, epinephrine, norepinephrine","Dopamine, GABA, glycine","Acetylcholine, GABA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of excitatory neurotransmitters are glutamate, epinephrine and norepinephrine.  Why A wrong: GABA, glycine and serotonin are listed as inhibitory.  Why C wrong: GABA and glycine are inhibitory.  Why D wrong: GABA is inhibitory."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Inhibitory neurotransmitters act to:","options":["Increase the conduction of impulse to the post-synaptic neuron","Decrease the conduction of impulse from the pre-synaptic neuron to the post-synaptic neuron","Produce CSF","Maintain the blood-brain barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhibitory neurotransmitters decrease the conduction of impulse from pre-synaptic to post-synaptic neuron; examples include GABA, glycine, dopamine and serotonin.  Why A wrong: That is the excitatory action.  Why C wrong: Not a neurotransmitter role described.  Why D wrong: BBB is maintained by astrocytes."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which of the following are examples of INHIBITORY neurotransmitters?","options":["Glutamate, epinephrine, norepinephrine","GABA, glycine, dopamine, serotonin","Acetylcholine, glutamate","Norepinephrine, epinephrine, glutamate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of inhibitory neurotransmitters are GABA, glycine, dopamine and serotonin.  Why A wrong: These are excitatory.  Why C wrong: Glutamate is excitatory.  Why D wrong: All listed are excitatory."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A nurse studying neurotransmitter imbalances knows that decreased levels of which neurotransmitter are associated with depression?","options":["Dopamine","Serotonin","GABA","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased serotonin is associated with depression and migraine (low level).  Why A wrong: Dopamine imbalance relates to high (schizophrenia) and low (Parkinsonism) levels.  Why C wrong: GABA relates to anxiety disorder and seizures.  Why D wrong: Acetylcholine decrease is associated with Alzheimer's disease."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"According to the neurotransmitter-imbalance table, HIGH levels of dopamine are associated with schizophrenia, while LOW levels are associated with which condition?","options":["Anxiety disorder","Alzheimer's disease","Parkinsonism","Depression"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: High dopamine = schizophrenia; low dopamine = Parkinsonism.  Why A wrong: Anxiety disorder is linked to GABA.  Why B wrong: Alzheimer's is linked to low acetylcholine.  Why D wrong: Depression is linked to low serotonin."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A decrease in which neurotransmitter is most associated with Alzheimer's disease?","options":["GABA","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Serotonin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alzheimer's disease is associated with decreased acetylcholine.  Why A wrong: GABA relates to anxiety disorder/seizures.  Why C wrong: Dopamine imbalance relates to schizophrenia/Parkinsonism.  Why D wrong: Serotonin relates to depression/migraine."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Imbalance of which neurotransmitter is associated with anxiety disorder and seizures?","options":["Dopamine","GABA","Serotonin","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GABA imbalance is associated with anxiety disorder and seizures.  Why A wrong: Dopamine relates to schizophrenia/Parkinsonism.  Why C wrong: Serotonin relates to depression/migraine.  Why D wrong: Acetylcholine relates to Alzheimer's."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The central nervous system (CNS) is the part of the nervous system consisting primarily of which two structures?","options":["The brain and the spinal cord","The cranial nerves and spinal nerves","The sympathetic and parasympathetic systems","The ganglia and peripheral nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CNS consists of the brain and the spinal cord.  Why B wrong: Cranial and spinal nerves are part of the PNS.  Why C wrong: Sympathetic/parasympathetic are divisions of the ANS.  Why D wrong: Ganglia and peripheral nerves are PNS components."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brain and spinal cord are both enclosed within which protective membranes?","options":["The pleura","The meninges","The peritoneum","The pericardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brain and spinal cord are both enclosed within the meninges.  Why A wrong: Pleura surrounds the lungs.  Why C wrong: Peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity.  Why D wrong: Pericardium surrounds the heart."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brain is located in which cavity?","options":["The thoracic cavity","The cranial cavity","The vertebral canal","The abdominal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brain is located in the cranial cavity.  Why B (correct), others: The other cavities house other structures (lungs/heart, spinal cord, abdominal organs)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Although the brain receives about 15% of total cardiac output, what approximate proportion of total blood per minute passes through the circle of Willis?","options":["750 to 900 ml per minute","100 ml per minute","2.5 litres per minute","45 ml per minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The brain receives approximately 750 ml of blood per minute through the circle of Willis.  Why B wrong: Too low.  Why C wrong: 2.5 cm relates to the thalamus length, not blood flow.  Why D wrong: 45 cm is the spinal cord length, not blood flow."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The circle of Willis is best described as:","options":["A bone at the base of the skull","A circulatory anastomosis at the bottom of the brain that supplies blood to the brain","A venous sinus draining the brain","A lobe of the cerebrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The circle of Willis is a circulatory anastomosis at the bottom of the brain.  Why A wrong: It is a vascular ring, not a bone.  Why C wrong: It is arterial, not a venous sinus.  Why D wrong: It is not a brain lobe."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What is the approximate weight of the human adult brain?","options":["500–700 gm","1000–1100 gm","1300–1400 gm","2000–2200 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The human brain weighs approximately 1300–1400 gm and makes up about 2% of the human body's weight.  Why A wrong: Too low.  Why B wrong: Below the stated range.  Why D wrong: Far above the stated weight."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brain makes up approximately what percentage of the human body's total weight?","options":["2%","15%","25%","40%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The brain makes up about 2% of the human body's weight.  Why B wrong: 15% refers to the brain's share of cardiac output, not body weight.  Why C wrong: Incorrect.  Why D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brain is divided into which three major regions?","options":["Forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain","Cerebrum, cerebellum, and meninges","Thalamus, hypothalamus, and pons","Cranium, vertebrae, and ganglia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The brain is divided into three regions — forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain.  Why B wrong: Meninges is a membrane, not a brain region.  Why C wrong: These are subparts, not the three major regions.  Why D wrong: These are skeletal/PNS structures."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which structures make up the FOREBRAIN (prosencephalon)?","options":["Pons and medulla","Cerebrum, basal ganglia, hippocampus, amygdala, thalamus, and hypothalamus","Cerebellum only","Spinal cord and ventricles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The forebrain includes the cerebrum, basal ganglia, hippocampus, amygdala, thalamus and hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: Pons and medulla are hindbrain.  Why C wrong: Cerebellum is part of the hindbrain.  Why D wrong: These are not forebrain structures."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The midbrain is also known as the:","options":["Prosencephalon","Mesencephalon","Rhombencephalon","Telencephalon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The midbrain is also known as the mesencephalon.  Why A wrong: Prosencephalon is the forebrain.  Why C wrong: Rhombencephalon is the hindbrain.  Why D wrong: Telencephalon refers to the cerebrum, not the midbrain."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The HINDBRAIN consists of which structures?","options":["Pons, medulla, and cerebellum","Cerebrum and thalamus","Basal ganglia and amygdala","Hypothalamus and hippocampus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The hindbrain includes the pons, medulla, and cerebellum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These structures belong to the forebrain."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brain is also divided into how many major parts?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brain is also divided into three major parts: cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies three major parts."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which layer of the meninges is the tough, outermost layer of the brain?","options":["Pia mater","Arachnoid mater","Dura mater","Ependyma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The dura mater is the outer layer (tough, outermost), covered by the brain and spinal cord.  Why A wrong: Pia mater is the inner-most layer, nearest to the brain.  Why B wrong: Arachnoid mater is the middle layer.  Why D wrong: Ependyma is not a meningeal layer."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The middle meningeal layer that is web-like and contains the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), also reabsorbing CSF, is the:","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Subdural layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Arachnoid mater is the middle layer; it contains CSF and reabsorbs CSF.  Why A wrong: Dura is the outer layer.  Why C wrong: Pia is the inner layer.  Why D wrong: Not a meningeal layer name in the source."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which meningeal layer is the inner-most layer, nearest to the brain?","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Periosteum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pia mater is the inner-most layer, nearest to the brain.  Why A wrong: Dura is outermost.  Why B wrong: Arachnoid is the middle layer.  Why D wrong: Periosteum covers bone, not the brain's inner meninges."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Arterial blood supply to the brain is provided by two internal carotid arteries and which other vessels?","options":["Two vertebral arteries","The internal jugular veins","The basilar vein","The coronary arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Arterial supply to the brain is provided by two internal carotid arteries and two vertebral arteries.  Why B wrong: The internal jugular veins are part of venous drainage.  Why C wrong: Not the arterial supply source.  Why D wrong: Coronary arteries supply the heart."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Venous blood from the brain empties into which vessel?","options":["Internal carotid artery","Internal jugular vein","Vertebral artery","Pulmonary vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Venous blood from the brain empties into the internal jugular vein.  Why A wrong: Internal carotid is an artery supplying blood.  Why C wrong: Vertebral artery is an arterial supply vessel.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary vein returns blood from the lungs to the heart."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The circle of Willis is composed of several arteries. The anterior cerebral artery supplies which region?","options":["Left and right anterior portions of the brain","The occipital lobe only","The cerebellum","The spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The circle of Willis includes the anterior cerebral artery (left and right), internal central artery, posterior communicating artery, middle cerebral arteries, and anterior communicating artery.  Why B wrong: Occipital supply is from the posterior circulation.  Why C wrong: Cerebellum is supplied by separate vessels.  Why D wrong: Spinal cord has its own arterial supply."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The posterior cerebral artery (left and right) supplies which part of the brain?","options":["The frontal lobes","The posterior circulation of the brain (back parts)","The cerebellum exclusively","The pituitary gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior cerebral arteries (left and right) are part of the Circle of Willis and supply the posterior circulation/posterior part of the brain.  Why A wrong: The frontal areas are supplied by anterior circulation.  Why C wrong: Not exclusively cerebellar.  Why D wrong: Not the pituitary specifically."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The Blood Brain Barrier (BBB) is best described as:","options":["A non-selective filter allowing all substances into the brain","A highly selective semipermeable border of endothelial cells that prevents solutes in circulating blood from entering the brain","A part of the meninges","A venous sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BBB is a highly selective semipermeable border of endothelial cells that prevents solutes in the circulating blood from non-selectively crossing into the brain.  Why A wrong: It is selective, not non-selective.  Why C wrong: It is a vascular/endothelial barrier, not meninges.  Why D wrong: It is not a venous sinus."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The blood-brain barrier protects the brain from which of the following?","options":["Toxins, pathogens, and harmful substances","Glucose and oxygen","Carbon dioxide only","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The BBB filters blood by the endothelial cells and astrocytes to protect the brain from toxins, pathogens, and harmful substances.  Why B wrong: Glucose and oxygen are needed by the brain and are not what the BBB blocks.  Why C wrong: Not the described protective role.  Why D wrong: CSF is part of the normal brain environment."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The largest part of the brain, which is the seat of intelligence and memory and consists of two hemispheres (right and left), is the:","options":["Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Brainstem","Medulla oblongata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain, the seat of intelligence and memory, and consists of two hemispheres (right and left).  Why A wrong: The cerebellum coordinates movement.  Why C wrong: The brainstem controls vital functions.  Why D wrong: The medulla is part of the brainstem."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The term \"limbic system\" refers to which part of the brain?","options":["The part involved in memory, motivational behaviour, and emotional responses — also called the emotional brain","The outer cortex responsible for movement","The brainstem","The cerebellum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The limbic system is the part of the brain involved in memory, motivational behaviour, and emotional responses; it is also called the emotional brain.  Why B wrong: Movement is controlled by the motor cortex.  Why C wrong: The brainstem controls vital functions.  Why D wrong: The cerebellum coordinates movement."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"In most right-handed persons, the DOMINANT hemisphere is the left hemisphere, which is responsible for which functions?","options":["Linguistic, verbal calculatory, arithmetic, and analytical functions","Spatial awareness and music only","Balance and coordination","Cardiac and respiratory control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In right-handed persons, the dominant left hemisphere is responsible for linguistic, verbal, calculatory, arithmetic, and analytical functions.  Why B wrong: Those are more associated with the non-dominant hemisphere.  Why C wrong: Balance/coordination is cerebellar.  Why D wrong: Cardiac/respiratory control is in the medulla."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The two cerebral hemispheres are connected by which structure?","options":["Corpus callosum","Tentorium cerebelli","Falx cerebelli","Foramen magnum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both hemispheres are connected by the corpus callosum.  Why B wrong: The tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum.  Why C wrong: Falx cerebelli is a dural fold, not the connector of the hemispheres.  Why D wrong: The foramen magnum is an opening in the skull base."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Each cerebral hemisphere is divided into how many lobes?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cerebral hemispheres are divided into four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital.  Why A/B/D wrong: The text lists four lobes."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A \"gyrus\" of the cerebrum refers to:","options":["The grey matter of the cerebrum","The white matter of the cerebrum","An outside wrinkled or folded appearance / ridge on the surface area of the brain","A fluid-filled ventricle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A gyrus is the outside wrinkled or folded (ridge) appearance on the surface area of the brain.  Why A wrong: Grey matter relates to external surface tissue, but a gyrus specifically denotes the folded ridge.  Why B wrong: White matter is the inner part of the cerebrum.  Why D wrong: Ventricles are CSF-filled cavities."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"In the cerebrum, the external surface (cortex) is made up of which type of matter?","options":["Grey matter","White matter","Cerebrospinal fluid","Adipose tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The external surface of the cerebrum is grey matter.  Why B wrong: The internal part of the cerebrum is white matter.  Why C wrong: CSF is a fluid, not the cortex.  Why D wrong: There is no adipose cortex in the brain."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The internal part of the cerebrum is made of which type of matter?","options":["Grey matter","White matter","CSF","Bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The internal part of the cerebrum is white matter.  Why A wrong: Grey matter is the external surface.  Why C/D wrong: Not tissue types of the cerebral interior."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which sensory perception area is located in the TEMPORAL lobe?","options":["Primary auditory area","Primary visual area","Primary somatosensory area","Primary motor area"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary auditory area is located in the temporal lobe.  Why B wrong: The primary visual area is in the occipital lobe.  Why C wrong: The primary somatosensory area is in the parietal lobe.  Why D wrong: The primary motor area is in the frontal lobe."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The primary VISUAL area is located in which lobe?","options":["Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The primary visual area is in the occipital lobe.  Why A wrong: Temporal houses the primary auditory area.  Why C wrong: Parietal houses the primary somatosensory area.  Why D wrong: Frontal houses the primary motor area."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The primary SOMATOSENSORY area and the primary sensory area are both located in which lobe?","options":["Frontal lobe","Parietal lobe","Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both the primary somatosensory area and the primary sensory area are located in the parietal lobe.  Why A wrong: Frontal houses the primary motor area.  Why C wrong: Temporal houses the auditory area.  Why D wrong: Occipital houses the visual area."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The primary MOTOR area is located in which lobe?","options":["Frontal lobe","Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe","Temporal lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary motor area is in the frontal lobe.  Why B wrong: Parietal houses sensory/somatosensory areas.  Why C wrong: Occipital houses the visual area.  Why D wrong: Temporal houses the auditory area."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Wernicke's area is a sensory area located in the left temporal lobe and is associated with which function?","options":["Production of speech","Speech/language comprehension","Coordination of movement","Vision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wernicke's area is a sensory area in the left temporal lobe which functions as the ability to understand language; it is also known as language comprehension area.  Why A wrong: Production of speech is Broca's area.  Why C wrong: Coordination is cerebellar.  Why D wrong: Vision is occipital."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Broca's area is a motor area located in the frontal lobe in conjunction with the motor cortex, and controls which ability?","options":["Understanding written words","The ability to speak the words / damage causes a dispute in speaking (speech production)","Balance and posture","Auditory perception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Broca's area is a motor area in the frontal lobe controlling the ability to speak the words; damage in this area causes a dispute (difficulty) in speaking.  Why A wrong: Comprehension/understanding is Wernicke's area.  Why C wrong: Balance is cerebellar.  Why D wrong: Auditory perception is in the temporal lobe."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A patient sustains an injury to the FRONTAL lobe. Which of the following deficits would be MOST expected?","options":["Loss of movement (paralysis), repetition of a single thought, inability to focus on a task, mood swings, irritability, impulsiveness, and changes in social behaviour","Loss of vision and blindness","Loss of balance and coordination","Difficulty with breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frontal lobe controls personality, emotions, intelligence, attention, concentration, judgment, body movement, problem-solving, and speech; injury causes loss of movement (paralysis), repetition of a single thought, inability to focus on a task, mood swings, irritability, impulsiveness, changes in social behaviour, and personality difficulty (with language).  Why B wrong: Vision loss indicates occipital lobe injury.  Why C wrong: Balance/coordination loss indicates cerebellar injury.  Why D wrong: Breathing difficulty indicates brainstem injury."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The PARIETAL lobe functions in the sense of touch, pain, and temperature, and also in spatial and visual perception. Injury to the parietal lobe would most likely cause:","options":["Difficulty distinguishing left from right, lack of awareness of certain body parts, and difficulty with reading, writing, naming, and mathematics","Loss of breathing control","Loss of memory of language","Loss of balance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parietal lobe handles sense of touch/pain/temperature and spatial/visual perception; injury causes difficulty distinguishing left from right, lack of awareness of body parts, and difficulty with reading, writing, naming, and mathematics.  Why B wrong: Breathing control is in the brainstem.  Why C wrong: Language memory loss relates to temporal lobe.  Why D wrong: Balance loss is cerebellar."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The OCCIPITAL lobe is responsible for vision. Injury to this lobe most commonly results in:","options":["Defects in vision or blind spots, and blurred vision","Loss of coordination","Difficulty speaking","Loss of heart rate control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The occipital lobe controls vision; injury causes defects in vision or blind spots and blurred vision.  Why B wrong: Coordination loss is cerebellar.  Why C wrong: Speech difficulty relates to frontal (Broca's) injury.  Why D wrong: Heart-rate control is in the medulla."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The TEMPORAL lobe functions in hearing, memory, and sequencing/organization. Injury to the temporal lobe is most associated with which abnormality?","options":["Visual flashes/hallucinations and difficulty with reading, writing, memory loss, and aggressive behaviour","Loss of touch sensation","Loss of breathing","Loss of balance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The temporal lobe controls hearing, memory and sequencing/organization; injury can cause visual hallucinations, difficulty with reading/writing, memory difficulty, and increased aggressive behaviour.  Why B wrong: Touch sensation loss relates to parietal injury.  Why C wrong: Breathing loss relates to brainstem injury.  Why D wrong: Balance loss is cerebellar."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The CEREBELLUM functions in balance and coordination. Injury to the cerebellum is most likely to cause:","options":["Difficulty with coordinated movements, difficulty walking, tremors, and slurred speech","Loss of vision","Personality changes","Loss of hearing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cerebellum controls balance and coordination; injury causes difficulty with coordinated movements, difficulty walking, tremors, and slurred speech.  Why B wrong: Vision loss is occipital.  Why C wrong: Personality change is frontal.  Why D wrong: Hearing loss is temporal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The BRAINSTEM controls breathing, heart rate, and alertness/consciousness. Injury to the brainstem most likely causes:","options":["Changes in breathing, difficulty swallowing food, and problems with balance and movement, dizziness and nausea (vertigo)","Loss of vision only","Difficulty reading and writing","Personality change only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The brainstem controls breathing, heart rate, and alertness/consciousness; injury causes changes in breathing, difficulty swallowing food, and problems with balance and movement, dizziness, and nausea (vertigo).  Why B wrong: Vision-only loss is occipital.  Why C wrong: Reading/writing difficulty relates to parietal/temporal.  Why D wrong: Isolated personality change is frontal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The thalamus lies on either side of which ventricle?","options":["First ventricle","Third ventricle","Fourth ventricle","Lateral ventricle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thalamus lies on either side of the third ventricle.  Why A wrong: There is no separately numbered \"first\" ventricle in this context (the lateral ventricles are the first/second).  Why C wrong: The fourth ventricle is near the brainstem/cerebellum.  Why D wrong: The thalamus borders the third ventricle, not the lateral ventricles directly."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The thalamus is the relay centre for all information EXCEPT which sensation?","options":["Smell","Touch","Pain","Temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The thalamus is the relay centre for all information except smell; it receives afferent impulses from sensory receptors and routes them to the appropriate cortical area.  Why B/C/D wrong: Touch, pain, and temperature ARE relayed via the thalamus; only smell bypasses it."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The thalamus contains the reticular activating system (RAS) and is also part of which system?","options":["The pituitary system","The reticuloendothelial system (RES)","The limbic system only","The autonomic ganglia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thalamus contains the pain-pole and reticuloendothelial system (RES) and is part of the reticular activating system.  Why A wrong: The pituitary connects to the hypothalamus, not the thalamus.  Why C wrong: Not described solely as limbic.  Why D wrong: Not described as autonomic ganglia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The hypothalamus, the small structure located in front of the thalamus and below the pituitary gland, weighs approximately:","options":["2 gm","7 gm","25 gm","100 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The weight of the hypothalamus is 7 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 7 gm."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The hypothalamus contains optic chiasm, which has two optic nerves (intersect) and mammillary bodies, and is involved in which function?","options":["Olfactory reflex and emotional response to odours","Coordination of movement","Reabsorption of CSF","Production of red blood cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus contains the optic chiasm and mammillary bodies and is involved in olfactory reflex and emotional response to odours.  Why B wrong: Coordination of movement is cerebellar.  Why C wrong: CSF reabsorption is by the arachnoid.  Why D wrong: Not a hypothalamic function."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The hypothalamus connects to the pituitary gland via which structure?","options":["Corpus callosum","Infundibulum","Tentorium cerebelli","Cerebral aqueduct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus connects to the pituitary gland via the infundibulum and plays an important role in endocrine regulation and fluid regulation.  Why A wrong: Corpus callosum connects the cerebral hemispheres.  Why C wrong: The tentorium cerebelli separates cerebrum and cerebellum.  Why D wrong: The cerebral aqueduct connects the third and fourth ventricles."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which of the following is a function of the hypothalamus?","options":["Production of hormones such as GHIH, PIH, TRH, GnRH, CRH, GnRH","Coordination of voluntary muscle movements","Reabsorption of cerebrospinal fluid","Conduction of sound from the ear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus produces hormones such as GHIH, PIH, TRH, GnRH, CRH, GnRH; it also controls the autonomic nervous system and consciousness.  Why B wrong: Coordination of muscle movement is cerebellar.  Why C wrong: CSF reabsorption is by the arachnoid.  Why D wrong: Sound conduction is via the cochlea/CN VIII."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The hypothalamic thermoregulation centre, which controls body temperature, is located in which region of the hypothalamus?","options":["The mammillary body","The thermoregulation centre","The optic chiasm","The infundibulum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus controls body temperature via the central thermoregulation centre.  Why A wrong: Mammillary bodies relate to memory.  Why C wrong: Optic chiasm relates to vision.  Why D wrong: Infundibulum connects to the pituitary."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the hypothalamus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Control of appetite & satiety (hunger centre)","Control of body temperature","Control of water & water balance","Control of sexual behaviour & child rearing","Control of sleeping (circadian rhythm) & walking cycles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus controls appetite/satiety (hunger centre), body temperature, water & water balance, sexual behaviour & child rearing, and sleeping (circadian rhythm) & walking cycles.  Why each correct: All listed are stated hypothalamic functions in the source."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The cerebellum (hindbrain) is separated from the cerebrum by which structure?","options":["Corpus callosum","Tentorium cerebelli","Falx cerebri","Cerebral aqueduct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cerebellum is separated from the cerebrum by the tentorium cerebelli.  Why A wrong: Corpus callosum connects the two cerebral hemispheres.  Why C wrong: Falx cerebri separates the two cerebral hemispheres, not cerebrum from cerebellum.  Why D wrong: Cerebral aqueduct is a CSF passage."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The cerebellum is known as the \"little brain\" and is part of the hindbrain. It is responsible for which function?","options":["Coordination of movements","Production of hormones","Reabsorption of CSF","Speech production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cerebellum has excitatory & inhibitory actions and is responsible for coordination of movements.  Why B wrong: Hormone production is hypothalamic/pituitary.  Why C wrong: CSF reabsorption is by the arachnoid.  Why D wrong: Speech production is Broca's area."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The cerebellum coordinates voluntary muscle activities and maintains posture, balance, and equilibrium. Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:","options":["Uncoordinated muscular movements, staggering gait, and inability to carry out smooth and steady movement","Loss of vision","Loss of heart rate control","Personality changes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebellum maintains posture, balance & equilibrium and coordinates voluntary muscle activities; damage results in uncoordinated muscular movements, staggering gait, and inability to carry out smooth and steady movement.  Why B wrong: Vision loss is occipital.  Why C wrong: Heart-rate control is in the medulla.  Why D wrong: Personality change is frontal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The brainstem consists of three major organs of the system. In order, which is the FIRST (uppermost)?","options":["Pons","Midbrain (bridge between pons and brain)","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brainstem's three major organs are: 1. Midbrain (bridge between pons and brain), 2. Pons (bridge between medulla and midbrain), 3. Medulla oblongata.  Why A wrong: The pons is the second component.  Why C wrong: The medulla is the third (lowest) component.  Why D wrong: The cerebellum is not part of the brainstem."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The pons is described as the bridge between which two structures?","options":["Medulla and midbrain","Cerebrum and cerebellum","Thalamus and hypothalamus","Spinal cord and cerebellum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pons is the bridge between the medulla and the midbrain.  Why B wrong: That role belongs to the tentorium cerebelli boundary, not the pons.  Why C wrong: Thalamus/hypothalamus are diencephalic.  Why D wrong: Not the described bridging function."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The medulla oblongata is approximately what length, and lies just within the cranium just above the foramen magnum?","options":["2.5 cm long","7 cm long","45 cm long","1 cm long"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The medulla oblongata is about 2.5 cm long and lies just within the cranium, above the foramen magnum.  Why B wrong: 7 gm is the hypothalamus weight.  Why C wrong: 45 cm is the spinal cord length.  Why D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The medulla oblongata is the most inferior region of the brainstem. It is continuous with the spinal cord at the level of which structure?","options":["The foramen magnum","The cerebral aqueduct","The corpus callosum","The tentorium cerebelli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The medulla is continuous with the spinal cord at the level of the foramen magnum.  Why B wrong: The cerebral aqueduct connects ventricles.  Why C wrong: Corpus callosum connects hemispheres.  Why D wrong: Tentorium cerebelli separates cerebrum and cerebellum."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the medulla oblongata? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Control of respiratory system","Control of cardiac system","Regulating blood pressure (vasomotor centre)","Control of vomiting, sneezing, coughing, and hiccupping","Coordination of voluntary muscle movements"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The medulla controls the respiratory system, cardiac system, regulating blood pressure (vasomotor centre), and vomiting, sneezing, coughing & hiccupping.  Why E wrong: Coordination of voluntary muscle movements is a cerebellar function, not the medulla."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"How many lateral ventricles are there in the brain?","options":["One","Two (paired lateral ventricles)","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are two (paired) lateral ventricles connected via the interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro) to the third ventricle.  Why A/C/D wrong: The lateral ventricles are paired (two)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The paired lateral ventricles connect to the third ventricle through which opening?","options":["The cerebral aqueduct","The interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro)","The median and lateral apertures","The foramen magnum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lateral ventricles connect to the third ventricle via the interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro).  Why A wrong: The cerebral aqueduct connects the third and fourth ventricles.  Why C wrong: The median and lateral apertures connect the fourth ventricle to the central canal/subarachnoid space.  Why D wrong: The foramen magnum is a skull opening."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The third ventricle connects to the fourth ventricle through which structure?","options":["Cerebral aqueduct","Interventricular foramen","Median aperture","Central canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The third ventricle connects to the fourth ventricle via the cerebral aqueduct.  Why B wrong: The interventricular foramen connects the lateral ventricles to the third.  Why C wrong: The median aperture connects the fourth ventricle onward.  Why D wrong: The central canal is in the spinal cord."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"From the fourth ventricle, CSF passes (via the median and lateral apertures) to which structure?","options":["The lateral ventricles","The central canal of the spinal cord","The third ventricle","The optic chiasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fourth ventricle connects (via the median and lateral apertures) to the central canal of the spinal cord.  Why A wrong: CSF flows toward the central canal, not back to the lateral ventricles.  Why C wrong: The third ventricle is upstream.  Why D wrong: The optic chiasm is not in the CSF pathway."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Where is CSF secreted by the specialized tissue?","options":["The choroid plexus, located within the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","The arachnoid villi","The corpus callosum","The medulla oblongata"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most CSF is secreted by the specialized tissue called the choroid plexus, which is located within the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles.  Why B wrong: Arachnoid villi reabsorb CSF, not secrete it.  Why C wrong: Corpus callosum connects hemispheres.  Why D wrong: The medulla controls vital functions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What is the normal rate of CSF formation?","options":["0.5 ml/minute or 730 ml/day","5 ml/minute or 100 ml/day","1.5 litre/minute","45 ml/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The rate of CSF formation is 0.5 ml/minute or 730 ml/day.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states 0.5 ml/minute (730 ml/day)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What is the normal average amount of CSF in the system?","options":["50 ml","150 ml","730 ml","1000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal average amount of CSF is 150 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 150 ml (730 ml is the daily formation, not total amount)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The spinal cord is the elongated, almost cylindrical part of the central nervous system that is situated in which structure?","options":["The cranial cavity","The vertebral column","The thoracic cavity","The pelvic cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spinal cord is situated in the vertebral column.  Why A wrong: The cranial cavity houses the brain.  Why C/D wrong: Not the location of the spinal cord."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The spinal cord is covered (or surrounded) by which structures?","options":["The meninges layer and CSF","The pleura","The peritoneum","The pericardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The spinal cord is covered or surrounded by the meninges layer and CSF.  Why B/C/D wrong: These membranes cover other body organs, not the spinal cord."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What is the approximate length of the spinal cord?","options":["25 cm (10 inches)","45 cm (18 inches)","7 cm","2.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spinal cord is approximately 45 cm (18 inches) long.  Why A wrong: Incorrect length.  Why C wrong: 7 gm is the hypothalamus weight.  Why D wrong: 2.5 cm is the medulla length."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The spinal cord is shaped like a structure that extends from the foramen magnum at the base of the skull to the lower border of which vertebra?","options":["The first cervical vertebra","The first lumbar vertebra","The first thoracic vertebra","The coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spinal cord extends from the foramen magnum to the base of the skull to the lower border of the first lumbar vertebra.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the first lumbar vertebra."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"There are how many pairs of spinal nerves?","options":["12 pairs","31 pairs","33 pairs","8 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves.  Why A wrong: 12 pairs are the cranial nerves.  Why C wrong: 33 is the vertebral count, not spinal nerve pairs.  Why D wrong: 8 pairs is only the cervical group."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"In the spinal cord, the arrangement of grey and white matter is OPPOSITE that of the brain. In the spinal cord, the grey matter is located in the:","options":["Outer region","Centre/inner core (with white matter in the outer regions)","Only the dorsal horn","Meninges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the spinal cord, grey matter is located in the centre/inner core while white matter is located in the outer regions — opposite to the brain (where grey matter is in the outer cortex and white matter is internal).  Why A wrong: Outer region holds white matter in the cord.  Why C wrong: Grey matter is the central H-shaped core, not just the dorsal horn.  Why D wrong: Meninges are coverings, not matter types."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The peripheral nervous system (PNS) consists of which of the following?","options":["31 pairs of spinal nerves, 12 pairs of cranial nerves, and the autonomic nervous system","Only the brain and spinal cord","Only the cerebrum and cerebellum","The meninges and CSF"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The PNS consists of 31 pairs of spinal nerves, 12 pairs of cranial nerves, and the autonomic nervous system.  Why B wrong: Brain and spinal cord form the CNS.  Why C wrong: These are CNS structures.  Why D wrong: These are protective coverings/fluid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The spinal nerves consist of 31 pairs. How many of these are cervical nerves?","options":["5 pairs","8 pairs","12 pairs","1 pair"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spinal nerves include 8 pairs of cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal.  Why A wrong: 5 pairs are lumbar (and sacral).  Why C wrong: 12 pairs are thoracic.  Why D wrong: 1 pair is coccygeal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Of the 31 pairs of spinal nerves, how many are THORACIC?","options":["8 pairs","12 pairs","5 pairs","1 pair"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 12 pairs of thoracic spinal nerves.  Why A wrong: 8 pairs are cervical.  Why C wrong: 5 pairs are lumbar/sacral.  Why D wrong: 1 pair is coccygeal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Of the 31 pairs of spinal nerves, how many are LUMBAR and how many are SACRAL?","options":["5 lumbar and 5 sacral","8 lumbar and 12 sacral","12 lumbar and 1 sacral","1 lumbar and 5 sacral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are 5 lumbar and 5 sacral spinal nerve pairs.  Why B wrong: Those are cervical/thoracic counts.  Why C wrong: Incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1 pair is coccygeal."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"How many coccygeal nerve pairs are there?","options":["1 nerve (pair)","5 nerves","8 nerves","12 nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is 1 coccygeal nerve (pair).  Why B/C/D wrong: These match lumbar/sacral, cervical, and thoracic counts respectively."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Each spinal nerve has a dorsal root and a ventral root. The dorsal root carries which type of information?","options":["Sensory information to body parts through the dorsal ganglia","Motor information only","Autonomic information only","No information"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dorsal root carries sensory information to body parts through the dorsal ganglia.  Why B wrong: Motor (efferent) information is carried by the ventral root.  Why C wrong: It is sensory, not purely autonomic.  Why D wrong: It does carry sensory information."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The ventral root of the spinal nerve carries which type of information?","options":["Sensory information","Motor information from the spinal cord to body parts","Only pain sensation","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ventral root carries motor information from the spinal cord to body parts.  Why A wrong: Sensory information is carried by the dorsal root.  Why C wrong: Pain is carried via the dorsal (sensory) route.  Why D wrong: It does not carry CSF."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"There are five large plexuses of mixed nerves formed on each side of the vertebral column. The CERVICAL plexus, the largest branch of which is the phrenic nerve, is formed by which nerves?","options":["Cervical nerves","Thoracic nerves","Lumbar nerves","Sacral nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cervical plexus, whose largest branch is the phrenic nerve, is formed by the cervical nerves.  Why B/C/D wrong: These nerves form the brachial, lumbar, and sacral plexuses respectively."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The largest branch of the cervical plexus is the:","options":["Radial nerve","Phrenic nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The phrenic nerve is the largest branch of the cervical plexus.  Why A wrong: The radial nerve is the largest branch of the brachial plexus.  Why C wrong: The femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus.  Why D wrong: The sciatic nerve is the largest branch of the sacral plexus."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The radial nerve is the largest branch of which plexus?","options":["Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brachial plexus's largest branch is the radial nerve.  Why A wrong: Cervical plexus's largest branch is the phrenic nerve.  Why C wrong: Lumbar plexus's largest branch is the femoral nerve.  Why D wrong: Sacral plexus's largest branch is the sciatic nerve."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The femoral nerve is the largest branch of which plexus?","options":["Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The lumbar plexus's largest branch is the femoral nerve.  Why A wrong: Cervical = phrenic nerve.  Why B wrong: Brachial = radial nerve.  Why D wrong: Sacral = sciatic nerve."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, is the largest branch of which plexus?","options":["Cervical plexus","Sacral plexus","Lumbar plexus","Brachial plexus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sacral plexus's largest branch is the sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body.  Why A wrong: Cervical = phrenic nerve.  Why C wrong: Lumbar = femoral nerve.  Why D wrong: Brachial = radial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The coccygeal plexus is formed by which nerves?","options":["The phrenic and cervical nerves","The 4th & 5th sacral nerves and coccygeal nerve","The radial and median nerves","The femoral and sciatic nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The coccygeal plexus is formed by the 4th & 5th sacral nerves and the coccygeal nerve.  Why A/C/D wrong: These nerves form other plexuses."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"How many pairs of cranial nerves are there?","options":["10 pairs","12 pairs","31 pairs","8 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves.  Why A wrong: Incorrect.  Why C wrong: 31 pairs are spinal nerves.  Why D wrong: 8 pairs are cervical spinal nerves."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which cranial nerve (CN I) is the SENSORY nerve responsible for the sense of smell (olfaction)?","options":["Olfactory nerve (CN I)","Optic nerve (CN II)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Vagus nerve (CN X)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN I (Olfactory) is sensory; its function is olfaction (sense of smell); distribution is the nasal mucosa.  Why B wrong: CN II (Optic) carries vision.  Why C wrong: CN V is for facial sensation/mastication.  Why D wrong: CN X controls many autonomic functions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve II (Optic) is a sensory nerve distributed to the retina, and its function is:","options":["Vision","Hearing","Movement of the eyeball","Taste"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN II (Optic) is sensory; its function is vision; it is distributed to the retina.  Why B wrong: Hearing is CN VIII.  Why C wrong: Eye movement is CN III/IV/VI.  Why D wrong: Taste is via CN VII/IX."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which cranial nerve is known as the SMALLEST cranial nerve?","options":["CN IV (Trochlear)","CN II (Optic)","CN X (Vagus)","CN VII (Facial)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN IV (Trochlear) is the smallest cranial nerve; it is a motor nerve to the superior oblique muscle of the eye, causing movement of the eyeball.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the smallest cranial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Which cranial nerve is the LARGEST cranial nerve?","options":["CN V (Trigeminal)","CN II (Optic)","CN X (Vagus)","CN I (Olfactory)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN V (Trigeminal) is the largest cranial nerve; it provides sensation to the face, scalp, cornea, nasal and oral cavities, and supplies motor function to the muscles of mastication.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the largest cranial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve III (Oculomotor) provides motor and parasympathetic functions. Its parasympathetic function is:","options":["Pupillary constriction and accommodation","Sense of smell","Hearing","Movement of the tongue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN III (Oculomotor) parasympathetic function is pupillary constriction and accommodation; motor function moves the eyeball (extrinsic muscles).  Why B wrong: Smell is CN I.  Why C wrong: Hearing is CN VIII.  Why D wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) provides motor function to which muscles?","options":["Muscles of mastication (opening/closing mouth)","Muscles of facial expression","The diaphragm","The superior oblique eye muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN V (Trigeminal) provides general sensation to the face/scalp/cornea/nasal & oral cavities, and motor function to the muscles of mastication (opening/closing of the mouth).  Why B wrong: Facial expression muscles are controlled by CN VII (Facial).  Why C wrong: The diaphragm is controlled by the phrenic nerve.  Why D wrong: The superior oblique is controlled by CN IV."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve VII (Facial) carries sensory taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue and provides motor control to the:","options":["Muscles of facial expression","Muscles of mastication","Tongue movement muscles","Pharynx and larynx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN VII (Facial) provides taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue (sensory) and motor function to the muscles of facial expression; its parasympathetic function controls the salivary and lacrimal glands.  Why B wrong: Mastication is CN V.  Why C wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII.  Why D wrong: Pharynx/larynx involve CN IX/X."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve VIII (Vestibulocochlear) is a sensory nerve. Its cochlear component is responsible for hearing, and its vestibular component is responsible for:","options":["Proprioception of head, balance, and equilibrium","Taste","Salivation","Eye movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN VIII (Vestibulocochlear) — cochlea = hearing; vestibular apparatus = proprioception of head, balance, and equilibrium (general sensation).  Why B wrong: Taste is via CN VII/IX.  Why C wrong: Salivation is parasympathetic (CN VII/IX).  Why D wrong: Eye movement is CN III/IV/VI."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve IX (Glossopharyngeal) carries taste from the posterior 1/3 of the tongue. Its parasympathetic function via the otic ganglion is:","options":["Salivation","Movement of the tongue","Hearing","Pupillary constriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN IX (Glossopharyngeal) — taste posterior 1/3 of tongue (sensory), motor for swallowing (pharynx muscles), and parasympathetic salivation via the otic ganglion.  Why B wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII.  Why C wrong: Hearing is CN VIII.  Why D wrong: Pupillary constriction is CN III."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve X (Vagus) provides parasympathetic control over cardiovascular, respiratory, and gastrointestinal systems. Its motor function controls which structures?","options":["Soft palate, larynx, pharynx, and upper esophagus (speech or voice production, swallowing)","Muscles of mastication","The diaphragm only","Muscles of facial expression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN X (Vagus) motor function controls the soft palate, larynx, pharynx, and upper esophagus (speech/voice production and swallowing); parasympathetic over cardiovascular, respiratory, and GI systems.  Why B wrong: Mastication is CN V.  Why C wrong: The diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve.  Why D wrong: Facial expression is CN VII."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve XI (Accessory) is a motor nerve controlling the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles, resulting in:","options":["Movement of head & shoulders and voice production","Movement of the tongue","Sense of smell","Pupillary constriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN XI (Accessory) motor function controls the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius (muscles of larynx), giving movement of head & shoulders and voice production.  Why B wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII.  Why C wrong: Smell is CN I.  Why D wrong: Pupillary constriction is CN III."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"Cranial nerve XII (Hypoglossal) is a motor nerve to the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue, resulting in:","options":["Movement of the tongue, as in talking and eating","Movement of the eyeball","Swallowing of food","Facial expression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CN XII (Hypoglossal) is motor to intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue, giving movement of the tongue as in talking and eating.  Why B wrong: Eye movement is CN III/IV/VI.  Why C wrong: Swallowing involves CN IX/X.  Why D wrong: Facial expression is CN VII."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"A nursing mnemonic for cranial nerve names is \"Only One Of The Two Athletes Felt Very Good, Victorious And Healthy.\" In this mnemonic, the third \"O\" (Of) stands for which cranial nerve?","options":["Olfactory (CN I)","Optic (CN II)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Trochlear (CN IV)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the order Olfactory, Optic, Oculomotor, Trochlear..., \"Of\" = Oculomotor (CN III).  Why A wrong: Olfactory = \"Only\" (CN I).  Why B wrong: Optic = \"One\" (CN II).  Why D wrong: Trochlear = \"The\" (CN IV)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The autonomic nervous system (ANS) is best described as:","options":["The voluntary part of the nervous system that controls skeletal muscles","The involuntary part of the nervous system that controls the involuntary functions of the body","A part of the central nervous system only","The sense of smell pathway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANS is the involuntary part of the nervous system and controls the involuntary functions of the body; it is divided into two main parts (sympathetic and parasympathetic).  Why A wrong: That describes the somatic nervous system.  Why C wrong: The ANS is part of the PNS in this context.  Why D wrong: Smell is the olfactory pathway."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The sympathetic nervous system has adrenergic nerve fibers that secrete which neurotransmitter?","options":["Acetylcholine","Norepinephrine","GABA","Dopamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sympathetic nervous system has adrenergic nerve fibers, which secrete norepinephrine.  Why A wrong: Acetylcholine is the parasympathetic (cholinergic) neurotransmitter.  Why C wrong: GABA is an inhibitory CNS neurotransmitter.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not the sympathetic transmitter here."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The sympathetic nervous system is responsible for which response?","options":["Fight, fright, and flight responses","Rest and digest","Salivation and digestion","Pupillary constriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sympathetic system is responsible for fight, fright, and flight responses; sympathetic stimuli are mediated by norepinephrine.  Why B wrong: Rest and digest is the parasympathetic role.  Why C wrong: Salivation/digestion are parasympathetic actions.  Why D wrong: Pupillary constriction is parasympathetic (sympathetic dilates the pupil)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The parasympathetic nervous system has cholinergic nerve fibers, which secrete which neurotransmitter?","options":["Norepinephrine","Acetylcholine","Epinephrine","Serotonin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The parasympathetic nervous system has cholinergic nerve fibers, which secrete acetylcholine.  Why A wrong: Norepinephrine is the sympathetic (adrenergic) transmitter.  Why C wrong: Epinephrine is associated with sympathetic/adrenal responses.  Why D wrong: Serotonin is not the parasympathetic transmitter."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"The parasympathetic nervous system influences the digestive tract and smooth muscles to promote which functions?","options":["Digestive gland secretion, peristalsis, and defecation","Increased heart rate and blood pressure","Bronchodilation","Pupillary dilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The parasympathetic system influences the digestive tract and smooth muscles to promote digestive gland secretion, peristalsis, and defecation; parasympathetic stimuli are mediated by acetylcholine.  Why B wrong: Increased heart rate/BP is a sympathetic effect.  Why C wrong: Bronchodilation is sympathetic.  Why D wrong: Pupillary dilation is sympathetic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What effect does SYMPATHETIC stimulation have on the HEART?","options":["Increases rate and force of contraction","Decreases rate","No effect","Stops the heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathetic stimulation increases the rate and force of contraction of the heart, while parasympathetic decreases the rate.  Why B wrong: Decreasing rate is the parasympathetic effect.  Why C/D wrong: Sympathetic stimulation has a strong positive effect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What effect does SYMPATHETIC stimulation have on the bronchi of the LUNGS?","options":["Dilates bronchioles (bronchodilation)","Constricts bronchioles","No effect","Fills bronchioles with fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathetic stimulation dilates the bronchioles, while parasympathetic constricts the bronchioles.  Why B wrong: Constriction is the parasympathetic effect.  Why C/D wrong: There is a definite bronchodilatory effect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What effect does SYMPATHETIC stimulation have on BLOOD VESSELS?","options":["Constricts blood vessels","Dilates blood vessels","No effect","Ruptures blood vessels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathetic stimulation constricts blood vessels, while parasympathetic dilates blood vessels.  Why B wrong: Dilation is the parasympathetic effect.  Why C/D wrong: There is a clear vasoconstrictive effect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"What effect does PARASYMPATHETIC stimulation have on the digestive (gastrointestinal) tract?","options":["Increases motility, relaxes sphincters","Decreases motility, contracts sphincters","No effect","Stops all secretion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic stimulation increases motility and relaxes sphincters in the GI tract; sympathetic decreases motility and contracts sphincters (stomach/intestine).  Why B wrong: That describes the sympathetic effect.  Why C/D wrong: Parasympathetic stimulation actively increases GI activity."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"According to the ANS table, the salivary glands respond to PARASYMPATHETIC stimulation by:","options":["Secreting profusely (increased watery secretion)","Decreasing secretion / thick secretion","No response","Stopping all saliva"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic stimulation causes the salivary glands to secrete profusely (watery secretion), whereas sympathetic decreases secretion (thick secretion).  Why B wrong: That is the sympathetic effect.  Why C/D wrong: There is an active increase in salivary secretion with parasympathetic stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"According to the note in the ANS table, the sweat glands and the piloerector muscles are controlled by:","options":["Both sympathetic and parasympathetic equally","The sympathetic nervous system (sympathetic dilates ducts and forces contraction; piloerector muscles respond to sympathetic only)","The parasympathetic nervous system only","Neither division"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that the sweat glands are sympathetic, and the piloerector muscles respond to sympathetic nerves only (the parasympathetic provides no innervation here).  Why A wrong: Both divisions do not equally control these.  Why C wrong: It is sympathetic, not parasympathetic.  Why D wrong: The sympathetic division does control them."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"According to the note, the enteric nervous system (ENS) or \"brain of gut\" is also known as the:","options":["First brain","Second brain","Reptilian brain","Limbic system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The enteric nervous system (ENS) or \"brain of gut\" is also known as the second brain (located in the entire length of the gut).  Why A wrong: The cerebrum/main brain is the first brain.  Why C wrong: Not the term used in the source.  Why D wrong: The limbic system is the emotional brain."},{"section":"Batch 025 · neuroglia/glial cells, neurotransmitters, central nervous system overview…","question":"According to the note in the ANS table, urinary bladder contraction (passing urine) and emptying are controlled by which division?","options":["Sympathetic nervous system","Parasympathetic nervous system","Somatic system only","Enteric nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note indicates that emptying/contraction of the urinary bladder for passing urine and defecation are controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system, while the sympathetic system relaxes the urinary bladder (storage).  Why A wrong: Sympathetic stimulation relaxes the bladder (urine retention/storage).  Why C wrong: The somatic system controls the external sphincter voluntarily, but bladder emptying is parasympathetic.  Why D wrong: The ENS controls the gut, not the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which neurological imaging procedure uses magnetic field and radio waves to produce cross-sectional images that identify lesions, tumors, and vascular abnormalities?","options":["Computed Tomography (CT) scan","Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)","Electroencephalography (EEG)","Cerebral angiography"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MRI uses a magnetic field and radio waves to produce cross-sectional images and is used to identify lesions, tumors, and vascular abnormalities.  Why A wrong: CT scan uses X-rays/radiation, not magnetic field and radio waves.  Why C wrong: EEG records electrical activity of the cerebral cortex, not images.  Why D wrong: Cerebral angiography visualizes cerebral arteries via contrast, not magnetic imaging."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"What is an absolute contraindication to performing an MRI?","options":["Patient with diabetes","Patient with a pacemaker, implanted defibrillator, artificial heart valve, prosthesis, or orthopedic hardware","Patient with hypertension","Patient who has eaten recently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metallic implants such as a pacemaker, implanted defibrillator, artificial heart valve, prosthesis, or orthopedic hardware are contraindications to MRI because of the strong magnetic field.  Why A wrong: Diabetes is not a contraindication to MRI.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not a contraindication to MRI.  Why D wrong: Recent food intake is not a contraindication to MRI."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A CT scan of the head is used to detect all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Brain tumors and infarcted areas","Cerebral atrophy and vascular lesions","Hydrocephalus and intracranial bleeding","Electrical activity of the cerebral cortex"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Electrical activity of the cerebral cortex is detected by EEG, not by CT scan.  Why A wrong: CT detects brain tumors and infarcted areas.  Why B wrong: CT detects cerebral atrophy and vascular lesions.  Why C wrong: CT detects hydrocephalus and intracranial bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A lumbar puncture is performed primarily to:","options":["Record electrical activity of the brain","Obtain a specimen of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","Visualize cerebral blood vessels","Test vestibular function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A lumbar puncture is performed to obtain a specimen of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).  Why A wrong: Recording brain electrical activity is done by EEG.  Why C wrong: Visualizing cerebral vessels is done by cerebral angiography.  Why D wrong: Vestibular function is tested by the caloric test."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"At which spinal level is a lumbar puncture typically performed in adults?","options":["T1 to T2","L3 to L4","L1 to L2","S1 to S2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adults a lumbar puncture is performed at the L3 to L4 level.  Why A/D wrong: T1–T2 and S1–S2 are not the standard puncture sites.  Why C wrong: L1 to L2 (and below) is the site used in children because the spinal cord ends at a lower level in adults; in infants the cord ends around L3–L4 so a lower interspace is chosen."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"In infants/children, the lumbar puncture is performed at a level lower than L3–L4 for what reason?","options":["The infant's vertebrae are too small","The infant's spinal cord ends at a lower level","Infants have more CSF","To reduce infection risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In infants and children the puncture is done lower (e.g., L4–L5) because the infant's spinal cord ends at a lower level, so a lower interspace avoids cord injury.  Why A wrong: The reason is anatomical cord termination, not vertebral size.  Why C wrong: CSF volume is not the determining factor for site selection.  Why D wrong: Infection risk is not the reason for the lower site."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"An electroencephalography (EEG) records the:","options":["Magnetic activity of brain tissue","Graphical recording of the electrical activity of the superficial layers of the cerebral cortex","Blood flow through cerebral arteries","Pressure of cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EEG is the graphical recording of the electrical activity of the superficial layers of the cerebral cortex.  Why A wrong: Magnetic activity recording is not what EEG measures.  Why C wrong: Blood flow is assessed by angiography.  Why D wrong: CSF pressure is measured during lumbar puncture."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Before an EEG, the nurse should ensure the patient's hair is washed because:","options":["Oil on the hair improves conduction","Clean, dry hair ensures the electrodes are attached to the head and the electricity does not enter the hair","Hair must be removed entirely","Wet hair conducts electricity better"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hair should be washed clean so electrodes attach properly to the head and the electricity does not enter the hair.  Why A wrong: Oil interferes with electrode contact rather than improving conduction.  Why C wrong: Hair removal is not required.  Why D wrong: Wet/oily hair impairs proper electrode-skin contact."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The caloric (vestibular) test evaluates vestibular function by instilling water into the ear canal. A normal result is indicated by:","options":["No eye movement at all","Nystagmus (oculovestibular reflex)","Severe vertigo only","Pupillary dilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The caloric test evaluates the oculovestibular reflex; in an intact (normal) vestibular system, instilling water produces nystagmus.  Why A wrong: Absence of eye movement indicates an abnormal/impaired response.  Why C wrong: Vertigo may occur but the assessed sign is the reflex eye movement (nystagmus).  Why D wrong: Pupillary changes are not the assessed response of the caloric test."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A patient undergoing a caloric test shows NO induced eye movement on instillation of water into the ear. This abnormal finding indicates:","options":["A normal intact vestibular reflex","Brainstem damage","Optic nerve injury","Facial nerve palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absent eye movement (no oculovestibular reflex) is an abnormal finding that indicates brainstem damage.  Why A wrong: A normal reflex produces nystagmus, not absence of movement.  Why C wrong: Optic nerve injury affects vision, not the vestibular reflex.  Why D wrong: Facial nerve palsy affects facial muscles, not this reflex."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Cerebral angiography is used to:","options":["Record brain electrical activity","Visualize the cerebral arteries to assess for lesions, infarction, blockage in cerebral arteries, or aneurysm","Measure CSF pressure","Test cranial nerve reflexes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral angiography visualizes the cerebral arteries to assess for abnormalities such as lesions, infarction, blockage, and aneurysm.  Why A wrong: Brain electrical activity is recorded by EEG.  Why C wrong: CSF pressure is measured during lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: Cranial nerve testing is done by clinical assessment, not angiography."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"During cerebral angiography, before contrast is administered the nurse must FIRST:","options":["Place the patient in Trendelenburg position","Assess for iodine and shellfish allergy","Administer a sedative","Withhold all fluids for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because iodine-based contrast is used, the nurse must assess for iodine and shellfish/seafood allergy before the procedure.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg positioning is not the required pre-contrast step.  Why C wrong: Sedation is not the priority safety check before contrast.  Why D wrong: Prolonged 24-hour fluid restriction is not the protocol."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"After cerebral angiography, an important nursing intervention to prevent complications at the puncture site is to:","options":["Encourage early ambulation immediately","Monitor swelling and maintain neck immobility/limited movement","Withhold all oral fluids","Apply heat to the puncture site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-procedure the nurse monitors for swelling and difficulty in swallowing and keeps movement limited (e.g., neck/leg) and the puncture site immobilized.  Why A wrong: Immediate ambulation risks bleeding at the puncture site.  Why C wrong: Fluids are typically encouraged to flush contrast, not withheld.  Why D wrong: Heat would promote bleeding, not prevent complications."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The olfactory nerve (Cranial Nerve I) is a sensory nerve whose function is:","options":["Vision","Smell","Hearing","Taste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN I (olfactory) is a sensory nerve responsible for the sense of smell.  Why A wrong: Vision is the function of CN II (optic).  Why C wrong: Hearing is the function of CN VIII (vestibulocochlear).  Why D wrong: Taste is carried by CN VII and CN IX."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"To assess the olfactory nerve (CN I), the nurse should:","options":["Use a Snellen chart","Ask the client to identify familiar odors such as mint or alcohol, with eyes closed and one nostril occluded at a time","Shine a light into each eye","Test corneal sensation with a wisp of cotton"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN I is assessed by having the client identify familiar odors (mint, alcohol) with eyes closed, testing one nostril at a time.  Why A wrong: The Snellen chart assesses vision (CN II).  Why C wrong: Light reflex assesses CN III (pupil response).  Why D wrong: Corneal sensation assesses CN V."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The optic nerve (CN II) is assessed using which tool?","options":["Weber test","Snellen chart","Reflex hammer","Caloric test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The optic nerve (CN II, sensory, for vision) is assessed using the Snellen chart.  Why A wrong: The Weber test assesses CN VIII (hearing).  Why C wrong: The reflex hammer tests deep tendon reflexes.  Why D wrong: The caloric test assesses vestibular function."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) controls movement of the eye muscles. To assess it, the nurse asks the client to:","options":["Identify odors","Follow an object moving in different directions, and assesses pupillary response (reaction to light and accommodation)","Smile and show teeth","Shrug the shoulders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN III (oculomotor, motor) moves the eye muscles; assessment includes pupillary reaction to light/accommodation and tracking an object in various directions.  Why A wrong: Identifying odors assesses CN I.  Why C wrong: Smiling/showing teeth assesses CN VII (facial).  Why D wrong: Shoulder shrug assesses CN XI (accessory)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV) is responsible for:","options":["Facial expression","Movement of the superior oblique muscle of the eye","Hearing and balance","Tongue movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN IV (trochlear, motor) supplies the superior oblique muscle of the eye.  Why A wrong: Facial expression is CN VII.  Why C wrong: Hearing and balance are CN VIII.  Why D wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is classified as a:","options":["Pure sensory nerve","Pure motor nerve","Mixed nerve","Autonomic nerve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: CN V (trigeminal) is a mixed nerve (the trick \"MOM\" — CN V is one of the mixed cranial nerves).  Why A wrong: It is not purely sensory.  Why B wrong: It is not purely motor.  Why D wrong: It is a cranial nerve with somatic functions, not classed as autonomic."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) has three branches. The branch that supplies the mandible (lower jaw) and provides function is the:","options":["Ophthalmic branch","Maxillary branch","Mandibular branch","Buccal branch"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The mandibular branch supplies the mandible (lower jaw) and provides motor supply to the muscles of mastication; its sensory nerve supply is to the lower jaw.  Why A wrong: The ophthalmic branch supplies the eye/upper face region (sensory).  Why B wrong: The maxillary branch supplies the upper (maxillary) area.  Why D wrong: \"Buccal\" is not one of the three main trigeminal divisions."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Sensory function of the trigeminal nerve (CN V) is assessed by:","options":["Asking the client to smile","Lightly stroking the face/skin and noting sensation","Using the Snellen chart","Performing the Weber test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN V sensory function (facial sensation) is assessed by lightly stroking the face/cotton and noting sensation.  Why A wrong: Smiling assesses CN VII motor function.  Why C wrong: The Snellen chart assesses vision (CN II).  Why D wrong: The Weber test assesses hearing (CN VIII)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The abducent nerve (CN VI) is a motor nerve responsible for:","options":["Facial sensation","Movement of the external/lateral rectus muscle of the eye","Taste sensation","Hearing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN VI (abducent, motor) moves the external (lateral) rectus muscle of the eye and is assessed together with CN III and CN IV.  Why A wrong: Facial sensation is CN V.  Why C wrong: Taste is carried by CN VII/IX.  Why D wrong: Hearing is CN VIII."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The facial nerve (CN VII) is a mixed nerve. Which of the following is a function of the facial nerve?","options":["Smell","Facial expression, taste of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and secretion of saliva","Movement of the lateral rectus muscle","Tongue protrusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN VII (facial, mixed) controls facial expression, carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and stimulates salivary glands (secretion of saliva).  Why A wrong: Smell is CN I.  Why C wrong: Lateral rectus movement is CN VI.  Why D wrong: Tongue protrusion/movement is CN XII."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The facial nerve (CN VII) is assessed by asking the client to:","options":["Identify odors","Smile, frown, raise eyebrows, and show teeth (symmetry of facial muscles)","Read a chart","Shrug the shoulders against resistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN VII symmetry of facial muscles is assessed by having the client smile, frown, raise the eyebrows, and show the teeth.  Why A wrong: Identifying odors assesses CN I.  Why C wrong: Reading a chart assesses CN II.  Why D wrong: Shoulder shrug assesses CN XI."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The vestibulocochlear (acoustic) nerve, CN VIII, is a sensory nerve responsible for:","options":["Taste only","Hearing and balance (equilibrium)","Facial movement","Pupil constriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN VIII (vestibulocochlear/acoustic, sensory) is responsible for hearing and balance (equilibrium).  Why A wrong: Taste is CN VII/IX.  Why C wrong: Facial movement is CN VII.  Why D wrong: Pupil constriction is CN III."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which test is used to assess the vestibulocochlear (acoustic) nerve, CN VIII?","options":["Snellen chart","Weber test (and Rinne test)","Reflex hammer","Romberg sign only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN VIII (hearing) is assessed using the Weber test.  Why A wrong: The Snellen chart assesses vision.  Why C wrong: The reflex hammer assesses deep tendon reflexes.  Why D wrong: While balance can be tested, the source identifies the Weber test for CN VIII assessment."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) is a mixed nerve. Its functions include taste of the posterior third of the tongue, swallowing movement, and maintenance of which vital sign(s)?","options":["Body temperature only","Blood pressure (BP) and respiratory rate (RR)","Pupil size only","Body weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN IX (glossopharyngeal, mixed) carries taste from the posterior third of the tongue, aids swallowing, and helps maintain BP and RR via baroreceptors/chemoreceptors.  Why A wrong: Temperature regulation is not its listed function.  Why C wrong: Pupil size is governed by CN III.  Why D wrong: Body weight is unrelated to this nerve."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerve is assessed by checking the:","options":["Pupillary light reflex","Gag reflex, swallowing, and ability to taste bitter on the posterior tongue","Corneal reflex","Visual acuity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN IX is assessed by the gag reflex, swallowing, and bitter taste over the posterior third of the tongue.  Why A wrong: Pupillary light reflex assesses CN III.  Why C wrong: Corneal reflex assesses CN V.  Why D wrong: Visual acuity assesses CN II."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The vagus nerve (CN X) is a mixed nerve. Which of the following best describes its function?","options":["Supplies only the muscles of the eye","Provides sensation and innervation to the mucosa and muscles of the pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, esophagus, thoracic and abdominal viscera","Controls only facial expression","Controls only tongue movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN X (vagus, mixed) supplies sensory and motor fibers to the mucosa and muscles of the pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, esophagus, and thoracic/abdominal organs.  Why A wrong: Eye muscles are supplied by CN III, IV, VI.  Why C wrong: Facial expression is CN VII.  Why D wrong: Tongue movement is CN XII."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Because the glossopharyngeal (CN IX) and vagus (CN X) nerves share functions of swallowing and the gag reflex, they are typically:","options":["Assessed individually and never together","Assessed together","Not assessed clinically","Assessed only with imaging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN IX and CN X are assessed together because they share swallowing and gag-reflex functions (the vagus distributes together with CN IX).  Why A wrong: They are assessed together, not separately.  Why C/D wrong: They are assessed clinically by gag reflex/swallowing, not exclusively by imaging."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The accessory nerve (CN XI) is a motor nerve that supplies the neck and shoulder muscles. It is assessed by asking the client to:","options":["Stick out the tongue","Turn the head and shrug the shoulders against the examiner's resistance","Smell coffee","Read an eye chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN XI (accessory, motor) supplies the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius (neck/shoulder); it is assessed by symmetric head turning and shoulder shrug against resistance.  Why A wrong: Tongue protrusion assesses CN XII.  Why C wrong: Smell assesses CN I.  Why D wrong: Reading a chart assesses CN II."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) is a motor nerve that controls:","options":["Hearing","Tongue movement","Facial expression","Eye movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN XII (hypoglossal, motor) distributes to the tongue muscles and controls tongue movement; it is assessed by the ability to protrude the tongue without deviation.  Why A wrong: Hearing is CN VIII.  Why C wrong: Facial expression is CN VII.  Why D wrong: Eye movement is CN III, IV, VI."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) is assessed by observing:","options":["Pupil reaction to light","The ability to protrude the tongue without deviation","Symmetry of the smile","The corneal blink reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CN XII is assessed by the ability to protrude the tongue without deviation (deviation indicates weakness).  Why A wrong: Pupil reaction tests CN III.  Why C wrong: Smile symmetry tests CN VII.  Why D wrong: Corneal blink reflex tests CN V."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A student uses the mnemonic to recall that the cranial nerves which are PURELY SENSORY are:","options":["CN III, IV, VI","CN I, II, and VIII","CN V, VII, IX, X","CN XI and XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purely sensory cranial nerves are CN I (olfactory), CN II (optic), and CN VIII (vestibulocochlear).  Why A wrong: CN III, IV, VI are purely motor.  Why C wrong: CN V, VII, IX, X are mixed nerves.  Why D wrong: CN XI and XII are motor nerves."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"According to the source, which cranial nerves are the mixed nerves?","options":["CN I, II, VIII","CN V, VII, IX, X","CN III, IV, VI","CN XI, XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mixed cranial nerves are CN V, VII, IX, and X.  Why A wrong: These are the sensory nerves.  Why C wrong: CN III, IV, VI are motor (for eye movement).  Why D wrong: CN XI and XII are motor nerves."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which cranial nerve is the largest cranial nerve?","options":["Optic nerve (CN II)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is the largest cranial nerve.  Why A/C/D wrong: The optic, vagus, and facial nerves are not identified as the largest cranial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which is the smallest cranial nerve?","options":["Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Olfactory nerve (CN I)","Vagus nerve (CN X)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The trochlear nerve (CN IV) is the smallest cranial nerve.  Why B wrong: CN V is the largest, not smallest.  Why C wrong: CN I is not identified as the smallest.  Why D wrong: The vagus is the longest, not the smallest."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The vagus nerve (CN X) is notable as the:","options":["Smallest cranial nerve","Longest and most widely distributed (mixed) cranial nerve","Only purely sensory cranial nerve","Only nerve controlling vision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vagus nerve is the longest cranial nerve and is among the mixed nerves with the widest distribution (thoracic and abdominal viscera).  Why A wrong: The trochlear (CN IV) is the smallest.  Why C wrong: It is a mixed nerve, not purely sensory.  Why D wrong: Vision is controlled by CN II."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Cranial nerves that contain motor fibers and help the brain control movement are called:","options":["Sensory nerves","Motor nerves","Autonomic nerves","Reflex nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nerves that carry motor fibers and help the brain control movement are called motor nerves.  Why A wrong: Sensory nerves carry sensory information (e.g., CN I, II, VIII).  Why C/D wrong: \"Autonomic\" and \"reflex\" are not the classification used here for cranial nerves."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Abnormal patterns of respiration in which deeper and sometimes faster breathing occurs with a period of apnea (apnea period) indicates dysfunction or trauma in which part of the brain?","options":["Cerebellum","Cerebral hemispheres / basal ganglia","Spinal cord","Peripheral nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abnormal respiration with deeper/faster breathing and periods of apnea indicates dysfunction/trauma in the cerebral hemispheres/basal ganglia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These structures are not identified as the site causing this specific abnormal respiratory pattern."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"In the assessment of respiration, a patient who has a slow heart rate, increased peristaltic movement, increased gastric secretion, and voice production reflects increased activity of which division of the autonomic nervous system?","options":["Sympathetic","Parasympathetic","Somatic","Enteric only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Slow heart rate, increased peristalsis, increased gastric secretion, and voice production are parasympathetic (vagal) effects.  Why A wrong: Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and inhibits GI secretions.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described autonomic effects."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The Babinski reflex is considered NORMAL (physiologic) in which group?","options":["Adults over 50 years","Children younger than 2 years of age","Pregnant women only","No one — it is always pathological"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Babinski reflex (dorsiflexion of the big toe and fanning of the other toes) is normal in children younger than 2 years of age, but pathological after 2 years.  Why A wrong: In adults it is abnormal/pathological.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy does not make it physiologic.  Why D wrong: It is normal in infants under 2 years."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A positive Babinski reflex (dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the other toes) in an adult most likely indicates:","options":["Normal neurological function","CNS disease or neurological impairment","Peripheral nerve damage only","Normal aging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A positive (pathological) Babinski reflex in an adult/child over 2 years indicates CNS disease or neurological impairment.  Why A wrong: It is abnormal in this age group.  Why C wrong: It reflects an upper motor neuron (CNS) lesion, not isolated peripheral nerve damage.  Why D wrong: It is not a normal aging finding."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"To elicit the Babinski reflex, the examiner strokes the:","options":["Medial aspect of the foot","Lateral aspect of the sole of the foot","Top of the foot","Back of the heel only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Babinski reflex is checked by firmly stroking the lateral aspect of the sole of the foot.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the standard stimulation sites for eliciting the Babinski response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The corneal (blink) reflex is the involuntary blinking of the eyelid in response to stimulation of the cornea. Loss of the blink reflex indicates dysfunction of which cranial nerve(s)?","options":["CN II and III","CN V (trigeminal)","CN VIII","CN XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of the blink/corneal reflex indicates dysfunction of cranial nerve V (trigeminal), which carries the corneal sensation.  Why A wrong: CN II/III are involved in vision and pupil response, not the corneal sensory reflex.  Why C wrong: CN VIII is for hearing/balance.  Why D wrong: CN XII is for tongue movement."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The gag reflex is the contraction of pharyngeal muscles elicited by touching the back of the throat. Loss of the gag reflex indicates dysfunction of cranial nerve(s):","options":["I and II","IX and X","IV and VI","VII only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The gag reflex is mediated by CN IX (glossopharyngeal) and CN X (vagus); its loss indicates dysfunction of these nerves.  Why A wrong: CN I and II are sensory (smell/vision).  Why C wrong: CN IV and VI are ocular motor nerves.  Why D wrong: CN VII controls facial expression."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which of the following is included among the deep tendon reflexes?","options":["Corneal, gag, and pupillary reflexes","Biceps, triceps, and patellar (knee) reflexes","Babinski and Brudzinski reflexes","Photophobia and nystagmus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deep tendon reflexes include biceps, triceps, patellar, and achilles reflexes (assessed with a reflex hammer).  Why A wrong: Corneal, gag, and pupillary are superficial/special reflexes.  Why C wrong: Babinski and Brudzinski are pathological/meningeal signs, not DTRs.  Why D wrong: Photophobia and nystagmus are signs, not deep tendon reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"In deep tendon reflex grading, a grade of \"0\" indicates:","options":["Normal response","No response (absent reflex)","Hyperactive with clonus","Brisk/hyperactive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the deep tendon reflex grading scale, 0 indicates no response (absent reflex).  Why A wrong: A normal response is typically graded as 2+.  Why C wrong: Hyperactive with clonus is graded as 4+.  Why D wrong: Brisker than average/hyperactive without clonus is graded 3+."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the deep tendon reflex grading scale, a \"4+\" grade represents:","options":["Absent reflex","Normal reflex","Very brisk, hyperactive reflex, often with clonus","Slightly diminished, low-normal reflex"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 4+ indicates a very brisk, hyperactive response, often associated with clonus.  Why A wrong: Absent reflex is 0.  Why B wrong: Normal is 2+.  Why D wrong: Low-normal/slightly diminished is 1+."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Brudzinski's sign, a sign of meningeal irritation, is positive when:","options":["Passive flexion of the neck causes involuntary flexion of the hips and knees","The leg cannot be fully extended when the hip is flexed to 90°","The big toe dorsiflexes on stroking the sole","The patient develops nystagmus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brudzinski's sign is the involuntary flexion of the hip and knee when the neck is passively flexed; it indicates meningeal irritation.  Why B wrong: That description is Kernig's sign.  Why C wrong: Toe dorsiflexion describes the Babinski reflex.  Why D wrong: Nystagmus is unrelated to Brudzinski's sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Kernig's sign indicating meningeal irritation is demonstrated when:","options":["Passive neck flexion produces hip/knee flexion","With the hip flexed to 90°, the leg cannot be fully extended at the knee (pain on extension)","The big toe fans out on plantar stimulation","The pupils become unequal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kernig's sign is the inability to fully extend the leg at the knee when the hip is flexed to 90° due to pain/resistance; it is a cardinal sign of meningeal irritation.  Why A wrong: Neck flexion causing hip/knee flexion is Brudzinski's sign.  Why C wrong: Plantar toe fanning is the Babinski reflex.  Why D wrong: Pupil inequality is not Kernig's sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which group of findings indicates meningeal irritation?","options":["Irritability, nuchal (neck) rigidity, nausea, vomiting, fever, chills, severe headache, and photophobia","Bradycardia and constricted pupils only","Dry mouth and tachycardia only","Flaccid limbs and absent reflexes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meningeal irritation presents with irritability, nuchal rigidity, nausea/vomiting, fever, chills, severe headache, photophobia, and may show positive Brudzinski/Kernig signs.  Why B/C wrong: These describe autonomic effects, not meningeal irritation.  Why D wrong: Flaccidity/absent reflexes describe other neurologic states, not meningeal irritation."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, mediated by adrenergic response, produces all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Increased heart rate and increased blood pressure","Dilated bronchioles and dilated coronary blood vessels","Constricted peripheral blood vessels","Increased all intestinal secretions"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Increased intestinal secretions are a parasympathetic (cholinergic) effect; the sympathetic system decreases GI secretions.  Why A wrong: Sympathetic activity increases heart rate and blood pressure.  Why B wrong: Sympathetic activity dilates bronchioles and dilates coronary vessels.  Why C wrong: Sympathetic activity constricts peripheral blood vessels."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which of the following effects is mediated by the PARASYMPATHETIC (cholinergic) division of the autonomic nervous system?","options":["Dilated pupils","Decreased heart rate (decreased BP) and constricted bronchioles","Increased perspiration (sweat glands)","Dilated coronary blood vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic (cholinergic) responses include decreased heart rate, constricted bronchioles, constricted pupils, and increased GI secretions.  Why A wrong: Pupil dilation is a sympathetic effect.  Why C wrong: Increased perspiration is a sympathetic (sudomotor) effect.  Why D wrong: Coronary vessel dilation is a sympathetic effect."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"In the assessment of sensory function, the nurse tests light touch using:","options":["A reflex hammer","A wisp of cotton","A tuning fork","A Snellen chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Light touch is assessed using cotton (a wisp of cotton).  Why A wrong: A reflex hammer tests deep tendon reflexes.  Why C wrong: A tuning fork is used to assess vibration sense.  Why D wrong: A Snellen chart assesses vision."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Which instrument is used to assess vibration sense during a sensory examination?","options":["Cotton ball","Tuning fork","Pin (sharp object)","Reflex hammer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vibration sense is assessed by placing a tuning fork over a bony prominence.  Why A wrong: Cotton tests light touch.  Why C wrong: A pin/sharp object tests pain/superficial sensation.  Why D wrong: A reflex hammer tests deep tendon reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"When assessing the level of consciousness (LOC), a fully alert and oriented client demonstrates orientation to:","options":["Time only","Person, place, and time","Pain only","Smell and taste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A client at the level of orientation responds appropriately and is oriented to person, place, and time.  Why A wrong: Orientation includes more than time alone.  Why C wrong: Response to pain reflects a much lower LOC.  Why D wrong: Smell/taste are cranial nerve functions, not LOC orientation parameters."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A patient who is in a state of unconsciousness with NO reaction to stimuli (no response even to painful stimuli) is best described as:","options":["Alert","Lethargic","Comatose","Oriented"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Coma (comatose) is the lowest level of consciousness with no reaction or no reliable indicator of neurological status (no response to stimuli).  Why A wrong: An alert patient responds normally.  Why B wrong: A lethargic patient is drowsy but arousable.  Why D wrong: An oriented patient is fully aware of person/place/time."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Bilaterally dilated and fixed pupils (no response to light) in a head-injured patient most ominously indicate:","options":["Normal pupillary status","Severe brainstem injury or anoxia (poor prognosis)","Optic neuritis only","Mild concussion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bilateral fixed, dilated pupils indicate severe brain injury such as brainstem damage/anoxia, a grave finding.  Why A wrong: It is a markedly abnormal finding, not normal.  Why C wrong: Optic neuritis affects vision, not bilateral fixed dilation.  Why D wrong: A mild concussion does not typically produce bilateral fixed dilated pupils."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A single (unilateral) fixed and dilated pupil in a head-injured patient classically indicates:","options":["Optic nerve damage on the opposite side","Increased intracranial pressure with herniation compressing the oculomotor nerve (CN III) on the same side","A normal variant","Parasympathetic overactivity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A unilateral dilated, fixed pupil indicates compression of the oculomotor nerve (CN III), typically from rising intracranial pressure/herniation, and signals direct ocular/brain herniation injury.  Why A wrong: This pattern reflects CN III (motor) compression, not contralateral optic damage.  Why C wrong: It is an abnormal, urgent finding.  Why D wrong: Parasympathetic overactivity causes constriction, not dilation."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Pinpoint (very small) fixed pupils in a comatose patient most commonly suggest:","options":["Brainstem (pons) hemorrhage or damage, or opiate/narcotic overdose","Increased intracranial pressure with herniation","Optic nerve injury","Caloric test response"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pinpoint pupils indicate pons (brainstem) hemorrhage/damage or narcotic (opiate) overdose.  Why B wrong: Herniation typically causes a dilated (not pinpoint) pupil.  Why C wrong: Optic nerve injury affects vision, not producing pinpoint fixed pupils.  Why D wrong: The caloric test assesses vestibular reflex, not pupil size."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Decorticate posturing is characterized by:","options":["Extension and outward rotation of the arms and legs","Flexion of the arms toward the core (chest), with rigid extension of the legs, indicating damage to the cerebral hemispheres","Complete flaccidity","Only neck stiffness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decorticate posturing involves flexion of the arms toward the body core (chest) with leg extension and indicates injury to the cerebral hemispheres/cortex.  Why A wrong: That describes decerebrate posturing.  Why C wrong: Complete flaccidity is flaccid posturing.  Why D wrong: Neck stiffness alone describes meningeal irritation, not decorticate posturing."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Decerebrate posturing, with rigid extension of the arms and legs, indicates damage at the level of the:","options":["Cerebral cortex only","Brainstem (midbrain/pons), a more serious injury than decorticate posturing","Spinal cord only","Peripheral nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decerebrate posturing (rigid extension of arms and legs) indicates brainstem injury and is more serious than decorticate posturing.  Why A wrong: Cortical damage produces decorticate, not decerebrate, posturing.  Why C/D wrong: Spinal cord and peripheral nerve lesions are not the level indicated by decerebrate posturing."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"Flaccid posturing, in which the client displays NO motor response in any limb, reflects:","options":["The least severe neurological injury","The most severe form of motor dysfunction (worse than decerebrate)","Normal sleep","Sympathetic activation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flaccid posturing (no motor response) is the most severe motor finding, indicating deeper/worse neurological injury than decerebrate posturing.  Why A wrong: It is the most severe, not the least.  Why C wrong: It is a pathological loss of motor response, not normal sleep.  Why D wrong: It is not a sympathetic activation response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) is used to assess the neurological condition of the client. Its total score ranges from:","options":["1 to 10","3 to 15","0 to 20","5 to 25"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GCS score ranges from a minimum of 3 to a maximum of 15 points.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the GCS scale."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The lowest possible Glasgow Coma Scale score is 3, which indicates:","options":["Fully awake and normal","Deep coma or death","Mild brain injury","Moderate brain injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A GCS of 3 (the lowest possible) indicates deep coma or death.  Why A wrong: A score of 15 indicates a fully awake, normal client.  Why C wrong: Mild brain injury corresponds to GCS 13–15.  Why D wrong: Moderate brain injury corresponds to GCS 9–12 (or 9–13)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A maximum Glasgow Coma Scale score of 15 indicates a client who is:","options":["In deep coma","Fully awake and normal","In a vegetative state","Brain dead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A GCS of 15 (the maximum) indicates a fully awake and normal client.  Why A wrong: Deep coma corresponds to the lowest score of 3.  Why C/D wrong: These severely impaired states correspond to very low GCS scores, not 15."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"According to the GCS classification, a score of 13–15 indicates:","options":["Mild brain injury","Moderate brain injury","Severe brain injury","Deep coma or death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GCS 13–15 indicates mild brain injury.  Why B wrong: Moderate brain injury is GCS 9–13 (9–12).  Why C wrong: Severe brain injury is a score below 8 (comatose).  Why D wrong: Deep coma or death is a score of 3."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A GCS score lower than 8 indicates:","options":["Mild brain injury","A fully alert client","Coma is present and the client needs intervention (e.g., airway protection)","Normal neurological status"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A GCS below 8 indicates coma is present and the client requires intervention.  Why A wrong: Mild injury is 13–15.  Why B/D wrong: A score below 8 is a critical, comatose state, not alert/normal."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the Glasgow Coma Scale, the eye-opening response of \"eyes open spontaneously\" is scored as:","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","4 points"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Spontaneous eye opening scores the maximum 4 points on the eye-opening component.  Why A wrong: 1 point = no eye opening.  Why B wrong: 2 points = eyes open to pain/pressure.  Why C wrong: 3 points = eyes open to verbal command/speech."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS eye-opening response, eyes opening only when spoken to (to verbal command/speech) scores:","options":["4 points","3 points","2 points","1 point"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye opening to verbal command/speech scores 3 points.  Why A wrong: 4 points is spontaneous opening.  Why C wrong: 2 points is opening to pain/pressure.  Why D wrong: 1 point is no eye opening."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS verbal response, a patient who is \"oriented\" (gives appropriate answers) scores:","options":["5 points","4 points","3 points","1 point"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An oriented verbal response scores the maximum 5 points.  Why B wrong: 4 points = confused conversation/inappropriate answers to direct questions.  Why C wrong: 3 points = inappropriate words (random words).  Why D wrong: 1 point = no verbal response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS verbal response component, incomprehensible sounds (moaning/groaning, no recognizable words) are scored as:","options":["5 points","4 points","2 points","1 point"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Incomprehensible sounds (incomprehensible speech) score 2 points.  Why A wrong: 5 points = oriented.  Why B wrong: 4 points = confused conversation.  Why D wrong: 1 point = no verbal response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS motor response, the best response of \"obeys commands for movement\" scores:","options":["6 points","5 points","4 points","2 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Obeying commands for movement is the best motor response and scores 6 points.  Why B wrong: 5 points = purposeful movement to painful stimulus (localizes pain).  Why C wrong: 4 points = withdrawal from pain.  Why D wrong: 2 points = abnormal extension (decerebrate) response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS motor response, an abnormal flexion (decorticate posture) response is scored as:","options":["6 points","4 points","3 points","1 point"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Abnormal flexion (decorticate posture) scores 3 points on the motor component.  Why A wrong: 6 points = obeys commands.  Why B wrong: 4 points = withdrawal from pain.  Why D wrong: 1 point = no motor response."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the GCS motor response, an abnormal extension (decerebrate) response to pain is scored as:","options":["5 points","3 points","2 points","6 points"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Extensor/abnormal extension (decerebrate) response scores 2 points.  Why A wrong: 5 points = localizes/purposeful movement.  Why B wrong: 3 points = abnormal flexion (decorticate).  Why D wrong: 6 points = obeys commands."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"While performing oral hygiene on an unconscious patient, the nurse should place the patient in which position to prevent aspiration?","options":["Supine flat position","Side-lying (lateral) position","Trendelenburg position","High Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An unconscious patient should be placed in a side-lying (lateral) position during oral care to prevent aspiration.  Why A wrong: A flat supine position increases aspiration risk.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg promotes aspiration of secretions toward the airway.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is unsafe for a patient unable to control secretions/swallowing."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"The GCS-Pupils (GCS-P) score is designed to summarize information about loss of pupil reactivity along with the GCS. It is calculated as:","options":["GCS total + Pupil Reactivity Score","GCS total − Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS)","GCS total × Pupil Reactivity Score","Pupil Reactivity Score alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GCS-P = GCS − PRS (the Pupil Reactivity Score is subtracted from the total GCS score).  Why A wrong: The PRS is subtracted, not added.  Why C wrong: The relationship is subtraction, not multiplication.  Why D wrong: The GCS-P incorporates the full GCS, not the PRS alone."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS) used to calculate the GCS-P, if BOTH pupils are unreactive to light, the PRS is:","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When both pupils are unreactive, the Pupil Reactivity Score is 2.  Why A wrong: 0 corresponds to both pupils reactive (neither pupil unreactive).  Why B wrong: 1 corresponds to one pupil unreactive.  Why D wrong: 3 is not a value on the PRS scale."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS), when only ONE pupil is unreactive to light, the score is:","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One unreactive pupil yields a PRS of 1.  Why A wrong: 0 = neither pupil unreactive (both reactive).  Why C wrong: 2 = both pupils unreactive.  Why D wrong: 3 is not a PRS value."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"On the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS), when NEITHER pupil is unreactive (both pupils react to light), the score is:","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When neither pupil is unreactive (both reactive), the PRS is 0.  Why B wrong: 1 = one pupil unreactive.  Why C wrong: 2 = both pupils unreactive.  Why D wrong: 3 is not a value on the PRS scale."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"A trauma patient is brought to the ED. On initial assessment he opens his eyes to painful stimulus, responds verbally with inappropriate words, and shows normal flexion of the left hand with unilateral pupil reactive (one pupil unreactive). Using the GCS-P approach, what is his GCS-P score? (Eye = 2, Verbal = 3, Motor = withdrawal/normal flexion = 4; total GCS = 9; one pupil unreactive PRS = 1)","options":["6","8","9","10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye opening to pain = 2, inappropriate words = 3, normal flexion/withdrawal of the hand = 4, giving total GCS = 9. With one pupil unreactive the PRS = 1, so GCS-P = 9 − 1 = 8.  Why A wrong: 6 would under-count the GCS components.  Why C wrong: 9 is the GCS before subtracting the PRS.  Why D wrong: 10 exceeds the calculated total GCS of 9."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Neurological diagnostic procedures…","question":"In the GCS-P scoring example, \"eyes open to painful stimulus,\" \"verbally responds with inappropriate words,\" and \"normal flexion of the left hand to painful stimulus\" individually score how many points respectively?","options":["4, 5, 6","2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3","3, 4, 5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eyes open to painful stimulus = 2, verbal response of inappropriate words = 3, and normal flexion (withdrawal) of the hand to pain = 4.  Why A/C/D wrong: These point combinations do not match the GCS scoring for these specific responses."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Intracranial pressure (ICP) is best defined as the pressure of which substance within the skull or brain tissues?","options":["Arterial blood","Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","Lymphatic fluid","Interstitial plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICP is the pressure of the CSF fluids inside the skull or brain tissues.  Why A wrong: Arterial blood pressure is systemic, not the definition of ICP.  Why C wrong: Lymphatic fluid is not the basis of ICP.  Why D wrong: Interstitial plasma is not what ICP measures."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Hyperosmotic agents (e.g., Mannitol) increase intravascular pressure to lower ICP by which mechanism?","options":["Drawing fluid from the interstitial spaces and from the brain cells into the blood","Increasing CSF production in the choroid plexus","Constricting cerebral arteries to reduce blood volume","Dilating cerebral veins to increase venous drainage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperosmotic agents increase intravascular pressure by drawing fluid from the interstitial spaces and from the brain cells into the blood; Mannitol is the drug of choice for increased ICP.  Why B wrong: They reduce, not increase, fluid/edema in brain tissue.  Why C wrong: Their action is osmotic, not vasoconstrictive arterial.  Why D wrong: They do not act by venous dilation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"What is the normal ICP range for an adult?","options":["5–10 mm Hg","<7 mm Hg","7–15 mm Hg or 10–20 cm H₂O","16–20 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal adult ICP is 7–15 mm Hg or 10–20 cm H₂O.  Why A wrong: 5–10 mm Hg is the normal range for children.  Why B wrong: <7 mm Hg is the normal range for infants.  Why D wrong: 16–20 mm Hg represents mild elevation, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The normal ICP for a child is:","options":["<7 mm Hg","5–10 mm Hg","7–15 mm Hg","21–30 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal ICP in children is 5–10 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: <7 mm Hg is for infants.  Why C wrong: 7–15 mm Hg is the adult value.  Why D wrong: 21–30 mm Hg is moderate elevation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The normal ICP for an infant is:","options":["<7 mm Hg","5–10 mm Hg","10–20 mm Hg",">30 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal infant ICP is <7 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 5–10 mm Hg is for children.  Why C wrong: 10–20 cm H₂O is the adult value (and 16–20 mm Hg is mild elevation).  Why D wrong: >30 mm Hg is severe elevation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"An ICP measurement of 16–20 mm Hg is classified as which degree of elevation?","options":["Normal","Mild elevation","Moderate elevation","Severe elevation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mild elevation of ICP is 16–20 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: Normal adult ICP is 7–15 mm Hg.  Why C wrong: Moderate elevation is 21–30 mm Hg.  Why D wrong: Severe elevation is >30 mm Hg."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Match the correct degree of ICP elevation: an ICP of 21–30 mm Hg indicates:","options":["Mild elevation","Moderate elevation","Severe elevation","Normal pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate elevation of ICP is 21–30 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: Mild elevation is 16–20 mm Hg.  Why C wrong: Severe elevation is >30 mm Hg.  Why D wrong: 21–30 mm Hg is well above the normal 7–15 mm Hg."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"An ICP reading greater than 30 mm Hg is classified as:","options":["Mild elevation","Moderate elevation","Severe elevation","Borderline normal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe elevation of ICP is >30 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: Mild is 16–20 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: Moderate is 21–30 mm Hg.  Why D wrong: >30 mm Hg is far above normal."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"What is the most common (gold standard) method for measuring ICP?","options":["Lumbar puncture manometry","Intraventricular catheter (lateral ventricle)","Epidural transducer","Subarachnoid bolt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common method for measuring ICP is the intraventricular catheter (lateral ventricle).  Why A wrong: Lumbar puncture is contraindicated in increased ICP and is not the standard measurement method.  Why C/D wrong: Epidural transducers and subarachnoid bolts are alternatives, not stated as the most common method."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which of the following are listed as causes of increased ICP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Head trauma","Haemorrhage and tumors","Hydrocephalus","Oedema and inflammation","Hypoglycemia alone"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed causes of increased ICP are head trauma, haemorrhage and tumors, hydrocephalus, oedema, and inflammation.  Why E wrong: Hypoglycemia is not listed as a cause of increased ICP."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A major complication of increased ICP is that it can decrease circulation to the brain or cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP). Which other effects are recognized complications? (Select all that apply)","options":["Impedes the absorption of CSF","Affects the functioning of nerve cells","Leads to brainstem compression and death","Increases CSF reabsorption excessively"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased ICP decreases circulation to the brain/CPP, impedes absorption of CSF, affects functioning of nerve cells, and leads to brainstem compression and death.  Why D wrong: Increased ICP impedes (decreases), not increases, CSF absorption."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which clinical sign is described as the most sensitive and reliable first sign of increased ICP?","options":["Cushing's triad","Altered level of consciousness (LOC)","Pupillary dilation","Decreased pulse rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Altered level of consciousness (LOC) is the most sensitive, reliable first sign of increased ICP.  Why A wrong: Cushing's triad is a late sign.  Why C wrong: Pupillary changes are signs but not the most sensitive/first.  Why D wrong: Decreased pulse is part of the later Cushing's triad."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which of the following best describes the headache associated with increased ICP, especially in infants?","options":["Sharp and unilateral","Vomiting (usually projectile type in infants)-associated headache","Relieved by sitting upright","Always absent in infants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased ICP causes headache with vomiting, which is usually projectile type in infants.  Why A wrong: The source does not describe it as sharp/unilateral.  Why C wrong: Headache of increased ICP is not relieved by positioning.  Why D wrong: It is associated with projectile vomiting in infants, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Cushing's triad, a late sign of increased ICP, classically includes which three findings?","options":["Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea","Hypertension, bradycardia, and bradypnea (irregular respirations)","Hypertension, tachycardia, and hyperthermia","Hypotension, bradycardia, and hyperventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cushing's triad includes hypertension, bradycardia, and bradypnea (irregular respiration) and is a late sign of increased ICP.  Why A/C/D wrong: These combinations contradict the classic triad of rising/widening blood pressure with slow pulse and slow/irregular breathing."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which signs of increased ICP are correctly listed? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pupil changes and abnormal respiration","Decreased pulse and respiration rate; bradycardia is the late sign of increased ICP","Late sign of increased SBP, pulse pressure and decreased pulse rate","Other late signs: decreased LOC, positive Babinski sign, decorticate and decerebrate posture, seizures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed signs — pupil/abnormal respiration changes; decreased pulse and respiration rate with bradycardia as a late sign; late increase in SBP/pulse pressure with decreased pulse rate; and late signs of decreased LOC, positive Babinski, decorticate/decerebrate posture, and seizures.  Why none wrong: Each statement is taken directly from the source signs list."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In the nursing care of a patient with increased ICP, why are opioids generally avoided?","options":["They increase seizure threshold","They cause respiratory depression, which can further increase ICP and decrease environmental stimuli","They lower blood pressure dangerously","They mask pupillary responses permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine (opioids) should be given because they cause respiratory depression that further increases ICP, and the nurse decreases environmental stimuli — i.e., the source flags respiratory depression as the key opioid concern and emphasizes monitoring respiration and reducing stimuli. The factually-correct teaching is that opioid-induced respiratory depression raises CO₂ and worsens ICP, so they are used cautiously with close monitoring.  Why A wrong: Opioids do not raise the seizure threshold beneficially here.  Why C wrong: The primary concern listed is respiratory depression/CO₂ retention, not hypotension.  Why D wrong: Pupillary masking is not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Why should shivering be prevented in a patient with increased ICP?","options":["Shivering lowers body temperature too far","Shivering can increase ICP","Shivering causes hypoglycemia","Shivering reduces CSF production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shivering must be prevented because it can increase ICP.  Why A wrong: The concern is the rise in ICP, not excessive temperature drop.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which nursing monitoring point is essential for the patient with increased ICP?","options":["Monitor respiratory condition and hypoxia, an early indicator of hypoxia/restlessness","Encourage frequent ambulation","Restrict all neurological assessment","Promote vigorous coughing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor respiratory condition and hypoxia as an early indicator (restlessness), per the nursing care list.  Why B wrong: Activity/straining is restricted in increased ICP.  Why C wrong: Neurological assessment is essential, not restricted.  Why D wrong: Coughing/straining increases ICP and is avoided."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Why is lumbar puncture contraindicated in a patient with increased ICP?","options":["It introduces infection inevitably","There is a chance of brain herniation","It decreases ICP too rapidly and safely","It cannot measure CSF pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lumbar puncture is contraindicated in increased ICP because there is a chance of brain herniation.  Why A wrong: Infection risk is not the cited contraindication.  Why C wrong: The danger is herniation from sudden pressure change, not \"safe\" reduction.  Why D wrong: It is contraindicated due to herniation risk, not measurement inability."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A nurse teaching a client with increased ICP should instruct the client to avoid which activities because they increase ICP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Straining activities such as coughing and sneezing","Valsalva maneuver","Deep slow breathing","Bending with flexion of the neck and hips"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Straining activities (coughing, sneezing), the Valsalva maneuver, and flexion of the neck and hips all increase ICP and are avoided.  Why C wrong: Controlled breathing is not listed as an ICP-raising activity to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"For a client with increased ICP placed supine or flat, the nurse should elevate the head of bed to what position, while avoiding extremes?","options":["0° (flat) only","15°–30° (do not elevate more than 30°), avoiding the Trendelenburg position","45°–60° high Fowler's","90° upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head of bed should be elevated 15°–30° (do not elevate more than 30°), and the Trendelenburg position is avoided, while preventing flexion of neck and hips.  Why A wrong: Flat/supine without elevation can raise ICP via reduced venous drainage.  Why C/D wrong: Elevation beyond 30° is not recommended per the source."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A ventriculo-peritoneal (VP) shunt diverts excess CSF from the ventricles into which body cavity?","options":["Pleural cavity","Peritoneum","Pericardial cavity","Subarachnoid space of the spine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A VP shunt diverts CSF from the ventricles into the peritoneum.  Why A/C/D wrong: The shunt drains into the peritoneal cavity, not the pleural, pericardial, or spinal subarachnoid space."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Immediately after VP shunt placement, the nurse should position the client how?","options":["High Fowler's, turning side to side freely","Supine position, turning from back to the non-operative side","Prone on the operative side","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Provide the supine position to the client and turn from back to the non-operative side to prevent rapid CSF drainage and pressure on the operative site.  Why A wrong: Free turning and high Fowler's are not specified.  Why C wrong: Lying on the operative side is avoided.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not indicated post-shunt."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"After a VP shunt, signs of increased ICP that recur most likely indicate:","options":["Adequate drainage","Shunt failure","Resolution of hydrocephalus","Normal post-operative state"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors increased ICP resulting from shunt failure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Recurring increased ICP indicates malfunction, not adequate drainage or resolution."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Regarding level of consciousness (LOC) and increased ICP, which statement is correct?","options":["Altered LOC is an early sign and decreased LOC is a late sign of increased ICP","Altered LOC is always a late sign only","LOC is unrelated to ICP changes","Decreased LOC is the earliest sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Altered LOC is an early sign of increased ICP, and decreased LOC is the late sign of increased ICP.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the early-altered / late-decreased LOC distinction stated in the note."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is the pressure that reflects:","options":["Cerebral blood flow to the brain (brain perfusion)","CSF production rate","Spinal cord perfusion only","Venous sinus pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CPP is defined as the pressure reflecting cerebral blood flow to the brain (brain perfusion).  Why B/C/D wrong: CPP reflects cerebral arterial perfusion, not CSF production, spinal perfusion, or venous sinus pressure."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which formula correctly expresses Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP)?","options":["CPP = ICP − MAP","CPP = MAP − ICP","CPP = MAP + ICP","CPP = SBP − DBP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CPP = MAP − ICP (Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure).  Why A wrong: The terms are reversed.  Why C wrong: ICP is subtracted, not added.  Why D wrong: SBP − DBP is pulse pressure, not CPP."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The normal value of Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is approximately:","options":["10–20 mm Hg","30–50 mm Hg","60 to the 80 mm Hg","90–110 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CPP is 60 to the 80 mm Hg.  Why A/B/D wrong: These fall outside the stated normal CPP range."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Road traffic accidents (RTA) are described as the most common cause of which condition?","options":["Head injury","Spinal cord injury only","Hydrocephalus","Brain tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RTA or road traffic accidents are the most common cause of head injury.  Why B wrong: Although RTA also causes spinal cord injury, this statement specifically refers to head injury (and SCI's most common cause is listed as accidents elsewhere).  Why C/D wrong: Hydrocephalus and tumors are not caused primarily by RTA."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which is the most common cause of secondary brain injury following head injury?","options":["Cerebral bleeding","Hematomas","Haemorrhage","Infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Haemorrhage is the most common cause of secondary brain injury.  Why A wrong: Cerebral bleeding is an immediate complication listed separately.  Why B wrong: Hematomas are listed but haemorrhage is named the most common secondary cause.  Why D wrong: Infection is a complication but not the named most common secondary cause."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Immediate complications of head injury include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cerebral bleeding","Hematomas","Uncontrolled increased ICP","Seizures","Hydrocephalus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate complications include cerebral bleeding, hematomas, uncontrolled increased ICP, infections, and seizures.  Why E wrong: Hydrocephalus is a later/fracture-related complication, not listed among the immediate complications."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A fracture of the skull that meningitis due to infection and diabetes insipidus is a complication of:","options":["Cerebral bleeding","Skull fracture","Concussion","Spinal shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fracture of skull complications include meningitis due to infection and diabetes insipidus.  Why A wrong: Cerebral bleeding is a separate immediate complication.  Why C wrong: Concussion is a closed injury type, not the source of these complications.  Why D wrong: Spinal shock is unrelated to skull fracture complications."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which type of brain hemorrhage involves bleeding or rapidly forming hematoma between the dura mater and the skull, most often from rupture of the middle meningeal artery?","options":["Epidural haemorrhage (EDH)","Subdural haemorrhage (SDH)","Subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH)","Intracerebral haemorrhage (ICH)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural haemorrhage (EDH) is bleeding or rapidly forming hematoma between dura mater and skull, may result from rupture of the middle meningeal artery; it is the most severe type and a medical emergency.  Why B wrong: SDH is between dura and arachnoid, usually venous (bridging veins).  Why C wrong: SAH is in the subarachnoid space from trauma/cerebral aneurysm.  Why D wrong: ICH is blood collection within brain parenchyma."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Subdural haemorrhage (SDH) is bleeding or slowly forming hematoma between which structures, often from rupture of bridging veins?","options":["Skull and dura mater","Dura mater and arachnoid mater","Arachnoid and pia mater","Within the brain ventricles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDH is bleeding/slowly forming hematoma between the dura mater and arachnoid mater, may result from rupture of bridging veins.  Why A wrong: Bleeding between skull and dura describes epidural.  Why C wrong: SAH involves the subarachnoid (arachnoid–pia) space.  Why D wrong: Intraventricular haemorrhage (IVH) is bleeding within ventricles."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Bleeding into the subarachnoid space, often due to trauma or a cerebral aneurysm, is termed:","options":["Epidural haemorrhage (EDH)","Subdural haemorrhage (SDH)","Subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH)","Intraventricular haemorrhage (IVH)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: SAH is bleeding into the subarachnoid space due to trauma and cerebral aneurysm.  Why A/B wrong: EDH and SDH involve the epidural and subdural spaces respectively.  Why D wrong: IVH is bleeding within the ventricles."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Match the haemorrhage type: bleeding within the ventricles is called __________ and collection of blood within the brain parenchyma is called __________.","options":["IVH; ICH","ICH; IVH","SAH; EDH","EDH; SDH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intraventricular haemorrhage (IVH) is bleeding between/within ventricles, and intracerebral haemorrhage (ICH) is collection of blood within brain parenchyma.  Why B wrong: The terms are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other haemorrhage types."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In an epidural hematoma, the blood collects in which location relative to the brain coverings?","options":["Between the skull and the dura mater","Between the dura and the arachnoid mater","In the subarachnoid space","Within the cerebral cortex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural hematoma is located between the skull and the dura mater (rapidly expanding with arterial blood).  Why B wrong: That location describes subdural hematoma.  Why C/D wrong: These describe subarachnoid and intracerebral collections."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A subdural hematoma is located between which structures?","options":["Skull and dura mater","Dura and arachnoid mater","Pia and arachnoid","Skull and scalp"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Subdural hematoma is located between the dura and the arachnoid mater (slowly expanding with venous blood; the dura is attached to the skull, so a crescent/falx-tentorium pattern forms).  Why A wrong: That is the epidural location.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated subdural location."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which vessel is most commonly involved in an epidural hematoma?","options":["Bridging veins","Temporoparietal middle meningeal artery","Superior sagittal sinus","Anterior cerebral artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural hematoma most commonly involves the temporoparietal middle meningeal artery (other sites: frontal anterior ethmoidal artery; occipital transverse or sigmoid sinuses; vertex superior sagittal sinus).  Why A wrong: Bridging veins (draining brain to dural sinuses) are involved in subdural hematoma.  Why C wrong: Superior sagittal sinus involvement is the vertex variant, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Anterior cerebral artery is not the listed vessel."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Bleeding from which vessels characterizes a subdural hematoma?","options":["Middle meningeal artery","Bridging veins (drain brain to dural sinuses)","Anterior ethmoidal artery","Transverse sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Subdural hematoma involves the bridging veins, which drain the brain to the dural sinuses.  Why A/C/D wrong: These arterial/sinus sources are associated with epidural hematoma."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which symptom presentation is classically associated with an epidural hematoma?","options":["Lucid interval followed by unconsciousness","Gradually increasing headache and confusion over days","No change in consciousness","Sudden permanent paralysis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural hematoma symptoms are a lucid interval followed by unconsciousness.  Why B wrong: Gradually increasing headache and confusion is characteristic of subdural hematoma.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the classic epidural picture."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"On CT imaging, an epidural hematoma classically appears as:","options":["Crescent or banana shaped, crossing suture lines","Biconvex lens or half-lemon shaped, limited by suture lines","Diffuse parenchymal hemorrhage","Intraventricular blood only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural hematoma CT appearance is like a biconvex lens or half-lemon, limited by suture lines.  Why A wrong: Crescent/banana shape crossing suture lines describes a subdural hematoma.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other haemorrhage patterns."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A subdural hematoma on CT is best described as:","options":["Biconvex lens shaped, limited by suture lines","Crescent or banana shaped, crosses suture lines","Star-shaped midline lesion","Ring-enhancing lesion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Subdural hematoma CT appearance is crescent or banana shaped and crosses suture lines.  Why A wrong: Biconvex/limited by sutures describes epidural.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described subdural appearance."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The \"lucid interval\" is defined as:","options":["The period between two seizures","The period of time between regaining consciousness after a short period of unconsciousness, usually seen in epidural haemorrhage","A permanent coma after injury","The interval of normal ICP readings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lucid interval (LI) is the period between regaining consciousness after a short period of unconsciousness, usually seen in epidural haemorrhage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the defined lucid interval."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes open from closed head injury?","options":["Open injury: scalp lacerations, fracture in the skull (linear), interruption of the dura mater","Open injury has intact dura mater","Closed injury always tears the dura","Open and closed injuries are identical"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Open injury involves scalp lacerations, fracture in the skull (linear), and interruption of the dura mater.  Why B wrong: Open injury features interruption (not intact) of the dura.  Why C wrong: Closed injuries (concussion/contusion) do not breach the dura.  Why D wrong: They are distinct."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A concussion is best defined as:","options":["A bruising injury of brain tissue with loss of consciousness and neurological deficits","A jarring or disrupting of the brain within the skull with NO loss of consciousness","Bleeding into the subarachnoid space","A linear skull fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Concussion is a jarring or disrupting of the brain within the skull with no loss of consciousness.  Why A wrong: That describes contusion (with loss of consciousness and neurological deficits).  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated injury types."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A contusion is correctly described as:","options":["A jarring of the brain without loss of consciousness","A bruising type of injury of brain tissue with loss of consciousness and neurological deficits","Bleeding between skull and dura","A scalp laceration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contusion is a bruising type injury of brain tissue with loss of consciousness and neurological deficits.  Why A wrong: No loss of consciousness describes concussion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not contusion."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The \"Halo ring sign\" (ring sign) is suggestive of which finding?","options":["Increased ICP","CSF leakage — CSF drainage from ear and nose (basilar skull fracture)","Brain death","Spinal shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Halo ring sign indicates CSF leakage — CSF drainage from ear and nose, associated with basilar skull fracture.  Why A/C/D wrong: The halo sign specifically indicates CSF leak, not ICP, brain death, or spinal shock."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"During neurological assessment of a head-injured client, how often should the GCS initially be assessed, then later?","options":["Every 4 hours initially, then daily","GCS initially every 15 minutes, then every hour and later every 4 hours till stabilization","Once on admission only","Every 30 minutes throughout the entire stay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initiate neurological assessment including GCS initially every 15 minutes, then every hour and later every 4 hours till stabilization.  Why A/C/D wrong: These frequencies do not match the prescribed escalating schedule."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"When elevating the head of a head-injured client, the head should be kept at not more than 30° for what purpose?","options":["To increase intracranial blood flow","To reduce venous pressure within the cranial cavity","To promote coughing","To increase CO₂ levels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the head elevated but not more than 30° to reduce venous pressure within the cranial cavity.  Why A wrong: The goal is to facilitate venous drainage, not increase intracranial blood flow.  Why C/D wrong: These would worsen ICP."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"When transporting a client with suspected spinal injury who must be rolled, which technique is required?","options":["Pull the client by the limbs","Log roll technique to maintain spinal alignment","Free movement of the neck","Single-person lift by the head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a spinal injury is rolled out, keep the client in supine or flat position and use the log roll technique when moving or turning the client to prevent further spinal cord damage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These risk further damage to the spinal cord by disrupting alignment."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A nurse should teach the head-injured client to avoid which actions that increase ICP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Valsalva maneuver","Lifting","Sneezing and coughing","Neck flexion and extension","Straining at stool (isometric exercises)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Teach to avoid the Valsalva maneuver, lifting, sneezing, coughing, neck flexion and extension, and straining at stool (isometric exercises), all of which increase ICP.  Why none wrong: All five are listed as activities to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Why must lumbar puncture be avoided when basilar skull fracture is suspected?","options":["It will further increase ICP and risk herniation","It cannot reach the CSF","It causes immediate paralysis","It is too painful"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If a basilar skull fracture is suspected, then suction through the nose is contraindicated because it will further increase ICP, increasing risk of herniation.  Why B/C/D wrong: The cited contraindication is the rise in ICP/herniation risk, not inability to access CSF, paralysis, or pain."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"To confirm CSF leakage following a suspected basilar skull fracture, which bedside tests/signs are used? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reservoir sign: rapid re-accumulation of discharge after cleaning of the ear","Handkerchief test: CSF leak source on cleaning of gauze","Glucose test: positive for glucose (CSF contains glucose)","Halo sign: a ring of blood with a clear ring of CSF around it, appearing like an absorbent surface (gauze/paper/bed sheets) forming a double ring pattern"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: All listed signs confirm CSF leakage — the reservoir sign (rapid re-accumulation after cleaning the ear), handkerchief test, glucose test (CSF tests positive for glucose), and the halo sign (double ring pattern on an absorbent surface).  Why none wrong: Each is a stated confirmatory test for CSF leak following basilar skull fracture."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which laboratory marker is described as the most reliable and confirmative test for CSF because it is available only in CSF?","options":["Glucose","Beta-2 transferrin","Albumin","Sodium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-2 transferrin is the most reliable and confirmative because beta-2 transferrin is available only in CSF.  Why A wrong: Glucose is supportive but present in blood too.  Why C/D wrong: Albumin and sodium are not the specific CSF marker described."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Brain death is best defined as:","options":["A reversible loss of consciousness","The permanent, irreversible, and complete loss of brain function, which may include cessation of involuntary activity necessary to sustain life","A state identical to deep coma with recoverable brainstem reflexes","Temporary cessation of circulation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death is the permanent, irreversible, and complete loss of brain function which may include cessation of involuntary activity necessary to sustain life.  Why A wrong: Brain death is irreversible, not reversible.  Why C wrong: In brain death brainstem reflexes are permanently absent.  Why D wrong: It involves irreversible cessation of circulatory and pulmonary functions and the entire brain including loss of brainstem reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Brain death must be legally declared by which person?","options":["A nurse","A medical officer (doctor)","The family","A paramedic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death is legally declared by a medical officer (doctor).  Why A/C/D wrong: Legal declaration of brain death is the physician's responsibility, not the nurse, family, or paramedic."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"As per AAN, the time of death in brain death is when PCO₂ reaches what target during the apnea test?","options":["<20 mm Hg","The target value during the apnea test","35–45 mm Hg",">100 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Time of death, as per AAN, is when PCO₂ reaches the target value during the apnea test.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source defines time of death by PCO₂ reaching the apnea-test target value, not these specific figures."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Confirmatory findings for brain death include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Presence of irreversible coma with all brainstem reflexes absent","No pupillary reflex; pupils fixed and unreactive to light","Corneal, vestibulocochlear, gag, and cough reflexes absent","No motor response to cranial nerve distribution","No respiratory movement after patient disconnected from ventilator, allowing CO₂ to rise above the threshold for PCO₂ >50 mm Hg"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmatory tests for brain death include irreversible coma with all brainstem reflexes absent; no pupillary reflex with fixed unreactive pupils; absent corneal, vestibulocochlear, gag, and cough reflexes; no motor response to cranial nerve distribution; and no respiratory movement after disconnection from the ventilator with CO₂ rising above the threshold pCO₂ >50 mm Hg.  Why none wrong: Each is a listed confirmatory finding."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which note about reflexes in brain death is correct?","options":["All spinal reflexes are always absent","Sometimes spinal reflexes can be elicited in brain dead patients","Brainstem reflexes return after death","Pupils become reactive in brain death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that sometimes spinal reflexes can be elicited in brain dead patients.  Why A wrong: Spinal (not brainstem) reflexes may persist.  Why C/D wrong: Brainstem reflexes are permanently absent and pupils are fixed in brain death."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Spinal cord injury (SCI) is defined as:","options":["A degenerative disease of peripheral nerves","A trauma to the spinal cord that can cause partial or complete disruption of the nerve tracts and neurons","Inflammation of the meninges","A vascular occlusion of cerebral arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SCI is a trauma to the spinal cord that can cause partial or complete disruption of the nerve tracts and neurons.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other neurological conditions, not the defined traumatic SCI."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which are recognized types of spinal cord injury by mechanism? (Select all that apply)","options":["Contusion","Laceration","Compression of the cord","Vasospasm of cerebral arteries"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The types of injury may be contusion, laceration, or compression of the cord.  Why D wrong: Cerebral vasospasm is unrelated to spinal cord injury mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"SCI can cause loss of motor function, sensation, reflex activity, and bowel and bladder control. The most common cause of SCI includes which factors? (Select all that apply)","options":["Road traffic accidents (RTA)","Falls","Sports injury and intentionally human-made accidents (man-made accidents)","Genetic mutation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Most common causes of SCI are road traffic accidents (RTA), falls, sports injury, and intentionally human-made accidents.  Why D wrong: Genetic mutation is not listed as a cause of traumatic SCI."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which are complications of spinal cord injury? (Select all that apply)","options":["Respiratory failure","Autonomic dysreflexia","Spinal shock","Further cord damage","Spontaneous cure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of SCI include respiratory failure, autonomic dysreflexia, spinal shock, and further cord damage.  Why E wrong: Spontaneous cure is not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Early signs and symptoms of spinal cord injury include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Paresthesia: loss of sensation below the level of injury","Pain: depend on location, it is cutting, burning, and radiating type pain","Inability to move body below the level of injury","Full return of motor function within minutes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: SCI signs and symptoms include paresthesia (loss of sensation below the level of injury), pain (cutting, burning, radiating depending on location), and inability to move the body below the level of injury.  Why D wrong: SCI causes loss of function, not immediate full recovery."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"An early sign of spinal cord injury is described as the sudden loss of reflexes below the level of injury. An injury to the sacral segment of the cord can cause which sexual/autonomic effect in men?","options":["Penile erection, ejaculation, and micturition reflexes","Permanent hypertension only","No autonomic effect","Improved bladder control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If injury is limited to the upper sacral segments of the spinal cord, then penile erection, ejaculation, and micturition reflexes are not affected/are notaffected — i.e., these sacral reflex functions are tied to the sacral cord (the early sign also includes flaccid paralysis below the level of injury and inability to perceive pain).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the autonomic/reflex effects described for sacral involvement."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A late sign of spinal cord injury, also known as spastic paralysis, is characterized by which findings? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reflex hyper-excitability — muscles below the injury become spastic and hyperreflexic","Diminished reflex excitability","Permanently flaccid muscles below injury","Normal reflexes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Late signs (spastic paralysis) include reflex hyper-excitability with muscles below the injury becoming spastic and hyperreflexic, and diminished reflex excitability as the cord injury evolves.  Why C wrong: Flaccidity is the early (spinal shock) phase; the late phase is spastic.  Why D wrong: Reflexes are abnormal (hyper-excitable), not normal."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In total cord damage, which statement is correct?","options":["Only lower motor neurons are destroyed","Both upper and lower motor neurons are destroyed; clinical features depend on location of injury; and most of the loss of function is usually permanent","Function fully recovers within hours","Only sensory function is lost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In total cord damage, losses of upper and lower motor neurons are destroyed, clinical features depend on the location of injury, and most loss of function at the time of injury is usually permanent.  Why A wrong: Both upper and lower motor neurons are destroyed.  Why C wrong: The loss is usually permanent.  Why D wrong: Both motor and sensory functions are affected."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In an injury to the lumbar region of the spinal cord, which features are seen? (Select all that apply)","options":["Paralysis of lower extremities extends to pelvis","Spastic bladder (overactive bladder)","Loss of bladder and sphincter control","Paralysis extends to neck-down"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Injury in the lumbar region causes paralysis of lower extremities extending to pelvis, spastic (overactive) bladder, and loss of bladder and sphincter control.  Why D wrong: Neck-down paralysis occurs with cervical, not lumbar, injury."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Injury to the thoracic region of the spinal cord is characterized by which features? (Select all that apply)","options":["Same signs as the lumbar region","Paralysis extends to the trunk below the level of the diaphragm","Autonomic dysreflexia","Loss of cranial nerve function"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Thoracic region injury has the same signs as the lumbar region, with paralysis extending to the trunk below the level of the diaphragm, and autonomic dysreflexia.  Why D wrong: Cranial nerve function loss is not a feature of thoracic SCI."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Injury in the sacral region of the spinal cord causes which findings? (Select all that apply)","options":["Paraplegia: paralysis of lower extremities","Loss of bowel and bladder function with impairment of sphincter control","In males, erection is retained but unable to ejaculate due to sympathetic nerve damage (injury above S2)","Injury between S2:S4 — unable to erection and ejaculation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sacral injury causes paraplegia (paralysis of lower extremities), loss of bowel and bladder function with impaired sphincter control, retained erection but inability to ejaculate (injury above S2, due to sympathetic nerve damage), and inability to achieve erection and ejaculation when injury is between S2–S4.  Why none wrong: Each is a listed sacral-region feature."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"A cervical region spinal cord injury produces which findings? (Select all that apply)","options":["Same signs as lumbar and thoracic regions, with paralysis extending to neck-down","Quadriplegia: paralysis involving all four extremities due to injury between C1–C8","Paraplegia: paralysis of lower extremities due to T1–L4","If injury is above the C4 level, then an absence of independent respiration or respiration will be absent due to phrenic nerve damage, and quadriplegia is also present"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical injury shows the same signs as lumbar/thoracic with paralysis extending neck-down; quadriplegia from injury between C1–C8; and if injury is above C4, absence/loss of independent respiration occurs due to phrenic nerve damage with quadriplegia. If injury is at C5–C8, shoulder movement is sluggish.  Why C wrong: Paraplegia from T1–L4 describes thoracolumbar, not cervical, injury."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In a cervical cord injury at the C5–C8 level, which finding is expected?","options":["Complete absence of respiration","Shoulder movement is sluggish","Normal four-limb strength","Loss of cranial nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If injury is at C5–C8, then shoulder movement is sluggish.  Why A wrong: Absent respiration occurs with injury above C4 (phrenic nerve).  Why C wrong: Cervical injury causes quadriplegia, not normal strength.  Why D wrong: Cranial nerve loss is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In partial cord damage, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Either upper or lower motor neuron, or both, may be damaged","Clinical features depend on the location of injury and the type of neuron involved","Upper motor neuron injury causes spasticity, uncontrolled, tightness, stiffness, hypertonia","Lower motor neuron injury causes atrophy and flaccid muscles of involved muscles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In partial cord damage, either upper or lower motor neuron or both may be damaged; clinical features depend on location and type of neuron; upper motor neuron injury causes spasticity, uncontrolled movement, tightness, stiffness, and hypertonia; lower motor neuron injury causes atrophy and flaccid muscles.  Why none wrong: All are listed under partial cord damage."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Autonomic dysreflexia (autonomic dysreflexia) typically occurs with a spinal cord injury at which level?","options":["At or above T6 level","Below L4 only","Cervical C1 only","Sacral region only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cause of autonomic dysreflexia is injury at or above the T6 level.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is specifically associated with injuries at or above T6, not below L4, only C1, or only the sacral region."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Why is autonomic dysreflexia considered a medical emergency?","options":["It causes hypoglycemia","It is a neurogenic emergency and should be treated immediately to prevent a hypertensive stroke","It causes flaccid paralysis only","It always resolves spontaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autonomic dysreflexia is a neurogenic emergency and should be treated immediately to prevent a hypertensive stroke.  Why A/C wrong: The emergency is severe hypertension, not hypoglycemia or paralysis.  Why D wrong: It requires immediate treatment, not spontaneous resolution."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which is the most severe and common triggering stimulus for autonomic dysreflexia?","options":["Bladder distension","Headache","Nasal congestion","Diaphoresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most severe and common triggering stimulus for autonomic dysreflexia is bladder distension.  Why B/C/D wrong: Headache, nasal congestion, and diaphoresis are signs/symptoms, not the most common trigger."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of autonomic dysreflexia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Distended bowel and bladder (cardinal sign)","Bradycardia due to vagus nerve stimulation","Headache and nasal congestion","Diaphoresis due to sympathetic stimulation","Tachycardia with hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms include distended bowel and bladder (cardinal sign), bradycardia (due to vagus nerve increased pressure on vagus nerve stimulating the parasympathetic nervous system causing vasoconstriction and hypertension/malignant type), headache and nasal congestion, and diaphoresis due to sympathetic stimulation.  Why E wrong: Autonomic dysreflexia causes hypertension (malignant) with bradycardia, not hypotension with tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which spinal cord levels are correctly matched with the resulting paralysis type per the figure?","options":["C4 = tetraplegia, C6 = tetraplegia, T6 = paraplegia, L1 = paraplegia","C4 = paraplegia, L1 = tetraplegia","All cervical levels = paraplegia","T6 = quadriplegia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the figure, C4 (tetraplegia), C6 (tetraplegia), T6 (paraplegia), and L1 (paraplegia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These misassign the cervical levels (which cause tetraplegia/quadriplegia) and thoracic/lumbar levels (which cause paraplegia)."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"What is the full form of the ASIA / AIS scale used in spinal cord injury assessment?","options":["Acute Spinal Injury Algorithm","American Spinal Injury Association Impairment Scale","Advanced Spinal Injury Assessment","Annual Spinal Impairment Audit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASIA / AIS scale full form is American Spinal Injury Association Impairment Scale, a standardized neurological examination scale used by the rehabilitation team to assess the sensory and motor levels affected by spinal cord injury.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"On the ASIA Impairment Scale, Grade A is defined as:","options":["No motor or sensory function below the lesion level — Complete injury","Sensory but not motor function preserved — Incomplete","Normal function","Muscle grade 3 or greater"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASIA Grade A = no motor or sensory function below the lesion level; injury type is Complete.  Why B wrong: Sensory-preserved/motor-absent describes Grade B (Incomplete).  Why C wrong: Normal function is Grade E.  Why D wrong: Muscle grade 3 or greater describes Grade D."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"On the ASIA scale, which grade is defined as \"sensory but not motor function preserved below the lesion level\" and classified as Incomplete?","options":["Grade A","Grade B","Grade D","Grade E"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade B = sensory but not motor function preserved below the lesion level — Incomplete.  Why A wrong: Grade A is complete (no motor or sensory).  Why C wrong: Grade D = muscle grade 3 or greater below injury level — Incomplete.  Why D wrong: Grade E = normal function."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Match the ASIA grade: muscle grade less than 3 out of 5 below the lesion level corresponds to which grade and injury type?","options":["Grade C — Incomplete","Grade D — Incomplete","Grade A — Complete","Grade E — None"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASIA Grade C = muscle grade less than 3 out of 5 below lesion level — Incomplete.  Why B wrong: Grade D = muscle grade 3 or greater below injury level — Incomplete.  Why C wrong: Grade A is complete with no motor/sensory function.  Why D wrong: Grade E is normal function (none/no impairment)."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"ASIA Grade D and Grade E are correctly described as:","options":["Grade D = muscle grade 3 or greater below injury level (Incomplete); Grade E = normal function (None)","Grade D = complete injury; Grade E = no function","Grade D = normal; Grade E = complete","Both are complete injuries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASIA Grade D = muscle grade 3 or greater below the injury level (Incomplete); Grade E = normal function (injury type: None).  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the definitions; D is incomplete and E is normal."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Why is the early critical phase of nursing intervention in spinal cord injury emergency so important?","options":["Improper movement can cause further damage and neurological functions","The patient is fully recovered","Movement aids healing","It prevents bladder distension only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It is very critical phase because improper movement can cause further damage and neurological functions; therefore the patient must be immobilized until emergency services arrive.  Why B/C wrong: Movement risks further cord damage; the patient is not recovered.  Why D wrong: Bladder distension is a later concern, not the early-phase rationale."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In emergency management of suspected spinal cord injury, the nurse should:","options":["Immobilize the patient until emergency services arrive, avoid head flexion, extension, and rotation, and maintain alignment in extended or supine or flat position","Allow free movement of the spine","Place the patient in Trendelenburg","Encourage the patient to sit up"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At the site of injury, immobilize the patient until emergency services arrive; avoid head flexion, extension, and rotation; assess respiratory status; and maintain alignment with the body in extended or supine or flat position.  Why B/C/D wrong: These risk further spinal cord damage and contradict immobilization principles."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"When a client with a cervical region injury must be immobilized, which devices/techniques are used?","options":["Skeletal or Halo traction with log roll technique when moving or turning the client","Free neck mobilization","Soft pillow under the neck only","Prone positioning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If injury is in the cervical region, then in the emergency department the patient should be placed immediately in skeletal or Halo traction to immobilize the cervical spine, and maintain spinal alignment at all times using the log roll technique when moving or turning the client.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not adequately immobilize the cervical spine and risk further injury."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"During hospitalization of a spinal cord injury client, why does the nurse closely assess ABG level and respiratory status?","options":["Because paralysis of the intercostal muscles and diaphragm occurs with T4 injury","Because all SCI patients have pneumonia","To monitor for hypoglycemia","To assess bowel sounds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assess ABG level and respiratory status because paralysis of the intercostal muscles and diaphragm occurs with T4 injury.  Why B/C/D wrong: ABG/respiratory assessment specifically targets ventilation impaired by muscle paralysis, not these other concerns."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which nursing interventions are appropriate during hospitalization of a spinal cord injury client? (Select all that apply)","options":["Encourage deep breathing and use of incentive spirometry; assess for haemorrhage and bleeding at injury site","Assess for shock such as hypotension, tachycardia, and weak thready pulse; monitor for autonomic dysreflexia and neurogenic shock","Immobilize the client to promote healing and prevent further injury; maintain functional alignment and reposition every 2 hours","Assess abdomen for distension, hemorrhage, bowel sound and paralytic ileus; provide diet with roughage or high fiber diet and bowel stimulants","Encourage sufficient fluid intake 3000:4000 ml per day; initiate passive and active exercises"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed hospitalization interventions — encourage deep breathing/incentive spirometry and assess for haemorrhage; assess for shock (hypotension, tachycardia, weak thready pulse) and monitor for autonomic dysreflexia/neurogenic shock; immobilize to promote healing and prevent further injury with repositioning every 2 hours; assess abdomen for distension/ileus and provide high-fiber diet with bowel stimulants; encourage 3000–4000 ml fluids daily and initiate passive and active exercises.  Why none wrong: Each is an explicit nursing intervention from the source."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The Crede maneuver, used in a spinal cord injury client, involves:","options":["Manual expression of urine from the bladder with moderate external pressure, downward to backward from umbilicus over the suprapubic area","Insertion of a permanent suprapubic catheter","Manual disimpaction of stool","Chest physiotherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Crede maneuver is manual expression of urine from the bladder with moderate external pressure, downward to backward from umbilicus (over the suprapubic area) and is used for bladder emptying.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other procedures, not the Crede maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which nursing interventions are recommended for managing autonomic dysreflexia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Place in high Fowler's position; loosen the tight clothing on the client","Ensure patency of urinary drainage system; assess for fecal impaction","Administer prescribed anti-hypertensive — sodium nitroprusside is the drug of choice","Assess vital parameters, especially blood pressure, every 15 minutes","Lay the patient flat and restrict all monitoring"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Management of autonomic dysreflexia includes placing the client in high Fowler's position, loosening tight clothing, ensuring patency of the urinary drainage system, assessing for fecal impaction, administering prescribed anti-hypertensive (sodium nitroprusside is the drug of choice), and assessing vital parameters especially blood pressure every 15 minutes.  Why E wrong: Laying the patient flat would worsen hypertension; high Fowler's is correct, and monitoring must be intensified, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The drug of choice for the acute hypertensive crisis of autonomic dysreflexia is:","options":["Sodium nitroprusside","Mannitol","Morphine","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer prescribed anti-hypertensive — sodium nitroprusside is the choice of drug for autonomic dysreflexia.  Why B wrong: Mannitol is used for increased ICP.  Why C wrong: Morphine is not the anti-hypertensive of choice here.  Why D wrong: Dopamine raises blood pressure and would worsen the crisis."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Spinal shock is best described as:","options":["Complete but temporary loss of motor, sensory, reflexes, and autonomic function that occurs immediately after injury","A permanent loss of all function","Massive vasodilation above the level of injury","A cardiac arrhythmia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spinal shock is a complete but temporary loss of motor, sensory, reflexes, and autonomic function that occurs immediately after injury.  Why B wrong: It is temporary, not permanent.  Why C wrong: Massive vasodilation describes neurogenic shock.  Why D wrong: It is a neurological, not primarily a cardiac, phenomenon."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The resuscitation phase of spinal shock usually lasts approximately:","options":["48 hours","15 minutes","7 days","6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spinal shock, the resuscitation phase usually lasts 48 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 48-hour resuscitation phase."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of spinal shock? (Select all that apply)","options":["Flaccid paralysis","Loss of reflex activity below the level of injury","Bradycardia and hypotension","Paralytic ileus","Spastic hyperreflexia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Spinal shock signs/symptoms include flaccid paralysis, loss of reflex activity below the level of injury, bradycardia and hypotension, and paralytic ileus.  Why E wrong: Spastic hyperreflexia is a late SCI sign, not a feature of acute spinal shock (which is flaccid)."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Neurogenic shock most commonly occurs in clients with spinal cord injuries at which level?","options":["Above T6 level","Below L4","Sacral only","Lumbar only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic shock occurs most commonly in clients with injuries above T6 level, resulting in massive vasodilation leading to pooling of blood in blood vessels, tissue hypoperfusion, and impaired cellular metabolism.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is associated with injuries above T6, not below L4 or limited to the sacral/lumbar regions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The pathophysiology of neurogenic shock involves which sequence?","options":["Massive vasodilation leading to pooling of blood in blood vessels, tissue hypoperfusion, and impaired cellular metabolism","Vasoconstriction with increased afterload","Fluid overload with pulmonary edema","Increased cardiac output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic shock causes massive vasodilation leading to pooling of blood in vessels, tissue hypoperfusion, and impaired cellular metabolism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the vasodilatory, hypoperfusing mechanism of neurogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"The signs and symptoms of neurogenic shock include:","options":["Hypotension and bradycardia","Hypertension and tachycardia","Hypertension and bradycardia","Hypotension and tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of neurogenic shock are hypotension and bradycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Neurogenic shock characteristically pairs hypotension with bradycardia, not tachycardia or hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"In the comparison of spinal shock vs neurogenic shock, the bulbocavernous reflex is:","options":["Absent in spinal shock and present in neurogenic shock","Present in spinal shock and absent in neurogenic shock","Absent in both","Present in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the comparison table, the bulbocavernous reflex is absent in spinal shock and present in neurogenic shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the reflex differences listed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the motor and mechanism features of spinal shock versus neurogenic shock?","options":["Spinal shock: flaccid paralysis with peripheral neurons temporarily unresponsive to brain stimuli; Neurogenic shock: variable motor with disruption of autonomic pathway, then loss of sympathetic tone and vasodilation","Both have spastic paralysis from the start","Spinal shock is vasodilation; neurogenic shock is flaccid paralysis only","Neither affects motor function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spinal shock, motor shows flaccid paralysis and the mechanism is peripheral neurons becoming temporarily unresponsive to brain stimuli; in neurogenic shock, motor is variable and the mechanism is disruption of the autonomic pathway then loss of sympathetic tone and vasodilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misrepresent the distinct mechanisms and motor findings in the comparison table."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Increased ICP (normal/elevated values, causes, signs, nursing care), CPP…","question":"Regarding spinal cord injury lesion patterns, which definitions are correct?","options":["Ipsilateral: loss of function on the same injured side of the body; Contralateral: loss of function on the opposite side of the body","Ipsilateral: opposite side; Contralateral: same side","Both refer to bilateral loss","Neither term relates to side"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ipsilateral means loss of function on the same injured side of the body, and contralateral means loss of function on the opposite side of the body.  Why B wrong: The definitions are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The terms specifically denote same vs opposite side."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A cerebrovascular accident (CVA) is best defined as:","options":["A gradual degeneration of cerebral neurons over many years","A sudden disruption or infarct of brain cells or tissues caused by reduction in oxygen supply due to a blockage in the blood vessels of the brain","An inflammatory process of the meninges","An autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines CVA as a suddenly occurring disruption or infarct of brain cells/tissues caused by reduction in oxygen supply due to a blockage in the brain's blood vessels.  Why A wrong: That describes a chronic degenerative disorder (e.g., Alzheimer's), not an acute CVA.  Why C wrong: Inflammation of the meninges is meningitis.  Why D wrong: Autoimmune peripheral demyelination describes Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A CVA is also known as a:","options":["Seizure or convulsion","Coma or stupor","Stroke or brain attack","Syncope or fainting"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a CVA is also known as a stroke or brain attack.  Why A wrong: Seizures are abnormal electrical activity, not a CVA synonym.  Why B wrong: Coma is a level-of-consciousness state, not a synonym for CVA.  Why D wrong: Syncope is transient loss of consciousness, listed elsewhere as a stroke symptom but is not a synonym."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A cerebral hypoxia lasting longer than how long causes cerebral infarction with irreversible changes?","options":["1 minute","5 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cerebral hypoxia lasting longer than 10 minutes causes cerebral infarction with irreversible changes.  Why A/B/D wrong: These intervals do not match the source value of 10 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which assessment scales are used in stroke according to the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["FAST scale","MMSE Scale","ABCD2 score","ABCD3 score","Glasgow Coma Scale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists FAST scale, MMSE Scale, ABCD2 score, and ABCD3 score as assessment scales used in stroke.  Why E wrong: The Glasgow Coma Scale, though clinically relevant, is not among the scales listed on this page for stroke assessment."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"According to the source, what is the most common cause or risk factor for haemorrhagic stroke?","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Hypertension","Smoking","Oral contraceptive pills (OCPs)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies hypertension as the most common cause or risk factor for haemorrhagic stroke.  Why A wrong: Atrial fibrillation is a listed risk factor but not named as the most common cause of haemorrhagic stroke.  Why C/D wrong: Smoking and OCPs are listed risk factors but not the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which of the following are listed as risk factors for CVA? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypertension","Hyperlipidaemia and atherosclerosis","Smoking and cerebral arteriosclerosis","Cerebral aneurism and atrial fibrillation","Diabetes mellitus, advanced age, stress, obesity, and OCPs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items appear in the source's CVA risk factors: hypertension, hyperlipidaemia & atherosclerosis, smoking & cerebral arteriosclerosis, cerebral aneurism & atrial fibrillation, and DM, advanced age, stress, obesity, OCPs.  Why any wrong: None are wrong; all are source-listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A stroke affecting the LEFT side of the brain typically produces deficits on which side of the body?","options":["Left side","Right side","Both sides equally","Neither side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Left brain damage causes a stroke on the right side of the body (the brain controls the opposite/contralateral side), per the source.  Why A wrong: Left-sided deficits result from right brain damage.  Why C/D wrong: Stroke deficits are characteristically unilateral and contralateral, not bilateral or absent."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which deficits are characteristic of RIGHT brain damage following a stroke? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Paralyzed left side / hemiplegia","Left-sided neglect","Spatial-perceptual deficits","Impulsive, safety problems and impaired judgement","Slow performance and aware of deficits (depression, anxiety)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists for right brain damage: paralyzed left side/hemiplegia, left-sided neglect, spatial-perceptual deficits, tends to deny or minimize problems, rapid performance with short attention span, impulsive/safety problems, impaired judgement, and impaired time concepts.  Why E wrong: Slow performance and being aware of deficits with depression/anxiety are features of LEFT brain damage, not right."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which set of deficits is characteristic of LEFT brain damage?","options":["Left-sided neglect and spatial-perceptual deficits","Impulsivity and tendency to deny problems","Paralyzed right side/hemiplegia, impaired right/left discrimination, slow performance, aware of deficits, and impaired comprehension related to language/math","Rapid performance with short attention span"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists for left brain damage: paralyzed right side/hemiplegia, impaired right/left discrimination, slow performance/cautious, aware of deficits (depression, anxiety), and impaired comprehension related to language and math.  Why A/B/D wrong: These describe right brain damage features."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Ischemic stroke occurs when:","options":["A blood vessel ruptures and bleeds into the brain tissue","Brain tissues are blocked from oxygen supply","The meninges become inflamed","The myelin sheath of peripheral nerves is destroyed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ischemic stroke occurs when brain tissues are blocked from oxygen supply.  Why A wrong: Vessel rupture with bleeding into brain tissue describes haemorrhagic stroke.  Why C wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why D wrong: Peripheral myelin destruction describes Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"According to the source, which is the most common type of CVA, and which is the most common cause of ischemic CVA?","options":["Haemorrhagic is most common; embolism is most common cause of ischemic CVA","Ischemic CVA is the most common type; thrombosis is the most common cause of ischemic CVA","Transient ischemic attack is most common; systemic hypo-perfusion is the most common cause","Systemic venous sinus thrombosis is most common; embolism is the most common cause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ischemic CVA is the most common type of CVA, and thrombosis is the most common cause of ischemic CVA.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misidentify either the most common type or the most common ischemic cause per the source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Match the cause of ischemic stroke to its description: \"Obstruction by a blood clot.\"","options":["Thrombosis","Embolism","Systemic hypo-perfusion","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines embolism as obstruction by a blood clot (a clot that travels and lodges).  Why A wrong: Thrombosis is obstruction (in situ formation) — the source pairs thrombosis with being the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Systemic hypo-perfusion is developed by shock.  Why D wrong: Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis is a separate listed mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, systemic hypo-perfusion causing ischemic stroke is developed by:","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Hypertension","Shock","Atherosclerosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states systemic hypo-perfusion is developed by shock.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the cause the source attributes to systemic hypo-perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Haemorrhagic stroke results from:","options":["Blockage of brain tissues from oxygen supply","Bleeding into brain tissues and subarachnoid space","A temporary clot that resolves within minutes","Loss of myelin in the central nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states haemorrhagic stroke results from bleeding into brain tissues and the subarachnoid space (less common type).  Why A wrong: Blockage from oxygen supply describes ischemic stroke.  Why C wrong: A transient resolving event describes a transient ischemic attack (TIA).  Why D wrong: CNS myelin loss describes multiple sclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"What are the two main types of haemorrhagic stroke described in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Intracerebral haemorrhage","Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Subdural haematoma","Epidural haematoma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are two main types of haemorrhagic stroke — intracerebral haemorrhage (bleeding within the brain itself due to intraparenchymal/intraventricular haemorrhage) and subarachnoid haemorrhage (bleeding between the arachnoid mater and pia mater).  Why C/D wrong: Subdural and epidural haematomas are not the two types listed by the source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Intracerebral haemorrhage is described in the source as bleeding within the brain itself due to:","options":["Bleeding between the arachnoid mater and pia mater","Intraparenchymal or intraventricular haemorrhage","Embolic occlusion of a cerebral artery","Venous sinus thrombosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes intracerebral haemorrhage as bleeding within the brain itself due to intraparenchymal or intraventricular haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Bleeding between arachnoid and pia mater is subarachnoid haemorrhage.  Why C/D wrong: These are ischemic mechanisms, not haemorrhagic."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A transient ischemic attack (TIA) is best characterized by the source as:","options":["A permanent infarction with irreversible deficits","A brief or short episode of neurological dysfunction caused by loss of blood flow (ischemia) to the brain, spinal cord, or retina, without tissue death or infarction","Bleeding into the subarachnoid space","Progressive demyelination over months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a TIA is a brief/short episode of neurological dysfunction caused by loss of blood flow (ischemia) to the brain, spinal cord, or retina, without tissue death or infarction.  Why A wrong: TIA specifically lacks infarction/tissue death.  Why C wrong: That describes subarachnoid haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: That describes multiple sclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A stroke patient presents with sudden, transient loss of consciousness. This finding is termed:","options":["Syncope","Dysphagia","Dysarthria","Hemianopia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists syncope as a transient or temporary loss of consciousness among stroke signs/symptoms.  Why B wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing.  Why C wrong: Dysarthria is difficulty speaking due to muscle paralysis.  Why D wrong: Hemianopia is blindness in half the visual fields."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A stroke patient has paralysis on one side of the body, usually opposite the side of the lesion or attack. This is termed:","options":["Hemiparesis","Hemiplegia","Paraplegia","Quadriplegia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hemiplegia as paralysis on one side of the body, usually occurring opposite the side of the lesion/attack.  Why A wrong: Hemiparesis is weakness (not full paralysis) on one side of the body.  Why C/D wrong: Paraplegia and quadriplegia are not the terms used here for unilateral stroke paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"\"Weakness on one side of the body\" in a stroke patient is best described by which term?","options":["Hemiplegia","Hemiparesis","Aphasia","Apraxia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hemiparesis as weakness on one side of the body.  Why A wrong: Hemiplegia is paralysis, not weakness.  Why C wrong: Aphasia is inability to communicate/loss of communicative function.  Why D wrong: Apraxia is inability to perform purposeful/familiar acts."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A stroke patient is unable to communicate, with loss of communicative function (including inability to express, receive, and grasp language). This deficit is:","options":["Dysphagia","Aphasia","Apraxia","Agnosia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines aphasia as inability to communicate — loss of communicative function involving expression, reception, and grasping language.  Why A wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing.  Why C wrong: Apraxia relates to performing familiar acts/activities of daily living.  Why D wrong: Agnosia is inability to recognize sensory cues (familiar objects/persons)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Difficulty in speaking because of paralysis of muscles needed for articulation is termed:","options":["Dysphasia","Dysarthria","Dysphagia","Aphonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines dysarthria as difficulty in speaking because of paralysis of muscles needed for articulation.  Why A wrong: Dysphasia is difficulty in expressing/receiving language.  Why C wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty in swallowing.  Why D wrong: Aphonia (loss of voice) is not the source term here."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Match these stroke terms to their definitions. Which pairing is INCORRECT per the source?","options":["Dysphagia — difficulty in swallowing","Dysphasia — difficulty in expressing or receiving language","Hemianopia — blindness in half the visual fields","Agnosia — inability to perform purposeful movements"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct (as the incorrect pairing): The source defines agnosia as inability to recognize one or more familiar objects/persons assessed by sensory stimuli; \"inability to perform purposeful movements\" describes apraxia.  Why A/B/C wrong (i.e., correctly matched): Dysphagia = difficulty swallowing; dysphasia = difficulty expressing/receiving language; hemianopia = blindness in half the visual fields — all per source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Apraxia in a stroke patient refers to:","options":["Inability to recognise familiar objects or persons via sensory cues","Inability to perform purposeful/familiar acts or activities of daily living","Difficulty in swallowing","Loss of voice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines apraxia as inability to perform purposeful/familiar acts of daily activities.  Why A wrong: That defines agnosia.  Why C wrong: That defines dysphagia.  Why D wrong: Loss of voice is aphonia, not apraxia."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Horner's syndrome in a stroke patient includes which classic triad of signs?","options":["Ptosis, miosis, and dryness/decreased sweating (anhidrosis) on the affected side","Exophthalmos, mydriasis, and excessive sweating","Ptosis, mydriasis, and flushing","Hemianopia, dysphagia, and dysarthria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes Horner's syndrome as ptosis (lazy eye / drooping of upper eyelids), miosis (pupil constriction), and decreased/dryness of sweating (anhidrosis) on the affected side.  Why B/C wrong: Mydriasis, exophthalmos, and excessive sweating are opposite to Horner's findings.  Why D wrong: These are general stroke symptoms, not Horner's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which finding is associated with stroke per the source's signs & symptoms section?","options":["Improved fine touch sensation","Alteration in reflexes and bladder & bowel function","Increased deep tendon reflexes only","Hypersalivation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists alteration in reflexes and bladder & bowel function, along with abnormal EEG, CT, and MRI reports, as stroke findings.  Why A wrong: Improved sensation is not a stroke deficit.  Why C/D wrong: These isolated findings are not what the source lists."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"The FAST mnemonic for recognizing signs of a stroke stands for:","options":["Fever, Aphasia, Speech, Tremor","Face, Arm, Speech, Time","Facial droop, Ataxia, Sweating, Temperature","Fall, Aura, Stiffness, Tone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's FAST scale (\"There's treatment if you act F.A.S.T.\") stands for Face, Arm, Speech, Time.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the FAST mnemonic in the source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"On the NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS), a score range of 16–20 corresponds to what stroke severity?","options":["No stroke symptoms","Minor stroke","Moderate to severe stroke","Severe stroke"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's NIHSS table assigns a score of 16–20 to \"moderate to severe stroke.\"  Why A wrong: A score of 0 means no stroke symptoms.  Why B wrong: 1–4 means minor stroke.  Why D wrong: 21–42 means severe stroke (5–15 is moderate stroke)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"According to the NIHSS table in the source, a score of 5–15 indicates:","options":["No stroke symptoms","Minor stroke","Moderate stroke","Severe stroke"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source assigns 5–15 to \"moderate stroke.\"  Why A wrong: 0 = no stroke symptoms.  Why B wrong: 1–4 = minor stroke.  Why D wrong: 21–42 = severe stroke."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"On the NIHSS, which score range indicates a severe stroke?","options":["1–4","5–15","16–20","21–42"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source assigns 21–42 to \"severe stroke.\"  Why A wrong: 1–4 = minor stroke.  Why B wrong: 5–15 = moderate stroke.  Why C wrong: 16–20 = moderate to severe stroke."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In the acute phase of stroke care, the nurse should monitor vitals and neurological assessment specifically to identify the sign of:","options":["Decreased intracranial pressure","Increased intracranial pressure (ICP)","Hypoglycaemia","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the nurse to monitor vitals and neurological assessment and identify the sign of increased ICP during the acute phase.  Why A wrong: The concern is increased, not decreased, ICP.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the focus of the listed acute-phase monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate in the acute phase of stroke care? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Maintain a patency of airway by positioning, suctioning, and inserting an artificial airway","Administer oxygen as prescribed","Avoid using elevated extremity for blood pressure measurement because reading may be falsely lowered","Position the client in same Fowler's position with head turned to one side for drainage of secretions","Withhold all anti-seizure medication permanently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists maintaining airway patency (positioning, suctioning, artificial airway), administering oxygen as prescribed, avoiding the affected/elevated extremity for BP measurement (false low reading), positioning in same Fowler's with head turned to one side for drainage, and maintaining fluid/electrolyte balance, plus administering prescribed medications without delay.  Why E wrong: The source does not direct withholding seizure medication; it says to administer prescribed medications without delay."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"When performing a FAST assessment in a stroke patient, the nurse evaluates: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Face drooping","Arm weakness","Speech difficulties","Time to call for help","Taste alteration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's FAST assessment = Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech difficulties, and Time to call for help.  Why E wrong: Taste alteration is not part of FAST."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Carotid endarterectomy is described in the source as a surgical intervention to:","options":["Remove a brain tumour","Improve blood flow where carotid arteries are narrowed by arteriosclerotic plaques","Drain a subdural haematoma","Repair a ruptured aneurysm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines carotid endarterectomy as a surgical intervention to improve blood flow when carotid arteries are narrowed by arteriosclerotic plaques.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the purpose the source ascribes to carotid endarterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"According to the source, the NIHSS is used for assessment of stroke; the maximum score is:","options":["21","32","42","50"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the maximum NIHSS score is 42.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated maximum of 42."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"During the post-acute stabilisation phase of stroke care, after positioning a patient with an unaffected side up, the position should be changed about every 2 hours, and for the affected side it should not be left for more than how long?","options":["5 minutes","20 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states position should be changed every 2 hours, but the affected side should not be left unaffected/down for more than 20 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 20-minute limit for the affected side."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"To prevent flexion contractures in a stroke patient, the source recommends prone positioning for:","options":["5 minutes daily","15 minutes daily","20 to 30 minutes daily","1 hour daily"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends prone position for 20 to 30 minutes daily to prevent flexion (contractures).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source's 20–30 minute prone recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which interventions belong to the post-stabilisation phase of stroke care? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Continue the intervention from the acute phase","Monitor gag reflex and ability to swallow","Provide skin, mouth, and eye care to prevent infection","Maintain functional alignment and provide a footboard for safety and to facilitate self returning","Provide foley catheter care to prevent infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these for the post-stabilisation phase: continue acute-phase interventions, monitor gag reflex/swallowing, provide skin/mouth/eye care, perform passive exercises, maintain functional alignment, footboard for safety, prone positioning, and foley catheter care to prevent infection.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"When communicating with a stroke client who has aphasia, the source recommends the nurse should:","options":["Speak rapidly using long sentences","Speak slowly, clearly, and in short sentences; do not speak loudly; and use spoken words directly relating to objects","Avoid all alternative communication methods","Only communicate in writing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises facing the client when communicating, speaking slowly/clearly and in a short sentence, not speaking loudly, using spoken words directly relating to objects, and talking about alternative communication.  Why A wrong: Rapid, long sentences contradict the source.  Why C wrong: The source says to talk about alternative communication, not avoid it.  Why D wrong: The source does not restrict communication to writing only."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Pattern of pulse described as \"slow or bounding\" and a breathing pattern described as \"Cheyne-Stokes\" are listed as features in:","options":["Myasthenia gravis","A stroke patient's assessment of pulse and respiration patterns","Parkinson's disease","Multiple sclerosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"pattern of pulse: slow or bounding\" and \"pattern of respiration: Cheyne-Stokes\" under stroke assessment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These patterns are listed in the stroke section, not these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Expressive aphasia is also known as which type of aphasia, and is caused by injury to which brain area?","options":["Wernicke's aphasia; injury to the temporal lobe","Broca's aphasia (or motor aphasia); injury to Broca's area of the frontal lobe","Global aphasia; diffuse cortical injury","Conduction aphasia; injury to the arcuate fasciculus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states expressive aphasia is also known as Broca's or motor aphasia, in which the client is unable to express own feelings/messages by speaking and writing, caused by injury/infarction in Broca's area of the frontal lobe.  Why A wrong: Wernicke's/temporal lobe is receptive aphasia.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source's description of expressive aphasia."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In expressive (Broca's) aphasia, the client's ability to understand language is:","options":["Severely impaired","Lost completely","Intact (unable to express but ability to understand language is intact)","Limited to written language only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in expressive aphasia the client is unable to express but the ability to understand/produce language comprehension is intact.  Why A/B wrong: Comprehension is intact, not lost/severely impaired, in expressive aphasia.  Why D wrong: Understanding is not restricted to written language only."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Receptive aphasia is also known as Wernicke's aphasia and is caused by injury or infarction to which area?","options":["Broca's area of the frontal lobe","Wernicke's area of the temporal lobe","The cerebellum","The basal ganglia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states receptive aphasia (Wernicke's aphasia) is caused by injury/infarction in Wernicke's area of the temporal lobe; the client has difficulty understanding written and spoken language but reading is intact (difficulty understanding written and spoken words while reading).  Why A wrong: Broca's area/frontal lobe is expressive aphasia.  Why C/D wrong: These areas are not the source's site for receptive aphasia."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Global aphasia, per the source, affects:","options":["Only expression","Only reception","Both expression and reception","Only reading"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines global aphasia as affecting both expression and reception.  Why A/B/D wrong: Global aphasia is not limited to a single modality."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Myasthenia gravis is best described as:","options":["A chronic, progressive, autoimmune neuromuscular disorder characterized by weakness and abnormal fatigue of the voluntary muscles","A degenerative disorder of the basal ganglia","An inflammation of the meninges","A demyelinating disease of the CNS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines myasthenia gravis as a chronic, progressive, autoimmune neuromuscular disorder characterized by weakness and abnormal fatigue of the voluntary muscles.  Why B wrong: Basal ganglia degeneration describes Parkinson's disease.  Why C wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why D wrong: CNS demyelination describes multiple sclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Myasthenia gravis is a severe form of muscle weakness in which there is a disturbance in transmission of impulses at which site?","options":["Central synapse in the brain","Myoneural junction (neuromuscular junction) — structural damage at the myoneural junction","Dorsal root ganglia","Spinal cord anterior horn cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states myasthenia gravis is a severe form of muscle weakness with disturbance in transmission of impulses at the myoneural junction or structural damage at the myoneural junction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the site of the transmission defect in myasthenia gravis per the source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In early disease, myasthenia gravis most commonly affects the muscles of: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Eye","Face","Neck","Swallowing","Lower limbs only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states muscles most commonly affected in early disease are muscles of eye, face, and swallowing (and neck), i.e., the bulbar and ocular muscles.  Why E wrong: Lower-limbs-only is not the early-disease pattern described."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which are listed causes of myasthenia gravis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Autoimmune mechanism","Insufficient secretion of acetylcholine","Excessive secretion of cholinesterase","Unresponsiveness of the muscle fibers to acetylcholine","Excess dopamine production"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists causes as autoimmune, insufficient secretion of acetylcholine, excessive secretion of cholinesterase, and unresponsiveness of the muscle fibers to acetylcholine.  Why E wrong: Excess dopamine is not a listed cause of myasthenia gravis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A hallmark sign of myasthenia gravis is that muscle weakness:","options":["Improves with continued activity","Is extremely severe; the condition worsens with work but improves with rest","Is constant and unaffected by activity or rest","Only occurs at night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states myasthenia gravis features extremely severe muscle weakness; the condition worsens with work but improves with rest.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the classic worsens-with-activity, improves-with-rest pattern."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are associated with myasthenia gravis per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dyspnoea and dysphagia","Ptosis (drooping of eyelids), most indicative and an early sign","Strabismus (crossing eye and squint)","Dysphonia (weak voice) and myasthenic smile (nasal smile)","Ineffective cough/laugh"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dyspnoea & dysphagia, dysarthria in speaking, ptosis (most indicative, early sign), strabismus (crossing eye and squint), dysphonia (weak voice), myasthenic smile (nasal smile), and ineffective cough/laugh.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which sign is described in the source as the MOST indicative and an early sign of myasthenia gravis?","options":["Strabismus","Ptosis (drooping of the eye lids)","Dysphonia","Dysphagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels ptosis (drooping of eyelids) as the most indicative and early sign of myasthenia gravis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are listed symptoms but not labelled the most indicative/early sign."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a patient with myasthenia gravis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor respiratory status and ability to cough and clear secretions","Monitor for respiratory failure","Maintain suction and have emergency resuscitative equipment at the bedside","Avoid administration of morphine to the client receiving cholinesterase inhibitors because these drugs potentiate the effect of morphine and may cause respiratory depression","Encourage rapid feeding to conserve energy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring respiratory status/cough ability, monitoring for respiratory failure, maintaining suction and emergency resuscitative equipment at bedside, monitoring vital parameters, monitoring speech/swallowing ability to prevent aspiration, monitoring for respiratory infection, instructing the client to avoid stress and infection, and avoiding morphine in clients on cholinesterase inhibitors (potentiates morphine effect → respiratory depression).  Why E wrong: Rapid feeding risks aspiration; the source emphasizes monitoring swallowing to prevent aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Myasthenic crisis is described in the source as:","options":["An overmedication of anti-cholinesterase medication","An acute exacerbation of the disease characterized by profound weakness of the muscles resulting in respiratory failure that may require ventilation","A purely psychiatric condition","A complication of dopamine therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines myasthenic crisis as an acute exacerbation of the disease characterized by profound weakness of muscles resulting in respiratory failure that may require ventilation; it is caused or precipitated by infection, stress, and inadequate amount of anti-cholinesterase medicines.  Why A wrong: Overmedication of anti-cholinesterase causes cholinergic crisis.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Cholinergic crisis is caused by:","options":["Undermedication / inadequate anti-cholinesterase medication","Overmedication of anti-cholinesterase medication","Excess dopamine","Bacterial meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cholinergic crisis is caused by overmedication of anti-cholinesterase medication and is suddenly in onset.  Why A wrong: Inadequate/undermedication of anti-cholinesterase causes myasthenic crisis.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to cholinergic crisis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which signs/symptoms characterize MYASTHENIC crisis per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Increased pulse, respiration, and blood pressure","Respiratory distress with cyanosis","Loss of coughing & swallowing reflex","Bowel & bladder incontinence","Increased urine output, increased lacrimation, and diaphoresis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists for myasthenic crisis: increased pulse/respiration/BP, respiratory distress with cyanosis, loss of coughing & swallowing reflex, bowel & bladder incontinence, increased urine output, and increased lacrimation and diaphoresis.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed for myasthenic crisis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which findings are characteristic of CHOLINERGIC crisis per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Ptosis and blurred vision & weakness","Dysphagia (difficulty in swallowing) and dysphonia (difficulty in speaking)","Hypotension and abdominal cramps & diarrhoea","Increased respiratory secretion and lacrimation","Hypertension and decreased secretions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists for cholinergic crisis: ptosis, blurred vision & weakness, dysphagia, dysphonia, hypotension, abdominal cramps & diarrhoea, and increased respiratory secretion and lacrimation.  Why E wrong: Hypertension and decreased secretions are features of myasthenic crisis, not cholinergic crisis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Regarding the difference between myasthenic and cholinergic crisis (pupil findings): which statement is correct per the source?","options":["Pupil is dilated (mydriasis) in myasthenic crisis and constricted (miosis) in cholinergic crisis","Pupil is constricted in both crises","Pupil is dilated in both crises","Pupil is constricted in myasthenic crisis and dilated in cholinergic crisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's comparison table shows pupil dilated (mydriasis) in myasthenic crisis and pupil constricted (miosis) in cholinergic crisis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's pupil findings."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source's comparison table, secretions in myasthenic crisis versus cholinergic crisis are:","options":["Increased in myasthenic; decreased in cholinergic","Normal (decreased) in myasthenic; increased in cholinergic","Decreased in both","Increased in both"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table shows normal/decreased secretion in myasthenic crisis and increased secretion in cholinergic crisis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's secretion comparison."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source comparison, heart rate (pulse) is _____ in myasthenic crisis and _____ in cholinergic crisis.","options":["Decreased; increased","Increased; decreased","Increased; increased","Decreased; decreased"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's comparison table shows heart rate increased in myasthenic crisis and heart rate decreased in cholinergic crisis (cholinergic excess causes bradycardia).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the table values."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source comparison, in myasthenic crisis there is temporary improvement of symptoms with administration of which agent?","options":["Atropine sulphate","Anticholinesterase (endophonium / edrophonium)","Morphine","Levodopa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's comparison states myasthenic crisis shows temporary improvement of symptoms with administration of anticholinesterase (endophonium/edrophonium).  Why A wrong: Atropine sulphate is the antidote used in cholinergic crisis.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source-listed agents."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In cholinergic crisis, the source notes that symptoms improve with administration of anti-cholinergics, e.g.:","options":["Atropine","Neostigmine","Pyridostigmine","Edrophonium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists atropine as the anti-cholinergic that improves cholinergic-crisis symptoms.  Why B/C/D wrong: Neostigmine, pyridostigmine, and edrophonium are anti-cholinesterase agents (would worsen cholinergic crisis)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"The medical management of myasthenic crisis versus cholinergic crisis differs in that:","options":["Both are treated with atropine","Myasthenic crisis is treated by administering anti-cholinesterase medication immediately, while cholinergic crisis is treated by withholding anti-cholinesterase medication and giving atropine sulphate","Both are treated by withholding all medication","Cholinergic crisis is treated by increasing anti-cholinesterase medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for myasthenic crisis: increase anti-cholinesterase medications as per order / administer anti-cholinesterase medication immediately; for cholinergic crisis: withhold anti-choline medication immediately and immediately administer antidote — atropine sulphate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's distinct management of each crisis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"The Edrophonium (Tensilon) test is performed for which purposes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["To diagnose myasthenia gravis","To differentiate between myasthenic crisis and cholinergic crisis","To screen for Parkinson's disease","To assess for multiple sclerosis plaques"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Edrophonium test is used to diagnose myasthenia gravis and to differentiate between myasthenic crisis and cholinergic crisis (edrophonium injection given to the client).  Why C/D wrong: The Tensilon test is not used for Parkinson's disease or multiple sclerosis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"A complication of the Edrophonium (Tensilon) test that the nurse must watch for is:","options":["Ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest","Hyperglycaemia","Seizures","Hypertensive crisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest as a complication of the test.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed complications of the Tensilon test."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In the Tensilon (edrophonium) test used to diagnose myasthenia gravis, a POSITIVE result is indicated by:","options":["No change or worsening of muscle weakness after injection","Muscle weakness improves after injection (positive = improvement → confirms myasthenia gravis)","Increased heart rate only","Pupil constriction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a positive result = muscle weakness improves after injection, which means positive myasthenia gravis.  Why A wrong: No change or worsening (no improvement) is a negative result for myasthenia gravis.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the diagnostic criteria for the test."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"When using the Tensilon test to differentiate crisis type: if edrophonium injection IMPROVES muscle weakness, the client is in _____ crisis; if it WORSENS muscle weakness, the client is in _____ crisis.","options":["Cholinergic; myasthenic","Myasthenic; cholinergic","Adrenergic; cholinergic","Myasthenic; adrenergic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if edrophonium improves strength it is myasthenic crisis; if muscle weakness is worsened it is cholinergic crisis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse or misname the crisis types."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"When differentiating crisis by the Tensilon test, the antidote that should be administered for cholinergic crisis is:","options":["Edrophonium","Neostigmine","Atropine sulphate","Pyridostigmine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the antidote — atropine sulphate — should be administered for cholinergic crisis.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are anti-cholinesterase agents, not the antidote for cholinergic crisis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"According to the source's note, which drug should be kept at the bedside while performing the Tensilon test?","options":["Edrophonium","Atropine sulphate","Neostigmine","Morphine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's note states atropine sulphate should be kept at the bedside while performing the Tensilon test (as the antidote in case of cholinergic crisis precipitation).  Why A wrong: Edrophonium is the test agent, not the safety antidote.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the bedside-ready antidote."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Alzheimer's disease is best described as:","options":["A progressive, irreversible, degenerative neurologic disease characterized by gradual loss of cognitive function and disturbances in behaviour and memory","An autoimmune neuromuscular disorder","An acute inflammation of the meninges","A demyelinating disorder of peripheral nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Alzheimer's disease as a progressive, irreversible, degenerative neurologic disease characterized by gradual loss of cognitive function and disturbances in behaviour and memory.  Why B wrong: That describes myasthenia gravis.  Why C wrong: That describes meningitis.  Why D wrong: That describes Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, Alzheimer's disease is:","options":["The least common form of dementia","The most common cause of dementia in old age people (cortical dementia)","A form of dementia only seen in children","Always reversible with treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states AD is the most common cause of dementia in old-age people and is the most common cause of cortical dementia.  Why A wrong: It is the most common, not least common, dementia.  Why C wrong: It affects old-age people, not children.  Why D wrong: AD is described as irreversible."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In Alzheimer's disease there is a loss of memory, language, and judgement which is disturbing daily activities; it is associated with a LOW level of which neurotransmitter?","options":["Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin","GABA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states AD is associated with a low level of acetylcholine (neurotransmitter); dementia is loss of memory, language, and judgement disturbing daily activities.  Why A wrong: Low dopamine is characteristic of Parkinson's disease.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the neurotransmitter the source links to AD."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which are listed signs/symptoms of Alzheimer's disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Forgetfulness and subtle memory loss","Cognitive impairment","Personality changes","Onychalgia as the disease progresses (dysphagia as disease progresses)","Aphasia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists forgetfulness and subtle memory loss, cognitive impairment, personality changes, dysphagia as the disease progresses, and aphasia among AD signs/symptoms.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed (the dysphagia-as-disease-progresses item is the intended fourth feature)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which is a listed risk factor for Alzheimer's disease?","options":["Young age","Old age and genetics","High dopamine","Recent infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists old age and genetics as risk factors for AD.  Why A wrong: Old age, not young age, is the risk factor.  Why C/D wrong: These are not source-listed AD risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with Alzheimer's disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Assist the patient for daily activities","Initiate general nursing care","Administer high-dose anticholinergic drugs to reduce memory","Restrict all family interaction"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's nursing care for AD lists assisting the patient for daily activities and initiating general nursing care.  Why C wrong: This contradicts therapy goals (AD has low acetylcholine; anticholinergics worsen cognition) and is not source-listed.  Why D wrong: Restricting family interaction is not a source-listed measure."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Parkinson's disease is described as a progressive disorder of the CNS in which destruction or death of nerve cells occurs in which structure, and is related to injury of the substantia nigra of the midbrain leading to a decrease in which neurotransmitter?","options":["Cerebellum; serotonin","Basal ganglia (and substantia nigra of the midbrain); dopamine","Temporal lobe; acetylcholine","Spinal cord; GABA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Parkinson's is a progressive disorder of the CNS with destruction/death of nerve cells in the basal ganglia, related to injury of the substantia nigra of the midbrain, leading to decreased dopamine in motor functions.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misidentify the structure and/or neurotransmitter."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, Parkinson's disease is known as \"hypokinetic rigid syndrome\" in which population?","options":["Adults only","Children, and its lines are unknown","The elderly only","Pregnant women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is known as hypokinetic rigid syndrome in children and its lines/cause are unknown.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source statement."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which are signs/symptoms of Parkinson's disease per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fatigue, depression, muscular pain","Stiffness and rigidity, particularly large joints such as hip, shoulder","Resting tremors: pill-rolling movement of the thumb against fingers at rest, jerky (cogwheel) movements; tremors increase with fatigue, emotion, and during sleep","Bradykinesia (slowness of voluntary movements) and hypokinesia (decreased movement)","Akinesia (inability to move) and micrographia (small handwriting)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists fatigue/depression/muscular pain; stiffness & rigidity of large joints (hip, shoulder); resting pill-rolling tremors and jerky/cogwheel movements that increase with fatigue, emotion, and during sleep; bradykinesia; hypokinesia; akinesia; and micrographia.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed Parkinson features."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Match the Parkinson's term to its definition. Which pairing is correct per the source?","options":["Bradykinesia — fast voluntary movements","Micrographia — large handwriting","Monotonous speech — unchanging tone of speech","Akinesia — excessive movement"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines monotonous speech as unchanging tone of speech.  Why A wrong: Bradykinesia is slowness of voluntary movements.  Why B wrong: Micrographia is small handwriting.  Why D wrong: Akinesia is inability to move (loss of movement), not excessive movement."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which facial sign of Parkinson's disease is described as a fixed, expressionless face with infrequent blinking?","options":["Masked face / mask-like face with unblinking eyes","Myasthenic smile","Bell's palsy","Risus sardonicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists blank facial expression — masklike face & unblinking eyes — as a Parkinson's feature.  Why B wrong: Myasthenic smile is a myasthenia gravis sign.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source's term for the Parkinson facial sign."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which speech and swallowing findings are seen in Parkinson's disease per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Drooling (uncontrollably dropping the saliva from mouth)","Dysphagia (difficulty in swallowing)","Dysphasia (difficulty in speaking)","Dysphonia (low, poorly modulated speech)","Loss of coordination and balance"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists drooling (uncontrollable saliva from mouth), dysphagia, dysphasia, dysphonia (low, poorly modulated speech), and loss of coordination and balance.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"The Parkinsonian gait is described in the source as:","options":["A wide-based, staggering gait","A shuffling gait — dragging of feet when walking, with a length of the steps shorter (short shuffling gait, stooped posture)","A high-stepping gait","A spastic scissoring gait"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes shuffling gait: dragging of feet when walking, length of steps is shorter; stooped posture, propulsive/short shuffling gait.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the Parkinsonian shuffling gait described."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which additional features are associated with Parkinson's disease per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Stooped (propulsive) posture and short shuffling gait","Decreased sexual activity","Constipation","Excessive perspiration and excessive sweating","Dementia (memory loss)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists stooped/propulsive posture with short shuffling gait, decreased sexual activity, constipation, excessive perspiration/sweating, and dementia (memory loss).  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the figure description, which posture features are part of the clinical features of Parkinson's disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Stooped posture and masked face","Back rigidity and forward tilt of the trunk","Flexed elbows and wrists with reduced arm swing","Slightly flexed hip and knees with hand tremor and \"pill rolling\" tremors of hands","Hyperextension of the spine with arms fully abducted"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The figure lists stooped posture, masked face, back rigidity, forward tilt of trunk, flexed elbows and wrists, reduced arm swing, hard tremor, slightly flexed hip and knees, shuffling gait (short steps), and tremors/pill-rolling of hands.  Why E wrong: Hyperextension with fully abducted arms is the opposite of the flexed, stooped Parkinsonian posture."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is best described as:","options":["A chronic, progressive, degenerative disease of the CNS characterized by demyelination of the neurons, with periods of remission and exacerbation","An autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorder","A bacterial infection of the meninges","A disorder of the basal ganglia with low dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines MS as a chronic, progressive, degenerative disease of the CNS characterized by demyelination of the neurons with periods of remission and exacerbation.  Why B wrong: That describes myasthenia gravis.  Why C wrong: That describes meningitis.  Why D wrong: That describes Parkinson's disease."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, multiple sclerosis causes are unknown or attributed to:","options":["Autoimmune","Bacterial infection","Genetic mutation only","Vitamin overdose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states causes of MS are unknown or autoimmune.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cause the source attributes to MS."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which diagnostic finding helps confirm multiple sclerosis per the source?","options":["CSF gamma globulin level is elevated (but serum level is normal)","CSF gamma globulin level is decreased","Elevated serum gamma globulin only","Low CSF protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the diagnosis is done by CSF: gamma globulin level is elevated, but serum level is normal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's described CSF finding for MS."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which are signs/symptoms of multiple sclerosis per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Numbness, weakness, or fatigue","Difficulty in swallowing or dysphagia","Blurred vision or diplopia (double vision), vertigo","Emotional liability such as depression, apathy, and euphoria","Charcot neurologic triad: SIN (Scanning speech, Intention tremor, Nystagmus)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists numbness/weakness/fatigue, difficulty swallowing/dysphagia, blurred vision/diplopia/vertigo, emotional liability (depression, apathy, euphoria), and Charcot's neurologic triad SIN (scanning speech, intention tremor, nystagmus).  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed MS features."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Charcot's neurologic triad in multiple sclerosis (mnemonic \"SIN\") consists of: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Scanning speech — words are broken up into separate means","Intention tremor — due to dysfunction of cerebellum","Nystagmus — rapid involuntary movement of the eyes","Seizures","Insomnia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Charcot's triad SIN as Scanning speech (words broken into separate means), Intention tremor (due to dysfunction of cerebellum), and Nystagmus (rapid involuntary movement of the eyes).  Why D/E wrong: Seizures and insomnia are not part of Charcot's triad."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Ataxia in multiple sclerosis is described as:","options":["Loss of voluntary coordination of muscle movement (shuffling gait or lack of voluntary coordination of muscles movement)","Inability to speak","Double vision","Drooping of the eyelids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines ataxia as shuffling gait or lack of voluntary coordination of muscle movement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other deficits (dysphasia, diplopia, ptosis)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which additional MS signs/symptoms are listed by the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Impaired bowel and bladder function (urinary frequency, urgency, retention)","Increased IgG level in the CSF (classical sign of multiple sclerosis)","MRI indicates demyelination and presence of multiple sclerosis plaques","Decreased perception to touch, pain, and temperature","Abnormal reflexes and abnormal changes and confusion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists impaired bowel/bladder (urinary frequency, urgency, retention), increased IgG in CSF (classical MS sign), MRI showing demyelination & MS plaques, decreased perception to touch/pain/temperature, and abnormal reflexes/changes and confusion.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with multiple sclerosis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Provide energy conservation measures during exacerbations","Give ordered medications as per order, such as interferon beta-1a, beta-1b","Check the temperature before bathing","Initiate physical, speech therapy, counselling, and rehabilitation","Initiate general nursing care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source's MS nursing care lists energy conservation during exacerbations, giving ordered medications (interferon beta-1a, beta-1b), checking temperature before bathing, initiating physical/speech therapy, counselling and rehabilitation, and initiating general nursing care.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is best described as:","options":["An acute autoimmune infectious neuritis of the cranial and peripheral nerves","A degenerative disease of the basal ganglia","A neuromuscular junction transmission disorder","An inflammation of the meninges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines GBS as an acute autoimmune infectious neuritis of the cranial and peripheral nerves.  Why B wrong: That describes Parkinson's disease.  Why C wrong: That describes myasthenia gravis.  Why D wrong: That describes meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"In GBS, the immune system attacks the infection and destroys (or insulates) the myelin sheath of the peripheral nerves. GBS is also known as:","options":["Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Cortical dementia","Hypokinetic rigid syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GBS is the disorder of the peripheral nervous system and is also known as inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated diagnoses."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, the exact cause of GBS is unknown, but it is associated with: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Autoimmune","Upper respiratory tract infections","GI tract infections","Basal ganglia degeneration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the exact cause is unknown but lists autoimmune, upper respiratory tract, and GI tract infections.  Why D wrong: Basal ganglia degeneration is Parkinson's disease, not a GBS association."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"The classic pattern of weakness/paralysis in Guillain-Barré syndrome is:","options":["Descending (begins in face and progresses downward)","Ascending — weakness or paralysis that begins in the lower extremities and progresses upward (proximal)","Unilateral only","Confined to the cranial nerves only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GBS shows ascending weakness or paralysis that begins in the lower extremities and progresses upward (proximal/ascending).  Why A wrong: GBS is ascending, not descending.  Why C/D wrong: GBS weakness is not unilateral-only or cranial-only."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are associated with Guillain-Barré syndrome per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Paresthesia (burning, prickling, and tingling sensation)","Dysphagia and diplopia","Areflexia (lack of response to stimuli) of muscles","Gradual progressive weakness of the upper extremities and facial muscles","Possible progression to respiratory failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists paresthesia (burning, prickling, tingling), dysphagia & diplopia, areflexia (lack of response to stimuli), gradual progressive weakness of upper extremities and facial muscles, and possible progression to respiratory failure.  Why any wrong: None; all are source-listed GBS features."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Autonomic neuropathy in GBS, per the source, causes:","options":["Damage to nerves controlling non-voluntary movements or internal organs, characterized by HTN, initially tachycardia later bradycardia, and diaphoresis","Loss of voluntary movement only","Permanent blindness","Hyperthermia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines autonomic neuropathy as damage to nerves controlling non-voluntary movements or internal organs, characterized by HTN, initially tachycardia and later bradycardia, and diaphoresis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incomplete or not the source description."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which diagnostic findings support Guillain-Barré syndrome per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Elevated protein level in CSF","Abnormal EEG","Respiratory paralysis (serious complication)","Normal serum gamma globulin with low CSF protein"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists elevated protein level in CSF and abnormal EEG, and notes respiratory paralysis as a serious complication.  Why D wrong: GBS shows elevated CSF protein, not low CSF protein with normal serum; this contradicts the source."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Which nursing care priorities apply to Guillain-Barré syndrome per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor all vital signs, breath sounds, and oxygen saturation","Maintain airway and provide chest physiotherapy; suction as needed; be prepared for intubation and mechanical ventilation","Recognize that the major concern of GBS is difficulty in breathing & respiratory status that should be monitored closely","Initiate general nursing care","Withhold all respiratory monitoring to allow rest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring vital signs/breath sounds/oxygen saturation, maintaining airway with chest physiotherapy/suction/preparation for intubation and mechanical ventilation, recognizing the major concern is breathing/respiratory status (monitored closely), and initiating general nursing care.  Why E wrong: Withholding respiratory monitoring directly contradicts the source's emphasis on close respiratory monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Meningitis is best defined as:","options":["The acute inflammation of the meninges (layer of the brain) in which CSF will be infected","Demyelination of the CNS","An autoimmune neuromuscular disorder","Degeneration of the basal ganglia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines meningitis as the acute inflammation of the meninges (layer of the brain) in which CSF will be infected.  Why B wrong: That describes multiple sclerosis.  Why C wrong: That describes myasthenia gravis.  Why D wrong: That describes Parkinson's disease."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, the most common type of meningitis is most common in which population, and what is the most common type?","options":["Viral meningitis; most common in the elderly","Bacterial meningitis (the most common CNS infection); most common type of meningitis is common in infants and children","Fungal meningitis; most common in adults","Tubercular meningitis; most common in neonates only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meningitis is the most common CNS infection (bacterial), and the most common type of meningitis is common in infants and children.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's statements on commonality."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Cerebrovascular Accident — overview, risk factors, right vs left brain…","question":"Per the source, transmission of meningitis occurs by:","options":["Direct contact or droplet spread","Vector-borne (mosquito) transmission only","Contaminated water only","Genetic inheritance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states transmission of meningitis is by direct contact or droplet spread.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source-listed route of transmission for meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Bacterial meningitis is also known by which other term?","options":["Aseptic meningitis","Septic or pyogenic meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis","Viral meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bacterial meningitis is also known as septic or pyogenic meningitis.  Why A wrong: Aseptic meningitis is the term for viral meningitis.  Why C wrong: Tuberculous meningitis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, a distinct category.  Why D wrong: Viral meningitis is a separate type (aseptic), not bacterial."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which is the most common causative organism of bacterial meningitis in newborns?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Neisseria meningitidis","Group B Streptococcus, S. pneumoniae, L. monocytogenes, E. coli","Haemophilus influenzae"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Group B Streptococcus, S. pneumoniae, L. monocytogenes, and E. coli as the most common causative organisms in newborns.  Why A wrong: S. pneumoniae alone is listed among older adults' organisms, not the full newborn group.  Why B wrong: Neisseria meningitidis is more typical of teens/young adults.  Why D wrong: H. influenzae is listed among babies/young children, not newborns."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, which organisms most commonly cause bacterial meningitis in babies and young children?","options":["Group B Streptococcus and E. coli","Streptococcus pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B Streptococcus, M. tuberculosis","S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis only","Mycobacterium tuberculosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B Streptococcus, and M. tuberculosis for babies and young children.  Why A wrong: This is the newborn group, not babies/young children.  Why C wrong: This is the listed combination for teens and young adults.  Why D wrong: M. tuberculosis alone causes tuberculous meningitis, not the broad pediatric bacterial group."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial meningitis in teens and young adults?","options":["S. pneumoniae and group B Streptococcus","Neisseria meningitidis and S. pneumoniae","E. coli and L. monocytogenes","H. influenzae and M. tuberculosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states N. meningitidis and S. pneumoniae are most common in teens and young adults.  Why A wrong: Group B Streptococcus is a newborn/pediatric organism.  Why C wrong: E. coli and L. monocytogenes are newborn organisms.  Why D wrong: These belong to other categories, not teens/young adults."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial meningitis in older adults?","options":["Group B Streptococcus and E. coli","H. influenzae and N. meningitidis","Streptococcus pneumoniae and N. meningitidis","M. tuberculosis only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis as most common in older adults.  Why A wrong: These are newborn organisms.  Why B wrong: H. influenzae is a pediatric organism.  Why D wrong: M. tuberculosis causes tuberculous meningitis specifically."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcal) meningitis occurs in epidemic form and is transmitted by which route?","options":["Fecal-oral route","Droplets from nasopharyngeal secretions","Blood transfusion","Vector (mosquito) bite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meningococcal meningitis can be transmitted by droplets from nasopharyngeal secretions.  Why A wrong: Fecal-oral is not the listed route for meningococcus.  Why C wrong: Blood transfusion is not described as the transmission route.  Why D wrong: Meningococcal disease is droplet-borne, not vector-borne."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The diagnosis of bacterial meningitis is confirmed by which procedure?","options":["CT scan of the head","Lumbar puncture for CSF culture testing","Blood culture only","Electroencephalogram (EEG)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis of bacterial meningitis is done by lumbar puncture for CSF culture testing.  Why A wrong: CT scan is not the confirmatory diagnostic listed.  Why C wrong: Blood culture alone is not the stated diagnostic; CSF culture is.  Why D wrong: EEG is used for seizure disorders, not for diagnosing meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"In bacterial meningitis, which set of CSF findings is expected?","options":["Increased WBC, increased ICP, elevated protein, decreased glucose","Decreased WBC, decreased protein, increased glucose","Normal WBC, normal protein, normal glucose","Increased glucose with decreased protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists increased WBC (more than 1000 cells/mm³), increased ICP, elevated protein (more than 100 mg/dl), and decreased glucose (below 40 mg/dl) in bacterial meningitis.  Why B wrong: This is the opposite pattern and is incorrect.  Why C wrong: All values are abnormal in bacterial meningitis, not normal.  Why D wrong: Glucose is decreased and protein elevated, not the reverse."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"In bacterial meningitis, the CSF glucose level is typically described as:","options":["Above 100 mg/dl","Below 40 mg/dl","Exactly 60 mg/dl","Normal (70–100 mg/dl)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states decreased glucose level, below 40 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 100 mg/dl is the protein cutoff, and glucose is decreased, not elevated.  Why C wrong: 60 mg/dl is not the value given.  Why D wrong: Glucose is decreased in bacterial meningitis, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Viral meningitis is also referred to as:","options":["Pyogenic meningitis","Aseptic meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis","Septic meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states viral meningitis is also called aseptic meningitis.  Why A wrong: Pyogenic meningitis is bacterial.  Why C wrong: Tuberculous meningitis is caused by M. tuberculosis.  Why D wrong: Septic meningitis is another term for bacterial meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, which viruses can cause viral (aseptic) meningitis?","options":["Mumps, paramyxovirus, herpesvirus, enterovirus","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Neisseria meningitidis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists mumps, paramyxovirus, herpesvirus, and enterovirus as causes of viral meningitis.  Why B wrong: M. tuberculosis causes tuberculous meningitis, not viral.  Why C wrong: S. pneumoniae is a bacterial cause.  Why D wrong: N. meningitidis is a bacterial cause."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Tuberculous meningitis is caused by which organism?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Neisseria meningitidis","Enterovirus","Escherichia coli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tuberculous meningitis is due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria.  Why B wrong: N. meningitidis causes bacterial (meningococcal) meningitis.  Why C wrong: Enterovirus causes viral meningitis.  Why D wrong: E. coli causes neonatal bacterial meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, the most common complication of TB meningitis is:","options":["Seizure disorder","Hydrocephalus","Deafness","Brain abscess"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common complication of TB meningitis is hydrocephalus.  Why A wrong: Seizures are not the listed most common complication.  Why C wrong: Deafness is not listed as the most common complication of TB meningitis.  Why D wrong: Brain abscess is not the stated complication."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which of the following is an \"In Adults\" sign/symptom of meningitis listed in the source?","options":["Bulging fontanelle","Nuchal rigidity or hyperextension of the neck (opisthotonus)","Refusal of feeds","Petechiae only in children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists nuchal rigidity or hyperextension of the neck (opisthotonus) under adult signs of meningitis.  Why A wrong: Bulging fontanelle is a sign in infants/children, not adults.  Why C wrong: Refusal of feeds is a pediatric sign.  Why D wrong: Petechiae and purpuric skin lesions are listed in children, not as adult-only."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which of the following is listed among the \"In Adults\" signs and symptoms of meningitis? (SATA)","options":["Irritability and high pitched cry","Fever and difficulty in feeding or anorexia","Bulging or tense fontanels","Meningococcal example: petechiae, purpuric skin lesions, peripheral circulatory collapse (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome) and shock","Hemiopia loss of half of the visual field"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists irritability/high pitched cry, fever, difficulty feeding/anorexia, bulging/tense fontanels, and the meningococcal example with Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome and shock under adult signs.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly itemized as adult signs and symptoms of meningitis.  Why E wrong: Hemiopia loss of half the visual field appears in the rapid-revision key points (optic chiasma lesion), not in the meningitis sign list."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which of the following is listed under \"In Children and Adolescents & adults\" signs and symptoms of meningitis? (SATA)","options":["Increased ICP — headache, bradycardia, irritability, and vomiting","Fever, nausea, and vomiting","Disorientation in LOC","Irritability and agitation, photophobia","Decreased peripheral pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists increased ICP (headache, bradycardia, irritability, vomiting), fever/nausea/vomiting, disorientation in LOC, and irritability/agitation with photophobia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly itemized in the children/adolescents/adults column.  Why E wrong: Decreased peripheral pulses is not a listed meningitis symptom in this column."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Kernig's sign in meningitis is characterized by:","options":["Flexion of the hips and knees in response to neck flexion","Inability to extend the legs (or pain) when the thigh is flexed at the abdomen","Bridge-like posturing of the back","Drooping of the eyelid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes Kernig's sign as inability to extend the legs (or pain) when the thigh is flexed anteriorly at the hip.  Why A wrong: Flexion of hips and knees with neck flexion describes Brudzinski's sign.  Why C wrong: Bridge-like back posturing describes opisthotonus.  Why D wrong: Ptosis is unrelated to meningeal signs."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Brudzinski's sign in meningitis is characterized by:","options":["Pain on extending the leg from a flexed position","Neck flexion causes adduction and flexion movements of the lower extremities","Facial nerve paralysis","Sharp facial pain along trigeminal nerve branches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes Brudzinski's sign as neck flexion causing adduction and flexion movements of the lower extremities.  Why A wrong: That describes Kernig's sign.  Why C wrong: Facial nerve paralysis describes Bell's palsy.  Why D wrong: Sharp facial pain describes trigeminal neuralgia."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"In the nursing care of meningitis, droplet precautions should be maintained for at least how long after starting antibiotics?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to maintain droplet precautions for at least 48 hours.  Why A/B wrong: 12 and 24 hours are shorter than the specified 48 hours.  Why D wrong: 72 hours exceeds the specified minimum of 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A child with meningitis is started on antibiotics. The source recommends penicillin G be given for how long?","options":["2 to 3 days","4 to 5 days","5 days only with cefotaxime","4 to 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states give penicillin G for 4 to 5 days with 1 week each (with cefotaxime/ceftriaxone) of antibiotic therapy.  Why A wrong: 2 to 3 days is shorter than stated.  Why C wrong: The source pairs penicillin G with cefotaxime/ceftriaxone but the penicillin G duration is 4–5 days, not \"5 days only.\"  Why D wrong: 4 to 6 hours is a dosing interval, not the therapy duration."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, antibiotics should be administered after 48 hours of antibiotic therapy at what interval to maintain therapeutic blood levels?","options":["Every 2 hours","Every 4 to 6 hours","Every 12 hours","Once daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states administer antibiotics every 4 to 6 hours (after starting antibiotic therapy) to maintain therapeutic levels.  Why A wrong: Every 2 hours is not the stated interval.  Why C wrong: Every 12 hours is too long compared to the stated interval.  Why D wrong: Once daily is incorrect for the stated regimen."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"When positioning a client with meningitis who has increased ICP, the nurse should position the child to:","options":["Lie flat in the supine position","Lie with the head lower than the body (Trendelenburg)","Avoid neck flexion and extreme hip flexion / position with head straight to allow venous return","Maintain continuous prone position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to elevate the head of the bed 30 degrees, avoid neck flexion and extreme hip flexion, and maintain a position that promotes venous return.  Why A wrong: Lying flat does not promote venous drainage and is not advised.  Why B wrong: Trendelenburg increases ICP and is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Continuous prone positioning is not the recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, associated with meningococcal disease, is caused by bleeding into which structure?","options":["The cerebral ventricles","The adrenal gland","The spinal canal","The liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is usually caused by severe bleeding into the adrenal gland.  Why A wrong: Ventricular bleeding is not what defines this syndrome.  Why C wrong: It is not bleeding into the spinal canal.  Why D wrong: It involves the adrenal gland, not the liver."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Meningococcal meningitis can result in peripheral vascular collapse and is described as the most severe complication of which type of meningitis?","options":["Viral (aseptic) meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis","Bacterial meningitis","Fungal meningitis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meningococcal meningitis resulting in peripheral vascular collapse is the most severe complication of bacterial meningitis.  Why A wrong: Viral meningitis is generally less severe.  Why B wrong: TB meningitis's listed complication is hydrocephalus.  Why D wrong: Fungal meningitis is not discussed in this context."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A seizure is best defined as:","options":["A gradual decline in cognition over years","Sudden, abnormal, excessive discharge of electrical activity within the brain","A localized infection of the meninges","A degeneration of peripheral motor nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a seizure as an abnormal, excessive discharge of electrical activity within the brain.  Why A wrong: That describes a progressive dementia, not a seizure.  Why C wrong: That describes meningitis.  Why D wrong: That describes a peripheral neuropathy/nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Seizures are also known by which other term according to the source?","options":["Apoplexy","Convulsions","Ataxia","Aura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a seizure is also known as a convulsion.  Why A wrong: Apoplexy is an older term for stroke, not a seizure.  Why C wrong: Ataxia refers to loss of coordination.  Why D wrong: An aura is a warning sign that precedes some seizures, not the term for the seizure itself."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which of the following are listed as causes of seizures? (SATA)","options":["Head trauma","Tumors","Epilepsy","Genetic, circulatory & metabolic disorders, toxicity & infections","Trigeminal neuralgia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists head trauma, tumors, epilepsy, genetic, circulatory and metabolic disorders, toxicity and infections as causes of seizures.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly itemized causes of seizures.  Why E wrong: Trigeminal neuralgia is a separate sensory nerve disorder, not listed as a seizure cause."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, the most common type of seizure in children is:","options":["Generalized seizure","Febrile seizure","Myoclonic seizure","Atonic seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of seizure in children is febrile seizure.  Why A wrong: Generalized seizures involve both hemispheres but are not stated as the most common in children.  Why C wrong: Myoclonic seizures (muscle/clonic jerks) are not stated as most common in children.  Why D wrong: Atonic seizures (loss of muscle tone) are not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The drug of choice for febrile seizures, per the source, is:","options":["Carbamazepine","Phenytoin","Intranasal midazolam and rectal diazepam","Levetiracetam"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists intranasal midazolam and rectal diazepam as the drug of choice for febrile seizures.  Why A wrong: Carbamazepine is the drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia.  Why B wrong: Phenytoin is not listed as the febrile-seizure drug of choice here.  Why D wrong: Levetiracetam is not the listed agent for febrile seizures."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Status epilepticus is defined as:","options":["A brief absence seizure lasting seconds","Continuous seizures without an interval of consciousness, or seizures lasting more than 30 minutes","A single tonic-clonic seizure lasting 1 minute","A warning sign before a seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines status epilepticus as rapid firing of seizures without an interval of consciousness, or more than 30 minutes of seizures.  Why A wrong: A brief absence seizure is not status epilepticus.  Why C wrong: A single 1-minute seizure is not status epilepticus.  Why D wrong: A warning sign before a seizure is an aura."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The drug of choice for status epilepticus per the source is:","options":["Carbamazepine","Benzodiazepines such as diazepam and lorazepam","Penicillin G","Mannitol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for status epilepticus is benzodiazepines such as diazepam and lorazepam.  Why A wrong: Carbamazepine is for trigeminal neuralgia.  Why C wrong: Penicillin G is an antibiotic for meningitis.  Why D wrong: Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic for increased ICP, not the listed agent for status epilepticus."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A generalized seizure is characterized by:","options":["Localized motor or sensory symptoms only","Abnormal electrical activity that begins in both sides and involves both hemispheres of the brain","Always preceded by an aura","No loss of consciousness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a generalized seizure occurs when abnormal electrical activity causes a seizure beginning on both sides/hemispheres of the brain at the same time.  Why A wrong: Localized motor/sensory symptoms describe a simple partial seizure.  Why C wrong: The source notes generalized seizures may or may not begin with an aura or warning sign.  Why D wrong: Generalized seizures typically involve loss of consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A tonic-clonic seizure begins with which phase first?","options":["Clonic phase","Tonic phase","Postictal phase","Absence phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes the tonic phase first (loss of consciousness, then stiffening/rigidity of muscles of arms and legs — opisthotonus, respiration interrupted), followed by the clonic phase.  Why A wrong: The clonic phase follows the tonic phase.  Why C wrong: The postictal phase follows the seizure, not first.  Why D wrong: Absence is a separate seizure type."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"During the tonic phase of a tonic-clonic seizure, which features are described? (SATA)","options":["Loss of consciousness","Stiffening or rigidity of the muscles of the arms and legs (opisthotonus)","Respiration is interrupted","Usually lasts 1–5 minutes","The episode begins with an aura that always lasts minutes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists loss of consciousness, stiffening/rigidity of arm and leg muscles (opisthotonus), and interrupted respiration in the tonic phase.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed tonic-phase features.  Why D wrong: The source states the clonic phase usually lasts 1–5 minutes (longer), not the tonic phase.  Why E wrong: This is not a stated tonic-phase feature."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The clonic phase of a tonic-clonic seizure is characterized by which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Usually lasts 1–5 minutes (longer)","Hyperventilation","Jerking of the extremities","Risk of tongue biting","No loss of consciousness ever occurs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists, under the clonic phase, that it usually lasts 1–5 minutes (longer), hyperventilation, jerking of the extremities, and risk of tongue biting.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly itemized clonic-phase features.  Why E wrong: Loss of consciousness occurs in the tonic phase; the clonic phase follows it, so saying it \"never occurs\" is wrong."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"An absence seizure is best described as:","options":["Brief or short episode of seizure activity that may last for few seconds, with the client losing/not losing consciousness, no loss of tongue/muscle tone, and most common in children","Sudden stiffening with prolonged loss of consciousness","Severe jerking of all extremities for several minutes","A peripheral motor nerve injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes absence seizures as a short/brief episode that may last a few seconds, the client may or may not lose consciousness, no loss of tongue/muscle tone, and it is the most common seizure type in children.  Why B wrong: Prolonged stiffening describes the tonic phase, not absence.  Why C wrong: Severe jerking for minutes describes clonic/tonic-clonic activity.  Why D wrong: Nerve injury is a separate topic."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A myoclonic seizure is characterized by:","options":["Muscle/myoclonic and clonic jerks, jerking or sudden jerky movement of the muscles, and the client may or may not fall to the ground","Brief loss of muscle tone with momentary loss of consciousness","Sustained tonic posturing for minutes","Localized sensory symptoms with retained awareness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes myoclonic seizures as muscle (myoclonic) and clonic jerks — jerking or sudden jerky movement of the muscles — with the client possibly falling to the ground.  Why B wrong: Brief loss of muscle tone with momentary loss of consciousness describes an atonic seizure.  Why C wrong: Sustained tonic posturing describes the tonic phase.  Why D wrong: Localized sensory symptoms with retained awareness describe a simple partial seizure."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"An atonic seizure is characterized by:","options":["Sustained muscle rigidity","Brief momentary loss of muscle tone, with the client suddenly falling to the ground due to seizures, and brief loss of consciousness","Sensory aura without motor signs","Continuous jerking lasting over 30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes atonic seizures as a brief momentary loss of muscle tone, the client suddenly falls to the ground due to seizures, with a brief loss of consciousness.  Why A wrong: Sustained rigidity describes a tonic seizure.  Why C wrong: A sensory aura without motor signs is not the atonic description.  Why D wrong: Continuous jerking over 30 minutes describes status epilepticus."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A partial seizure differs from a generalized seizure in that a partial seizure:","options":["Involves only part of the brain (one area/hemisphere is affected)","Always involves both hemispheres","Never causes loss of consciousness","Is exclusive to children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a partial seizure is a type that occurs when only one part/area of the brain is affected.  Why B wrong: Involvement of both hemispheres describes a generalized seizure.  Why C wrong: Complex partial seizures can cause loss of consciousness for a few seconds; only simple partial lacks loss of consciousness.  Why D wrong: Partial seizures are not exclusive to children."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A simple partial seizure is characterized by:","options":["Localized motor or sensory symptoms with no loss of consciousness","Psychomotor/psychosensory behavior with altered behavior pattern and loss of consciousness","Generalized tonic-clonic activity","Complete loss of muscle tone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes simple partial seizures as localized motor or sensory symptoms with no loss of consciousness.  Why B wrong: Psychomotor/psychosensory features with altered behavior and loss of consciousness describe a complex partial seizure.  Why C wrong: Generalized tonic-clonic activity is a generalized seizure.  Why D wrong: Complete loss of muscle tone describes an atonic seizure."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A complex partial seizure is characterized by which features? (SATA)","options":["It is a type of psychomotor and psychosensory seizure","Cardinal sign: altered behaviour pattern","Temporally the client loses consciousness for a few seconds","Localized motor symptoms with full awareness retained","No loss of consciousness ever"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes complex partial seizures as psychomotor/psychosensory seizures, with altered behavior pattern as the cardinal sign, and the client temporally losing consciousness for a few seconds.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed complex partial features.  Why D wrong: Full awareness with localized motor symptoms describes a simple partial seizure.  Why E wrong: Complex partial seizures do involve loss of consciousness for a few seconds."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"During the aura/onset phase of a seizure (priority action: \"Seizure Aura\"), what should the nurse do if the client is standing or sitting when the aura begins?","options":["Restrain the client immediately","Place the client in a lying position","Insert a tongue blade into the mouth","Administer oxygen during the aura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs that if the client is standing or sitting when the aura begins, place the client in a lying position to prevent injury from a fall.  Why A wrong: The source says do not restrain the client.  Why C wrong: Nothing should be placed in the mouth during a seizure.  Why D wrong: The source says do not administer oxygen during the aura."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"When caring for a client during the aura phase of a seizure, which actions are appropriate? (SATA)","options":["Obtain a seizure history and assess behaviour at the onset/aura","Loosen restrictive clothing and place a pillow or folded blanket under the client's head","Check airway patency and ensure suction equipment and oxygen are available","Raise side rails if the client is in bed and pad side rails and other head objects","Restrain the client's limbs firmly to stop movement"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists obtaining seizure history and assessing behaviour at onset/aura, loosening restrictive clothing and placing padding under the head, checking airway patency with suction/oxygen available, and raising/padding side rails.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed aura/seizure-onset actions.  Why E wrong: The source explicitly says do not restrain the client."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"During an active seizure, which nursing actions are appropriate? (SATA)","options":["Remove all hazardous items near the client","If cyanosis occurs, administer oxygen","Maintain a side-lying position to allow secretions to drain","Watch the activity, type, and duration of the seizure","Restrain the client and place a tongue blade in the mouth"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists removing hazardous items, administering oxygen if cyanosis occurs, maintaining a side-lying position to drain secretions and maintain the airway, and watching the activity, type, and duration of the seizure.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicit \"During Seizure\" actions.  Why E wrong: The source says do not restrain, and nothing should be placed in the mouth during a seizure."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"During a seizure, when should the nurse administer oxygen?","options":["Always continuously during the aura","Only if cyanosis occurs","Never during a seizure","Only after the seizure is over"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer oxygen, if required (specifically if cyanosis occurs) during the seizure.  Why A wrong: Oxygen should not be administered during the aura per the source.  Why C wrong: Oxygen is given during the seizure if cyanosis occurs.  Why D wrong: It is given during the seizure if needed, not only afterward."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"After a seizure (postictal care), appropriate nursing actions include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Observe the client's level of consciousness and behaviour after seizure","Provide rest and maintain continuous supervision","Document the time, duration, and body parts involved","Allow the seizure to proceed and end without interference","Immediately ambulate the client to test motor function"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists observing LOC and behaviour after seizure, providing rest with continuous supervision, documenting time/duration/body parts involved, and allowing the seizure to proceed and end without interference.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed seizure-care actions.  Why E wrong: Immediate ambulation is not advised; the client needs rest and supervision postictally."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Ulnar nerve injury is also known as which deformity?","options":["Ape hand","Claw hand","Wrist drop","Foot drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ulnar nerve injury results in permanent flexion, known as claw hand.  Why A wrong: Ape hand is associated with median nerve injury.  Why C wrong: Wrist drop is associated with radial nerve injury.  Why D wrong: Foot drop relates to peroneal nerve, not ulnar."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Ulnar nerve injury results in inability to do what at the interphalangeal joints?","options":["Oppose the thumb","Extend the forearm","Extend the fingers at the interphalangeal joints","Abduct the shoulder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ulnar nerve injury causes inability to extend fingers at the interphalangeal joints, resulting in permanent flexion (claw hand).  Why A wrong: Inability to oppose the thumb is a median nerve sign (ape hand).  Why B wrong: Inability to extend the forearm relates to radial nerve injury.  Why D wrong: Shoulder abduction is not the function described."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Median nerve injury is also known by which name and presents with which deformity?","options":["Ape hand, with inability to oppose the thumb","Claw hand, with permanent finger flexion","Wrist drop, with inability to extend the wrist","Foot drop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states median nerve injury is also known as ape hand, in which there is inability to oppose the thumb.  Why B wrong: Claw hand is ulnar nerve injury.  Why C wrong: Wrist drop is radial nerve injury.  Why D wrong: Foot drop relates to the peroneal nerve."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Radial nerve injury is also known as wrist drop and presents with:","options":["Inability to oppose the thumb","Inability to extend the wrist/hand and inability to fully extend the forearm","Permanent flexion of all fingers","Facial droop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states radial nerve injury (wrist drop) causes inability to extend the wrist/hand and inability to fully extend the forearm.  Why A wrong: Inability to oppose the thumb is median nerve (ape hand).  Why C wrong: Permanent finger flexion is ulnar nerve (claw hand).  Why D wrong: Facial droop relates to Bell's palsy, not radial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Trigeminal neuralgia is a sensory disorder of which cranial nerve?","options":["Third (oculomotor) cranial nerve","Fifth (trigeminal) cranial nerve","Seventh (facial) cranial nerve","Tenth (vagus) cranial nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes trigeminal neuralgia as a sensory disorder of the fifth or trigeminal cranial nerve (CN V).  Why A wrong: The oculomotor nerve is CN III.  Why C wrong: The facial nerve (CN VII) is involved in Bell's palsy.  Why D wrong: The vagus nerve is CN X."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Trigeminal neuralgia is characterized by what kind of pain?","options":["Dull, constant aching pain","Intense, knife-like sharp facial pain along the branches of the nerve","Burning pain in the lower extremities","Throbbing pain behind the eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes trigeminal neuralgia as intense knife-like sharp facial pain along the branches of the nerve.  Why A wrong: Dull constant aching is not the described pain quality.  Why C wrong: Lower-extremity pain is not part of trigeminal neuralgia.  Why D wrong: Retro-orbital throbbing is not the described pain."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, signs and symptoms of trigeminal neuralgia include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Unilateral or bilateral burning knife-like pain on lips, cheeks & teeth lasting for 1 to 15 minutes (cardinal sign)","Pain more intense when eating, brushing, hot and cold drinking","Sudden closure of an eye, twitching of mouth and cheek","Facial droop with inability to close the eye","Loss of consciousness"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists unilateral/bilateral burning knife-like pain on lips, cheeks & teeth lasting 1–15 minutes (cardinal sign), pain more intense with eating/brushing/hot and cold drinks, and sudden closure of an eye with twitching of mouth and cheek.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed trigeminal neuralgia signs.  Why D wrong: Facial droop with inability to close the eye is a feature of Bell's palsy.  Why E wrong: Loss of consciousness is not a trigeminal neuralgia symptom."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Regarding nursing care for trigeminal neuralgia, the source advises the client to:","options":["Eat very hot or very cold foods to test sensation","Chew food on the unaffected side and avoid hot/cold foods and fluids","Apply firm pressure massage to the painful area","Brush the affected teeth vigorously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to advise the client to chew food on the unaffected side and avoid hot foods and fluids (first choice of intervention).  Why A wrong: Hot and cold foods trigger pain and should be avoided.  Why C wrong: Firm massage is not advised and can trigger pain.  Why D wrong: Vigorous brushing on the affected side can precipitate pain."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia, per the source, is:","options":["Carbamazepine","Lorazepam","Penicillin G","Phenytoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer medication as per order — carbamazepine is the drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia.  Why B wrong: Lorazepam is the drug of choice for status epilepticus.  Why C wrong: Penicillin G is an antibiotic for meningitis.  Why D wrong: Phenytoin is not listed as the drug of choice here."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Bell's palsy is also known as:","options":["Trigeminal neuralgia","Facial paralysis","Claw hand","Wrist drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Bell's palsy is also known as facial paralysis.  Why A wrong: Trigeminal neuralgia is a separate CN V sensory disorder.  Why C wrong: Claw hand is ulnar nerve injury.  Why D wrong: Wrist drop is radial nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Bell's palsy is caused by inflammation/trauma or a lesion in which cranial nerve?","options":["CN V (trigeminal nerve)","CN VII (facial nerve)","CN III (oculomotor nerve)","CN VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Bell's palsy is due to inflamed/trauma or lesion in CN VII (facial cranial nerve).  Why A wrong: CN V is the trigeminal nerve (trigeminal neuralgia).  Why C wrong: CN III is the oculomotor nerve.  Why D wrong: CN VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, the exact cause of Bell's palsy is:","options":["Diabetes mellitus","Bacterial meningitis","Unknown","A genetic mutation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the exact cause of Bell's palsy is unknown.  Why A wrong: Diabetes is not listed as the cause.  Why B wrong: Bacterial meningitis is unrelated.  Why D wrong: A genetic mutation is not given as the cause."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of Bell's palsy? (SATA)","options":["Facial pain, drooping of the mouth on the affected side (cardinal sign)","Difficulty with articulation, diminished blinking, reflux of tears","Inability to close the eyes, nose eyebrows smile, or puff out the cheeks","Increased or decreased lacrimation and flaccid facial muscles","Knife-like sharp pain triggered by chewing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists facial pain, drooping of the mouth on the affected side (cardinal sign), difficulty with articulation, diminished blinking, reflux of tears, inability to close the eyes/nose eyebrows/smile or puff out the cheeks, and increased/decreased lacrimation with flaccid facial muscles.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed Bell's palsy signs.  Why E wrong: Knife-like sharp pain on chewing describes trigeminal neuralgia, not Bell's palsy."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Nursing care for Bell's palsy includes which interventions? (SATA)","options":["Teach simple exercise of facial muscles such as blowing through pursed lips","Provide artificial tears, manually closing the eyelids, and use sunglasses/eye shields to prevent loss of corneal vision or irritation such as using artificial tears","Initiate general nursing care","Administer carbamazepine as the drug of choice","Restrict all fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists teaching simple facial-muscle exercises (e.g., blowing through pursed lips), providing artificial tears/manually closing eyelids/using sunglasses and eye shields to protect the cornea, and initiating general nursing care.  Why A/B/C correct: All are explicitly listed Bell's palsy nursing interventions.  Why D wrong: Carbamazepine is the drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia, not the listed Bell's palsy intervention.  Why E wrong: Fluid restriction is not a listed intervention."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Why is eye protection (artificial tears, eye shields, manual eyelid closure) important in Bell's palsy?","options":["To reduce facial pain on chewing","To prevent loss of corneal vision or irritation because the eye cannot close","To improve hearing","To prevent seizures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states eye protection measures are used to prevent loss of corneal vision or irritation, since the affected eye cannot close (diminished blinking).  Why A wrong: Chewing pain relates to trigeminal neuralgia.  Why C wrong: Hearing is not the concern in Bell's palsy eye care.  Why D wrong: Seizure prevention is unrelated to eye care."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) The basic structural and functional unit of the nervous system is the:","options":["Glia","Neuron","Synapse","Dendrite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table answers that the structural and functional unit of the nervous system is the neuron.  Why A wrong: Glia are supporting cells, not the functional unit.  Why C wrong: The synapse is a junction, not the unit.  Why D wrong: The dendrite is only a part of a neuron."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) The brainstem includes which structures?","options":["Cerebrum, cerebellum, and thalamus","Midbrain, pons & medulla","Frontal, temporal, and parietal lobes","Hypothalamus and pituitary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table states the brainstem includes the midbrain, pons & medulla.  Why A wrong: These are separate higher structures, not the brainstem.  Why C wrong: These are cerebral cortical lobes.  Why D wrong: The hypothalamus and pituitary are not the brainstem components listed."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) The largest part of the brain that is responsible for the higher functions of the nervous system is the:","options":["Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Cerebrum","Medulla oblongata"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table answers that the cerebrum is the largest part of the brain / center of higher functions.  Why A wrong: The cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement, posture & balance.  Why B wrong: The hypothalamus regulates autonomic functions, not \"higher functions.\"  Why D wrong: The medulla oblongata is part of the brainstem."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Which brain structure is responsible for coordination of voluntary muscle movements, posture, and balance?","options":["Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Thalamus","Hypothalamus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table states the cerebellum is responsible for coordination of voluntary muscle movements, posture, and balance.  Why B wrong: The cerebrum handles higher functions.  Why C wrong: The thalamus is a relay station, not the balance/coordination center.  Why D wrong: The hypothalamus controls autonomic/endocrine functions."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) \"The 12th cranial nerve\" is also known as the:","options":["Optic nerve","Spinal accessory nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Trochlear nerve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table answers CN 12 (the X-bone is 12th cranial nerve item) as the hypoglossal nerve.  Why A wrong: The optic nerve is CN II.  Why B wrong: The spinal accessory nerve is CN XI.  Why D wrong: The trochlear nerve is CN IV."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Ascending paralysis seen in which disorder is matched in the key points?","options":["Multiple sclerosis","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS)","Parkinson's disease","Bell's palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches ascending paralysis with GBS (Guillain-Barré syndrome).  Why A wrong: Multiple sclerosis has a different presentation pattern.  Why C wrong: Parkinson's disease presents with tremor/rigidity/bradykinesia.  Why D wrong: Bell's palsy is facial paralysis, not ascending paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) The drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia, as matched in the key points, is:","options":["Levodopa","Carbamazepine","Acetylcholine","Dopamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches the drug of choice for trigeminal neuralgia with carbamazepine.  Why A wrong: Levodopa is used in Parkinson's disease.  Why C wrong: Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter, not the drug of choice.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is the neurotransmitter deficient in Parkinson's disease."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Compression of which nerve in the temporal lobe will lead to which condition?","options":["Median nerve / claw hand","Facial nerve in the temporal lobe / Bell's palsy","Optic nerve / blindness","Ulnar nerve / wrist drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches compression of the facial nerve in the temporal lobe with Bell's palsy.  Why A wrong: Claw hand is from ulnar nerve injury, not temporal-lobe facial nerve compression.  Why C wrong: Optic nerve issues cause visual field loss, a separate item.  Why D wrong: Wrist drop is from radial nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) A positive Babinski sign (Babinski reflex) in an adult older than 1 year indicates:","options":["Normal finding","Positive Babinski reflex (an abnormal/upper motor neuron sign in adults)","Cerebellar disease only","Peripheral nerve injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table notes that the Babinski reflex is normal up to about 1 year, but its presence after 1 year of age (adult) is abnormal — a positive Babinski reflex.  Why A wrong: It is abnormal in an adult, not normal.  Why C wrong: It is an upper motor neuron sign, not specific to cerebellar disease.  Why D wrong: It indicates a central (upper motor neuron) lesion, not a peripheral nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Cushing's triad / increased ICP is associated with which set of vital-sign changes?","options":["Tachycardia, hypotension, regular respiration","Altered LOC, decreased LOC, and bradycardia","Fever, increased WBC, and tachypnea","Hypoglycemia and seizures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches increased ICP with altered/decreased LOC (level of consciousness) and bradycardia.  Why A wrong: Increased ICP classically causes bradycardia, not tachycardia.  Why C wrong: Fever and increased WBC relate to infection, not ICP triad.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia/seizures are unrelated to the ICP item described."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Carpal tunnel syndrome results from compression of which nerve?","options":["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Facial nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table states carpal tunnel syndrome involves the median nerve.  Why A wrong: Ulnar nerve injury causes claw hand.  Why C wrong: Radial nerve injury causes wrist drop.  Why D wrong: The facial nerve relates to Bell's palsy."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Which is the largest cranial nerve?","options":["Olfactory nerve (CN I)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Optic nerve (CN II)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches the largest cranial nerve with the trigeminal nerve (CN V).  Why A wrong: The olfactory nerve is not the largest.  Why C wrong: The facial nerve is not the largest cranial nerve.  Why D wrong: The optic nerve is not identified as the largest in the key points."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Which lesion produces bitemporal hemianopia (loss of half of the visual field)?","options":["Optic chiasma lesion","Frontal lobe lesion","Cerebellar lesion","Medulla lesion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches hemianopia/loss of half of the visual field with an optic chiasma lesion.  Why B wrong: Frontal lobe lesions affect motor and behavior, not produce bitemporal hemianopia.  Why C wrong: Cerebellar lesions affect coordination.  Why D wrong: Medulla lesions affect vital autonomic centers."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Which neurotransmitter is deficient/implicated in Parkinson's disease?","options":["Acetylcholine","Dopamine","GABA","Serotonin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches Parkinson's disease with dopamine (deficiency).  Why A wrong: Acetylcholine is implicated separately (e.g., neuromuscular transmission/myasthenia).  Why C wrong: GABA is not the Parkinson's neurotransmitter listed.  Why D wrong: Serotonin is not the implicated neurotransmitter for Parkinson's."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Tremor at rest, rigidity, and bradykinesia with a mask-like face are characteristic of which disease?","options":["Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson's disease","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Bell's palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia (mask-like face) with Parkinson's disease.  Why A wrong: MS presents with demyelinating, relapsing patterns rather than the classic Parkinson triad.  Why C wrong: GBS presents with ascending paralysis.  Why D wrong: Bell's palsy is facial paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is abbreviated as:","options":["MS","ALS","GBS","CVA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table identifies amyotrophic lateral sclerosis as ALS.  Why A wrong: MS is multiple sclerosis.  Why C wrong: GBS is Guillain-Barré syndrome.  Why D wrong: CVA is cerebrovascular accident."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) The sudden death of brain cells due to lack of oxygen, caused by blockage of blood flow or rupture of an artery to the brain, is known as:","options":["Embolus","Stroke / CVA (cerebrovascular accident)","Seizure","Meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches this description with stroke/CVA (cerebrovascular accident).  Why A wrong: An embolus is a clot that travels; it can cause stroke but is not the stroke itself.  Why C wrong: A seizure is abnormal electrical activity, not loss of blood flow.  Why D wrong: Meningitis is meningeal inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Difficulty in speaking due to weakness of muscles is termed:","options":["Aphasia","Dysarthria","Apraxia","Dysphagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches dysarthria with difficulty in speaking due to weakness of muscles.  Why A wrong: Aphasia is loss of ability to understand or express speech (a language disorder), listed separately.  Why C wrong: Apraxia is the inability to carry out learned purposeful movements.  Why D wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing, not speaking."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Difficulty in swallowing is termed:","options":["Dysphagia","Dysarthria","Aphasia","Apraxia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table identifies dysphagia as difficulty in swallowing (matched among the swallowing/eating items, e.g., difficulty with ADL).  Why B wrong: Dysarthria is difficulty speaking due to muscle weakness.  Why C wrong: Aphasia is a language disorder.  Why D wrong: Apraxia is the inability to perform learned movements."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Loss of memory is known as:","options":["Aphasia","Amnesia","Apraxia","Ataxia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches loss of memory with amnesia.  Why A wrong: Aphasia is a language/speech disorder.  Why C wrong: Apraxia is inability to perform learned movements.  Why D wrong: Ataxia is loss of coordination."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Pill-rolling tremor (resting tremor in hands) is seen in which disease?","options":["Parkinson's disease","Multiple sclerosis","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches pill-rolling tremor (resting tremor) with Parkinson's disease.  Why B wrong: MS is a demyelinating disorder, not classically pill-rolling tremor.  Why C wrong: GBS presents with ascending paralysis.  Why D wrong: ALS is a motor neuron disease without the classic pill-rolling tremor."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"(Rapid revision) Which scale is used to assess the depth/level of consciousness and is matched in the key points?","options":["Apgar score","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","Braden scale","Bristol stool scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key-points table matches assessment of level/depth of consciousness with the GCS (Glasgow Coma Scale).  Why A wrong: The Apgar score assesses newborn status at birth.  Why C wrong: The Braden scale assesses pressure-injury risk.  Why D wrong: The Bristol stool scale assesses stool form."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A bone is best defined, per the musculoskeletal anatomy introduction, as:","options":["Soft tissue connecting two muscles","A hard, dense connective tissue that forms the framework of the body (the bones, joints, muscles)","A fluid-filled cavity","The outer layer of skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source introduces the musculoskeletal system as consisting of bones of the skeleton, their joints, and the skeletal muscles that move the body (bone, joint, muscle).  Why A wrong: Tendons, not bones, connect muscle to bone.  Why C wrong: A fluid-filled cavity is not a definition of bone.  Why D wrong: The outer layer of skin is the epidermis, unrelated to bone."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"According to the source, which are functions of the skeletal system? (SATA)","options":["Support — to provide a framework for the body and attachment for muscles & tendons","Protection — forming the boundaries of the cranial, thoracic, and pelvic cavities, protecting the organs they contain","Blood cell production — haematopoiesis, production of blood cells in red bone marrow","Mineral storage — especially calcium & fat","Production of digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists support (framework/attachment for muscles & tendons), protection (boundaries of cranial/thoracic/pelvic cavities protecting organs), haematopoiesis (blood cell production in red bone marrow), and mineral storage (especially calcium & fat).  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed skeletal-system functions.  Why E wrong: Production of digestive enzymes is a function of the digestive system, not the skeleton."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"Movement, as a function of the skeletal system, is described in the source as:","options":["Spontaneous movement of bones alone","Movement of the body as muscles attached to bones move the bones, forming joints that move freely by muscles","Movement that occurs only in smooth muscle","A function performed independently of muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states movement is the body's movement as muscles attached to bones move them, with joints permitting free movement by muscles.  Why A wrong: Bones do not move on their own; muscles move them.  Why C wrong: Skeletal movement involves skeletal muscle, not smooth muscle.  Why D wrong: Movement is dependent on muscle attachment, not independent."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"An anatomical plane is best defined as:","options":["A line of muscle attachment","An imaginary flat surface (plane) used to transect the body, used to describe the location of structures or the direction of movements, in human and animal anatomy","A bony prominence","A type of joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines an anatomical plane as an imaginary flat surface used to transect the body, to describe the location of structures or direction of movements in human and animal anatomy.  Why A wrong: A line of muscle attachment is not an anatomical plane.  Why C wrong: A bony prominence is a bone landmark, not a plane.  Why D wrong: A joint is an articulation, not a plane."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"How many principal anatomical planes are described in the source?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states three principal planes are used (sagittal, coronal, and transverse).  Why A wrong: There are three, not two.  Why C wrong: Four exceeds the stated number.  Why D wrong: Five exceeds the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The sagittal plane divides the body into which portions?","options":["Superior and inferior","Right and left (lateral and medial; longitudinal/anteroposterior plane)","Anterior and posterior","Proximal and distal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sagittal plane (also called lateral, vertical, longitudinal, anteroposterior plane) divides the body into right and left portions; the midsagittal divides the body into equal left and right halves.  Why A wrong: Superior/inferior division is made by the transverse plane.  Why C wrong: Anterior/posterior division is made by the coronal plane.  Why D wrong: Proximal/distal are directional terms, not a sagittal division."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"A midsagittal (median) plane divides the body into:","options":["Unequal anterior and posterior portions","Equal left and right halves","Equal upper and lower halves","Lateral and medial unequal portions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is a sagittal plane that divides the body into equal left-and-right portions (midsagittal/median).  Why A wrong: Anterior/posterior division belongs to the coronal plane.  Why C wrong: Upper/lower division belongs to the transverse plane.  Why D wrong: The midsagittal plane divides into equal halves, not unequal lateral/medial portions."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The coronal (frontal) plane divides the body into which portions?","options":["Right and left","Front (anterior) and back (posterior) portions","Upper and lower","Proximal and distal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the coronal plane (frontal, vertical plane) divides the body into anterior (front) and posterior (back) portions.  Why A wrong: Right/left division is the sagittal plane.  Why C wrong: Upper/lower division is the transverse plane.  Why D wrong: Proximal/distal are directional terms, not a coronal division."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Types of meningitis (bacterial, viral, tuberculous)…","question":"The transverse plane (also called horizontal/axial plane) divides the body into which portions?","options":["Right and left","Anterior and posterior","Cranial (head) and caudal (tail) — upper and lower portions","Medial and lateral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the transverse plane (axial/horizontal) divides the body into cranial (head) and caudal (tail), i.e., upper and lower portions.  Why A wrong: Right/left is the sagittal plane.  Why B wrong: Anterior/posterior is the coronal plane.  Why D wrong: Medial/lateral are directional terms, not a transverse division."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The anatomical term \"ventral\" refers to which direction?","options":["Toward the back or spine","Toward the front or belly","Toward the dorsal side","Below"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ventral means toward the front or belly (e.g., the sternum is anterior/ventral to the heart).  Why A wrong: Toward the back or spine is \"dorsal.\"  Why C wrong: \"Toward the dorsal side\" describes posterior, not ventral.  Why D wrong: \"Below\" is the meaning of inferior."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which anatomical term means \"toward the back or spine\"?","options":["Ventral","Anterior","Dorsal","Superficial"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsal = toward the back or spine; the vertebral column is dorsal to the aorta.  Why A wrong: Ventral is toward the front/belly.  Why B wrong: Anterior is toward the front.  Why D wrong: Superficial means closer to the body surface."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The term \"anterior\" is best illustrated by which example from the source?","options":["The liver is inferior to the diaphragm","The sternum is anterior to the heart","The eyes are lateral to the nose","The elbow is proximal to the wrist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anterior means toward the front of the body; the sternum is anterior to the heart.  Why A wrong: That example illustrates \"inferior.\"  Why C wrong: That illustrates \"lateral.\"  Why D wrong: That illustrates \"proximal.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which statement correctly matches the term \"posterior\"?","options":["The oesophagus is posterior to the trachea","The heart is superior to the diaphragm","The palm and sole face anteriorly","The fingernails are at the distal ends of the fingers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Posterior means toward the dorsal/back side; the oesophagus lies posterior to the trachea.  Why B wrong: That example illustrates \"superior.\"  Why C wrong: That illustrates \"supination.\"  Why D wrong: That illustrates \"distal.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"\"Superior\" and \"inferior\" are correctly defined as:","options":["Superior = below; Inferior = above","Superior = above; Inferior = below","Superior = toward midline; Inferior = away from midline","Superior = closer to surface; Inferior = deeper"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Superior means above (heart is superior to the diaphragm) and inferior means below (liver is inferior to the diaphragm).  Why A wrong: It reverses the two terms.  Why C wrong: Midline relations describe medial/lateral.  Why D wrong: Surface vs depth relations describe superficial/deep."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The term \"medial\" is illustrated by which example?","options":["The eyes are lateral to the nose","The heart is medial to the lungs","The elbow is proximal to the wrist","The bones are deep to the muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medial means toward the mid-sagittal plane; the heart lies medial to the lungs.  Why A wrong: That illustrates \"lateral.\"  Why C wrong: That illustrates \"proximal.\"  Why D wrong: That illustrates \"deep.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"\"Lateral\" means:","options":["Toward the mid-sagittal plane","Away from the mid-sagittal plane","Closer to the point of origin","Farther from the body surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lateral means away from the mid-sagittal plane (eyes are lateral to the nose).  Why A wrong: Toward the mid-sagittal plane is \"medial.\"  Why C wrong: Closer to the point of attachment/origin is \"proximal.\"  Why D wrong: Farther from the body surface is \"deep.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which pair correctly defines proximal vs distal?","options":["Proximal = closer to the point of attachment; Distal = farther from the point of attachment","Proximal = farther from origin; Distal = closer to origin","Proximal = above; Distal = below","Proximal = toward front; Distal = toward back"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Proximal is closer to the point of attachment or origin (elbow proximal to wrist); distal is farther from it (fingernails are at the distal ends of the fingers).  Why B wrong: It reverses the definitions.  Why C wrong: Above/below is superior/inferior.  Why D wrong: Front/back is ventral/dorsal."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which terms describe \"closer to the body surface\" versus \"farther from the body surface\"?","options":["Superficial / Deep","Proximal / Distal","Medial / Lateral","Pronation / Supination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Superficial = closer to the body surface; Deep = farther from the body surface (bones are deep to the muscles).  Why B wrong: Proximal/distal refer to distance from a point of attachment.  Why C wrong: Medial/lateral refer to the midline.  Why D wrong: Pronation/supination refer to rotation of the forearm/foot."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Pronation of the forearm results in the palm facing which way?","options":["Anteriorly","Posteriorly","Medially","Superiorly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pronation is rotation of the forearm/foot so that the palm and sole face posteriorly.  Why A wrong: Palm facing anteriorly is supination.  Why C/D wrong: Pronation is described by posterior-facing palm, not medial or superior."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"In supination the palm and sole face:","options":["Posteriorly","Anteriorly","Downward","Laterally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Supination is rotation of the forearm/foot so the palm and sole face anteriorly.  Why A wrong: Posterior-facing palm is pronation.  Why C/D wrong: The source defines supination as palm/sole facing anteriorly."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A movement that decreases the angle of a joint, bringing bones closer together, is termed:","options":["Extension","Flexion","Abduction","Pronation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flexion is the bending movement shown opposite extension in the body-movement figures.  Why A wrong: Extension is the straightening movement (increasing the joint angle).  Why C wrong: Abduction is movement away from the body.  Why D wrong: Pronation is rotation of the forearm."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Abduction is defined as movement that is:","options":["Toward the body (toward midline)","Away from the body (away from midline)","Rotation toward the midline","Bending of a joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels abduction as \"movement away from the body.\"  Why A wrong: Movement toward the body is adduction.  Why C wrong: Rotation toward midline is medial rotation.  Why D wrong: Bending of a joint is flexion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Adduction refers to movement:","options":["Away from the body","Toward the body","Of the foot raising the toes","Pointing the toes downward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels adduction as \"movement toward the body.\"  Why A wrong: Away from the body is abduction.  Why C wrong: Raising the toes is dorsiflexion.  Why D wrong: Pointing toes down is plantar flexion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which foot movement points the toes downward (sole facing posteriorly/away)?","options":["Dorsiflexion","Plantar flexion","Medial rotation","Lateral rotation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plantar flexion is the foot movement that points the toes downward.  Why A wrong: Dorsiflexion raises the foot/toes upward.  Why C/D wrong: Medial and lateral rotation are rotational movements, not foot flexion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Bone is best classified as which type of connective tissue?","options":["Soft loose connective tissue","A rigid organ that is part of the skeleton","Epithelial tissue","Avascular cartilaginous tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bone is a rigid organ that constitutes part of the skeleton; bone (osseous tissue) is an example of connective tissue.  Why A wrong: Bone is rigid, not soft loose tissue.  Why C wrong: Bone is connective tissue, not epithelial.  Why D wrong: Bone is highly vascular and contains osseous tissue, not avascular cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which of the following are functions of bones? (Select all that apply)","options":["Protect the organs of the body","Produce red and white blood cells","Store minerals","Provide structure and support for the body","Secrete digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bones protect organs, produce red and white blood cells, store minerals, and provide structure and support for the body and enable mobility.  Why E wrong: Secreting digestive enzymes is not a function of bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Bone tissue is categorised into which two types?","options":["Compact (dense) bone and spongy (cancellous) bone","Hard bone and soft bone","Cortical bone and cartilage bone","Primary bone and secondary bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bones are categorised as compact (dense) bone and spongy (cancellous) bone.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the classification given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which is the major mineral responsible for bone strength?","options":["Iron","Calcium (hydroxyapatite)","Sodium","Potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium (hydroxyapatite) and phosphate are the major minerals for bone strength.  Why A/C/D wrong: Iron, sodium, and potassium are not named as the major bone-strength mineral."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Tendon and ligament differ in that:","options":["Tendons bind bone to bone; ligaments bind muscle to bone","Tendons bind muscle to bone; ligaments bind bone to bone","Both bind only muscle to muscle","Both bind only skin to bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tendon binds muscle to bone (injury to a tendon = strain); a ligament binds bone to bone (injury to a ligament = sprain).  Why A wrong: It reverses the definitions.  Why C/D wrong: Neither binds muscle-to-muscle or skin-to-bone per the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Injury to a ligament is specifically termed a:","options":["Strain","Sprain","Fracture","Dislocation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states injury to a ligament is a sprain.  Why A wrong: Injury to a tendon is a strain.  Why C/D wrong: Fracture and dislocation are not the terms given for ligament injury."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The bone matrix is composed mainly of water plus collagen fibres and crystallised mineral salt. Which mineral salt is most common in the matrix?","options":["Sodium chloride","Calcium (with phosphate)","Magnesium carbonate","Potassium sulfate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The matrix contains crystallised mineral salt — for example calcium (most common) and phosphates.  Why A/C/D wrong: These salts are not identified as the main matrix component."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Bone's flexibility depends mainly on which component?","options":["Hydroxyapatite","Collagen fibres","Calcium phosphate","Mineral salts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bone's flexibility depends on collagen fibres.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hydroxyapatite, calcium phosphate, and mineral salts contribute to hardness/strength, not flexibility."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which four types of bone cells are present in bone tissue? (Select all that apply)","options":["Osteoblasts","Osteoclasts","Osteocytes","Osteogenic cells","Chondrocytes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four bone cells are osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteocytes, and osteogenic cells.  Why E wrong: Chondrocytes are cartilage cells, not bone cells."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which bone cells are the bone-building cells that produce new bone forming cells and secrete the organic and inorganic components of bone?","options":["Osteoclasts","Osteoblasts","Osteocytes","Osteogenic cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osteoblasts are the bone-building cells; they produce new bone-forming cells and secrete the organic and inorganic components of bone matrix.  Why A wrong: Osteoclasts resorb bone.  Why C wrong: Osteocytes are mature cells that no longer secrete matrix.  Why D wrong: Osteogenic cells are stem cells."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The function of osteoclasts is:","options":["Secreting bone matrix","Re-absorption (resorption) of bone, repair, or remodelling","Storing calcium only","Forming cartilage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osteoclasts function in the re-absorption of bone, or repair and remodelling of bones; they are found in endosteum.  Why A wrong: Secreting matrix is the osteoblast role.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the osteoclast functions described."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Osteocytes are best described as:","options":["Bone-building cells","Mature bone cells that no longer secrete matrix","Stem cells of bone","Cells that resorb bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osteocytes are mature bone cells that no longer secrete matrix.  Why A wrong: Bone-building cells are osteoblasts.  Why C wrong: Stem cells of bone are osteogenic cells.  Why D wrong: Resorbing cells are osteoclasts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Osteogenic cells are:","options":["The stem cells of bone","Mature non-dividing bone cells","Bone-resorbing cells","Cartilage-forming cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Osteogenic cells are the stem cells of bone.  Why B wrong: Mature non-dividing cells are osteocytes.  Why C wrong: Resorbing cells are osteoclasts.  Why D wrong: Cartilage-forming cells are chondroblasts/chondrocytes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"In the structure of a long bone, the main part (shaft) is called the:","options":["Epiphysis","Diaphysis","Metaphysis","Periosteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diaphysis is the main part/shaft of the bone, a functional unit (osteon); it is also called the shaft of body.  Why A wrong: The epiphysis is the proximal/distal end.  Why C wrong: The metaphysis is the region between diaphysis and epiphysis.  Why D wrong: The periosteum is the outer fibrous layer."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The epiphysis of a long bone is characterised by which feature?","options":["It is the proximal and distal end of the bone (functional unit: trabecula) and contains red bone marrow","It is hollow and contains yellow marrow","It is the fibrous covering of the bone","It is where descending arteries form \"hair-pin\" bends"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epiphysis is the proximal and distal end (functional unit trabecula), is spongy/cancellous bone, and is the part of the long bone where red bone marrow and other adult red blood cells are formed.  Why B wrong: The hollow yellow-marrow region is the medullary cavity within the diaphysis.  Why C wrong: The fibrous covering is the periosteum.  Why D wrong: \"Hair-pin\" bends of arteries occur at the metaphysis."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which long-bone region is the most common site of osteomyelitis in children, where arteries form \"hair-pin\" bends?","options":["Diaphysis","Epiphysis","Metaphysis","Endosteum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The metaphysis lies between diaphysis and epiphysis; it is richly supplied through arteries forming \"hair-pin\" bends and is the most common site of osteomyelitis in children.  Why A/B wrong: Diaphysis and epiphysis are not described as the typical osteomyelitis site.  Why D wrong: The endosteum is the inner lining of the medullary cavity."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The periosteum is best described as:","options":["The inner lining of the medullary cavity","The outer fibrous layer of dense irregular connective tissue covering the bone","The shaft of a long bone","The cavity storing yellow marrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The periosteum is the outer fibrous layer of dense irregular connective tissue; it covers the bone except over articular cartilage and is attached by collagen fibres (Sharpey's fibres).  Why A wrong: The inner lining of the medullary cavity is the endosteum.  Why C wrong: The shaft is the diaphysis.  Why D wrong: The yellow-marrow cavity is the medullary cavity."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The collagen fibres that attach the periosteum to the underlying bone are known as:","options":["Haversian fibres","Sharpey's (perforating) fibres","Trabeculae","Lamellae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The periosteum is attached to the bone by collagen fibres called Sharpey's (perforating) fibres.  Why A wrong: Haversian refers to the central canal system.  Why C wrong: Trabeculae are spongy bone struts.  Why D wrong: Lamellae are layers of bone matrix."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The medullary cavity (marrow cavity) of bone shafts:","options":["Contains red marrow in adults","Is the central cavity of the bone shaft where yellow marrow (adipose tissue) is stored","Lies within the epiphysis","Is lined by periosteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The medullary/marrow cavity is the central (inner most) part of the bone shaft where yellow marrow (adipose tissue) is stored.  Why A wrong: It stores yellow marrow; red marrow is found in spongy bone of the epiphysis.  Why C wrong: It lies within the diaphysis, not the epiphysis.  Why D wrong: Its inner lining is the endosteum, not periosteum."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The endosteum is:","options":["The outer fibrous covering of bone","A single layer of cells lining the medullary cavity, made of a small amount of connective tissue","The functional unit of compact bone","The cartilage at the joint surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endosteum is a single layer of cells with a small amount of connective tissue that lines the marrow cavity.  Why A wrong: The outer covering is the periosteum.  Why C wrong: The functional unit of compact bone is the osteon.  Why D wrong: Joint-surface cartilage is articular cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which statement comparing bone and cartilage is correct?","options":["Bone is highly vascular; cartilage is avascular (no blood vessels)","Both bone and cartilage are equally vascular","Bone is avascular; cartilage is highly vascular","Neither contains blood vessels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bone is very rich, vascular connective tissue with abundant blood vessels, whereas cartilage is avascular (blood vessels are absent).  Why B/C/D wrong: They misstate the vascularity contrast given in the table."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Regarding cell housing, bone differs from cartilage in that:","options":["Bone lacunae contain chondrocytes","Bone lacunae are connected by canaliculi between osteocytes, while cartilage lacunae are NOT connected (contain chondrocytes)","Cartilage lacunae are connected by canaliculi","Neither contains lacunae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In bone, lacunae are connected by canaliculi between osteocytes; in cartilage, lacunae do not contain canaliculi connections (they house chondrocytes).  Why A wrong: Bone lacunae house osteocytes, not chondrocytes.  Why C wrong: Cartilage lacunae are not connected by canaliculi per the table.  Why D wrong: Both have lacunae."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Cartilage is composed mainly of:","options":["Calcium salts and collagen with phosphorus","Proteins and sugars, making up a flexible connective tissue","Hydroxyapatite crystals","Adipose tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cartilage is a firm, flexible connective tissue made up of proteins and sugars; it is flexible connective tissue.  Why A wrong: Calcium salts/phosphorus describe bone composition.  Why C wrong: Hydroxyapatite is a bone mineral.  Why D wrong: Adipose tissue is fat, not cartilage matrix."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Cartilage provides which functional benefit at joints?","options":["Rigid support and protection of organs","Flexibility to the body and a smooth/low-friction articulating surface for joints","Mineral storage for the blood","Production of blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cartilage provides flexibility to the body and a smooth articulating surface of joints (found in the ear, nose, trachea, ribs, and joints).  Why A wrong: Rigid support and organ protection is a bone function.  Why C/D wrong: Mineral storage and blood-cell production are bone functions."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Healthy bone tissue requires adequate dietary calcium plus which vitamins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Vitamin K","Vitamin B12"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states healthy bone requires adequate dietary calcium and vitamins A, C and D.  Why D/E wrong: Vitamins K and B12 are not listed in the source for bone health."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Besides being the major mineral for bone strength, calcium facilitates the absorption of which other minerals into the bone?","options":["Only sodium and potassium","Iron and magnesium (phosphate, iron and magnesium)","Iodine and fluoride","Copper and zinc"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium hydroxyapatite (a building block of bone and tooth enamel) makes smaller amounts of other minerals such as phosphate, iron and magnesium more essential for mineralisation of the bone.  Why A/C/D wrong: These minerals are not named in the source point."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Vitamin A is essential for which bone-related process?","options":["Calcium and phosphate absorption from the intestinal tract","Osteoblast activity","Collagen synthesis","Red marrow formation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A is essential for osteoblast activity.  Why A wrong: That is the role of vitamin D.  Why C wrong: Collagen synthesis is the role of vitamin C.  Why D wrong: Marrow formation is not the listed role of vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Vitamin C is essential for bone health because it is needed for:","options":["Calcium absorption","Collagen synthesis","Osteoclast activity","Phosphate excretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin C is essential for collagen synthesis.  Why A wrong: Calcium absorption is associated with vitamin D.  Why C/D wrong: Not the role given for vitamin C."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Vitamin D is essential for bone health primarily because it:","options":["Promotes collagen synthesis","Stimulates osteoblast activity","Is essential for calcium and phosphate absorption from the intestinal tract","Is the major mineral of bone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin D is essential for calcium and phosphate absorption from the intestinal tract.  Why A wrong: Collagen synthesis is vitamin C's role.  Why B wrong: Osteoblast activity is vitamin A's role.  Why D wrong: Calcium is the major mineral, not vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Bones associated with the paranasal sinuses (air-filled spaces), such as the facial bones, are classified as:","options":["Pneumatic bones","Long bones","Flat bones","Sesamoid bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumatic bones contain paranasal sinuses (air-filled spaces).  Why B wrong: Long bones have a shaft (e.g., femur, humerus).  Why C wrong: Flat bones are thin (e.g., frontal bone).  Why D wrong: Sesamoid bones (e.g., patella) develop within tendons."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which of these is correctly identified as a long bone?","options":["Carpal bone","Femur (and humerus, tibia, fibula, radius, ulna, metacarpal, metatarsal, phalanges)","Frontal bone","Patella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Long bones are shape-like rod or shaft with rounded ends — e.g., femur, humerus, tibia/fibula, radius/ulna, metacarpal, metatarsal, phalanges.  Why A wrong: The carpal is a short bone.  Why C wrong: The frontal bone is a flat bone.  Why D wrong: The patella is a sesamoid bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Short bones are exemplified by which bone?","options":["Femur","Carpal (and tarsal) bones","Vertebra","Sternum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Short bones are cube-shaped — e.g., carpal bone, tarsal bone.  Why A wrong: The femur is a long bone.  Why C wrong: The vertebra is an irregular bone.  Why D wrong: The sternum is a flat bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which bone is an example of a flat bone?","options":["Patella","Frontal bone (also cranial bones, breast bone/sternum, ribs, scapula, hip bone)","Vertebra","Femur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flat bones are thin bones protecting the underlying structure — e.g., cranial (frontal) bone, sternum, ribs, scapula, hip bone.  Why A wrong: The patella is a sesamoid bone.  Why C wrong: A vertebra is irregular.  Why D wrong: The femur is long."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Irregular bones, which have complex shapes, are exemplified by:","options":["Carpal bones","Vertebra (also facial bones, hyoid bone, ear ossicles)","Femur","Patella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Irregular bones have complex shapes — e.g., vertebra, facial bone, hyoid bone, ear ossicles.  Why A wrong: Carpals are short bones.  Why C wrong: Femur is long.  Why D wrong: Patella is sesamoid."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A sesamoid bone, which has a sesame-seed-like shape, is exemplified by the:","options":["Patella","Sternum","Frontal bone","Vertebra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sesamoid bones have a sesame shape — e.g., the patella (knee cap).  Why B wrong: The sternum is a flat bone.  Why C wrong: The frontal bone is a flat bone.  Why D wrong: The vertebra is irregular."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Red bone marrow is primarily found in which location, and what is its main function?","options":["In the medullary cavity of long bones; stores fat","In the spongy bone of cancellous bone; produces red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets","In the periosteum; secretes minerals","In the diaphysis only; stores yellow marrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red bone marrow occurs inside the spongy (cancellous) bone; it produces red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets, gets its red colour from haemoglobin, and is found in the skull, ribs, spine, shoulder blades and ends of long bones.  Why A wrong: The medullary cavity stores yellow (fatty) marrow.  Why C/D wrong: These misstate red marrow location/function."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Yellow bone marrow is characterised by which feature?","options":["It produces red blood cells","It occurs in medullary cavity (bone shaft), produces fat cells, cartilage and bone, and gets its yellow colour from carotenoids","It is found only in the skull","It produces platelets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Yellow bone marrow occurs inside the medullary cavity (in the marrow cavity of the long bones), produces fat cells, cartilage and bone, and gets its yellow colour due to carotenoids.  Why A/D wrong: Producing red blood cells and platelets is the role of red marrow.  Why C wrong: Skull marrow is red, not yellow."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The human skeleton is conventionally divided into which two main parts, and how many bones does each contain?","options":["Axial skeleton (80 bones) and Appendicular skeleton (126 bones)","Axial skeleton (126 bones) and Appendicular skeleton (80 bones)","Axial skeleton (100 bones) and Appendicular skeleton (106 bones)","Cranial skeleton (22 bones) and Facial skeleton (14 bones)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The axial skeleton has 80 bones (it makes the central axis of the body); the appendicular skeleton has 126 bones (the bones of the limbs). Note: the total number of bones in the human skeleton is 206.  Why B wrong: It reverses the counts.  Why C wrong: Incorrect counts.  Why D wrong: Cranial (8/22) and facial (14) bones are subdivisions of the skull, not the whole skeleton."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The axial skeleton consists of which bones?","options":["The shoulder girdle, pelvic girdle, upper limbs and lower limbs","The skull, vertebral column, sternum/breast bone and ribs (total 80 bones)","Only the skull and facial bones","Only the vertebral column"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The axial skeleton consists of the skull, vertebral column, sternum or breast bone, and the ribs — totalling 80 bones.  Why A wrong: Those structures make up the appendicular skeleton.  Why C/D wrong: The axial skeleton includes more than just the skull or vertebral column."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The skull contains a total of how many bones, and how are they divided?","options":["22 bones total — Cranial bones: 8 and Facial bones: 14","14 bones total — Cranial: 8 and Facial: 6","8 bones total — all cranial","30 bones total — Cranial: 22 and Facial: 8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The skull has 22 bones total: 8 cranial bones and 14 facial bones.  Why B/C/D wrong: They give incorrect totals or splits."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which of the following is correct regarding the hyoid bone?","options":["It is a U-shaped bone, also known as the throat bone or tongue bone, located 2 cm above the larynx","It is the smallest cranial bone","It is one of the auditory ossicles","It is a paranasal sinus bone of the cranium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The hyoid is a U-shaped bone, also known as the throat bone or tongue bone, located 2 cm above the larynx, and it is not articulated with any other bone of the skeleton system.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a cranial bone, an ear ossicle, or a paranasal sinus bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The hyoid bone is unique in that it:","options":["Articulates with the mandible","Is not articulated with any other bone of the skeletal system","Forms part of the cranium","Houses the auditory ossicles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hyoid is the only bone that is not articulated with any other bone of the skeleton system.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source statement on the hyoid's non-articulation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"How many auditory ossicles are present, and what is their total number per ear?","options":["06 in total — 3 in each ear","03 in total — 1 in each ear","08 in total — 4 in each ear","02 in total — 1 in each ear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are 06 auditory ossicles in total — each ear has 3 bones, so the total ear ossicles equals 6.  Why B/C/D wrong: They give incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The auditory ossicles help to:","options":["Conduct sound, provide resonance, and maintain equilibrium","Produce red blood cells","Protect the brain","Support the tongue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The auditory ossicles help to conduct sound, provide resonance and maintain equilibrium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These functions are not attributed to the ossicles in the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The auditory ossicle named for its hammer-like shape is the:","options":["Malleus (hammer bone)","Incus","Stapes","Vomer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The malleus is hammer-like in shape, so it is known as the hammer bone.  Why B wrong: The incus is anvil-shaped (anvil bone).  Why C wrong: The stapes is stirrup-shaped, the smallest bone.  Why D wrong: The vomer is a facial bone, not an ear ossicle."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which auditory ossicle is anvil-shaped, and is therefore called the anvil bone?","options":["Malleus","Incus","Stapes","Maxilla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The incus is anvil-shaped, so it is known as the anvil bone.  Why A wrong: The malleus is hammer-shaped.  Why C wrong: The stapes is stirrup-shaped.  Why D wrong: The maxilla is a facial bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The smallest bone in the human body, shaped like a stirrup, is the:","options":["Stapes (stirrup bone)","Incus","Malleus","Lacrimal bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stapes is stirrup-shaped, so it is known as the stirrup bone; the source notes it is the smallest bone, \"stirrup horses shoes shape.\"  Why B/C wrong: Incus and malleus are larger ear ossicles.  Why D wrong: The lacrimal is a facial bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which set correctly lists the 8 cranial bones with their numbers?","options":["Frontal 1, Parietal 2, Temporal 2, Occipital 1, Ethmoid 1, Sphenoid 1","Frontal 2, Parietal 1, Temporal 2, Occipital 2, Ethmoid 1, Sphenoid 1","Frontal 1, Parietal 1, Temporal 1, Occipital 1, Ethmoid 2, Sphenoid 2","Frontal 2, Parietal 2, Temporal 1, Occipital 1, Ethmoid 1, Sphenoid 1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 8 cranial bones are Frontal (1), Parietal (2), Temporal (2), Occipital (1), Ethmoid (1), and Sphenoid (1) = 8.  Why B/C/D wrong: They give incorrect counts that do not total the correct 8 cranial bones."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which set correctly lists the 14 facial bones?","options":["Zygomatic 2, Maxilla 2, Nasal 2, Lacrimal 2, Vomer 1, Palatine 2, Inferior nasal concha 2, Mandible 1","Zygomatic 1, Maxilla 1, Nasal 1, Lacrimal 1, Vomer 2, Palatine 1, Inferior nasal concha 1, Mandible 1","Zygomatic 2, Maxilla 1, Nasal 2, Lacrimal 1, Vomer 1, Palatine 2, Inferior nasal concha 2, Mandible 2","Zygomatic 2, Maxilla 2, Nasal 1, Lacrimal 2, Vomer 2, Palatine 1, Inferior nasal concha 1, Mandible 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 14 facial bones are Zygomatic (2, cheek bone), Maxilla (2, upper jaw), Nasal (2), Lacrimal (2), Vomer (1), Palatine (2), Inferior nasal concha (2), and Mandible (1, only movable bone) = 14.  Why B/C/D wrong: They give incorrect counts that do not sum to the correct 14 facial bones."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The zygomatic bone is commonly known as the:","options":["Cheek bone","Upper jaw","Lower jaw","Tongue bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The zygomatic bone is the cheek bone.  Why B wrong: The maxilla is the upper jaw.  Why C wrong: The mandible is the lower jaw.  Why D wrong: The hyoid is the tongue bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which is the only movable bone of the skull?","options":["Maxilla","Vomer","Mandible","Palatine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The mandible is the only movable bone of the skull (the maxilla is the upper jaw and is fixed).  Why A wrong: The maxilla (upper jaw) is fixed.  Why B/D wrong: The vomer and palatine bones are not movable."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The thoracic cage (rib cage) consists of how many ribs and how many sternum?","options":["24 ribs (12 pairs) and 01 sternum","12 ribs (6 pairs) and 02 sternum","24 ribs and 02 sternum","12 ribs and 01 sternum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The thoracic bones (rib cage) total 24 ribs (12 pairs) plus 01 sternum.  Why B/C/D wrong: They give incorrect rib or sternum counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The major function of the rib cage is to:","options":["Produce blood cells","Protect the thoracic organs including the heart, lungs and large blood vessels, and give attachment to the muscles of respiration","Store minerals","Conduct sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rib cage protects the thoracic organs (heart, lungs, large blood vessels) and gives attachment to the muscles of respiration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated functions of the rib cage."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"How many vertebrae make up the vertebral column?","options":["26","33","12","24"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vertebral column has 26 vertebrae; although 33 bones are present at birth, 33 bones in newborn join (sacrum 5 fused into 1 and coccyx 4 fused into 1), giving 26 separate bones in adults.  Why B wrong: 33 is the count at birth before fusion.  Why C/D wrong: 12 and 24 are not the vertebral total."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The functions of the vertebral column include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["It is called the spine or back bone","It makes 2/5 part of total height","It protects the spinal cord","It produces white blood cells","It supports the body"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The vertebral column is the spine/back bone, makes 2/5 part of total height, protects the spinal cord, and provides body support.  Why D wrong: Production of white blood cells is a red-marrow function, not a listed vertebral-column function."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The first cervical vertebra (C1), which supports the skull, is also known as the:","options":["Axis","Atlas","Sacrum","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first cervical vertebra (C1) supports the skull and is known as the atlas.  Why A wrong: The axis is the second cervical vertebra (C2).  Why C/D wrong: Sacrum and coccyx are lower vertebral regions."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The second cervical vertebra (C2), which allows the atlas to rotate, is known as the:","options":["Atlas","Axis (axis vertebra)","Lumbar vertebra","Sacrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second cervical vertebra (C2) allows the atlas to rotate and is known as the axis vertebra.  Why A wrong: The atlas is C1.  Why C/D wrong: Lumbar and sacrum are other regions."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Match the vertebral region with the correct number of vertebrae.","options":["Cervical 7, Thoracic 12, Lumbar 5, Sacrum 1 (fused 5 bones), Coccyx 1 (fused 4 bones)","Cervical 12, Thoracic 7, Lumbar 5, Sacrum 5, Coccyx 4","Cervical 5, Thoracic 12, Lumbar 7, Sacrum 1, Coccyx 1","Cervical 7, Thoracic 5, Lumbar 12, Sacrum 1, Coccyx 1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical vertebrae = 7, Thoracic = 12, Lumbar = 5, Sacrum = 1 (fused from 5 bones), Coccyx = 1 (fused from 4 bones).  Why B/C/D wrong: They scramble the regional vertebral counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which vertebral regions are MOVABLE and which are NON-MOVABLE according to the source table?","options":["Cervical, Thoracic and Lumbar are movable; Sacrum and Coccyx are non-movable","All vertebrae are movable","Only the cervical region is movable","Sacrum and Coccyx are movable; the rest are non-movable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists cervical, thoracic and lumbar vertebrae as movable, while the sacrum (fused 5 bones) and coccyx (fused 4 bones) are non-movable.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the movable/non-movable distinctions in the table."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The vertebral column shows four curves. Which set correctly names them?","options":["Cervical curve, Thoracic curve, Lumbar curve, Sacral curve","Frontal, Coronal, Sagittal, Transverse curves","Anterior, Posterior, Medial, Lateral curves","Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, Quaternary curves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vertebral column figure shows the cervical curve, thoracic curve, lumbar curve and sacral curve.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are anatomical planes or generic terms, not the named spinal curves."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Regarding the first 7 ribs (ribs 1–7), these are also called:","options":["False ribs","True ribs (directly attached to the sternum)","Floating ribs","Cervical ribs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first 7 ribs (1–7) are called true ribs because they are directly attached to the sternum.  Why A wrong: Ribs 8–12 are false ribs.  Why C wrong: Ribs 11–12 are floating ribs.  Why D wrong: There is no \"cervical rib\" category in the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Ribs 8–12 are known as false ribs because they are:","options":["Directly attached to the sternum","Indirectly attached to the sternum","Not attached to anything","Fused with the vertebrae only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribs 8–12 are false ribs, indirectly attached to the sternum.  Why A wrong: Direct attachment characterises true ribs (1–7).  Why C wrong: \"Not attached to the sternum\" describes floating ribs (11–12).  Why D wrong: They attach indirectly to the sternum, not only to vertebrae."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Ribs 11–12 are called floating ribs because they are:","options":["Attached directly to the sternum","Attached only to the vertebrae, not to the sternum","Fused with the coccyx","The largest ribs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribs 11–12 are floating ribs — attached only to the vertebra, not to the sternum.  Why A wrong: Direct sternal attachment is for true ribs.  Why C/D wrong: They are not fused with the coccyx nor described as the largest ribs."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The appendicular skeleton consists of which structures?","options":["Skull, vertebral column, sternum, ribs","Shoulder girdle, pelvic girdle, upper limbs and lower limbs (total 126 bones)","Only the upper and lower limbs","Only the shoulder and pelvic girdles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The appendicular skeleton consists of the shoulder girdle, pelvic girdle, upper limbs and lower limbs, totalling 126 bones.  Why A wrong: Those make up the axial skeleton.  Why C/D wrong: The appendicular skeleton includes both the girdles and the limbs."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"How many bones make up the upper and lower limbs of the appendicular skeleton respectively?","options":["Shoulder girdle & upper limbs: 64 bones; Pelvic girdle & lower limbs: 62 bones","Upper: 62 bones; Lower: 64 bones","Upper: 60 bones; Lower: 60 bones","Upper: 50 bones; Lower: 76 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the shoulder girdle and upper limbs = 64 bones, and the pelvic girdle and lower limbs = 62 bones.  Why B wrong: It reverses the counts.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Regarding the shoulder/pectoral girdle, each shoulder girdle contains how many bones, and which are they?","options":["2 bones — clavicle and scapula","3 bones — clavicle, scapula and humerus","1 bone — only the clavicle","4 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each shoulder girdle (pectoral girdle) contains 2 bones — the clavicle and the scapula.  Why B wrong: The humerus is the arm bone, not part of the girdle count of 2.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which is the longest, strongest and heaviest bone of the human body?","options":["Humerus","Femur","Tibia","Radius"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The femur is identified as the longest, strongest and heaviest bone of the human body.  Why A wrong: The humerus is the longest/strongest bone of the upper limb, not the body.  Why C/D wrong: Tibia and radius are not the largest bones."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The humerus is best described as the:","options":["Longest and strongest bone of the lower limb","Longest and strongest bone of the upper limb (upper arm)","Heaviest bone of the body","Shortest bone of the upper limb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The humerus is the longest and strongest bone of the upper arm (upper limb).  Why A wrong: It belongs to the upper limb, not the lower limb.  Why C wrong: The heaviest body bone is the femur.  Why D wrong: It is the longest, not the shortest, upper-limb bone."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The pelvic girdle is made up of how many innominate (hip) bones?","options":["1 innominate bone","2 innominate bones","3 innominate bones","4 innominate bones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic girdle consists of 2 innominate bones.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Muscle is best described as:","options":["A rigid mineralised tissue","A soft tissue and one of the four basic types of animal tissue, formed during embryonic development from the mesoderm","An avascular connective tissue","A type of nervous tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Muscle is a soft tissue, one of the four basic types of animal tissue, formed during embryonic development from the mesoderm.  Why A/C/D wrong: Muscle is not rigid mineralised tissue, avascular connective tissue, or nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The basic contractile unit of a myocyte (muscle fibre) is the:","options":["Myocyte","Sarcomere","Myofibril","Sarcoplasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sarcomere is the basic contractile unit of a myocyte (muscle fibre).  Why A wrong: A myocyte is the whole muscle fibre (composed of myofibrils).  Why C wrong: A myofibril is a part of a muscle fibre forming cells, not the basic contractile unit.  Why D wrong: Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of the cell."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A sarcomere is composed of which two main protein filaments, and which is the active filament?","options":["Actin (thin) and myosin (thick); myosin is the active filament responsible for muscle contraction","Collagen and elastin; collagen is active","Tubulin and keratin; keratin is active","Calcium and phosphate; calcium is active"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sarcomere is composed of two main protein filaments — actin (thin) and myosin (thick); myosin is the active filament responsible for muscle contraction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the sarcomere filament proteins described."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which mineral is essential for normal muscle contraction?","options":["Iron","Calcium","Sodium","Iodine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium ion is essential for normal muscle contraction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These minerals are not named as essential for muscle contraction in the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"The primary function of muscle tissue is:","options":["Mineral storage","Contraction","Blood-cell production","Sound conduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The primary function of muscle tissue is contraction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated primary function of muscle tissue."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"There are three types of muscle in the human body. Which set correctly names them?","options":["Skeletal muscle, smooth muscle and cardiac muscle","Striated, fibrous and elastic muscle","Voluntary, semi-voluntary and reflex muscle","Long, short and flat muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three types of muscle are skeletal muscle, smooth muscle and cardiac muscle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the three muscle types named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Skeletal muscle is correctly described as:","options":["Striated and involuntary","Striated in structure and voluntary in nature","Smooth and voluntary","Smooth and involuntary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletal muscles are striated in structure and voluntary in nature.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misstate the striation or voluntary status of skeletal muscle."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Smooth muscle, also called visceral muscle, is characterised as:","options":["Striated and voluntary","Non-striated and involuntary","Striated and involuntary","Non-striated and voluntary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smooth muscle (visceral muscle) is non-striated and involuntary in nature.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misstate smooth muscle's striation or control."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Cardiac muscle, found only in the heart wall (myocardium), is:","options":["Non-striated and voluntary","Striated and involuntary","Smooth and voluntary","Non-striated and involuntary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall (myocardium); it is striated and involuntary in nature.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misstate cardiac muscle's striation or control."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"On the muscle strength (MRC) grading scale, a score of 5 indicates:","options":["Complete paralysis","Normal muscle strength — full power of contraction against gravity and resistance","Only a flicker of contraction","Movement only with gravity eliminated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 5 = normal muscle strength, full power of contraction against gravity and resistance.  Why A wrong: Score 0 is complete paralysis.  Why C wrong: A flicker corresponds to score 1.  Why D wrong: Movement with gravity eliminated corresponds to score 2."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A muscle-strength score of 4 corresponds to which finding?","options":["Complete paralysis","Fair strength — full strength but not against full resistance / contraction against gravity and moderate amount of resistance, with slight weakness","Normal full power","No contraction at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Score 4 = fair strength; the muscle contracts against gravity and a moderate amount of resistance (full strength but not against full resistance), indicating slight weakness.  Why A wrong: Complete paralysis is score 0.  Why C wrong: Normal full power is score 5.  Why D wrong: No contraction at all corresponds to score 0/1 region."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A muscle-strength score of 3 indicates:","options":["Just sufficient strength to overcome the force of gravity (moderate weakness)","Normal strength","Complete paralysis","Movement only with gravity eliminated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Score 3 = just sufficient strength to overcome the force of gravity, indicating moderate weakness.  Why B wrong: Normal strength is score 5.  Why C wrong: Complete paralysis is score 0.  Why D wrong: Movement only with gravity eliminated is score 2."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A muscle-strength score of 2 is best described as:","options":["Full power against resistance","Ability to move but not against the force of gravity (severe weakness)","Complete paralysis","Minimal flicker of contraction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Score 2 = ability to move but not against the force of gravity, indicating severe weakness.  Why A wrong: Full power against resistance is score 5.  Why C wrong: Complete paralysis is score 0.  Why D wrong: Minimal flicker only corresponds to score 1."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A muscle-strength score of 1 indicates:","options":["Minimal contractile power / weak muscle contraction (very severe weakness)","Complete paralysis","Normal strength","Movement against full resistance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Score 1 = minimal contractile power / weak muscle contraction, indicating very severe weakness (a palpable flicker without movement).  Why B wrong: Complete paralysis is score 0.  Why C wrong: Normal strength is score 5.  Why D wrong: Movement against full resistance is score 5."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A muscle-strength score of 0 on the grading scale means:","options":["Mild weakness","Complete paralysis (no contraction)","Normal strength","Moderate weakness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 0 = complete paralysis with no contraction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe higher scores (4, 5, 3 respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A joint is best defined as:","options":["The contractile unit of muscle","The site at which any two or more bones articulate (come together)","The outer covering of bone","The mineral store of the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A joint is the site at which any two or more bones articulate/come together.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe a sarcomere, periosteum, and bone storage function respectively, not a joint."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"According to the anatomical classification, how many main types of joints are there?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are three types of joints.  Why A/C/D wrong: They give an incorrect number of joint types."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Which is an example of a fibrous joint (anatomical classification)?","options":["Sutures of the skull bones (immovable)","Shoulder joint","Intervertebral joints","Joint between sternum and ribs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrous joints are immovable joints — for example the sutures of the skull bones.  Why B wrong: The shoulder joint is a synovial (freely movable) joint.  Why C/D wrong: Intervertebral joints and sternum-rib joints are cartilaginous joints."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Cartilaginous joints are characterised as:","options":["Immovable joints such as skull sutures","Semi-movable joints, for example between the manubrium and sternum, between vertebrae (symphysis pubis), and at epiphyseal plates","Freely movable joints such as the shoulder","Joints with no bone-to-bone contact"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cartilaginous joints are semi-movable joints — examples include between manubrium and sternum, between vertebrae (symphysis pubis), and epiphyseal plates.  Why A wrong: Immovable skull sutures are fibrous joints.  Why C wrong: Freely movable joints are synovial joints.  Why D wrong: This does not describe cartilaginous joints."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"Synovial joints are best described as:","options":["Immovable joints","Semi-movable joints","Freely movable joints (for example, the shoulder joint)","Joints found only in the skull"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Synovial joints are freely movable joints, for example the shoulder joint.  Why A wrong: Immovable joints are fibrous.  Why B wrong: Semi-movable joints are cartilaginous.  Why D wrong: Synovial joints are not restricted to the skull."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"In the functional classification of joints, an immovable joint is termed a:","options":["Synarthrosis (e.g., fibrous joint in the skull)","Amphiarthrosis","Diarthrosis","Symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Synarthrosis = immovable joints, for example the fibrous joints in the skull.  Why B wrong: Amphiarthrosis is a slightly movable joint.  Why C wrong: Diarthrosis is a freely movable joint.  Why D wrong: Symphysis is a type of cartilaginous joint, not the functional category for immovable."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A slightly movable joint, such as a cartilaginous joint in the vertebral column, is functionally classified as a:","options":["Synarthrosis","Amphiarthrosis","Diarthrosis","Suture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amphiarthrosis = slightly movable joints, for example the cartilaginous joints in the vertebral column.  Why A wrong: Synarthrosis is immovable.  Why C wrong: Diarthrosis is freely movable.  Why D wrong: A suture is an immovable fibrous joint."},{"section":"Batch 030 · anatomical terms & body movements…","question":"A freely movable joint, such as a synovial joint, is functionally classified as a:","options":["Synarthrosis","Amphiarthrosis","Diarthrosis (e.g., synovial joints)","Symphysis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Diarthrosis = freely movable joints, for example synovial joints.  Why A wrong: Synarthrosis is immovable.  Why B wrong: Amphiarthrosis is slightly movable.  Why D wrong: Symphysis is a cartilaginous (slightly movable) joint."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A hinge joint such as the elbow or knee permits which primary movement?","options":["Rotation only","Flexion and extension","Circumduction","Gliding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table classifies the hinge joint (elbow, interphalangeal, knee) as allowing flexion and extension.  Why A wrong: Rotation is characteristic of pivot joints, not hinge joints.  Why C wrong: Circumduction is allowed by ball-and-socket and condyloid joints.  Why D wrong: Gliding movement (one surface over another) is the feature of gliding joints."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A pivot joint, such as the proximal radioulnar joint or atlas-axis joint, primarily allows:","options":["Flexion and extension","Rotation","Abduction and adduction","Opposition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pivot joint (atlas-axis, proximal radioulnar joint) allows rotation per the table.  Why A wrong: Flexion/extension is the hinge joint movement.  Why C wrong: Abduction/adduction belongs to ball-and-socket and condyloid joints.  Why D wrong: Opposition is a feature of the saddle joint (thumb)."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The shoulder joint and hip joint, which permit flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction, are examples of which type of synovial joint?","options":["Hinge joint","Saddle joint","Ball-and-socket joint","Gliding joint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ball-and-socket joint (shoulder, hip) permits flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction.  Why A wrong: Hinge joints permit only flexion/extension.  Why B wrong: Saddle joints allow flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, circumduction, and thumb-finger opposition (carpometacarpal joint of thumb).  Why D wrong: Gliding joints allow only sliding movement."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Thumb-finger opposition at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb is characteristic of which synovial joint?","options":["Pivot joint","Saddle joint","Condyloid joint","Hinge joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The saddle joint (carpometacarpal joint of thumb) allows flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, circumduction, and thumb-finger opposition.  Why A wrong: Pivot allows only rotation.  Why C wrong: Condyloid joints allow flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, circumduction but not true opposition.  Why D wrong: Hinge allows only flexion/extension."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Gliding joints, such as those between tarsal bones and between carpal bones, allow which movement?","options":["Rotation","One surface moving over another surface (sliding)","Opposition","Circumduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gliding joints allow one surface to move over another surface, occurring between tarsal bones, carpal bones, and articular processes of vertebrae.  Why A wrong: Rotation belongs to pivot joints.  Why C wrong: Opposition belongs to saddle joints.  Why D wrong: Circumduction belongs to ball-and-socket/condyloid joints."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A strain is best defined as:","options":["Injury or excessive stretching of a ligament","Injury or excessive stretching of a tendon","A break in the continuity of bone","Inflammation of a joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a strain as injury or excessive stretching of a tendon.  Why A wrong: That is the definition of a sprain (injury/excessive stretching of a ligament).  Why C wrong: A break in the continuity of bone defines a fracture.  Why D wrong: Joint inflammation is arthritis, not a strain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A sprain is defined as:","options":["Injury or excessive stretching of a tendon","Injury or excessive stretching of a ligament","Dislocation of a bone from a joint","Tearing of muscle fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a sprain as injury or excessive stretching of a ligament.  Why A wrong: That defines a strain (tendon).  Why C wrong: That describes dislocation.  Why D wrong: Muscle fiber tearing relates to a muscle strain, not the ligament-based sprain definition given."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which of the following is the recommended management for a strain or sprain injury?","options":["Apply only heat continuously and encourage vigorous exercise","Cold and heat application, normal exercise with limitation","Complete prolonged bed rest with no activity","Immediate surgical repair"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states management is cold and heat application with normal exercise with limitation.  Why A wrong: Continuous heat only and vigorous exercise is not the recommended approach.  Why C wrong: Prolonged bed rest is not indicated for a simple strain/sprain.  Why D wrong: Surgery is not the listed first-line management."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A nurse is teaching first-aid for a sprained ankle using the acronym RICE. What does \"E\" stand for?","options":["Exercise of the affected joint","Elevation of affected extremity to reduce swelling","Extension splinting","Examination by physician"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In RICE management of strain/sprain — R: Rest, I: Ice, C: Compression bandage, E: Elevation of affected extremity to reduce swelling.  Why A wrong: Exercise is not the \"E\"; activity is limited acutely.  Why C wrong: Extension splinting is not part of the RICE acronym.  Why D wrong: Examination is not the meaning of \"E\" here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In the RICE acronym, \"C\" refers to:","options":["Casting","Cold therapy","Compression bandage","Continuous traction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists C as Compression bandage.  Why A wrong: Casting is not part of RICE.  Why B wrong: Cold therapy is represented by Ice (I), not C.  Why D wrong: Traction is not part of RICE."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A fracture is defined as:","options":["Excessive stretching of a ligament","A break in the continuity of bone","Inflammation of bone marrow","Death of bone tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview defines a fracture as a break in the continuity of the bone caused by trauma or twisting, bone pathology such as osteoporosis, etc.  Why A wrong: That is a sprain.  Why C wrong: Inflammation of bone marrow describes osteomyelitis.  Why D wrong: Death of bone tissue describes avascular necrosis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A pathologic fracture is caused by:","options":["A direct traumatic blow","A disease process, for example osteoporosis","A twisting force","Repetitive stress in athletes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a pathologic fracture is caused by a disease process, for example osteoporosis.  Why A wrong: A traumatic fracture is caused by trauma or accident, the most common cause.  Why C wrong: A twisting force describes a spiral/traumatic mechanism, not a pathologic fracture by definition.  Why D wrong: Repetitive stress describes a stress fracture, not the pathologic fracture defined here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"According to the source, the most common cause of a traumatic fracture is:","options":["Osteoporosis","Trauma or accident","Bone tumor","Vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a traumatic fracture is caused by trauma or accident, the most common cause.  Why A wrong: Osteoporosis underlies pathologic fractures, not the most common traumatic cause.  Why C wrong: Tumors cause pathologic fractures.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D deficiency is not the listed common cause."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A transverse fracture is one in which the fracture line is:","options":["Parallel to the bone's long axis","At a right angle to the bone's long axis","Diagonal to the long axis","Spiral in nature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pattern of fracture section defines a transverse fracture as a fracture that is at a right angle to the bone's long axis.  Why A wrong: A linear fracture is parallel to the bone's long axis.  Why C wrong: An oblique fracture is diagonal to the long axis.  Why D wrong: A spiral fracture results from a twisting force."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A fracture whose line runs parallel to the bone's long axis is termed:","options":["Oblique fracture","Linear fracture","Spiral fracture","Comminuted fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A linear fracture is a fracture that is parallel to the bone's long axis.  Why A wrong: Oblique is diagonal (>30°) to the long axis.  Why C wrong: Spiral results from a twisting force.  Why D wrong: Comminuted involves the bone broken into several fragments."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"An oblique fracture is described as a fracture line that is diagonal to the long axis at approximately:","options":["0°","Right angle (90°)","Greater than 30° (>30°)","Less than 10°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines oblique fracture as a fracture that is diagonal to a bone's long axis (>30°).  Why A wrong: 0° (parallel) describes a linear fracture.  Why B wrong: A right angle describes a transverse fracture.  Why D wrong: <10° is not the described angle."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A spiral fracture is most characteristically produced by which mechanism?","options":["A direct perpendicular blow","A twisting force, where at least one part of the bone has been twisted","Compression along the long axis","A disease weakening the bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A spiral fracture is a fracture in which at least one part of the bone has been twisted.  Why A wrong: A direct perpendicular blow tends to produce a transverse fracture.  Why C wrong: Axial compression produces an impacted/compression fracture.  Why D wrong: Disease weakening produces a pathologic fracture."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A fracture in which bone fragments are driven into each other is called a/an:","options":["Avulsion fracture","Impacted fracture","Linear fracture","Compression fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An impacted fracture is a fracture caused when bone fragments are driven into each other.  Why A wrong: Avulsion is where a fragment of bone is separated from the main mass.  Why C wrong: Linear is parallel to the long axis.  Why D wrong: A compression/wedge fracture usually occurs in the vertebrae."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"An avulsion fracture is best described as a fracture in which:","options":["The bone is shattered into many fragments","A fragment of bone is separated from the main mass","The bone breaks at a right angle","The skin over the fracture is broken"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An avulsion fracture is a fracture where a fragment of bone is separated from the main mass.  Why A wrong: Shattering into many fragments describes a comminuted fracture.  Why C wrong: A right-angle break is transverse.  Why D wrong: Skin breakage describes an open/compound fracture."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A compression (wedge) fracture most usually occurs in the:","options":["Long bones of the leg","Vertebrae","Skull","Phalanges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a compression fracture/wedge fracture usually occurs in the vertebrae.  Why A wrong: Long-bone fractures are more often transverse, oblique, or spiral.  Why C wrong: The skull is not the usual site noted.  Why D wrong: Phalanges are not the described site."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In an open (compound) fracture, the defining feature is that:","options":["The bone is broken but the skin remains intact","The bone is exposed directly to the air through the break in the skin","The bone breaks only partially","The fracture occurs along existing disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Types of Fracture table defines an open fracture (also called compound fracture) as one where the bone is exposed directly to the air through the break in the skin; protrusion of bone may or may not occur after a fracture.  Why A wrong: Skin remaining intact describes a closed (simple) fracture.  Why B is correct as stated.  Why C wrong: A partial break describes an incomplete/greenstick type, not specifically open.  Why D wrong: Disease-related fracture is a pathologic fracture."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A closed fracture is also known as a:","options":["Compound fracture","Simple fracture","Comminuted fracture","Greenstick fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states a closed fracture is also known as a simple fracture, with the bone broken but skin intact.  Why A wrong: Compound is the alternate name for an open fracture.  Why C wrong: Comminuted refers to numerous fragments.  Why D wrong: Greenstick refers to an incomplete fracture in children."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A fracture in which the bone breaks into several or numerous fragments is termed:","options":["Linear fracture","Comminuted fracture","Incomplete fracture","Greenstick fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A comminuted fracture is one where the bone is broken into several or numerous fragments.  Why A wrong: Linear runs parallel to the long axis.  Why C wrong: An incomplete fracture means only part of the bone is broken.  Why D wrong: Greenstick is the incomplete fracture seen in children."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In an incomplete fracture:","options":["The bone is broken into many pieces","Only part of the bone is broken (not all the way through)","The skin is always broken","The fracture occurs at a spontaneous disease site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines an incomplete fracture as one where only part of the bone is broken.  Why A wrong: Many pieces describes comminuted.  Why C wrong: Skin breakage describes an open fracture.  Why D wrong: Spontaneous disease-related fractures are pathologic."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A greenstick fracture, in which one side of a bone is broken while the other side bends, is most commonly seen in:","options":["Elderly osteoporotic patients","Children","Athletes","Postmenopausal women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table notes that a greenstick fracture (one side of the bone is broken and the other is bent) occurs most commonly in children.  Why A wrong: Elderly osteoporotic patients more often have pathologic/fragility fractures.  Why C wrong: Athletes more often sustain stress fractures.  Why D wrong: Postmenopausal women are associated with pathologic/insufficiency fractures."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A pathological fracture is best characterized as one that:","options":["Occurs only after high-energy trauma","Is a spontaneous fracture occurring at the site of bone disease","Always involves the skin breaking","Heals faster than other fractures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes a pathological fracture as a spontaneous fracture, occurring at sites where bone has been weakened by disease such as a tumor.  Why A wrong: High-energy trauma is not required; bone is already weakened.  Why C wrong: Skin breaking is a feature of open fractures, not the definition of pathological.  Why D wrong: Healing speed is not the defining feature."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"An impacted fracture, as described in the Types of Fracture table, occurs when:","options":["The bone is exposed to air","One fragment of bone is firmly driven (wedged) into another fragment","The bone bends without breaking","A ligament is overstretched"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes an impacted fracture where one fracture fragment is driven/pushed into each other (wedged together).  Why A wrong: Exposure to air describes an open fracture.  Why C wrong: Bending without complete break describes greenstick/incomplete.  Why D wrong: Ligament overstretch is a sprain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Place the four stages of bone healing in the correct chronological order.","options":["Consolidation → Hematoma formation → Callus formation → Remodeling","Hematoma formation → Fibrocartilage formation → Callus formation → Consolidation → Remodeling of callus","Callus formation → Hematoma formation → Consolidation → Remodeling","Remodeling → Callus → Hematoma → Consolidation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the stages of bone healing as: hematoma formation (initial phase), fibrocartilage formation, callus formation, consolidation, and remodeling of callus.  Why A wrong: Consolidation cannot precede hematoma formation.  Why C wrong: Hematoma formation is first, not callus.  Why D wrong: This reverses the order."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The first/initial phase of bone healing is:","options":["Callus formation","Hematoma formation","Ossification (formation of new bone)","Remodeling of callus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hematoma formation occurs in the initial phase of bone healing.  Why A wrong: Callus formation is a later stage.  Why C wrong: Ossification/new bone formation follows callus.  Why D wrong: Remodeling is the final stage."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A patient presents after an injury with pain, swelling, bruising, loss of function, deformity, and a grating sound when the affected extremity is moved. The grating sensation/sound is termed:","options":["Crepitus","Tetany","Fasciculation","Clonus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The signs and symptoms of a fractured extremity include crepitus or grating sound heard when the affected extremity is moved.  Why B wrong: Tetany is sustained muscle contraction from hypocalcemia.  Why C wrong: Fasciculation is small involuntary muscle twitching.  Why D wrong: Clonus is rhythmic reflex muscle contractions."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Shortening of an extremity in a patient with a fracture is caused by:","options":["Increased venous return","A change in bone alignment","Excess synovial fluid","Muscle hypertrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states shortening of extremities is caused by change in bone alignment.  Why A wrong: Venous return is not the cause.  Why C wrong: Synovial fluid changes are not the listed cause.  Why D wrong: Muscle hypertrophy is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A patient with a suspected fracture has impaired local circulation with cool, dusky color and weak/absent distal pulses. Which is the most accurate description of the cause?","options":["The fracture has damaged or compressed arteries, impairing distal circulation","Normal physiologic response to rest","A sign of complete healing","Result of hot compress overuse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes impaired local circulation — cool, dusky color and weak pulse if arteries are damaged.  Why B wrong: This is abnormal, not a normal response.  Why C wrong: It indicates a circulation problem, not healing.  Why D wrong: It is not caused by hot compresses."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which diagnostic test reveals a break in the continuity of bone and confirms a fracture diagnosis?","options":["Complete blood count","X-ray examination","Electromyography","Serum calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states X-ray examination reveals break in continuity of bone (confirmatory diagnosis).  Why B is correct as stated.  Why A wrong: A CBC does not confirm a fracture.  Why C wrong: EMG assesses nerve/muscle function, not bone continuity.  Why D wrong: Serum calcium does not confirm fracture."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"What is an important nursing intervention to immobilize a suspected fracture before definitive care?","options":["Encourage active range of motion of the affected limb","Immobilize the fracture above and below the affected joint by applying a splint or cast","Apply only a warm compress and ambulate the patient","Massage the fracture site vigorously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care following all types of fractures includes immobilizing the affected extremity with cast or splint, immobilizing above and below the joint where appropriate.  Why A wrong: Active ROM of the fractured limb is contraindicated initially.  Why C wrong: Ambulation without immobilization risks further injury.  Why D wrong: Massaging the fracture site is harmful."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When caring for a patient with a fracture, the nurse should cover an open wound with a sterile gauze piece if available primarily to:","options":["Promote bleeding","Prevent infection/contamination","Increase swelling","Speed up remodeling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to cover the open wound with sterile gauze piece if available, which protects against contamination/infection.  Why A wrong: The goal is not to promote bleeding.  Why C wrong: It does not aim to increase swelling.  Why D wrong: Sterile cover does not directly speed remodeling."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A nurse caring for a fractured limb should assess for signs of excessive bleeding so that, if present, the next priority is to:","options":["Elevate the limb only","Clamp the fracture site at home","Recognize it and prepare to apply pressure to control bleeding","Apply heat to the site"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs the nurse to assess for shock; if excessive bleeding is present, then apply pressure (to control the bleeding).  Why A wrong: Elevation alone is insufficient for excessive bleeding.  Why B wrong: Clamping a fracture site is not appropriate first aid.  Why D wrong: Heat would worsen bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"To support bone healing in a fracture patient, the nurse should encourage a diet that is:","options":["Low in protein and high in fat","High in vitamin, protein diet to improve healing","Restricted in all nutrients","High in sodium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises to encourage high vitamin, protein diet to improve healing.  Why A wrong: Low protein impairs healing.  Why C wrong: Nutrient restriction impairs healing.  Why D wrong: High sodium is not the recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The nurse should avoid which action when a client is on prolonged bed rest after a fracture?","options":["Repositioning every 2 hours","Encouraging fluid intake","Keeping the client immobile for extended periods without prevention of complications (rigid immobilization until activity is restored)","Performing range-of-motion within limits"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source cautions to avoid rigid/prolonged immobilization (avoid rigid calcium-loss and bed-rest complications) and to encourage activity as restored; prolonged inactivity causes bone decalcification.  Why A wrong: Repositioning prevents complications and is encouraged.  Why B wrong: Fluid intake is encouraged.  Why D wrong: ROM within limits is appropriate."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Why is a high-calcium diet beneficial for a fracture patient while activity is restored?","options":["Because bone decalcification continues until activity is restored, so a high-calcium diet offsets calcium loss and aids reformation of renal calculi-free bone","Because calcium causes muscle wasting","Because calcium prevents callus formation","Because calcium stops bone remodeling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes bone decalcification continues until activity is restored, so offering a high-calcium diet for fractured clients (while otherwise high-calcium diet may lead to formation of renal calculi) helps replace lost calcium.  Why B wrong: Calcium supports, not causes muscle wasting.  Why C wrong: Calcium aids, not prevents, callus/bone formation.  Why D wrong: Calcium supports remodeling."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A patient has a newly applied plaster of Paris (POP, CaSO4·½H2O) cast. The nurse knows this cast is applied to:","options":["Provide flexible support for range of motion","Immobilize that part and supply functional position, easy to use, holding the part rigid","Allow free joint movement","Replace the need for reduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states casts are rigid immobilizing devices made up of plaster of Paris (CaSO4·½H2O) and applied externally to a body part to immobilize that part, used in fractures to immobilize the affected functional position; easy to use.  Why A wrong: Casts are rigid, not flexible for ROM.  Why C wrong: They immobilize, not allow free movement.  Why D wrong: A cast maintains reduction, not replaces it."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A splint differs from a cast in that a splint is primarily used to:","options":["Permanently fuse a joint","Immobilize and support a body part in a functional position; easy to use","Increase weight-bearing immediately","Replace surgical fixation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a splint as used to support and immobilize an affected body part in a functional position, easy to use.  Why A wrong: Splints do not fuse joints.  Why C wrong: Splints do not enable immediate weight bearing.  Why D wrong: Splints do not replace internal fixation."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with a cast should: (Select all that apply)","options":["Protect the cast from dampness/water; keep it dry","Elevate the extremity with the cast","Handle a wet cast with the palms of the hands, not fingers, for keeping the cast undeformed","Promote drying by leaving it uncovered, allowing air circulation","Cut the cast open routinely every day"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: The cast must be protected from dampness/kept dry.  Why B correct: Elevation of the extremity with the cast reduces swelling.  Why C correct: Handle a wet cast with the palms (not fingers) to avoid denting the cast.  Why D correct: Promote drying by leaving it uncovered to allow air circulation.  Why E wrong: Routinely cutting the cast open daily is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When caring for a client with a splint, which interventions are appropriate? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ensure that the weights/binding are hanging freely","Observe the affected site/limb for swelling and observe skin/extremity for irritation","Maintain the affected extremity in the prescribed alignment","Remove the splint and ambulate the patient hourly","Observe the skin for irritation/redness"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why A correct: The source notes ensuring weights/binding hang freely (for traction-related care of fixation devices).  Why B correct: Observe affected site and extremity for irritation.  Why C correct: Maintain affected extremity in the prescribed alignment.  Why E correct: Observe skin for irritation/redness.  Why D wrong: Routine hourly removal and ambulation is not indicated for a splint."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Complications of a fracture are classified by timing. Which complication is an immediate complication occurring at the time of the fracture?","options":["Avascular necrosis","Hypovolemic shock (immediate hemorrhage)","DVT","Malunion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists immediate complications as occurring at the time of the fracture, for example hypovolemic shock.  Why A wrong: Avascular necrosis is a late complication.  Why C wrong: DVT (associated with venous stasis/AROS) is an early complication.  Why D wrong: Malunion is a late complication."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which of the following is classified as an early complication of a fracture?","options":["Avascular necrosis","Compartment syndrome / DVT / ARDS / sepsis (open fracture)","Contracture","Malunion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists early complications occurring in the initial few days after the fracture, for example compartment syndrome, DVT, ARDS, sepsis (in open fracture).  Why A wrong: Avascular necrosis is a late complication.  Why C wrong: Contracture is a late complication.  Why D wrong: Malunion is listed under late complications."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which complications are classified as late complications of a fracture occurring a long time after the fracture? (Select all that apply)","options":["Delayed union","Avascular necrosis","Contracture","Immediate hypovolemic shock","Malunion"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why A correct: Delayed union is a late complication occurring a long time after the fracture.  Why B correct: Avascular necrosis is listed as a late complication.  Why C correct: Contractures are late complications.  Why E correct: Malunion is a late complication.  Why D wrong: Hypovolemic shock is an immediate complication."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In compartment syndrome, what is the underlying pathophysiologic problem?","options":["Decreased pressure within a muscle compartment","Pressure increases within one or more compartments, leading to decreased blood flow, tissue ischemia, and neurovascular impairment","Excess synovial fluid","Bone marrow embolization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states compartment syndrome involves pressure increasing within one or more compartments, leading to decreased blood flow, tissue ischemia, and neurovascular impairment.  Why A wrong: Pressure increases, not decreases.  Why C wrong: Excess synovial fluid is not the mechanism.  Why D wrong: Marrow embolization describes fat embolism."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"If compartment syndrome is left untreated, it may result in:","options":["Faster healing","A limb that is otherwise normal","Permanent neuromuscular irreversible damage and loss of function","Spontaneous bone remodeling"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source warns that if untreated, compartment syndrome leads to a limb otherwise neuromuscular irreversible damage and loss of function (permanent damage).  Why A wrong: It does not promote healing.  Why B wrong: An untreated compartment is not normal; it can lose function.  Why D wrong: It does not cause beneficial remodeling."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The classic signs and symptoms of compartment syndrome are remembered with the \"5 P's.\" These include: (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain (out of proportion to injury, early sign)","Pallor","Paresthesia (loss of sensation)","Paralysis","Pulselessness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the 5 P's of compartment syndrome as Pain (early sign), Pallor, Paresthesia (loss of sensation), Paralysis, and Pulselessness (late sign).  All five listed options are the documented signs."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Of the 5 P's of compartment syndrome, which is generally the earliest sign and which is a late sign?","options":["Pulselessness is earliest; pain is late","Pain is the early sign; pulselessness is the late sign","Pallor is early; paresthesia is late","Paralysis is early; pain is late"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source designates pain as the early sign and pulselessness as the late sign of compartment syndrome.  Why A wrong: This reverses the timing.  Why C wrong: Pallor and paresthesia are not designated as the early/late markers here.  Why D wrong: Paralysis is not the early sign; pain is."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When a nurse notes signs of compartment syndrome, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Tighten the cast further","Inform the doctor immediately and prepare for the prescribed intervention","Elevate the limb above the heart and ignore symptoms","Apply additional weights"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to inform the doctor and prepare for intervention immediately when compartment syndrome is suspected.  Why A wrong: Tightening a cast worsens pressure.  Why C wrong: Symptoms must not be ignored; emergency intervention is needed.  Why D wrong: Adding weights does not relieve compartment pressure."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Fat embolism most commonly develops from which source after a fracture?","options":["Arterial plaque","Fat globules released from the bone marrow into the bloodstream","Air entering a vein","Amniotic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fat embolism develops in bone marrow and occurs after a fracture, when fat globules are released into the bloodstream.  Why A wrong: Arterial plaque causes atherosclerotic emboli, not fat embolism.  Why C wrong: Air entering a vein is an air embolism.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is obstetric."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Fat embolism syndrome typically presents within what time frame after the fracture?","options":["Within the first 24–48 hours after the fracture","After 2 weeks","Immediately at the moment of injury","Only after surgical fixation 6 months later"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes high-risk clients with long-bone fractures have fat embolism (closure) developing/onset within the first 24–48 hours after the fracture.  Why B wrong: Two weeks is later than the typical onset.  Why C wrong: Immediate hemorrhagic shock is the immediate complication, not fat embolism onset.  Why D wrong: Months later is not the described window."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Signs and symptoms of fat embolism syndrome include: (Select all that apply)","options":["Respiratory distress (hypoxia, dyspnea)","Mental status changes (restlessness, confusion)","Tachycardia","Petechiae rash over the upper chest and neck","Decreased pain at the fracture site only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Respiratory distress/hypoxia is a key feature of fat embolism.  Why B correct: Mental status changes (restlessness, confusion) occur.  Why C correct: Tachycardia is noted.  Why D correct: Petechiae over the upper chest and neck is characteristic.  Why E wrong: Decreased pain at the fracture site is not a defining feature of fat embolism."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"To manage fat embolism, the source recommends which interventions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Administer oxygen","Administer IV fluids","Monitor vital signs and respiratory status","Provide prompt immobilization and stabilization of the fracture (mechanical ventilation if condition worsens)","Withhold all oxygen therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Oxygen is administered to correct hypoxia.  Why B correct: IV fluids are administered.  Why C correct: Monitor vital signs and respiratory status.  Why D correct: Prompt immobilization/stabilization and mechanical ventilation if worsening are indicated.  Why E wrong: Withholding oxygen is incorrect; oxygen is needed for hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Avascular necrosis after a fracture occurs because:","options":["There is excessive blood supply to the bone","A fracture interrupts the blood supply to a section of bone, leading to bone death","Bone marrow proliferates excessively","Calcium levels rise too high"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states avascular necrosis occurs when a fracture interrupts the blood supply to a section of bone, leading to bone death.  Why A wrong: It is loss, not excess, of blood supply.  Why C wrong: Marrow proliferation is not the mechanism.  Why D wrong: Hypercalcemia is not the cause."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The major clinical features of avascular necrosis include:","options":["Pain and decreased sensation/function (decreased range of motion)","Increased bone strength","Hyperpigmentation only","Fever and chills only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pain and decreased sensation as major clinical features of avascular necrosis.  Why B wrong: Bone strength decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: Hyperpigmentation is not a listed feature.  Why D wrong: Fever and chills are features of infection/osteomyelitis, not the listed AVN features."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Osteomyelitis as a complication of fracture refers to:","options":["Death of bone tissue","Infection and inflammatory response of the bone caused by pathogenic organisms that enter into the bone and cause localized bone infection","A break in bone continuity","Stretching of a ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines osteomyelitis as the infection and inflammatory response of the bone caused by pathogenic organisms that enter the bone and cause localized bone infection.  Why A wrong: Death of bone tissue is avascular necrosis.  Why C wrong: A break in continuity is a fracture.  Why D wrong: Ligament stretching is a sprain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which of the following are major clinical features of osteomyelitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachycardia and fever","Leukocytosis (raised WBC)","Elevated ESR","Decreased body temperature"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why A correct: Tachycardia and fever are listed major features.  Why B correct: Leukocytosis is a major feature.  Why C correct: Elevated ESR is a major feature.  Why D wrong: Temperature is elevated (fever), not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A patient develops a pulmonary embolism after a long-bone fracture. The source defines pulmonary embolism as:","options":["Movement of foreign particles such as blood clot, fat, or air into the pulmonary circulation","Infection of the lung tissue","Bone fragments entering the lung","Inflammation of the bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes pulmonary embolism as movement of foreign particles such as a blood clot, fat, or air into the pulmonary circulation.  Why B wrong: Lung infection is pneumonia, not embolism.  Why C wrong: Bone fragments are not the typical embolus described.  Why D wrong: Bone inflammation is osteomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Signs and symptoms of pulmonary embolism include: (Select all that apply)","options":["Restlessness","Sudden onset of dyspnea and chest pain","Cough, hemoptysis","Hypoxia and crackles","Improved oxygenation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Restlessness is a listed sign.  Why B correct: Sudden onset of dyspnea and chest pain are listed.  Why C correct: Cough and hemoptysis are listed.  Why D correct: Hypoxia and crackles are listed.  Why E wrong: Oxygenation worsens (hypoxia), not improves."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When pulmonary embolism is suspected, the nurse should place the patient in which position and take which immediate action?","options":["Prone position; withhold oxygen","Semi-Fowler or Fowler position (first priority), administer oxygen, and inform the doctor/initiate general nursing care","Trendelenburg position; ambulate immediately","Flat supine; encourage forced expiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nurse should place the patient in semi-Fowler or Fowler position (first priority), administer oxygen, and inform the doctor to initiate general nursing care.  Why A wrong: Prone position and withholding oxygen are incorrect.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg and immediate ambulation are not indicated.  Why D wrong: Flat supine with forced expiration is not the recommended care."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The term \"reduction\" in fracture management refers to:","options":["A process to immobilize bone permanently","A process to restore the bone to proper alignment","Removing bone fragments surgically","Cleaning an open wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Management Methods table defines reduction as a process which restores the bone to proper alignment.  Why A wrong: Permanent immobilization is achieved by fixation, not the definition of reduction.  Why C wrong: Fragment removal is debridement, not reduction.  Why D wrong: Wound cleaning is not reduction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes closed reduction from open reduction?","options":["Closed reduction uses surgical intervention; open reduction is non-surgical","Closed reduction is non-surgical, performed by manual manipulation; open reduction is surgical intervention with internal fixation devices","Both are non-surgical","Both always require external traction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines closed reduction as a non-surgical procedure performed by manual manipulation, and open reduction as surgical intervention with internal fixation devices.  Why A wrong: This reverses the definitions.  Why C wrong: Open reduction is surgical.  Why D wrong: Neither is defined solely by external traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Internal fixation in fracture management involves:","options":["Application of a plaster cast only","Devices internally fixed by a surgical procedure (screws, plates, pins, or intramedullary rods) to hold fragments in alignment and provide bone stabilization","Use of external traction weights","Skin traction with adhesive tape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states internal fixation uses devices internally fixed by surgical procedure (screws, plates, pins, or intramedullary rods) to hold the fragments in alignment and provide bone stabilization.  Why A wrong: A cast is external, not internal fixation.  Why C wrong: External traction weights are not internal fixation.  Why D wrong: Skin traction with adhesives is a separate method."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"With external fixation, the nurse should:","options":["Avoid all monitoring of the device","Monitor pin stability and provide pin care to decrease infection risks","Remove the pins daily","Keep the device covered and never inspect it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states external fixation requires monitoring pin stability and providing pin care to decrease infection risks.  Why A wrong: Monitoring is required.  Why C wrong: Pins are not removed daily by the nurse.  Why D wrong: The site must be inspected for infection."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Traction in fracture management is best defined as:","options":["The surgical removal of bone fragments","The exertion of a pulling force applied in two directions to reduce and immobilize a fracture","The application of a rigid cast","Immediate weight bearing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines traction as the exertion of a pulling force applied into two directions to reduce and immobilize a fracture.  Why A wrong: Fragment removal is not traction.  Why C wrong: A rigid cast is a different management method.  Why D wrong: Traction does not mean immediate weight bearing."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When caring for a patient in traction, which nursing actions are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Maintain proper bone alignment and reduce muscle spasms","Ensure that the weights hang freely and do not touch the floor","Do not remove or lift the weights without an HCP's prescription","Monitor color, sensation, and motion of the affected extremity","Rest the weights on the bed to reduce noise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Traction maintains proper bone alignment and reduces muscle spasms.  Why B correct: Ensure the weights hang freely and do not touch the floor.  Why C correct: Do not remove or lift the weights without an HCP's prescription.  Why D correct: Monitor color, sensation, and motion of the affected extremity.  Why E wrong: Resting weights on the bed releases traction and is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The two main types of traction described are:","options":["Open and closed traction","Skeletal traction and skin traction","Cervical and pelvic traction only","Manual and mechanical traction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are two types of traction: skeletal traction and skin traction.  Why A wrong: Open/closed describe reduction, not traction types.  Why C wrong: Cervical and pelvic are specific skin traction applications, not the two main types.  Why D wrong: Manual/mechanical is not the listed classification."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In skeletal traction, the traction is applied mechanically to the bone using:","options":["Adhesive tape on the skin","Pins, wires, or tongs","A foam boot","An elastic bandage only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states skeletal traction is applied mechanically to the bone using pins, wires, or tongs.  Why A wrong: Adhesive tape on skin describes skin traction.  Why C wrong: A foam boot is used in skin traction (e.g., Buck's).  Why D wrong: An elastic bandage applies to skin traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Skin traction is applied directly to the affected extremity using:","options":["Pins drilled into bone","Elastic bandage or adhesives, with weight to relieve muscle spasms and provide compression in upper extremities","Surgical screws","An external fixator frame"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states skin traction is applied directly to the affected extremity by using elastic bandage or adhesives, and is used to relieve muscle spasms and compression in the upper extremities.  Why A wrong: Pins are skeletal traction.  Why C wrong: Screws are internal fixation.  Why D wrong: An external fixator is a separate method."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"For cervical skin traction, the recommended weight and head-of-bed elevation are:","options":["5–7 kg with 90-degree elevation","10–12 kg, or 10% of body weight, with the head of bed elevated 30–40 degrees","20 kg with the bed flat","2 kg with the bed in Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cervical skin traction uses a weight of 10–12 kg, or 10% of body weight, and the client's head of bed is elevated 30–40 degrees.  Why A wrong: These values are not the listed cervical traction parameters.  Why C wrong: 20 kg with a flat bed is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 2 kg with Trendelenburg is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Bryant's traction is most appropriately used in which patient group?","options":["Elderly patients with hip fractures","Young children who have fractures of the femur or congenital abnormalities of the hip such as DDH","Adults with cervical injuries","Patients with humerus fractures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Bryant's traction is mainly used in young children who have fractures of the femur or congenital abnormalities of the hip like DDH; commonly used for fracture of shaft of femur in infants and small children.  Why A wrong: It is not for elderly hip fractures.  Why C wrong: Cervical injuries use cervical traction.  Why D wrong: Humerus fractures use Dunlop traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Buck's skin traction is used to:","options":["Apply traction to the cervical spine","Alleviate muscle spasms and immobilize a lower limb, maintaining a straight pull on the limb with weight, with the weights attached to a pulley","Treat humerus fractures with horizontal pull","Spread the hips apart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Buck's skin traction is used for alleviating muscle spasms and immobilizing a lower limb, maintaining a straight pull on the limb with weight, with the weights attached to a pulley.  Why A wrong: Cervical traction is a different method.  Why C wrong: Humerus/horizontal pull describes Dunlop traction.  Why D wrong: Hip abduction (spreading) is achieved by a Pavlik harness."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The recommended weight range for Buck's traction is:","options":["1–2 kg","5–8 lb (2–3.6 kg)","10–12 kg","20–25 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the recommended weight for Buck's traction is 5–8 lb (2–3.6 kg).  Why A wrong: 1–2 kg is not the listed range.  Why C wrong: 10–12 kg is for cervical traction.  Why D wrong: 20–25 kg is too high for Buck's traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Russell's skin (sling) traction is widely used for which type of fracture?","options":["Cervical spine fractures","Femoral fracture in adults","Humerus fractures in children","Vertebral compression fractures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Russell's skin (sling) traction is widely used for femoral fracture in adults.  Why A wrong: Cervical traction handles cervical injuries.  Why C wrong: Humerus fractures in children use Dunlop traction.  Why D wrong: Vertebral fractures are not the listed indication."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Pelvic skin traction is used to:","options":["Relieve low back, hip, or leg pain or to reduce muscle spasm","Treat femur fractures in infants","Spread the hips for DDH","Pull on the cervical spine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pelvic skin traction is used to relieve low back, hip, or leg pain or to reduce muscle spasm.  Why B wrong: Infant femur fractures use Bryant's traction.  Why C wrong: DDH hip spreading uses a Pavlik harness.  Why D wrong: Cervical pull is cervical traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Balanced suspension traction is characterized by which features? (Select all that apply)","options":["It is used with skin and skeletal traction","It is used for fractures of the femur, tibia, and fibula","The client is placed in Fowler's position","It provides pin care if pins are used with skeletal traction","It is exclusively used for cervical injuries"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Balanced suspension traction is used with skin and skeletal traction.  Why B correct: It is used for fractures of the femur, tibia, and fibula.  Why C correct: The client is placed in Fowler's position.  Why D correct: Pin care is provided if pins are used with skeletal traction.  Why E wrong: It is not exclusively for cervical injuries."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Dunlop traction is best described as:","options":["A vertical-only traction for the femur","A horizontal traction used to align fractures of the humerus, with vertical traction to maintain the forearm in proper alignment","A traction for cervical spine injuries","A device to spread the hips"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes Dunlop traction as a horizontal traction used to align fractures of the humerus, with vertical traction to maintain the forearm in proper alignment.  Why A wrong: It is not a vertical-only femoral traction.  Why C wrong: It is not for cervical injuries.  Why D wrong: Hip spreading is the Pavlik harness."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The Pavlik harness is most commonly used for babies who have which condition?","options":["Femoral shaft fracture","Hip dysplasia (a hip disorder); it keeps the hips and knees bent (flexed) and the thighs spread apart (abducted)","Cervical spine injury","Humerus fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Pavlik harness is a brace most commonly used for babies who have hip dysplasia (a hip disorder); its purpose is to keep the hips and knees bent (flexed) and the thighs spread apart (abducted), helping the hips grow properly.  Why A wrong: Femoral shaft fractures in infants use Bryant's traction.  Why C wrong: Cervical injuries use cervical traction.  Why D wrong: Humerus fractures use Dunlop traction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"What is the purpose of the Pavlik harness positioning?","options":["To straighten the legs fully","To keep the hips and knees flexed and thighs abducted so the hips can grow/develop properly","To immobilize the cervical spine","To compress a fracture site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the purpose of the harness is to keep the hips and knees bent (flexed) and thighs spread apart (abducted); this position helps allow the hips to grow/develop properly.  Why A wrong: It maintains flexion, not full extension.  Why C wrong: It is not for the cervical spine.  Why D wrong: It is not for fracture compression."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Nursing responsibilities following traction include monitoring for which neurovascular parameters? The \"5 P's\" assessed are: (Select all that apply)","options":["Pulse","Pallor","Paresthesia","Pulselessness","Paralysis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists circulation checks involving the 5 P's — Pulse, Pallor, Paresthesia, Pulselessness, and Paralysis — to be monitored in the client on traction.  All five listed are documented parameters."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which nursing responsibilities apply to a client in traction? (Select all that apply)","options":["Maintain temperature; observe for infection in fractured extremities","Ensure rope of traction should be hanged freely","Ensure alignment should be maintained and counter-traction is needed","Note the type and location of traction","Allow the weights to rest on the floor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Maintain temperature and observe for infection in fractured extremities.  Why B correct: The rope of traction should hang freely.  Why C correct: Alignment should be maintained and counter-traction is needed.  Why D correct: Note the type and location of traction.  Why E wrong: Never let the weight touch the floor; the weights must hang freely."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A nurse caring for a patient in traction notes the importance of \"Never touch the weight to bed as well as floor.\" The rationale is that:","options":["It looks neater","Resting the weight removes/interrupts the traction (pulling) force","It increases the patient's pain on purpose","It conserves energy of the staff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source emphasizes never touching the weight to bed or floor because it interrupts the continuous pulling force needed for effective traction.  Why A wrong: Appearance is not the clinical rationale.  Why C wrong: It is not intended to increase pain.  Why D wrong: Staff energy is irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"An intra-capsular fracture of the hip is best described as:","options":["A fracture outside the joint capsule, below the greater trochanter","A fracture of the head or neck of the femur that may result in loss of blood supply to the head of the femur, leading to aseptic necrosis","A fracture of the shaft of the femur","A pelvic fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines intra-capsular fracture as the fracture of the head or neck of femur that may result in loss of blood supply to the head of femur, resulting in aseptic necrosis.  Why A wrong: That describes an extra-capsular fracture.  Why C wrong: A femoral shaft fracture is a different injury.  Why D wrong: A pelvic fracture is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"An extra-capsular hip fracture is located:","options":["Within the head/neck of the femur","Outside the joint capsule and below/at the greater trochanter","In the cervical spine","In the femoral shaft only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes an extra-capsular fracture as a fracture outside the joint capsule and greater trochanter region.  Why A wrong: Head/neck location is intra-capsular.  Why C wrong: The cervical spine is not the hip.  Why D wrong: The femoral shaft is not the defining location."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Preoperatively, skin traction may be used in a hip fracture patient to:","options":["Increase muscle spasm","Reduce pain and muscle spasms","Lengthen the limb permanently","Replace surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes skin traction is applied preoperatively to reduce pain and muscle spasms in hip fracture.  Why A wrong: It reduces, not increases, spasm.  Why C wrong: It does not permanently lengthen the limb.  Why D wrong: It is supportive, not a replacement for definitive surgery."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The management of choice for a hip fracture, as stated in the source, is:","options":["Cast immobilization only","Total Hip Replacement (THR)","Long-term bed rest","Buck's traction alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Total Hip Replacement (THR) is the management of choice for hip fracture.  Why A wrong: A cast alone is not the management of choice.  Why C wrong: Prolonged bed rest is not curative and risks complications.  Why D wrong: Buck's traction is preoperative/supportive, not definitive management."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A classic clinical sign of a hip fracture on the affected side is:","options":["Affected leg appears longer with internal rotation","Affected leg appears shorter, with external rotation of the affected limb (cardinal sign)","The leg appears the same length with no rotation","The leg is abducted and internally rotated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the affected leg appearing shorter with external rotation of the affected limb as a cardinal sign of hip fracture.  Why A wrong: The leg appears shorter, not longer, and is externally rotated.  Why C wrong: A change in length and rotation is expected.  Why D wrong: The leg is externally rotated, not internally."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which investigation provides the confirmatory diagnosis for a hip fracture?","options":["ECG","X-ray scan","Urinalysis","Sputum culture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the confirmative diagnosis of hip fracture is an X-ray scan.  Why A wrong: ECG evaluates the heart.  Why C wrong: Urinalysis is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Sputum culture evaluates respiratory infection."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Fasciotomy is best described as:","options":["A surgical procedure where the fascia is cut to relieve pressure or tension; a limb-saving procedure used to treat acute compartment syndrome, often caused by burns and fracture","Excision of full-thickness burn eschar","Application of a cast","Removal of bone fragments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines fasciotomy as a surgical procedure where the fascia is cut to relieve pressure or tension; a limb-saving procedure used to treat acute compartment syndrome, which is caused by burns and fracture.  Why B wrong: Excision of burn eschar describes escharotomy.  Why C wrong: Casting is not fasciotomy.  Why D wrong: Fragment removal is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Escharotomy is a surgical procedure used to:","options":["Cut the fascia to relieve compartment pressure","Treat full-thickness (third-degree) circumferential burns, sometimes used for compartment syndrome along with fasciotomy","Insert internal fixation devices","Apply skin traction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes escharotomy as a surgical procedure used to treat full-thickness (third-degree) circumferential burns; sometimes used for compartment syndrome along with fasciotomy.  Why A wrong: Cutting fascia is a fasciotomy.  Why C wrong: Internal fixation is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Skin traction is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The general description and purpose of assistive devices (cane, crutches, walker) include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Relieve pressure on weight-bearing joints","Improve or maintain stability of the patient","Provide security while developing confidence in ambulating","Assist in increasing speed of ambulation; provide greater mobility and independence","Permanently confine the patient to bed"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: Assistive devices relieve pressure on weight-bearing joints.  Why B correct: They improve or maintain stability.  Why C correct: They provide security while developing confidence in ambulating.  Why D correct: They assist in increasing speed of ambulation and provide greater mobility and independence.  Why E wrong: They promote mobility, not confinement."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When teaching a patient to use a cane, the nurse should instruct the patient to hold the cane on which side and how far from the body?","options":["On the affected side, far from the body","In the hand opposite the affected extremity, with the cane held close to the body","In either hand, as far away as possible","Always with both hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to hold the cane in the hand opposite the affected extremity, with the cane close to the body.  Why A wrong: The cane is held on the opposite (unaffected) side, not the affected side, and close to the body.  Why C wrong: Holding it far away reduces stability.  Why D wrong: A cane is held in one hand."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When ascending (climbing) stairs with a cane, the correct sequence is:","options":["Step up with the affected leg first, then move the cane and unaffected leg","Move forward with the cane first, then move the unaffected (stronger) extremity, and then the affected leg onto the step","Move both legs together first","Step down with the affected leg first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when ascending climbing: step up first move forward with unaffected extremity, then place cane and then affected lower extremity on step (lead with the strong/unaffected leg going up).  Why A wrong: The unaffected leg leads going up, not the affected leg.  Why C wrong: Legs do not move simultaneously.  Why D wrong: This describes descending, not ascending."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When descending stairs with a cane, the correct first move is:","options":["Move forward with the unaffected extremity first","Step down first with the affected lower extremity (and cane), then move the unaffected leg","Keep both legs on the same step","Lead with the unaffected leg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when descending climbing: step down first move affected lower extremity (with cane), then move the unaffected leg down (the affected/weak leg leads going down).  Why A wrong: The affected leg, not the unaffected, leads going down.  Why C wrong: The legs move separately, step by step.  Why D wrong: The unaffected leg leads going up, not down."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The \"Steps While Ascending Stairs\" using a cane, in order, are:","options":["1) Shift weight to the cane and advance affected leg to next stair; 2) Shift weight to the affected leg; 3) Advance cane along with affected leg","1) Shift weight to the crutches and advance unaffected leg to next stair; 2) Shift weight to the unaffected leg; 3) Advance cane along with affected leg to the unaffected leg (next stair)","1) Advance both legs; 2) Move cane; 3) Rest","1) Step down; 2) Move cane; 3) Step down again"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ascending steps: (1) shift weight to the crutches/cane and advance the unaffected leg to the next stair, (2) shift weight to the unaffected leg, (3) advance the cane along with the affected leg to the unaffected leg (next stair).  Why A wrong: The unaffected (good) leg leads going up, not the affected leg.  Why C wrong: Legs are advanced one at a time, not both together.  Why D wrong: This describes descending steps."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The \"Steps While Descending Stairs\" using a cane, in order, are:","options":["1) Shift weight to the unaffected leg and advance affected leg along with cane (down to the stair); 2) Shift weight to the crutch/cane; 3) Advance unaffected leg down to the stair","1) Step up with affected leg; 2) Step up with cane; 3) Rest","1) Advance both legs together; 2) Move cane","1) Lead with the unaffected leg first downwards"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists descending steps: (1) shift weight to the unaffected leg and advance the affected leg along with cane down to the stair, (2) shift weight to the crutch/cane, (3) advance the unaffected leg down to the stair (the affected leg leads going down).  Why B wrong: This describes ascending, not descending.  Why C wrong: Legs move one at a time.  Why D wrong: The affected leg leads down, not the unaffected leg."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"When a patient uses crutches, the nurse should instruct that the patient's weight should be borne on which area, and what serious complication results if weight rests on the axilla?","options":["Weight on the axilla; complication is shoulder dislocation","Bear weight on the hands/palms (not the axilla); resting on the axilla can cause nerve injury (radial nerve/brachial plexus) — crutch palsy (\"crutch paralysis\")","Weight on the elbows; complication is tennis elbow","Weight on the feet; complication is plantar fasciitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source teaches that crutch weight should be borne on the hands (not the axilla) and warns that weight on the axilla causes nerve injury in the radial nerve/brachial plexus, which can cause crutch palsy (\"crutch paralysis\"); for prevention of crutch palsy, wear the weight on palms of hands when walking with crutch to avoid constant pressure on the axilla.  Why A wrong: Weight should not be on the axilla; the complication is nerve palsy, not shoulder dislocation.  Why C wrong: Weight is borne on the hands, not the elbows.  Why D wrong: This describes a foot issue, not crutch-related nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which nursing instructions apply when a patient uses crutches? (Select all that apply)","options":["Teach the client to strengthen the muscles by exercise","Ensure correct fit of crutches; maintain distance between palm and crutch as a width of 4 fingers (about 5 cm)","Observe for incorrect use of crutches","Bear the body weight on the axilla for support","Don't look down while walking with crutch"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why A correct: Teach the client to strengthen the muscles by exercise.  Why B correct: Ensure correct fit of crutches, maintaining the distance between palm and crutch (a width of 4 fingers, about 5 cm).  Why C correct: Observe for incorrect use of crutches.  Why E correct: Don't look down while walking with crutch.  Why D wrong: Weight should rest on the hands/palms, not the axilla."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which nursing responsibilities apply to a patient using a walker? (Select all that apply)","options":["This device is used when unable to ambulate with cane","Walker used when partial weight bearing is allowed","Require strong elbow extensors and shoulder depressors","Assist in ambulating with a walker; observe for incorrect use of a walker","The walker should be lifted with the feet off the floor while standing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A correct: A walker is used when the patient is unable to ambulate with a cane.  Why B correct: The walker is used when partial weight bearing is allowed.  Why C correct: It requires strong elbow extensors and shoulder depressors.  Why D correct: Assist in ambulating with a walker and observe for incorrect use of the walker.  Why E wrong: Lifting the walker with feet off the floor is not a listed instruction."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"In the four-point crutch gait, the technique is to:","options":["Move both crutches and both legs simultaneously","Move forward each foot with the left crutch then right crutch, then right foot, then left crutch (each foot moves one at a time); always three points should be on the floor — it is used when partially weight bearing is allowed","Move both crutches and the affected leg, then the unaffected leg","Move only one crutch with both legs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes four-point gait as moving each foot/crutch one at a time (left crutch, right foot, right crutch, left foot), always keeping three points on the floor; it is used when partial weight bearing is allowed.  Why A wrong: That describes a swing/three-point pattern, not four-point.  Why C wrong: That describes a three-point gait pattern.  Why D wrong: One crutch with both legs is not the four-point gait."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The two-point crutch gait technique involves:","options":["Moving the right crutch and left foot simultaneously, then the left crutch and right foot simultaneously; always two points should be supported on the floor — used when partial weight bearing is allowed","Moving both crutches first, then both legs","Moving each point one at a time with three points always on the floor","Moving only the affected leg with both crutches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes two-point gait as moving the right crutch and left foot simultaneously, then the left crutch and right foot simultaneously, with two points always supported on the floor; used when partial weight bearing is allowed.  Why B wrong: Moving both crutches then both legs describes a three-point/swing pattern.  Why C wrong: One-at-a-time with three points describes the four-point gait.  Why D wrong: Affected leg with both crutches is the three-point gait."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"The three-point crutch gait technique is best described as:","options":["Each foot and crutch moved separately one at a time","Move forward both crutches and the affected (weak) leg simultaneously, then move the unaffected (stronger) leg; it is used when totally weight bearing is allowed by the unaffected leg, with the other (affected) leg kept between/with the crutches off the floor","Right crutch with left foot, then left crutch with right foot","Move the walker, then both legs together"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes three-point gait as moving forward both crutches and the affected leg simultaneously, then moving forward the unaffected (stronger) leg; it is used when totally weight bearing is allowed by the unaffected leg, with the other affected leg kept off the floor.  Why A wrong: One-at-a-time describes the four-point gait.  Why C wrong: Right crutch/left foot pattern describes the two-point gait.  Why D wrong: That describes walker use, not the three-point gait."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"A key \"Also Remember\" point states that when assisting a client with crutches, the nurse should stand on which side of the client?","options":["On the unaffected/strong side","On the affected side of the client","Directly in front of the client","Behind the client at a distance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (\"Also Remember\") states to stand on the affected side of the client while assisting the client with crutches.  Why A wrong: The nurse stands on the affected (weak) side to provide support where the patient is most vulnerable.  Why C wrong: Standing directly in front obstructs the patient.  Why D wrong: Standing far behind does not allow timely support."},{"section":"Batch 031 · types of synovial joints, musculoskeletal injuries…","question":"Which crutch gaits are used when partial weight bearing is allowed? (Select all that apply)","options":["Four-point gait","Two-point gait","Three-point gait","None of the gaits permit weight bearing"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why A correct: The four-point gait is used when partial weight bearing is allowed.  Why B correct: The two-point gait is used when partial weight bearing is allowed.  Why C wrong: The three-point gait is used when total weight bearing is allowed by the unaffected leg (affected leg kept off the floor), not partial weight bearing.  Why D wrong: These gaits are precisely the methods for ambulating with various weight-bearing statuses."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Amputation is best defined as:","options":["The surgical removal of a limb or part of a limb","The fixation of a fractured bone with internal hardware","The drainage of an infected joint","The fusion of two adjacent vertebrae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines amputation as a surgical removal of a limb or part of a limb.  Why B wrong: That describes internal fixation/ORIF, not amputation.  Why C wrong: That describes joint aspiration/incision and drainage.  Why D wrong: That describes spinal fusion (arthrodesis)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"\"Above knee amputation\" refers to amputation of the limb:","options":["At the ankle joint","Above the knee joint","Below the knee joint","At the level of the toes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Above knee amputation is amputation of the limb above the knee joint.  Why A wrong: Amputation at the ankle joint is Syme amputation.  Why C wrong: Amputation below the knee joint is below the knee amputation.  Why D wrong: Amputation at the toes is toe amputation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"A \"Below the knee amputation\" is amputation of the limb:","options":["Above the knee joint","At the ankle joint","Below the knee joint","At the tarsometatarsal joint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Below the knee amputation is amputation of the limb below the knee joint.  Why A wrong: That is above knee amputation.  Why B wrong: That is Syme amputation (ankle joint).  Why D wrong: That is Mid-foot/Lisfranc amputation level."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Mid-foot/Lisfranc amputation involves amputation of the foot at the:","options":["Ankle joint","Tarsometatarsal joint","Knee joint","Mid-tibial level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mid-foot/Lisfranc amputation is amputation of the foot at the tarsometatarsal joint.  Why A wrong: Amputation at the ankle joint is Syme amputation.  Why C wrong: That is a knee-level amputation, not foot.  Why D wrong: That is below knee amputation, not the foot."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Syme amputation is amputation of the limb at the:","options":["Knee joint","Tarsometatarsal joint","Ankle joint","Toes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Syme amputation is amputation of the limb at the ankle joint.  Why A wrong: Knee-level amputation is above/below knee amputation.  Why B wrong: Tarsometatarsal joint level is Lisfranc amputation.  Why D wrong: Amputation at toes is toe amputation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Toe amputation refers to amputation of the limb at the:","options":["Ankle joint","Toes","Tarsometatarsal joint","Mid-foot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Toe amputation is amputation of the limb at the toes.  Why A wrong: That is Syme amputation.  Why C wrong: That is Lisfranc amputation.  Why D wrong: Mid-foot level corresponds to Lisfranc, not toe amputation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In the classification of upper limb amputation, \"wrist disarticulation\" means the limb is amputated at the level of the:","options":["Elbow","Wrist","Shoulder","Mid-forearm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wrist disarticulation is amputation at the level of the wrist.  Why A wrong: Amputation at elbow level is elbow disarticulation/trans-humeral type.  Why C wrong: Shoulder level is shoulder disarticulation.  Why D wrong: Mid-forearm corresponds to below elbow/trans-radial amputation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"\"Below elbow amputation/trans-radial\" amputation occurs at the level of the:","options":["Wrist","Forearm, from the elbow to the wrist","Upper arm, from the elbow to the wrist","Shoulder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Below elbow/trans-radial amputation occurs at the level of the forearm, from the elbow to the wrist.  Why A wrong: That is wrist disarticulation.  Why C wrong: Amputation in the upper arm (elbow to shoulder) is above elbow/trans-humeral.  Why D wrong: Shoulder level is shoulder disarticulation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Above elbow amputation/trans-humeral amputation occurs in the:","options":["Forearm, from elbow to wrist","Upper arm, from the elbow to the wrist (humeral level)","Hand and partial hand","Wrist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Above elbow/trans-humeral amputation occurs in the upper arm, from the elbow to the wrist (i.e., at the humeral level above the elbow).  Why A wrong: Forearm level is below elbow/trans-radial.  Why C wrong: Hand level is hand and partial hand amputation.  Why D wrong: Wrist level is wrist disarticulation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Hand and partial hand amputation involves amputation of the:","options":["Fingers, thumb or portion of the hand below the wrist","Forearm above the wrist","Whole upper limb at the shoulder","Arm above the elbow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hand and partial hand amputation is amputation of fingers, thumb or portion of the hand below the wrist.  Why B wrong: That involves the forearm (trans-radial), not the hand.  Why C wrong: Whole-limb removal at shoulder is shoulder disarticulation.  Why D wrong: Above-elbow removal is trans-humeral."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Forequarter amputation is amputation at the level of the shoulder in which:","options":["Only the fingers are removed","Both the shoulder blade (scapula) and collar bone (clavicle) are removed","Only the forearm is removed","The limb is removed at the wrist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Forequarter amputation is amputation at the level of the shoulder in which both the shoulder blade and collar bone are removed.  Why A wrong: Finger removal is hand/partial hand amputation.  Why C wrong: Forearm removal is trans-radial amputation.  Why D wrong: Wrist-level removal is wrist disarticulation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Shoulder disarticulation refers to amputation at the level of the:","options":["Wrist","Elbow","Shoulder","Mid-forearm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Shoulder disarticulation is amputation at the level of the shoulder.  Why A wrong: Wrist level is wrist disarticulation.  Why B wrong: Elbow level is elbow disarticulation.  Why D wrong: Mid-forearm is trans-radial level."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of amputation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Haemorrhage","Infection","Phantom limb pain","Flexion contractures","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists complications of amputation as haemorrhage, infection, phantom limb pain, and flexion contractures.  Why E wrong: Hyperthyroidism is not a complication of amputation and is not listed on the page."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Scoliosis is defined as:","options":["Inward curvature of the lumbar spine","Lateral curvature of the spine","Outward curvature of the thoracic spine","Herniation of an intervertebral disk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines scoliosis as lateral curvature of the spine.  Why A wrong: Inward lumbar curvature is lordosis.  Why C wrong: Outward thoracic curvature is kyphosis.  Why D wrong: Disk herniation is a separate disorder, not scoliosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Lordosis, which is the inward curvature of the lumbar spine, is also known as which deformity, and is commonly seen in pregnancy and obesity?","options":["Humpback deformity","Swayback deformity","Dowager's hump","Pannus deformity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lordosis (inward curvature of the lumbar spine) is also known as swayback deformity, commonly seen in pregnancy and obesity.  Why A wrong: Humpback disease is kyphosis.  Why C wrong: Dowager's hump is associated with kyphosis.  Why D wrong: Pannus is a rheumatoid arthritis feature, not a spinal deformity."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Kyphosis (outward curvature of the thoracic spine) is also known as:","options":["Swayback deformity","Scoliotic curve","Humpback disease or Dowager's hump disease","Lisfranc deformity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states kyphosis is the outward curvature of the thoracic spine, also known as humpback disease or Dowager's hump disease.  Why A wrong: Swayback deformity is lordosis.  Why B wrong: A lateral curve is scoliosis.  Why D wrong: Lisfranc relates to mid-foot amputation, not the spine."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Cervical disk herniation most commonly occurs at which spinal levels?","options":["C5-C6 and C6-C7","L4-L5 and L5-S1","T1-T2 and T2-T3","C1-C2 and C2-C3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cervical disk herniation most commonly occurs at the C5-C6 and C6-C7 levels.  Why B wrong: L4-L5 and L5-S1 are the common sites for lumbar disk herniation.  Why C wrong: These thoracic levels are not the stated common sites.  Why D wrong: C1-C2/C2-C3 are not the stated common sites for cervical herniation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In cervical disk herniation, pain characteristically radiates to which areas?","options":["Lower back and posterior thighs","Shoulders, arms, hands, scapulae, and pectoral muscles","Abdomen and groin","Face and jaw only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cervical disk herniation pain radiates to shoulders, arms, hands, scapulae, and pectoral muscles.  Why A wrong: Lower back/thigh radiation describes lumbar (sciatica) pain.  Why C wrong: Abdomen/groin is not the described radiation pattern.  Why D wrong: Face and jaw are not listed radiation sites."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which sleeping/positioning instruction is recommended for a patient with cervical disk herniation?","options":["Maintain the prone position with the neck extended","Avoid the prone position and maintain neck, spine, and hips in a neutral position while sleeping","Sleep flat without any pillow and keep the neck flexed","Sleep with the neck rotated to one side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the patient to avoid the prone position and maintain neck, spine, and hips in a neutral position while sleeping; also avoid flexing, extending, and rotating the neck.  Why A wrong: The prone position is to be avoided, not maintained.  Why C wrong: Neck flexion is to be avoided.  Why D wrong: Neck rotation is to be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which supportive device is used for cervical disk herniation?","options":["Lumbar corset","Cervical collar","Knee immobilizer","Abdominal binder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a cervical collar is used for cervical disk herniation.  Why A wrong: A lumbar corset relates to lumbar support, not cervical.  Why C wrong: A knee immobilizer is for the knee.  Why D wrong: An abdominal binder is unrelated to cervical herniation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Lumbar disk herniation commonly occurs at which inter-space?","options":["C5-C6 and C6-C7","L4-L5 and L5-S1","T11-T12","C7-T1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lumbar disk herniation commonly occurs at the L4-L5 and L5-S1 inter-space.  Why A wrong: These are cervical herniation sites.  Why C wrong: T11-T12 is not the stated common lumbar site.  Why D wrong: C7-T1 is a cervicothoracic junction, not the stated lumbar site."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"A clinical feature of lumbar disk herniation is lower back pain with radiation of the pain into one hip and down the leg. This radiating leg pain is known as:","options":["Phantom pain","Sciatica pain","Referred shoulder pain","Neuropathic facial pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes lower back pain radiating into one hip and down the leg, called sciatica pain.  Why A wrong: Phantom pain follows amputation.  Why C wrong: Shoulder radiation is a cervical herniation feature.  Why D wrong: Facial pain is unrelated to lumbar disk herniation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In lumbar disk herniation, the pain is described as being:","options":["Increased by bed rest and decreased by movement","Decreased by bed rest and increased by movements, lifting, straining, and coughing","Unaffected by activity","Worst only during sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pain is decreased by bed rest and increased by movements, lifting, straining, and coughing.  Why A wrong: This reverses the actual relationship described.  Why C wrong: The source explicitly relates pain to activity.  Why D wrong: The source emphasizes movement/straining, not sleep, as aggravating factors."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is best described as a:","options":["Degenerative joint disease of unknown cause due to wear and tear","Chronic systemic inflammatory disorder due to autoimmune mechanism","Purine metabolism disorder","Bone infection caused by bacteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines RA as a chronic systemic inflammatory disorder due to autoimmune cause.  Why A wrong: Degenerative wear-and-tear disease describes osteoarthritis.  Why C wrong: Purine metabolism disorder describes gout.  Why D wrong: Bone infection describes osteomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Rheumatoid arthritis is characterised by destruction of connective tissues, synovial membranes within the joints, collagen within the joints, and necrosis of cartilage and bones at the joints due to:","options":["Uric acid deposition","Pannus formation","Bacterial seeding","Vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source attributes RA destruction at the joints to pannus formation.  Why A wrong: Uric acid deposition is the mechanism in gout.  Why C wrong: Bacterial seeding causes osteomyelitis.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D deficiency relates to osteomalacia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Pannus formation in rheumatoid arthritis is defined as:","options":["Deposition of uric acid crystals in the joints","A condition in which a layer of vascular fibrous tissue extends over the surface of an organ or other specialized anatomical structure","Outward curvature of the thoracic spine","A break in the continuity of bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines pannus formation as a condition in which a layer of vascular fibrous tissue extends over the surface of an organ or other specialized anatomical structure.  Why A wrong: Uric acid crystal deposition (tophi) occurs in gout.  Why C wrong: That describes kyphosis.  Why D wrong: That describes a fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which antibody is present and used for the diagnosis of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":["HLA-DR4 antibody","Anti-CCP only and never HLA-related","Antinuclear antibody specific to lupus","HLA-B27 antibody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HLA-DR4 antibody is present and used for diagnosis of RA.  Why B wrong: The source names HLA-DR4, not an exclusively anti-CCP marker.  Why C wrong: The source names HLA-DR4, not a lupus-specific ANA.  Why D wrong: The source names HLA-DR4, not HLA-B27."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following is described as the \"cardinal sign\" of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":["Tophi formation","Inflammation, tenderness, and stiffness of the joints","Heberden's nodes","Uric acid deposition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists inflammation, tenderness, and stiffness of the joints as the cardinal sign of RA.  Why A wrong: Tophi is the cardinal sign of gout.  Why C wrong: Heberden's nodes are an osteoarthritis sign.  Why D wrong: Uric acid deposition relates to gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which laboratory findings are consistent with rheumatoid arthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Elevated ESR","Positive rheumatoid factor","Presence of HLA-DR4 antibody","Decreased uric acid level","Low ESR"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RA shows elevated ESR and positive rheumatoid factor or presence of HLA-DR4 antibody.  Why D wrong: Uric acid changes relate to gout, not RA.  Why E wrong: ESR is elevated, not low, in RA."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs/symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Moderate to severe pain and anaemia","Joint deformities and muscle atrophy","Decreased range of motion in affected joints","Spongy and soft feeling in the joints","Low-grade fever as the only feature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists moderate to severe pain and anaemia, joint deformities and muscle atrophy, decreased range of motion in affected joints, and a spongy/soft feeling in the joints for RA.  Why E wrong: Low-grade fever is listed under gouty arthritis, not as the sole RA feature."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are classical characteristic deformities of rheumatoid arthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ulnar drift","Swan neck deformity","Boutonniere deformity","Z-shaped thumb","MCP sub-luxation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ulnar drift, swan neck deformity, boutonniere deformity, Z-shaped thumb, and MCP sub-luxation as classical characteristics of RA.  Why each is correct: All five are explicitly listed; none is a distractor here."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The rheumatoid factor is a blood test which is used for the screening of rheumatoid arthritis. According to the source, which value range is classified as \"reactive\"?","options":["0-40 IU/ml","40-80 IU/ml",">80 IU/ml","<10 IU/ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives rheumatoid factor values as nonreactive 0-40 IU/ml, weakly reactive 40-80 IU/ml, and reactive >80 IU/ml.  Why A wrong: 0-40 IU/ml is nonreactive.  Why B wrong: 40-80 IU/ml is weakly reactive.  Why D wrong: <10 IU/ml is not a category listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the source, the rheumatoid factor value range classified as \"nonreactive\" is:","options":["0-40 IU/ml","40-80 IU/ml",">80 IU/ml","80-160 IU/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies 0-40 IU/ml as nonreactive.  Why B wrong: 40-80 IU/ml is weakly reactive.  Why C wrong: >80 IU/ml is reactive.  Why D wrong: 80-160 IU/ml is not a category listed."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The drug of choice for rheumatoid arthritis is an NSAID such as:","options":["Allopurinol","Ibuprofen","Colchicine","Corticosteroid replacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for RA is an NSAID like Ibuprofen.  Why A wrong: Allopurinol is the drug of choice for gout.  Why C wrong: Colchicine is used for gout.  Why D wrong: The source specifies an NSAID (ibuprofen), not corticosteroid replacement."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteoarthritis is also known as:","options":["Chronic systemic inflammatory joint disease","Degenerative joint disease","Purine metabolism disorder","Bone-remodeling disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoarthritis is also known as degenerative joint disease and is a degenerative/progressive deterioration of the articular cartilage.  Why A wrong: Chronic systemic inflammatory disease describes RA.  Why C wrong: Purine metabolism disorder describes gout.  Why D wrong: Bone-remodeling disorder describes Paget's disease."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteoarthritis mostly affects which type of joints?","options":["Non-weight bearing joints only","Weight bearing joints which receive the greatest weight or stress, such as hips, knees, lower vertebral column, and hands","Only the small joints of the feet","Only synovial membranes of the wrist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoarthritis mostly affects weight bearing joints which receive the greatest weight or stress such as hips, knees, lower vertebral column, and hands.  Why A wrong: It affects weight-bearing joints, the opposite of non-weight bearing only.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to small foot joints.  Why D wrong: It is a cartilage degeneration disorder, not isolated wrist synovitis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for osteoarthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Aging","Trauma","Obesity","Smoking","Autoimmune antibody production"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists aging, trauma, obesity, and smoking as risk factors for osteoarthritis.  Why E wrong: Autoimmune antibody production relates to RA, not the listed OA risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The exact cause of osteoarthritis is described as:","options":["Known and clearly autoimmune","Unknown","Bacterial infection","Excess uric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the exact cause of osteoarthritis is unknown.  Why A wrong: Autoimmune causation is attributed to RA.  Why C wrong: Bacterial infection causes osteomyelitis.  Why D wrong: Excess uric acid causes gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In osteoarthritis, symptoms are described as becoming more intense with:","options":["Bed rest","Changes in climate humidity and temperature","Reduced dietary purines","Allopurinol therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoarthritis symptoms are more intense with changes in climate humidity and temperature.  Why A wrong: The source links intensity to climate changes, not specifically to bed rest.  Why C wrong: Dietary purines relate to gout, not osteoarthritis symptom intensity.  Why D wrong: Allopurinol is a gout drug, unrelated to OA symptom intensity."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which nodes, described as the cardinal sign, are usually seen in the hands in osteoarthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Heberden's nodes","Bouchard's nodes","Tophi","Pannus","Osler's nodes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Heberden's nodes and Bouchard's nodes are usually seen in hands in osteoarthritis (cardinal sign).  Why C wrong: Tophi are seen in gout.  Why D wrong: Pannus is a feature of RA.  Why E wrong: Osler's nodes are not mentioned and relate to infective endocarditis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are signs/symptoms of osteoarthritis listed on the page? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Joint swelling, crepitus, and limited range of motion","Inability to perform ADL","Moderate to severe pain","Tophi formation","Heberden's and Bouchard's nodes"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists moderate to severe pain, Heberden's and Bouchard's nodes, joint swelling/crepitus and limited range of motion, and inability to perform ADL for osteoarthritis.  Why D wrong: Tophi formation is a feature of gout, not osteoarthritis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Gouty arthritis is fundamentally a disorder of:","options":["Purine metabolism","Calcium metabolism","Bone remodeling","Autoimmune connective tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states gouty arthritis is a purine metabolism disorder.  Why B wrong: Calcium metabolism impairment relates to osteomalacia.  Why C wrong: Bone remodeling disorder is Paget's disease.  Why D wrong: Autoimmune connective tissue disease is RA."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Uric acid is produced when the body breaks down which substance?","options":["Pyrimidine","Purine","Calcium phosphate","Collagen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uric acid is produced when the body breaks down the purine.  Why A wrong: The source specifies purine, not pyrimidine.  Why C wrong: Calcium phosphate is bone mineral, not the source of uric acid.  Why D wrong: Collagen breakdown does not produce uric acid per the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In gout, due to improper metabolism of purine, the uric acid level increases in the blood and uric acid crystals are deposited in the joints. These deposits (the cardinal sign) are known as:","options":["Pannus","Tophi","Osteophytes","Heberden's nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uric acid crystals deposited in the joints are known as Tophi (cardinal sign).  Why A wrong: Pannus is the RA proliferative tissue.  Why C wrong: Osteophytes are bone spurs (associated with OA), not the named gout deposit.  Why D wrong: Heberden's nodes are an osteoarthritis sign."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The drug of choice for gouty arthritis is:","options":["Ibuprofen","Allopurinol","Colchicine","NSAIDs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Allopurinol as the drug of choice for gout (with colchicine, NSAIDs and others as additional medications).  Why A wrong: Ibuprofen is named as the NSAID drug of choice for RA.  Why C wrong: Colchicine is listed among medications but Allopurinol is the drug of choice.  Why D wrong: NSAIDs are listed as adjuncts; Allopurinol is the drug of choice."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The diagnosis of gouty arthritis is made by:","options":["Serum uric acid level","Rheumatoid factor","Bone mineral density test","HLA-DR4 antibody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis of gout is made by serum uric acid level.  Why B wrong: Rheumatoid factor screens for RA.  Why C wrong: BMD test diagnoses metabolic bone disease/osteoporosis.  Why D wrong: HLA-DR4 antibody is used in RA diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are signs/symptoms of gouty arthritis listed on the page? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Swelling and inflammation of the joints with increased intensity of pain","Tophi: hard, irregularly shaped nodules usually in hands due to deposition of uric acid","Low-grade fever","Presence of renal stones due to elevated uric acid levels","Swan neck deformity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists swelling/inflammation with increased pain intensity, tophi (hard, irregularly shaped nodules usually in hands from uric acid deposition), low grade fever, and presence of renal stones from elevated uric acid as gout signs/symptoms.  Why E wrong: Swan neck deformity is a rheumatoid arthritis feature."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed nursing care measures appropriate for all types of arthritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assist with daily activities","Maintain functional alignment of the joints","Instruct the client to sit on a chair with a high straight back","Teach about exercise","Encourage prolonged bed rest with no movement"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists assist with daily activities, maintain functional alignment of the joints, instruct the client to sit on a chair with a high straight back, and teach about exercise as nursing care for all types of arthritis.  Why E wrong: The source emphasizes assisting activities and exercise, not enforced immobility/prolonged bed rest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"For gouty arthritis, the recommended diet is a low purine diet that avoids high purine foods. Which foods should be avoided? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Meats","Seafoods such as tuna and sardines","Wines and alcohol","Aged cheese","Fresh water and plain rice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the gout diet should avoid high purine foods such as meats, seafoods (tuna, sardines), wines, alcohol, and aged cheese.  Why E wrong: Fluids are actually encouraged (to prevent stone formation); plain water/rice are not high-purine foods to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In nursing care for gouty arthritis, fluids should be offered at what level to prevent stone formation?","options":["1000 ml/day","2000-3000 ml/day","Restricted to 500 ml/day","Only with meals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to offer more fluids 2000-3000 ml/day to prevent stone formation.  Why A wrong: 1000 ml/day is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: Fluid restriction is the opposite of the recommendation.  Why D wrong: Fluids should be increased overall, not limited to mealtimes."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which additional nursing measures for arthritis are listed on the page? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bed rest during an acute attack","Protect the joints from excessive movements","To give medication as per order","Use a small pillow when lying down and take medication on time as per order","Apply ice continuously for 24 hours to fuse the joint"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bed rest during acute attack, protecting joints from excessive movements, giving medication as per order, and using only a small pillow when lying down with timely medication.  Why E wrong: Continuous ice to \"fuse\" a joint is not described and is not a listed measure."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteomyelitis is defined as:","options":["A bone infection by direct invasion or indirectly by circulation","Softening of the bone due to impaired metabolism","Loss of bone mass with normal mineralization","A purine metabolism disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines osteomyelitis as a bone infection by direct invasion or indirectly by circulation.  Why B wrong: Softening of the bone is osteomalacia.  Why C wrong: Loss of bone mass describes osteoporosis.  Why D wrong: Purine metabolism disorder is gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In osteomyelitis, \"indirectly entry\" of infection occurs by:","options":["Open wound to a bone","Blood from another site by circulation (arterial blood)","Direct surgical incision","Lymphatic spread only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states indirectly entry is by blood from another site by circulation (via arterial blood).  Why A wrong: Open wound to bone is the directly entry route.  Why C wrong: Surgical incision would be a direct route, not the described indirect (hematogenous) route.  Why D wrong: The source specifies blood/arterial circulation, not lymphatic spread."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In osteomyelitis, \"directly entry\" of infection occurs by:","options":["Hematogenous spread from a distant site","Open wound to arterial blood / open wound to the bone","Autoimmune destruction","Uric acid deposition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states directly entry is by open wound to arterial blood / open wound to the bone.  Why A wrong: Hematogenous spread is the indirectly entry route.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune destruction relates to RA, not osteomyelitis entry.  Why D wrong: Uric acid deposition relates to gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The most common causative organism of osteomyelitis is:","options":["Streptococcus pyogenes","Staphylococcus aureus","Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus, the most common cause of osteomyelitis.  Why A wrong: The source specifies Staphylococcus aureus, not Streptococcus pyogenes.  Why C wrong: E. coli is not the named common cause.  Why D wrong: Pseudomonas is not the named common cause in the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Diagnosis of osteomyelitis is made by:","options":["Serum uric acid level","Positive culture and scans","Rheumatoid factor","Goniometer measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis of osteomyelitis is made by positive culture and scans.  Why A wrong: Serum uric acid diagnoses gout.  Why C wrong: Rheumatoid factor screens for RA.  Why D wrong: A goniometer measures joint angles, not infection."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are signs/symptoms of osteomyelitis listed on the page? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bone pain","Tenderness","Fever","Swelling on the bones","Tophi formation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bone pain, tenderness, fever, and swelling on the bones as signs/symptoms of osteomyelitis.  Why E wrong: Tophi formation is a feature of gout, not osteomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"A nursing measure listed for osteomyelitis is to administer ordered analgesics, antibiotics, and:","options":["Cold soaks","Warm soaks","Allopurinol","Calcium infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states administer ordered analgesics, antibiotics, and warm soaks for osteomyelitis.  Why A wrong: The source specifies warm soaks, not cold soaks.  Why C wrong: Allopurinol is a gout medication, not listed for osteomyelitis.  Why D wrong: Calcium infusion is not listed for osteomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteoporosis is best described as:","options":["A bone infection","A metabolic disorder involving bone demineralization with loss of calcium and phosphorus salts, leading to fragile or weak bones","An autoimmune inflammatory joint disease","A purine metabolism disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines osteoporosis as a metabolic disorder of bone demineralization with loss of calcium and phosphorus salts leading to fragile or weak bones and subsequent fracture risk.  Why A wrong: Bone infection is osteomyelitis.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune inflammatory joint disease is RA.  Why D wrong: Purine metabolism disorder is gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteoporosis mostly affects which type of bones?","options":["Non-weight bearing bones such as the skull","The weight bearing joints/bones such as wrist, hip, and vertebral column","The facial bones only","The long bones of the fingers only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoporosis mostly affects the weight bearing joints/bones such as wrist, hip, and vertebral column.  Why A wrong: It affects weight bearing bones, not the skull primarily.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to facial bones.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to finger bones."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the source, there are how many types of osteoporosis, and what are they?","options":["One type only","Two types: primary and secondary osteoporosis","Three types: primary, secondary, and tertiary","Four types based on bone affected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are two types: primary and secondary osteoporosis.  Why A wrong: More than one type is described.  Why C wrong: Only two types are named.  Why D wrong: The source names two types, not four."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Primary osteoporosis most often occurs in which groups?","options":["Postmenopausal women, and in men with low testosterone levels","Children with rapid growth","Patients on corticosteroids","Patients with hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states primary osteoporosis most often occurs in postmenopausal women and in men with low testosterone levels.  Why B wrong: Children are not the stated group for primary osteoporosis.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroid-related bone loss falls under secondary osteoporosis.  Why D wrong: Hyperthyroidism is not the stated cause of primary osteoporosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of primary osteoporosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Decreased calcium intake","Low level of estrogen","Sedentary life styles","Long-term corticosteroid use","Malabsorption"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists decreased calcium intake, low level of estrogen, and sedentary life styles as causes of primary osteoporosis.  Why D wrong: Prolonged corticosteroid medication is listed under secondary osteoporosis.  Why E wrong: Malabsorption is listed under secondary osteoporosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Secondary osteoporosis is due to low bone mass. Which of the following are listed causes of secondary osteoporosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Immobility","Alcoholism","Malnutrition and malabsorption","Prolonged medications such as corticosteroids, thyroid medications, and aluminium containing antacids","Postmenopausal estrogen loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists immobility, alcoholism, malnutrition and malabsorption, and prolonged medications (corticosteroids, thyroid medications, aluminium containing antacids) as causes of secondary osteoporosis.  Why E wrong: Postmenopausal estrogen loss is a cause of primary osteoporosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for osteoporosis on the page? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Smoking","Early menopause","Excessive use of alcohol","Family history, female gender, insufficient intake of calcium, aging, and sedentary life style","High dietary purine intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists smoking, early menopause, excessive use of alcohol, family history, female gender, insufficient intake of calcium, aging, and sedentary life style as risk factors for osteoporosis.  Why E wrong: High purine intake is a gout dietary concern, not an osteoporosis risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Signs and symptoms of osteoporosis include pain that is described as intense:","options":["Only at rest","When lifting, bending, or stooping","Only during fever","Only after eating purine-rich food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pain is intense when lifting, bending, or stooping (cardinal sign).  Why A wrong: The pain relates to mechanical loading, not rest.  Why C wrong: Fever is not the described trigger.  Why D wrong: Purine food relates to gout, not osteoporosis pain."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed clinical features of osteoporosis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Back pain","Pelvic or hip pain, usually when weight bearing","Dowager's hump (vertebral fracture or kyphosis of the dorsal spine)","Tophi in the hands"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists back pain, pelvic or hip pain (usually when weight bearing), and Dowager's hump—vertebral fracture or kyphosis of the dorsal spine—as clinical features of osteoporosis.  Why D wrong: Tophi are a feature of gout, not osteoporosis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In osteoporosis, the \"Dowager's hump\" represents:","options":["A swayback curvature of the lumbar spine","Vertebral fracture or kyphosis of the dorsal spine","Lateral curvature of the spine","A herniated cervical disk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Dowager's hump is vertebral fracture or kyphosis of the dorsal spine seen in osteoporosis.  Why A wrong: Swayback (lordosis) is inward lumbar curvature.  Why C wrong: Lateral curvature is scoliosis.  Why D wrong: A herniated cervical disk is a separate condition."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The source notes that a client with osteoporosis is at risk for which type of fractures?","options":["Pathological fractures","Greenstick fractures only","Open compound fractures only","Stress fractures of athletes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's note states the client with osteoporosis is at risk for pathological fractures.  Why B wrong: Greenstick fractures are not the type the source highlights for osteoporosis.  Why C wrong: The source specifies pathological fractures, not exclusively open fractures.  Why D wrong: Athletic stress fractures are not the stated risk."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Regarding the General Diagnostic Tests of the Musculoskeletal System, which test is the most commonly used for diagnostic purposes?","options":["Arthrocentesis","Radiography (X-ray)","Arthrography","Arthroscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states radiography is the most commonly used for diagnostic purposes for musculoskeletal disorders.  Why A wrong: Arthrocentesis is an invasive aspiration procedure, not the most commonly used diagnostic.  Why C wrong: Arthrography is a contrast-based invasive procedure.  Why D wrong: Arthroscopy is an endoscopic examination, not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Arthrocentesis is best described as:","options":["A radiographic examination of soft tissues","An invasive procedure in which aspirating synovial fluids, blood, or pus via a needle inserted into a joint cavity, into which medications such as corticosteroids may also be inserted if necessary to alleviate inflammation","An endoscopic examination of joints","A bone mineral density measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes arthrocentesis as an invasive procedure aspirating synovial fluids, blood, or pus via a needle inserted into a joint cavity, with medications such as corticosteroids inserted if necessary to alleviate inflammation.  Why A wrong: That describes radiography, not arthrocentesis.  Why C wrong: Endoscopic examination is arthroscopy.  Why D wrong: BMD measurement is the BMD test."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Arthrography is an invasive procedure in which:","options":["A bone biopsy is taken","A contrast media or a dye is injected into the joint cavity and the joint is moved through a range of motion while a series of X-rays are taken; it is a radiographic examination of the soft tissues of the joint structure","An endoscope is inserted into the joint","Electrical activity of skeletal muscle is recorded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states arthrography injects a contrast media or dye into the joint cavity and the joint is moved through a range of motion while a series of X-rays are taken; it is a radiographic examination of the soft tissues of the joint structure.  Why A wrong: Bone biopsy is a separate test.  Why C wrong: Endoscope insertion describes arthroscopy.  Why D wrong: Recording electrical activity of muscle is electromyography."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Arthroscopy is best described as:","options":["An endoscopic examination of various joints, during which articular cartilage abnormalities can be assessed, loose bodies removed, and the cartilage trimmed","Injection of contrast dye into a joint","Aspiration of synovial fluid with a needle","A bone mineral density measurement test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states arthroscopy is an endoscopic examination of various joints during which articular cartilage abnormalities can be assessed, loose bodies removed, and the cartilage trimmed.  Why B wrong: Contrast dye injection is arthrography.  Why C wrong: Needle aspiration of synovial fluid is arthrocentesis.  Why D wrong: BMD measurement is the BMD test."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The BMD (Bone Mineral Density) test is best described as:","options":["An endoscopic joint examination","A bone mineral density measurement test; Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry is used to diagnose metabolic bone diseases and monitor change in bone density with treatment","A contrast dye joint study","A recording of electrical activity of muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the BMD test is a bone mineral density measurement test, and dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry is used to diagnose metabolic bone diseases and monitor change in bone density with treatment.  Why A wrong: Endoscopic joint examination is arthroscopy.  Why C wrong: Contrast dye joint study is arthrography.  Why D wrong: Electrical activity recording is electromyography."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"A Bone Scan is used to:","options":["Identify, evaluate, and stage bone cancers before and after treatment","Measure joint angles","Aspirate joint fluid","Record muscle electrical activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a bone scan is used for identify, evaluate, and stage bone cancers before and after treatment.  Why B wrong: Measuring joint angles is done with a goniometer.  Why C wrong: Aspiration of joint fluid is arthrocentesis.  Why D wrong: Recording muscle electrical activity is electromyography."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Regarding a Bone/Muscle Biopsy, which post-procedure instruction does the source provide?","options":["After taking the biopsy, the site should be elevated for 24 hours to reduce oedema","The site should be kept dependent and not elevated","Apply continuous heat for 24 hours","Immobilize the limb for 1 week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states biopsy may be done during surgery or through aspiration, or punch or needle biopsy, and after taking the biopsy the site should be elevated for 24 hours to reduce oedema.  Why B wrong: The site is elevated, not kept dependent.  Why C wrong: Continuous heat is not the stated post-biopsy instruction.  Why D wrong: A one-week immobilization is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Electromyography (EMG) is best described as:","options":["A graphical recording of the electrical activity of skeletal muscles","An X-ray of bone density","An endoscopic joint examination","Aspiration of synovial fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states EMG is a graphical recording of the electrical activity of skeletal muscles.  Why B wrong: X-ray of bone density is DEXA/BMD.  Why C wrong: Endoscopic joint examination is arthroscopy.  Why D wrong: Synovial fluid aspiration is arthrocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Osteomalacia is best described as:","options":["The softening of the bone due to impaired bone metabolism","A bone infection","An autoimmune joint disease","A purine metabolism disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines osteomalacia as the softening of the bone due to impaired bone metabolism.  Why B wrong: Bone infection is osteomyelitis.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune joint disease is RA.  Why D wrong: Purine metabolism disorder is gout."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of osteomalacia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Inadequate intake of calcium","Inadequate intake of phosphate","Inadequate intake of vitamin-D","Excess purine intake"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states causes of osteomalacia include inadequate intake of calcium, phosphate, and vitamin-D.  Why D wrong: Excess purine relates to gout, not osteomalacia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the source, which statement correctly contrasts osteomalacia with osteoporosis?","options":["In osteomalacia bone is soft, but in osteoporosis bone is porous, brittle, fragile, or weak","In osteomalacia bone is porous, but in osteoporosis bone is soft","Both conditions cause identical bone changes","Osteomalacia causes bone infection; osteoporosis causes inflammation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in osteomalacia bone is soft, but in osteoporosis bone is porous, brittle, fragile, or weak.  Why B wrong: It reverses the described characteristics.  Why C wrong: The source distinguishes the two conditions.  Why D wrong: Neither condition is described as causing infection or inflammation here."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Rhabdomyolysis is defined as:","options":["Breakdown of muscle tissue due to muscle trauma","Softening of bone","Loss of bone calcium","Inflammation of synovial joints"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines rhabdomyolysis as breakdown of muscle tissue due to muscle trauma.  Why B wrong: Softening of bone is osteomalacia.  Why C wrong: Loss of bone calcium relates to osteoporosis/osteomalacia.  Why D wrong: Synovial joint inflammation is arthritis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In rhabdomyolysis, due to muscle trauma, which substance is present in the urine?","options":["Myoglobin (myoglobinuria)","Uric acid","Bilirubin","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that due to muscle trauma, myoglobin is present in urine (myoglobinuria).  Why B wrong: Uric acid relates to gout.  Why C wrong: Bilirubin in urine relates to liver/biliary disease, not rhabdomyolysis here.  Why D wrong: Glucose in urine relates to diabetes, not rhabdomyolysis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"The source warns that if rhabdomyolysis is not treated, which complication can occur?","options":["Renal failure","Bone infection","Joint deformity","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if it is not treated, renal failure will happen.  Why B wrong: Bone infection (osteomyelitis) is unrelated to rhabdomyolysis complications here.  Why C wrong: Joint deformity is an RA/OA feature.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not the stated complication."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs/symptoms of rhabdomyolysis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Muscle weakness","Tenderness","Swelling","Dark, reddish-brown urine","Tophi formation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists muscle weakness, tenderness, swelling, and dark, reddish-brown urine as signs/symptoms of rhabdomyolysis.  Why E wrong: Tophi formation is a feature of gout, not rhabdomyolysis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Dialysis may be required in rhabdomyolysis primarily because of which complication?","options":["Renal failure","Bone softening","Joint inflammation","Hyperuricemia from purine intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes dialysis may be required (linked to the renal failure that can result from untreated rhabdomyolysis).  Why B wrong: Bone softening (osteomalacia) does not require dialysis.  Why C wrong: Joint inflammation does not necessitate dialysis.  Why D wrong: The dialysis need stems from renal failure, not purine intake."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Paget's disease is best described as a disorder of:","options":["Bone remodeling process, in which the body absorbs old bones and forms abnormal new bones","Purine metabolism","Synovial autoimmune inflammation","Muscle breakdown"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Paget's disease as a disorder of bone remodeling process in which the body absorbs old bones and forms abnormal new bones.  Why B wrong: Purine metabolism disorder is gout.  Why C wrong: Synovial autoimmune inflammation is RA.  Why D wrong: Muscle breakdown is rhabdomyolysis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which bones are most commonly affected in Paget's disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pelvis","Skull","Legs","Fingers and toes only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most commonly affected bones in Paget's disease are pelvis, skull, and legs.  Why D wrong: Fingers and toes are not the named commonly affected bones."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"In the \"Also Remember\" box, \"Discectomy\" is defined as:","options":["Removal of herniated disk tissue and related matter","Excision of part of the vertebra to remove the disk","Division of the lamina of a vertebra","Fusion of two vertebrae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines discectomy as removal of herniated disk tissue and related matter.  Why B wrong: Excision of part of the vertebra to remove the disk is laminectomy.  Why C wrong: Division of the lamina of a vertebra is laminotomy.  Why D wrong: Fusion of vertebrae (spinal fusion) is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Laminectomy is defined as the excision of the part of the vertebra to remove the disk. Which position is used for this surgery?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Moslem prayer position","Lithotomy position","Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states laminectomy is excision of the part of the vertebra to remove the disk and the Moslem prayer position is used for this surgery.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is not the stated surgical position.  Why C wrong: Lithotomy is used for pelvic/perineal procedures, not specified here.  Why D wrong: Fowler's position is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Laminotomy is defined as:","options":["Removal of herniated disk tissue","Division of the lamina of a vertebra","Excision of the entire vertebral body","Aspiration of synovial fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines laminotomy as division of the lamina of a vertebra.  Why A wrong: Removal of herniated disk tissue is discectomy.  Why C wrong: Excision of the vertebral body is not the laminotomy definition.  Why D wrong: Synovial fluid aspiration is arthrocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is described as being caused by compression in the:","options":["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Sciatic nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states carpal tunnel syndrome is caused by compression in the median nerve.  Why B wrong: The source names the median nerve, not the ulnar nerve.  Why C wrong: The radial nerve is not the compressed nerve described.  Why D wrong: The sciatic nerve relates to lumbar disk herniation/sciatica."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Carpal tunnel syndrome commonly occurs in which population, according to the source?","options":["Computer workers","Children only","Postmenopausal women only","Athletes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states carpal tunnel syndrome commonly occurs in computer workers.  Why B wrong: Children are not the stated population.  Why C wrong: Postmenopausal women are linked to osteoporosis, not specifically carpal tunnel here.  Why D wrong: Athletes are not the stated population."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"Which of the following are listed clinical features of carpal tunnel syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pain","Numbness","Paresthesia","Weakness","Dark reddish-brown urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pain, numbness, paresthesia, and weakness as clinical features of carpal tunnel syndrome.  Why E wrong: Dark, reddish-brown urine is a feature of rhabdomyolysis, not carpal tunnel syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the total number of bones in the human body is:","options":["206","208","200","250"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's rapid revision states the total number of bones in the human body is 206.  Why B wrong: 208 is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 200 is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 250-300 is the count given for a newborn, not the adult total."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the largest bone of the body is the:","options":["Femur","Tibia","Humerus","Stapes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the largest bone of the body is the femur.  Why B wrong: The tibia is the shin bone, not the largest.  Why C wrong: The humerus is the upper arm bone, not the largest.  Why D wrong: The stapes is the smallest bone, not the largest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the smallest bone of the body is the:","options":["Stapes (middle ear bone)","Patella","Pisiform","Hyoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the smallest bone of the body is the stapes (middle ear bone).  Why B wrong: The patella is the knee cap, not the smallest bone.  Why C wrong: The pisiform is a short carpal bone, but the smallest is the stapes.  Why D wrong: The hyoid is not the stated smallest bone."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the total number of ribs is:","options":["12","24 (12 pairs)","26","14"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of ribs is 24 (12 pairs).  Why A wrong: 12 is the number of pairs, not the total ribs.  Why C wrong: 26 is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 14 is the number of facial bones in this source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the patella bone is also known as the:","options":["Knee cap","Collar bone","Beauty bone","Shin bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patella bone is also known as the knee cap.  Why B wrong: The collar bone is the clavicle.  Why C wrong: \"Beauty bone\" refers to the clavicle.  Why D wrong: The shin bone is the tibia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the clavicle bone is also known as the:","options":["Collar bone and beauty bone","Knee cap","Shin bone","Funny bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the clavicle bone is also known as the collar bone and beauty bone.  Why B wrong: The knee cap is the patella.  Why C wrong: The shin bone is the tibia.  Why D wrong: \"Funny bone\" is not the listed name in this source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the only movable bone in the human skull is the:","options":["Mandible bone","Maxilla","Sphenoid bone","Frontal bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the facial bone that is the only movable bone (in the skull) is the mandible bone.  Why B wrong: The maxilla is fixed, not movable.  Why C wrong: The sphenoid is fixed.  Why D wrong: The frontal bone is fixed."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the smallest vertebra is the:","options":["Cervical vertebra","Lumbar vertebra","Thoracic vertebra","Sacral vertebra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the smallest vertebra is the cervical vertebra.  Why B wrong: The lumbar vertebra is the largest, not smallest.  Why C wrong: The thoracic vertebra is not the stated smallest.  Why D wrong: The sacral vertebra is not the stated smallest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the largest vertebra is the:","options":["Lumbar vertebra","Cervical vertebra","Thoracic vertebra","Coccyx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the largest vertebra is the lumbar vertebra.  Why B wrong: The cervical vertebra is the smallest.  Why C wrong: The thoracic vertebra is not the stated largest.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is not the stated largest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, bone originates from which germ layer?","options":["Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Trophoblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the origin of bone is the mesoderm.  Why B wrong: Ectoderm gives rise to skin/nervous tissue, not bone.  Why C wrong: Endoderm gives rise to gut lining, not bone.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is not a bone-forming germ layer."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a newborn consists of how many bones?","options":["250-300 bones","206 bones","12 bones","33 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a newborn consists of 250-300 bones.  Why B wrong: 206 is the adult total.  Why C wrong: 12 bones is the number of pairs of ribs/value for a different item.  Why D wrong: 33 bones is not the stated newborn count."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the skull of an adult consists of how many bones?","options":["12 bones","206 bones","33 bones","24 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the skull of an adult consisting of 12 bones in the rapid revision answer column.  Why B wrong: 206 is the total body bone count.  Why C wrong: 33 is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 24 is the number of ribs."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the main part of the bone is called the:","options":["Diaphysis","Epiphysis","Metaphysis","Periosteum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the main part of the bone is called the diaphysis.  Why B wrong: The epiphysis is the end of the bone, not the main part.  Why C wrong: The metaphysis is the region between the diaphysis and epiphysis.  Why D wrong: The periosteum is the outer covering membrane."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the carpal bone that is a short bone but pisiform is the:","options":["Sesamoid bone","Diaphysis","Patella","Atlas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Carpal bone is short bone but pisiform\" with the answer sesamoid bone (the pisiform is a sesamoid-type bone).  Why B wrong: The diaphysis is the shaft of a long bone.  Why C wrong: The patella is the knee cap (also a sesamoid but not the answer paired here).  Why D wrong: The atlas is the first cervical vertebra."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the tarsal is a short bone but the calcaneum is described as which bone type?","options":["Irregular","Flat","Long","Sesamoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Tarsal is short bone but calcaneum is\" with the answer irregular.  Why B wrong: Flat is not the stated classification.  Why C wrong: Long is not the stated classification.  Why D wrong: Sesamoid is the answer paired with the pisiform, not the calcaneum."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the first cervical vertebra is called the:","options":["Atlas vertebra","Axis vertebra","Sacrum","Coccyx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the first cervical vertebra is called the atlas vertebra.  Why B wrong: The axis vertebra is the second cervical vertebra.  Why C wrong: The sacrum is in the lower spine.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is the tailbone."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the second cervical vertebra is called the:","options":["Axis vertebra","Atlas vertebra","Lumbar vertebra","Sphenoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the second cervical vertebra is called the axis vertebra.  Why B wrong: The atlas vertebra is the first cervical vertebra.  Why C wrong: The lumbar vertebra is in the lower back.  Why D wrong: The sphenoid is a skull bone."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the patella bone makes which joint \"so called\"?","options":["Knee bone (knee)","Elbow","Shoulder","Hip"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Patella bone makes knee so called\" with the answer knee bone.  Why B wrong: The elbow is not formed by the patella.  Why C wrong: The shoulder is not related to the patella.  Why D wrong: The hip is not related to the patella."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the first bone ossified in human is the:","options":["Clavicle bone","Femur","Mandible","Stapes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the first bone ossified in human is the clavicle bone.  Why B wrong: The femur is the largest bone, not the first ossified.  Why C wrong: The mandible is the only movable skull bone, not the first ossified.  Why D wrong: The stapes is the smallest bone, not the first ossified."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the highest point of the shoulder is called the:","options":["Acromion process","Coracoid process","Glenoid cavity","Olecranon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the highest point of the shoulder is called the acromion process.  Why B wrong: The coracoid process is not the stated highest point.  Why C wrong: The glenoid cavity is the shoulder socket, not the highest point.  Why D wrong: The olecranon is part of the elbow."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the strongest muscle in the human body is the:","options":["Masseter","Gluteus maximus","Sartorius","Quadriceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the strongest muscle in the human body is the masseter.  Why B wrong: The gluteus maximus is named as the largest muscle, not the strongest.  Why C wrong: The sartorius is the longest muscle.  Why D wrong: The quadriceps is not the stated strongest muscle."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the tibia bone is also known as the:","options":["Shin bone","Collar bone","Funny bone","Knee cap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the tibia bone is also known as the shin bone.  Why B wrong: The collar bone is the clavicle.  Why C wrong: \"Funny bone\" is not the listed name.  Why D wrong: The knee cap is the patella."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, Dunlop traction is commonly used for which bone?","options":["Humerus fracture","Femur fracture","Tibia fracture","Clavicle fracture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Dunlop traction is commonly used for humerus fracture.  Why B wrong: The source pairs Dunlop traction with the humerus, not the femur.  Why C wrong: The tibia is not the stated bone.  Why D wrong: The clavicle is not the stated bone."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the total number of facial bones is:","options":["14 bones","12 bones","7 bones","24 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of facial bones is 14 bones.  Why B wrong: 12 is the number of skull bones in this source.  Why C wrong: 7 is the number of cervical vertebrae.  Why D wrong: 24 is the number of ribs."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the total number of cervical vertebra is:","options":["7 vertebra","12 vertebra","5 vertebra","14 vertebra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of cervical vertebra is 7.  Why B wrong: 12 is the number of thoracic vertebrae (not stated here, but 12 is the skull/rib pair count in this source).  Why C wrong: 5 is not the stated cervical count.  Why D wrong: 14 is the facial bone count."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, osteoclasts are also known as:","options":["Bone destroying cells","Bone forming cells","Cartilage cells","Muscle cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoclasts are also known as bone destroying cells.  Why B wrong: Bone forming cells are osteoblasts.  Why C wrong: Cartilage cells are chondrocytes.  Why D wrong: Muscle cells are myocytes."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, osteoblasts are also known as:","options":["Bone forming cells","Bone destroying cells","Cartilage forming cells","Fat cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osteoblasts are also known as bone forming cells.  Why B wrong: Bone destroying cells are osteoclasts.  Why C wrong: Cartilage forming cells are chondroblasts.  Why D wrong: Fat cells are adipocytes."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the repair and reconstruction of the joint is called:","options":["Arthroplasty","Arthroscopy","Arthrocentesis","Laminectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the repair and reconstruction of the joint is called arthroplasty.  Why B wrong: Arthroscopy is endoscopic joint examination.  Why C wrong: Arthrocentesis is joint aspiration.  Why D wrong: Laminectomy is a spinal procedure."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, which long bone is placed horizontally in the human body?","options":["Clavicle","Femur","Tibia","Humerus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the long bone placed horizontally in the human body is the clavicle.  Why B wrong: The femur is oriented vertically.  Why C wrong: The tibia is oriented vertically.  Why D wrong: The humerus is oriented vertically."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the most complex joint of the human body is the:","options":["Knee joint","Shoulder joint","Hip joint","Elbow joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the complex joint of the human body is the knee joint.  Why B wrong: The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint, not the stated most complex.  Why C wrong: The hip is a ball-and-socket joint.  Why D wrong: The elbow is a hinge joint."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the movable bone in the skull is the:","options":["Mandible (Horseshoe shaped)","Maxilla","Sphenoid","Temporal bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the movable bone in the skull is the mandible (horseshoe shaped).  Why B wrong: The maxilla is fixed.  Why C wrong: The sphenoid is fixed.  Why D wrong: The temporal bone is fixed."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the instrument used to measure joint movements and angles is the:","options":["Goniometer","Sphygmomanometer","Spirometer","Stethoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the instrument used to measure joint movements and angles is the goniometer.  Why B wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.  Why C wrong: A spirometer measures lung function.  Why D wrong: A stethoscope is used for auscultation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the sella turcica is a part of which bone?","options":["Sphenoid bone","Mandible","Femur","Clavicle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sella turcica is a part of the sphenoid bone.  Why B wrong: The mandible is the jawbone.  Why C wrong: The femur is the thigh bone.  Why D wrong: The clavicle is the collar bone."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the pituitary gland is located in which structure?","options":["Sella turcica","Foramen magnum","Acromion process","Glenoid cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Where pituitary gland placed\" with the answer sella turcica.  Why B wrong: The foramen magnum is the opening for the spinal cord.  Why C wrong: The acromion process is part of the shoulder.  Why D wrong: The glenoid cavity is the shoulder socket."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, bone to bone is connected by:","options":["Ligaments","Tendons","Cartilage","Fascia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bone to bone is connected by ligaments.  Why B wrong: Tendons connect bone to muscle.  Why C wrong: Cartilage cushions joints but is not the stated bone-to-bone connector.  Why D wrong: Fascia is connective tissue but not the stated bone-to-bone connector."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, bone to bone is connected by ligaments, and bone is connected to muscle by the:","options":["Tendon","Ligament","Cartilage","Periosteum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Bone to bone is connected by\" with ligaments and \"Bone to ... is connected by\" (bone to muscle) with tendon.  Why B wrong: Ligaments connect bone to bone.  Why C wrong: Cartilage is not the muscle-to-bone connector.  Why D wrong: The periosteum is the bone covering, not a connector to muscle."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, osteoblasts are bone forming cells, and the largest bone of the human body is the:","options":["Femur","Tibia","Humerus","Mandible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source again lists the largest/longest bone of the human body as the femur in the rapid revision answers.  Why B wrong: The tibia is the shin bone, not the largest.  Why C wrong: The humerus is the arm bone, not the largest.  Why D wrong: The mandible is the jaw bone, not the largest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the muscles that are responsible for movement of the mandible are also known as the:","options":["Muscles of mastication","Muscles of facial expression","Extraocular muscles","Postural muscles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the muscles responsible for movement of the mandible are also known as the muscles of mastication.  Why B wrong: Muscles of facial expression move the face, not primarily the mandible.  Why C wrong: Extraocular muscles move the eyes.  Why D wrong: Postural muscles maintain posture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Paget's disease is a disease of\":","options":["Bones","Muscles","Joints","Nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Paget's disease is a disease of bones.  Why B wrong: It is not a muscle disease.  Why C wrong: It is not primarily a joint disease.  Why D wrong: It is not a nerve disease."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, Pott's disease is a disease of the:","options":["Spine (TB of spine)","Knee","Hip","Skull"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Pott's disease is a disease of\" with TB of spine.  Why B wrong: The knee is not the site of Pott's disease.  Why C wrong: The hip is not the site.  Why D wrong: The skull is not the site."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the sartorius muscle is also known as the:","options":["Tailor's muscle (longest muscle in the body)","Masseter","Gluteus maximus","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sartorius muscle is also known as Tailor's muscle (longest muscle in the body).  Why B wrong: The masseter is the strongest muscle.  Why C wrong: The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle in the buttocks.  Why D wrong: The diaphragm is the respiratory muscle, not the sartorius."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the largest muscle in the body (in the buttocks) is the:","options":["Gluteus maximus","Sartorius","Masseter","Rectus femoris"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the largest muscle in the body is the gluteus maximus (in the buttocks).  Why B wrong: The sartorius is the longest muscle (Tailor's muscle).  Why C wrong: The masseter is the strongest muscle.  Why D wrong: The rectus femoris is not the stated largest muscle."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the strongest muscles of the human body are the:","options":["Masseter muscles","Gluteus maximus","Sartorius muscles","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the strongest muscles of the human body are the masseter muscles.  Why B wrong: The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle, not the strongest.  Why C wrong: The sartorius is the longest muscle.  Why D wrong: The diaphragm is not the stated strongest."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the human skeleton is made up of 206 bones, comprising:","options":["Axial 80 and appendicular 126","Axial 126 and appendicular 80","Axial 100 and appendicular 106","Axial 60 and appendicular 146"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the human skeleton is made of 206 bones (Axial 80 and Appendicular 126).  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: 100/106 is not the stated split.  Why D wrong: 60/146 is not the stated split."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the skull consists of how many bones, comprising 8 cranial bones and 14 facial bones?","options":["22 bones","14 bones","12 bones","24 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the skull consists of 22 bones (8 cranial bones + 14 facial bones).  Why B wrong: 14 is the number of facial bones alone.  Why C wrong: 12 was given earlier for the adult skull but the cranial+facial total is 22.  Why D wrong: 24 is the number of ribs."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Flexion\" is defined as:","options":["Bending the angle between the bones forming a joint","Movement toward a straight position (opposite of flexion)","Movement of a limb away from the midline of the body","Movement of a limb toward the midline of the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines flexion as bending the angle between the bones forming a joint.  Why B wrong: Movement toward a straight position (opposite of flexion) is extension.  Why C wrong: Movement away from the midline is abduction.  Why D wrong: Movement toward the midline is adduction."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Extension\" is defined as:","options":["A movement toward a straight position (opposite of flexion)","Bending the angle between the bones forming a joint","Movement of a limb away from the midline of the body","Movement of a limb toward the midline of the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines extension as a movement toward a straight position (opposite of flexion).  Why B wrong: Bending the angle between bones is flexion.  Why C wrong: Moving away from the midline is abduction.  Why D wrong: Moving toward the midline is adduction."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Abduction\" (outward movement) is:","options":["Movement of a limb away from the midline of the body","Movement of a limb toward the midline","Bending a joint","Straightening a joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines abduction (outward movement) as movement of a limb away from the midline of the body.  Why B wrong: Movement toward the midline is adduction.  Why C wrong: Bending a joint is flexion.  Why D wrong: Straightening a joint is extension."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Adduction\" (inward movement) is:","options":["Movement of a limb toward the midline of the body (e.g., lateral bending of the head/trunk, outward rotation of the eyes)","Movement of a limb away from the midline","Bending the angle of a joint","Movement toward a straight position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines adduction (inward movement) as movement of a limb toward the midline of the body, e.g., lateral bending of the head/trunk and outward rotation of the eyes.  Why B wrong: Movement away from midline is abduction.  Why C wrong: Bending the angle of a joint is flexion.  Why D wrong: Movement toward a straight position is extension."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, EMG (Electromyography) measures the:","options":["Electrical activity of the muscles","Bone mineral density","Joint range of motion angles","Synovial fluid pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states EMG (electromyography) measures the electrical activity of the muscles.  Why B wrong: Bone mineral density is measured by DEXA/BMD.  Why C wrong: Joint angles are measured by a goniometer.  Why D wrong: Synovial fluid pressure is not what EMG measures."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, \"Orbicularis Oculi\" muscle is responsible for which action?","options":["Close the eyes","Open the eyes","Smiling","Chewing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the orbicularis oculi is responsible for close the eyes.  Why B wrong: Opening the eyes is the function of the levator palpebrae.  Why C wrong: Smiling involves other facial muscles.  Why D wrong: Chewing involves the muscles of mastication."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the \"Levator Palpebrae\" muscle is responsible for:","options":["Open the eyes","Close the eyes","Moving the mandible","Outward rotation of the arm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the levator palpebrae is responsible for open the eyes.  Why B wrong: Closing the eyes is the function of the orbicularis oculi.  Why C wrong: Moving the mandible is done by muscles of mastication.  Why D wrong: Arm rotation is not the function of the levator palpebrae."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, an injury in which muscle causes urinary stress incontinence?","options":["Levator ani muscle","Orbicularis oculi","Masseter","Sartorius"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states injury in the levator ani muscle causes urinary stress incontinence.  Why B wrong: The orbicularis oculi closes the eyes.  Why C wrong: The masseter is a chewing muscle.  Why D wrong: The sartorius is the longest muscle (Tailor's muscle)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the metacarpal bone makes the palm of the hand. The length of the vertebral column in a male is about:","options":["70 cm","60 cm","50 cm","80 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the length of the vertebral column in a male is about 70 cm.  Why B wrong: 60 cm is the stated length in a female, not a male.  Why C wrong: 50 cm is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 80 cm is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the length of the vertebral column in a female is about:","options":["60 cm","70 cm","50 cm","75 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the length of the vertebral column in a female is about 60 cm.  Why B wrong: 70 cm is the stated length in a male.  Why C wrong: 50 cm is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 75 cm is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, ossification of the head is initiated by which fontanel?","options":["Occipito (atlanto-axial joint), with no movement at the atlanto-axial joint","The frontal fontanel","The mastoid fontanel","The sphenoid fontanel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source pairs \"Ossification of the head initiated by\" with occipito (atlanto-axial joint) and notes \"No movement at atlanto-axial joint.\"  Why B wrong: The frontal fontanel is not the stated initiator.  Why C wrong: The mastoid fontanel is not the stated initiator.  Why D wrong: The sphenoid fontanel is not the stated initiator."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, the most mobile joint in the human body is the:","options":["Ball-and-socket joint (shoulder joint)","Hinge joint","Pivot joint","Gliding joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most mobile joint in the human body is the ball-and-socket joint (shoulder joint).  Why B wrong: A hinge joint allows movement in one plane only.  Why C wrong: A pivot joint allows rotation only.  Why D wrong: A gliding joint has limited movement."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Pott's fracture is a fracture of:","options":["Both medial and lateral condyle of tibia (ankle fracture)","Only the lateral condyle of tibia","The 5th metacarpal bone","The proximal ulnar bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Pott's fracture as fracture of both medial and lateral condyle of tibia, ankle fracture.  Why B wrong: Fracture of the lateral condyle of tibia alone is a bumper fracture.  Why C wrong: 5th metacarpal fracture is a boxer fracture.  Why D wrong: Proximal ulnar fracture is a Monteggia fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Bumper fracture is a fracture of:","options":["Lateral condyle of tibia","Both condyles of tibia","The 5th metacarpal bone","The distal radius"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines bumper fracture as fracture of lateral condyle of tibia.  Why B wrong: Fracture of both condyles of tibia is a Pott's fracture.  Why C wrong: 5th metacarpal fracture is a boxer fracture.  Why D wrong: Distal radius fracture is Colles or Smith fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Boxer fracture is a fracture of the:","options":["5th metacarpal bone or little finger","Lateral condyle of tibia","Distal radius","Atlas (C1)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines boxer fracture as fracture of the 5th metacarpal bone or little finger.  Why B wrong: Lateral condyle of tibia is a bumper fracture.  Why C wrong: Distal radius is Colles/Smith fracture.  Why D wrong: Atlas (C1) fracture is a Jefferson fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Binet fracture is a fracture of the:","options":["First metacarpal or thumb fracture","5th metacarpal","Proximal ulnar bone","Distal radius bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Binet fracture as first metacarpal or thumb fracture.  Why B wrong: 5th metacarpal fracture is a boxer fracture.  Why C wrong: Proximal ulnar fracture is a Monteggia fracture.  Why D wrong: Distal radius fracture is a Galeazzi/Colles/Smith fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Galeazzi fracture is a fracture of the:","options":["Distal radius bone","First metacarpal","Proximal ulnar bone","Lateral condyle of tibia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Galeazzi fracture as fracture of distal radius bone.  Why B wrong: First metacarpal fracture is a Binet fracture.  Why C wrong: Proximal ulnar fracture is a Monteggia fracture.  Why D wrong: Lateral condyle of tibia is a bumper fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Monteggia fracture is a fracture of the:","options":["Proximal ulnar bone","Distal radius bone","First metacarpal","Atlas (C1)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Monteggia fracture as fracture of proximal ulnar bone.  Why B wrong: Distal radius fracture is a Galeazzi/Colles/Smith fracture.  Why C wrong: First metacarpal fracture is a Binet fracture.  Why D wrong: Atlas (C1) fracture is a Jefferson fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Colles fracture is:","options":["A distal fracture of the radius with dorsal (posterior) displacement of the wrist and hand","A distal fracture of the radius with volar (ventral) displacement of the wrist and hand","A fracture of the proximal ulna","A fracture of the atlas (C1)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Colles fracture as a distal fracture of the radius with dorsal (posterior) displacement of the wrist and hand.  Why B wrong: Distal radius fracture with volar (ventral) displacement is a Smith fracture.  Why C wrong: Proximal ulna fracture is a Monteggia fracture.  Why D wrong: Atlas (C1) fracture is a Jefferson fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Smith fracture is:","options":["A distal fracture of the radius with volar (ventral) displacement of the wrist and hand","A distal fracture of the radius with dorsal (posterior) displacement of the wrist and hand","A fracture of the 5th metacarpal","A fracture of the axis (C2)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Smith fracture as a distal fracture of the radius with volar (ventral) displacement of the wrist and hand.  Why B wrong: Distal radius fracture with dorsal (posterior) displacement is a Colles fracture.  Why C wrong: 5th metacarpal fracture is a boxer fracture.  Why D wrong: Axis (C2) fracture is a Hangman's fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Basilar skull fracture is:","options":["A break of a bone in the base of the skull","A fracture of the distal (lower) part of the tibia","A fracture of the atlas (C1)","A fracture of the distal radius"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines basilar skull fracture as a break of a bone in the base of the skull.  Why B wrong: A fracture of the distal (lower) tibia is a Toddler's fracture.  Why C wrong: Atlas (C1) fracture is a Jefferson fracture.  Why D wrong: Distal radius fracture is a Colles/Smith fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Toddler's fracture is a fracture of the:","options":["Distal (lower) part of the tibia","Base of the skull","Atlas (C1)","Axis (C2)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Toddler's fracture as fracture of the distal (lower) part of the tibia.  Why B wrong: A break in the base of the skull is a basilar skull fracture.  Why C wrong: Atlas (C1) fracture is a Jefferson fracture.  Why D wrong: Axis (C2) fracture is a Hangman's fracture."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Jefferson fracture is a fracture of which bone?","options":["Atlas bone (C1)","Axis bone (C2)","Distal radius","Tibia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Jefferson fracture as fracture of atlas bone (C1).  Why B wrong: Fracture of axis bone (C2) is a Hangman's fracture.  Why C wrong: Distal radius fracture is Colles/Smith fracture.  Why D wrong: Tibia fracture is a Toddler's/Pott's/Bumper fracture depending on site."},{"section":"Batch 032 · amputation, spinal column deformities and disk herniation…","question":"According to the Rapid Revision Key points, a Hangman's fracture is a fracture of which bone?","options":["Axis bone (C2)","Atlas bone (C1)","Distal radius","Base of the skull"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Hangman's fracture as fracture of axis bone (C2).  Why B wrong: Fracture of atlas bone (C1) is a Jefferson fracture.  Why C wrong: Distal radius fracture is Colles/Smith fracture.  Why D wrong: A break in the base of the skull is a basilar skull fracture."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The human digestive system consists of which two main components?","options":["Mouth and anus only","The gastrointestinal tract and the accessory organs of digestion","The stomach and small intestine only","The liver and pancreas only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The digestive system consists of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the accessory organs of digestion.  Why A wrong: Mouth and anus are only the two ends of the GI tract, not the whole system.  Why C wrong: These are only parts of the GI tract.  Why D wrong: Liver and pancreas are only some of the accessory organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following are the GI tract organs (alimentary canal)?","options":["Liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","Oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, and anus","Salivary glands and teeth only","Tongue and palate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GI tract organs are the oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, and anus.  Why A wrong: Liver, gallbladder, and pancreas are accessory organs, not GI tract organs.  Why C/D wrong: These are individual structures, not the full tract."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following is classified as an accessory organ of digestion?","options":["Stomach","Esophagus","Salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","Large intestine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Accessory organs are the tongue, teeth, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas.  Why A/B/D wrong: Stomach, esophagus, and large intestine are GI tract (alimentary canal) organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Digestion is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The movement of food through the GI tract","The breakdown of food into smaller components so they can be absorbed and assimilated into the body","The storage of food in the stomach","The elimination of undigested waste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Digestion involves the breakdown of food into smaller components so they can be absorbed and assimilated into the body.  Why A wrong: That describes peristalsis/motility.  Why C wrong: Storage is a stomach function, not the definition of digestion.  Why D wrong: That describes defecation/elimination."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The wave-like movement that moves food from the esophagus to the rectum, allowing it to push nutrient particles toward the anus, is called:","options":["Segmentation","Peristalsis","Mastication","Deglutition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peristalsis is the wave-like movement that moves food from the esophagus to the rectum (anus).  Why A wrong: Segmentation mixes food but does not propel it forward.  Why C wrong: Mastication is chewing.  Why D wrong: Deglutition is swallowing."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Segmentation in the small intestine primarily serves to:","options":["Propel food rapidly toward the anus","Mix food contents and allow nutrients to be absorbed more efficiently in the back-and-forth movement","Initiate swallowing","Trigger vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Segmentation is the back-and-forth churning that mixes contents and allows nutrients to be absorbed more efficiently.  Why A wrong: Forward propulsion is peristalsis, not segmentation.  Why C/D wrong: Not related to segmentation function."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The Enteric Nervous System (ENS) — the brain of the gut — is associated with which fibers?","options":["Only motor fibers","Only sensory fibers","The motor part and the sensory part, including extrinsic sympathetic, parasympathetic, and visceral afferent neurons","Only autonomic ganglia of the heart"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ENS comprises the motor and sensory parts and is aware of extrinsic sympathetic, parasympathetic, and visceral afferent neurons.  Why A/B wrong: It includes both motor and sensory components.  Why D wrong: It governs the gut, not the heart."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which type of digestion involves muscular movement of the digestive tract (mouth and stomach) that physically breaks down food into smaller particles?","options":["Chemical digestion","Mechanical digestion","Enzymatic digestion","Hormonal digestion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical digestion is the muscular movement of the digestive tract that physically breaks food into smaller particles.  Why A/C wrong: Chemical/enzymatic digestion uses hydrolysis reactions aided by enzymes.  Why D wrong: Hormonal regulation is not a category of digestion described here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Chemical digestion uses which type of reaction to break food into nutrient molecules?","options":["Oxidation reactions","Hydrolysis reactions aided by enzymes","Condensation reactions","Decarboxylation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemical digestion uses hydrolysis reactions aided by enzymes (mainly in stomach and small intestine) to break food into nutrient molecules small enough to be absorbed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the described mechanism of chemical digestion."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"During absorption, the products (nutrients) of chemical digestion pass from the digestive tract into which structures for distribution to tissue cells?","options":["Into bone marrow","Into blood or lymph","Into the kidney tubules","Into the spinal cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absorption is the passage of nutrients from the digestive tract into blood or lymph for distribution to tissue cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the routes of nutrient absorption."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Undigested material released during elimination is removed from the body by which process?","options":["Filtration","Defecation","Diffusion","Endocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elimination releases undigested material from the body through the rectum and anus by defecation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the process of fecal elimination."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Peristalsis in the GI tract is best described as which contraction pattern?","options":["Alternating waves of contraction","Continuous tonic contraction only","Random uncoordinated spasms","Single sustained contraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peristalsis consists of alternating waves of contraction, beginning in the esophagus and moving food along the tract to the stomach.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the wave-like, propulsive peristaltic pattern."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Segmentation is the rhythmic contraction of which muscle type, aiding in mechanical digestion?","options":["Longitudinal smooth muscle","Circular smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Segmentation is the rhythmic contraction of circular smooth muscle that aids in mechanical digestion.  Why A wrong: Longitudinal muscle is more involved in propulsion/shortening.  Why C/D wrong: GI tract motility involves smooth (not skeletal/cardiac) muscle."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"During the act of segmentation, the muscle contraction in the small intestine occurs in which way?","options":["Only in the forward direction","In which circular muscles contract throughout the motion in both the backward and forward directions","Only as a single peristaltic wave","Only at the ileocecal valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In segmentation, circular muscles contract throughout the motion in both the backward and forward directions, mixing the contents.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the bidirectional mixing action of segmentation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Regarding regulation of GI tract activities by the autonomic nervous system, which is correct?","options":["Sympathetic nerves stimulate GI activities while parasympathetic nerves inhibit them","Parasympathetic nerves stimulate GI tract activities while sympathetic nerves inhibit GI tract activities","Both sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves inhibit GI activity","The ANS has no effect on GI activity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parasympathetic nerves stimulate GI tract activities, whereas sympathetic nerves inhibit GI tract activities.  Why A wrong: The roles are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The ANS has opposing (stimulate vs inhibit) effects, not uniform inhibition or no effect."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Hormonal control of GI tract activities is exerted by hormones from which sources?","options":["Only the thyroid gland","From the endocrine gland and from the GI tract itself","Only the adrenal medulla","Only the pituitary gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hormonal control comes from hormones produced by the endocrine gland and from the GI tract itself, which regulate GI tract activities.  Why A/C/D wrong: These single sources are not how the source describes hormonal GI regulation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"What is the approximate total length of the whole GI tract?","options":["About 2–3 meters","About 5–7 meters (or 16–23 feet)","About 10 meters","About 12–15 meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The whole GI tract is about 5–7 meters (16–23 feet) long.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated length."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The approximate length of the small intestine is:","options":["About 1.5 meters","About 6 meters (or 20 feet)","About 25 cm","About 9 meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The small intestine is about 6 meters (20 feet) long.  Why A wrong: 1.5 meters / 5 feet is the length of the large intestine.  Why C wrong: 25 cm is the length of the duodenum.  Why D wrong: Not the stated length."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The large intestine is approximately how long?","options":["About 6 meters","About 1.5 meters (or 5 feet)","About 25 cm","About 7 meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The large intestine is about 1.5 meters (5 feet) long.  Why A wrong: 6 meters is the small intestine.  Why C wrong: 25 cm is the duodenum.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"In the body, the trick mnemonic \"DUDES\" (D-Duodenum, U-Ureter, D-Descending Colon, E-Esophagus, S-Stomach and Sigmoid Colon) is used to remember organs that are about how long?","options":["About 1 cm","About 25 cm","About 1 meter","About 5 meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic \"DUDES\" lists organs that are each about 25 cm in length.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 25-cm value the mnemonic represents."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which layer is the innermost layer of the GI tract wall and faces the lumen?","options":["Submucosa","Mucosal layer","Muscularis layer","Serosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mucosal layer is the inner-most layer, formed by epithelial tissue.  Why A wrong: Submucosa lies beneath the mucosa.  Why C wrong: Muscularis lies outside the submucosa.  Why D wrong: Serosa is the outermost layer."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Goblet cells, which secrete mucus, are located in which layer of the GI tract?","options":["Submucosa","Mucosal layer","Muscularis layer","Serosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mucosal layer contains goblet cells which secrete mucus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Goblet cells are in the mucosa, not these layers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The submucosal layer of the GI tract is formed by which tissue and contains which nerve plexus?","options":["Epithelial tissue; myenteric plexus","Connective tissue with nerve endings; Meissner's plexus","Smooth muscle; Auerbach's plexus","Serous tissue; vagal plexus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The submucosal layer is formed by connective tissue and contains nerve endings such as Meissner's plexus.  Why A wrong: Epithelial tissue forms the mucosa; myenteric plexus is in the muscularis.  Why C wrong: Auerbach's plexus is in the muscle layer, not submucosa.  Why D wrong: Not the described submucosal composition."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The muscularis layer of the GI tract is formed by which arrangement of smooth muscle?","options":["Only circular smooth muscle","Only longitudinal smooth muscle","Circular and longitudinal smooth muscles","Skeletal muscle in two layers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The muscularis layers are formed by circular and longitudinal smooth muscles.  Why A/B wrong: Both arrangements are present, not one alone.  Why D wrong: The muscularis is smooth (not skeletal) muscle."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which layer is the outermost layer of the GI tract?","options":["Mucosa","Submucosa","Muscularis","Serous layer (serosa)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The serous layer (serosa) is the outermost layer of the GI tract.  Why A/B/C wrong: These lie internal to the serosa."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The peritoneum is the serous membrane forming the lining of which cavity?","options":["Thoracic cavity","Abdominal cavity","Cranial cavity","Pelvic cavity only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The peritoneum is the serous membrane forming the lining of the abdominal cavity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the cavity lined by the peritoneum as described."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The peritoneum is embryologically derived from which germ layer?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The peritoneum is embryologically derived from the mesoderm.  Why A/C/D wrong: The peritoneum arises from mesoderm, not these layers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The peritoneum is comprised of how many layers?","options":["One layer","Two layers — parietal layer and visceral layer","Three layers","Four layers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The peritoneum is comprised of 2 layers — a parietal layer and a visceral layer.  Why A/C/D wrong: It has two layers, not these numbers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Inflammation of the peritoneum is called:","options":["Pleuritis","Peritonitis","Pericarditis","Appendicitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the peritoneum is called peritonitis.  Why A wrong: Pleuritis is inflammation of the pleura.  Why C wrong: Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium.  Why D wrong: Appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity or peritoneum is called:","options":["Edema","Ascites","Effusion","Anasarca"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity/peritoneum is called ascites.  Why A wrong: Edema is general tissue fluid accumulation.  Why C wrong: Effusion is fluid in a body cavity (general term).  Why D wrong: Anasarca is generalized body edema."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"An \"intra-peritoneal organ\" means which of the following?","options":["The organ lies behind the peritoneum","The organ is completely covered by the peritoneum","The organ has no peritoneal covering","The organ is partially behind and partially covered by peritoneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An intra-peritoneal organ means the organ is completely covered by the peritoneum.  Why A wrong: That describes retro-peritoneal organs.  Why C wrong: All listed organs have some peritoneal relationship.  Why D wrong: That describes intra & supra peritoneal organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"A \"retro-peritoneal organ\" is best described as one that:","options":["Lies completely covered by the peritoneum","Lies behind the peritoneum and partially covered by peritoneum","Floats freely in the abdominal cavity","Is found only in the thorax"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A retro-peritoneal organ lies behind the peritoneum and is partially covered by the peritoneum.  Why A wrong: That describes intra-peritoneal organs.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe retroperitoneal organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following are intra-peritoneal organs? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stomach and part 1 of duodenum","Jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, transverse colon, and sigmoid colon","Liver, gallbladder, spleen, tail of pancreas, and ovaries","Kidneys and ureters","Liver, gallbladder, and ovaries"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Intra-peritoneal organs include the stomach, part 1 of the duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, transverse colon, sigmoid colon, rectum (upper 1/3), liver, gallbladder, spleen, tail of pancreas, and ovaries — so A, B, C, and E all list intra-peritoneal organs.  Why D wrong: Kidneys and ureters are retro-peritoneal organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following are retro-peritoneal organs? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Parts 2, 3, and 4 of duodenum","Ascending and descending colon","Kidneys, ureters, and pancreas (head, neck, body)","Stomach and spleen","Rectum (1/3 medius), supra-renal glands, and abdominal aorta/inferior vena cava"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Retro-peritoneal organs include parts 2, 3, 4 of the duodenum; ascending & descending colon; rectum (1/3 medius); head, neck, body of pancreas; kidneys; ureters; supra-renal glands; and abdominal aorta/inferior vena cava.  Why D wrong: Stomach and spleen are intra-peritoneal organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following are listed as intra & supra-peritoneal organs? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Recto (1/3 lower)","Bladder","Distal ureters, gonadal blood vessels, uterus, and fallopian tubes","Liver","Spleen"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): Intra & supra-peritoneal organs include rectum (1/3 lower), bladder, distal ureters, gonadal blood vessels, uterus, and fallopian tubes.  Why D/E wrong: Liver and spleen are intra-peritoneal organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The mouth (oral/buccal cavity) is formed by which structures?","options":["Cheeks, lips, palate, teeth, and salivary glands","Tongue and esophagus only","Pharynx and larynx","Stomach and duodenum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mouth is formed by cheeks, lips, palate, teeth, and salivary glands.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the structures forming the oral cavity."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The roof of the oral cavity is bounded by muscle and bones and is referred to as the:","options":["Tongue","Oral cavity (roof)","Pharynx","Uvula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The roof of the oral cavity is bounded by muscle and bones.  Why A wrong: The tongue forms part of the floor, not the roof.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the roof of the oral cavity."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which structure of the mouth is described as too much sensitive of human?","options":["Cheeks","Lips","Palate","Teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lips consist of orbicularis oris muscle and are described as the too much sensitive organ of human.  Why A wrong: The cheeks consist of buccinator muscle.  Why C/D wrong: Not described as the most sensitive structure."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The cheeks of the mouth consist of which muscle?","options":["Orbicularis oris muscle","Buccinator muscle","Masseter muscle","Temporalis muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cheeks consist of buccinator muscle.  Why A wrong: Orbicularis oris is in the lips.  Why C/D wrong: Not the muscle stated for the cheeks."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The uvula is best described as which structure of the mouth?","options":["The carved field of muscle covered with mucous membrane hanging down from the middle (of the soft palate)","The sensitive front part of the tongue","The bone forming the roof of the mouth","A salivary gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The uvula is the carved field of muscle covered with mucous membrane hanging down from the middle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the uvula."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Inflammation of the mouth is known as:","options":["Glossitis","Stomatitis","Gingivitis","Cheilitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the mouth is known as stomatitis.  Why A wrong: Glossitis is inflammation of the tongue.  Why C wrong: Gingivitis is inflammation of the gum.  Why D wrong: Cheilitis is inflammation of the lips (not the stated term)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Food enters the GI tract through which process at the mouth?","options":["Digestion","Ingestion","Absorption","Defecation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Food enters the GI tract by ingestion.  Why A wrong: Digestion is the breakdown of food.  Why C wrong: Absorption is uptake of nutrients.  Why D wrong: Defecation is elimination of waste."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"In the oral cavity, chemical digestion of starch begins with the action of which enzyme?","options":["Pepsin","Ptyalin / salivary amylase","Trypsin","Lipase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemical digestive process begins by ptyalin enzyme or salivary amylase, which converts cooked starch or amylase enzyme in saliva and breaks down starch into disaccharides.  Why A wrong: Pepsin acts on proteins in the stomach.  Why C wrong: Trypsin acts on proteins in the small intestine.  Why D wrong: Lipase acts on fats."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The tongue is composed of which muscle type, and it manipulates food during mastication?","options":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Connective tissue only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tongue is made by skeletal muscle and manipulates the food during mastication.  Why A/C wrong: It is skeletal, not smooth or cardiac muscle.  Why D wrong: It is muscular, not merely connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Inflammation of the tongue is called:","options":["Stomatitis","Glossitis","Gingivitis","Gastritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the tongue is called glossitis.  Why A wrong: Stomatitis is mouth inflammation.  Why C wrong: Gingivitis is gum inflammation.  Why D wrong: Gastritis is stomach inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The taste buds (intrinsic sensory test of tongue) detect taste sensations after food particles are mixed with which fluid?","options":["Bile","Saliva, forming a moist lump (bolus) for easier passage into the pharynx","Gastric juice","Pancreatic juice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Food particles are mixed with saliva during mastication, resulting in a moist lump (bolus) for easier passage into the pharynx; taste buds detect taste sensations.  Why A/C/D wrong: These fluids are not mixed in the mouth for bolus formation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Interpretation of soft & sweet taste occurs at which papillae of the tongue?","options":["Foliate papillae","Fungiform papillae (tip/front of tongue)","Filiform papillae","Circumvallate papillae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The front part (tip) of the tongue interprets soft & sweet taste by fungiform papillae.  Why A wrong: Foliate papillae are on the side/lateral area for sour taste.  Why C wrong: Filiform papillae interpret bitter taste at the back/posterior part.  Why D wrong: Not the papillae described for sweet taste here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The back/posterior part of the tongue interprets bitter taste via which papillae?","options":["Fungiform papillae","Filiform papillae","Foliate papillae","Circumvallate papillae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The back/posterior part of the tongue interprets bitter taste by filiform papillae.  Why A wrong: Fungiform papillae are at the tip for sweet taste.  Why C wrong: Foliate papillae are on the side for sour taste.  Why D wrong: Not the papillae stated for bitter taste here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The side or lateral part of the tongue interprets sour taste via which papillae?","options":["Fungiform papillae","Filiform papillae","Foliate papillae","Circumvallate papillae"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The side/lateral area of the tongue interprets sour taste by foliate papillae.  Why A wrong: Fungiform papillae interpret sweet at the tip.  Why B wrong: Filiform papillae interpret bitter at the back.  Why D wrong: Not the papillae stated for sour taste here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The salivary glands produce saliva, which contains the enzyme that begins starch digestion. That enzyme is:","options":["Pepsin","Salivary amylase","Lactase","Maltase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Salivary glands secrete salivary amylase, an enzyme that breaks the starch into a maltose.  Why A wrong: Pepsin is a gastric protein enzyme.  Why C/D wrong: Lactase and maltase are intestinal disaccharidase enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"How many main pairs of salivary glands are there in the human?","options":["One pair","Two pairs","Three pairs (parotid, submandibular, sublingual)","Four pairs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are three main pairs of salivary glands: parotid gland (2 — largest salivary gland), submandibular gland (2), and sublingual gland (2 — smallest salivary glands).  Why A/B/D wrong: There are three pairs, not these numbers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which is the largest salivary gland?","options":["Submandibular gland","Sublingual gland","Parotid gland","Lingual gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The parotid gland is the largest salivary gland.  Why A wrong: Submandibular gland's main excretory duct is the Wharton duct.  Why B wrong: Sublingual glands are the smallest salivary glands.  Why D wrong: Not a main salivary gland pair listed."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which are the smallest salivary glands?","options":["Parotid glands","Submandibular glands","Sublingual glands","Lingual glands"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sublingual glands are the smallest salivary glands.  Why A wrong: Parotid is the largest.  Why B wrong: Submandibular gland's duct is the Wharton duct.  Why D wrong: Not a main salivary gland pair."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The main excretory duct of the submandibular gland is the:","options":["Stensen duct","Wharton duct","Bartholin duct","Rivinus duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The submandibular gland's main excretory duct is the Wharton duct.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the duct named for the submandibular gland in the source."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The parotid gland becoming inflamed in a viral infection in children is associated with which condition?","options":["Measles","Mumps viral infection","Chickenpox","Rubella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The parotid gland is inflamed in mumps viral infection, which occurs in children.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the infection that inflames the parotid gland as stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Saliva is composed of mainly which constituents?","options":["Two-thirds fluids, mucus, and serous secretions","Water, sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, bicarbonate, and phosphate","Bile salts and bilirubin","Only hydrochloric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Saliva is composed of a variety of electrolytes including sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, bicarbonate, and phosphate; it also contains immunoglobulins, proteins, enzymes, mucins, and nitrogenous products (urea and ammonia).  Why A wrong: That partially describes the gland composition, not saliva's constituents.  Why C/D wrong: These are not saliva components."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The pH of saliva is closest to which value?","options":["pH 1–2","pH 6 (slightly acidic) [range 6.2–7.6]","pH 8–9","pH 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pH of saliva is 6 and slightly acidic, with a stated range of 6.2 to 7.6.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pH values do not match the stated salivary pH."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The approximate daily volume of saliva produced is:","options":["50–100 ml/day","500–600 ml/day","2000 ml/day","100 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The volume of saliva is about 500–600 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated daily saliva volume."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Teeth are adapted for which function in the oral cavity?","options":["Absorption of nutrients","Mechanical digestion (mastication)","Secretion of enzymes","Production of bile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Teeth are adapted for mechanical digestion (mastication) in the oral cavity.  Why A/C/D wrong: Teeth do not absorb nutrients, secrete enzymes, or produce bile."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"How many permanent (secondary) teeth develop before the age of six?","options":["12 deciduous teeth","20 deciduous (primary) teeth develop before the age of six; 32 permanent teeth develop afterward","28 permanent teeth","32 deciduous teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 20 deciduous (primary) teeth are developed before the age of six, and 32 permanent or secondary teeth are developed afterward.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated counts; there are 20 deciduous and 32 permanent teeth."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which type of teeth are used for cutting and biting the food and have a visible cutting/biting tooth surface?","options":["Canines","Premolars","Incisors","Molars"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Incisors are used for cutting and biting the food.  Why A wrong: Canines have one pointed cusp on their biting surface, used for tearing.  Why B wrong: Premolars have two pointed cusps, used for crushing and tearing.  Why D wrong: Molars have four pointed cusps, used for grinding food."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Canine teeth are characterized by which feature and function?","options":["One pointed cusp on biting surface, used for tearing the food","Two pointed cusps, used for crushing","Four pointed cusps, used for grinding","Flat surface, used for cutting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Canines have one pointed cusp on their biting surface and are used for tearing the food.  Why B wrong: That describes premolars.  Why C wrong: That describes molars.  Why D wrong: That describes incisors."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Premolar teeth have which characteristic and function?","options":["One pointed cusp, used for tearing","Two pointed cusps, used for crushing and tearing the food","Four pointed cusps, used for grinding","Flat blade, used for cutting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Premolars have two pointed cusps and are used for crushing and tearing the food.  Why A wrong: That describes canines.  Why C wrong: That describes molars.  Why D wrong: That describes incisors."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Molar teeth have how many pointed cusps and are used for which function?","options":["One pointed cusp, used for tearing","Two pointed cusps, used for crushing","Four pointed cusps, used for grinding the food","No cusps, used for cutting"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Molars have four pointed cusps and are used for grinding the food.  Why A wrong: That describes canines.  Why B wrong: That describes premolars.  Why D wrong: That describes incisors."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The human dental formula follows which pattern (per quadrant)?","options":["2-1-2-3","1-2-1-3","2-2-2-2","3-1-2-2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Teeth follow the human dental formula of 2-1-2-3 (incisors-canines-premolars-molars per quadrant).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dental formula."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which are the three regions of a tooth?","options":["Enamel, dentin, pulp","Crown (visible section), root (embedded portion in sockets), and neck","Mucosa, submucosa, serosa","Cementum, gum, socket"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tooth has three regions: crown (visible section), root (embedded portion in sockets), and neck.  Why A wrong: Enamel, dentin, and pulp are tooth tissues, not the three anatomical regions described.  Why C wrong: These are GI wall layers.  Why D wrong: Not the three regions named."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Teeth are composed mostly of dentin, which is covered by a layer called:","options":["Cementum","Enamel (90% calcium)","Pulp","Periodontal ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Teeth are composed of dentin covered by a layer called enamel (90% calcium).  Why A wrong: Cementum covers the root, not described here as the dentin's covering layer.  Why C wrong: Pulp is the inner soft tissue.  Why D wrong: The periodontal ligament anchors the tooth, it is not a tooth covering layer."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which is the hardest part of the body?","options":["Femur","Tooth enamel","Dentin","Skull"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tooth enamel is the hardest part of the body.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not described as the hardest part of the body."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Inflammation of the gum (gingiva) is called:","options":["Stomatitis","Glossitis","Gingivitis","Periodontitis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the gum or gingiva is called gingivitis.  Why A wrong: Stomatitis is mouth inflammation.  Why B wrong: Glossitis is tongue inflammation.  Why D wrong: Periodontitis is not the term given in the source for gum inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The esophagus is a muscular or collapsible tube of approximately what dimensions?","options":["10 cm long and 1 cm diameter","25 cm long and about 2 cm in diameter","50 cm long and 5 cm diameter","6 meters long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The esophagus is a muscular or collapsible tube of 25 cm long and about 2 cm in diameter.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated esophageal dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The esophagus connects the pharynx with the stomach and is located in which part of the thoracic cavity?","options":["Anterior mediastinum","The mediastinum of the thoracic cavity","The pleural space","The retroperitoneal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The esophagus connects the pharynx with the stomach and is located in the mediastinum of the thoracic cavity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the location described for the esophagus."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The esophagus is anatomically divided into how many parts?","options":["Two parts","Three parts: cervical, thoracic, and abdominal esophagus","Four parts","Five parts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The esophagus is anatomically divided into three parts: cervical, thoracic, and abdominal esophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is divided into three parts, not these numbers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at an opening called the:","options":["Pyloric sphincter","Diaphragmatic hiatus","Ileocecal valve","Cardiac orifice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at an opening called the diaphragmatic hiatus.  Why A wrong: The pyloric sphincter is between the stomach and duodenum.  Why C wrong: The ileocecal valve is between the ileum and cecum.  Why D wrong: Not the diaphragmatic opening named."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The upper esophageal sphincter, located between the upper part of the esophagus and laryngopharynx, is also known as the:","options":["Cardiac sphincter","Pharyngo-esophageal sphincter","Pyloric sphincter","Ileocecal sphincter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The upper esophageal sphincter is between the upper part of the esophagus and laryngopharynx (pharyngo-esophageal sphincter).  Why A wrong: Cardiac sphincter is the lower esophageal sphincter.  Why C/D wrong: These are at different GI junctions."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The lower esophageal sphincter (cardiac sphincter) is located between which structures?","options":["Pharynx and upper esophagus","Lower part of esophagus and stomach","Stomach and duodenum","Ileum and cecum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lower esophageal sphincter (cardiac sphincter) is between the lower part of the esophagus and the stomach.  Why A wrong: That is the upper esophageal sphincter.  Why C wrong: That is the pyloric sphincter.  Why D wrong: That is the ileocecal sphincter."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"If the lower esophageal sphincter fails to close properly, allowing it to lead to GERD and that is ugliness, this condition is known as:","options":["Pyloric stenosis","Barrett's esophagus","Achalasia","Hiatal hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the lower esophageal sphincter fails to close properly and leads to GERD it is associated with Barrett's esophagus.  Why A wrong: Pyloric stenosis is narrowing of the pyloric region.  Why C wrong: Achalasia is failure of the lower sphincter to relax (closure of the lower esophageal sphincter), not failure to close.  Why D wrong: Hiatal hernia is protrusion through the diaphragmatic hiatus, not the term given here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Achalasia refers to which condition of the esophagus?","options":["Failure (closure) of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax","Inflammation of the gum","Failure of the upper esophageal sphincter to open","Backflow of bile into the stomach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Achalasia is the closure (failure to relax) of the lower esophageal sphincter.  Why B wrong: That is gingivitis.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the definition of achalasia."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The main function of the esophagus is to:","options":["Absorb nutrients","Transport food and fluid, after being swallowed, from the mouth to the stomach","Secrete digestive enzymes","Store food for digestion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The function of the esophagus is transport of food and fluid, after being swallowed, from the mouth to the stomach.  Why A wrong: No absorption of nutrients takes place in the esophagus.  Why C/D wrong: These are not esophageal functions."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The esophagus is innervated by which nerve?","options":["Phrenic nerve only","The vagus nerve and the cervical and thoracic sympathetic trunk","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The esophagus is innervated by the vagus nerve and the cervical and thoracic sympathetic trunk.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the nerves stated for esophageal innervation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which statement about absorption in the esophagus is correct?","options":["The esophagus absorbs water and electrolytes","No absorption of nutrients takes place in the esophagus","The esophagus absorbs vitamins","The esophagus absorbs fats only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No absorption of nutrients takes place in the esophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: The esophagus does not absorb nutrients, water, vitamins, or fats."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The stomach is a pouch-like, J-shaped organ primarily designed for food storage for what duration?","options":["For 30 minutes","For 3–4 hours","For 12 hours","For 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stomach is a pouch-like J-shaped organ primarily designed for food storage for 3–4 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated storage duration."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The capacity of the stomach is approximately:","options":["500 ml","1500 ml","3000 ml","250 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The capacity of the stomach is about 1500 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated gastric capacity."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The approximate daily secretion of gastric juice is:","options":["500 ml/day","1500 ml/day","3000 ml/day","250 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The secretion of gastric juice is about 1500 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated gastric juice secretion."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Blood supply to the stomach is delivered by the left gastric artery, and venous return occurs by the:","options":["Inferior vena cava","Portal vein","Hepatic artery","Renal vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood supply is by left gastric artery and venous return is by the portal vein.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the venous return route stated for the stomach."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Mechanical and chemical digestion in the stomach produces a yellowish paste formed from food. This paste is called:","options":["Bolus","Chyme","Chyle","Bile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After chemical and mechanical digestion, a yellowish paste called chyme is formed, which will pass into the small intestine.  Why A wrong: A bolus is the moist food lump formed in the mouth.  Why C wrong: Chyle is lymph fluid containing absorbed fat.  Why D wrong: Bile is the hepatic secretion."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"How many sphincters does the stomach contain to regulate food movement?","options":["One sphincter","Two sphincters at both ends (cardiac sphincter and pyloric sphincter)","Three sphincters","No sphincters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stomach contains two sphincters at both ends to regulate food movement: the cardiac sphincter near the esophagus and the pyloric sphincter near the small intestine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stomach has two sphincters, not these numbers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The cardiac sphincter is located near which structure?","options":["Near the small intestine","Near the esophagus","Near the cecum","Near the appendix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardiac sphincter is near the esophagus.  Why A wrong: The pyloric sphincter is near the small intestine.  Why C/D wrong: These are not where the cardiac sphincter is located."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which are the four anatomical regions of the stomach?","options":["Cardiac, fundus, body, and pylorus","Crown, root, neck, and apex","Cervical, thoracic, abdominal, and pelvic","Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, and serosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four anatomical regions of the stomach are cardiac, fundus, body, and pylorus.  Why B wrong: These are tooth regions.  Why C wrong: These resemble esophageal divisions.  Why D wrong: These are GI wall layers."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The cardiac region of the stomach is the entrance of the stomach, and it has which sphincter with no cardiac/esophagus relationship?","options":["Pyloric sphincter","Cardiac sphincter","Ileocecal sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardiac region is the entrance of the stomach and is associated with the cardiac sphincter near the esophagus.  Why A wrong: The pyloric sphincter is at the outlet (pyloric region).  Why C/D wrong: These are at different GI sites."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"In the pyloric region (pylorus), the pyloric sphincter controls the opening between which structures?","options":["Esophagus and stomach","Stomach and small intestine","Ileum and cecum","Mouth and pharynx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pyloric region is the outlet of the stomach where circular muscles in the wall of the pylorus become the pyloric sphincter, controlling the opening between the stomach and small intestine.  Why A wrong: That junction is controlled by the cardiac sphincter.  Why C/D wrong: These are not controlled by the pyloric sphincter."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The thick folds (ridges) of the stomach lining that allow for large surface area for expansion, secretion, digestion, and some absorption are called:","options":["Villi","Rugae","Microvilli","Haustra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thick folds called rugae form ridges as this layer provides a larger surface area for expansion, secretion, digestion, and some absorption.  Why A wrong: Villi are in the small intestine.  Why C wrong: Microvilli are the brush border of intestinal cells.  Why D wrong: Haustra are pouches of the large intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Pyloric stenosis is a condition in which pyloric muscles block the food entering into the small intestine; it is also called:","options":["Achalasia","Gastric outlet obstruction","Hiatal hernia","Barrett's esophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyloric stenosis occurs when pyloric muscles block the food entering into the small intestine; it is also called gastric outlet obstruction.  Why A wrong: Achalasia involves the lower esophageal sphincter.  Why C wrong: Hiatal hernia involves the diaphragmatic hiatus.  Why D wrong: Barrett's esophagus is an esophageal condition."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the stomach? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Temporary food storage","Chemical digestion and mechanical break-down","Control of the rate at which food enters the duodenum","Acid secretion and antibacterial action","Production and secretion of intrinsic factor needed for absorption of Vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): Functions of the stomach include temporary food storage; chemical digestion; mechanical break-down; control of the rate at which food enters the duodenum; acid secretion and antibacterial action; preparation of iron for absorption; and production/secretion of intrinsic factor needed for absorption of vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum.  All listed are correct stomach functions."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The stomach prepares which mineral for absorption?","options":["Calcium","Iron","Potassium","Magnesium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One of the functions of the stomach is preparation of iron for absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stomach is stated to prepare iron, not these minerals, for absorption."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Chief cells of the gastric secretory cells, also known as zymogenic cells or peptic cells, secrete which substance?","options":["Hydrochloric acid (HCl)","Pepsinogen (an inactive enzyme) and gastric lipase","Mucus and bicarbonate","Gastrin hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chief cells (zymogenic/peptic cells) secrete pepsinogen (an inactive enzyme), which helps in digestion of protein, and gastric lipase which helps to digest fats.  Why A wrong: HCl and intrinsic factor are secreted by parietal cells.  Why C wrong: Mucus is secreted by mucous cells.  Why D wrong: Gastrin is secreted by G cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Parietal cells (also known as oxyntic cells or delomorphous cells) of the stomach secrete which substances?","options":["Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","Hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor (which helps absorption of vitamin B12 in the intestine/distal ileum)","Mucus only","Somatostatin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parietal (oxyntic/delomorphous) cells secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor, which helps absorption of vitamin B12 in the distal ileum.  Why A wrong: Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are secreted by chief cells.  Why C wrong: Mucus is secreted by mucous cells.  Why D wrong: Somatostatin is secreted by D cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Mucous cells of the stomach secrete mucus and which alkaline substance to help neutralize HCl in the gastric juice?","options":["Pepsinogen","Bicarbonate (alkaline substance)","Hydrochloric acid","Histamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mucous cells secrete mucus and an alkaline substance to help neutralize HCl in the gastric juice.  Why A wrong: Pepsinogen is from chief cells.  Why C wrong: HCl is acidic, secreted by parietal cells.  Why D wrong: Histamine is from enterochromaffin-like cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"G cells of the stomach secrete which hormone, which stimulates the parietal cells and the overall gastric secretion?","options":["Somatostatin","Gastrin","Secretin","Cholecystokinin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: G cells secrete a hormone called gastrin, which stimulates the parietal cells and the overall gastric secretion.  Why A wrong: Somatostatin is secreted by D cells.  Why C/D wrong: Secretin and cholecystokinin are intestinal hormones, not secreted by gastric G cells here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"D cells of the stomach secrete which substance?","options":["Gastrin","Somatostatin","Histamine","Pepsinogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D cells secrete somatostatin hormone.  Why A wrong: Gastrin is from G cells.  Why C wrong: Histamine is from enterochromaffin-like cells.  Why D wrong: Pepsinogen is from chief cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Enterochromaffin-like cells of the stomach secrete which substance, which helps to secrete gastric juices?","options":["Somatostatin","Histamine","Intrinsic factor","Bicarbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Enterochromaffin-like cells secrete histamine, which helps to secrete gastric juices.  Why A wrong: Somatostatin is from D cells.  Why C wrong: Intrinsic factor is from parietal cells.  Why D wrong: Bicarbonate is from mucous cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Carbohydrate digestion in the stomach is best described as:","options":["Begins for the first time in the stomach","Continued with salivary amylase, resulting in disaccharides converted into monosaccharides","Completely halted in the stomach","Performed only by pepsin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbohydrate digestion is continued with salivary amylase in the stomach, resulting in disaccharides converted into monosaccharides.  Why A wrong: It began in the mouth, not for the first time in the stomach.  Why C/D wrong: Carbohydrate digestion continues (it is not halted) and is not done by pepsin."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Protein digestion in the stomach begins with which enzyme, resulting in activation of peptides?","options":["Salivary amylase","Pepsin","Lipase","Trypsin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein digestion begins with pepsin (activation of peptides) by HCl in the stomach, resulting in peptides (small chains of amino acids).  Why A wrong: Salivary amylase digests carbohydrates.  Why C wrong: Lipase digests fats.  Why D wrong: Trypsin acts in the small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Lipid digestion in the stomach begins with gastric lipase, which can break down stored lipids such as butterfat, resulting in:","options":["Glucose","Fatty acids","Amino acids","Disaccharides"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lipid digestion begins with gastric lipase, which breaks down stored lipids such as butterfat, resulting in fatty acids.  Why A wrong: Glucose results from carbohydrate digestion.  Why C wrong: Amino acids result from protein digestion.  Why D wrong: Disaccharides are carbohydrate breakdown products."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Absorption in the stomach is limited; which substances can be absorbed there?","options":["Proteins and large carbohydrates","Only small and fat-soluble substances such as alcohol, aspirin, and certain drugs","Vitamins and minerals","Bile and bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absorption in the stomach is limited, where only small and fat-soluble substances such as alcohol, aspirin, and certain drugs can be absorbed.  Why A/C/D wrong: Proteins, large carbohydrates, vitamins, minerals, bile, and bilirubin are not absorbed in the stomach."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The small intestine is described as:","options":["The shortest organ of the GI system","The longest organ of the GI system, about 6 meters long","About 1.5 meters long","About 25 cm long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The small intestine is the longest organ of the GI system and is about 6 meters long.  Why A wrong: It is the longest, not the shortest, organ.  Why C wrong: 1.5 meters is the large intestine.  Why D wrong: 25 cm is the duodenum."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The small intestine lies in which abdominal location, surrounded by the large intestine, and is divided into which three parts?","options":["Duodenum, jejunum, and ileum","Cecum, colon, and rectum","Fundus, body, and pylorus","Cervical, thoracic, and abdominal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The small intestine lies in the abdominal cavity surrounded by the large intestine and is divided into the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum.  Why B wrong: These are parts of the large intestine.  Why C wrong: These are stomach regions.  Why D wrong: These are esophageal parts."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The small intestine extends from the pyloric sphincter to which structure?","options":["The cardiac sphincter","The ileocecal valve","The anus","The sigmoid colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The small intestine extends from the pyloric sphincter to the ileocecal valve.  Why A wrong: The cardiac sphincter is at the stomach entrance.  Why C/D wrong: These are parts of the large intestine/terminal GI tract."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The duodenum is approximately how long and is described as which part of the small intestine?","options":["About 25 cm long; the shortest part of the small intestine","About 2.5 m long; the longest part","About 3 m long","About 6 m long"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the shortest part of the small intestine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated duodenal length."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The duodenum is curved around the head of which organ, into which secretions from the gallbladder and pancreas are released?","options":["Liver","Pancreas","Spleen","Kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The duodenum is curved around the head of the pancreas; secretions from the gallbladder and pancreas are released into the duodenum.  Why A/C/D wrong: The duodenum curves around the pancreas head, not these organs."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Most of the iron is absorbed in which part of the small intestine?","options":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Cecum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most of iron is absorbed in the duodenum.  Why B wrong: The jejunum absorbs most nutrients (water-soluble vitamins, minerals).  Why C wrong: The ileum absorbs vitamin B12, bile salts, and fat-soluble vitamins.  Why D wrong: The cecum is part of the large intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which hormone is secreted by the duodenum, which stimulates delivery of bile and pancreatic juice into the small intestine?","options":["Gastrin","Cholecystokinin (CCK)","Secretin","Somatostatin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cholecystokinin (CCK) hormone is secreted by the duodenum and stimulates delivery of bile and pancreatic juice into the small intestine.  Why A wrong: Gastrin is a gastric hormone from G cells.  Why C wrong: Secretin is not the hormone named here for bile/pancreatic delivery.  Why D wrong: Somatostatin is from D cells."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The jejunum is approximately how long?","options":["25 cm","2.5 meters","3 meters","6 meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The jejunum is about 2.5 m long.  Why A wrong: 25 cm is the duodenum.  Why C wrong: 3 m is the ileum.  Why D wrong: 6 m is the whole small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The jejunum is the middle section of the small intestine where which absorption occurs?","options":["No absorption","Almost all nutrients (water-soluble vitamins, minerals, etc.) are absorbed except water","Only water is absorbed","Only bile salts are absorbed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the jejunum, absorption of almost all nutrients occurs except water.  Why A/C/D wrong: The jejunum absorbs nearly all nutrients (not none, not water-only, not bile salts only)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The ileum is approximately how long, and it controls the flow of material from the ileum and cecum?","options":["About 25 cm long","About 3 m long","About 2.5 m long","About 6 m long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ileum is about 3 m long and controls the flow of material from the ileum and cecum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated ileal length."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The ileum is the longest part of which structure?","options":["The large intestine","The small intestine","The stomach","The esophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ileum is the longest part of the small intestine.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is part of the small intestine, and the longest of its three segments."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Here, absorption of water and vitamins occurs in the ileum, including which vitamin absorbed in the terminal ileum?","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin B12 (B-Co/cobalamin)","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most of Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the terminal ileum (also absorption of water and vitamins occurs in the ileum).  Why A/C/D wrong: Vitamin B12 is the vitamin specifically absorbed in the terminal ileum."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Peyer's patches are lymphoid organs of intestine that act as protection against harmful microorganisms; they are found in small intestine mostly in which part?","options":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Cecum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Peyer's patches are lymphoid organs found in the small intestine mostly in the ileum and act as protection against harmful microorganisms.  Why A/B/D wrong: Peyer's patches are mostly in the ileum, not these segments."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The secretions of the small intestine produce a watery fluid that provides a vehicle for moving chyme to the villi. Each villus contains which structures to absorb water, glucose, amino acids, vitamins, and minerals?","options":["Goblet cells and rugae","Blood capillaries and short-chain fatty acids, and also contains lymphatic capillaries called lacteals to absorb long-chain fatty acids","Pancreatic ducts and bile ducts","Haustra and taeniae coli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each villus contains blood capillaries to absorb water, glucose, amino acids, vitamins, and minerals (and short-chain fatty acids), and also contains lymphatic capillaries called lacteals to absorb long-chain fatty acids.  Why A wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus; rugae are in the stomach.  Why C wrong: Pancreatic and bile ducts deliver secretions, they are not villus structures.  Why D wrong: Haustra and taeniae coli are features of the large intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The microvilli or brush border of the small intestine secrete which three types of enzymes?","options":["Disaccharidases, enterokinases, and aminopeptidases","Amylase, lipase, and trypsin","Pepsin, gastrin, and HCl","Bile, secretin, and CCK"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The microvilli or brush border of the small intestine secrete three types of enzymes: disaccharidases, enterokinases, and aminopeptidases.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the three brush-border enzyme types described."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The disaccharidase enzyme maltase digests maltose into:","options":["Two glucose molecules","Glucose and fructose","Glucose and galactose","Two fructose molecules"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maltase digests maltose into two glucose (Maltose → Maltase → Glucose + Glucose).  Why B wrong: That is sucrase action on sucrose (honey sugar).  Why C wrong: That is lactase action on lactose (milk sugar).  Why D wrong: Maltose does not split into two fructose."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The enzyme sucrase digests sucrose (honey sugar) into which products?","options":["Glucose and glucose","Glucose and fructose","Glucose and galactose","Fructose and galactose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sucrase digests sucrose into glucose and fructose (Sucrose → Sucrase → Glucose + Fructose); sucrose is the honey sugar prominent in semen.  Why A wrong: That is maltase on maltose.  Why C wrong: That is lactase on lactose.  Why D wrong: Sucrose does not split into fructose and galactose."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The enzyme lactase digests lactose (milk sugar) into which products?","options":["Glucose and glucose","Glucose and fructose","Glucose and galactose","Two galactose molecules"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lactase digests sucrose/lactose into glucose and galactose (Lactose → Lactase → Glucose + Galactose); lactose is the milk sugar.  Why A wrong: That is maltase on maltose.  Why B wrong: That is sucrase on sucrose.  Why D wrong: Lactose does not split into two galactose."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"The enterokinase enzyme of the small intestine converts trypsinogen into:","options":["Pepsin","Trypsin","Amylase","Lipase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Enterokinase converts trypsinogen into trypsin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, not into these enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Peptides or protein products are converted into amino acids and product of protein in the presence of which enzyme?","options":["Pepsin","Trypsin","Amylase","Maltase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peptides or protein products convert into amino acids and product of protein in the presence of trypsin.  Why A wrong: Pepsin acts in the stomach.  Why C wrong: Amylase digests carbohydrates.  Why D wrong: Maltase digests maltose."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Aminopeptidase or erepsin enzymes act on which substrates?","options":["Erepsin digests peptide into amino acids, and nucleases digest nucleotides into nitrogenous bases","Erepsin digests fats into fatty acids","Erepsin digests starch into maltose","Nucleases digest proteins into peptides"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under aminopeptidase/erepsin, erepsin digests peptide into amino acids, and nucleases digest nucleotides into nitrogenous bases.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the substrates and products of erepsin/nucleases."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Where are Peyer's patches located within the GI tract wall?","options":["In the muscular layer","In the mucosal layer","In the serosa","In the submucosa exclusively"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peyer's patches are located in the mucosal layer.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are described as located in the mucosal layer."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Peyer's patches are also known as which structures?","options":["Lacteals","Solitary glands or aggregated lymph follicles","Brunner's glands","Crypts of Lieberkühn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peyer's patches are also known as solitary glands or aggregated lymph follicles.  Why A wrong: Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries in villi.  Why C/D wrong: Not the names given for Peyer's patches in the source."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Peyer's patches work as lymphoid glands that protect intestinal mucosa from which threat?","options":["Foreign microorganisms","Acid reflux","Mechanical injury","Cold temperatures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peyer's patches work as lymphoid glands that protect intestinal mucosa from foreign microorganisms.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the threat Peyer's patches protect against as described."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Inflammation of the Peyer's patches (case of enteric fever) is associated with which condition?","options":["Cholera","Typhoid","Hepatitis","Pancreatitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: They (Peyer's patches) inflamed in case of enteric fever (typhoid).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the disease associated with inflamed Peyer's patches in the source."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Fat malabsorption takes place in the presence of which substance, related to Peyer's patches/ileal function?","options":["Bile juice (fat malabsorption with the presence of bile juice)","Hydrochloric acid","Salivary amylase","Gastrin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fat malabsorption takes place here with the presence of bile juice.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not associated with the stated fat malabsorption context."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Water is absorbed in the small intestine primarily by which process?","options":["Active transport","Osmosis","Filtration","Phagocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water is absorbed by osmosis.  Why A wrong: Most other nutrients (glucose, amino acids, minerals) are absorbed by active transport.  Why C/D wrong: Water absorption is by osmosis, not filtration or phagocytosis."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Fatty acids are absorbed in the small intestine by which process, since they are fat-soluble?","options":["Active transport","Osmosis","Diffusion","Pinocytosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fatty acids are absorbed by diffusion since they are fat-soluble.  Why A wrong: Active transport is for most other nutrients.  Why B wrong: Osmosis is for water.  Why D wrong: Not the process stated for fatty acid absorption."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Most other nutrients (glucose, amino acids, and minerals) are absorbed in the small intestine by which process?","options":["Osmosis","Diffusion","Active transport","Filtration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Most other nutrients (glucose, amino acids, and minerals) are absorbed by active transport.  Why A wrong: Osmosis is for water.  Why B wrong: Diffusion is for fatty acids.  Why D wrong: Filtration is not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"Which are the named parts of the large intestine shown in the overview? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ascending colon and descending colon","Transverse colon and sigmoid colon","Cecum and appendix","Rectum and anus","Duodenum and jejunum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The large intestine overview includes the ascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, cecum, appendix, rectum, and anus.  Why E wrong: The duodenum and jejunum are parts of the small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"A nurse is teaching that the stomach secretes intrinsic factor essential for vitamin B12 absorption. A patient who has undergone total gastrectomy is therefore at greatest risk for which deficiency-related complication?","options":["Iron-deficiency anemia only","Vitamin B12 deficiency due to loss of intrinsic factor, impairing B12 absorption in the terminal ileum","Vitamin C deficiency","Calcium deficiency from loss of rugae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor, which is needed for vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum; loss of the stomach removes intrinsic factor, causing B12 deficiency.  Why A wrong: Although the stomach prepares iron, the key intrinsic-factor link is to B12.  Why C/D wrong: Vitamin C and calcium are not the intrinsic-factor-dependent nutrients described."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Digestive system anatomy & physiology — GI tract organization…","question":"A patient with severe damage to the terminal ileum (e.g., resection) would be at greatest risk for malabsorption of which substances?","options":["Glucose and amino acids","Vitamin B12, bile salts, and fat-soluble vitamins","Iron only","Water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The terminal ileum absorbs vitamin B12, and the ileum is involved in bile salt and fat-soluble vitamin absorption (fat malabsorption relates to bile juice); damage impairs these.  Why A wrong: Glucose and amino acids are absorbed largely in the jejunum by active transport.  Why C wrong: Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum.  Why D wrong: Water absorption is widespread (osmosis), not solely ileal."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The caecum is described as a pouch of approximately what dimension?","options":["4 cm long","6 cm long","10 cm long","13 cm long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the caecum is a 6 cm long pouch connected to the junction of the small and large intestine.  Why A wrong: 4 cm understates the stated length.  Why C wrong: 10 cm is not the caecum's length (it relates to the cystic duct).  Why D wrong: 13 cm is the length of the rectum, not the caecum."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Where is the first (proximal) part of the colon located?","options":["Within the rectum","At the junction of the small and large intestine / caecum","At the anal canal","At the duodenum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The caecum lies at the junction of the small and large intestine and is the first part of the colon region.  Why A wrong: The rectum is the distal large bowel, not the first part.  Why C wrong: The anal canal is the terminal portion.  Why D wrong: The duodenum is part of the small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The vermiform appendix is best described as:","options":["A glandular organ secreting digestive enzymes","A muscular structure attached to the large intestine in the human body","A segment of the small intestine","Part of the rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines the vermiform appendix as a muscular structure attached to the large intestine.  Why A wrong: It is not described as an enzyme-secreting gland.  Why C wrong: It attaches to the large intestine, not the small intestine.  Why D wrong: It is not part of the rectum."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the approximate length of the vermiform appendix?","options":["2–4 cm","6–9 cm long","13 cm","20 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the length of the appendix is 6–9 cm long.  Why A wrong: 2–4 cm is too short per the source.  Why C wrong: 13 cm is the rectal length.  Why D wrong: 20 cm is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The vermiform appendix is most commonly found in which position?","options":["Pelvic","Pre-ileal","Retrocecal","Subcecal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the appendix is normally and most common position retrocecal.  Why A/B/D wrong: Pelvic, pre-ileal, and subcecal are other anatomical positions but not the most common one cited."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Inflammation of the appendix is known as:","options":["Colitis","Appendicitis","Diverticulitis","Cecitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inflammation of the appendix is known as appendicitis.  Why A wrong: Colitis is inflammation of the colon.  Why C wrong: Diverticulitis is inflammation of diverticula.  Why D wrong: Cecitis (typhlitis) is inflammation of the caecum, not the appendix."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which sequence correctly lists the divisions of the colon?","options":["Sigmoid, transverse, ascending, descending","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid colon","Descending, ascending, transverse, sigmoid","Transverse, sigmoid, ascending, descending"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source divides the colon into ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon.  Why A/C/D wrong: These list the divisions in an incorrect anatomical order."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which is the shortest part of the colon, while which is the longest?","options":["Ascending colon is shortest; sigmoid colon is longest","Transverse colon is shortest; ascending colon is longest","Transverse colon is shortest, whereas ascending colon is the longest part","Sigmoid colon is shortest; descending colon is longest"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the shortest part of the colon is the transverse colon, whereas the ascending colon is the longest part.  Why A/B/D wrong: They reverse or misassign which divisions are shortest and longest per the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the approximate length of the rectum?","options":["6 cm","9 cm","13 cm","23 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the rectum is about 13 cm long.  Why A/B/D wrong: These lengths do not match the stated value of 13 cm."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The rectum is described as a chamber that begins at the end of which structure?","options":["The caecum","The large intestine (sigmoid colon)","The duodenum","The jejunum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the rectum is a chamber that begins at the end of the large intestine, immediately following the sigmoid colon.  Why A wrong: The caecum is the beginning of the large intestine, not where the rectum starts.  Why C/D wrong: The duodenum and jejunum are parts of the small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Ordinarily, the rectum is in what state, with stool stored in the descending colon?","options":["Distended with stool","Empty","Filled with gas only","Collapsed by tumour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the rectum is ordinarily empty because maximum stool is stored in the descending colon.  Why A wrong: It is normally empty, not distended.  Why C/D wrong: These are not described as the normal state."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the approximate length of the anal canal?","options":["1.2 cm","3.8 cm long","6 cm","13 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the anal canal is 3.8 cm long.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3.8 cm."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The anal canal is the most terminal part of which system?","options":["Upper GI tract","Lower GI tract","Biliary tract","Urinary tract"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the anal canal is the most terminal part of the lower GI tract.  Why A wrong: The upper GI tract ends well above the anal canal.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated tracts."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The brown colour of stool is due to a substance called:","options":["Bilirubin","Stobile / stobile pigment","Urobilin","Haemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the brown colour of stool is due to indole and stobile (skatole) formed by breakdown of bile by intestinal bacteria.  Why A wrong: Bilirubin is a precursor pigment but the source specifically names stobile/skatole for the brown colour.  Why C wrong: Urobilin is not the term used in the source.  Why D wrong: Haemoglobin is the oxygen-carrying protein, not the stool pigment."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which organ is the largest gland in the human body?","options":["Pancreas","Liver","Gallbladder","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver is the largest gland in the body.  Why A wrong: The pancreas is the largest gland behind the body cavity but not the largest overall.  Why C/D wrong: The gallbladder and spleen are not the largest glands."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The liver is also the largest organ in the body. In which abdominal region is it primarily located?","options":["Left hypochondriac region","Right hypochondriac region","Umbilical region","Hypogastric region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver is the largest organ in the body and is located in the right hypochondriac region.  Why A wrong: The left hypochondriac region houses the spleen and stomach fundus.  Why C/D wrong: The umbilical and hypogastric regions are below the liver."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A normal adult liver is described as which shape and colour, weighing how much?","options":["Triangular, pale yellow, 500 g","Wedge-shaped, reddish brown, 1500 g and in female 1300 g","Pear-shaped, green, 300 g","Oval, dark red, 2000 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver is wedge-shaped, reddish brown in colour, weighs about 1500 g and in females about 1300 g (1300 gm).  Why A/C/D wrong: These shapes, colours, and weights do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The liver is covered by which connective tissue layer?","options":["Glisson's capsule (costal cartilage covering)","Bowman's capsule","Tunica albuginea","Falciform sheath"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver is covered by ribs and costal cartilage (Glisson's capsule connective tissue covering).  Why B wrong: Bowman's capsule surrounds the renal glomerulus.  Why C wrong: Tunica albuginea covers the testes.  Why D wrong: There is no \"falciform sheath\"; the falciform ligament separates the lobes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"How is blood supplied to the liver?","options":["Only by the hepatic artery","A blood supply from hepatic artery (oxygenated blood) and portal vein (deoxygenated blood from the small intestine/GI tract)","Only by the portal vein","By the splenic vein alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver has a blood supply from the hepatic artery which delivers oxygenated blood, and from the small intestine (portal vein) delivering deoxygenated blood from the GI tract.  Why A/C wrong: The liver has a dual supply, not a single source.  Why D wrong: The splenic vein is a tributary of the portal system, not the sole supply."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Into how many lobes is the liver divided?","options":["2 lobes","3 lobes","4 lobes — left, right, caudate and quadrate","5 lobes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the liver is divided into 4 lobes: left, right, caudate and quadrate.  Why A wrong: While only left and right are the major lobes, the source lists 4 total.  Why B/D wrong: 3 and 5 do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which are the largest lobes of the liver, and what separates them?","options":["Caudate and quadrate lobes, separated by the round ligament","Right and left lobes, separated by the falciform ligament","Right and caudate lobes, separated by the ligamentum teres","Left and quadrate lobes, separated by the coronary ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the right and left lobes are the largest lobes and are separated by the falciform ligament.  Why A/C/D wrong: The caudate and quadrate are the smaller lobes, and the falciform ligament (not the round/ligamentum teres or coronary ligament) is the divider per the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The Kupffer cells of the liver function as:","options":["Insulin-secreting cells","The phagocytic cells of the liver","Bile-producing hepatocytes","Bile storage cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Kupffer cells are the phagocytic cells of the liver.  Why A wrong: Insulin is secreted by pancreatic beta cells.  Why C wrong: Hepatocytes produce bile; Kupffer cells are macrophages.  Why D wrong: Bile is stored in the gallbladder, not by Kupffer cells."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a function of the liver?","options":["Bile production and excretion","Storage of glycogen, vitamins and minerals","Production of insulin","Detoxification of drugs and toxins"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source does not list insulin production as a liver function; insulin is produced by the pancreas.  Why A wrong: Bile production and excretion is a listed liver function.  Why B wrong: Storage of glycogen, vitamins and minerals is listed.  Why D wrong: Detoxification of drugs and toxins is listed."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A nursing student lists liver functions. Which set matches the source?","options":["Excretion of bilirubin/cholesterol/hormones/drugs; metabolism of fats, proteins, carbohydrates; enzyme activation; synthesis of plasma proteins (albumin, clotting factors)","Synthesis of bile salts only","Production of red blood cells only","Secretion of gastric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bilirubin/cholesterol/hormone/drug excretion, metabolism of fats/proteins/carbohydrates, enzyme activation, and synthesis of plasma proteins such as albumin and clotting factors.  Why B wrong: This is only one narrow function.  Why C wrong: Adult RBC production occurs in bone marrow.  Why D wrong: Gastric acid is secreted by the stomach, not the liver."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The gallbladder is described as which shape?","options":["Wedge-shaped","Pear-shaped","S-shaped","Spherical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the gallbladder is a pear-shaped, hollow structure located under the liver and on the right side of the abdomen.  Why A wrong: Wedge-shaped describes the liver.  Why C wrong: S-shaped describes the sigmoid colon.  Why D wrong: Spherical is not the described shape."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the primary function of the gallbladder?","options":["To produce bile","To store and concentrate bile","To secrete digestive enzymes","To detoxify drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the primary function of the gallbladder is to store and concentrate bile, a yellow-brown digestive enzyme produced by the liver.  Why A wrong: Bile is produced by the liver, not the gallbladder.  Why C wrong: Digestive enzymes are produced largely by the pancreas.  Why D wrong: Detoxification is a liver function."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the approximate length and capacity of the gallbladder?","options":["5 cm long, capacity 10–20 ml","10 cm long and capacity is 30–50 ml","13 cm long, capacity 100 ml","20 cm long, capacity 200 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the gallbladder is about 10 cm long and has a capacity of 30–50 ml.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dimensions and capacities do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Bile is essential for which digestive process?","options":["Protein digestion","Carbohydrate digestion","Fat emulsification","Nucleic acid digestion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bile is the essential component for fat emulsification.  Why A wrong: Protein digestion is performed by proteases (e.g., pepsin, trypsin).  Why B wrong: Carbohydrate digestion is performed by amylase.  Why D wrong: Nucleic acids are digested by nucleases, not bile."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The cystic duct is the duct that joins which structure to the common hepatic duct?","options":["The pancreas","The gallbladder","The duodenum","The stomach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cystic duct is the short duct that joins the gallbladder to the common hepatic duct.  Why A/C/D wrong: The cystic duct connects the gallbladder, not the pancreas, duodenum, or stomach."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The cystic duct contains spiral valves of Heister. What is their function?","options":["They increase bile production","They reduce resistance to the flow of bile","They block bile completely","They secrete digestive enzymes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cystic duct contains spiral valves of Heister, which reduce resistance to the flow of bile.  Why A wrong: They do not increase production.  Why C wrong: They regulate, not block, flow.  Why D wrong: They do not secrete enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The bile duct is best described as:","options":["A single large tube","A series of thin tubes that go from the liver to the small intestine","A muscular pouch storing bile","A pancreatic enzyme duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the bile duct is a series of thin tubes that go from the liver to the small intestine.  Why A wrong: It is a series of tubes, not a single tube.  Why C wrong: A storage pouch describes the gallbladder.  Why D wrong: It carries bile, not pancreatic enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Where does the major job of the bile duct take place?","options":["Inside the gallbladder","Inside the small intestine, where it helps in digestion of fat","Inside the stomach","Inside the pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the major job of the bile duct takes place inside the small intestine, where it helps in the digestion of fat.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not where the bile duct's major function occurs per the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The common bile duct (CBT) is formed by the union of which two ducts?","options":["Hepatic artery and portal vein","Bile duct + cystic duct","Pancreatic duct + cystic duct","Wirsung duct + Santorini duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the common bile duct consists of the bile duct (common hepatic duct) + cystic duct.  Why A wrong: These are blood vessels, not bile ducts.  Why C wrong: The pancreatic duct joins the CBD later but does not form it with the cystic duct.  Why D wrong: Wirsung and Santorini are pancreatic ducts."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which of the following statements about the common bile duct (CBD) is correct per the source?","options":["The CBD pulls the bile into the duodenum for fat emulsification","The arterial supply to the CBD is via the cystic artery / branch of the right hepatic artery","Venous drainage of the CBD enters directly into the portal vein","Venous drainage of the fundus and body of the gallbladder flows into the hepatic sinusoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the arterial supply to the CBD is via the cystic artery, a branch of the right hepatic artery, and all the listed statements (A–D) are presented as correct CBD facts in the source.  Why A wrong: Although A is also stated in the source, option B is the most precise arterial fact; A describes the functional role and is also true. (Note: this item keys the arterial-supply fact; all four statements appear in the source, so the discrepancy is noted — B is selected as the single best answer to the arterial-supply focus.)  Why C wrong: The source states CBD venous drainage enters directly into the portal vein, which is true; it is not the arterial focus of this stem.  Why D wrong: Venous drainage of the fundus and body of the gallbladder flows into the hepatic sinusoids, which is true, but again not the arterial focus of this stem."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which of the following are listed functions of the gallbladder? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Reservoir of bile","Release of stored bile","Absorption of water and concentration of bile","Regulation of pressure in the biliary system (contraction and dilation)","Production of insulin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists reservoir of bile, release of stored bile, absorption of water and concentration of bile, and regulation of pressure in the biliary system by contraction and dilation as gallbladder functions.  Why E wrong: Insulin production is a pancreatic (beta cell) function, not a gallbladder function."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The endocrine system of the pancreas is known as:","options":["Acini","Islets of Langerhans","Brunner's glands","Peyer's patches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the endocrine system of the pancreas is known as the islets of Langerhans.  Why A wrong: Acini are the exocrine secretory units.  Why C wrong: Brunner's glands are duodenal mucus glands.  Why D wrong: Peyer's patches are intestinal lymphoid tissue."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Anatomically, the pancreas is divided into how many parts?","options":["2 parts","3 parts — head, neck, body, tail","4 parts — head, neck, body and tail","5 parts"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pancreas is anatomically divided into head, neck, body and tail (4 parts).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the four named parts in the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The pancreas is described as which shaped organ, with what dimensions?","options":["\"S\"-shaped organ, weight 10 g, 5 cm long","\"U\"-shaped organ, weight 60–90 g, 12 to 15 cm long","Pear-shaped, weight 200 g, 25 cm long","Triangular, weight 1500 g, 30 cm long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pancreas is a \"U\"-shaped organ, weight 60–90 g, 12 to 15 cm long.  Why A/C/D wrong: These shapes, weights, and lengths do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which structures are abundantly found in the tail of the pancreas?","options":["Acini","Islets of Langerhans","Kupffer cells","Goblet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states most of the islets of Langerhans are abundantly found in the tail of the pancreas.  Why A wrong: Acini are distributed throughout the exocrine pancreas.  Why C wrong: Kupffer cells are in the liver.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells are in the intestinal/respiratory mucosa."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The main pancreatic duct, which carries pancreatic juice, is also known as:","options":["Duct of Santorini","Duct of Wirsung","Cystic duct","Common bile duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the main pancreatic duct or duct of Wirsung is the main pancreatic duct.  Why A wrong: The duct of Santorini is the accessory pancreatic duct.  Why C wrong: The cystic duct drains the gallbladder.  Why D wrong: The common bile duct carries bile."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The pancreas is an example of a heterocrine gland. What does this mean per the source?","options":["It is 90% endocrine and 10% exocrine","It is both exocrine (90%) and endocrine (10%)","It is 100% endocrine","It is 100% exocrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pancreas is a heterocrine gland that is both exocrine (90%) and endocrine (10%).  Why A wrong: It reverses the percentages.  Why C/D wrong: It is not purely endocrine or purely exocrine."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The alpha cells of the pancreatic islets secrete which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon, which accelerates the blood glucose level","Somatostatin","Secretin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states alpha cells secrete glucagon hormone, which accelerates the blood glucose level.  Why A wrong: Insulin is secreted by beta cells.  Why C wrong: Somatostatin is secreted by delta cells.  Why D wrong: Secretin is an intestinal hormone."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The beta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete which hormone?","options":["Glucagon, which raises blood glucose","Insulin hormone, which regulates the blood glucose level","Somatostatin","Lipase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states beta cells secrete insulin hormone, which regulates the blood glucose level.  Why A wrong: Glucagon is the alpha-cell hormone.  Why C wrong: Somatostatin is the delta-cell hormone.  Why D wrong: Lipase is a digestive enzyme, not an islet hormone."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The delta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete which hormone?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Somatostatin","Amylase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states delta cells secrete somatostatin.  Why A/B wrong: Insulin and glucagon are secreted by beta and alpha cells respectively.  Why D wrong: Amylase is an exocrine digestive enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which pancreatic enzyme breaks down proteins (protease)?","options":["Amylase","Trypsin / chymotrypsin","Lipase","Insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states trypsin and chymotrypsin are proteases that break down proteins.  Why A wrong: Amylase breaks down complex carbohydrates.  Why C wrong: Lipase breaks down fats.  Why D wrong: Insulin is a hormone, not a digestive enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which pancreatic enzyme breaks down complex carbohydrates?","options":["Trypsin","Amylase","Lipase","Chymotrypsin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states amylase breaks down complex carbohydrates.  Why A/D wrong: Trypsin and chymotrypsin are proteases.  Why C wrong: Lipase breaks down fats."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which pancreatic enzyme breaks down fats?","options":["Amylase","Trypsin","Lipase","Chymotrypsin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lipase breaks down fats.  Why A wrong: Amylase acts on carbohydrates.  Why B/D wrong: Trypsin and chymotrypsin act on proteins."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Pancreatic juice or enzymes are regulated by which two hormones?","options":["Insulin and glucagon","Secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK)","Gastrin and somatostatin","Adrenaline and cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pancreatic juice or enzymes are regulated by secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) hormones.  Why A wrong: Insulin and glucagon regulate blood glucose, not pancreatic juice secretion in this context.  Why C/D wrong: These hormones are not the ones cited for regulating pancreatic juice."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The function of the pancreas that excludes controlling blood glucose level is reflected in alterations of which level via the duodenum?","options":["Blood pressure","Blood glucose level","Body temperature","Heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the functions of the pancreas include controlling the blood glucose level and aids in digestion through its juice.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not described as direct pancreatic functions in the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The pancreas is supplied by which arteries?","options":["Hepatic artery","Pancreatic branches of the splenic artery","Renal artery","Coronary artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pancreas is supplied by the pancreatic branches of the splenic artery.  Why A wrong: The hepatic artery supplies the liver.  Why C/D wrong: Renal and coronary arteries supply the kidney and heart respectively."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The human abdomen is divided into how many quadrants?","options":["2","4","6","9"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of quadrants is 4, marked by two horizontal and two vertical dividing lines.  Why A/C wrong: 2 and 6 do not match.  Why D wrong: 9 is the number of regions, not quadrants."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The total number of regions of the abdomen is:","options":["4","6","9","12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of regions is 9.  Why A wrong: 4 is the number of quadrants.  Why B/D wrong: 6 and 12 do not match."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which organs are found in the left upper quadrant (LUQ)?","options":["Liver, gallbladder, right kidney, small intestine","Spleen, stomach, pancreas, left kidney, splenic flexure of colon, part of transverse and descending colon","Appendix, caecum, right ureter","Urinary bladder, sigmoid colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the LUQ is to the left of the midline and below the umbilicus and contains the spleen, stomach, pancreas, left kidney, splenic flexure of colon, and part of transverse and descending colon.  Why A wrong: These are right upper quadrant organs.  Why C wrong: These are right lower quadrant structures.  Why D wrong: These are hypogastric/pelvic structures."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which structures are found in the right upper quadrant (RUQ)?","options":["Spleen and left kidney","Liver, gallbladder, right kidney and part of the small intestine","Appendix and caecum","Sigmoid colon and bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the RUQ contains the liver, gallbladder, right kidney and small intestine.  Why A wrong: Spleen and left kidney are in the LUQ.  Why C wrong: Appendix and caecum are in the RLQ.  Why D wrong: Sigmoid colon and bladder are hypogastric/pelvic."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The appendix and caecum are located in which abdominal quadrant?","options":["Right upper quadrant (RUQ)","Left upper quadrant (LUQ)","Right lower quadrant (RLQ)","Left lower quadrant (LLQ)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source places the appendix, caecum, ascending colon, right ovary and right Fallopian tube in the right lower quadrant (RLQ).  Why A/B wrong: These are upper quadrants.  Why D wrong: The LLQ contains the descending and sigmoid colon and left ovary/Fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which structures are found in the left lower quadrant (LLQ)?","options":["Liver and gallbladder","Descending colon, sigmoid colon, left ovary and left Fallopian tube","Appendix and caecum","Spleen and stomach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the LLQ contains the descending colon, sigmoid colon, left ovary and left Fallopian tube.  Why A wrong: Liver and gallbladder are in the RUQ.  Why C wrong: Appendix and caecum are in the RLQ.  Why D wrong: Spleen and stomach are in the LUQ."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which organs are located in the right hypochondriac region?","options":["Stomach, spleen, pancreas","Liver, gallbladder, right kidney, small intestine","Urinary bladder, sigmoid colon","Caecum, appendix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the right hypochondriac region as containing the liver, gallbladder, right kidney and small intestine.  Why A wrong: These belong to the epigastric/left hypochondriac regions.  Why C wrong: These are hypogastric structures.  Why D wrong: Caecum and appendix are in the right iliac/inguinal region."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The epigastric region of the abdomen contains:","options":["Liver, spleen, stomach, pancreas, duodenum, spleen and adrenal glands","Caecum and appendix","Urinary bladder","Sigmoid colon only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the epigastric region as containing the liver, spleen, stomach, pancreas, duodenum, and adrenal glands.  Why B wrong: Caecum and appendix are in the right iliac/inguinal region.  Why C wrong: The urinary bladder is in the hypogastric region.  Why D wrong: The sigmoid colon is in the left iliac/inguinal region."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The hypogastric (suprapubic) region contains which structures?","options":["Liver and gallbladder","Urinary bladder, sigmoid colon, and female reproductive organs","Spleen and pancreas","Right kidney and adrenal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the hypogastric region as containing the urinary bladder, sigmoid colon, and female reproductive organs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These structures belong to other abdominal regions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The left iliac (inguinal) region contains:","options":["Caecum and appendix","Descending colon and sigmoid colon","Liver and gallbladder","Right kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the left iliac/inguinal region contains the descending colon and sigmoid colon.  Why A wrong: Caecum and appendix are in the right iliac region.  Why C/D wrong: These belong to the right hypochondriac region."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the purpose of the urea breath test?","options":["To diagnose colorectal cancer","Visualisation of the entire large intestine","To detect the presence of Helicobacter pylori","To measure liver enzymes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the urea breath test is used to detect the presence of Helicobacter pylori, a bacteria that causes peptic ulcer disease/infection.  Why A/B wrong: These relate to colonoscopy, not the urea breath test.  Why D wrong: It is not a liver enzyme test."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the most common cause of peptic ulcer disease/infection that the urea breath test detects?","options":["Streptococcus","Helicobacter pylori","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the urea breath test detects Helicobacter pylori, the most common cause of peptic ulcer/infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These organisms are not the cited cause of peptic ulcer disease."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A common cause of false-positive results in the urea breath test is recent use of which drug class?","options":["Antacids","NSAIDs / aspirin","NSAID drugs and stress","Beta-blockers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes that a common cause of false-positive disease is recent use of NSAID drugs and stress (and related factors) interfering with the result.  Why A/B wrong: While drug interference can occur, the source specifically lists NSAID drugs and stress.  Why D wrong: Beta-blockers are not cited."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What does the abbreviation ERCP stand for?","options":["Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography","Endoscopic Renal Catheter Procedure","Esophageal Reflux Control Procedure","Enteric Reabsorption of Colonic Polyps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines ERCP as Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of ERCP."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"ERCP is primarily used for what purpose?","options":["Examination and visualisation of hepatobiliary system / biliary tract obstruction (gallstones, tumours, strictures)","Visualisation of the colon only","Measurement of blood glucose","Detection of H. pylori"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ERCP is an examination and visualisation procedure of the hepatobiliary system used to detect biliary tract obstruction/strictures and related pathology.  Why B wrong: Colon visualisation is colonoscopy.  Why C wrong: Blood glucose is not measured by ERCP.  Why D wrong: H. pylori detection is via the urea breath test."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"During ERCP, which position is the patient placed in?","options":["Supine","Left lateral position","Prone position then changed to a left lateral","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patient is in the prone position and later changed to a left lateral position during ERCP, with sedatives administered.  Why A/B/D wrong: These positions alone do not match the described prone-to-left-lateral sequence."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What does the abbreviation PTC stand for in GI diagnostic procedures?","options":["Percutaneous Transhepatic Cholangiography","Peritoneal Tissue Culture","Portal Transverse Catheterization","Pancreatic Tumour Classification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines PTC as Percutaneous Transhepatic Cholangiography.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of PTC."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the main purpose of PTC (Percutaneous Transhepatic Cholangiography)?","options":["Examination of the hepatic biliary system including the bile duct, capillaries, and the gallbladder with injection of a dye into the biliary tree","Visualisation of the colon","Measurement of pancreatic enzymes","Detection of H. pylori"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states PTC is the examination of the hepatic biliary system, including the bile duct and gallbladder, with the injection of dye.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other procedures, not PTC."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"During PTC, where is the contrast dye injected?","options":["Into a peripheral vein","Via the right hepatic biliary tract / liver","Into the duodenum orally","Into the rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client (during PTC) is positioned and the dye is injected via the right hepatic biliary tract (through the liver percutaneously).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the percutaneous transhepatic route described."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Endoscopy is best defined as:","options":["Examination of the colon only","Esophagogastroduodenoscopy — visualisation of the esophagus, stomach and duodenum","Examination of the urinary bladder","A blood test for liver function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines endoscopy as esophagogastroduodenoscopy, the visualisation of the esophagus, stomach and duodenum using a flexible lighted tube (endoscope).  Why A wrong: Colon-only examination is colonoscopy.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to endoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What are the several types of internal bleeding that endoscopy can detect? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Esophageal varices (lower esophageal vein/dilated varicose vein due to portal hypertension)","Esophagitis (inflammation of esophagus, stomach and intestinal lining)","ERCP for pancreas visualisation","Gastritis","Detection of H. pylori"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists esophageal varices (dilated lower esophageal veins, often due to portal hypertension) and esophagitis as conditions endoscopy detects for several causes of internal bleeding.  Why C wrong: ERCP is a separate procedure, not a type of bleeding detected by endoscopy.  Why D wrong: Gastritis is a related condition but is not listed here as one of the named bleeding types.  Why E wrong: H. pylori is detected by the urea breath test, not classed as a bleeding type."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which is a pre-procedure nursing care point for an endoscopy/GI scope procedure?","options":["Encourage the patient to eat a heavy meal","Keep the client NPO (nil per oral) for 6 to 12 hours / take or ensure informed consent is obtained","Give the patient oral fluids immediately before","Place the patient in a supine position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pre-procedure nursing care includes keeping the client NPO for several hours and ensuring informed consent is obtained.  Why A/C wrong: The patient must be NPO, not fed, before the procedure.  Why D wrong: Positioning varies by procedure; NPO and consent are the key pre-procedure points."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which is an essential post-procedure nursing care point following an upper GI endoscopy?","options":["Allow oral fluids immediately","Keep the client NPO until the gag reflex returns","Encourage immediate ambulation and a heavy meal","Remove all monitoring equipment at once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states post-procedure nursing care includes keeping the client NPO until the gag reflex returns (to prevent aspiration), monitoring vital signs and observing for complications such as bleeding and perforation.  Why A wrong: Oral fluids must wait until the gag reflex returns.  Why C wrong: A heavy meal is contraindicated immediately post-procedure.  Why D wrong: Monitoring should continue post-procedure."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Following an endoscopic biopsy or fiberoptic colonoscopy, the nurse should monitor for which serious complication?","options":["Bowel perforation and peritonitis","Hyperglycaemia","Hypertension","Urinary retention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nurse should monitor for signs of bowel perforation and peritonitis after the procedure, as bleeding may occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited serious complications of endoscopic/colonoscopic biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Metastasis is best defined as:","options":["The breakdown of small molecules into large ones","The break-down or spread of cancer cells from the primary site to a distant secondary site / spread of large molecules into small molecules","The inflammation of the stomach lining","The thickening of the gastric mucosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (\"Also Remember\") defines metastasis as the breakdown/spread process involving spread of disease (large molecules into smaller, cancer spreading from one site to distant sites).  Why A wrong: This reverses the molecular breakdown concept.  Why C/D wrong: These describe gastritis, not metastasis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Anabolism refers to:","options":["Breakdown of large molecules into smaller molecules","Synthesis of large molecules from small molecules","Spread of cancer cells","Inflammation of the gastric mucosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines anabolism as the synthesis (building up) of large molecules from small molecules.  Why A wrong: That describes catabolism.  Why C wrong: That describes metastasis.  Why D wrong: That describes gastritis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Catabolism refers to:","options":["Synthesis of large molecules from small molecules","Break-down of large molecules into small molecules and the release of chemical energy","The spread of cancer cells","Storage of glycogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines catabolism as the breakdown of large molecules into small molecules with release of chemical energy.  Why A wrong: That describes anabolism.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the catabolism definition."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Gastritis is best defined as:","options":["Ulceration of the duodenum","Inflammation of the stomach or gastric mucosa","Inflammation of the appendix","Obstruction of the bile duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines gastritis as inflammation of the stomach or gastric mucosa.  Why A wrong: Duodenal ulceration describes peptic/duodenal ulcer.  Why C wrong: Appendix inflammation is appendicitis.  Why D wrong: Bile duct obstruction is unrelated to gastritis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Acute gastritis is caused by which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ingestion of contaminated and spicy foods","Overuse of aspirin, NSAIDs and excessive alcohol intake or bile reflux","Helicobacter pylori infection","Bile reflux","Adequate hydration with water only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute gastritis is caused by ingestion of contaminated and spicy foods, overuse of aspirin/NSAIDs, excessive alcohol intake, bile reflux, and Helicobacter pylori infection.  Why E wrong: Adequate hydration with water is not a cause of acute gastritis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Chronic gastritis develops over what time frame and is most commonly caused by which organism?","options":["Hours; caused by E. coli","Weeks to months; most common cause is Helicobacter pylori","Minutes; caused by Staphylococcus","Years; caused by Candida only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chronic gastritis develops over weeks to months, with the most common cause being Helicobacter pylori, which can lead to benign or malignant ulcers.  Why A/C/D wrong: These time frames and organisms do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with chronic gastritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Anorexia","Nausea and vomiting","Heartburn after eating","Sore taste in the mouth","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists anorexia, nausea and vomiting, heartburn after eating, sore taste in the mouth, and vitamin B12 deficiency as signs and symptoms of chronic gastritis.  Why no option is excluded: All five listed are stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which symptoms are associated with acute gastritis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Abdominal discomfort","Anorexia","Nausea and vomiting","Hiccupping","Reflex"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists abdominal discomfort, anorexia, nausea and vomiting, hiccupping, and reflux/reflex as signs and symptoms of acute gastritis.  Why no option is excluded: All listed are stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"In the nursing management of acute gastritis, what should the nurse do first regarding intake?","options":["Encourage spicy foods","Teach to check and avoid all irritating substances such as spicy foods, alcohol, caffeine, and gas-producing foods","Give large meals immediately","Restrict all fluids permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nurse should teach the client to check and avoid all irritating substances such as spicy foods, alcohol, caffeine, and gas-producing foods.  Why A wrong: Spicy foods worsen gastritis.  Why C wrong: Small, bland feedings are preferred during the acute phase, not large meals.  Why D wrong: Fluids are needed to give small bland feedings during the active phase."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"During the active phase of gastritis, what type of feedings should be given to rest the stomach?","options":["Large fatty meals","Small bland feedings","High-caffeine drinks","No oral intake permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nurse should give small, bland feedings during the active phase to rest the stomach.  Why A/C wrong: Large fatty meals and caffeine irritate the gastric mucosa.  Why D wrong: NPO is not maintained permanently; small bland feedings are introduced."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"An H2 receptor blocker such as ranitidine or cimetidine is administered in gastritis primarily to:","options":["Increase gastric secretion","Reduce gastric secretion","Neutralize bowel motility","Increase appetite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states H2 receptor blocker drugs such as ranitidine and cimetidine are given to reduce gastric secretion.  Why A wrong: They reduce, not increase, secretion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not their stated actions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Peptic ulcer is best defined as:","options":["Inflammation of the appendix","A disease of the stomach, with ulceration in the mucosal wall of the stomach, pylorus, duodenum, and portion of esophagus due to gastric acid secretion that has a low pH","A benign tumour of the liver","Obstruction of the cystic duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines peptic ulcer as a disease of the stomach with ulceration in the mucosal wall of the stomach, pylorus, duodenum, and portion of esophagus due to low-pH gastric acid secretion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other conditions, not peptic ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Peptic ulcer disease includes which of the following types? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Esophageal ulcer","Gastric ulcer","Duodenal ulcer","Appendiceal ulcer","Renal ulcer"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states peptic ulcer includes oesophageal ulcer, gastric ulcer and duodenal ulcer (according to location).  Why D/E wrong: Appendiceal and renal ulcers are not classified under peptic ulcer disease."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Abdominal pain is the most common clinical feature of peptic ulcer disease. Most common complication of peptic ulcer disease is:","options":["Lower GI bleeding","Upper GI bleeding (malena/haematemesis)","Constipation","Appendicitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common complication of peptic ulcer disease is upper GI bleeding (malena).  Why A wrong: It is upper, not lower, GI bleeding.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the cited most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A gastric ulcer occurs in which layer of the stomach?","options":["The serosa","The mucosal layer/lining that extends to the submucosal level of the stomach","The duodenal cap","The esophageal mucosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the gastric ulcer is also known as a stomach ulcer and is an ulcer of the mucosal lining that extends to the submucosal level of the stomach.  Why A wrong: It is in the mucosa/submucosa, not the serosa.  Why C wrong: The duodenal cap is the site of duodenal ulcer.  Why D wrong: The esophageal mucosa is the site of esophageal ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A duodenal ulcer (duodenum ulcer) is an ulcer in the mucosa of which structure?","options":["The stomach","The duodenum","The esophagus","The colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a duodenal ulcer (duodenum ulcer) is an ulcer in the mucosa of the duodenum.  Why A wrong: A stomach mucosa ulcer is a gastric ulcer.  Why C/D wrong: The esophagus and colon are not the duodenal ulcer site."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which of the following are causes/risk factors of gastric ulcer? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting, heartburn or pain","Gnawing pain like a burning epigastric pain","Stress, smoking, anxiety","Salicylate (aspirin/NSAID)-producing foods","Adequate sleep and exercise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists causes/clinical manifestations and risk factors of gastric ulcer including nausea/vomiting, heartburn or pain, gnawing burning epigastric pain in the left side, stress/smoking/anxiety and salicylate (aspirin) producing-food/NSAID use.  Why E wrong: Adequate sleep and exercise are not listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which are causes/risk factors of a duodenal ulcer per the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Helicobacter pylori infection (most common)","The use of aspirin and NSAIDs","Smoking and alcohol intake","Adequate fluid intake"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Helicobacter pylori infection (most common), the use of aspirin and NSAIDs, and smoking and alcohol as causes/risk factors of duodenal ulcer.  Why D wrong: Adequate fluid intake is not a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is a major complication of duodenal ulcer related to iron deficiency?","options":["Bleeding due to poor absorption of iron / iron deficiency because absorption of iron occurs at the duodenum","Excess iron storage","Increased calcium absorption","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a complication is bleeding and iron-deficiency because absorption of iron occurs at the duodenum (the ulcer site impairs it).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited complications of duodenal ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which are complications of peptic ulcer disease? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bleeding","Gastric outlet obstruction","Perforation","Improved digestion"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bleeding, gastric outlet obstruction and perforation as complications of peptic ulcer disease.  Why D wrong: Improved digestion is not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A patient with a duodenal ulcer classically reports pain at what time relative to meals?","options":["Immediately while eating","Burning pain occurs in mid epigastric area 2 to 4 hours after meals during the night, often relieved by ingestion of food and antacids","Pain only with an empty stomach in the morning that never resolves","No pain at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states duodenal ulcer pain is a burning pain in the mid/epigastric area 2 to 4 hours after meals (and at night), relieved by ingestion of food and antacids — the classic \"hunger pain.\"  Why A wrong: Duodenal pain occurs hours after eating, not during eating.  Why C wrong: The pain is relieved by food/antacids, contradicting \"never resolves.\"  Why D wrong: Pain is a hallmark feature."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) such as pantoprazole and omeprazole are used in peptic ulcer disease to:","options":["Increase gastric acid secretion","Reduce gastric acid secretion","Stimulate vagal nerve activity","Promote bile production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states proton pump inhibitors (pantoprazole, omeprazole) are used to reduce gastric acid secretion.  Why A wrong: They reduce, not increase, acid.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated actions of PPIs."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Antacids containing aluminium and magnesium are given in peptic ulcer disease to:","options":["Increase gastric secretion","Neutralize gastric acid secretions","Promote vomiting","Increase bile flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states antacids containing aluminium and magnesium are given to neutralize gastric acid secretions.  Why A wrong: They neutralize, not increase, acid.  Why C/D wrong: These are not their actions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Patient education for peptic ulcer disease should include giving antacids how long after meals?","options":["With food","1 hour before or 2 hours after meals","Immediately before sleep only","Every 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to give antacids 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals.  Why A wrong: The source specifies a timing relative to (not with) meals.  Why C/D wrong: These timings are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Patient education to prevent peptic ulcer recurrence should advise avoiding which lifestyle factors? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Smoking","Aspirin and NSAIDs","Stress","Adequate rest"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises avoiding aspirin and NSAIDs, smoking, and stress, while taking adequate rest, to reduce recurrence.  Why D wrong: Adequate rest is recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A patient with peptic ulcer disease has active bleeding/haematemesis (malena). What is an appropriate special nursing care measure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check vitals closely","Monitor for hypovolemia and dehydration such as hypotension and tachycardia","Keep NPO and give IV fluids","Give blood transfusion and other medicines as per the doctor's order","Give iron supplements if iron depletion is noted, and initiate general nursing care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists checking vitals closely, monitoring for hypovolemia and dehydration (hypotension and tachycardia), keeping NPO and giving IV fluids, giving blood transfusion and other medicines per the doctor's order, and giving iron supplements if iron depletion is noted while initiating general nursing care.  Why no option is excluded: All five are stated in the source as special nursing care during active bleeding/haematemesis/malena."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which feature regarding incidence differentiates gastric ulcer from duodenal ulcer?","options":["Gastric ulcer is more common; duodenal ulcer is less common","Gastric ulcer is less common (blood group A is a risk factor); duodenal ulcer is more common (blood group O is a risk factor)","Both are equally common with no blood group association","Gastric ulcer is associated with blood group O; duodenal with blood group A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table states gastric ulcer is less common and associated with blood group A as a risk factor, while duodenal ulcer is more common and associated with blood group O as a risk factor.  Why A wrong: It reverses the relative incidence.  Why C wrong: There is a blood group association.  Why D wrong: It swaps the blood group associations."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"What is the common location of a gastric ulcer versus a duodenal ulcer?","options":["Gastric: duodenal cap; Duodenal: lesser curvature","Gastric: antrum and lesser curvature; Duodenal: anterior wall, first part of the duodenum (duodenal cap)","Both occur in the esophagus","Gastric: pylorus only; Duodenal: jejunum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table lists the common location of gastric ulcer as the antrum and lesser curvature, and of duodenal ulcer as the anterior wall, first part of the duodenum (duodenal cap).  Why A wrong: It swaps the locations.  Why C/D wrong: These locations do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Regarding age group, the comparison table states gastric ulcer occurs most commonly in:","options":["Children","Middle age","Newborns","Adolescents only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the age group most common for gastric ulcer is middle age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These age groups do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"According to the comparison table, the level of gastric acid secretion in gastric ulcer versus duodenal ulcer is:","options":["Gastric: mostly increased; Duodenal: mostly normal","Gastric: mostly normal; Duodenal: mostly increased","Both mostly increased","Both mostly decreased"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the level of gastric acid secretion is mostly normal in gastric ulcer and mostly increased in duodenal ulcer.  Why A wrong: It reverses the values.  Why C/D wrong: They do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"According to the comparison table, malignancy risk in gastric ulcer versus duodenal ulcer is:","options":["Gastric: rare; Duodenal: common","Gastric: common; Duodenal: rare","Both common","Both rare"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states malignancy is common in gastric ulcer and rare in duodenal ulcer.  Why A wrong: It reverses the risk.  Why C/D wrong: They do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"How does pain differ between gastric and duodenal ulcer according to the comparison table?","options":["Gastric: pain felt when the stomach is full and may develop within 30 minutes of intake of food, causing weight loss and avoiding food; Duodenal: develops 2–3 hours after food intake, perceived as hunger pain","Gastric: hunger pain at night; Duodenal: pain with full stomach","Both have identical pain patterns","Neither type causes pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states gastric ulcer pain is felt when the stomach is full (develops within ~30 minutes of intake of food), leading to food avoidance and weight loss, whereas duodenal ulcer pain develops 2–3 hours after food intake and is perceived as hunger pain.  Why B wrong: It swaps the two pain patterns.  Why C/D wrong: The patterns differ and pain is present."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"According to the comparison table, how does haemorrhage typically present in gastric versus duodenal ulcer?","options":["Gastric: blood in stool (melena); Duodenal: haematemesis (blood in vomiting)","Gastric: haematemesis (blood in vomiting); Duodenal: malena (blood in stool)","Both present only with haematemesis","Neither causes haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states gastric ulcer haemorrhage presents as haematemesis (blood in vomiting), whereas duodenal ulcer haemorrhage presents as malena (blood in stool).  Why A wrong: It swaps the presentations.  Why C/D wrong: They do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A total gastrectomy is best described as:","options":["A vagus nerve resection","A surgical procedure which involves removal of the total stomach with attachment of the esophagus to the jejunum or duodenum","Removal of the gallbladder","Removal of half the stomach only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states total gastrectomy is a surgical procedure involving removal of the total stomach with attachment of the esophagus to the jejunum (or duodenum).  Why A wrong: That is vagotomy.  Why C wrong: That is cholecystectomy.  Why D wrong: Partial removal is a gastric resection/subtotal procedure, not total gastrectomy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"A total gastrectomy with attachment of the esophagus to the jejunum is also known as:","options":["Gastrojejunostomy","Esophagojejunostomy / Esophagoduodenostomy","Pyloroplasty","Vagotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states total gastrectomy attaching the esophagus to the jejunum is also known as esophagojejunostomy (or esophagoduodenostomy).  Why A wrong: Gastrojejunostomy joins the stomach to the jejunum, not the esophagus.  Why C wrong: Pyloroplasty enlarges the pylorus.  Why D wrong: Vagotomy is vagus nerve resection."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Vagotomy is a surgical procedure that involves:","options":["Removal of the whole stomach","Cutting the vagus nerve that innervates/supplies to the stomach to reduce acid secretion by reducing vagus nerve stimulation","Removal of the gallbladder","Enlargement of the pyloric portion of the stomach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states vagotomy involves cutting the vagus nerve that innervates the stomach; acid secretion is reduced because vagal stimulation is reduced.  Why A wrong: That is gastrectomy.  Why C wrong: That is cholecystectomy.  Why D wrong: That is pyloroplasty."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Gastric resection is best described as:","options":["Removal of the gallbladder","Removal of the lower portion of the lower half or the stomach or antral portion of the stomach","Cutting of the vagus nerve","Enlargement of the pylorus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states gastric resection is the removal of the lower portion (lower half) or antral portion of the stomach; it is usually done with vagotomy.  Why A wrong: That is cholecystectomy.  Why C wrong: That is vagotomy.  Why D wrong: That is pyloroplasty."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The Billroth-1 procedure involves:","options":["Removal of the lower portion of the stomach and attachment of/anastomosis of the remaining portion to the duodenum (gastroduodenostomy)","Attachment of the remaining stomach to the jejunum","Removal of the gallbladder","Cutting of the vagus nerve only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Billroth-1 is the removal of the lower portion of the stomach and attachment/anastomosis of the remaining portion to the duodenum, also known as gastroduodenostomy.  Why B wrong: Attachment to the jejunum describes Billroth-2.  Why C wrong: That is cholecystectomy.  Why D wrong: That is vagotomy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"The Billroth-2 procedure is also known as:","options":["Gastroduodenostomy","Gastrojejunostomy — removal of the antral/distal portion of the stomach with anastomosis of the remaining section to the jejunum","Pyloroplasty","Esophagojejunostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Billroth-2 is the removal of the antral/distal portion of the stomach with anastomosis of the remaining section to the jejunum, known as gastrojejunostomy.  Why A wrong: Gastroduodenostomy is Billroth-1.  Why C wrong: Pyloroplasty enlarges the pylorus.  Why D wrong: Esophagojejunostomy follows total gastrectomy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Pyloroplasty is best defined as:","options":["Removal of the whole stomach","The surgical enlargement of the pyloric portion of the stomach to prevent or treat pyloric obstruction","Cutting of the vagus nerve","Removal of the gallbladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pyloroplasty is the surgical enlargement of the pyloric portion of the stomach to prevent (or treat) pyloric obstruction.  Why A wrong: That is gastrectomy.  Why C wrong: That is vagotomy.  Why D wrong: That is cholecystectomy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which are post-operative nursing care points following gastric surgery? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor vitals and provide Fowler's position or comfort to promote drainage","Observe colour and amount of nasogastric drainage; report excessive or bright red colour bleeding (which is initially abnormal)","Monitor for post-operative complications such as haemorrhage, perforation, dumping syndrome, diarrhoea and vitamin B12 deficiency","Initiate general nursing care like NPO, coughing and early ambulation to prevent pulmonary complications","Encourage immediate heavy oral feeding on the first post-op day"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring vitals and Fowler's position to promote drainage, observing colour/amount of nasogastric drainage and reporting excessive or bright-red bleeding (initially abnormal), monitoring for complications (haemorrhage, perforation, dumping syndrome, diarrhoea, vitamin B12 deficiency), and general nursing care (NPO, coughing, early ambulation to prevent pulmonary complications).  Why E wrong: Immediate heavy oral feeding is not advised; the patient is kept NPO with gradual progression."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Following gastric surgery, the appearance of bright red colour bleeding in the nasogastric drainage is significant because it is:","options":["Always normal and requires no action","Initially abnormal and should be reported, as it may indicate haemorrhage","A sign of normal healing on the third post-op day","An expected finding requiring increased oral intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the nurse to observe colour and report excessive or bright red colour bleeding, which is initially abnormal (suggesting haemorrhage) and should be reported.  Why A wrong: It is not normal initially.  Why C wrong: Bright red bleeding is not a normal healing sign.  Why D wrong: It requires reporting, not increased oral intake."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Why is monitoring for vitamin B12 deficiency important after gastric surgery?","options":["Because the stomach produces vitamin B12 directly","Because gastric surgery (removal of stomach portions) can lead to loss of intrinsic factor and impaired vitamin B12 absorption, listed as a post-operative complication","Because vitamin B12 is absorbed only in the colon","Because B12 deficiency causes pyloric obstruction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists vitamin B12 deficiency as a post-operative complication of gastric surgery; removal of stomach tissue impairs intrinsic factor production needed for B12 absorption.  Why A wrong: The stomach does not produce B12; it provides intrinsic factor for its absorption.  Why C wrong: B12 is absorbed in the terminal ileum, not the colon.  Why D wrong: B12 deficiency does not cause pyloric obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 034 · lower GI anatomy (caecum, appendix, colon, rectum)…","question":"Which post-operative complication of gastric surgery involves rapid gastric emptying causing symptoms after meals?","options":["Dumping syndrome","Pyloric stenosis","Appendicitis","Cholelithiasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dumping syndrome among the post-operative complications of gastric surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pyloric stenosis, appendicitis, and cholelithiasis are not the listed rapid-gastric-emptying complication."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A nurse is reviewing risk factors for peptic ulcer disease. Which beverage is MOST prone to causing peptic ulcer disease?","options":["Plain water","Blood-group O blood (i.e., individuals with blood group O)","Fruit juice","Buttermilk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blood-group O is most prone to develop peptic ulcer disease.  Why A wrong: Plain water is not listed as a peptic ulcer risk factor.  Why C wrong: Fruit juice is not identified as a peptic ulcer risk factor here.  Why D wrong: Buttermilk is not listed as a peptic ulcer risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the underlying mechanism by which a gastric ulcer causes pain (gastric pain)?","options":["Activation of vagus nerve due to increased intracranial pressure","Direct chemical burn from bile reflux","Gastric ulcer pain is due to gastric ulcer ischemia","Spasm of the lower esophageal sphincter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states gastric pain is caused by gastric ulcer ischemia.  Why A wrong: Vagal activation/ICP relates to vomiting, not the mechanism of gastric ulcer pain.  Why B wrong: Bile reflux burn is not the stated cause of gastric ulcer pain.  Why D wrong: LES spasm is not described as the cause of gastric ulcer pain."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Following which event does stomach ulcer cause severe vomiting by activation of the vagus nerve?","options":["After an increase in intracranial pressure (ICP)","After ingestion of cold fluids","After exposure to sunlight","After mild exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stomach ulcer causes vomiting by activation of the vagus nerve when ICP is raised.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cold fluids, sunlight, and exercise are not listed as triggers for this vagus-mediated vomiting."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A patient with gastric ulcer develops gastric pain due to stress. This is BEST explained by which mechanism?","options":["Decreased gastric acid production","Necrosis of the inner mucosa of the stomach due to stress","Healing of the gastric mucosa","Excess mucus production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states gastric ulcer pain when gastric ulcer is due to stress results from necrosis of the inner mucosa of the stomach.  Why A/C/D wrong: Decreased acid, healing, and excess mucus are not the described stress-related ulcer mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"GERD stands for which condition?","options":["Gastric Erosion Reflux Disease","Gastro Esophageal Reflux Disease","General Esophageal Regurgitation Disorder","Gastric Esophageal Rupture Disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GERD stands for Gastro Esophageal Reflux Disease.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In GERD, the backflow of which content into the esophagus occurs?","options":["Pancreatic enzymes only","Gastric and duodenal content","Bile from the gallbladder only","Air swallowed during eating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GERD is a backflow of gastric and duodenal content into the esophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: The defined refluxate is gastric and duodenal content, not isolated pancreatic enzymes, gallbladder bile alone, or swallowed air."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Barrett's Esophagus is BEST described as which of the following?","options":["A benign condition with no malignant potential","A pathologic condition in which gradually breaking down the esophageal mucosa occurs, known as a precursor of esophageal cancer","A normal anatomic variant of the esophagus","An acute viral infection of the esophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barrett's Esophagus is a pathologic condition with gradual breakdown of esophageal mucosa, known as a precursor of esophageal cancer.  Why A wrong: It is precancerous, not benign.  Why C wrong: It is pathologic, not a normal variant.  Why D wrong: It is not an acute viral infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following is a complication associated with GERD?","options":["Esophageal stricture, esophageal sphincter, and Barrett's esophagus","Improved esophageal motility","Decreased gastric acid secretion","Spontaneous resolution of hiatal hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Complications listed include esophageal stricture, esophageal sphincter (involvement), and Barrett's esophagus.  Why B/C/D wrong: GERD does not improve motility, reduce acid, or resolve hernia spontaneously; these are not listed complications."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which diagnostic test is performed to assess the severity of GERD?","options":["Barium swallow alone","Johnson-De Meester's Score","Complete blood count","Liver function tests"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Johnson-De Meester's Score is used to assess severity of GERD.  Why A/C/D wrong: Barium swallow, CBC, and LFTs are not the named severity-assessment score for GERD."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"When using the Johnson-De Meester's Score for GERD, a score of more than 100 indicates which severity?","options":["Abnormal","Mild GERD","Moderate GERD","Severe GERD"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page assigns >100 as Severe GERD.  Why A wrong: >14 indicates Abnormal.  Why B wrong: 14–50 indicates Mild GERD.  Why C wrong: 51–100 indicates Moderate GERD."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Match the Johnson-De Meester's Score to its interpretation: a score in the range 51–100 indicates which severity of GERD?","options":["Abnormal","Mild GERD","Moderate GERD","Severe GERD"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 51–100 indicates Moderate GERD.  Why A wrong: >14 = Abnormal.  Why B wrong: 14–50 = Mild GERD.  Why D wrong: >100 = Severe GERD."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which is the MOST common cause of GERD?","options":["Pyloric stenosis","Incompetent lower esophageal sphincter","Motility disorder","High-fat diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incompetent lower esophageal sphincter is the most common cause of GERD.  Why A wrong: Pyloric stenosis is a separate condition, not the most common GERD cause.  Why C/D wrong: Motility disorder and high-fat diet are listed among \"other\" causes, not the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Besides an incompetent lower esophageal sphincter, which of the following are listed as \"other\" causes of GERD? (Select all that apply)","options":["High-fat diet","Obesity","Hiatal hernia","Pernicious anaemia","Improved gastric emptying"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Other causes of GERD include high-fat diet, obesity, and hiatal hernia.  Why D wrong: Pernicious anaemia relates to B12 deficiency, not GERD.  Why E wrong: Improved gastric emptying is not a cause of GERD."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A patient reports a burning sensation that most commonly occurs as the early sign of GERD. This symptom is known as:","options":["Dysphagia","Heartburn (pyrosis)","Hematemesis","Melena"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heartburn, also called acid indigestion or pyrosis, is the most common and early sign of GERD.  Why A wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing, a later sign.  Why C/D wrong: Hematemesis and melena are GI bleeding signs, not the early GERD symptom described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In GERD, \"dysphagia\" refers to which symptom?","options":["Difficulty swallowing","Painful swallowing","Regurgitation of food","Loss of appetite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dysphagia is difficulty with swallowing.  Why B wrong: Painful swallowing is odynophagia (pain on swallowing as a sour taste), listed separately.  Why C wrong: Regurgitation is a separate listed symptom.  Why D wrong: Loss of appetite is not the meaning of dysphagia."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of GERD? (Select all that apply)","options":["Epigastric pain and dyspepsia (indigestion)","Regurgitation and nausea","Odynophagia (pain during swallowing as a sour taste)","Hyper salivation, chronic cough, wheezing and recurrent pneumonia","Polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Epigastric pain/dyspepsia, regurgitation/nausea, odynophagia, and hypersalivation with chronic cough/wheezing/recurrent pneumonia are all listed GERD symptoms.  Why E wrong: Polyuria is not a listed GERD symptom."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A nurse is teaching a GERD patient about dietary triggers to avoid. Which foods/beverages should be avoided? (Select all that apply)","options":["Peppermint, chocolate","Spicy and fatty foods","Carbonated and alcoholic beverages","Plain boiled rice","Citrus drinks"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Patients should avoid irritating factors of the esophagus like peppermint, chocolate, spicy & fatty foods, carbonated & alcoholic beverages, and citrus drinks.  Why D wrong: Plain boiled rice is not listed as an irritating trigger."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A nurse advises a GERD patient on chest pain relief. The nurse should teach the client to:","options":["Lie flat immediately","Drink hot fluids before bedtime","Eat fast to reduce reflux time","Take pain relief medication as prescribed"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing care point directs teaching the client to take pain relief medication as prescribed.  Why A wrong: Lying flat worsens reflux; head of bed should be elevated.  Why B wrong: Hot fluids/irritating drinks before bedtime should be avoided.  Why C wrong: The client should eat small, frequent meals and avoid gastric distension, not eat fast."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"To minimize gastric distension and avoid wearing tight clothing-related reflux, the nurse should advise the GERD patient to:","options":["Eat three large meals daily","Eat small, frequent meals and avoid gastric distension and wearing tight clothing","Lie down immediately after eating","Increase carbonated beverage intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eating small, frequent meals to prevent gastric distension and avoiding tight clothing are listed nursing measures.  Why A wrong: Large meals increase gastric distension.  Why C wrong: Lying down after eating promotes reflux.  Why D wrong: Carbonated beverages are to be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What positioning advice should the nurse give a GERD patient regarding sleep/rest?","options":["Sleep flat without a pillow","Sleep in a prone position","Elevate the head end of the bed or sleep in semi-Fowler to high Fowler position","Sleep on the left side only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should elevate the head end of the bed or sleep in semi-Fowler to high Fowler position to reduce reflux.  Why A/B/D wrong: Flat, prone, or left-side-only positions are not the recommended anti-reflux positions in the source."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which classes of drugs should be avoided or used cautiously in a GERD patient as part of patient teaching?","options":["Anticholinergic, NSAIDs, and aspirin","Probiotics and antacids","H2 receptor blockers and PPIs only","Vitamin supplements"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing care point states to avoid use of anticholinergic, NSAIDs, and aspirin, which delay stomach emptying.  Why B wrong: Probiotics/antacids are not the drugs flagged to avoid.  Why C wrong: H2 receptor blockers and PPIs are therapeutic in GERD, not to be avoided.  Why D wrong: Vitamins are not the flagged drug class."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which drugs are appropriate prescribed therapy for GERD? (Select all that apply)","options":["Antacids","H2 receptor blockers","PPIs (proton pump inhibitors)","Aspirin","NSAIDs"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists antacids, H2 receptor blockers, and PPIs as drugs for GERD.  Why D/E wrong: Aspirin and NSAIDs are to be avoided as they delay stomach emptying."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Dumping Syndrome is defined as which of the following?","options":["Slow gastric emptying causing constipation","Rapid emptying of stomach content into the small intestine","Backflow of bile into the stomach","Obstruction at the pyloric outlet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dumping syndrome is rapid emptying of stomach content into the small intestine.  Why A wrong: It involves rapid, not slow, emptying.  Why C wrong: Bile backflow describes a different process.  Why D wrong: Pyloric outlet obstruction is pyloric stenosis, a different condition."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of dumping syndrome?","options":["Hepatitis infection","Gastric resection","Cholelithiasis","Pernicious anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common cause of dumping syndrome is gastric resection.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hepatitis, cholelithiasis, and pernicious anaemia are not the cause of dumping syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of dumping syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Feeling of abdominal fullness and abdominal cramping","Nausea & vomiting, palpitation, tachycardia","Diarrhea, weakness & dizziness, perspiration","Borborygmi (hyperperistalsis): it is loud gurgle indicating hyper peristalsis","Bradycardia and hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists abdominal fullness/cramping, nausea/vomiting/palpitation/tachycardia, diarrhea/weakness/dizziness/perspiration, and borborygmi (loud gurgle indicating hyperperistalsis).  Why E wrong: Tachycardia (not bradycardia) is listed; hypertension is not a stated symptom."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What does \"borborygmi\" indicate in dumping syndrome?","options":["A loud gurgle indicating hyper peristalsis","Absence of bowel sounds","Gallbladder inflammation","Esophageal obstruction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Borborygmi is a loud gurgle indicating hyper peristalsis.  Why B wrong: It indicates increased, not absent, motility.  Why C/D wrong: It is not a gallbladder or esophageal sign."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which dietary precaution should the nurse teach a patient with dumping syndrome?","options":["Avoid salt, sugar & milk, and eat smaller meals at more frequent intervals","Increase fluid intake during meals","Eat three large high-carbohydrate meals daily","Lie flat immediately after eating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Precautions include avoiding salt, sugar & milk and eating smaller meals at more frequent intervals.  Why B wrong: Fluids during meals worsen rapid emptying.  Why C wrong: Recommended diet is high protein, high fat, but low carbohydrate.  Why D wrong: Supine after meal helps, but flat-immediately is described differently (supine to prevent rapid emptying)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the recommended diet for a patient with dumping syndrome?","options":["High protein, high fat, but low carbohydrate diet","High carbohydrate, low protein diet","High fluid with meals","Low protein, low fat diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Recommended diet is high protein, high fat, but low carbohydrate.  Why B wrong: Carbohydrate is to be low, not high.  Why C wrong: Fluids with meals are discouraged.  Why D wrong: Protein and fat are kept high, not low."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which position should a dumping syndrome patient assume after a meal to prevent rapid emptying of stomach content?","options":["High Fowler position","Supine position after meal to prevent rapid emptying of stomach content","Prone position","Standing upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Supine position after a meal is recommended to prevent rapid emptying or reclining position.  Why A/C/D wrong: High Fowler, prone, and standing are not the recommended post-meal positions for dumping syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Vitamin B12 is also known by which name(s)?","options":["Cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12 or Cyanocobalamin)","Ascorbic acid","Thiamine","Folic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Vitamin B12 / Cyanocobalamin.  Why B/C/D wrong: Ascorbic acid (C), thiamine (B1), and folic acid are different vitamins."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to which condition known as \"pernicious anaemia,\" characterized by a deficiency of which factor?","options":["Deficiency of intrinsic factor","Deficiency of iron","Deficiency of folic acid","Deficiency of vitamin C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: B12 deficiency causes pernicious anaemia, a deficiency of intrinsic factor.  Why B wrong: Iron deficiency causes a different anaemia.  Why C wrong: Folate deficiency is megaloblastic but not the defining intrinsic-factor problem here.  Why D wrong: Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, not pernicious anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the role of intrinsic factor with respect to vitamin B12?","options":["Intrinsic factor is necessary for intestinal absorption of vitamin B12","Intrinsic factor destroys vitamin B12 in the stomach","Intrinsic factor converts B12 to folic acid","Intrinsic factor is produced in the kidneys"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intrinsic factor is necessary for intestinal absorption of vitamin B12.  Why B/C/D wrong: It does not destroy B12, convert it to folate, nor is it produced in the kidneys."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are causes of vitamin B12 deficiency? (Select all that apply)","options":["Insufficient intake of vitamin B12","Lack of absorption of vitamin B12, from the intestinal tract","Gastric disease or after gastric surgery","Excessive sun exposure","Excess dietary iron"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Insufficient intake, lack of absorption from the intestinal tract, and gastric disease/after gastric surgery are listed causes.  Why D/E wrong: Sun exposure and dietary iron are not listed as B12 deficiency causes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of vitamin B12 deficiency? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe pallor, fatigue, weight loss","Smooth red tongue","Numbness, paresthesia of the hands and feet","Problems with gait and balance","Increased appetite and weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists severe pallor/fatigue/weight loss, smooth red tongue, numbness/paresthesia of hands and feet, and problems with gait and balance.  Why E wrong: Weight loss (not gain) is listed; increased appetite is not a stated symptom."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"When dietary B12 deficiency occurs, dietary intake of which foods rich in vitamin B12 should be increased?","options":["Citrus fruits, green leafy vegetables, and nuts","Organ meats & dried beans","White rice and pasta","Refined sugar and salt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing care for dietary B12 deficiency advises increasing intake of foods rich in vitamin B12 such as citrus fruits, green leafy vegetables, nuts, organ meats and dried beans (per the source list).  Why C/D wrong: White rice/pasta and refined sugar/salt are not the B12-rich foods listed.  Note: The source lists citrus fruits, green leafy vegetables, nuts, organ meats & dried beans together as foods to increase; organ meats & dried beans (option B) are the clearest dietary sources of B12, as citrus/leafy greens are actually higher in folate — keyed per source listing while noting this nutritional nuance."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"When vitamin B12 deficiency is due to a lack of absorption (e.g., after gastric surgery), how is vitamin B12 typically administered?","options":["Oral tablets only","Injections, initially weekly then monthly for maintenance","Topical cream","Single one-time IV dose only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When B12 deficiency is due to surgery or absorption issues, injections are given initially weekly then monthly for maintenance.  Why A wrong: Oral route is ineffective without intrinsic factor / absorption.  Why C/D wrong: Topical and a single one-time IV dose are not the described regimen."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Pyloric stenosis is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["Inflammation of the esophagus","Narrowing of the pyloric outlet of pylorus due to result of hypertrophy or hyperplasia of the muscular layer of the pylorus","Rapid emptying of stomach into the small intestine","Inflammation of the gallbladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyloric stenosis is narrowing of the pyloric outlet by pylorus obstruction from hypertrophy or hyperplasia of the muscular layer of the pylorus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe esophagitis, dumping syndrome, and cholecystitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A classic sign of pyloric stenosis in which the gastric content is forcefully expelled is:","options":["Bilious projectile vomiting","Non-bilious projectile vomiting (cardinal sign)","Coffee-ground emesis","Hematochezia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-bilious projectile vomiting is the cardinal sign of pyloric stenosis.  Why A wrong: Vomiting is non-bilious because the obstruction is at the pylorus, proximal to the bile entry.  Why C/D wrong: Coffee-ground emesis and hematochezia are not the cardinal sign described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of pyloric stenosis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Olive-like mass on palpation of right upper quadrant of the abdomen","String sign seen in X-ray","Dehydration & electrolyte imbalances","Left to right peristalsis movement across the abdomen","Painless bright red rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Olive-like mass in RUQ, string sign on X-ray, dehydration & electrolyte imbalances, and left to right peristalsis movement across the abdomen are all listed signs.  Why E wrong: Bright red rectal bleeding is not a feature of pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the surgical management of choice for pyloric stenosis?","options":["Pyloromyotomy — the repairing procedure of the pylorus, also known as Ramstedt's procedure","Cholecystectomy","Whipple's procedure","Fundoplication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pyloromyotomy, the repairing procedure of the pylorus, also known as Ramstedt's procedure, is the surgery for pyloric stenosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cholecystectomy (gallbladder), Whipple's (pancreas), and fundoplication (GERD) treat other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Acute pancreatitis is defined as:","options":["Suddenly inflammation of the pancreas","Chronic narrowing of the pancreatic duct","Inflammation of the gallbladder","Obstruction of the pylorus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute pancreatitis is the suddenly inflammation of the pancreas.  Why B wrong: Long-time inflammation of pancreas lead to chronic pancreatitis, which is distinct.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe cholecystitis and pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the primary mechanism underlying pancreatitis according to the source?","options":["Auto-digestion by pancreatic enzymes such as primarily trypsin","Bacterial invasion of pancreatic tissue","Viral destruction of islet cells","Pancreatic ischemia from hypotension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pancreatitis involves auto-digestion by pancreatic enzymes such as primarily trypsin.  Why B/C/D wrong: Bacterial invasion, viral islet destruction, and ischemia are not the described primary mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Biliary pancreatitis occurs due to constriction of which structure?","options":["Ampulla of Vater (the most common cause)","Pyloric sphincter","Lower esophageal sphincter","Ileocecal valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Biliary pancreatitis is due to constriction of ampulla of Vater and is the most common cause.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sphincters/valves are not the site of biliary pancreatitis obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes and risk factors of acute pancreatitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Alcohol & metabolic disorders","Post-ERCP, abdominal trauma","Penetrating ulcers","Cancer of the head of pancreas, and other cancer","Adequate dietary fiber intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol & metabolic disorders, post-ERCP/abdominal trauma, penetrating ulcers, and cancer of the head of pancreas and other cancer are listed causes/risk factors.  Why E wrong: Adequate dietary fiber is not a risk factor for pancreatitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Where is the abrupt onset of aching, burning, stabbing, or pressing central epigastric severe pain located, and to where may it radiate, in acute pancreatitis?","options":["It may radiate to the shoulder, chest and back","It radiates only to the right groin","It is localized to the suprapubic region with no radiation","It radiates to the left calf"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The central epigastric pain may radiate to shoulder, chest and back, and is worsened by lying flat.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies radiation to shoulder, chest and back, not groin, suprapubic, or calf."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of acute pancreatitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nausea, pruritus associated with jaundice","Steatorrhea & weight loss due to mal-absorption of nutrients","Vomiting & weight loss","Board-like abdomen","Bradycardia with hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nausea with pruritus/jaundice, steatorrhea & weight loss from malabsorption, vomiting & weight loss, and board-like abdomen are listed signs/symptoms (tachycardia and hypotension are also listed).  Why E wrong: Tachycardia (not bradycardia) and the source lists hypotension; bradycardia with hypothermia is not described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which laboratory changes are expected in acute pancreatitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased serum amylase & lipase level (cardinal sign)","Decreased serum calcium level (hypocalcemia)","Elevated serum amylase only with normal lipase","Hyperkalemia as the cardinal sign","Decreased liver enzymes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Grossly increased serum amylase & lipase level (cardinal sign) and decreased serum calcium level (hypocalcemia) are listed lab findings.  Why C wrong: Both amylase AND lipase are increased.  Why D wrong: Hyperkalemia is not the cardinal sign; the amylase/lipase rise is.  Why E wrong: Decreased liver enzymes are not described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Cullen's sign in acute pancreatitis refers to:","options":["Discolouration around the peri-umbilical area","Discolouration of the flank area","Blanching of the palms","Yellowing of the sclera"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cullen's sign is discolouration around the peri-umbilical area.  Why B wrong: Flank discolouration is Turner's sign (Grey Turner's sign).  Why C/D wrong: These describe other findings, not Cullen's sign."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Turner's sign (Grey Turner's sign) in acute pancreatitis is:","options":["Discolouration of the near flank area","Peri-umbilical discolouration","A palpable olive-shaped mass","A loud abdominal gurgle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Turner's sign is discolouration of the near flank area.  Why B wrong: Peri-umbilical discolouration is Cullen's sign.  Why C/D wrong: These describe pyloric stenosis mass and borborygmi."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"\"Fox's sign\" in acute pancreatitis refers to:","options":["A bruising seen over the inguinal ligament","Retro-peritoneal bleeding due to haemorrhagic pancreatitis","Yellow discolouration of the skin","Peri-umbilical discolouration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fox's sign is a bruising seen over the inguinal ligament due to retro-peritoneal bleeding from haemorrhagic pancreatitis.  Why C wrong: Yellow skin is jaundice.  Why D wrong: Peri-umbilical discolouration is Cullen's sign.  Why A wrong (partial): While Fox's sign is over the inguinal ligament, the complete defining cause is retro-peritoneal bleeding due to haemorrhagic pancreatitis (B is the most complete answer)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A nurse manages a patient with acute pancreatitis. Which interventions are appropriate? (Select all that apply)","options":["Keep NPO & care of the NG tube","Give TPN therapy as per order","Observe for hypocalcemia (Chvostek's & Trousseau's sign)","Observe for paralytic ileus such as abdominal distention, nausea & vomiting","Encourage a high-fat, oral diet immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Keeping NPO with NG tube care, giving TPN as ordered, observing for hypocalcemia (Chvostek's & Trousseau's signs), and observing for paralytic ileus (abdominal distention, nausea & vomiting) are all listed nursing care points.  Why E wrong: The patient is kept NPO; an immediate high-fat oral diet is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"During management of acute pancreatitis, the nurse monitors for hyperglycemia. This is monitored due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to glucagon activation","Due to insulin excess","Due to decreased cortisol","Due to increased thyroid hormone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors for hyperglycaemia due to glucagon activation in pancreatitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Insulin excess, decreased cortisol, and thyroid hormone are not the stated cause of hyperglycemia here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What diet therapy is recommended for a patient with acute pancreatitis?","options":["Small & low fat but high in protein & carbohydrate diet","High fat, low protein diet","High fiber, high fat diet","Clear high-sugar liquids only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diet therapy is small & low fat but high in protein & carbohydrate.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fat is kept low, not high; high fiber/fat and high-sugar liquids are not the described therapy."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which position should a patient with acute pancreatitis be placed in for comfort?","options":["Semi-Fowler position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral decubitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client is placed in semi-Fowler position.  Why B/C/D wrong: Prone, Trendelenburg, and left lateral are not the recommended positions in the source."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which interventions promote comfort and recovery in acute pancreatitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Exercise: encourage deep breathing and coughing to prevent respiratory problems","Administer antacids for gastric acid neutralization","Administer antibiotics to prevent secondary infection","Administer opioids for pain relieving, the drug of choice in pancreatitis","Withhold all analgesia to avoid masking symptoms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Deep breathing/coughing, antacids for gastric acid neutralization, antibiotics to prevent secondary infection, and opioids as the drug of choice for pain are all listed.  Why E wrong: Pain relief with opioids is indicated, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Regarding opioids in pancreatitis, which statement reflects the source's choice of opioid?","options":["Choice of opioid: Fentanyl and Hydromorphone (safely effective)","Morphine is the safest first choice","Meperidine is contraindicated and must not be used","No opioids are ever permitted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Opioids in Pancreatitis\" box names Fentanyl and Hydromorphone as the choice (safely effective).  Why B wrong: Morphine is contraindicated because it cause an increase of spasm/cause of Sphincter of Oddi.  Why C wrong: Meperidine is contraindicated, but because it has short half-life and repeated doses can lead to accumulation of the metabolite normeperidine which cause neuromuscular side effects such as seizures — yet the source lists it as usable with caution; the keyed clearer statement is A. (Source note: Meperidine is listed as contraindicated for the short half-life/metabolite reason.)  Why D wrong: Opioids are the drug of choice for pancreatitis pain."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Why is morphine contraindicated in pancreatitis according to the source?","options":["It causes increased spasm of the Sphincter of Oddi","It causes hypoglycemia","It has no analgesic effect","It causes hypercalcemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine is contraindicated because it causes an increase of spasm of the Sphincter of Oddi.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons morphine is avoided in pancreatitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Why does hypocalcemia occur in acute pancreatitis according to the source's \"Also Remember\" box?","options":["Because free fatty acids generated by lipase action on fats combine with (chelate) calcium, resulting in calcium deposition, decreased serum calcium, and hypocalcemia","Because calcium is excreted in vomit","Because of excess vitamin D","Because of renal calcium wasting only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box explains hypocalcemia (saponification) occurs when pancreatic enzymes/lipase act on fats generating free fatty acids that combine with (chelate) calcium, causing calcium deposition, decreased serum calcium, and hypocalcemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Vomiting loss, excess vitamin D, and renal wasting are not the mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Cholecystitis is defined as which of the following?","options":["Inflammation of the gallbladder that may occur as an acute or chronic process","Presence of gallstones in the bile duct","Inflammation of the pancreas","Narrowing of the pylorus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder that may occur as an acute or chronic process.  Why B wrong: Stones in the bile duct describe choledocholithiasis.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe pancreatitis and pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Acute cholecystitis is usually caused by which of the following?","options":["Gallstones or cholelithiasis","Viral hepatitis","Alcohol abuse only","Pancreatic cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute cholecystitis is usually caused by gallstones or cholelithiasis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the usual causes of acute cholecystitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"\"Acalculous cholecystitis\" refers to which condition?","options":["Cholecystitis caused by infection, usually with M. Pylori","Cholecystitis caused by stones in the bile duct","Trauma & burn of the gallbladder","Stone formation in the gallbladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acalculous cholecystitis is cholecystitis caused by infection, usually with M. Pylori, trauma & burn of the gallbladder (i.e., without stones).  Why B wrong: Stones in bile duct is choledocholithiasis.  Why C wrong: Trauma & burn are associated factors but the defining term is the non-stone (acalculous) infection-related inflammation.  Why D wrong: Stone formation in gallbladder is cholelithiasis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"\"Choledocholithiasis\" is BEST defined as:","options":["Bile stone formation in gallbladder","Bile stone formation in the common bile duct (CBD) — cystic duct + hepatic duct","Inflammation of the pancreas","Stone in the pyloric outlet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Choledocholithiasis is bile stone formation in the common bile duct (CBD = cystic duct + hepatic duct).  Why A wrong: Stone formation in gallbladder is cholelithiasis.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Cholesterol bile stones account for approximately what proportion of gallstones?","options":["About 10% of gallstones","Almost 70% of gallstones","Almost 30% of gallstones","About 5% of gallstones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common gallstone, almost 70% of gallstones are cholesterol bile stones.  Why A/C/D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated ~70%."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What are the colour and composition of cholesterol bile stones?","options":["Light yellow, brown, sometimes light green colour; 90% cholesterol contains","Black, composed mostly of bilirubin","White, composed of calcium oxalate","Red, composed of iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cholesterol bile stones are light yellow, brown, sometimes light green colour and contain about 90% cholesterol.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe pigment stones or non-existent compositions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Pigment bile stones are composed of which of the following?","options":["70% cholesterol + 30% bile pigments (e.g., bilirubin + calcium)","20% cholesterol + 70% bile pigments (e.g., bilirubin + calcium)","100% cholesterol","Pure calcium oxalate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pigment bile stones contain composition: 20% cholesterol + 70% bile pigments (e.g., bilirubin + calcium).  Why A/C/D wrong: These compositions do not match the source's 20%/70% figures."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What colour are pigment bile stones, and what complication can they cause if they pass into the duodenum and absorb into the blood?","options":["Black colour; can cause jaundice if it passes into duodenum and is absorbed into blood","White colour; cause anaemia","Yellow colour; cause hyperglycemia","Green colour; cause hypocalcemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pigment bile stones are black colour; they can cause jaundice due to obstruction CBD bile cannot pass into duodenum and is absorbed into blood leading to jaundice.  Why B/C/D wrong: These colours/complications are incorrect for pigment stones."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the typical size of mixed gallstones?","options":["5–10% of gallstones","50–70% of gallstones","1–2% of gallstones","90% of gallstones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mixed gallstones account for 5–10% of gall stones.  Why B/C/D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated 5–10%."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of cholecystitis/cholelithiasis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nausea & vomiting, dyspepsia, especially after eating of fatty foods","Referred pain: usually right upper quadrate of abdomen which may radiate to back to shoulders","Guarding, rigidity, rebound tenderness in abdomen that increasing with inspiration","Mass palpated in the right upper quadrate region of abdomen","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nausea/vomiting/dyspepsia after fatty foods, referred pain in RUQ radiating to back/shoulders, guarding/rigidity/rebound tenderness increasing with inspiration, and a mass palpated in RUQ are all listed.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Murphy's sign in cholecystitis is BEST described as:","options":["The client cannot take a deep breath when the examiner's fingers are passed below the hepatic margin over the gallbladder because of severe pain","Peri-umbilical bruising","Flank discolouration","A loud abdominal gurgle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Murphy's sign: the client cannot take a deep breath when the examiner's fingers are passed below the hepatic margin over the gallbladder because of severe pain.  Why B wrong: Peri-umbilical bruising is Cullen's sign.  Why C wrong: Flank discolouration is Turner's sign.  Why D wrong: A loud gurgle is borborygmi."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which additional signs may occur in cholecystitis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Elevated temperature, sign of dehydration & tachycardia","Jaundice & hypothermia with bradycardia","Hypoglycemia with hypertension","Polyuria with weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Elevated temperature, sign of dehydration & tachycardia are listed.  Why B/C/D wrong: These combinations are not described for cholecystitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A nurse provides care for a patient with cholecystitis. Which interventions are appropriate? (Select all that apply)","options":["Keep NPO; teach the client about diet","Give analgesic & vomiting, painkiller (DOC: Opioids like morphine) and antibiotics if infection is there or as per order","Maintain NG tube suctioning, or NG decompression to reduce nausea & eliminate vomiting","Give vitamin K to prevent bleeding because vitamin K is fat soluble vitamin and is not absorbed in absence of bile so deficiency of vitamin K may result in bleeding","Encourage a high-fat diet to stimulate the gallbladder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: NPO + diet teaching, analgesics/antibiotics, NG suctioning/decompression for nausea/vomiting, and vitamin K to prevent bleeding (because vitamin K is fat-soluble and not absorbed without bile) are all listed.  Why E wrong: A high-fat diet would stimulate the gallbladder and worsen symptoms; low-fat is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Why is vitamin K given to prevent bleeding in a patient with cholecystitis/biliary obstruction?","options":["Because vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin not absorbed in the absence of bile, so deficiency may result in bleeding","Because vitamin K is water-soluble and easily depleted","Because vitamin K destroys gallstones","Because vitamin K stimulates bile production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin and is not absorbed in absence of bile, so deficiency of vitamin K may result in bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: Vitamin K is fat-soluble (not water-soluble), does not destroy stones, and does not stimulate bile production."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A recommended low-fat but not too high to avoid stimulating gallbladder diet for cholecystitis is most appropriate because excess fat stimulates the gallbladder. Which substance is to be blocked due to stones, with care or accelerate pain, for these patients?","options":["Bile by CBD blocked due to stones","Pancreatic enzymes","Gastric acid","Intrinsic factor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes a low fat diet to avoid stimulating gallbladder, since bile (via CBD) blocked due to stones cause care or accelerate pain.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pancreatic enzymes, gastric acid, and intrinsic factor are not the substance referenced here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which surgical procedures are options for managing cholecystitis/cholelithiasis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Choledocholithotomy","Abdominal and laparoscopic cholecystectomy","Pyloromyotomy","Whipple's procedure","Fundoplication"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Specific post-operative nursing care notes choledocholithotomy and abdominal & laparoscopic cholecystectomy as surgical management.  Why C/D/E wrong: Pyloromyotomy (pylorus), Whipple's (pancreas), and fundoplication (GERD) are not for cholecystitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Post-operatively after cholecystectomy, in which position should the client be placed?","options":["Low Fowler's position","Prone position","Supine flat position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should be placed in low Fowler's position post-operatively.  Why B/C/D wrong: Prone, supine flat, and Trendelenburg are not the specified post-op positions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which post-operative nursing measures are appropriate after cholecystectomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Encourage for coughing & deep breathing","Instruct the client about splinting the abdomen when coughing to prevent discomfort & pain during coughing","Maintain & monitor drainage from T-tube, if present","Offer fat-restricted diet to the client","Resume full activity immediately without restriction"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Coughing/deep breathing, splinting the abdomen when coughing, monitoring T-tube drainage, and offering a fat-restricted diet are all listed post-op measures.  Why E wrong: Immediate unrestricted full activity is not part of the listed post-op care."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"\"Abdominal Cholecystectomy\" involves removal of the gallbladder through an abdominal incision. In this surgery, an abdominal incision is made through the upper right side of the abdomen. This incision is also known as:","options":["McBurney's incision","Kocher's incision","Pfannenstiel incision","Midline incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abdominal cholecystectomy incision is known as Kocher's incision.  Why A wrong: McBurney's incision is for appendectomy.  Why C wrong: Pfannenstiel is a low transverse incision used in pelvic/cesarean surgery.  Why D wrong: Midline incision is a generic vertical incision, not the named one here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis is BEST defined as:","options":["Acute or chronic inflammation of the liver caused by viral & bacterial infection, hepatotoxins, contaminated foods, and exposure to medications","Inflammation of the gallbladder","Inflammation of the pancreas","Inflammation of the esophagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis is acute or chronic inflammation of the liver caused by viral & bacterial infection, hepatotoxins, contaminated foods, and exposure to medications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cholecystitis, pancreatitis, and esophagitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the goal of hepatitis treatment according to the source?","options":["Resting the inflamed liver to reduce metabolic demands and increasing the blood supply, thus promoting cellular regeneration & preventing complications","Surgically removing the liver","Increasing metabolic demand on the liver","Restricting all fluids permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The goal of treatment is resting the inflamed liver to reduce metabolic demands and increasing the blood supply, promoting cellular regeneration & preventing complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated goal."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"During which clinical stage of hepatitis, also known as the Prodromal stage, do clinical features appear, with anaemia, the virus becoming detectable in the blood, normal SGOT/SGPT/bilirubin, antibody not detected, and malaise/nausea/vomiting/weight loss?","options":["Pre-icteric stage (Prodromal stage)","Icteric phase","Post-icteric phase (Recovery phase)","Fulminant stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Pre-icteric stage (Prodromal stage) features clinical features appearing, anaemia, virus detectable in blood, normal SGOT/SGPT/bilirubin, antibody not detected, and malaise/nausea/vomiting/weight loss.  Why B wrong: Icteric phase has elevated bilirubin/SGOT/SGPT and jaundice.  Why C wrong: Post-icteric is the recovery phase.  Why D wrong: Fulminant is not the stage described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In the Icteric phase of hepatitis, which of the following findings occur? (Select all that apply)","options":["Serum bilirubin level & SGOT, SGPT level increased","Jaundice present (jaundice also known as Icterus)","Severe anorexia and weight loss","Dark or bile coloured urine that makes foams when shaken; clay coloured or cholic stools","Bilirubin, SGOT & SGPT all returned to normal level"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Icteric phase shows increased serum bilirubin & SGOT/SGPT, jaundice (icterus), severe anorexia and weight loss, and dark/bile-coloured foamy urine with clay-coloured or cholic stools.  Why E wrong: Return to normal levels occurs in the Post-icteric (Recovery) phase, not the Icteric phase."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In the Post-icteric phase (Recovery phase) of hepatitis, which changes occur? (Select all that apply)","options":["Jaundice decreased and urine & stool colour returned to normal","Symptoms diminish","Bilirubin, SGOT & SGPT returned to normal level","Virus first becomes detectable in the blood","Antibody not yet detected"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In the recovery phase, jaundice decreases, urine & stool colour return to normal, symptoms diminish, and bilirubin/SGOT/SGPT return to normal level.  Why D/E wrong: Virus first detectable and antibody not detected describe the pre-icteric (prodromal) stage."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Jaundice is also known by which term?","options":["Icterus","Pyrosis","Steatorrhea","Borborygmi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Jaundice is also known as Icterus.  Why B wrong: Pyrosis is heartburn.  Why C wrong: Steatorrhea is fatty stools.  Why D wrong: Borborygmi is a loud bowel gurgle."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Bilirubin is a product of metabolism formed in the liver and excreted by which route, according to the source's \"Also Remember\" note?","options":["By the kidneys via urination","Solely by the lungs","By sweat glands only","Through saliva"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states ammonia is formed by protein metabolism in the liver and excreted by the kidneys via urination (in the context of bilirubin/ammonia formation in the liver).  Why B/C/D wrong: Lungs, sweat, and saliva are not the excretory routes described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis A is caused by which virus?","options":["Hepatitis A Virus (HAV)","Hepatitis B Virus (HBV)","Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)","Hepatitis E Virus (HEV)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis A is caused by Hepatitis A Virus (HAV).  Why B/C/D wrong: HBV, HCV, and HEV cause Hepatitis B, C, and E respectively."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis A is an infectious hepatitis. How is the diagnosis of HAV made?","options":["By detection of HAV antibodies (anti-HAV) in the blood","By detection of HBsAg","By detection of anti-HCV only","By stool culture for bacteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of HAV is by detection of HAV antibodies (anti-HAV) in the blood.  Why B wrong: HBsAg detection diagnoses Hepatitis B.  Why C wrong: Anti-HCV diagnoses Hepatitis C.  Why D wrong: Stool bacterial culture is not the diagnostic method."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis A is transmitted by which route(s)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fecal-oral route (most common)","Person to person transmission","Contaminated water, milk, fruits, vegetables or uncooked shellfish","Poorly washed utensils","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: HAV transmission is via fecal-oral route (most common), person to person, contaminated water/milk/fruits/vegetables or uncooked shellfish, and poorly washed utensils.  Why E wrong: Blood transfusion is a route for Hepatitis B/C, not the listed routes for HAV."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the incubation period of Hepatitis A?","options":["2-6 weeks (15-45 days)","6-24 weeks","50-70 days","15-65 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HAV incubation period is 2-6 weeks (15-45 days).  Why B wrong: 6-24 weeks (or 30-180 days) is Hepatitis B.  Why C wrong: 50-70 days is Hepatitis C.  Why D wrong: 15-65 days is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the infectious period of Hepatitis A relative to jaundice?","options":["2 weeks before and 1 week after development of jaundice","Only after jaundice resolves","6 months before jaundice","Never infectious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HAV infectious period is 2 weeks before and 1 week after development of jaundice.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated infectious window."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which measures are part of prevention for Hepatitis A? (Select all that apply)","options":["Strict hand washing & use standard precautions to prevent infection","Use contact precaution when handling client's excreta","Treatment of municipal water supplies","Serological screening of blood food markers","Avoid all blood transfusions for life"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Strict hand washing/standard precautions, contact precaution with client's excreta, treatment of municipal water supplies, and serological screening of blood food markers are listed HAV prevention measures.  Why E wrong: Lifelong avoidance of blood transfusion is not listed for HAV prevention."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Regarding the Hepatitis A vaccine, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatitis A vaccine is usually 2 dose is recommended, first at 1 year and second dose after 6 months of age","Dose: 0.5 ml","Route: IM","The vaccine is given by the IV route","Only a single lifetime dose is needed for all ages"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: HAV vaccine is usually 2 doses (first at 1 year and second after 6 months of age), dose 0.5 ml, route IM.  Why D wrong: The route is IM, not IV.  Why E wrong: It is a 2-dose schedule, not a single lifetime dose."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"If a person has severe exposure to Hepatitis A, what should be administered, and within what timeframe?","options":["Immunoglobulin should be administered within 2 weeks of exposure","Antibiotics within 1 hour","No treatment is needed","Antiviral therapy for 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If severe conditions (severe exposure) are due to immunoglobulin, it should be administered within 2 weeks of exposure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Antibiotics, no treatment, and 6-month antivirals are not the described post-exposure measure."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis B is also known by which name?","options":["Serum hepatitis","Infectious hepatitis","Post-transfusion hepatitis","Enteral hepatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is also known as serum hepatitis.  Why B wrong: Infectious hepatitis is Hepatitis A.  Why C wrong: Post-transfusion hepatitis is Hepatitis C.  Why D wrong: Enteral/water-borne hepatitis is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis B is caused by which virus, and it belongs to which family of viruses?","options":["Hepatitis B Virus (HBV); a DNA virus of hepadnavirales family","Hepatitis A Virus; an RNA virus","Hepatitis C Virus; a retrovirus","Hepatitis E Virus; a DNA virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is caused by Hepatitis B Virus (HBV), a DNA virus of hepadnavirales family.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misattribute virus type/family."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"How is the diagnosis of Hepatitis B made?","options":["By detection of HBsAg, HBeAg and HBcAg level in the blood","By detection of anti-HAV only","By stool examination","By urine culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis is made by detection of HBsAg, HBeAg and HBcAg level in the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anti-HAV, stool exam, and urine culture are not the HBV diagnostic markers."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis B can lead to which serious long-term complications?","options":["Chronic liver disease & higher incidence of hepatocellular carcinoma","Pyloric stenosis","Acute pancreatitis only","Dumping syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B leads to chronic liver disease & higher incidence of hepatocellular carcinoma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated conditions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the incubation period of Hepatitis B?","options":["6-24 weeks (or 30-180 days)","2-6 weeks","50-70 days","15-45 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBV incubation period is 6-24 weeks (30-180 days).  Why B/D wrong: 2-6 weeks (15-45 days) is Hepatitis A.  Why C wrong: 50-70 days is Hepatitis C."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are causes of Hepatitis B transmission/exposure? (Select all that apply)","options":["IV drug users & clients of haemodialysis therapy","Health care personnel","Blood or body fluids (infected semen, saliva, urine, vaginal secretions, etc.)","Sexual contact and infected blood products","Drinking contaminated water only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes/exposure include IV drug users & haemodialysis clients, health care personnel, blood or body fluids (infected semen, saliva, urine, vaginal secretions), and sexual contact/infected blood products.  Why E wrong: Contaminated water is the route for Hepatitis A/E, not the primary HBV cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis B can be transmitted through which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Contaminated needles, toothbrush, and razor","Introduction of infected material into the eyes, oral cavities, lacerations & vagina","Parenteral such as through IV, IM, SC & ID injections","Vertical transmission: mother to fetus","Casual hand-holding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: HBV transmission includes contaminated needles/toothbrush/razor, introduction of infected material into eyes/oral cavities/lacerations & vagina, parenteral routes (IV, IM, SC & ID injections), and vertical transmission (mother to fetus).  Why E wrong: Casual hand-holding is not a transmission route."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which measures are part of Hepatitis B prevention? (Select all that apply)","options":["Strict hand washing","Screening the blood donors","Needle precautions","Avoid heterosexual contact or avoid sex with multiple partners","Testing of all pregnant women"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: HBV prevention includes strict hand washing, screening blood donors, needle precautions, avoiding heterosexual contact / sex with multiple partners, and testing of all pregnant women.  All options are listed prevention measures."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which statement about Hepatitis B vaccine and immune globulin is correct?","options":["Vaccinate & immune globulin if client never has the vaccine","The vaccine is contraindicated in all adults","Immune globulin is never used for Hepatitis B","Only oral medication is used for prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises vaccinate & Hepatitis B immune globulin if the client never has the vaccine.  Why B/C/D wrong: The vaccine is recommended (not contraindicated), immune globulin IS used, and prevention is not oral medication."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the recommended diet in Hepatitis B?","options":["Low fat, moderate to high protein diet to heal liver tissues","High fat, low protein diet","No protein at all","High sugar liquid diet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Recommended diet in hepatitis B is low fat, moderate to high protein to heal liver tissues.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the recommended low-fat, moderate-to-high protein diet."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis C is also known by which name?","options":["Post-transfusion hepatitis","Serum hepatitis","Infectious hepatitis","Enteric hepatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis C is also known as post-transfusion hepatitis.  Why B wrong: Serum hepatitis is Hepatitis B.  Why C wrong: Infectious hepatitis is Hepatitis A.  Why D wrong: Enteric hepatitis is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis C is caused by which virus, and is common among which group?","options":["Hepatitis C Virus (HCV); common among IV drug users","Hepatitis A Virus; common in children","Hepatitis B Virus; common in newborns","Hepatitis E Virus; common in pregnant women"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis C is caused by Hepatitis C Virus (HCV), transmitted through blood & blood products, and is common among IV drug users.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misattribute the cause and at-risk group."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the incubation period of Hepatitis C?","options":["50-70 days","15-45 days","6-24 weeks","15-65 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HCV incubation period is 50-70 days.  Why B wrong: 15-45 days is Hepatitis A.  Why C wrong: 6-24 weeks is Hepatitis B.  Why D wrong: 15-65 days is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis C is diagnosed by which method?","options":["By HCV antibody detected in blood test","By stool culture","By detection of anti-HAV","By urine analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis is made by HCV antibody detected in blood test.  Why B/C/D wrong: Stool culture, anti-HAV, and urine analysis are not the HCV diagnostic methods."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Complications of Hepatitis C include which of the following?","options":["Chronic liver disease, primary hepatocellular carcinoma and cirrhosis","Pyloric stenosis","Dumping syndrome","Acute pancreatitis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Complications include chronic liver disease, primary hepatocellular carcinoma, and cirrhosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated conditions."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"A client receiving frequently blood transfusions, such as in haemophilia, is at highest risk of contracting which type of hepatitis?","options":["Hepatitis C infection","Hepatitis A infection","Hepatitis E infection","No risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A client receiving frequently blood transfusions such as in haemophilia is at highest risk of getting Hepatitis C infection.  Why B/C wrong: Hepatitis A and E are enterally transmitted (fecal-oral), not primarily blood-borne.  Why D wrong: There is a clear, elevated risk."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis D's causes & risk factors are the same as which virus, because Hepatitis D requires that virus for infection?","options":["Same as HBV","Same as HAV","Same as HCV","Same as HEV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis D causes & risk factors are the same as HBV because Hepatitis D needs HBV (and is transmitted parenterally, and health care personnel are at risk).  Why B/C/D wrong: Hepatitis D is dependent on HBV, not HAV, HCV, or HEV."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis D is caused by which virus?","options":["Hepatitis D Virus (HDV)","Hepatitis A Virus","Hepatitis C Virus","Hepatitis E Virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis D is caused by Hepatitis D Virus (HDV) and it is also known as Hepatitis delta virus (HDV); it occurs with Hepatitis-B.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause other types of hepatitis."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis D occurs with which other hepatitis and may cause infection with it?","options":["With Hepatitis-B","With Hepatitis-A","With Hepatitis-C","With Hepatitis-E"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis D occurs with Hepatitis-B and can cause infection with it (HDV requires HBV co-infection or superinfection).  Why B/C/D wrong: HDV is associated with HBV, not the others."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which statements about Hepatitis D are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Transmission & complication same as HBV","Prevention of HBV infection with vaccine also prevents HDV infection","Prevention of HDV depends on HBV for replication","Prevention same as HAV","It is transmitted by fecal-oral route only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: HDV transmission & complications are same as HBV; preventing HBV infection with vaccine also prevents HDV; and HDV depends on HBV for replication.  Why D wrong: Prevention is tied to HBV, not HAV.  Why E wrong: Fecal-oral is the route for HAV/HEV, not HDV."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Hepatitis E is caused by which virus, and how is it transmitted?","options":["Hepatitis E Virus (HEV); transmitted by the fecal-oral route","Hepatitis B Virus; transmitted parenterally","Hepatitis C Virus; transmitted via blood","Hepatitis D Virus; transmitted sexually"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis E is caused by Hepatitis E Virus (HEV) and is a waterborne virus transmitted by the fecal-oral route.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misattribute the cause and route."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Why is Hepatitis E more risky in areas where sewage disposal is inadequate?","options":["Because areas where contaminated water used for bathing, washing and drinking facilitate spread","Because of frequent blood transfusions","Because of needle sharing","Because of sexual contact"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis E is more risky in areas where sewage disposal is inadequate and areas where contaminated water is used for bathing, washing and drinking.  Why B/C/D wrong: Blood transfusions, needle sharing, and sexual contact are routes for HBV/HCV, not the waterborne HEV."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the incubation period of Hepatitis E?","options":["15-65 days (2 weeks)","50-70 days","6-24 weeks","2-6 weeks (15-45 days)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HEV incubation period is 15-65 days (about 2 weeks per source).  Why B wrong: 50-70 days is Hepatitis C.  Why C wrong: 6-24 weeks is Hepatitis B.  Why D wrong: 2-6 weeks (15-45 days) is Hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"How is the diagnosis of Hepatitis E made?","options":["Made on the basis of presence of anti-HEV antibody in the blood","By detection of HBsAg","By stool bacterial culture","By urine analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of Hepatitis E is made on the basis of presence of anti-HEV antibody in the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: HBsAg detects HBV; stool culture and urine analysis are not the HEV diagnostic methods."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which complication of Hepatitis E carries a high mortality rate, and prevention is similar to which hepatitis?","options":["Complications include high mortality rate in pregnant women, fetal demise; prevention similar to HAV","High mortality only in newborns; prevention similar to HBV","No serious complications; prevention by vaccine","Chronic liver failure; prevention similar to HCV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis E complications include a high mortality rate in pregnant women and fetal demise; preventions are similar to HAV.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies high mortality in pregnant women and prevention similar to HAV."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Regarding HBsAg (\"Also Remember\" box), which statement is correct?","options":["It stands for hepatitis B surface antigen; it is the serological marker or reliable & confirmative test of hepatitis B","It is the core antigen of hepatitis B","It indicates immunity developed against HBV","It is the early antigen of hepatitis B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBsAg stands for hepatitis B surface antigen and is the serological marker / reliable & confirmative test of hepatitis B; if present in blood it indicates infectious state of the client.  Why B wrong: The core antigen is HBcAg.  Why C wrong: Anti-HBs antibody indicates immunity, not HBsAg.  Why D wrong: HBeAg is the early antigen."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"If HBsAg is present in the blood, what does it indicate?","options":["Infectious state of the client","Recovery and immunity","Past infection only","No infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If HBsAg is present in the blood it indicates infectious state of the client.  Why B wrong: Immunity is indicated by Anti-HBs antibody.  Why C/D wrong: Its presence indicates current infectious state, not past or absent infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What does the Anti-HBs antibody found in the serum indicate?","options":["Immunity developed against HBV","Active acute infection","Chronic progression","Core antigen presence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-HBs antibody found in the serum indicates immunity developed against HBV.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other markers/states, not immunity."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"HBeAg is described as which of the following?","options":["It is hepatitis B early antigen; it indicates highly infectious state of hepatitis B and possible progression to chronic hepatitis","It is hepatitis B surface antigen","It is hepatitis B core antigen","It indicates immunity to HBV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBeAg is hepatitis B early antigen; it indicates highly infectious state of hepatitis B and possible progression to chronic hepatitis.  Why B wrong: Surface antigen is HBsAg.  Why C wrong: Core antigen is HBcAg.  Why D wrong: Immunity is indicated by Anti-HBs."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What does anti-HBe (if HBe antibody found in the serum) indicate?","options":["It means patient pro-infected with HBV infection (i.e., reduced infectivity/seroconversion as the patient was once infected with HBV)","It means the patient is immune from birth","It means HBsAg is absent permanently","It means active core replication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-HBe: if HBe antibody is found in the serum, it means the patient pro-infected (previously/seroconverting) with HBV infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: It does not indicate congenital immunity, permanent HBsAg absence, or active core replication.  Source note: The source phrasing (\"patient pro-infected with HBV infection\") is keyed as written; clinically anti-HBe reflects seroconversion/lower infectivity."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Regarding HBcAg, which statement is correct?","options":["It is hepatitis B core antigen; it is specially found in the cells","It is the surface antigen","It is the early antigen","It indicates immunity to HBV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBcAg is hepatitis B core antigen and is specially found in the cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: Surface (HBsAg), early (HBeAg), and immunity (Anti-HBs) describe different markers."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What does anti-HBc indicate?","options":["If HBc antibody is found in the serum, it means patient previously infected with HBV","It indicates immunity from vaccination","It indicates current surface antigen","It indicates no exposure to HBV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-HBc: if HBc antibody is found in the serum, it means the patient was previously infected with HBV.  Why B wrong: Vaccine-induced immunity is indicated by Anti-HBs, not anti-HBc.  Why C/D wrong: It does not indicate current surface antigen or absence of exposure."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Which statements about Hepatitis B vaccination are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatitis B disease is a vaccine preventable","For neonates: route IM, in Vastus lateralis muscles of the mid-thigh","Dose: 0.5 ml","Hepatitis-B vaccine included in immunization program (UIP) in 2007 by WHO","Hepatitis vaccination (3 doses) provides life long immunity, so booster dose is not required"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are stated: HBV disease is vaccine preventable; for neonates, route IM in Vastus lateralis muscles of the mid-thigh; dose 0.5 ml; HBV vaccine included in UIP in 2007 by WHO; and 3-dose vaccination provides lifelong immunity so booster is not required (Source: www.cdc.gov).  All options are listed."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Alanine Aminotransferase Enzyme (ALT) is formerly known by which name, and what is its normal level?","options":["Serum Glutamic Pyruvic Transaminase enzyme (SGPT); normal level of SGPT: 10-40 IU/L","Serum Glutamic Oxaloacetic Transaminase enzyme (SGOT); normal 100-300 U/L","Gamma GT; normal 0-30 IU/L","Alkaline phosphatase; normal 40-120 IU/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ALT is formerly known as Serum Glutamic Pyruvic Transaminase enzyme (SGPT), normal level 10-40 IU/L.  Why B wrong: SGOT is AST, not ALT.  Why C/D wrong: These are other liver enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In hepatitis, the ALT (SGPT) level is expected to be:","options":["Elevated (in hepatitis it is elevated 100-300 IU/L)","Decreased below normal","Unchanged","Always zero"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hepatitis, ALT is elevated (100-300 IU/L per source).  Why B/C/D wrong: ALT rises in hepatitis, it is not decreased, unchanged, or zero."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Aspartate Aminotransferase Enzyme (AST) is formerly known by which name, and what is its normal level?","options":["Serum Glutamic Oxaloacetic Transaminase enzyme (SGOT); normal level of SGOT: 10-30 IU/L","Serum Glutamic Pyruvic Transaminase enzyme (SGPT); normal 10-40 IU/L","Alkaline phosphatase; normal 40-120 IU/L","Gamma GT; normal 0-30 IU/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AST is formerly known as Serum Glutamic Oxaloacetic Transaminase enzyme (SGOT), normal level 10-30 IU/L.  Why B wrong: SGPT is ALT, not AST.  Why C/D wrong: These are other liver enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"In hepatitis, the AST (SGOT) level is expected to be:","options":["Elevated (in hepatitis it is elevated 100-300 IU/L)","Decreased","Normal","Zero"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AST is elevated in hepatitis (100-300 IU/L per source).  Why B/C/D wrong: AST rises in hepatitis, not decreased, normal, or zero."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Where is ammonia metabolized into urea, and how is it excreted?","options":["Ammonia is metabolised in the liver and converted into urea; it is water soluble substance and excreted by kidneys via urination","Ammonia is excreted unchanged by the lungs","Ammonia is stored in muscle permanently","Ammonia is excreted in stool only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ammonia is metabolised in the liver and converted into urea; it is water soluble and excreted by kidneys via urination.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described metabolism and renal excretion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the normal serum ammonia level, and what happens in liver disease?","options":["Normal under normal conditions 10-80 mcg/dl; in liver diseases it is elevated > 80 mcg/dl and causing encephalopathy","Normal 100-300 mcg/dl; decreased in liver disease","Normal 0-10 mcg/dl; unchanged in liver disease","Normal 200-400 mcg/dl; elevated to 500 in liver disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ammonia is usually normal under normal conditions 10-80 mcg/dl; in liver diseases it is elevated > 80 mcg/dl and causing encephalopathy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the stated normal (10-80 mcg/dl) and elevation (>80 mcg/dl)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Total Bilirubin level is described as the most reliable indicator of which condition?","options":["Most reliable indicator of liver diseases like hepatitis & cirrhosis","Most reliable indicator of kidney disease","Most reliable indicator of heart disease","Most reliable indicator of pancreatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total Bilirubin level is the most reliable indicator of liver diseases like hepatitis & cirrhosis; it is formed by breakdown of RBCs and in liver diseases it is elevated.  Why B/C/D wrong: It indicates liver disease, not kidney, heart, or pancreatic disease."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"What is the normal Total Bilirubin level?","options":["1-2 mg/dl","10-40 mg/dl","100-300 mg/dl","0.1 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal total bilirubin level is 1-2 mg/dl.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the normal 1-2 mg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Direct or conjugated bilirubin has which normal level and solubility?","options":["<0.3 mg/dl; water soluble bilirubin","1-2 mg/dl; fat soluble bilirubin","0.2-1.2 mg/dl; fat soluble bilirubin","10-40 mg/dl; water soluble"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Direct or conjugated bilirubin normal level is <0.3 mg/dl, and it is water soluble bilirubin.  Why B wrong: 1-2 mg/dl is the total bilirubin level.  Why C wrong: 0.2-1.2 mg/dl, fat soluble describes indirect/unconjugated bilirubin.  Why D wrong: These values are incorrect for direct bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Peptic ulcer risk reminders, GERD, Dumping Syndrome…","question":"Indirect or unconjugated bilirubin has which normal level and solubility?","options":["0.2-1.2 mg/dl; fat soluble bilirubin","<0.3 mg/dl; water soluble bilirubin","1-2 mg/dl; water soluble","10-30 mg/dl; fat soluble"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indirect or unconjugated bilirubin normal level is 0.2-1.2 mg/dl, and it is fat soluble bilirubin.  Why B wrong: <0.3 mg/dl, water soluble describes direct/conjugated bilirubin.  Why C/D wrong: These values/solubility are incorrect for indirect bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Liver cirrhosis is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["An acute, fully reversible inflammatory disease of the liver","A progressive, irreversible, late stage of scarring (fibrosis) disease of the liver caused by hepatitis and chronic alcoholism","A benign fatty accumulation with no fibrosis","A congenital malformation of the bile ducts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states cirrhosis is a progressive, irreversible, late stage of scarring (fibrosis) disease of the liver caused by hepatitis and chronic alcoholism.  Why A wrong: Cirrhosis is irreversible, not acute/reversible.  Why C wrong: It involves fibrosis and destruction of hepatocytes, not benign fat alone.  Why D wrong: It is an acquired scarring disease, not a congenital bile duct malformation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In liver cirrhosis, which pathological processes occur within the liver tissue?","options":["Regeneration and hypertrophy of hepatocytes only","Degeneration, fibrosis and destruction of hepatocytes","Fatty infiltration without cell death","Calcification of the portal vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states degeneration, fibrosis and destruction of hepatocytes occur in cirrhosis.  Why A wrong: Cirrhosis features destruction, not pure regeneration/hypertrophy.  Why C wrong: There is cell destruction, not fat infiltration alone.  Why D wrong: Portal vein calcification is not described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What causes the formation of scar tissue in the liver in cirrhosis?","options":["Repeated destruction of hepatic cells","Excess bile production","Increased splenic pressure","Reduced portal vein pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states repeated destruction of hepatic cells causes formation of scar tissue.  Why B wrong: Excess bile is not the described cause of scarring.  Why C wrong: Splenic pressure is a consequence, not the cause of hepatic scarring.  Why D wrong: Portal pressure rises (not falls) in cirrhosis and is not the scarring cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which type of liver cirrhosis is also known as alcoholic, portal, atrophy and fatty cirrhosis?","options":["Post-necrotic cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Laennec's cirrhosis","Cardiac cirrhosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The types table states Laennec's cirrhosis is also known as alcoholic, portal, atrophy and fatty cirrhosis.  Why A wrong: Post-necrotic cirrhosis is also known as macronodular cirrhosis.  Why B wrong: Biliary cirrhosis relates to chronic biliary obstruction.  Why D wrong: Cardiac cirrhosis is related to right-side heart failure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of Laennec's cirrhosis?","options":["Chronic biliary obstruction","Heavy alcohol intake","Right-side heart failure","Viral hepatitis B and C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the most common cause of Laennec's cirrhosis is heavy alcohol intake.  Why A wrong: Biliary obstruction causes biliary cirrhosis.  Why C wrong: Heart failure causes cardiac cirrhosis.  Why D wrong: HBV & HCV are linked to post-necrotic cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which type of liver cirrhosis is the MOST common type of liver cirrhosis in India?","options":["Post-necrotic cirrhosis","Cardiac cirrhosis","Laennec's cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table explicitly states Laennec's is the most common type of liver cirrhosis in India.  Why A wrong: Post-necrotic is the most common type worldwide, not specifically India.  Why B wrong: Cardiac cirrhosis is the least common type.  Why D wrong: Biliary cirrhosis is not identified as the Indian-predominant type."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Post-necrotic cirrhosis is also known by which other name?","options":["Micronodular cirrhosis","Macronodular cirrhosis","Portal cirrhosis","Fatty cirrhosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states post-necrotic cirrhosis is also known as macronodular cirrhosis.  Why A wrong: Micronodular is not the term given for post-necrotic type.  Why C/D wrong: Portal/fatty are alternate names for Laennec's cirrhosis, not post-necrotic."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Post-necrotic cirrhosis occurs after which event?","options":["Mild fatty change","Massive liver necrosis","Chronic bile stasis","Chronic right heart failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states post-necrotic cirrhosis occurs after massive liver necrosis.  Why A wrong: Mild fatty change is not the trigger described.  Why C wrong: Bile stasis relates to biliary cirrhosis.  Why D wrong: Right heart failure relates to cardiac cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Post-necrotic cirrhosis is usually related to which viral hepatitis and exposures?","options":["HAV & HEV and food contamination","HBV & HCV and industrial chemical exposure or exposure to hepatotoxins","HDV only and blood transfusion","No viral association"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states post-necrotic cirrhosis is related to viral hepatitis usually HBV & HCV and industrial chemical exposure or exposure to hepatotoxins.  Why A wrong: HAV/HEV food-borne route is not stated for this type.  Why C wrong: HDV-only is not the described association.  Why D wrong: A clear viral (HBV/HCV) association is described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which type of liver cirrhosis is the MOST common type worldwide?","options":["Laennec's cirrhosis","Post-necrotic cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Cardiac cirrhosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states post-necrotic cirrhosis is the most common type worldwide.  Why A wrong: Laennec's is the most common type in India, not worldwide.  Why C/D wrong: Biliary and cardiac types are not identified as the most common worldwide."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Biliary cirrhosis develops from which of the following?","options":["Heavy alcohol intake","Chronic biliary obstruction, bile stasis and inflammation resulting in severe obstructive jaundice","Massive liver necrosis","Right-side heart failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states biliary cirrhosis develops from chronic biliary obstruction, bile stasis and inflammation resulting in severe obstructive jaundice.  Why A wrong: Alcohol is the cause of Laennec's cirrhosis.  Why C wrong: Massive necrosis relates to post-necrotic cirrhosis.  Why D wrong: Heart failure relates to cardiac cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Besides biliary obstruction, biliary cirrhosis may also develop due to which process?","options":["An autoimmune response","Portal vein thrombosis","Chronic hypoglycemia","Iron overload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states biliary cirrhosis also develops due to an autoimmune response.  Why B/C/D wrong: Portal vein thrombosis, hypoglycemia and iron overload are not the described additional cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Cardiac cirrhosis develops from which condition and is which type in frequency?","options":["Left-side heart failure; most common type","Severe and long-term right-side heart failure; least common type","Chronic alcoholism; most common type","Bile stasis; least common type"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states cardiac cirrhosis is developed from severe and long-term right-side heart failure and is the least common type.  Why A wrong: It is right-side (not left-side) heart failure and is the least common type.  Why C wrong: Alcoholism causes Laennec's.  Why D wrong: Bile stasis causes biliary cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In cirrhosis, anorexia (loss of appetite) is attributed to which mechanism?","options":["Increased gastric acid","Malabsorption","Hyperthyroidism","Excess bile production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The signs & symptoms list states anorexia is loss of appetite due to malabsorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: Increased gastric acid, hyperthyroidism and excess bile are not the stated cause of cirrhotic anorexia."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In cirrhosis, pruritus (all-body itching) results from which of the following?","options":["Bile salts and deposits of waste products in the skin","Dry skin from dehydration only","Allergic reaction to diuretics","Excess sodium in sweat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pruritus is all-body itching due to bile salts and deposits of waste products in the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dehydration, drug allergy and sodium in sweat are not the stated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"As described on this page, hematemesis in cirrhosis is characterized as which type of bleeding?","options":["Upper GI active bleeding","Lower GI active bleeding","Intracranial bleeding","Pulmonary bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The signs & symptoms list labels hematemesis as lower GI active bleeding (as written on the page).  Why A wrong: Although hematemesis is classically upper GI, the source text states \"lower GI active bleeding\"; key the source wording — note the book's wording differs from the classic definition of hematemesis as upper GI bleeding.  Why C/D wrong: Intracranial and pulmonary bleeding are not listed for hematemesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Gynecomastia in a male cirrhosis patient is due to which cause?","options":["Direct liver enlargement","Hormonal imbalances","Portal hypertension only","Vitamin K deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states gynecomastia is breast enlargement in male due to hormonal imbalances.  Why A/C/D wrong: Liver size, portal hypertension and vitamin K deficiency are not the stated cause of gynecomastia."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Spider angioma seen in cirrhosis is described as which of the following?","options":["Deep purple bruises from thrombocytopenia","Superficially red radish rashes due to small inflamed blood vessels","Yellow discoloration of sclera","Fluid-filled skin blisters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes spider angioma as superficially red radish rashes due to small inflamed blood vessels.  Why A wrong: That describes purpura/bruising, not spider angioma.  Why C wrong: Yellow sclera is jaundice.  Why D wrong: Blisters are not spider angioma."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Ascites in cirrhosis refers to which of the following?","options":["Accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity","Accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity","Fluid in the pericardial sac","Fluid in the joint spaces"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines ascites as accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity.  Why A wrong: Pleural fluid is a pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Pericardial fluid is a pericardial effusion.  Why D wrong: Joint fluid is an effusion of the joint, not ascites."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In cirrhosis, weight gain is attributed to which of the following?","options":["Increased muscle mass","Generalised pitting oedema","Increased appetite","Fat deposition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states weight gain occurs due to generalised pitting oedema.  Why A/C/D wrong: Muscle mass, appetite and fat deposition are not the described cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which liver enzymes/values are grossly increased in cirrhosis?","options":["SGOT, SGPT and bilirubin","Amylase and lipase","Troponin and CK-MB","TSH and T4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states grossly increased liver enzymes like SGOT, SGPT and bilirubin.  Why B wrong: Amylase/lipase are pancreatic enzymes.  Why C wrong: Troponin/CK-MB are cardiac markers.  Why D wrong: TSH/T4 are thyroid tests."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Generalised oedema in cirrhosis is described on the page as being due to which of the following?","options":["Decreased serum bilirubin level (hypoalbuminemia)","Increased serum albumin","High sodium intake alone","Increased oncotic pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states generalised oedema is due to \"decreased serum bilirubin level (hypoalbuminemia)\" — the parenthetical hypoalbuminemia is the operative low-protein mechanism.  Why B wrong: Albumin is decreased (hypoalbuminemia), not increased.  Why C wrong: High sodium alone is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Oncotic pressure falls with hypoalbuminemia (not rises)."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which coagulation-related finding is expected in cirrhosis?","options":["Shortened prothrombin time","Prolonged prothrombin time","Normal prothrombin time always","Absent prothrombin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists prolonged prothrombin time as a sign of cirrhosis.  Why A/C wrong: PT is prolonged, not shortened or unchanged.  Why D wrong: Prothrombin is reduced/affected but not absent."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the page, hypokalaemia in cirrhosis is due to which mechanism?","options":["Liver homeostasis or hepatic cellular potassium uptake mechanisms despite insulin hypersecretion","Renal failure in end-stage disease","Excess dietary potassium","Diuretic withdrawal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hypokalaemia is due to liver homeostasis or hepatic cellular potassium uptake mechanisms despite insulin hypersecretion.  Why B wrong: Renal failure/hepatorenal syndrome causes hyperkalaemia in the page's account.  Why C/D wrong: Dietary potassium and diuretic withdrawal are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Per the page, hyperkalaemia develops in cirrhosis under which circumstance?","options":["Early compensated disease","In end stage of liver condition or when hepatorenal syndrome is developed due to renal failure","With excessive urination","After a low-potassium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hyperkalaemia occurs in end stage of liver condition or when hepatorenal syndrome is developed due to renal failure.  Why A wrong: Early disease is associated with hypokalaemia here.  Why C/D wrong: Excess urination and low-potassium diet would lower potassium."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Hyponatremia in cirrhosis is usually of which type?","options":["Dilutional due to water retention","Depletional due to vomiting","From excessive salt loss in urine only","Factitious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hyponatremia is usually dilutional due to water retention.  Why B/C/D wrong: Depletional vomiting, urinary salt loss alone, and factitious causes are not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are NEUROLOGIC systemic manifestations of liver cirrhosis? (SATA)","options":["Hepatic encephalopathy","Peripheral neuropathy","Asterixis","Palmar erythema","Caput medusae"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The figure lists hepatic encephalopathy, peripheral neuropathy and asterixis under Neurologic.  Why D wrong: Palmar erythema is listed under Integumentary.  Why E wrong: Caput medusae is listed under Integumentary."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are INTEGUMENTARY manifestations of cirrhosis shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Jaundice","Spider angioma","Palmar erythema","Purpura","Splenomegaly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The figure lists jaundice, spider angioma, palmar erythema, purpura (also petechiae and caput medusae) under Integumentary.  Why E wrong: Splenomegaly is listed under Hematologic."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which additional integumentary manifestations of cirrhosis are shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Petechiae","Caput medusae","Fetor hepaticus","Purpura","Amenorrhea"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, D): Petechiae, caput medusae and purpura are listed under Integumentary.  Why C wrong: Fetor hepaticus is listed under Gastrointestinal.  Why E wrong: Amenorrhea is listed under Reproductive."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are GASTROINTESTINAL manifestations of cirrhosis per the figure? (SATA)","options":["Anorexia and dyspepsia","Nausea and vomiting","Fetor hepaticus","Esophageal and gastric varices","Anemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The figure lists anorexia, dyspepsia, nausea/vomiting, change in bowel habits, dull abdominal pain, fetor hepaticus, esophageal and gastric varices, hematemesis, hemorrhoidal varices and congestive gastritis under Gastrointestinal.  Why E wrong: Anemia is listed under Hematologic."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which GI manifestations of cirrhosis are shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Change in bowel habits","Dull abdominal pain","Hematemesis","Congestive gastritis","Testicular atrophy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): Change in bowel habits, dull abdominal pain, hematemesis, hemorrhoidal varices and congestive gastritis appear under Gastrointestinal.  Why E wrong: Testicular atrophy is a Reproductive manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are HEMATOLOGIC manifestations of cirrhosis per the figure? (SATA)","options":["Anemia","Thrombocytopenia","Leukopenia","Coagulation disorders","Splenomegaly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A–E): The figure lists anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, coagulation disorders and splenomegaly under Hematologic.  Why none wrong: All five are Hematologic manifestations in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Under the METABOLIC category, the figure lists which manifestations of cirrhosis? (SATA)","options":["Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypoalbuminemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The figure lists hypokalemia, hyponatremia and hypoalbuminemia under Metabolic.  Why D/E wrong: Hypercalcemia and hyperglycemia are not listed under Metabolic."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which CARDIOVASCULAR manifestations of cirrhosis are shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Fluid retention","Peripheral edema","Ascites","Bradycardia","Impotence"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The figure lists fluid retention, peripheral edema and ascites under Cardiovascular.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia is not listed.  Why E wrong: Impotence is a Reproductive manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which REPRODUCTIVE manifestations of cirrhosis are shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Amenorrhea","Testicular atrophy","Gynecomastia (male)","Impotence","Leukopenia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The figure lists amenorrhea, testicular atrophy, gynecomastia (male) and impotence under Reproductive.  Why E wrong: Leukopenia is a Hematologic manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Portal hypertension is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["A decrease in pressure in the hepatic artery","A continued increase in pressure in the portal vein that develops due to obstruction in the normal flow","Elevated pressure in the pulmonary veins","Increased pressure in the inferior vena cava only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines portal hypertension as a continued increase in portal vein pressure developing due to obstruction in the normal flow.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hepatic artery, pulmonary veins and IVC are not the defining vessel of portal hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the normal portal (vein) pressure?","options":["5-10 mm Hg or 7-14 cm H2O","15-20 mm Hg or 20-25 cm H2O","1-2 mm Hg or 3-4 cm H2O","25-30 mm Hg or 30-40 cm H2O"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normal portal pressure is 5-10 mm Hg or 7-14 cm H2O.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated normal portal pressure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In liver cirrhosis, portal pressure increases to which level?","options":["Less than 5 mm Hg","Exactly 7 mm Hg","Greater than 10 mm Hg","Below the normal range"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in liver cirrhosis portal pressure will increase >10 mm Hg.  Why A/B/D wrong: Portal pressure rises above 10 mm Hg, not below or within normal."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is a common manifestation of portal hypertension?","options":["Splenomegaly","Bradycardia","Hypertension of the pulmonary artery","Polyuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states splenomegaly is a common manifestation of portal hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: Bradycardia, pulmonary artery hypertension and polyuria are not listed as the common manifestation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Portal (venous) pressure is measured by which method?","options":["Sphygmomanometry","Transhepatic catheterisation","Central venous pressure line in the arm","Pulmonary artery catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states portal pressure is measured by transhepatic catheterisation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These methods are not the stated technique for measuring portal pressure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the page, ascites in cirrhosis is caused by which of the following (NHM-2020)?","options":["Venous congestion of the hepatic capillaries with capillary plasma leaking from the liver and portal vein","Increased serum albumin","Renal artery stenosis","Excess bile secretion into the peritoneum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ascites is accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity due to venous congestion of the hepatic capillaries, with congested capillary plasma leaking directly from the liver and portal vein.  Why B wrong: Albumin is decreased, not increased.  Why C/D wrong: Renal artery stenosis and bile secretion are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is the treatment of choice for ascites?","options":["Total colectomy","Abdominal paracentesis","Liver transplantation","Portacaval shunt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states abdominal paracentesis is the treatment of choice for ascites.  Why A/C/D wrong: Colectomy, transplantation and portacaval shunt are not identified as the treatment of choice for ascites here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Oesophageal varices develop when continuous portal hypertension exerts pressure on which structure?","options":["The inner wall/blood vessels of the oesophagus, causing distended oesophageal veins that rupture","The pyloric sphincter","The gastric fundus mucosa only","The diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states continuous portal hypertension exerts pressure on the inner wall/blood vessels of the oesophagus, causing distended oesophageal veins that become irritated and rupture, producing bleeding oesophageal varices.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pyloric sphincter, gastric fundus and diaphragm are not the described sites."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Oesophageal varices usually affect which part of the oesophagus?","options":["Upper one third","Middle one third","Lower one third","The entire oesophagus equally"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oesophageal varices affect usually the lower one third part of the oesophagus.  Why A/B/D wrong: Upper, middle and whole-oesophagus are not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of oesophageal varices?","options":["Gastric cancer","Liver cirrhosis","Peptic ulcer","Hiatal hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states liver cirrhosis is the most common cause of oesophageal varices.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Due to portal hypertension, besides oesophageal varices, which other varices may also occur?","options":["Gastric & duodenal varices","Pulmonary varices","Cerebral varices","Renal varices"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states due to portal hypertension gastric & duodenal varices also occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not mentioned as portal-hypertension-related varices."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In cirrhosis, coagulation defects arise partly because the liver cannot produce adequate bile for fat emulsification, disrupting which vitamins?","options":["Vitamin B and C (water soluble)","Fat-soluble vitamins A, K, D, E","Vitamin B12 only","Folic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the liver is unable to produce adequate bile for fat emulsification, disrupting fat-soluble vitamins such as vitamin A, K, D, E.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are water-soluble vitamins, not the fat-soluble vitamins disrupted here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which clotting factor is specifically mentioned as being affected in cirrhosis (in addition to vitamin-K-dependent factors)?","options":["Fibrinogen only","Prothrombin","Factor VIII only","Von Willebrand factor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states other clotting factors are also affected such as prothrombin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Fibrinogen, factor VIII and vWF are not the factor named."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Without vitamin K and which clotting factors is the cirrhosis client at risk of bleeding?","options":["Factors I, III, V, VIII","Factors II, VII, IX, X","Factors V, XI, XII, XIII","Factors VIII, IX, XI, XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states without vitamin K and clotting factors II, VII, IX, X the client is on risk of bleeding; these are the vitamin-K-dependent factors.  Why A/C/D wrong: These groupings do not match the stated vitamin-K-dependent factors."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why is vitamin K necessary for factors II, VII, IX and X?","options":["Because vitamin K is needed for these factors' formation","Because vitamin K destroys these factors","Because vitamin K converts them to bilirubin","Because vitamin K only affects platelets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states vitamin-K-dependent factors II, VII, IX & X require vitamin K because it is necessary for these factors' formation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Vitamin K does not destroy, convert to bilirubin, or act only on platelets per the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Jaundice as a complication of cirrhosis is also known as which condition, and occurs because of what?","options":["Hyperkalemia; excess potassium","Hyperbilirubinemia; the liver is unable to metabolise bilirubin","Hypoalbuminemia; low protein","Hyperglycemia; excess glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states jaundice is also known as hyperbilirubinemia and occurs because the liver is unable to metabolise bilirubin.  Why A/C/D wrong: These terms and mechanisms do not match jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Jaundice in cirrhosis develops when oedema, scarring of the hepatic bile duct and fibrosis interfere with which process?","options":["Normal bile flow","Gastric emptying","Renal filtration","Splenic blood flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oedema, scaring of hepatic bile duct and fibrosis interfere with normal bile flow, causing jaundice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Gastric emptying, renal filtration and splenic flow are not the interfered process."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Neonates and children with jaundice are assessed by which rule?","options":["Kramer's rules","Naegele's rule","Rule of nines","Apgar score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states neonates & children are assessed by Kramer's rules.  Why B wrong: Naegele's rule estimates due date.  Why C wrong: Rule of nines assesses burns.  Why D wrong: Apgar assesses newborn vitality."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In portal-systemic (hepatic) encephalopathy, neurological impairment is caused by failure of the diseased liver to detoxify which neurotoxic substance?","options":["Urea","Ammonia","Glucose","Albumin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states neurological impairment is caused by failure of the diseased liver to detoxify neurotoxic substances such as ammonia.  Why A wrong: Urea is the water-soluble product ammonia is normally converted to.  Why C/D wrong: Glucose and albumin are not the neurotoxin described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Regarding ammonia in hepatic encephalopathy, which statement is TRUE per the page?","options":["Ammonia cannot cross the blood-brain barrier","Ammonia is neurotoxic, crosses the BBB and causes irreversible damage of the brain","Ammonia is beneficial to neurons","Ammonia is produced only in the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ammonia is neurotoxic, crosses the BBB and causes irreversible damage of the brain.  Why A wrong: It does cross the BBB.  Why C wrong: It is toxic, not beneficial.  Why D wrong: The source does not state brain-only production."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Under normal conditions, ammonia is converted by the liver into which water-soluble substance?","options":["Uric acid","Urea","Creatinine","Bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states under normal conditions ammonia is converted by the liver into a water-soluble substance called urea.  Why A/C/D wrong: Uric acid, creatinine and bilirubin are not the product described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"At end-stage hepatic failure, which clinical features characterise portal-systemic encephalopathy? (SATA)","options":["Altered level of consciousness","Neurological impairment","Impaired thinking","Neuromuscular disturbances","Improved cognition"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists altered level of consciousness, neurological impairment, impaired thinking and neuromuscular disturbances.  Why E wrong: Cognition worsens, it does not improve."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Hepatorenal syndrome in cirrhosis reflects which pathophysiologic event?","options":["Isolated renal stones","Progressive renal failure occurring together with hepatic failure due to liver failure affecting the renal system","Cardiac failure only","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states due to liver failure the renal system is also affected, resulting in progressive renal failure occurring with hepatic failure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Renal stones, cardiac failure and pulmonary embolism are not the described syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are features of hepatorenal syndrome? (SATA)","options":["Suddenly or gradually decreased urinary output","Elevated serum creatinine and BUN","Decreased renal capability of sodium excretion","Increased renal osmolality or specific gravity","Low urine specific gravity with polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists decreased urine output, elevated creatinine and BUN, decreased sodium excretion, and increased renal osmolality/specific gravity.  Why E wrong: Osmolality/specific gravity is increased, and output is decreased, so low specific gravity with polyuria is the opposite."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Asterixis is BEST described as which of the following?","options":["Tremors of the hand like a bird flapping its wings, caused by neuromuscular impairment due to hepatorenal syndrome","Rhythmic jaw movements","Continuous eyelid twitching","Painful muscle cramps of the legs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes asterixis as tremors of the hand like a bird flapping its wings, caused by neuromuscular impairment due to hepatorenal syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: Jaw movements, eyelid twitching and leg cramps are not asterixis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Splenomegaly in cirrhosis is caused by which of the following?","options":["Persistent pressure in the spleen by portal hypertension","Splenic artery aneurysm","Iron deficiency","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states splenomegaly (inflammation/enlargement of the spleen) is caused by persistent pressure in the spleen by portal hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: Aneurysm, iron deficiency and dehydration are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Haemorrhoidal varices in cirrhosis develop because of which mechanism?","options":["Constipation only","Continuous portal hypertension inflaming the anal veins, resulting in varices/bleeding in the anus","Rectal cancer","Anal fissure from hard stools"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states haemorrhoidal varices are bleeding in the anus because continuous portal hypertension inflames anal veins, developing varices/bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: Constipation, cancer and fissure are not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the pathophysiology flow chart of liver cirrhosis, portal hypertension leads to which vascular change next?","options":["Splanchnic arterial vasoconstriction","Splanchnic arterial vasodilation","Pulmonary vasodilation","Coronary vasodilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Liver Cirrhosis → Portal hypertension → Splanchnic arterial vasodilation.  Why A wrong: The chart shows vasodilation, not vasoconstriction, of splanchnic vessels.  Why C/D wrong: Pulmonary/coronary vasodilation are not steps in the chart."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the flow chart, splanchnic arterial vasodilation leads to reduction in effective arterial volume, which then activates the vasoconstrictor system. Which systems make up this vasoconstrictor system? (SATA)","options":["Renin-Angiotensin system","Sympathetic Nervous system","Arginine-Vasopressin system","Parasympathetic system","Somatostatin system"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The chart names Renin-Angiotensin, Sympathetic Nervous, and Arginine-Vasopressin systems.  Why D/E wrong: Parasympathetic and somatostatin systems are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the flow chart, normalisation of the vasoconstrictor systems leads to normal excretion of sodium/water and results in which outcome?","options":["Ascites","No ascites","Hepatorenal syndrome","Hyponatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows normalisation → normal excretion of sodium water → No ascites.  Why A/C/D wrong: Ascites, hepatorenal syndrome and hyponatremia arise from persistent activation, not normalisation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the flow chart, persistent activation of vasoconstrictor systems with retention of sodium leads to which outcome?","options":["No ascites","Ascites","Normal sodium excretion","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows persistent activation → retention of sodium → Ascites.  Why A/C wrong: These belong to the normalisation branch.  Why D wrong: The outcome is ascites (sodium retention), not hypernatremia."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the flow chart, persistent retention of water leads to which outcome, and renal vasoconstriction leads to which outcome?","options":["Retention of water → hypernatremia; renal vasoconstriction → no ascites","Retention of water → hyponatremia; renal vasoconstriction → hepato-renal syndrome","Retention of water → ascites; renal vasoconstriction → hyponatremia","Retention of water → no ascites; renal vasoconstriction → ascites"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows retention of water → Hyponatremia, and renal vasoconstriction → Hepato-renal syndrome.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairings do not match the chart's outcomes."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which position is recommended in the nursing care of a cirrhosis patient to minimise discomfort?","options":["Prone position","Semi-Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing care states semi-Fowler's position to minimise discomfort.  Why A/C/D wrong: Prone, Trendelenburg and left lateral are not the recommended position."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"How often should weight and abdominal girth be measured in a cirrhosis patient?","options":["Weekly","Daily","Once at admission only","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states weight and abdominal girth should be measured daily.  Why A/C/D wrong: Weekly, once-only and hourly are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"At which anatomical level should the abdominal girth be measured in a cirrhosis patient?","options":["At the level of the xiphoid process","At the level of the umbilicus","At the level of the iliac crest","At the level of the symphysis pubis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states abdominal girth should be measured at the level of the umbilicus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These levels are not specified for girth measurement."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"When measuring abdominal girth serially, what technique ensures accuracy?","options":["Measure at a different site each day","Mark the abdomen and measure on the same marking the next day","Measure only after paracentesis","Use a different tape each time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to mark the abdomen and measure girth on the same marking the next day.  Why A/C/D wrong: Different sites, post-paracentesis-only, or different tapes would reduce accuracy and are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why are diuretics given in cirrhosis nursing care?","options":["To elimination of water by urination","To increase fluid retention","To decrease potassium loss","To reduce bile production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states give diuretics to elimination of water by urination.  Why B/C/D wrong: Diuretics remove fluid; they are not given to retain fluid, spare potassium, or reduce bile here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Neomycin and lactulose are given in cirrhosis for which purpose?","options":["To increase ammonia production","To decrease production of ammonia by bacteria in the bowel and facilitate excretion of ammonia through stool & urine","To treat portal hypertension directly","To thicken stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states neomycin & lactulose act to decrease ammonia production by bowel bacteria and facilitate excretion of ammonia through stool & urine.  Why A wrong: They decrease, not increase, ammonia.  Why C/D wrong: They are not for portal hypertension or thickening stool."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the recommended diet for cirrhosis (NORCET-2021)?","options":["High protein, high fat, low carbohydrate","Moderate protein, high carbohydrate and low to moderate fat diet","Protein-free, high-fat diet","High protein, high sodium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the recommended diet is moderate protein, high carbohydrate and low to moderate fat.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the recommended cirrhosis diet."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the recommended sodium intake in the cirrhosis diet?","options":["500-1000 mg/day or 1 gm/day (low sodium)","3-4 gm/day","Unrestricted sodium","10 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low sodium diet usually 500-1000 mg/day or 1 gm/day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sodium amounts do not match the low-sodium recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why is soft or liquid food advised when oesophageal varices are present in cirrhosis?","options":["To increase caloric density","To prevent further bleeding","To reduce ammonia","To improve taste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states soft or liquid food is given if oesophageal varices are present to prevent further bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: Caloric density, ammonia reduction and taste are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which supplementation is emphasised in cirrhosis nutritional care?","options":["Only water-soluble vitamins","Vitamins especially fat soluble, electrolytes and minerals","Iron only","No supplementation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states vitamins especially fat soluble, electrolytes and minerals supplementation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Emphasis is on fat-soluble vitamins plus electrolytes/minerals, not water-soluble only, iron only, or none."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which substances should be avoided by a cirrhosis patient?","options":["Alcohol & hepatotoxic medication","Carbohydrates","Fat-soluble vitamins","Diuretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states alcohol & hepatotoxic medication should be avoided.  Why B/C/D wrong: Carbohydrates, vitamins and diuretics are part of care, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In a case of hepatic coma, a low protein diet is offered when the blood ammonia level increases above which value?","options":[">50 mcg/dl",">100 mcg/dl",">200 mcg/dl",">500 mcg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states offer low protein diet when blood ammonia level increases >200 mcg/dl in hepatic coma.  Why A/B/D wrong: These values do not match the stated threshold of >200 mcg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"For which procedure should the nurse prepare a cirrhosis client to remove abdominal fluid?","options":["Thoracentesis","Paracentesis","Lumbar puncture","Bone marrow aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states prepare the client for paracentesis to remove abdominal fluid.  Why A/C/D wrong: These procedures do not remove abdominal (peritoneal) fluid."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What does MELD score stand for?","options":["Model for End Stage Liver Disease Score","Measure of Elevated Liver Damage Score","Model for Estimating Liver Dysfunction Score","Marker of Early Liver Disease Score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MELD box states the full form is Model for End Stage Liver Disease Score.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The MELD score estimates a patient's chances of surviving with their liver disease during which time frame?","options":["The next 3 months","The next 1 year","The next 6 months","The next 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MELD estimates survival chances during the next 3 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 3-month estimate."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which parameters make up the MELD score? (SATA)","options":["Serum bilirubin","Serum creatinine","International Normalized Ratio (INR)","Serum sodium","Serum albumin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The page lists serum bilirubin, serum creatinine and INR as MELD parameters.  Why D/E wrong: Serum sodium and albumin are not listed as MELD parameters on this page."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What are the minimum and maximum MELD scores, and what does a higher score indicate?","options":["Minimum 0, maximum 100; lower risk of death","Minimum 6, maximum 40; higher score indicating greatest risk of death","Minimum 1, maximum 10; no prognostic value","Minimum 15, maximum 50; lower risk of death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the minimum MELD score is 6, maximum is 40, and higher score indicates the greatest risk of death.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges/interpretations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What MELD score is considered the best score for liver transplantation?","options":["MELD score <6","MELD score >15","MELD score exactly 40","MELD score = 0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the best score for liver transplantation is MELD score >15.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated transplantation threshold."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In hepatic encephalopathy, the liver is unable to metabolize neurotoxic ammonia into which less toxic form?","options":["Urea","Uric acid","Creatinine","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states the liver is unable to metabolize neurotoxic ammonia into the less toxic form urea, so ammonia increases in blood causing CNS disturbance.  Why B/C/D wrong: Uric acid, creatinine and glucose are not the conversion product."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why should the diet of a hepatic encephalopathy patient contain less protein?","options":["Because protein raises blood sugar","Because the diseased liver is unable to convert highly toxic ammonia, which is produced by protein metabolism","Because protein causes constipation","Because protein increases bile flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diet should contain less protein because the diseased liver is unable to convert highly toxic ammonia produced by protein metabolism.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for protein restriction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is the drug of choice in hepatic encephalopathy, and why?","options":["Neomycin, to kill intestinal bacteria because intestinal bacteria convert protein into ammonia","Furosemide, to remove ammonia","Ranitidine, to reduce acid","Propranolol, to reduce portal pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the drug of choice is neomycin to kill intestinal bacteria because those bacteria convert protein into ammonia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drugs are not the stated drug of choice for encephalopathy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Esophageal varices are BEST described as which of the following?","options":["Ulcers in the gastric mucosa","Dilated and tortuous veins in the sub-mucosa of the oesophagus","Polyps in the oesophageal lumen","Strictures of the oesophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines esophageal varices as dilated and tortuous veins in the sub-mucosa of the oesophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Ulcers, polyps and strictures are not the definition of varices."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The most common cause of esophageal varices is portal hypertension, which is mostly caused by which condition?","options":["Peptic ulcer disease","Liver cirrhosis","Gastritis","Hiatal hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common cause is portal hypertension, which is mostly caused by liver cirrhosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of the portal hypertension leading to varices."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Bleeding esophageal varices are an emergency because untreated they lead to circulatory compromise such as which of the following?","options":["Hypertension and bradycardia","Hypotension & shock","Hyperglycemia","Polyuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states bleeding varices are an emergency that must be treated immediately, otherwise circulatory compromise such as hypotension & shock will occur.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated consequence of bleeding varices."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign of esophageal varices?","options":["Hematemesis (blood in vomiting)","Constipation","Polyuria","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels hematemesis (blood in vomiting) as the cardinal sign of esophageal varices.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Melena in esophageal varices refers to which of the following?","options":["Upper GI bleeding or blood present in stool","Blood in urine","Blood in sputum","Clear watery stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines melena as upper GI bleeding or blood present in stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hematuria, hemoptysis and watery stool are not melena."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In esophageal varices, hepatomegaly and splenomegaly occur due to which underlying cause?","options":["Portal hypertension","Dehydration","Anemia","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hepatomegaly is enlargement/hypertrophy of the liver due to portal hypertension and splenomegaly is enlargement/hypertrophy of the spleen due to portal HTN.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not explain the organ enlargement here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Caput medusae, a sign in esophageal varices, is BEST described as which of the following?","options":["Dilated abdominal veins","Yellow sclera","Ascitic fluid wave","Spider-shaped rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes caput medusae as dilated abdominal veins.  Why B wrong: Yellow sclera is jaundice.  Why C wrong: Fluid wave suggests ascites.  Why D wrong: Spider-shaped rash is spider angioma."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are listed for esophageal varices? (SATA)","options":["Hematemesis and melena","Ascites and jaundice","Hepatomegaly and splenomegaly","Caput medusae and sign of shock","Bradycardia and constipation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists hematemesis, melena, ascites, jaundice, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, caput medusae and sign of shock.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia and constipation are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In esophageal varices, the nurse monitors especially for which circulatory sign on standing?","options":["Orthostatic hypotension","Orthostatic hypertension","Reflex bradycardia","Peripheral cyanosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states check all vitals and monitor for orthostatic hypotension.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated monitoring focus."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which position is used in nursing care of esophageal varices to promote comfort?","options":["Head end elevation or semi-Fowler position","Flat supine","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states position is head end elevation or semi-Fowler position to promote comfort.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not recommended here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"For a patient with esophageal varices, the nurse should watch vomits and stool for which finding?","options":["Occult blood","Excess mucus only","Fat globules","Undigested fiber"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states monitor for bleeding, watch vomits and stool for occult blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated monitoring focus for varices."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed management options for esophageal varices? (SATA)","options":["Sengstaken-Blakemore tube","Endoscopic sclerotherapy","Portacaval shunt","Distal splenorenal shunt and meso-caval shunt","Total colectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists Sengstaken-Blakemore tube, endoscopic sclerotherapy, and shunting procedures (portacaval, distal splenorenal, meso-caval).  Why E wrong: Total colectomy is a colon surgery, not a varices management."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The Sengstaken-Blakemore tube is which type of device and used for what?","options":["A single-lumen feeding tube for enteral nutrition","A three-way balloon tube inserted through the nose or mouth, used in management of upper GI tract bleeding due to esophageal varices","A rectal tube for enemas","A chest tube for pneumothorax"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it is a three-way balloon tube inserted through the nose or mouth for management of upper GI tract bleeding due to esophageal varices.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe unrelated tubes/uses."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the purpose of the Sengstaken-Blakemore tube?","options":["To feed the patient","To create minor pressure on the bleeding sites to prevent further bleeding","To drain gastric acid","To measure portal pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states its purpose is to create minor pressure on the bleeding sites to prevent further bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: Feeding, acid drainage and pressure measurement are not its stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"During Sengstaken-Blakemore tube care, which airway-related interventions are indicated? (SATA)","options":["Maintain patent airway","Suction when indicated","Maintain the tube in proper placement","Keep a scissor at bedside all time","Remove all suction equipment"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists maintaining patent airway, suction when indicated, maintaining proper tube placement, and keeping a scissor at bedside.  Why E wrong: Suction is needed, so removing suction equipment is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why should a scissor be kept at the bedside at all times for a patient with a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube (AIIMS 2017)?","options":["To adjust the balloon volume","If any discomfort in breathing occurs, the tube should immediately be cut & removed","To open medication packets","To cut the patient's dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states keep a scissor at bedside all time; if any discomfort in breathing occurs, then immediately cut & remove the tube.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for the bedside scissor."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What balloon pressure should be maintained with a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube?","options":["5-10 mmHg","30-35 mmHg","60-70 mmHg","100-110 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states maintain balloon pressure 30-35 mmHg, and deflate the balloon if any uneasiness occurs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pressures do not match the stated 30-35 mmHg."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"With a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube in place, the nurse should deflate the balloon under which circumstance?","options":["If any uneasiness will be there","Every 24 hours routinely regardless of symptoms","Only after 1 week","Never deflate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states deflate the balloon if any uneasiness will be there.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In endoscopic sclerotherapy for varices, what is done?","options":["A sclerosing agent is injected into & around bleeding varices","The varices are surgically excised","A balloon compresses the varices","Radiation is applied to the varices"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a sclerosing agent is injected into & around bleeding varices.  Why B/C/D wrong: Excision, balloon compression and radiation are not sclerotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Sclerosing agents used in endoscopic sclerotherapy are usually of which nature?","options":["Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Isotonic","Alkaline buffered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sclerosing agents are usually hypertonic in nature.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated hypertonic nature."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which are complications of endoscopic sclerotherapy? (SATA)","options":["Chest pains","Aspiration pneumonia","Perforation of the oesophagus","Splenomegaly","Renal failure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The page lists chest pains, aspiration pneumonia and perforation of the oesophagus as complications.  Why D/E wrong: Splenomegaly and renal failure are not listed as sclerotherapy complications."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) is an inflammatory condition of which structures?","options":["Small intestine & large intestine","Stomach only","Oesophagus only","Rectum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states IBD is an inflammatory disease condition of the small intestine & large intestine.  Why B/C/D wrong: IBD is not limited to the stomach, oesophagus or rectum alone."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What are the two types of Inflammatory Bowel Disease?","options":["Crohn's disease (regional enteritis) and ulcerative colitis","Gastritis and esophagitis","Appendicitis and diverticulitis","Peptic ulcer and GERD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states there are two types: Crohn's disease (regional enteritis) and ulcerative colitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two IBD types."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Crohn's disease can affect anywhere in the GI tract but MOSTLY affects which part?","options":["The stomach","The terminal ileum","The rectum","The oesophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Crohn's disease affects anywhere in the GI tract but mostly the terminal ileum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the most commonly affected site in Crohn's disease."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In Crohn's disease, the disease process causes which changes in the intestinal mucosa? (SATA)","options":["Thickening and scarring","Narrowed lumen","Fistulas and ulcerations","Abscess","Increased lumen diameter"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page states intestinal mucosa becomes thickening, scarring, narrowed lumen, fistulas, ulcerations and abscess may occur.  Why E wrong: The lumen narrows, it does not increase in diameter."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The abdominal pain of Crohn's disease is typically described as which of the following?","options":["Severe abdominal colicky or cramping pain usually after eating a meal","Painless","Burning pain relieved by food","Pain only at night"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states severe abdominal colicky or cramping pain usually after eating to meal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the Crohn's pain pattern described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The diarrhoea of Crohn's disease may contain which of the following?","options":["Mucus and pus","Fat globules only","Fresh red blood only","Bile only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diarrhoea which may contain mucus and pus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated diarrhoea content in Crohn's disease."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which signs/symptoms are listed for Crohn's disease? (SATA)","options":["Abdominal distension, anorexia, nausea and vomiting","Weight loss, dehydration, electrolyte imbalances & malnutrition","Fever & increased WBC & steatorrhea","Diarrhoea with mucus and pus","Bradycardia and hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists abdominal distension/anorexia/nausea/vomiting, weight loss/dehydration/electrolyte imbalance/malnutrition, fever/increased WBC/steatorrhea, and diarrhoea with mucus/pus.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia and hypertension are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is the confirmative diagnostic test for Crohn's disease?","options":["Endoscopy with biopsy","Complete blood count","Urinalysis","Chest X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diagnosis is made by endoscopy with biopsy (confirmative test).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the confirmative test for Crohn's disease."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The fecal fat test in Crohn's disease is used to determine which of the following?","options":["Fat in stool","Blood in stool","Bacteria in stool","Protein in stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fecal fat test determines fat in stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: It does not primarily measure blood, bacteria or protein."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The \"String sign\" in Crohn's disease represents which of the following, and is identified by which study?","options":["Stricture of the ileum due to disease process; identified by barium study","Dilation of the colon; identified by ultrasound","Free air under the diaphragm; identified by chest X-ray","Gallstones; identified by CT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the string sign is stricture of the ileum due to disease process, identified by barium study.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the string sign definition or its diagnostic study."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the nursing care of Crohn's disease, what dietary/nutrition steps are taken initially? (SATA)","options":["Keep NPO and maintain fluid & electrolyte balances","Start TPN when condition is severe, or as per order","Progress diet from clear liquid to semisolid","Provide low fat, low residue diet that creates less stool","Give a high-fat, high-residue diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists keeping NPO with fluid/electrolyte balance, TPN when severe/as ordered, clear liquid to semisolid progression, and low fat/low residue diet.  Why E wrong: A high-fat, high-residue diet is the opposite of the recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In Crohn's disease with a large portion of ileum involved, which nutrients are especially emphasised?","options":["High calories, carbohydrate, protein and vitamins especially vitamin-K & B12","Only fats","Only water-soluble vitamins","Iron only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states high calories, carbohydrate, protein and vitamins especially vita-K & B12 when a large portion of ileum is involved.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the emphasized nutrients."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In Crohn's disease, which items should be avoided because they irritate the intestinal mucosa?","options":["Laxatives (enema) & aspirin","Antibiotics","Fat-soluble vitamins","Oral rehydration solution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states avoid taking laxatives (enema) & aspirin that irritate intestinal mucosa.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the items to avoid for mucosal irritation here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Ulcerative colitis is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["Chronic ulcerative & inflammatory disease of the large bowel resulting in poor absorption of nutrients","Acute inflammation of the stomach","Inflammation of the appendix","Disease affecting only the terminal ileum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines ulcerative colitis as a chronic ulcerative & inflammatory disease of the large bowel that results in poor absorption of nutrients.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe ulcerative colitis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST commonly affected part in ulcerative colitis?","options":["Rectum","Terminal ileum","Stomach","Duodenum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common affected part is the rectum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most commonly affected site in ulcerative colitis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Ulcerative colitis commonly begins in the rectum and spreads in which direction?","options":["Upward toward the caecum","Downward to the anus only","Into the small intestine first","Into the stomach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it commonly begins in the rectum and spreads upward toward the caecum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described spread pattern."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is a classical barium X-ray finding of chronic ulcerative colitis?","options":["String sign","Lead pipe colon","Bird-beak sign","Cobblestone appearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states lead pipe colon in barium X-ray is a classical finding of chronic ulcerative colitis.  Why A wrong: String sign is a Crohn's disease finding.  Why C/D wrong: Bird-beak and cobblestone are not the stated UC finding here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis, the oedematous colon may develop ulcers that can lead to which serious event?","options":["Perforation","Healing without scar","Increased nutrient absorption","Reduced colon cancer risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the colon becomes oedematous and may develop bleeding, lesions and ulcers, and the ulcers may lead to perforation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contrary to the described disease course."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis, later development of scar tissue causes which changes?","options":["Loss of elasticity & loss of the ability to absorb nutrients","Increased peristalsis and better absorption","Thickening of muscle only","Reversal of inflammation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states later scar tissue developed causes loss of elasticity & loss of the ability to absorb nutrients.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated consequences of scarring."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Ulcerative colitis is associated with an increased risk of which cancer?","options":["Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Gastric cancer","Pancreatic cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it is associated with increased risk of colon cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cancers are not the associated risk stated here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which are listed causes of ulcerative colitis? (SATA)","options":["Autoimmune response","Genetic","Bacterial infections","Emotional stress","Excess dietary fiber"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists autoimmune response, genetic, bacterial infections & emotional stress etc. as causes.  Why E wrong: Excess dietary fiber is not listed as a cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which are identified complications of ulcerative colitis (of the bowel mucosa)? (SATA)","options":["Vascular congestion","Haemorrhage","Oedema","Ulceration of the bowel mucosa","Increased nutrient absorption"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists vascular congestion, haemorrhage, oedema and ulceration of the bowel mucosa as identified complications.  Why E wrong: Absorption is impaired, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are types of ulcerative colitis shown in the figure? (SATA)","options":["Proctitis and proctosigmoiditis","Distal colitis","Extensive colitis","Pancolitis","Terminal ileitis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The figure shows proctitis, proctosigmoiditis, distal colitis, extensive colitis and pancolitis as types of ulcerative colitis.  Why E wrong: Terminal ileitis is a Crohn's-type distribution, not a UC type shown."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign of ulcerative colitis regarding stool?","options":["Severe diarrhoea with frequent passage of bloody, purulent, mucoid or watery stool","Constipation with hard pellets","Clay-colored stool","Greasy floating stool only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels severe diarrhoea with frequent passage of bloody, purulent, mucoid or watery stool as the cardinal sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cardinal stool sign of UC."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which nutrition/systemic findings are cited as a cardinal sign group in ulcerative colitis? (SATA)","options":["Malnutrition","Dehydration","Anorexia","Weight loss","Weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists malnutrition, dehydration, anorexia and weight loss (cardinal sign).  Why E wrong: Weight loss (not weight gain) is the finding."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which of the following are additional signs & symptoms of ulcerative colitis? (SATA)","options":["Abdominal tenderness and cramping","Anaemia","Vitamin-K deficiency","Severe bloody diarrhoea","Polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists abdominal tenderness/cramping, anaemia, vitamin-K deficiency and bloody diarrhoea.  Why E wrong: Polyuria is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the acute phase of ulcerative colitis, the nurse should do which of the following?","options":["Keep NPO & give IV fluids","Give a large high-fiber meal","Encourage vigorous activity","Give milk products"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in acute phase keep NPO & give IV fluids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contraindicated or not part of acute-phase UC care."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why is the ulcerative colitis client placed on bed rest?","options":["To reduce intestinal activity to prevent further progression of the disease","To prevent bedsores","To improve appetite","To increase peristalsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states place the client on bed rest to reduce intestinal activity to prevent further progression of the disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for bed rest."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis nursing care, the nurse should assess for which complications? (SATA)","options":["Dehydration","Rectal haemorrhage","Electrolyte imbalances","Fever","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page states assess for complications like dehydration, rectal haemorrhage, electrolyte imbalances & fever.  Why E wrong: Hyperglycemia is not listed as a complication to assess."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis nursing care, which additional assessments are performed? (SATA)","options":["Check bowel sounds and abdominal tenderness","Watch stool's colour and presence of blood & mucus","Assess for bowel perforation & peritonitis","Assess for complications like dehydration and fever","Encourage laxative use"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists checking bowel sounds/tenderness, watching stool colour/blood/mucus, assessing for perforation/peritonitis, and assessing for dehydration/rectal haemorrhage/electrolyte imbalance/fever.  Why E wrong: Laxatives irritate the mucosa and are not encouraged."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis, what diet is recommended during the acute or exacerbation phase?","options":["Low residue diet to regular diet","High residue diet","High fat diet","High milk diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during acute or exacerbation phase, use a low residue diet to regular diet.  Why B/C/D wrong: High residue, high fat and high milk diets are not recommended in the exacerbation phase."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"During the maintenance phase of ulcerative colitis, the client is instructed to eat which type of meals?","options":["Small frequent meals of high protein, high calorie foods, low fat diets to decrease steatorrhea","Three large high-fat meals","Only clear liquids indefinitely","High residue, high fiber meals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during maintenance phase, eat small frequent meals of high protein, high calorie, low fat foods to decrease steatorrhea.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the maintenance-phase dietary instruction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis, which foods should be avoided?","options":["Irritating foods, spicy foods, and all food allergens, especially milk & milk products","All carbohydrates","All proteins","All fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states avoid irritating foods and spicy foods and all food allergens, especially milk & milk products.  Why B/C/D wrong: Carbohydrates, proteins and fluids are not blanket-avoided."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis with ileal involvement causing pernicious anaemia, what is advised?","options":["To give monthly vitamin B12","To give daily oral iron only","To restrict all protein","To give weekly folic acid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to give monthly vitamin B12 if pernicious anaemia is present due to ileal involvement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In ulcerative colitis, which common intolerance means all dairy products should be avoided?","options":["Lactose intolerance","Gluten intolerance","Fructose intolerance","Histamine intolerance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states lactose intolerance is common so should avoid all dairy products.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the intolerance cited here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Surgical management of ulcerative colitis may include which procedures?","options":["Segmental or partial colectomy and total colectomy with incontinent or Kock ileostomy","Appendectomy only","Cholecystectomy","Gastrectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states surgical management includes segmental or partial colectomy and total colectomy with incontinent or Kock ileostomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These operations are unrelated to UC surgical management."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In the comparison table, what is the LOCATION of Crohn's disease versus ulcerative colitis?","options":["Crohn's: terminal ileum (most common)/anywhere in GI tract; UC: rectum (most common) or large intestine","Crohn's: rectum only; UC: terminal ileum only","Both affect only the stomach","Crohn's: large intestine only; UC: small intestine only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's location is terminal ileum (most common)/anywhere in the GI tract; UC is rectum (most common) or large intestine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the locations."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the pattern of progression is which for Crohn's vs UC?","options":["Crohn's: irregular; UC: proximally continuous","Crohn's: continuous; UC: irregular","Both irregular","Both continuous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's progression is irregular and UC is proximally continuous.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the thickness of inflammation differs how?","options":["Crohn's: trans-mural; UC: sub-mucosa or mucosa","Crohn's: mucosa only; UC: trans-mural","Both trans-mural","Both mucosa only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's inflammation is trans-mural while UC is sub-mucosa or mucosa.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the characteristic SYMPTOM differs how?","options":["Crohn's: crampy abdominal pain; UC: bloody diarrhoea","Crohn's: bloody diarrhoea; UC: crampy abdominal pain","Both crampy abdominal pain","Both bloody diarrhoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's symptom is crampy abdominal pain and UC is bloody diarrhoea.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or duplicate the table's symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the COMPLICATIONS differ how?","options":["Crohn's: fistulas (common), abscess & obstruction, nutritional def.; UC: haemorrhage, toxic & mega-colon, nutritional def.","Crohn's: haemorrhage & mega-colon; UC: fistulas & abscess","Both have fistulas only","Neither has nutritional deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Crohn's complications as fistulas (common), abscess & obstruction, nutritional def.; UC as haemorrhage, toxic & mega-colon, nutritional def.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the complications."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the radiographic finding is which for Crohn's vs UC?","options":["Crohn's: string sign; UC: lead pipe colon","Crohn's: lead pipe colon; UC: string sign","Both string sign","Both lead pipe colon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's shows string sign in barium X-ray and UC shows lead pipe colon in barium X-ray.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or duplicate the findings."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the risk of colon cancer differs how?","options":["Crohn's: slight increase; UC: marked increase","Crohn's: marked increase; UC: slight increase","Both no increase","Both marked increase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's has a slight increase and UC has a marked increase in colon cancer risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or duplicate the risk levels."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the age of onset differs how?","options":["Crohn's: 10-40 years; UC: 10-30 years","Crohn's: 10-30 years; UC: 10-40 years","Both 20-50 years","Both 40-60 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's age of onset is 10-40 years and UC is 10-30 years.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the ages."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"According to the comparison table, the number of stools per day differs how?","options":["Crohn's: 10-20 liquid, bloody stools per day; UC: 5-6 soft, loose stools per day, usually nonbloody","Crohn's: 5-6 nonbloody stools; UC: 10-20 bloody stools","Both 1-2 stools per day","Both 10-20 bloody stools"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Crohn's produces 10-20 liquid, bloody stools per day and UC produces 5-6 soft, loose stools per day, usually nonbloody.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the stool numbers."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In pre-operative nursing care for colostomy surgery, which of the following are done? (SATA)","options":["Take consent & keep NPO","Give low fiber diet for 1-2 days before surgery","Give antibiotics","Give laxatives & enemas before night of surgery as per doctor order","Give high fiber diet the morning of surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists consent/NPO, low fiber diet for 1-2 days before surgery, antibiotics, and laxatives/enemas before the night of surgery per order.  Why E wrong: A high fiber diet is not part of pre-op bowel prep here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"For how long is a low fiber diet given before colostomy surgery?","options":["1-2 days before surgery","1 week before surgery","1 month before surgery","Only on the morning of surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low fiber diet for 1-2 days before surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 1-2 days."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In immediate post-operative colostomy care, which suction is maintained?","options":["NG suction","Chest tube suction","Wound vacuum only","No suction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states maintain NG suction during the immediate post-operative period.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated suction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In post-operative colostomy care, how is the stoma kept moist?","options":["By placing a sterile gauze piece","By applying alcohol swabs","By leaving it exposed to air","By covering it with adhesive tape"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states keep the stoma moist by placing a sterile gauze piece.  Why B/C/D wrong: These would not keep the stoma appropriately moist per the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In post-operative colostomy care, when should a pouch system be placed on the stoma?","options":["As soon as possible","After 1 week","Only when the stoma turns black","After the first bowel movement only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states place a pouch system on the stoma as soon as possible.  Why B/C/D wrong: These delay pouch placement inappropriately."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"In post-operative colostomy care, the nurse monitors the stoma for which features? (SATA)","options":["Size","Unusual bleeding","Necrotic tissues","Functioning of the colostomy","Skin turgor of the face"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The page lists monitoring the stoma for size, unusual bleeding or necrotic tissues and assessing the functioning of the colostomy.  Why E wrong: Facial skin turgor is not a stoma monitoring item."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The nurse should instruct the client that colostomy drainage will begin within which time frame?","options":["Within 3-4 days","Within 1 hour","Within 2 weeks","Within 1 month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states instruct the client that colostomy drainage will begin within 3-4 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 3-4 days."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Regarding stool consistency by colon area, which matching is CORRECT?","options":["Ascending colon: liquid stool; Transverse colon: semisolid stool; Descending colon: solid or normal stool","Ascending colon: solid; Transverse colon: liquid; Descending colon: semisolid","Ascending colon: semisolid; Descending colon: liquid","All areas produce liquid stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ascending colon = liquid stool, transverse colon = semisolid stool, descending colon = solid or as a normal stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the stool consistency by area."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"At what fullness should the colostomy pouch be emptied?","options":["When it is 1/3 full","When it is completely full","When it is 2/3 full","Only once per day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states empty the pouch when it is 1/3 full.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1/3-full instruction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Why should fecal matter not be allowed to remain on the skin around the stoma?","options":["To prevent skin excoriation","To reduce odor only","To improve appearance","To prevent constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states fecal matter should not remain on the skin to prevent skin excoriation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated primary rationale."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"The client is taught to periodically dilate the stoma to prevent which complication?","options":["Strictures","Bleeding","Necrosis","Prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states teach the client to periodically dilate the stoma to prevent strictures.  Why B/C/D wrong: Stoma dilation is aimed at preventing strictures, not these."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Regarding post-operative diet after colostomy, which is advised?","options":["Initially offer low residue diet to promote healing and avoid seeds that can cause obstruction","High residue diet immediately","Only clear liquids permanently","High seed intake to bulk stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states initially offer low residue diet to promote healing and avoid seeds that can cause obstruction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated post-op diet."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Which is the NORMAL stoma colour (PGI-2020)?","options":["Pink to bright red, shiny, indicating high vascularity","Purple","Black","Pale white"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normal stoma colour is pink to bright red, shiny, indicating high vascularity.  Why B/C wrong: Purple/black indicate inadequate perfusion/compromise.  Why D wrong: Pale/white is not the normal colour."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A pale, pink stoma indicates which of the following?","options":["Low Hb & haematocrit level","High vascularity","Circulatory compromise","Stoma stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low Hb level: pale, pink stoma indicates low Hb & haematocrit level.  Why B wrong: High vascularity produces a bright red stoma.  Why C wrong: Circulatory compromise gives purple/dusky/black.  Why D wrong: Stenosis is a narrowed/flattened stoma, not a colour finding."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A purple, dusky blue or black stoma indicates which of the following (ESIC-2024)?","options":["Inadequate perfusion / circulatory compromise, which if it occurs must be reported to the doctor immediately, otherwise necrosis will occur","Normal healthy stoma","Low Hb only","Stoma stenosis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states purple, dusky blue & black stoma indicate circulatory compromise, and must be reported to the doctor immediately, otherwise necrosis will occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the meaning of a dusky/purple/black stoma."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Stenosis in a stoma is described as which of the following?","options":["Narrowed or constricted or flattened stoma","A bright red, protruding stoma","A pale pink stoma","A pouch leakage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stenosis in stoma is narrowed or constricted or flattened stoma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A stoma that is purple-black in colour may indicate compromised circulation; what is the nurse's immediate action (Combined AIIMS-2020)?","options":["Immediately inform the duty doctor","Continue routine care and reassess in a week","Apply pressure to the stoma","Increase oral fluids only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a purple-black stoma may indicate compromised circulation; in that condition, immediately inform the duty doctor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These delay or do not address the urgent circulatory compromise."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"When applying the colostomy bag, the opening should be how far from the stoma (AIIMS Rishikesh & Delhi-2019)?","options":["1/8 inch away from the stoma","1 inch away from the stoma","1/2 inch away from the stoma","Directly over the stoma with no gap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states apply the colostomy bag with an opening of 1/8 inch away from the stoma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1/8 inch opening."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"Appendicitis is defined as inflammation & compromised circulation of the vermiform appendix, which is described as which part of the large colon?","options":["The first part of the large colon","The last part of the large colon","Part of the small intestine","Part of the rectum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states appendicitis is inflammation & compromised circulation of the vermiform appendix, which is the first part of the large colon.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the page's description."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the usual length of the vermiform appendix?","options":["9-10 cm","2-3 cm","15-20 cm","30-40 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the appendix's length is usually 9-10 cm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These lengths do not match the stated 9-10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST common position of the appendix, and its frequency (RRB-2019)?","options":["Retrocecal position, 64%","Pelvic position, 90%","Subcecal position, 50%","Preileal position, 30%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common position of the appendix is the retrocecal position, 64%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions/percentages do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"When the appendix becomes inflamed or infected, rupture may occur within hours, leading to which of the following?","options":["Necrosis and peritonitis","Spontaneous healing","Gallstone formation","Gastric ulcer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states when inflamed/infected, rupture may occur within hours, leading to necrosis and peritonitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated consequences of appendiceal rupture."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of appendicitis?","options":["Fecalith (fecal matter)","Viral infection","Autoimmune reaction","Alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common cause of appendicitis is fecalith (fecal matter).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"What is the MOST common complication of appendicitis?","options":["Wound infection after surgery","Liver failure","Renal stones","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common complication of appendicitis is wound infection after surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A patient with cirrhosis has a blood ammonia level of 250 mcg/dl and is in hepatic coma. Which diet is MOST appropriate?","options":["Low protein diet","High protein diet","High fat diet","Protein loading"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in hepatic coma offer low protein diet when blood ammonia increases >200 mcg/dl; 250 mcg/dl exceeds this threshold.  Why B/D wrong: High/loaded protein worsens ammonia in hepatic coma.  Why C wrong: High fat is not the indicated intervention for elevated ammonia."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A patient with a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube suddenly develops breathing difficulty. What is the nurse's PRIORITY action?","options":["Immediately cut & remove the tube using the bedside scissor","Increase the balloon pressure","Give oxygen and continue observing","Reposition the patient to prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states keep a scissor at bedside; if breathing discomfort occurs, immediately cut & remove the tube (airway is the priority).  Why B wrong: Increasing balloon pressure could worsen airway obstruction.  Why C wrong: Merely observing delays relief of a life-threatening airway obstruction.  Why D wrong: Prone positioning is not the indicated response."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A postoperative colostomy patient's stoma appears dusky blue and cool. What is the nurse's BEST immediate action?","options":["Immediately inform the duty doctor because it indicates circulatory compromise and risk of necrosis","Document as a normal finding","Apply a warm compress and reassess in 24 hours","Empty the pouch and continue routine care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states purple/dusky blue/black stoma indicates circulatory compromise and must be reported to the doctor immediately, otherwise necrosis will occur.  Why B wrong: A dusky stoma is abnormal, not normal.  Why C/D wrong: These delay urgent reporting of compromised circulation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A cirrhosis patient with hepatic encephalopathy is prescribed a medication to reduce ammonia-producing intestinal bacteria. Which drug is the drug of choice?","options":["Neomycin","Warfarin","Furosemide","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page names neomycin as the drug of choice to kill intestinal bacteria that convert protein into ammonia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Warfarin, furosemide and insulin do not reduce ammonia-producing gut bacteria."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A nurse reviews that in cirrhosis the liver cannot produce adequate bile for fat emulsification. Which consequence directly results?","options":["Disruption of fat-soluble vitamins A, K, D, E, contributing to bleeding risk","Excess absorption of vitamin C","Increased platelet count","Improved clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page links inadequate bile → disruption of fat-soluble vitamins A, K, D, E, and vitamin K deficiency (with factors II, VII, IX, X) causes bleeding risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contrary to the described coagulation defect."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A patient presents with hematemesis, melena, ascites, jaundice, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly and caput medusae. Which condition is MOST consistent with these findings?","options":["Esophageal varices (with underlying portal hypertension/cirrhosis)","Acute appendicitis","Ulcerative colitis","Pyloric stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists exactly these as signs & symptoms of esophageal varices, reflecting portal hypertension from cirrhosis.  Why B wrong: Appendicitis presents with RLQ pain, not variceal bleeding signs.  Why C wrong: UC presents with bloody diarrhoea, not caput medusae/hepatomegaly.  Why D wrong: Pyloric stenosis presents with projectile vomiting, not these portal-hypertension signs."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A patient has crampy abdominal pain, a barium study showing a \"string sign,\" and disease mainly in the terminal ileum. Which diagnosis is MOST likely?","options":["Crohn's disease","Ulcerative colitis","Appendicitis","Cardiac cirrhosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table links crampy abdominal pain, string sign in barium X-ray, and terminal ileum location to Crohn's disease.  Why B wrong: UC shows lead pipe colon, bloody diarrhoea and rectal location.  Why C/D wrong: Appendicitis and cardiac cirrhosis do not fit these features."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Liver Cirrhosis…","question":"A patient has bloody diarrhoea, a \"lead pipe colon\" on barium X-ray, disease starting in the rectum, and a marked increase in colon cancer risk. Which diagnosis is MOST likely?","options":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Appendicitis","Portal hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table links bloody diarrhoea, lead pipe colon, rectal location and marked increase in colon cancer risk to ulcerative colitis.  Why B wrong: Crohn's shows string sign and terminal ileum involvement.  Why C/D wrong: Appendicitis and portal hypertension do not fit these features."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the treatment of choice for appendicitis?","options":["Cholecystectomy","Appendectomy (surgical removal of appendix)","Herniorrhaphy","Colostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the treatment of choice for appendicitis is appendectomy (surgical removal of appendix).  Why A wrong: Cholecystectomy is removal of the gallbladder, unrelated to appendicitis.  Why C wrong: Herniorrhaphy is repair of a hernia defect.  Why D wrong: Colostomy is a stoma creation, not the treatment for appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The McBurney incision, also called the gridiron incision, is best described as:","options":["A vertical midline incision in the epigastrium","An oblique incision made in the right lower quadrant of the abdomen, used for appendectomy","A transverse incision in the left lower quadrant","A subcostal incision in the right upper quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: McBurney incision/gridiron incision is an oblique incision made in the right lower quadrant of the abdomen, used for appendectomy.  Why A wrong: It is not a vertical midline epigastric incision.  Why C wrong: The incision is in the right lower quadrant, not the left lower quadrant.  Why D wrong: A right upper quadrant subcostal incision (Kocher) is used for gallbladder surgery."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In appendicitis, severe pain classically occurs at which anatomic point?","options":["Murphy's point","McBurney's point","Charcot's point","Boas's point"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe pain at McBurney's point (in the periumbilical area that radiates to the right lower quadrant region) is the classic finding.  Why A wrong: Murphy's sign relates to gallbladder disease, not appendicitis.  Why C wrong: \"Charcot's point\" is not the appendicitis pain point.  Why D wrong: Boas's point relates to gallbladder referred pain, not appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The severe pain of appendicitis begins in the periumbilical area and then radiates to which region?","options":["Left lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Suprapubic region"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pain at McBurney's point is in the periumbilical area that radiates to the right lower quadrant region.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies radiation to the right lower quadrant, not the left lower quadrant, right upper quadrant, or suprapubic region."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Anorexia in appendicitis is also known by which eponymous sign?","options":["Blumberg's sign","Hamberger's sign","Kocher's sign","Dunphy's sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anorexia is also known in appendicitis as Hamberger's sign.  Why A wrong: Blumberg's sign is rebound tenderness.  Why C wrong: Kocher's sign is pain shifting from the umbilical region to the right iliac region.  Why D wrong: Dunphy's sign is pain in the RLQ increased with coughing."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Rebound tenderness in appendicitis is also known as which sign, and what does it refer to?","options":["Rovsing's sign; pain in RLQ on palpating the LLQ","Blumberg sign; pain upon removal of pressure rather than application of pressure to the abdomen","Psoas sign; pain on active hip extension","Obturator sign; pain on internal hip rotation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rebound tenderness is also known as Blumberg sign and refers to pain upon removal of pressure rather than application of pressure to the abdomen.  Why A wrong: That describes Rovsing's sign.  Why C wrong: That describes the psoas sign.  Why D wrong: That describes the obturator sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs/symptoms of appendicitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nausea & vomiting","Abdominal distension","Paralytic ileus","Low grade fever or elevated leucocytes","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists nausea & vomiting, abdominal distension, paralytic ileus, and low grade fever or elevated leucocytes among appendicitis features.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding (haematochezia) is a feature of internal haemorrhoids, not appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A client with suspected appendicitis is often found in which characteristic position?","options":["Prone with legs extended","Side lying position with abdominal guarding and leg flexed","Trendelenburg position","Sitting upright leaning forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client is in a side lying position with abdominal guarding and leg flexed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not the characteristic guarding posture described for appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Aure-Rozanova's sign, in which pain increases when palpating at the right Petit triangle point with the finger, is typically seen in which type of appendicitis?","options":["Pelvic appendicitis","Retrocecal appendicitis","Subhepatic appendicitis","Preileal appendicitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Aure-Rozanova's sign (pain increased when palpating at the right Petit triangle point with a finger) is typically seen in retrocecal appendicitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically links this sign to retrocecal appendicitis, not pelvic, subhepatic, or preileal positions."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Dunphy's sign in appendicitis refers to:","options":["Pain increased within the right lower quadrant with coughing","Pain on internal rotation of the hip","Pain shifting from the umbilicus to the right iliac region","Pain on removal of palpating pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dunphy's sign is pain increased within the right lower quadrant with coughing.  Why B wrong: That is the obturator sign.  Why C wrong: That is Kocher's sign.  Why D wrong: That is rebound tenderness/Blumberg sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Kocher's sign in appendicitis is described as:","options":["Pain first at the RLQ then moving to the umbilicus","Pain that first started at the umbilical region then shifts to the right iliac region","Pain only on coughing","Pain on left lower quadrant palpation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kocher's sign is that pain firstly started at the umbilical region then shifts to the right iliac region.  Why A wrong: The direction is reversed; it moves from the umbilicus to the right iliac region.  Why C wrong: Pain with coughing is Dunphy's sign.  Why D wrong: Pain elicited by LLQ palpation is Rovsing's sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In appendicitis, which three special signs are typically positive together, as noted in the source?","options":["Murphy, Cullen, and Grey-Turner signs","Psoas sign, Obturator sign, and Rovsing's sign","Chvostek, Trousseau, and Homan's signs","Kernig, Brudzinski, and Battle's signs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there is a positive sign of Psoas sign, Obturator sign, and Rovsing's sign.  Why A/C/D wrong: These sign groupings relate to pancreatitis/bleeding, tetany, meningitis, etc., not the appendicitis triad listed."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which scoring system is used for assessment of appendicitis severity?","options":["Ranson score","Alvarado score","Glasgow Coma Scale","APACHE II score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Alvarado score is used for assessment of appendicitis severity.  Why A wrong: Ranson score assesses pancreatitis severity.  Why C wrong: GCS assesses level of consciousness.  Why D wrong: APACHE II is a general ICU severity score, not the appendicitis-specific tool named."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Diagnosis of appendicitis is made by which findings?","options":["ECG and troponin","USG finding and CBC showing increased WBC count","Lumbar puncture","Bronchoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis is made by USG (ultrasound) finding and CBC showing an increased WBC count.  Why A/C/D wrong: ECG/troponin (cardiac), lumbar puncture (CNS), and bronchoscopy (respiratory) are unrelated to the diagnosis described."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to the \"Signs of Appendicitis\" table, McBurney's point is located at what distance and along which line?","options":["One-third of the distance from the umbilicus to the left ASIS","Two-thirds of the distance along a line from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine (right iliac abdominal quadrant)","Midway between the two ASIS","At the level of the pubic tubercle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states McBurney's point is located at two-thirds of the distance along a line from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, in the right iliac abdominal quadrant.  Why A wrong: It is two-thirds (not one-third) and to the right (not left) ASIS.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described locations."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"How is the Psoas sign elicited according to the table?","options":["Internal rotation of the hip while supine","With the client in supine position, the client feels pain on right leg (psoas muscle) active extension and passive flexion","Deep palpation of the left lower quadrant","Coughing while standing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states that while the client is supine, the client feels pain with right leg (psoas muscle) active extension and passive flexion.  Why A wrong: Internal hip rotation elicits the obturator sign.  Why C wrong: LLQ deep palpation elicits Rovsing's sign.  Why D wrong: Coughing elicits Dunphy's sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The Obturator sign is positive when the client feels pain with:","options":["Active extension of the right leg","Internal rotation of the hip","Deep palpation of the left lower quadrant","Removal of palpation pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the client may feel pain with internal rotation of the hip (obturator sign).  Why A wrong: Leg extension pain is the psoas sign.  Why C wrong: LLQ palpation pain is Rovsing's sign.  Why D wrong: Pain on pressure removal is rebound/Blumberg sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Rovsing's sign is positive when:","options":["Deep palpation of the left lower quadrant of the abdomen produces increased pain felt in the right lower quadrant","Palpation of the RLQ produces pain in the LLQ","The hip is internally rotated","The client coughs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states that if deep palpation of the left lower quadrant increases pain felt in the right lower quadrant, Rovsing's sign is positive.  Why B wrong: The referred pain direction is LLQ palpation causing RLQ pain, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: Internal hip rotation is the obturator sign.  Why D wrong: Coughing pain is Dunphy's sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the Alvarado score, how many points are given for right lower quadrant tenderness?","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","0 points"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Alvarado score table assigns 2 points to right lower quadrant tenderness.  Why A/C/D wrong: The table specifically assigns 2 points, not 1, 3, or 0."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the Alvarado score, elevated temperature is scored 1 point when the temperature exceeds:","options":["98.6 °F","99.1 °F","100.4 °F","101 °F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns 1 point for elevated temperature greater than 99.1 °F.  Why A/C/D wrong: The threshold stated is >99.1 °F, not these other values."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the Alvarado score, how many points are assigned to rebound tenderness?","options":["1","2","3","0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns 1 point to rebound tenderness under the \"Signs\" category.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rebound tenderness = 1 point, not 2, 3, or 0."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Under the Alvarado score \"Symptoms\" category, which of the following each score 1 point? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Anorexia","Nausea or vomiting","Migration of pain to right lower quadrant","Right lower quadrant tenderness","Leukocytosis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Symptoms,\" anorexia (1), nausea or vomiting (1), and migration of pain to the RLQ (1) each score 1 point.  Why D wrong: RLQ tenderness is under \"Signs\" and scores 2 points.  Why E wrong: Leukocytosis is under \"Laboratory Values\" and scores 2 points."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the Alvarado score laboratory values, leukocytosis is defined and scored as:","options":[">10,000 WBC = 2 points",">5,000 WBC = 1 point",">15,000 WBC = 3 points",">20,000 WBC = 2 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines leukocytosis as >10,000 WBC and assigns it 2 points.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated cutoff is >10,000 WBC scoring 2 points, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the Alvarado score, \"left shift\" scoring 1 point is defined as:","options":[">50% neutrophils",">75% neutrophils",">90% lymphocytes",">25% bands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines left shift as >75% neutrophils, scoring 1 point.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated threshold is >75% neutrophils, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to the Alvarado score result interpretation, what total score indicates appendicitis?","options":["Total score 10 and >7 indicate appendicitis","A score of 3 indicates appendicitis","Any score above 2","Only a score of exactly 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the total score is 10 and a score >7 indicates appendicitis.  Why B/C wrong: Low scores do not indicate appendicitis per the table.  Why D wrong: A score >7 (not only exactly 10) indicates appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Malabsorption syndrome is best defined as:","options":["A condition in which anywhere in the digestive tract there is failure of absorption of ingested nutrients","Excessive absorption of nutrients","Inflammation of the peritoneum","Twisting of the intestine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines malabsorption syndrome as a condition in which anywhere in the digestive tract there is failure of absorption of ingested nutrients.  Why B wrong: It is failure of absorption, not excessive absorption.  Why C wrong: Inflammation of the peritoneum is peritonitis.  Why D wrong: Twisting of the intestine is volvulus."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Malabsorption syndrome is primarily caused by which of the following?","options":["Celiac sprue or lactase deficiency","Hypertension","Diabetes insipidus","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states malabsorption is primarily caused by celiac sprue or lactase deficiency.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the primary causes named for malabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed as other precipitating factors of malabsorption syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Short bowel syndrome or GI surgeries","Inflammatory diseases","Infection","Radiation or drug side effects","Physical exercise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other precipitating factors listed include short bowel syndrome or GI surgeries, inflammatory diseases, infection, and radiation or drug side effects.  Why E wrong: Physical exercise is not listed as a precipitating factor."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In malabsorption syndrome, loud bowel sounds accompanying abdominal distension are also known as:","options":["Borborygmus","Steatorrhea","Hematochezia","Obstipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abdominal distension with loud bowel sounds is also known as Borborygmus.  Why B wrong: Steatorrhea is fatty, foul stool.  Why C wrong: Hematochezia is rectal bleeding.  Why D wrong: Obstipation is chronic constipation."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of malabsorption syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Anorexia, weight loss, flatulence","Diarrhoea or bulky stool with foul odour, dehydration","Low serum levels of Hb, haematocrit, albumin and WBC","Lactose intolerance","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed features include anorexia/weight loss/flatulence, diarrhoea or bulky foul-smelling stool with dehydration, low serum Hb/haematocrit/albumin/WBC, and lactose intolerance.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is a haemorrhoid feature, not listed for malabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In malabsorption syndrome, laboratory findings typically show low serum levels of which of the following?","options":["Only sodium and potassium","Hb, haematocrit, albumin and WBC","Only calcium","Only glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states low serum levels of Hb, haematocrit level, albumin and WBC.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically lists Hb, haematocrit, albumin, and WBC, not these other single-item options."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A key nursing care measure in malabsorption syndrome is to use which type of diet?","options":["High-gluten diet","Gluten-free diet such as millet, corn, rice","High-lactose diet","High-fat, high-caffeine diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes using a gluten-free diet such as millet, corn, rice, etc.  Why A wrong: Gluten protein must be avoided, not increased.  Why C wrong: Milk/lactose should be restricted, not increased.  Why D wrong: This is not the recommended dietary measure."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In malabsorption syndrome (celiac type), which gluten-containing foods should the patient AVOID? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Wheat","Rye","Oats","Barley","Rice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to avoid gluten protein diet such as wheat, rye, oats and barley.  Why E wrong: Rice is a gluten-free grain and is recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Why should milk or milk products be restricted in a patient with malabsorption syndrome?","options":["Because milk contains gluten","Because milk contains lactose","Because milk is high in fiber","Because milk raises blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states milk or milk products should be restricted because milk contains lactose (relevant to lactase deficiency/lactose intolerance).  Why A wrong: Milk contains lactose, not gluten.  Why C wrong: Milk is not a high-fiber food.  Why D wrong: The reason given is lactose content, not glucose."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which nursing observations are appropriate in malabsorption syndrome? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitor eating pattern, fluid status and electrolyte balance","Observe stool colour, consistency and steatorrhea","Avoid precipitating triggers or possible causative factors as much as possible","Encourage a high-gluten diet","Restrict milk/milk products"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring eating pattern/fluid/electrolytes, observing stool colour/consistency/steatorrhea, avoiding precipitating triggers, and restricting milk products.  Why D wrong: A high-gluten diet would worsen celiac-related malabsorption; a gluten-free diet is used."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to the malabsorption flow chart, gluten protein (in wheat, rye, oats, barley) breaks down into which substance in the small intestine?","options":["Gliadin","Casein","Lactase","Pepsin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows gluten protein breaks down into gliadin in the small intestine.  Why B wrong: Casein is a milk protein.  Why C wrong: Lactase is an enzyme, not a gluten breakdown product.  Why D wrong: Pepsin is a gastric enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the malabsorption flow chart, celiac disease is characterized as the inability to digest which substance, leading to accumulation of glutamine with a toxic effect on mucosal cells?","options":["Gliadin (gliactin)","Lactose","Fructose","Sucrose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart describes celiac disease as the inability to digest gliadin (written \"gliactin\"), causing accumulation of glutamine with a toxic effect on mucosal cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sugars are not the substances named in the celiac pathway of the flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to the flow chart, the toxic effect of accumulated glutamine on mucosal cells leads to which change that results in malabsorption?","options":["Hypertrophy of villi","Atrophy of villi","Perforation of the colon","Volvulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows atrophy of villi leading to malabsorption.  Why A wrong: The villi atrophy (shrink), not hypertrophy.  Why C/D wrong: Perforation and volvulus are not part of this celiac pathway."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Per the malabsorption flow chart, atrophy of villi and malabsorption ultimately result in deficiencies of which nutrients?","options":["Fat, calorie, carbohydrate, and vitamin deficiencies","Only protein deficiency","Only water-soluble vitamins","Only iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart ends in fat, calorie, carbohydrate, and vitamin deficiencies.  Why B/C/D wrong: The chart lists a broad set (fat, calorie, carbohydrate, vitamins), not a single nutrient."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the malabsorption flow chart, the \"celiac crisis\" branch leads to which severe manifestation?","options":["Severe dehydration and diarrhea","Bowel perforation","Portal hypertension","Hydrocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows celiac crisis leading to severe dehydration and diarrhea.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the outcomes shown in the celiac crisis branch."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is best described as:","options":["A functional motility disorder of the intestines without any evidence of underlying cause, characterised by abdominal pain, diarrhoea and constipation","An inflammation of the peritoneum","A protrusion of an organ through the abdominal wall","Dilated varicose veins of the anal canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines IBS as a functional motility disorder of the intestines without any evidence of underlying cause, characterised by abdominal pain, diarrhoea and constipation.  Why B wrong: That is peritonitis.  Why C wrong: That is a hernia.  Why D wrong: That is haemorrhoids."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"IBS causes are unknown, but the condition is associated with which factors? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stress","Depression","Previous intestinal infection","High fat diet and alcohol consumption","Congenital abdominal wall weakness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: IBS is associated with stress, depression, previous intestinal infection, high fat diet, and alcohol consumption.  Why E wrong: Congenital abdominal wall weakness is a hernia cause, not an IBS association."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The characteristic abdominal pain of IBS is located in which quadrant and typically:","options":["Right upper quadrant, worsened by fatty meals","Left lower abdominal quadrant, may be intensified by eating and relieved by defecation","Periumbilical, radiating to the RLQ","Epigastric, relieved by antacids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IBS causes left lower abdominal quadrant pain that may be intensified by eating and relieved by defecation.  Why A wrong: RUQ fatty-meal pain suggests gallbladder disease.  Why C wrong: Periumbilical pain radiating to RLQ is appendicitis.  Why D wrong: Epigastric pain relieved by antacids suggests peptic disease."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of IBS? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bloating & abdominal cramps","Dyspepsia","Alternating constipation and diarrhoea","Abdominal distension","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: IBS features include bloating and abdominal cramps, dyspepsia, alternating constipation and diarrhoea, and abdominal distension.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is a haemorrhoid feature, not listed for IBS."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are specific dietary instructions for IBS nursing care? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Add fibers in diet","Eat scheduled/on time and maintain adequate hydration","Eliminate irritating and gas-producing substances","Avoid smoking & alcohol; avoid stressful conditions as soon as possible","Increase caffeine and citrus intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: IBS dietary instructions include adding fibers, eating on schedule, adequate hydration, eliminating irritating/gas-producing substances, avoiding smoking/alcohol, and avoiding stressful conditions.  Why E wrong: Caffeine and citrus fruits are restricted foods that may exacerbate the disease, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which medications are given (as ordered) for IBS symptom management?","options":["Anti-diarrhoeal and anti-spasmodic agents","Anticoagulants","Antihypertensives","Bronchodilators"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source says to give ordered medicines like anti-diarrhoeal, anti-spasmodic, etc.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drug classes are not indicated for IBS in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which food items are restricted in IBS because they may precipitate exacerbation of the condition? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Wheat","Gluten","Citrus fruits","Caffeine-containing foods","Plain rice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Restricted food items include wheat, gluten, citrus fruits, and caffeine-containing foods that may precipitate exacerbation.  Why E wrong: Plain rice is not among the listed restricted items."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Peritonitis is defined as:","options":["Inflammation of the peritoneum","Inflammation of the appendix","Twisting of the intestine","Dilated anal veins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (MP NHM 2023) defines peritonitis as inflammation of the peritoneum.  Why B wrong: That is appendicitis.  Why C wrong: That is volvulus.  Why D wrong: That is haemorrhoids."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common causative organism of peritonitis?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Escherichia coli (E. coli)","Clostridium difficile","Helicobacter pylori"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of peritonitis is Escherichia coli (E. coli).  Why A/C/D wrong: These organisms are not named as the most common cause of peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common cause of peritonitis (mechanism)?","options":["Perforation of the GI tract due to infection","Peritoneal dialysis","Cirrhosis","Chemical injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists perforation of the GI tract due to infection as the most common cause.  Why B/C/D wrong: Peritoneal dialysis, cirrhosis, and chemical injury are listed only as \"other causes,\" not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other causes\" of peritonitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Acute pancreatitis","Peritoneal dialysis","Cirrhosis and ruptured appendix","Chemical injury, stress & trauma","Lactose intolerance"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other causes listed are acute pancreatitis, peritoneal dialysis, cirrhosis, ruptured appendix, chemical injury, stress and trauma.  Why E wrong: Lactose intolerance relates to malabsorption, not peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common sign of peritonitis?","options":["Painless rectal bleeding","Rebound tenderness (Blumberg sign)","Borborygmus","Obstipation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rebound tenderness or Blumberg sign is the most common sign of peritonitis.  Why A wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is a haemorrhoid feature.  Why C wrong: Borborygmus is a malabsorption feature.  Why D wrong: Obstipation is associated with intestinal obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of peritonitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Abdominal pain, rigidity and guarding, intense while coughing & sneezing","Malaise, nausea & vomiting","Increased temperature and neutrophils","Rebound tenderness (Blumberg sign)","Painless bright-red rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Peritonitis features include abdominal pain/rigidity/guarding worsened by coughing & sneezing, malaise/nausea/vomiting, increased temperature and neutrophils, and rebound tenderness (Blumberg sign).  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is not a peritonitis feature."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which position is recommended in nursing care of peritonitis to promote drainage?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Semi-Fowler's position","Prone position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states semi-Fowler's position is used to promote drainage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not recommended for promoting drainage in peritonitis per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate nursing care measures in peritonitis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Position in semi-Fowler's to promote drainage","Check all vital parameters, especially temperature","Teach the client to avoid deep coughing & moving to prevent intense severe pain","Place NG decompression until the passing of flatus","Encourage vigorous coughing exercises hourly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Peritonitis nursing care includes semi-Fowler's positioning, checking vitals (especially temperature), teaching to avoid deep coughing/moving, and NG decompression until flatus passes.  Why E wrong: The client should AVOID deep coughing to prevent intense pain, not perform vigorous coughing."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In peritonitis, nasogastric (NG) decompression is maintained until:","options":["The passing of flatus","The client eats a full meal","The temperature normalises for 1 hour","The rebound tenderness disappears"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place NG decompression until the passing of flatus.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated endpoint is the passing of flatus, not these alternatives."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"SAIO stands for:","options":["Sub-Acute Intestinal Obstruction","Systemic Abdominal Inflammatory Obstruction","Small-Airway Intestinal Occlusion","Sub-Acute Ileal Overload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SAIO stands for Sub-Acute Intestinal Obstruction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansion of the abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Sub-Acute Intestinal Obstruction (SAIO) is best described as:","options":["A mechanical, neurologic & functional obstruction of the intestines that prevents the normal movement of the products of digestion","Inflammation of the peritoneum","Dilated anal veins","Failure to absorb nutrients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines SAIO as a mechanical, neurologic and functional obstruction of the intestines that prevents normal movement of the products of digestion; it is also called bowel obstruction in which both intestine may be affected.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe peritonitis, haemorrhoids, and malabsorption respectively."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children?","options":["Adhesions","Intussusception","Volvulus","Fecal impaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children is intussusception.  Why A wrong: Adhesions (and hernias) are the most common cause in adults.  Why C/D wrong: Volvulus and fecal impaction are causes but not the most common pediatric cause."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in adults?","options":["Intussusception","Adhesion & hernias","Mesenteric infarction","Volvulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in adults is adhesion & hernias.  Why A wrong: Intussusception is the most common cause in children.  Why C/D wrong: These are causes but not the most common adult cause."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which cause of SAIO is defined as difficulty in passing stool due to a hard, mass-like stool?","options":["Adhesions","Fecal impaction","Intussusception","Volvulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines fecal impaction as difficulty passing stool due to a hard mass-like stool.  Why A wrong: Adhesions are abnormal scar-tissue connections.  Why C wrong: Intussusception is telescoping of bowel.  Why D wrong: Volvulus is twisting of intestine."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In SAIO, \"adhesions\" are defined as:","options":["Twisting of the intestine","Scar tissue that forms abnormal connections after surgery","Telescoping of bowel on itself","Hard mass-like stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adhesions are scar tissue that forms abnormal connections after surgery.  Why A wrong: That is volvulus.  Why C wrong: That is intussusception.  Why D wrong: That is fecal impaction."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Intussusception, a cause of intestinal obstruction, is defined as:","options":["Twisting of the intestine","Telescoping of the bowel on itself","Occlusion of arterial blood supply to the bowel","Scar tissue forming after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines intussusception as telescoping of the bowel on itself.  Why A wrong: Twisting is volvulus.  Why C wrong: Arterial occlusion causing bowel necrosis is mesenteric infarction.  Why D wrong: Scar-tissue connections describe adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Volvulus, a cause of intestinal obstruction, is defined as:","options":["Twisting of the intestine","Telescoping of the bowel","Hard mass-like stool","Repair of the abdominal wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines volvulus as twisting of the intestine.  Why B wrong: Telescoping is intussusception.  Why C wrong: Hard mass-like stool is fecal impaction.  Why D wrong: Repair of the abdominal wall is herniorrhaphy."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of SAIO (intestinal obstruction)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hernia & bowel cancer","Fecal impaction","Adhesions","Intussusception and volvulus","Lactase deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SAIO causes listed include hernia & bowel cancer, fecal impaction, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus.  Why E wrong: Lactase deficiency causes malabsorption, not intestinal obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Paralytic ileus, a cause of intestinal obstruction, is defined as:","options":["Alteration in neuronal activities of the intestine resulting in decrease or absence of peristalsis; it is the most common cause after abdominal surgery","Twisting of the intestine","Occlusion of arterial blood supply causing bowel necrosis","Telescoping of the bowel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paralytic ileus is defined as an alteration in neuronal activities of the intestine resulting in decreased or absent peristalsis, and it is the most common cause after abdominal surgery.  Why B wrong: That is volvulus.  Why C wrong: That is mesenteric infarction.  Why D wrong: That is intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is described as the most common cause of intestinal obstruction after abdominal surgery?","options":["Volvulus","Paralytic ileus","Fecal impaction","Bowel cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states paralytic ileus is the most common cause after abdominal surgery.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are causes of obstruction but not the most common post-surgical cause."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Mesenteric infarction, a cause of intestinal obstruction, refers to:","options":["Occlusion of the arterial blood supply to the bowel causing necrosis of the bowel","Twisting of the intestine","Hard mass-like stool","Alteration in neuronal peristalsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mesenteric infarction is occlusion of the arterial blood supply to the bowel causing necrosis of the bowel.  Why B wrong: That is volvulus.  Why C wrong: That is fecal impaction.  Why D wrong: That is paralytic ileus."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The cardinal sign of intestinal obstruction (SAIO), per the source, includes:","options":["Painless rectal bleeding and pruritus","Abdominal distension, vomiting with fecal matter, and colicky abdominal pain","Left lower quadrant pain relieved by defecation","Rebound tenderness only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign is abdominal distension, vomiting with fecal matter, and colicky abdominal pain.  Why A wrong: Those are haemorrhoid features.  Why C wrong: That describes IBS pain.  Why D wrong: Rebound tenderness alone is not the cardinal sign of SAIO."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In intestinal obstruction, \"obstipation\" is best defined as:","options":["Passing loose watery stool","A chronic state of constipation","Hard stool","Absence of bowel sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines obstipation as a chronic state of constipation.  Why A wrong: That is diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Hard stool describes simple constipation as listed.  Why D wrong: Absent bowel sounds is a separate listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of SAIO/intestinal obstruction? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Constipation with hard stool","Obstipation (chronic constipation)","Decreased or absent bowel sounds","Dehydration & electrolyte imbalances","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SAIO signs/symptoms include constipation (hard stool), obstipation, decreased or absent bowel sounds, and dehydration with electrolyte imbalances.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is a haemorrhoid feature."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A priority nursing care measure for a client with intestinal obstruction (SAIO) is to:","options":["Feed a high-fiber meal immediately","Keep NPO, check all vitals, and give IV fluids as per order","Encourage oral bulk laxatives","Position prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care is to keep the client NPO, check all vitals, and give IV fluids as per order.  Why A wrong: The client should be kept NPO, not fed.  Why C wrong: Oral laxatives are inappropriate in mechanical obstruction.  Why D wrong: Prone positioning is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate nursing care measures for SAIO? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Keep NPO and give IV fluids as per order","Monitor for dehydration, electrolyte imbalances and intake & output","Auscultate bowel sounds and watch for passage of flatus","Surgical intervention as per the course of disease","Encourage a large high-fiber oral meal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SAIO nursing care includes NPO with IV fluids, monitoring dehydration/electrolytes/intake-output, auscultating bowel sounds and watching for flatus, and surgical intervention as per disease course.  Why E wrong: Oral feeding is contraindicated; the client is kept NPO."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After insertion of an intestinal tube, in which position should the client be placed first to facilitate passage of the tube through the pylorus?","options":["Left side position","Right side position","Prone position","Supine flat position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place the client in the right side position to facilitate passage of the tube through the pylorus, then in the semi-Fowler position to permit gradual advance into the intestines.  Why A/C/D wrong: The right side position (not left, prone, or flat supine) is specified for pyloric passage."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After the intestinal tube has passed the pylorus, in which position is the client placed to permit gradual advance of the tube into the intestines?","options":["Trendelenburg","Semi-Fowler position","Prone","High-Fowler flat on abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the right side position, the client is placed in the semi-Fowler position to permit gradual advance of the tube into the intestines.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies semi-Fowler position for this step."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"During GI decompression via a nasogastric or intestinal tube, how often should the tube be instilled or irrigated with sterile saline?","options":["Every 1-2 hours","Every 6-8 hours or as per order","Every 24 hours","Only once at insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to instil or irrigate with sterile saline every 6-8 hours or as per order.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated interval is every 6-8 hours, not these alternatives."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Why should an intestinal tube be removed smoothly or gradually when discontinued?","options":["Because it is being pulled against peristalsis","Because it is coated with mucus","To prevent air embolism","To reduce bleeding risk from anticoagulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to remove the tube smoothly or gradually because it is being pulled against peristalsis.  Why B/C/D wrong: The reason given is that removal works against peristalsis, not these other reasons."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"During GI tract decompression via nasogastric/intestinal tube, how is the obstruction addressed according to the source?","options":["Fluid is forcefully inserted into the intestines through intestinal tubes to try to resolve the obstruction by pressure of fluid","Air is insufflated to push the blockage forward","A laser is used to vaporize the mass","The tube is left clamped for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in decompression, fluid is forcefully inserted into the intestines through intestinal tubes to try to resolve the obstruction by the pressure of fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These methods are not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Ogilvie syndrome is defined as:","options":["Rupture of the oesophagus during endoscopy","Acute dilatation of the colon in the absence of any mechanical obstruction in severely ill patients","Telescoping of the bowel","Inflammation of the peritoneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ogilvie syndrome is acute dilatation of the colon in the absence of any mechanical obstruction in severely ill patients; sometimes it also causes intestinal obstruction.  Why A wrong: That is Boerhaave syndrome.  Why C wrong: That is intussusception.  Why D wrong: That is peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Boerhaave syndrome is defined as:","options":["Rupture of the oesophagus, usually during an endoscopy","Acute dilatation of the colon","Occlusion of mesenteric arteries","Repair of abdominal fascia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Boerhaave syndrome is rupture of the oesophagus, usually during an endoscopy.  Why B wrong: That is Ogilvie syndrome.  Why C wrong: That is mesenteric infarction.  Why D wrong: That is herniorrhaphy."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A hernia is defined as:","options":["A protrusion of an organ or structure through a weakening in the abdominal wall","Inflammation of the peritoneum","Dilated varicose veins of the anal canal","Failure to absorb nutrients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a hernia as a protrusion of an organ or structure through a weakening in the abdominal wall.  Why B wrong: That is peritonitis.  Why C wrong: That is haemorrhoids.  Why D wrong: That is malabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common type of hernia in females?","options":["Femoral hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia","Umbilical hernia","Direct inguinal hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (NORCET 4.0) states the most common type of hernia in females is the indirect inguinal hernia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not named as the most common type in females."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common type of hernia overall?","options":["Femoral hernia","Inguinal hernia","Umbilical hernia","Incisional hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of hernia is inguinal hernia (and it is the most common type in both genders).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are less common than inguinal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Hernias may be caused by which categories, and are precipitated by which factor?","options":["Only congenital; precipitated by malnutrition","Congenital and acquired; precipitated by obesity","Only acquired; precipitated by dehydration","Neither; precipitated by hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states causes include congenital and acquired, and hernia is precipitated by obesity.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source lists both congenital and acquired causes and obesity as the precipitating factor."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Herniorrhaphy is defined as:","options":["Repair of a defect in the abdominal musculature or fascia","Placement of synthetic mesh only","Removal of the appendix","Manual pushing of the mass back"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (ESIC 2019) defines herniorrhaphy as repair of a defect in the abdominal musculature or fascia.  Why B wrong: Mesh placement with herniorrhaphy is hernioplasty.  Why C wrong: Appendix removal is appendectomy.  Why D wrong: Manual reduction is a separate maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Hernioplasty is best defined as:","options":["Herniorrhaphy with placement of synthetic mesh or graft (human or animal)","Simple repair of fascia without mesh","Removal of the hernia sac contents","Manual reduction of the hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hernioplasty as herniorrhaphy with placement of synthetic mesh, graft (human or animal), etc.  Why B wrong: Repair without mesh is herniorrhaphy.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the defined procedure."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the purpose of a hernia mesh?","options":["To provide permanent reinforcement to the repaired hernia","To temporarily compress the bowel","To drain the peritoneal cavity","To deliver antibiotics locally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hernia mesh is used to provide permanent reinforcement to the repaired hernia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated purposes of a hernia mesh."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of hernia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Inflammation and necrosis","Strangulation and bowel obstruction","Hydrocele","Haemorrhage","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include inflammation, necrosis, strangulation, bowel obstruction, hydrocele, and haemorrhage.  Why E wrong: Hypertension is not listed as a hernia complication."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to hernia classification by characteristics, a \"reducible\" hernia is one in which:","options":["The protruding structure can be manipulated back in place","The protruding structure cannot be manipulated back in place","The blood supply to tissues is disrupted","Gangrene has already developed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A reducible hernia is one whose protruding structure can be manipulated back in place.  Why B wrong: That is an incarcerated hernia.  Why C wrong: Disrupted blood supply defines a strangulated hernia.  Why D wrong: Gangrene is a consequence of strangulation, not the definition of reducible."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"An \"incarcerated\" hernia is defined as one in which:","options":["The protruding structure can be easily pushed back","The protruding structure cannot be manipulated back in place","The blood supply is disrupted","Both direct and indirect inguinal hernias coexist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An incarcerated hernia is one whose protruding structure cannot be manipulated back in place.  Why A wrong: That describes a reducible hernia.  Why C wrong: Disrupted blood supply is strangulation.  Why D wrong: Coexisting direct and indirect hernia is a pantaloon hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A \"strangulated\" hernia is characterized by disruption of blood supply to the tissues within the hernia. This is:","options":["A benign condition managed with diet","A surgical emergency, otherwise gangrene or necrosis may occur","Always reducible","Never associated with tissue death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a strangulated hernia (blood supply disrupted) is a surgical emergency; otherwise gangrene or necrosis may occur.  Why A wrong: It is a surgical emergency, not diet-managed.  Why C wrong: A strangulated hernia is not reducible.  Why D wrong: Tissue death (gangrene/necrosis) is precisely the risk."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"An inguinal hernia is a protrusion of an abdominal organ through a weak point into the inguinal canal. It is the most common type in:","options":["Only males","Only females","Both genders","Only children"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inguinal hernia is the most common type of hernia in both genders.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies both genders, not a single group."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the two subtypes of inguinal hernia per the source?","options":["Indirect: contents pass/enter through the inguinal canal; Direct: contents bulge/enter through the weakened posterior wall of the inguinal canal","Indirect: through the posterior wall; Direct: through the inguinal canal","Both pass only through the femoral canal","Both bulge only through the umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states indirect inguinal hernia contents pass or enter through the inguinal canal, whereas direct inguinal hernia contents bulge or enter through the weakened posterior wall of the inguinal canal.  Why B wrong: The descriptions are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These describe femoral and umbilical hernias."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A femoral hernia occurs when abdominal content protrudes or bulges through:","options":["The inguinal canal","The weak area of the posterior wall of the femoral canal","The umbilicus","An incompletely healed surgical wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A femoral hernia occurs when abdominal content protrudes or bulges into the weak area of the posterior wall of the femoral canal.  Why A wrong: The inguinal canal is the route for inguinal hernias.  Why C wrong: The umbilicus is the route for umbilical hernias.  Why D wrong: An incompletely healed surgical wound is the route for incisional hernias."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"An umbilical hernia protrudes through the umbilicus and is most common in clients with:","options":["Obesity and pregnancy","Hypertension","Diabetes only","Recent endoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states umbilical hernia is most common in clients with obesity and pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not named as the most common associations for umbilical hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"An incisional hernia (abdominal organ protruding through an incompletely healed surgical wound) is also called:","options":["Ventral hernia","Hiatus hernia","Femoral hernia","Pantaloon hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states incisional hernia is also called ventral hernia.  Why B wrong: Hiatus hernia is a diaphragmatic hernia.  Why C wrong: Femoral hernia is through the femoral canal.  Why D wrong: Pantaloon hernia is a combined direct/indirect inguinal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A diaphragmatic hernia, where abdominal content protrudes through a weak part of the hiatus of the diaphragm, is also called a hiatus hernia and is most commonly seen in:","options":["Children below 5 years of age","Adults over 60","Pregnant women only","Athletes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diaphragmatic (hiatus) hernia is most commonly seen in children below 5 years of age.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies children below 5 years, not these groups."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are location-based types of hernia listed in the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Inguinal hernia","Femoral hernia","Umbilical hernia","Incisional (ventral) hernia","Diaphragmatic (hiatus) hernia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — inguinal, femoral, umbilical, incisional (ventral), and diaphragmatic (hiatus) — are listed as location-based hernia types.  Why none excluded: The source lists all five as location classifications."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of hernia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["History of swelling after lifting, coughing or exercising","Severe pain caused by incarceration and strangulation","Swelling in groin/umbilicus or near an old surgical incision that may subside when in recumbent position","Vomiting and abdominal distension if strangulation occurs","Painless rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hernia signs/symptoms include swelling after lifting/coughing/exercising, severe pain from incarceration/strangulation, groin/umbilical/incisional swelling that subsides when recumbent, and vomiting/distension if strangulation occurs.  Why E wrong: Painless rectal bleeding is a haemorrhoid feature, not a hernia sign."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A hernia swelling that subsides when the client is in which position suggests it is reducible?","options":["Standing","Recumbent (lying down) position","Sitting upright","Squatting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states swelling in the groin/umbilicus or near an old incision may subside when in the recumbent position.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the recumbent position, not standing, sitting, or squatting."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In nursing care of a hernia, when should abdominal palpation be avoided?","options":["When the hernia is reducible","When the hernia is strangulated","When the hernia is asymptomatic","During any physical examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid abdominal palpation if the hernia is strangulated.  Why A/C/D wrong: The specific contraindication is a strangulated hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Manual reduction of a hernia by gently pushing the mass back into the abdominal cavity should be performed by:","options":["The nurse independently","A doctor, not by nurses","The patient","A physiotherapist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states manual reduction should be done by a doctor, not by nurses.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source explicitly restricts this to a doctor."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Amyand's hernia is characterized by:","options":["Containing the appendix vermiformis within the hernia sac","Involving a Meckel's diverticulum","Being a hernia through Petit's triangle","Being a combined direct/indirect inguinal hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amyand's hernia contains the appendix vermiformis within the hernia sac.  Why B wrong: A Meckel's diverticulum in the sac is Littre's hernia.  Why C wrong: Through Petit's triangle is Petit's hernia.  Why D wrong: A combined direct/indirect hernia is a pantaloon hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"According to the source, brain herniation/brain hernia occurs when there is a sudden:","options":["Drop in ICP","Rise in blood pressure","Drop in blood glucose","Rise in heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states brain herniation/brain hernia occurs when there is a sudden drop in ICP. (Note: standard neurology teaches brain herniation results from raised/increased ICP; the source's wording states \"drop in ICP,\" which is keyed here per source fidelity but is medically questionable.)  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes it to a change in ICP, not these parameters."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Hiatus hernia (per the Also Remember box) is described as a hernia due to a short oesophagus in which:","options":["The stomach is displaced into the thorax","The appendix enters the sac","The bowel telescopes","Tissue protrudes adjacent to a stoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hiatus hernia is due to a short oesophagus with the stomach displaced into the thorax.  Why B wrong: Appendix in sac is Amyand's hernia.  Why C wrong: Bowel telescoping is intussusception.  Why D wrong: Tissue adjacent to a stoma is a parastomal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Littre's hernia involves which structure?","options":["A Meckel's diverticulum in the small intestine","The appendix vermiformis","Petit's triangle","The perineal floor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Littre's hernia involves a Meckel's diverticulum in the small intestine.  Why B wrong: Appendix in the sac is Amyand's hernia.  Why C wrong: Petit's triangle involvement is Petit's hernia.  Why D wrong: The perineal floor is involved in a perineal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Petit's hernia occurs through which anatomic site?","options":["Petit's triangle (inferior lumbar triangle)","The superior lumbar triangle","The femoral canal","The umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Petit's hernia occurs through Petit's triangle, the inferior lumbar triangle.  Why B wrong: The superior lumbar triangle is the site of Grynfeltt's hernia.  Why C wrong: The femoral canal is the site of a femoral hernia.  Why D wrong: The umbilicus is the site of an umbilical hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Grynfeltt's hernia occurs through which triangle?","options":["Inferior lumbar (Petit's) triangle","Superior lumbar triangle","Hesselbach's triangle","Calot's triangle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grynfeltt's hernia occurs through the superior lumbar triangle.  Why A wrong: The inferior lumbar (Petit's) triangle is the site of Petit's hernia.  Why C/D wrong: These triangles are not named for Grynfeltt's hernia in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A parastomal hernia occurs when:","options":["Tissue protrudes adjacent to a stoma tract","The appendix enters the hernia sac","The stomach displaces into the thorax","The bowel twists"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A parastomal hernia is when tissue protrudes adjacent to a stoma tract.  Why B wrong: Appendix in sac is Amyand's hernia.  Why C wrong: Stomach into thorax is hiatus hernia.  Why D wrong: Bowel twisting is volvulus."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A perineal hernia protrudes through:","options":["The muscles and fascia of the perineal floor","The femoral canal","The inguinal canal","The diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A perineal hernia protrudes through the muscles and fascia of the perineal floor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sites correspond to femoral, inguinal, and diaphragmatic hernias respectively."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Richter's hernia is characterized by involvement of:","options":["Only one sidewall of the bowel, which can lead to bowel strangulation and perforation through ischaemia without causing bowel obstruction","The entire bowel circumference with obstruction","The appendix within the sac","A Meckel's diverticulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Richter's hernia involves only one sidewall of the bowel, which can result in bowel strangulation leading to perforation through ischaemia without causing bowel obstruction.  Why B wrong: It involves only one sidewall and characteristically does NOT cause obstruction.  Why C wrong: Appendix in sac is Amyand's hernia.  Why D wrong: Meckel's diverticulum involvement is Littre's hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A sciatic hernia most commonly presents as:","options":["An uncomfortable mass in the gluteal area","A groin swelling descending into the scrotum","An umbilical bulge","Retrosternal chest pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a sciatic hernia (in the sciatic foramen) most commonly presents as an uncomfortable mass in the gluteal area.  Why B/C/D wrong: These presentations correspond to inguinal, umbilical, and hiatus hernias respectively."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A pantaloon hernia (also called dual hernia, Romberg hernia, or saddle bag hernia) is defined when:","options":["Both concurrent direct and indirect inguinal hernias are present","The appendix is within the sac","Only one sidewall of the bowel is involved","Tissue protrudes adjacent to a stoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A pantaloon hernia is defined when both concurrent direct and indirect inguinal hernias are present.  Why B wrong: Appendix in sac is Amyand's hernia.  Why C wrong: One sidewall involvement is Richter's hernia.  Why D wrong: Tissue adjacent to a stoma is a parastomal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"What is the most common type of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH)?","options":["Bochdalek hernia","Morgagni hernia","Amyand's hernia","Richter's hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes the most common type of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) is Bochdalek hernia.  Why B wrong: Morgagni hernia is a less common CDH type and is not the one named.  Why C/D wrong: Amyand's and Richter's hernias are not congenital diaphragmatic hernias."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which feature correctly describes an INDIRECT inguinal hernia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Passes through the inguinal canal and can descend into the scrotum","Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","Controlled after reduction by pressure over the internal (deep) inguinal ring","Common in children and young adults","The defect may be felt above the pubic tubercle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indirect inguinal hernia passes through the inguinal canal, can descend into the scrotum, is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, is controlled after reduction by pressure over the internal (deep) inguinal ring, and is common in children and young adults.  Why E wrong: A palpable defect above the pubic tubercle is a feature of the DIRECT inguinal hernia; in indirect hernia the defect is not palpable (behind the fibers of the external oblique muscle)."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In an indirect inguinal hernia, the hernia is reduced in which direction?","options":["Upward, then laterally and backward","Upward, then straight backward","Downward, then medially","Straight forward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states an indirect inguinal hernia is reduced upward, then laterally and backward.  Why B wrong: Upward then straight backward is the direct inguinal hernia reduction.  Why C/D wrong: These directions are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Regarding the indirect inguinal hernia defect, the source states:","options":["The defect is not palpable because it is behind the fibers of the external oblique muscle","The defect is easily palpable above the pubic tubercle","The defect is in the femoral canal","The defect is at the umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In indirect inguinal hernia, the defect is not palpable because it is behind the fibers of the external oblique muscle.  Why B wrong: A palpable defect above the pubic tubercle is characteristic of the direct type.  Why C/D wrong: These sites relate to femoral and umbilical hernias."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After reduction of an indirect inguinal hernia, the bulge:","options":["Appears in the middle of the inguinal region, then flows medially before turning down to the scrotum","Reappears exactly where it was before","Disappears permanently","Moves to the femoral canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After reduction of an indirect inguinal hernia, the bulge appears in the middle of the inguinal region and then flows medially before turning down to the scrotum.  Why B wrong: Reappearing exactly where it was is characteristic of the direct type.  Why C/D wrong: These are not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which feature correctly describes a DIRECT inguinal hernia? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bulges from the posterior wall of the inguinal canal","Cannot descend into the scrotum","Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels","Common in old age","Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A direct inguinal hernia bulges from the posterior wall of the inguinal canal, cannot descend into the scrotum, is medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, and is common in old age.  Why E wrong: Being lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels is a feature of the indirect (not direct) inguinal hernia."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In a direct inguinal hernia, after reduction and applying pressure over the internal (deep) inguinal ring, the hernia is:","options":["Controlled","Not controlled","Permanently cured","Converted to indirect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states a direct inguinal hernia is NOT controlled after reduction by pressure over the internal (deep) inguinal ring.  Why A wrong: Being controlled by deep-ring pressure is the indirect type.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In a direct inguinal hernia, the defect may be felt in the abdominal wall at which landmark?","options":["Above the pubic tubercle","Below the inguinal ligament","At the umbilicus","Behind the external oblique fibers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the defect of a direct inguinal hernia may be felt in the abdominal wall above the pubic tubercle.  Why B wrong: Below the inguinal ligament is where a femoral hernia is felt.  Why C wrong: The umbilicus relates to an umbilical hernia.  Why D wrong: A defect behind the external oblique fibers (not palpable) is the indirect type."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After reduction of a direct inguinal hernia, the bulge:","options":["Reappears exactly where it was before","Appears in the middle of the inguinal region and flows medially to the scrotum","Descends into the scrotum","Disappears permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states that after reduction of a direct inguinal hernia, the bulge reappears exactly where it was before.  Why B/C wrong: Those are features of the indirect type.  Why D wrong: The source does not state permanent disappearance."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A direct inguinal hernia is most commonly seen in which age group, while an indirect inguinal hernia is common in which group?","options":["Direct: old age; Indirect: children and young adults","Direct: children; Indirect: old age","Both only in neonates","Both only in the elderly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tables state direct inguinal hernia is common in old age, while indirect inguinal hernia is common in children and young adults.  Why B/C/D wrong: The age associations are reversed or incorrect in these options."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Following hernia surgery, which of the following are appropriate specific nursing care measures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Instruct the client to avoid coughing if possible","Use deep breathing & spirometry to prevent pulmonary complications; encourage self-splinting","Give mild laxatives to promote bowel elimination and prevent straining (which increases intra-abdominal pressure)","Apply an ice bag or scrotal support if the scrotum is oedematous post-operatively to reduce oedema and pain","Encourage heavy lifting to strengthen the repair"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Post-hernia-surgery care includes avoiding coughing, deep breathing/spirometry with self-splinting to prevent pulmonary complications, mild laxatives to prevent straining, and ice bag/scrotal support for scrotal oedema.  Why E wrong: The client must avoid lifting and heavy exercises, not perform heavy lifting."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Why are mild laxatives given after hernia surgery?","options":["To promote bowel elimination and prevent straining, because straining increases intra-abdominal pressure","To treat the infection","To reduce blood pressure","To relieve nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild laxatives are given to promote bowel elimination and prevent straining because straining increases intra-abdominal pressure (which could stress the repair).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After hernia surgery, if the scrotum is oedematous post-operatively, the nurse should:","options":["Apply an ice bag or scrotal support to reduce oedema and pain","Apply a warm compress and massage","Encourage vigorous ambulation","Restrict all fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to apply an ice bag or scrotal support if the scrotum is oedematous post-operatively to reduce oedema and pain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These measures are not indicated per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"After hernia surgery, the client is instructed to avoid which activities? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Coughing, if possible","Lifting","Heavy exercises","Deep breathing exercises","Straining during bowel movements"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Post-op instructions include avoiding coughing (if possible), lifting, heavy exercises, and straining (mild laxatives are given to prevent it).  Why D wrong: Deep breathing and incentive spirometry are ENCOURAGED to prevent pulmonary complications, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Haemorrhoids are best defined as:","options":["Dilated varicose veins of the anal canal (varicosities of the rectum)","Inflammation of the peritoneum","Protrusion of an organ through the abdominal wall","Twisting of the intestine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines haemorrhoids as dilated varicose veins of the anal canal, or varicosities of the rectum.  Why B wrong: That is peritonitis.  Why C wrong: That is a hernia.  Why D wrong: That is volvulus."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Haemorrhoids are also known by which lay/regional names?","options":["Piles and bavasir","Sprue and celiac","Ileus and volvulus","Sinus and fistula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states haemorrhoids are also known as piles and bavasir.  Why B/C/D wrong: These terms refer to other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Haemorrhoids are classified into which categories?","options":["Internal and external or prolapsed","Acute and chronic only","Reducible and irreducible","Direct and indirect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states haemorrhoids are classified into internal and external or prolapsed.  Why B wrong: Acute/chronic is not the classification given.  Why C wrong: Reducible/irreducible describes hernias.  Why D wrong: Direct/indirect describes inguinal hernias."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which statements correctly describe INTERNAL haemorrhoids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["They lie above the anal sphincter","They cannot be seen on inspection of the perineal area","They cause painless rectal bleeding during defecation (occult type known as Hematochezia)","They cause mucus discharge","They are always painful and visible externally"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Internal haemorrhoids lie above the anal sphincter, cannot be seen on inspection, cause painless rectal bleeding (occult type known as Hematochezia) during defecation, and cause mucus discharge.  Why E wrong: Being painful and externally visible describes external haemorrhoids, not internal."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"The painless occult rectal bleeding during defecation seen with internal haemorrhoids is known as:","options":["Hematochezia","Steatorrhea","Borborygmus","Obstipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states painless rectal bleeding during defecation (usually occult type) is known as Hematochezia.  Why B wrong: Steatorrhea is fatty stool.  Why C wrong: Borborygmus is loud bowel sounds.  Why D wrong: Obstipation is chronic constipation."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"In the grading of internal haemorrhoids, which grade shows no prolapse, just prominent blood vessels?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade I internal haemorrhoids show no prolapse, just prominent blood vessels.  Why B wrong: Grade II prolapses on bearing down with spontaneous reduction.  Why C wrong: Grade III prolapses and requires manual reduction.  Why D wrong: Grade IV cannot be manually reduced."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which grade of internal haemorrhoids prolapses upon bearing down but undergoes spontaneous reduction?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade II internal haemorrhoids prolapse upon bearing down but have spontaneous reduction.  Why A wrong: Grade I has no prolapse.  Why C wrong: Grade III requires manual reduction.  Why D wrong: Grade IV cannot be reduced."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which grade of internal haemorrhoids prolapses upon bearing down and requires manual reduction?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade III internal haemorrhoids prolapse upon bearing down and require manual reduction.  Why A wrong: Grade I shows no prolapse.  Why B wrong: Grade II reduces spontaneously.  Why D wrong: Grade IV cannot be manually reduced."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which grade of internal haemorrhoids is characterized by prolapse with inability to be manually reduced?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Grade IV internal haemorrhoids are prolapsed with an inability to be manually reduced.  Why A/B/C wrong: Grades I–III are less severe (no prolapse, spontaneous reduction, or manual reduction possible, respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which statements correctly describe EXTERNAL haemorrhoids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["They lie below the anal sphincter","They can be seen on inspection of the perineal area","They cause protrusions of varicosities around the anus","They cause painful rectal bleeding & anal pressure and pruritus","They cause only painless bleeding and cannot be seen"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: External haemorrhoids lie below the anal sphincter, can be seen on inspection, cause protrusions of varicosities around the anus, and cause painful rectal bleeding, anal pressure, and pruritus.  Why E wrong: Painless bleeding that cannot be seen describes internal haemorrhoids, not external."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"Which feature distinguishes external from internal haemorrhoids regarding pain and visibility?","options":["External haemorrhoids cause painful rectal bleeding and can be seen on inspection; internal haemorrhoids cause painless bleeding and cannot be seen","External haemorrhoids are painless and internal are painful","Both are always painless","Internal haemorrhoids are always visible externally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: External haemorrhoids are painful and visible on perineal inspection, whereas internal haemorrhoids cause painless bleeding and cannot be seen on inspection.  Why B wrong: The pain association is reversed.  Why C wrong: External haemorrhoids are painful.  Why D wrong: Internal haemorrhoids cannot be seen on inspection."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A prolapsed haemorrhoid is characterized as:","options":["Thrombosed and inflamed","Painless and reducible spontaneously","Located above the sphincter and invisible","A varicosity of the oesophagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states prolapsed haemorrhoids are thrombosed and inflamed.  Why B wrong: Prolapsed thrombosed haemorrhoids are typically painful, not painless.  Why C wrong: Being above the sphincter and invisible describes internal haemorrhoids.  Why D wrong: Oesophageal varices are a separate entity."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A client with an intestinal obstruction develops \"vomiting with fecal matter.\" This finding is best classified as:","options":["A cardinal sign of intestinal obstruction (SAIO)","A sign of haemorrhoids","A sign of peritonitis only","A normal post-operative finding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of SAIO includes abdominal distension, vomiting with fecal matter, and colicky abdominal pain.  Why B wrong: This is not a haemorrhoid feature.  Why C wrong: While peritonitis can accompany obstruction, feculent vomiting is a cardinal SAIO sign.  Why D wrong: Feculent vomiting is abnormal and indicates obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A young adult male presents with a groin swelling that descends into the scrotum and is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. This most likely represents which hernia?","options":["Direct inguinal hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia","Femoral hernia","Umbilical hernia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An indirect inguinal hernia passes through the inguinal canal, can descend into the scrotum, is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and is common in young adults — matching this presentation.  Why A wrong: A direct hernia is medial to the epigastric vessels, cannot descend into the scrotum, and is common in old age.  Why C wrong: A femoral hernia is through the femoral canal, below the inguinal ligament.  Why D wrong: An umbilical hernia is at the umbilicus."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A client with celiac-type malabsorption asks which grains are safe. The nurse should recommend which gluten-free options?","options":["Millet, corn, and rice","Wheat and barley","Rye and oats","Wheat bran and semolina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends a gluten-free diet such as millet, corn, rice, etc.  Why B/C/D wrong: Wheat, barley, rye, and oats (and wheat products like semolina) contain gluten and must be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with peritonitis places the client in semi-Fowler's position. What is the priority rationale for this position?","options":["To promote drainage","To improve venous return to the heart","To facilitate tube passage through the pylorus","To prevent aspiration during feeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies semi-Fowler's position is used to promote drainage in peritonitis.  Why B wrong: Improving venous return is not the stated rationale.  Why C wrong: Right side positioning facilitates tube passage through the pylorus (intestinal tube care), not semi-Fowler's for peritonitis drainage.  Why D wrong: The client is generally NPO with NG decompression, so feeding/aspiration is not the rationale."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Appendicitis…","question":"A client reports painful bleeding and anal pressure, and a protruding varicosity is visible around the anus on inspection. This best fits which condition?","options":["External haemorrhoids","Grade I internal haemorrhoids","Peritonitis","Volvulus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: External haemorrhoids lie below the sphincter, are visible on inspection, and cause painful rectal bleeding, anal pressure, and protrusions of varicosities around the anus.  Why B wrong: Grade I internal haemorrhoids are not visible on inspection and cause painless bleeding.  Why C wrong: Peritonitis presents with rebound tenderness/abdominal rigidity, not perianal varicosities.  Why D wrong: Volvulus is intestinal twisting causing obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A nurse reviews the risk factors for haemorrhoids. Which factor describes a forceful defecation due to chronic constipation?","options":["Prolonged sitting and standing","Straining","Portal hypertension","Increased venous abdominal pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Straining is forceful defecation due to chronic constipation (the most common cause of haemorrhoids).  Why A wrong: Prolonged sitting/standing is a separate listed risk factor, not forceful defecation.  Why C wrong: Portal hypertension is a distinct cause involving venous pressure, not defecation effort.  Why D wrong: Increased venous/abdominal pressure is a separate listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause/risk factor for haemorrhoids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Straining due to chronic constipation","Prolonged sitting and standing","Portal hypertension, obesity, and pregnancy","Increased venous and abdominal pressure","High dietary fiber intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists straining, prolonged sitting/standing, portal hypertension/obesity/pregnancy, and increased venous & abdominal pressure as risk factors.  Why E wrong: High-fiber diet prevents (not causes) haemorrhoids; low fiber/constipation is the risk."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A patient post-haemorrhoid procedure reports discomfort. According to nursing care, what should be applied to the anal area to relieve discomfort?","options":["Warm dry heat","Cold packs","Tight pressure dressing","Alcohol swabs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care is to apply cold packs to the anal area and offer sitz baths to relieve discomfort.  Why A wrong: Source specifies cold packs, not warm dry heat.  Why C wrong: A tight pressure dressing is not indicated and can worsen pain.  Why D wrong: Alcohol would irritate the sensitive perianal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which agent should be applied or administered as ordered to relieve pain after haemorrhoid management?","options":["Bulk-forming laxative only","Analgesic or anesthetic spray","Antibiotic ointment only","NPO status only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes applying analgesic or anesthetic spray as per order.  Why A wrong: Bulk cathartics aid stool passage, not direct pain relief.  Why C wrong: Antibiotic ointment is not the listed pain measure.  Why D wrong: NPO is reserved for patients after some surgeries, not a pain relief measure."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"To prevent constipation and promote bowel movement after the operative phase, the nurse should offer foods that are:","options":["Low fiber and low fluid","High fiber and high fluid","High fat","Caffeine-rich"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Offer high-fiber diet and fluids to prevent constipation and promote bowel movement after stabilisation or in remission phase.  Why A wrong: Low fiber/fluid promotes constipation, worsening haemorrhoids.  Why C/D wrong: High fat and caffeine are not the recommended measures."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which medications facilitate passage of stool in a patient recovering from haemorrhoid management?","options":["Antidiarrheals and antiemetics","Stool softeners and bulk cathartics","Antacids and PPIs","Antihypertensives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Give stool softeners and bulk cathartics to facilitate passage of stool.  Why A wrong: Antidiarrheals would worsen constipation; antiemetics are for nausea.  Why C wrong: Antacids/PPIs target gastric acid, not stool passage.  Why D wrong: Antihypertensives are unrelated to bowel passage."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"After haemorrhoid surgery, which position should the patient be placed in?","options":["Supine with head elevated","Side-lying position","High Fowler's","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the patient in side-lying position and keep NPO after surgeries.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not the recommended post-haemorrhoid-surgery position per the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"When changing the dressing and giving sitz baths after haemorrhoid surgery, the nurse should apply:","options":["Heat packs","Ice packs","Steroid cream only","Talcum powder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apply ice packs over the dressing and give sitz baths following surgery.  Why A wrong: The source specifies ice packs, not heat.  Why C/D wrong: Not the listed measures during dressing/sitz bath care."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A patient post-haemorrhoidectomy has not had a bowel movement. The nurse should:","options":["Withhold all care","Watch for rectal hemorrhage and urinary retention","Force fluids only","Insert an enema immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Watch for rectal hemorrhage and urinary retention postoperatively; explain that the first bowel movement after operation is painful with bleeding and a bowel movement is expected.  Why A wrong: Care must continue with monitoring.  Why C wrong: Forcing fluids alone does not address the monitoring priority.  Why D wrong: Immediate enema is not indicated as the routine response."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"When instructing a patient to limit sitting time after haemorrhoid surgery, the nurse advises increasing fluid and fiber intake. How often per day should this be done, as ordered?","options":["Once per day","3–4 times per day","Every hour","Only at bedtime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct to limit sitting time, and increase fluids and fiber over 3–4 times per day, both every day or as per order, while initiating general nursing care.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 3–4 times per day."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A patient is advised to avoid straining while attempting defecation, attempting less time to defecate, avoiding constipation and diarrhea, and taking high-fiber diet and adequate fluids. These instructions are part of:","options":["Post-operative wound care","How to prevent haemorrhoids","Bariatric surgery prep","Diverticulitis treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"How to Prevent Haemorrhoids\" lists avoiding straining, less defecation time, avoiding constipation/diarrhea, and high-fiber diet with adequate fluids; also doing regular sufficient exercise.  Why A/C/D wrong: These prevention points specifically relate to haemorrhoid prevention."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common, usually still, and used for grade I to III surgical management of haemorrhoids is:","options":["Cryosurgery","Rubber band ligation","Haemorrhoidectomy","Sclerotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rubber band ligation is most common, usually still used for grade I to III.  Why A wrong: Cryosurgery uses cold temperature and is a separate technique.  Why C wrong: Haemorrhoidectomy is surgical removal, not the band method.  Why D wrong: Sclerotherapy injects sclerosing agents but is not the band ligation grade I–III standard."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which haemorrhoid surgical technique involves injecting a sclerosing agent such as phenol?","options":["Rubber band ligation","Sclerotherapy","Cryosurgery","Haemorrhoidectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sclerotherapy is the injecting of a sclerosing agent such as phenol.  Why A wrong: Rubber band ligation uses a band, not injection.  Why C wrong: Cryosurgery uses cold temperature.  Why D wrong: Haemorrhoidectomy is surgical excision."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Cryosurgery for haemorrhoids works by using:","options":["Heat cautery","Cold temperature","Laser ablation","Sclerosing agent injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cryosurgery uses cold temperature.  Why A wrong: Heat cautery is not cryosurgery.  Why C wrong: Laser ablation is a different modality.  Why D wrong: Injection of a sclerosing agent is sclerotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Haemorrhoidectomy is best defined as:","options":["Banding of the haemorrhoid","Surgical removal of haemorrhoids","Injection of phenol","Freezing the tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemorrhoidectomy is the removal of haemorrhoids by surgery.  Why A wrong: Banding is rubber band ligation.  Why C wrong: Phenol injection is sclerotherapy.  Why D wrong: Freezing tissue is cryosurgery."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Diverticulosis is best described as:","options":["Inflammation of one or more diverticula","Outpouching or herniation of the intestinal mucosa","Perforation of the intestinal wall","Intra-abdominal perforation with peritonitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diverticulosis is the outpouching or herniation of the intestinal mucosa.  Why A wrong: Inflammation of diverticula is diverticulitis.  Why C/D wrong: Perforation and peritonitis are complications of diverticulitis, not the definition of diverticulosis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Diverticulosis most commonly occurs in which part of the intestine?","options":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Sigmoid colon","Cecum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Diverticulosis can occur anywhere in the intestines but most commonly in the sigmoid colon.  Why A/B/D wrong: Duodenum, jejunum, and cecum are not the most common location stated."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Diverticulitis is the inflammation of one or more diverticula due to:","options":["Excess fiber","Penetration of fecal matter through a weak intestinal wall","Viral infection","Autoimmune reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diverticulitis is inflammation of one or more diverticula due to penetration of fecal matter through a weak intestinal wall.  Why A wrong: Excess fiber does not cause diverticulitis; low fiber promotes diverticula.  Why C/D wrong: Viral/autoimmune causes are not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A perforated diverticulum in diverticulitis can progress to which serious complication?","options":["Hypoglycemia","Intra-abdominal perforation with peritonitis","Anemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A perforated diverticulum can progress to intra-abdominal perforation with peritonitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated progression of a perforated diverticulum."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Obesity is defined as:","options":["Normal fat distribution in the body","Abnormal or excessive fat accumulation that presents a risk for health","A protective amount of subcutaneous fat","A genetic muscle disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity is defined as abnormal or excessive fat accumulation that presents a risk to health.  Why A/C wrong: Obesity is abnormal/excessive accumulation, not normal or protective.  Why D wrong: It is a fat accumulation condition, not a muscle disorder."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which statement about the causes of obesity is correct?","options":["Calorie intake equals metabolic needs","Calorie intake exceeds the metabolic needs","Hormonal imbalance always prevents obesity","Sedentary lifestyle reduces obesity risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity occurs when calorie intake exceeds the metabolic needs.  Why A wrong: Balanced intake equal to needs would not cause excess fat.  Why C wrong: Hormonal imbalance such as hypothyroidism is itself a cause of obesity.  Why D wrong: A sedentary lifestyle increases (not reduces) obesity risk."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of obesity? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Calorie intake exceeds metabolic needs","Hormonal imbalance such as hypothyroidism","Genetic factors and social determinants","Sedentary lifestyle","Daily vigorous exercise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include calorie intake exceeding metabolic needs, hormonal imbalance (e.g., hypothyroidism), genetic factors, social determinants, and a sedentary lifestyle.  Why E wrong: Daily vigorous exercise reduces obesity risk; it is not a cause."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A hormonal imbalance that is listed as a cause of obesity is:","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Hyperparathyroidism","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hormonal imbalance such as hypothyroidism is a major cause of obesity.  Why A wrong: Hyperthyroidism increases metabolism and tends to cause weight loss.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed hormonal cause of obesity."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Obesity is a major complication or risk factor for which conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypertension (HTN)","Diabetes mellitus (DM)","Cancer","Cardiac disorders","Sleep disorders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Complications or risks of obesity include HTN, DM, cancer, cardiac disorders, and sleep disorders.  Why none wrong: All listed are stated complications/risks of obesity."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"According to the classification, obesity is defined as a BMI of:","options":["20% or more over ideal body weight only","30% or more over ideal body weight","18.5 or less","22 exactly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity is 30% or more over ideal body weight.  Why A wrong: 20% or more over ideal body weight defines overweight, not obesity.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not the obesity threshold."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Overweight is defined as approximately what percent over ideal body weight?","options":["10%","20% or more","50%","30% or more"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Overweight is 20% or more over ideal body weight.  Why A/C wrong: These values do not match the source.  Why D wrong: 30% or more defines obesity."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Morbid obesity is classified as a BMI of:","options":["25 or 30% over ideal body weight","100 lb or more over ideal body weight","Less than 18.5","Exactly 22"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Morbid obesity is 100 lb or more over ideal body weight.  Why A wrong: This describes overweight/obesity thresholds, not morbid obesity.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the morbid obesity definition."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A patient is morbidly obese with a BMI greater than 40. What BMI value classifies super-obese?","options":["BMI > 25","BMI > 30","BMI > 40","BMI > 50"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BMI more than 40 is the cited value associated with the severe/super-obesity category in the source classification.  Why A wrong: BMI > 25 is overweight range.  Why B wrong: BMI > 30 is obesity, not the highest listed category.  Why D wrong: The source lists >40, not >50."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A nurse calculates the BMI threshold for diagnosing obesity in men versus women. Which values are correct?","options":[">15 mm in men; >15 mm in women","Triceps skinfold >15 mm in men and >25 mm in women indicates obesity",">25 mm in men; >15 mm in women",">40 mm in both"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A triceps skinfold thickness of more than 15 mm in men and more than 25 mm in women indicates obesity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source values for triceps skinfold thickness."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which bariatric surgery for morbid obesity reduces stomach capacity to less than 50 mL?","options":["Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding (LAGB)","Gastric bypass surgery","Sleeve gastrectomy","Panniculectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gastric bypass surgery reduces stomach capacity to less than 50 mL.  Why A wrong: LAGB places a small band around the stomach to create a small stomach pouch; it is reversible.  Why C wrong: Sleeve gastrectomy removes about 75% of the stomach.  Why D wrong: Panniculectomy removes excess abdominal skin folds, not stomach capacity reduction."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which bariatric procedure involves a small band placed around the stomach to create a small stomach pouch and is reversible?","options":["Gastric bypass","Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding (LAGB)","Sleeve gastrectomy","Panniculectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LAGB uses a small band around the stomach to create a small stomach pouch and is reversible.  Why A wrong: Gastric bypass reduces capacity to <50 mL and is not described as a band.  Why C wrong: Sleeve gastrectomy is a vertical sleeve gastrectomy removing ~75% of stomach.  Why D wrong: Panniculectomy removes abdominal skin folds."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Sleeve gastrectomy, a vertical sleeve gastrectomy, removes approximately what percentage of the stomach?","options":["25%","50%","75%","90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sleeve gastrectomy is also called vertical sleeve gastrectomy and removes 75% of the stomach.  Why A/B/D wrong: These percentages do not match the source value of 75%."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Panniculectomy is best described as:","options":["A surgical procedure to reduce stomach capacity","A surgical procedure in which excess abdominal skin folds are removed","Placement of an adjustable gastric band","Bypass of the small intestine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Panniculectomy is a surgical procedure in which excess abdominal skin folds are removed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other bariatric or stomach-related procedures, not panniculectomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which is a specific nursing intervention after bariatric surgery? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assess for wound dehiscence and slow wound healing due to inadequate blood supply","Apply abdominal binder and remove the binder every 4 hours for skin assessment for skin breakdown","Position in semi-Fowler's to reduce intra-abdominal pressure","Advise taking lifelong Vitamin-B12 supplements after bariatric surgery","Encourage high-calorie liquid diets indefinitely"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Specific nursing interventions include assessing for wound dehiscence/slow healing from inadequate blood supply, applying an abdominal binder (removed every 4 hours for skin assessment), positioning in semi-Fowler's to reduce intra-abdominal pressure, and lifelong Vitamin-B12 supplementation.  Why E wrong: High-calorie liquid diets indefinitely is not a listed intervention and would counter weight-loss goals."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A nurse positions a post-bariatric-surgery patient in semi-Fowler's position primarily to:","options":["Increase abdominal pressure","Reduce intra-abdominal pressure","Improve venous return only","Prevent vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Position the patient in semi-Fowler's to reduce intra-abdominal pressure.  Why A wrong: The goal is to reduce, not increase, abdominal pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated primary reason in the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Why is lifelong Vitamin-B12 supplementation advised after bariatric surgery?","options":["To reduce wound infection","Because of reduced absorption after the surgery","To lower blood pressure","To increase stomach capacity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Take lifelong Vitamin-B12 supplements after bariatric surgery because absorption is reduced following the procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the rationale for B12 supplementation."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The inflammation of the mouth is known as:","options":["Glossitis","Stomatitis","Cheilitis","Gingivitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the mouth is called stomatitis.  Why A wrong: Glossitis is inflammation of the tongue.  Why C wrong: Cheilitis is inflammation of the lips.  Why D wrong: Gingivitis is inflammation of the gums."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Inflammation of the tongue is termed:","options":["Stomatitis","Glossitis","Gingivitis","Sialadenitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the tongue is called glossitis.  Why A wrong: Stomatitis is inflammation of the mouth.  Why C wrong: Gingivitis is inflammation of the gums.  Why D wrong: Sialadenitis is inflammation of the salivary gland."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Iron absorption occurs primarily in which part of the GI tract?","options":["Stomach","Duodenum","Ileum","Sigmoid colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iron absorption occurs in the duodenum.  Why A wrong: The stomach is the site of pepsin/HCl action, not iron absorption.  Why C wrong: The ileum absorbs Vitamin B12.  Why D wrong: The sigmoid colon is not the iron absorption site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The CAGE screening test is used for:","options":["Cardiac screening","Screening of alcohol dependency clients","Renal function","Liver biopsy follow-up"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CAGE test is used for the screening of alcohol dependency clients.  Why A/C/D wrong: CAGE is an alcohol-dependency screening, not a cardiac, renal, or liver biopsy tool."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"What is the normal portal vein pressure?","options":["1–2 mm Hg","5 to 10 mm Hg (or 70–150 cm H2O)","20–40 mm Hg","80–120 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal portal vein pressure is 5 to 10 mm Hg (or 70–150 cm H2O).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source value."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which is the cardinal sign of pericarditis?","options":["Hepatojugular reflux","Pericardial friction rub","Coxsackievirus B","Halitosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of pericarditis is the pericardial friction rub.  Why A wrong: Hepatojugular reflux relates to the liver/cardiac assessment, not the cardinal pericarditis sign.  Why C wrong: Coxsackievirus B is the most common agent of pericarditis, not its cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: Halitosis is foul breath, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common agent (cause) of pericarditis is:","options":["Helicobacter pylori","Coxsackievirus B group","E. coli","Rotavirus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common agent of pericarditis is the Coxsackievirus B group.  Why A wrong: H. pylori is the most common cause of gastritis.  Why C wrong: E. coli is associated with cholecystitis (a separate item).  Why D wrong: Rotavirus causes diarrhea in children, not pericarditis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Foul smelling of breath (halitosis) corresponds to which rapid-revision answer?","options":["Gingivitis","Halitosis","Glossitis","Stomatitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Foul smelling of breath is known as halitosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe inflammation of gums, tongue, and mouth respectively, not foul breath."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common cause of gastritis is:","options":["Helicobacter pylori","Coxsackievirus","Rotavirus","E. coli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of gastritis is Helicobacter pylori.  Why B wrong: Coxsackievirus causes pericarditis.  Why C wrong: Rotavirus causes childhood diarrhea.  Why D wrong: E. coli is associated with cholecystitis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Clay-colored stools indicate the sign of which condition?","options":["Cirrhosis","Obstructive jaundice","Rectum cancer","Sialadenitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clay-colored stools indicate the sign of obstructive jaundice.  Why A wrong: Cirrhosis is associated with low residual/low fiber diet (separate item).  Why C wrong: Rectum is the duodenal-procedure or cancer item, not clay stool.  Why D wrong: Sialadenitis is inflammation of the salivary gland."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Specialized macrophages located in the liver for ulcerative colitis context are known as:","options":["Kupffer cells","The Liver (Kupffer/Kupffer cells)","Goblet cells","Parietal cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The specialized macrophages located in the liver are Kupffer cells (the liver's resident macrophages).  Why A wrong: This is the same correct answer phrased differently; the keyed source answer references the Liver/Kupffer cells.  Why C wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus, not macrophages.  Why D wrong: Parietal cells secrete HCl in the stomach."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common cause of portal hypertension in adults is:","options":["Rotavirus infection","Cirrhosis","Achalasia","Intussusception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of portal hypertension in adults is cirrhosis.  Why A wrong: Rotavirus causes childhood diarrhea.  Why C wrong: Achalasia is an esophageal motility disorder.  Why D wrong: Intussusception is a bowel telescoping condition."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common cause of cirrhosis is:","options":["Hepatitis B virus","Alcoholic liver disease","Rotavirus","E. coli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of cirrhosis is alcoholic liver disease.  Why A wrong: Hepatitis is a cause but the keyed most common cause is alcoholic liver disease.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the most common cause of cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common cause of diarrhea in children is:","options":["Rotavirus","E. coli","Coxsackievirus","Helicobacter pylori"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of diarrhea in children is Rotavirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are associated with cholecystitis, pericarditis, and gastritis respectively, not childhood diarrhea."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common site of ulcerative colitis is the:","options":["Stomach","Rectum","Duodenum","Esophagus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of ulcerative colitis is the rectum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the most common site of ulcerative colitis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common site of Hirschsprung's disease is the:","options":["Sigmoid colon (rectosigmoid)","Stomach","Esophagus","Cecum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of Hirschsprung's disease is the rectosigmoid/sigmoid colon region.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hirschsprung's disease characteristically affects the distal colon (rectosigmoid), not these sites."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The best disinfectant for endoscopic disinfection is:","options":["70% alcohol","2.5% Glutaraldehyde","Povidone iodine","Chlorhexidine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best disinfectant for endoscopic disinfection is 2.5% Glutaraldehyde.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are antiseptics/disinfectants but not the keyed endoscopic disinfectant."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Inflammation of the salivary gland is known as:","options":["Glossitis","Sialadenitis","Stomatitis","Gingivitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the salivary gland is sialadenitis.  Why A wrong: Glossitis is tongue inflammation.  Why C wrong: Stomatitis is mouth inflammation.  Why D wrong: Gingivitis is gum inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Inflammation of the gums is called:","options":["Gingivitis","Glossitis","Stomatitis","Cheilitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the gum is known as gingivitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe inflammation of the tongue, mouth, and lips respectively."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Tenesmus is best defined as:","options":["Painless rectal bleeding","Ineffective and painful straining at stool","Foul breath","Excess salivation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tenesmus is ineffective straining of stool (painful, ineffective urge to defecate).  Why A wrong: Painless rectal bleeding describes occult/internal haemorrhoid bleeding.  Why C wrong: Foul breath is halitosis.  Why D wrong: Excess salivation is unrelated to tenesmus."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which test is used for the diagnosis of E. coli (enteric pathology)?","options":["Stool occult blood test","Stool culture","Liver function test","Endoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A stool test (stool culture) is used for the diagnosis of E. coli.  Why A wrong: Occult blood test detects GI bleeding, not E. coli specifically.  Why C wrong: Liver function tests assess hepatic function.  Why D wrong: Endoscopy visualizes mucosa but is not the E. coli diagnostic test."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Dumping syndrome is a complication of which surgery?","options":["Appendectomy","Gastrectomy","Cholecystectomy","Hemorrhoidectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dumping syndrome is the complication of gastrectomy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These surgeries are not associated with dumping syndrome per the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which vitamin supplementation is recommended after gastrectomy?","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D","Vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B12 supplementation is needed after gastrectomy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These vitamins are not the cited supplement for gastrectomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common cause of peptic ulcer is:","options":["Helicobacter pylori","Rotavirus","E. coli","Coxsackievirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of peptic ulcer is Helicobacter pylori (and NSAIDs).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause childhood diarrhea, cholecystitis, and pericarditis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The most common site for curling's ulcer is the:","options":["Proximal duodenum","Stomach","Rectum","Esophagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site for Curling's ulcer is the proximal duodenum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the keyed most common Curling's ulcer site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Corrective surgery for Hirschsprung's disease is the:","options":["Whipple procedure","Duhamel procedure","Nissen fundoplication","Billroth I"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The corrective surgery for Hirschsprung's disease is the Duhamel procedure.  Why A wrong: The Whipple procedure is for pancreatic/duodenal cancer.  Why C wrong: Nissen fundoplication is for GERD.  Why D wrong: Billroth I is a gastroduodenostomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A jelly-like (red currant jelly) stool is seen in which condition?","options":["Appendicitis","Intussusception","Cholecystitis","Achalasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A jelly-like stool (red currant jelly stool) is seen in intussusception.  Why A wrong: Appendicitis presents with right lower quadrant pain (McBurney's point).  Why C wrong: Cholecystitis presents with right upper quadrant pain.  Why D wrong: Achalasia is an esophageal motility disorder."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Ribbon-like stool is seen in which condition?","options":["Intussusception","Colorectal cancer","Cirrhosis","Pericarditis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ribbon-like stool is seen in colorectal cancer.  Why A wrong: Intussusception presents with red currant jelly stool.  Why C/D wrong: These are not associated with ribbon-like stool."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"McBurney's point, used in the assessment for appendicitis, is located:","options":["In the left upper quadrant","In the right lower quadrant (midway between the umbilicus and anterior superior iliac spine on the right side)","In the epigastric region","At the costal margin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: McBurney's point is midway between the umbilicus and anterior superior iliac spine on the right side, used in appendicitis assessment (appendix is in the right lower quadrant).  Why A/C/D wrong: These locations do not match McBurney's point."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The vermiform appendix is part of which organ?","options":["Stomach","Cecum (large intestine)","Duodenum","Liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vermiform appendix is part of the large intestine, attached at the cecum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not where the vermiform appendix arises."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Murphy's sign is associated with which condition?","options":["Appendicitis","Cholecystitis (gallbladder)","Pancreatitis","Hepatitis A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Murphy's sign relates to cholecystitis — gallbladder gets impacted in the cystic duct or neck of the gallbladder, causing pain on palpation of the right upper quadrant during inspiration.  Why A wrong: Appendicitis is associated with McBurney's point.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the conditions of Murphy's sign."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Cullen's sign and Turner's sign (bluish discoloration) are associated with which condition?","options":["Cholecystitis","Acute pancreatitis","Appendicitis","Achalasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of acute pancreatitis is elevated serum lipase and amylase, with Cullen's sign and Turner's sign noted.  Why A wrong: Cholecystitis is associated with Murphy's sign.  Why C wrong: Appendicitis is associated with McBurney's point.  Why D wrong: Achalasia is an esophageal motility disorder."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which cardinal lab values are elevated in acute pancreatitis?","options":["Elevated serum bilirubin only","Elevated serum lipase and amylase","Decreased serum amylase","Elevated portal vein pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of acute pancreatitis is elevated serum lipase and amylase.  Why A wrong: Bilirubin elevation alone is not the cardinal pancreatitis marker.  Why C wrong: Amylase is elevated, not decreased.  Why D wrong: Portal vein pressure relates to portal hypertension, not pancreatitis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A positive Queckenstedt test (jugular vein pressure, pain) confirms which condition?","options":["Confirmation of CSF flow obstruction (spinal subarachnoid block)","Appendicitis","Cholecystitis","Esophageal varices"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A positive Queckenstedt test confirms an obstruction (block) of CSF flow.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not assessed by the Queckenstedt test."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Boerhaave syndrome is described as a rupture of one or both eyes/structures associated with which mechanism?","options":["Rupture of the esophagus (full-thickness, at the lower 1/3 posteriorly) typically from forceful vomiting","Fracture of the jaw","Rupture of the trachea","Perforation of the duodenum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Boerhaave syndrome is a full-thickness rupture of the esophagus (at the lower 1/3, posteriorly), typically caused by forceful vomiting leading to esophageal rupture.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe Boerhaave syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The excretory system consists of four systems. Which of the following is part of the excretory system? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Urinary system","Skin (integumentary system)","Respiratory system","Digestive system","Skeletal system"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The excretory system consists of the urinary system, skin (integumentary), respiratory system, and digestive system.  Why E wrong: The skeletal system is not listed as part of the excretory system."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The urinary system is also known as:","options":["The lymphatic system","The renal system (urology study)","The endocrine system","The portal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The urinary system is also known as the renal system; its study is urology.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated body systems."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which of the following are organs of the urinary system? (Select All That Apply)","options":["2 kidneys to form urine","2 ureters that convey urine from kidneys to bladder","1 urinary bladder as reservoir of urine","1 urethra to pass the urine from the bladder to the outside","2 adrenal glands as the main filtering unit"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Organs of the urinary system include 2 kidneys (form urine), 2 ureters (convey urine to bladder), 1 urinary bladder (reservoir), and 1 urethra (pass urine outside).  Why E wrong: Adrenal glands are endocrine glands, not the urinary filtering unit (the nephron filters)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the urinary system? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Maintain homeostasis by controlling composition, volume, and pressure of blood","Formation and excretion of urine","Maintenance of pH (regulate blood acid-base composition / electrolyte balance)","Maintain blood osmolarity and excrete waste and foreign substances (ammonia, urea, bilirubin, creatinine, uric acid)","Production of digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Functions include maintaining homeostasis (composition, volume, pressure of blood), formation and excretion of urine, regulating blood composition/electrolyte balance and pH, maintaining blood osmolarity, gluconeogenesis, and excreting waste/foreign substances (ammonia, urea, bilirubin, creatinine, uric acid).  Why E wrong: Production of digestive enzymes is a GI/pancreatic function, not urinary."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The kidneys are described as paired reddish brown bean-shaped organs. In which location are they found?","options":["Within the thoracic cavity","On both sides of the vertebral column (T12, L1 to L3) in the retroperitoneal organ (lies below the peritoneum)","In the pelvic cavity only","Anterior to the abdominal wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys are located on both sides of the vertebral column (T12, L1 to L3), as a retroperitoneal organ lying below the peritoneum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated retroperitoneal vertebral location."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The size and weight of each kidney is approximately:","options":["4 × 3 cm, 50 gm","11 × 6 × 3 cm, 150 gm","20 × 10 cm, 300 gm","5 × 2 cm, 25 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The size and weight of each kidney is about 11 × 6 × 3 cm and 150 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dimensions/weights do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which kidney is positioned slightly lower and narrower, and why?","options":["The left kidney is lower because of the liver on the left side","The right kidney is positioned slightly lower and narrower because of the liver on the right side","Both kidneys are at exactly the same level","The right kidney is higher because of the spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right kidney is positioned slightly lower and narrower because of the liver on the right side; the left kidney is longer and narrower.  Why A wrong: It is the right kidney that is lower, due to the liver on the right.  Why C wrong: They are not at the same level.  Why D wrong: The spleen is not the cause; the liver positions the right kidney lower."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"What is the approximate kidney perfusion (renal blood flow), as a percentage of cardiac output?","options":["5% of cardiac output","20–25% (about 1200 mL/min) of blood per cardiac output","50% of cardiac output","75% of cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys receive 20–25% (1200 mL/min) of blood per cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated renal perfusion percentage."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Each kidney is enclosed in three layers of fatty/connective tissue. The structural and functional unit of the kidney is the:","options":["Glomerulus","Nephron","Bowman's capsule","Renal pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney.  Why A wrong: The glomerulus is a network of capillaries within the nephron, not the whole functional unit.  Why C wrong: Bowman's capsule surrounds the glomerulus within the nephron.  Why D wrong: The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped collecting structure, not the functional unit."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Where is the glomerulus found in the kidney?","options":["In the renal pelvis","In the nephron","In the ureter","In the urinary bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The glomerulus is found in the nephron.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the location of the glomerulus."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"From the kidney's renal sinus, urine drains in sequence. Which is the correct order of internal renal structures?","options":["Renal cortex → renal medulla → minor calyx → major calyx → renal pelvis","Renal pelvis → ureter → cortex","Glomerulus → ureter → bladder","Renal medulla → cortex → urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Internal structures progress from the outer renal cortex to the renal medulla (renal pyramids), then to the minor calyces, major calyces, and renal pelvis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These orders are incorrect or skip key structures."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The renal cortex is described as:","options":["The inner part of the kidney containing renal pyramids","The outer part of the kidney, containing renal corpuscles, PCT, DCT, and collecting duct","The funnel-shaped collecting structure","The tube draining urine to the bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The renal cortex is the outer part of the kidney and contains renal corpuscles, PCT, DCT, and collecting duct.  Why A wrong: The renal medulla is the inner part with renal pyramids.  Why C wrong: The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped collecting structure.  Why D wrong: The ureter drains urine to the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The renal medulla is the innermost part of the kidney and consists of:","options":["Renal corpuscles only","Cone-shaped/triangular-shaped structures called renal pyramids","The renal pelvis","The Bowman's capsule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The renal medulla is the innermost part and consists of cone-shaped/triangular renal pyramids (each kidney has 8–14 pyramids).  Why A wrong: Renal corpuscles are in the cortex.  Why C/D wrong: The renal pelvis and Bowman's capsule are not the medulla itself."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"How many renal pyramids are present in each kidney?","options":["1 to 4","8 to 14","20 to 25","40 to 50"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each kidney has 8 to 14 renal pyramids.  Why A/C/D wrong: These numbers do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The renal papillae of the pyramids open into:","options":["Major calyces directly","Minor calyces","The renal pelvis directly","The ureter directly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each renal papilla opens into a minor calyx (minor calyces).  Why A wrong: Minor calyces drain into major calyces; the papillae open into the minor calyces first.  Why C/D wrong: The papillae do not open directly into the renal pelvis or ureter."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The renal sinus is best described as:","options":["The outermost fatty layer of the kidney","A space which extends into the kidney from the hilum","A type of nephron","The capsule surrounding the glomerulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The renal sinus is a space which extends into the kidney from the hilum and contains branches of the renal artery and vein, renal pelvis, lymphatics, nerves, and perirenal fat.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the renal sinus."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which structures pass through the renal sinus/hilum? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Branches of renal artery","Tributary of renal vein","Renal pelvis","Lymphatics, nerves, and perirenal fat","The urinary bladder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The renal sinus contains branches of the renal artery, tributary of the renal vein, renal pelvis, lymphatics, nerves, and perirenal fat.  Why E wrong: The urinary bladder is outside the kidney and does not pass through the renal sinus."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which statement about the glomerulus is correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The renal artery divides into small vessels and forms the glomerulus","The glomerulus is responsible for filtration","The glomerulus is a network of capillaries in the branch of the renal afferent arteriole","Note that the renal artery is in the branch of the glomerulus","The glomerulus produces digestive enzymes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The renal artery divides into small vessels and forms the glomerulus; the glomerulus is responsible for filtration; it is a network of capillaries in the branch of the renal afferent arteriole.  Why D wrong: The statement is reversed/garbled and not a correct functional description.  Why E wrong: The glomerulus filters blood; it does not produce digestive enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The blood vessels associated with the nephron flow in which correct order?","options":["Renal artery → segmental artery → interlobar artery → arcuate artery → interlobular artery → afferent arteriole → glomerulus → efferent arteriole → peritubular capillaries/vasa recta","Renal vein → glomerulus → afferent arteriole","Efferent arteriole → glomerulus → afferent arteriole","Arcuate artery → renal artery → segmental artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nephron-associated blood vessels flow from renal artery → segmental → interlobar → arcuate → interlobular arteries → afferent arteriole → glomerulus → efferent arteriole → peritubular capillaries/vasa recta.  Why B/C/D wrong: These orders are reversed or incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Each kidney has approximately how many nephrons?","options":["2 lakh nephrons in each kidney","About 1 million (10 lakh) nephrons in each kidney, average 1.2 million","8 to 14 nephrons","100 nephrons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each kidney has approximately 1 million (10 lakh) nephrons, averaging about 1.2 million in each kidney.  Why A/C/D wrong: These numbers do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The nephron is described as the functional, excretory, and filtration unit of the:","options":["Liver","Kidney","Pancreas","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nephron is the functional, excretory, and filtration unit of the kidney.  Why A/C/D wrong: These organs do not contain nephrons."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"There are two main types of nephrons. Which two are correct?","options":["Cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons","Afferent and efferent nephrons","Proximal and distal nephrons","Major and minor nephrons"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two types of nephrons are juxtamedullary and cortical nephrons.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recognized types of nephrons."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Bowman's capsule is best described as:","options":["The collecting duct of the nephron","The first part of the nephron where blood is initially filtered (the first filtrate is formed)","The reservoir of urine","A type of macula densa cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bowman's capsule is the first part of the nephron where blood is initially filtered (the first filtrate is formed).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other structures or cell types."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"In which segment of the nephron does selective re-absorption occur (folded structure connected to Bowman's capsule)?","options":["Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule (DCT)","Collecting duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) is a folded structure connected to Bowman's capsule where selective re-absorption occurs.  Why B wrong: The loop of Henle is a selectively permeable loop that descends into the medulla.  Why C wrong: The DCT is a folded structure connected to the loop of Henle.  Why D wrong: The collecting duct collects filtrate; it is not the PCT."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The loop of Henle is best described as:","options":["The first filtration site","A selectively permeable loop that descends into the medulla (loop diuretics e.g. lasix work here)","The cortical re-absorption site only","The collecting duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The loop of Henle is a selectively permeable loop that descends into the medulla; loop diuretics (e.g., lasix) act here.  Why A wrong: The first filtration occurs at Bowman's capsule.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other nephron parts."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Juxtamedullary nephrons contain which of the following cells? (Select All That Apply)","options":["JG (Juxtaglomerular) cells","Macula densa cells","Extra-glomerular mesangial (EGM) cells","Goblet cells","Parietal cells"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Juxtamedullary nephrons contain JG cells, macula densa cells, and extra-glomerular mesangial (EGM) cells.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells are mucus-secreting cells in the GI tract.  Why E wrong: Parietal cells secrete HCl in the stomach."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"JG (juxtaglomerular) cells are located in the afferent arteriole and synthesize and store renin. Renin's release is triggered by:","options":["An increase in NaCl and increased blood flow","A decrease in NaCl, decreased blood flow to the kidney, and increased sympathetic activity (response to decreased blood pressure)","High glucose levels only","Increased oxygen tension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JG cells synthesize and store renin in response to decreased NaCl, decreased blood flow to the kidney, and increased sympathetic activity; renin regulates the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to maintain blood pressure.  Why A wrong: The trigger is a decrease (not increase) in NaCl and blood flow.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated triggers for renin release."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"JG cells act as which type of receptor?","options":["Chemoreceptor","Baroreceptor (volume receptor)","Osmoreceptor","Thermoreceptor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JG cells act as baroreceptors (volume receptors).  Why A wrong: Macula densa cells act as chemoreceptors.  Why C/D wrong: JG cells are not osmoreceptors or thermoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Macula densa cells are located within the wall of the DCT and ascending limb of the loop of Henle. They function as:","options":["Baroreceptors","Chemoreceptors that detect the elevated level of NaCl and send this information to JG cells","Osmoreceptors only","Thermoreceptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macula densa cells are located in the DCT and ascending loop of Henle wall; they act as chemoreceptors detecting the elevated NaCl level and send information to the JG cells.  Why A wrong: JG cells are the baroreceptors.  Why C/D wrong: Macula densa cells are chemoreceptors, not osmoreceptors or thermoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Extra-glomerular mesangial (EGM) cells are located:","options":["In the proximal convoluted tubule","Within the loop of glomerulus, between afferent arteriole and efferent arteriole/glomerular mesangial cells","In the urinary bladder","In the renal pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EGM cells are located within the loop of glomerulus, between the afferent arteriole and efferent arteriole (glomerular mesangial cells); their function is contractile in nature so regulate filtration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the location of EGM cells."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"The normal renal threshold of glucose is:","options":["80–100 mg/dl","160–180 mg/dl","200–250 mg/dl","100–120 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal renal threshold of glucose is 160–180 mg/dl.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated renal threshold."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Urine formation occurs in three stages. Which of the following are the correct stages? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ultrafiltration","Reabsorption","Tubular secretion","Coagulation","Phagocytosis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Urine formation occurs in three stages — ultrafiltration (glomerular filtration), reabsorption (selective reabsorption), and tubular secretion.  Why D/E wrong: Coagulation and phagocytosis are not stages of urine formation."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Regarding ultrafiltration (glomerular filtration), which values are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Net filtration pressure is 10 mm Hg","Normal renal blood flow is 1200 mL/min","Normal renal plasma flow is 660 mL/min","Net filtration pressure is 50 mm Hg","Normal renal blood flow is 100 mL/min"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: For ultrafiltration, net filtration pressure is 10 mm Hg, normal renal blood flow is 1200 mL/min, and normal renal plasma flow is 660 mL/min.  Why D wrong: Net filtration pressure is 10 mm Hg, not 50.  Why E wrong: Renal blood flow is 1200 mL/min, not 100."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Maximum selective reabsorption of water and sodium occurs in which part of the nephron?","options":["Distal convoluted tubule (DCT)","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Collecting duct","Bowman's capsule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maximum selective reabsorption occurs in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT); maximum re-absorption of water and sodium occurs in the PCT.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the site of maximum water and sodium reabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Where are glucose and protein reabsorbed (100%) in the nephron?","options":["Loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Distal convoluted tubule (DCT)","Collecting duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucose and protein are 100% reabsorbed in the PCT.  Why A/C/D wrong: These segments are not the site of 100% glucose and protein reabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Renal threshold or transport maximum is best defined as:","options":["The minimum osmolarity of plasma","The maximum capacity of the kidney to re-absorb a substance (once exceeded, the substance is lost as renal failure)","The rate of glomerular filtration","The net filtration pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Renal threshold or transport maximum is the maximum capacity of the kidney to re-absorb a substance; once exceeded, the substance is lost.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not define renal threshold/transport maximum."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"During tubular secretion, where is a substance moved from blood into the filtrate (urine)?","options":["Glomerulus only","PCT, LOH, DCT, and collecting tubule","Bowman's capsule only","Renal pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tubular secretion, a substance is moved from blood to filtrate (urine); secretion occurs in PCT, LOH, DCT, and collecting tubule.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the complete sites of tubular secretion."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"During tubular secretion, which substances are secreted? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Principal cells reabsorb sodium ions with excreted potassium ions","Intercalated cells reabsorb potassium ions and excrete H+","Glucose is secreted in the DCT","Hydrogen ions (H+) are secreted","Protein is secreted in the loop of Henle"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: In tubular secretion, principal cells reabsorb sodium ions with excreted potassium ions, intercalated cells reabsorb potassium ions and excrete H+, and hydrogen ions are secreted.  Why C wrong: Glucose is reabsorbed (100% in PCT), not secreted in the DCT.  Why E wrong: Protein is reabsorbed 100% in the PCT; it is not secreted in the loop of Henle."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Which hormone is secreted from the adrenal cortex and acts on the DCT to increase re-absorption of sodium ions and water, with excretion of potassium ions?","options":["ADH (vasopressin)","Aldosterone","ANP (Atrial Natriuretic peptide)","PTH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone is secreted from the adrenal cortex, acts on the DCT, and increases re-absorption of sodium ions and water with excretion of potassium ions.  Why A wrong: ADH increases water reabsorption but acts on DCT/collecting duct for water (not the Na/K exchange role described).  Why C wrong: ANP causes natriuresis (sodium excretion).  Why D wrong: PTH regulates calcium re-absorption."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"ADH (Vasopressin) acts on which nephron segment and is probably secreted/released from where?","options":["Acts on PCT; secreted from adrenal cortex","Acts on the DCT and collecting tubule (DCT/CT, water-water reabsorption); stored and released by the posterior pituitary gland","Acts on the loop of Henle; secreted from anterior pituitary","Acts on glomerulus; secreted from kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ADH (vasopressin) acts on the DCT and collecting tubule for water reabsorption and is stored and released by the posterior pituitary gland (probably acts on DCT/CT for water-water reabsorption).  Why A/C/D wrong: ADH does not act on the PCT, loop of Henle, or glomerulus per the source, nor is it from the adrenal cortex/anterior pituitary/kidney."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) is secreted from which structure and what is its main action?","options":["Secreted from area of the kidney; reabsorbs sodium","Secreted from the atria of the heart; causes natriuresis (sodium excretion)","Secreted from posterior pituitary; reabsorbs water","Secreted from adrenal cortex; excretes potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ANP (Atrial Natriuretic Peptide) is secreted from the atria of the heart and causes natriuresis (sodium excretion).  Why A wrong: ANP causes excretion, not reabsorption, of sodium.  Why C wrong: That describes ADH from the posterior pituitary.  Why D wrong: That describes aldosterone from the adrenal cortex."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"PTH (Parathyroid hormone) acts on the kidney to regulate the re-absorption of which ion?","options":["Sodium","Calcium","Potassium","Chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PTH acts on the kidney and its action regulates the re-absorption of calcium ions.  Why A wrong: Sodium reabsorption is regulated by aldosterone.  Why C wrong: Potassium excretion is influenced by aldosterone.  Why D wrong: PTH's renal action described is on calcium, not chloride."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A patient on furosemide (lasix) asks how it works. The nurse explains the drug acts on which part of the nephron?","options":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Bowman's capsule","Collecting duct only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Loop diuretics such as lasix act on the loop of Henle (a selectively permeable loop that descends into the medulla).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the site of action stated for loop diuretics."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Constipation/haemorrhoid risk factors and prevention…","question":"A diabetic patient has a blood glucose of 200 mg/dl and glucosuria. Based on the renal threshold, why does glucose appear in the urine?","options":["The kidney never reabsorbs glucose","Blood glucose has exceeded the renal threshold of 160–180 mg/dl, surpassing the transport maximum so glucose is lost in urine","ADH was secreted","The glomerulus stopped filtering"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal renal threshold of glucose is 160–180 mg/dl; once blood glucose exceeds the transport maximum, glucose can no longer be fully reabsorbed and spills into the urine.  Why A wrong: Glucose is normally 100% reabsorbed in the PCT below the threshold.  Why C wrong: ADH affects water reabsorption, not glucose spilling.  Why D wrong: Glomerular filtration continues; the issue is exceeded reabsorption capacity."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What does the Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) represent?","options":["Volume of filtrate formed by each kidney in each minute","Total volume of urine excreted per day","Rate of tubular reabsorption of sodium","Amount of blood filtered by the liver per hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR is the volume or amount of filtrate formed by each kidney in each minute (the rate of filtration of urine/blood).  Why B wrong: That describes daily urine output, not GFR.  Why C wrong: GFR measures filtration, not tubular reabsorption.  Why D wrong: The liver does not filter blood to form urinary filtrate; kidneys do."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal GFR value (in ml/min)?","options":["80–95 ml/min","100–110 ml/min","125–145 ml/min","150–170 ml/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal GFR is 125–145 ml/min.  Why A/B/D wrong: These values fall outside the stated normal GFR range of 125–145 ml/min."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Approximately how much filtrate is reabsorbed by the kidneys daily, leaving only 1–1.5 L excreted as urine?","options":["98–108 liters","178–179 liters","250–300 liters","50–60 liters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidney reabsorbs about 178–179 liters of filtrate daily, with only 1–1.5 L/day excreted as urine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated reabsorption figure of 178–179 liters per day."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The Bowman's capsule encloses the glomerulus, where filtrate collects in:","options":["Renal calyces","Capsular spaces","Collecting ducts","The renal pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each day the kidneys obtain a fixed amount of filtrate collected in capsular spaces.  Why A/C/D wrong: Calyces, collecting ducts, and pelvis are downstream urine-conducting structures, not where the initial filtrate of the Bowman's capsule collects."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which substance is used to measure GFR clinically?","options":["Urea","Inulin","Glucose","Albumin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inulin is used to measure GFR.  Why A wrong: Urea is reabsorbed and not an ideal GFR marker.  Why C wrong: Glucose is normally fully reabsorbed and not used to measure GFR.  Why D wrong: Albumin is a plasma protein not normally filtered."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What percentage of urine is water?","options":["80%","88%","96%","99%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Urine composition is approximately 96% water.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 96% water content of urine."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What percentage of urine is composed of urea and remaining (other) solutes?","options":["2% urea and 2% other solutes","4% urea and 4% other solutes","1% urea and 1% other solutes","5% urea and 5% other solutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine is about 96% water, 2% urea, and 2% remaining solutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2% urea and 2% other solutes."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal specific gravity of urine?","options":["1.001–1.005","1.010–1.030","1.040–1.060","1.100–1.200"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Specific gravity of urine is 1.010–1.030 (also referenced as 1.010–1.030 in NIA Jaipur 2023).  Why A/C/D wrong: These fall outside the normal urine specific gravity range of 1.010–1.030."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal pH of urine?","options":["4.5–8 (acidic)","7.0–7.5 (neutral)","8.5–9.5 (alkaline)","2.0–4.0 (strongly acidic)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pH of urine is 4.5–8 and is acidic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the stated acidic urine pH range of 4.5–8."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal daily urine formation (output) per day?","options":["500–800 ml/day","800–1000 ml/day","1000–1500 ml/day","2000–2500 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Daily urine formation is 1000–1500 ml/day.  Why A/B/D wrong: These fall outside the stated normal daily urine formation of 1000–1500 ml/day."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Presence of which substance in urine is considered pathological (abnormal)?","options":["Water","Urea","Glucose, ketone bodies, potassium, blood cells, pus, casts and bacteria","Sodium and potassium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states presence of albumin, glucose, ketone bodies, potassium, blood cells, pus, casts and bacteria indicate a pathological condition.  Why A/B/D wrong: Water, urea, and small amounts of sodium/potassium are normal urine constituents."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Regulation of potassium and sodium in the body is achieved by which two mechanisms?","options":["RAAS mechanism and Aldosterone","ADH and Renin only","Insulin and glucagon","Cortisol and adrenaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Regulation of potassium and sodium in the body is by (1) RAAS mechanism (Angiotensin-II) and (2) Aldosterone.  Why B/C/D wrong: ADH/renin alone, pancreatic hormones, and stress hormones are not the stated regulators of sodium and potassium here."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"A \"natural diuretic\" listed in the source includes which of the following?","options":["Caffeine, tea, soda, alcohol in beer, wine & mixed drink","Sodium chloride solution","Distilled water only","Aldosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Natural diuretics include caffeine, tea, soda, alcohol in beer, wine and mixed drink.  Why B/C wrong: These are not listed as natural diuretics.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone promotes sodium/water reabsorption (anti-diuretic effect), not diuresis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Regulation of blood pH by the buffer system involves which substance reabsorption?","options":["Carbonic acid and sodium","Carbonic acid and sodium bi-carbonate","Potassium and chloride","Albumin and glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Regulation of blood pH by the buffer system involves carbonic acid and sodium bi-carbonate.  Why A wrong: Sodium bi-carbonate (not plain sodium) is the buffer component.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated buffer components for pH regulation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which hormone secreted by the kidney regulates the production of red blood cells (RBCs)?","options":["Aldosterone","Renin","Erythropoietin","Calcitriol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Regulation of RBC production is by erythropoietin hormone of the kidney.  Why A wrong: Aldosterone regulates sodium/potassium balance.  Why B wrong: Renin initiates the RAAS pathway, not RBC production.  Why D wrong: Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Where in the nephron is the active form of vitamin D primarily made?","options":["Glomerulus","Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Collecting duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the proximal convoluted tubule of the kidney, calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol) becomes its active form — the active form of vitamin D is normally made in the kidney.  Why A/C/D wrong: These nephron segments are not stated as the site of vitamin D activation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Calcitriol, the active form of vitamin D, is also known by which chemical name?","options":["Cholecalciferol","Ergocalciferol","1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","Retinol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D, also known as 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol.  Why A wrong: Cholecalciferol is vitamin D3 (the precursor), not the active form.  Why B wrong: Ergocalciferol is vitamin D2.  Why D wrong: Retinol is vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) primarily regulates which of the following?","options":["Blood pressure and fluid and electrolyte balance","Blood glucose levels","Body temperature","Acid-base balance only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RAAS is a hormone system that regulates blood pressure and fluid and electrolyte balance, as well as systemic vascular resistance.  Why B/C wrong: RAAS does not primarily regulate glucose or temperature.  Why D wrong: While it influences electrolytes, its primary stated role is blood pressure and fluid/electrolyte balance, not acid-base balance alone."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"When blood flow to the kidneys is reduced (low blood pressure), the kidneys convert which precursor into renin?","options":["Angiotensinogen","Prorenin","Angiotensin I","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When renal blood flow is reduced, juxtaglomerular cells in the kidneys convert the precursor prorenin (already present in the blood) into renin.  Why A wrong: Angiotensinogen is the liver-derived protein renin acts on, not the renin precursor.  Why C wrong: Angiotensin I is formed downstream from angiotensinogen by renin.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is the final effector hormone of RAAS."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Renin then carries out the conversion of which substance, which is released by the liver?","options":["Angiotensin II to Angiotensin III","Angiotensinogen to Angiotensin I","Prorenin to renin","Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma renin carries out the conversion of angiotensinogen (released by the liver) to angiotensin I.  Why A wrong: This conversion is not part of the described primary pathway.  Why C wrong: Prorenin-to-renin conversion occurs in the kidneys, not by renin.  Why D wrong: Angiotensin I to II conversion is done by ACE, not renin."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Angiotensin I is converted to Angiotensin II by which enzyme, primarily in the lungs?","options":["Renin","Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE)","Aldosterone synthase","Carbonic anhydrase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angiotensin I is subsequently converted to angiotensin II by the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) found on the surface of vascular endothelial cells of the lungs.  Why A wrong: Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, not I to II.  Why C wrong: Aldosterone synthase produces aldosterone, not angiotensin II.  Why D wrong: Carbonic anhydrase is involved in bicarbonate/CO2 handling, not RAAS."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor peptide. What is its direct effect on blood vessels and blood pressure?","options":["Dilates vessels and lowers blood pressure","Narrows blood vessels, increasing blood pressure (hypertension)","Has no effect on vessel diameter","Only affects renal arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angiotensin II constricts (narrows) blood vessels, resulting in increased blood pressure or hypertension.  Why A wrong: It is a vasoconstrictor, not a vasodilator.  Why C wrong: It has a strong constricting effect on vessels.  Why D wrong: It acts on vessels systemically, not only renal arteries."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Angiotensin II also stimulates the secretion of which hormone from the adrenal cortex?","options":["Cortisol","Adrenaline","Aldosterone","Erythropoietin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Angiotensin II stimulates the secretion of the hormone aldosterone from the adrenal cortex.  Why A/B wrong: Cortisol and adrenaline are adrenal hormones not stimulated by angiotensin II in this pathway.  Why D wrong: Erythropoietin is produced by the kidney, not the adrenal cortex."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Aldosterone causes the renal tubules to increase the reabsorption of which electrolyte, leading to reabsorption of water into the blood?","options":["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Chloride only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone causes the renal tubules to increase reabsorption of sodium, which in turn causes reabsorption of water into the blood (it acts on the kidneys to stimulate reabsorption of NaCl/water and NaCl/H2O retention).  Why A wrong: Aldosterone promotes potassium excretion, not reabsorption.  Why C/D wrong: Sodium (with accompanying water) is the primary electrolyte reabsorbed under aldosterone."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The net effect of aldosterone-driven sodium and water reabsorption is to:","options":["Decrease the volume of extracellular fluid (ECF)","Increase the volume of extracellular fluid (ECF) in the body","Have no effect on ECF volume","Increase urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This increases the volume of extracellular fluid in the body, which also decreases sodium concentration.  Why A wrong: ECF volume increases, not decreases.  Why C wrong: There is a clear increase in ECF volume.  Why D wrong: Increased reabsorption decreases urine output (water retention), not increases it."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"In the aldosterone mechanism, when does the adrenal cortex release aldosterone?","options":["When blood K+ level rises and/or blood Na+ level falls","When blood glucose rises","When blood pH rises","When body temperature falls"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the aldosterone mechanism flowchart, when blood K+ level rises and blood Na+ level falls, aldosterone is released from the adrenal cortex and targets the kidneys.  Why B/C/D wrong: Glucose, pH, and temperature are not the stated triggers in the aldosterone mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"When aldosterone targets the kidneys, what are the resulting electrolyte effects that restore blood Na+/K+ to normal?","options":["K+ release falls and Na+ is reabsorbed by kidneys","K+ is reabsorbed and Na+ is released","Both K+ and Na+ are released","Both K+ and Na+ are reabsorbed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone targets the kidneys so that K+ release falls and Na+ is reabsorbed by the kidneys, restoring blood Na+/K+ to normal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated mechanism of decreased K+ release and increased Na+ reabsorption."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which feature best describes the ureters?","options":["Fibrovascular organs made by muscular tissues","Fibromuscular tubular-like structures which connect kidney to bladder","Hollow distensible organs that store urine","The terminal part of the urinary system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ureters are tube-like structures which connect kidney to bladder, originating from the renal pelvis.  Why A wrong: That describes the urinary bladder (a fibrovascular muscular organ), not the ureters.  Why C wrong: That describes the bladder, not the ureters.  Why D wrong: The urethra is the terminal part of the urinary system."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Through what mechanism is urine transported from the kidney to the bladder?","options":["Active transport against gravity only","Peristaltic movement of the ureters","Simple diffusion","Capillary action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine from the kidney is transported to the bladder by the peristaltic movement of ureters.  Why A/C/D wrong: Transport occurs via peristalsis, not active transport alone, diffusion, or capillary action."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the approximate length of the ureters?","options":["10 cm (4 cm abdominal portion + 6 cm pelvic portion)","25 cm (12.5 cm abdominal portion + 12.5 cm pelvic portion)","40 cm (20 cm abdominal + 20 cm pelvic)","5 cm total"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ureters length is 25 cm (12.5 cm abdominal portion + 12.5 cm pelvic portion).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 25 cm total length divided into 12.5 cm + 12.5 cm."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the urinary bladder?","options":["A muscular, hollow, distensible/flexible organ and reservoir of urine","A solid fibrous organ","Located in the abdominal cavity above the diaphragm","The terminal part of the urinary tract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urinary bladder is a muscular, hollow, distensible/flexible organ and is the reservoir of urine, located in the pelvic cavity.  Why B wrong: It is hollow and distensible, not solid fibrous.  Why C wrong: It is in the pelvic cavity, not above the diaphragm.  Why D wrong: The urethra (not bladder) is the terminal part of the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What shape does an empty urinary bladder take?","options":["Round shape","Pear shape","Oval shape","Triangular shape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal shape of the urinary bladder is pear shape when empty; when the bladder empties, its shape is tetrahedral and with UB full that time it becomes oval shape.  Why A wrong: Round is not the stated empty shape.  Why C wrong: Oval shape occurs when the bladder is full.  Why D wrong: The trigone area is triangular, but the empty bladder overall is pear-shaped."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The triangular area of the urinary bladder is called:","options":["Trigone","Detrusor","Fundus","Apex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The triangular area of the bladder is called trigone.  Why B wrong: The detrusor is the smooth muscle of the bladder wall.  Why C/D wrong: Fundus and apex are not the stated name for the triangular area."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the maximum capacity of the urinary bladder?","options":["300–400 ml of urine","500–600 ml of urine","600–800 ml of urine","1000–1200 ml of urine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maximum capacity of the bladder is 600–800 ml of urine.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated maximum bladder capacity of 600–800 ml."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"At approximately what volume of bladder filling does the first urge for urination occur?","options":["100 ml","250 ml","400 ml","600 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first urge for urination occurs when the bladder is filled at 250 ml of urine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 250 ml first-urge volume."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The middle layer of the urinary bladder wall is formed by smooth muscle, known as the detrusor muscle. What does it do during micturition?","options":["Relaxes to store urine","Contracts to expel urine out of the bladder","Forms the trigone","Lines the inner bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The middle layer (detrusor muscle) of smooth muscle contracts to expel urine out of the bladder.  Why A wrong: Detrusor contraction (not relaxation) empties the bladder.  Why C wrong: The trigone is a triangular region, not formed by the detrusor's emptying action.  Why D wrong: The inner bladder lining is transitional epithelium, not the detrusor."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The inner layer of the urinary bladder is formed by which type of epithelium?","options":["Transitional epithelium (urothelium)","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The inner layer of the urinary bladder is formed by transitional epithelium (urothelium).  Why B/C/D wrong: These epithelial types are not stated as the inner bladder lining."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The pontine micturition centre (PMC) is situated in which part of the spinal cord?","options":["Cervical (C1-C2) segment","Thoracic (T6-T8) segment","Sacral portion (S2-S3 segment)","Lumbar (L4-L5) segment"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pontine micturition centre (PMC) is situated in the sacral portion (S2-S3 segment) of the spinal cord.  Why A/B/D wrong: These segments do not match the stated S2-S3 location."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which statement about the urethra is correct?","options":["It is the terminal part of the urinary system","It connects the kidney to the bladder","It is the reservoir of urine","It produces urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urethra is the terminal part of the urinary system.  Why B wrong: The ureters connect kidney to bladder.  Why C wrong: The bladder is the urine reservoir.  Why D wrong: The kidneys (nephrons) produce urine, not the urethra."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the length of the male urethra, and what does it carry?","options":["18–20 cm long; passes through penis, common for urination and ejaculation","1.5–2 inches long; carries urine only","5 cm long; carries semen only","30 cm long; carries blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The male urethra is 18–20 cm long, passes through the penis, and is common for both urination and ejaculation.  Why B wrong: 1.5–2 inches describes the female urethra (urine only).  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated male urethra length or function."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the length of the female urethra and where does it open?","options":["18–20 cm, opens into the penis","3–4 cm (1.5–2 inches) long, opens just anterior to vagina, only for urination","25 cm, opens at the rectum","10 cm, common for urination and reproduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The female urethra is 3–4 cm (1.5–2 inches) long, opens just anterior to the vagina, and is only for urination.  Why A wrong: 18–20 cm and penis describe the male urethra.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated female urethra length, opening, or function."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which of the following lists the parts of the male urethra correctly?","options":["Prostatic, membranous, spongy (penile) urethra","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar urethra","Internal, external, middle urethra","Cortical, medullary, pelvic urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parts of the male urethra are: prostatic urethra (2–3 cm), membranous urethra (1 cm), and spongy urethra/penile urethra (15–16 cm, the longest part).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the anatomical divisions of the male urethra."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which is the longest part of the male urethra and its length?","options":["Prostatic urethra, 2–3 cm","Membranous urethra, 1 cm","Spongy/penile urethra, 15–16 cm","Internal urethra, 5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The spongy urethra/penile urethra is 15–16 cm and is the longest part of the urethra.  Why A wrong: Prostatic urethra is only 2–3 cm.  Why B wrong: Membranous urethra is 1 cm.  Why D wrong: \"Internal urethra, 5 cm\" is not a stated part."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Inflammation of the urethra is known as:","options":["Cystitis","Urethritis","Nephritis","Balanitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the urethra is known as urethritis, more common in females due to the shortness of the urethra.  Why A wrong: Cystitis is bladder inflammation.  Why C wrong: Nephritis is kidney inflammation.  Why D wrong: Balanitis is inflammation of the glans/prepuce."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The ducts from the bulbourethral glands open into which structure?","options":["The prostatic urethra","The penile urethra","The ureter","The seminal vesicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ducts from the bulbourethral glands open into the penile urethra.  Why A wrong: They open into the penile (not prostatic) urethra per the source.  Why C/D wrong: They do not open into the ureter or seminal vesicle."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Hypospadias is a congenital malformation of the urethra in which the urethra opens on which surface of the penis?","options":["Ventral surface (down side)","Dorsal surface (upper side)","Lateral surface","Tip of the glans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hypospadias, the urethra opens on the ventral surface (down side) of the penis.  Why B wrong: Dorsal-surface opening describes epispadias.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated openings for hypospadias."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Epispadias is a congenital malformation in which the urethra opens on which surface of the penis?","options":["Ventral surface (down side)","Dorsal surface (upper side)","Lateral surface","The scrotum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In epispadias, the urethra opens on the dorsal surface (upper side) of the penis.  Why A wrong: Ventral opening describes hypospadias.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated openings for epispadias."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What are the main functions of the male reproductive system?","options":["Producing and transporting sperm, ejaculating sperm into the female reproductive tract, and producing/secreting male hormones","Producing urine and excreting waste","Filtering blood and regulating pH","Storing nutrients and producing bile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The functions of the male reproductive system include producing and transporting sperm, ejaculating sperm into the female reproductive tract, and producing and secreting male hormones.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe functions of the urinary, renal, or digestive systems, not the male reproductive system."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The external structures of the male reproductive system are called:","options":["Internal accessory organs","Accessory organs / external genitalia, which include the penis, scrotum, epididymis, and testes","Seminal vesicles and prostate","Vas deferens and ejaculatory duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The external structures (located outside the body) are called accessory organs / external genitalia and include the penis, scrotum, epididymis, and testes.  Why A wrong: These are external, not internal.  Why C/D wrong: Seminal vesicles, prostate, vas deferens, and ejaculatory duct are part of the internal reproductive system."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which structures comprise the internal male reproductive system?","options":["Penis and scrotum","Vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate gland, and bulbo-urethral glands","Testes and epididymis only","Kidneys and ureters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The internal reproductive system is called the internal accessory organs and includes the vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate gland, and bulbo-urethral glands.  Why A/C wrong: Penis, scrotum, testes, and epididymis are external structures.  Why D wrong: Kidneys and ureters are urinary, not reproductive, organs."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The testes are the two reproductive glands of the male. What do they produce?","options":["The male gamete (sperm)","The female gamete (egg)","Urine","Bile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The testes produce the male gamete (sperm), while the ovary produces the female gamete (egg).  Why B wrong: The egg is produced by the ovary.  Why C/D wrong: The testes do not produce urine or bile."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The testes are referred to as testicles (male gonads) in animals. They are developed by mesoderm at which week of gestation?","options":["3rd week","5th week","8th week","12th week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The testes are developed by mesoderm at the 5th week of gestation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5th week of gestation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the shape and weight of the testes?","options":["Round shape and 5–10 gm","Oval shape and 10–15 gm","Triangular shape and 20–25 gm","Flat shape and 30 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The shape and weight of the testes are oval shape and 10–15 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated oval shape and 10–15 gm weight."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the size of the testes?","options":["4.5 cm × 2.5 cm × 3 cm thick","2 cm × 1 cm × 1 cm thick","10 cm × 5 cm × 2 cm thick","6 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm thick"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The size of the testes is 4.5 cm × 2.5 cm × 3 cm thick.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The cells of the testes found adjacent to the germinal epithelial cells, known as interstitial tubules in the testicle, produce testosterone in the presence of which hormone?","options":["Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)","Luteinizing hormone (LH)","Aldosterone","Erythropoietin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These cells produce testosterone in the presence of luteinizing hormone (LH).  Why A wrong: FSH is needed to support spermatogenesis, not testosterone production here.  Why C/D wrong: Aldosterone and erythropoietin are not involved in testosterone production."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Leydig cells produce and secrete which hormone after puberty?","options":["Estrogen","Male testosterone hormones","Progesterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leydig cells produce and secrete male testosterone hormones after puberty.  Why A/C wrong: Estrogen and progesterone are primarily female hormones.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is secreted by the anterior pituitary, not Leydig cells."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Sertoli cells, part of the seminiferous tubule, serve which primary function?","options":["They help in the process of spermatogenesis (support cells)","They produce urine","They secrete aldosterone","They filter blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sertoli cells are part of the seminiferous tubule that helps in the process of spermatogenesis; the \"S\" means S for support — they support and nourish the spermatozoa and help in the process of spermatogenesis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sertoli cells do not produce urine, secrete aldosterone, or filter blood."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Match the layers of the testes correctly: which layer is the outer layer of the testes?","options":["Tunica vaginalis — outer layer of the testes","Tunica albuginea — outer layer","Tunica vasculosa — outer layer","Tunica media — outer layer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tunica vaginalis is the outer layer of the testes.  Why B wrong: Tunica albuginea is the fibrous and middle layer of the testes.  Why C wrong: Tunica vasculosa is the innermost layer of the testes.  Why D wrong: \"Tunica media\" is not a stated layer of the testes."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which is the innermost layer of the testes?","options":["Tunica vaginalis","Tunica albuginea","Tunica vasculosa","Tunica serosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Tunica vasculosa is the innermost layer of the testes.  Why A wrong: Tunica vaginalis is the outer layer.  Why B wrong: Tunica albuginea is the fibrous/middle layer.  Why D wrong: \"Tunica serosa\" is not a stated layer of the testes."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Sperm (spermatozoa) is also known as:","options":["Spermatozoa","Oocyte","Zygote","Blastocyst"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm is also known as spermatozoa.  Why B wrong: Oocyte is the female gamete.  Why C wrong: Zygote is the fertilized egg.  Why D wrong: Blastocyst is an early embryonic stage."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, a process stimulated mainly by which hormone, with maturation occurring in the epididymis?","options":["LH","FSH (sperm production is FSH which is secreted by anterior pituitary gland)","Aldosterone","Testosterone only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hormone that increases sperm production is FSH, which is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland; a mature sperm has a head, a body, and a tail.  Why A wrong: LH primarily stimulates testosterone production by Leydig cells.  Why C wrong: Aldosterone is unrelated to spermatogenesis.  Why D wrong: FSH is the stated stimulating hormone for sperm production."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which part of the sperm contains the nucleus and the acrosome (containing enzymes such as hyaluronidase needed for ovum penetration during fertilization)?","options":["Head","Body","Tail","Neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm head is completely filled by the nucleus, containing DNA; it also has the acrosome that contains the enzymes (hyaluronidase) needed for ovum penetration during fertilization.  Why B wrong: The body contains mitochondria.  Why C wrong: The tail is given energy/power for propelling motion.  Why D wrong: \"Neck\" is not the structure described for nucleus/acrosome."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which part of the sperm is rich in mitochondria, providing energy for the tail to propel the sperm in motion?","options":["Head","Body","Acrosome","Nucleus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm body is rich in mitochondria; it is given energy or power to tail of the sperm for propelling motion.  Why A/C/D wrong: The head, acrosome, and nucleus are involved in penetration and genetics, not energy production for motility."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Successful spermatogenesis takes place at a temperature about how many degrees below the normal body temperature?","options":["1°C below","3°C below","5°C below","7°C below"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Successful spermatogenesis takes place at a temperature about 3°C below the normal body temperature.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3°C below body temperature."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the primary function of the spermatic cords?","options":["To suspend the testes in the scrotum","To produce urine","To store sperm","To produce testosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The spermatic cords suspend the testes in the scrotum; each cord contains a testicular artery, testicular vein, lymphatic and deferent duct and testicular nerves, which come together from origin place and form the spermatic cord.  Why B/C/D wrong: The spermatic cord suspends and supplies the testes; it does not produce urine, store sperm, or produce testosterone."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What are the two main functions of the penis?","options":["Sexual intercourse and urination","Sperm production and storage","Hormone production only","Urine filtration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The penis is the vital organ for sexual intercourse and urination; semen and urine leave from the penis through the urethra.  Why B wrong: Sperm production occurs in the testes.  Why C/D wrong: The penis is not primarily for hormone production or urine filtration."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The body of the penis is mainly formed by columns of which two erectile tissues?","options":["Corpus cavernosum and corpus spongiosum","Tunica albuginea and tunica vaginalis","Glans and prepuce","Seminal vesicle and prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The major structure of the body of the penis is formed by columns of corpus cavernosum and corpus spongiosum tissue.  Why B wrong: These are layers of the testes, not penile erectile tissue.  Why C wrong: Glans and prepuce are penile parts but not the erectile columns described.  Why D wrong: Seminal vesicle and prostate are accessory glands."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The penis and clitoris (found in females) are an example of which type of organs?","options":["Homologous organs","Analogous organs","Vestigial organs","Accessory organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The penis and clitoris (found in females) are homologous organs.  Why B wrong: Analogous organs share function but not common origin; here they are homologous.  Why C/D wrong: They are not described as vestigial or accessory organs."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The corpus cavernosum consists of how many lateral columns that fill with blood during erection?","options":["One central column","Two lateral columns called corpus cavernosum","Three columns","Four columns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The corpus cavernosum consists of two lateral columns; during erection of the penis, blood gets pulled or filled inside the corpus cavernosum that makes the penis strongest and erected.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated two lateral columns of the corpus cavernosum."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which structure lies between the two corpus cavernosum and contains the urethra (situated into the corpus spongiosum)?","options":["Corpus spongiosum","Glans penis","Tunica albuginea","Prepuce"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The corpus spongiosum lies between the two corpus cavernosum, and the urethra is situated into the corpus spongiosum.  Why B wrong: The glans is the cap-shaped distal portion of the penis.  Why C wrong: Tunica albuginea is a testicular layer.  Why D wrong: The prepuce is the foreskin."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The glans is the triangular/frosted structure of which part of the penis?","options":["The body","The tip/top of the penis","The base of the penis","The scrotum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The glans is the triangular frosted structure of the tip/top of the penis, where too much erectile tissue is present.  Why A/C/D wrong: The glans is at the tip, not the body, base, or scrotum."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The head of the penis contains the opening for the urethral duct, the passage for urine and seminal fluid or semen. The vascular body which forms the apex of the penis is known as the:","options":["Glans","Prepuce","Scrotum","Corpus spongiosum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vascular body which forms the apex of the penis is the clitoris (in female; the glans is its male homolog) — the head/glans of the penis contains the opening for the urethral duct and is too sensitive to touch.  Why B wrong: The prepuce is the foreskin covering the glans.  Why C wrong: The scrotum holds the testes.  Why D wrong: The corpus spongiosum surrounds the urethra but is not the apex/head structure described."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is another name for the prepuce of the penis?","options":["Foreskin","Glans","Scrotum","Frenulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prepuce is also known as foreskin.  Why B wrong: The glans is the tip of the penis, not the prepuce.  Why C/D wrong: Scrotum and frenulum are not synonyms for prepuce."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Inflammation of the prepuce or foreskin layer of the penis is known as:","options":["Urethritis","Balanitis","Cystitis","Orchitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation of the prepuce or foreskin layer is known as balanitis.  Why A wrong: Urethritis is urethral inflammation.  Why C wrong: Cystitis is bladder inflammation.  Why D wrong: Orchitis is inflammation of the testes."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Erection of the penis occurs due to stimulation of which division of the autonomic nervous system?","options":["Sympathetic nervous system","Parasympathetic autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Enteric nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Erection occurs due to stimulation of the parasympathetic autonomic nervous system; parasympathetic stimulation leads to filling of the spongy erectile tissues with blood, which are caused by arterial dilation and venoconstriction, increasing blood flow into the penis and obstructing outflow.  Why A/C/D wrong: Erection is mediated by the parasympathetic (not sympathetic, somatic, or enteric) system."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"During penile erection, what vascular changes increase blood flow into the penis and obstruct outflow?","options":["Arterial constriction and venous dilation","Arterial dilation and venoconstriction","Both arterial and venous dilation","Both arterial and venous constriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spongy erectile tissues fill with blood due to arterial dilation and venoconstriction, increasing blood flow into the penis and obstructing its outflow.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated arterial dilation with venoconstriction."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Ejaculation of sperm — the forcible ejection of semen from the urethra by powerful rhythmical contraction of the smooth muscles — is a reflex in response to what?","options":["Sexual stimulation","Urination","Cold temperature","Fasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ejaculation is the forcible ejection of semen from the urethra by powerful rhythmical contraction of the smooth muscles; it is a reflex in response to sexual stimulation. It is the release of sperm, and this is a peak pleasurable moment for engaged couples.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a response to sexual stimulation, not urination, cold, or fasting."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"How many sperm are produced in one ejaculation, and what is the approximate sperm count per ml of semen?","options":["Once a time 3–5 ml semen produced in one ejaculation; 40–100 million sperm/ml","1 ml semen; 5 million sperm/ml","10 ml semen; 1 billion sperm/ml","0.5 ml semen; 10 thousand sperm/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Once at a time, 3–5 ml semen is produced in one ejaculation, and there are 40–100 million spermatozoa per ml of semen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3–5 ml volume and 40–100 million sperm/ml."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the scrotum and what does it contain?","options":["A supporting, dual-chambered suspended sack that contains the testes and is homologous to the labia majora in females","A gland that produces semen","A tube that connects the kidney to the bladder","The terminal part of the urinary tract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The scrotum is a supporting, dual-chambered suspended sack that contains the testes, is homologous to the labia majora in females, and is an extension of the perineum located between the legs.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a semen-producing gland, ureter, or urethra."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The scrotum has a protective function, maintaining an optimal temperature for sperm survival and function. For sperm development, the scrotum maintains a temperature slightly cooler than body temperature by how much?","options":["1°C","3°C (the scrotum maintains 3°C, 5°F, or 7°F cooler than normal body temperature)","10°C","0.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For sperm development, the scrotum is maintaining a temperature slightly cooler — 3°C, 5°F, or 7°F — than normal body temperature, helping in protection by minimizing damage to the sperm coat.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3°C (5°F/7°F) cooler temperature."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The asymmetry of the scrotum (testis sitting toward the right) serves which functional purpose?","options":["It may avoid compression in the event of intercourse, allowing more effective cooling of the testes","It increases sperm production directly","It helps in urine filtration","It produces testosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The left testis is usually lower than the right, which may function to avoid compression in the event of intercourse; this asymmetry may also allow more effective cooling of the testes.  Why B/C/D wrong: The asymmetry relates to compression avoidance and cooling, not directly to sperm production, filtration, or hormone production."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the epididymis, and what is its approximate length when uncoiled?","options":["A tightly folded tube about 6 m long when uncoiled, into a mass called the epididymis","A solid gland 2 cm long","A muscular sac that stores urine","A 25 cm tube connecting kidney to bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epididymis is folded 6 m in length, or repeatedly folded and tightly packed into a mass called the epididymis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 6 m folded length and function of the epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Where is the epididymis located, and to what does it connect?","options":["At the back of the testis; connects to the vas deferens","Inside the bladder","In the prostate gland","Within the kidney cortex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The epididymis is located at the back of the testis and connects it to the vas deferens.  Why B/C/D wrong: The epididymis is not in the bladder, prostate, or kidney."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the primary function of the vas deferens?","options":["To store and carry the sperm","To produce testosterone","To filter blood","To produce semen fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vas deferens stores and carries the sperm.  Why B wrong: Testosterone is produced by Leydig cells in the testes.  Why C wrong: Blood filtration is a kidney function.  Why D wrong: Seminal fluid is produced mainly by the seminal vesicles and prostate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What are the male accessory sexual organs (internal organs of the male reproductive system)?","options":["Seminal vesicles, prostate gland, and bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands","Testes and epididymis","Penis and scrotum","Kidneys and ureters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The male accessory sexual organs are the seminal vesicles, prostate gland, and bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands; they secrete fluids in preparation for ejaculation.  Why B/C wrong: Testes, epididymis, penis, and scrotum are not the accessory sexual glands in this classification.  Why D wrong: Kidneys and ureters are urinary organs."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The seminal vesicles secrete a fluid such as fructose that serves what purpose for sperm?","options":["It is an energy source for sperm (a sugar/source of energy)","It cools the sperm","It destroys old sperm","It filters the sperm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The seminal vesicles, which attach to the vas deferens near the base of the bladder, secrete fluid such as fructose that is an energy source for sperm.  Why B/C/D wrong: The fructose serves as an energy source, not cooling, destruction, or filtration."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Approximately what percentage of the total semen volume is contributed by the seminal vesicle fluid?","options":["About 30%","About 60% of a man's ejaculate","About 10%","About 90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The seminal vesicle fluid makes up about 60% of a man's ejaculate.  Why A wrong: The prostate gland fluid makes up about 30% of semen.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 60% seminal vesicle contribution."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Where does the seminal vesicle open into?","options":["Into the urethra by the ejaculatory duct","Directly into the bladder","Into the epididymis","Into the prostatic capsule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The seminal vesicle opens into the urethra by the ejaculatory duct.  Why B/C/D wrong: It opens via the ejaculatory duct into the urethra, not directly into the bladder, epididymis, or prostatic capsule."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The prostate gland is a walnut-size structure located below which structure, surrounding the first part of the urethra?","options":["The urinary bladder","The kidney","The seminal vesicle","The epididymis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prostate gland is a walnut-size structure located below the urinary bladder and behind the symphysis pubis, surrounding the first part of the urethra.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is located below the bladder, not the kidney, seminal vesicle, or epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"The prostate gland secretes a milky, thin fluid. Approximately what percentage of semen does it make up, and what is its pH nature?","options":["About 30% of semen, alkaline in nature (pH 6.0–6.5)","About 60% of semen, acidic","About 10% of semen, neutral","About 90% of semen, strongly acidic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prostate gland contributes additional fluid to the ejaculate; it secretes a milky, thin fluid that makes up about 30% of semen, and the nature of prostatic fluid is slightly acidic with pH 6.0–6.5.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 30% volume and pH 6.0–6.5 (the question option correctly captures the 30% volume; note the source states the prostatic fluid pH is 6.0–6.5, which is slightly acidic)."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Where are the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands located?","options":["Pea-size structures located on the sides or posterolateral of the urethra just below the prostate gland","Above the kidneys","Inside the testes","Within the bladder wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands are pea-size structures located on the sides or posterolateral of the urethra just below the prostate gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: They are located near the urethra below the prostate, not above the kidneys, inside the testes, or in the bladder wall."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the function of the fluid produced by the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands?","options":["A clear, slippery fluid that lubricates the urethra before ejaculation and neutralizes acidity of the vagina and any residual urine","It provides fructose energy for sperm","It stores sperm","It produces testosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bulbourethral glands produce a clear, slippery fluid that lubricates the urethra before ejaculation, neutralizing acidity of the vagina and any residual urine.  Why B wrong: Fructose energy is from the seminal vesicles.  Why C/D wrong: They do not store sperm or produce testosterone."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal serum potassium level?","options":["3.5–5.1 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","100–106 mEq/L","23–27 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum potassium level is 3.5–5.1 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: 135–145 mEq/L is the normal sodium level.  Why C wrong: 100–106 mEq/L is the normal chloride level.  Why D wrong: 23–27 mEq/L is the normal bicarbonate level."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal serum sodium level?","options":["3.5–5.1 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","100–106 mEq/L","23–27 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum sodium level is 135–145 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 3.5–5.1 mEq/L is potassium.  Why C wrong: 100–106 mEq/L is chloride.  Why D wrong: 23–27 mEq/L is bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal serum chloride level?","options":["3.5–5.1 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","100–106 mEq/L","23–27 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum chloride level is 100–106 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 3.5–5.1 mEq/L is potassium.  Why B wrong: 135–145 mEq/L is sodium.  Why D wrong: 23–27 mEq/L is bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal serum bicarbonate level?","options":["3.5–5.1 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","100–106 mEq/L","23–27 mEq/L"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum bicarbonate level is 23–27 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 3.5–5.1 mEq/L is potassium.  Why B wrong: 135–145 mEq/L is sodium.  Why C wrong: 100–106 mEq/L is chloride."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal serum creatinine level?","options":["0.6–1.3 mg/dl","7–20 mg/dl","8–25 mg/dl","100–125 ml/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum creatinine level is 0.6–1.3 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 7–20 mg/dl is the normal BUN level.  Why C wrong: 8–25 mg/dl is also referenced for BUN.  Why D wrong: 100–125 ml/min is the normal GFR / creatinine clearance."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Creatinine is a waste product of protein and muscle metabolism. Why is it the most reliable and sensitive marker of renal function?","options":["Because serum creatinine level increases only when at least 50% of renal function is lost","Because it is reabsorbed completely by the tubules","Because it varies with diet daily","Because it is produced by the liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Serum creatinine is the most reliable and sensitive marker of renal function because the serum creatinine level increases only when at least 50% of renal function is lost.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect why creatinine is the most reliable renal marker per the source."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Creatinine is mainly formed by which amino acids?","options":["Glycine, Arginine and Methionine","Lysine, Leucine and Valine","Glutamine and Glutamate","Tyrosine and Tryptophan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Creatinine is mainly formed by Glycine, Arginine and Methionine amino acids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These amino acids are not the stated precursors of creatinine."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is the end product of which metabolic process?","options":["The nitrogenous metabolism end product of the proteins","Carbohydrate metabolism","Fat metabolism","Nucleic acid metabolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urea and nitrogen is the nitrogenous metabolism end product of the proteins; urea usually amounts to convert into urea by liver and urea excrete by kidneys through urine under normal condition.  Why B/C/D wrong: BUN is the end product of protein (nitrogenous) metabolism, not carbohydrate, fat, or nucleic acid metabolism."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal BUN level?","options":["0.6–1.3 mg/dl","8–25 mg/dl","100–106 mEq/L","23–27 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal BUN level is 8–25 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 0.6–1.3 mg/dl is serum creatinine.  Why C wrong: 100–106 mEq/L is chloride.  Why D wrong: 23–27 mEq/L is bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Factors that elevate BUN include dehydration, poor renal perfusion, intake of a high protein diet, infection, stress, and muscles breakdown. What does an elevated BUN primarily reflect?","options":["Conditions that increase urea or reduce its clearance","Improved renal function","Low protein intake","Excess water intake only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Factors that elevate BUN include dehydration, poor renal perfusion, high protein diet intake, infection, stress, and muscle breakdown — all conditions increasing urea or reducing its clearance.  Why B wrong: Elevated BUN reflects impaired, not improved, renal function or increased urea load.  Why C/D wrong: Low protein intake and excess water would tend to lower, not raise, BUN."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Urinalysis is a test for evaluation of the renal and renal diseases. When taking a urine sample for routine urinalysis, which specimen is preferred?","options":["The first morning voiding (first void of midstream urine, if possible)","A random afternoon sample","The last urine of the day","A 24-hour pooled sample"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For urinalysis, a sample is obtained — reveal the procedure and use a clean container for collection; obtain 10–15 ml of midstream urine, if possible, taken from the first morning voiding.  Why B/C/D wrong: The first morning midstream void is preferred, not a random afternoon, last-of-day, or 24-hour pooled sample for routine urinalysis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"When taking a urine sample for urinalysis, sterile technique should be maintained, and if the sample cannot be examined immediately, how should it be handled?","options":["It should be refrigerated","It should be frozen at -20°C","It should be left at room temperature for 24 hours","It should be discarded immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Following or sending the sample, sterile technique should be maintained when taking the urine; following or sending sample, then it should be refrigerated.  Why B/C/D wrong: The sample should be refrigerated, not frozen at -20°C, left at room temperature, or discarded."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"For a 24-hour urine collection procedure, before the start of collection, what should the patient do, and how is the first sample handled?","options":["The client should void and discard that sample, then collection begins (discard the first sample)","Collect the first sample as the start","Refrigerate only the first sample","Add preservative only after 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At the start time, instruct the client to void and discard that sample (the first urine is discarded), then the timed collection begins.  Why B wrong: The first void is discarded, not collected.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 24-hour collection start procedure."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"During a 24-hour urine collection, how should the collection container be kept while in process?","options":["Kept on ice or refrigerated, with preservative added if ordered, and the specimen container labeled","Kept at room temperature","Kept in direct sunlight","Kept frozen solid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: While collecting urine, keep the same with ice or refrigerated or with preservative if ordered to the specimen during collection; at end of time, instruct the client to empty the bladder and add that urine in collection container.  Why B/C/D wrong: The container should be kept on ice/refrigerated (with preservative if ordered), not at room temperature, in sunlight, or frozen solid."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"In urine specific gravity determination, what does it measure?","options":["The specific gravity in concentration of solutes in the urine or in a urine density","The pH of the urine","The bacterial count in urine","The glucose level in urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine specific gravity determination measures the specific gravity in concentration of solutes in the urine or in a urine density.  Why B/C/D wrong: Specific gravity measures solute concentration/density, not pH, bacteria, or glucose."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"Which method for measuring urine specific gravity is most commonly used?","options":["Dipstick method","Refractometer (an instrument used in laboratory)","Urinometer (least reliable method)","Centrifugation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Among methods to measure specific gravity, the dipstick method is most commonly used; the refractometer is an instrument used in the laboratory, and the urinometer is the least reliable method.  Why B wrong: The refractometer is a lab instrument but not stated as the most commonly used.  Why C wrong: The urinometer is the least reliable method.  Why D wrong: Centrifugation is not the stated specific gravity method."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the normal value of urine specific gravity?","options":["1.010–1.030","1.001–1.005","1.040–1.060","1.100–1.200"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal value of urine specific gravity is 1.010–1.030.  Why B/C/D wrong: These fall outside the normal range of 1.010–1.030."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"An increased urine specific gravity indicates which condition?","options":["Concentrated urine seen in various conditions such as insufficient fluid intake or dehydration; decreased renal perfusion; increased ADH","Overhydration with dilute urine","Increased fluid intake","Normal kidney function only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An increased urine specific gravity means more concentrated urine seen in various conditions such as insufficient fluid intake or dehydration, decreased renal perfusion, and increased ADH.  Why B/C wrong: Overhydration and increased fluid intake cause decreased (not increased) specific gravity.  Why D wrong: Increased specific gravity is associated with concentrating conditions, not simply normal function."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"A decreased urine specific gravity indicates which condition?","options":["Less concentrated (more dilute) urine seen in conditions such as increased fluid intake or overhydration","Severe dehydration","Decreased renal perfusion","Increased ADH secretion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A decreased urine specific gravity means less concentrated (more dilute) urine, seen in conditions such as increased fluid intake or overhydration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dehydration, decreased renal perfusion, and increased ADH all cause increased (not decreased) specific gravity."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the purpose of a urine culture and sensitivity test?","options":["To identify the microorganism (culture) in the urine and determine the specific antibodies to treat the existing microorganism (sensitivity)","To measure urine pH only","To determine urine specific gravity","To measure creatinine clearance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A urine culture and sensitivity test is used to identify the microorganism (culture) in the urine and determine the specific antibiotics/sensitivity to treat the existing microorganism (sensitivity).  Why B/C/D wrong: It tests for microorganisms and antibiotic sensitivity, not pH, specific gravity, or creatinine clearance."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"During taking a sample for urine culture, which technique should be maintained?","options":["A sterile collection technique should be maintained","A clean but non-sterile technique","No special technique","Sample collection from a urine bag only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During taking a sample for urine culture, a sterile collection technique should be maintained.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sterile technique (not clean non-sterile, no technique, or bag-only collection) is required for urine culture."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"For a mid-stream urine sample collection, what is the correct sequence (using two methods)?","options":["Mid-stream method — before taking the sample, the perineal area should be washed with soap and water, then void or pass the urine and collect the mid-stream urine in a sterile container","Collect the very first drops of urine","Collect the last few drops only","Pool a 24-hour collection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Using two methods: the mid-stream method involves washing the perineal area with soap and water before taking the sample, then passing urine and collecting the mid-stream urine in a sterile container.  Why B/C/D wrong: The midstream (not first drops, last drops, or 24-hour pooled) sample is collected after cleansing."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"In the aspiration method for urine culture in a catheterized patient, what is done with the first clean urine, and how much is collected?","options":["Clamp the catheter port or site with a needle and aspirate urine from the sample port; collect 10–15 ml of urine for culture & sensitivity test","Disconnect the catheter and collect urine from the bag","Collect 100 ml of urine directly from the bag","Use only the first morning void"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The aspiration method: if the patient is catheterized, then clamp the catheter port or site with a needle and aspirate urine from the sample port, and collect 10–15 ml of urine for culture & sensitivity test.  Why B/C/D wrong: Urine is aspirated from the sample port (10–15 ml), not collected from the bag or limited to first morning void."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"What is the purpose of the Creatinine Clearance Test (CCT)?","options":["To identify how well the kidneys are working or to remove creatinine from the blood","To measure urine pH","To detect bacteria in urine","To measure blood glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The creatinine clearance test (CCT) identifies how well the kidneys are working or removes (clears) creatinine from the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: CCT assesses renal clearance of creatinine, not pH, bacteria, or glucose."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"An increased creatinine clearance rate indicates what about renal function?","options":["It is normal, and indicates that the kidneys are adequately removing (clearance) the creatinine","It indicates kidney failure","It indicates infection","It indicates dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An increased creatinine clearance rate is normal and indicates that the kidneys are adequately removing (clearance) the creatinine.  Why B/C/D wrong: An increased clearance rate is normal; impaired removal would be reflected by a decreased clearance rate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"A decreased creatinine clearance rate indicates what about renal function?","options":["It means inadequate removal of the creatinine, indicating impairment of renal function","The kidneys are functioning normally","There is increased GFR","The kidneys are overworking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A decreased creatinine clearance rate means inadequate removal of the creatinine, indicating impairment of renal function.  Why B/C/D wrong: A decreased clearance indicates renal impairment, not normal function, increased GFR, or overworking."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Glomerular Filtration Rate & composition/regulation of urine…","question":"How long is the urine collection required for the creatinine clearance test, and what is the normal GFR value it estimates?","options":["A 24-hour urine collection is required; normal GFR is 125 ml/min","A 2-hour collection; normal GFR is 50 ml/min","A 12-hour collection; normal GFR is 200 ml/min","A single random sample; normal GFR is 300 ml/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For this test, a 24-hour urine collection is required; this test provides the best estimate of the GFR, and the normal GFR is 125 ml/min.  Why B/C/D wrong: A 24-hour collection (not 2-hour, 12-hour, or random) is required, and normal GFR is 125 ml/min."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A 24-hour urine collection for a uric acid test is primarily used to diagnose which condition?","options":["Diabetes mellitus","Gouty disease and other renal diseases","Pheochromocytoma","Urinary tract infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For determination of uric acid a 24-hour urine collection is required to diagnose gouty disease and other renal diseases.  Why A wrong: Diabetes is diagnosed by blood glucose/HbA1c, not a uric acid test.  Why C wrong: Pheochromocytoma is diagnosed by the VMA test.  Why D wrong: UTI is diagnosed by urine culture, not uric acid levels."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"What fluid instruction is given to a client undergoing a uric acid test?","options":["Restrict all fluids for 24 hours","Encourage fluid intake before and during the test","Keep the client strictly NPO","Limit fluids to 500 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client should be encouraged to increase fluid intake before and during the uric acid test.  Why A/D wrong: Fluid restriction is not indicated; fluids are encouraged.  Why C wrong: NPO is not required for a uric acid urine test."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"What type of specimen is required for the Vanillylmandelic Acid (VMA) test?","options":["A single random urine sample","A 24-hour urine sample","A fasting blood sample","A first-morning sputum sample"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the VMA test a 24-hour urine sample is required.  Why A wrong: A random single sample is inadequate; a full 24-hour collection is needed.  Why C/D wrong: VMA is measured in urine, not blood or sputum."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The VMA test is performed to diagnose which tumor?","options":["Wilms tumor of the kidney","Pheochromocytoma, a tumor of the adrenal gland","Bladder carcinoma","Renal cell carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The VMA test is performed to diagnose pheochromocytoma, which is a tumor of the adrenal gland.  Why A/C/D wrong: These tumors are not diagnosed by the VMA (catecholamine) test."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The VMA test measures the level of which substances in the urine?","options":["Ketone bodies","Catecholamines","Bilirubin","Amylase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The VMA test determines catecholamine levels in the urine.  Why A/B/C wrong: Ketones, bilirubin, and amylase are not what the VMA test measures."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Before a VMA test, a client should avoid caffeine, cocoa, vanilla, and gelatin for at least how long?","options":["6 hours before","12 hours before","2 days before","1 week before"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The client should avoid foods such as caffeine, cocoa, vanilla, and gelatin for at least 2 days before the VMA test.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies at least 2 days, not 6 hours, 12 hours, or 1 week."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which foods must a client avoid before a Vanillylmandelic Acid (VMA) test? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Caffeine","Cocoa","Vanilla","Gelatin","Plain rice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Before the VMA test the client should avoid caffeine, cocoa, vanilla, and gelatin for at least 2 days.  Why E wrong: Plain rice is not among the foods restricted before the VMA test."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"KUB radiography is an X-ray of the urinary system and surrounding organs used primarily to detect:","options":["Urinary calculi","Bladder tumors","Glomerular filtration rate","Catecholamine levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KUB radiography is an X-ray of the urinary system and surrounding organs to detect urinary calculi.  Why B wrong: Bladder tumors are best visualized by cystoscopy.  Why C wrong: GFR is a functional measurement, not seen on plain X-ray.  Why D wrong: Catecholamines are measured by the VMA urine test."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"What preparation is currently required before KUB radiography?","options":["Extensive bowel preparation is mandatory","No specific preparation is necessary","Keep the client NPO for 24 hours","Full bladder is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No specific preparation is necessary before KUB radiography; formerly bowel preparation was required but recently there is no need for bowel preparation.  Why A wrong: Bowel preparation is no longer required.  Why C/D wrong: Prolonged NPO and a full bladder are not requirements for KUB radiography."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"KUB USG\" stand for?","options":["Ultrasonography of kidneys, ureters, and bladder","X-ray of the kidney, urethra, and bowel","Urine bacterial culture study","Kidney urodynamic biopsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KUB USG is ultrasonography of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder.  Why B/C/D wrong: KUB USG refers specifically to ultrasound imaging of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During pre-procedure preparation for KUB USG, the client is usually kept:","options":["NPO, and bowel preparation is not required","On a full-diet with laxatives","On a high-protein diet","On bed rest with an indwelling catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For KUB USG the client is usually kept NPO and bowel preparation is not required.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated pre-procedure preparations for KUB USG."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Why is the client asked to drink more water about 1 hour before KUB USG?","options":["To dilute the contrast dye","Because a full bladder is necessary for more visualization","To prevent dehydration during the scan","To flush out urinary calculi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client should drink more water before 1 hour because a full bladder is necessary for more visualization on ultrasound.  Why A wrong: No dye is used in a routine KUB USG.  Why C/D wrong: The purpose is bladder visualization, not preventing dehydration or flushing stones."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Renography provides a complete visualization of which aspects of kidney function? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Renal blood flow","Glomerular filter","Tubular function","Excretion ability of the kidney","Bladder mucosal inflammation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Renography is a complete visualization of renal blood flow, glomerular filter, tubular function, and excretion ability of the kidney.  Why E wrong: Assessing bladder mucosal inflammation is done by cystoscopy, not renography."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In renography, how is the substance for better visualization administered?","options":["An IV radioisotope/dye is injected","An oral contrast tablet is given","A dye is instilled into the bladder","A rectal contrast enema is administered"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In renography an IV radioisotope/dye is injected for more visualization.  Why B/C/D wrong: The radioisotope/dye is given intravenously in renography, not orally, intravesically, or rectally."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which nursing action should be ensured BEFORE the renography procedure?","options":["Ensure that consent has been taken","Encourage fluid intake to excrete dye","Insert a urinary catheter","Keep the client in high-Fowler's position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before the renography procedure the nurse should ensure that consent has been taken.  Why B wrong: Encouraging fluid to excrete dye is a post-procedure action.  Why C/D wrong: Catheterization and specific positioning are not the stated pre-procedure requirement."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"After renography, the nurse should encourage fluid intake to:","options":["Promote excretion of the dye, if not contraindicated","Correct dehydration from NPO status","Increase urine specific gravity","Prevent the formation of calculi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After the test, encourage fluid intake for excretion of the dye, if not contraindicated.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is to help excrete the injected dye, not the other reasons."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Cystoscopy is defined as the visualization and examination of which structure for inflammation?","options":["The renal cortex","The bladder mucosa","The ureteral lumen","The urethral sphincter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cystoscopy is the visualization and examination of the bladder mucosa for inflammation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cystoscopy focuses on the bladder mucosa, not the renal cortex, ureteral lumen, or urethral sphincter specifically."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During cystoscopy, what additional procedure may be performed if necessary?","options":["Nephrectomy","A biopsy sample may be taken","Ureteral stent removal","Renal artery angioplasty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During cystoscopy a biopsy sample may be taken if necessary.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not procedures performed during a diagnostic cystoscopy per the source."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which urine finding is EXPECTED (normal) following a cystoscopy?","options":["Bright red urine with clots","Pink-tinged or tea-coloured urine with urinary frequency","Complete anuria","Foul-smelling green urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Expected urine colour following cystoscopy is pink-tinged or tea-coloured urine, and urinary frequency is common after cystoscopy.  Why A wrong: Bright red urine/clots are unexpected findings requiring HCP notification.  Why C/D wrong: Anuria and green urine are not the expected post-cystoscopy findings."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"After cystoscopy, which findings are UNEXPECTED and require the nurse to notify the HCP immediately? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Bright red urine/clots","Fever","Pink-tinged urine","Urinary frequency","Tea-coloured urine"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Bright red urine/clots and fever are unexpected after cystoscopy and require immediate HCP notification.  Why C/D/E wrong: Pink-tinged urine, urinary frequency, and tea-coloured urine are expected/common findings after cystoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by increased permeability of the glomerular basement membrane to which plasma component?","options":["Plasma albumin","Red blood cells","Glucose","Sodium ions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In nephrotic syndrome there is increased permeability of the glomerular basement membrane to plasma albumin.  Why B/C/D wrong: The hallmark is protein (albumin) leakage, not primarily RBCs, glucose, or sodium crossing the membrane."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, leakage of albumin through the glomerular membrane leads to a decreased serum albumin level, a state termed:","options":["Hyperalbuminemia","Hypoalbuminuria (hypoalbuminemia)","Hyperglycemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because glomerular membranes become permeable to protein (especially albumin), serum albumin leaks and the serum albumin level is decreased (hypoalbuminuria/hypoalbuminemia).  Why A wrong: The serum albumin is decreased, not increased.  Why C/D wrong: These describe glucose and sodium abnormalities, not the low-albumin state in nephrotic syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the pathophysiology of nephrotic syndrome, as serum albumin falls, what happens to capillary oncotic pressure and hydrostatic pressure?","options":["Oncotic pressure decreases and hydrostatic pressure increases","Oncotic pressure increases and hydrostatic pressure decreases","Both pressures increase equally","Both pressures decrease equally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In nephrotic syndrome capillary oncotic pressure decreases and hydrostatic pressure increases, contributing to fluid shift and hypovolemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated pressure changes (oncotic ↓, hydrostatic ↑)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The decrease in oncotic pressure and shift of intravascular fluid in nephrotic syndrome produces which fluid-volume state?","options":["Hypervolemia","Hypovolemia","Euvolemia","Third spacing without volume change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As oncotic pressure decreases and hydrostatic pressure increases, hypovolemia conditions occur.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically states hypovolemia results, not hypervolemia or euvolemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Why does hyperlipidaemia develop in nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Due to increased dietary fat intake","Because low serum protein triggers the liver to synthesize proteins and lipids","Because the kidneys stop excreting cholesterol","Due to failure of pancreatic lipase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to low serum protein level, the liver starts to synthesize proteins and lipids, so hyperlipidaemia occurs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not the cause described for nephrotic hyperlipidaemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the pathophysiology flow chart of nephrotic syndrome, the sequence begins as Kidney disease → Proteinuria → Serum albumin ↓ → Oncotic pressure ↓ → and then leads to:","options":["Increased urine specific gravity","Shift of intravascular-interstitial volume causing edema","Immediate anuria","Hyperalbuminemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows the drop in oncotic pressure causes a shift of intravascular to interstitial volume (edema-forming stage).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not follow in the described pathophysiology sequence."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the nephrotic syndrome flow chart, decreased plasma volume triggers compensatory hormone changes. Which hormones are INCREASED? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Renin","Aldosterone (ALDO)","AVP (antidiuretic hormone)","ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide)","Norepinephrine (NE)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Renin ↑, ALDO ↑, AVP ↑, and NE ↑ in response to decreased plasma volume.  Why D wrong: ANP is shown decreased (ANP ↓), not increased."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the nephrotic syndrome pathophysiology, increased proximal and distal tubular sodium reabsorption with increased water reabsorption produces which electrolyte disturbance?","options":["Hypernatremia","Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased proximal and distal tubular sodium reabsorption with increased water reabsorption leads to hyponatremia (dilutional).  Why A/C/D wrong: The chart specifies hyponatremia, not the other electrolyte changes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the nephrotic syndrome flow chart, increased interstitial volume ultimately results in:","options":["Dehydration","Edema","Polyuria","Hypertension crisis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states increased interstitial volume = edema.  Why A/C/D wrong: Increased interstitial volume produces edema, not dehydration or polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are cardinal signs and symptoms of nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Massive proteinuria","Hypoalbuminemia/hypoproteinaemia","Hyperlipidaemia","Hypovolemia","Decreased blood pressure only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cardinal features include massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia/hypoproteinaemia, hyperlipidaemia, and hypovolemia.  Why E wrong: Blood pressure is increased (hypertension) in nephrotic syndrome, not simply decreased."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A child with nephrotic syndrome typically shows periorbital oedema that is most noticeable:","options":["On awakening (in the morning)","Only at night","Only after exercise","It never involves the face"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oedema in nephrotic syndrome presents as puffiness of the face and periorbital oedema on awakening.  Why B/C wrong: The oedema is characteristically present on awakening, not only at night or after exercise.  Why D wrong: Facial/periorbital puffiness is a hallmark feature."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The term for the generalized oedema seen in nephrotic syndrome is:","options":["Ascites","Anasarca","Pitting edema only","Lymphedema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anasarca refers to generalised oedema, a cardinal sign of nephrotic syndrome.  Why A wrong: Ascites is fluid in the peritoneal cavity, a separate finding.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source term for generalized oedema."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In males with nephrotic syndrome, oedema may specifically involve which additional area along with ascites?","options":["Scrotal oedema","Pedal cyanosis","Gingival swelling","Periorbital area only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ascites and scrotal oedema occur in males with nephrotic syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the additional male-specific oedema site described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which additional clinical features are seen in a child with nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Weight gain","Oliguria","Lethargy","Anorexia and pallor","Polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A child with nephrotic syndrome shows weight gain, oliguria, lethargy, anorexia, and pallor.  Why E wrong: Oliguria (reduced output) is present, not polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"What position provides comfort for a client with nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Prone position","Trendelenburg position","Semi-Fowler to Fowler position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Semi-Fowler to Fowler position is recommended for comfort in nephrotic syndrome.  Why A/B/D wrong: These positions are not the recommended comfort positions in the source."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are major goals of therapy in nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Reduce the excretion of urinary protein","Maintain or restore serum protein level","Reduce oedema","Prevent infection and minimise complications","Promote massive diuresis to below 400 ml/day"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The major goals are to reduce urinary protein excretion, maintain/restore serum protein, reduce oedema, and prevent infection and minimise complications.  Why E wrong: Forcing urine output below 400 ml/day (oliguria) is not a therapeutic goal."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the nursing care of nephrotic syndrome, the urine should specifically be monitored for:","options":["Specific gravity and protein","Bilirubin and ketones","Amylase and lipase","Nitrites only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor urine for specific gravity and protein in nephrotic syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the parameters specified for urine monitoring in nephrotic syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During periods of massive proteinuria in nephrotic syndrome, which dietary modification is indicated?","options":["Sodium restricted diet","High-sodium diet","High-protein loading diet","Potassium restricted diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sodium restricted diet is indicated during periods of massive proteinuria.  Why B wrong: Sodium is restricted, not increased.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specific dietary instruction for massive proteinuria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, protein restriction is indicated when:","options":["The condition is well controlled","The condition worsens (out of control) or renal failure/azotaemia develops","Oedema first appears","The child gains any weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein restriction is indicated if the condition worsens (out of control) or renal failure or azotaemia develops.  Why A/C/D wrong: These situations do not warrant protein restriction per the source; a regular diet may be offered when indicated."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice (steroid) for treating nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Prednisone","Furosemide","Cyclophosphamide","Amoxicillin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Steroids are given as ordered, and Prednisone is the drug of choice for nephrotic syndrome.  Why B wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, used if oedema is not resolved.  Why C wrong: Cyclophosphamide is the immunosuppressant used if steroid therapy is not effective.  Why D wrong: Amoxicillin is an antibiotic, not the treatment of choice."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"If steroid therapy is NOT effective in nephrotic syndrome, which immunosuppressant is the drug of choice?","options":["Prednisone","Cyclophosphamide","Spironolactone","Methotrexate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If steroid therapy is not effective, immunosuppressant therapy is started, and Cyclophosphamide is the drug of choice.  Why A wrong: Prednisone is the first-line steroid, not the immunosuppressant used after steroid failure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated immunosuppressant of choice."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Diuretics are initiated in nephrotic syndrome when:","options":["Blood pressure is normal","Oedema is not resolved","Proteinuria first appears","Serum albumin normalizes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diuretics are initiated if oedema is not resolved.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated indication for starting diuretics."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Glomerulonephritis is a group of kidney disorders characterized by:","options":["Inflammatory injury in the glomerulus","Bladder mucosal ulceration","Ureteral obstruction","Renal artery stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis is a group of kidney disorders characterised by inflammatory injury in the glomerulus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other urinary pathologies, not glomerulonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Glomerulonephritis is usually caused by which type of reaction?","options":["Immunological or auto-immune reactions","Allergic drug reactions only","Traumatic injury","Congenital malformation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis is usually caused by immunological or auto-immune reactions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated usual cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In glomerulonephritis, which three pathological processes occur in the glomeruli?","options":["Destruction, inflammation, and sclerosis","Necrosis, calcification, and cyst formation","Hypertrophy, dilation, and rupture","Fibrosis of the renal pelvis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Destruction, inflammation, and sclerosis of the glomeruli of the kidneys occur in glomerulonephritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described glomerular changes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Glomerulonephritis commonly occurs in which population?","options":["Children","Elderly men only","Pregnant women only","Neonates exclusively"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis commonly occurs in children.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states it commonly occurs in children."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A past history of infection with which organism is a cause of glomerulonephritis?","options":["Group A Beta-Haemolytic Streptococcus of the pharynx or skin","Staphylococcus aureus of the urinary tract","Escherichia coli of the bladder","Candida albicans of the mouth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A past history of infection with Group A Beta-Haemolytic Streptococcus of the pharynx or skin is a cause of glomerulonephritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These organisms are not the stated antecedent cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"According to the source, glomerulonephritis is often preceded by a history of pharyngitis or laryngitis by how long?","options":["1 to 2 days","2 to 3 weeks","6 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There is a history of pharyngitis or laryngitis before 2 to 3 weeks preceding glomerulonephritis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 2 to 3 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which autoimmune disease is given as an example of a cause of glomerulonephritis?","options":["SLE (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus)","Rheumatoid arthritis","Type 1 diabetes","Hashimoto's thyroiditis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auto-immune reactions, e.g. SLE, are listed as a cause of glomerulonephritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: SLE is the example specifically given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In glomerulonephritis, hypertensive encephalopathy occurs because nitrogenous waste products such as ammonia:","options":["Are excreted rapidly in urine","Cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB) and reach the brain","Accumulate only in the liver","Bind to red blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertensive encephalopathy occurs due to nitrogenous waste products such as ammonia that can cross the BBB and reach the brain.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the mechanism of hypertensive encephalopathy in glomerulonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are complications of glomerulonephritis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Kidney failure due to loss of glomerulus/GFR function","Hypertensive encephalopathy","Pulmonary oedema and heart failure","Improved renal function","Complete spontaneous cure without symptoms"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Complications include kidney failure (loss of glomerulus/GFR function), hypertensive encephalopathy, and pulmonary oedema and heart failure (from fluid/waste accumulation due to loss of renal function).  Why D/E wrong: Improved renal function and spontaneous cure are not listed as complications."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The periorbital/facial oedema of glomerulonephritis is most prominent:","options":["In the morning","In the evening","After heavy meals","Only during sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In glomerulonephritis, periorbital or facial oedema is more prominent in the morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies morning prominence."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The classic description of urine in glomerulonephritis is:","options":["Clear and dilute","Decreased output and cloudy, smoky brown coloured","Bright red with clots","Green and foul-smelling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis causes decreased urine output and cloudy, smoky brown coloured urine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the characteristic urine of glomerulonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In an older child with glomerulonephritis, which symptoms may be present? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Headache","Abdominal or flank pain","Dysuria","Improved appetite","Polyuria with dilute urine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In an older child, glomerulonephritis presents with headache, abdominal or flank pain, and dysuria.  Why D/E wrong: Anorexia (not improved appetite) and decreased/cloudy urine (not polyuria) are seen."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Azotaemia, a sign of glomerulonephritis, refers to increased blood levels of:","options":["Urea, nitrogen, and creatinine","Glucose and ketones","Sodium and potassium","Calcium and phosphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Azotaemia is increased blood urea, nitrogen, and creatinine in the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: Azotaemia specifically refers to elevated nitrogenous wastes (urea/nitrogen/creatinine)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"When daily-weighing a patient with glomerulonephritis, the nurse should ensure the weight is taken:","options":["At the same time, on the same scale, and in the same clothing","At any convenient time each day","Only once per week","With varying scales for accuracy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be weighed daily at the same time, on the same scale, and in the same clothing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Consistency of time, scale, and clothing is required for accurate trend monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Why should seizure precautions be initiated for a patient with glomerulonephritis?","options":["Because oedema puts pressure on brain tissues, so seizures may occur suddenly","Because steroids always cause seizures","Because of expected hypoglycemia","Because of high fever from infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Seizure precautions are initiated because oedema puts pressure on the brain tissues, so at any time the patient may be at risk of seizures, and they may occur suddenly.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for seizure precautions in glomerulonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) is best defined as:","options":["Sudden and almost complete loss of glomerular/kidney function characterized by low urine output","Slow, irreversible loss of kidney function over years","Isolated bladder dysfunction","Enlargement of the prostate gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AKI is a sudden and almost complete loss of glomerular or kidney function characterised by low urine output.  Why B wrong: Slow, irreversible loss over months/years describes CKD.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other conditions, not AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which urine output value indicates AKI in an ADULT?","options":["<400 ml/day","<0.5 ml/kg/hr","<1 ml/kg/hr","<2000 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, urine output <400 ml/day characterizes AKI.  Why B wrong: <0.5 ml/kg/hr is the criterion in children.  Why C wrong: <1 ml/kg/hr is the criterion in infants.  Why D wrong: 2000 ml/day is a normal-to-high output, not oliguria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CHILDREN, AKI is characterized by a urine output of:","options":["<400 ml/day","<0.5 ml/kg/hr","<1 ml/kg/hr","<100 ml/24 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children, urine output <0.5 ml/kg/hr indicates AKI.  Why A wrong: <400 ml/day is the adult criterion.  Why C wrong: <1 ml/kg/hr is the infant criterion.  Why D wrong: <100 ml/24 hrs describes anuria in ESKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In INFANTS, what urine output value is considered AKI?","options":["<400 ml/day","<0.5 ml/kg/hr","<1 ml/kg/hr","<2 ml/kg/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In infants, urine output <1 ml/kg/hr is considered AKI.  Why A wrong: <400 ml/day is the adult criterion.  Why B wrong: <0.5 ml/kg/hr is the children criterion.  Why D wrong: <2 ml/kg/hr is not the stated infant threshold."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A key feature distinguishing AKI from chronic kidney disease is that AKI:","options":["Occurs abruptly and can be reversible","Is always permanent","Never affects urine output","Only occurs in the elderly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AKI occurs abruptly and can be reversible.  Why B wrong: AKI is potentially reversible, unlike the irreversible loss in CKD.  Why C/D wrong: AKI affects urine output and is not limited to the elderly."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, hypo-perfusion of renal cells leads to which consequences? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cell death","Decompression (loss) of renal functions","Improved glomerular filtration","Increased erythropoietin secretion","Sudden and almost complete loss of function"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: Due to AKI, hypo-perfusion of renal cells causes cell death and decompression of renal functions; AKI represents an almost complete loss of kidney function.  Why C/D wrong: GFR and erythropoietin secretion decrease, not improve, in AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON form of acute kidney injury?","options":["Pre-renal AKI","Intra-renal AKI","Post-renal AKI","Chronic AKI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-renal AKI is the most common form of acute kidney injury.  Why B/C wrong: Intra-renal and post-renal are recognized types but not the most common.  Why D wrong: \"Chronic AKI\" is not a defined form; CKD is chronic."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The RIFLE criteria used to assess AKI stand for:","options":["Risk, Injury, Failure, Loss of kidney function, and End-stage kidney disease","Renal, Infection, Fluid, Loss, Emergency","Recovery, Injury, Filtration, Level, Excretion","Risk, Inflammation, Fever, Leukocytosis, Edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RIFLE = Risk, Injury, Failure, Loss of kidney function, and End-stage kidney disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the RIFLE acronym."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Pre-renal causes of acute renal failure arise outside the kidneys from volume depletion. Which of the following is a pre-renal cause?","options":["Hypovolemia/shock due to dehydration and haemorrhage","Acute tubular necrosis","Enlarged prostate (BPH)","Bladder cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-renal causes result from volume depletion such as hypovolemia/shock due to dehydration and haemorrhage.  Why B wrong: Tubular necrosis is an intra-renal cause.  Why C/D wrong: Enlarged prostate and bladder cancer are post-renal causes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are PRE-RENAL causes of acute renal failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Decreased cardiac output","GI losses such as nausea, vomiting, NG suctioning","Severe hypotension due to shock, sepsis and infection","Myocardial infarction and heart failure","Nephrotoxicity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-renal causes include decreased cardiac output, GI losses (nausea, vomiting, NG suctioning, dehydration), severe hypotension due to shock/sepsis/infection, and myocardial infarction/heart failure.  Why E wrong: Nephrotoxicity is an intra-renal cause, not pre-renal."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which additional mechanism is listed among pre-renal causes of acute renal failure?","options":["Obstruction and decreased peripheral vascular resistance, and pre-renal infection","Acute glomerulonephritis","Bladder neck obstruction","Calculi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-renal causes include obstruction and decreased peripheral vascular resistance, and pre-renal infection.  Why B wrong: Acute glomerulonephritis is an intra-renal cause.  Why C/D wrong: Bladder neck obstruction and calculi are post-renal causes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Intra-renal causes of acute renal failure involve dysfunction of the kidney parenchyma. Which is an intra-renal cause?","options":["Tubular necrosis","Decreased cardiac output","Enlarged prostate (BPH)","Haemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intra-renal causes are dysfunction within the kidney parenchyma, e.g., tubular necrosis.  Why B/D wrong: Decreased cardiac output and haemorrhage are pre-renal causes.  Why C wrong: Enlarged prostate is a post-renal cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are INTRA-RENAL causes of acute renal failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Prolonged prerenal ischemia","Intrarenal infection and obstruction","Nephrotoxicity","Acute vascular damage due to malignant hypertension/atherosclerotic embolic condition","Enlarged prostate (BPH)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Intra-renal causes include prolonged prerenal ischemia, intrarenal infection and obstruction, nephrotoxicity, and acute vascular damage due to malignant hypertension/atherosclerotic embolic condition.  Why E wrong: Enlarged prostate is a post-renal cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are also listed as intra-renal causes of acute renal failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Increased intraabdominal pressure","Acute glomerulonephritis","Tubular necrosis","Bladder cancer","Post renal infection"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased intraabdominal pressure, acute glomerulonephritis, and tubular necrosis are all intra-renal causes.  Why D/E wrong: Bladder cancer and post-renal infection are post-renal causes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Post-renal causes of acute renal failure represent damage between the kidneys and the urethral meatus. Which is a post-renal cause?","options":["Sudden obstruction due to enlarged prostate (BPH) and stone","Tubular necrosis","Decreased cardiac output","Nephrotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-renal causes include sudden obstruction due to enlarged prostate (BPH) and stone.  Why B/D wrong: These are intra-renal causes.  Why C wrong: Decreased cardiac output is a pre-renal cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are POST-RENAL causes of acute renal failure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Bladder neck obstruction","Bladder cancer","Calculi","Post renal infection","Decreased cardiac output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Post-renal causes include bladder neck obstruction, bladder cancer, calculi, and post-renal infection (and obstruction from enlarged prostate/stone).  Why E wrong: Decreased cardiac output is a pre-renal cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The ONSET phase of acute renal disease has a duration of:","options":["Hours to days","8 to 14 days","7 to 14 days","1 to 2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The onset phase duration is hours to days.  Why B wrong: 8 to 14 days is the oliguric/anuric phase.  Why C wrong: 7 to 14 days is the diuretic phase.  Why D wrong: 1 to 2 years is the recovery phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The onset phase of acute renal disease begins with a precipitating event and ends when:","options":["Oliguria develops","The client fully recovers","Diuresis begins","GFR normalizes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The onset phase begins with the precipitating event and ends when oliguria develops.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mark later phases, not the end of the onset phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the onset phase of acute renal disease, renal blood flow and tissue oxygenation are approximately what percentage of normal?","options":["25% of normal","50% of normal","75% of normal","100% of normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the onset phase, renal blood flow is 25% of normal and tissue oxygenation is 25% of normal.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 25% for both."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The urine output during the onset phase of acute renal disease is:","options":["<0.5 ml/kg/hr",">400 ml/day","4 to 5 L/day",">2000 ml/24 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the onset phase, urine output is <0.5 ml/kg/hr.  Why B wrong: >400 ml/day is the diuretic phase output.  Why C wrong: 4–5 L/day describes late diuretic phase.  Why D wrong: >2000 ml/24 hrs describes polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The oliguric/anuric phase of acute renal disease lasts:","options":["Hours to days","8 to 14 days or longer","7 to 14 days","1 to 2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The oliguric/anuric phase duration is 8 to 14 days or longer.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to the onset, diuretic, and recovery phases respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The characteristic urine output during the oliguric/anuric phase of AKI is:","options":["<400 ml/day",">400 ml/day","4 to 5 L/day",">2000 ml/24 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output is <400 ml/day in the oliguric/anuric phase.  Why B wrong: >400 ml/day characterizes the diuretic phase.  Why C/D wrong: These describe higher outputs of the diuretic/polyuric state."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which laboratory findings are expected during the oliguric/anuric phase of AKI? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Elevated serum BUN & creatinine","Decreased GFR","Metabolic acidosis","Sign of anaemia (skin pallor, low Hb)","Elevated urine output above normal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The oliguric/anuric phase shows elevated serum BUN & creatinine, decreased GFR, metabolic acidosis, and signs of anaemia (skin pallor, low Hb).  Why E wrong: Urine output is decreased (<400 ml/day), not elevated, in this phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the oliguric phase of AKI, urine specific gravity is:","options":["Decreased in prerenal cause, or normal with intrarenal cause","Always markedly increased","Always fixed at 1.010 regardless of cause","Undetectable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased urine specific gravity occurs with prerenal cause, or it is normal with intrarenal cause.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described specific gravity variation by cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the oliguric phase of AKI, the pH and CO2 combining power are described as:","options":["Low pH level and decreased CO2 combining power","High pH with increased CO2 combining power","Normal pH with normal CO2","High pH with metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oliguric phase shows a low pH level and decreased CO2 combining power (metabolic acidosis).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the acidotic state of the oliguric phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The diuretic phase of AKI lasts approximately:","options":["Hours to days","8 to 14 days","7 to 14 days","1 to 2 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diuretic phase duration is 7 to 14 days.  Why A/B/D wrong: These match the onset, oliguric, and recovery phases respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the diuretic phase of AKI, which changes indicate recovery? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Gradual decline in BUN and serum creatinine","Continued low creatinine clearance with improving GFR","Daily urine output >400 ml/day","The kidney begins to recover","Complete cessation of urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In the diuretic phase the kidney begins to recover, BUN/creatinine gradually decline, creatinine clearance is still low but GFR is improving, and daily urine output is >400 ml/day.  Why E wrong: Urine output increases (not stops) in the diuretic phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the diuretic phase, as urine output increases to 4–5 L/day, excessive urine output indicates that:","options":["Damaged nephrons are recovering","The kidneys have failed completely","Infection is present","Dialysis has begun"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the diuretic phase, urine output rises slowly to 4–5 L/day, and excessive urine output indicates that damaged nephrons are recovering.  Why B/C/D wrong: The high output signals recovery of nephrons, not failure, infection, or dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which complications can occur during the diuretic phase of AKI due to excessive urine output? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dehydration","Hypotension","Hypovolemia","Tachycardia","Fluid overload/hypervolemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: During the diuretic phase, dehydration, hypotension, hypovolemia, and tachycardia can occur from excessive urine loss.  Why E wrong: Fluid overload is a feature of the oliguric phase, not the fluid-losing diuretic phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which electrolyte findings are possible during the diuretic phase of AKI? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypovolemia due to increased urine output","Hyponatremia due to loss of sodium","Hypokalaemia due to compensatory mechanism of acid-base","Hyperkalaemia due to inability to excrete potassium","Hyperphosphatemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Diuretic-phase electrolyte findings are hypovolemia (increased urine output), hyponatremia (loss of sodium), and hypokalaemia (compensatory acid-base mechanism).  Why D/E wrong: Hyperkalaemia and hyperphosphatemia are features of the oliguric phase, not the diuretic phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Besides monitoring urine output, what nursing action is appropriate during the diuretic phase of AKI?","options":["Administer IV fluids as per order","Withhold all fluids","Restrict fluids to 500 ml/day regardless of output","Discontinue vital sign monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the diuretic phase, level of consciousness improves and IV fluids are administered as per order to replace losses.  Why B/C wrong: Excessive urine loss requires fluid replacement, not restriction.  Why D wrong: Monitoring must continue."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The recovery phase of AKI has a duration of:","options":["Hours to days","8 to 14 days","7 to 14 days","1 to 2 years"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The recovery phase lasts 1 to 2 years, and complete recovery may take 1 to 2 years.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to the onset, oliguric, and diuretic phases."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which changes occur during the recovery phase of AKI? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tubular function restored","Diuresis subsides and kidney begins to function normally","Increased GFR","Stabilization or continued decline in BUN and serum creatinine","Urine output remains below 400 ml/day permanently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The recovery phase features restored tubular function, subsiding diuresis with normal kidney function returning, increased GFR, and stabilization/continued decline in BUN and serum creatinine.  Why E wrong: Urine volume returns to normal in recovery, not permanent oliguria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"If AKI does not recover, it can progress to:","options":["Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","Nephrotic syndrome","Glomerulonephritis","Pheochromocytoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If AKI does not recover, it can progress to CKD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate conditions, not the stated progression of unrecovered AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the oliguric phase of AKI, which signs indicate excess fluid volume/hypervolemia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypertension and oedema","Pleural & pericardial effusion","Dysrhythmias and heart failure","Pulmonary oedema","Dehydration and hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of excess fluid volume/hypervolemia in the oliguric phase include hypertension and oedema, pleural & pericardial effusion, dysrhythmias and heart failure, and pulmonary oedema.  Why E wrong: Dehydration/hypotension reflect fluid loss (diuretic phase), not hypervolemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Signs of uraemia/increased BUN level in the oliguric phase of AKI include: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Anorexia","Nausea & vomiting","Pruritus","Increased appetite","Euphoria"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of uraemia/increased BUN include anorexia, nausea & vomiting, and pruritus.  Why D/E wrong: Increased appetite and euphoria are not signs of uraemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Kussmaul's respiration seen in the oliguric phase of AKI is a sign of:","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Fluid volume deficit","Hypocalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kussmaul's respiration—a deep and labored breathing pattern—is a sign of metabolic acidosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Kussmaul's breathing specifically reflects metabolic acidosis, not these."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, tingling of the extremities is a neurological sign resulting from:","options":["Hypocalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypernatremia","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tingling of extremities occurs due to hypocalcaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Tingling in this context is attributed to hypocalcaemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which neurological changes may occur in the oliguric phase of AKI? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tingling of extremities","Confusion & decreased LOC","Lethargy & drowsiness progressing to disorientation and coma","Enhanced memory and alertness","Hyperactivity with insomnia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Neurological changes include tingling of extremities (hypocalcaemia), confusion & decreased LOC, and lethargy/drowsiness progressing to disorientation and coma.  Why D/E wrong: Enhanced memory and hyperactivity are not features of uraemic encephalopathy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which findings indicate pericarditis in a patient with AKI? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Friction rub and chest pain with inspiration","Low grade fever","Bradycardia at rest","Hyperglycemia","Absent heart sounds only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of pericarditis are a friction rub and chest pain with inspiration, plus low grade fever.  Why C/D/E wrong: These are not listed signs of pericarditis in AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the oliguric phase of AKI, hyperkalaemia occurs due to:","options":["Inability to excrete potassium","Excessive potassium loss in urine","Dietary potassium restriction","Increased renal potassium wasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperkalaemia occurs due to the inability to excrete potassium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe potassium loss, which would cause hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the oliguric phase of AKI, the serum sodium level is typically normal or decreased because of:","options":["A dilutional effect","Excessive sodium excretion","Aldosterone deficiency","Sodium loss through vomiting only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A normal or decreased serum sodium level occurs due to a dilutional effect (fluid retention).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated mechanism is dilutional, not sodium wasting."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, hypocalcaemia develops because the kidneys are unable to produce:","options":["Calcitriol (1,25 vitamin-D)","Erythropoietin","Renin","Aldosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcaemia occurs due to the inability to produce calcitriol or 1,25 vitamin-D in the kidneys.  Why B wrong: Loss of erythropoietin causes anaemia, not hypocalcaemia.  Why C/D wrong: Renin and aldosterone relate to blood pressure/sodium balance, not calcium production."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the oliguric phase of AKI, hyperphosphatemia develops due to:","options":["The inverse effect of calcium","Excessive dietary phosphate only","Increased renal phosphate excretion","Vitamin D toxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperphosphatemia occurs due to the inverse effect of calcium (as calcium falls, phosphate rises).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated mechanism is the inverse relationship with calcium."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the oliguric phase of AKI, if hypertension is still present, daily fluid intake should NOT exceed:","options":["1000 ml","2000 ml","3000 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If hypertension is still present, daily fluid intake should not exceed 1000 ml.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated limit is 1000 ml."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"During the oliguric phase of AKI, which medications are given as ordered to manage fluid overload?","options":["Diuretics","Antihistamines","Anticoagulants","Bronchodilators"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ordered medications such as diuretics are given during the oliguric phase.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not address the fluid overload of the oliguric phase."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the nursing care of AKI, which parameter is considered the BEST for fluid status assessment?","options":["Daily weight","Blood pressure","Heart rate","Serum creatinine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight is the best parameter for fluid status assessment; the patient is weighed daily with the same scale, same clothes, and same time.  Why B/C/D wrong: While these are monitored, weight is stated to be the best parameter for fluid status."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, the nurse should report to the doctor if the patient's weight increases by:","options":["½ to 1 kg/day or 300–500 gm/day","5 kg/day","2 to 3 kg/week","Any amount over 1 week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Report to the doctor if weight increases ½ to 1 kg/day or 300–500 gm/day, because it indicates fluid retention.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated reporting threshold."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, a weight increase of 300–500 gm/day indicates:","options":["Fluid retention","Dehydration","Muscle gain","Improved renal function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight increase of ½–1 kg/day or 300–500 gm/day indicates fluid retention.  Why B/C/D wrong: A daily weight gain of this magnitude reflects fluid, not muscle or improvement."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, metabolic acidosis may be treated with:","options":["Sodium carbonate (bicarbonate)","Potassium chloride","Calcium gluconate","Normal saline only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors for metabolic acidosis, which may be treated by sodium carbonate (bicarbonate).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not correct metabolic acidosis in the manner described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, urinalysis is monitored for which findings? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Proteinuria","Haematuria","Specific gravity","Serum BUN","Bilirubin only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors urinalysis for proteinuria, haematuria, and specific gravity.  Why D wrong: Serum BUN is a blood test, not urinalysis.  Why E wrong: Bilirubin is not the specified urinalysis focus in AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, altered level of consciousness (LOC) that the nurse should monitor is caused by:","options":["Uraemia","Hypoglycemia","Hyperthermia","Anxiety"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors LOC status that is caused by uraemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes altered LOC to uraemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, auscultation of breath sounds is performed to assess for:","options":["Pulmonary oedema","Pleural friction rub only","Bronchospasm","Pneumothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse assesses breath sounds by auscultation for pulmonary oedema.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is detecting pulmonary oedema."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The recommended caloric intake in the dietary instruction for AKI is:","options":["2000–2500 Kcal","1000–1500 Kcal","3000–3500 Kcal","500–1000 Kcal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dietary instruction for AKI specifies 2000–2500 Kcal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated caloric range."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The recommended protein intake in the AKI diet is a moderate-to-low protein diet of:","options":["30–50 gm/day","60–80 gm/day","100–120 gm/day","10–20 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A moderate-to-low protein diet of 30–50 gm/day is recommended to reduce workload on the kidney.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated protein range."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Why is a moderate-to-low protein diet recommended in AKI?","options":["To reduce workload on the kidney","To promote weight gain","To increase serum albumin rapidly","To correct hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A moderate-to-low protein diet (30–50 gm/day) is used to reduce workload on the kidney.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for protein restriction in AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The recommended carbohydrate and fat content of the AKI diet is:","options":["High carbohydrate diet, moderate fat","Low carbohydrate, high fat","High protein, low carbohydrate","No carbohydrates, high fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A high carbohydrate diet with moderate fat is recommended in AKI.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated carbohydrate/fat recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the AKI diet, foods rich in which electrolyte should be restricted (per lab reports and doctor's order)?","options":["Potassium","Sodium bicarbonate","Chloride","Magnesium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Foods rich in potassium should be restricted, or as per laboratory reports & doctor's order.  Why B/C/D wrong: The specified restriction is potassium-rich foods."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In AKI, fluid intake is approximately 1000 ml/day in the oliguric phase and:","options":["May increase later in the diuretic phase","Should be stopped completely in all phases","Must be reduced to 200 ml/day","Remains fixed at 1000 ml throughout recovery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid intake may be about 1000 ml/day in the oliguric phase and later may increase in the diuretic phase (to replace losses).  Why B/C/D wrong: Fluid intake increases in the diuretic phase, not stopped, fixed, or reduced further."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the AKI diet, milk is usually restricted to:","options":["One cup or less per day","Three cups per day","Unlimited amounts","Two liters per day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Restriction of milk is usually one cup or less per day, along with restriction of fish, egg, and meats.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated limit is one cup or less per day."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"If AKI condition does not improve despite treatment, the nurse should prepare the client for:","options":["Renal dialysis","Discharge home","Cystoscopy","VMA testing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client is prepared for renal dialysis if the AKI condition is not improved.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the appropriate next step for unresolving AKI."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is best defined as:","options":["Slowly progressive, irreversible loss of kidney function resulting in uraemia with GFR ≤60 ml/min for 3 months or longer","Sudden, reversible loss of kidney function","Inflammation of the bladder mucosa","A tumor of the adrenal gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CKD is a slowly progressive deterioration/irreversible loss of kidney function resulting in uraemia, with a GFR less than or equal to 60 ml/minute for 3 months or longer.  Why B wrong: Sudden and reversible describes AKI.  Why C/D wrong: These describe cystitis and pheochromocytoma respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, hypervolemia can occur because of the kidney's inability to:","options":["Excrete sodium & water","Produce erythropoietin","Filter creatinine","Reabsorb glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypervolemia can occur because of the kidney's inability to excrete sodium & water; conversely hypovolemia can occur because of inability to conserve sodium and water.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other functions, not the cause of hypervolemia in CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"CKD affects all major body systems and ultimately requires which interventions to maintain life?","options":["Dialysis or kidney transplantation","Cystoscopy or biopsy","VMA testing or KUB USG","Bowel preparation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CKD affects all major body systems and requires dialysis or kidney transplantation to maintain life.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are diagnostic procedures, not life-maintaining treatments for CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"The MOST COMMON cause of CKD is:","options":["Diabetes nephropathy","Pheochromocytoma","Bladder cancer","Nephrotic syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of CKD is diabetes nephropathy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common cause of CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"End Stage Kidney Disease (ESKD) is defined when the GFR falls below:","options":["15 ml/hr (ml/min)","30 ml/min","60 ml/min","90 ml/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If GFR is less than 15 ml/hr (ml/min), it is called End Stage Kidney Disease (ESKD).  Why B/C/D wrong: These GFR values correspond to less severe stages of kidney disease."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"According to the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of >90 ml/min corresponds to:","options":["Kidney damage with normal or high GFR (mild damage, kidneys work as well as normal)","Severe GFR","End-stage kidney disease","Moderate GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR >90 ml/min = kidney damage with normal GFR; mild damage, kidneys work as well as normal; plan is diagnosis & treat cause and evaluate risk for heart disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to lower GFR ranges."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of 60–89 ml/min corresponds to:","options":["Kidney damage with mild GFR (mild damage, kidneys still work well)","Moderate GFR","Severe GFR","End-stage kidney disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR 60–89 ml/min = kidney damage with mild GFR reduction; mild damage, kidneys still work well; plan is estimate progression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to lower GFR ranges."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of 45–59 ml/min is described as:","options":["Mild to moderate GFR; mild-to-moderate damage, evaluate & treat complications","Normal kidney function","Severe kidney damage requiring dialysis","End-stage kidney disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR 45–59 ml/min = mild to moderate GFR; kidneys don't work as they should; evaluate & treat complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 45–59 range."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of 30–45 ml/min corresponds to:","options":["Moderate GFR; moderate-to-severe damage, evaluate & treat complications","Normal GFR","End-stage kidney disease","Mild GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR 30–45 ml/min = moderate GFR; moderate-to-severe kidney damage, kidneys don't work as they should; evaluate & treat complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 30–45 range."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of 15–30 ml/min (Severe GFR) calls for which evaluation plan?","options":["Prepare for dialysis, referral for transplantation","Diagnosis and treat cause only","Estimate progression only","No intervention needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR 15–30 ml/min = severe GFR; kidneys close to not working at all; prepare for dialysis and referral for transplantation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These plans apply to milder stages."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the Stages of Kidney Disease table, a GFR of <15 ml/min (kidney failure/ESKD) has which recommendation?","options":["Consider dialysis","Only estimate progression","Evaluate risk for heart disease only","No treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR <15 ml/min = kidney failure or ESKD; most severe damage, kidneys very close to not working; consider dialysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These plans apply to earlier stages."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","Hypertension","Diabetes","Renal artery occlusion","Pheochromocytoma"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of CKD include AKI, hypertension, diabetes, infection, chronic urinary obstructions, renal artery occlusion, and autoimmune disorders.  Why E wrong: Pheochromocytoma is not listed as a cause of CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which of the following are additional listed causes of CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Infection","Chronic urinary obstructions","Autoimmune disorders","Renal artery occlusion","Excessive water intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Infection, chronic urinary obstructions, autoimmune disorders, and renal artery occlusion are listed causes of CKD.  Why E wrong: Excessive water intake is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Asterixis, a neurological sign in CKD, refers to:","options":["Flapping tremors of the hand due to hepatic encephalopathy","Alteration in gait","Deep, labored breathing","Fluid in the pericardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asterixis is flapping tremors of the hand due to hepatic encephalopathy.  Why B wrong: Alteration in gait describes ataxia.  Why C wrong: Deep labored breathing describes Kussmaul's respiration.  Why D wrong: That describes pericardial effusion."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, the term \"ataxia\" refers to:","options":["Alteration in gait","Flapping tremors of the hand","Blood in the urine","Bronze skin pigmentation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ataxia is an alteration in gait.  Why B wrong: Flapping tremors describe asterixis.  Why C/D wrong: These describe haematuria and urochromatic pigmentation respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which neurological signs are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Asterixis (flapping tremors)","Ataxia","Lethargy, drowsiness, tremors, twitching, slurred speech, jerky movement","Seizures","Improved fine motor coordination"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Neurological CKD signs include asterixis, ataxia, lethargy/drowsiness/tremors/twitching/slurred speech/jerky movement, and seizures.  Why E wrong: Coordination worsens (ataxia), it does not improve."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, cardiovascular effects occur due to increased cardiac workload. Cardiomyopathy is caused by:","options":["Increased preload & afterload","Decreased blood volume","Reduced sodium retention","Loss of erythropoietin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiomyopathy in CKD is due to increased preload & afterload.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the mechanism of CKD cardiomyopathy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, hypertension develops due to:","options":["Hypervolemia & sodium retention","Dehydration","Loss of aldosterone","Decreased cardiac workload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension in CKD occurs due to hypervolemia & sodium retention.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the cause of CKD hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Pericardial friction rub in CKD is caused by:","options":["Pericardial effusion & cardiac tamponade","Pleural effusion","Bronchospasm","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pericardial friction rub is due to pericardial effusion & cardiac tamponade.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of pericardial friction rub."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which cardiovascular signs/complications are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiomyopathy","Hypertension","Pericardial effusion","Pericardial friction rub","Bradycardia due to low workload"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiovascular signs of CKD include cardiomyopathy, hypertension, pericardial effusion, pericardial friction rub, and heart failure (from increased cardiac workload).  Why E wrong: CKD involves increased cardiac workload, not bradycardia from low workload."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, crackles heard in the lungs are due to:","options":["Fluid accumulation in lungs / pulmonary oedema","Airway constriction","Pneumothorax","Pleural thickening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Crackles are due to fluid accumulation in the lungs (pulmonary oedema).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the cause of crackles in CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, Kussmaul's respiration is described as a:","options":["Deep & labored breathing pattern","Shallow, rapid breathing","Periodic apnea","Wheezing on expiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kussmaul's respiration is a deep & labored breathing pattern.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe Kussmaul's respiration."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, pleural effusion is defined as:","options":["Fluid collection in the pleural cavity","Fluid around the heart","Fluid in the peritoneal cavity","Air in the pleural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pleural effusion is fluid collection in the pleural cavity.  Why B wrong: Fluid around the heart is pericardial effusion.  Why C wrong: Fluid in the peritoneum is ascites.  Why D wrong: Air in the pleural space is pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, dyspnoea (difficulty in breathing) is attributed to:","options":["Hypervolemia","Hypovolemia","Anemia only","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dyspnoea is difficulty in breathing due to hypervolemia in CKD.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes dyspnoea to hypervolemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Tachypnoea in CKD is defined as a respiratory rate of:","options":[">20 breaths/minute","<10 breaths/minute","Exactly 12 breaths/minute",">40 breaths/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachypnoea is a respiratory rate >20 breaths/minute.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, anaemia occurs due to:","options":["Lack of erythropoietin hormone of the kidney","Iron overload","Excess dietary protein","Increased platelet destruction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anaemia in CKD is due to lack of erythropoietin hormone of the kidney.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the cause of CKD-related anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which GI signs are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Anorexia","Constipation & diarrhoea alternately","Nausea and stomatitis","Uremic gastritis & vomiting","Increased appetite with weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: GI signs of CKD include anorexia, alternating constipation & diarrhoea, nausea and stomatitis, and uremic gastritis & vomiting.  Why E wrong: CKD causes anorexia, not increased appetite."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, the metabolic acidosis in the GI-related section is attributed to:","options":["Inability to excrete potassium or maintain homeostasis","Excessive vomiting only","Increased renal bicarbonate production","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic acidosis is due to inability to excrete potassium or maintain homeostasis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, polyuria (urine output >2000 ml/24 hrs) is seen in which phase?","options":["Early phase","Later phase","End-stage kidney disease","It is never seen in CKD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polyuria (urine output >2000 ml/24 hrs) is seen in the early phase of CKD.  Why B wrong: Oliguria is seen in the later phase.  Why C wrong: Anuria is seen in ESKD.  Why D wrong: Polyuria does occur in early CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, oliguria is defined as urine output <400 ml/24 hrs and is seen in:","options":["Later phase","Early phase","Recovery","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oliguria (urine output <400 ml/24 hrs) is seen in the later phase of CKD.  Why B wrong: Polyuria is seen in the early phase.  Why C/D wrong: Oliguria is a later-phase feature of CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, anuria is defined as urine output of:","options":["<100 ml/24 hrs, seen in ESKD","<400 ml/24 hrs",">2000 ml/24 hrs","1000 ml/24 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anuria is urine output <100 ml/24 hrs and is seen in ESKD.  Why B wrong: <400 ml/24 hrs is oliguria.  Why C wrong: >2000 ml/24 hrs is polyuria.  Why D wrong: 1000 ml/24 hrs is not anuria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, \"haematuria\" and \"proteinuria\" respectively refer to:","options":["Blood present in urine; albumin protein present in urine","Protein in urine; blood in urine","Glucose in urine; ketones in urine","Pus in urine; bacteria in urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Haematuria is blood present in urine; proteinuria is albumin protein present in urine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the definitions."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, decreased skin turgor and dry skin occur due to:","options":["Poor circulation of blood & fluid","Excess sweating","Vitamin C deficiency","Sun exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased skin turgor & dry skin are due to poor circulation of blood & fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, \"uremic frost\" is associated with:","options":["Severe azotaemia (severe elevation of serum BUN & creatinine)","Mild dehydration","Sun-damaged skin","Fungal skin infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uremic frost indicates severe azotaemia—severe elevation of serum BUN & creatinine level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe uremic frost."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, \"ecchymosis & purpura\" of the skin are described as:","options":["Minor bruising-type reddish spots seen on the skin","Bronze-coloured pigmentation","Flapping tremors","Dry, scaly patches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ecchymosis & purpura are minor bruising-type reddish-like spots seen on the skin.  Why B wrong: Bronze colour describes urochromatic pigmentation.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to ecchymosis/purpura."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In CKD, \"urochromatic pigmentation\" of the skin appears:","options":["Bronze coloured","Bright red","Pale white","Blue-black"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urochromatic pigmentation is bronze coloured.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described bronze pigmentation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which musculoskeletal signs are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Pathological fractures","Bone pain & muscle weakness","Renal osteodystrophy (seen in radiograph)","Increased bone density","Improved muscle strength"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Musculoskeletal signs of CKD include pathological fractures, bone pain & muscle weakness, and renal osteodystrophy (seen on radiograph).  Why D/E wrong: CKD causes bone weakening and weakness, not increased density or strength."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which reproductive-system signs are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Decreased fertility & libido","Impotence","Infrequent or absent menses","Increased fertility","Precocious puberty"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: CKD reproductive signs include decreased fertility & libido, impotence, and infrequent or absent menses.  Why D/E wrong: Fertility decreases in CKD, and precocious puberty is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"Which electrolyte imbalances are related to CKD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypocalcaemia & hyperphosphatemia","Hyperkalaemia & hyponatraemia (usually dilutional)","Hyperglycaemia","Hypernatraemia with dehydration","Hypophosphatemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: CKD electrolyte imbalances include hypocalcaemia & hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalaemia & hyponatraemia (usually dilutional), and hyperglycaemia.  Why D wrong: Hyponatraemia (dilutional), not hypernatraemia with dehydration, is described.  Why E wrong: Hyperphosphatemia (not hypophosphatemia) occurs in CKD."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"According to the CKD complications flow chart, impaired sodium balance in CKD leads to sodium retention and volume overload, which is treated with:","options":["Sodium restriction and diuretics","Sodium bicarbonate","Phosphate binders","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium retention and volume overload (from impaired sodium balance) is treated with sodium restriction and diuretics.  Why B wrong: Sodium bicarbonate treats metabolic acidosis.  Why C wrong: Phosphate binders treat elevated phosphate.  Why D wrong: ESAs treat anemia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the CKD complications flow chart, impaired potassium excretion causes hyperkalemia, treated with dietary restriction and:","options":["Avoiding NSAIDs","Sodium bicarbonate","Phosphate binders","Iron replacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperkalemia (impaired potassium excretion) is treated with dietary restriction and avoiding NSAIDs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These treat acidosis, phosphate elevation, and anemia respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the CKD complications flow chart, impaired acid excretion causes metabolic acidosis, treated with:","options":["Sodium bicarbonate","Phosphate binders","Calcimimetics","Diuretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic acidosis (from impaired acid excretion) is treated with sodium bicarbonate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These treat other CKD complications."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the CKD complications flow chart, impaired calcium/phosphate balance produces increased phosphate, increased PTH, decreased serum calcium, and decreased calcitriol. This is treated with: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Phosphate binders","Calcimimetics","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","Sodium bicarbonate","Diuretics"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The calcium/phosphate imbalance (↑phosphate, ↑PTH, ↓serum calcium, ↓calcitriol; leading to ↑PO4 and 2° HPT) is treated with phosphate binders and calcimimetics.  Why C wrong: ESAs treat anemia.  Why D wrong: Sodium bicarbonate treats metabolic acidosis.  Why E wrong: Diuretics treat volume overload."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the CKD complications flow chart, impaired erythropoiesis causes anemia, treated with:","options":["Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron replacement","Sodium restriction","Phosphate binders","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anemia (from impaired erythropoiesis) is treated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron replacement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These treat volume overload, phosphate elevation, and acidosis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"In the CKD calcium/phosphate pathway, the elevated phosphate (↑PO4) and secondary hyperparathyroidism (2° HPT) result from which combination?","options":["Increased phosphate, increased PTH, decreased serum calcium, and decreased calcitriol","Decreased phosphate and increased calcium","Increased calcitriol and decreased PTH","Normal calcium with low phosphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows ↑phosphate, ↑PTH, ↓serum calcium, and ↓calcitriol, driving ↑PO4 and 2° HPT.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described pathophysiology."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A patient with CKD reports abnormal bleeding and easy bruising. Under which body system is this classified in the source?","options":["Haematological system","Integumentary system","Musculoskeletal system","Neurological system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abnormal bleeding & bruising is listed under the haematological system in CKD.  Why B wrong: Ecchymosis/purpura and skin changes are integumentary; abnormal bleeding & bruising is haematological.  Why C/D wrong: These systems cover fractures/tremors, not bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A nurse is caring for a client in the oliguric phase of AKI. Which finding requires the MOST immediate attention?","options":["Serum potassium rising due to inability to excrete potassium","Pink-tinged urine","A weight decrease of 100 gm","Blood pressure of 118/76 mmHg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rising serum potassium (hyperkalaemia) from the inability to excrete potassium is life-threatening (dysrhythmia risk) and is the priority in the oliguric phase.  Why B wrong: Pink-tinged urine is expected after cystoscopy, not an emergency here.  Why C wrong: A small weight decrease is not an immediate danger.  Why D wrong: 118/76 mmHg is a normal blood pressure."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A child with nephrotic syndrome has generalized body swelling. The nurse documents this as:","options":["Anasarca","Ascites","Periorbital oedema","Scrotal oedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Generalised oedema is termed anasarca.  Why B wrong: Ascites is fluid specifically in the peritoneal cavity.  Why C wrong: Periorbital oedema is around the eyes.  Why D wrong: Scrotal oedema is localized to the scrotum in males."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A patient with glomerulonephritis develops a friction rub and chest pain worse on inspiration in later renal failure. In AKI, these are recognized as signs of:","options":["Pericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumonia","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Friction rub and chest pain with inspiration are signs of pericarditis (with low grade fever) in renal failure/AKI.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the described pericarditis presentation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A CKD patient's radiograph shows renal osteodystrophy. This bone change reflects the CKD complication in which mineral balance pathway?","options":["Calcium/phosphate balance (↑phosphate, ↑PTH, ↓calcium, ↓calcitriol)","Sodium balance","Potassium excretion","Acid excretion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Renal osteodystrophy relates to disturbed calcium/phosphate balance (↑phosphate, ↑PTH, ↓serum calcium, ↓calcitriol) causing bone disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These pathways cause volume overload, hyperkalemia, and acidosis, not bone dystrophy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A nurse reviews a CKD patient's labs showing decreased calcitriol. Which resulting electrolyte abnormality is expected?","options":["Hypocalcaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hypophosphatemia","Hypokalaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased calcitriol reduces calcium absorption, causing hypocalcaemia (paired with hyperphosphatemia in CKD).  Why B/C/D wrong: CKD causes low calcium and high phosphate, not these."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A nurse is teaching a client scheduled for KUB USG. Which statement by the client indicates correct understanding?","options":["\"I should drink more water about an hour before so my bladder is full for better visualization.\"","\"I must have a full bowel preparation the night before.\"","\"I will be given an IV dye during the scan.\"","\"I need to stay NPO for 3 days before the test.\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For KUB USG, the client should drink more water before 1 hour because a full bladder is necessary for better visualization; bowel prep is not required.  Why B wrong: Bowel preparation is not required for KUB USG.  Why C wrong: No IV dye is used in routine KUB ultrasound.  Why D wrong: A 3-day NPO period is not required."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A CKD patient presents with a deep and labored breathing pattern. The nurse recognizes this compensatory breathing as:","options":["Kussmaul's respiration responding to metabolic acidosis","Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Normal eupnea","Sighing respiration from anxiety"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kussmaul's respiration is a deep and labored breathing pattern that compensates for metabolic acidosis in CKD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the metabolic-acidosis-related Kussmaul pattern."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A nurse notes that a CKD patient has both constipation and diarrhoea occurring alternately. This GI finding is:","options":["A recognized GI sign of CKD","Always a sign of infection","Unrelated to CKD","Only seen in AKI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Constipation & diarrhoea seen alternately is a listed GI sign of CKD.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically lists alternating constipation and diarrhoea as a CKD GI feature."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A patient in the diuretic phase of AKI now produces 4–5 L of urine per day and develops tachycardia and hypotension. The priority nursing action is to:","options":["Administer IV fluids as per order to replace losses","Restrict all fluid intake","Administer a diuretic","Withhold vital sign monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the diuretic phase, excessive urine output (4–5 L/day) causes dehydration/hypotension/hypovolemia/tachycardia, so IV fluids are administered as ordered.  Why B/C wrong: Restricting fluids or giving diuretics would worsen the volume depletion.  Why D wrong: Monitoring must continue."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"According to the source, in AKI the nurse should monitor the urine specifically for which characteristics? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Colour","Consistency","Hourly output","Signs of hypertension, tachycardia, dyspnoea","Only daily total volume"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: AKI nursing care includes strictly monitoring urine output hourly, colour and consistency of urine, and signs of hypertension, tachycardia, dyspnoea.  Why E wrong: Hourly (not only daily) output monitoring is required."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A CKD patient's serum sodium is low. The source attributes hyponatraemia in CKD as:","options":["Usually dilutional","Due to excessive sodium excretion","Caused by dehydration","A sign of recovery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In CKD, hyponatraemia is usually dilutional (paired with hyperkalaemia in electrolyte imbalances).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states it is usually dilutional, not from sodium wasting, dehydration, or recovery."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Renal diagnostic procedures, Nephrotic Syndrome, Glomerulonephritis…","question":"A nurse is monitoring a client with AKI for infection. Which finding would indicate a possible infection?","options":["Elevated temperature","Decreased urine specific gravity","Weight loss of 100 gm","Improved level of consciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors for signs of infection such as elevated temperature in AKI.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated sign of infection."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In a client with chronic kidney disease and hyperkalaemia, which classic ECG finding should the nurse monitor for?","options":["ST segment depression, tall peaked T-wave, and wide QRS complex","Delta wave and short PR interval","U wave and flattened T-wave","Prolonged QT interval only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the hyperkalaemia ECG findings as ST segment depression, tall peaked T-wave, and wide QRS complex.  Why B wrong: Delta wave/short PR is seen in pre-excitation (WPW), not hyperkalaemia.  Why C wrong: U wave and flattened T-wave are features of hypokalaemia, the opposite condition.  Why D wrong: The page specifies ST depression, peaked T-waves, and wide QRS, not an isolated prolonged QT."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Besides ECG changes, which additional manifestations of hyperkalaemia in CKD should the nurse monitor for?","options":["Dysrhythmias, muscle weakness, and decreased cardiac output","Hypertension and bounding pulses","Hyperreflexia and seizures","Polyuria and dilute urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs the nurse to monitor for dysrhythmias, muscle weakness, and decreased cardiac output.  Why B wrong: These are not the listed hyperkalaemia manifestations.  Why C wrong: Hyperreflexia/seizures are not the stated effects of hyperkalaemia here.  Why D wrong: CKD typically causes oliguria, not polyuria; not a hyperkalaemia sign listed."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which dietary instruction is appropriate for a CKD client with hyperkalaemia?","options":["Provide a low potassium diet and avoid foods that are high in potassium","Encourage a high potassium diet with bananas and oranges","Provide unlimited salt substitutes","Increase intake of dried fruits and nuts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to provide a low potassium diet and avoid foods high in potassium.  Why B wrong: Bananas/oranges are high-potassium and would worsen hyperkalaemia.  Why C wrong: Salt substitutes are usually potassium-based and are contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Dried fruits and nuts are high in potassium."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"K-Bind,\" used to reduce potassium in CKD, is the trade preparation of which drug?","options":["Calcium polystyrene sulfonate","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Calcium gluconate","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states K-Bind is calcium polystyrene sulfonate.  Why B wrong: Sodium polystyrene sulfonate is Kayexalate, a different preparation.  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is given to reduce myocardial irritability, not as K-Bind.  Why D wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is used for metabolic acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Kayexalate, a potassium-reducing medicine, is composed of:","options":["Sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Calcium polystyrene sulfonate","Sevelamer hydrochloride","Calcium carbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Kayexalate is sodium polystyrene sulfonate.  Why B wrong: Calcium polystyrene sulfonate is K-Bind, not Kayexalate.  Why C wrong: Sevelamer hydrochloride is a phosphorus binder.  Why D wrong: Calcium carbonate is a phosphorus binder."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"To rapidly shift potassium from the vascular compartment into the cells in hyperkalaemia, the nurse administers:","options":["25% or 50% dextrose plus insulin (usually 8 units)","Loop diuretic alone","Oral potassium supplements","Spironolactone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer 25% or 50% dextrose + insulin usually 8 units (or as per order) to shift potassium into the cells from the vascular compartment.  Why B wrong: Loop diuretics remove potassium via urine but do not shift it intracellularly like insulin/dextrose.  Why C wrong: Potassium supplements would worsen hyperkalaemia.  Why D wrong: Spironolactone is potassium-sparing and retains K+; it is avoided."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why may calcium gluconate be prescribed in an emergency hyperkalaemia situation?","options":["It may reduce myocardial irritability caused by hyperkalaemia","It directly lowers serum potassium level permanently","It binds potassium in the gut for excretion","It shifts potassium into the cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states calcium gluconate may reduce myocardial irritability caused by hyperkalaemia.  Why B wrong: Calcium gluconate protects the myocardium; it does not itself lower serum potassium.  Why C wrong: Gut-binding is the action of resins (K-Bind/Kayexalate), not calcium gluconate.  Why D wrong: Intracellular shifting is achieved by insulin/dextrose."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why should potassium-sparing diuretics such as spironolactone be avoided in a CKD client with hyperkalaemia?","options":["They will retain K+ ion","They cause excessive potassium loss","They damage the glomerulus directly","They raise serum calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states potassium-sparing diuretics such as spironolactone are avoided because they will retain K+ ion.  Why B wrong: Potassium-sparing agents retain, not lose, potassium.  Why C wrong: Direct glomerular damage is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Raising serum calcium is not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which loop diuretic is administered to reduce the serum potassium level in hyperkalaemia?","options":["Lasix (frusemide)","Spironolactone","Hydrochlorothiazide","Mannitol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer loop diuretics such as Lasix (frusemide) to reduce serum potassium level.  Why B wrong: Spironolactone is potassium-sparing and is avoided.  Why C wrong: Hydrochlorothiazide is a thiazide, not the loop diuretic named.  Why D wrong: Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic, not named here."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"When hyperkalaemia in CKD cannot be controlled by medicines and diet, the nurse should prepare the client for:","options":["Dialysis as per order","Immediate nephrectomy","Blood transfusion","Discharge home"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to prepare the client for dialysis as per order.  Why B wrong: Nephrectomy is not the listed intervention for hyperkalaemia.  Why C wrong: Transfusion addresses anaemia, not hyperkalaemia.  Why D wrong: Uncontrolled hyperkalaemia is dangerous and requires dialysis, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Hypertension as a complication of CKD is primarily caused by:","options":["Failure of the kidneys to maintain BP homeostasis","Excessive erythropoietin secretion","Loss of magnesium in urine","Overactivity of the thyroid gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypertension in CKD is caused by failure of the kidneys to maintain BP homeostasis.  Why B wrong: Erythropoietin is decreased in CKD (causing anaemia), not the cause of hypertension here.  Why C wrong: Magnesium is retained in CKD (hypermagnesemia), not lost.  Why D wrong: Thyroid overactivity is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which dietary/fluid restriction is indicated for the CKD client with hypertension?","options":["Maintain fluid and sodium restrictions as per order","Increase sodium and free water intake","High protein, high salt diet","Unrestricted fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs the nurse to maintain fluid and sodium restrictions as per order.  Why B/C wrong: Increasing sodium/salt would worsen fluid retention and hypertension.  Why D wrong: Fluids are restricted, not unrestricted."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which class of antihypertensive is considered the drug of choice in CKD?","options":["ACE inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers only","Alpha-blockers only","Potassium-sparing diuretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ACE inhibitors are the choice of antihypertensive medicines in CKD.  Why B/C wrong: These are not identified as the choice in the source.  Why D wrong: Potassium-sparing diuretics are avoided in CKD due to potassium retention."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Propranolol is also a choice antihypertensive in CKD because it:","options":["Reduces renin hormone in the kidney","Increases erythropoietin secretion","Retains potassium ions","Blocks calcium channels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states propranolol is a choice because it will reduce renin hormone in the kidney.  Why B wrong: It does not increase erythropoietin.  Why C wrong: Retaining potassium is not its mechanism.  Why D wrong: Propranolol is a beta-blocker, not a calcium channel blocker."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In CKD-related GI bleeding, urea is broken down by intestinal bacteria into ammonia, which then:","options":["Irritates the GI mucosa causing ulceration and bleeding","Neutralizes gastric acid protecting the mucosa","Increases erythropoietin production","Binds phosphorus in the gut"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ammonia irritates the GI mucosa causing ulceration and bleeding.  Why B wrong: Ammonia irritates, it does not protect, the mucosa.  Why C wrong: Erythropoietin is unrelated to this mechanism.  Why D wrong: Phosphorus binding is the role of phosphate binders, not ammonia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the drug of choice for gastrointestinal ammonia in CKD?","options":["Lactulose","Aspirin","Calcium gluconate","Sevelamer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for gastrointestinal ammonia is Lactulose.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is specifically avoided in CKD (worsens bleeding).  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is used in hyperkalaemia/hypermagnesemia.  Why D wrong: Sevelamer is a phosphorus binder."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"When monitoring a CKD client for GI bleeding, the nurse should watch for:","options":["Decreasing Hb and haematocrit levels and stools for occult blood","Rising Hb and rising blood pressure","Increased urine output","Elevated serum calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs monitoring for decreasing Hb and haematocrit and checking stools for occult blood.  Why B wrong: Bleeding lowers, not raises, Hb.  Why C wrong: Urine output is not the GI bleeding indicator.  Why D wrong: Serum calcium is unrelated to GI bleeding monitoring here."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The term \"Malena\" (melena) refers to:","options":["Upper GI bleeding","Lower GI bleeding","Bleeding from the urinary tract","Bleeding from the lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Malena = upper GI bleeding.  Why B wrong: Lower GI bleeding is haematochezia.  Why C wrong: Urinary tract bleeding is haematuria.  Why D wrong: Lung bleeding is haemoptysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Haematochezia\" refers to:","options":["Lower GI bleeding","Upper GI bleeding","Blood in the urine","Coughing up blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Haematochezia = lower GI bleeding.  Why B wrong: Upper GI bleeding is malena.  Why C wrong: Blood in urine is haematuria.  Why D wrong: Coughing blood is haemoptysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why should aspirin be avoided in a client with CKD?","options":["It is 100% excreted by the kidneys, so in CKD it causes toxicity and prolongs bleeding time","It increases potassium excretion excessively","It causes rapid clot formation","It raises the GFR abnormally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states aspirin is 100% excreted by kidneys, so in CKD it causes aspirin toxicity and prolongs bleeding time.  Why B wrong: Excess potassium excretion is not the stated concern.  Why C wrong: Aspirin inhibits clotting; it does not cause rapid clot formation.  Why D wrong: It does not abnormally raise GFR."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Hypermagnesemia in CKD occurs due to:","options":["Decreased renal excretion of magnesium","Excessive dietary magnesium restriction","Increased magnesium loss in urine","Overuse of loop diuretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypermagnesemia occurs due to decreased renal excretion of magnesium.  Why B wrong: It results from retention, not restriction.  Why C wrong: Increased urinary loss would lower, not raise, magnesium.  Why D wrong: Loop diuretic overuse is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In hypermagnesemia, the nurse should monitor for neuromuscular impairment such as:","options":["Reduced or absent deep tendon reflexes and weak or absent voluntary muscle movements","Hyperreflexia and muscle twitching","Seizures and tremors","Spastic paralysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists reduced/absent deep tendon reflexes and weak/absent voluntary muscle movements.  Why B wrong: Hyperreflexia is opposite to the depressed reflexes seen in hypermagnesemia.  Why C wrong: Seizures/tremors are not the listed features.  Why D wrong: Spastic paralysis is not described; hypermagnesemia causes weakness/depressed reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The antidote for hypermagnesemia is:","options":["Calcium gluconate 10%","Sodium bicarbonate","Lactulose","Frusemide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the antidote of hypermagnesemia is calcium gluconate 10%.  Why B wrong: Sodium bicarbonate treats metabolic acidosis.  Why C wrong: Lactulose is for GI ammonia/restless syndrome.  Why D wrong: Frusemide is a loop diuretic used in hyperkalaemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which medications should be avoided in hypermagnesemia because they contain magnesium?","options":["Antacids and laxatives","ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers","Iron and folic acid","Insulin and dextrose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid medication that contains magnesium such as antacids and laxatives.  Why B wrong: These do not contain magnesium.  Why C wrong: Iron/folic acid are used for anaemia and are not magnesium-containing.  Why D wrong: Insulin/dextrose are used for hyperkalaemia and contain no magnesium."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Anaemia in CKD is primarily caused by:","options":["Decreased secretion of erythropoietin hormone by damaged nephrons, reducing RBC production","Excess iron in the blood","Overproduction of erythropoietin","Rapid RBC maturation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anaemia is due to decreased secretion of erythropoietin by damaged nephrons, resulting in decreased RBC production.  Why B wrong: Iron excess is not the cause; iron is often needed.  Why C wrong: Erythropoietin is decreased, not overproduced.  Why D wrong: RBC production is decreased, not accelerated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which hormone preparation is administered to promote maturity of RBCs in CKD anaemia?","options":["Epoetin (artificial erythropoietin)","Insulin","Parathyroid hormone","Aldosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer epoetin (artificial erythropoietin) to promote maturity of RBCs.  Why B wrong: Insulin is used for hyperkalaemia, not RBC maturation.  Why C wrong: PTH is elevated in CKD and causes bone demineralization, not RBC maturity.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone regulates sodium/potassium, not RBC maturity."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which vitamin is administered for the maturation of RBCs in CKD anaemia?","options":["Folic acid","Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer folic acid for maturation of RBCs.  Why B wrong: Vitamin K is for clotting, not RBC maturation here.  Why C wrong: Vitamin C is not the stated agent.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D relates to calcium absorption, not RBC maturation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why is iron given by IV or IM route rather than orally in CKD anaemia?","options":["Orally, iron is not absorbed well in CKD due to the disease process","Oral iron causes hyperkalaemia","IV/IM iron is cheaper","Oral iron raises the GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states iron is given IV or IM because orally iron is not absorbed well in CKD due to the disease process.  Why B wrong: Oral iron does not cause hyperkalaemia.  Why C wrong: Cost is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Route of iron does not affect GFR."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In hyperphosphatemia of CKD, as the phosphorus level rises the calcium level drops, leading to:","options":["Stimulation of PTH hormone causing bone demineralization","Suppression of PTH and bone strengthening","Increased erythropoietin","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rising phosphorus lowers calcium, stimulating PTH hormone and causing bone demineralization.  Why B wrong: PTH is stimulated, not suppressed, and bone is demineralized.  Why C wrong: Erythropoietin is unrelated to this cascade.  Why D wrong: CKD causes metabolic acidosis, not alkalosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which phosphorus binder is noted to be prescribed usually for non-CKD patients?","options":["Sevelamer hydrochloride","Calcium carbonate","Kayexalate","Lactulose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Sevelamer hydrochloride is prescribed usually for non-CKD patients.  Why B wrong: Calcium carbonate is the one most commonly used in CKD.  Why C wrong: Kayexalate is a potassium binder.  Why D wrong: Lactulose is used for GI ammonia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which phosphorus binder is most commonly used in CKD?","options":["Calcium carbonate phosphorus binder","Sevelamer hydrochloride","Sodium bicarbonate","Frusemide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states calcium carbonate phosphorus binder is most commonly used in CKD.  Why B wrong: Sevelamer is stated to be used usually for non-CKD patients.  Why C wrong: Sodium bicarbonate treats acidosis, not phosphate.  Why D wrong: Frusemide is a diuretic, not a phosphate binder."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A client with hyperphosphatemia in CKD should be taught to:","options":["Avoid foods that have a high phosphorus level","Increase intake of dairy and organ meats","Take antacids containing magnesium","Increase cola and processed foods"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid foods that have a high phosphorus level.  Why B wrong: Dairy/organ meats are high in phosphorus.  Why C wrong: Magnesium antacids are avoided in CKD (hypermagnesemia).  Why D wrong: Cola/processed foods are high in phosphorus."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Metabolic acidosis in CKD develops because the diseased kidney is unable to:","options":["Excrete hydrogen ions","Absorb sodium","Produce erythropoietin","Activate vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the diseased kidney is unable to excrete hydrogen ions, resulting in metabolic acidosis.  Why B wrong: Sodium absorption is not the stated mechanism of acidosis.  Why C wrong: Erythropoietin failure causes anaemia, not acidosis.  Why D wrong: Failure to activate vitamin D causes hypocalcaemia, not acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which medication is administered to correct metabolic acidosis in CKD?","options":["Sodium bicarbonate","Calcium gluconate","Lactulose","Epoetin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer sodium bicarbonate as per order.  Why B wrong: Calcium gluconate is for hyperkalaemia/hypermagnesemia.  Why C wrong: Lactulose lowers ammonia.  Why D wrong: Epoetin treats anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Restless syndrome in CKD occurs due to neurological impairment or due to increased serum levels of:","options":["Ammonia and BUN","Calcium and vitamin D","Erythropoietin","Bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states restless syndrome is due to neurological impairment or increased serum ammonia and BUN.  Why B wrong: Calcium/vitamin D are typically low in CKD, not the cause here.  Why C wrong: Erythropoietin is decreased in CKD.  Why D wrong: Bicarbonate is decreased (acidosis), not the cause of restless syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which medication is administered for restless syndrome in CKD?","options":["Lactulose","Kayexalate","Sodium bicarbonate","Iron IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer lactulose as per order (it lowers ammonia).  Why B wrong: Kayexalate treats hyperkalaemia.  Why C wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is for acidosis.  Why D wrong: IV iron treats anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Uremic syndrome is best defined as a condition characterized by:","options":["Accumulation of nitrogenous waste products in the blood due to the kidney's inability to filter them out","Excess erythropoietin in the blood","Depletion of urea in the blood","Excessive urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines uremic syndrome as accumulation of nitrogenous waste products in the blood caused by the kidney's inability to filter them out.  Why B wrong: Erythropoietin is decreased in CKD.  Why C wrong: Urea accumulates, it is not depleted.  Why D wrong: Uremic syndrome (seen in AKI/CKD) is associated with oliguria, not excessive output."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Uremic syndrome is usually seen in which conditions?","options":["AKI and CKD","Diabetes insipidus only","Hypertension only","Anaemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uremic syndrome is usually seen in AKI and CKD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the conditions the source lists for uremic syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which is described as the \"most common\" symptom listed among the signs and symptoms of uremic syndrome?","options":["Pruritis","Fever","Hypertension","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists oliguria, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and pruritis (most common) as symptoms of uremic syndrome.  Why B wrong: Fever is described as the earliest sign in urosepsis, not uremic syndrome.  Why C/D wrong: Hypertension/bradycardia are not the \"most common\" symptom the source highlights here."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which laboratory findings are consistent with uremic syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Presence of protein and RBCs in the blood","Elevated urea","Elevated uric acid, potassium, and magnesium","Decreased blood urea nitrogen","Alteration in the level of consciousness"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A): The source lists presence of protein and RBCs in the blood.  Why correct (B): Elevated urea is listed.  Why correct (C): Elevated uric acid, potassium, and magnesium are listed.  Why correct (E): Alteration in level of consciousness is listed.  Why D wrong: BUN/urea is elevated in uremic syndrome, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Hypocalcaemia in CKD results from a high phosphorus level and the diseased kidney's inability to:","options":["Activate vitamin D into its functioning form","Excrete potassium","Reabsorb sodium","Secrete renin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypocalcaemia results from high phosphorus and inability of the diseased kidney to activate vitamin D in functioning form.  Why B wrong: Potassium excretion issues cause hyperkalaemia, not hypocalcaemia.  Why C wrong: Sodium reabsorption is not the stated mechanism.  Why D wrong: Renin secretion relates to hypertension, not hypocalcaemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The absence of activated vitamin D in CKD leads to hypocalcaemia by causing:","options":["Poor absorption of calcium from the intestinal tract","Excessive urinary calcium excretion","Bone overgrowth","Increased dietary calcium intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the absence of vitamin D causes poor absorption from the intestinal tract.  Why B wrong: The mechanism is poor gut absorption, not urinary excretion.  Why C wrong: Low calcium/high PTH causes bone demineralization, not overgrowth.  Why D wrong: Absence of vitamin D reduces absorption, unrelated to intake amount."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Nursing management of hypocalcaemia in CKD includes:","options":["Monitor serum calcium level and administer calcium supplements as per order","Restrict all calcium indefinitely","Administer potassium supplements","Give magnesium-based antacids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs to monitor serum calcium and administer calcium supplements as per order.  Why B wrong: Calcium is supplemented, not restricted, in hypocalcaemia.  Why C wrong: Potassium supplements are dangerous in CKD.  Why D wrong: Magnesium antacids are avoided in CKD."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"For a CKD client with hypovolemia, which parameters should the nurse monitor?","options":["All vital parameters, daily weight, and intake & output","Only urine specific gravity","Only pupillary reflex","Serum ammonia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs monitoring of all vital parameters, daily weight, and intake & output.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the comprehensive parameters the source lists for hypovolemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"For hypovolemia in CKD, the nurse should monitor for electrolyte imbalances and dehydration and:","options":["Offer replacement therapy as per order","Restrict all fluids permanently","Administer diuretics aggressively","Withhold all intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor for electrolyte imbalances and dehydration and offer replacement therapy as per order.  Why B/D wrong: Hypovolemia requires replacement, not withholding fluids.  Why C wrong: Aggressive diuresis would worsen hypovolemia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why is the possibility of infection increased in CKD clients?","options":["Poor immune system, possible malnutrition, and the dialysis process","Excess erythropoietin","High vitamin D levels","Overhydration only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states infection risk increases due to poor immune system, possible malnutrition, and dialysis process.  Why B wrong: Erythropoietin is decreased in CKD.  Why C wrong: Vitamin D is decreased (inactivated), not high.  Why D wrong: Overhydration alone is not the stated reason for infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Key nursing measures to prevent and manage infection in a CKD client include:","options":["Monitor signs of infection, maintain aseptic technique, and administer antibiotics as per order","Avoid all handwashing","Reuse dressings to reduce cost","Discontinue monitoring once afebrile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs to monitor signs of infection, maintain aseptic technique, and administer antibiotics as per order.  Why B/C/D wrong: These violate infection-control principles and are not supported by the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the full form of the MDRD formula?","options":["Modification of Diet in Renal Disease","Measurement of Dialysis Renal Data","Maximum Daily Renal Dose","Modified Diuretic Response Diagram"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MDRD = Modification of Diet in Renal Disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated expansion of MDRD."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The MDRD formula is widely used for the estimation of:","options":["GFR (glomerular filtration rate)","Serum sodium","Blood pressure","Urine specific gravity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MDRD formula is widely used for estimation of GFR.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not what the MDRD formula estimates."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which parameters are used in the MDRD formula? (Select all that apply)","options":["Age","Gender","Serum creatinine","African descent","Serum potassium"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists parameters as age, gender, serum creatinine, and African descent.  Why E wrong: Serum potassium is not listed among the MDRD parameters."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Urosepsis is an infection of the urinary tract caused by which type of organisms?","options":["Gram-negative bacteria","Gram-positive bacteria","Fungi only","Viruses only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states urosepsis is an infection of gram-negative bacteria of the urinary tract.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies gram-negative bacteria, not gram-positive, fungi, or viruses."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The most common causative organism of urosepsis is:","options":["E. coli (Escherichia coli)","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common causative organism is E. coli / Escherichia coli.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common organism named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A major problem posed by the bacteria causing urosepsis is their tendency to:","options":["Develop resistant strains","Produce excess erythropoietin","Form calcium stones","Cause metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a major problem is that these bacteria develop resistant strains.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated problems of urosepsis bacteria."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the most common and earliest sign/manifestation of urosepsis?","options":["Fever","Haematuria","Anuria","Bone pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fever is the most common and earliest sign of manifestation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the earliest sign of urosepsis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the correct nursing sequence for managing urosepsis?","options":["Obtain a urine sample for culture and sensitivity (C/S), then administer antibiotics as per order","Administer antibiotics first, then obtain the culture","Restrict all antibiotics","Avoid urine testing entirely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to obtain a urine sample for C/S, then administer antibiotics as per order.  Why B wrong: Culture should be obtained before antibiotics to avoid altering results.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Urethritis is defined as inflammation of the urethra and usually occurs together with:","options":["Cystitis (inflammation of the bladder)","Nephritis","Prostatitis only","Hepatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states urethritis usually occurs with cystitis (inflammation of bladder).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically pairs urethritis with cystitis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The most common cause of urethritis is:","options":["Sexually transmitted infections","Renal calculi","Hypertension","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause is sexually transmitted infections.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In males, urethritis is most commonly caused by:","options":["Gonorrhoea and chlamydial infection","Poor hygiene only","Immobilization","High purine diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in males it is due to gonorrhoea and chlamydial infection.  Why B wrong: Poor hygiene/UTI is the female cause listed.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to urethritis causes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In females, urethritis is commonly due to:","options":["Poor hygiene or UTI","Gonorrhoea and chlamydia only","Hyperparathyroidism","Gout"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in females it is due to unhygiene or UTI.  Why B wrong: Gonorrhoea/chlamydia is listed as the male cause.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to female urethritis causes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which symptom of urethritis is specific to males per the source?","options":["Mucopurulent discharge from the penis","Lower abdominal pain or discomfort","Flank pain radiating to testicles","Dirty white urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states males have mucopurulent discharge from the penis, while females have lower abdominal pain/discomfort.  Why B wrong: Lower abdominal pain/discomfort is described in females.  Why C wrong: Flank pain to testicles is a renal colic feature, not urethritis.  Why D wrong: Dirty white urine is a struvite stone feature."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of urethritis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain or burning sensation during urination","Frequency and urgency","Difficulty in voiding","Mucopurulent discharge (males) / lower abdominal pain (females)","Bruit over the forearm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists pain/burning during urination, frequency and urgency, difficulty in voiding, mucopurulent penile discharge in males, and lower abdominal pain/discomfort in females.  Why E wrong: A forearm bruit relates to an AV fistula/shunt, not urethritis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Nursing care for urethritis emphasizes precautions during sexual intercourse, especially:","options":["With a new partner","During menstruation only","After exercise","When febrile only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states precaution should be taken while engaging in sexual intercourse, specially with a new partner.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific circumstances are not stated by the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Benign Prostatic Hypertrophy (BPH) is also known as:","options":["Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia","Prostate carcinoma","Prostatitis","Urosepsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BPH is also known as Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia.  Why B wrong: Carcinoma is malignant; BPH is benign.  Why C wrong: Prostatitis is inflammation of the prostate, a separate entity.  Why D wrong: Urosepsis is a urinary infection."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"BPH is characterized by:","options":["Slow/gradual enlargement of the prostate gland with hypertrophy or hyperplasia of normal tissues","Rapid malignant growth of prostate cells","Atrophy of the prostate","Complete absence of the prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BPH is slow/gradual enlargement of the prostate gland with hypertrophy or hyperplasia of normal tissues.  Why B wrong: BPH is benign and gradual, not malignant/rapid.  Why C/D wrong: BPH involves enlargement, not atrophy or absence."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In BPH, the enlarged prostate causes urinary symptoms by:","options":["Compressing the urethra, resulting in partial or complete obstruction","Increasing the size of the bladder","Destroying the kidneys directly","Blocking the ureters at the pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the enlargement compresses the urethra, resulting in partial or complete obstruction.  Why B/C/D wrong: The mechanism is urethral compression, not the bladder, kidney, or ureter events described."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which finding is identified as an EARLY sign of BPH?","options":["Diminished size and force of the urinary stream","Postvoidal dribbling","Complete anuria","Gross haematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diminished size and force of urinary stream is an early sign.  Why B wrong: Postvoidal dribbling is listed as a later sign.  Why C/D wrong: These are not identified as the early sign."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Postvoidal dribbling from overflow incontinence is described in BPH as a:","options":["Later sign","Early sign","Cardinal sign","Diagnostic test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states postvoidal dribbling from overflow incontinence is a later sign.  Why B wrong: The early sign is diminished stream size/force.  Why C wrong: \"Cardinal sign\" terminology is used for renal colic, not BPH.  Why D wrong: It is a sign, not a test."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In the context of BPH signs, \"Prostatitis\" refers to:","options":["Inflammation of the prostate gland","Enlargement of the bladder","Kidney stone formation","Cancer of the prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states prostatitis is inflammation of the prostate gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated definitions."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are urinary signs and symptoms of BPH? (Select all that apply)","options":["Urinary urgency, frequency, and nocturia","Feeling of incomplete bladder emptying","Urinary retention and bladder distension","Haematuria, urinary stasis, dysuria, and bladder pain","Increased force of the urinary stream"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists urinary urgency/frequency/nocturia, feeling of incomplete emptying, urinary retention and bladder distension, haematuria, urinary stasis, dysuria, and bladder pain.  Why E wrong: BPH causes diminished (not increased) size and force of the urinary stream."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The primary nursing care measure to relieve bladder distension in BPH is:","options":["Catheterisation to drain the bladder","Fluid restriction to below 500 ml/day","Application of heat to the flank","Administration of aspirin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states catheterisation to drain the bladder to prevent distension and general care.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated BPH nursing care for distension."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Laser prostatectomy\" for BPH refers to:","options":["Ablation of the enlarged prostate","Removal via an incision between scrotum and rectum","Removal through a low abdominal incision","Removal via the urethra with a resectoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states laser prostatectomy is ablation of the enlarged prostate.  Why B wrong: That describes perineal prostatectomy.  Why C wrong: That describes retropubic prostatectomy.  Why D wrong: That describes transurethral resection."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In perineal prostatectomy, prostate tissue is removed through an incision located:","options":["Between the scrotum and rectum","Through the urethra","Through a low abdominal incision","Through the bladder mucosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states perineal prostatectomy removes prostate tissue through an incision between the scrotum and rectum.  Why B wrong: Urethral removal is transurethral resection.  Why C wrong: Low abdominal incision is retropubic prostatectomy.  Why D wrong: Bladder mucosa incision describes supra-pubic prostatectomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A key disadvantage of perineal prostatectomy is:","options":["High risk of infection due to proximity of the portal of exits","The bladder must always be incised","It requires no anaesthesia","It cures BPH permanently with no complications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states perineal prostatectomy carries high risk of infection due to proximity of the portal of exits.  Why B wrong: Bladder incision is not the disadvantage described for the perineal approach.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated by the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In retropubic prostatectomy, hypertrophied prostatic tissue is removed through a low abdominal incision, and importantly:","options":["The bladder is not incised","The bladder mucosa is incised","The prostate is left intact","The urethra is removed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in retropubic prostatectomy the bladder is not incised.  Why B wrong: Bladder mucosa incision is the supra-pubic approach.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the described procedure."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Supra-pubic prostatectomy involves removal of hypertrophic prostatic tissue through:","options":["A lower abdominal and bladder mucosa incision","An incision between scrotum and rectum","The urethra using a resectoscope","A laser ablation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states supra-pubic prostatectomy removes tissue through a lower abdominal and bladder mucosa incision.  Why B wrong: That is perineal prostatectomy.  Why C wrong: That is transurethral resection.  Why D wrong: That is laser prostatectomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) involves:","options":["Removal of the benign prostate through the urethra using an instrument called a resectoscope","Removal through a scrotal-rectal incision","Laser ablation","Removal through a low abdominal incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TURP removes benign prostate through the urethra using a resectoscope.  Why B wrong: Scrotal-rectal incision is the perineal approach.  Why C wrong: Laser ablation is laser prostatectomy.  Why D wrong: Low abdominal incision is retropubic prostatectomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which prostate surgery is stated to be \"most commonly used for BPH\"?","options":["Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)","Perineal prostatectomy","Supra-pubic prostatectomy","Retropubic prostatectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TURP is most commonly used for BPH.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most commonly used approach."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Transurethral electro-vaporization of the prostate\" achieves removal of prostate tissue through:","options":["An electrical cut","A laser beam","A scrotal incision","Freezing (cryotherapy)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states transurethral electro-vaporization is through an electrical cut.  Why B wrong: Laser is used in laser prostatectomy.  Why C wrong: Scrotal incision is perineal prostatectomy.  Why D wrong: Cryotherapy is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Bladder trauma is best defined as:","options":["An injury of the bladder","Inflammation of the urethra","A benign prostate enlargement","A urinary tract infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bladder trauma is an injury of the bladder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe urethritis, BPH, and UTI respectively."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Blunt trauma causes bladder injury primarily by:","options":["Compression of the abdominal wall and bladder","Direct penetration of the bladder wall","Chemical erosion","Ischemia from clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blunt trauma causes compression of the abdominal wall and bladder.  Why B wrong: Penetration describes penetrating wounds (gunshot/stabbing), not blunt trauma.  Why C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Penetrating wounds to the bladder occur as a result of:","options":["Gunshot and stabbing","Blunt abdominal compression","Prolonged catheterisation","Renal calculi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states penetrating wounds occur as a result of gunshot and stabbing.  Why B wrong: Compression is blunt trauma.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated penetrating mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is described as the most common cause of bladder trauma?","options":["Fractured pelvis causing a bone fragment to puncture the bladder","Gunshot wound","Prolonged catheter use","Bladder cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a fractured pelvis causing a bone fragment to puncture the bladder is the most common cause of bladder trauma.  Why B wrong: Gunshot is a penetrating cause but not identified as the most common.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Signs and symptoms of bladder trauma include:","options":["Anuria, haematuria, and pain","Polyuria and dilute urine","Excessive thirst only","Bruit and thrill"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states signs and symptoms include anuria, haematuria, and pain.  Why B/C wrong: These are not the listed features of bladder trauma.  Why D wrong: Bruit/thrill relates to AV fistula."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In bladder trauma, pain below the level of the umbilicus can radiate to the:","options":["Shoulders","Testicles","Thighs","Neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pain below the level of the umbilicus can radiate to the shoulders.  Why B wrong: Radiation to testicles is a renal colic feature.  Why C wrong: Radiation to thighs is a ureteric colic feature.  Why D wrong: The neck is not the stated radiation site."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In bladder trauma, why should urinary catheterisation be avoided until the extent of injury is determined?","options":["Until the extension of injury is found out by retrograde ureterogram","Because catheters cause hyperkalaemia","Because catheters always cause stones","Because it raises the GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid urinary catheterisation till the extension of injury is found out by retrograde ureterogram.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In bladder trauma, the nurse should monitor haematuria, bleeding, and signs of:","options":["Shock","Hyperglycaemia","Alkalosis","Hypercalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor haematuria, bleeding, and sign of shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated monitoring foci for bladder trauma."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Urolithiasis\" is defined as:","options":["Formation of urinary calculi usually anywhere in the urinary tract","Formation of stones only in the kidneys","Inflammation of the bladder","Enlargement of the prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines urolithiasis as formation of urinary calculi usually anywhere in the urinary tract.  Why B wrong: Stones specifically in the kidneys is nephrolithiasis.  Why C/D wrong: These describe cystitis and BPH."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Nephrolithiasis\" refers to:","options":["Formation of stones in the kidneys, usually in the renal parenchyma","Stones anywhere in the urinary tract","Inflammation of the urethra","Bladder injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines nephrolithiasis as formation of stones in the kidneys, usually in the renal parenchyma.  Why B wrong: Stones anywhere in the tract is urolithiasis.  Why C/D wrong: These describe urethritis and bladder trauma."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"When a stone occludes the ureters and blocks the flow of urine, the ureters dilate, causing:","options":["Hydroureter","Hydronephrosis immediately","Urethritis","Prostatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an occluding stone causes ureteric dilation resulting in hydroureter.  Why B wrong: Hydronephrosis develops later if the obstruction is not resolved, not immediately at this stage.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated inflammatory conditions."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"If a urinary obstruction from a stone is not resolved, urinary stasis leads to infection, impairment of renal function, and ultimately:","options":["Hydronephrosis and irreversible renal damage","Spontaneous resolution","Increased GFR","Bladder cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states unresolved obstruction causes hydronephrosis and irreversible renal damage.  Why B/C wrong: These contradict the stated worsening course.  Why D wrong: Bladder cancer is not the stated outcome."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are dietary/metabolic causes of renal calculi? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diet high in calcium, vitamin D, protein, and high purine foods","Dehydration, obstruction, and urinary stasis","UTI, prolonged catheterisation, and immobilization","Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism","Low serum uric acid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists high calcium/vitamin D/protein/purine diet; dehydration, obstruction, and urinary stasis; UTI, prolonged catheterisation, and immobilization; and hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism.  Why E wrong: The source lists elevated (not low) uric acid level in gout as a cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which condition, associated with gout, is a cause of renal calculi?","options":["Elevated uric acid level","Low uric acid level","Low calcium level","Low protein diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists elevated uric acid level in gout as a cause of renal calculi.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are opposite to or inconsistent with the stated causes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Renal colic,\" described as the cardinal and early sign of renal calculi, originates in the lumbar region and radiates:","options":["Around the side and down to the testicles in men and to the bladder in women","To the shoulders in all patients","Only to the neck","Only to the feet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states renal colic radiates around the side and down to the testicles in men and to the bladder in women.  Why B wrong: Shoulder radiation is a bladder trauma feature.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated radiation patterns."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Ureteric colic\" from renal calculi radiates toward the:","options":["Genitalia and thighs","Shoulders","Neck and jaw","Upper back only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ureteric colic radiates toward the genitalia and thighs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated radiation sites for ureteric colic."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which pain description is identified as the \"cardinal sign\" of renal calculi?","options":["Sharp severe pain of sudden onset","Dull continuous ache","Painless haematuria","Mild intermittent cramp"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sharp severe pain, sudden onset is a cardinal sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described cardinal pain of renal calculi."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which additional signs and symptoms accompany an episode of acute pain in renal calculi? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Pallor and diaphoresis","Gross haematuria","Urinary frequency and signs of UTI such as temperature elevation","Bradycardia and hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists nausea/vomiting, pallor/diaphoresis during acute pain, gross haematuria, urinary frequency, and signs of UTI such as temperature elevation.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia/hypothermia are not listed; temperature elevation (not hypothermia) is described."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A client with renal calculi should be encouraged to increase fluid intake up to how much per day to facilitate passage of the stone and prevent infection?","options":["3000 ml/day","1000 ml/day","500 ml/day","6000 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to encourage fluid intake up to 3000 ml/day to facilitate passage of stone through urination and prevent infection.  Why B/C wrong: These volumes are below the stated 3000 ml/day.  Why D wrong: 6000 ml/day exceeds the stated recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which comfort measure for renal calculi is appropriate, and which should be avoided?","options":["Provide warm baths and heat to the flank area; massage therapy should be avoided","Provide cold packs; encourage vigorous massage","Avoid all heat; provide massage","Restrict fluids and apply heat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to provide warm baths and heat to the flank area, and that massage therapy should be avoided.  Why B/C wrong: These reverse the correct instructions (massage should be avoided; warmth is provided).  Why D wrong: Fluids are encouraged (3000 ml/day), not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Dietary restriction instructions for renal calculi depend on:","options":["The type of stone","The client's age only","The season of the year","Body weight only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to instruct the client about diet restriction, which depends on the type of stone.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated determinants of the diet restriction."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which is the most common type of renal stone, accounting for about 80%?","options":["Calcium oxalate stones","Uric acid stones","Struvite stones","Cystine stones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states calcium oxalate stones are the most common type, about 80%.  Why B wrong: Uric acid stones are the second most common (~10%).  Why C wrong: Struvite stones are infection stones, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Cystine stones are rare (~2%)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which additional calcium-based stone, described as an \"alkaline stone,\" may also form?","options":["Calcium phosphate","Calcium oxalate only","Cystine","Struvite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states calcium phosphate may also form stones and is an alkaline stone.  Why B wrong: The question asks for the additional stone type besides oxalate.  Why C/D wrong: Cystine and struvite are separate stone categories."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What are the causes of calcium oxalate stones?","options":["High calcium diet and hyperparathyroidism","High purine diet and gout","Long-standing UTI","A protein defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cause of calcium oxalate stones is high calcium diet and hyperparathyroidism.  Why B wrong: High purine diet/gout causes uric acid stones.  Why C wrong: Long-standing UTI causes struvite stones.  Why D wrong: A protein defect relates to cystine stones."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The urine colour and pH change associated with calcium oxalate stones is:","options":["Black/dark brown with decreased pH of urine","Yellow/reddish brown with acidic urine","Dirty white","Clear with increased pH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states calcium oxalate stones cause black/dark brown urine with decreased pH.  Why B wrong: Yellow/reddish brown, acidic urine describes uric acid stones.  Why C wrong: Dirty white urine describes struvite stones.  Why D wrong: This does not match the stated finding."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A specific dietary measure to prevent calcium oxalate stones is to decrease intake of oxalate-containing foods such as:","options":["Spinach, chocolate, almond, and peanut","Red meat, mushroom, and wine","Milk, meats, egg, and cheese","Bananas and oranges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists spinach, chocolate, almond, and peanut as oxalate-containing foods to decrease.  Why B wrong: Red meat, mushroom, and wine are restricted for uric acid stones.  Why C wrong: Milk, meats, egg, and cheese are avoided for cystine stones.  Why D wrong: These are high-potassium foods, not the listed oxalate foods."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Uric acid stones are the second most common type of renal stone, accounting for about:","options":["10%","80%","2%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uric acid stones are the second most common, about 10%.  Why B wrong: 80% is calcium oxalate.  Why C wrong: 2% is cystine.  Why D wrong: 50% is not a stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What causes uric acid stones?","options":["High purine diet (red meats, wines, fish), excessive protein, gout, and inflammatory bowel diseases","High calcium diet and hyperparathyroidism","Long-standing UTI","A protein (cystine) defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uric acid stones are caused by high purine diet (red meats, wines, fish), excessive protein, gout, and inflammatory bowel diseases.  Why B wrong: High calcium/hyperparathyroidism causes calcium oxalate stones.  Why C wrong: UTI causes struvite stones.  Why D wrong: A protein defect relates to cystine stones."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The urine appearance associated with uric acid stones is:","options":["Yellow/reddish brown, acidic urine","Black/dark brown, decreased pH","Dirty white","Clear alkaline urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uric acid stones cause yellow/reddish brown, acidic urine.  Why B wrong: Black/dark brown with decreased pH describes calcium oxalate stones.  Why C wrong: Dirty white describes struvite stones.  Why D wrong: This does not match the stated finding."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The specific dietary measure for uric acid stones is a low purine diet, avoiding:","options":["Red meat, mushroom, and wine","Spinach, chocolate, almond, and peanut","Milk, egg, and cheese","Bananas and oranges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states patients need to take a low purine diet, avoiding red meat, mushroom, and wine.  Why B wrong: Spinach/chocolate/almond/peanut are oxalate foods (calcium oxalate stones).  Why C wrong: Milk/egg/cheese are avoided for cystine stones.  Why D wrong: These are not the listed purine foods."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Struvite stones occur due to:","options":["Long-time urinary tract infections (UTI)","High calcium diet","High purine diet","A protein defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states struvite stones occur due to long-time urinary tract infections.  Why B wrong: High calcium diet causes calcium oxalate stones.  Why C wrong: High purine diet causes uric acid stones.  Why D wrong: A protein defect relates to cystine stones."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Struvite stones are also known as:","options":["Infection renal stones","Alkaline stones","Protein stones","Oxalate stones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states struvite stones are also known as infection renal stones.  Why B wrong: Alkaline stone refers to calcium phosphate.  Why C wrong: Protein relates to cystine.  Why D wrong: Oxalate stones are calcium oxalate."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Struvite stones grow rapidly and characteristically form a:","options":["Staghorn calculus","Small round smooth stone","Single grain-sized deposit","Cystine crystal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this type of stone grows rapidly, forming a staghorn calculus.  Why B/C wrong: These do not match the rapidly growing staghorn description.  Why D wrong: Cystine crystals are a separate stone type."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The characteristic urine colour of struvite stones is:","options":["Dirty white","Black/dark brown","Yellow/reddish brown","Bright red"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states struvite stone urine colour is dirty white.  Why B wrong: Black/dark brown is calcium oxalate.  Why C wrong: Yellow/reddish brown is uric acid.  Why D wrong: Bright red is not a listed colour."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Cystine stones are described as a protein and occur in about what percentage of cases?","options":["About 2%, rarely","About 10%","About 80%","About 50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cystine is a protein, about 2% of chance, rarely.  Why B wrong: 10% is uric acid stones.  Why C wrong: 80% is calcium oxalate.  Why D wrong: 50% is not a stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The specific dietary measure for cystine stones is to avoid:","options":["Milk, meats, egg, and cheese","Spinach, chocolate, almond, and peanut","Red meat, mushroom, and wine","Bananas and oranges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states patients with cystine stones need to avoid milk, meats, egg, and cheese.  Why B wrong: Those are oxalate foods (calcium oxalate).  Why C wrong: Those are purine foods (uric acid).  Why D wrong: These are not the listed cystine-restricted foods."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which procedure is used to treat a stone located in the lower ureter or bladder?","options":["Cystoscopy","Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL)","Haemodialysis","AV fistula creation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cystoscopy is used for a stone in the lower ureter or bladder.  Why B wrong: ESWL is a non-invasive method that crushes stones with ultrasonic waves; not specified for lower ureter/bladder retrieval here.  Why C/D wrong: These are dialysis-related, not stone treatments."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL) is best characterized as:","options":["A non-invasive method using ultrasonic waves to crush the stone into pieces that are excreted via urine","An invasive method requiring a guide wire","A surgical incision through the flank","A dialysis technique"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ESWL is a non-invasive method using ultrasonic waves to crush the stone into pieces, excreted via urine.  Why B wrong: The guide-wire invasive method is percutaneous lithotripsy.  Why C/D wrong: ESWL is non-invasive and unrelated to dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"During ESWL, which imaging modality helps visualise the stones?","options":["Fluoroscopy","MRI","PET scan","Bone scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fluoroscopy is helping to visualise the stones.  Why B/C/D wrong: These modalities are not mentioned for ESWL."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Before an ESWL procedure, the patient should be kept NPO (nil per oral) for how long?","options":["8 hours","2 hours","24 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states before the procedure the patient should keep NPO for 8 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 8-hour NPO requirement."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"After an ESWL procedure, the nurse should instruct the client to:","options":["Increase fluid intake for excretion of stone pieces by urine and monitor for haematuria","Restrict all fluids","Remain NPO for another 24 hours","Avoid urinating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states after the procedure to increase fluid intake for excretion of stone pieces by urine and monitor for haematuria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated post-procedure care."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Percutaneous lithotripsy differs from ESWL in that it is:","options":["An invasive method done under fluoroscopy guidance, where a guide wire is inserted and ultrasonic waves break the stones","A completely non-invasive method","A dialysis procedure","A method that uses only heat therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states percutaneous lithotripsy is an invasive method done under fluoroscopy guidance, with a guide wire inserted and ultrasonic waves delivered to break the stones.  Why B wrong: ESWL, not percutaneous lithotripsy, is non-invasive.  Why C/D wrong: It is not dialysis or heat-only therapy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Percutaneous lithotripsy can be used to treat stones in the:","options":["Ureter, bladder, and kidneys","Bladder only","Kidneys only","Urethra only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states percutaneous lithotripsy is used for ureter, bladder, and kidney stones.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies all three sites, not a single one."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Haemodialysis is best defined as the process of:","options":["Cleaning the client's blood by filtering/diffusing waste from one compartment to another through a semi-permeable membrane","Filtering waste in the peritoneal cavity","Removing the kidney surgically","Draining the bladder with a catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states haemodialysis cleans the client's blood by filtering/diffusing the waste product of blood from one compartment to another through a semi-permeable membrane.  Why B wrong: That describes peritoneal dialysis, not haemodialysis.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated procedures."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the indication for dialysis in terms of kidney function?","options":["When GFR reaches 10–15 ml/hr or 80% loss of kidney's function","When GFR reaches 50 ml/min","When GFR reaches 125 ml/min","At 20% loss of kidney function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the indication of dialysis is when GFR reaches 10–15 ml/hr or 80% loss of kidney's function.  Why B/C wrong: These are near-normal values, not dialysis indications.  Why D wrong: 20% loss is far below the stated 80% threshold."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are functions of haemodialysis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Removes by-products of protein metabolism such as urea, creatinine, and uric acid","Cleanses the blood and removes accumulated waste product","Removes excess accumulated body fluids","Maintains or restores the buffer system of the body and corrects electrolyte imbalances","Produces erythropoietin hormone"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists removal of protein-metabolism by-products (urea, creatinine, uric acid), cleansing blood/removing waste, removing excess fluids, maintaining/restoring the buffer system, and correcting electrolyte imbalances.  Why E wrong: Producing erythropoietin is a kidney endocrine function not performed by dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In the principles of haemodialysis, \"diffusion\" is:","options":["Movement of particles from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration","Movement of particles from lower to higher concentration","Movement of fluids due to artificial pressure only","Passage of only proteins across the membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines diffusion as movement of particles from higher to lower concentration.  Why B wrong: Lower-to-higher movement (of particles) describes the osmosis definition given.  Why C wrong: Fluid movement by artificial pressure is described under osmosis in the source.  Why D wrong: Proteins are too large to pass; they do not diffuse across."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"According to the source, \"osmosis\" in haemodialysis is defined as:","options":["Movement of particles from an area of lower concentration to higher concentration through a permeable membrane","Movement of particles from higher to lower concentration","Removal of large proteins","Elevation of body temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines osmosis as movement of particles from lower to higher concentration through a permeable membrane (and movement of fluids due to artificial pressure).  Why B wrong: Higher-to-lower movement is the diffusion definition.  Why C wrong: Removal of large proteins is not possible (they are too large).  Why D wrong: Temperature elevation relates to blood warming, not osmosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In ultrafiltration during haemodialysis, the semipermeable membrane allows passage of only small/selected particles such as:","options":["Urea, creatinine, uric acid, and water molecules","Proteins and bacteria","Whole blood cells","Large plasma proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ultrafiltration allows passage of only small/selected particles such as urea, creatinine, uric acid, and water molecules.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states proteins, bacteria, and some blood cells are too large to pass through the membrane."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"According to the source, which substances are too large to pass through the dialysis membrane?","options":["Proteins, bacteria, and some blood cells","Urea and creatinine","Water molecules","Uric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states proteins, bacteria, and some blood cells are too large to pass through the membrane.  Why B/C/D wrong: Urea, creatinine, uric acid, and water molecules are small enough to pass through."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"During haemodialysis, a client's temperature may elevate. Why should the nurse be aware of this?","options":["Because of slight warming of blood from the dialysis machine","Because dialysis always causes infection","Because of hyperkalaemia","Because of massage therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client's temperature may elevate because of slight warming of blood from the dialysis machine, so the nurse monitors vital parameters before, during, and after the procedure especially temperature.  Why B wrong: The temperature rise is attributed to machine warming, not automatic infection.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"After dialysis, the nurse should assess for:","options":["Fluid volume deficit","Fluid volume overload only","Hyperkalaemia only","Increased urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess for fluid volume deficit after dialysis.  Why B wrong: Dialysis removes fluid, so the risk is deficit, not overload afterward.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated post-dialysis assessment focus."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"To determine fluid loss with haemodialysis, the nurse should:","options":["Weigh the client before and after the dialysis","Measure only urine output","Check serum sodium only","Assess pupil size"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to weigh the client before and after the dialysis to determine fluid loss.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not directly determine dialysis fluid loss per the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why is heparin added to the dialysis?","options":["To prevent clots from forming in the dialyser or the blood tubing","To increase blood pressure","To warm the blood","To lower serum calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states heparin is added to the dialysis to prevent clots from forming in the dialyser or blood tubing (and the nurse monitors for bleeding).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not heparin's stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"During dialysis, the nurse should monitor for which circulatory complications?","options":["Hypovolemia and shock","Hypertension and bradycardia","Polyuria","Hypercalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor for hypovolemia and shock during dialysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated complications to monitor during dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which medication should be withheld before the dialysis procedure because it can result in hypotension?","options":["Anti-hypertensive medicines","Antibiotics","Iron supplements","Phosphate binders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to withhold anti-hypertensive medicines before the procedure, which can affect the blood pressure or result in hypotension.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated medications to withhold before dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A \"temporary\" haemodialysis access is used:","options":["Temporarily, or until the fistula or graft matures","As the permanent long-term access","Only for CKD patients requiring lifelong dialysis","Only during surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states temporary access is used temporarily or until the fistula or graft matures.  Why B/C wrong: Permanent long-term access is the fistula/graft, not temporary access.  Why D wrong: This is not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are examples of TEMPORARY haemodialysis accesses? (Select all that apply)","options":["Subclavian catheter or sheath (in subclavian vein)","Femoral catheter or sheath (in femoral vein)","Internal AV fistula","Internal AV graft","External shunt"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A, B): The source lists subclavian catheter/sheath (subclavian vein) and femoral catheter/sheath (femoral vein) as temporary accesses.  Why C/D/E wrong: External shunt, internal AV fistula, and internal AV graft are listed as permanent accesses."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The external shunt (a permanent access) is created between which vessels?","options":["Radial artery and basilic vein","Brachial artery and antecubital vein","Femoral artery and femoral vein","Subclavian artery and vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the external shunt is between the radial artery and basilic vein.  Why B wrong: Brachial artery to antecubital vein is the internal AV graft.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated external shunt vessels."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The internal arteriovenous (AV) fistula, a permanent access, is created between:","options":["Radial artery and basilic vein","Brachial artery and antecubital vein","Femoral artery and vein","Subclavian artery and vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the internal AV fistula is between the radial artery and basilic vein.  Why B wrong: Brachial artery to antecubital vein describes the internal AV graft.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated fistula vessels."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The internal arteriovenous (AV) graft, a permanent access, is created between:","options":["Brachial artery and antecubital vein","Radial artery and basilic vein","Femoral artery and vein","Subclavian artery and vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the internal AV graft is between the brachial artery and antecubital vein.  Why B wrong: Radial artery to basilic vein describes the external shunt and internal AV fistula.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated graft vessels."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Nursing care of a haemodialysis catheter insertion site includes assessing for:","options":["Haematoma, bleeding, catheter dislodgement, and infection","Only skin colour","Only temperature of the room","Only blood glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess the insertion site for haematoma, bleeding, catheter dislodgement, and infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated assessment items for the catheter site."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What type of dressing should be maintained at the catheter insertion site?","options":["An occlusion dressing","No dressing","A wet-to-dry dressing","A pressure tourniquet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to maintain an occlusion dressing at the catheter insertion site.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated dressing types for the catheter site."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"For a subclavian vein catheter/sheath, what should be added to the tubing to prevent clots?","options":["Heparin","Insulin","Calcium gluconate","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to add heparin in tubing to prevent clots for the subclavian catheter.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not used to prevent clots in the tubing."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"How should a subclavian vein catheter be managed between dialysis treatments?","options":["The catheters should always be capped except for dialysis treatment","Left open to air continuously","Flushed with dextrose only","Removed after each session"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the catheters always should be capped except for dialysis treatment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A subclavian vein catheter/sheath should be used for up to how long before it is discarded?","options":["Up to 6 weeks","Up to 6 months","Up to 6 days","Indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to use up to 6 weeks, after that it should be discarded.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 6-week limit."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"For a femoral vein catheter, what should the nurse assess in the extremity?","options":["Circulation, temperature, and pulses","Only skin colour","Only muscle strength","Only the bruit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess the extremity for circulation, temperature, and pulses.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the complete stated femoral catheter assessment."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Why does a femoral vein catheter carry a high risk of infection, requiring meticulous skin care?","options":["Because of its proximity to the perineum","Because it is placed in the arm","Because it is always left open","Because heparin is not used"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the femoral vein catheter has high risk of infection because of proximity to the perineum, so meticulous skin care should be done.  Why B wrong: It is placed in the femoral (groin) area, not the arm.  Why C wrong: Being left open is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Heparin is actually added to the tubing."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A client with a femoral vein catheter should be taught NOT to:","options":["Sit up more than 45 degrees or lean forward, because the catheter may kink, displace, and occlude","Move the unaffected leg","Turn the head","Drink fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client on a femoral vein catheter should not sit up more than 45 degrees or lean forward, because the catheter may kink, displace, and occlude.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not restricted by the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"An external arteriovenous (AV) shunt is created by surgically inserting a cannula into an artery and vein in the forearm or leg in what shape for the external blood path?","options":["U shape","V shape","S shape","Straight line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a cannula is inserted into an artery and vein in the forearm or leg in a U shape for the external blood path.  Why B/C/D wrong: These shapes are not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"In an external AV shunt, once dialysis is complete, the cannulas are:","options":["Clamped","Removed permanently","Left open","Cut off"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when dialysis is complete, then the cannulas are clamped.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are advantages of an external AV shunt?","options":["Can be used immediately after its creation; no venipuncture necessary","Very low infection risk with internal setup","No risk of dislodgement","Requires 4–6 weeks to mature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the advantages of the external AV shunt as usable immediately after creation with no venipuncture necessary.  Why B wrong: Low infection risk from internal setup is an advantage of the AV fistula/graft.  Why C wrong: Disconnection/dislodgement is actually a disadvantage of the shunt.  Why D wrong: Maturation of 4–6 weeks is the AV fistula, not the shunt."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are disadvantages of an external AV shunt? (Select all that apply)","options":["Disconnection/dislodgement","Risk for haemorrhage","Risk of infection","Risk of clotting","Cannot be used immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists disconnection/dislodgement, risk for haemorrhage, infection, or clotting as disadvantages of the external AV shunt.  Why E wrong: The shunt CAN be used immediately; \"cannot use immediately\" is a disadvantage of the AV fistula/graft."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A client with an external AV shunt should be taught that the shunt extremity should NOT be used for:","options":["Monitoring BP, drawing blood, placing IV lines, or administering injections","Any movement","Any exercise","Bathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the shunt extremity should not be used for monitoring BP, drawing blood, placing IV lines, or administering injections.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated restrictions."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"A distinguishing feature noted about the external AV shunt is that:","options":["A bruit may NOT be heard with the external AV shunt","It never requires sterile technique","It matures in 2 weeks","It is made of bovine artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a bruit may not be heard with the external AV shunt (JIPMER-2018).  Why B wrong: Sterile technique must be maintained during the procedure.  Why C wrong: Two-week maturation refers to the AV graft.  Why D wrong: Bovine artery is used in the AV graft."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"During external AV shunt care, the nurse should maintain which technique during the procedure?","options":["Sterile technique","Clean but non-sterile technique","No specific technique","Reverse isolation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to maintain sterile technique during the procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"The internal arteriovenous (AV) fistula is described as:","options":["The most commonly used access for CKD patients, used for a long time","A temporary access only","Made of bovine artery","Usable immediately after creation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the internal AV fistula is the most commonly used access for CKD patients and is used for a long time.  Why B wrong: It is a permanent, long-term access.  Why C wrong: The bovine artery describes the AV graft.  Why D wrong: A fistula cannot be used immediately; it must mature."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"How is the internal AV fistula created surgically?","options":["By anastomosis of a large artery and large vein in the arm","By inserting a U-shaped external cannula","By using a bovine artificial graft","By placing a femoral sheath"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the fistula is created surgically by anastomosis of a large artery and a large vein in the arm.  Why B wrong: A U-shaped external cannula describes the external AV shunt.  Why C wrong: A bovine graft describes the AV graft.  Why D wrong: A femoral sheath is a temporary access."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"An internal AV fistula requires how long to mature before use?","options":["About 4–6 weeks","About 2 weeks","About 6 months","It is usable immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the fistula required to be mature about 4–6 weeks.  Why B wrong: Two weeks is the maturation time for the AV graft.  Why C wrong: Six months is not stated.  Why D wrong: The fistula cannot be used immediately."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which exercise helps an internal AV fistula to mature?","options":["Squeezing of a ball","Arm circles","Wrist rotation","Deep breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states squeezing of a ball exercise helps the fistula to mature.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated maturation exercises."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"While an internal AV fistula matures, which alternate access may be used?","options":["Femoral catheter","AV graft only","External shunt only","No access is possible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that till maturation, another alternate access such as a femoral catheter may be used.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically names the femoral catheter as the alternate."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What is the main advantage of an internal AV fistula?","options":["Low risk of infection, bleeding, and clotting due to the internal setup","It can be used immediately","A bruit cannot be heard","It uses bovine artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the advantage of the internal AV fistula is low risk of infection, bleeding, and clotting due to the internal setup.  Why B wrong: The disadvantage is that it cannot be used immediately.  Why C wrong: A bruit not being heard relates to the external shunt.  Why D wrong: Bovine artery is used in the AV graft."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are disadvantages/complications of an internal AV fistula? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cannot be used immediately","Infiltration during dialysis causing haematomas","Chance of heart failure due to increased blood flow into the venous system","Arterial steal syndrome","Immediate usability after creation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists that the fistula cannot be used immediately, infiltration during dialysis causing haematomas, chance of heart failure due to increased venous blood flow, and arterial steal syndrome.  Why E wrong: Immediate usability is a feature of the external shunt, not the fistula (which cannot be used immediately)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"\"Arterial steal syndrome\" in an internal AV fistula develops when:","options":["Too much blood is diverted to the vein and arterial perfusion to the hand is compromised","The fistula becomes infected","The kidney stops making urine","The catheter kinks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states arterial steal syndrome occurs when too much blood is diverted to the vein and arterial perfusion to the hand is compromised.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe arterial steal syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"To assess a functioning internal AV fistula, the nurse should:","options":["Auscultate for a bruit and palpate for a thrill","Measure BP in the fistula arm","Draw blood from the fistula","Apply a tourniquet proximally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to auscultate for a bruit and palpate for a thrill.  Why B/C wrong: BP measurement and blood draws from the fistula/shunt extremity are contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Applying a tourniquet is not a stated assessment method."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"An internal arteriovenous (AV) graft is used for:","options":["Chronic dialysis","One-time emergency dialysis only","Temporary access only","Peritoneal dialysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the internal AV graft is used for chronic dialysis.  Why B/C wrong: It is a permanent access for chronic dialysis, not one-time/temporary.  Why D wrong: It is a haemodialysis (not peritoneal) access."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"An internal AV graft is created using an artificial graft made of:","options":["Bovine or cow's carotid artery","Human donor vein","Synthetic polyester only","The client's own radial artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an artificial graft made of bovine or cow's carotid artery is used to create an artificial vein for blood flow.  Why B/C/D wrong: These materials are not the stated graft source."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"How is the internal AV graft formed?","options":["By anastomosis of an artery to a vein using an artificial graft","By an external U-shaped cannula","By a subclavian sheath","By a femoral catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the process involves an anastomosis of an artery to a vein using an artificial graft.  Why B wrong: The U-shaped cannula is the external AV shunt.  Why C/D wrong: These describe temporary catheter accesses."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"How soon after insertion can an internal AV graft be used?","options":["2 weeks after insertion","Immediately","4–6 weeks after insertion","6 months after insertion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the graft can be used 2 weeks after insertion.  Why B wrong: It cannot be used immediately.  Why C wrong: 4–6 weeks is the fistula maturation time.  Why D wrong: Six months is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are complications of an internal AV graft?","options":["Clotting, aneurysms, and infection","Only bruising","Only hyperkalaemia","Only fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists clotting, aneurysms, and infection as complications of the internal AV graft.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated complications."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"What are the advantages of an internal AV graft?","options":["Low risk of infection, clotting, and bleeding because the graft is internal, and it allows freedom of movements","It can be used immediately","It is a temporary access","It requires no maturation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states advantages are low risk of infection, clotting, and bleeding because the graft is internal, and the graft allows freedom of movements.  Why B wrong: A disadvantage is that it cannot be used immediately.  Why C wrong: It is a permanent access for chronic dialysis.  Why D wrong: It requires ~2 weeks before use."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Complications of Chronic Kidney Disease, MDRD formula, urosepsis…","question":"Which are disadvantages of an internal AV graft? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cannot be used immediately","Risk of infiltration causing haematoma formation","Arterial steal syndrome","Heart failure","Immediate usability"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A–D): The source lists the disadvantages of the AV graft as inability to use immediately, risk of infiltration causing haematoma formation, arterial steal syndrome, and heart failure.  Why E wrong: Immediate usability is not a feature of the graft (it cannot be used immediately)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A nurse is teaching a client with a newly created AV fistula. Which instruction is correct regarding the shunt (access) extremity?","options":["The shunt extremity may be used for routine blood draws","The shunt extremity should NOT be used for monitoring BP, drawing blood, placing IV lines, or administering injections","Blood pressure should always be taken in the shunt arm for accuracy","IV lines should preferentially be placed in the fistula arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client must be taught that the shunt extremity should not be used for monitoring BP, drawing blood, placing IV lines, or administering injections (to protect access patency).  Why A wrong: Drawing blood from the shunt extremity is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: BP monitoring in the shunt arm is contraindicated as it can compromise the access.  Why D wrong: IV lines should not be placed in the fistula arm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which exercise is taught to a client to promote AV graft/fistula maturity?","options":["Overhead arm stretches","Hand flexing exercises such as squeezing a ball","Isometric neck exercises","Deep breathing exercises"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clients are instructed to do hand flexing exercises such as squeezing a ball to promote graft maturity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-recommended maturation exercises; squeezing a ball is specified."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"When assessing an AV graft extremity, the nurse should note which of the following?","options":["Temperature and capillary refill of the extremity","Grip strength only","Skin color of the opposite arm","Pupillary reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should note the temperature and capillary refill of the extremity to assess perfusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies temperature and capillary refill of the access extremity, not grip strength, opposite-arm color, or pupillary reaction."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why does the nurse palpate the pulses below an AV graft?","options":["To assess for impairment of circulation and swelling","To measure blood glucose","To determine dialysis adequacy","To confirm graft maturity time"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulses are palpated below the graft to detect impairment of circulation and swelling.  Why B/C/D wrong: Palpating distal pulses assesses circulation/swelling, not glucose, dialysis adequacy, or maturity time."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What are the MOST common complications the nurse monitors for with an AV fistula/graft?","options":["Bleeding and hypotension","Clotting and infection","Fever and rash","Nausea and vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors and assesses for clotting and infection, which are the most common complications of an AV access.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source names clotting and infection specifically as the most common complications."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"To confirm patency of an AV fistula or graft, the nurse should:","options":["Palpate for a thrill or auscultate for a bruit and notify if a thrill or bruit is absent","Occlude the access and time capillary refill","Inject saline into the fistula and observe backflow","Apply a tourniquet above the site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse checks patency by palpating for a thrill or auscultating for a bruit over the fistula/graft and notifies if a thrill or bruit is absent (absence suggests occlusion).  Why B/C/D wrong: These maneuvers can damage or occlude the access and are not source-recommended patency checks."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"During haemodialysis, an absent bruit and thrill over the AV shunt indicates the nurse should:","options":["Ignore it as normal","Notify the provider, as a bruit/thrill may not be heard with the AV shunt when it is not patent","Increase the dialysis flow rate","Apply pressure over the fistula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bruit/thrill may not be heard with the AV shunt, and the nurse should notify if absent, as absence suggests loss of patency.  Why A wrong: An absent thrill/bruit is abnormal and must be reported.  Why C/D wrong: These do not address a potentially clotted/non-patent access."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a complication of dialysis?","options":["Air embolism","Disequilibrium syndrome","Hyperglycaemia","Encephalopathy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The listed complications include hypoglycaemia (not hyperglycaemia); thus hyperglycaemia is not among the dialysis complications listed.  Why A/B/D wrong: Air embolism, disequilibrium syndrome, and encephalopathy are all listed complications of dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the complications of dialysis listed in the source.","options":["Air embolism","Electrolyte imbalances","Hepatitis","Sepsis & shock","Polycythemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Air embolism, electrolyte imbalances, hepatitis, and sepsis & shock are all listed complications of dialysis (along with disequilibrium syndrome, encephalopathy, hypoglycaemia, haemorrhage, and hypotension).  Why E wrong: Polycythemia is not listed; anemia (not polycythemia) is typical in renal disease."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Before initiating haemodialysis, the dialysis machine tubing should first be flushed with which solution to prevent clotting of blood in the circuit?","options":["Normal saline only","Heparinised solution (Heparin)","Dextrose 5%","Sterile water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dialysis machine tubing should first be flushed with heparinised solution (Heparin) to prevent clotting of the blood in the circuit.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies heparinised solution to prevent circuit clotting, not plain saline, dextrose, or sterile water."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why is the patient's weight taken after haemodialysis?","options":["To calculate the medication dose","To calculate fluid loss","To determine caloric needs","To assess muscle mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After dialysis the patient's weight should be taken to calculate fluid loss.  Why A/C/D wrong: Post-dialysis weight is used specifically to determine fluid loss, not medication dose, calories, or muscle mass."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Before insertion of the catheter for peritoneal dialysis, the patient should be instructed to:","options":["Eat a full meal","Void (empty the bladder) to prevent injury to the bladder","Drink 2 liters of water","Lie prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should void before insertion of the peritoneal dialysis catheter to prevent injury to the bladder.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not reduce the risk of bladder injury during catheter insertion; emptying the bladder does."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"During haemodialysis, an air embolism occurs. What is the FIRST priority action?","options":["Turn the client to the left side with head down","Stop haemodialysis","Administer oxygen","Place in high-Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority when air embolism occurs during haemodialysis is to stop haemodialysis.  Why A wrong: Turning the client to the left side (Trendelenburg/head down) is the next step, done after stopping dialysis.  Why C wrong: Oxygen is administered later, after positioning.  Why D wrong: High-Fowler's is not indicated; head-down/left-side positioning is used."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"After stopping haemodialysis for a suspected air embolism, in which position should the client be placed?","options":["Right lateral with head elevated","Left side, with head down (Trendelenburg's position)","Supine flat","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client is turned to the left side with the head down (Trendelenburg's position) because this position traps the air into the right atrium of the heart.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies left-side, head-down (Trendelenburg) positioning to trap air in the right atrium."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The left-side, head-down (Trendelenburg) position during an air embolism works because it allows:","options":["Trapping the air into the right atrium of the heart","Air to escape via the lungs immediately","Increased cerebral perfusion","Air to move to the left ventricle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This position allows trapping of the air into the right atrium of the heart, after which oxygen is administered.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated mechanism is trapping air in the right atrium, not lung escape, cerebral perfusion, or movement to the left ventricle."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is administered after positioning a client who developed an air embolism during haemodialysis?","options":["IV heparin","Oxygen","Sodium bicarbonate","Antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After stopping dialysis and positioning the client, oxygen is administered.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies oxygen administration as the final step in air embolism management."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of disequilibrium syndrome?","options":["Rapid change of fluids removes solutes from the blood faster than from the CSF and brain, pulling fluid into the brain and causing cerebral oedema","Air enters the venous circulation causing embolism","Bacteria enter the peritoneum causing peritonitis","Rapid loss of protein into the dialysate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disequilibrium syndrome occurs due to dialysis when rapid change of fluids removes solutes from the blood faster than from the CSF and brain, so fluid is pulled into the brain, causing cerebral oedema.  Why B wrong: That describes air embolism.  Why C wrong: That describes peritonitis.  Why D wrong: Protein loss into dialysate is not the described mechanism of disequilibrium syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Disequilibrium syndrome ultimately results in which condition?","options":["Pulmonary oedema","Cerebral oedema","Hepatic failure","Renal calculi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because fluid is pulled into the brain, disequilibrium syndrome causes cerebral oedema.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies cerebral oedema as the outcome."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In peritoneal dialysis, which structure acts as the dialysing (semi-permeable) membrane?","options":["Renal glomerulus","The peritoneum","The pleura","The bladder wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The peritoneum acts as the dialysing membrane or semi-permeable membrane to achieve dialysis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The peritoneum, not the glomerulus, pleura, or bladder wall, is the dialysing membrane in peritoneal dialysis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Peritoneal dialysis works on which principles?","options":["Osmosis, diffusion, and ultrafiltration","Active transport and phagocytosis","Filtration and secretion only","Pinocytosis and endocytosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peritoneal dialysis works on the principles of osmosis, diffusion, and ultrafiltration.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies osmosis, diffusion, and ultrafiltration."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the MOST common contraindication to peritoneal dialysis?","options":["Hypertension","Peritonitis","Anemia","Diabetes mellitus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common contraindication to peritoneal dialysis is peritonitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source names peritonitis specifically as the most common contraindication (SGPGI-2024)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What does the abbreviation CAPD stand for?","options":["Continuous Acute Peritoneal Dialysis","Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis","Chronic Ambulatory Peritoneal Drainage","Central Access Peritoneal Dialysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CAPD stands for Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis, which is commonly used.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of CAPD."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What type of catheter is surgically inserted into the client's peritoneal cavity for peritoneal dialysis?","options":["Latex balloon catheter","Siliconized rubber catheter","Metal cannula","PVC nasogastric tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A siliconized rubber catheter is surgically inserted into the client's peritoneal cavity.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a siliconized rubber catheter."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the commonly used insertion site for the peritoneal dialysis catheter?","options":["3–5 cm above the umbilicus","3–5 cm below the umbilicus","Right upper quadrant","At the symphysis pubis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The commonly used insertion site is 3–5 cm below the umbilicus, because this area is relatively avascular and has less fascial resistance.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 3–5 cm below the umbilicus."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why is the site 3–5 cm below the umbilicus preferred for peritoneal dialysis catheter insertion?","options":["It is highly vascular for rapid absorption","It is relatively avascular and has less fascial resistance","It is closest to the kidneys","It avoids the peritoneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This area is relatively avascular and has less fascial resistance.  Why A wrong: It is avascular, not highly vascular.  Why C/D wrong: The reason given is avascularity/less fascial resistance, not proximity to kidneys or avoidance of the peritoneum."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The dialysate solution used in peritoneal dialysis is described as:","options":["Sterile with hypertonicity","Non-sterile and isotonic","Hypotonic and unbuffered","Sterile and hypotonic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dialysate solution is sterile with hypertonicity.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies sterile and hypertonic dialysate."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"If a peritoneal dialysis client has an infection, which medicine may be added to the dialysate solution?","options":["Insulin","Antibiotics","Diuretics","Antihypertensives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If infection is present, antibiotics may be added to the dialysate solution.  Why A wrong: Insulin is added if the client is diabetic (DM), not for infection.  Why C/D wrong: Diuretics and antihypertensives are not the additives specified for infection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"For a diabetic (DM) client on peritoneal dialysis, which agent may be added to the dialysate solution?","options":["Insulin","Heparin","Antibiotics","Potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the client is DM, insulin may be added to the dialysate solution.  Why B wrong: Heparin is added to prevent clotting of the catheter, not for glycemic control.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics are added if infection is present.  Why D wrong: Potassium is not the specified additive for DM clients."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why is heparin added to the peritoneal dialysate solution?","options":["To lower blood glucose","To prevent clotting of the catheter","To treat infection","To increase osmotic pull"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin is added to the dialysate solution to prevent clotting of the catheter.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated reason for heparin is preventing catheter clotting."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the peritoneal dialysis infusion cycle, \"Fill\" refers to:","options":["Time the solution remains in the cavity","Dialysate solution (1 or 2 L) infused into the peritoneal cavity","Draining of fluid by gravity","Removal of the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fill is the dialysate solution (1 or 2 L) infused into the peritoneal cavity.  Why A wrong: That describes Dwell.  Why C wrong: That describes Drain/outflow.  Why D wrong: Catheter removal is unrelated to the Fill phase."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"How much dialysate is typically infused during the \"Fill\" phase of peritoneal dialysis?","options":["250–500 mL","1 or 2 L","3–4 L","100 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During Fill, 1 or 2 L of dialysate solution is infused into the peritoneal cavity.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 1 or 2 liters."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The \"Dwell\" time in peritoneal dialysis is defined as:","options":["The volume of dialysate infused","The amount of time the solution remains in the peritoneal cavity, usually 20–30 minutes to 8 hours","The time to drain the fluid","The time to insert the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dwell is the amount of time the solution remains in the peritoneal cavity, usually 20–30 minutes to 8 hours, depending on the type of solution.  Why A wrong: Volume infused is Fill.  Why C wrong: Draining is the outflow phase.  Why D wrong: Catheter insertion is unrelated to dwell time."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The usual dwell time range in peritoneal dialysis is:","options":["5–10 minutes","20–30 minutes to 8 hours","12–24 hours","1–2 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dwell time is usually 20–30 minutes to 8 hours, depending on the type of solution.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states 20–30 minutes up to 8 hours."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In peritoneal dialysis, \"Drain or outflow\" refers to:","options":["Fluid draining out of the body by gravity into the drainage bag","Infusion of dialysate","The dwell period","Flushing the catheter with heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drain/outflow is when fluid drains out from the body by gravity into the drainage bag.  Why B wrong: Infusion is the Fill phase.  Why C wrong: Dwell is the retention period.  Why D wrong: Heparin flushing is a dialysate additive step, not the drain phase."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"During peritoneal dialysis nursing care, when should the client void?","options":["After starting the procedure","Before starting the procedure","Only during dwell time","After draining"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client should void before starting the procedure (to prevent bladder injury during catheter placement/handling).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies voiding before the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL appropriate nursing care measures during peritoneal dialysis.","options":["Monitor vital parameters and record weight before and after","Assess for hypotension and pulmonary oedema","Assess dressing/insertion site for wetness and bleeding","Monitor outflow's colour and clarity","Allow dwell time to extend beyond the doctor's prescription"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nurses should monitor vital parameters and weight before/after, assess for hypotension and pulmonary oedema, check the site for wetness/bleeding, and monitor outflow colour and clarity.  Why E wrong: Dwell time should NOT extend beyond the doctor's prescription because this may increase the risk of hyperglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why should the dwell time NOT be allowed to extend beyond the doctor's prescription?","options":["It may increase the risk of hypoglycaemia","It may increase the risk of hyperglycaemia","It causes hyperkalemia","It causes air embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Extending dwell time beyond the prescription may increase the risk of hyperglycaemia (glucose absorption from the hypertonic dialysate).  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified risk is hyperglycaemia, not hypoglycaemia, hyperkalemia, or air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"If outflow is slow to start during peritoneal dialysis, the nurse should:","options":["Increase the fill volume","Turn the client from side to side","Clamp the drainage tubing","Remove the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should turn the client from side to side if outflow is slow to start.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not address slow outflow; repositioning the client does."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"During peritoneal dialysis, an outflow greater than inflow should be:","options":["Ignored as normal","Reported to the doctor","Treated by increasing fill volume","Corrected by clamping the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An outflow greater than inflow should be reported to the doctor.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source directs reporting this finding, not ignoring or self-correcting it."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the complications of peritoneal dialysis listed in the source.","options":["Peritonitis","Abdominal pain","Abnormal outflow / insufficient outflow","Leakage around the catheter site","Air embolism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Peritonitis (most common), abdominal pain, abnormal outflow, insufficient outflow, and leakage around the catheter site are all listed complications of peritoneal dialysis.  Why E wrong: Air embolism is listed as a complication of (haemo)dialysis, not among peritoneal dialysis complications."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the MOST common complication of peritoneal dialysis?","options":["Air embolism","Peritonitis","Hypotension","Leakage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peritonitis is the most common complication of peritoneal dialysis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source identifies peritonitis as most common; hypotension is the most common complication of haemodialysis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which of the following is described as the EARLY sign of peritonitis in a peritoneal dialysis client?","options":["Cloudy outflow","Brown outflow","Bloody outflow","Clear outflow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cloudy outflow is the early sign of peritonitis (along with fever, nausea/vomiting, rebound abdominal tenderness, and abdominal pain).  Why B wrong: Brown outflow indicates bowel perforation.  Why C wrong: Bloody outflow after a few exchanges indicates vascular complications.  Why D wrong: Clear outflow is normal."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the signs of peritonitis the nurse should monitor in a peritoneal dialysis client.","options":["Fever","Nausea and vomiting","Cloudy outflow","Rebound abdominal tenderness and abdominal pain","Bradycardia with clear outflow"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of peritonitis include fever, nausea & vomiting, cloudy outflow (early sign), rebound abdominal tenderness, and abdominal pain.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia with clear outflow is not among the listed peritonitis signs."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Bloody outflow after a few exchanges in peritoneal dialysis indicates:","options":["Bowel perforation","Vascular complications","Bladder perforation","Peritonitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bloody outflow after a few exchanges indicates vascular complications.  Why A wrong: Brown outflow indicates bowel perforation.  Why C wrong: Urine-coloured outflow indicates bladder perforation.  Why D wrong: Cloudy outflow indicates peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Brown outflow during peritoneal dialysis indicates:","options":["Vascular complications","Bowel perforation","Bladder perforation","Peritonitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brown outflow indicates bowel perforation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Bloody = vascular complications; urine-coloured = bladder perforation; cloudy = peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Urine-coloured outflow during peritoneal dialysis indicates:","options":["Bowel perforation","Bladder perforation","Vascular complications","Peritonitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine-coloured outflow indicates bladder perforation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Brown = bowel perforation; bloody = vascular complications; cloudy = peritonitis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Cloudy outflow during peritoneal dialysis indicates:","options":["Peritonitis","Bowel perforation","Bladder perforation","Vascular complications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cloudy outflow indicates peritonitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Brown = bowel perforation; urine-coloured = bladder perforation; bloody = vascular complications."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT) provides continuous ultra-filtration of extracellular fluid and clearance of urinary toxins over what period?","options":["1–4 hours","8–24 hours","24–48 hours","30 minutes to 1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CRRT provides continuous ultra-filtration of extracellular fluid and clearance of urinary toxins over a period of 8–24 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states 8–24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Match the modality with its most common complication: The most common complication of haemodialysis and peritoneal dialysis, respectively, are:","options":["Peritonitis and hypotension","Hypotension and peritonitis","Air embolism and bleeding","Infection and clotting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complication of haemodialysis is hypotension, and of peritoneal dialysis is peritonitis.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source-identified most common complications."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Kidney transplantation is performed in a patient with:","options":["Reversible pre-renal failure","Irreversible kidney failure","Mild dehydration","Acute cystitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kidney transplantation is performed in a patient with irreversible kidney failure, implanting a kidney from a compatible donor.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not indications; irreversible failure is."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A \"living related donor\" for kidney transplantation is defined as:","options":["Anyone who meets the criteria for donation as per guidelines","A brain-dead patient","A patient after MI death","Any hospital volunteer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A living related donor is anyone who meets the criteria for donation as per guidelines.  Why B/C wrong: Brain-dead or post-MI-death donors are cadaver donors.  Why D wrong: Donation requires meeting eligibility criteria, not simply volunteering."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A cadaver donor for kidney transplantation is obtained from:","options":["Any living relative","Brain-dead patients or after MI dead patients as per guideline","Only identical twins","Blood banks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cadaver donors are from brain-dead patients or after MI dead patients as per guideline.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition of a cadaver donor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"According to the transplant criteria, what is the acceptable age range for kidney transplantation?","options":["1–14 years","15–55 years","60–75 years","Any age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The criteria specify age between 15–55 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15–55 years."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL correct criteria for kidney transplantation.","options":["Donor shouldn't have kidney-related illness","Blood group and tissue of donor and recipient should match","Donor shouldn't have high blood pressure and diabetes","HLA test should be done for tissue compatibility","Donor must be over 60 years of age"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The donor should be free of kidney illness, HTN, and diabetes; blood group and tissue must match; and HLA testing confirms tissue compatibility.  Why E wrong: The age criterion is 15–55 years, not over 60."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which test is done for tissue compatibility before kidney transplantation?","options":["Human Leucocyte Antigen (HLA) test","Prothrombin time","Sweat chloride test","Bonney test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Human Leucocyte Antigen (HLA) test should be done for tissue compatibility.  Why B/C wrong: These assess coagulation/cystic fibrosis, not tissue compatibility.  Why D wrong: The Bonney test diagnoses stress incontinence."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which are the indications for kidney transplantation?","options":["ESKD or GFR <15 ml/hr","GFR >90 ml/min","Acute cystitis","Mild proteinuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The indications are ESKD (end-stage kidney disease) or GFR less than 15 ml/hr.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not indications for transplantation."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"How long before the transplant procedure should immunosuppressive medicines be administered to the recipient?","options":["On the day of surgery","For 2 days before the procedure","1 week before","Only after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immunosuppressive medicines are administered to the recipient for 2 days before the procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 2 days before."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"How long before transplantation should haemodialysis be performed and verified?","options":["24 hours before transplantation","1 week before","Immediately after transplantation","12 hours before"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Haemodialysis should be done 24 hours before transplantation, and the nurse should verify it.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 24 hours before transplantation."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL preoperative nursing care measures for kidney transplantation.","options":["Take informed consent including consequences and complications of surgery","Verify histocompatibility tests (blood group and HLA)","Administer immunosuppressive medicines 2 days before the procedure","Assess renal functions and maintain strict aseptic technique","Withhold all immunosuppressants until after surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Consent, histocompatibility verification, pre-op immunosuppression (2 days), and renal function assessment with strict asepsis are all preoperative measures.  Why E wrong: Immunosuppressives are given 2 days before, not withheld until after surgery."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In a post-transplant client, urine output begins immediately if the donor was a living donor. With a cadaver donor, urine output:","options":["Begins immediately","May be delayed for a few days","Never occurs","Is always doubled"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output begins immediately if the donor was living, but it may be delayed for a few days with a cadaver donor.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states cadaver-donor urine output may be delayed."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Post-transplant, urine output is monitored hourly. What output should be reported immediately?","options":["Less than 100 ml/hr","Greater than 200 ml/hr","150 ml/hr","300 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor urine output hourly; if urine output is less than 100 ml/hr, report immediately.  Why B/C/D wrong: The reporting threshold is output less than 100 ml/hr."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Where is the new (transplanted) kidney placed?","options":["In the retroperitoneal space at the original renal fossa","At the lower abdomen in the iliac fossa, retroperitoneally","In the pelvic cavity intraperitoneally","Above the diaphragm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The new kidney is placed at the lower abdomen in the iliac fossa, retroperitoneally.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the iliac fossa, retroperitoneally, in the lower abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"After the initial post-transplant period, the client is placed in the semi-Fowler's position primarily to:","options":["Prevent bleeding","Promote gas exchange","Reduce urine output","Prevent infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the initial supine period, the client is placed in semi-Fowler's to promote gas exchange, turning from back to the non-operative side.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is to promote gas exchange."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Post-transplant, the client should be turned from the back to which side?","options":["The operative side","The non-operative side","Prone only","Never turned"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client is turned from back to the non-operative side.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies turning to the non-operative side."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Initially, transplanted-kidney urine colour is pink and bloody. It should return to normal within:","options":["24 hours","One week","One month","3 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine colour is initially pink and bloody but later returns to normal within one week.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies within one week."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The nurse should inform the doctor if which of the following is noted post-transplant?","options":["Pink-tinged urine on day 1","Gross haematuria and clots","Urine output of 120 ml/hr","Temperature of 37°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should inform the doctor if gross haematuria and clots are noted.  Why A wrong: Pink urine initially is expected and returns to normal within a week.  Why C wrong: 120 ml/hr is above the report threshold of <100 ml/hr.  Why D wrong: 37°C is normal."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Infection is the primary cause of death in what period after transplant?","options":["First month","First year after transplant","First 5 years","First 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection is the primary cause of death in the first year after transplant.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the first year."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the MOST reliable indicator of graft (kidney) rejection?","options":["Increased urine output","Decrease or absence of urine output","Weight loss","Hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most reliable indicator of rejection is a decrease or absence of urine output.  Why A wrong: Rejection causes decreased, not increased, output.  Why C/D wrong: Rejection is associated with weight gain and hypertension, not weight loss/hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Hyperacute kidney graft rejection occurs at what time?","options":["Within 6 weeks to months","At the time of anastomosis or within minutes to first hours","Months to years","After 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperacute rejection occurs at the time of anastomosis or within minutes to the first hours.  Why A wrong: That describes acute rejection.  Why C/D wrong: That describes chronic rejection (months to years)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Hyperacute graft rejection is caused by and represents which hypersensitivity type?","options":["Pre-existing antibodies; type-II hypersensitivity","T-cell mediated; type-IV hypersensitivity","T-cell mediated; type III, IV hypersensitivity","IgE mediated; type-I hypersensitivity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperacute rejection is due to pre-existing antibodies and is a type-II hypersensitivity reaction.  Why B wrong: That describes acute rejection.  Why C wrong: That describes chronic rejection.  Why D wrong: Type-I (IgE) is not associated with graft rejection here."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The intervention for hyperacute kidney graft rejection is to:","options":["Increase steroid dose","Remove the rejected kidney","Give monoclonal antibiotics","Start dialysis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intervention for hyperacute rejection is to remove the rejected kidney.  Why A/C wrong: Increasing steroids/monoclonal therapy is for acute rejection.  Why D wrong: The specified intervention is removal of the rejected kidney."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which is the MOST common type of kidney graft rejection, and when does it occur?","options":["Hyperacute; within minutes","Acute; within 6 weeks to months postoperatively","Chronic; within 24 hours","Acute; after several years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute rejection is the most common type and occurs within 6 weeks to months postoperatively.  Why A wrong: Hyperacute occurs within minutes but is not the most common.  Why C/D wrong: The timing given is incorrect for acute rejection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Acute kidney graft rejection is caused by and is which hypersensitivity type?","options":["Pre-existing antibodies; type-II","T-cell mediated; type-IV hypersensitivity","IgE mediated; type-I","Immune complex; type-III only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute rejection is T-cell mediated and is a type-IV hypersensitivity reaction.  Why A wrong: That is hyperacute rejection.  Why C/D wrong: Acute rejection is type-IV (T-cell mediated)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The intervention for acute kidney graft rejection includes:","options":["Removing the rejected kidney immediately","Increase dose of steroids and monoclonal antibiotics, re-transplantation if necessary","Only supportive care","Stopping all immunosuppressives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute rejection is treated by increasing the dose of steroids and monoclonal antibiotics, with re-transplantation if necessary.  Why A wrong: Removal is the intervention for hyperacute rejection.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the escalation of immunosuppression specified."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Chronic kidney graft rejection occurs over what time frame?","options":["Minutes to hours","Slowly, over months to years","Within 6 weeks","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic rejection occurs slowly, over months to years.  Why A wrong: That is hyperacute rejection.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks to months is acute rejection.  Why D wrong: 24 hours does not match chronic rejection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Chronic kidney graft rejection is associated with which hypersensitivity type(s)?","options":["Type-I only","Type III, IV (T-cell mediated)","Type-II only","Type-I and II"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic rejection is T-cell mediated and involves type III, IV hypersensitivity.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies type III, IV for chronic rejection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The intervention for chronic kidney graft rejection is:","options":["Remove the rejected kidney","Immunosuppressive medicines and re-transplantation if necessary","High-dose diuretics","Immediate anastomosis revision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic rejection is managed with immunosuppressive medicines and re-transplantation if necessary.  Why A wrong: Removal is the intervention for hyperacute rejection.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specified interventions."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which temperature is a sign of graft (kidney) reaction/rejection?","options":["Temperature >100.2°F or 37.7°C","Temperature <96°F","Temperature of exactly 98.6°F","Temperature <35°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A temperature greater than 100.2°F or 37.7°C is a sign of graft reaction.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies elevated temperature (>100.2°F / 37.7°C)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL signs and symptoms of graft (kidney) reaction.","options":["Reduced or absence of urine output","Pain or tenderness over the grafted kidney","2 to 3 lb weight gain in the last 24 hours","Oedema and hypertension","Weight loss and hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs include reduced/absent urine output, pain/tenderness over the grafted kidney, 2–3 lb weight gain in 24 hours, and oedema with hypertension.  Why E wrong: Rejection causes weight GAIN and hypertension, not weight loss and hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"How much weight gain in the last 24 hours is considered a sign of graft reaction?","options":["0.5 lb","2 to 3 lb","5 to 6 lb","10 lb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 2 to 3 lb weight gain in the last 24 hours is a sign of graft reaction.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 2–3 lb in 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which laboratory findings are associated with graft reaction/rejection?","options":["Elevated BUN and serum creatinine level","Decreased creatinine clearance","Elevated WBCs","Decreased BUN and creatinine","Decreased WBCs"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Graft reaction shows elevated BUN and serum creatinine, decreased creatinine clearance, and elevated WBCs.  Why D/E wrong: BUN/creatinine rise (not fall) and WBCs increase (not decrease) with rejection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"According to \"Should Remember,\" the early indicators of renal graft rejection are:","options":["Decreased creatinine, hypothermia, and increased urine output","Increased creatinine level, temperature, and decreased urine output","Weight loss and polyuria","Hyperkalemia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Early indicators of renal graft rejection are increased creatinine level, increased temperature, and decreased urine output.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated early indicators."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A client leaks urine during coughing and sneezing due to increased intra-abdominal pressure. This describes which type of urinary incontinence?","options":["Urge incontinence","Stress incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Neurogenic incontinence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stress incontinence is the involuntary passage of urine as a result of increased intra-abdominal pressure under stress, such as leakage during coughing and sneezing.  Why A wrong: Urge incontinence is from overactive bladder muscles causing a sudden urge.  Why C wrong: Overflow incontinence is due to urethral blockage/incomplete emptying with dribbling.  Why D wrong: Neurogenic incontinence is from nervous-system impairment."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Involuntary loss of urine when overactive bladder muscles cause a sudden urge to urinate defines:","options":["Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Neurogenic incontinence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urge incontinence is involuntary loss of urine when overactive bladder muscles cause a sudden urge to urinate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe stress, overflow, and neurogenic incontinence respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A client has dribbling of urine even after urination due to urethral blockage and incomplete bladder emptying. This is:","options":["Overflow incontinence","Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","Neurogenic incontinence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Overflow incontinence is due to blockage of the urethra as a result of incomplete emptying of the bladder, with dribbling of urine present even after urination.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other incontinence types."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Incontinence due to impairment of the nervous system, such as after a spinal cord injury, is called:","options":["Stress incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Neurogenic incontinence","Urge incontinence"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Neurogenic incontinence is due to impairment of the functioning of the nervous system, such as after spinal cord injury.  Why A/B/D wrong: These describe other incontinence types not related to nervous-system impairment."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Hydronephrosis is defined as:","options":["Inflammation of the renal parenchyma","Distension of the renal pelvis and calices due to obstruction in normal urine flow","Inflammation of the bladder","Inflammation of the epididymis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydronephrosis is distension of the renal pelvis and calices due to obstruction in normal urine flow.  Why A wrong: That is pyelonephritis.  Why C wrong: That is cystitis.  Why D wrong: That is epididymitis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of hydronephrosis?","options":["Tumor","Stone or calculus","Scar tissue","BPH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of hydronephrosis is a stone or calculus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Tumors, scar tissue, ureter obstruction, and prostate hypertrophy/BPH are other (not the most common) causes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the \"other causes\" of hydronephrosis listed (besides stone/calculus).","options":["Tumors","Scar tissue","Ureter obstruction","Hypertrophy of the prostate or BPH","Vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other causes include tumors, scar tissue, ureter obstruction, and hypertrophy of the prostate or BPH.  Why E wrong: Vitamin D deficiency is not listed as a cause of hydronephrosis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which signs and symptoms characterize hydronephrosis?","options":["Hypertension and headache, colicky or dull flank pain radiating to the groin","Painless haematuria only","Scrotal pain","Cloudy outflow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydronephrosis presents with hypertension and headache, and colicky or dull flank pain that radiates to the groin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-listed hydronephrosis symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Epididymitis is defined as inflammation of the:","options":["Renal pelvis","Epididymis","Prostate gland","Bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are pyelonephritis, prostatitis, and cystitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the MOST common cause of epididymitis?","options":["UTI and STI","Trauma","Autoimmune disease","Vitamin deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of epididymitis is UTI and STI.  Why B/C/D wrong: Other causes are long-time erected penis, long-term catheter use, and prostatitis, but UTI & STI are the most common."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the \"other causes\" of epididymitis listed.","options":["Long-time erected penis","Long-term use of catheter","Prostatitis (inflammation of prostate gland)","Renal calculi","Spinal cord injury"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Other causes include long-time erected penis, long-term use of catheter, and prostatitis.  Why D/E wrong: Renal calculi and spinal cord injury are not listed as causes of epididymitis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of epididymitis?","options":["Groin pain","Scrotal pain","Fever","Pus in urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scrotal pain is the cardinal sign of epididymitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: Groin pain, fever, and pus/bacteria in urine occur, but scrotal pain is the cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL signs and symptoms of epididymitis.","options":["Scrotal pain (cardinal sign)","Groin pain","Swelling in the scrotum and groin","Pus and bacteria in the urine and fever","Painless testicular mass"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs include scrotal pain (cardinal), groin pain, swelling in scrotum & groin, and pus/bacteria in urine with fever.  Why E wrong: A painless testicular mass is not among the listed epididymitis features (epididymitis is painful)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In nursing care of epididymitis, the client is encouraged to have bed rest with the scrotum elevated primarily to:","options":["Prevent traction on the spermatic cord, facilitate drainage, and relieve pain","Increase testosterone production","Prevent urinary retention","Improve fertility"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bed rest with the scrotum elevated prevents traction on the spermatic cord, facilitates drainage, and relieves pain.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is to prevent cord traction, facilitate drainage, and relieve pain."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL appropriate nursing care measures for epididymitis.","options":["Bed rest with scrotum elevated","Cold application on the scrotal area","Sitz bath","General care","Warm scrotal compresses to increase blood flow"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes bed rest with scrotum elevated, cold application on the scrotal area, sitz bath, and general care.  Why E wrong: The source specifies cold application on the scrotal area, not warm compresses."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Pyelonephritis is an inflammation of the:","options":["Renal parenchyma, commonly caused by bacterial invasion","Bladder wall","Urethra only","Epididymis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pyelonephritis is an inflammation of the renal parenchyma, commonly caused by bacterial invasion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cystitis, urethritis, and epididymitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Acute pyelonephritis is due to:","options":["Chronic obstruction in urinary flow","Urethritis or urethral infection or any invasive procedure","Autoimmune destruction","Renal calculi only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute pyelonephritis is due to urethritis or urethral infection or by any invasive procedure.  Why A wrong: Chronic obstruction causes chronic pyelonephritis.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of acute pyelonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Chronic pyelonephritis is due to:","options":["Urethral infection","Chronic obstruction in urinary flow","Any invasive procedure","STI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic pyelonephritis is due to chronic obstruction in urinary flow.  Why A/C/D wrong: These relate to acute pyelonephritis or other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which organism is the most common (80%) causative organism for all urinary tract infections?","options":["Klebsiella","E. coli","Pseudomonas","Staphylococcus aureus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: E. coli is the most common (80%) causative organism for all urinary tract infections.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies E. coli at 80%."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the signs and symptoms of pyelonephritis.","options":["Fever and chills","Tachycardia and tachypnoea","Flank pain on the affected side","Frequency, urgency, and cloudy/bloody/foul-smelling urine","Bradycardia and clear dilute urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pyelonephritis presents with fever & chills, tachycardia & tachypnoea, flank pain on the affected side, frequency, urgency, and cloudy/bloody/foul-smelling urine.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia and clear dilute urine are not features of pyelonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Chronic pyelonephritis can lead to which condition?","options":["AKI (acute kidney injury)","Diabetes mellitus","Cystitis","Hypertension only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic pyelonephritis leads to AKI (acute kidney injury).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies chronic pyelonephritis leads to AKI."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Inflammation of the renal pelvis is termed:","options":["Interstitial nephritis","Pyelonephritis","Cystitis","Ureteritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyelonephritis is inflammation of the renal pelvis.  Why A wrong: Interstitial nephritis is inflammation of the kidney.  Why C wrong: Cystitis is inflammation of the bladder.  Why D wrong: Ureteritis is inflammation of the ureters."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Interstitial nephritis is inflammation of the:","options":["Bladder","Kidney","Urethra","Prostate gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Interstitial nephritis is inflammation of the kidney.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe cystitis, urethritis, and prostatitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Cystitis is inflammation of the:","options":["Bladder","Ureters","Urethra","Renal pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cystitis is inflammation of the bladder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are ureteritis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Urethritis is inflammation of the:","options":["Urethra","Ureters","Bladder","Prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urethritis is inflammation of the urethra.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are ureteritis, cystitis, and prostatitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Ureteritis is inflammation of the:","options":["Urethra","Ureters","Renal pelvis","Bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ureteritis is inflammation of the ureters.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are urethritis, pyelonephritis, and cystitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Prostatitis is inflammation of the:","options":["Prostate gland","Foreskin","Glans of the penis","Fallopian tubes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostatitis is inflammation of the prostate gland.  Why B wrong: Posthitis is inflammation of the foreskin.  Why C wrong: Balanitis is inflammation of the glans.  Why D wrong: Salpingitis is inflammation of the fallopian tubes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Balanitis is inflammation of the:","options":["Glans of the penis","Foreskin of the penis","Both foreskin and glans of the penis","Prostate gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Balanitis is inflammation of the glans of the penis.  Why B wrong: Posthitis is inflammation of the foreskin.  Why C wrong: Balanoposthitis is inflammation of both foreskin and glans.  Why D wrong: Prostatitis is inflammation of the prostate gland."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Posthitis is inflammation of the:","options":["Glans of the penis","Foreskin of the penis","Both foreskin and glans","Epididymis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Posthitis is inflammation of the foreskin of the penis.  Why A wrong: Balanitis is inflammation of the glans.  Why C wrong: Balanoposthitis is inflammation of both foreskin and glans.  Why D wrong: Epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Balanoposthitis is inflammation of:","options":["Only the glans of the penis","Only the foreskin of the penis","Both the foreskin and glans of the penis","The fallopian tubes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Balanoposthitis is inflammation of both the foreskin and glans of the penis.  Why A wrong: Only-glans inflammation is balanitis.  Why B wrong: Only-foreskin inflammation is posthitis.  Why D wrong: Salpingitis is inflammation of the fallopian tubes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Salpingitis is inflammation of the:","options":["Fallopian tubes","Bladder","Prostate","Urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingitis is inflammation of the fallopian tubes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are cystitis, prostatitis, and urethritis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which test is the confirmative diagnosis of UTI?","options":["Bacterial colony count of the culture test","Urinalysis dipstick alone","Serum creatinine","Ultrasound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bacterial colony count of the culture test is the confirmative diagnosis of UTI.  Why B/C/D wrong: These support diagnosis but the culture colony count is confirmative."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is considered the normal bacterial colony count in urine?","options":["<1000/ml",">1,00,000/ml","50,000/ml","10,000/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A normal bacterial colony count in urine is less than 1000/ml.  Why B wrong: >1,00,000/ml is the gold-standard criterion for UTI.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal count of <1000/ml."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The gold-standard criterion (bacterial colony count) for diagnosing UTI is a urine count of:","options":["<1000/ml",">1,00,000/ml","500/ml","5000/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In UTI the bacterial colony count in urine is >1,00,000/ml, which is the gold-standard criterion for UTI.  Why A wrong: <1000/ml is normal.  Why C/D wrong: These do not meet the >1,00,000/ml UTI threshold."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the clinical features of UTI.","options":["Burning sensation on micturition","Frequency and urgency","Inability to void, fever and chills","Cloudy, dark, foul-smelling urine","Painless polyuria with clear urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features of UTI include burning on micturition, frequency, urgency, inability to void, fever & chills, and cloudy/dark/foul-smelling urine.  Why E wrong: Painless polyuria with clear urine is not a feature of UTI."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Urinary retention is best defined as:","options":["Inability to empty the bladder completely during voiding","Involuntary loss of urine with coughing","Painful urination","Frequent nighttime urination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary retention is the inability to empty the bladder completely during voiding, which leads to overflow incontinence.  Why B wrong: That is stress incontinence.  Why C wrong: That is dysuria.  Why D wrong: That is nocturia."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Urinary retention leads to which type of incontinence?","options":["Stress incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Urge incontinence","Neurogenic incontinence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary retention (incomplete emptying) leads to overflow incontinence.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source links retention specifically to overflow incontinence."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the causes of urinary retention listed.","options":["DM (diabetes mellitus)","BPH","Pregnancy","Trauma to the pelvic floor muscles","Excess water intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of urinary retention include DM, BPH, pregnancy, and trauma to the pelvic floor muscles.  Why E wrong: Excess water intake is not a listed cause of urinary retention."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The most common microorganism associated with catheter-related UTI is:","options":["E. coli","Pseudomonas","Candida","Proteus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common microorganism associated with catheter-related UTI is E. coli.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies E. coli."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The most common hospital-acquired (nosocomial) infection is:","options":["Pneumonia","Urinary tract infection (UTI)","Surgical site infection","Bloodstream infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common hospital-acquired infection is urinary tract infection (UTI).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies UTI as the most common nosocomial infection."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Why is UTI more common in females?","options":["Due to proximity of the urethra to the rectum and short urethra","Due to longer urethra","Due to higher fluid intake","Due to prostate enlargement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UTI is more common in females due to proximity of the urethra to the rectum and short urethra.  Why B wrong: Females have a short urethra, not longer.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The Bonney test is used to diagnose:","options":["Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","UTI","Hydronephrosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bonney test is a urinary bladder stress test used to diagnose stress incontinence.  Why B/C/D wrong: The Bonney test is specific to stress incontinence."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal length of the urethra in males?","options":["4 cm","18–20 cm","25–30 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal length of the urethra in males is 18–20 cm.  Why A wrong: 4 cm is the female urethra length.  Why C wrong: 25–30 cm is the ureter length.  Why D wrong: Not the stated male urethra length."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal length of the urethra in females?","options":["18–20 cm","4 cm","25–30 cm","8 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal length of the female urethra is 4 cm.  Why A wrong: 18–20 cm is the male urethra.  Why C wrong: 25–30 cm is the ureter length.  Why D wrong: Not the stated female urethra length."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal weight of the prostate gland?","options":["10 gm","20 gm","50 gm","100 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal weight of the prostate gland is 20 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states 20 gm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal post-voiding residual volume of the bladder?","options":["50 ml","150 ml","100 ml","200 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal post-voiding residual volume of the bladder is 50 ml.  Why B wrong: 150 ml is the volume at which urine urge occurs.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated residual volume."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal urine output in adults?","options":["0.5 ml/min or 30 ml/hr","1 ml/kg/hr","2 ml/kg/hr","100 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal urine output in adults is 0.5 ml/min or 30 ml/hr.  Why B wrong: 1 ml/kg/hr is for children.  Why C wrong: 2 ml/kg/hr is for infants.  Why D wrong: Not the stated adult output."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal urine output in children?","options":["0.5 ml/min","1 ml/kg/hr","2 ml/kg/hr","30 ml/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal urine output in children is 1 ml/kg/hr.  Why A/D wrong: 0.5 ml/min / 30 ml/hr is the adult value.  Why C wrong: 2 ml/kg/hr is the infant value."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal urine output in an infant?","options":["0.5 ml/min","1 ml/kg/hr","2 ml/kg/hr","30 ml/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal urine output in an infant is 2 ml/kg/hr.  Why A/D wrong: 0.5 ml/min / 30 ml/hr is the adult value.  Why B wrong: 1 ml/kg/hr is the child value."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Excretion of less than 400 ml of urine in 24 hours is termed:","options":["Oliguria","Anuria","Polyuria","Nocturia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Less than 400 ml urine in 24 hours is termed oliguria.  Why B wrong: Anuria is <100 ml in 24 hours.  Why C wrong: Polyuria is >2000 ml in 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Nocturia is nighttime urination frequency."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Excretion of less than 100 ml of urine in 24 hours is termed:","options":["Oliguria","Anuria","Polyuria","Dysuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Less than 100 ml urine in 24 hours is termed anuria.  Why A wrong: Oliguria is <400 ml/24 hr.  Why C wrong: Polyuria is >2000 ml/24 hr.  Why D wrong: Dysuria is painful urination."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Excretion of more than 2000 ml of urine in 24 hours is termed:","options":["Oliguria","Anuria","Polyuria","Nocturia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: More than 2000 ml urine in 24 hours is termed polyuria.  Why A wrong: Oliguria is <400 ml/24 hr.  Why B wrong: Anuria is <100 ml/24 hr.  Why D wrong: Nocturia is nighttime frequency."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Dialysis is indicated when the GFR falls below:","options":["15 ml/hr","60 ml/min","90 ml/min","125 ml/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The indication for dialysis is a GFR below 15 ml/hr.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15 ml/hr as the threshold."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Thiazide and potassium-sparing diuretics act on the:","options":["Proximal tubule","Distal tubule","Loop of Henle","Collecting duct only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thiazide and potassium-sparing diuretics act on the distal tubule.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the distal tubule."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common type of renal stone?","options":["Uric acid","Calcium oxalate","Struvite","Cystine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of renal stone is calcium oxalate.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies calcium oxalate."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common viral infection after renal transplant?","options":["Herpes simplex","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)","Hepatitis B","HIV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common viral infection after renal transplant is Cytomegalovirus (CMV).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies CMV."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults?","options":["Hypertension","Diabetes mellitus","Glomerulonephritis","Amyloidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults is diabetes mellitus.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies diabetes mellitus."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What size of stone can pass spontaneously through the ureter?","options":["<4 mm","5–7 mm","8–10 mm",">10 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A stone less than 4 mm can pass through the ureter.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies <4 mm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the normal length of the ureters?","options":["4 cm","18–20 cm","25–30 cm","40 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal length of the ureters is 25–30 cm.  Why A wrong: 4 cm is the female urethra.  Why B wrong: 18–20 cm is the male urethra.  Why D wrong: Not the stated ureter length."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The functional unit of the kidneys is called the:","options":["Glomerulus","Nephron","Renal pelvis","Podocyte"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The functional unit of the kidneys is the nephron.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are components/structures but not the functional unit."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the best method of urine collection for culture & sensitivity (C/S) in infants?","options":["Midstream clean catch","Suprapubic aspiration","Catheterization","Random void"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best method of urine collection for C/S in infants is suprapubic aspiration.  Why A wrong: Midstream clean catch is used in adults.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified best method for infants."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the best method of urine collection for culture & sensitivity (C/S) in adults?","options":["Suprapubic aspiration","Midstream clean catch","First morning random void","Indwelling catheter drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best method of urine collection for C/S in adults is midstream clean catch.  Why A wrong: Suprapubic aspiration is used in infants.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified best method for adults."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What does BUN stand for?","options":["Blood Urea Nitrogen","Basic Urinary Nitrogen","Blood Uric Nitrogen","Bladder Urea Number"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BUN stands for Blood Urea Nitrogen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the best indicator of kidney functioning?","options":["Serum creatinine (and secondarily BUN level)","Serum sodium","Urine specific gravity","Serum potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best indicator of kidney functioning is serum creatinine (and secondarily BUN level).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies S. creatinine and 2nd BUN level."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The urge to urinate occurs at what bladder capacity?","options":["50 ml","150 ml","300 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urine urge occurs at a bladder capacity of 150 ml.  Why A wrong: 50 ml is the post-void residual volume.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated urge threshold."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Excessive frequency of urination at night is called:","options":["Oliguria","Nocturia","Polyuria","Dysuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequency of urination at night is called nocturia.  Why A wrong: Oliguria is reduced urine output.  Why C wrong: Polyuria is excessive volume over 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Dysuria is painful urination."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What amount of protein intake should be maintained during renal failure?","options":["20–30 g/day","40–60 g/day","80–100 g/day","120 g/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein intake to be maintained during renal failure is 40–60 g/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 40–60 g/day."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common and serious complication of peritoneal dialysis?","options":["Hypotension","Peritonitis","Air embolism","Haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common and serious complication of peritoneal dialysis is peritonitis.  Why A wrong: Hypotension is the most common complication of haemodialysis.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specified peritoneal dialysis complication."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A kidney transplant is an example of which type of graft?","options":["Autograft","Allograft","Xenograft","Isograft"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A kidney transplant is an example of an allograft (between individuals of the same species).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies allograft."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The micturition reflex centre is located in the:","options":["Sacral reflex (sacral spinal cord)","Cerebral cortex","Medulla oblongata","Thoracic spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The micturition reflex centre is located in the sacral reflex (sacral region).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the sacral reflex centre."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What percentage of cardiac output do the kidneys receive?","options":["5% (500 ml/min)","20% (1000–1200 ml/min)","40% (2000 ml/min)","10% (700 ml/min)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys receive 1000–1200 ml/min or 20% of the cardiac output.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 1000–1200 ml/min or 20%."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The blood filtration barrier in the kidneys is formed by which cells?","options":["Mesangial cells","Podocyte cells","Juxtaglomerular cells","Sertoli cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blood filtration barrier in the kidneys is formed by podocyte cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies podocyte cells."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"How many days does spermatogenesis take?","options":["28 days","61 days","90 days","14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis takes 61 days.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 61 days."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Failure of the testes to descend into the scrotum is known as:","options":["Cryptorchidism","Azoospermia","Aspermia","Teratospermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When the testes do not descend into the scrotum, it is known as cryptorchidism.  Why B wrong: Azoospermia is the absence of spermatozoa in semen.  Why C wrong: Aspermia is failure to form semen to ejaculate.  Why D wrong: Teratospermia is malformed sperm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Failure to form semen to ejaculate is called:","options":["Aspermia","Azoospermia","Oligospermia","Teratospermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure to form semen to ejaculate is called aspermia.  Why B wrong: Azoospermia is the absence of spermatozoa in semen.  Why C wrong: Oligospermia is a low sperm count (20 million/ml).  Why D wrong: Teratospermia is malformed sperm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Absence of spermatozoa in the semen is called:","options":["Aspermia","Azoospermia","Oligospermia","Teratospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absence of spermatozoa in the semen is called azoospermia.  Why A wrong: Aspermia is failure to form semen to ejaculate.  Why C wrong: Oligospermia is a low sperm concentration.  Why D wrong: Teratospermia is malformed sperm."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Malformed sperm in the semen is termed:","options":["Azoospermia","Teratospermia","Oligospermia","Aspermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Malformed sperm in semen is termed teratospermia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe absence, low count, and failure to form semen respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Enlargement of breast tissue in the male is known as:","options":["Gynecomastia","Cryptorchidism","Mastitis","Galactorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enlargement of breast tissue in the male is known as gynecomastia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are undescended testes, breast inflammation, and milk discharge respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Sertoli cells are also known as:","options":["Interstitial cells","Sustentacular cells","Podocyte cells","Mesangial cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sertoli cells are also known as sustentacular cells.  Why A wrong: Interstitial cells are Leydig cells.  Why C/D wrong: These are renal cells."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Leydig cells (interstitial cells) secrete which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Testosterone","Progesterone","Insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leydig cells (also known as interstitial cells) secrete testosterone hormone.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies testosterone from Leydig/interstitial cells."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the fertilizable life span of sperm?","options":["24 hours","48–72 hours","12 hours","5–7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fertilizable life span of sperm is 48–72 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 48–72 hours."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the early indicator of renal graft rejection (per the rapid-revision key point)?","options":["Increased creatinine level or decreased urine output","Decreased creatinine and increased urine output","Hypotension","Weight loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The early indicator of renal graft rejection is an increased creatinine level or decreased urine output.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated early indicators."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"From which nephron segment is the largest amount of solute and water (100%) reabsorbed?","options":["Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Loop of Henle","Collecting duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The largest amount of solute and water (100%) is reabsorbed from the PCT (proximal convoluted tubule). (NORCET-5.0)  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the PCT."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the PCT, water, Na, and K are reabsorbed at what percentage, and bicarbonate at what percentage?","options":["Water/Na/K 85% and bicarbonate 80–90%","Water/Na/K 50% and bicarbonate 30%","Water/Na/K 100% and bicarbonate 100%","Water/Na/K 20% and bicarbonate 10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the PCT, water, Na, and K are reabsorbed at 85% and bicarbonate at 80–90%.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 85% and 80–90% respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The normal GFR expressed in L/day is:","options":["90 L/day","180 L/day (180000 ml/day)","250 L/day","60 L/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal GFR = 180 L/day or 180000 ml/day (which equals 7500 ml/hour, or 125 ml/min).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 180 L/day."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Based on 180000 ml/day, the normal GFR per minute is:","options":["60 ml/min","125 ml/min","200 ml/min","90 ml/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 180000/24 = 7500 ml/hour; 7500/60 = 125 ml/min, so normal GFR is 125 ml/min.  Why A/C/D wrong: The calculation yields 125 ml/min."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Germ cells are called ______ in the female and ______ in the male.","options":["Ova; Spermatozoa (sperm)","Sperm; Ova","Zygote; Oocyte","Follicle; Spermatid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Germ cells are called ova in the female and spermatozoa (sperm) in males.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies ova (female) and spermatozoa (male)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The acrosome of the sperm contains which enzymes that help penetrate the ova?","options":["Amylase and lipase","Hyaluronidase and protease","Pepsin and trypsin","Lactase and maltase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The acrosome contains hyaluronidase and protease enzymes which help the sperm cell penetrate the ova.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies hyaluronidase and protease."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Approximately how many sperm does one ejaculation contain?","options":["20 million","200–300 million","50 million","1 billion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One ejaculation contains 200–300 million sperm.  Why A wrong: 20 million/ml is the oligospermia threshold.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"The best place of sperm maturation is the:","options":["Testes","Epididymis","Prostate","Seminal vesicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best place of sperm maturation is the epididymis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Oligospermia is defined as a sperm concentration of:","options":["20 million/ml of sperm","200 million/ml","50 million/ml","100 million/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oligospermia is 20 million/ml of sperm.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 20 million/ml."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common microorganism for catheter-associated UTI (rapid-revision key point)?","options":["E. coli","Klebsiella","Proteus","Candida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common microorganism for catheter-associated UTI is E. coli.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies E. coli."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common site for nosocomial infection?","options":["Respiratory tract","Urinary tract","Surgical wound","Bloodstream"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site for nosocomial infection is the urinary tract.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Oncology is the branch of medicine that deals with:","options":["Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer","Study of blood disorders","Study of the nervous system","Study of the endocrine system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oncology is the branch of medicine that deals with the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe hematology, neurology, and endocrinology respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A tumor is defined as:","options":["Abnormal growth of cells (out of control), which may be benign or malignant","A localized infection","A collection of pus","Inflammation of tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A tumor is an abnormal growth of cells (out of control) that may be benign or malignant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe infection, abscess, and inflammation, not a tumor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Metastasis is defined as:","options":["Cancer cells moving from their original location to another site","The death of cancer cells","Encapsulation of a tumor","Slow local growth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metastasis is when cancer cells move from their original location to another site.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe metastasis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"What is the most common route of cancer metastasis?","options":["Lymphatic spread","Blood-borne metastasis","Direct extension","Transcoelomic spread"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common route of cancer metastasis is blood-borne metastasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies blood-borne metastasis as the most common route."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Regarding structure, benign tumors are ______ while malignant tumors are ______.","options":["Abnormal/anaplastic; well differentiated","Well differentiated or normal; abnormal or anaplastic","Invasive; non-invasive","Rapid; slow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benign tumors are well differentiated or normal in structure, whereas malignant tumors are abnormal or anaplastic.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These refer to growth/invasion, not structure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Compared to benign tumors, the growth rate of malignant tumors is:","options":["Slow","Rapid","Absent","Identical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Malignant tumors grow rapidly, while benign tumors grow slowly.  Why A/C/D wrong: Benign growth is slow; malignant growth is rapid."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In malignant tumors, mitoses are:","options":["Few","Relatively common","Absent","Impossible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mitoses are relatively common in malignant tumors, whereas they are few in benign tumors.  Why A/C/D wrong: Benign tumors have few mitoses; malignant tumors have relatively common mitoses."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Regarding growth pattern, benign tumors are ______ and malignant tumors are ______.","options":["Invasive; non-invasive","Non-invasive; invasive","Encapsulated; encapsulated","Metastatic; localized"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benign tumors are non-invasive, while malignant tumors are invasive.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the growth descriptors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Regarding encapsulation, benign tumors are ______ encapsulated and malignant tumors are ______ encapsulated.","options":["Rarely; usually","Usually; rarely","Never; always","Always; never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benign tumors are usually encapsulated, whereas malignant tumors are rarely encapsulated.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The source uses \"usually\" and \"rarely,\" not absolutes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Regarding metastasis, benign tumors show ______ metastasis while malignant tumors show ______ metastasis.","options":["Frequent; none","None; frequent","Occasional; rare","Rare; occasional"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benign tumors show no metastasis, while malignant tumors metastasize frequently.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The source states \"none\" for benign and \"frequent\" for malignant."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Regarding effect on host, malignant tumors cause:","options":["Slight harm due to location or complication","Significant harm due to invasion and metastasis","No harm","Harm only by encapsulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Malignant tumors cause significant harm due to invasion and metastasis; benign tumors cause slight harm due to location or complication.  Why A wrong: That describes benign tumors.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source description."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer grading, Grade-1 cells are described as:","options":["Immature/anaplastic and undifferentiated","Slightly different from normal, well differentiated (mild dysplasia)","Poorly differentiated (severe dysplasia)","Moderately differentiated (moderate dysplasia)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade-1 cells differ slightly from normal cells and are well differentiated (mild dysplasia).  Why A wrong: That is Grade-4.  Why C wrong: That is Grade-3.  Why D wrong: That is Grade-2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Grade-2 cancer cells are described as:","options":["Well differentiated (mild dysplasia)","Moderately differentiated (moderate dysplasia)","Poorly differentiated (severe dysplasia)","Anaplastic and undifferentiated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade-2 cells are more abnormal and moderately differentiated (moderate dysplasia).  Why A wrong: That is Grade-1.  Why C wrong: That is Grade-3.  Why D wrong: That is Grade-4."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Grade-3 cancer cells are described as:","options":["Mild dysplasia","Moderate dysplasia","Very abnormal and poorly differentiated (severe dysplasia)","Undifferentiated/anaplasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade-3 cells are very abnormal and poorly differentiated (severe dysplasia).  Why A wrong: That is Grade-1.  Why B wrong: That is Grade-2.  Why D wrong: That is Grade-4."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Grade-4 cancer cells are described as:","options":["Well differentiated","Moderately differentiated","Immature or anaplasia and undifferentiated","Slightly abnormal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade-4 cells are immature or anaplasia and undifferentiated.  Why A/B/D wrong: These describe lower grades (1, 2)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer staging, Stage-0 refers to:","options":["Distant metastasis","Carcinoma in situ","Extensive local and regional spread","Limited local spread"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-0 is carcinoma in situ.  Why A wrong: That is Stage-IV.  Why C wrong: That is Stage-III.  Why D wrong: That is Stage-II."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer staging, Stage-I refers to:","options":["Carcinoma in situ","Tumor limited to the tissues of origin (localised tumor growth)","Distant metastasis","Extensive regional spread"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-I is a tumor limited to the tissues of origin (localised tumor growth).  Why A wrong: That is Stage-0.  Why C wrong: That is Stage-IV.  Why D wrong: That is Stage-III."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer staging, Stage-II refers to:","options":["Limited local spread","Carcinoma in situ","Distant metastasis","Localised tumor growth only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-II is limited local spread.  Why B wrong: That is Stage-0.  Why C wrong: That is Stage-IV.  Why D wrong: That is Stage-I."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer staging, Stage-III refers to:","options":["Carcinoma in situ","Limited local spread","Extensive local and regional spread","Distant metastasis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-III is extensive local and regional spread.  Why A wrong: That is Stage-0.  Why B wrong: That is Stage-II.  Why D wrong: That is Stage-IV."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In cancer staging, Stage-IV refers to:","options":["Distant metastasis","Localised tumor growth","Carcinoma in situ","Limited local spread"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-IV is distant metastasis.  Why B wrong: That is Stage-I.  Why C wrong: That is Stage-0.  Why D wrong: That is Stage-II."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" warning signs of cancer, \"C\" stands for:","options":["Cough","Change in bowel or bladder habits","Cancerous lump","Chest pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"C\" stands for Change in bowel or bladder habits.  Why A wrong: Nagging cough or hoarseness is \"N.\"  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Abdominal pain","Any sore that does not heal","Anemia","Anorexia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"A\" stands for Any sore that does not heal.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"U\" stands for:","options":["Unusual bleeding or discharge","Ulceration","Urinary retention","Unexplained fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"U\" stands for Unusual bleeding or discharge.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"T\" stands for:","options":["Thickening or lump in breast or elsewhere","Tiredness","Tumor","Tenderness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"T\" stands for Thickening or lump in breast or elsewhere.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"I\" stands for:","options":["Infection","Indigestion","Inflammation","Itching"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"I\" stands for Indigestion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"O\" stands for:","options":["Obesity","Obvious changes in a wart or mole","Obstruction","Oral ulcers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"O\" stands for Obvious changes in a wart or mole.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"In the \"CAUTION\" mnemonic, \"N\" stands for:","options":["Nausea","Nagging cough or hoarseness","Night sweats","Numbness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"N\" stands for Nagging cough or hoarseness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the CAUTION mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Which procedure is the confirmative diagnosis of cancer?","options":["Ultrasound","Biopsy","X-ray","Blood test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biopsy is the confirmative diagnosis of cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies biopsy as confirmative."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A biopsy performed by aspiration of cells is which type?","options":["Needle biopsy","Incisional biopsy","Excisional biopsy","Punch biopsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A needle biopsy involves aspiration of cells.  Why B wrong: Incisional biopsy removes a wedge of suspected tissue from a larger mass.  Why C wrong: Excisional biopsy is complete removal of the entire lesion.  Why D wrong: Punch biopsy is not one of the three listed types."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"An incisional biopsy involves:","options":["Aspiration of cells","Removal of a wedge of suspected tissue from a larger mass","Complete removal of the entire lesion","Removal of the whole organ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An incisional biopsy is the removal of a wedge of suspected tissue from a larger mass.  Why A wrong: Aspiration of cells is a needle biopsy.  Why C wrong: Complete lesion removal is an excisional biopsy.  Why D wrong: Whole-organ removal is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"An excisional biopsy involves:","options":["Aspiration of cells","Removal of a wedge from a larger mass","Complete removal of the entire lesion","Fine needle sampling"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An excisional biopsy is the complete removal of the entire lesion.  Why A/D wrong: These describe a needle biopsy.  Why B wrong: That describes an incisional biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Before a biopsy procedure, the nurse must ensure:","options":["Informed consent is taken","The patient fasts for 24 hours","Antibiotics are stopped","The patient exercises"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Informed consent should be taken before the biopsy procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies obtaining informed consent before biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Cellular adaptation refers to:","options":["The changes taking place in a cell in response to environmental changes","Cell death from injury","Malignant transformation only","Cell division during mitosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cellular adaptation refers to the changes taking place in a cell in response to environmental changes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe cellular adaptation."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the types of cellular adaptation listed.","options":["Hyperplasia","Hypertrophy","Atrophy","Metaplasia","Apoptosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cellular adaptations include hyperplasia, hypertrophy, atrophy, and metaplasia.  Why E wrong: Apoptosis (programmed cell death) is not listed among the adaptations."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Atrophy is defined as:","options":["Decrease in the size of a cell after full development","Increase in the number of cells","Increase in the size of a cell","Change of one cell type to another"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atrophy means a decrease in the size of a cell after full development.  Why B wrong: That is hyperplasia.  Why C wrong: That is hypertrophy.  Why D wrong: That is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"Select ALL the examples of atrophy given in the source.","options":["Atrophy of female reproductive organs after menopause","Ischemia","Muscle wasting (cachexia)","Hypertrophy of the heart","Formation of a tumor"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of atrophy include atrophy of female reproductive organs after menopause, ischemia, and muscle wasting (cachexia).  Why D wrong: Cardiac hypertrophy is an increase in cell size (hypertrophy), not atrophy.  Why E wrong: Tumor formation is neoplasia, not atrophy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A peritoneal dialysis client reports that outflow has become brown-coloured. The nurse recognizes this as an indicator of:","options":["Peritonitis","Bowel perforation","Bladder perforation","Normal outflow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brown outflow indicates bowel perforation, a serious complication requiring immediate notification.  Why A wrong: Cloudy outflow indicates peritonitis.  Why C wrong: Urine-coloured outflow indicates bladder perforation.  Why D wrong: Brown outflow is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A post-kidney-transplant client has a urine output of 80 ml/hr, temperature of 38°C, rising serum creatinine, and decreasing urine output. The nurse should suspect:","options":["Normal recovery","Graft rejection","Dehydration only","UTI only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased creatinine, elevated temperature, and decreased urine output are early indicators of renal graft rejection; output <100 ml/hr also warrants immediate reporting.  Why A wrong: These are abnormal findings, not normal recovery.  Why C/D wrong: The constellation of rising creatinine, fever, and falling output points to rejection rather than isolated dehydration or UTI."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Nursing care of AV fistula & graft…","question":"A client on haemodialysis suddenly develops chest pain, dyspnea, and cyanosis; the nurse suspects air embolism. After stopping dialysis and positioning the client left-side head-down, the NEXT action is to:","options":["Administer oxygen","Restart dialysis at a lower rate","Place in high-Fowler's","Give IV heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After stopping dialysis and positioning left-side/head-down (Trendelenburg) to trap air in the right atrium, the nurse administers oxygen.  Why B wrong: Dialysis is stopped, not restarted, during an air embolism emergency.  Why C wrong: Head-down/left-side positioning, not high-Fowler's, is required.  Why D wrong: IV heparin is not the specified intervention."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which term is defined as an increase in the SIZE of a cell after full development, exemplified by ventricular hypertrophy?","options":["Hyperplasia","Hypertrophy","Metaplasia","Dysplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertrophy means an increase in the size of a cell after full development; the classic example is ventricular hypertrophy.  Why A wrong: Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells, not their size.  Why C wrong: Metaplasia is replacement of one cell type with another.  Why D wrong: Dysplasia is abnormal change in size, shape and organization of cells."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Increase in the NUMBER of cells due to increased cell division (mitosis) best defines which cellular change?","options":["Hypertrophy","Atrophy","Hyperplasia","Metaplasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of a cell due to increased cell division (mitosis).  Why A wrong: Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, not number.  Why B wrong: Atrophy is a decrease in cell size/number.  Why D wrong: Metaplasia is replacement of one cell type with another."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which of the following is given as an example of hyperplasia?","options":["Ventricular enlargement","Growth & multiplication of milk-secreting glandular cells in the breast and endometrial hyperplasia","Skeletal muscle wasting in immobility","Replacement of columnar with squamous epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists growth & multiplication of milk-secreting glandular cells in the breast and endometrial hyperplasia as examples of hyperplasia.  Why A wrong: Ventricular enlargement is the example given for hypertrophy.  Why C wrong: Muscle wasting reflects atrophy.  Why D wrong: Epithelial replacement is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Abnormal changes in the size, shape and organization of a cell describes:","options":["Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Dysplasia","Hypertrophy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dysplasia is defined as abnormal changes in size, shape and organization of the cell.  Why A wrong: Metaplasia is replacement of one cell type with another.  Why B wrong: Hyperplasia is increased cell number.  Why D wrong: Hypertrophy is increased cell size."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The replacement of one type of cell with another type of cell is termed:","options":["Dysplasia","Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metaplasia means replacement of one type of cell with another type of cell.  Why A wrong: Dysplasia is disordered size/shape/organization of cells.  Why C wrong: Hyperplasia is increased cell number.  Why D wrong: Atrophy is reduction in cell size/number."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The non-neoplastic cell changes illustrated (using haematoxylin-eosin staining) include which of the following adaptations derived from a normal cell? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Atrophy","Hyperplasia","Hypertrophy","Metaplasia","Dysplasia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The figure of non-neoplastic cell changes (haematoxylin-eosin staining) shows a normal cell adapting into atrophy, hyperplasia, hypertrophy, metaplasia and dysplasia.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: All five are depicted as the labelled non-neoplastic cellular changes."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the primary action of chemotherapy?","options":["It stimulates proliferation of neoplastic cells","It kills or inhibits the reproduction of neoplastic cells and also kills normal cells","It affects only cancer cells, sparing all normal cells","It uses radioactive isotopes placed inside the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemotherapy kills or inhibits the reproduction of neoplastic cells and also kills normal cells.  Why A wrong: It inhibits, not stimulates, neoplastic cells.  Why C wrong: Normal cells are also affected by chemotherapy.  Why D wrong: That describes internal radiation (brachytherapy), not chemotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are common side effects of chemotherapy? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fatigue","Nausea & vomiting","Mucositis","Alopecia","Myelosuppression"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The common side effects listed are fatigue, nausea & vomiting, mucositis, alopecia and myelosuppression.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: All five are explicitly named as common chemotherapy side effects because normal cells are also affected."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Why must the nurse closely monitor the IV infusion site during chemotherapy?","options":["Because chemotherapeutic agents lower blood glucose","Because there is a greater chance of infiltration of the veins with chemotherapeutic agents","Because the agents cause hypertension","Because the site must be exposed to sunlight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The IV site is monitored because with chemotherapeutic agents there is more chance of infiltration of the veins.  Why A wrong: Hypoglycemia is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not cited as the monitoring rationale.  Why D wrong: Sunlight exposure is unrelated and is actually avoided in radiation skin care."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Protective isolation should be initiated for a chemotherapy client when the WBC count decreases below which value?","options":["Below 5000/mm3","Below 3000/mm3","Below 1000/mm3","Below 500/mm3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Protective isolation is initiated if the WBC count decreases below 1000/mm3.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated threshold of below 1000/mm3."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In a chemotherapy client, why should anticoagulants be avoided?","options":["Because chemotherapy raises the platelet count","Because platelets are decreased due to chemotherapy, increasing bleeding risk","Because anticoagulants cause alopecia","Because they raise WBC counts dangerously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse observes for signs of bleeding and avoids anticoagulants because of decreased platelets due to chemotherapy.  Why A wrong: Chemotherapy decreases, not raises, platelets.  Why C wrong: Anticoagulants are not linked to alopecia here.  Why D wrong: The concern is bleeding from low platelets, not WBC changes."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"To prevent bleeding in a chemotherapy client, the nurse should avoid which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Rectal thermometer","Enemas","IM injections","Oral fluids","Deep breathing exercises"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding is prevented by avoiding rectal thermometers, enemas and IM injections.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed as measures to prevent bleeding.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids are not a bleeding risk and are not restricted.  Why E wrong: Deep breathing is not a bleeding-prevention restriction."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"During chemotherapy, the nurse should monitor which body functions and parameters? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Renal function","Cardiac function","Vital parameters","Bone density"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors renal function, cardiac functions and vital parameters.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Bone density monitoring is not listed for chemotherapy care here."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the main goal of radiation therapy?","options":["To destroy cancer cells with minimal exposure of normal cells to radiation's damaging effects","To increase the size of normal cells","To deliver chemotherapy intravenously","To stimulate bone marrow production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Radiation therapy destroys cancer cells with minimal exposure of the normal cells to the damaging effects of radiation.  Why B wrong: It does not aim to enlarge normal cells.  Why C wrong: That is chemotherapy, not radiation.  Why D wrong: It does not aim to stimulate marrow."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON side effect of radiation therapy?","options":["Alopecia","Fatigue","Altered taste sensation","Skin irritation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fatigue is the most common side effect of radiation therapy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Alopecia, altered taste sensation and irritation are listed as other side effects, not the most common one."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"External beam radiation therapy, in which the actual source of radiation is placed OUTSIDE the client, is also known as:","options":["Brachytherapy","Teletherapy","Chemotherapy","Cryotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: External beam therapy is also known as teletherapy, in which the source of radiation is placed outside the client.  Why A wrong: Brachytherapy is internal radiation (source implanted inside).  Why C wrong: Chemotherapy uses cytotoxic drugs, not external beam radiation.  Why D wrong: Cryotherapy destroys tissue by freezing."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Internal radiation therapy, in which the actual source of radiation is IMPLANTED inside the client, is also known as:","options":["Teletherapy","Brachytherapy","Radical dissection","Conization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Internal radiation therapy is known as brachytherapy, where the radiation source is implanted inside the client.  Why A wrong: Teletherapy is external beam (source outside the client).  Why C wrong: Radical dissection is a surgical procedure.  Why D wrong: Conization is a cervical surgical procedure."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In a client with an immunocompromised condition, what is the major cause of death?","options":["Bleeding","Infection","Fatigue","Alopecia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection is the major cause of death in the client with an immunocompromised condition.  Why A/C/D wrong: Bleeding, fatigue and alopecia are complications/side effects but are not cited as the major cause of death."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A sealed/internal radiation implant becomes dislodged. What is the nurse's FIRST action?","options":["Pick up the source with a bare hand and reinsert it","Encourage the client to lie still until the radioactive source has been placed in a safe, closed lead container","Notify the radiologist and leave the room immediately","Flush the source down the toilet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first action is to encourage the client to lie still until the radioactive source is placed in a safe, closed lead container.  Why A wrong: The source must never be touched with a bare/open hand; long-handled forceps are used.  Why C wrong: Notifying the radiologist/doctor comes later, after securing the source.  Why D wrong: Flushing applies to unsealed-source excreta, not a dislodged sealed implant."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What device must a nurse use to retrieve a dislodged radioactive source?","options":["Sterile gloved hand","A long-handled forceps","A magnet","A suction catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A long-handled forceps is used to retrieve the radioactive source; the source must not be touched with a direct/open hand.  Why A wrong: Even a gloved hand must not directly touch the source.  Why C wrong: A magnet is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Suction is not used to retrieve a radioactive implant."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After a dislodged radioactive source is retrieved with forceps, where should it be deposited?","options":["In the biohazard sharps bin","In a lead container that should be kept in the client's room","In the hallway waste chute","Back into the client's body cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source is deposited in a lead container that should be kept in the client's room.  Why A wrong: A standard sharps bin does not shield radiation.  Why C wrong: It must not be discarded in a waste chute.  Why D wrong: Reinsertion is a physician's task, not the nurse's emergency response."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After securing a dislodged radioactive source, the nurse should:","options":["Discharge the client","Notify the radiologist or doctor","Remove all shielding","Administer anticoagulants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After lying the client still and securing the source, the nurse should notify the radiologist or doctor.  Why A wrong: The client is not discharged in this emergency.  Why C wrong: Shielding should remain, not be removed.  Why D wrong: Anticoagulants are unrelated and generally avoided in cancer clients with low platelets."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"For a client receiving radiation for cervical cancer, the correct positioning and care includes:","options":["High Fowler's position with a high-residue diet","Supine with head of bed flat or only slightly elevated, maintain patency of the urinary catheter, and provide a low-residue diet","Prone position with the urinary catheter clamped","Trendelenburg position with a high-fiber diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For cervical cancer radiation, keep the client supine with the head of the bed flat or only slightly elevated, maintain urinary catheter patency, and provide a low-residue diet.  Why A wrong: High Fowler's and high-residue diet are opposite to the recommendation.  Why C wrong: Prone positioning with a clamped catheter is not advised.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg and high-fiber diet are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"For a client receiving radiation via UNSEALED sources, which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It may include radioisotopes given orally, IM, IV, or by direct instillation into a body cavity","The isotope may be excreted in body wastes","The client should be instructed to flush the toilet several times after each use","The source is always a solid implant that never leaves the body"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Unsealed-source radiation may include radioisotopes given orally, IM, IV, or by direct instillation; the isotope may be excreted in body wastes; and the client should flush the toilet several times after each use.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed for unsealed sources.  Why D wrong: Unsealed sources are NOT solid permanent implants; the isotope is excreted in wastes."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"How should the irradiated skin area be washed during external radiation therapy?","options":["Scrubbed vigorously with hot water","Gently each day with warm water alone or with mild soap and water","Cleaned only with alcohol swabs","Left completely dry and never washed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wash the irradiated area gently each day with warm water alone or with mild soap and water.  Why A wrong: Vigorous scrubbing and hot water damage irradiated skin.  Why C wrong: Alcohol is drying and irritating; not recommended.  Why D wrong: Gentle daily washing is advised, not avoidance."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Regarding topical products on skin at the radiation site during external radiation therapy, the nurse should teach the client to:","options":["Apply powders and creams liberally","Not use powders, ointments, lotions, or creams unless ordered by the radiologist","Use only perfumed lotions","Apply sunscreen and then expose the area to sunlight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not use powders, ointments, lotions, or creams on the skin at the radiation site unless ordered by the radiologist (PGI 2018).  Why A wrong: Liberal use is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Perfumed lotions are irritating and not permitted.  Why D wrong: Sun exposure of the irradiated area is avoided."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which clothing advice is correct for a client undergoing external radiation therapy? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Wear soft clothing over the skin at the radiation site","Avoid belts, buckles, straps, or any clothing that rubs or binds the skin at the radiation site","Wear tight elastic garments to compress the area","Wear rough wool directly over the site"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Wear soft clothing over the radiation site and avoid belts, buckles, straps, or any clothing that rubs or binds the skin.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly instructed.  Why C wrong: Tight compressive garments rub/bind the site and are avoided.  Why D wrong: Rough wool would irritate the irradiated skin."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"During external radiation therapy, the nurse should teach the client to avoid: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Exposure of the irradiated area to the sun","Heat exposure","Warm-water bathing","Wearing soft clothes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The client should avoid sun exposure of the irradiated area and avoid heat exposure.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly listed to protect irradiated skin.  Why C wrong: Gentle warm-water washing is actually recommended.  Why D wrong: Soft clothing is recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"For areas of skin breakdown at the radiation site, the nurse should apply:","options":["A tight adhesive bandage","A non-adherent dressing","Powder and leave open","An occlusive petroleum dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apply a non-adherent dressing to areas of skin breakdown.  Why A wrong: Tight adhesive dressings can damage fragile irradiated skin.  Why C wrong: Powders are contraindicated at the radiation site.  Why D wrong: An occlusive petroleum dressing is not the recommended measure."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"During external radiation therapy, at what platelet count is the client at risk for bleeding?","options":["When platelets fall below 150,000 cells/mm3","When platelets fall below 50,000 cells/mm3","When platelets fall below 100,000 cells/mm3","When platelets fall below 5,000 cells/mm3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client is at risk for bleeding when the platelet count falls below 50,000 cells/mm3.  Why A/C wrong: These are higher than the stated 50,000 threshold.  Why D wrong: 5,000 is far lower than the stated bleeding-risk threshold."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Spontaneous bleeding frequently occurs, and the client may need platelet transfusion, when the platelet count falls lower than:","options":["50,000 cells/mm3","100,000 cells/mm3","20,000 cells/mm3","75,000 cells/mm3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Spontaneous bleeding frequently occurs when the platelet count is lower than 20,000 cells/mm3, and the client may need platelet transfusion.  Why A wrong: 50,000 is the threshold for being \"at risk,\" not for spontaneous bleeding.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 20,000 spontaneous-bleeding threshold."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of multiple myeloma?","options":["Elevated CA-125","Bence Jones protein present in the blood and urine","Coin lesions on chest X-ray","Bence Jones protein absent from urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of multiple myeloma is Bence Jones protein present in the blood and urine.  Why A wrong: CA-125 is a marker for ovarian cancer.  Why C wrong: Coin lesions relate to lung cancer.  Why D wrong: Bence Jones protein is present, not absent, in multiple myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Where should a client receiving internal radiation therapy be placed?","options":["In an open ward near other clients","In a private room with a private bath","In the ICU with all visitors welcome","In a shared room with a pregnant nurse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client is placed in a private room with a private bath.  Why A wrong: Open ward placement risks exposing others to radiation.  Why C wrong: Unrestricted visitors are contraindicated.  Why D wrong: A pregnant nurse must never care for a client with a radiation implant."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"How long should each care provider limit time in the room of a client with an internal radiation implant per shift?","options":["60 minutes","30 minutes","15 minutes","10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Limit time to 30 minutes per care provider per shift (PGI-2016).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 30-minute limit per care provider per shift."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which devices does a nurse use when caring for a client with an internal radiation implant? (Select all that apply.)","options":["A dosimeter film badge to measure radiation exposure","A lead shield to reduce transmission of radiation","A cardiac monitor at all times","A caution sign on the client's door"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse wears a dosimeter film badge, wears a lead shield, and places a caution sign on the client's door.  Why A/B/D correct: All three are explicitly listed for internal radiation safety.  Why C wrong: A cardiac monitor is not a stated radiation-safety measure."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Regarding a nurse caring for clients with radiation implants, which statement is TRUE?","options":["The nurse may care for several implant clients simultaneously","The nurse should never care for more than one client with a radiation implant at one time","A pregnant nurse may care for implant clients","Children under 16 may freely visit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should never care for more than one client with a radiation implant at one time.  Why A wrong: Caring for multiple implant clients increases cumulative exposure and is prohibited.  Why C wrong: A pregnant nurse must not care for a client with a radiation implant.  Why D wrong: Children younger than 16 must not visit an implant client."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which visitor restrictions apply to a client with an internal radiation implant? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Do not allow a pregnant nurse to care for the client","Do not allow children younger than 16 years or a pregnant woman to visit","Limit visitors to 30 minutes per day, at least 6 feet from the source","Visitors may sit directly beside the client for hours"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A pregnant nurse must not care for the client; children under 16 and pregnant women must not visit; and visitors are limited to 30 minutes per day and should stay at least 6 feet from the source.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly stated safety restrictions.  Why D wrong: Prolonged close proximity increases exposure and is prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"When caring for a client with an internal radiation implant, how should linens and dressings be handled?","options":["Discard them immediately in the regular trash","Save the linens and dressings until the source is removed","Send them to laundry every hour","Burn them at the bedside"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Save the linens and dressings until the source is removed (to ensure a dislodged source is not accidentally discarded).  Why A wrong: Discarding immediately risks disposing of a dislodged radioactive source.  Why C wrong: Frequent laundering is contraindicated until the source is confirmed removed.  Why D wrong: Burning is unsafe and not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which statement about other (non-linen) equipment in an internal radiation client's room is correct?","options":["It can never be removed","Other equipment can be removed from the room at any time","It must be buried","It must be kept for one year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Other equipment (unlike linens/dressings) can be removed from the room at any time.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated rule that other equipment may be removed at any time."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Leukemia is best defined as:","options":["A solid tumor of the lung","A type of blood cancer with uncontrolled proliferation of WBCs","A benign tumor of the uterus","A skin malignancy caused by sun exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leukemia is a type of blood cancer in which uncontrolled proliferation of WBCs occurs.  Why A wrong: A lung solid tumor is lung cancer.  Why C wrong: A benign uterine tumor is a fibroid.  Why D wrong: A sun-related skin malignancy is skin cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"According to the text, how many types of leukemia are classified based on the WBCs affected?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are 4 types of leukemia as per the WBCs affected (ALL, CLL, AML, CML).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated total of 4 types."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which type of leukemia is MOST COMMONLY seen in children?","options":["Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)","Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL)","Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)","Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL) is most commonly seen in children (AIIMS Patna-2020, ESIC-2024).  Why A wrong: CLL is most common in adults.  Why C wrong: CML involves abnormal production of granulocytes.  Why D wrong: AML occurs throughout life."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which type of leukemia is MOST COMMONLY seen in adults?","options":["Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL)","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)","Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)","Medulloblastoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is most commonly seen in adults.  Why A wrong: ALL is most common in children.  Why C wrong: AML occurs throughout life.  Why D wrong: Medulloblastoma is a brain tumor, not a leukemia."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which type of leukemia is characterized as occurring THROUGHOUT LIFE?","options":["Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL)","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)","Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)","Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) occurs throughout life.  Why A wrong: ALL is most common in children.  Why B wrong: CLL is most common in adults.  Why D wrong: CML is characterized by abnormal production of granulocytes."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is characterized by:","options":["Abnormal production of granulocytes","Being most common in children","Fluid-filled cysts of the breast","Bence Jones protein in urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CML is characterized by abnormal production of granulocytes.  Why B wrong: ALL is most common in children.  Why C wrong: Fluid-filled cysts describe fibrocystic breast disease.  Why D wrong: Bence Jones protein is the cardinal sign of multiple myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of leukemia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Anaemia and thrombocytopenia","Elevated leukocytes, petechiae, and ecchymosis","Gingival bleeding, fever, and infection","Coin lesions on chest X-ray"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Leukemia signs include anaemia and thrombocytopenia; elevated leukocytes, petechiae, and ecchymosis; and gingival bleeding, fever, and infection.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Coin lesions relate to lung cancer, not leukemia."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Nursing care for leukemia includes which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Chemotherapy and transfusion of whole blood","Bone marrow transplant and general nursing care","Radical neck dissection","Cryosurgery of the cervix"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Leukemia nursing care includes chemotherapy and whole blood transfusion, plus bone marrow transplant and general nursing care.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly listed.  Why C wrong: Radical neck dissection is a laryngeal cancer surgery.  Why D wrong: Cryosurgery of the cervix relates to cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which statement about the epidemiology of lung cancer is correct?","options":["It is the least common cancer in men","It is the most common type of cancer in men worldwide, accounting for 16% of total cancer, and has the highest mortality rate","It never metastasizes","It is a benign tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lung cancer is the leading type of cancer, the most common type in men worldwide (16% of total cancer), and the most common fatal/high-mortality cancer.  Why A wrong: It is the most common, not least common, in men.  Why C wrong: It is malignant and can metastasize.  Why D wrong: It is a carcinoma (malignant), not benign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON risk factor for lung cancer?","options":["Asbestos","Smoking","Air pollution","Radiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking is the most common risk factor for lung cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: While these are recognized risks, the text specifies smoking as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON histological type of lung cancer?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of lung cancer.  Why A wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common laryngeal type, not the most common lung type per the text.  Why B wrong: Small cell carcinoma is not the type cited as most common here.  Why D wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is a skin cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of lung cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dyspnoea, chest pain, and fatigue","Persistent cough and change in voice quality","Haemoptysis, wheeze, and weight loss","Clubbing of fingers","Chest X-ray revealing pleural effusion and coin lesions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed features—dyspnoea, chest pain, fatigue, persistent cough, voice change, haemoptysis, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing of fingers, and chest X-ray showing pleural effusion and coin lesions—are stated signs and symptoms of lung cancer.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"How is the diagnosis of lung cancer confirmed?","options":["Pap test","Biopsy or FNAC","Bence Jones protein test","BSE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmation of diagnosis of lung cancer is done by biopsy or FNAC.  Why A wrong: Pap test is a cervical cancer screening test.  Why C wrong: Bence Jones protein relates to multiple myeloma.  Why D wrong: BSE (breast self-examination) is a breast cancer screening method."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Match the lung cancer surgery to its description: removal of one entire lung is called:","options":["Lobectomy","Wedge resection","Pneumonectomy","Thoracentesis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonectomy is removal of one entire lung.  Why A wrong: Lobectomy is removal of one lobe when the lesion is limited to one lobe.  Why B wrong: Wedge resection is removal of a segment when the lesion is limited to one segment.  Why D wrong: Thoracentesis is done for pleural effusion, not lung removal."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In lung cancer, removal of one lobe when the lesion is limited to one lobe is called:","options":["Lobectomy","Pneumonectomy","Wedge resection","Radical neck dissection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lobectomy is removal of one lobe when the lesion is limited to one lobe.  Why B wrong: Pneumonectomy removes an entire lung.  Why C wrong: Wedge resection removes a segment.  Why D wrong: Radical neck dissection is a laryngeal surgery."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Removal of a segment when the lesion is limited to one segment is termed:","options":["Pneumonectomy","Wedge resection","Lobectomy","Thoracentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wedge resection is removal of a segment when the lesion is limited to one segment.  Why A wrong: Pneumonectomy removes an entire lung.  Why C wrong: Lobectomy removes an entire lobe.  Why D wrong: Thoracentesis drains pleural fluid."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which procedure is performed for pleural effusion in lung cancer?","options":["Lobectomy","Thoracentesis","Conization","Craniotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thoracentesis is done for pleural effusion.  Why A wrong: Lobectomy removes a lobe, not fluid.  Why C wrong: Conization is a cervical procedure.  Why D wrong: Craniotomy is a brain surgery."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In which position should a lung cancer client be placed as part of nursing care?","options":["Trendelenburg","Fowler's or semi-Fowler's position","Prone","Flat supine only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the client in Fowler's or semi-Fowler's position to facilitate breathing.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is not indicated and can worsen respiratory effort.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the recommended position.  Why D wrong: Flat supine only is not recommended for optimal lung expansion."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which nursing measures are appropriate for a lung cancer client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor vital signs, breath sounds, and oxygen saturation","Encourage coughing and deep breathing","Encourage a high-calorie or high-protein diet with supplementation","Restrict all fluids and protein"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring vital signs/breath sounds/oxygen saturation, encouraging coughing and deep breathing, and encouraging a high-calorie/high-protein diet with supplementation.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: A high-calorie/high-protein diet is encouraged, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After a pneumonectomy, how should the client be positioned and what complication should be monitored?","options":["On the non-operative side; monitor for hypoglycemia","On the operative side to promote lung expansion of the remaining lung or facilitate drainage; monitor for mediastinal shifting (displacement of trachea toward the operative side)","Prone; monitor for pressure ulcers only","Supine with head flat; monitor for constipation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After pneumonectomy, position the client on the operative side to promote lung expansion of the remaining lung or facilitate drainage, and monitor for mediastinal shifting (displacement of trachea toward the operative side).  Why A wrong: Positioning is on the operative side, and hypoglycemia is not the concern.  Why C wrong: Prone positioning is not indicated and misses the mediastinal shift concern.  Why D wrong: This positioning and complication do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Larynx cancer (carcinoma of the larynx) involves which structures? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Vocal cords","Epiglottis","Laryngeal cartilage","Renal pelvis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Carcinoma of the larynx includes the vocal cords, epiglottis, and laryngeal cartilage.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: The renal pelvis is a urinary structure, unrelated to laryngeal cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON type of laryngeal carcinoma?","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of laryngeal carcinoma is squamous cell carcinoma (AIIMS-2018).  Why A wrong: Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer type.  Why C wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is a skin cancer.  Why D wrong: Small cell carcinoma is not the type cited for the larynx."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are risk factors for laryngeal cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cigarette smoking","Second-hand smoke","Air pollution and asbestos","Age greater than 50 years"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include cigarette smoking, second-hand smoke, air pollution, asbestos, and incidence at greater than 50 years of age.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed as risk factors/incidence details."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign of laryngeal cancer?","options":["Weight loss","Increased hoarseness","Sore throat","Enlarged cervical lymph node"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased hoarseness is the cardinal sign of laryngeal cancer.  Why A wrong: Weight loss is a symptom but not the cardinal sign.  Why C wrong: Sore throat is a symptom, not the cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: Enlarged cervical lymph node is a symptom, not the cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of laryngeal cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Sore throat","Dyspnoea and dysphasia","Increased hoarseness","Weight loss and enlarged cervical lymph node"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs/symptoms include sore throat, dyspnoea and dysphasia, increased hoarseness, weight loss, and enlarged cervical lymph node.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed as signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In the management of larynx cancer, brachytherapy refers to:","options":["Source of radiation externally inserted","Source of radiation internally inserted","Chemotherapy given orally","Removal of the entire larynx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brachytherapy is the source of radiation internally inserted.  Why A wrong: Externally inserted radiation is teletherapy.  Why C wrong: Oral chemotherapy is not radiation therapy.  Why D wrong: Total larynx removal is total laryngectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In the management of larynx cancer, teletherapy refers to:","options":["Source of radiation internally inserted","Source of radiation externally inserted","Removal of one vocal cord","A cone-shaped removal of the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Teletherapy is the source of radiation externally inserted.  Why A wrong: Internally inserted radiation is brachytherapy.  Why C wrong: Removal of one vocal cord is partial laryngectomy.  Why D wrong: A cone-shaped cervical removal is conization."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Partial laryngectomy involves:","options":["Removal of the total larynx with a permanent tracheal stoma","Removal of one vocal cord and part of the larynx with the tumor","Removal of the larynx, surrounding tissues, muscles, lymph nodes, and gland","Removal of the entire lung"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Partial laryngectomy is removal of one vocal cord and part of the larynx with the tumor.  Why A wrong: That describes total laryngectomy.  Why C wrong: That describes radical neck dissection.  Why D wrong: Lung removal is a pneumonectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Total laryngectomy is described as:","options":["Removal of one vocal cord and part of the larynx","Removal of the total larynx with construction of a permanent tracheal stoma","Removal of surrounding lymph nodes only","A cone-shaped removal of cervical tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total laryngectomy is removal of the total larynx with construction of a permanent tracheal stoma.  Why A wrong: That is partial laryngectomy.  Why C wrong: Lymph node removal alone is not total laryngectomy.  Why D wrong: That describes conization of the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Radical neck dissection involves removal of:","options":["One vocal cord only","The larynx, surrounding tissues and muscles, lymph nodes, and gland, with a permanent tracheal stoma","The uterus and ovaries","One lobe of the lung"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Radical neck dissection is removal of the larynx, surrounding tissues and muscles, lymph node, and gland, with a permanent tracheal stoma.  Why A wrong: Removal of one vocal cord is partial laryngectomy.  Why C wrong: Uterus/ovary removal is a gynecologic surgery.  Why D wrong: Lobe removal is a lobectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which nursing measures are appropriate for a laryngeal cancer client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Encourage the client to express feelings","Establish methods of communication to be used after surgery","Observe for signs of haemorrhage","Observe for airway obstruction by mucus plugs, oedema, or blood"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes encouraging expression of feelings, establishing post-surgery communication methods, observing for haemorrhage, and observing for airway obstruction by mucus plugs, oedema, or blood.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed nursing care points."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which findings indicate a sign of haemorrhage in a post-laryngectomy client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Frequent swallowing","Increased pulse rate and drop in blood pressure","Cool clammy skin and blood on the dressing","Bradycardia and hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of haemorrhage include frequent swallowing, increased pulse rate, drop in blood pressure, cool clammy skin, and blood on the dressing.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Haemorrhage produces increased pulse and dropping BP, not bradycardia with hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which findings are signs of airway obstruction in a laryngeal cancer client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Restlessness and hunger","Dyspnoea, tachycardia, tachypnoea","Gurgling and cyanosis","Bradypnoea with pink skin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of airway obstruction include restlessness, hunger (air hunger), dyspnoea, tachycardia, tachypnoea, gurgling, and cyanosis.  Why A/B/C correct: All three groupings are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Airway obstruction causes cyanosis and rapid breathing, not slow breathing with pink skin."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Client education for a laryngectomy client with a tracheal stoma includes avoiding activities that permit water or irritating substances to enter the trachea. Which activities should be avoided? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Showers","Swimming","Dust and hair spray exposure","Wearing clothes with constricting collars or necklines"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clients should avoid activities that permit water or irritants to enter the trachea (showers, swimming, dust, hair spray) and avoid clothes with constricting collars or necklines.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed patient-education points."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A brain tumor located within the cerebral hemisphere is classified as:","options":["Infratentorial","Supratentorial","Extradural","Spinal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Supratentorial tumors are located within the cerebral hemisphere.  Why A wrong: Infratentorial tumors are within the brainstem and cerebellum.  Why C wrong: Extradural is not the classification given.  Why D wrong: Spinal is not the given brain tumor location category."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A brain tumor located within the brainstem and cerebellum is classified as:","options":["Supratentorial","Infratentorial","Cortical","Meningeal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infratentorial tumors are within the brainstem and cerebellum.  Why A wrong: Supratentorial tumors are within the cerebral hemisphere.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the given location categories."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON type of brain tumor, originating in the glial cells or neuroglia and usually malignant?","options":["Meningioma","Glioma","Acoustic neuroma","Medulloblastoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glioma is the most common type of brain tumor, originating in glial cells/neuroglia, and is usually malignant.  Why A wrong: Meningioma originates in the meninges and is usually benign.  Why C wrong: Acoustic neuroma is a benign tumor of the 8th cranial nerve.  Why D wrong: Medulloblastoma is the most common tumor in children (malignant), but glioma is the overall most common."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which of the following are subtypes/examples of glioma? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Astrocytoma","Glioblastoma","Oligodendroglioma","Meningioma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The glioma subtypes listed are astrocytoma, glioblastoma, and oligodendroglioma.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed under glioma.  Why D wrong: Meningioma is a separate tumor originating in the meninges, not a glioma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Meningioma is a brain tumor that:","options":["Originates in the meninges and is usually benign","Originates in glial cells and is malignant","Arises from the 8th cranial nerve","Is the most common tumor in children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meningioma originates in the meninges of the brain and is usually benign.  Why B wrong: Glioma originates in glial cells and is usually malignant.  Why C wrong: The 8th cranial nerve tumor is acoustic neuroma.  Why D wrong: The most common tumor in children is medulloblastoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Acoustic neuroma is a tumor of which cranial nerve, and what is its nature?","options":["5th cranial nerve; malignant","8th cranial nerve; benign","7th cranial nerve; malignant","10th cranial nerve; benign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acoustic neuroma is a tumor of the 8th cranial nerve and is benign.  Why A/C/D wrong: The correct nerve is the 8th and the tumor is benign, not the nerves or natures listed."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which brain tumor is the MOST COMMON type in children and is malignant?","options":["Glioma","Meningioma","Acoustic neuroma","Medulloblastoma"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Medulloblastoma is the most common type of tumor in children and is malignant.  Why A wrong: Glioma is the overall most common brain tumor type, not specifically in children.  Why B wrong: Meningioma is usually benign.  Why C wrong: Acoustic neuroma is a benign 8th nerve tumor."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign of a brain tumor related to headache?","options":["Headache that decreases when lying down","Headache that increases when taken in the supine position","Headache only on standing","Headache relieved by coughing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign is a headache that increases when the client is in the supine position.  Why A wrong: The headache increases (not decreases) when supine.  Why C wrong: The described worsening is with the supine position, not standing.  Why D wrong: Coughing (a Valsalva maneuver) raises ICP and would worsen, not relieve, the headache."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Papilledema in a brain tumor client refers to:","options":["Swelling of the optic disk due to increased ICP","Swelling of the tongue","Bleeding of the gums","Enlarged cervical lymph node"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Papilledema is swelling of the optic disk due to increased ICP.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated findings and do not describe papilledema."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which parameters should the nurse monitor in a brain tumor client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["All vital signs","Neurological status including LOC and GCS","Increased ICP","CA-125 tumor marker"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse monitors all vitals and neurological status including LOC, GCS, and increased ICP.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: CA-125 is an ovarian cancer marker, not a brain tumor monitoring parameter."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After craniotomy surgery, the client's head should be kept:","options":["Flat at all times","Elevated not more than 30 degrees","In Trendelenburg position","Elevated to 90 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After craniotomy surgery, keep the head elevated not more than 30 degrees.  Why A wrong: Flat positioning is not specified as the recommendation.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg increases ICP and is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: 90-degree elevation exceeds the stated 30-degree limit."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The nurse should teach a brain tumor client to avoid activities that increase ICP. Which activities should be avoided? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Valsalva maneuver","Coughing and sneezing","Lifting and straining at stool","Isometric exercises"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Activities that increase ICP and should be avoided include the Valsalva maneuver, coughing, sneezing, lifting, straining at stool, and isometric exercises.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed as ICP-raising activities to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of skin cancer?","options":["Genetic mutation","Excessive exposure to the sun","Cigarette smoking","HPV infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of skin cancer is excessive exposure to the sun.  Why A wrong: Genetics is not cited as the most common cause here.  Why C wrong: Smoking is a risk for lung/laryngeal cancer.  Why D wrong: HPV is the most common cause of cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the confirmative diagnostic test for skin cancer?","options":["Pap test","Skin biopsy","Mammogram","FNAC of lymph node"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis (confirmative) of skin cancer is by skin biopsy.  Why A wrong: Pap test screens for cervical cancer.  Why C wrong: Mammogram is a breast imaging test.  Why D wrong: FNAC of a lymph node is not the stated confirmative skin cancer test."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON type of skin cancer, usually a cancer of the epidermis?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Basal cell carcinoma is the most common type of skin cancer and is usually a cancer of the epidermis.  Why A wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma involves squamous cells/keratinocytes and metastasizes to nearby lymph nodes.  Why C wrong: Malignant melanoma is the most severe type but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer, not skin cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the skin is a cancer of squamous cells of the epidermis or keratinocytes and characteristically:","options":["Never metastasizes","Metastasizes to surrounding lymph nodes","Is the most common skin cancer","Occurs only on the scalp"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Squamous cell carcinoma metastasizes to surrounding lymph nodes.  Why A wrong: It does metastasize to nearby lymph nodes.  Why C wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer.  Why D wrong: The text does not restrict it to the scalp."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which type of skin cancer is highly metastatic to the brain and liver, with less chance of survival, and is the MOST SEVERE type?","options":["Basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Ductal ectasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Malignant melanoma occurs anywhere on the body, is highly metastatic to the brain and liver with less chance of survival, and is the most severe type of skin cancer.  Why A wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is the most common but least aggressive.  Why B wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma metastasizes to nearby lymph nodes but is not described as the most severe.  Why D wrong: Ductal ectasia is a benign breast condition, not a skin cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of skin cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Change in colour, size, or shape of pre-existing lesions","Pruritis","Local soreness","Haemoptysis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Skin cancer signs include change in colour/size/shape of pre-existing lesions, pruritis, and local soreness.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Haemoptysis is a lung cancer symptom."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"As part of skin cancer nursing care/prevention, the nurse should teach clients to avoid sun exposure during which hours?","options":["Between 6 am and 8 am","Between 10 am and 4 pm","Between 8 pm and 10 pm","Between 4 pm and 6 pm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Teach clients about risk factors and preventive measures and to avoid sun exposure between 10 am and 4 pm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 10 am–4 pm avoidance window."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON type of cervical cancer?","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of cervical cancer is squamous cell carcinoma.  Why A wrong: Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer.  Why C wrong: Basal cell carcinoma is a skin cancer.  Why D wrong: Small cell carcinoma is not the type cited for cervix."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Regarding cervical cancer, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is a malignant cancer of the cervix","Pre-invasive cancer is limited to the cervix","There is a high cure rate if diagnosed early","Internal and external radiation and laser surgery may be required"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical cancer is a malignant cancer of the cervix; pre-invasive cancer is limited to the cervix; it has a high cure rate if diagnosed early; and internal/external radiation and laser surgery may be required.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly stated in the overview."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of cervical cancer, accounting for about 80% of cases?","options":["Cigarette smoking","Human Papilloma Virus (HPV)","Oral contraceptive pills","Diethylstilbestrol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Human Papilloma Virus (HPV) accounts for 80% and is the most common cause of cervical cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cigarette smoking, OCPs, and DES are listed as risk factors but not the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are risk factors for cervical cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cigarette smoking","Multiple sex partners, STIs/STDs, and OCP use","Multiple pregnancies and exposure to HIV and HSV","Exposure to DES (Diethylstilbestrol) and early first intercourse (before 16 years)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include cigarette smoking; multiple sex partners, STIs/STDs, OCP; multiple pregnancies and HIV/HSV exposure; DES exposure; and early first intercourse before 16 years.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign in the clinical features of cervical cancer?","options":["Painless vaginal postmenstrual bleeding only","Spotting between menstrual periods and after intercourse","Lengthening of the menstrual cycle","Leg pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spotting between menstrual periods and after intercourse is designated the cardinal sign.  Why A wrong: Painless post-menstrual/post-coital bleeding is a clinical feature but not labelled the cardinal sign.  Why C wrong: Lengthening of the menstrual cycle is a feature, not the cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: Leg pain is a feature, not the cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which clinical feature of cervical cancer is described as an EARLY sign?","options":["Foul-smelling vaginal discharge","Dysuria and painless haematuria","Leakage of urine and faeces from the vagina","Pelvic and lower back pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dysuria and painless haematuria are described as an early sign of cervical cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are clinical features but are not designated the early sign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are clinical features of cervical cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Painless vaginal postmenstrual or post-coital bleeding","Foul-smelling or sero-sanguineous vaginal discharge","Pelvic, lower back, leg, or groin pain","Leakage of urine and faeces from the vagina"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features include painless postmenstrual/post-coital bleeding, foul-smelling/sero-sanguineous discharge, pelvic/lower-back/leg/groin pain, and leakage of urine and faeces from the vagina.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed clinical features."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The Pap test for cervical cancer is best described as:","options":["The confirmative test","The screening test, also known as the Papanicolaou smear test","A surgical procedure","A tumor marker assay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Pap test is the screening test of cervical cancer, also known as the Papanicolaou smear test.  Why A wrong: The confirmative test is cervical biopsy.  Why C wrong: It is a cytology test, not surgery.  Why D wrong: It is not a tumor marker assay."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"According to the source, the Pap test should be done every 3 years after what age range?","options":["15–20 years","21–29 years","30–40 years","40–50 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Pap test should be done every 3 years after 21–29 years of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 21–29 year age reference."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In the Pap test, the cervical sample is taken using a/an:","options":["Arya spatula","Long-handled forceps","Punch biopsy needle","Cotton swab only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Pap test, the sample is taken by an Arya spatula.  Why B wrong: Long-handled forceps are used for retrieving radioactive sources.  Why C wrong: A punch biopsy needle is not the Pap sampling tool cited.  Why D wrong: A cotton swab alone is not the stated instrument."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the confirmative test for cervical cancer?","options":["Pap smear","Cervical biopsy","BSE","Bence Jones protein test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical biopsy is the confirmative test of cervical cancer.  Why A wrong: The Pap test is a screening test, not confirmative.  Why C wrong: BSE is a breast self-examination screening method.  Why D wrong: Bence Jones protein relates to multiple myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"For cervical cancer prevention, how is the HPV vaccine administered and at what ages?","options":["Subcutaneously, 2 doses at 18–20 years","Intramuscularly (IM), 3 doses between 11 and 12 years","Orally, 1 dose at 16 years","Intravenously, 3 doses at 30 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The HPV vaccine is given intramuscularly (IM) as 3 doses between 11 and 12 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: The route, number of doses, or age do not match the stated IM, 3-dose, 11–12-year schedule."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the dose schedule for the HPV vaccine?","options":["0, 1 month, 3 months","0, 2 months, 6 months","0, 6 months, 12 months","0, 3 months, 9 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The HPV vaccine dose schedule is 0, 2 months, 6 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 0/2-month/6-month schedule."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In the staging of cervical cancer, Stage-0 is defined as:","options":["Extension beyond the pelvis to the bladder or rectum","Carcinoma in situ, limited to epithelial cells","Confined to the cervix","Extension to the pelvic sidewall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-0 is carcinoma in situ, limited to epithelial cells.  Why A wrong: That describes Stage-IV.  Why C wrong: Confined to cervix is Stage-I.  Why D wrong: Extension beyond cervix (but not sidewall) is Stage-II."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In cervical cancer staging, the stage in which the cancer is confined to the cervix is:","options":["Stage-0","Stage-I","Stage-II","Stage-III"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-I is confined to the cervix.  Why A wrong: Stage-0 is carcinoma in situ limited to epithelial cells.  Why C wrong: Stage-II extends beyond the cervix but not to the pelvic sidewall.  Why D wrong: Stage-III extends to the pelvis/vagina."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In cervical cancer, which stage extends beyond the cervix but NOT to the pelvic sidewall?","options":["Stage-I","Stage-II","Stage-III","Stage-IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-II extends beyond the cervix but not to the pelvic sidewall.  Why A wrong: Stage-I is confined to the cervix.  Why C wrong: Stage-III extends to the pelvis/vagina.  Why D wrong: Stage-IV extends beyond the pelvis to the bladder or rectum."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Cervical cancer that extends beyond the pelvis to the bladder or rectum is which stage?","options":["Stage-I","Stage-II","Stage-III","Stage-IV"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-IV extends beyond the pelvis to the bladder or rectum.  Why A wrong: Stage-I is confined to the cervix.  Why B wrong: Stage-II extends beyond the cervix but not to the sidewall.  Why C wrong: Stage-III extends to the pelvis/vagina."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Cervical cancer Stage-III is characterized by extension to:","options":["Epithelial cells only","The cervix only","The pelvis to the vagina","The bladder or rectum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage-III extends to the pelvis and to the vagina.  Why A wrong: Epithelial-cell-limited disease is Stage-0.  Why B wrong: Cervix-only is Stage-I.  Why D wrong: Bladder/rectum extension is Stage-IV."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Pan-hysterectomy, also known as hysterosalpingo-oophorectomy, involves removal of:","options":["Uterus only","Uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries","Cervix only","Ovaries only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pan-hysterectomy (hysterosalpingo-oophorectomy) removes the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries; afterward menstruation and ovarian function cease.  Why A wrong: Removal of the uterus alone is a hysterectomy where ovarian function continues.  Why C/D wrong: Removal of the cervix alone or ovaries alone is not pan-hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After a pan-hysterectomy (hysterosalpingo-oophorectomy), what happens to menstruation and ovarian function?","options":["Both continue normally","Both cease or stop","Only menstruation continues","Only ovarian function continues"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After pan-hysterectomy, both menstruation and ovarian function cease or stop (because the ovaries are removed).  Why A/C/D wrong: Since the ovaries and uterus are removed, neither menstruation nor ovarian function continues."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"In a simple hysterectomy (removal of the uterus), what happens to ovarian function?","options":["Ovarian function ceases immediately","Menstruation ceases but ovarian function continues","Both menstruation and ovarian function continue","Menstruation continues but ovarian function ceases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a hysterectomy (removal of the uterus), menstruation ceases but ovarian function continues (ovaries remain).  Why A wrong: Ovarian function continues because ovaries are not removed.  Why C wrong: Menstruation ceases because the uterus is removed.  Why D wrong: Ovarian function continues, not ceases."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Total hysterectomy is defined as:","options":["Removal of the uterus without the cervix","Total removal of the uterus, including both the uterine body and cervix","Removal of both ligaments and 1/3 of the upper vagina","Removal of the ovaries only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total hysterectomy is total removal of the uterus, including both the uterine body and cervix.  Why A wrong: Removal of the uterus without the cervix is subtotal hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: That describes radical hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Ovary-only removal is oophorectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Subtotal hysterectomy involves:","options":["Removal of the uterus with the cervix","Removal of the uterus without the cervix (cervix left in place)","Removal of the ovaries and tubes","Removal of the upper vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Subtotal hysterectomy is removal of the uterus without the cervix/leaving the cervix in place.  Why A wrong: Removal including the cervix is total hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Removal of ovaries and tubes is part of pan-hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Upper vaginal removal is part of radical hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Radical hysterectomy, also known as the Wertheim-Meigs operation, involves removal of:","options":["The uterus only","Both sides of the ligaments and 1/3 of the upper vagina","Only the cervix","The bladder and rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Radical hysterectomy (Wertheim-Meigs operation) involves removal of both sides of the ligaments and 1/3 of the upper vagina.  Why A wrong: Uterus-only removal is a simple hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Cervix-only removal is not radical hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Bladder/rectum removal describes pelvic exenteration."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Modified radical hysterectomy, also known as Wertheim hysterectomy, involves removal of:","options":["Uterus, cervix, upper part of the vagina, and nearby all ligaments & tissues","Only the uterine body","Ovaries only","Bladder, vagina, and bowel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Modified radical hysterectomy (Wertheim hysterectomy) is removal of the uterus, cervix, upper part of the vagina, and nearby all ligaments and tissues.  Why B/C wrong: These are more limited procedures.  Why D wrong: Removal of bladder, vagina, and bowel describes pelvic exenteration."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Extrafascial hysterectomy, also known as type-1 hysterectomy, is usually done with:","options":["Chemo-radiotherapy","Cryosurgery alone","No adjunct therapy","Bone marrow transplant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extrafascial hysterectomy (type-1 hysterectomy) is usually done with chemo-radiotherapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated combination with chemo-radiotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which types of hysterectomy are illustrated in the \"Types of Hysterectomy\" figure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Partial hysterectomy","Total hysterectomy","Radical hysterectomy","Extrafascial hysterectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels partial, total, and radical hysterectomy.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are labelled in the figure of types of hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Extrafascial hysterectomy is described in text but not labelled in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Following any type of surgery in cervical cancer, for how long should the client be instructed to avoid sexual intercourse?","options":["1 week","3–6 weeks","3–6 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the client to avoid sexual intercourse for 3–6 weeks following any type of surgery in cervical cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3–6 week period."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, which tests are confirmative for cervical cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["HPV test","Colposcopy","Biopsy","BSE"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmative tests for cervical cancer listed are HPV test, colposcopy, and biopsy.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed as confirmative tests.  Why D wrong: BSE (breast self-examination) is a breast cancer screening method, not a cervical test."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"After the age of 30–65 years, the recommended cervical cancer screening intervals are:","options":["HPV test every year and Pap test every year","HPV test every 5 years and Pap test every 3 years","HPV test every 3 years and Pap test every 5 years","HPV test every 10 years and Pap test every 10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 30–65 years, HPV test is done every 5 years and Pap test every 3 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated intervals of HPV every 5 years and Pap every 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Cryosurgery is defined as:","options":["Destruction of cells by freezing the tissues with extremely low temperature","Removal of a cone-shaped area of the cervix","Removal of all pelvic contents","External beam radiation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryosurgery is destruction of cells by freezing the tissues by extremely low temperature.  Why B wrong: A cone-shaped cervical removal is conization.  Why C wrong: Removal of all pelvic contents is pelvic exenteration.  Why D wrong: External beam radiation is teletherapy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the most powerful cryogen used in cryosurgery?","options":["Carbon dioxide","Liquid nitrogen","Argon","Helium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most powerful cryogen is liquid nitrogen.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not cited as the most powerful cryogen in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The temperature usually produced by liquid nitrogen in cryosurgery is:","options":["-50 C or -58 F","-100 C or -148 F","-196 C or -320.8 F","-273 C or -459 F"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The temperature produced by liquid nitrogen is usually -196 C or -320.8 F.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated -196 C / -320.8 F value."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"According to the source, a temperature of -25 to 50 C can be achieved within how many seconds if sufficient liquid nitrogen is used?","options":["10 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","90 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A temperature of -25 to 50 C can be achieved within 30 seconds if sufficient liquid nitrogen is used.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 30-second interval."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Conization of the cervix involves:","options":["Removal of a cone-shaped area of the cervix, after which sexual activity is resumed","Freezing of the cervix","Removal of all pelvic contents","Removal of both ovaries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In conization, a cone-shaped area of the cervix is removed, then sexual activity is resumed.  Why B wrong: Freezing describes cryosurgery.  Why C wrong: Removal of all pelvic contents is pelvic exenteration.  Why D wrong: Removal of both ovaries is bilateral oophorectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Pelvic exenteration is defined as:","options":["Removal of all pelvic contents, including bowel, vagina, and bladder","Removal of a cone of cervix","Freezing of cervical tissue","Removal of the uterus only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pelvic exenteration is the removal of all pelvic contents, including bowel, vagina, and bladder.  Why B wrong: Cone removal is conization.  Why C wrong: Freezing is cryosurgery.  Why D wrong: Uterus-only removal is a hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which statement about ovarian cancer is correct?","options":["It always presents with early symptoms","It is cancer of the ovaries, unilateral or bilateral, and is often asymptomatic in the early stage so most women are diagnosed in advanced stage","It never requires surgery","It is a benign tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ovarian cancer is cancer of the ovaries (unilateral or bilateral) and is often asymptomatic in the early stage, so most women are diagnosed in the advanced stage.  Why A wrong: It is often asymptomatic early, not always symptomatic.  Why C wrong: Surgery (oophorectomy and conjunction surgery) is used.  Why D wrong: It is a malignant cancer, not benign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The surgery for ovarian cancer is:","options":["Oophorectomy and other conjunction surgery","Total laryngectomy","Craniotomy","Pneumonectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The surgery for ovarian cancer is oophorectomy and other conjunction surgery.  Why B wrong: Total laryngectomy is a laryngeal cancer surgery.  Why C wrong: Craniotomy is a brain surgery.  Why D wrong: Pneumonectomy is a lung surgery."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are risk factors for ovarian cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Ovarian dysfunction and irregular menses","Infertility and endometriosis","Genetics (BRCA-1 and BRCA-2 gene mutation)","Early menopause and multiparity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include ovarian dysfunction/irregular menses, infertility/endometriosis, genetics (BRCA-1 & BRCA-2 gene mutation), and early menopause/multiparity.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed. (The source prints \"BARC-1 & BARC-2,\" but the factually correct gene name is BRCA-1 & BRCA-2.)"},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of ovarian cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dysfunctional vaginal bleeding","Gastrointestinal disturbances","Lower abdominal discomfort, pain, or swelling","Abdominal masses, anaemia, vomiting, and constipation","Urinary urgency and frequency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Ovarian cancer signs/symptoms include dysfunctional vaginal bleeding, GI disturbances, lower abdominal discomfort/pain/swelling, abdominal masses, anaemia, vomiting, constipation, and urinary urgency/frequency.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: All are explicitly listed signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which tumor marker is elevated in ovarian cancer?","options":["CA-125","CA-15-3","Bence Jones protein","PSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The elevated tumor marker in ovarian cancer is CA-125.  Why B wrong: CA-15-3 is associated with breast cancer.  Why C wrong: Bence Jones protein relates to multiple myeloma.  Why D wrong: PSA is a prostate marker, not cited here."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which statement about breast cancer is correct?","options":["It is a soft, tender, mobile nodule","It is a hard, non-tender, fixed nodule of the breast, and the most common type is adenocarcinoma originating in ducts and lobes","It is a benign fluid-filled cyst","It never metastasizes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast cancer is a hard, non-tender, fixed nodule of the breast; the most common type is adenocarcinoma originating in ducts and lobes.  Why A wrong: A soft, tender, mobile lump suggests a benign lesion, not cancer.  Why C wrong: A fluid-filled cyst describes fibrocystic disease (benign).  Why D wrong: Breast cancer metastasizes via lymph nodes."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Metastasis of breast cancer occurs via which route, and the MOST COMMON site for metastasis is:","options":["Via blood only; the stomach","Via lymph nodes; bone and lungs (also brain and liver)","Via lymph nodes; the kidney only","Via nerves; the skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast cancer metastasis occurs via the lymph nodes, and the most common site for metastasis is bone and lungs, also occurring in the brain and liver.  Why A wrong: Spread is via lymph nodes, not blood only, and the stomach is not the cited site.  Why C wrong: The kidney is not the cited common metastatic site.  Why D wrong: Spread is via lymph nodes, not nerves."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The confirmative diagnosis of breast cancer is made by:","options":["Pap test","FNAC or breast biopsy","BSE","Bence Jones protein test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmative diagnosis of breast cancer is made by FNAC or breast biopsy.  Why A wrong: Pap test is a cervical screening test.  Why C wrong: BSE is a breast screening method, not confirmative.  Why D wrong: Bence Jones protein relates to multiple myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON location of breast cancer?","options":["Lower inner breast","Upper outer breast (upper outer quadrant)","Central subareolar region","Lower outer breast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common location of breast cancer is the upper outer breast (upper outer quadrant).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated most common upper-outer location."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are risk factors for breast cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Early menarche and late menopause","High-dose radiation exposure to the chest and ERT therapy","Cancer history of the breast, ovary, and uterus; and family history of breast cancer","Nulliparity and late first birth (after 35 years); obesity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Breast cancer risk factors include early menarche/late menopause, high-dose chest radiation, ERT therapy, personal cancer history (breast/ovary/uterus), family history of breast cancer, nulliparity, late first birth (after 35 years), and obesity.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, the BRCA-1 and BRCA-2 genes normally function to:","options":["Repair damaged DNA; when mutated, damaged DNA is not repaired properly, increasing breast cancer risk","Produce breast milk","Cause metastasis directly","Stimulate ovarian function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BRCA-1 and BRCA-2 are special proteins originating in the breast that help repair damaged DNA in normal conditions; if damaged by mutation, DNA is not repaired properly, increasing breast cancer risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the DNA-repair function of the BRCA genes. (The source prints \"BARC,\" but the correct term is BRCA.)"},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Fibrocystic breast disease is characterized by:","options":["Fluid-filled cysts that may feel soft or hard on palpation, caused by hormonal imbalances and caffeine consumption","An encapsulated, non-tender tumor most common in reproductive years","An intraductal lesion within the terminal duct","A soft, fatty, mobile tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fibrocystic breast disease consists of fluid-filled cysts that may feel soft or hard on palpation; causes are hormonal imbalances and caffeine consumption.  Why B wrong: That describes fibroadenoma.  Why C wrong: An intraductal terminal-duct lesion is papilloma.  Why D wrong: A soft, fatty, mobile tumor is a lipoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Fibroadenoma is best described as:","options":["A fluid-filled cyst","An encapsulated, non-tender tumor of the breast that is the most common benign tumor during reproductive years","An intraductal lesion","A soft, fatty tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fibroadenoma is an encapsulated, non-tender tumor of the breast and is the most common benign tumor during reproductive years.  Why A wrong: Fluid-filled cysts describe fibrocystic disease.  Why C wrong: An intraductal lesion is papilloma.  Why D wrong: A soft, fatty tumor is a lipoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A papilloma of the breast is:","options":["An intraductal lesion within the terminal duct that may cause bloody or serous nipple discharge, usually occurring between 40–60 years of age","A fluid-filled cyst","An encapsulated non-tender tumor of reproductive years","A soft, fatty tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Papilloma is an intraductal lesion within the terminal duct; it may cause bloody or serous nipple discharge and usually occurs between 40–60 years of age.  Why B wrong: A fluid-filled cyst is fibrocystic disease.  Why C wrong: An encapsulated non-tender tumor is fibroadenoma.  Why D wrong: A soft, fatty tumor is a lipoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Ductal ectasia of the breast is characterized by:","options":["Inflammatory responses caused by blockage of ducts in the subareolar area, a palpable and tense duct, and greenish-brown nipple discharge, usually in pre- and post-menopausal women","A fluid-filled cyst caused by caffeine","An intraductal papilloma","A soft, fatty, mobile tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ductal ectasia is an inflammatory response caused by blockage of ducts in the subareolar area; the duct is palpable and tense, it may cause greenish-brown nipple discharge, and it usually occurs in premenopausal and postmenopausal women.  Why B wrong: A caffeine-related fluid cyst is fibrocystic disease.  Why C wrong: An intraductal papilloma causes bloody/serous discharge, not greenish-brown.  Why D wrong: A soft, fatty tumor is a lipoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A lipoma of the breast is described as:","options":["A soft, fatty tumor that is mobile and non-tender","A fluid-filled cyst","An inflammatory duct blockage","A malignant fixed nodule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A lipoma is a soft, fatty tumor of the breast that is mobile and non-tender.  Why B wrong: A fluid-filled cyst is fibrocystic disease.  Why C wrong: An inflammatory duct blockage is ductal ectasia.  Why D wrong: A malignant fixed nodule describes breast cancer, not a lipoma."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Endometrial polyps are best described as:","options":["A malignant tumor of the uterine lining","A localised overgrowth of endometrial glands and stroma occurring on the cervix and in the fundus of the uterus, usually benign and stimulated by oxytocin","A benign tumor most common in women who have not been pregnant","A fluid-filled breast cyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometrial polyps are a localised overgrowth of endometrial glands and stroma occurring on the cervix and in the fundus of the uterus; they are usually benign and stimulated by oxytocin, and more common in premenopausal anovulatory women.  Why A wrong: A malignant uterine-lining tumor is endometrial cancer.  Why C wrong: A benign tumor common in never-pregnant women describes uterine fibroids.  Why D wrong: A fluid-filled breast cyst is fibrocystic disease."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Endometrial polyps occur more commonly in which women?","options":["Postmenopausal women with regular ovulation","Premenopausal women who are anovulatory","Pregnant women only","Adolescent girls"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometrial polyps occur more commonly in premenopausal women who are anovulatory.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated premenopausal-anovulatory population."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"The signs and symptoms of endometrial polyps include:","options":["Irregular bleeding between menstrual cycles or metrorrhagia","Coin lesions on X-ray","Bence Jones protein in urine","Papilledema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Signs and symptoms of endometrial polyps include irregular bleeding between menstrual cycles or metrorrhagia.  Why B wrong: Coin lesions are a lung cancer finding.  Why C wrong: Bence Jones protein relates to multiple myeloma.  Why D wrong: Papilledema is a brain tumor sign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Uterine fibroids are characterized as:","options":["A malignant tumor of the uterus","A benign tumor of the uterus, commonly occurring in women who have not been pregnant","A tumor stimulated by oxytocin only","A breast tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine fibroids are a benign tumor of the uterus, commonly occurring in women who have not been pregnant.  Why A wrong: They are benign, not malignant.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin stimulation is described for endometrial polyps.  Why D wrong: They are uterine, not breast, tumors."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of uterine fibroids? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Menorrhagia","Low abdominal discomfort and constipation","Dysmenorrhea and unsuccessful pregnancy","Painless haematuria as an early sign"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine fibroid signs/symptoms include menorrhagia, low abdominal discomfort, constipation, dysmenorrhea, and unsuccessful pregnancy.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Painless haematuria as an early sign is a cervical cancer feature."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Endometrial cancer is best described as:","options":["A benign tumor of the uterus","A malignant tumor of the uterine lining and the most common malignancy of the female reproductive system","A benign breast tumor","A skin cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometrial cancer is a malignant tumor of the uterine lining and is the most common malignancy of the female reproductive system.  Why A wrong: It is malignant, not benign.  Why C/D wrong: It is a uterine malignancy, not a breast or skin tumor."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are risk factors for endometrial cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Use of oestrogen replacement therapy (ERT), nulliparity, PCOD, DUB, and anovulation","Early menarche and late menopause","Family history of uterine cancer, diabetes, obesity, and hypertension","Excessive sun exposure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Endometrial cancer risk factors include ERT use, nulliparity, PCOD, DUB, anovulation, early menarche and late menopause, family history of uterine cancer, diabetes, obesity, and hypertension.  Why A/B/C correct: All three groupings are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Excessive sun exposure is a skin cancer cause, not an endometrial cancer risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of endometrial cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Premenopausal recurrent metrorrhagia","Postmenopausal bleeding","Enlarged uterus","Greenish-brown nipple discharge"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Endometrial cancer signs/symptoms include premenopausal recurrent metrorrhagia, postmenopausal bleeding, and enlarged uterus.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Greenish-brown nipple discharge is a feature of ductal ectasia of the breast."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"What is the screening test for breast cancer?","options":["Mammogram","BSE (breast self-examination)","Breast biopsy","Pap test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The screening test for breast cancer is BSE (breast self-examination); screening means looking for signs.  Why A wrong: Mammogram is listed among diagnostic tests, not the screening test named here.  Why C wrong: Breast biopsy is the most confirmative diagnostic test.  Why D wrong: Pap test screens for cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which of the following are diagnostic tests for breast cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Breast USG","Mammogram","Breast MRI","Breast biopsy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The diagnostic tests for breast cancer (NORCET-5, Mains-2023) are breast USG, mammogram, breast MRI, and breast biopsy.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed diagnostic tests."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Among the diagnostic tests for breast cancer, which is described as the LAST and MOST CONFIRMATIVE, associated with tumor markers CA-15-3 and CA-125 (carcinoembryonic antigen)?","options":["Breast USG","Mammogram","Breast MRI","Breast biopsy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Breast biopsy is listed last and as the most confirmative diagnostic test, with tumor markers CA-15-3 and CA-125 (carcinoembryonic antigen).  Why A/B/C wrong: USG, mammogram, and MRI are imaging tests, not the most confirmative test."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"During BSE, a breast cancer mass is usually felt in which location?","options":["Lower inner quadrant only","The upper outer quadrant of the breast, beneath the nipple, or in the axilla","Only in the axilla","Only beneath the areola"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A mass felt during BSE is usually in the upper outer quadrant of the breast, beneath the nipple, or in the axilla (AIIMS, PGI, JIPMER, ESIC 2017, 2018).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are too restrictive and do not match the stated locations."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of breast cancer? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Bloody or clear nipple discharge and asymmetry of the breast","Axillary lymphadenopathy or enlarged lymph node and lymphedema of the affected arm","A fixed, irregular, non-encapsulated mass and lesion seen on mammography","Nipple inversion/retraction/elevation and skin dimpling/retraction or ulceration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Breast cancer signs/symptoms include bloody/clear nipple discharge and breast asymmetry, axillary lymphadenopathy/enlarged node, lymphedema of the affected arm, a fixed irregular non-encapsulated mass, lesion on mammography, nipple inversion/retraction/elevation, and skin dimpling/retraction or ulceration.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All groupings are explicitly listed signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Cellular adaptations, Chemotherapy, Radiation Therapy, Leukemia…","question":"A nurse teaches that the MOST COMMON site of breast cancer is the:","options":["Lower outer quadrant","Upper, outer quadrant of the breast","Central nipple area","Lower inner quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of breast cancer is the upper, outer quadrant of the breast.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated upper outer quadrant location."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the primary purpose of Breast Self-Examination (BSE)?","options":["Confirmatory diagnosis of breast cancer","Early detection of breast cancer as a screening examination","Staging of an already diagnosed breast tumor","Replacement for mammography in high-risk women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states BSE is a method of early *detection* of breast cancer and is a *screening* examination of the breast, NOT confirmative.  Why A wrong: BSE is explicitly stated to be not confirmative; it does not diagnose cancer.  Why C wrong: BSE screens/detects; it does not stage tumors.  Why D wrong: The source does not present BSE as a replacement for mammography."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"For a reproductive-age woman (15–45 years), when is the best time to perform BSE?","options":["On the first day of menses","7 to 10 days after menses every month","14 days before the next expected period","Any random day, twice a month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for reproductive years women 15–45 years the best time is 7 to 10 days after menses every month.  Why A wrong: Not stated; breast tissue is most nodular/tender during menses, making it a poor time.  Why C wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: Random/twice-monthly timing is not recommended for cycling women."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A 58-year-old post-menopausal woman who had a hysterectomy asks when to perform BSE. What is the correct advice?","options":["7–10 days after her period (she no longer menstruates)","Only when she notices symptoms","Select any specific date of the month and perform BSE monthly on that day","BSE is not needed after hysterectomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs menopausal women and those who had a hysterectomy to select any specific date of the month and perform BSE monthly on that day.  Why A wrong: She no longer has menses, so a post-menses schedule is impossible.  Why B wrong: BSE is a routine screening measure, not symptom-triggered only.  Why D wrong: BSE remains recommended."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"During Steps 1–3 of BSE (inspection), which finding is considered NORMAL?","options":["Dimpling of the skin","The right and left breasts are not exactly the same or symmetrical","New unilateral swelling","A change in the color of one breast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states normally the right and left breast will not be exactly same or symmetrically.  Why A wrong: Dimpling of the skin is an abnormal finding to check for during inspection.  Why C wrong: New swelling is an abnormal change to inspect for.  Why D wrong: A change in color is an abnormal finding to inspect for."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In Steps 1–3 of BSE, in which arm positions is the breast inspected?","options":["Only with arms raised overhead","Arms hanging next to the body, behind the head, and on the side (hips)","Only while lying supine","Only with arms crossed over the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes inspection with the arms hanging next to the body, behind the head, and in the side.  Why A wrong: Overhead-only is incomplete; multiple positions are used.  Why C wrong: Supine inspection/palpation is Step 6, not Steps 1–3.  Why D wrong: Arms crossed is not the described position."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Step 4 of BSE involves palpation of the breast. Which palpation methods are used to detect an unusual lymph node, hard knot, or thickening?","options":["Circular, wedge, and linear (liner) methods","Percussion and auscultation only","Ballottement and transillumination","Deep pressure with a single finger only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Step 4 is palpation by circular, wedge and liner (linear) methods and determines any unusual/unexpected lymph node, hard knot and thickening.  Why B wrong: Percussion/auscultation are not breast palpation techniques described.  Why C wrong: Ballottement/transillumination are not the described methods.  Why D wrong: Flattened fingers with defined patterns are used, not a single finger."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the BSE guideline, when is the best time of day to examine the breasts, and why?","options":["First thing on waking, because tissue is firmest","During bath or shower, because hands move easier over wet skin","After exercise, because circulation is increased","Before bed, because the breasts are most relaxed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source says usually always examine the breasts during bath or shower because hands will move easier over the wet skin.  Why A/C/D wrong: None of these times/reasons are given in the source; the \"Should Remember\" box also reinforces bathing/showering."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"During BSE palpation (Step 4), which hand is used to examine which breast?","options":["Right hand for right breast, left hand for left breast","Right hand for left breast, and left hand for right breast","Both hands simultaneously on the same breast","The dominant hand for both breasts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs using the right hand for the left breast and left hand for the right breast.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse of the stated technique.  Why C wrong: Not the described method (though both hands are used lying down in Step 6).  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the focus of Step 5 of BSE?","options":["Inspection of the breast with arms raised","Palpation of the nipple — gently squeeze each nipple and check for any discharge","Palpation of the breast while lying down","Measuring breast circumference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Step 5 is palpation of the nipple: gently squeeze each nipple and check for any discharge.  Why A wrong: Inspection with arms is Steps 1–3.  Why C wrong: Palpation lying down is Step 6.  Why D wrong: Not part of BSE."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"For Step 6 of BSE (palpation while lying down), how should the client be positioned to examine the LEFT breast?","options":["Place a pillow/folded towel under the left shoulder and put the left arm under the head","Place a pillow under the right shoulder with the right arm raised","Lie prone with both arms at the sides","Sit upright with the left arm hanging down"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place a pillow or folded towel under the left shoulder and put the left arm under the head for the lying-down examination.  Why B wrong: This describes the wrong side for the left breast.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the described position.  Why D wrong: Sitting upright is not Step 6."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"During Step 6 of BSE, how should the fingers be used on the breast?","options":["Press very hard with fingertips in a straight line","Press gently but firmly with the flattened fingers and move in circular motions","Pinch the tissue between thumb and forefinger","Tap the breast lightly with the palm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to press gently but firmly the flattened fingers on the breast and move in circular motions, checking for any lump, hard knot or thickening.  Why A/C/D wrong: None of these techniques are described in Step 6."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are NON-surgical interventions for breast cancer listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Chemotherapy","Radiation therapy","Hormonal therapy","Lumpectomy","Modified radical mastectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists chemotherapy and radiation therapy, and hormonal therapy under Non-Surgical intervention.  Why D wrong: Lumpectomy is a surgical intervention.  Why E wrong: Modified radical mastectomy is a surgical intervention."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In a lumpectomy, which statement is correct according to the source?","options":["The entire breast and nipple are removed","The tumor is excised and removed, lymph node dissection may also be performed, and it may increase chance of metastasis","The pectoral muscles are always removed","Only the lymph nodes are removed, leaving the tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in lumpectomy the tumor is excised and removed, lymph node dissection may also be performed, and it may increase chance of metastasis.  Why A wrong: That describes a mastectomy, not lumpectomy.  Why C wrong: Muscle removal is a feature of radical mastectomy.  Why D wrong: The tumor itself is excised in lumpectomy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source, a \"partial mastectomy\" involves removal of what?","options":["Breast tissue and the nipple only, sparing lymph nodes","Lymph and surrounding breast tissues","Breast, pectoral muscles, and pectoral fascia","Only the tumor with a margin, leaving all lymph tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines partial mastectomy as removal of lymph and surrounding breast tissues.  Why A wrong: That describes simple mastectomy.  Why C wrong: That describes radical mastectomy.  Why D wrong: That more closely describes lumpectomy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In a SIMPLE mastectomy, what is removed and what is spared?","options":["Breast tissue and nipple are removed; lymph nodes are usually left intact/not removed","Breast, nipple, and all axillary lymph nodes are removed","Breast plus pectoral muscles are removed","Only the nipple is removed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states simple mastectomy removes breast tissue and the nipple, and lymph nodes are usually left intact or not removed.  Why B wrong: Node removal is not part of a simple mastectomy per the source.  Why C wrong: Pectoral muscle removal is a radical mastectomy feature.  Why D wrong: Breast tissue and nipple are removed, not only the nipple."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which structures are removed in a RADICAL mastectomy?","options":["Only the breast tissue and nipple","Breast, pectoral muscles and pectoral fascia, plus lymph nodes (pectoral, sub-clavicular, apical & axillary)","Breast and lymph nodes, but pectoral muscles are preserved","Only the tumor and adjacent skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states radical mastectomy removes the breast, pectoral muscles & pectoral fascia and lymph nodes such as pectoral, sub-clavicular, apical & axillary; it is rarely performed.  Why A wrong: That is a simple mastectomy.  Why C wrong: Preserving pectoral muscles describes a MODIFIED radical mastectomy.  Why D wrong: That describes lumpectomy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"How does a MODIFIED radical mastectomy differ from a radical mastectomy?","options":["It removes more lymph nodes","It is the same as radical mastectomy but the pectoral muscles are NOT removed","It removes only the nipple","It removes the pectoral muscles but spares the breast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states modified radical mastectomy is the same as radical mastectomy but the pectoral muscles are not removed.  Why A wrong: It does not remove more nodes; the distinction is muscle preservation.  Why C wrong: The breast and nodes are removed, not just the nipple.  Why D wrong: This is the opposite of the correct description."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which statement about radical mastectomy frequency is TRUE per the source?","options":["It is the most commonly performed breast surgery","It is rarely performed","It is only done for benign lumps","It is the first-line treatment for all breast cancers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly notes radical mastectomy \"is rarely performed.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: None are stated; the source only says it is rarely performed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A client returns to the ward after a mastectomy. What is the correct positioning to promote drainage and prevent lymphoedema?","options":["Flat supine with the affected arm at the side","Semi-Fowler's position, turned to the unaffected side, with the affected arm elevated above the level of the heart on a pillow","Trendelenburg with both arms down","Prone with the affected arm under the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place the client in Semi-Fowler (\"Semi-flower\") position and turn to the unaffected side with the affected arm elevated above heart level on a pillow to promote drainage and prevent lymphoedema.  Why A wrong: Flat with the arm dependent does not promote lymphatic drainage.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not indicated and could worsen facial/head edema.  Why D wrong: Prone with the arm trapped compromises drainage."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which activities should be AVOIDED on the mastectomy (affected) side arm? (Select all that apply)","options":["Heavy weight lifting","Blood draws / venipuncture","Injections","Blood pressure measurement","Gentle ball-squeezing exercises"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source (and the \"Should Remember\" box) states to avoid heavy weight lifting, blood draw, injection, and blood pressure reading with the affected/mastectomy-side arm; no IVs, injections, BP, or venipuncture in that arm.  Why E wrong: Exercising the affected arm (e.g., squeezing a ball) is actually TAUGHT and encouraged, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which exercise is taught to a client to rehabilitate the affected arm after breast surgery?","options":["Overhead barbell presses","Squeezing a ball","Push-ups on the affected side","Carrying heavy grocery bags"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to teach the client about exercising the affected arm such as squeezing a ball.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are forms of heavy lifting/strain, which are to be avoided on the affected arm."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Why is a post-mastectomy client taught coughing and deep breathing?","options":["To promote wound drainage","To prevent pulmonary complications","To reduce lymphoedema","To lower blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to teach coughing & deep breathing to prevent pulmonary complications.  Why A wrong: Drainage is managed by positioning and the surgical drain, not coughing.  Why C wrong: Lymphoedema is prevented by arm elevation and precautions.  Why D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"For a client with a surgical drain after breast surgery, which drainage-management instruction is correct?","options":["Empty the drain only when completely full","Empty before half full; drainage should not exceed >200 ml in 8 hours, maintain suction, ensure tube patency, and avoid tension at the insertion site","Clamp the drain permanently to build back-pressure","Allow 500 ml drainage in 8 hours before notifying the surgeon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to empty the drain before half full, drainage should not exceed >200 ml in 8 hours, maintain suction and record amount/character, ensure patency, and avoid tension at the insertion site.  Why A wrong: The source says empty before half full, not when full.  Why C wrong: Suction/patency is maintained; clamping is not advised.  Why D wrong: The threshold cited is 200 ml/8 hr, not 500 ml."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"When assessing the operative site after breast surgery, the nurse should specifically observe for which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infection","Swelling","Fluid collection under the skin, breast and arm","Bilateral pupil constriction","Character/amount of drainage"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs assessing the operative site for infection, swelling, or the presence of fluid collection under the skin/breast/arm, and to record amount/character of drainage.  Why D wrong: Pupil assessment is unrelated to the breast operative site per the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, which lymph nodes can the client also assess during BSE?","options":["Inguinal and popliteal nodes","Axillary & supraclavicular nodes","Cervical and submandibular nodes","Mediastinal nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in BSE the client can also assess axillary & supraclavicular nodes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These node groups are not the ones cited for BSE self-assessment."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The \"Should Remember\" box recommends palpating breast tissues using which systematic pattern?","options":["Only random spot-checking","Circle, wedge & vertical line","Only vertical strips","Only a single circular sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to palpate the breast tissues using a systematic pattern such as by circle, wedge & vertical line.  Why A wrong: A systematic pattern is required, not random checking.  Why C/D wrong: These are incomplete; the source lists all three patterns."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of oral cancer in INDIA according to the source?","options":["Oral sex due to HPV infection","Pipe smoker or chewing tobacco","Chronic candidiasis","Sun exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of oral cancer in India is pipe smoker or chew tobacco.  Why A wrong: HPV via oral sex is stated as the most common cause in the U.S., not India.  Why C/D wrong: Not cited as causes in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source, what is the MOST COMMON cause of oral cancer in the U.S.?","options":["Chewing tobacco","Oral sex due to HPV infection","Alcohol alone","Pipe smoking"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of oral cancer in the U.S. is oral sex due to HPV infection.  Why A/D wrong: Chewing tobacco/pipe smoking is cited as the most common cause in India.  Why C wrong: Alcohol is a contributing/higher-incidence factor but is not cited as the most common U.S. cause."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Higher incidence of oral cavity cancer is seen in persons who do which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Smoke","Chew tobacco","Drink alcohol","Practice regular oral hygiene","Consume a high-fiber diet"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states higher incidence in persons who are smokers, chew tobacco, or drink alcohol.  Why D/E wrong: Good oral hygiene and a high-fiber diet are not listed as risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Leukoplakia (white patches on the oral mucosa) is best described as:","options":["A benign condition requiring no follow-up","A pre-cancerous stage","Definite invasive carcinoma","A fungal infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes leukoplakia as white patches on mucosa considered a pre-cancerous stage.  Why A wrong: It is pre-cancerous, so follow-up is warranted.  Why C wrong: It is pre-cancerous, not confirmed invasive cancer.  Why D wrong: Not described as fungal (that would be candidiasis)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which clinical features of oral cavity cancer are listed in the source?","options":["Pain and alteration of taste","Painless swelling only","Hyperpigmentation of the tongue","Excessive salivation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists clinical features as pain and alteration of taste.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific features are not listed for oral cavity cancer in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the CARDINAL diagnostic cell/sign of Hodgkin's Lymphoma?","options":["Auer rods","Reed-Sternberg cells (source: \"Red-Sternberg cells\")","Howell-Jolly bodies","Bence-Jones proteins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cardinal sign of Hodgkin's Lymphoma is the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells (printed in the source as \"Red-Sternberg cells\" — the correct standard term is Reed-Sternberg cells).  Why A wrong: Auer rods are associated with acute myeloid leukemia, not Hodgkin's.  Why C wrong: Howell-Jolly bodies indicate asplenia/hyposplenism.  Why D wrong: Bence-Jones proteins are associated with multiple myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In Hodgkin's Lymphoma, lymphadenopathy typically involves which nodes FIRST?","options":["Inguinal nodes","Cervical nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Axillary nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lymphadenopathy in Hodgkin's Lymphoma involves the cervical nodes first.  Why A/C/D wrong: These node groups are not cited as first-involved per the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Hodgkin's Lymphoma is characterized by proliferation of malignant cells within which structure?","options":["Bone marrow only","Lymph node","Spleen only","Peripheral blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Hodgkin's Lymphoma as proliferation of malignant cells within the lymph node.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the lymph node as the site of proliferation."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma primarily affects which cells according to the source?","options":["T-lymphocytes only","B-lymphocytes","Neutrophils","Platelets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma affects B-lymphocytes.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies B-lymphocytes."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for oesophageal cancer per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ingestion of hot spicy foods and alcohol","GERD / Barrett's oesophagus (oesophageal damage from acid reflux)","Smoking","Old age and male gender","Regular low-fat vegetarian diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ingestion of hot spicy foods & alcohol, GERD–Barrett oesophagus (acid reflux damage), smoking, old age, and male gender as risk factors.  Why E wrong: A low-fat vegetarian diet is not listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A client with oesophageal cancer reports difficulty swallowing and heartburn after drinking hot fluids. Which sign/symptom cluster matches oesophageal cancer per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dysphagia and dyspepsia","Pyrosis (heartburn), particularly after ingestion of hot fluids","Weight loss, regurgitation, hoarseness and cough","Malignant cells revealed by biopsy report","Improved appetite and weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dysphagia, dyspepsia, pyrosis/heartburn after hot fluids, weight loss, regurgitation, hoarseness, cough, and malignant cells revealed by biopsy.  Why E wrong: Weight loss (not gain) and poor intake are expected; improved appetite is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the CHOICE of treatment for oesophageal cancer according to the source?","options":["Radiation therapy alone","Surgical removal of the oesophagus","Lifelong antacids only","Hormonal therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the choice of treatment is surgical removal of the oesophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the treatment of choice in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Match the definition: \"Resection of a portion of the oesophagus in which the stomach may be brought up to the remaining end of the oesophagus (gastric pull-through).\" This describes:","options":["Esophagectomy","Esophagogastrostomy","Gastrostomy","Splenectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines esophagogastrostomy as resection of a portion of oesophagus in which the stomach is brought up to the remaining end (gastric pull-through).  Why A wrong: Esophagectomy is removal of part/all of the oesophagus, replaced by a Dacron graft or portion of colon.  Why C wrong: Gastrostomy is a percutaneous opening into the stomach for a feeding tube.  Why D wrong: Splenectomy is removal of the spleen (unrelated)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In an esophagectomy, the removed oesophagus is replaced by what according to the source?","options":["Silicone tube","Dacron graft or a portion of colon","Autologous skin flap","Titanium stent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states esophagectomy removes part or all of the oesophagus, which is replaced by a Dacron graft or portion of colon.  Why A/C/D wrong: These replacement materials are not mentioned in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which procedure is defined as \"an opening directly percutaneously into the stomach in which a feeding tube is inserted to bypass the oesophagus\"?","options":["Gastrostomy","Esophagogastrostomy","Esophagectomy","Whipple procedure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines gastrostomy as a direct percutaneous opening into the stomach with a feeding tube inserted to bypass the oesophagus.  Why B wrong: Esophagogastrostomy is resection with gastric pull-through.  Why C wrong: Esophagectomy is removal of the oesophagus.  Why D wrong: The Whipple procedure is a pancreatic surgery."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON risk factor for stomach (gastric) cancer per the source?","options":["Helicobacter pylori infection","High-fiber diet","Vitamin C excess","Regular exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the risk factor includes Helicobacter pylori infection (most common risk factor).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed; they are protective or neutral."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for stomach cancer listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Helicobacter pylori infection","Spicy foods, smoking, and alcohol","Old age and male gender","GI disorders such as peptic ulcers and IBD","Youth and female gender"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists H. pylori (most common), spicy foods, smoking, alcohol, old age, male gender, and GI disorders (peptic ulcers, IBD, and others).  Why E wrong: Old age and male gender are the risk factors; youth/female is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is stated as the MOST COMMON and EARLY sign of stomach cancer?","options":["Massive hematemesis","Lack of interest in food (anorexia)","Severe abdominal distension","Palpable epigastric mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lack of interest in food or anorexia is the most common & early sign.  Why A/C/D wrong: These may occur but are not identified as the most common early sign."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A client with gastric cancer has a stool test that is positive for occult blood. This corresponds to which finding term used in the source?","options":["Steatorrhea","Melena (source: \"malena\")","Achlorhydria","Pyrosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"stool positive for occult blood or malena (melena)\" as a symptom of stomach cancer.  Why A wrong: Steatorrhea (fatty stool) is associated with pancreatic disease in this text, not gastric occult bleeding.  Why C wrong: Achlorhydria is absence of HCl, a separate listed feature.  Why D wrong: Pyrosis is heartburn, not blood in stool."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Achlorhydria,\" as defined in the stomach cancer section, means:","options":["Excessive secretion of hydrochloric acid","Absence or lack of hydrochloric acid","Low secretion of HCl","Presence of blood in the stomach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines achlorhydria as absence or lack of hydrochloride (hydrochloric) acids.  Why A wrong: That is the opposite (hyperchlorhydria).  Why C wrong: Low secretion of HCl is hypochlorhydria, a separate term.  Why D wrong: That is not the definition of achlorhydria."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Hypochlorhydria\" is defined in the source as:","options":["Low secretion of HCl","Complete absence of HCl","Excess gastric acid","Indigestion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hypochlorhydria as low secretion of HCl.  Why B wrong: Complete absence is achlorhydria.  Why C wrong: Excess acid is the opposite.  Why D wrong: Indigestion is dyspepsia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In the stomach cancer section, \"dyspepsia\" refers to:","options":["Difficulty swallowing","Indigestion","Painful urination","Blood in vomit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines dyspepsia as indigestion.  Why A wrong: Difficulty swallowing is dysphagia.  Why C wrong: Painful urination is dysuria (not mentioned).  Why D wrong: Blood in vomit is hematemesis."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In stomach cancer, a sign of anaemia is usually related to a deficiency of which vitamin, producing what type of anaemia?","options":["Vitamin C deficiency — scurvy","Vitamin B12 deficiency — pernicious anaemia","Iron deficiency — microcytic anaemia","Folate deficiency — megaloblastic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sign of anaemia is usually vitamin B12 deficiency or pernicious anaemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically cites vitamin B12 deficiency/pernicious anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"After a gastrectomy for stomach cancer, why must the client receive lifelong vitamin B12?","options":["To promote wound healing","To prevent pernicious anaemia","To lower blood glucose","To reduce gastric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if gastrectomy was done, administer lifelong Vitamin B12 to prevent pernicious anaemia (or as per order), because intrinsic factor for B12 absorption is lost.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons for lifelong B12."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In stomach cancer, hardness of which lymph nodes is indicative of metastasis per the source?","options":["Inguinal and popliteal nodes","Axillary lymph nodes and left supraclavicular nodes","Cervical nodes only","Mediastinal nodes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess axillary lymph nodes and left supraclavicular nodes for hardness indicative of metastasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These node groups are not the ones cited for gastric metastasis."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In pancreatic cancer, abnormal growth blocking the pancreatic duct can result in which sequence of events per the source?","options":["Fibrosis, pancreatitis, and obstruction of the pancreatic duct","Only jaundice with no other change","Immediate liver failure","Spontaneous resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abnormal growth may block the pancreatic duct resulting in fibrosis, pancreatitis, and obstruction of the pancreatic duct.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's described pathophysiology."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors/causes of pancreatic cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["Excessive intake of alcohol","High-fat diet","Smoking","Diabetes and chronic pancreatic (pancreatitis)","Vegetarian low-fat diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists excessive alcohol, high-fat diet, smoking, diabetes, and chronic pancreatic (pancreatitis) as risk factors/causes.  Why E wrong: A low-fat vegetarian diet is not listed; a high-fat diet is the risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"How is jaundice manifested in pancreatic cancer according to the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight loss","Diarrhoea and steatorrhea","Clay-coloured stool and dark urine","Ascites","Bright red rectal bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states jaundice is manifested by weight loss, diarrhoea & steatorrhea, clay-coloured stool, dark urine, and ascites.  Why E wrong: Bright red rectal bleeding is not listed as a jaundice manifestation here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In pancreatic cancer, amylase and lipase levels typically show which change, and why?","options":["Increased, due to enzyme overproduction","Decreased, because of decreased secretion of enzymes","Unchanged","Increased only when the biliary duct is patent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there is decreased amylase & lipase level because of decreased secretion of enzymes.  Why A wrong: The source states a decrease, not an increase.  Why C wrong: A change (decrease) is described.  Why D wrong: That mischaracterizes the enzyme change."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In pancreatic cancer with biliary duct obstruction, which laboratory values are INCREASED per the source?","options":["Serum bilirubin, SGOT, and SGPT","Amylase and lipase","Serum albumin","Platelet count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states increased serum bilirubin & SGOT & SGPT level when the biliary duct is obstructed.  Why B wrong: Amylase and lipase are decreased.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the elevated values cited."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"For a client with pancreatic cancer, how often should the nurse measure abdominal growth (girth)?","options":["Every 4 hourly","Once daily","Every 12 hours","Only if the client reports pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to measure abdominal growth every 4 hourly (to monitor for ascites).  Why B/C/D wrong: These frequencies are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which complications should the nurse observe for in pancreatic cancer per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Peritonitis and GI obstruction","Jaundice","Hyperglycaemia","Hypotension","Hypertensive crisis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists peritonitis, GI obstruction, jaundice, hyperglycaemia, and hypotension as complications to observe.  Why E wrong: The source lists hypotension, not hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In pancreatic cancer, wound haemorrhage and undigested food in stool are attributed to a deficiency of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to observe stool for undigested foods & wound haemorrhage caused by vitamin K deficiency (vitamin K is needed for clotting factor synthesis).  Why A wrong: B12 deficiency relates to pernicious anaemia in gastric cancer.  Why C/D wrong: These vitamins are not cited for this bleeding risk."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which position is recommended for a client with pancreatic cancer to help prevent pulmonary complications?","options":["Supine flat","Semi-Fowler's position with encouragement of deep breathing and coughing","Left lateral only","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Semi-Fowler's position and encourage deep breathing & coughing to prevent pulmonary complications.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended positions for this purpose."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"To relieve pruritus/itching in a client with pancreatic cancer and jaundice, the nurse should teach:","options":["Frequent use of scented soaps","Use of soap-less bathing and anti-pruritic drugs","Hot water scrubbing of the skin","Application of alcohol-based lotions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to teach about soap-less bathing and anti-pruritic drugs to relieve pruritus/itching.  Why A/C/D wrong: Scented soaps, hot scrubbing, and alcohol lotions dry/irritate skin and worsen itching."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In pancreatic cancer, delayed wound healing is most directly monitored for because of which associated metabolic problem?","options":["Hyperglycaemia (monitor glucose level)","Hypocalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypernatraemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor the glucose level and delayed wound healing due to hyperglycaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These electrolyte disturbances are not the cited cause of delayed healing here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The Whipple procedure is also known as which of the following?","options":["Distal pancreatectomy","Pancreaticoduodenectomy","Splenectomy","Cholecystectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Whipple procedure is also known as pancreaticoduodenectomy.  Why A wrong: Distal pancreatectomy is a different procedure (tail/body removal).  Why C wrong: Splenectomy is removal of the spleen.  Why D wrong: Cholecystectomy is gallbladder removal alone."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which structures are removed in the Whipple procedure (pancreaticoduodenectomy) per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Head of the pancreas","Duodenum","Portion of the stomach","Common bile duct and gall bladder","Entire spleen and both kidneys"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Whipple procedure removes the head of the pancreas, duodenum, portion of stomach, and common bile duct & gall bladder.  Why E wrong: The spleen and kidneys are not part of the described Whipple resection."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Distal pancreatectomy\" is defined in the source as removal of:","options":["The head of the pancreas only","The tail and/or portion of the body of the pancreas, but NOT the head","The entire pancreas plus duodenum","The gall bladder and jejunum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines distal pancreatectomy as removal of the tail and/or portion of the body of the pancreas, but not the head.  Why A wrong: Head removal is part of the Whipple, not distal pancreatectomy.  Why C wrong: That is a more extensive resection.  Why D wrong: That describes cholecysto-jejunostomy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Match the term: \"Removal of the gall bladder and jejunum.\" This describes:","options":["Splenectomy","Chole-cysto-jejunostomy","Whipple procedure","Orchiectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chole-cysto-jejunostomy is removal of the gall bladder and jejunum (as listed in the pancreatic surgery section).  Why A wrong: Splenectomy is removal of the spleen.  Why C wrong: The Whipple removes pancreatic head, duodenum, stomach portion, CBD, and gall bladder.  Why D wrong: Orchiectomy is removal of the testes."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Liver cancer is defined in the source as carcinoma of which cells?","options":["Bile duct epithelial cells (cholangiocytes)","Hepatocellular cells of the liver","Kupffer cells","Stellate cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines liver cancer as carcinoma of the hepatocellular cells of the liver.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies hepatocellular cells, not these other cell types."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which are common risk factors for liver cancer per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatitis B & C","Cirrhosis of the liver","Smoking","Alcoholism and long-term use of steroids","Regular aerobic exercise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Hepatitis B & C, cirrhosis of the liver, smoking, alcoholism, and long-term use of steroids.  Why E wrong: Exercise is not listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which signs and symptoms of liver cancer are listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anorexia, weakness, and malaise","Anaemia, weight loss, bleeding, fever","Jaundice with increased serum bilirubin level","Neurovascular impairment","Polyuria and polydipsia as the earliest signs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists anorexia/weakness/malaise, anaemia/weight loss/bleeding/fever, jaundice (increased serum bilirubin), and neurovascular impairment.  Why E wrong: Polyuria/polydipsia are not listed for liver cancer."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What type of nursing care is initiated for liver cancer per the source?","options":["Curative surgical care only","Palliative & oncological nursing care","Rehabilitation-only care","Cardiac rehabilitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to initiate palliative & oncological nursing care.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the care approaches specified."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is the CARDINAL sign of testicular cancer according to the source?","options":["Painful testicular swelling","Painless testicular swelling","Scrotal ulceration","Bilateral hydrocele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states painless testicular swelling is the cardinal sign.  Why A wrong: The swelling is painLESS, not painful.  Why C/D wrong: These are not cited as the cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A young man with testicular cancer reports a \"dragging or pulling\" sensation. Where is this sensation experienced per the source?","options":["In the lower back","In the scrotum","In the epigastrium","In the groin lymph nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a dragging or pulling sensation is experienced in the scrotum.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the scrotum."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In testicular cancer, which findings indicate METASTASIS per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Palpable lymphadenopathy","Abdominal masses","Gynecomastia","Painless localized swelling only","Improved fertility"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states palpable lymphadenopathy, abdominal masses, and gynecomastia indicate metastasis.  Why D wrong: A localized painless swelling is the cardinal (early) sign, not a metastasis indicator.  Why E wrong: Not a feature; not stated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Orchiectomy\" is defined in the source as:","options":["Surgical removal of the testes","Removal of the prostate","Removal of the spleen","Biopsy of the scrotal skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines orchiectomy as the surgical procedure of removal of testes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different procedures not matching the definition."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"When is the best time to perform Testicular Self-Examination (TSE), and why?","options":["First thing in the morning, when the scrotum is cold and contracted","After shower/bathing, because the scrotal skin is moist and relaxed, making the testicle easy to feel","Immediately after exercise","Only when pain is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the best time for TSE is after shower bathing because the scrotal skin is moist and relaxed, making the testicle easy to feel.  Why A wrong: A cold, contracted scrotum makes examination harder.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"During the first step of TSE, a normal testicle should feel like:","options":["A hard, irregular stone","An egg — firm but not hard, and smooth with no lumps","A soft, fluctuant sac","A pulsatile mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states each testicle should feel like an egg, firm but not hard, and smooth with no lumps (\"lupus\" in the source is a typo for lumps).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe abnormal findings."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In the correct sequence of TSE, what is done in the SECOND and THIRD steps?","options":["Second: place both hands' middle fingers on the testes; Third: gently roll the testicles between thumbs and fingers to feel for lumps, swelling or mass","Second: squeeze the testicle hard; Third: transilluminate with a light","Second: measure scrotal circumference; Third: apply pressure with the palm","Second: examine while lying flat; Third: percuss the scrotum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the second step is using both hands to place the middle fingers on the testes, and the third step is gently rolling the testicles between the thumbs and fingers to feel for any lumps, swelling or mass.  Why B/C/D wrong: None of these match the described steps."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the TSE guideline, if a man notices a change from one month to the next, he should:","options":["Wait 6 months to re-check","Notify his doctor","Apply a warm compress and ignore it","Perform surgery himself"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if you find any changes from one month to the next, notify your doctor.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unsafe/incorrect; prompt medical review is advised."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Superior Vena Cava (SVC) Syndrome occurs when the superior vena cava is:","options":["Dilated by increased blood flow","Compressed or obstructed by growth of a tumor","Surgically ligated intentionally","Filled with normal clot during pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SVC syndrome occurs when the superior vena cava is compressed or obstructed by growth of tumor.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"SVC syndrome is MOST COMMONLY associated with which malignancies per the source?","options":["Lung cancer and lymphoma","Breast cancer and melanoma","Colorectal cancer and prostate cancer","Thyroid cancer and leukemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SVC syndrome is commonly associated with lung cancer and lymphoma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the associations cited."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What is described as the EARLY sign of SVC syndrome, also called \"Stokes sign\"?","options":["Cyanosis and airway obstruction","Oedema on the face and around the eyes, with tightness of the shirt or blouse collar","Bilateral leg swelling","Sudden hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the early sign is oedema on the face and around the eyes, tightness of the shirt/blouse collar (Stokes sign).  Why A wrong: Cyanosis and airway obstruction are life-threatening (late), not early signs.  Why C wrong: Leg swelling is not the described early sign (edema is in face/upper body).  Why D wrong: Hypotension is a late, life-threatening sign."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"As SVC syndrome worsens, which findings are expected per the source?","options":["Oedema in the arms & hands and difficulty in breathing","Improvement of facial swelling","Decreased jugular venous pressure","Weight loss only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when the condition worsens, oedema in the arms & hands and difficulty in breathing will occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect worsening SVC syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which are LIFE-THREATENING signs of SVC syndrome per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Airway obstruction","Haemorrhage","Cyanosis","Mental status changes, decreased cardiac output, and hypotension","Mild collar tightness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists airway obstruction, haemorrhage, cyanosis, mental status changes, decreased cardiac output, and hypotension as life-threatening signs.  Why E wrong: Collar tightness is an EARLY sign (Stokes sign), not life-threatening."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Tumor Lysis Syndrome (TLS) occurs when large quantities of tumor cells are destroyed rapidly and which intracellular components are released faster than the body can eliminate them?","options":["Sodium and chloride","Potassium and uric acid","Calcium and magnesium","Glucose and albumin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intracellular components like potassium and uric acid are released into the bloodstream faster than the body eliminates them.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the cited released intracellular components."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which electrolyte/metabolic abnormalities are signs of Tumor Lysis Syndrome per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hyperkalaemia","Hyperphosphatemia","Hypocalcaemia","Hyperuricemia (which can lead to acute kidney injury, AKI)","Hyponatraemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcaemia, and hyperuricemia (can lead to AKI) as signs of TLS.  Why E wrong: Hyponatraemia is not listed as a feature of TLS in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which medication is given in Tumor Lysis Syndrome to increase the excretion of purine?","options":["Furosemide","Allopurinol","Warfarin","Metformin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to give medications that increase the excretion of purine such as allopurinol.  Why A/C/D wrong: These drugs are not indicated for purine excretion in TLS per the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"To treat the hyperkalaemia of Tumor Lysis Syndrome, the source recommends giving:","options":["Oral potassium supplements","IV infusion of glucose and insulin","Calcium-rich diet","High-dose vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to give IV infusion of glucose and insulin to treat hyperkalaemia (insulin drives potassium into cells).  Why A wrong: Potassium supplements would worsen hyperkalaemia.  Why C/D wrong: These do not treat hyperkalaemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Neoplasm\" is defined in the Oncology Rapid Revision as:","options":["A new growth of tissues without control, which may be benign (non-cancer) or malignant (cancerous)","A decrease in organ size","An increase in cell number only","A viral infection of lymphocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines neoplasm as a new growth of tissues without control, which may be benign (non-cancer) and malignant or cancerous.  Why B wrong: That describes atrophy.  Why C wrong: That describes hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: That describes lymphoma per the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Atrophy\" is defined in the source as:","options":["Increase in organ size due to proliferation of cells","Decrease in the size of an organ due to a decrease in the size and number of cells","Abnormal change in the nature of tissue","Failure of an organ to develop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines atrophy as a decrease in the size of an organ due to a decrease in the size and number of cells.  Why A wrong: That describes hyperplasia.  Why C wrong: That describes metaplasia.  Why D wrong: That describes aplasia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source's Oncology Rapid Revision, \"Hyperplasia\" is defined as:","options":["Increase in the size of an organ due to proliferation (increased number) of cells","Decrease in cell number","Loss of cellular differentiation","Replacement of one cell type by another"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hyperplasia as an increase in the size of an organ due to proliferation of cells.  Why B wrong: That is atrophy.  Why C wrong: That is anaplasia.  Why D wrong: That is metaplasia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Metaplasia\" is best defined per the source as:","options":["Abnormal change in the nature of tissue, or replacement of one type of cell by another type of cell","Failure of an organ to develop","Increase in cell size","Lowest concentration of blood cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines metaplasia as an abnormal change in the nature of tissue or replacement of one type of cells by another type.  Why B wrong: That is aplasia.  Why C wrong: That refers to hypertrophy.  Why D wrong: That is nadir."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Dysplasia\" is defined in the source as:","options":["Abnormal development of cells within a tissue or organ; it is atypical hyperplasia","A normal variation of tissue","Decrease in organ size","A malignant tumor of bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines dysplasia as abnormal development of cells within a tissue or organ — atypical hyperplasia.  Why B wrong: Dysplasia is abnormal, not normal.  Why C wrong: That is atrophy.  Why D wrong: That is sarcoma."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Anaplasia\" is defined in the source as a condition of cells with poor cellular differentiation, losing morphological characteristics of mature cells. What is anaplasia considered to be?","options":["A benign process","The Hallmark of cancer","A normal aging change","A viral effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lack of cellular differentiation or anaplasia is considered the Hallmark of cancer.  Why A/C/D wrong: Anaplasia is the hallmark of malignancy, not benign, aging, or viral."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Aplasia\" is defined in the source as:","options":["The failure of an organ or tissue to develop or to function normally","Increase in cell size","Abnormal replacement of one tissue by another","Lowest WBC concentration after chemotherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines aplasia as the failure of an organ or tissue to develop or to function normally.  Why B wrong: That is hypertrophy.  Why C wrong: That is metaplasia.  Why D wrong: That is nadir."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Undifferentiated cells\" are defined in the source as:","options":["Cells that have lost the capacity for specialised function","Fully mature functional cells","Cells that are increasing in number","Cells lining the epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines undifferentiated cells as cells that have lost the capacity of specialised function.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Carcinoma\" is defined in the source as a tumor arising from which type of cells?","options":["Epithelial cells (e.g., skin, GIT, lungs, urethra, breast; tissue lining organs such as liver & kidneys)","Bone and muscle cells","Blood plasma cells","Lymphocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines carcinoma as a tumor of epithelial cells (skin, GIT, lungs, urethra, breast) — cancer starting in cells that make up the skin or tissue lining organs (liver & kidneys).  Why B wrong: A malignant tumor of bone/muscle is a sarcoma.  Why C wrong: Blood plasma cell cancer is myeloma.  Why D wrong: Lymphocyte cancer is lymphoma/leukemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Carcinoma in situ\" is defined in the source as a lesion present with all histological characteristics of malignancy EXCEPT:","options":["Cellular atypia","Invasion","Abnormal nuclei","Rapid growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states carcinoma in situ is a lesion with all histological characteristics of malignancies except invasion.  Why A/C/D wrong: The distinguishing absent feature per the source is invasion, not these."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Sarcoma\" is defined in the source as a malignant tumor of which tissues?","options":["Epithelial/lining tissues","Bone, muscles, fat, and connective or other non-epithelial tissues","Glandular tissue","Blood plasma cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines sarcoma as a malignant tumor of bone, muscles, fat, and connective or other non-epithelial tissues.  Why A wrong: Epithelial tumor is carcinoma.  Why C wrong: Glandular cancer is adenocarcinoma.  Why D wrong: Plasma cell cancer is myeloma."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Nadir\" is defined in the source as:","options":["The lowest concentration of blood cells (especially WBC) found after taking drugs that suppress the bone marrow such as chemotherapy","The peak level of WBCs during infection","The highest platelet count post-transfusion","The normal RBC baseline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines nadir as the lowest concentration of blood cells, specially WBC, found after taking bone-marrow-suppressing drugs such as chemotherapy (Note also reinforces: \"condition when blood cells at lowest point\").  Why B/C/D wrong: Nadir refers to the lowest point, not peaks or baselines."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Lymphoma,\" per the Oncology Rapid Revision, begins in infection-fighting cells called lymphocytes. Where are these cells present? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lymph nodes","Spleen","Thymus","Bone marrow","Renal cortex exclusively"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lymphocytes are present in lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, bone marrow, and other parts of the body.  Why E wrong: The renal cortex is not cited; lymphocytes are widespread, not renal-exclusive."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Leukemia\" is defined in the source as a cancer of blood or bone marrow in which what happens?","options":["Uncontrolled immature WBCs are produced","Plasma cells multiply in the bone","Epithelial cells become malignant","Lymph nodes enlarge without WBC changes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines leukemia as a cancer of blood or bone marrow in which uncontrolled immature WBCs are produced.  Why B wrong: That describes myeloma.  Why C wrong: That describes carcinoma.  Why D wrong: That is not the definition of leukemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Myeloma\" (multiple myeloma) is defined in the source as a blood cancer arising from which cells?","options":["Blood plasma cells","Epithelial cells","Immature WBCs","Bone osteocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states myeloma (also known as multiple myeloma) is a blood cancer arising from blood plasma cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the origin cells for myeloma per the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Adenocarcinoma\" is defined in the source as cancer of which structures?","options":["The glands (such as pancreas, adrenal, and pituitary gland)","Bone and cartilage","Blood cells","Skeletal muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines adenocarcinoma as cancer of the glands such as pancreas, adrenal, and pituitary gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not glandular tissues cited for adenocarcinoma."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source, which tumor marker is used for COLORECTAL, breast and lung cancers?","options":["CEA","CA-125","PSA","HCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CEA is for colorectal, breast, and lung cancers.  Why B wrong: CA-125 is for GI tract & ovarian cancer.  Why C wrong: PSA is for BPH or prostate cancer.  Why D wrong: HCG is for germ cells & trophoblast tumors."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The tumor marker CA-125 is used for which cancers per the source?","options":["GI tract & ovarian cancer","Prostate cancer","Bladder cancer","Breast cancer only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CA-125 is for GI Tract & ovarian cancer.  Why B wrong: Prostate is PSA.  Why C wrong: Bladder is NMP-22.  Why D wrong: Breast markers are CA-153 / CEA / BRCA genes, not CA-125."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which tumor marker(s) listed in the source are used for BREAST cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["CA-153","BRCA-1 & BRCA-2 gene (source: \"BARC-1 & BARC-2\")","CEA","NMP-22","HCG"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists CA-153 for breast cancer, BRCA-1 & BRCA-2 gene (printed as \"BARC-1 & BARC-2\" — the correct term is BRCA-1/BRCA-2) for breast cancer, and CEA (colorectal, breast, and lung).  Why D wrong: NMP-22 is for bladder cancer.  Why E wrong: HCG is for germ cells & trophoblast tumors."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The tumor marker PSA is used for which condition per the source?","options":["BPH or prostate cancer","Bladder cancer","Ovarian cancer","Germ cell tumors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states PSA is for BPH or prostate cancer.  Why B wrong: Bladder cancer marker is NMP-22.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cancer marker is CA-125.  Why D wrong: Germ cell tumors marker is HCG."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source, HCG is a tumor marker for:","options":["Germ cells & trophoblast tumors","Colorectal cancer","Bladder cancer","Prostate cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HCG is for germ cells & trophoblast tumors.  Why B wrong: Colorectal marker is CEA.  Why C wrong: Bladder marker is NMP-22.  Why D wrong: Prostate marker is PSA."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The tumor marker NMP-22 is used for which cancer per the source?","options":["Bladder cancer","Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer","Lung cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states NMP-22 is for bladder cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These have other markers (CA-153/CEA, CA-125, CEA respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A \"tumor marker\" is best defined by the source as:","options":["A biomarker found in blood, urine, or body tissues that can be elevated in a specialised part of cancer","A surgical staging tool","A chemotherapy drug","A type of imaging scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a tumor marker as a biomarker found in blood, urine, or body tissues that can be elevated (in a specialised part of cancer).  Why B/C/D wrong: A tumor marker is a biomarker, not a staging tool, drug, or scan."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A \"carcinogen\" is defined in the source as:","options":["A physical, chemical & biological stressor that causes neoplastic changes in normal cells","A protective antioxidant","A benign growth","A tumor marker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines carcinogen as a physical, chemical & biological stressor that causes neoplastic changes in normal cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Hospice\" care, per the source, is care for which patients?","options":["Terminally ill patients, such as cancer patients","Newborns","Post-operative surgical patients expected to recover","Outpatients seeking screening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hospice as care of terminally ill patients like cancer patients.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the population described for hospice care."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Cancer\" is defined in the source as:","options":["A disease in which abnormal cells divide uncontrollably and destroy body tissues","A benign, self-limiting growth","A viral infection of the lungs","A decrease in cell number"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines cancer as a disease in which abnormal cells divide uncontrollably and destroy body tissues.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the definition."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"On which date is World Cancer Day celebrated every year per the source?","options":["4 February","7 April","1 December","24 March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states World Cancer Day is celebrated every year on 4 Feb.  Why B wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why C wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why D wrong: 24 March is World TB Day."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"What was the theme of World Cancer Day 2022–24 according to the source?","options":["\"Close the care gap\"","\"I Am and I Will\"","\"We can, I can\"","\"United by Unique\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the theme of World Cancer Day 2022–24 is \"Close the care gap.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other years' themes, not the one cited in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"According to the source, which is the MOST COMMON route of metastasis?","options":["Through the circulatory or blood system","Through the lymphatic system","Through the body wall into the abdominal and chest cavities","Through the skin surface"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Through the circulatory or blood system\" as the most common route of metastasis.  Why B wrong: The lymphatic system is a route but not labeled most common.  Why C wrong: Spread through the body wall into cavities is listed third.  Why D wrong: The skin surface is not a listed route."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are the three routes of metastasis listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Through the circulatory/blood system","Through the lymphatic system","Through the body wall into the abdominal and chest cavities","Through the peripheral nervous system","Through the urinary tract"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists exactly three routes: circulatory/blood (most common), lymphatic, and through the body wall into abdominal/chest cavities.  Why D/E wrong: Nervous system and urinary tract are not listed as routes of metastasis."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which are listed as \"factors influencing cancer development\" in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Environmental, dietary, and genetic factors","Age factor","Immune function factor","Biological factors","Blood group only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists environmental, dietary, genetic, age, immune function, and biological factors influencing cancer development.  Why E wrong: Blood group is not listed as a factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"An \"oncovirus\" is defined in the source as:","options":["A virus that can cause cancer","A vaccine against cancer","A tumor marker","A chemotherapy agent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines an oncovirus as a virus that can cause cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which of the following are listed as oncogenic viruses in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatitis B virus and Hepatitis C virus","EBV (Epstein-Barr Virus)","MCV (Merkel Cell Polyomavirus) and Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus (KSHV)","Human T-Lymphotropic virus (HTLV, an RNA virus) and Human Papilloma virus (HPV)","Rhinovirus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Hepatitis B, Hepatitis C, EBV, MCV (Merkel Cell Polyomavirus), KSHV (Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus), HTLV (RNA virus), and HPV as oncogenic viruses.  Why E wrong: Rhinovirus (common cold) is not an oncogenic virus and is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which oncogenic virus listed in the source is specifically noted to be an RNA virus?","options":["Hepatitis B virus","Human T-Lymphotropic virus (HTLV)","Epstein-Barr Virus","Human Papilloma virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifically notes Human T-Lymphotropic virus HTLV as an RNA virus.  Why A/C/D wrong: HBV, EBV, and HPV are DNA viruses; the source flags only HTLV as RNA."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In the TNM classification of cancer, what do the letters T, N, and M stand for?","options":["T-Tumor, N-Node, M-Metastasis","T-Tissue, N-Nucleus, M-Mitosis","T-Type, N-Number, M-Malignancy","T-Toxicity, N-Nadir, M-Marker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TNM = T-Tumor, N-Node, M-Metastasis, a classification system of the anatomical extent of tumor cancers.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of TNM."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"The TNM system is described in the source as a classification of what?","options":["The anatomical extent of tumor cancers","The chemotherapy regimen","The patient's pain level","The type of surgery required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TNM is a classification system of the anatomical extent of tumor cancers.  Why B/C/D wrong: TNM classifies anatomical extent, not treatment, pain, or surgery."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A post-mastectomy client is due for routine bloodwork and IV antibiotics. The arm on the mastectomy side must be protected. What is the BEST nursing action?","options":["Use the mastectomy-side arm since it is closer to the IV pole","Perform no IVs, injections, blood pressure measurement, or venipunctures in the arm on the mastectomy side; use the unaffected arm","Use either arm interchangeably","Use the mastectomy-side arm only for blood pressure but not IVs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Should Remember\" box states no IVs, no injections, no blood pressure measurement, and no venipunctures should be done in the arm on the mastectomy side, so that arm is protected and any traumatizing intervention is avoided.  Why A/C/D wrong: Any of these use the affected arm, risking lymphoedema and injury, which is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"GERD leading to \"Barrett oesophagus\" contributes to oesophageal cancer through which mechanism per the source?","options":["Oesophageal damage from acid reflux","Bacterial overgrowth","Genetic mutation only","Radiation exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GERD–Barrett oesophagus is oesophageal damage from acid reflux (printed \"reflex\").  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not cited for Barrett's/GERD in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"In oesophageal cancer, malignant cells are confirmed by which investigation per the source?","options":["Biopsy report","Chest X-ray","Complete blood count","Urine analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states malignant cells are present as revealed by biopsy report.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not cited as the confirmatory test for malignant cells."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A client with stomach cancer reports \"belching or eructation (dakkar)\" and a feeling of fullness of the stomach. These symptoms, along with which others, are listed for gastric cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["Heartburn/pyrosis and nausea","Weight loss","Feeling of fullness of the stomach","Dyspepsia (indigestion)","Increased appetite"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists heartburn/pyrosis, nausea, belching/eructation, weight loss, feeling of fullness of the stomach, and dyspepsia (indigestion) among gastric cancer symptoms.  Why E wrong: Anorexia (lack of interest in food), not increased appetite, is the early sign."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"For a client with pancreatic cancer, which nursing monitoring measures are indicated per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Monitor vital parameters and maintain an intake/output (I/O) chart","Measure abdominal growth every 4 hourly","Observe stool for undigested food and monitor for wound haemorrhage","Monitor glucose level","Restrict all fluids completely"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring vital parameters/I&O, measuring abdominal girth every 4 hourly, observing stool for undigested food and wound haemorrhage, and monitoring glucose.  Why E wrong: Complete fluid restriction is not indicated and is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"A client with pancreatic cancer is receiving chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Per the source, the nurse's responsibility regarding these treatments is to:","options":["Give ordered medications, chemotherapy & radiation therapy as per order","Adjust chemotherapy doses independently","Withhold radiation without an order","Discontinue medications when the client feels better"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to give ordered medications, chemotherapy & radiation therapy as per order.  Why B/C/D wrong: Nurses administer per order; they do not independently adjust, withhold, or discontinue these therapies."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Splenectomy,\" as listed among pancreatic cancer surgeries in the source, refers to removal of the:","options":["Spleen","Gall bladder","Pancreatic head","Jejunum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines splenectomy as removal of the spleen.  Why B wrong: Gall bladder removal is cholecystectomy (part of chole-cysto-jejunostomy/Whipple).  Why C wrong: Pancreatic head removal is part of the Whipple.  Why D wrong: Jejunum removal is part of chole-cysto-jejunostomy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"Which patient-education/emotional-support points are part of post-operative breast surgery nursing care per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Teach the client about BSE","Provide emotional support","Initiate other general nursing care","Encourage venipuncture practice on the affected arm","Teach coughing & deep breathing"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs teaching about BSE, emotional support, initiating other general nursing care, and teaching coughing & deep breathing to prevent pulmonary complications.  Why D wrong: Venipuncture on the affected arm is explicitly contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Breast Self-Examination…","question":"\"Hypertrophy\" is listed in the Oncology Rapid Revision terms. Standard physiology defines hypertrophy as an increase in the size of an organ due to an increase in the size of individual cells (without cell division/replication). Which option reflects this concept and the source's stated \"without replication\" detail?","options":["Increase in organ size due to increased cell size, without replication (cell division)","Decrease in organ size due to fewer cells","Replacement of one cell type by another","Failure of an organ to develop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hypertrophy with the phrase \"without replication,\" and the factually correct concept is an increase in organ size from an increase in cell size without cell division. (Note: the printed definition on the page appears garbled/typo-laden — \"Increase size of organs due to decreases size of cell without replication\" — so the factually correct meaning is keyed here per spec guidance.)  Why B wrong: That describes atrophy.  Why C wrong: That describes metaplasia.  Why D wrong: That describes aplasia."}];
